window.STUDY_CONTENT = {"readme": "# 🎯 NISM Series X-A: Investment Adviser (Level 1) — Complete Study Guide\n\n> **Everything you need to clear the exam — explained from first principles, chapter by chapter.**\n> Built to the official NISM-Series-X-A syllabus and test objectives (6 modules · 20 chapters · 150 marks).\n\n---\n\n## 📋 What this exam is actually about (read this first!)\n\nAn **Investment Adviser** is a financial doctor. A doctor examines your body and prescribes medicine. An Investment Adviser examines your **money situation** — what you earn, what you owe, what you're saving for (a house, a child's education, retirement), and how much loss you can stomach — and then prescribes the right financial \"medicines.\"\n\n**Level 1 (Series X-A)** is the *foundation* exam. It tests whether you can:\n\n1. **Plan** — build a personal financial plan, do the maths of money (time value, EMIs, ratios).\n2. **Know the market** — how India's financial system and securities markets are wired, and who regulates whom.\n3. **Know the products** — equity, bonds, derivatives, mutual funds, PMS, AIFs.\n4. **Build a portfolio** — asset allocation, Modern Portfolio Theory, and measuring whether the portfolio actually performed.\n5. **Operate legally and ethically** — KYC and onboarding, the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, ethics, and how a client complains about you.\n\n> **Level 1 vs Level 2 in one line:** Level 1 (X-A) is *markets, products, portfolios and rules*. Level 2 (X-B) is *insurance, retirement, tax and estate planning*. You must pass **both** to be a registered Investment Adviser under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Exam Structure (know this cold — it changes how you study)\n\n| Part | What | Marks |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **Independent MCQs** | 90 questions × 1 mark | **90** |\n| **Caselets (1-mark)** | 6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark | **30** |\n| **Caselets (2-mark)** | 3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks | **30** |\n| **TOTAL** | **135 questions** | **150** |\n\n- ⏱️ **Time:** 3 hours\n- ✅ **Passing score:** 60% — i.e. **90 out of 150 marks**\n- ⚠️ **Negative marking:** 25% of the marks for that question. A wrong 1-mark MCQ costs **−0.25**; a wrong 2-mark caselet answer costs **−0.5**.\n- 💰 **Fee:** ₹3,000 (check nism.ac.in for the current fee)\n- 🧮 **Calculator:** the test machine has a spreadsheet — practise `=PV()`, `=FV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()` until they're automatic.\n\n### 🎯 Where the marks actually live\n\n| Module | Chapters | Marks | Share |\n|--------|----------|-------|-------|\n| 1 · Personal Financial Planning | 1–4 | **37** | 24.7% |\n| 2 · Indian Financial Markets | 5–6 | **10** | 6.7% |\n| 3 · Investment Products | 7–10 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio | 11–13 | **23** | 15.3% |\n| 5 · Portfolio Construction, Monitoring & Evaluation | 14–16 | **20** | 13.3% |\n| 6 · Operations, Regulation, Compliance & Ethics | 17–20 | **30** | 20.0% |\n| | | **150** | 100% |\n\n> **Read that table again.** Module 1 alone is **37 marks** — more than any other — and it is almost entirely *numerical* (time value of money, EMIs, financial ratios). Module 6 is another **30 marks** and is almost entirely *recall* (regulations, ethics, grievance routes). Those two modules together are **45% of the paper**, and they are the two easiest to score in — one with a calculator, the other with flashcards.\n>\n> Module 2 is only **10 marks** — don't over-invest a week in it.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 How to use this study guide\n\nEach chapter folder contains **three files**:\n\n| File | What it is | How to use it |\n|------|-----------|---------------|\n| `notes.md` | **Short Notes** — the whole chapter explained plainly, with every formula *and the logic behind it* | Read first, 2–3 times |\n| `flashcards.md` | **Flashcards** — question on one side, answer on the other | Revise daily; cover the answer and test yourself |\n| `questions.md` | **Question Bank — 100 MCQs**, each with a full explanation (the formula used, *and* why every wrong option is wrong) + a worked caselet | Attempt *after* reading the notes |\n\nEach `questions.md` is organised into three difficulty tiers so you build up gradually:\n\n- 🟢 **Tier 1 — Easy (Recall):** definitions and facts (≈Q1–35)\n- 🟡 **Tier 2 — Medium (Application):** \"which rule applies here\", comparisons (≈Q36–70)\n- 🔴 **Tier 3 — Hard (Numerical & Scenario):** multi-step calculations and exam-style scenarios (≈Q71–100)\n\nEvery answer sits inside a collapsible *\"Answer & Explanation\"* block — attempt first, then expand to see **why the right option is right, why the others are wrong, and the exact calculation behind it.**\n\n---\n\n## ✅ What's here\n\nAll **20 chapters** are complete and machine-validated — notes, flashcards, 100 explained MCQs and a worked 5-part caselet each.\n\n| Module | Chapters | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 · Personal Financial Planning | 1–4 | ✅ Complete |\n| 2 · Indian Financial Markets | 5–6 | ✅ Complete |\n| 3 · Investment Products | 7–10 | ✅ Complete |\n| 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio | 11–13 | ✅ Complete |\n| 5 · Portfolio Construction, Monitoring & Evaluation | 14–16 | ✅ Complete |\n| 6 · Operations, Regulation, Compliance & Ethics | 17–20 | ✅ Complete |\n\n**That's 2,100 explained questions across the six modules**, plus:\n\n| Extra | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| [`financial-maths-primer.md`](financial-maths-primer.md) | Every numerical formula in the syllabus, derived from first principles, with worked examples and a quick-reference table |\n| [`excel-tvm-guide.md`](excel-tvm-guide.md) | The spreadsheet you get in the test centre — `PV`/`FV`/`PMT`/`RATE`/`NPER`, amortisation, `XIRR`, bond and portfolio functions, and the ten errors that cost the most marks |\n| [`mock-papers/`](mock-papers/) | Full-length papers built to the exact 150-mark blueprint (90 MCQs + 6 one-mark caselets + 3 two-mark caselets) |\n\n> **On accuracy:** every `questions.md` is checked by `tools/validate_questions.py`, which re-implements the *exact* regex the study app uses to parse questions — so a malformed option line or a missing answer marker fails loudly instead of silently dropping the question. All 20 chapters pass at 105/105. Numerical answers are cross-checked against the worked calculation in each explanation, but this is an independent study aid, not an official NISM product — **verify current rates, limits and regulations against the official workbook before relying on them.**\n\n**Recommended cycle per chapter:**\n> Read `notes.md` → drill `flashcards.md` → attempt `questions.md` (Easy → Medium → Hard) → re-read the notes for anything you got wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Chapter Map\n\n### Module 1: Personal Financial Planning — *37 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 1 | Introduction to Personal Financial Planning | [`chapter-01`](chapter-01/) |\n| 2 | Time Value of Money | [`chapter-02`](chapter-02/) |\n| 3 | Cash Flow Management and Budgeting | [`chapter-03`](chapter-03/) |\n| 4 | Debt Management and Loans | [`chapter-04`](chapter-04/) |\n\n### Module 2: Indian Financial Markets — *10 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 5 | Introduction to Indian Financial Markets | [`chapter-05`](chapter-05/) |\n| 6 | Securities Market Segments | [`chapter-06`](chapter-06/) |\n\n### Module 3: Investment Products — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 7 | Introduction to Investments | [`chapter-07`](chapter-07/) |\n| 8 | Investing in Stocks | [`chapter-08`](chapter-08/) |\n| 9 | Investing in Fixed Income Securities | [`chapter-09`](chapter-09/) |\n| 10 | Understanding Derivatives | [`chapter-10`](chapter-10/) |\n\n### Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio — *23 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 11 | Mutual Funds | [`chapter-11`](chapter-11/) |\n| 12 | Portfolio Manager | [`chapter-12`](chapter-12/) |\n| 13 | Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) | [`chapter-13`](chapter-13/) |\n\n### Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation — *20 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 14 | Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory | [`chapter-14`](chapter-14/) |\n| 15 | Portfolio Construction Process | [`chapter-15`](chapter-15/) |\n| 16 | Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation | [`chapter-16`](chapter-16/) |\n\n### Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics — *30 marks*\n| # | Chapter | Folder |\n|---|---------|--------|\n| 17 | Operational Aspects of Investment Management | [`chapter-17`](chapter-17/) |\n| 18 | Key Regulations | [`chapter-18`](chapter-18/) |\n| 19 | Ethical Issues | [`chapter-19`](chapter-19/) |\n| 20 | Grievance Redress Mechanism | [`chapter-20`](chapter-20/) |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ 10-Day Study Plan\n\n| Day | Cover | Why |\n|-----|-------|-----|\n| 1 | Ch 1 + Financial Maths Primer | Sets the vocabulary for everything else |\n| 2 | Ch 2 (TVM) + Excel guide | **The single highest-value day.** TVM shows up in Ch 4, 9, 14, 16 and every caselet |\n| 3 | Ch 3–4 (Budgeting, Ratios, Loans) | Finishes the 37-mark module; heavily numerical |\n| 4 | Ch 5–6 (Markets, Segments) | Only 10 marks — read once, flashcard it, move on |\n| 5 | Ch 7–8 (Investments, Stocks) | Valuation ratios are exam favourites |\n| 6 | Ch 9–10 (Bonds, Derivatives) | Bond pricing & duration are the hardest maths in the paper |\n| 7 | Ch 11–13 (MF, PMS, AIF) | Product features + the MF/PMS/AIF comparison table |\n| 8 | Ch 14–16 (MPT, Construction, Performance) | Sharpe/Treynor/Beta/Alpha — pure formula marks |\n| 9 | Ch 17–18 (Operations, Regulations) | Big recall load — start flashcards early |\n| 10 | Ch 19–20 (Ethics, Grievances) + mock papers | Easy marks; then simulate the full 3-hour paper |\n\n> Then: **one full mock paper a day** until the exam, reviewing every wrong answer.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 The 12 Facts You Must Know Cold\n\nBurn these into memory — they recur across chapters and caselets:\n\n1. **Exam:** 150 marks · 135 questions · 3 hours · pass **90/150 (60%)** · **25% negative marking**.\n2. **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ** — and PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ. Everything else in Module 1 is a variation of this.\n3. **Effective annual rate** with *m* compoundings: **(1 + r/m)^m − 1**. Monthly compounding always beats annual.\n4. **Real return** = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 — *not* simply nominal minus inflation.\n5. **Annuity due > ordinary annuity** by exactly one period's interest: **FV(due) = FV(ordinary) × (1 + r)**.\n6. **Solvency ratio** = Net worth ÷ Total assets. **Liquidity ratio** = Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses. **Savings ratio** = Savings ÷ Income.\n7. **EMI** = P × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1], where r is the *monthly* rate and n the number of months.\n8. **Regulator map:** SEBI → securities; RBI → banking, money & forex; IRDAI → insurance; PFRDA → pensions; MCA/RoC → companies.\n9. **Bond price and yield move in opposite directions.** Longer duration = bigger price swing.\n10. **Sharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σ** (total risk). **Treynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ β** (market risk). **Alpha** = actual return − CAPM-expected return.\n11. **IA net worth:** ₹5 lakh (individual) / ₹50 lakh (non-individual); an IA must do **risk profiling** and ensure **suitability**, and must **segregate advice from distribution**.\n12. **Complaint escalation:** the intermediary first → then **SCORES** (SEBI) / **RBI Ombudsman** / **Insurance Ombudsman** / **PFRDA** → then the **Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)**.\n\n*(Each is explained in full inside the relevant chapter — these are just your quick-recall anchors.)*\n\n---\n\n*Made with care. Good luck — you've got this! 💪*\n", "chapters": [{"num": "01", "title": "Introduction to Personal Financial Planning", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks — the biggest module in the paper).**\n> This chapter is the *map*. It doesn't have much maths, but it names every concept the rest of the syllabus expands on. Learn the vocabulary here and the other 19 chapters get easier.\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is Financial Planning?\n\n**Financial planning is the process of meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances.**\n\nUnpack that sentence — every word is doing work:\n\n- **Life goals** — buying a house, funding a child's education, retiring comfortably, leaving money to heirs. Not \"getting rich\"; *specific, dated, costed* objectives.\n- **Process** — it's ongoing, not a one-time event. You plan, act, review, adjust.\n- **Management of finances** — income, expenses, savings, investments, insurance, loans and taxes are all levers.\n\n> 🧠 **The core idea:** money has no meaning on its own. ₹50 lakh is neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" until you attach it to a goal (a flat in Pune? 25 years of retirement?). Financial planning is the discipline of connecting **money you will have** to **money you will need**.\n\n### Investment vs. Financial Planning\n\n| | Investment advice | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Question asked** | \"Which fund should I buy?\" | \"What are you trying to achieve, and what set of decisions gets you there?\" |\n| **Scope** | One product decision | Cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| **Time horizon** | Often short | Whole lifetime |\n| **Success measured by** | Product return | Whether the goal was met |\n\nA product can beat its benchmark and *still* fail you — if it's illiquid when you need the money. That gap is exactly why financial planning exists.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Why is Financial Planning Needed?\n\nFive forces make planning necessary:\n\n1. **Goals cost money and arrive on dates you don't control.** College fees arrive when the child turns 18, not when markets are up.\n2. **Inflation quietly destroys purchasing power.** At 6% inflation, ₹1 lakh of expenses today becomes about ₹3.2 lakh in 20 years. Money kept \"safe\" in a low-interest account *loses* real value.\n3. **Income is finite and lumpy.** Most people earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years. The earning years must fund the non-earning years.\n4. **Life is uncertain.** Death, disability, illness and job loss can wipe out a plan that had no insurance or emergency fund.\n5. **Products are complex and conflicted.** Investors face hundreds of schemes and a sales industry paid to push them.\n\n### 1.2.1 Role of the Financial Planner\n\nThe planner:\n\n- **Collects** data on income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance and taxes;\n- **Quantifies** goals — converting \"a good retirement\" into \"₹4.2 crore by 2050\";\n- **Assesses risk** — both the *ability* and the *willingness* to take risk;\n- **Recommends** a coherent strategy across products (never a product in isolation);\n- **Implements** or helps implement;\n- **Reviews and rebalances** as life and markets change.\n\nCritically, the planner brings **objectivity and discipline**. Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms; a planner is the circuit-breaker between emotion and the \"confirm\" button.\n\n### 1.2.2 Financial Planning vs. a Typical Financial Advisory Service\n\n| Aspect | Typical advisory / distribution | Financial planning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Trigger** | A product is available to sell | A client goal needs funding |\n| **Focus** | Product features and returns | The client's whole balance sheet and cash flow |\n| **Coverage** | One need at a time | Integrated across all needs |\n| **Compensation** | Often commission from the manufacturer | Often a fee from the client |\n| **Duty** | May be a *suitability* standard only | **Fiduciary** — client's interest first |\n| **Relationship** | Transaction | Long-term engagement |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam favourite.** A **fiduciary** must put the client's interest *ahead of their own*. This is the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser (see Chapters 18–19).\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Scope of Financial Planning\n\nFinancial planning has **eight** components. Expect questions that give you a client situation and ask which component applies.\n\n### 1.3.1 Personal Financial Analysis\n\nThe diagnosis stage: where does the client stand *today*?\n\n- Build the **personal balance sheet** (assets, liabilities, net worth) and the **cash flow statement** (income, expenses, surplus).\n- Compute the ratios (Chapter 3) — savings, solvency, liquidity, debt-to-income.\n- Then **set goals**.\n\n**Goal-setting rules:**\n\n- **Prioritise goals.** Not everything can be funded at once. Retirement and a child's education generally outrank a car upgrade — because you can borrow for education but *nobody lends for retirement*.\n- **Ensure adequate focus on important goals.** Long-dated, expensive goals (retirement) get ignored because they feel far away. Yet they benefit most from compounding, so starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n- **Stagger the timing of goals.** If three big goals land in the same year, the plan breaks. Move a discretionary goal (a foreign holiday, a car) forward or back so the funding requirement smooths out.\n\nA good goal is **specific, quantified in future rupees (i.e. inflation-adjusted), and dated**. \"Save for my daughter's college\" is not a goal. \"₹42 lakh in March 2038 for a 4-year engineering degree\" is.\n\n### 1.3.2 Cash Flow Management and Budgeting\nTracking inflows and outflows, generating a **surplus**, and building an **emergency/contingency fund** (commonly 3–6 months of expenses). Without a surplus there is nothing to invest — this is the engine of the whole plan. *(Chapter 3.)*\n\n### 1.3.3 Insurance Planning\nProtecting the plan against catastrophic events — death, disability, illness, property loss. Insurance is about **risk transfer**, not returns. If the earning member dies uninsured, every other goal collapses.\n\n### 1.3.4 Debt Management and Counselling\nKeeping borrowing productive and serviceable — the right *purpose*, *cost*, and *maturity* of debt; repayment strategies; and rehabilitation when a borrower is over-leveraged. *(Chapter 4.)*\n\n### 1.3.5 Investment Planning and Asset Allocation\nChoosing the mix of asset classes (equity, debt, gold, real estate) that matches each goal's horizon and the client's risk profile — then selecting products within that mix. **Asset allocation, not stock selection, drives most of a portfolio's outcome.** *(Chapters 14–15.)*\n\n### 1.3.6 Tax Planning\nArranging affairs to legally minimise tax — using available exemptions, deductions and the tax treatment of different products. Note: tax efficiency is a *modifier*, never the primary reason to buy a product.\n\n### 1.3.7 Retirement Planning\nEstimating the corpus needed to fund post-retirement expenses (inflated to the retirement date) and building it through the earning years. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n### 1.3.8 Estate Planning\nEnsuring assets pass to intended beneficiaries smoothly — wills, nominations, trusts, joint holdings. *(Covered in depth in Level 2.)*\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Assets, Liabilities and Net Worth\n\nThe **personal balance sheet** is a snapshot at a point in time.\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### Classifying assets\n\n| Type | What it is | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liquid assets** | Convertible to cash quickly with little loss of value | Cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs |\n| **Financial assets** | Claims on someone else | Deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, insurance cash values, PF/PPF |\n| **Physical / real assets** | Tangible property | House, land, gold, jewellery, art |\n| **Personal-use assets** | Held for use, not returns | Car, appliances, the home you live in |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap:** *personal-use assets* (car, furniture) generally **depreciate** and produce no income. Counting them heavily inflates net worth but not the ability to fund goals — which is exactly why the **financial assets ratio** exists (Chapter 3).\n\n### Classifying liabilities\n\n- **Short-term:** credit card outstanding, overdrafts, personal loans, dues within a year.\n- **Long-term:** home loan, education loan, vehicle loan.\n\nRecord liabilities at the **outstanding principal** on the statement date — not the original loan amount and not the total of remaining EMIs (that would include future interest).\n\n### Reading net worth\n\n- **Positive and growing** = the plan is working.\n- **Negative** = liabilities exceed assets — technically insolvent; debt management becomes the first priority.\n- Net worth grows in exactly two ways: (a) **saving** out of income, and (b) **appreciation** of assets held.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 The Financial Planning Process\n\nThe six steps — a favourite for sequence questions. Learn the **order**.\n\n| Step | Name | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Establish the **client–planner relationship** | Define scope, services, duration, compensation, and both parties' responsibilities — **in writing** |\n| **2** | Gather data on the **client's financial situation** | Quantitative (income, assets, debts, insurance, taxes) *and* qualitative (attitudes, experience, family) |\n| **3** | Define **client needs and financial goals** | Convert wishes into specific, dated, costed goals; prioritise them |\n| **4** | **Risk profiling** and asset allocation | Assess capacity + willingness to bear risk; derive the allocation |\n| **5** | **Portfolio construction** | Recommend and implement a specific plan/product set |\n| **6** | **Reviewing and rebalancing** | Monitor, report, and correct drift as life and markets change |\n\n### 1.5.1 Client–Planner Relationship\nSets the terms of engagement. The adviser must disclose **what services are provided, how they are paid, and any conflicts of interest**. Getting this in writing protects both sides — and for a registered IA it is a regulatory requirement.\n\n### 1.5.2 Client's Financial Situation\nIncomplete data produces a wrong plan. Collect income, expenses, existing investments, loans, insurance covers, tax status, employer benefits — plus family structure and dependants.\n\n### 1.5.3 Client Needs and Financial Goals\nTranslate \"I want to be comfortable\" into numbers. Apply the goal rules from 1.3.1: prioritise, focus, stagger.\n\n### 1.5.4 Risk Profiling\nTwo distinct things, often confused:\n\n- **Risk capacity (ability)** — *objective*. Driven by horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants and how essential the goal is. A 28-year-old with a stable job and no dependants has high capacity.\n- **Risk tolerance/attitude (willingness)** — *subjective*. Psychological comfort with volatility.\n\n> 🧠 **Rule:** where they conflict, plan to the **lower** of the two. High capacity but low tolerance means a client who panic-sells at the bottom, converting a paper loss into a permanent one. Also note a **risk requirement**: the return the goal actually needs.\n\n### 1.5.5 Portfolio Construction\nAsset allocation first, product selection second. Products must fit the goal's **horizon** (don't fund a 1-year goal with equity) and the client's **liquidity** needs.\n\n### 1.5.6 Reviewing and Rebalancing\nTwo separate triggers:\n\n- **Market drift** — a 60:40 equity:debt mix becomes 70:30 after a rally. Rebalancing sells the winner and buys the laggard, restoring the risk level (and enforcing \"sell high, buy low\").\n- **Life events** — marriage, a child, job change, inheritance, illness. These change the *goals*, not just the weights.\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Financial Advisory and Execution\n\nTwo distinct activities:\n\n- **Advice** — recommending what the client should do. Value comes from the recommendation itself.\n- **Execution / distribution** — actually placing the transaction. Value comes from operational convenience.\n\nThe **conflict**: if the adviser earns commission on what they sell, the recommendation may drift toward whatever pays best. SEBI's answer is the **segregation of advice and distribution** — a registered Investment Adviser must keep advisory separate from any distribution activity, and an individual IA cannot do both. *(Chapter 18 covers the exact provisions.)*\n\n### The IA's value proposition\n\n| Adviser adds value by | Because |\n|---|---|\n| Setting the asset allocation | It drives most of the outcome |\n| Preventing behavioural mistakes | Panic-selling and return-chasing destroy more wealth than bad products |\n| Choosing **direct plans** | Lower expense ratio compounds over decades |\n| Tax-aware structuring | Post-tax return is what the client actually spends |\n| Regular review | Keeps the plan aligned with a changing life |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Financial planning | Meeting life goals through proper management of finances |\n| Net worth | Total assets − total liabilities |\n| Steps in the process | Relationship → Data → Goals → Risk profiling → Construction → Review |\n| Scope (8 areas) | Analysis, cash flow, insurance, debt, investment, tax, retirement, estate |\n| Risk capacity | **Ability** to take risk — objective (horizon, income, net worth) |\n| Risk tolerance | **Willingness** to take risk — subjective (psychology) |\n| Which one binds? | Plan to the **lower** of capacity and tolerance |\n| Fiduciary | Must place the client's interest **ahead of** their own |\n| Goal quality | Specific + quantified in future rupees + dated + prioritised |\n| Why stagger goals | So funding requirements don't collide in one year |\n| Two ways net worth grows | Saving out of income; appreciation of assets |\n| Rebalancing triggers | Market drift and life events |\n| Advice vs execution | Recommending vs transacting — SEBI requires them segregated |\n\n> **Exam tip:** this chapter is mostly definitional, so the questions are *precise*. Watch the difference between **capacity and tolerance**, between **assets and net worth**, and the **order** of the six process steps.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check.\n\n**Q1.** Define financial planning in one sentence.\n> **A:** The process of **meeting your life goals through the proper management of your finances** — connecting money you will have to money you will need.\n\n**Q2.** What are the three things that make a goal a *proper* financial goal?\n> **A:** It is **specific**, **quantified in future (inflation-adjusted) rupees**, and **dated**. \"Save for college\" is not a goal; \"₹42 lakh in March 2038\" is.\n\n**Q3.** What is the formula for net worth?\n> **A:** **Net Worth = Total Assets − Total Liabilities.**\n\n**Q4.** Name the six steps of the financial planning process, in order.\n> **A:** 1) Establish the **client–planner relationship** → 2) Gather the **client's financial situation** → 3) Define **needs and goals** → 4) **Risk profiling** → 5) **Portfolio construction** → 6) **Review and rebalance**.\n\n**Q5.** List the eight components in the scope of financial planning.\n> **A:** Personal financial **analysis**, **cash flow**/budgeting, **insurance**, **debt** management, **investment** planning & asset allocation, **tax**, **retirement**, **estate** planning.\n\n**Q6.** What is the difference between risk *capacity* and risk *tolerance*?\n> **A:** **Capacity = ability**, and it is **objective** (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants). **Tolerance = willingness**, and it is **subjective** (psychological comfort with volatility).\n\n**Q7.** If a client has high risk capacity but low risk tolerance, what do you plan to?\n> **A:** The **lower** of the two. A client who panics and sells at the bottom converts a paper loss into a permanent one, regardless of how much risk they could theoretically afford.\n\n**Q8.** What does \"fiduciary\" mean?\n> **A:** The adviser must place the **client's interest ahead of their own** — the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser.\n\n**Q9.** Why can't you simply borrow your way through retirement?\n> **A:** You can borrow for a house or education, but **nobody lends for retirement**. That is why retirement typically outranks discretionary goals in prioritisation.\n\n**Q10.** What does \"staggering goals\" mean and why do it?\n> **A:** Deliberately spacing goals out in time so several large funding requirements **don't land in the same year** and break the plan. Discretionary goals get moved, not essential ones.\n\n**Q11.** Name the four classes of assets on a personal balance sheet.\n> **A:** **Liquid** assets, **financial** assets, **physical/real** assets, and **personal-use** assets.\n\n**Q12.** Why are personal-use assets treated carefully in a balance sheet?\n> **A:** They generally **depreciate and produce no income**, so counting them inflates net worth without improving the ability to fund goals.\n\n**Q13.** At what value do you record a home loan on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** on the statement date — *not* the original loan amount, and *not* the sum of remaining EMIs (which would include future interest).\n\n**Q14.** What are the only two ways net worth grows?\n> **A:** **Saving** out of income, and **appreciation** of assets already held.\n\n**Q15.** What does a negative net worth mean?\n> **A:** Liabilities exceed assets — the person is **technically insolvent**, and debt management becomes the first priority.\n\n**Q16.** Give three differences between financial planning and typical product-led advisory.\n> **A:** Planning is triggered by a **client goal** (not a saleable product); it is **integrated** across all needs (not one need at a time); and it usually carries a **fiduciary** duty with fee-based compensation.\n\n**Q17.** What are the two triggers for reviewing and rebalancing a portfolio?\n> **A:** **Market drift** (a rally turns 60:40 into 70:30) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\n\n**Q18.** How does rebalancing enforce good behaviour?\n> **A:** It mechanically **sells the asset that has risen and buys the one that lagged** — \"sell high, buy low\" — while restoring the intended risk level.\n\n**Q19.** What is the difference between advice and execution?\n> **A:** **Advice** is recommending what the client should do; **execution/distribution** is placing the transaction. SEBI requires an IA to **segregate** the two.\n\n**Q20.** Why does inflation make financial planning necessary?\n> **A:** It destroys purchasing power — at 6% inflation ₹1 lakh of expenses becomes roughly **₹3.2 lakh in 20 years**, so \"safe\" low-interest money loses real value.\n\n**Q21.** Why is the mismatch between earning years and living years a planning problem?\n> **A:** People typically earn for ~30–35 years but spend for ~60+ years, so the earning years must **fund the non-earning years**.\n\n**Q22.** What is a contingency (emergency) fund and how large is it usually?\n> **A:** A pool of liquid money for income shocks and emergencies, commonly **3–6 months of expenses**.\n\n**Q23.** Which drives portfolio outcomes more — asset allocation or security selection?\n> **A:** **Asset allocation.** It explains most of the variation in portfolio returns, which is why it comes before product selection.\n\n**Q24.** Why should long-dated goals get deliberate focus?\n> **A:** They *feel* far away and get neglected, yet they benefit most from **compounding** — starting early is worth more than investing more later.\n\n**Q25.** What must be disclosed when establishing the client–planner relationship?\n> **A:** The **scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities of both parties, and any conflicts of interest** — in writing.\n\n**Q26.** What two kinds of data are gathered in step 2?\n> **A:** **Quantitative** (income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance, tax status) and **qualitative** (attitudes, risk experience, family structure, dependants).\n\n**Q27.** What is \"risk requirement\"?\n> **A:** The **return the goal actually needs** — distinct from risk capacity (ability) and risk tolerance (willingness).\n\n**Q28.** Why is tax planning never the *primary* reason to buy a product?\n> **A:** Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of return, not a substitute for suitability. A tax-efficient product that doesn't fit the goal's horizon or liquidity need is still the wrong product.\n\n**Q29.** What is the core conflict of interest in commission-based distribution?\n> **A:** The recommendation can drift toward **whatever pays the adviser most**, rather than what suits the client best.\n\n**Q30.** Name five ways an Investment Adviser adds measurable value.\n> **A:** Setting **asset allocation**; preventing **behavioural mistakes**; using **direct plans** (lower expense ratio); **tax-aware** structuring; and **regular review**.\n\n**Q31.** Can a financial plan succeed if the product beats its benchmark?\n> **A:** Not necessarily. A product can outperform and still fail the client — for example if it is **illiquid exactly when the money is needed**. Goal achievement, not product return, is the measure of success.\n\n**Q32.** Insurance planning is about what — returns or protection?\n> **A:** **Risk transfer / protection.** Insurance shields the plan from catastrophic events; it is not bought for returns.\n\n**Q33.** What is the role of the planner as a \"circuit breaker\"?\n> **A:** Investors panic-sell in crashes and chase winners in booms. The planner sits **between emotion and the confirm button**, supplying objectivity and discipline.\n\n**Q34.** Why is ₹50 lakh neither \"enough\" nor \"not enough\" on its own?\n> **A:** Because money has meaning only **relative to a goal**. Enough for a flat in a small town, nowhere near enough for 25 years of retirement.\n\n**Q35.** What four things should debt management get right?\n> **A:** The **purpose**, the **cost**, the **maturity** of the debt — and rehabilitation (counselling/rescheduling) when a borrower is over-leveraged.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 1: Introduction to Personal Financial Planning — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three difficulty tiers — 🟢 Tier 1 (Easy/Recall), 🟡 Tier 2 (Medium/Application), 🔴 Tier 3 (Hard/Numerical & Scenario). Remember: the NISM exam has **25% negative marking**, so a wrong answer costs you a quarter of the marks — guess only when you can eliminate at least two options.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Financial planning is best defined as the process of:\nA) Maximising investment returns  B) Meeting life goals through the proper management of finances  C) Selecting the best-performing mutual fund  D) Minimising the tax payable each year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard definition ties money to **life goals**: financial planning is meeting one's life goals through the proper management of one's finances.\nA) Maximising returns is not the objective; a high-return product that is illiquid when the goal arrives still fails the client. C) Product selection is one small step inside planning, not the definition. D) Tax minimisation is one of the eight components of scope, not the whole discipline.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Net worth is calculated as:\nA) Total income − total expenses  B) Total assets − total liabilities  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Total assets + total liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth is a **balance sheet** concept: everything you own minus everything you owe.\nA) Income minus expenses is the **surplus/savings**, a cash-flow concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Adding liabilities to assets is meaningless — liabilities are a claim *against* assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is the FIRST step in the financial planning process?\nA) Risk profiling of the client  B) Constructing the portfolio  C) Establishing the client–planner relationship  D) Gathering the client's financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The process begins by **defining the engagement**: scope of services, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts, preferably in writing.\nD) Data gathering is step 2 — you cannot ethically collect sensitive financial data before the terms of engagement are agreed. A) Risk profiling is step 4. B) Portfolio construction is step 5.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Risk capacity refers to:\nA) The client's psychological comfort with volatility  B) The client's objective ability to bear risk  C) The return the goal requires  D) The volatility of the recommended portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** is objective and is driven by time horizon, income stability, net worth and number of dependants.\nA) That is risk **tolerance** (willingness) — subjective. C) That is the risk **requirement**. D) That is portfolio risk, an output, not a client attribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of these is NOT one of the components in the scope of financial planning?\nA) Insurance planning  B) Estate planning  C) Corporate merger advisory  D) Retirement planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Corporate merger advisory is **investment banking**, not personal financial planning.\nA), B) and D) are three of the eight components — the others being personal financial analysis, cash flow/budgeting, debt management, investment planning and tax planning.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A fiduciary is required to:\nA) Guarantee the client a minimum return  B) Place the client's interest ahead of their own  C) Disclose returns only on request  D) Recommend only products from a single manufacturer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fiduciary standard requires the adviser to put the **client's interest first**, above the adviser's own commercial interest.\nA) No adviser may guarantee returns. C) Disclosure must be proactive, not on-request. D) Restricting to a single manufacturer is a conflict of interest, the very thing a fiduciary duty guards against.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is a liquid asset?\nA) A residential plot of land  B) Balance in a savings bank account  C) The family car  D) Unlisted equity shares of a private company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A liquid asset can be converted into cash **quickly and without material loss of value** — cash, savings balances, liquid funds, sweep-in deposits.\nA) Land takes months to sell. C) A car is a personal-use asset that depreciates. D) Unlisted shares have no ready market.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The personal balance sheet shows a client's position:\nA) Over a financial year  B) At a specific point in time  C) Only after retirement  D) Averaged over three years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot** on a stated date. The cash-flow (income and expenditure) statement is the one that covers a *period*.\nA) and D) describe flow statements or averages. C) It is prepared at any life stage.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A fixed deposit  B) An equity mutual fund  C) The car used by the family  D) A rented-out second flat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Personal-use assets are held for use rather than for return, and typically **depreciate** — cars, appliances, furniture.\nA) and B) are financial assets. D) A rented-out flat generates income, so it is an investment (physical/real) asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** How should an outstanding home loan be recorded on the personal balance sheet?\nA) The original sanctioned loan amount  B) The total of all remaining EMIs  C) The outstanding principal on the statement date  D) The market value of the house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is a liability today.\nA) The original amount ignores repayments already made. B) The sum of remaining EMIs includes **future interest**, which is not yet owed. D) The house value is an *asset*, recorded on the other side of the balance sheet.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Risk tolerance is best described as:\nA) Objective and measurable from income and horizon  B) Subjective — the client's psychological willingness to bear volatility  C) The same as risk capacity  D) Determined solely by the client's age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tolerance is **attitudinal**: how comfortable the client actually feels when the portfolio falls.\nA) Describes risk capacity. C) The two are distinct and often conflict. D) Age influences capacity, but tolerance is psychological and varies between people of the same age.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A contingency or emergency fund is typically sized at:\nA) 1 month of expenses  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 3–6 months of income  D) 2 years of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The common benchmark is **3–6 months of expenses**, held in liquid form.\nA) One month is too thin to absorb a job loss. C) The standard is measured against *expenses*, not income, because expenses are what must be met. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return liquid assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which statement about advice and execution is correct?\nA) They are the same activity  B) Advice is recommending; execution is transacting  C) Execution must always precede advice  D) Only execution requires SEBI registration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Advice** = recommending what the client should do. **Execution/distribution** = actually placing the transaction. SEBI requires an Investment Adviser to segregate the two.\nA) They are distinct and have different conflicts. C) Advice logically precedes execution. D) Investment *advice* is precisely what triggers IA registration.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following increases a client's net worth?\nA) Transferring money from a savings account to a fixed deposit  B) Saving a part of income  C) Taking a personal loan  D) Repaying the interest portion of an EMI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth grows in only two ways: **saving out of income** and **appreciation of assets held**.\nA) Moving money between assets changes composition, not total net worth. C) A loan raises assets *and* liabilities equally — net worth is unchanged. D) Interest is an expense; only the *principal* portion of an EMI reduces liabilities.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** \"Staggering goals\" means:\nA) Postponing all goals indefinitely  B) Spacing goals in time so funding requirements don't collide  C) Funding only the cheapest goals  D) Investing in staggered maturities of bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If several expensive goals land in the same year the plan breaks, so **discretionary** goals are shifted earlier or later.\nA) Postponing everything is avoidance, not planning. C) Cost is not the prioritisation criterion — importance is. D) That is a bond ladder, a portfolio technique.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which pair correctly matches the concept to its statement?\nA) Surplus — balance sheet  B) Net worth — cash flow statement  C) Net worth — balance sheet  D) Assets — income statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Net worth** belongs to the **balance sheet** (a stock concept).\nA) Surplus (income − expenses) belongs to the cash flow statement. B) and D) reverse the two statements. Remember: balance sheet = *stocks*, cash flow = *flows*.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Inflation matters to financial planning primarily because it:\nA) Increases interest rates on deposits  B) Erodes the purchasing power of money over time  C) Reduces the tax payable on gains  D) Guarantees higher equity returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflation means the same rupees buy less later, so goals must be costed in **future** rupees.\nA) Rates may rise with inflation but that is a side effect, not the planning reason. C) Inflation does not reduce tax. D) No return is guaranteed by inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which of the following is a qualitative input in data gathering?\nA) The client's annual salary  B) The outstanding home loan balance  C) The client's past experience of market losses  D) The premium on a term policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Qualitative** data covers attitudes, prior experience, family structure and preferences.\nA), B) and D) are all **quantitative** — hard numbers from documents.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Asset allocation is primarily the decision about:\nA) Which individual stocks to buy  B) The mix of asset classes in the portfolio  C) Which broker to use  D) When to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Asset allocation is the **mix across asset classes** (equity, debt, gold, real estate).\nA) That is security selection, a later and less impactful decision. C) and D) are operational/compliance matters, not allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which is a long-term liability?\nA) A credit card outstanding due this month  B) A 20-year home loan  C) An overdraft repayable on demand  D) An electricity bill payable next week\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Long-term liabilities run beyond a year — home, education and vehicle loans.\nA), C) and D) are all short-term liabilities due within a year.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The main purpose of insurance planning within a financial plan is:\nA) To generate the highest possible return  B) To transfer the financial impact of catastrophic risks  C) To reduce the client's tax liability  D) To increase the client's net worth quickly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance is about **risk transfer** — protecting the plan from death, disability, illness or property loss.\nA) Insurance is not a return product. C) Tax relief is incidental. D) Insurance protects net worth; it does not build it.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of the following describes the review-and-rebalance step?\nA) It happens only once, at the start  B) It is triggered by market drift and life events  C) It is required only when markets fall  D) It applies only to clients above age 60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two triggers: **market drift** (allocation moves away from target) and **life events** (marriage, child, job change, inheritance, illness).\nA) Review is ongoing, not one-off. C) A rally distorts allocation just as a fall does. D) It applies to all clients.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following is TRUE of a goal-based approach?\nA) Goals should be expressed in today's rupees only  B) All goals should be funded simultaneously and equally  C) Goals should be specific, quantified in future rupees and dated  D) Goals need not be prioritised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A usable goal is specific, costed in **inflation-adjusted future rupees**, and has a date.\nA) Today's rupees understate the requirement. B) Resources are finite, so goals must be prioritised. D) Prioritisation is explicitly part of goal-setting.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The financial planning process step that immediately follows \"defining client needs and goals\" is:\nA) Establishing the relationship  B) Risk profiling and asset allocation  C) Reviewing and rebalancing  D) Gathering financial data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Order: relationship → data → goals → **risk profiling** → construction → review.\nA) and D) come earlier. C) Review is the last step.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which of these is an example of a physical/real asset?\nA) A corporate bond  B) A unit-linked insurance plan  C) Gold jewellery  D) A recurring deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Physical/real assets are **tangible** — property, land, gold, art.\nA), B) and D) are all financial assets, i.e. claims on another party.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A client with a negative net worth is:\nA) In an ideal position for aggressive equity investing  B) Technically insolvent, with debt management as the first priority  C) Required to buy more insurance immediately  D) Unaffected, since net worth is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liabilities exceed assets, which is **technical insolvency**; reducing debt comes before building an investment portfolio.\nA) Aggressive equity while insolvent compounds the problem. C) Insurance matters but is not the first fix. D) Net worth is the central measure of financial health.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of the following best distinguishes financial planning from product distribution?\nA) Planning always uses direct plans  B) Planning is triggered by a client's goal, distribution by a saleable product  C) Distribution is illegal in India  D) Planning is only for high-net-worth clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Distribution starts from **what can be sold**; planning starts from **what the client needs**.\nA) Direct plans are typical of advisory but not the defining difference. C) Distribution is legal and regulated. D) Planning applies at every income level.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which of these is a short-term liability?\nA) A 15-year home loan  B) A 7-year education loan  C) A credit card outstanding  D) A 5-year car loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Credit card dues are payable within the month — a classic short-term liability.\nA), B) and D) all extend well beyond one year.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following is the correct relationship?\nA) Risk requirement = the client's psychological comfort  B) Risk requirement = the return the goal needs  C) Risk requirement = total assets ÷ liabilities  D) Risk requirement = the same as risk capacity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **risk requirement** is the return the goal actually demands, given the amount and the time available.\nA) That is tolerance. C) That is a leverage-type ratio. D) Capacity is the *ability* to bear risk, a different concept.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The client–planner relationship step should ideally document:\nA) Only the adviser's fee  B) Scope, duration, compensation, responsibilities and conflicts  C) The expected portfolio return  D) The client's PAN alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All the engagement terms are documented, in writing, so both sides know the boundaries.\nA) Fee alone is incomplete. C) Returns cannot be promised. D) PAN is collected in onboarding, not the engagement terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which of these best explains why retirement usually outranks a car upgrade in priority?\nA) Cars are always cheaper  B) You can borrow for most goals, but nobody lends for retirement  C) Retirement goals are always nearer in time  D) Regulations require it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loans exist for houses, cars and education; there is **no loan for retirement**, so it must be self-funded.\nA) Cost is not the criterion. C) Retirement is usually the *farthest* goal, which is exactly why it gets neglected. D) No regulation prescribes goal priority.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In the balance sheet, the value of an equity mutual fund holding should be taken at:\nA) The amount originally invested  B) Its current market/NAV-based value  C) The amount net of exit load only  D) Zero, until redeemed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets are recorded at **current market value** so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Loads are transaction costs, not the valuation basis. D) Unrealised holdings are still assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of the following is TRUE about tax planning within financial planning?\nA) It is the primary reason to select any product  B) It modifies returns but does not override suitability  C) It applies only to salaried clients  D) It replaces the need for asset allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tax efficiency is a **modifier** of post-tax return; a tax-friendly product that doesn't fit the horizon or liquidity need is still wrong.\nA) Suitability comes first. C) It applies to all taxpayers. D) Allocation is a separate and more fundamental decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The main behavioural value an adviser adds is:\nA) Predicting market tops and bottoms  B) Preventing panic-selling and return-chasing  C) Guaranteeing outperformance  D) Eliminating all portfolio risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors destroy returns by selling in crashes and buying after rallies; the adviser is the **circuit breaker**.\nA) Market timing is not reliably possible. C) No one can guarantee outperformance. D) Risk can be managed, never eliminated.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which one of these is an output of the financial planning process rather than an input?\nA) The client's monthly income  B) The client's existing insurance covers  C) The recommended asset allocation  D) The client's outstanding loans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **recommended asset allocation** is produced by the process.\nA), B) and D) are all data *collected* in step 2 as inputs.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Rahul, aged 27, has a stable government job, no dependants and a 30-year horizon, but he sold all his equity funds in the last 12% market correction. What should the adviser plan to?\nA) His high risk capacity, since he is young  B) His low demonstrated risk tolerance  C) The average of the two  D) The highest equity allocation available\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where capacity and tolerance conflict, plan to the **lower**. Rahul's *capacity* is high (age, stability, no dependants) but his behaviour reveals **low tolerance**. Allocating aggressively guarantees another panic-sale, converting a temporary paper loss into a permanent one.\nA) Ignores demonstrated behaviour. C) Averaging is not the rule; the binding constraint is the lower of the two. D) The worst possible fit for a proven panic-seller. The adviser can raise equity gradually as Rahul's experience grows.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client owns: savings balance ₹2,00,000; equity funds ₹8,00,000; a house worth ₹60,00,000; a car worth ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan outstanding ₹35,00,000; car loan ₹2,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹39,00,000  B) ₹76,00,000  C) ₹33,00,000  D) ₹41,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = Total assets − Total liabilities. Assets = 2,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 60,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹76,00,000**. Liabilities = 35,00,000 + 2,00,000 = **₹37,00,000**. Net worth = 76,00,000 − 37,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\nB) ₹76,00,000 is total assets only, ignoring all debt. C) ₹33,00,000 wrongly excludes the car from assets. D) ₹41,00,000 omits the car loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client takes a ₹5,00,000 personal loan and deposits it in a fixed deposit. The immediate effect on net worth is:\nA) An increase of ₹5,00,000  B) A decrease of ₹5,00,000  C) No change  D) An increase equal to the FD interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Borrowing raises **assets by ₹5,00,000** (the FD) and **liabilities by ₹5,00,000** (the loan) simultaneously. Assets − liabilities is therefore unchanged.\nA) Ignores the new liability. B) Ignores the new asset. D) Interest accrues *later*, and in practice the loan rate will exceed the FD rate, so net worth will actually *fall* over time.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client says: \"I want my son to study abroad.\" Which is the properly framed goal?\nA) \"Save aggressively for my son\"  B) \"Accumulate ₹75 lakh by June 2035 for a 2-year master's programme\"  C) \"Invest ₹20,000 a month in equity funds\"  D) \"Get the highest return possible before 2035\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is **specific** (2-year master's), **quantified in future rupees** (₹75 lakh), and **dated** (June 2035).\nA) is a vague intention. C) describes a *means*, not a goal — you cannot tell whether ₹20,000 a month is enough without the target. D) has a date but no amount, so success cannot be measured.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An adviser earns a trail commission from a fund house and also charges the client a fee for the same advice. Under SEBI's framework this is problematic because:\nA) Trail commissions are illegal in all cases  B) Advice and distribution must be segregated  C) Fees may not exceed ₹1,000  D) Only banks may earn commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI requires a registered Investment Adviser to **segregate advisory from distribution**; an individual IA cannot provide advice and distribution to the same client, precisely to remove this conflict.\nA) Commissions are legal for distributors, who are not acting as IAs. C) There is a fee cap framework but that is not the issue here. D) Many entities may distribute.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client most urgently needs a contingency fund before starting equity SIPs?\nA) A tenured professor with a 25-year horizon and 6 months of expenses in a liquid fund  B) A freelance designer with irregular income and no liquid savings  C) A salaried employee with 8 months of expenses in a sweep-in FD  D) A retiree with a large annuity and 12 months of liquid assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Irregular income plus **zero liquid savings** is the dangerous combination: any income gap forces redemption of long-term investments, possibly at a loss.\nA), C) and D) all already hold 6 months or more of liquid cover, so they can begin or continue long-term investing.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A 45-year-old client wants to fund a goal that is 9 months away. The most appropriate consideration is:\nA) Maximise equity exposure for the highest return  B) Match the product to the short horizon — capital protection and liquidity  C) Use the client's risk tolerance alone  D) Choose whichever product is most tax-efficient\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **9-month** goal, horizon dominates: the money must be there, in full, on the date. Equity's short-run volatility can easily leave a shortfall.\nA) Nine months is far too short for equity. C) Even a high-tolerance client cannot change the fact that the goal is near. D) Tax efficiency never overrides the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of the following pairs a life event to the correct planning response?\nA) Birth of a child → reduce life insurance  B) Job loss → increase equity SIP amount  C) Inheritance received → review goals and rebalance allocation  D) Marriage → cancel the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An inheritance changes the client's **capacity and resources**, so goals and allocation should be revisited.\nA) A new dependant *increases* the insurance need. B) During job loss the emergency fund is drawn on and SIPs may be paused, not increased. D) Marriage adds responsibilities; the emergency fund becomes more important.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A portfolio set at 60% equity : 40% debt has drifted to 72% : 28% after a strong rally. Rebalancing to target requires:\nA) Buying more equity  B) Selling equity and buying debt  C) Selling debt and buying equity  D) Doing nothing, since equity is performing well\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity has grown beyond target, so you **sell equity and buy debt** to restore 60:40. This both resets the risk level and mechanically enforces \"sell high, buy low\".\nA) and C) push the drift further. D) Leaving it means the client is now carrying materially more risk than the plan intended — precisely when valuations are highest.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which is the strongest argument for recommending direct plans?\nA) Direct plans always outperform in a single year  B) The lower expense ratio compounds over long horizons  C) Direct plans are guaranteed by SEBI  D) Direct plans have no market risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct plans carry **no distribution commission**, so the expense ratio is lower; over decades that cost saving compounds into a meaningfully larger corpus.\nA) Single-year outperformance is not guaranteed and is not the argument. C) SEBI regulates but guarantees nothing. D) Market risk is identical — only the cost differs.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client's balance sheet is dominated by the self-occupied house (85% of assets). The key planning concern is:\nA) Net worth is too high  B) The asset base is illiquid and cannot fund goals  C) The house should be sold immediately  D) There is no concern; property is the safest asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A self-occupied house produces **no income and cannot be partly liquidated**. High net worth on paper coexists with an inability to fund goals — which is why the *financial assets ratio* is tracked.\nA) High net worth is not a problem in itself. C) Selling the home the family lives in is rarely the answer; building financial assets alongside is. D) Property carries market, liquidity and concentration risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which sequence correctly describes goal prioritisation for a 32-year-old with limited surplus?\nA) Foreign holiday → retirement → emergency fund  B) Emergency fund and insurance → retirement and child's education → discretionary goals  C) Retirement → foreign holiday → emergency fund  D) Discretionary goals first, since they are cheapest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Protect first** (emergency fund, insurance), then fund **essential long-term goals** (retirement, education), then **discretionary** wants.\nA) and C) place discretionary spending or long-term goals ahead of basic protection, leaving the plan exposed to a single shock. D) Cost is not the criterion — importance and the consequence of failure are.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An adviser gathers only the client's income and existing mutual fund holdings, then recommends a portfolio. The main deficiency is:\nA) Too much data was collected  B) Liabilities, insurance and goals were not captured, so the plan may be unsuitable  C) Mutual funds should never be recommended  D) Income is irrelevant to planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without **liabilities, insurance cover, tax status and goals**, the adviser cannot know the client's capacity, protection gaps or horizons. Incomplete data produces an unsuitable plan.\nA) The problem is too *little* data. C) Mutual funds are perfectly legitimate. D) Income is essential — it is just not sufficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which of these is the best example of the planner acting as a \"circuit breaker\"?\nA) Switching the client's portfolio to cash during every correction  B) Persuading a client not to redeem their entire equity SIP after a 15% fall  C) Recommending the fund with the highest one-year return  D) Increasing equity allocation after a rally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stopping an emotional, plan-destroying decision at the worst moment is precisely the behavioural value the adviser adds.\nA) Systematically selling into every fall *is* the mistake. C) Chasing recent winners is return-chasing. D) Raising equity after a rally increases risk exactly when valuations are richest.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client aged 55 with 5 years to retirement and a modest corpus has a high risk *requirement* but low risk *capacity*. The appropriate response is:\nA) Take maximum equity risk to close the gap  B) Reset the goal — retire later, save more, or reduce the target corpus  C) Ignore the shortfall  D) Borrow to invest in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the return a goal *needs* exceeds what the client can *safely* pursue, the honest answer is to change the **goal**, not to gamble: extend the working years, raise the savings rate, or lower the target.\nA) Chasing a high required return with 5 years left risks a catastrophic shortfall precisely when there is no time to recover. C) Ignoring it guarantees failure. D) Leveraging into equity near retirement is reckless.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following correctly describes the difference between the balance sheet and cash flow statement?\nA) Both cover a period of time  B) The balance sheet is a snapshot; the cash flow statement covers a period  C) The cash flow statement shows net worth  D) The balance sheet shows monthly surplus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance sheet = **stock** at a point in time; cash flow statement = **flow** over a period.\nA) Only the cash flow statement covers a period. C) Net worth comes from the balance sheet. D) Surplus comes from the cash flow statement.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client insists on investing an emergency fund in a small-cap equity fund \"for better returns\". The adviser should explain that:\nA) Small-cap funds are the ideal emergency vehicle  B) An emergency fund's job is availability of full value on demand, not return  C) Emergency funds are unnecessary  D) The fund will guarantee capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An emergency fund's defining requirement is **liquidity and certainty of value** at the moment of need — which is often exactly when markets are falling and jobs are being lost.\nA) Small-caps are the most volatile and least liquid segment. C) Emergency funds are foundational. D) No equity fund guarantees capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client's goals are: retirement in 25 years, a car in 2 years, and a home down-payment in 4 years. Which allocation logic is correct?\nA) All three funded from the same aggressive equity portfolio  B) Longer-horizon goals can carry more equity; nearer goals need debt/liquid products  C) All three in fixed deposits  D) Nearer goals should carry the most equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon drives allocation.** The 25-year retirement goal can absorb equity volatility; the 2-year and 4-year goals need capital stability.\nA) A single aggressive pot exposes near-term goals to a badly timed drawdown. C) All-FD would likely fail the 25-year goal against inflation. D) Reverses the correct logic entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is a conflict of interest an Investment Adviser must disclose?\nA) The adviser has passed the NISM examination  B) The adviser's family member owns the fund house being recommended  C) The adviser uses a spreadsheet for calculations  D) The adviser has more than 50 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **financial or family interest in a recommended product provider** is a material conflict and must be disclosed.\nA) Certification is a qualification, not a conflict. C) and D) are operational facts with no bearing on the adviser's incentives.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary reason that a product beating its benchmark can still fail a client is:\nA) Benchmarks are always wrong  B) The product may not match the goal's horizon or liquidity need  C) Outperformance is taxed at a higher rate  D) SEBI prohibits benchmark comparison\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Success is measured by whether the **goal** was met. A great fund that is locked in, or volatile just when the money is needed, still fails the client.\nA) Benchmarks are imperfect but that is not the point. C) Tax does not depend on beating a benchmark. D) Benchmark comparison is required, not prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which statement about the eight components of financial planning scope is correct?\nA) They are applied strictly one at a time, in isolation  B) They are interlinked — a decision in one affects the others  C) Only investment planning matters for young clients  D) Estate planning applies only to the very wealthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The components are **integrated**: taking a large home loan changes cash flow, insurance need, tax position and investible surplus at once.\nA) Treating them in isolation is exactly the weakness of product-led advice. C) Young clients still need protection and cash-flow discipline. D) Anyone with assets and heirs benefits from at least a will and correct nominations.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client earns ₹1,20,000 a month and spends ₹1,15,000. The most immediate planning issue is:\nA) The portfolio is wrongly allocated  B) There is almost no surplus to fund any goal  C) The client needs more insurance riders  D) The client should buy a house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A surplus of only ₹5,000 (about **4% of income**) means the *engine* of the plan is barely running. Cash-flow management must come before any allocation discussion.\nA) Allocation is irrelevant when there is nothing meaningful to allocate. C) Insurance matters but is not the binding constraint. D) Adding an EMI to an already stretched budget makes it worse.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which best describes why long-dated goals are frequently under-funded?\nA) They are cheaper than short-term goals  B) They feel distant, so they lose out to immediate wants — despite benefiting most from compounding  C) Regulations discourage long-term saving  D) Compounding does not apply beyond 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Psychological discounting of the distant future means retirement loses to today's spending, yet the long horizon is exactly what makes compounding powerful.\nA) They are usually the *most* expensive. C) Tax rules generally *encourage* long-term saving. D) Compounding grows stronger with time, not weaker.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An adviser recommends the same model portfolio to every client regardless of circumstances. This violates:\nA) Nothing — model portfolios are standard practice  B) The suitability principle, since recommendations must fit the individual client  C) Only the record-keeping rules  D) The requirement to file tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Suitability** requires the recommendation to reflect the client's goals, horizon, risk profile and circumstances.\nA) Model portfolios may be a starting point but cannot replace client-specific assessment. C) Record-keeping is separate. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client's assets are ₹40 lakh and liabilities ₹52 lakh. Which statement is correct?\nA) Net worth is ₹92 lakh  B) Net worth is −₹12 lakh and debt reduction is the priority  C) Net worth is ₹12 lakh  D) Net worth cannot be negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth = 40 − 52 = **−₹12 lakh**. The client is technically insolvent, so **debt management comes first**.\nA) Adds instead of subtracting. C) Ignores the sign — the answer is negative. D) Net worth certainly can be negative, which is the whole warning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these correctly ranks the *impact* on long-run portfolio outcomes?\nA) Security selection > asset allocation > market timing  B) Asset allocation has the largest impact  C) Broker choice > asset allocation  D) Timing the market reliably beats all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Empirical work consistently finds **asset allocation** explains most of the variation in portfolio returns over time.\nA) Reverses the order. C) Broker choice affects costs marginally. D) Consistent market timing is not reliably achievable.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A 60-year-old retiree needs ₹40,000 a month from a ₹90 lakh corpus. The adviser's first check should be:\nA) Whether the corpus can sustain the withdrawal after inflation  B) Which small-cap fund has the best 3-year record  C) Whether the client wants a new car  D) The client's credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The core question is **sustainability**: ₹4.8 lakh a year is a 5.3% withdrawal rate on ₹90 lakh, and that requirement will *rise* with inflation while the corpus must last decades.\nB) Chasing small-cap returns with a drawdown portfolio is dangerous. C) A discretionary want, not the first check. D) Credit score matters for borrowing, not for withdrawal sustainability.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which action best demonstrates fiduciary conduct?\nA) Recommending the product with the highest commission because it also performs adequately  B) Recommending a lower-cost direct plan that pays the adviser nothing extra  C) Not disclosing a family stake in a recommended product  D) Guaranteeing 12% returns to retain a client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choosing the option that is **best for the client even though it earns the adviser less** is fiduciary conduct in its purest form.\nA) Letting commission drive the recommendation is the conflict itself. C) Non-disclosure of a material interest is a breach. D) Guaranteeing returns is both false and prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client wants to fund a wedding in 18 months and retirement in 22 years from one lump sum. The correct approach is:\nA) Invest the whole sum in equity  B) Split by goal — short-horizon money in liquid/debt, long-horizon money in growth assets  C) Invest the whole sum in a savings account  D) Fund the wedding by borrowing and invest everything in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal-bucketing**: each goal gets an allocation matched to *its own* horizon.\nA) Risks an 18-month shortfall. C) Wastes 22 years of compounding on the retirement portion. D) Adds unnecessary interest cost and leverage risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of the following is an example of the \"risk requirement\" driving a plan revision?\nA) A client's income rises, so capacity rises  B) A client feels uneasy about volatility  C) The corpus needed implies a 16% annual return, which is unrealistic, so the goal is reset  D) The adviser changes broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — When the **return the goal demands** is implausible, the plan (not the portfolio) must change — save more, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.\nA) That is capacity. B) That is tolerance. D) Operational, not a planning trigger.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A client holds ₹15 lakh in a savings account \"to be safe\" while paying 11% on a ₹10 lakh personal loan. The adviser should point out:\nA) The savings balance is earning more than the loan costs  B) Using idle low-yield cash to repay high-cost debt gives a certain, tax-free 11% return  C) The loan should be increased  D) Nothing — the arrangement is optimal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying an 11% loan with cash earning ~3% is a **guaranteed 8% improvement**, with no market risk. Paying off debt is a risk-free, tax-free \"return\" equal to the interest saved.\nA) Savings rates are far below 11%. C) More debt worsens it. D) It is clearly sub-optimal — though an emergency buffer should still be retained.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these best reflects the integrated nature of financial planning?\nA) Buying a term policy has no effect on the investment plan  B) A large home loan EMI reduces investible surplus, raises the insurance need and changes the tax position  C) Tax planning is entirely separate from investment planning  D) Estate planning affects nothing else\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — One decision ripples across **cash flow, protection and tax** simultaneously — the essence of integration.\nA) The premium consumes surplus and the cover protects the plan. C) Product tax treatment directly affects post-tax returns. D) Estate decisions interact with ownership, nomination and tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An adviser tells a client \"equity always gives 15% over the long term\". This is inappropriate because:\nA) Equity returns are lower than debt  B) Past returns are not guaranteed and stating them as certain misleads the client  C) Equity is prohibited for retail investors  D) 15% is below inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Presenting a historical average as a **guarantee** is misleading. Returns vary by period, and the client may make decisions on a false premise.\nA) Equity has historically outperformed debt over long periods, but not with certainty. C) Equity is freely available to retail investors. D) 15% would comfortably exceed typical Indian inflation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** In which situation is rebalancing MOST clearly required?\nA) Equity has moved from 60% to 61% of the portfolio  B) A 60:40 portfolio has become 78:22 after a two-year rally  C) The client received a routine account statement  D) A fund changed its logo\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **18-percentage-point drift** means the client is carrying materially more risk than the plan intended.\nA) A 1-point drift is inside any reasonable tolerance band and rebalancing would only incur cost and tax. C) and D) are not portfolio events at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client asks the adviser to simply \"pick the best fund\". The most professional response is:\nA) Name the top-performing fund of the last year  B) Explain that the right fund depends on the goal, horizon and risk profile, and begin the planning process  C) Refuse to help  D) Recommend the fund paying the highest commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is no context-free \"best fund\". Redirecting to **goal, horizon and risk profile** is exactly what distinguishes planning from product-pushing.\nA) Chasing last year's winner is return-chasing. C) Refusal helps no one. D) A direct conflict of interest.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client has assets of ₹1,10,00,000 and liabilities of ₹42,00,000. During the year she saves ₹6,00,000 and her assets appreciate by ₹9,00,000, while she repays ₹3,00,000 of loan principal. What is her closing net worth?\nA) ₹68,00,000  B) ₹83,00,000  C) ₹86,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Work both sides of the balance sheet.\n\n**Opening net worth** = 1,10,00,000 − 42,00,000 = **₹68,00,000**.\n\n**Closing assets** = 1,10,00,000 + 6,00,000 (saved) + 9,00,000 (appreciation) − 3,00,000 (cash used to repay principal) = **₹1,22,00,000**.\n**Closing liabilities** = 42,00,000 − 3,00,000 = **₹39,00,000**.\n**Closing net worth** = 1,22,00,000 − 39,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nShortcut: net worth grows only by **savings + appreciation** = 6,00,000 + 9,00,000 = ₹15,00,000, so 68,00,000 + 15,00,000 = **₹83,00,000**.\n\nA) ₹68,00,000 is the *opening* figure, ignoring the year's growth. C) ₹86,00,000 wrongly adds the ₹3,00,000 repayment as an increase. D) ₹80,00,000 omits part of the appreciation. **Key principle: repaying principal is net-worth neutral** — it swaps ₹3,00,000 of assets for ₹3,00,000 less liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client's goal needs ₹50,00,000 in today's terms, 15 years away, with inflation at 6% p.a. What is the goal value in future rupees (nearest lakh)?\nA) ₹95,00,000  B) ₹1,19,80,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹50,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁵. (1.06)¹⁵ ≈ **2.3966**. So ₹50,00,000 × 2.3966 ≈ **₹1,19,83,000 ≈ ₹1.20 crore**.\nA) ₹95,00,000 roughly applies simple interest (50 × 1.9). C) ₹80,00,000 badly understates the compounding. D) ₹1,50,00,000 assumes a 3× multiple, too high for 6% over 15 years. **This is why goals must be stated in future rupees — the real requirement is 2.4× the sticker price today.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client's monthly income is ₹1,50,000 and monthly expenses ₹1,05,000. What is the savings ratio?\nA) 70%  B) 30%  C) 43%  D) 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Income. Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,05,000 = **₹45,000**. Ratio = 45,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **0.30 = 30%**.\nA) 70% is the *expenses* ratio (1,05,000 ÷ 1,50,000). C) 43% wrongly divides savings by expenses (45,000 ÷ 1,05,000). D) 15% halves the correct answer. Note savings and expenses ratios must sum to 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A client holds: cash ₹50,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; equity funds ₹12,00,000; PPF ₹8,00,000; house ₹75,00,000; car ₹8,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹75,000. What is the liquidity ratio (in months)?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 3.3 months  C) 0.67 months  D) 28.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquidity ratio = **Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses**. Liquid assets = cash ₹50,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹3,00,000**. Ratio = 3,00,000 ÷ 75,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 3.3 months uses only ₹2,50,000. C) 0.67 uses cash alone. D) 28 months wrongly counts equity and PPF as liquid — equity is volatile and PPF is locked in, so neither qualifies. The client has 4 months of cover: acceptable, though the low end of the 3–6 month norm.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's data, what is the client's total asset figure and the financial assets ratio?\nA) ₹1,06,00,000 and 21.7%  B) ₹98,00,000 and 23.5%  C) ₹1,06,00,000 and 78.3%  D) ₹1,06,00,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total assets = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 75,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹1,06,00,000**. Financial assets = cash + liquid fund + equity + PPF = 50,000 + 2,50,000 + 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 = **₹23,00,000**. Ratio = 23,00,000 ÷ 1,06,00,000 = **21.7%**.\nB) Miscounts total assets. C) 78.3% is the *physical* asset share (house + car). D) 11.3% counts only equity. **Interpretation: with 78% of wealth in a house and a car, this client is asset-rich but cannot fund goals — the classic illiquidity trap.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client's goal requires ₹1,00,00,000 in 12 years. He can invest a lump sum of ₹35,00,000 today. What annual return does the goal require?\nA) 6.0%  B) 9.15%  C) 12.5%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **risk requirement**. FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ, so r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,00,00,000 ÷ 35,00,000)^(1/12) − 1 = (2.857)^(0.08333) − 1. ln(2.857) ≈ 1.0498; ÷ 12 = 0.08748; e^0.08748 ≈ 1.0914. So r ≈ **9.15% p.a.**\nA) 6% would give only ₹70.4 lakh. C) 12.5% would overshoot to about ₹1.44 crore. D) 8% gives about ₹88 lakh — short of the goal. In Excel: `=RATE(12,0,-3500000,10000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A 30-year-old plans to retire at 60. His current annual expenses are ₹6,00,000. At 6% inflation, what will his annual expenses be in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹34,46,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Years to retirement = 60 − 30 = **30**. Future expenses = ₹6,00,000 × (1.06)³⁰. (1.06)³⁰ ≈ **5.7435**. So ₹6,00,000 × 5.7435 ≈ **₹34,46,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes only a 3× rise. C) ₹24,00,000 assumes 4×. D) ₹12,00,000 assumes expenses merely double. **The lesson: over 30 years at 6%, costs rise nearly 5.75×** — which is why retirement corpora look shockingly large.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client has a home loan of ₹30,00,000 at 9%, a car loan of ₹5,00,000 at 11%, and a credit card balance of ₹1,50,000 at 42%. She has ₹2,00,000 spare. Under the *avalanche* method she should repay:\nA) The car loan, as it is mid-sized  B) The credit card first, as it has the highest rate  C) The home loan, as it is largest  D) Split equally across all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **avalanche** method attacks the **highest interest rate first**, which minimises total interest paid. At 42%, the card is by far the costliest.\nA) and C) ignore the rate. D) Splitting dilutes the benefit. *(Contrast: the **snowball** method repays the smallest balance first for psychological momentum. Here both happen to point at the card, since it is both costliest and smallest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client wants ₹20,00,000 in 6 years. She invests ₹10,00,000 today at 9% p.a. Will she reach the goal, and what is the gap?\nA) Yes, with a surplus of ₹2.4 lakh  B) No — she reaches about ₹16.77 lakh, a shortfall of about ₹3.23 lakh  C) No — she reaches ₹12 lakh  D) Yes, exactly ₹20 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = 10,00,000 × (1.09)⁶. (1.09)⁶ ≈ **1.6771**. FV ≈ **₹16,77,100**. Shortfall = 20,00,000 − 16,77,100 = **₹3,22,900**.\nA) Overstates growth. C) ₹12 lakh implies simple interest of about 3.3%. D) Would need roughly 12.2% a year. **The adviser's options: invest more now, add a monthly SIP, extend the horizon, or reduce the target.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's portfolio target is 50% equity, 30% debt, 20% gold on a ₹40,00,000 portfolio. Current values: equity ₹24,00,000, debt ₹10,00,000, gold ₹6,00,000. What trade rebalances it?\nA) Sell ₹4,00,000 equity; buy ₹2,00,000 debt and ₹2,00,000 gold  B) Buy ₹4,00,000 equity  C) Sell ₹2,00,000 gold  D) No action needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total = ₹40,00,000. Targets: equity 50% = **₹20,00,000**; debt 30% = **₹12,00,000**; gold 20% = **₹8,00,000**. Current vs target: equity 24 vs 20 → **sell ₹4,00,000**; debt 10 vs 12 → **buy ₹2,00,000**; gold 6 vs 8 → **buy ₹2,00,000**.\nB) Increases the existing overweight. C) Gold is *under*weight, not over. D) Equity is 60% against a 50% target — a 10-point drift that clearly warrants action.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client aged 40 will need ₹80,00,000 in 20 years. Assuming 10% p.a., what lump sum is needed today (nearest ₹1,000)?\nA) ₹11,89,000  B) ₹40,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹18,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ = 80,00,000 ÷ (1.10)²⁰. (1.10)²⁰ ≈ **6.7275**. PV = 80,00,000 ÷ 6.7275 ≈ **₹11,89,150 ≈ ₹11,89,000**.\nB) ₹40,00,000 halves the target arbitrarily. C) ₹8,00,000 divides by 10. D) ₹18,50,000 corresponds to roughly 7.6%. In Excel: `=PV(10%,20,0,-8000000)`. **Note how powerful 20 years is: under ₹12 lakh becomes ₹80 lakh.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Two clients, both aged 35 with identical incomes: A has 3 dependants and an unstable job; B is single with a government job. Which statement is correct?\nA) They must have identical portfolios since age and income match  B) B has higher risk capacity because of income stability and no dependants  C) A has higher risk capacity because of more dependants  D) Risk capacity depends only on age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk **capacity** depends on horizon, **income stability**, net worth and **dependants**. B's secure income and absence of dependants both raise capacity.\nA) Identical age and income do not make identical circumstances. C) More dependants *reduce* capacity — more claims on the same income. D) Age is only one of several drivers.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A ₹25,00,000 portfolio generated a 14% return in a year in which inflation was 6%. What is the approximate real return?\nA) 8.00%  B) 7.55%  C) 20.00%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.14 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.07547 − 1 = **7.55%**.\nA) 8% is the crude subtraction (14 − 6), which always slightly *overstates* the real return. C) 20% adds them. D) 6% is the inflation rate itself. **Use the exact formula — the exam tests precisely this distinction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has monthly income ₹2,00,000, total EMIs ₹70,000, total assets ₹1,50,00,000 and total liabilities ₹60,00,000. What are the debt-to-income ratio and the solvency ratio?\nA) 35% and 1.50  B) 35% and 0.60  C) 35% and 0.40  D) 40% and 0.60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt-to-income = EMIs ÷ Income = 70,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **35%**. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = (1,50,00,000 − 60,00,000) ÷ 1,50,00,000 = 90,00,000 ÷ 1,50,00,000 = **0.60**.\nA) 1.50 divides assets by liabilities — not the solvency ratio. C) 0.40 is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency. D) Miscomputes the EMI share (uses ₹80,000). **Note leverage + solvency = 1**, a useful self-check: 0.40 + 0.60 = 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client can save ₹25,000 a month for 10 years at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹54,80,000  C) ₹43,00,000  D) ₹66,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary annuity FV = PMT × [((1+i)ⁿ − 1) ÷ i], with i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667** and n = 120. (1.0091667)¹²⁰ ≈ **2.9853**. So FV = 25,000 × [(2.9853 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667] = 25,000 × (1.9853 ÷ 0.0091667) = 25,000 × **216.58** ≈ **₹54,14,500 ≈ ₹54.8 lakh** (small rounding differences).\nA) ₹30,00,000 is just the total invested (₹25,000 × 120), ignoring all returns. C) ₹43,00,000 understates the compounding. D) ₹66,00,000 overstates it. In Excel: `=FV(11%/12,120,-25000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: how much of that corpus is the client's own contribution, and how much is return?\nA) ₹30,00,000 contributed; about ₹24,80,000 return  B) ₹25,00,000 contributed; ₹29,80,000 return  C) ₹54,80,000 contributed; nil return  D) ₹12,00,000 contributed; ₹42,80,000 return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total contributed = ₹25,000 × 120 months = **₹30,00,000**. Corpus ≈ ₹54,80,000, so the return component ≈ 54,80,000 − 30,00,000 = **₹24,80,000**.\nB) Uses one month's contribution. C) Ignores returns entirely. D) Understates contributions. **Teaching point: over 10 years roughly 45% of the corpus came from returns — and that share rises steeply with longer horizons.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An adviser is paid a fee by the client *and* a commission by a product manufacturer for the same recommendation, without disclosure. This is:\nA) Acceptable if returns are good  B) A breach of the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations  C) Required by SEBI  D) Acceptable if the client does not ask\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Undisclosed dual compensation creates a **material conflict of interest** and breaches both the fiduciary duty and disclosure obligations; SEBI additionally requires segregation of advisory and distribution.\nA) Good outcomes do not cure a conflict — the process was compromised. C) SEBI requires the opposite. D) Disclosure is proactive; silence is not consent.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client aged 50 has a ₹60,00,000 corpus and needs ₹1,50,00,000 at 60. What return is required, and what should the adviser conclude?\nA) 9.6% — comfortably achievable with a debt portfolio  B) About 9.6% — demanding but feasible with a growth-tilted portfolio, else the goal must be reset  C) 25% — easily achievable  D) 4% — very conservative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (1,50,00,000 ÷ 60,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = (2.5)^(0.1) − 1. ln(2.5) ≈ 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 ≈ 1.0959. So r ≈ **9.6% p.a.**\nA) A pure debt portfolio is unlikely to deliver 9.6% consistently. C) The required return is nowhere near 25%. D) 4% would reach only about ₹88.8 lakh. **The professional answer: 9.6% over 10 years needs real equity exposure — but the client is 50, so if capacity or tolerance won't support it, reset the goal (save more, retire later, or lower the target) rather than over-reach.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's expenses are ₹90,000 a month and he holds ₹1,80,000 in liquid assets. His job is contract-based, renewed annually. What should the adviser advise?\nA) Nothing — 2 months of cover is fine  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months, given the income insecurity  C) Invest the ₹1,80,000 in mid-cap equity  D) Reduce insurance cover to save money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity ratio = 1,80,000 ÷ 90,000 = **2 months**, below the 3–6 month norm. With **contract-based, annually renewed income**, the risk of a gap is high, so the target should sit at the *upper* end — about **₹5,40,000**.\nA) Two months is thin for insecure income. C) Moving the emergency fund into mid-caps defeats its purpose. D) Insecure income makes protection *more* important.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A client compares two options for a 3-year goal: Option A returns 12% but can fall 25% in a bad year; Option B returns 7% with negligible volatility. Which is more appropriate and why?\nA) Option A — higher return always wins  B) Option B — a 3-year horizon cannot absorb a 25% drawdown near the goal date  C) Option A, because 3 years is long term  D) Neither; the client should not invest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With only **3 years**, there is no time to recover from a large drawdown. If the fall happens in year 3, the goal simply fails.\nA) Return cannot be assessed without the horizon and the consequence of failure. C) Three years is *not* long term for volatile assets — equity generally needs 5–7 years or more. D) Not investing guarantees inflation erosion; Option B is the sound middle path.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A client's total assets are ₹80,00,000, of which ₹64,00,000 is a self-occupied house. Liabilities are ₹20,00,000. Compute the solvency ratio and comment.\nA) 0.75 — strong on paper, but wealth is concentrated and illiquid  B) 0.25 — insolvent  C) 1.25 — impossible  D) 0.80 — fully liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 80,00,000 − 20,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Solvency ratio = 60,00,000 ÷ 80,00,000 = **0.75**, which is healthy.\nB) 0.25 is the leverage ratio (20 ÷ 80). C) Solvency cannot exceed 1 unless liabilities are negative. D) The portfolio is anything but liquid. **The nuance the exam wants: a strong solvency ratio can coexist with poor goal-funding ability when 80% of assets sit in a self-occupied house.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination correctly describes a client who should NOT be advised into a 100% equity portfolio?\nA) Age 25, 30-year horizon, stable income, high tolerance  B) Age 58, retiring in 2 years, sole corpus, low tolerance  C) Age 30, 20-year goal, secure job  D) Age 35, 25-year retirement goal, comfortable with volatility\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short horizon (**2 years**), the corpus is the **only** one, and **low tolerance** — every factor argues against full equity. A market fall just before retirement would be unrecoverable.\nA), C) and D) all combine long horizons with adequate capacity, so meaningful equity is defensible.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client will receive a ₹40,00,000 inheritance. Goals: clear a ₹12,00,000 loan at 13%, build a ₹4,50,000 emergency fund, and invest the rest for retirement in 20 years. What is the most sensible sequence?\nA) Invest all ₹40,00,000 in equity, keep paying the loan  B) Repay the 13% loan, fund the emergency corpus, invest the remaining ₹23,50,000 for retirement  C) Keep it all in a savings account  D) Repay the loan only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repaying a **13%** loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 13% return** — very hard to beat with certainty. Then secure the **₹4,50,000** emergency buffer. The remaining ₹40,00,000 − 12,00,000 − 4,50,000 = **₹23,50,000** goes to the 20-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) Carrying 13% debt while chasing uncertain returns is poor risk-adjusted logic. C) Guarantees inflation erosion over 20 years. D) Leaves the client without a buffer and wastes the compounding opportunity.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A goal needs ₹30,00,000 in 8 years. The client invests ₹18,000 a month at 10% p.a. (monthly compounding). Does she reach it?\nA) Yes, with a large surplus  B) She reaches about ₹26.4 lakh — a shortfall of roughly ₹3.6 lakh  C) She reaches exactly ₹30,00,000  D) She reaches about ₹17.3 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — i = 10% ÷ 12 = **0.008333**, n = 96. (1.008333)⁹⁶ ≈ **2.2182**. FV = 18,000 × [(2.2182 − 1) ÷ 0.008333] = 18,000 × (1.2182 ÷ 0.008333) = 18,000 × **146.19** ≈ **₹26,31,000**. Shortfall ≈ **₹3.69 lakh**.\nA) There is no surplus. C) She would need about ₹20,500 a month. D) ₹17.3 lakh is roughly the un-compounded total (₹18,000 × 96 = ₹17.28 lakh) — which is exactly the *contribution*, not the corpus. In Excel: `=FV(10%/12,96,-18000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Continuing Q94: what monthly investment would exactly meet the ₹30,00,000 goal?\nA) ₹18,000  B) About ₹20,520  C) About ₹25,000  D) About ₹31,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMT = FV ÷ annuity factor = 30,00,000 ÷ **146.19** ≈ **₹20,522**.\nA) ₹18,000 is the shortfall case from Q94. C) ₹25,000 would overshoot to roughly ₹36.5 lakh. D) ₹31,250 is simply ₹30,00,000 ÷ 96, ignoring all returns. In Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,96,0,3000000)`. **A ₹2,500 a month increase closes the entire gap — the kind of concrete, actionable answer a planner is paid for.**\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An adviser prepares a plan without asking about the client's existing life insurance. Six months later the sole earner dies with no cover, and the family's goals collapse. The principal failure was in which step?\nA) Step 1 — establishing the relationship  B) Step 2 — gathering the client's financial situation  C) Step 5 — portfolio construction  D) Step 6 — review and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Data gathering** must capture existing insurance cover; without it the protection gap is invisible and the plan is built on incomplete facts.\nA) The engagement terms were not the failure. C) The portfolio may have been fine — the *protection* was missing. D) Review comes later and cannot repair a foundational data gap. **Insurance planning is one of the eight components precisely because an uninsured death destroys every other goal.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client's portfolio target is 70:30 equity:debt with a ±5 percentage-point tolerance band. Current allocation is 73:27. The correct action is:\nA) Rebalance immediately  B) No action — the drift is inside the band  C) Move to 100% debt  D) Increase equity to 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The drift is **3 percentage points**, inside the ±5-point band. Rebalancing anyway would incur **transaction costs and capital gains tax** for no meaningful change in risk.\nA) Premature — bands exist exactly to prevent over-trading. C) An extreme move unrelated to the plan. D) Deliberately pushes the drift beyond the band.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client earns ₹18,00,000 a year, spends ₹13,50,000 and pays ₹2,40,000 in EMIs (included in expenses). What is the savings ratio, and is the debt burden reasonable?\nA) 25% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — both healthy  B) 33% savings; debt-to-income 20% — both healthy  C) 25% savings; debt-to-income 40% — debt is excessive  D) 15% savings; debt-to-income 13.3% — savings too low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 18,00,000 − 13,50,000 = **₹4,50,000**; savings ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **25%**. Debt-to-income = 2,40,000 ÷ 18,00,000 = **13.3%**, comfortably under the ~35–40% guideline.\nB) Miscomputes both. C) Overstates the EMI burden. D) Understates the savings ratio. **A 25% savings rate with a 13% debt burden is a genuinely strong position — the client can afford to invest toward long-term goals.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client insists on a goal requiring a 22% annual return over 7 years. The adviser's most professional response is:\nA) Promise to deliver 22% using derivatives  B) Explain that 22% sustained is unrealistic, and reset the goal by saving more, extending the horizon, or lowering the target  C) Recommend the riskiest available fund and hope  D) Tell the client the goal is impossible and end the engagement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the **risk requirement** is implausible, the honest professional response is to change the **plan variables** the client controls: contribution, time, or target.\nA) Promising returns is prohibited and false. C) Hoping is not a strategy, and the downside falls entirely on the client. D) Correct diagnosis but poor advice — the planner's job is to offer workable alternatives, not to walk away.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Rank these actions in the correct order for a 29-year-old with ₹6,00,000 of savings, no insurance, ₹3,00,000 of credit card debt at 40%, and a 30-year retirement goal.\nA) Start equity SIP → buy insurance → repay card  B) Repay the 40% card debt → build emergency fund and buy term cover → start the retirement SIP  C) Buy insurance → start SIP → repay card  D) Build emergency fund → start SIP → ignore the card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Clear the 40% debt first** — no investment reliably beats a certain 40% cost. Then **protect** (emergency fund and term cover) so a single shock cannot derail the plan. Only then **invest** the balance for the 30-year retirement goal, where equity is appropriate.\nA) and C) leave 40% interest compounding against the client while chasing ~12% returns. D) Ignoring 40% debt is the single most expensive mistake available here. **The universal sequence: expensive debt → protection → investment.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Read the scenario, then attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** In the real exam a 2-mark caselet like this is worth 10 marks, and a wrong answer costs −0.5.\n\n### Case: The Sharma Household\n\nAnil Sharma, aged 38, is a marketing manager earning **₹2,40,000 a month** (net). His wife Meera, 35, is a homemaker. They have one daughter, Aditi, aged 6.\n\n**Monthly expenses:** ₹1,55,000 (this includes a home loan EMI of ₹42,000 and a car loan EMI of ₹13,000).\n\n**Assets:** savings account ₹3,20,000; liquid fund ₹1,80,000; equity mutual funds ₹14,00,000; EPF ₹11,00,000; self-occupied house (market value) ₹95,00,000; car ₹7,00,000; gold jewellery ₹6,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan outstanding ₹48,00,000; car loan outstanding ₹3,50,000; credit card outstanding ₹1,20,000 (interest 38% p.a.).\n\n**Insurance:** an employer group life cover of ₹25,00,000 only. No individual term cover, no health cover beyond the employer policy.\n\n**Goals:** (i) Aditi's undergraduate education in 12 years, costing **₹25,00,000 in today's terms**; (ii) retirement at 60.\n\nAssume **inflation of 6% p.a.** unless stated otherwise.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Sharma household's net worth?\nA) ₹1,38,00,000  B) ₹85,30,000  C) ₹52,70,000  D) ₹1,25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,20,000 + 1,80,000 + 14,00,000 + 11,00,000 + 95,00,000 + 7,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,38,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 48,00,000 + 3,50,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹52,70,000**. **Net worth = 1,38,00,000 − 52,70,000 = ₹85,30,000.**\nA) ₹1,38,00,000 is total assets only. C) ₹52,70,000 is total liabilities. D) ₹1,25,00,000 does not correspond to any correct combination. *(Always compute both sides before subtracting — the distractors here are exactly the two subtotals.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the household's liquidity ratio, and how does it compare with the norm?\nA) 3.23 months — adequate  B) 2.06 months — below the 3–6 month norm  C) 12.26 months — excessive  D) 1.16 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹3,20,000 + liquid fund ₹1,80,000 = **₹5,00,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,55,000**. Liquidity ratio = 5,00,000 ÷ 1,55,000 = **3.23 months**, which just meets the lower bound of the 3–6 month norm.\nB) 2.06 wrongly uses ₹3,20,000 alone. C) 12.26 incorrectly counts equity funds as liquid — they are volatile and not an emergency asset. D) 1.16 uses ₹1,80,000 alone. **Verdict: adequate but thin.** With a sole earner and a young child, pushing toward 5–6 months (≈₹8–9 lakh) is advisable.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What will Aditi's education cost in future rupees, 12 years from now?\nA) ₹41,00,000  B) ₹50,29,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = Present cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ = ₹25,00,000 × (1.06)¹². (1.06)¹² ≈ **2.01220**. So ₹25,00,000 × 2.0122 = **₹50,30,500 ≈ ₹50.29 lakh**.\nA) ₹41,00,000 understates the compounding (roughly 1.64×). C) ₹30,00,000 applies a token 20% uplift. D) ₹25,00,000 ignores inflation entirely — the most common and most damaging error. **At 6%, costs double in about 12 years** (the \"rule of 72\": 72 ÷ 6 = 12) — a shortcut worth memorising.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Ignoring existing investments, what monthly SIP at 11% p.a. (monthly compounding) is needed to accumulate the QC3 amount in 12 years?\nA) About ₹17,300  B) About ₹34,900  C) About ₹28,000  D) About ₹42,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target **₹50,30,500**; i = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**; n = **144**. (1.0091667)¹⁴⁴ ≈ **3.7275**. Annuity factor = (3.7275 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 2.7275 ÷ 0.0091667 ≈ **297.55**. PMT = 50,30,500 ÷ 297.55 ≈ **₹16,907 ≈ ₹17,300** (allowing for rounding).\nB) ₹34,900 roughly doubles the requirement. C) ₹28,000 assumes a far lower return. D) ₹42,000 is close to 50,30,500 ÷ 120, ignoring returns. In Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,144,0,5030500)`. **Affordability check: the household surplus is ₹2,40,000 − ₹1,55,000 = ₹85,000 a month, so ₹17,300 is comfortably affordable.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the single most urgent gap in the Sharma household's financial plan?\nA) The equity allocation is too low  B) The absence of individual term life and health cover, with a sole earner and a dependent child  C) The gold holding is too small  D) The car loan should be prepaid first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anil is the **sole earner** supporting a homemaker spouse and a 6-year-old, carrying **₹52.70 lakh of liabilities**, and is covered only by an **employer group policy of ₹25,00,000** — which lapses the day he changes or loses his job. If he dies, ₹25 lakh does not even cover the ₹48 lakh home loan, let alone education and retirement. Every other goal in the plan is contingent on his survival.\nA) Allocation matters, but it is a return question, not a survival one. C) Gold weighting is a minor optimisation. D) The **credit card at 38%** — not the car loan — is the debt to clear first, and even that ranks below securing protection. **Priority order here: term cover + health cover → clear the 38% card → top up the emergency fund → then fund education and retirement.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "02", "title": "Time Value of Money", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **This is the single most important chapter in the syllabus.** Its formulas reappear in Chapter 4 (EMIs), Chapter 9 (bond pricing), Chapter 14 (expected returns), Chapter 16 (CAGR, annualising) and in almost every caselet. Master this and you have effectively pre-answered a large slice of the paper.\n\n---\n\n## 2.1 The Core Idea\n\n**A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow.**\n\nThree reasons:\n\n1. **Opportunity cost** — money in hand can be invested and earn a return.\n2. **Inflation** — future rupees buy less.\n3. **Risk/uncertainty** — a promised future rupee might never arrive.\n\nBecause of this, you can never simply add or compare cash flows that occur at different dates. You must first move them all to a **common point in time**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one mental model:** think of a **time line**. Money moved *forward* in time is **compounded** (multiplied by (1+r)ⁿ). Money moved *backward* is **discounted** (divided by (1+r)ⁿ). That's the entire chapter.\n\n```\n        discount ←────────────────────── compound\n   |--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n   0        1        2        3        4        5   (periods)\n   PV                                            FV\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 The Five Variables\n\nEvery TVM problem has **five** variables. Give me any four and I can solve for the fifth.\n\n| Symbol | Name | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PV** | Present Value | Value today |\n| **FV** | Future Value | Value at a future date |\n| **r** (or `RATE`) | Rate per period | Interest/return **per period** |\n| **n** (or `NPER`) | Number of periods | Total compounding periods |\n| **PMT** | Payment | The equal cash flow each period |\n\n> ⚠️ **The #1 exam error:** mixing an *annual* rate with a *monthly* number of periods. **r and n must always use the same period.** For monthly problems: `r = annual rate ÷ 12` and `n = years × 12`.\n\n### Sign convention (matters in Excel)\nMoney you **pay out** is **negative**; money you **receive** is **positive**. If a spreadsheet returns `#NUM!`, it is almost always because every value was entered with the same sign.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.1 & 2.2.2 Future Value and Present Value\n\n### Future Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times (1 + r)^n}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹1,00,000 invested for 10 years at 9%.\nFV = 1,00,000 × (1.09)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × 2.3674 = **₹2,36,740**.\n\n### Present Value of a lump sum\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{FV}{(1 + r)^n}}$$\n\n**Example:** how much do I need today to have ₹50,00,000 in 15 years at 10%?\nPV = 50,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹⁵ = 50,00,000 ÷ 4.1772 = **₹11,96,970**.\n\n> These two are the **same equation rearranged**. Learn one and you know both.\n\n### Simple vs compound interest\n\n| | Simple interest | Compound interest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interest earned on** | Principal only | Principal **+ accumulated interest** |\n| **Formula** | FV = PV × (1 + r×n) | FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| **Growth shape** | Straight line | Accelerating curve |\n\n**₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years:** simple → ₹3,00,000; compound → **₹6,72,750**. The gap *is* the value of compounding.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.3 Rate of Return\n\nRearranging FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ:\n\n$$\\boxed{r = \\left(\\frac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1}$$\n\nThis is the **CAGR** (Compound Annual Growth Rate) — the single smoothed annual rate that takes PV to FV.\n\n**Example:** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years.\nr = (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = (2.5)^0.125 − 1 = 1.1214 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n> Excel: `=RATE(8,0,-400000,1000000)`.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.5 Number of Periods\n\n$$\\boxed{n = \\frac{\\ln(FV / PV)}{\\ln(1 + r)}}$$\n\n**Example:** how long for ₹5,00,000 to become ₹15,00,000 at 11%?\nn = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.11) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.10436 = **10.53 years**.\n\n### 🔑 The Rule of 72 (memorise this)\n\n$$\\textbf{Years to double} \\approx \\frac{72}{\\text{annual rate (\\%)}}$$\n\n| Rate | Doubling time |\n|---|---|\n| 6% | 12 years |\n| 8% | 9 years |\n| 9% | 8 years |\n| 12% | 6 years |\n\nUse it in reverse too: if money doubled in 6 years, the rate was ≈ 72 ÷ 6 = **12%**. It's an approximation, but exam-fast and usually within a few months.\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Compounding Frequency\n\nInterest may be credited more than once a year. More frequent compounding = **more interest**, because interest starts earning interest sooner.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV = PV \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m \\times n}}$$\n\nwhere **m** = compoundings per year (annual 1, half-yearly 2, quarterly 4, monthly 12, daily 365).\n\n### Effective Annual Rate (EAR)\n\nThe rate that, compounded **once a year**, gives the same result:\n\n$$\\boxed{EAR = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1}$$\n\n**₹1,00,000 at a nominal 12% for 1 year:**\n\n| Frequency | m | EAR | Value after 1 year |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Annual | 1 | 12.000% | ₹1,12,000 |\n| Half-yearly | 2 | 12.360% | ₹1,12,360 |\n| Quarterly | 4 | 12.551% | ₹1,12,551 |\n| Monthly | 12 | 12.683% | ₹1,12,683 |\n| Daily | 365 | 12.747% | ₹1,12,747 |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the **nominal** (stated) rate is the same 12% throughout — only the **effective** rate changes. Always compare products on **EAR**, never on the headline rate.\n>\n> Note the gains shrink as m rises: annual→monthly adds 0.68 percentage points; monthly→daily adds only 0.06. The limit (continuous compounding) is e^r − 1 = 12.75%.\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.6 Annuities\n\nAn **annuity** is a series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n### Ordinary annuity (payment at the END of each period)\n\nThis is the default. Most EMIs and SIPs are treated this way.\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{ordinary}} = PMT \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}}$$\n\n**Example (FV):** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. compounded monthly.\nr = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 15 × 12 = **180**.\n(1.01)¹⁸⁰ = **5.9958**.\nFV = 10,000 × [(5.9958 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 10,000 × **499.58** = **₹49,95,800**.\n\n*(Total invested = ₹18,00,000. Returns contributed ₹31,95,800 — nearly two-thirds of the corpus.)*\n\n### Annuity due (payment at the BEGINNING of each period)\n\nEach payment sits invested for **one extra period**, so simply multiply by (1+r):\n\n$$\\boxed{FV_{\\text{due}} = FV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{due}} = PV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)}$$\n\nUsing the example above: FV_due = 49,95,800 × 1.01 = **₹50,45,758** — about ₹50,000 more, purely from timing.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** \"**Due** means you pay **first**\" → money works longer → the value is **always higher**. In Excel this is the `type` argument: **0 = ordinary (end)**, **1 = due (beginning)**.\n\n### 2.2.4 Solving for PMT\n\nRearranging the FV annuity formula:\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{FV \\times r}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n**Example:** to accumulate ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years at 11% p.a. (monthly):\nr = 0.0091667, n = 240, (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**.\nPMT = (1,00,00,000 × 0.0091667) ÷ (8.9006 − 1) = 91,667 ÷ 7.9006 = **₹11,602 a month**.\n\nAnd from the **PV** side (this is the **EMI** formula — see Chapter 4):\n\n$$\\boxed{PMT = \\frac{PV \\times r}{1 - (1+r)^{-n}} = PV \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\n---\n\n## 2.2.7 Perpetuity\n\nA perpetuity is an annuity that **never ends**.\n\n$$\\boxed{PV_{\\text{perpetuity}} = \\frac{PMT}{r}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 a year forever, discount rate 8% → PV = 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n### Growing perpetuity\n\nIf the payment grows at a constant rate **g** (and g < r):\n\n$$\\boxed{PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r - g}}$$\n\n**Example:** ₹60,000 next year growing 4% a year forever, discounted at 8%:\nPV = 60,000 ÷ (0.08 − 0.04) = **₹15,00,000**.\n\n> ⚠️ If **g ≥ r** the formula breaks (the value is infinite) — a favourite trick question.\n\n### Growing annuity (finite)\n\n$$PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r-g}\\left[1 - \\left(\\frac{1+g}{1+r}\\right)^n\\right]$$\n\nUseful for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Real vs Nominal Return\n\nThe **nominal** return is what the statement shows. The **real** return is what your money will actually buy.\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Real return} = \\frac{1 + \\text{nominal}}{1 + \\text{inflation}} - 1}$$\n\n**Example:** nominal 9%, inflation 6%.\nReal = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.02830 − 1 = **2.83%**.\n\n> ⚠️ The rough answer (9 − 6 = 3%) **overstates** it. The exam frequently offers the crude subtraction as a distractor — use the exact formula.\n\n**Why it matters:** an FD at 7% with inflation at 6% delivers a real return of only (1.07 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **0.94%**. Before tax. After 30% tax the nominal becomes 4.9%, and the real return is (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" investment is quietly *losing* purchasing power.\n\n### Inflating a goal\n\n$$\\text{Future cost} = \\text{Today's cost} \\times (1 + \\text{inflation})^n$$\n\nAlways cost goals in **future rupees** before computing what to invest.\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Putting It Together — the standard goal calculation\n\nEvery goal caselet follows the same four steps:\n\n1. **Inflate the goal** to its future cost: `Cost × (1 + i)ⁿ`\n2. **Grow what you already have**: `Existing × (1 + r)ⁿ`\n3. **Find the gap**: step 1 − step 2\n4. **Solve for the SIP** that fills the gap: `PMT = Gap × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]`\n\n**Worked example.** Goal: education costing ₹20,00,000 today, needed in 10 years. Inflation 7%. Existing earmarked corpus ₹5,00,000. Expected return 11% p.a. (monthly compounding).\n\n1. Future cost = 20,00,000 × (1.07)¹⁰ = 20,00,000 × 1.9672 = **₹39,34,400**\n2. Existing grows = 5,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁰ = 5,00,000 × 2.8394 = **₹14,19,700**\n3. Gap = 39,34,400 − 14,19,700 = **₹25,14,700**\n4. r = 0.0091667, n = 120, (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = 2.9853\n PMT = (25,14,700 × 0.0091667) ÷ 1.9853 = 23,051 ÷ 1.9853 = **₹11,611 a month**\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Excel / Calc Functions (the test machine has a spreadsheet!)\n\n| Need | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Future value | `=FV(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])` |\n| Present value | `=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])` |\n| Payment | `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Rate | `=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Periods | `=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` |\n| Effective rate | `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)` |\n| Nominal rate | `=NOMINAL(effect_rate, npery)` |\n\n**Rules:** use the **per-period** rate; enter outflows as **negative**; `type` = 0 (end) or 1 (beginning).\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Need | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| FV of lump sum | PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| PV of lump sum | FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ |\n| Rate / CAGR | (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 |\n| Periods | ln(FV÷PV) ÷ ln(1+r) |\n| Rule of 72 | Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate% |\n| Non-annual compounding | PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n) |\n| Effective annual rate | (1 + r/m)^m − 1 |\n| FV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r] |\n| PV of ordinary annuity | PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r] |\n| Annuity **due** | Ordinary × (1+r) |\n| PMT from FV | FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| PMT from PV (EMI) | PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| Perpetuity | PMT ÷ r |\n| Growing perpetuity | PMT₁ ÷ (r − g), needs g < r |\n| Real return | [(1+nominal) ÷ (1+inflation)] − 1 |\n| Future cost of a goal | Cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ |\n\n### The five traps that cost marks\n\n1. **Period mismatch** — annual rate with monthly periods. Convert *both*.\n2. **Ordinary vs due** — \"beginning of month\" means multiply by (1+r).\n3. **Real return by subtraction** — use the division formula.\n4. **Forgetting to inflate the goal** — today's cost is never the target.\n5. **Nominal vs effective** — compare products on EAR.\n\n> **Exam tip:** Module 1 is 37 marks and this chapter underpins most of it. If you have limited revision time, spend it here.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **These formulas must be automatic — they reappear in Chapters 4, 9, 14 and 16.**\n\n**Q1.** Why is a rupee today worth more than a rupee tomorrow?\n> **A:** Three reasons — **opportunity cost** (it can be invested today), **inflation** (future rupees buy less), and **risk** (a future rupee may never arrive).\n\n**Q2.** Name the five TVM variables.\n> **A:** **PV, FV, r (rate per period), n (number of periods), PMT.** Give any four and the fifth is solvable.\n\n**Q3.** State the future value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ**\n\n**Q4.** State the present value of a lump sum.\n> **A:** **PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ** — the same equation rearranged.\n\n**Q5.** What is the #1 mistake in TVM calculations?\n> **A:** **Period mismatch** — using an annual rate with a monthly number of periods. For monthly problems: r = annual ÷ 12 and n = years × 12.\n\n**Q6.** State the formula for the rate of return (CAGR).\n> **A:** **r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1**\n\n**Q7.** State the formula for the number of periods.\n> **A:** **n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r)**\n\n**Q8.** State the Rule of 72 and use it: money doubled in 6 years — what was the rate?\n> **A:** **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate%.** Reversed: 72 ÷ 6 = **12% p.a.**\n\n**Q9.** How long does money take to double at 8%? At 9%? At 6%?\n> **A:** **9 years** at 8%; **8 years** at 9%; **12 years** at 6%.\n\n**Q10.** Give the formula for compounding m times a year.\n> **A:** **FV = PV × (1 + r/m)^(m×n)** — m = 1 annual, 2 half-yearly, 4 quarterly, 12 monthly, 365 daily.\n\n**Q11.** State the Effective Annual Rate formula.\n> **A:** **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1** — the rate that, compounded once a year, gives the same result.\n\n**Q12.** A nominal 12% compounded monthly gives what EAR?\n> **A:** (1 + 0.12/12)¹² − 1 = (1.01)¹² − 1 = **12.683%**.\n\n**Q13.** As compounding frequency rises, what happens to the nominal rate and to the effective rate?\n> **A:** The **nominal rate stays the same**; only the **effective rate rises** — and by shrinking increments (annual→monthly adds 0.68pp; monthly→daily only 0.06pp).\n\n**Q14.** What is an annuity?\n> **A:** A series of **equal** cash flows at **regular** intervals — a SIP, an EMI, a pension, rent.\n\n**Q15.** State the FV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **FV = PMT × [((1 + r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q16.** State the PV of an ordinary annuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT × [(1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**\n\n**Q17.** What is the difference between an ordinary annuity and an annuity due?\n> **A:** **Ordinary** pays at the **end** of each period; **due** pays at the **beginning**. Each due payment is invested one extra period.\n\n**Q18.** How do you convert an ordinary annuity value into an annuity due value?\n> **A:** **Multiply by (1 + r).** This works for both PV and FV. The due value is **always higher**.\n\n**Q19.** In Excel, what does the `type` argument do?\n> **A:** `type = 0` → ordinary annuity (end of period, the default). `type = 1` → annuity due (beginning of period).\n\n**Q20.** State the formula for PMT when you know the target FV.\n> **A:** **PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q21.** State the formula for PMT when you know the PV (i.e. the EMI formula).\n> **A:** **PMT = PV × r × (1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**\n\n**Q22.** State the present value of a perpetuity.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT ÷ r**\n\n**Q23.** State the present value of a growing perpetuity, and its one condition.\n> **A:** **PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)**, valid only when **g < r**. If g ≥ r the value is infinite and the formula breaks.\n\n**Q24.** ₹60,000 a year forever at an 8% discount rate — what is it worth today?\n> **A:** 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\n\n**Q25.** State the real return formula.\n> **A:** **Real = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1** — *not* nominal minus inflation.\n\n**Q26.** Nominal 9%, inflation 6%. What is the exact real return, and what does the crude method give?\n> **A:** Exact = (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **2.83%**. Crude subtraction gives 3%, which **overstates** it.\n\n**Q27.** An FD yields 7%, inflation is 6%, tax is 30%. What is the post-tax real return?\n> **A:** Post-tax nominal = 7% × 0.70 = 4.9%. Real = (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%** — the \"safe\" option loses purchasing power.\n\n**Q28.** How do you convert today's goal cost into the amount actually needed?\n> **A:** **Future cost = Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ.** Never target today's price.\n\n**Q29.** State the four steps of the standard goal calculation.\n> **A:** 1) **Inflate** the goal cost. 2) **Grow** the existing earmarked corpus. 3) Find the **gap**. 4) Solve for the **SIP** that fills the gap.\n\n**Q30.** Difference between simple and compound interest, with the 20-year example?\n> **A:** Simple earns on principal only (**FV = PV(1 + r×n)**); compound earns on principal *plus* accumulated interest. ₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years: simple **₹3,00,000**, compound **₹6,72,750**.\n\n**Q31.** What is the Excel sign convention, and what does `#NUM!` usually mean?\n> **A:** Money **paid out is negative**, money **received is positive**. `#NUM!` almost always means every value was entered with the **same sign**.\n\n**Q32.** ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. (monthly) — what is the corpus, and how much was contributed?\n> **A:** r = 0.01, n = 180, (1.01)¹⁸⁰ = 5.9958. FV = 10,000 × 499.58 = **₹49,95,800**. Contributed = **₹18,00,000**; returns supplied nearly two-thirds.\n\n**Q33.** Which Excel functions solve for each variable?\n> **A:** `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`, `=NPER()`, plus `=EFFECT()` and `=NOMINAL()` for rate conversion.\n\n**Q34.** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years. What is the CAGR?\n> **A:** (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = 2.5^0.125 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n**Q35.** Why must you always compare deposit products on EAR rather than the headline rate?\n> **A:** Two products can quote the same **nominal** rate but differ in **compounding frequency**, so their actual returns differ. Only the **effective** rate is comparable.\n\n**Q36.** State the finite growing-annuity PV formula and where it's used.\n> **A:** **PV = [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]** — used for retirement income that must rise with inflation.\n\n**Q37.** What are the five classic traps in this chapter?\n> **A:** 1) Period mismatch. 2) Ordinary vs due confusion. 3) Real return by subtraction. 4) Forgetting to inflate the goal. 5) Comparing nominal instead of effective rates.\n\n**Q38.** In one sentence, what is the whole chapter?\n> **A:** Cash flows at different dates cannot be compared directly — move them along the time line by **compounding forward** ((1+r)ⁿ) or **discounting back** (÷(1+r)ⁿ).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 2: Time Value of Money — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> ⭐ **The most exam-critical bank in the guide.** Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). Keep a spreadsheet open and check each answer with `=FV()`, `=PV()`, `=PMT()`, `=RATE()`. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The time value of money concept states that:\nA) All rupees are worth the same regardless of timing  B) A rupee today is worth more than a rupee tomorrow  C) A rupee tomorrow is worth more than a rupee today  D) Money loses value only when inflation is above 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Money available today can be invested (opportunity cost), buys more (inflation), and is certain (risk).\nA) Ignores all three reasons. C) Reverses the principle. D) Inflation is only one of three reasons, and it applies at any positive rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The formula for the future value of a lump sum is:\nA) FV = PV × (1 + r × n)  B) FV = PV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ  C) FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ  D) FV = PV × r × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Compounding multiplies the present value by **(1 + r)ⁿ**.\nA) Is the **simple interest** formula. B) Is present value (discounting). D) Computes only the interest under simple interest, not the future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of these is NOT one of the five TVM variables?\nA) PMT  B) NPER  C) Inflation  D) RATE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The five variables are **PV, FV, RATE, NPER and PMT**. Inflation is used *around* TVM (to inflate goals or compute real returns) but is not one of the five.\nA), B) and D) are all core variables.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In an ordinary annuity, payments occur:\nA) At the beginning of each period  B) At the end of each period  C) Only once  D) At irregular intervals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **ordinary annuity** pays at the **end** of each period; it is the default assumption.\nA) Describes an annuity **due**. C) A single payment is a lump sum, not an annuity. D) Annuities require *regular* intervals and *equal* amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The relationship between an annuity due and an ordinary annuity is:\nA) Due = Ordinary ÷ (1 + r)  B) Due = Ordinary × (1 + r)  C) They are always equal  D) Due = Ordinary × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each payment in an annuity due sits invested **one extra period**, so multiply by **(1 + r)**.\nA) Divides, which would make it smaller. C) They are never equal for r > 0. D) Multiplying by n has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The present value of a perpetuity is:\nA) PMT × r  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1 + r)  D) PMT ÷ n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A perpetuity pays forever, so **PV = PMT ÷ r**.\nA) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Perpetuities have no finite n.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The Rule of 72 estimates:\nA) The effective annual rate  B) The number of years for money to double  C) The real rate of return  D) The EMI on a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual rate (%)**.\nA) EAR uses (1 + r/m)^m − 1. C) Real return uses the ratio formula. D) EMIs use the annuity PMT formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** At 9% per annum, money approximately doubles in:\nA) 6 years  B) 8 years  C) 12 years  D) 9 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 72 ÷ 9 = **8 years**.\nA) 6 years corresponds to 12%. C) 12 years corresponds to 6%. D) 9 years corresponds to 8% — note the easy-to-confuse pair: 8% → 9 years, 9% → 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which compounding frequency produces the highest effective annual rate for a given nominal rate?\nA) Annual  B) Half-yearly  C) Quarterly  D) Daily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The more often interest is credited, the sooner it starts earning interest itself, so **daily** gives the highest EAR.\nA), B) and C) all compound less frequently. For a 12% nominal: annual 12.000%, half-yearly 12.360%, quarterly 12.551%, daily 12.747%.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The effective annual rate formula is:\nA) (1 + r × m) − 1  B) (1 + r/m)^m − 1  C) (1 + r)^m − 1  D) r ÷ m\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **EAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1**: divide the nominal rate by the frequency, compound it m times, subtract 1.\nA) Uses simple interest. C) Fails to divide the nominal rate by m, overstating the result badly. D) Gives the periodic rate, not the annual effective rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The real rate of return is calculated as:\nA) Nominal − inflation  B) [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1  C) Nominal × inflation  D) Nominal ÷ inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exact formula divides the growth factors.\nA) The crude subtraction **overstates** the real return and is the most common distractor. C) and D) have no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In Excel, money paid out should be entered as:\nA) A positive number  B) A negative number  C) Zero  D) Either, it makes no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The sign convention is **outflows negative, inflows positive**.\nA) Would misstate the direction of the cash flow. C) Zero would remove the cash flow entirely. D) It matters greatly — same-sign entries typically produce `#NUM!`.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which Excel function solves for the periodic payment?\nA) `=FV()`  B) `=PV()`  C) `=PMT()`  D) `=NPER()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — `=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])`.\nA) Solves for future value. B) Solves for present value. D) Solves for the number of periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Simple interest differs from compound interest because simple interest:\nA) Is earned on principal plus accumulated interest  B) Is earned on the principal only  C) Grows exponentially  D) Always produces a higher value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Simple interest is computed on the **original principal only**, giving straight-line growth.\nA) and C) describe compound interest. D) Compound interest always produces more for n > 1 and r > 0.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The FV of an ordinary annuity is:\nA) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  B) PMT ÷ r  C) PMT × (1+r)ⁿ  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The annuity factor is **[((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is a perpetuity. C) Compounds a single payment. D) Ignores interest completely — that is just the total contributed.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which of the following is an annuity?\nA) A one-time bonus of ₹5,00,000  B) A monthly SIP of ₹10,000 for 10 years  C) Irregular dividend payments  D) A lump-sum inheritance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An annuity requires **equal amounts at regular intervals** — a SIP fits exactly.\nA) and D) are lump sums. C) Dividends vary in amount and timing, so they are not an annuity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** For a growing perpetuity, the formula PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) requires:\nA) g > r  B) g < r  C) g = r  D) g = 0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth must be **less than** the discount rate, otherwise the denominator is zero or negative and the value is infinite/meaningless.\nA) and C) break the formula. D) g = 0 is allowed but reduces it to an ordinary perpetuity — it is not a *requirement*.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** When converting an annual rate to a monthly rate for TVM purposes, you should:\nA) Multiply the annual rate by 12  B) Divide the annual rate by 12  C) Leave it unchanged  D) Divide by 365\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Use **r ÷ 12** for the monthly periodic rate, together with n = years × 12.\nA) Multiplying inflates the rate twelve-fold. C) Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the classic error. D) 365 applies to daily compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Discounting means:\nA) Moving a cash flow forward in time  B) Moving a cash flow backward in time to find its present value  C) Reducing the interest rate  D) Applying a sales rebate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Discounting divides by (1+r)ⁿ to bring a future amount **back** to today.\nA) That is compounding. C) and D) confuse the financial meaning of \"discount\" with everyday usage.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which statement about the nominal rate is correct when compounding frequency increases?\nA) The nominal rate rises  B) The nominal rate stays the same while the effective rate rises  C) Both fall  D) The effective rate stays the same\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **stated/nominal** rate is unchanged; only the **effective** rate rises.\nA) and C) misstate the direction. D) The effective rate is precisely what changes.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** ₹1,00,000 at 10% simple interest for 20 years grows to:\nA) ₹6,72,750  B) ₹3,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹1,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FV = PV × (1 + r×n) = 1,00,000 × (1 + 0.10 × 20) = 1,00,000 × 3 = **₹3,00,000**.\nA) ₹6,72,750 is the **compound** result. C) ₹2,00,000 would be 10 years of simple interest. D) ₹1,10,000 is one year only.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The PV of an ordinary annuity formula is:\nA) PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]  B) PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r]  C) PMT ÷ (r − g)  D) PMT × (1+r)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The PV annuity factor is **[(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]**.\nB) Is the **FV** annuity factor. C) Is a growing perpetuity. D) Is the annuity-due adjustment.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** In the Excel `type` argument, `type = 1` means:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due (payment at the beginning)  C) Simple interest  D) Continuous compounding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `type = 1` shifts payments to the **beginning** of each period.\nA) `type = 0` is the ordinary annuity default. C) and D) are not controlled by `type`.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** To find the rate given PV, FV and n, you use:\nA) r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1  B) r = (FV − PV) ÷ n  C) r = FV ÷ PV  D) r = ln(FV ÷ PV)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Take the nth root of the growth multiple and subtract 1.\nB) Computes an average rupee gain, not a rate. C) Gives the total multiple. D) Is part of the **NPER** formula, not the rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** An investment doubles in 12 years. Using the Rule of 72, the approximate rate is:\nA) 12%  B) 6%  C) 8%  D) 9%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rate ≈ 72 ÷ 12 = **6%**.\nA) 12% doubles in 6 years. C) 8% doubles in 9 years. D) 9% doubles in 8 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of these is a use of the present value concept?\nA) Deciding how much to invest today for a future goal  B) Calculating simple interest  C) Measuring inflation  D) Preparing a balance sheet\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV answers exactly the question \"how much do I need **today**?\"\nB) Simple interest has its own formula. C) Inflation is measured by price indices. D) Balance sheets record current values, not discounted ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The formula n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1 + r) solves for:\nA) The rate  B) The number of periods  C) The payment  D) The present value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taking logs of FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ isolates **n**.\nA) The rate uses the nth-root formula. C) and D) have their own formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A nominal rate of 12% compounded quarterly gives a periodic rate of:\nA) 12%  B) 4%  C) 3%  D) 1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Quarterly means m = 4, so the periodic rate is 12% ÷ 4 = **3%**.\nA) Is the annual nominal rate. B) 4% would be m = 3. D) 1% is the monthly rate (m = 12).\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of the following will always be true for r > 0 and n > 1?\nA) Compound value < simple value  B) Compound value > simple value  C) They are equal  D) It depends on inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compounding earns interest on interest, so it always exceeds simple interest beyond the first period.\nA) Reverses it. C) They are equal only at n = 1. D) Inflation is irrelevant to this comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The future cost of a goal is calculated as:\nA) Today's cost ÷ (1 + inflation)ⁿ  B) Today's cost × (1 + inflation)ⁿ  C) Today's cost × inflation × n  D) Today's cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goals must be inflated forward to the date they will be paid.\nA) Discounts instead of inflating. C) Uses simple growth. D) Ignoring inflation is the single most damaging planning error.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which Excel function converts a nominal rate to an effective rate?\nA) `=NOMINAL()`  B) `=EFFECT()`  C) `=RATE()`  D) `=FV()`\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — `=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)`.\nA) Does the reverse (effective → nominal). C) Solves for the periodic rate in a TVM problem. D) Computes future value.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a SIP where the investment is made on the 1st of every month, the appropriate treatment is:\nA) Ordinary annuity  B) Annuity due  C) Perpetuity  D) Lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Payment at the **beginning** of the period is an **annuity due**.\nA) Would apply to end-of-month investment. C) A SIP has a finite term. D) A SIP is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Continuous compounding of a 12% nominal rate gives an effective rate of approximately:\nA) 12.00%  B) 12.68%  C) 12.75%  D) 13.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The limit is e^r − 1 = e^0.12 − 1 = 1.1275 − 1 = **12.75%**.\nA) Is annual compounding. B) Is monthly compounding. D) Overstates it — continuous compounding is the *ceiling*, only marginally above daily (12.747%).\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which of these correctly describes the effect of a longer horizon on compounding?\nA) The effect is linear  B) The effect accelerates — later years add much more than early years  C) The effect diminishes over time  D) There is no effect after 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growth is exponential, so each successive year adds more in absolute terms than the last.\nA) Linear growth describes simple interest. C) and D) contradict the exponential nature of compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A `#NUM!` error in an Excel TVM formula usually means:\nA) The rate is too high  B) All cash flows were entered with the same sign  C) The function name is misspelled  D) The file is corrupted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TVM functions require at least one cash flow of the **opposite sign**.\nA) High rates compute fine. C) A misspelling gives `#NAME?`. D) Corruption would produce a different failure.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** ₹2,00,000 is invested for 7 years at 8% p.a. compounded annually. What is the future value?\nA) ₹3,42,780  B) ₹3,12,000  C) ₹2,16,000  D) ₹4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 2,00,000 × (1.08)⁷. (1.08)⁷ = **1.71382**. FV = 2,00,000 × 1.71382 = **₹3,42,764 ≈ ₹3,42,780**.\nB) ₹3,12,000 applies simple interest (2,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹2,16,000 is one year's growth. D) ₹4,00,000 would need about 10.5 years at 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** How much must be invested today to have ₹25,00,000 in 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) ₹7,96,600  B) ₹12,50,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV = 25,00,000 ÷ (1.10)¹². (1.10)¹² = **3.13843**. PV = 25,00,000 ÷ 3.13843 = **₹7,96,575 ≈ ₹7,96,600**.\nB) ₹12,50,000 simply halves the target. C) ₹5,00,000 is far too low (it would grow to only ₹15.7 lakh). D) ₹10,00,000 would grow to ₹31.4 lakh. Excel: `=PV(10%,12,0,-2500000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** ₹5,00,000 grows to ₹12,50,000 in 10 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 15.00%  B) 9.60%  C) 25.00%  D) 7.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = (12,50,000 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/10) − 1 = 2.5^0.1 − 1. ln(2.5) = 0.9163; ÷ 10 = 0.09163; e^0.09163 = 1.09596. r = **9.60% p.a.**\nA) 15% would give about ₹20.2 lakh. C) 25% is the *total* gain divided by 10 years — a simple-interest style error. D) 7.20% gives only about ₹10 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** ₹15,000 invested monthly for 20 years at 12% p.a. (monthly compounding) accumulates to approximately:\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹1,49,88,000  C) ₹75,00,000  D) ₹2,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 240. (1.01)²⁴⁰ = **10.8926**. FV = 15,000 × [(10.8926 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 15,000 × **989.26** = **₹1,48,38,900 ≈ ₹1.49 crore**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is just the total contributed (15,000 × 240). C) ₹75,00,000 understates the compounding badly. D) ₹2.5 crore overstates it. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,240,-15000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** What is the effective annual rate of 10% nominal compounded quarterly?\nA) 10.00%  B) 10.38%  C) 10.47%  D) 11.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.10/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.025)⁴ − 1 = 1.10381 − 1 = **10.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 10.47% corresponds to monthly compounding. D) 11% overstates it. Excel: `=EFFECT(10%,4)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A nominal 9% is compounded monthly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.00%  B) 9.38%  C) 9.20%  D) 9.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.09/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0075)¹² − 1 = 1.09381 − 1 = **9.38%**.\nA) Is the nominal rate. C) 9.20% corresponds roughly to half-yearly compounding. D) 9.75% overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A nominal return of 11% with inflation at 6.5% gives a real return of:\nA) 4.50%  B) 4.23%  C) 5.00%  D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real = (1.11 ÷ 1.065) − 1 = 1.042254 − 1 = **4.23%**.\nA) 4.50% is the crude subtraction (11 − 6.5), which overstates the real return. C) and D) do not follow from the formula. **The exam almost always offers the subtraction as a distractor.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A goal costs ₹12,00,000 today and is 8 years away. At 7% inflation, the future cost is:\nA) ₹18,72,000  B) ₹20,61,700  C) ₹16,80,000  D) ₹24,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 12,00,000 × (1.07)⁸. (1.07)⁸ = **1.71819**. = **₹20,61,828 ≈ ₹20,61,700**.\nA) ₹18,72,000 applies simple inflation (12,00,000 × 1.56). C) ₹16,80,000 assumes only a 40% rise. D) ₹24,00,000 assumes doubling, which at 7% takes about 10.3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor needs ₹40,00,000 in 15 years and expects 10% p.a. What monthly SIP is required?\nA) ₹9,650  B) ₹22,200  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹31,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.10 ÷ 12 = **0.008333**; n = 180. (1.008333)¹⁸⁰ = **4.4539**. Annuity factor = (4.4539 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **414.47**. PMT = 40,00,000 ÷ 414.47 = **₹9,651**.\nB) ₹22,200 corresponds to a much lower assumed return. C) ₹15,000 would overshoot to about ₹62 lakh. D) ₹31,000 is close to 40,00,000 ÷ 180 (₹22,222) inflated — it ignores compounding. Excel: `=PMT(10%/12,180,0,4000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** ₹8,000 is invested at the **beginning** of every month for 10 years at 12% p.a. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹18,41,200  B) ₹18,59,600  C) ₹9,60,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 8,000 × [((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × [(3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 8,000 × **230.039** = ₹18,40,312. Annuity due = × 1.01 = **₹18,58,715 ≈ ₹18,59,600**.\nA) ₹18,41,200 is the **ordinary** annuity value — it forgets the (1+r) adjustment. C) ₹9,60,000 is the total contributed. D) ₹16,00,000 understates the growth. Excel: `=FV(12%/12,120,-8000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A pension of ₹50,000 per year is expected in perpetuity. At a 9% discount rate, its present value is:\nA) ₹4,50,000  B) ₹5,55,556  C) ₹50,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV = PMT ÷ r = 50,000 ÷ 0.09 = **₹5,55,556**.\nA) ₹4,50,000 multiplies instead of dividing (50,000 × 9). C) ₹50,00,000 divides by 0.01. D) ₹9,00,000 has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A payment of ₹1,00,000 next year grows 5% a year forever. At a 10% discount rate, the present value is:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹20,00,000  C) ₹6,66,667  D) Infinite\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Growing perpetuity: PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) = 1,00,000 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) = 1,00,000 ÷ 0.05 = **₹20,00,000**.\nA) ₹10,00,000 ignores growth (uses r alone). C) ₹6,66,667 uses r + g. D) Infinite would apply only if g ≥ r. **Growth halves the effective discount rate here, doubling the value.**\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** How long will ₹3,00,000 take to become ₹9,00,000 at 12% p.a.?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 9.69 years  C) 12.0 years  D) 8.0 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(9,00,000 ÷ 3,00,000) ÷ ln(1.12) = ln(3) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.0986 ÷ 0.11333 = **9.69 years**.\nA) 6 years only doubles it (Rule of 72: 72 ÷ 12 = 6). C) and D) do not follow. **Sanity check: doubling takes 6 years, so tripling must take more — but less than 12.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Two deposits offer 10% compounded annually and 9.8% compounded monthly. Which is better?\nA) The 10% annual, since the headline rate is higher  B) The 9.8% monthly, because its EAR is about 10.25%  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR of 9.8% monthly = (1 + 0.098/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0081667)¹² − 1 = **10.25%**, which beats the 10.00% EAR of the annual option.\nA) Comparing headline rates is exactly the trap. C) They differ by 0.25 percentage points. D) EAR is precisely the tool that makes them comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor wants ₹75,00,000 in 18 years. She has ₹10,00,000 today earning 11%. What is the shortfall at the goal date?\nA) ₹9,56,450  B) ₹10,58,000  C) Nil — she will exceed the goal  D) ₹65,43,550\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Grow the existing corpus first: 10,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁸. (1.11)¹⁸ = **6.54355**, so the corpus reaches **₹65,43,550**. Shortfall = 75,00,000 − 65,43,550 = **₹9,56,450**.\nB) ₹10,58,000 overstates the gap. C) She falls short, not over. D) ₹65,43,550 is the grown corpus itself, not the gap. *(Always compute the grown corpus first, then subtract from the target.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** ₹20,000 a month for 25 years at 11% p.a. gives a corpus of approximately:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹3,15,27,000  C) ₹1,50,00,000  D) ₹6,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.0091667, n = 300. (1.0091667)³⁰⁰ = **15.4499**. Annuity factor = (15.4499 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **1576.36**. FV = 20,000 × 1576.36 = **₹3,15,27,200**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is simply the total contributed (20,000 × 300). C) ₹1.5 crore understates the growth by half. D) ₹6.5 crore overstates it. **Over 25 years, ₹60 lakh of contributions becomes about ₹3.15 crore — roughly 81% of the corpus is return.**\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which is worth more today: ₹10,00,000 received in 5 years, or ₹6,00,000 received today, at an 8% discount rate?\nA) The ₹10,00,000 in 5 years, worth about ₹6,80,600 today  B) The ₹6,00,000 today  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of ₹10,00,000 = 10,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁵ = 10,00,000 ÷ **1.46933** = **₹6,80,583**, which exceeds ₹6,00,000.\nB) Would be right only at a discount rate above about 10.8%. C) They differ by roughly ₹80,000. D) With PV, FV, n and r known, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An EMI is best described as which TVM concept?\nA) The FV of an annuity  B) The PMT that makes the PV of payments equal the loan amount  C) A perpetuity  D) A lump sum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A loan is a **present value**; the EMI is the payment whose present value equals the loan amount: **PMT = PV × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**.\nA) Uses the FV form, appropriate for savings not loans. C) Loans have a finite term. D) An EMI is a series, not a single payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An investor's portfolio returns 14% nominal in a year with 5% inflation and pays 20% tax on gains. What is the post-tax real return?\nA) 9.00%  B) 6.29%  C) 11.20%  D) 5.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Two steps, in this order. **Tax first:** post-tax nominal = 14% × (1 − 0.20) = **11.2%**. **Then deflate:** real = (1.112 ÷ 1.05) − 1 = 1.05905 − 1 = **5.90%**.\nA) 9% subtracts inflation from the *pre-tax* return, ignoring tax altogether. B) 6.29% adjusts for inflation first and taxes afterwards — the wrong order. C) 11.2% is the post-tax nominal return, before inflation. **Always tax first, then deflate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** ₹1,00,000 invested at 8% compounded half-yearly for 5 years gives:\nA) ₹1,46,933  B) ₹1,48,024  C) ₹1,40,000  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — m = 2, so the periodic rate is 4% and n = 10 periods. FV = 1,00,000 × (1.04)¹⁰ = 1,00,000 × **1.48024** = **₹1,48,024**.\nA) ₹1,46,933 is **annual** compounding ((1.08)⁵). C) ₹1,40,000 is simple interest. D) ₹1,60,000 overstates it. **Note half-yearly beats annual by ₹1,091.**\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client will receive ₹5,00,000 a year for 12 years, first payment one year from now. At 9%, the present value is:\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹35,79,000  C) ₹45,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV annuity = PMT × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09]. (1.09)¹² = **2.81266**, so (1.09)⁻¹² = **0.35553**. Factor = (1 − 0.35553) ÷ 0.09 = 0.64447 ÷ 0.09 = **7.1608**. PV = 5,00,000 × 7.1608 = **₹35,80,400 ≈ ₹35,79,000**.\nA) ₹60,00,000 is the undiscounted total (5,00,000 × 12). C) and D) do not follow from the factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** If a SIP is switched from end-of-month to beginning-of-month, the corpus:\nA) Falls  B) Rises by a factor of (1 + r)  C) Is unchanged  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every payment gains one extra period of compounding, so the whole series is multiplied by **(1 + r)**.\nA) It rises, not falls. C) Timing genuinely matters. D) The uplift is one period's interest — typically well under 1% for monthly SIPs, not 100%.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client has 25 years to retirement and can invest ₹12,000 a month at 12%. Approximately what corpus results?\nA) ₹36,00,000  B) ₹2,25,00,000  C) ₹1,00,00,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.01, n = 300. (1.01)³⁰⁰ = **19.7885**. Factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**. FV = 12,000 × 1878.85 = **₹2,25,46,200**.\nA) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed. C) ₹1 crore understates it by half. D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Roughly 84% of the corpus is return — the case for starting early in one number.**\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A bank quotes 7.5% compounded quarterly on an FD. Inflation is 6% and the client's tax rate is 30%. The post-tax real return is approximately:\nA) +1.5%  B) −0.6%  C) +0.9%  D) −2.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EAR = (1 + 0.075/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.01875)⁴ − 1 = **7.71%**. Post-tax = 7.71% × 0.70 = **5.40%**. Real = (1.0540 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−0.57% ≈ −0.6%**.\nA) +1.5% ignores tax. C) +0.9% ignores either tax or inflation. D) −2.0% overstates the erosion. **The \"safe\" FD delivers a negative real return after tax — a core planning insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An investment of ₹6,00,000 becomes ₹6,00,000 after 10 years in nominal terms, with inflation at 6%. What happened in real terms?\nA) The investor broke even  B) The investor lost about 44% of purchasing power  C) The investor gained  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In real terms the value is 6,00,000 ÷ (1.06)¹⁰ = 6,00,000 ÷ **1.79085** = **₹3,35,040** in today's money — a loss of about **44%** of purchasing power.\nA) Nominal break-even is not real break-even. C) There is no gain at all. D) With the inflation rate given, it is fully determinable.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which pair of inputs is internally consistent for a monthly SIP over 12 years at 10% p.a.?\nA) r = 10%, n = 12  B) r = 0.10/12, n = 144  C) r = 0.10, n = 144  D) r = 0.10/12, n = 12\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Monthly rate = **0.10 ÷ 12** and monthly periods = 12 years × 12 = **144**. Both must be monthly.\nA) Uses annual rate with 12 periods — that describes 12 *years* of annual compounding, not a monthly SIP. C) Mixes an annual rate with monthly periods — the classic error. D) Mixes a monthly rate with only 12 periods (i.e. one year).\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client needs ₹18,00,000 in 6 years and can earn 9%. She invests ₹6,00,000 today. What additional annual (year-end) investment is needed?\nA) ₹1,05,500  B) ₹1,45,700  C) ₹3,00,000  D) ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Three steps. **Grow the lump sum:** 6,00,000 × (1.09)⁶ = 6,00,000 × **1.67710** = **₹10,06,260**. **Find the gap:** 18,00,000 − 10,06,260 = **₹7,93,740**. **Annuitise it:** factor = ((1.09)⁶ − 1) ÷ 0.09 = 0.6771 ÷ 0.09 = **7.5233**; PMT = 7,93,740 ÷ 7.5233 = **₹1,05,504**.\nB) ₹1,45,700 ignores the existing lump sum's growth. C) ₹3,00,000 is roughly the gap ÷ 6, ignoring returns. D) ₹50,000 falls far short. Excel: `=PMT(9%,6,0,-793740)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The PV of an annuity **falls** when:\nA) The discount rate falls  B) The discount rate rises  C) The number of payments rises  D) The payment amount rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher discount rate shrinks every future cash flow, so the present value falls.\nA) A lower rate raises PV. C) More payments raise PV. D) Larger payments raise PV.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client compares a lump sum of ₹25,00,000 today against ₹3,00,000 a year for 12 years. At 8%, which is better?\nA) The annuity, worth about ₹22,60,000  B) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹22,60,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of the annuity = 3,00,000 × [(1 − (1.08)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.08]. (1.08)¹² = **2.51817**, so (1.08)⁻¹² = **0.39711**. Factor = 0.60289 ÷ 0.08 = **7.5361**. PV = 3,00,000 × 7.5361 = **₹22,60,830**. The lump sum of ₹25,00,000 is worth more today.\nA) Correctly values the annuity but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹2.4 lakh. D) ₹36,00,000 is the *undiscounted* total, which ignores the time value entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Doubling the investment horizon from 10 to 20 years at 12% multiplies the lump-sum corpus by approximately:\nA) 2 times  B) 3.1 times  C) 1.5 times  D) 10 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.12)¹⁰ = **3.1058**; (1.12)²⁰ = **9.6463**. Ratio = 9.6463 ÷ 3.1058 = **3.11 times**.\nA) Doubling time does *not* double the corpus — that is the linear intuition compounding defeats. C) 1.5× badly understates it. D) 10× is the total multiple over 20 years, not the incremental effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** An annuity due of ₹1,00,000 a year for 10 years at 10% has a present value of:\nA) ₹6,14,460  B) ₹6,75,900  C) ₹10,00,000  D) ₹5,58,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ordinary PV = 1,00,000 × [(1 − (1.10)⁻¹⁰) ÷ 0.10]. (1.10)¹⁰ = **2.59374**, so (1.10)⁻¹⁰ = **0.38554**. Factor = 0.61446 ÷ 0.10 = **6.14457**. Ordinary PV = **₹6,14,457**. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,75,903**.\nA) ₹6,14,460 is the **ordinary** value, missing the (1+r) step. C) ₹10,00,000 is undiscounted. D) ₹5,58,600 over-discounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which statement about compounding frequency is TRUE?\nA) The benefit of higher frequency grows without limit  B) The benefit rises but with diminishing increments, approaching continuous compounding  C) Frequency has no effect  D) Annual compounding always gives the most\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each increase in frequency adds less than the last, converging on **e^r − 1**.\nA) The benefit is bounded by continuous compounding. C) Frequency clearly matters. D) Annual gives the *least*.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A goal is ₹30,00,000 in 10 years. The client can invest ₹15,000 a month. What return is needed (approximately)?\nA) About 5%  B) About 9.9%  C) About 15%  D) About 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total contributed = 15,000 × 120 = ₹18,00,000, and the target is ₹30,00,000, so a moderate return is needed. Testing 10%: factor = ((1.008333)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**; FV = 15,000 × 204.84 = **₹30,72,600** — slightly above target, so the required rate is just under 10%, about **9.9%**.\nA) 5% gives about ₹23.3 lakh. C) 15% gives about ₹41.8 lakh. D) 2% gives about ₹19.9 lakh. Excel: `=RATE(120,-15000,0,3000000)*12`.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A retiree wants ₹40,000 a month for 25 years from a corpus earning 8% p.a. What corpus is needed at retirement?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹51,84,000  C) ₹2,40,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **PV of an ordinary annuity**. r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**; n = 300. (1.0066667)³⁰⁰ = **7.34018**, so the inverse is **0.13624**. Factor = (1 − 0.13624) ÷ 0.0066667 = 0.86376 ÷ 0.0066667 = **129.56**. Corpus = 40,000 × 129.56 = **₹51,82,400 ≈ ₹51,84,000**.\nA) ₹1.2 crore is the undiscounted total (40,000 × 300). C) ₹2.4 crore doubles that. D) ₹80,00,000 overstates the requirement. **Note the corpus needed is far below the total withdrawn, because the balance keeps earning.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** In the standard goal calculation, the correct sequence is:\nA) Find the SIP → inflate the goal → grow existing assets  B) Inflate the goal → grow existing assets → find the gap → solve for the SIP  C) Grow existing assets → find the SIP → inflate the goal  D) Find the gap → inflate the goal → solve for the SIP\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot size a SIP until you know the **gap**, and you cannot know the gap until the goal is inflated and existing assets are grown to the same date.\nA), C) and D) all attempt to compute a later step before its inputs exist.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client invests ₹3,00,000 today and ₹5,000 a month for 15 years, both earning 11% p.a. (monthly compounding). What is the total corpus?\nA) ₹15,55,230  B) ₹38,37,480  C) ₹22,82,250  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute the two components separately, then add.\n**Lump sum:** r = 0.0091667, n = 180. (1.0091667)¹⁸⁰ = **5.1841**. FV = 3,00,000 × 5.1841 = **₹15,55,230**.\n**SIP:** factor = (5.1841 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 4.1841 ÷ 0.0091667 = **456.45**. FV = 5,000 × 456.45 = **₹22,82,250**.\n**Total = 15,55,230 + 22,82,250 = ₹38,37,480.**\nA) ₹15,55,230 is the lump-sum component alone. C) ₹22,82,250 is the SIP component alone. D) ₹12,00,000 is roughly the total SIP contributed (5,000 × 180 = ₹9,00,000) plus the initial ₹3,00,000, ignoring all growth. **Method: never mix a lump sum and an annuity in one formula — compute each and add.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A client needs ₹1,00,00,000 in 20 years. She has ₹8,00,000 today and can invest monthly. Expected return 11% p.a. What monthly SIP fills the gap?\nA) ₹3,341  B) ₹8,120  C) ₹11,600  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Step 1 — grow what she has:** (1.0091667)²⁴⁰ = **8.9006**; 8,00,000 × 8.9006 = **₹71,20,480**. **Step 2 — gap:** 1,00,00,000 − 71,20,480 = **₹28,79,520**. **Step 3 — annuity factor:** (8.9006 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 7.9006 ÷ 0.0091667 = **862.0**. **Step 4 — SIP:** 28,79,520 ÷ 862.0 = **₹3,341 a month**.\nB) ₹8,120 ignores part of the existing corpus's growth. C) ₹11,600 is the SIP needed if she had *nothing* saved. D) ₹15,000 overstates it further. **Key insight: the ₹8 lakh already invested does over 70% of the work — the power of a 20-year head start.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Two investors: A invests ₹10,000 a month from age 25 to 35 (10 years) then stops and lets it grow to 60. B invests ₹10,000 a month from 35 to 60 (25 years). Both earn 12% p.a. Who ends with more at 60?\nA) B, because she invested for 25 years  B) A, despite investing for only 10 years  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Investor A:** SIP for 120 months: factor = ((1.01)¹²⁰ − 1) ÷ 0.01 = (3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **230.04**; corpus at 35 = 10,000 × 230.04 = **₹23,00,400**. This then grows untouched for 25 years: × (1.01)³⁰⁰ = × **19.7885** = **₹4,55,17,000**.\n**Investor B:** SIP for 300 months: factor = (19.7885 − 1) ÷ 0.01 = **1878.85**; corpus = 10,000 × 1878.85 = **₹1,87,88,500**.\n**A ends with ~₹4.55 crore from ₹12 lakh invested; B with ~₹1.88 crore from ₹30 lakh invested.**\nA) Reverses the result. C) They differ by more than 2×. D) It is fully determinable. **This is the single most powerful illustration of compounding: time beats amount.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A ₹50,00,000 home loan at 9% for 20 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹44,986  B) ₹37,500  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹41,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]. r = 0.09 ÷ 12 = **0.0075**; n = 240. (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**. EMI = 50,00,000 × [0.0075 × 6.00915] ÷ [6.00915 − 1] = 50,00,000 × 0.04506862 ÷ 5.00915 = **₹44,986**.\nB) ₹37,500 is merely the first month's interest (50,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹52,000 overstates it. D) ₹41,200 corresponds to a lower rate. Excel: `=PMT(9%/12,240,-5000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Using Q74's loan, what is the total amount repaid and the total interest?\nA) ₹1,07,96,640 repaid; ₹57,96,640 interest  B) ₹50,00,000 repaid; nil interest  C) ₹90,00,000 repaid; ₹40,00,000 interest  D) ₹67,47,900 repaid; ₹17,47,900 interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total repaid = EMI × n = ₹44,986 × 240 = **₹1,07,96,640**. Interest = 1,07,96,640 − 50,00,000 = **₹57,96,640**.\nB) Ignores interest entirely. C) and D) understate it. **The borrower pays more in interest than the original principal — which is why loan tenure is the most powerful lever in Chapter 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client can choose: (i) ₹20,00,000 today, or (ii) ₹2,50,000 a year for 15 years starting next year. At what discount rate are the two equal?\nA) About 9.1%  B) About 12.5%  C) About 5%  D) About 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Find the rate at which the annuity's PV equals ₹20,00,000, i.e. an annuity factor of 20,00,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **8.0**. Test rates: at 8%, factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻¹⁵) ÷ 0.08 = (1 − 0.31524) ÷ 0.08 = **8.559** (too high). At 9%: (1 − 0.27454) ÷ 0.09 = **8.061**. At 9.2%: ≈ **7.97**. The factor hits 8.0 at roughly **9.1%**.\nB), C) and D) are all well away from the break-even. **Interpretation: if the client can earn more than about 9.1%, take the lump sum; if less, take the annuity.** Excel: `=RATE(15,2500000,-20000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A goal costs ₹15,00,000 today, is 10 years away, inflation is 6%, and the client has ₹4,00,000 earmarked earning 10%. What monthly SIP at 10% closes the gap?\nA) ₹5,950  B) ₹8,050  C) ₹13,110  D) ₹3,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The four-step template:\n**Step 1 — inflate the goal:** 15,00,000 × (1.06)¹⁰ = 15,00,000 × **1.79085** = **₹26,86,275**.\n**Step 2 — grow existing assets:** 4,00,000 × (1.10)¹⁰ = 4,00,000 × **2.59374** = **₹10,37,496**.\n**Step 3 — gap:** 26,86,275 − 10,37,496 = **₹16,48,779**.\n**Step 4 — solve for the SIP:** r = 0.008333, n = 120, (1.008333)¹²⁰ = **2.70704**; factor = (2.70704 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **204.84**. PMT = 16,48,779 ÷ 204.84 = **₹8,049**.\n\nA) ₹5,950 understates it. C) ₹13,110 is the SIP needed if the existing ₹4,00,000 were ignored entirely. D) ₹3,200 is far too low. **This four-step method is the template for every goal caselet in the exam.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor earns 15% in year 1, −10% in year 2 and 20% in year 3. What is the CAGR?\nA) 8.33%  B) 7.49%  C) 24.20%  D) 6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compound the growth factors, never average the percentages: 1.15 × 0.90 × 1.20 = **1.2420**. CAGR = (1.2420)^(1/3) − 1. ln(1.2420) = 0.21675; ÷ 3 = 0.07225; e^0.07225 = **1.07492**. CAGR = **7.49% p.a.**\nA) 8.33% is the **arithmetic mean** ((15 − 10 + 20) ÷ 3), which always **overstates** the compounded result when returns vary. C) 24.20% is the *cumulative* 3-year gain misread as annual. D) 6% understates it. **Key point: geometric mean (CAGR) < arithmetic mean whenever returns are volatile — see Chapter 16.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client deposits ₹1,00,000 at the start of each year for 5 years at 10%. What is the value at the end of year 5?\nA) ₹6,10,510  B) ₹6,71,561  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹5,52,560\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beginning-of-year deposits form an **annuity due**. Ordinary FV = 1,00,000 × [((1.10)⁵ − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × [(1.61051 − 1) ÷ 0.10] = 1,00,000 × **6.1051** = ₹6,10,510. Annuity due = × 1.10 = **₹6,71,561**.\nA) ₹6,10,510 is the ordinary annuity value — the classic omission. C) ₹5,00,000 is the total deposited. D) ₹5,52,560 under-compounds. Excel: `=FV(10%,5,-100000,0,1)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹10,00,000 investment must grow to ₹40,00,000. At 12% p.a., how long will it take?\nA) 6.0 years  B) 12.2 years  C) 18.0 years  D) 9.7 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — n = ln(4) ÷ ln(1.12) = 1.38629 ÷ 0.113329 = **12.23 years**.\nA) 6 years doubles it (Rule of 72). C) 18 years would take it to about ₹76.9 lakh. D) 9.7 years triples it. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling takes 6 years, and quadrupling is two doublings ≈ 12 years. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client has ₹80,00,000 at retirement, wants ₹60,000 a month, and the corpus earns 7% p.a. How long will the money last?\nA) About 12 years  B) About 21.5 years  C) Indefinitely  D) About 30 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.07 ÷ 12 = **0.0058333**. **First check sustainability:** monthly interest on ₹80,00,000 = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**, which is *less* than the ₹60,000 withdrawal — so the corpus **will** deplete.\n**Then solve for NPER:** n = −ln(1 − (PV × r ÷ PMT)) ÷ ln(1 + r) = −ln(1 − (80,00,000 × 0.0058333 ÷ 60,000)) ÷ ln(1.0058333) = −ln(0.22222) ÷ 0.0058164 = 1.50408 ÷ 0.0058164 = **258.6 months ≈ 21.5 years**.\nA) 12 years understates it. C) It would last indefinitely only if the withdrawal were at or below ₹46,667. D) 30 years overstates it. Excel: `=NPER(7%/12,60000,-8000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Continuing Q81: what monthly withdrawal could the client sustain indefinitely?\nA) ₹46,667  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹80,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **perpetuity**: withdraw only the interest earned. Monthly interest = 80,00,000 × (0.07 ÷ 12) = 80,00,000 × 0.0058333 = **₹46,667**.\nB) ₹60,000 exceeds the interest earned and depletes the corpus (as Q81 showed). C) and D) deplete it far faster. **Note this ignores inflation — a truly sustainable *real* withdrawal would be lower still, which is why the 3–4% rule exists.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** ₹5,00,000 is invested at 10% for 3 years compounded annually, then reinvested at 8% for 4 more years. What is the final value?\nA) ₹9,05,300  B) ₹6,65,500  C) ₹8,50,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Phase 1:** 5,00,000 × (1.10)³ = 5,00,000 × **1.331** = **₹6,65,500**. **Phase 2:** 6,65,500 × (1.08)⁴ = 6,65,500 × **1.36049** = **₹9,05,406**.\nB) ₹6,65,500 stops after phase 1. C) ₹8,50,000 understates phase 2. D) ₹10,00,000 overstates it. **Method: chain the phases — never average the two rates.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** In Q83, what single CAGR over the 7 years is equivalent?\nA) 9.00%  B) 8.86%  C) 10.00%  D) 8.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAGR = (9,05,406 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/7) − 1 = (1.810812)^(1/7) − 1. ln(1.810812) = 0.59380; ÷ 7 = 0.084829; e^0.084829 = **1.08853**. CAGR = **8.85%**.\nA) 9% is the simple average weighted wrongly. C) and D) are the two individual phase rates. **Note the CAGR (8.85%) sits between 8% and 10%, closer to 8% because the 8% period was longer — a good sanity check.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client wants ₹1,00,000 a month in today's terms at retirement in 20 years. Inflation is 6%. What monthly income will she need in the first year of retirement?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹3,20,700  C) ₹1,60,000  D) ₹5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate: 1,00,000 × (1.06)²⁰ = 1,00,000 × **3.20714** = **₹3,20,714**.\nA) ₹2,00,000 assumes only a doubling — at 6% doubling takes 12 years, so 20 years must give more. C) ₹1,60,000 badly understates it. D) ₹5,00,000 overstates it. **The lesson: retirement income targets must always be stated in future rupees.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing Q85: what corpus is needed at retirement to fund ₹3,20,714 a month for 25 years, if the corpus earns 8%?\nA) ₹4,15,50,000  B) ₹9,62,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹1,50,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of an ordinary annuity: r = 0.08 ÷ 12 = **0.0066667**, n = 300. From Q69 the factor is **129.56**. Corpus = 3,20,714 × 129.56 = **₹4,15,51,700**.\nB) ₹9.62 crore is the undiscounted total (3,20,714 × 300). C) and D) are far too low. **Combining Q85 and Q86 gives the complete retirement calculation: inflate the income, then discount it back as an annuity.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An investor is offered 8% compounded monthly or 8.2% compounded annually. Which is better and by how much in EAR terms?\nA) 8% monthly, by about 0.10pp  B) 8.2% annual, by about 0.10pp  C) They are identical  D) 8% monthly, by about 1pp\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EAR of 8% monthly = (1 + 0.08/12)¹² − 1 = (1.0066667)¹² − 1 = **8.30%**. The 8.2% annual option has an EAR of exactly **8.20%**. So 8% monthly wins by **0.10 percentage points**.\nB) Reverses the winner. C) They differ by 0.10pp. D) Overstates the gap tenfold. **Never compare headline rates — always convert to EAR.**\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client saves ₹20,000 a month for 5 years, then ₹40,000 a month for the next 10 years, all at 11% p.a. What is the total corpus after 15 years?\nA) ₹1,20,00,000  B) ₹1,34,05,000  C) ₹60,00,000  D) ₹85,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Split into two phases and value each.\n**Phase 1 (₹20,000 × 60 months):** (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**; factor = (1.72804 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **79.42**; value at year 5 = 20,000 × 79.42 = **₹15,88,400**. This then grows untouched for 10 more years: × (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = × **2.98529** = **₹47,42,000**.\n**Phase 2 (₹40,000 × 120 months):** factor = (2.98529 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **216.58**; value = 40,000 × 216.58 = **₹86,63,200**.\n**Total = 47,42,000 + 86,63,200 = ₹1,34,05,200.**\nA) ₹1,20,00,000 understates it. C) ₹60,00,000 is the total contributed (₹12L + ₹48L). D) ₹85,00,000 omits phase 1's carry-forward growth. **Method: value phase 1 at its end date, carry it forward, then add phase 2 — never annuitise a changing payment in one step.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A ₹30,00,000 loan at 10% for 15 years. If the client prepays ₹5,00,000 at the end of year 5, what happens?\nA) The EMI must rise  B) Either the EMI falls or the tenure shortens — the borrower usually chooses  C) Nothing changes  D) The interest rate falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A prepayment reduces the outstanding principal. The lender then offers either a **lower EMI** for the same remaining tenure, or the **same EMI with a shorter tenure**.\nA) A prepayment never forces a higher EMI. C) Reducing principal always reduces total interest. D) The contractual rate is unaffected. **Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure saves far more interest — the key Chapter 4 insight.**\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investment grows from ₹6,00,000 to ₹7,50,000 in 18 months. What is the annualised return?\nA) 25.00%  B) 16.05%  C) 16.67%  D) 12.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding-period return is 7,50,000 ÷ 6,00,000 = **1.25** over **1.5 years**. Annualised = 1.25^(1/1.5) − 1 = 1.25^0.6667 − 1. ln(1.25) = 0.22314; × 0.6667 = 0.14876; e^0.14876 = **1.16040**. Annualised = **16.04%**.\nA) 25% is the total (un-annualised) return. C) 16.67% divides 25% by 1.5 — a simple-interest shortcut that overstates it. D) 12.5% halves the total return. **Annualising requires compounding, not division.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which sequence of ₹1,00,000 invested annually for 3 years at 10% correctly shows the year-3 value (ordinary annuity)?\nA) 1,00,000×(1.10)³ + 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10)  B) 1,00,000×(1.10)² + 1,00,000×(1.10) + 1,00,000  C) 1,00,000×3×1.10  D) 1,00,000×(1.10)³\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In an **ordinary** annuity the last payment arrives *at* the end of year 3 and earns nothing. The year-1 payment compounds for 2 years, year-2's for 1 year, year-3's for 0. Total = 1,21,000 + 1,10,000 + 1,00,000 = **₹3,31,000**.\nA) Describes an **annuity due** (each payment gets one extra year). C) Ignores compounding. D) Compounds only one payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client's SIP of ₹25,000 a month for 12 years returns 10% p.a. She then stops and lets it grow for 8 more years at 8%. Final value?\nA) ₹1,25,00,000  B) ₹1,28,09,000  C) ₹69,00,000  D) ₹36,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two phases.\n**Accumulation:** r = 0.008333, n = 144. (1.008333)¹⁴⁴ = **3.30692**; factor = (3.30692 − 1) ÷ 0.008333 = **276.83**; corpus = 25,000 × 276.83 = **₹69,20,750**.\n**Growth (no further contribution):** 69,20,750 × (1.08)⁸ = 69,20,750 × **1.85093** = **₹1,28,09,300**.\nA) ₹1,25,00,000 is close but understates it. C) ₹69,00,000 stops after the accumulation phase. D) ₹36,00,000 is the total contributed (25,000 × 144). **Note the corpus nearly doubles in 8 years with no further contribution — compounding on an accumulated base.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Inflation is 6% and a client's salary rises 8% a year. What is the real growth in purchasing power?\nA) 2.00%  B) 1.89%  C) 14.00%  D) 0.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Real growth = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = 1.018868 − 1 = **1.89%**.\nA) 2% is the crude subtraction, which overstates it. C) 14% adds them. D) 0.75% understates it. **The same ratio formula that gives real *returns* gives real *income growth*.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client can invest ₹10,00,000 today at 9% or receive ₹1,30,000 a year for 12 years. Which is better?\nA) The lump sum, since the annuity's PV is about ₹9,30,000  B) The annuity, since its PV is about ₹9,30,000  C) They are equal  D) The annuity, worth ₹15,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PV of the annuity = 1,30,000 × [(1 − (1.09)⁻¹²) ÷ 0.09] = 1,30,000 × **7.1607** = **₹9,30,891**, which is *less* than ₹10,00,000. So investing the lump sum is better.\nB) Values it correctly but draws the wrong conclusion. C) They differ by about ₹69,000. D) ₹15,60,000 is the undiscounted total (1,30,000 × 12), which ignores the time value of money.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A ₹12,00,000 car loan at 11% for 5 years. What is the EMI and total interest?\nA) EMI ₹26,092; interest ₹3,65,520  B) EMI ₹20,000; interest ₹1,00,000  C) EMI ₹30,000; interest ₹6,00,000  D) EMI ₹22,000; interest ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 60. (1.0091667)⁶⁰ = **1.72804**. EMI = 12,00,000 × [0.0091667 × 1.72804] ÷ [1.72804 − 1] = 12,00,000 × 0.01583999 ÷ 0.72804 = **₹26,102**. Total repaid = 26,102 × 60 = ₹15,66,120; interest = **₹3,66,120**.\nB) ₹20,000 is roughly the principal ÷ 60, ignoring interest. C) Overstates both. D) Understates both. Excel: `=PMT(11%/12,60,-1200000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An investor's ₹20,00,000 portfolio must double in real terms in 12 years. Inflation is 6%. What nominal return is required?\nA) 6.0%  B) 12.25%  C) 18.0%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Doubling in **real** terms in 12 years needs a real return of 2^(1/12) − 1 = **5.946%**. The required **nominal** return = (1 + real) × (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.05946 × 1.06 − 1 = 1.12303 − 1 = **12.30%**.\nA) 6% is inflation alone, giving zero real growth. C) 18% overstates it. D) 8% gives only about 1.9% real. **The formula runs both ways: nominal = (1 + real)(1 + inflation) − 1.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A client invests ₹50,000 at the end of each quarter for 8 years at 10% p.a. compounded quarterly. What is the corpus?\nA) ₹16,00,000  B) ₹24,03,000  C) ₹20,00,000  D) ₹30,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Quarterly rate = 0.10 ÷ 4 = **0.025**; n = 8 × 4 = **32**. (1.025)³² = **2.20376**. Factor = (2.20376 − 1) ÷ 0.025 = **48.15**. FV = 50,000 × 48.15 = **₹24,07,500 ≈ ₹24,03,000**.\nA) ₹16,00,000 is the total contributed (50,000 × 32). C) and D) do not follow. **Note the period conversion: quarterly means divide the rate by 4 and multiply the years by 4.**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A retiree needs an income stream rising 5% a year to keep pace with inflation, starting at ₹6,00,000 next year, discounted at 9%, for 20 years. Which formula applies?\nA) PMT ÷ r  B) The growing annuity formula: [PMT₁ ÷ (r − g)] × [1 − ((1+g)/(1+r))ⁿ]  C) PMT × (1+r)  D) PMT × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **finite** stream that **grows** at a constant rate needs the growing-annuity formula. Here PV = [6,00,000 ÷ 0.04] × [1 − (1.05/1.09)²⁰] = 1,50,00,000 × [1 − 0.47615] = 1,50,00,000 × 0.52385 = **₹78,57,750**.\nA) Is a level perpetuity (infinite, no growth). C) Is the annuity-due adjustment. D) Ignores discounting and growth entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client is told \"your investment grew 100% in 6 years, that's 16.7% a year.\" This is:\nA) Correct  B) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 12.25%  C) Wrong — the correct CAGR is about 20%  D) Correct only if compounding is annual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividing the total gain by the number of years is a **simple-interest** error. The correct CAGR = 2^(1/6) − 1 = **12.25%**.\nA) 16.7% (100 ÷ 6) ignores compounding. C) 20% overstates it. D) The error exists regardless of frequency. **Sanity check with the Rule of 72: doubling in 6 years implies about 72 ÷ 6 = 12% — matching 12.25%. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which combination of errors would most inflate a client's projected retirement corpus?\nA) Using an annuity due instead of ordinary, and inflating the goal  B) Ignoring inflation on the goal, using the arithmetic mean return instead of CAGR, and ignoring tax  C) Using CAGR and post-tax returns  D) Using monthly compounding correctly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three errors push the same way: **ignoring inflation** understates what is needed, the **arithmetic mean** overstates the return achieved, and **ignoring tax** overstates what the client keeps. Together they can overstate readiness by a wide margin.\nA) Inflating the goal is *correct* practice, and the annuity-due difference is small. C) Both are the correct, conservative choices. D) Correct method, no distortion. **The professional habit: inflate the goal, use geometric (compound) returns, and project post-tax.**\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Use a spreadsheet — this is exactly how the 2-mark caselets are set.\n\n### Case: Planning Kavita's Retirement\n\nKavita is **34 years old** and plans to retire at **60**. Her current annual household expenses are **₹9,00,000**. She expects to live until **85**.\n\nShe has already accumulated **₹22,00,000** in an EPF and equity mutual fund portfolio.\n\n**Assumptions:**\n- Inflation before *and* after retirement: **6% p.a.**\n- Return on the portfolio before retirement: **11% p.a.** (monthly compounding for SIPs)\n- Return on the corpus after retirement: **8% p.a.**\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** How many years will Kavita spend in accumulation, and how many in retirement?\nA) 26 years accumulating, 25 years retired  B) 34 years accumulating, 26 years retired  C) 26 years accumulating, 51 years retired  D) 25 years accumulating, 25 years retired\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Accumulation** = retirement age − current age = 60 − 34 = **26 years**. **Retirement** = life expectancy − retirement age = 85 − 60 = **25 years**.\nB) 34 is her current age, not a period. C) 51 is her total remaining life expectancy (85 − 34), which spans *both* phases. D) Miscounts the accumulation phase. *(Always establish these two horizons first — every later step depends on them.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What will Kavita's annual expenses be in her first year of retirement?\nA) ₹18,00,000  B) ₹40,93,000  C) ₹27,00,000  D) ₹9,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Inflate for **26 years** at 6%: 9,00,000 × (1.06)²⁶. (1.06)²⁶ = **4.54938**. = **₹40,94,442 ≈ ₹40,93,000**.\nA) ₹18,00,000 assumes a mere doubling — but at 6% money doubles in 12 years, so 26 years gives more than 4×. C) ₹27,00,000 assumes 3×. D) ₹9,00,000 ignores inflation entirely. **Rule of 72 check: 72 ÷ 6 = 12 years per doubling; 26 years ≈ 2.17 doublings ≈ 4.5×. ✓**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Ignoring post-retirement inflation for simplicity, what corpus does Kavita need at 60 to fund ₹40,94,442 a year for 25 years at 8%?\nA) ₹10,23,61,050  B) ₹4,37,00,000  C) ₹2,00,00,000  D) ₹40,94,442\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PV of a 25-year ordinary annuity at 8%: factor = (1 − (1.08)⁻²⁵) ÷ 0.08. (1.08)²⁵ = **6.84848**, so the inverse is **0.146018**. Factor = (1 − 0.146018) ÷ 0.08 = 0.853982 ÷ 0.08 = **10.6748**. Corpus = 40,94,442 × 10.6748 = **₹4,37,07,000**.\nA) ₹10.24 crore is the **undiscounted** total (40,94,442 × 25) — it ignores that the corpus keeps earning 8% throughout retirement. C) ₹2 crore is far too low. D) Is one year's expense. **Note the corpus needed (₹4.37 cr) is well under half the total withdrawn (₹10.24 cr) — the balance does the rest.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Kavita's existing ₹22,00,000 grows at 11% for 26 years. What will it be worth at 60?\nA) ₹3,23,70,000  B) ₹1,50,00,000  C) ₹57,20,000  D) ₹5,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — FV = 22,00,000 × (1.11)²⁶. (1.11)²⁶ = **14.7138**. FV = **₹3,23,70,360**.\nB) ₹1.5 crore understates it badly. C) ₹57,20,000 applies a mere 2.6× (roughly simple interest). D) ₹5 crore overstates it. **Rule of 72 check: at 11%, money doubles roughly every 6.5 years; 26 years ≈ 4 doublings ≈ 16×. ✓ The head start does most of the heavy lifting.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given the corpus needed from QC3 and the existing portfolio's growth from QC4, what monthly SIP at 11% would close the remaining gap?\nA) About ₹6,900  B) About ₹25,000  C) No SIP is needed — the existing corpus is nearly sufficient  D) About ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Gap** = required corpus − grown existing corpus = ₹4,37,07,000 − ₹3,23,70,000 = **₹1,13,37,000**.\n**SIP:** r = 0.11 ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**, n = 26 × 12 = **312**. (1.0091667)³¹² = **16.9846**. Annuity factor = (16.9846 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 15.9846 ÷ 0.0091667 = **1743.8**. PMT = 1,13,37,000 ÷ 1743.8 = **₹6,502 a month**.\n\nSo roughly **₹6,500–6,900 a month** closes the gap — option **A)**.\nB) and D) massively overstate the requirement. C) Is tempting but wrong: the existing corpus reaches ₹3.24 crore against a ₹4.37 crore requirement, a real shortfall of over ₹1.1 crore.\n\n> ⚠️ **Important caveat the exam may probe:** this calculation **ignored post-retirement inflation**. Kavita's expenses will keep rising after 60, so the true corpus requirement is materially higher and the real SIP would be several times ₹6,500. Using the **growing annuity** formula (g = 6%, r = 8%) for the retirement phase is the technically correct approach.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "03", "title": "Cash Flow Management and Budgeting", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ **The ratios in section 3.9 are guaranteed exam marks.** There are ten of them, they are simple to compute, and caselets love them. Learn each formula *and* what a \"good\" value looks like.\n\n---\n\n## 3.1 Why Cash Flow Matters\n\n**Cash flow is the engine of a financial plan.** Investments, insurance premiums and loan EMIs are all funded from one place: the **surplus** between what comes in and what goes out.\n\n$$\\textbf{Surplus (Savings)} = \\textbf{Total Inflows} - \\textbf{Total Outflows}$$\n\n- **No surplus → no plan.** You cannot allocate money you never generated.\n- A client with a ₹5 lakh monthly income and ₹5 lakh of expenses is in a worse position than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000.\n- Cash flow problems are usually **behavioural**, not arithmetic — which is why budgeting is a planning discipline, not an accounting chore.\n\n### The two statements (don't confuse them)\n\n| | **Cash Flow Statement** | **Personal Balance Sheet** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Shows** | Inflows and outflows | Assets and liabilities |\n| **Covers** | A **period** (month/year) | A **point in time** |\n| **Key output** | **Surplus / deficit** | **Net worth** |\n| **Type** | A **flow** | A **stock** |\n\n> 🧠 The two link together: this period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, raising **net worth**.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Cash Inflows and Outflows\n\n### Inflows\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Regular / active** | Salary, professional fees, business income |\n| **Portfolio / passive** | Interest, dividends, rent |\n| **Irregular** | Bonus, incentives, gifts, maturity proceeds, capital gains |\n\n### Outflows\n\n| Type | Examples | Nature |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fixed / committed** | EMIs, rent, insurance premiums, school fees | Contractual — hard to cut quickly |\n| **Variable / discretionary** | Groceries, fuel, dining, travel, entertainment | Controllable |\n| **Periodic / lumpy** | Annual insurance, festivals, vacations, car servicing | Predictable but infrequent — the ones people forget |\n| **Savings / investment** | SIPs, PPF, RD | Should be treated as a *committed* outflow |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most common budgeting failure** is ignoring **periodic outflows**. A budget that balances every month collapses in the month the annual insurance premium falls due. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that aside monthly.\n\n### 3.3.1 Cash management\nEnsuring enough liquidity to meet obligations **as they fall due**, without holding so much idle cash that returns are sacrificed. Two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing or asset sales) and too much (opportunity cost).\n\n### 3.3.2 Income and Expenditure Statement\nThe formal record: list all inflows, list all outflows, and derive the surplus. Best prepared **monthly** with an **annualised** view alongside so lumpy items are visible.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 & 3.7 Preparing a Household Budget\n\nA **budget** is a *forward-looking* plan for income and spending; the income–expenditure statement is the *backward-looking* record of what actually happened.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Record** actual income and spending for 2–3 months (most people badly underestimate discretionary spending).\n2. **Categorise** into fixed, variable, periodic and savings.\n3. **Set targets** for each category.\n4. **Build in savings first** — see \"pay yourself first\" below.\n5. **Monitor** actual vs budget each month.\n6. **Revise** as circumstances change.\n\n### 🔑 \"Pay yourself first\"\n\nThe critical principle. Two orderings:\n\n| Approach | Order | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| ❌ Common | Income → Expenses → *whatever is left* is saved | Savings become the residual — usually near zero |\n| ✅ Correct | Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest | Savings are protected; spending adapts |\n\nAutomate it: schedule SIPs and recurring deposits for the **day after salary credit**.\n\n### The role of forecasting (3.4)\n\nBudgeting looks ahead — anticipating salary increments, a new EMI starting, school fees rising, a child's arrival, a planned car purchase. Forecasting lets the client see a shortfall **before** it becomes a crisis.\n\n### 3.5 Monitoring and provision for savings\n\nMonitoring answers: *where did the plan leak?* Variance analysis (budget vs actual, per category) locates it. Persistent overspending in one category means either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n---\n\n## 3.8 Contingency Planning\n\nThe **contingency (emergency) fund** protects the plan from income shocks and unplanned expenses.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Job loss, medical emergency, urgent repairs, income gap |\n| **Size** | **3–6 months of expenses** (go higher for irregular income, single-earner households, or many dependants) |\n| **Where** | Savings account, sweep-in FD, **liquid/overnight mutual funds** |\n| **Requirements** | **Immediate availability + certainty of value.** Return is secondary |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the emergency fund is sized against **expenses**, not income — because expenses are what must be paid. And it must **never** be parked in equity: the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Beyond cash:** adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** are part of contingency planning, because they stop a single event from consuming the entire fund.\n\n---\n\n## 3.6 Personal Balance Sheet and Net Worth\n\n$$\\textbf{Net Worth} = \\textbf{Total Assets} - \\textbf{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n**Assets** (at current market value):\n- **Liquid** — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs\n- **Financial (invested)** — deposits, bonds, shares, mutual funds, PPF/EPF, insurance cash values\n- **Physical/real** — property, land, gold\n- **Personal-use** — car, appliances, jewellery for use\n\n**Liabilities** (at outstanding principal):\n- **Short-term** — credit cards, overdrafts, dues within a year\n- **Long-term** — home, education, vehicle loans\n\n> ⚠️ Record loans at the **outstanding principal**, never the original amount and never the total of remaining EMIs (that would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n---\n\n## 3.9 ⭐ Evaluating the Financial Position — The Ten Ratios\n\n**Learn every formula and its healthy range. These are the most reliably examinable items in Module 1.**\n\n### 1. Savings Ratio\n$$\\text{Savings Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Savings (Surplus)}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: 20%+ (higher for late starters).** Measures the plan's fuel supply.\n\n### 2. Expenses Ratio\n$$\\text{Expenses Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Expenses}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**The complement:** Savings Ratio + Expenses Ratio = **100%**. A great self-check in the exam.\n\n### 3. Total Assets\nThe sum of all assets at current market value. Not a ratio, but the base for several below.\n\n### 4. Total Liabilities\nThe sum of all outstanding debts at principal value.\n\n### 5. Leverage Ratio (Debt-to-Assets)\n$$\\text{Leverage Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total Liabilities}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Lower is better.** Above ~0.5 means more than half the asset base is financed by debt.\n\n### 6. Net Worth\n$$\\text{Net Worth} = \\text{Total Assets} - \\text{Total Liabilities}$$\n\n### 7. Solvency Ratio\n$$\\text{Solvency Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Net Worth}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better.** Shows the share of assets genuinely owned.\n\n> 🔑 **Solvency Ratio + Leverage Ratio = 1.** Always. Use it to check your arithmetic.\n\n### 8. Liquid Assets\nAssets convertible to cash quickly with no material loss — cash, savings balance, liquid funds, sweep-in FDs. **Equity and PPF do not qualify** (volatile / locked in).\n\n### 9. Liquidity Ratio\n$$\\text{Liquidity Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Liquid Assets}}{\\text{Monthly Expenses}}$$\n**Answer is in *months*. Healthy: 3–6 months.**\n\n### 10. Financial Assets Ratio\n$$\\text{Financial Assets Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Financial Assets}}{\\text{Total Assets}}$$\n**Higher is better for goal funding.** A low ratio signals the \"asset-rich, cash-poor\" trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that cannot be partly sold or spent.\n\n### 11. Debt-to-Income Ratio\n$$\\text{Debt-to-Income Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total EMIs / Debt Servicing}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n**Healthy: below ~35–40%.** Lenders use this to decide eligibility. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n---\n\n### 📋 Ratio Cheat Sheet\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Healthy | Reads as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Savings | Savings ÷ Income | ≥ 20% | % |\n| Expenses | Expenses ÷ Income | ≤ 80% | % |\n| Leverage | Liabilities ÷ Assets | < 0.5 | decimal |\n| Solvency | Net worth ÷ Assets | > 0.5 | decimal |\n| Liquidity | Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses | 3–6 | **months** |\n| Financial assets | Financial assets ÷ Total assets | higher | decimal/% |\n| Debt-to-income | EMIs ÷ Income | < 35–40% | % |\n\n**Three self-checks:**\n1. Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%**\n2. Solvency ratio + leverage ratio = **1**\n3. Liquidity ratio is in **months** — if your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Worked Example\n\n**Ravi's data:** Monthly income ₹1,80,000. Monthly expenses ₹1,26,000 (including EMIs of ₹54,000).\n**Assets:** savings ₹2,40,000; liquid fund ₹1,60,000; mutual funds ₹18,00,000; EPF ₹14,00,000; house ₹80,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹5,00,000.\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹44,00,000; car loan ₹4,00,000.\n\n| Step | Working | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Savings | 1,80,000 − 1,26,000 | **₹54,000** |\n| Savings ratio | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n| Expenses ratio | 1,26,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **70%** (30 + 70 = 100 ✓) |\n| Total assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 + 80 + 9 + 5 (₹ lakh) | **₹1,30,00,000** |\n| Total liabilities | 44 + 4 (₹ lakh) | **₹48,00,000** |\n| Net worth | 1,30,00,000 − 48,00,000 | **₹82,00,000** |\n| Solvency ratio | 82,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.63** ✅ |\n| Leverage ratio | 48,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **0.37** (0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓) |\n| Liquid assets | 2,40,000 + 1,60,000 | **₹4,00,000** |\n| Liquidity ratio | 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 | **3.17 months** ⚠️ thin |\n| Financial assets | 2.4 + 1.6 + 18 + 14 (₹ lakh) | **₹36,00,000** |\n| Financial assets ratio | 36,00,000 ÷ 1,30,00,000 | **27.7%** |\n| Debt-to-income | 54,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30%** ✅ |\n\n**Verdict:** Ravi saves well (30%) and his debt burden is manageable (30%), but his **liquidity is thin at 3.17 months** and **68% of his assets are physical** (house + car + gold). Priorities: raise the emergency fund toward 6 months (≈₹7.6 lakh) and direct future surplus into financial assets.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Surplus | Inflows − Outflows |\n| Cash flow statement vs balance sheet | Flow over a **period** vs stock at a **point** |\n| The link between them | This period's surplus adds to next period's assets → net worth rises |\n| Four outflow types | Fixed, variable, **periodic**, savings |\n| Most-forgotten outflow | **Periodic** (annual premiums, festivals) — divide by 12 and set aside |\n| Pay yourself first | Save **before** spending, automated on salary day |\n| Emergency fund size | **3–6 months of expenses** (not income) |\n| Emergency fund location | Savings / sweep-in FD / liquid funds — **never equity** |\n| Savings + expenses ratios | Sum to **100%** |\n| Solvency + leverage ratios | Sum to **1** |\n| Liquidity ratio units | **Months** |\n| Liquid assets exclude | Equity, PPF, property, personal-use assets |\n| Debt-to-income ceiling | ~**35–40%** |\n| Low financial assets ratio signals | **Asset-rich, cash-poor** — wealth locked in property |\n\n> **Exam tip:** in every caselet, compute **total assets and total liabilities first**, then derive every ratio from those two numbers. The distractors are almost always the two subtotals themselves, or the complement ratio.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The ten ratios are near-certain exam marks — drill them until instant.**\n\n**Q1.** What is the formula for surplus?\n> **A:** **Surplus (Savings) = Total Inflows − Total Outflows.** It is the engine of the whole plan — no surplus, no plan.\n\n**Q2.** What is the difference between the cash flow statement and the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** Cash flow statement = **flows over a period**, output is the **surplus**. Balance sheet = **stock at a point in time**, output is **net worth**.\n\n**Q3.** How do the two statements connect?\n> **A:** This period's **surplus** becomes an addition to next period's **assets**, which raises **net worth**.\n\n**Q4.** Name the four types of cash outflow.\n> **A:** **Fixed/committed** (EMIs, rent, premiums), **variable/discretionary** (groceries, dining), **periodic/lumpy** (annual premiums, festivals, vacations), and **savings/investment**.\n\n**Q5.** What is the most common budgeting failure?\n> **A:** Ignoring **periodic (lumpy) outflows**. The fix: divide each annual expense by 12 and set that amount aside every month.\n\n**Q6.** Explain \"pay yourself first.\"\n> **A:** Save **before** you spend: Income → **Savings (automated)** → Expenses from the rest. The wrong order treats savings as the residual, which ends up near zero.\n\n**Q7.** What is a budget, versus an income and expenditure statement?\n> **A:** A **budget is forward-looking** (a plan); the **income and expenditure statement is backward-looking** (the record of what actually happened).\n\n**Q8.** How large should a contingency fund be, and measured against what?\n> **A:** **3–6 months of EXPENSES** — not income, because expenses are what must be paid. Go higher for irregular income, single-earner households or many dependants.\n\n**Q9.** Where should an emergency fund be held, and why not equity?\n> **A:** Savings account, sweep-in FD or **liquid/overnight funds**. Never equity — the moment you need it (recession, job loss) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n**Q10.** State the savings ratio formula and a healthy level.\n> **A:** **Savings ÷ Gross Income**; healthy is **20% or more**.\n\n**Q11.** State the expenses ratio, and its relationship to the savings ratio.\n> **A:** **Expenses ÷ Gross Income.** Savings ratio + expenses ratio = **100%** — a useful arithmetic self-check.\n\n**Q12.** State the leverage ratio.\n> **A:** **Total Liabilities ÷ Total Assets.** Lower is better; above ~0.5 means over half the asset base is debt-financed.\n\n**Q13.** State the solvency ratio and its relationship to leverage.\n> **A:** **Net Worth ÷ Total Assets.** **Solvency + Leverage = 1**, always.\n\n**Q14.** State the liquidity ratio — and what unit is the answer in?\n> **A:** **Liquid Assets ÷ Monthly Expenses.** The answer is in **months**; healthy is **3–6**.\n\n**Q15.** Which assets count as \"liquid\", and which commonly-mistaken ones do not?\n> **A:** Count cash, savings balance, liquid/overnight funds, sweep-in FDs. **Do NOT count** equity (volatile), PPF/EPF (locked), property or personal-use assets.\n\n**Q16.** State the financial assets ratio and what a low value signals.\n> **A:** **Financial Assets ÷ Total Assets.** A low value signals the **asset-rich, cash-poor** trap — wealth locked in a self-occupied house that can't be partly sold.\n\n**Q17.** State the debt-to-income ratio and its ceiling.\n> **A:** **Total EMIs ÷ Gross Income**; keep it below about **35–40%**. Above 50% the household is debt-stressed.\n\n**Q18.** Name the three arithmetic self-checks for the ratio questions.\n> **A:** 1) Savings % + expenses % = **100**. 2) Solvency + leverage = **1**. 3) Liquidity ratio is in **months** — a percentage-looking answer means you divided by the wrong thing.\n\n**Q19.** At what value are liabilities recorded on the personal balance sheet?\n> **A:** The **outstanding principal** — never the original loan amount, never the total of remaining EMIs (which would include unaccrued future interest).\n\n**Q20.** At what value are assets recorded?\n> **A:** **Current market value**, so the balance sheet reflects reality today.\n\n**Q21.** Name the three categories of cash inflow.\n> **A:** **Regular/active** (salary, fees), **portfolio/passive** (interest, dividends, rent), and **irregular** (bonus, gifts, maturity proceeds).\n\n**Q22.** What are the two failure modes of cash management?\n> **A:** **Too little** liquidity (forced borrowing or asset sales) and **too much** idle cash (opportunity cost of foregone returns).\n\n**Q23.** What are the six steps in preparing a budget?\n> **A:** **Record** actuals → **categorise** → **set targets** → **build in savings first** → **monitor** actual vs budget → **revise**.\n\n**Q24.** What does variance analysis tell you?\n> **A:** *Where the plan leaked.* Comparing budget vs actual by category locates persistent overspending — meaning either the target was unrealistic or behaviour must change.\n\n**Q25.** Is a client earning ₹5 lakh a month and spending ₹5 lakh better off than one earning ₹1 lakh and spending ₹70,000?\n> **A:** **No.** The first has a **zero surplus** and therefore no capacity to fund any goal; the second saves ₹30,000 a month. Income level is not financial health — surplus is.\n\n**Q26.** Besides cash, what else belongs to contingency planning?\n> **A:** Adequate **health insurance** and **life insurance** — they stop a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\n\n**Q27.** Why should savings be treated as a committed outflow rather than a residual?\n> **A:** Because discretionary spending expands to fill available income. Committing (and automating) savings on salary day protects the plan's fuel supply.\n\n**Q28.** A client has ₹4,00,000 liquid and spends ₹1,26,000 a month. What is the liquidity ratio and the verdict?\n> **A:** 4,00,000 ÷ 1,26,000 = **3.17 months** — just inside the 3–6 month norm, but **thin**; building toward 6 months is advisable.\n\n**Q29.** What is the very first thing to compute in any ratio caselet?\n> **A:** **Total assets and total liabilities.** Almost every other ratio derives from those two figures — and the distractors are usually the subtotals themselves.\n\n**Q30.** Total assets ₹1,30,00,000; total liabilities ₹48,00,000. Give net worth, solvency and leverage.\n> **A:** Net worth = **₹82,00,000**. Solvency = 82 ÷ 130 = **0.63**. Leverage = 48 ÷ 130 = **0.37**. Check: 0.63 + 0.37 = 1 ✓\n", "questions": "# Chapter 3: Cash Flow Management and Budgeting — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The ten ratios dominate this chapter and the exam.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Surplus (savings) is calculated as:\nA) Total assets − total liabilities  B) Total inflows − total outflows  C) Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses  D) Income × savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **cash flow** concept: what comes in minus what goes out over a period.\nA) Is net worth, a balance sheet concept. C) Is the liquidity ratio. D) Is circular — the savings rate is derived *from* the surplus.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The savings ratio is:\nA) Savings ÷ Total assets  B) Savings ÷ Gross income  C) Savings ÷ Expenses  D) Income ÷ Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Savings ÷ Gross Income**, expressed as a percentage.\nA) Divides a flow by a stock — meaningless. C) Would exceed 100% for good savers and is not the standard definition. D) Is the reciprocal.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The liquidity ratio is expressed in:\nA) Percentage  B) Rupees  C) Months  D) A decimal between 0 and 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Liquid assets ÷ **monthly** expenses answers \"how many months could I survive?\" — so the unit is **months**.\nA), B) and D) all indicate a wrong denominator. If your answer looks like a percentage, you divided by income or assets instead of monthly expenses.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The solvency ratio is:\nA) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  B) Net worth ÷ Total assets  C) Net worth ÷ Total liabilities  D) Total assets ÷ Net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Net worth ÷ Total assets** — the share of the asset base genuinely owned.\nA) Is the **leverage** ratio. C) and D) are not standard ratios in this syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Solvency ratio + leverage ratio equals:\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1  D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Since net worth + liabilities = assets, dividing through by assets gives solvency + leverage = **1**. A reliable arithmetic self-check.\nA), B) and D) do not follow from the identity.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** A contingency fund should typically cover:\nA) 3–6 months of income  B) 3–6 months of expenses  C) 1 month of expenses  D) 24 months of expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sized against **expenses**, because expenses are what must actually be paid during an income gap.\nA) Using income overstates the requirement for high savers. C) One month is too thin for a job loss. D) Two years parks far too much in low-return assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of these qualifies as a liquid asset?\nA) Public Provident Fund balance  B) Equity mutual fund units  C) Sweep-in fixed deposit  D) Residential property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A sweep-in FD converts to cash instantly with no loss of value.\nA) PPF is locked in for a long term. B) Equity is volatile — the value is not certain when needed. D) Property takes months to sell.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** \"Pay yourself first\" means:\nA) Pay your EMIs before anything else  B) Save before spending, ideally automated  C) Withdraw your salary in cash  D) Repay the smallest loan first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Route savings out **before** discretionary spending, automated on or just after salary day.\nA) EMIs are contractual outflows, not savings. C) Has nothing to do with the principle. D) Describes the debt \"snowball\" method (Chapter 4).\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of these is a periodic (lumpy) outflow?\nA) Monthly rent  B) The annual motor insurance premium  C) The monthly SIP  D) Daily commuting cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic outflows are **predictable but infrequent** — annual premiums, festivals, vacations, servicing.\nA), C) and D) all recur monthly or daily and are easy to budget.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The leverage ratio is:\nA) Net worth ÷ Total assets  B) Total liabilities ÷ Total assets  C) EMIs ÷ Income  D) Liquid assets ÷ Expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total liabilities ÷ Total assets**; lower is better.\nA) Is solvency. C) Is debt-to-income. D) Is liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Savings ratio plus expenses ratio equals:\nA) 50%  B) 100%  C) 1 month  D) It varies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Since savings + expenses = income, dividing by income gives **100%**.\nA) and C) do not follow. D) The identity always holds when both are measured against the same gross income.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The debt-to-income ratio is generally considered healthy below:\nA) 10%  B) 35–40%  C) 60%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lenders and planners generally regard **under 35–40%** as manageable.\nA) 10% is unnecessarily strict and few borrowers would qualify for a home loan. C) and D) indicate debt stress.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A budget differs from an income and expenditure statement because a budget is:\nA) Backward-looking  B) Forward-looking  C) Prepared only by companies  D) The same thing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **budget plans ahead**; the income and expenditure statement **records what happened**.\nA) Describes the income and expenditure statement. C) Households prepare budgets too. D) They serve different purposes.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The financial assets ratio is:\nA) Financial assets ÷ Total assets  B) Financial assets ÷ Income  C) Total assets ÷ Financial assets  D) Financial assets ÷ Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It measures what share of wealth is in **financial** (as opposed to physical/personal-use) form.\nB), C) and D) are not standard definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which statement about the personal balance sheet is correct?\nA) It covers a period of time  B) It is a snapshot at a point in time  C) It shows the monthly surplus  D) It records only liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A balance sheet is a **snapshot**; the cash flow statement covers a period.\nA) and C) describe the cash flow statement. D) It records both sides.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Liabilities on the personal balance sheet are recorded at:\nA) The original loan sanctioned  B) The outstanding principal  C) The sum of all remaining EMIs  D) The market value of the asset financed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only the **outstanding principal** is currently owed.\nA) Ignores repayments made. C) Includes unaccrued future interest, which is not yet a liability. D) That is the asset side.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Which of these is a fixed (committed) outflow?\nA) Dining out  B) A home loan EMI  C) A weekend trip  D) Impulse shopping\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EMIs are contractual and cannot be cut at short notice.\nA), C) and D) are all discretionary/variable and are the first place to look when a budget needs fixing.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which best describes cash management?\nA) Holding as much cash as possible  B) Meeting obligations as they fall due without holding excessive idle cash  C) Investing everything in equity  D) Avoiding all bank accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Balance the two failure modes: too little (forced borrowing) and too much (opportunity cost).\nA) Excessive cash sacrifices returns. C) Equity cannot meet near-term obligations reliably. D) Impractical.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of the following is a portfolio (passive) inflow?\nA) Monthly salary  B) Rental income from a let-out property  C) Professional consulting fees  D) A performance bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Portfolio inflows arise from **assets** — interest, dividends and rent.\nA) and C) are active/earned income. D) Is an irregular inflow tied to employment.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A client with zero surplus:\nA) Has an excellent financial position  B) Cannot fund any goal until cash flow is fixed  C) Should immediately buy equity  D) Has a high savings ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With no surplus there is nothing to allocate; **cash flow management must precede investment planning**.\nA) Zero surplus is the weakest possible position regardless of income. C) There is nothing to invest. D) The savings ratio is 0%.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Net worth is:\nA) Income − Expenses  B) Total assets − Total liabilities  C) Liquid assets − Short-term debt  D) Assets + Liabilities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The balance sheet identity.\nA) Is the surplus. C) Is a partial measure, not net worth. D) Adds instead of subtracting.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of these should NOT be counted as a liquid asset?\nA) Balance in a savings account  B) An overnight mutual fund  C) EPF balance  D) Cash in hand\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **EPF is locked in** until specified events, so it cannot serve an emergency.\nA), B) and D) are all immediately available at certain value.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Monitoring a budget primarily involves:\nA) Comparing budgeted with actual figures by category  B) Increasing income  C) Cancelling all insurance  D) Buying more assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Variance analysis** — budget vs actual, per category — locates where the plan leaked.\nB), C) and D) are not monitoring activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which is the correct order under \"pay yourself first\"?\nA) Income → Expenses → Savings  B) Income → Savings → Expenses  C) Savings → Income → Expenses  D) Expenses → Income → Savings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Savings are routed out **immediately after income is received**, and spending comes from the remainder.\nA) Is the common (failing) order that makes savings a residual. C) and D) are not feasible sequences.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A household with irregular income should hold a contingency fund at:\nA) The lower end of the 3–6 month range  B) The upper end of the range or beyond  C) Zero, since income is variable  D) Exactly one month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Greater income uncertainty means a longer possible gap, so the buffer should be **larger**.\nA) Understates the need. C) and D) leave the household badly exposed.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The expenses ratio measures:\nA) Expenses ÷ Total assets  B) Expenses ÷ Gross income  C) Expenses ÷ Savings  D) Fixed ÷ Variable expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Expenses ÷ Gross income**, the complement of the savings ratio.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of these is a personal-use asset?\nA) A let-out flat  B) The family car  C) A corporate bond  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Personal-use assets are held for use, typically depreciate and produce no income.\nA) Generates rent, so it is an investment asset. C) and D) are financial assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A savings ratio of 30% means:\nA) 30% of assets are savings  B) 30% of gross income is saved  C) 30 months of expenses are covered  D) Debt is 30% of income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The savings ratio measures the share of **income** saved.\nA) Would be an asset-composition measure. C) Is liquidity. D) Is debt-to-income.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The main reason to categorise expenses is to:\nA) Increase total spending  B) Identify which expenses are controllable  C) Reduce income  D) Satisfy tax authorities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Separating **fixed** from **variable** shows where cuts are actually possible.\nA) and C) are not objectives. D) Personal budgets are not filed with tax authorities.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which best describes the link between the two statements?\nA) They are unrelated  B) This period's surplus adds to assets, raising net worth  C) Net worth determines income  D) The balance sheet determines expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The surplus generated in a period flows onto the balance sheet as additional assets.\nA) They are tightly linked. C) and D) reverse the causation.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A leverage ratio of 0.65 indicates:\nA) 65% of assets are financed by debt  B) 65% of income goes to EMIs  C) 65 months of liquidity  D) A 65% savings rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets, so **65% of the asset base is debt-financed** — high, and above the ~0.5 comfort level.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Contingency planning includes which of the following besides a cash buffer?\nA) Buying more equity  B) Adequate health and life insurance  C) Taking a larger home loan  D) Cancelling the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurance stops a single event from consuming the entire emergency fund.\nA) Equity is not a contingency asset. C) More debt increases fragility. D) Removes the buffer entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which of these forecasting events should a budget anticipate?\nA) A school fee increase next April  B) Yesterday's grocery bill  C) Last year's bonus  D) The 2019 tax rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Forecasting is **forward-looking**: increments, new EMIs starting, fee rises, a child's arrival.\nB) and C) are historical records. D) Is obsolete data.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A low financial assets ratio typically signals:\nA) Excellent goal-funding capacity  B) Wealth locked in physical assets — \"asset-rich, cash-poor\"  C) High liquidity  D) Zero debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most wealth sits in a house or other physical assets that cannot be partly sold or spent.\nA) Reverses it. C) Physical assets are illiquid. D) The ratio says nothing about debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Assets on the personal balance sheet should be recorded at:\nA) Original cost  B) Current market value  C) Insured value  D) Zero until sold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Current market value** keeps the picture realistic.\nA) Cost ignores gains and losses. C) Insured value reflects replacement, not market worth. D) Unsold assets are still assets.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Monthly income ₹1,50,000, monthly expenses ₹1,08,000. What are the savings and expenses ratios?\nA) 28% and 72%  B) 72% and 28%  C) 38.9% and 61.1%  D) 30% and 70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 1,50,000 − 1,08,000 = **₹42,000**. Savings ratio = 42,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **28%**. Expenses ratio = 1,08,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **72%**. Check: 28 + 72 = 100 ✓\nB) Reverses the two. C) 38.9% wrongly divides savings by expenses (42,000 ÷ 1,08,000). D) Does not match the figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Total assets ₹90,00,000; total liabilities ₹36,00,000. What are net worth, solvency and leverage?\nA) ₹54,00,000; 0.60; 0.40  B) ₹1,26,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  C) ₹54,00,000; 0.40; 0.60  D) ₹54,00,000; 1.50; 0.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = 90 − 36 = **₹54,00,000**. Solvency = 54 ÷ 90 = **0.60**. Leverage = 36 ÷ 90 = **0.40**. Check: 0.60 + 0.40 = 1 ✓\nB) Adds instead of subtracting for net worth. C) Swaps solvency and leverage. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client holds cash ₹60,000, savings balance ₹2,40,000, liquid fund ₹3,00,000, equity funds ₹15,00,000 and PPF ₹9,00,000. Monthly expenses are ₹1,50,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 4.0 months  B) 2.0 months  C) 18.0 months  D) 1.6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Liquid assets = 60,000 + 2,40,000 + 3,00,000 = **₹6,00,000**. Ratio = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = **4.0 months**.\nB) 2.0 counts only the savings balance. C) 18.0 wrongly includes equity and PPF — neither is liquid. D) 1.6 counts only cash plus part of the savings balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A household's monthly EMIs total ₹68,000 against a gross monthly income of ₹1,70,000. The debt-to-income ratio is:\nA) 25%  B) 40%  C) 250%  D) 60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 68,000 ÷ 1,70,000 = **40%** — right at the upper limit of the healthy range, so no further borrowing capacity.\nA) 25% understates it. C) 250% is the reciprocal. D) 60% does not match.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A budget balances every month yet the client repeatedly runs a deficit in March and October. The most likely cause is:\nA) The savings ratio is too high  B) Periodic (lumpy) expenses were not provisioned monthly  C) Income is too high  D) The liquidity ratio is excessive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Annual premiums, school fees and festival spending cluster in particular months. **Divide each annual amount by 12 and set it aside monthly.**\nA) A high savings ratio is desirable and would not cause seasonal deficits. C) and D) are not causes of a deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client is in the stronger position?\nA) Income ₹6,00,000/month, expenses ₹6,00,000/month  B) Income ₹1,20,000/month, expenses ₹84,000/month  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — B saves **₹36,000 a month (30%)**; A saves **nothing (0%)**. Financial health is measured by **surplus**, not income level.\nA) A high income with zero surplus funds no goals. C) They are very different. D) The surplus makes them directly comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client's liquidity ratio is 1.2 months and she is the sole earner with two dependants. The adviser should:\nA) Recommend investing the buffer in mid-caps  B) Build the contingency fund toward at least 6 months before other investing  C) Take on a personal loan  D) Do nothing — 1.2 months is fine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1.2 months is far below the 3–6 month norm**, and a sole earner with dependants sits at the *upper* end of the requirement.\nA) Defeats the purpose of an emergency fund. C) Debt is not a substitute for savings. D) The household is one income shock from crisis.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Total assets ₹1,20,00,000, of which the self-occupied house is ₹92,00,000 and the car ₹8,00,000. What is the financial assets ratio?\nA) 83.3%  B) 16.7%  C) 76.7%  D) 23.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Physical + personal-use = 92,00,000 + 8,00,000 = ₹1,00,00,000. Financial assets = 1,20,00,000 − 1,00,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**. Ratio = 20 ÷ 120 = **16.7%**.\nA) 83.3% is the *physical* asset share. C) and D) do not follow. **A 16.7% financial assets ratio is the classic asset-rich, cash-poor signature.**\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which action most directly improves the savings ratio?\nA) Buying a larger house  B) Cutting discretionary spending and automating a SIP on salary day  C) Increasing the credit card limit  D) Revaluing assets upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reducing outflows and committing savings first both raise the surplus.\nA) Adds an EMI, lowering the surplus. C) Raises borrowing capacity, not savings. D) Changes the balance sheet, not the cash flow.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client's solvency ratio is 0.25. This means:\nA) Only 25% of the asset base is genuinely owned  B) 25% of income goes to EMIs  C) The client has 25 months of liquidity  D) The client saves 25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets, so **75% of the asset base is financed by debt** (leverage = 0.75) — a highly leveraged, fragile position.\nB) Is debt-to-income. C) Is liquidity. D) Is the savings ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which pair of expenses should be attacked first when a client must cut spending?\nA) Home loan EMI and insurance premium  B) Dining out and subscription services  C) School fees and utilities  D) SIP and PPF contribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Variable/discretionary** expenses are the controllable ones.\nA) EMIs and premiums are contractual — cutting insurance also destroys protection. C) Largely essential and committed. D) Cutting savings defeats the purpose of the exercise.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client's monthly surplus is ₹40,000 but the liquidity ratio is 0.8 months. What is the correct sequencing?\nA) Start an equity SIP with the entire surplus  B) Direct the surplus to the emergency fund until 3–6 months is reached, then invest  C) Prepay the home loan entirely  D) Buy a second property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **less than one month** of buffer, any shock forces debt or a distressed asset sale. Build the buffer first, then invest.\nA) Leaves the household exposed. C) Locks money into an illiquid asset while the buffer is empty. D) Worsens both liquidity and leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Gross monthly income ₹2,00,000; expenses ₹1,40,000 including ₹60,000 of EMIs. What are the savings ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 30% and 30%  B) 30% and 43%  C) 40% and 30%  D) 70% and 30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,00,000 − 1,40,000 = ₹60,000; savings ratio = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 60,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **30%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) Miscomputes savings. D) 70% is the expenses ratio. **Both ratios are healthy here.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Why is the emergency fund measured against expenses rather than income?\nA) Income is easier to measure  B) Because expenses are what must be paid during an income gap  C) Regulations require it  D) Expenses are always higher than income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — During unemployment, income stops but **expenses continue** — that is the amount the buffer must cover.\nA) Income is usually easier to measure, if anything. C) No regulation prescribes it. D) Expenses are normally *lower* than income for a solvent household.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client's leverage ratio rises from 0.35 to 0.58 in two years without new borrowing. The most likely explanation is:\nA) Assets fell in value  B) Income rose  C) EMIs were repaid  D) The savings ratio improved\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — With liabilities unchanged, leverage (liabilities ÷ assets) rises only if the **denominator falls** — i.e. asset values dropped.\nB) Income is not in the formula. C) Repayment would *lower* liabilities and hence leverage. D) The savings ratio is not in the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which is the best treatment of a ₹3,60,000 annual insurance premium in a monthly budget?\nA) Ignore it until it falls due  B) Provision ₹30,000 a month into a dedicated liquid holding  C) Borrow when it falls due  D) Pay it from the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Divide the annual amount by 12: 3,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹30,000 a month** set aside.\nA) Guarantees a deficit in that month. C) Borrowing for a *predictable* expense is poor practice. D) The emergency fund is for the *unpredictable*, not the foreseeable.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client has total assets ₹75,00,000 and a solvency ratio of 0.8. What are net worth and total liabilities?\nA) ₹60,00,000 and ₹15,00,000  B) ₹15,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  C) ₹60,00,000 and ₹60,00,000  D) ₹93,75,000 and ₹18,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Net worth = solvency × assets = 0.8 × 75,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**. Liabilities = assets − net worth = 75,00,000 − 60,00,000 = **₹15,00,000**. Check: leverage = 15 ÷ 75 = 0.2, and 0.8 + 0.2 = 1 ✓\nB) Reverses them. C) Is arithmetically impossible. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client insists on counting the self-occupied house as a liquid asset. The adviser should explain that:\nA) That is correct practice  B) Property cannot be converted to cash quickly or partly, so it is not liquid  C) All assets are liquid  D) Only gold is liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity requires **speed and certainty of value**. Property takes months to sell, cannot be sold in slices, and the family lives in it.\nA) Would badly overstate the liquidity ratio. C) Liquidity varies widely by asset. D) Gold is more liquid than property but is not the only liquid asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Monthly income ₹90,000; fixed outflows ₹40,000; variable ₹28,000; periodic (annualised monthly) ₹7,000. What is the surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹15,000 and 16.7%  B) ₹22,000 and 24.4%  C) ₹50,000 and 55.6%  D) ₹15,000 and 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total outflows = 40,000 + 28,000 + 7,000 = **₹75,000**. Surplus = 90,000 − 75,000 = **₹15,000**. Savings ratio = 15,000 ÷ 90,000 = **16.7%**.\nB) Omits the periodic provision. C) Counts only fixed outflows. D) Computes the ratio incorrectly. **At 16.7% the client is below the 20% guideline.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** The primary benefit of automating savings on salary day is:\nA) It increases the interest rate earned  B) It removes reliance on willpower, protecting the surplus  C) It reduces tax  D) It shortens loan tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Automation makes saving the **default**, so discretionary spending adapts to what remains.\nA) The rate depends on the product, not the timing. C) Automation itself gives no tax benefit. D) It has no direct effect on loans.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client's liquidity ratio is 14 months. The adviser should note that:\nA) This is ideal  B) Excess idle cash carries an opportunity cost and should be deployed  C) More cash should be added  D) The client is insolvent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond 3–6 months, extra cash earns low returns and **loses purchasing power to inflation**. The excess should be invested toward goals.\nA) It is well above the norm. C) Would worsen the drag. D) Liquidity says nothing about solvency.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which of these correctly ranks the priority when a client has both a low liquidity ratio and a high debt-to-income ratio?\nA) Invest in equity first  B) Address both — build a minimum buffer while restructuring or reducing debt  C) Ignore the buffer and repay all debt  D) Ignore the debt and build a 12-month buffer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are fragilities. Practice is to build a **minimum buffer (about 1–3 months)** so a shock doesn't force fresh borrowing, while simultaneously attacking the costliest debt.\nA) Investing while debt-stressed and illiquid is premature. C) Zero buffer means any shock creates new debt. D) A 12-month buffer while paying high interest is inefficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A client's expenses ratio is 92%. What does this imply?\nA) A savings ratio of 8% — very low, leaving little to fund goals  B) A savings ratio of 92%  C) A liquidity ratio of 8 months  D) A leverage ratio of 0.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 100% − 92% = **8%**, well below the 20% guideline.\nB) Reverses the two. C) and D) confuse this ratio with unrelated ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is the correct classification of a monthly SIP in a household budget?\nA) A variable/discretionary expense  B) A committed outflow, treated like a fixed obligation  C) An inflow  D) A liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treating the SIP as **committed** (like an EMI) is exactly what \"pay yourself first\" achieves.\nA) Treating it as discretionary is why savings get skipped. C) It is money going out. D) It creates an asset, not a liability.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client with a 45% debt-to-income ratio applies for another loan. The likely outcome and advice is:\nA) Easy approval; take the loan  B) Likely rejection or costly terms; reduce existing debt before borrowing more  C) The ratio is irrelevant to lenders  D) Increase the loan amount to improve the ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At **45%** the household is already above the ~35–40% comfort zone; lenders use this ratio directly for eligibility.\nA) Approval is unlikely on good terms. C) It is a primary lending criterion. D) More debt raises the ratio further.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which is the correct first step in preparing a household budget?\nA) Setting spending targets  B) Recording actual income and spending for 2–3 months  C) Buying insurance  D) Opening a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot set realistic targets without knowing the **actual** baseline — and most people significantly underestimate discretionary spending.\nA) Comes after recording and categorising. C) and D) are unrelated to budget preparation.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client's net worth is ₹40,00,000 and leverage ratio 0.60. What are total assets?\nA) ₹66,66,667  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹64,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage 0.60 means solvency = 1 − 0.60 = **0.40**. Since solvency = net worth ÷ assets, assets = net worth ÷ 0.40 = 40,00,000 ÷ 0.40 = **₹1,00,00,000**. (Check: liabilities = 0.60 × 1,00,00,000 = ₹60,00,000; 1,00,00,000 − 60,00,000 = ₹40,00,000 ✓)\nA) Divides by 0.60. C) Multiplies by 0.60. D) Does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which best explains why a high-income client can still be financially fragile?\nA) High income guarantees fragility  B) Lifestyle inflation can push the expenses ratio near 100%, leaving no surplus or buffer  C) High income prevents borrowing  D) Income has no effect on planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Lifestyle inflation**: spending rises to match income, so the surplus stays near zero and no emergency fund accumulates.\nA) High income does not *cause* fragility — spending behaviour does. C) High earners typically borrow more easily. D) Income matters, but only via the surplus it generates.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client receives a ₹5,00,000 annual bonus. The best budgeting treatment is:\nA) Treat it as regular monthly income  B) Treat it as an irregular inflow and allocate deliberately — buffer, debt reduction, goals  C) Spend it entirely  D) Ignore it in the plan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonuses are **irregular inflows**. Pre-committing them (to the buffer, costly debt or a goal) prevents the \"windfall gets absorbed\" effect.\nA) Treating a variable bonus as regular income risks over-committing. C) Wastes a significant planning opportunity. D) Ignoring it forfeits the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which combination indicates the healthiest financial position?\nA) Savings ratio 5%, liquidity 1 month, leverage 0.7  B) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 5 months, leverage 0.3  C) Savings ratio 28%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.8  D) Savings ratio 12%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three are healthy: savings **above 20%**, liquidity **inside 3–6 months**, leverage **below 0.5**.\nA) Fails on every measure. C) Saves well but is dangerously illiquid and over-leveraged. D) Is mediocre on all three.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The main purpose of the income and expenditure statement is to:\nA) Value the client's assets  B) Record actual inflows and outflows over a period and derive the surplus  C) Compute the loan EMI  D) File tax returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is the **backward-looking record** whose key output is the surplus.\nA) That is the balance sheet. C) EMIs use the annuity formula. D) It is a planning tool, not a tax filing.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client's liquid assets are ₹9,00,000 and annual expenses ₹21,60,000. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 5 months  B) 0.42 months  C) 41.7%  D) 2.4 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The denominator must be **monthly** expenses: 21,60,000 ÷ 12 = **₹1,80,000**. Ratio = 9,00,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **5 months**.\nB) Divides by annual expenses. C) Expresses it as a percentage of annual expenses — wrong unit. D) Does not follow. **The classic trap: always convert annual expenses to monthly first.**\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which statement about surplus is correct?\nA) It appears on the balance sheet  B) It is a flow that, once saved, becomes an asset and raises net worth  C) It equals net worth  D) It is a liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus is a **flow**; retaining it converts it into a **stock** (an asset), raising net worth.\nA) The balance sheet shows the *resulting asset*, not the flow. C) They are different concepts. D) It is not an obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A client cuts a ₹12,000 monthly discretionary expense and redirects it to a SIP. The immediate effect is:\nA) Savings ratio rises; expenses ratio falls  B) Both ratios rise  C) Net worth falls  D) The liquidity ratio falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Lower expenses raise the surplus, so the **savings ratio rises** and the **expenses ratio falls** by the same percentage-point amount.\nB) They are complements and must move in opposite directions. C) Saving *raises* net worth over time. D) Redirecting to a SIP does not reduce liquid assets already held.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which is the strongest argument for provisioning periodic expenses monthly?\nA) It reduces the annual amount payable  B) It smooths cash flow so no single month produces a deficit  C) It earns higher returns  D) It is a legal requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The total is unchanged; **the timing is what breaks budgets**, and monthly provisioning smooths it.\nA) The amount payable is fixed. C) A liquid provision earns modest returns at best. D) There is no such requirement.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A household: monthly income ₹2,20,000; expenses ₹1,54,000 (including EMIs of ₹66,000). Assets: savings ₹3,00,000; liquid fund ₹2,50,000; mutual funds ₹22,00,000; EPF ₹16,00,000; house ₹85,00,000; car ₹10,00,000; gold ₹6,00,000. Liabilities: home loan ₹50,00,000; car loan ₹5,00,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,44,50,000  B) ₹89,50,000  C) ₹55,00,000  D) ₹79,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 + 85,00,000 + 10,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹1,44,50,000**. **Total liabilities** = 50,00,000 + 5,00,000 = **₹55,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,44,50,000 − 55,00,000 = ₹89,50,000.**\nA) ₹1,44,50,000 is total assets. C) ₹55,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Miscomputes the subtraction. *(The distractors are the two subtotals — always the pattern.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's data, what are the solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.62 and 0.38  B) 0.38 and 0.62  C) 0.62 and 0.62  D) 1.62 and 0.38\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = net worth ÷ assets = 89,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.619 ≈ 0.62**. Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets = 55,00,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **0.381 ≈ 0.38**. Check: 0.62 + 0.38 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Violates the identity. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Using Q71's data, what is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 1.95 months  B) 3.57 months  C) 17.9 months  D) 5.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquid assets = savings ₹3,00,000 + liquid fund ₹2,50,000 = **₹5,50,000**. Monthly expenses = **₹1,54,000**. Ratio = 5,50,000 ÷ 1,54,000 = **3.57 months**.\nA) 1.95 counts only the savings balance. C) 17.9 wrongly includes mutual funds and EPF. D) 5.5 is the liquid assets in ₹ lakh, not the ratio. **Verdict: just inside the norm, but thin.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Using Q71's data, what are the savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and financial assets ratio?\nA) 30%, 30%, 30.1%  B) 30%, 43%, 30.1%  C) 70%, 30%, 16.6%  D) 28%, 30%, 44%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 2,20,000 − 1,54,000 = **₹66,000**; savings ratio = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 66,000 ÷ 2,20,000 = **30%**. Financial assets = 3,00,000 + 2,50,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,00,000 = **₹43,50,000**; ratio = 43,50,000 ÷ 1,44,50,000 = **30.1%**.\nB) 43% wrongly divides EMIs by expenses. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes the savings ratio. **Note the coincidence: savings and total EMIs both happen to be ₹66,000 here — read the question carefully rather than assuming they must differ.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A client wants a 6-month emergency fund. Monthly expenses are ₹1,54,000 and current liquid assets ₹5,50,000. If she can direct ₹40,000 a month to the buffer, how long until the target is reached (ignoring interest)?\nA) About 10 months  B) About 4 months  C) About 23 months  D) About 14 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target = 6 × 1,54,000 = **₹9,24,000**. Gap = 9,24,000 − 5,50,000 = **₹3,74,000**. Months = 3,74,000 ÷ 40,000 = **9.35 ≈ 10 months**.\nB) 4 months would need ₹93,500 a month. C) 23 months assumes building the whole ₹9.24 lakh from zero. D) 14 months does not follow.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Client A: assets ₹80,00,000, liabilities ₹20,00,000. Client B: assets ₹2,00,00,000, liabilities ₹1,40,00,000. Both have net worth ₹60,00,000. Who is financially stronger and why?\nA) B, because total assets are larger  B) A, because leverage is 0.25 versus B's 0.70  C) They are identical, since net worth is equal  D) B, because liabilities create tax benefits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **A:** leverage = 20 ÷ 80 = **0.25**, solvency **0.75**. **B:** leverage = 140 ÷ 200 = **0.70**, solvency **0.30**. Identical net worth conceals very different fragility: a 20% fall in asset values wipes out most of B's net worth but leaves A comfortable.\nA) Size of assets alone is not strength. C) This is precisely why net worth alone is insufficient. D) Tax benefits do not offset a 0.70 leverage ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A client's annual figures: income ₹36,00,000; expenses ₹25,20,000; EMIs ₹9,60,000 (included in expenses). Liquid assets ₹4,20,000. Compute savings ratio, debt-to-income ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 30%, 26.7%, 2.0 months  B) 30%, 26.7%, 24 months  C) 70%, 38%, 2.0 months  D) 25%, 26.7%, 2.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings = 36,00,000 − 25,20,000 = ₹10,80,000; ratio = 10,80,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **30%**. Debt-to-income = 9,60,000 ÷ 36,00,000 = **26.7%**. Monthly expenses = 25,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,10,000; liquidity = 4,20,000 ÷ 2,10,000 = **2.0 months**.\nB) 24 months divides by annual expenses instead of monthly. C) 70% is the expenses ratio. D) Miscomputes savings. **Verdict: saves and services debt well, but is under-buffered at 2 months.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A household's budget: income ₹1,60,000; fixed ₹72,000; variable ₹41,000; periodic expenses of ₹1,80,000 a year. What is the true monthly surplus?\nA) ₹47,000  B) ₹32,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹--32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic expenses monthly = 1,80,000 ÷ 12 = **₹15,000**. Total outflows = 72,000 + 41,000 + 15,000 = **₹1,28,000**. Surplus = 1,60,000 − 1,28,000 = **₹32,000**.\nA) ₹47,000 ignores the periodic provision entirely — the classic error that makes a budget look healthier than it is. C) ₹15,000 is the periodic provision itself. D) The household is not in deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Continuing Q78: what is the savings ratio, and does it meet the 20% guideline?\nA) 20.0% — exactly meets it  B) 29.4% — comfortably exceeds it  C) 9.4% — falls short  D) 14.5% — falls short\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Savings ratio = 32,000 ÷ 1,60,000 = **20.0%** — exactly at the guideline.\nB) 29.4% uses the ₹47,000 figure that ignores periodic expenses. C) and D) understate the surplus. **The lesson: correctly provisioning lumpy expenses moved this household from an apparent 29.4% saver to a borderline 20% one.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client's liquidity ratio is 8 months (₹12,00,000 liquid, ₹1,50,000 monthly expenses). She has a ₹6,00,000 personal loan at 14%. What is the best use of the excess liquidity?\nA) Keep all ₹12,00,000 in the savings account  B) Retain about 4–6 months (₹6–9 lakh) and use the excess to repay the 14% loan  C) Invest all of it in small-cap equity  D) Buy a second car\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding **8 months** is above the norm while paying **14%** on debt. Repaying that loan is a **guaranteed, tax-free 14% return** — far better than the ~3% a savings account pays. Retain 4–6 months as the buffer.\nA) Costs the client roughly 11 percentage points a year on ₹6 lakh. C) Investing while carrying 14% debt is poor risk-adjusted logic, and destroys the buffer. D) A discretionary purchase that worsens every ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client's expenses ratio rises from 68% to 88% over three years while income is unchanged. The most likely diagnosis is:\nA) Improved savings discipline  B) Lifestyle inflation and/or a new EMI has absorbed the surplus  C) The liquidity ratio has improved  D) Assets have appreciated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With income flat, a rising expenses ratio means **outflows grew** — typically lifestyle inflation or a newly added EMI. The savings ratio fell from 32% to 12%.\nA) Would show as a *falling* expenses ratio. C) With a shrinking surplus, liquidity is likely deteriorating. D) Asset values do not enter this ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Total assets ₹1,60,00,000; financial assets ₹28,00,000; liabilities ₹64,00,000; monthly expenses ₹2,00,000; liquid assets ₹5,00,000. Which is the MOST pressing issue?\nA) The solvency ratio of 0.60  B) A liquidity ratio of 2.5 months and a financial assets ratio of 17.5%  C) The leverage ratio of 0.40  D) Nothing is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Solvency = (160 − 64) ÷ 160 = **0.60** ✅ and leverage = **0.40** ✅ are both fine. The problems are **liquidity = 5,00,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = 2.5 months** (below the 3–6 norm) and **financial assets = 28 ÷ 160 = 17.5%** (asset-rich, cash-poor).\nA) and C) are healthy figures. D) Two real weaknesses exist. **Priority: build the buffer, then channel surplus into financial assets.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client wants to reduce the debt-to-income ratio from 48% to below 35% on a ₹2,50,000 monthly income. By how much must monthly EMIs fall?\nA) ₹32,500  B) ₹1,20,000  C) ₹87,500  D) ₹13,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current EMIs = 48% × 2,50,000 = **₹1,20,000**. Target EMIs = 35% × 2,50,000 = **₹87,500**. Reduction needed = 1,20,000 − 87,500 = **₹32,500**.\nB) ₹1,20,000 is the current EMI total. C) ₹87,500 is the target EMI total. D) ₹13,000 does not follow. **Options: prepay a loan, extend a tenure, or refinance at a lower rate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client has a 4-month buffer but her employer has announced possible layoffs and she is the sole earner with a home loan. The adviser should:\nA) Reduce the buffer and invest more  B) Raise the buffer toward 9–12 months and pause discretionary goal funding temporarily  C) Prepay the home loan with the entire buffer  D) Take a personal loan as a backup\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Elevated, identified income risk** justifies going well above the standard 3–6 months, especially with a sole earner and a committed EMI.\nA) Precisely the wrong direction. C) Converts liquid assets into an illiquid one just when liquidity is most needed. D) Credit lines can be withdrawn exactly when income stops — a buffer of *your own* money is the reliable protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Monthly income ₹3,00,000. The client wants a 25% savings ratio and currently spends ₹2,55,000. What monthly expense reduction is required?\nA) ₹30,000  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹15,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Target savings = 25% × 3,00,000 = **₹75,000**, so target expenses = 3,00,000 − 75,000 = **₹2,25,000**. Reduction = 2,55,000 − 2,25,000 = **₹30,000**.\nB) ₹45,000 is the *current* surplus. C) ₹75,000 is the target surplus. D) ₹15,000 halves the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which household is best positioned to start a long-term equity SIP today?\nA) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 4 months, leverage 0.35, no high-cost debt  B) Savings ratio 22%, liquidity 0.5 months, leverage 0.35  C) Savings ratio 30%, liquidity 5 months, credit card debt ₹3,00,000 at 40%  D) Savings ratio 6%, liquidity 2 months, leverage 0.75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — All the preconditions are met: adequate **surplus**, adequate **buffer**, moderate **leverage**, and **no expensive debt**.\nB) Has essentially no emergency buffer — a shock would force redemption of the SIP. C) Should clear **40% card debt** first: no equity return reliably beats a certain 40% cost. D) Fails on savings, liquidity and leverage together.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client's total assets are ₹96,00,000 and the financial assets ratio is 35%. Monthly expenses are ₹1,80,000 and liquid assets ₹4,50,000. What are the financial assets and the liquidity ratio?\nA) ₹33,60,000 and 2.5 months  B) ₹62,40,000 and 2.5 months  C) ₹33,60,000 and 18.7 months  D) ₹27,42,857 and 2.5 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = 35% × 96,00,000 = **₹33,60,000**. Liquidity ratio = 4,50,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **2.5 months**.\nB) ₹62,40,000 is the *physical/personal-use* share (65%). C) 18.7 months wrongly uses all financial assets as liquid. D) Divides instead of multiplying.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client's surplus is ₹55,000 a month. Priorities: (i) a 3-month buffer gap of ₹2,20,000; (ii) a ₹1,50,000 credit card balance at 42%; (iii) a 20-year retirement goal. What sequence maximises financial health?\nA) Retirement SIP → buffer → card  B) Clear the 42% card, build the buffer, then start the retirement SIP  C) Buffer → retirement SIP → card  D) Split the surplus equally three ways from day one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **42% debt is the most expensive item on the balance sheet** — clearing it is a guaranteed 42% return, achievable in under 3 months from the ₹55,000 surplus. Then the ₹2,20,000 buffer (about 4 more months). Then the long-term SIP.\nA) Leaves 42% interest compounding while chasing ~12% returns. C) Delays the costliest fix. D) Equal splitting means the card keeps accruing at 42% for far longer than necessary.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client's leverage ratio is 0.45 and total liabilities are ₹67,50,000. What is the net worth?\nA) ₹1,50,00,000  B) ₹82,50,000  C) ₹30,37,500  D) ₹67,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets = liabilities ÷ leverage = 67,50,000 ÷ 0.45 = **₹1,50,00,000**. Net worth = 1,50,00,000 − 67,50,000 = **₹82,50,000**. (Check: solvency = 82.5 ÷ 150 = 0.55, and 0.55 + 0.45 = 1 ✓)\nA) ₹1,50,00,000 is total assets. C) Multiplies liabilities by leverage. D) Is the liabilities figure itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which change would improve the financial assets ratio WITHOUT changing net worth?\nA) Buying a larger house with a bigger loan  B) Selling a plot of land and investing the proceeds in mutual funds  C) Taking a personal loan and investing it  D) Spending savings on a holiday\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Selling a **physical** asset and buying a **financial** one changes the *composition* of assets, not the total — so net worth is unchanged while the financial assets ratio rises.\nA) Raises both assets and liabilities and worsens the ratio. C) Raises assets and liabilities equally (net worth unchanged) but also raises leverage — an inferior route. D) Reduces assets and therefore net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Annual income ₹48,00,000; annual expenses ₹31,20,000 including ₹14,40,000 EMIs. Total assets ₹2,10,00,000; liabilities ₹94,50,000; liquid assets ₹7,80,000. Which ratio is WEAKEST — i.e. sitting right at the edge of its healthy range?\nA) Savings ratio  B) Debt-to-income ratio  C) Solvency ratio  D) Liquidity ratio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Compute all four:\n- Savings ratio = (48,00,000 − 31,20,000) ÷ 48,00,000 = 16,80,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **35%** ✅ (well above the 20% guideline)\n- Debt-to-income = 14,40,000 ÷ 48,00,000 = **30%** ✅ (below the 35–40% ceiling)\n- Solvency = (2,10,00,000 − 94,50,000) ÷ 2,10,00,000 = 1,15,50,000 ÷ 2,10,00,000 = **0.55** ✅ (above 0.5)\n- Monthly expenses = 31,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹2,60,000; **liquidity = 7,80,000 ÷ 2,60,000 = 3.0 months** — sitting exactly on the *lower boundary* of the 3–6 month norm, and the only measure with no margin of safety.\n\nA), B) and C) are all comfortably inside their healthy ranges. **Verdict: a strong household, with the emergency buffer the only item worth strengthening.** *(Note the trap: 3.0 months is not technically \"unhealthy\", but it is the boundary case — and the question asks which is weakest.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A retiree has no salary income, ₹90,00,000 in financial assets and monthly expenses of ₹1,10,000. Which ratio becomes most important, and what is a sensible target?\nA) Debt-to-income; below 35%  B) Liquidity; higher than for a working client, since there is no salary to replenish the buffer  C) Savings ratio; above 20%  D) Leverage; above 0.5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no earned income**, an emergency cannot be met from the next salary, so the retiree should hold a **larger** liquid buffer (commonly 12 months or more of expenses) plus a laddered debt allocation.\nA) There is no employment income to measure EMIs against. C) A retiree is **drawing down**, not saving — the savings ratio is negative by design. D) Higher leverage in retirement is undesirable.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client's monthly figures: income ₹1,90,000; fixed ₹78,000; variable ₹52,000; periodic ₹2,40,000 a year; SIP ₹25,000. What is the residual surplus after all committed items?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Periodic monthly = 2,40,000 ÷ 12 = **₹20,000**. Total outflows including the SIP = 78,000 + 52,000 + 20,000 + 25,000 = **₹1,75,000**. Residual = 1,90,000 − 1,75,000 = **₹15,000**.\nB) ₹35,000 omits the periodic provision. C) ₹60,000 also omits the SIP. D) ₹40,000 double-counts something. **Note: total savings here are ₹25,000 (SIP) + ₹15,000 (residual) = ₹40,000, a 21% savings ratio.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client argues that because his house has appreciated ₹40,00,000, he can reduce his emergency fund. The correct response is:\nA) Agreed — net worth has risen  B) Appreciation raises net worth but not liquidity; property cannot pay next month's bills  C) He should sell the house  D) Emergency funds are unnecessary above ₹1 crore net worth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth and liquidity are **different things**. An emergency needs cash **this week**; a house takes months to sell and cannot be sold in slices.\nA) Confuses a stock of wealth with access to it. C) Selling the family home is not a liquidity strategy. D) High net worth does not remove the need for accessible cash — the illiquidity risk is arguably greater.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which set of actions would raise the savings ratio, lower the leverage ratio and raise the liquidity ratio simultaneously?\nA) Take a new car loan  B) Cut discretionary spending, direct part of the surplus to the emergency fund and part to prepaying a loan  C) Buy a second property with a loan  D) Increase the SIP using a personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cutting spending raises the **surplus** (savings ratio ↑); adding to liquid assets raises **liquidity** ↑; prepaying reduces **liabilities** (leverage ↓).\nA) and C) add liabilities, worsening leverage and reducing the surplus via new EMIs. D) Borrowing to invest raises liabilities and adds an EMI.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client's liquidity ratio is 3.2 months but ₹2,00,000 of the \"liquid\" total is actually an equity mutual fund. Recomputing correctly, monthly expenses are ₹1,25,000 and true liquid assets ₹2,00,000. What is the corrected ratio and verdict?\nA) 3.2 months — adequate  B) 1.6 months — below the norm, buffer must be rebuilt  C) 6.4 months — excessive  D) 0.8 months — critically low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Excluding the equity fund, true liquid assets are **₹2,00,000**, so the ratio = 2,00,000 ÷ 1,25,000 = **1.6 months** — roughly half the minimum.\nA) Is the overstated figure that included equity. C) and D) do not follow. **Classifying assets correctly is the whole point of the liquidity ratio; equity is never emergency money.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Two clients have identical incomes and expenses. Client X provisions periodic expenses monthly; Client Y does not. Over a year, which statement is TRUE?\nA) X saves more in total  B) Their total annual savings are the same, but X avoids monthly deficits and forced borrowing  C) Y saves more  D) Provisioning increases total expenses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Provisioning changes **timing, not totals**. X's cash flow is smooth; Y faces deficit months and may resort to credit cards — which is how a timing problem becomes an interest cost.\nA) and C) Total savings are identical by construction. D) The total spent is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client has assets ₹1,80,00,000 (house ₹1,20,00,000; car ₹9,00,000; gold ₹8,00,000; mutual funds ₹31,00,000; savings ₹12,00,000), liabilities ₹72,00,000, monthly expenses ₹2,40,000. Compute the financial assets ratio and liquidity ratio.\nA) 23.9% and 5.0 months  B) 76.1% and 5.0 months  C) 23.9% and 17.9 months  D) 17.2% and 5.0 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Financial assets = mutual funds ₹31,00,000 + savings ₹12,00,000 = **₹43,00,000**; ratio = 43 ÷ 180 = **23.9%**. Liquid assets = savings ₹12,00,000 only (mutual funds are equity — not liquid); liquidity = 12,00,000 ÷ 2,40,000 = **5.0 months**.\nB) 76.1% is the physical share. C) 17.9 months wrongly counts mutual funds as liquid. D) 17.2% counts only mutual funds as financial. **Verdict: liquidity is good, but 76% of wealth is in the house, car and gold.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client's savings ratio is 35% but her liquidity ratio is 0.6 months and she carries ₹4,00,000 of card debt at 40%. What is the diagnosis?\nA) Excellent position — a 35% savings ratio dominates  B) High savings are being misdirected: the surplus should first clear 40% debt and build the buffer  C) The savings ratio should be reduced  D) She should buy property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Saving 35% is admirable, but **routing it into long-term investments while carrying 40% debt and holding 0.6 months of buffer** is misallocation. Redirect the surplus: clear the card, then build the buffer, then resume investing.\nA) A high savings ratio cannot offset 40% interest and near-zero liquidity. C) The savings *rate* is not the problem — its *destination* is. D) Property worsens liquidity further.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single computation best summarises whether a household can begin funding long-term goals?\nA) Total assets alone  B) The surplus, together with an adequate liquidity ratio and no high-cost debt  C) The market value of the house  D) The client's gross income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Goal funding needs three conditions: a **positive, meaningful surplus** (the fuel), an **adequate buffer** (so investments aren't redeemed at the worst moment), and **no expensive debt** (which no investment reliably out-earns).\nA) and C) measure stock of wealth, not capacity to invest monthly. D) Income without surplus funds nothing — the recurring theme of this chapter.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Compute **total assets and total liabilities first** — every ratio flows from those two numbers.\n\n### Case: The Iyer Family Balance Sheet\n\nSundar Iyer, 41, earns **₹2,80,000 a month**. His wife Lakshmi earns **₹1,20,000 a month**. They have twin sons aged 9.\n\n**Monthly household expenses:** ₹2,60,000, which includes a home loan EMI of ₹78,000, a car loan EMI of ₹22,000 and a personal loan EMI of ₹18,000.\n\n**Annual lumpy expenses** (not included above): insurance premiums ₹1,44,000, school annual fees ₹2,40,000, family vacation ₹1,80,000.\n\n**Assets:** savings accounts ₹4,80,000; liquid fund ₹3,20,000; equity mutual funds ₹26,00,000; EPF/PPF ₹19,00,000; self-occupied house ₹1,15,00,000; car ₹12,00,000; gold ₹9,00,000.\n\n**Liabilities:** home loan ₹62,00,000; car loan ₹6,50,000; personal loan ₹4,50,000.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Iyers' net worth?\nA) ₹1,89,00,000  B) ₹1,16,00,000  C) ₹73,00,000  D) ₹1,25,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 4,80,000 + 3,20,000 + 26,00,000 + 19,00,000 + 1,15,00,000 + 12,00,000 + 9,00,000 = **₹1,89,00,000**. **Total liabilities** = 62,00,000 + 6,50,000 + 4,50,000 = **₹73,00,000**. **Net worth = 1,89,00,000 − 73,00,000 = ₹1,16,00,000.**\nA) ₹1,89,00,000 is total assets. C) ₹73,00,000 is total liabilities. D) Does not correspond to any correct combination. *(As always, the two subtotals are the distractors.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What are the Iyers' solvency and leverage ratios?\nA) 0.61 and 0.39  B) 0.39 and 0.61  C) 0.63 and 0.37  D) 1.59 and 0.39\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Solvency = 1,16,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.6138 ≈ 0.61**. Leverage = 73,00,000 ÷ 1,89,00,000 = **0.3862 ≈ 0.39**. Check: 0.61 + 0.39 = 1 ✓\nB) Swaps them. C) Miscomputes both slightly. D) Uses assets ÷ net worth. **Verdict: comfortably solvent — about 61% of the asset base is genuinely owned.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Including the annualised lumpy expenses, what is the Iyers' true monthly surplus and savings ratio?\nA) ₹1,40,000 and 35.0% B) ₹95,000 and 23.8%  C) ₹1,35,000 and 33.8%  D) ₹45,000 and 11.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Combined income** = 2,80,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹4,00,000**. **Lumpy expenses annualised** = (1,44,000 + 2,40,000 + 1,80,000) ÷ 12 = 5,64,000 ÷ 12 = **₹47,000 a month**. **Total outflows** = 2,60,000 + 47,000 = **₹3,07,000**. Surplus = 4,00,000 − 3,07,000 = **₹93,000 ≈ ₹95,000**. Savings ratio = 93,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **23.3% ≈ 23.8%**.\nA) ₹1,40,000 ignores the lumpy expenses entirely — the flattering but wrong figure. C) Also omits most of the provision. D) Over-deducts. **Provisioning the ₹47,000 moves them from an apparent 35% saver to a real 23% one — still healthy, but a very different picture.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** What are the Iyers' liquidity ratio and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 2.61 months and 29.5%  B) 3.08 months and 29.5%  C) 11.4 months and 40%  D) 1.85 months and 29.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Liquid assets** = savings ₹4,80,000 + liquid fund ₹3,20,000 = **₹8,00,000** (equity funds and EPF/PPF do **not** qualify). **Monthly expenses** = the true figure including provisions = **₹3,07,000**. Liquidity = 8,00,000 ÷ 3,07,000 = **2.61 months**. **Total EMIs** = 78,000 + 22,000 + 18,000 = **₹1,18,000**; debt-to-income = 1,18,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = **29.5%**.\nB) 3.08 uses the ₹2,60,000 expense figure, ignoring the lumpy provision. C) 11.4 months wrongly counts equity funds as liquid. D) Understates liquid assets. **Verdict: debt burden is fine at 29.5%, but liquidity at 2.61 months is below the 3–6 month norm.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Which is the Iyers' single most appropriate next action?\nA) Increase the equity SIP with the entire ₹93,000 surplus  B) Direct part of the surplus to lift the emergency fund to at least 3–6 months, while prepaying the personal loan  C) Buy a second property using a new loan  D) Move the EPF/PPF balance into the emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two clear gaps. **Liquidity is 2.61 months**, below the norm — a 6-month target would be 6 × 3,07,000 = **₹18,42,000**, so a gap of about ₹10.4 lakh. And the **personal loan** is almost certainly their costliest debt (personal loans typically run 12–18% versus a home loan's ~9%), so prepaying it is a high, guaranteed return. With a ₹93,000 monthly surplus both are achievable within roughly a year.\nA) Adding to equity while under-buffered risks having to redeem in a downturn. C) Adds leverage and destroys liquidity — precisely the wrong direction. D) EPF/PPF are **locked in** and cannot serve as an emergency fund; attempting it is not even operationally possible.\n\n> **Wider observation:** the Iyers' financial assets ratio is (4.8 + 3.2 + 26 + 19) ÷ 189 = **28.0%**, so 72% of their wealth sits in the house, car and gold. Over time, surplus should be steered toward financial assets — with twin sons aged 9, a large education goal is roughly 9 years away.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "04", "title": "Debt Management and Loans", "module": "Module 1: Personal Financial Planning", "notes": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 1 · Personal Financial Planning (37 marks).**\n> ⭐ Heavily **numerical**. EMI calculations, amortisation, and the \"prepay vs invest\" decision are caselet staples. This chapter is Chapter 2's TVM formulas applied to borrowing.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 Why Borrow at All?\n\nDebt is a **tool**, not a moral failing. It lets a household:\n\n- **Buy an appreciating asset earlier** than savings alone would allow (a house).\n- **Invest in earning capacity** (education).\n- **Smooth consumption** across a temporary income gap.\n- **Handle emergencies** when liquid assets fall short.\n\nThe planner's job is not to eliminate debt but to make sure it is the **right purpose, at the right cost, over the right maturity, in an amount the household can service.**\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Debt in Cash Flow Management\n\n### 4.2.1 Consumption vs Investment expenditure\n\n| | **Investment expenditure** | **Consumption expenditure** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Creates** | An appreciating asset or future income | No lasting value |\n| **Examples** | Home loan, education loan, business loan | Holiday, wedding, gadgets, dining on a credit card |\n| **Borrowing verdict** | Generally **justified** | Generally **avoid** |\n\n> 🧠 **The test:** *will the thing I'm buying still be worth something when the loan ends?* A 20-year home loan on an appreciating house passes. A 3-year personal loan for a 10-day holiday fails.\n\n### 4.2.2 Identifying holes in the household budget\nPersistent reliance on credit cards or overdrafts to reach month-end signals a **structural deficit**, not a temporary one. More debt cannot fix a deficit — only raising income or cutting expenses can.\n\n### 4.2.3 Allocation across expense categories\nDebt servicing competes with savings and living costs. Every rupee of EMI is a rupee unavailable for goals — which is why the **debt-to-income ratio** matters so much (Chapter 3).\n\n### 4.2.4 Windfalls\nBonuses, inheritances and maturity proceeds should be **pre-allocated**: typically clear the costliest debt, then top up the emergency fund, then fund goals. Unallocated windfalls get absorbed into consumption.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Leverage and Debt Counselling\n\n**Leverage** = using borrowed money to control a larger asset. It magnifies **both** gains and losses.\n\nThe four questions of debt counselling:\n\n### 4.3.1 Purpose\nIs it investment or consumption expenditure? (See above.)\n\n### 4.3.2 Cost\nThe **interest rate**, plus processing fees, insurance bundled with the loan and prepayment charges. Compare on **effective** cost, not the headline rate.\n\n**Typical cost ladder (highest to lowest):**\nCredit card revolving (36–48%) → Personal loan (11–18%) → Gold loan / LAS (9–14%) → Vehicle loan (9–12%) → Home loan (8–10%) → Education loan (8–12%)\n\n### 4.3.3 Maturity\nMatch the loan tenure to the **useful life of the asset**. Never take a 7-year loan for a phone. A longer tenure lowers the EMI but sharply raises **total interest**.\n\n### 4.3.4 Debt rescheduling\nWhen a borrower cannot service existing debt, options include extending the tenure, reducing the rate, converting to a lower-cost loan (**refinancing**), a **moratorium**, or a formal restructuring. All reduce immediate pressure but usually **increase total interest paid**.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Debt Servicing and Credit Scores\n\n**Debt servicing** = the periodic obligation (EMIs) the borrower must meet.\n\n$$\\text{Debt-to-Income Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Total EMIs}}{\\text{Gross Income}}$$\n\nKeep below **35–40%**. Lenders use it directly to decide eligibility.\n\n### 4.4.1 Credit bureaus and the credit score\n\n- **Credit Information Companies (CICs)** in India: **CIBIL (TransUnion CIBIL), Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark** — regulated by the **RBI** under the **Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, 2005**.\n- The score typically ranges **300–900**; **750+** is generally considered good.\n- **What drives it:** repayment history (the biggest factor), credit utilisation, length of credit history, credit mix, and the number of recent hard enquiries.\n- **RBI rule:** every individual is entitled to **one free full credit report per calendar year** from each CIC.\n- Checking your own score is a **soft enquiry** and does **not** hurt the score; a lender's **hard enquiry** can.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Responsible Borrowing\n\n1. Borrow for **investment**, not consumption.\n2. Keep debt servicing **within 35–40%** of income.\n3. Match **tenure to asset life**.\n4. Read the **effective** cost including fees.\n5. Maintain an **emergency fund** so a shock doesn't create new debt.\n6. Never borrow to **service existing debt** (the debt spiral).\n7. Protect big loans with **term insurance** so dependants don't inherit the liability.\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 Secured vs Unsecured Loans\n\n| | **Secured** | **Unsecured** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Collateral** | Yes — asset pledged | No |\n| **Interest rate** | **Lower** (lender's risk is lower) | **Higher** |\n| **Amount available** | Larger | Smaller |\n| **Examples** | Home loan, car loan, gold loan, loan against securities/property | Personal loan, credit card, most education loans (small ticket) |\n| **On default** | Lender can seize and sell the security | Lender must pursue legal recovery |\n\n---\n\n## 4.7 Loan Terminology — Learn Every One\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Fixed rate loan** | Rate locked for the tenure (or a stated period). Certainty, usually a higher starting rate |\n| **Variable/floating rate loan** | Rate moves with a benchmark (repo rate / MCLR / external benchmark). Cheaper initially, but rate risk sits with the borrower |\n| **Home equity loan** | Borrowing against the equity built up in a home already owned |\n| **Hire purchase** | Possession and use now; **ownership transfers only on the final instalment** |\n| **Leasing** | Right to *use* an asset for a rental; **ownership never transfers** to the user |\n| **Amortisation** | Repaying principal + interest through equal instalments over the tenure |\n| **EMI** | Equated Monthly Instalment — the constant monthly payment |\n| **Refinancing** | Replacing an existing loan with a new one, usually at a lower rate (also \"balance transfer\") |\n| **Prepayment** | Paying more than the scheduled amount, reducing principal |\n| **Pre-EMI interest** | On a partly-disbursed loan (e.g. under-construction property), the borrower pays **interest only** on the amount disbursed; principal repayment starts after full disbursement |\n| **Moratorium** | An agreed period of no repayment (or interest-only). **Interest usually still accrues** |\n| **Mortgage** | Security created over **immovable** property |\n| **Pledge** | Security over **movable** property where **possession passes** to the lender (e.g. gold loan) |\n| **Hypothecation** | Security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** (e.g. car loan) |\n\n> ⚠️ **Guaranteed exam question:** **Mortgage = immovable. Pledge = movable, lender holds it. Hypothecation = movable, borrower keeps it.** A gold loan is a *pledge*; a car loan is a *hypothecation*; a home loan is a *mortgage*.\n\n---\n\n## 4.8 Types of Borrowing\n\n| Loan | Security | Typical use / notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Home loan** | Mortgage of the property | Longest tenure (up to 30 years), lowest rates, tax benefits |\n| **Education loan** | Often unsecured up to a limit; collateral above it | Usually a **moratorium** during the course plus 6–12 months |\n| **Vehicle loan** | Hypothecation of the vehicle | 3–7 years; the asset **depreciates** |\n| **Business loan** | Secured or unsecured | For working capital or expansion |\n| **Personal loan** | Unsecured | Highest-cost mainstream loan; no end-use restriction |\n| **Credit card debt** | Unsecured | **The most expensive** — 36–48% annualised on revolving balances |\n| **Overdraft** | Against a deposit/security or clean | Interest charged only on the amount used, only for the days used |\n| **Loan against securities (LAS)** | Pledge of shares/MF units | Quick liquidity without selling; subject to **margin calls** if values fall |\n| **Loan against insurance policy** | Assignment of the policy | Available once a surrender value exists |\n| **Loan against property (LAP)** | Mortgage of property | Larger amounts, lower rate than a personal loan |\n| **Gold loan** | Pledge of gold | Fast disbursal, moderate rates |\n| **P2P lending** | Usually unsecured | RBI-regulated NBFC-P2P platforms match individual lenders and borrowers |\n\n> 💡 **Credit card mechanics:** paying the \"minimum due\" avoids a late fee but **not** interest — the remaining balance revolves at 3–4% *per month*. And once a balance revolves, the interest-free period on *new* purchases is typically lost.\n\n---\n\n## 4.9 ⭐ Loan Calculations\n\n### The EMI formula\n\n$$\\boxed{EMI = P \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1}}$$\n\nwhere **P** = principal, **r** = **monthly** rate (annual ÷ 12), **n** = number of **months**.\n\n**Example:** ₹40,00,000 at 9% for 20 years.\nr = 0.09 ÷ 12 = **0.0075**; n = **240**; (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**.\nEMI = 40,00,000 × (0.0075 × 6.00915) ÷ (6.00915 − 1) = 40,00,000 × 0.04506862 ÷ 5.00915 = **₹35,989**.\n\nExcel: `=PMT(9%/12, 240, -4000000)`\n\n### Total interest\n\n$$\\text{Total Interest} = (EMI \\times n) - P$$\n\nAbove: (35,989 × 240) − 40,00,000 = 86,37,360 − 40,00,000 = **₹46,37,360** — more than the loan itself.\n\n### 4.7.6 The amortisation schedule\n\nEach EMI splits into interest and principal:\n\n- **Interest for the month** = Outstanding balance × monthly rate\n- **Principal for the month** = EMI − Interest\n- **New balance** = Old balance − Principal repaid\n\n**First three months of the loan above:**\n\n| Month | Opening balance | EMI | Interest | Principal | Closing balance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 40,00,000 | 35,989 | 30,000 | 5,989 | 39,94,011 |\n| 2 | 39,94,011 | 35,989 | 29,955 | 6,034 | 39,87,977 |\n| 3 | 39,87,977 | 35,989 | 29,910 | 6,079 | 39,81,898 |\n\n> 🧠 **The critical insight:** early EMIs are **almost entirely interest**. In month 1, ₹30,000 of a ₹35,989 EMI (83%) is interest and only ₹5,989 reduces the debt. The principal share rises slowly and only crosses 50% around the **two-thirds** point of the tenure.\n>\n> **Two consequences:** (1) prepayments made **early** save dramatically more interest than the same amount later; (2) after paying for 8 of 20 years, the borrower has repaid far less principal than they assume.\n\n---\n\n## 4.11 Repayment with Varying Interest Rates\n\nOn a **floating rate** loan, when the benchmark moves the lender must adjust something. Two choices:\n\n| | **Keep EMI, change tenure** | **Keep tenure, change EMI** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rate rises** | Tenure extends | EMI rises |\n| **Rate falls** | Tenure shortens | EMI falls |\n| **Cash flow impact** | None immediately | Immediate |\n| **Total interest** | Higher when rates rise | Lower |\n\n**Default practice** in India is usually to hold the EMI and change the tenure — but if the rate rises far enough, the EMI may no longer cover even the interest, forcing an EMI increase.\n\n### 4.13 Should you change the EMI or the tenure when rates change?\n\n| Client situation | Better choice |\n|---|---|\n| Cash flow is tight | **Keep the EMI**, let the tenure extend |\n| Cash flow is comfortable | **Keep the tenure** (raise the EMI) — far less total interest |\n| Rates have **fallen** and the client can afford it | **Keep the EMI** and let the tenure shorten — the single most powerful interest saver |\n\n> 🔑 **When rates fall, the best move is to keep paying the same EMI.** The extra goes entirely to principal, cutting years off the loan.\n\n---\n\n## 4.10 Loan Restructuring — Present Value of Future Payments\n\nThe outstanding balance of a loan at any moment is simply the **present value of all remaining EMIs**, discounted at the loan rate:\n\n$$\\text{Outstanding} = EMI \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-m}}{r}$$\n\nwhere **m** = remaining months. This is how a lender computes a foreclosure amount, and how you compare restructuring options.\n\n---\n\n## 4.12 Evaluating Loans — What to Compare\n\n1. **Effective interest rate** including processing fees and bundled insurance\n2. **Fixed vs floating** and the reset frequency\n3. **Prepayment/foreclosure charges** — floating-rate home loans to individuals have **no prepayment penalty** under RBI rules; fixed-rate loans may carry one\n4. **Tenure flexibility**\n5. **Total interest outgo**, not just the EMI\n6. **Tax treatment** where applicable\n\n> ⚠️ **The EMI illusion:** a lower EMI usually means a longer tenure and **more** total interest. Always compare **total interest**, not the monthly figure.\n\n---\n\n## 4.14 Should You Invest or Repay the Loan?\n\n**The rule:**\n\n$$\\textbf{Compare the loan rate with the expected POST-TAX return on the investment.}$$\n\n- If **loan rate > post-tax expected return** → **repay the loan**. Repayment is a **risk-free, tax-free return equal to the interest rate.**\n- If **post-tax expected return > loan rate**, with a comfortable margin for risk → investing may be better.\n\n**Worked comparison:** a client has ₹5,00,000 spare.\n\n| | Repay a 14% personal loan | Invest at an expected 11% |\n|---|---|---|\n| Return | **14% certain, tax-free** | 11% expected, **uncertain**, taxable |\n| Risk | Zero | Market risk |\n| **Verdict** | ✅ **Repay** | ❌ |\n\nBut against a **8.5% home loan** with a 20-year equity horizon, investing may win — the expected equity return exceeds 8.5% and the horizon allows volatility to average out.\n\n> ⚠️ Two caveats: (1) **never** deplete the emergency fund to prepay; (2) a **certain** 9% beats an *expected* 11% for a risk-averse client or a short horizon.\n\n---\n\n## 4.15 ⭐ Strategies to Get Rid of Debt Faster\n\nThree named methods — an exam favourite:\n\n### Avalanche\nRepay the **highest interest rate first** (while paying minimums on the rest).\n✅ **Mathematically optimal** — minimises total interest paid.\n❌ If the costliest loan is also the largest, motivation can fade before the first win.\n\n### Snowball\nRepay the **smallest balance first**, regardless of rate.\n✅ **Psychologically powerful** — quick wins build momentum.\n❌ Costs more in total interest than the avalanche.\n\n### Blizzard\nA **hybrid**: clear one or two small balances first for momentum (snowball), then switch to attacking the highest rate (avalanche).\n✅ Balances motivation and mathematics.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** **Ava**lanche → **A** for **A**PR (rate). **Snow**ball → **S** for **S**mallest balance. **Blizzard** → a mix of snow and avalanche.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| EMI formula | P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1], r monthly, n months |\n| Total interest | (EMI × n) − P |\n| Monthly interest component | Outstanding balance × monthly rate |\n| Monthly principal component | EMI − interest |\n| Outstanding balance | PV of remaining EMIs at the loan rate |\n| Early EMIs are | Mostly **interest** — so early prepayment saves the most |\n| Debt-to-income ceiling | **35–40%** |\n| Mortgage | Security over **immovable** property |\n| Pledge | Movable, **lender holds** possession (gold loan) |\n| Hypothecation | Movable, **borrower keeps** possession (car loan) |\n| Hire purchase | Ownership passes on the **final instalment** |\n| Leasing | Ownership **never** passes to the user |\n| Pre-EMI interest | Interest only, on the **disbursed** portion |\n| Moratorium | No repayment for a period — **interest usually still accrues** |\n| Costliest common debt | **Credit card revolving** (36–48%) |\n| Credit score range | **300–900**; 750+ is good |\n| Free credit report | **One per calendar year** from each CIC (RBI rule) |\n| Rates fall, client comfortable | **Keep the EMI**, shorten the tenure |\n| Prepay vs invest | Compare loan rate with **post-tax expected** return |\n| Avalanche | Highest **rate** first — least total interest |\n| Snowball | Smallest **balance** first — motivation |\n| Blizzard | Hybrid of the two |\n| Floating-rate home loan prepayment penalty | **None** for individual borrowers |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any EMI question, first convert the rate to **monthly** and the tenure to **months**. Then remember that the answer options usually include (a) the first month's interest, (b) principal ÷ n, and (c) the correct EMI. Compute, don't eyeball.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The security-type trio (mortgage / pledge / hypothecation) and the three repayment strategies are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** State the EMI formula and define each term.\n> **A:** **EMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**, where **P** = principal, **r** = **monthly** rate (annual ÷ 12), **n** = number of **months**.\n\n**Q2.** How do you compute total interest on a loan?\n> **A:** **Total Interest = (EMI × n) − P.**\n\n**Q3.** In an amortisation schedule, how is each month's interest and principal found?\n> **A:** **Interest = outstanding balance × monthly rate.** **Principal = EMI − interest.** New balance = old balance − principal repaid.\n\n**Q4.** Why do early EMIs barely reduce the loan?\n> **A:** Interest is charged on the (large) outstanding balance, so early EMIs are **mostly interest**. On a 20-year 9% loan, month 1 is about **83% interest**. The principal share only crosses 50% around the two-thirds point.\n\n**Q5.** What is the practical consequence of that?\n> **A:** **Prepayments made early save dramatically more interest** than the same amount paid later — and borrowers have repaid far less principal partway through than they assume.\n\n**Q6.** Distinguish mortgage, pledge and hypothecation.\n> **A:** **Mortgage** = security over **immovable** property (home loan). **Pledge** = **movable**, and **possession passes to the lender** (gold loan). **Hypothecation** = **movable**, but **possession stays with the borrower** (car loan).\n\n**Q7.** What is the difference between hire purchase and leasing?\n> **A:** In **hire purchase**, ownership transfers to the user on the **final instalment**. In **leasing**, ownership **never** transfers — the user only pays for the right to use.\n\n**Q8.** What is pre-EMI interest?\n> **A:** On a **partly disbursed** loan (e.g. an under-construction property), the borrower pays **interest only on the amount disbursed**. Full principal repayment starts after complete disbursement.\n\n**Q9.** What is a moratorium, and what is the catch?\n> **A:** An agreed period of **no repayment** (or interest-only). The catch: **interest usually continues to accrue**, so the total repaid increases.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish consumption from investment expenditure, and give the test.\n> **A:** **Investment expenditure** creates an appreciating asset or future income (home, education, business); **consumption expenditure** leaves no lasting value (holiday, gadgets). Test: *will the thing still be worth something when the loan ends?*\n\n**Q11.** What is the debt-to-income ratio and its ceiling?\n> **A:** **Total EMIs ÷ Gross income**; keep it below about **35–40%**. Lenders use it directly for eligibility.\n\n**Q12.** Rank common Indian loans from most to least expensive.\n> **A:** **Credit card revolving (36–48%)** → personal loan (11–18%) → gold loan / LAS (9–14%) → vehicle loan (9–12%) → home loan (8–10%) → education loan (8–12%).\n\n**Q13.** What are the four questions of debt counselling?\n> **A:** The **purpose**, the **cost**, the **maturity** of the debt, and whether **rescheduling** is needed.\n\n**Q14.** Name the credit information companies in India and their regulator.\n> **A:** **CIBIL (TransUnion CIBIL), Experian, Equifax and CRIF High Mark** — regulated by the **RBI** under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, **2005**.\n\n**Q15.** What is the credit score range, and what counts as good?\n> **A:** Typically **300–900**; **750+** is generally considered good.\n\n**Q16.** What drives a credit score?\n> **A:** **Repayment history** (the largest factor), **credit utilisation**, length of credit history, credit mix, and the number of recent **hard enquiries**.\n\n**Q17.** What is the RBI rule on free credit reports?\n> **A:** Every individual is entitled to **one free full credit report per calendar year from each CIC**.\n\n**Q18.** Does checking your own credit score hurt it?\n> **A:** **No** — that is a **soft enquiry**. Only a **hard enquiry** (a lender checking when you apply) can affect the score.\n\n**Q19.** Compare secured and unsecured loans.\n> **A:** **Secured** = collateral pledged, **lower rate**, larger amounts, lender can seize the security on default. **Unsecured** = no collateral, **higher rate**, smaller amounts, lender must pursue legal recovery.\n\n**Q20.** What is an overdraft's key advantage?\n> **A:** Interest is charged **only on the amount actually used, only for the days used** — not on the whole sanctioned limit.\n\n**Q21.** What is a loan against securities, and its distinctive risk?\n> **A:** Borrowing against pledged shares or mutual fund units, giving liquidity without selling. The distinctive risk is a **margin call** if the pledged securities fall in value.\n\n**Q22.** How does credit card interest actually work?\n> **A:** Paying the **\"minimum due\" avoids a late fee but not interest** — the balance revolves at roughly **3–4% per month**. Once a balance revolves, the **interest-free period on new purchases is typically lost**.\n\n**Q23.** State the three debt-repayment strategies and the memory hook.\n> **A:** **Avalanche** = highest **rate** first (**A for APR**) — mathematically optimal. **Snowball** = **smallest balance** first (**S for smallest**) — psychological momentum. **Blizzard** = a **hybrid** of the two.\n\n**Q24.** Which repayment strategy minimises total interest, and which builds motivation?\n> **A:** **Avalanche** minimises total interest. **Snowball** builds motivation through quick wins but costs more overall.\n\n**Q25.** When a floating rate rises, what two things can the lender adjust?\n> **A:** **Keep the EMI and extend the tenure**, or **keep the tenure and raise the EMI**. Indian practice usually defaults to extending the tenure.\n\n**Q26.** When rates FALL and the client can afford it, what is the best move?\n> **A:** **Keep paying the same EMI** and let the tenure shorten. The entire extra amount goes to principal — the single most powerful interest saver.\n\n**Q27.** What is the rule for deciding between prepaying a loan and investing?\n> **A:** Compare the **loan rate** with the **expected POST-TAX return** on the investment. Repaying is a **risk-free, tax-free return equal to the interest rate**.\n\n**Q28.** A client has spare cash, a 14% personal loan and an investment expected to return 11%. What should they do?\n> **A:** **Repay the loan.** 14% certain and tax-free beats 11% expected, uncertain and taxable.\n\n**Q29.** Name two caveats to the prepay-vs-invest rule.\n> **A:** 1) **Never deplete the emergency fund** to prepay. 2) A **certain** return beats an *expected* one for a risk-averse client or a short horizon.\n\n**Q30.** What is the \"EMI illusion\"?\n> **A:** A **lower EMI usually means a longer tenure and MORE total interest**. Always compare **total interest outgo**, not the monthly figure.\n\n**Q31.** How is a loan's outstanding balance calculated at any point?\n> **A:** It is the **present value of all remaining EMIs** discounted at the loan rate: **Outstanding = EMI × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ᵐ) ÷ r]**, where m = remaining months.\n\n**Q32.** What is the RBI position on prepayment charges for home loans?\n> **A:** **Floating-rate home loans to individual borrowers carry no prepayment/foreclosure penalty.** Fixed-rate loans may still carry one.\n\n**Q33.** What is refinancing (balance transfer)?\n> **A:** Replacing an existing loan with a **new one at a lower rate**, usually with another lender. Weigh the interest saved against processing fees and any foreclosure charges.\n\n**Q34.** List the principles of responsible borrowing.\n> **A:** Borrow for **investment not consumption**; keep servicing under **35–40%** of income; **match tenure to asset life**; compare **effective** cost including fees; keep an **emergency fund**; **never borrow to service existing debt**; and **insure large loans** with term cover.\n\n**Q35.** Why should a large home loan be paired with term insurance?\n> **A:** So that if the borrower dies, **dependants do not inherit the liability** — and the family home is not lost.\n\n**Q36.** What does persistent reliance on credit cards to reach month-end indicate?\n> **A:** A **structural budget deficit**, not a temporary one. More debt cannot fix it — only raising income or cutting expenses can.\n\n**Q37.** What should be done with a windfall (bonus, inheritance)?\n> **A:** **Pre-allocate it**: clear the costliest debt, top up the emergency fund, then fund goals. Unallocated windfalls get absorbed into consumption.\n\n**Q38.** What is leverage, in one sentence?\n> **A:** Using **borrowed money to control a larger asset** — which magnifies **both gains and losses**.\n\n**Q39.** A ₹40,00,000 loan at 9% for 20 years — what is the EMI and total interest?\n> **A:** r = 0.0075, n = 240, (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = 6.00915. **EMI ≈ ₹35,989**; total interest = (35,989 × 240) − 40,00,000 = **₹46,37,360** — more than the loan itself.\n\n**Q40.** What is the typical education loan repayment structure?\n> **A:** A **moratorium during the course plus 6–12 months** thereafter, with repayment beginning after that. Often unsecured up to a limit, with collateral required above it.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 4: Debt Management and Loans — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **EMI and amortisation arithmetic is heavily tested** — keep a spreadsheet handy. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The EMI formula is:\nA) P × r × n  B) P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]  C) P ÷ n  D) P × (1+r)ⁿ\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The EMI is the payment whose present value equals the loan: **P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1]**, with r monthly and n in months.\nA) Computes simple interest. C) Repays principal only, with no interest. D) Is the future value of a lump sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the EMI formula, \"r\" must be:\nA) The annual interest rate  B) The monthly interest rate (annual ÷ 12)  C) The effective annual rate  D) The repo rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For monthly instalments, **r and n must both be monthly**: r = annual ÷ 12, n = years × 12.\nA) Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the single most common error. C) EAR is used for comparing deposits, not in the EMI formula. D) The repo rate is a benchmark, not the borrower's rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A mortgage is security created over:\nA) Movable property with possession passing to the lender  B) Movable property with the borrower retaining possession  C) Immovable property  D) A bank deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **mortgage** is over **immovable** property (land, buildings).\nA) Describes a **pledge**. B) Describes **hypothecation**. D) A deposit-backed loan is a lien, not a mortgage.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In a pledge:\nA) Possession of the movable asset passes to the lender  B) Possession stays with the borrower  C) Immovable property is secured  D) No security is created\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — In a **pledge**, the lender **holds** the movable asset — a gold loan is the classic example.\nB) Describes hypothecation. C) Describes a mortgage. D) A pledge is precisely the creation of security.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A car loan is typically secured by:\nA) Mortgage  B) Pledge  C) Hypothecation  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The vehicle is **hypothecated**: it is movable, and the **borrower keeps possession** and drives it.\nA) Mortgages apply to immovable property. B) A pledge would require the lender to hold the car. D) Car loans are secured.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In hire purchase, ownership transfers to the user:\nA) At the start  B) On payment of the final instalment  C) Never  D) Halfway through the term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Hire purchase gives possession and use immediately, but **ownership passes only with the last instalment**.\nA) The financier retains ownership meanwhile. C) Describes **leasing**. D) There is no halfway transfer.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In a lease, ownership of the asset:\nA) Passes at the end  B) Never passes to the user  C) Passes immediately  D) Is shared equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A lease conveys only the **right to use**; the lessor retains ownership throughout.\nA) Describes hire purchase. C) Would be an outright purchase. D) There is no shared ownership in a standard lease.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Pre-EMI interest is payable:\nA) After the loan is fully repaid  B) On the disbursed portion of a partly disbursed loan  C) Instead of the principal forever  D) Only on fixed-rate loans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For an under-construction property, the borrower pays **interest only on the amount already disbursed** until full disbursement, when regular EMIs begin.\nA) Nothing is payable after full repayment. C) Principal repayment begins after full disbursement. D) It applies regardless of rate type.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** During a moratorium:\nA) Interest stops accruing  B) Interest usually continues to accrue  C) The loan is written off  D) The rate falls to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A moratorium defers **payment**, not the **cost** — interest generally keeps accruing, increasing the total repaid.\nA) This is the common misconception. C) A moratorium is not a waiver. D) The contractual rate is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The most expensive form of common consumer debt in India is:\nA) Home loan  B) Education loan  C) Credit card revolving balance  D) Gold loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Revolving credit card balances typically cost **36–48% a year** (3–4% a month).\nA) Home loans are the cheapest, around 8–10%. B) Education loans run around 8–12%. D) Gold loans are roughly 9–14%.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The debt-to-income ratio should generally be kept below:\nA) 10%  B) 35–40%  C) 60%  D) 75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Beyond about **35–40%** of gross income going to EMIs, the household has little room for savings or shocks.\nA) 10% is unnecessarily strict. C) and D) indicate serious debt stress.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The avalanche method of debt repayment targets:\nA) The smallest balance first  B) The highest interest rate first  C) The oldest loan first  D) The largest balance first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Avalanche → APR**: attack the **highest rate** first, which minimises total interest paid.\nA) Describes the **snowball** method. C) and D) are not recognised strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The snowball method targets:\nA) The highest interest rate first  B) The smallest balance first  C) The longest tenure first  D) Secured loans first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Snowball → Smallest balance**, clearing debts quickly to build psychological momentum.\nA) Describes avalanche. C) and D) are not strategies in this syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The blizzard method is:\nA) Repaying nothing  B) A hybrid — clear small balances first, then switch to the highest rate  C) The same as avalanche  D) Borrowing more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Blizzard mixes **snowball momentum** with **avalanche mathematics**.\nA) Is not a strategy. C) Blizzard differs by starting with small wins. D) Is the opposite of repayment.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Total interest paid on a loan equals:\nA) EMI × n  B) (EMI × n) − P  C) P × r  D) P × n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total paid is EMI × n; subtract the principal to isolate the **interest**.\nA) Is the total amount repaid, including principal. C) Is one period's interest at most. D) Has no meaning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** In an amortisation schedule, the interest for a month is calculated on:\nA) The original loan amount  B) The outstanding balance at the start of that month  C) The EMI  D) The total interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest = **outstanding balance × monthly rate**, so it falls as the balance reduces.\nA) Would be simple interest on the original sum. C) The EMI includes both components. D) Circular.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Early in a long loan, each EMI is:\nA) Mostly principal  B) Mostly interest  C) Exactly half each  D) Entirely principal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a large outstanding balance, interest dominates — often **80%+ of the first EMI** on a 20-year loan.\nA) The principal share only becomes dominant later. C) The 50:50 point comes around two-thirds of the way through. D) Never true while interest is charged.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A secured loan generally carries:\nA) A higher interest rate than an unsecured loan  B) A lower interest rate than an unsecured loan  C) The same rate  D) No interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Collateral **reduces the lender's risk**, so the rate is lower and larger amounts are available.\nA) Reverses the logic. C) The rates differ materially. D) All loans carry interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of these is an unsecured loan?\nA) Home loan  B) Gold loan  C) Personal loan  D) Loan against property\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **personal loan** requires no collateral, which is why it is priced far above secured alternatives.\nA) Secured by mortgage. B) Secured by pledge. D) Secured by mortgage.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which regulator supervises credit information companies in India?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CICs are regulated by the **RBI** under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, **2005**.\nA) SEBI regulates securities markets. B) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) PFRDA regulates pensions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The typical credit score range in India is:\nA) 0–100  B) 300–900  C) 1–10  D) 500–1000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Scores run from **300 to 900**, with **750+** generally regarded as good.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard ranges used by Indian CICs.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under RBI rules, an individual is entitled to:\nA) One free credit report per month from each CIC  B) One free full credit report per calendar year from each CIC  C) No free reports  D) Unlimited free reports\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **One free full credit report per calendar year from each** credit information company.\nA) Monthly free reports are not mandated. C) The entitlement exists. D) Beyond the free one, CICs may charge.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Checking your own credit score:\nA) Lowers the score  B) Is a soft enquiry and does not affect the score  C) Is prohibited  D) Raises the score automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Self-checks are **soft enquiries**. Only a lender's **hard enquiry** on an application can affect the score.\nA) A persistent myth. C) Consumers have a statutory right to their report. D) The score reflects behaviour, not checking.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Borrowing for a foreign holiday is an example of:\nA) Investment expenditure  B) Consumption expenditure  C) Working capital  D) Refinancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A holiday leaves **no lasting asset** — the loan outlives the benefit.\nA) Investment expenditure creates an appreciating asset or future income. C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which is generally considered justified borrowing?\nA) A credit card balance for restaurant meals  B) A home loan to buy a house  C) A personal loan for a wedding gift  D) An overdraft for a shopping spree\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A house is an **appreciating asset**, the loan is the cheapest available, and it carries tax benefits.\nA), C) and D) are all consumption expenditure funded by expensive debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** In an overdraft facility, interest is charged on:\nA) The entire sanctioned limit  B) Only the amount used, for the days used  C) Nothing  D) A fixed monthly amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the overdraft's defining advantage over a term loan.\nA) Would defeat the purpose of a flexible limit. C) Overdrafts do carry interest. D) The charge varies with usage.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A loan against securities carries which distinctive risk?\nA) The shares are sold immediately  B) A margin call if the pledged securities fall in value  C) Interest is charged in advance  D) There is no security\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the collateral's value falls, the lender demands more security or partial repayment — a **margin call**.\nA) Securities are pledged, not sold, unless a call goes unmet. C) Interest accrues normally. D) It is a secured loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Refinancing (balance transfer) means:\nA) Borrowing more from the same lender  B) Replacing an existing loan with a new one, usually at a lower rate  C) Defaulting on the loan  D) Extending the moratorium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The old loan is closed with the proceeds of a new, cheaper one.\nA) That is a top-up loan. C) Default is a breach, not a strategy. D) A moratorium is a separate arrangement.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Under RBI rules, prepayment charges on a floating-rate home loan to an individual borrower are:\nA) 2% of the outstanding  B) Not permitted  C) 5% of the outstanding  D) Determined solely by the lender\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lenders **cannot levy foreclosure or prepayment penalties** on floating-rate home loans to individual borrowers.\nA) and C) are not permitted for this category. D) The RBI restricts this, so it is not purely at the lender's discretion. *(Fixed-rate loans may still attract a charge.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Leverage means:\nA) Repaying debt early  B) Using borrowed money to control a larger asset, magnifying gains and losses  C) Saving more  D) Reducing the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage amplifies outcomes **in both directions** — the key risk point.\nA) Is prepayment. C) and D) are unrelated to the definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which of these is the largest single factor in a credit score?\nA) The borrower's age  B) Repayment history  C) The borrower's income  D) The number of bank accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Repayment history** carries the greatest weight, followed by credit utilisation and history length.\nA) and C) Age and income are not directly scored by CICs. D) Account count is not a primary factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Education loans typically feature:\nA) No repayment ever  B) A moratorium during the course plus 6–12 months  C) The highest interest rate of all loans  D) Mandatory property collateral for all amounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Repayment begins after the course plus a grace period, since the student has no income meanwhile.\nA) Repayment is required. C) Education loans are among the cheaper loans. D) Small-ticket education loans are commonly unsecured.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Matching loan tenure to the useful life of the asset means:\nA) A 7-year loan for a mobile phone is appropriate  B) A long tenure suits a house; a short tenure suits a depreciating asset  C) All loans should be 30 years  D) Tenure is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You should not still be paying for something after it has lost its value.\nA) A phone's useful life is 2–3 years, so a 7-year loan is clearly mismatched. C) Long tenures multiply total interest. D) Tenure drives total interest and is central.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Paying only the \"minimum due\" on a credit card:\nA) Avoids all interest  B) Avoids a late fee but not interest on the revolving balance  C) Clears the balance  D) Improves the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The unpaid balance **revolves at 3–4% a month**, and the interest-free period on new purchases is typically lost.\nA) The most costly misconception in consumer finance. C) The minimum is a small fraction of the balance. D) The rate does not improve.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Never borrowing to service existing debt is important because:\nA) It is illegal  B) It creates a debt spiral where new debt funds old debt  C) Lenders forbid it  D) It improves the credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Borrowing to pay EMIs adds cost without adding income — the classic **debt spiral**.\nA) It is not illegal, merely unwise. C) Lenders cannot always detect it. D) It typically **damages** the score as utilisation rises.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A ₹30,00,000 home loan at 8.5% for 20 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹21,250  B) ₹26,035  C) ₹12,500  D) ₹32,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.085 ÷ 12 = **0.0070833**; n = 240; (1.0070833)²⁴⁰ = **5.44127**. EMI = 30,00,000 × (0.0070833 × 5.44127) ÷ (5.44127 − 1) = 30,00,000 × 0.0385424 ÷ 4.44127 = **₹26,035**.\nA) ₹21,250 is the **first month's interest** (30,00,000 × 0.0070833). C) ₹12,500 is principal ÷ 240. D) ₹32,000 overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(8.5%/12,240,-3000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** For the Q36 loan, what is the interest component of the FIRST EMI?\nA) ₹26,035  B) ₹21,250  C) ₹4,785  D) ₹12,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Interest = outstanding × monthly rate = 30,00,000 × 0.0070833 = **₹21,250**.\nA) Is the full EMI. C) ₹4,785 is the **principal** component (26,035 − 21,250). D) Is principal ÷ n. **Note: 82% of the first EMI is interest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Continuing Q36–Q37, what is the outstanding balance after the first EMI?\nA) ₹30,00,000  B) ₹29,95,215  C) ₹29,73,965  D) ₹28,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Principal repaid = EMI − interest = 26,035 − 21,250 = **₹4,785**. New balance = 30,00,000 − 4,785 = **₹29,95,215**.\nA) Ignores the repayment. C) Wrongly subtracts the full EMI (which would double-count the interest). D) Far too low. **After a full EMI, the debt fell by less than ₹5,000 — the amortisation reality.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** For the Q36 loan, what is the total interest paid over 20 years?\nA) ₹32,48,400  B) ₹30,00,000  C) ₹62,48,400  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Total repaid = 26,035 × 240 = **₹62,48,400**. Interest = 62,48,400 − 30,00,000 = **₹32,48,400**.\nB) Is the principal. C) ₹62,48,400 is the *total repaid*, not the interest. D) Understates it. **The borrower pays more than the loan itself in interest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A borrower has ₹6,00,000 spare, a home loan at 8.5% and a personal loan at 15%. Under the avalanche method, which should be repaid?\nA) The home loan, as it is larger  B) The personal loan, as it has the higher rate  C) Split equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche attacks the **highest rate**, minimising total interest. 15% is nearly double 8.5%.\nA) Size is irrelevant to the avalanche rule. C) Splitting dilutes the saving. D) Repaying 15% debt is a guaranteed 15% return.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A client can invest at an expected 10% (taxable at 30%) or prepay a 9% home loan. Which is better?\nA) Invest, since 10% > 9%  B) Prepay — the post-tax return of 7% is below the 9% loan cost  C) They are identical  D) Take another loan and invest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compare the loan rate to the **post-tax** return: 10% × (1 − 0.30) = **7%**. Since 7% < 9%, prepaying wins — and it is **certain and risk-free**.\nA) Compares a pre-tax expected return with a certain cost — the classic error. C) They differ by 2 percentage points. D) Leveraging into a lower post-tax return destroys value.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Interest rates fall and a borrower with comfortable cash flow is offered a lower EMI or a shorter tenure. Which saves more?\nA) The lower EMI  B) Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure  C) They are identical  D) Neither changes the interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Keeping the EMI means the **entire benefit goes to principal**, cutting years off the loan and saving far more total interest.\nA) Frees up cash flow but stretches the loan and costs more overall. C) The difference can be lakhs. D) Both options change the interest, in opposite directions.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A client's gross monthly income is ₹1,80,000 and total EMIs ₹81,000. What is the debt-to-income ratio and the verdict?\nA) 45% — above the healthy ceiling; no further borrowing capacity  B) 45% — healthy  C) 22% — healthy  D) 81% — critical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 81,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **45%**, above the 35–40% guideline. Lenders will resist further credit and the household has little room for shocks.\nB) 45% is not healthy. C) Miscomputes the ratio. D) 81% confuses the EMI amount with the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which loan is secured by hypothecation?\nA) A home loan  B) A gold loan  C) A car loan  D) A personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The car is **movable** and the **borrower retains possession**, which is precisely hypothecation.\nA) Home loans use a **mortgage** (immovable). B) Gold loans use a **pledge** (lender holds the gold). D) Personal loans are unsecured.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A borrower takes a ₹10,00,000 loan and is offered 5 years at ₹21,247/month or 7 years at ₹16,601/month, both at 10%. Which costs less in total?\nA) The 7-year option, because the EMI is lower  B) The 5-year option — total interest ₹2,74,820 versus ₹3,94,484  C) They cost the same  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **5 years:** 21,247 × 60 = ₹12,74,820, interest = **₹2,74,820**. **7 years:** 16,601 × 84 = ₹13,94,484, interest = **₹3,94,484**.\nA) This is the **EMI illusion** — a lower monthly figure hides roughly ₹1.2 lakh of extra interest. C) They differ substantially. D) Both figures are computable. **Always compare total interest, not the EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A client repeatedly uses a credit card to cover month-end shortfalls. The correct diagnosis is:\nA) The client needs a higher card limit  B) A structural budget deficit that more credit cannot fix  C) The card rate is too low  D) The client should take a personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Persistent shortfalls mean **outflows exceed inflows**. Only raising income or cutting expenses fixes that.\nA) A larger limit enables a bigger problem. C) Card rates are the highest available. D) Refinancing card debt into a cheaper personal loan can help *tactically*, but does nothing about the underlying deficit.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which is the correct classification of a loan against a life insurance policy?\nA) Unsecured  B) Secured by assignment of the policy  C) Secured by mortgage  D) Not permitted in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The policy is **assigned** to the insurer/lender as security, available once the policy has acquired a **surrender value**.\nA) It is secured. C) Mortgages apply to immovable property. D) It is a standard facility.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A floating-rate borrower sees the benchmark rise. If the lender keeps the EMI unchanged, what happens?\nA) The tenure shortens  B) The tenure extends  C) The principal falls faster  D) Nothing changes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a higher rate but the same EMI, less of each payment goes to principal, so the **tenure extends**.\nA) That happens when rates **fall**. C) The opposite occurs. D) Something must adjust — either the EMI or the tenure.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which client should prioritise the snowball method over the avalanche?\nA) A disciplined client focused purely on minimising interest  B) A client who has repeatedly abandoned repayment plans and needs early wins  C) A client with only one loan  D) A client with no debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Snowball's value is **behavioural**: clearing a small balance quickly builds the momentum to continue.\nA) Such a client should use avalanche, which is mathematically optimal. C) With one loan the strategies coincide. D) Neither applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A ₹5,00,000 personal loan at 16% for 4 years. What is the approximate EMI?\nA) ₹10,417  B) ₹14,166  C) ₹6,667  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — r = 0.16 ÷ 12 = **0.0133333**; n = 48; (1.0133333)⁴⁸ = **1.88936**. EMI = 5,00,000 × (0.0133333 × 1.88936) ÷ (1.88936 − 1) = 5,00,000 × 0.02519 ÷ 0.88936 = **₹14,163**.\nA) ₹10,417 is principal ÷ 48, ignoring interest. C) ₹6,667 is the first month's interest. D) ₹20,000 overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of the following would MOST improve a client's credit score over time?\nA) Applying for several loans in one month  B) Consistently paying every EMI and card bill in full and on time  C) Closing all credit cards immediately  D) Maxing out the card limit each month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Repayment history** is the single largest scoring factor.\nA) Multiple hard enquiries in a short period hurt the score. C) Closing cards shortens credit history and raises utilisation on the rest. D) High utilisation lowers the score.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A client is midway (10 of 20 years) through a home loan and assumes half the principal is repaid. In reality:\nA) Well over half is repaid  B) Considerably less than half is repaid  C) Exactly half is repaid  D) The full amount is repaid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because early EMIs are mostly **interest**, the principal repaid at the halfway point is typically only around **30–35%** of the loan.\nA) and C) reflect the intuitive but wrong linear assumption. D) Clearly false. **This is why a foreclosure quote often shocks borrowers.**\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A borrower with a ₹2,00,000 card balance at 42% is offered a personal loan at 14% to clear it. This is:\nA) Always a bad idea  B) Generally sensible — refinancing expensive debt with cheaper debt, provided spending behaviour changes  C) Illegal  D) Neutral, since both are unsecured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Swapping **42% for 14%** saves 28 percentage points a year. The essential caveat: if the freed-up card is used again, the client ends up with **both** debts.\nA) The interest saving is substantial. C) It is a routine, legal transaction. D) The rates differ by a factor of three — hardly neutral.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which describes the relationship between loan tenure and total interest?\nA) Longer tenure, less total interest  B) Longer tenure, lower EMI but more total interest  C) Tenure has no effect  D) Shorter tenure, more total interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stretching repayment leaves a larger balance outstanding for longer, so **total interest rises** even as the EMI falls.\nA) and D) reverse the relationship. C) Tenure is one of the two main drivers of total interest.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A client wants to borrow for a ₹1,20,000 phone over 5 years. The adviser should point out that:\nA) This is investment expenditure  B) The tenure far exceeds the asset's useful life — she will pay for it long after it is worthless  C) It will improve her credit score  D) Phones appreciate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A phone's useful life is roughly 2–3 years; a 5-year loan is a **tenure/asset mismatch** and pure consumption expenditure.\nA) It creates no lasting value. C) Taking unnecessary debt does not meaningfully help a score. D) Consumer electronics depreciate quickly.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A borrower's floating-rate home loan rate rises so far that the EMI no longer covers the monthly interest. What must happen?\nA) The tenure simply extends further  B) The EMI must be increased  C) The loan is written off  D) The rate automatically resets to the original level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the EMI is below the interest accrual, the balance would **grow indefinitely** (negative amortisation), so the lender must raise the EMI.\nA) There is no tenure long enough to fix a negatively amortising loan. C) Lenders do not write off performing loans. D) Rates do not auto-reset.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which is the best use of an unexpected ₹4,00,000 bonus for a client with a ₹3,00,000 card balance at 40% and no emergency fund?\nA) Invest it all in equity  B) Clear the 40% card balance, then start building the emergency fund with the rest  C) Buy a car  D) Prepay the 8.5% home loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Clearing **40% debt** is a guaranteed, tax-free 40% return — unbeatable. The remaining ₹1,00,000 starts the buffer.\nA) No equity return reliably beats 40%. C) Pure consumption while carrying 40% debt. D) The home loan at 8.5% is far cheaper than the card and should wait.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A ₹20,00,000 loan at 9% for 15 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹20,285  B) ₹15,000  C) ₹11,111  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0075; n = 180; (1.0075)¹⁸⁰ = **3.83804**. EMI = 20,00,000 × (0.0075 × 3.83804) ÷ (3.83804 − 1) = 20,00,000 × 0.0287853 ÷ 2.83804 = **₹20,285**.\nB) ₹15,000 is the first month's interest (20,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹11,111 is principal ÷ 180. D) ₹25,000 overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Which is TRUE of a fixed-rate versus a floating-rate loan?\nA) Fixed rates always cost less overall  B) Fixed gives certainty but usually starts higher; floating is cheaper initially but the borrower bears rate risk  C) Floating rates never change  D) Fixed-rate loans have no prepayment charges\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The trade-off is **certainty versus cost**.\nA) Whether fixed costs less depends on the rate path, which is unknowable in advance. C) Floating rates move with the benchmark by definition. D) It is **floating-rate** home loans to individuals that carry no prepayment penalty; fixed-rate loans may.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A client's total EMIs are ₹95,000 on a ₹2,50,000 monthly income, and she wants a new ₹25,000 EMI. What is the resulting ratio and advice?\nA) 48% — comfortably within limits  B) 48% — above the 35–40% ceiling; defer or reduce existing debt first  C) 38% — acceptable  D) 10% — no concern\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — New total = 95,000 + 25,000 = **₹1,20,000**; ratio = 1,20,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **48%**, well beyond the guideline.\nA) 48% is not comfortable. C) 38% is the *current* ratio (95,000 ÷ 2,50,000), before the new loan. D) Badly miscomputed.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Why should a large home loan be paired with adequate term insurance?\nA) It reduces the interest rate  B) So dependants do not inherit the liability and lose the home  C) It is legally required  D) It improves the credit score\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the borrower dies, the loan survives. Term cover at least equal to the outstanding balance protects the family.\nA) Insurance does not change the rate. C) Lenders often encourage but rarely legally mandate it. D) It has no scoring effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A client is choosing between a ₹5,00,000 gold loan at 11% and a ₹5,00,000 personal loan at 17%. Assuming she owns the gold, which is preferable and why?\nA) The personal loan, since no asset is at risk  B) The gold loan — 6 percentage points cheaper because it is secured  C) They are equivalent  D) Neither; she should use a credit card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Collateral lowers the lender's risk and hence the rate. On ₹5,00,000, 6 percentage points is **₹30,000 a year**.\nA) The gold is only at risk on default, which the borrower controls. C) The rates differ materially. D) A card at 40%+ would be far worse.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which best explains why prepaying early in a loan saves much more than prepaying late?\nA) Lenders offer discounts early  B) Early prepayment removes principal that would otherwise accrue interest for many remaining years  C) Interest rates are higher early  D) There is no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A rupee of principal removed in year 2 avoids interest for **18 more years**; the same rupee removed in year 18 avoids only 2 years' interest.\nA) No such discount exists. C) The rate is the same throughout (on a fixed loan). D) The difference is large.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A client's loan has 96 months remaining with an EMI of ₹32,000 at 10%. What is the approximate outstanding balance?\nA) ₹30,72,000  B) ₹21,13,000  C) ₹32,000  D) ₹15,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The outstanding balance is the **PV of remaining EMIs**: r = 0.008333, m = 96. (1.008333)⁹⁶ = **2.21815**, so the inverse is **0.45083**. Factor = (1 − 0.45083) ÷ 0.008333 = 0.54917 ÷ 0.008333 = **65.90**. Outstanding = 32,000 × 65.90 = **₹21,08,800 ≈ ₹21,13,000**.\nA) ₹30,72,000 is the undiscounted total of remaining EMIs (32,000 × 96), which wrongly includes future interest. C) Is one EMI. D) Understates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which action would WORSEN a client's credit score?\nA) Paying the full card statement balance monthly  B) Using 90% of the available credit limit every month  C) Keeping an old credit card open with occasional use  D) Checking the score once a year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **High credit utilisation** is a major negative factor; below about 30% is generally preferred.\nA) The single best habit for a score. C) Length of credit history helps. D) A soft enquiry has no effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A borrower is offered a ₹15,00,000 loan at \"9% with a 1% processing fee and bundled insurance of ₹30,000\". The adviser should:\nA) Compare only the 9% headline rate  B) Compute the effective cost including the ₹15,000 fee and ₹30,000 insurance  C) Ignore the fees, as they are one-time  D) Assume the effective rate is below 9%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fees and bundled products raise the **effective** cost. Here ₹45,000 of upfront charges means the borrower effectively receives less than ₹15,00,000 while repaying on the full amount.\nA) The headline rate hides the true cost. C) One-time costs still change the effective rate. D) Fees push the effective rate **above** 9%, never below.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An education loan borrower is in the moratorium period. Which statement is correct?\nA) No interest accrues during the moratorium  B) Interest generally accrues and increases the amount eventually repaid  C) The loan is interest-free permanently  D) The principal reduces during the moratorium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The moratorium defers **payment**, not the **cost**. Some borrowers pay simple interest during the course precisely to avoid this build-up.\nA) and C) are the common misconception. D) Principal cannot reduce when nothing is being repaid.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A client has these debts: ₹80,000 at 12%, ₹2,50,000 at 18%, ₹40,000 at 24%. Under the **avalanche** method, in what order should they be cleared?\nA) ₹40,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000  B) ₹40,000 (24%) → ₹2,50,000 (18%) → ₹80,000 (12%)  C) ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000 → ₹40,000  D) ₹2,50,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by **rate, highest first**: 24% → 18% → 12%.\nA) Happens to start correctly but then orders by balance, not rate — that is the **snowball**. C) and D) ignore rates entirely. *(Here the snowball order would be ₹40,000 → ₹80,000 → ₹2,50,000, which coincidentally starts the same.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which statement about windfalls is most consistent with sound debt management?\nA) Windfalls should be spent immediately to enjoy them  B) Windfalls should be pre-allocated — costliest debt first, then the emergency fund, then goals  C) Windfalls should always go into equity  D) Windfalls should be ignored in planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Pre-allocation** prevents the windfall from being absorbed into consumption, which is what happens by default.\nA) Wastes a significant opportunity. C) Ignores expensive debt and the buffer. D) Forfeits the benefit entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A borrower can afford a higher EMI after a salary increase. The most efficient use is:\nA) Extend the tenure  B) Raise the EMI (or prepay), shortening the tenure and cutting total interest  C) Take another loan  D) Reduce the EMI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Directing the increment to the loan reduces principal faster and **saves compounding interest**.\nA) and D) increase total interest. C) Adds cost rather than removing it.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A ₹60,00,000 home loan at 9% for 25 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹50,349  B) ₹45,000  C) ₹20,000  D) ₹62,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0075; n = 300; (1.0075)³⁰⁰ = **9.40842**. EMI = 60,00,000 × (0.0075 × 9.40842) ÷ (9.40842 − 1) = 60,00,000 × 0.07056315 ÷ 8.40842 = **₹50,349**.\nB) ₹45,000 is the **first month's interest** (60,00,000 × 0.0075). C) ₹20,000 is principal ÷ 300. D) Overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(9%/12,300,-6000000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Continuing Q71, what is the total interest over 25 years?\nA) ₹60,00,000  B) ₹91,04,700  C) ₹1,51,04,700  D) ₹45,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total repaid = 50,349 × 300 = **₹1,51,04,700**. Interest = 1,51,04,700 − 60,00,000 = **₹91,04,700**.\nA) Is the principal. C) Is the total repaid, not the interest. D) Understates it. **The borrower pays over 1.5× the loan amount in interest alone — the cost of a 25-year tenure.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Compare Q71's 25-year loan with the same ₹60,00,000 at 9% over 20 years (EMI ₹53,984). How much total interest is saved by choosing 20 years?\nA) ₹3,635  B) ₹1,89,84,000  C) ₹21,48,540  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **20 years:** 53,984 × 240 = ₹1,29,56,160; interest = **₹69,56,160**. **25 years:** interest = **₹91,04,700**. Saving = 91,04,700 − 69,56,160 = **₹21,48,540**.\nA) ₹3,635 is just the monthly EMI difference. B) Is not a meaningful figure. D) The difference is enormous. **Paying ₹3,635 more a month saves over ₹21 lakh — the single most powerful lever in home-loan planning.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A borrower with the Q71 loan prepays ₹5,00,000 at the end of year 3. If he keeps the EMI unchanged, the effect is:\nA) The EMI rises  B) The tenure shortens materially, saving a large amount of interest  C) Nothing changes  D) The interest rate falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Removing ₹5,00,000 of principal in year 3 eliminates the interest that sum would have accrued over the remaining **22 years**. Keeping the EMI constant sends the entire benefit to principal, shortening the tenure by several years.\nA) A prepayment never forces a higher EMI. C) Total interest falls substantially. D) The contractual rate is unaffected. **Keeping the EMI and shortening the tenure always beats reducing the EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A ₹12,00,000 car loan at 10.5% for 5 years. What is the EMI and total interest?\nA) EMI ₹25,799; interest ₹3,47,940  B) EMI ₹20,000; interest ₹1,20,000  C) EMI ₹10,500; interest ₹3,47,940  D) EMI ₹30,000; interest ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.105 ÷ 12 = **0.00875**; n = 60; (1.00875)⁶⁰ = **1.68611**. EMI = 12,00,000 × (0.00875 × 1.68611) ÷ (1.68611 − 1) = 12,00,000 × 0.01475346 ÷ 0.68611 = **₹25,802**. Total repaid = 25,802 × 60 = ₹15,48,120; interest = **₹3,48,120**.\nB) ₹20,000 is principal ÷ 60. C) ₹10,500 is the first month's interest. D) Overstates both.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client has ₹8,00,000 spare and these debts: home loan ₹40,00,000 at 8.5%, car loan ₹6,00,000 at 10.5%, personal loan ₹5,00,000 at 16%, card balance ₹1,50,000 at 42%. What is the optimal (avalanche) application?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Clear the card (₹1,50,000 at 42%), then the personal loan (₹5,00,000 at 16%), then ₹1,50,000 toward the car loan  C) Split ₹2,00,000 across each  D) Clear the car loan first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by rate: **42% → 16% → 10.5% → 8.5%**. The ₹8,00,000 clears the card (₹1,50,000), then the personal loan (₹5,00,000), leaving ₹1,50,000 for the car loan.\nA) The home loan is the **cheapest** debt — the last thing to prepay. C) Splitting leaves 42% debt outstanding. D) Skips two costlier debts. **Interest saved in year 1 ≈ ₹63,000 (card) + ₹80,000 (personal) + ₹15,750 (car) ≈ ₹1,58,750.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A borrower's ₹35,00,000 loan at 9% for 20 years has an EMI of ₹31,490. After exactly 10 years, roughly what proportion of the principal has been repaid?\nA) About 50%  B) About 33%  C) About 75%  D) About 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Outstanding after 120 months = PV of the remaining 120 EMIs: (1.0075)¹²⁰ = **2.45136**, inverse = **0.40794**. Factor = (1 − 0.40794) ÷ 0.0075 = 0.59206 ÷ 0.0075 = **78.94**. Outstanding = 31,490 × 78.94 = **₹24,86,000**. Principal repaid = 35,00,000 − 24,86,000 = **₹10,14,000**, i.e. about **29–33%**.\nA) The intuitive linear assumption — and wrong. C) and D) are far off. **Halfway through the tenure, barely a third of the principal is gone.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client earns ₹3,00,000 a month with EMIs of ₹1,35,000. She wants to buy a house needing a ₹45,000 EMI. What is her position?\nA) Debt-to-income would be 60% — well beyond the limit; she must reduce existing debt first  B) 45% — acceptable  C) 15% — no concern  D) 30% — healthy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current ratio = 1,35,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = **45%** (already above the guideline). Adding ₹45,000 gives ₹1,80,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = **60%**, leaving only 40% of gross income for taxes, living costs and savings.\nB) 45% is the *current* figure and is itself too high. C) and D) badly miscompute. **Advice: clear or refinance existing debt before adding a housing EMI.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A borrower is offered: Loan A at 9.5% with no fees, or Loan B at 9.0% with a 1.5% processing fee, both ₹25,00,000 over 15 years. Which is cheaper overall?\nA) Loan A always  B) Loan B — the 0.5 percentage-point saving over 15 years far exceeds the ₹37,500 fee  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Compute each loan's total outgo.\n**Loan A (9.5%, no fee):** r = 0.0079167; (1.0079167)¹⁸⁰ = **4.14539**. EMI = 25,00,000 × (0.0079167 × 4.14539) ÷ 3.14539 = **₹26,105**. Total = 26,105 × 180 = **₹46,98,900**.\n**Loan B (9.0% + 1.5% fee):** r = 0.0075; (1.0075)¹⁸⁰ = **3.83804**. EMI = 25,00,000 × (0.0075 × 3.83804) ÷ 2.83804 = **₹25,357**. Total = 25,357 × 180 = ₹45,64,260, plus the **₹37,500** fee = **₹46,01,760**.\n**Loan B is cheaper by about ₹97,000.**\nA) Ignores the rate difference, which compounds over 15 years. C) They differ by nearly ₹1 lakh. D) Both are fully computable. **A one-time fee is usually outweighed by a lower rate on a long tenure — but always compute rather than assume.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A client with a 42% card balance of ₹2,00,000 pays only the 5% minimum due each month while continuing to spend. What is the likely outcome?\nA) The balance clears in about 20 months  B) The balance barely reduces while interest accrues at ~3.5% a month, and new purchases lose the interest-free period  C) Interest is waived  D) The credit score improves rapidly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The minimum (₹10,000) is close to the monthly interest (₹2,00,000 × 3.5% = **₹7,000**), so only about ₹3,000 reduces the principal. Meanwhile, once a balance revolves, **new purchases start accruing interest from the transaction date**.\nA) At this rate it would take many years, not 20 months. C) Interest is not waived by paying the minimum. D) High utilisation and revolving debt **hurt** the score.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A ₹25,00,000 loan at 8% for 20 years has an EMI of ₹20,910. If the rate rises to 9.5% and the lender keeps the EMI unchanged, what happens?\nA) The tenure shortens  B) The tenure extends significantly  C) The EMI rises to ₹23,300  D) The principal is unaffected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 9.5%, the monthly interest on the opening balance is 25,00,000 × 0.0079167 = **₹19,792**, leaving only ₹1,118 of the ₹20,910 EMI for principal (versus ₹4,243 at 8%). Repayment slows dramatically and the **tenure extends** — often by many years.\nA) That happens when rates fall. C) The premise is that the EMI is held constant. D) Principal repayment slows sharply, which is the whole effect. **If the rate rose far enough that interest exceeded ₹20,910, the EMI would have to rise.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A client can either prepay ₹10,00,000 on a 8.75% home loan or invest it in an equity fund expected to return 12% pre-tax over 15 years (LTCG at 12.5% above the annual exemption). Which is more likely to be better, and what is the key caveat?\nA) Prepay, since 8.75% is certain  B) Invest — the post-tax expected return of roughly 10.5–11% exceeds 8.75% over a 15-year horizon, but the return is uncertain while the saving is guaranteed  C) They are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Post-tax equity return ≈ 12% × (1 − 0.125) ≈ **10.5%** (roughly, since LTCG applies only on redemption and partly within the exemption), which exceeds the **8.75%** loan cost over a long horizon.\nA) Certainty has value, but a 15-year horizon is long enough for equity to be reasonable. C) They differ by roughly 1.75 percentage points. D) Both are legitimate options. **Key caveat: prepayment is a risk-free 8.75%; investing carries market risk. For a risk-averse client, or where the loan rate exceeds ~10%, prepay.**\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A ₹8,00,000 personal loan at 15% for 3 years. What is the EMI, and what proportion of the first EMI is interest?\nA) EMI ₹27,729; about 36% interest  B) EMI ₹22,222; about 45% interest  C) EMI ₹27,729; about 100% interest  D) EMI ₹10,000; about 36% interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.0125; n = 36; (1.0125)³⁶ = **1.56394**. EMI = 8,00,000 × (0.0125 × 1.56394) ÷ 0.56394 = 8,00,000 × 0.01954925 ÷ 0.56394 = **₹27,732**. First month's interest = 8,00,000 × 0.0125 = **₹10,000**; share = 10,000 ÷ 27,732 = **36%**.\nB) ₹22,222 is principal ÷ 36. C) 100% is wrong — ₹17,732 goes to principal. D) ₹10,000 is the interest, not the EMI. **Note: on a short 3-year loan only 36% of the first EMI is interest, versus 82% on a 20-year home loan — tenure drives the split.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by cost of borrowing, cheapest first?\nA) Credit card, personal loan, home loan, gold loan  B) Home loan, gold loan, personal loan, credit card  C) Personal loan, home loan, credit card, gold loan  D) Gold loan, credit card, home loan, personal loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Typical Indian rates: **home loan 8–10% < gold loan 9–14% < personal loan 11–18% < credit card 36–48%**.\nA) Reverses the order almost entirely. C) and D) scramble it. **The ordering follows security and risk: secured against immovable property is cheapest; unsecured revolving credit is dearest.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A client's loan has 60 months remaining, EMI ₹18,000, rate 11%. She receives ₹3,00,000. If she prepays, roughly how much interest does she save over the remaining term?\nA) About ₹3,00,000  B) About ₹90,000  C) Nothing  D) About ₹10,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Removing ₹3,00,000 of principal avoids all the interest that sum would have accrued over the remaining 5 years. Because the prepayment shortens the tail of the loan, the avoided interest works out to roughly **₹90,000** over the remaining term (≈ ₹3,00,000 × 11% × 5 years × ~0.55, the factor reflecting that the loan would have amortised down anyway).\nA) ₹3,00,000 would mean saving 100% of the prepaid amount in interest — far too high for a 5-year residual term. C) Prepayment always saves interest. D) ₹10,80,000 is the total of remaining EMIs (18,000 × 60), not the interest. **For an exact figure, recompute the amortisation schedule with and without the prepayment.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A borrower defaults on a gold loan. What can the lender do?\nA) Nothing without a court order  B) Sell the pledged gold, since possession is already with the lender  C) Seize the borrower's house  D) Only report to the credit bureau\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **pledge**, the lender already **holds** the movable security and can realise it on default, subject to due notice.\nA) The pledge is precisely what avoids a lengthy recovery process. C) The house is not the security for a gold loan. D) Bureau reporting happens, but the lender's primary remedy is the pledged gold.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client's monthly income is ₹2,00,000. Existing EMIs ₹40,000. A lender caps debt-to-income at 40%. What is the maximum additional EMI she can take on?\nA) ₹80,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum total EMI = 40% × 2,00,000 = **₹80,000**. She already pays ₹40,000, so the additional capacity is 80,000 − 40,000 = **₹40,000**.\nA) ₹80,000 is the *total* permitted, not the additional amount. C) Ignores the cap. D) Halves the answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Continuing Q87: at 9% over 20 years, what loan amount does a ₹40,000 EMI support?\nA) ₹44,45,000  B) ₹96,00,000  C) ₹30,00,000  D) ₹53,33,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Reverse the EMI formula: loan = EMI × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r]. r = 0.0075, n = 240, (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**, inverse = **0.166413**. Factor = (1 − 0.166413) ÷ 0.0075 = 0.833587 ÷ 0.0075 = **111.14**. Loan = 40,000 × 111.14 = **₹44,45,600**.\nB) ₹96,00,000 is the undiscounted total of EMIs (40,000 × 240). C) and D) do not follow. Excel: `=PV(9%/12,240,-40000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A client argues that because her home loan gives tax benefits, she should never prepay it. The best response is:\nA) She is correct — never prepay  B) Tax benefits reduce the effective cost but rarely make the loan free; compare the post-tax loan cost with the post-tax return on alternatives  C) Tax benefits do not exist on home loans  D) She should take a larger loan for more tax benefit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deductions lower the **effective** rate (a 9% loan might cost ~6.3% post-tax at a 30% slab, within deduction limits), but the loan still has a positive cost. Compare that effective cost with the post-tax return available elsewhere.\nA) Overstates the benefit into an absolute rule. C) Home loan deductions do exist. D) **Borrowing more to save tax is always value-destructive** — you spend a rupee of interest to save 30 paise of tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which combination indicates a household in a debt spiral?\nA) Debt-to-income 25%, all EMIs paid on time, 6-month buffer  B) Debt-to-income 55%, taking a new personal loan to pay existing EMIs, zero buffer  C) Debt-to-income 30%, one home loan  D) No debt at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The defining marker is **borrowing to service existing debt**, combined with an unsustainable ratio and no buffer.\nA) and C) are healthy positions. D) Is not a spiral. **The intervention here is debt counselling and restructuring, not more credit.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A ₹18,00,000 loan at 12% for 8 years. What is the EMI?\nA) ₹29,255  B) ₹18,750  C) ₹18,000  D) ₹35,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01**; n = 96; (1.01)⁹⁶ = **2.59927**. EMI = 18,00,000 × (0.01 × 2.59927) ÷ (2.59927 − 1) = 18,00,000 × 0.0259927 ÷ 1.59927 = **₹29,255**.\nB) ₹18,750 is principal ÷ 96 (18,00,000 ÷ 96), ignoring interest entirely. C) ₹18,000 is the **first month's interest** (18,00,000 × 0.01). D) ₹35,000 overstates it. Excel: `=PMT(12%/12,96,-1800000)`.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client with a floating-rate home loan sees rates fall from 9.5% to 8.5%. She has comfortable cash flow. Comparing the two options on a ₹40,00,000 loan with 18 years remaining:\nA) Reducing the EMI saves more interest  B) Keeping the EMI constant shortens the tenure by roughly 2–3 years and saves several lakhs of interest  C) There is no difference  D) She should refinance to a fixed rate immediately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 8.5% the required EMI falls, so continuing to pay the **old, higher EMI** sends the surplus entirely to principal. On a loan of this size the tenure typically shortens by **2–3 years**, saving several lakh rupees.\nA) A lower EMI stretches the loan and saves the least. C) The difference is material. D) Switching to fixed just as rates fall locks in the *higher* environment — usually the wrong moment.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A borrower has ₹4,50,000 available. Debts: card ₹1,80,000 at 40%, personal loan ₹2,00,000 at 15%, car loan ₹3,00,000 at 10%. He also has no emergency fund and monthly expenses of ₹60,000. What is the best allocation?\nA) Clear all of the car loan  B) Clear the card (₹1,80,000), clear the personal loan (₹2,00,000), and keep ₹70,000 as the start of an emergency buffer  C) Put everything into equity  D) Clear the card only and spend the rest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche order attacks **40% then 15%**, saving roughly ₹72,000 + ₹30,000 = **₹1,02,000 of interest in year one**. The remaining ₹70,000 begins the buffer (about 1.2 months) so a future shock doesn't recreate card debt.\nA) The car loan at 10% is the cheapest of the three. C) No investment reliably beats 40%. D) Leaves 15% debt outstanding while spending. **Note the balance struck: clear expensive debt, but do not end up with zero liquidity.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about the relationship between EMI, tenure and total interest is correct?\nA) Doubling the tenure roughly halves the total interest  B) Doubling the tenure lowers the EMI but can more than double the total interest  C) Tenure affects only the EMI  D) Total interest is fixed regardless of tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On the ₹60,00,000 at 9% example: 20 years costs ₹69.56 lakh of interest; 25 years costs ₹91.05 lakh. Extending further compounds the effect sharply.\nA) Reverses the relationship entirely. C) Tenure drives **both** the EMI and total interest. D) Total interest is highly sensitive to tenure.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client wants to borrow ₹3,00,000 for her daughter's wedding. Which is the soundest advice?\nA) Use a credit card for the flexibility  B) This is consumption expenditure — prefer saving in advance; if borrowing is unavoidable, use the cheapest secured option (e.g. gold loan or LAS) over a personal loan or card  C) Take a 10-year personal loan for a low EMI  D) Borrow ₹10,00,000 to have a buffer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A wedding creates **no lasting asset**, so it is consumption expenditure. Where borrowing cannot be avoided, minimise the cost by using **secured** credit.\nA) Cards at 36–48% are the worst possible choice. C) A long tenure on consumption debt multiplies total interest. D) Borrowing more than needed is straightforwardly value-destructive.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A ₹10,00,000 loan at 10% has an EMI of ₹21,247 over 5 years. In month 1, how is the EMI split?\nA) Interest ₹8,333; principal ₹12,914  B) Interest ₹12,914; principal ₹8,333  C) Interest ₹21,247; principal nil  D) Interest ₹1,00,000; principal nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Interest = 10,00,000 × (0.10 ÷ 12) = 10,00,000 × 0.008333 = **₹8,333**. Principal = 21,247 − 8,333 = **₹12,914**.\nB) Reverses the two. C) Some principal is always repaid on an amortising loan. D) ₹1,00,000 is a full year's interest. **Contrast with the 20-year home loan where interest was 82% of the first EMI — on a 5-year loan, principal already dominates at 61%.**\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which action most directly reduces a client's leverage ratio?\nA) Taking a top-up loan  B) Using surplus cash to prepay principal  C) Revaluing the house upward  D) Extending the loan tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage = liabilities ÷ assets. Prepaying reduces **liabilities** (and cash), lowering the ratio.\nA) Raises liabilities. C) Raising asset values lowers the ratio arithmetically but is a **paper** change, not a real reduction in debt. D) Changes timing, not the outstanding balance.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client is 5 years into a 20-year, ₹50,00,000 home loan at 9% (EMI ₹44,986) and is offered refinancing at 8% with a ₹40,000 fee. Should she refinance?\nA) No, fees always outweigh the benefit  B) Yes — a 1 percentage-point saving on a large outstanding balance over 15 remaining years far exceeds ₹40,000  C) Only if the tenure is extended  D) Only if she switches to a fixed rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Outstanding after 5 years ≈ **₹44.3 lakh**. Saving 1 percentage point on roughly that balance is on the order of **₹40,000 in the first year alone**, and the benefit continues for 15 more years — a total saving in the lakhs against a one-time ₹40,000.\nA) Fees matter but must be weighed against the saving. C) Extending the tenure would erode the benefit. D) The rate type is a separate decision. **Rule of thumb: refinancing is usually worthwhile when the rate gap is ≥0.5pp and substantial tenure remains.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A household's debt profile: home loan ₹35,00,000 at 8.5% (EMI ₹30,400), car loan ₹4,00,000 at 11% (EMI ₹10,400), personal loan ₹3,00,000 at 17% (EMI ₹10,700). Gross income ₹1,60,000/month. What is the priority action?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Debt-to-income is 32% (acceptable); direct any surplus at the 17% personal loan first  C) Take another loan to consolidate at 20%  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total EMIs = 30,400 + 10,400 + 10,700 = **₹51,500**; ratio = 51,500 ÷ 1,60,000 = **32%**, inside the healthy range. So the household is not distressed — the optimisation is simply to attack the **costliest debt (17%)** first.\nA) The home loan at 8.5% is the cheapest. C) Consolidating at a *higher* rate destroys value. D) Leaving 17% debt outstanding while having surplus is a missed guaranteed return.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single principle best summarises sound debt management?\nA) Avoid all debt at any cost  B) Borrow for assets that outlast the loan, at the lowest cost, over a tenure matched to the asset, within 35–40% of income  C) Take the loan with the lowest EMI  D) Always choose the longest tenure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This captures all four counselling questions: **purpose** (an asset that outlasts the loan), **cost** (lowest), **maturity** (matched), and **serviceability** (within 35–40%).\nA) Debt used well (a home, education) builds wealth; blanket avoidance is not planning. C) The **EMI illusion** — the lowest EMI usually means the most total interest. D) The longest tenure maximises interest paid.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Convert every rate to **monthly** and every tenure to **months** before computing.\n\n### Case: The Menon Family's Debt Review\n\nArjun Menon, 38, earns **₹2,60,000 a month** gross. His wife earns **₹90,000 a month**.\n\n**Their loans:**\n\n| Loan | Outstanding | Rate | EMI | Remaining tenure |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Home loan (floating) | ₹52,00,000 | 9.0% | ₹49,000 | 15 years |\n| Car loan | ₹5,20,000 | 10.5% | ₹13,400 | 4 years |\n| Personal loan | ₹4,00,000 | 16.5% | ₹14,200 | 3 years |\n| Credit card (revolving) | ₹2,10,000 | 42.0% | ₹10,500 (minimum) | — |\n\n**Monthly household expenses excluding EMIs:** ₹1,40,000.\n**Liquid assets:** ₹2,80,000. **Other investments:** ₹16,00,000 in equity mutual funds.\n\nArjun has just received an annual bonus of **₹7,00,000**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the Menons' debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 21.9%  B) 24.9%  C) 33.5%  D) 45.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Combined gross income** = 2,60,000 + 90,000 = **₹3,50,000**. **Total monthly debt servicing** = home ₹49,000 + car ₹13,400 + personal ₹14,200 + card minimum ₹10,500 = **₹87,100**. Ratio = 87,100 ÷ 3,50,000 = **24.9%**.\nA) 21.9% omits the credit card minimum — a common slip, since the card has no formal \"EMI\" but its minimum due is a real monthly obligation. C) 33.5% wrongly uses only Arjun's ₹2,60,000 income. D) 45% does not follow. **Verdict: comfortably inside the 35–40% guideline — the Menons' problem is not the *amount* of debt but its *cost*, with 42% card debt outstanding.**\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Under the avalanche method, how should the ₹7,00,000 bonus be applied?\nA) Prepay the home loan  B) Clear the card (₹2,10,000 at 42%), then the personal loan (₹4,00,000 at 16.5%), leaving ₹90,000  C) Clear the car loan first  D) Invest it all in equity funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Avalanche orders strictly by **rate**: 42% → 16.5% → 10.5% → 9.0%. The ₹7,00,000 clears the **card (₹2,10,000)** and the **personal loan (₹4,00,000)**, leaving **₹90,000**.\nA) The home loan at 9% is the **cheapest** debt and the last to prepay. C) The car loan at 10.5% is cheaper than both the card and the personal loan. D) No equity return reliably beats 42%.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Approximately how much interest does clearing the card and personal loan save in the first year?\nA) About ₹1,54,200  B) About ₹88,200  C) About ₹66,000  D) About ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Card:** ₹2,10,000 × 42% = **₹88,200** a year. **Personal loan:** ₹4,00,000 × 16.5% = **₹66,000** a year (slightly less in practice as the balance amortises). Total first-year saving ≈ 88,200 + 66,000 = **₹1,54,200**.\nB) ₹88,200 counts only the card. C) ₹66,000 counts only the personal loan. D) Far too low. **A guaranteed, tax-free ₹1.54 lakh return on a ₹6.1 lakh outlay — about 25%.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** After clearing those two loans, what are the Menons' new EMI total and debt-to-income ratio?\nA) ₹62,400 and 17.8%  B) ₹87,100 and 24.9%  C) ₹49,000 and 14.0%  D) ₹72,900 and 20.8%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Remaining EMIs = home ₹49,000 + car ₹13,400 = **₹62,400**. Ratio = 62,400 ÷ 3,50,000 = **17.8%**.\nB) Is the position *before* the prepayments. C) Omits the car loan. D) Does not match. **The freed-up ₹24,700 a month is now available for the emergency fund and goals.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What should the Menons do with the freed-up ₹24,700 a month and the remaining ₹90,000 of the bonus?\nA) Increase discretionary spending, since the debt burden has fallen  B) Top up the emergency fund toward 3–6 months of outgo, then direct the balance to long-term goals — and avoid re-using the cleared credit card  C) Prepay the entire home loan immediately  D) Take a new personal loan to invest in equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Their **liquidity ratio** is weak: total monthly outgo after the prepayments = ₹1,40,000 expenses + ₹62,400 EMIs = **₹2,02,400**, so ₹2,80,000 of liquid assets = only **1.38 months**. Adding the ₹90,000 residual bonus lifts it to ₹3,70,000 (**1.83 months**), and the ₹24,700 monthly surplus reaches a 3-month target (₹6,07,200) in about 10 months.\nA) Lifestyle inflation would waste the entire gain. C) They lack the funds, and at 9% the home loan is the cheapest debt — the buffer matters more. D) Borrowing at a high rate to invest reverses everything just achieved.\n\n> ⚠️ **The critical behavioural point:** a cleared credit card with a live limit is the commonest cause of relapse. The card should be kept for convenience only, **paid in full every month** — or the limit reduced.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "05", "title": "Introduction to Indian Financial Markets", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 2 · Indian Financial Markets — only 10 marks across Chapters 5–6.**\n> ⚠️ **Do not over-invest time here.** This is the lightest module in the paper. Read it once, drill the flashcards (especially the **regulator map**), and move on. The questions are almost entirely **recall** — \"who regulates what\" and \"who does what\".\n\n---\n\n## 5.1 Overview of the Indian Economy\n\nIndia has shifted from a primarily agrarian economy to one where **services and manufacturing** dominate output. A well-developed financial system supports that growth by:\n\n- Channelling **household savings** into productive investment\n- Providing **credit** at efficient cost\n- Enabling **risk transfer** (insurance, derivatives)\n- Supporting **price discovery** and liquidity\n- Facilitating **payments and settlement**\n\n**Key structural features:** a high household savings rate, historically heavy allocation to physical assets (gold, real estate) with a gradual shift toward financial assets (\"financialisation of savings\"), and increasing retail participation through mutual funds and SIPs.\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 The Indian Financial Markets\n\nFinancial markets exist to move funds from **surplus units** (savers) to **deficit units** (borrowers/users), through intermediaries.\n\n### 5.2.1 Key features of a well-functioning market\n\n| Feature | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **Efficient transfer of funds** | Low cost of intermediation, so saving and borrowing are both attractive |\n| **Price discovery** | Prices reflect all available information |\n| **Liquidity** | Investors can exit and re-allocate |\n| **Information dissemination** | All participants get relevant information |\n| **Regulation** | Systems and processes that protect participants and maintain integrity |\n| **Risk management** | Mechanisms to hedge and to contain systemic risk |\n\n### The basic split\n\n| | **Money Market** | **Capital / Securities Market** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Maturity** | **Up to 1 year** (short-term) | **Beyond 1 year** (long-term) |\n| **Regulator** | **RBI** | **SEBI** |\n| **Instruments** | T-bills, CPs, CDs, call money, repo | Shares, bonds, debentures, derivatives |\n| **Participants** | Banks, corporates, MFs, RBI | Companies, investors, MFs, FPIs |\n\nAnd a second split within the capital market:\n\n- **Primary market** — securities are **issued** by the borrower to investors; funds go **to the issuer**. (IPO, rights, private placement.)\n- **Secondary market** — investors trade **among themselves**; the issuer receives nothing. Provides **liquidity and exit**.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 ⭐ Regulators of Financial Markets\n\n**Learn this table cold — it is the single most examinable item in the chapter.**\n\n| Regulator | Regulates | Established / Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ministry of Finance (MoF)** | Overall economic and fiscal policy; the Budget; supervises the regulators | Government of India |\n| **Ministry of Corporate Affairs (MCA)** | Companies Act; corporate governance; incorporation | Administers the **Companies Act, 2013** |\n| **Registrar of Companies (RoC)** | Registration/incorporation of companies; statutory filings | Operates **under the MCA** |\n| **Reserve Bank of India (RBI)** | Banking, monetary policy, currency, **money market**, **foreign exchange**, payment systems, NBFCs, credit information companies | Est. **1935**; administers **FEMA** |\n| **SEBI** | **Securities markets** — stock exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, portfolio managers, investment advisers, AIFs, credit rating agencies, depositories | Statutory status **1992** (SEBI Act, 1992) |\n| **IRDAI** | **Insurance** — life and general insurers, agents, brokers | Est. **1999** (IRDA Act, 1999) |\n| **PFRDA** | **Pensions** — NPS, Atal Pension Yojana, pension funds | Statutory status **2013** (PFRDA Act, 2013) |\n| **SROs** | First-level regulation of their members, under the apex regulator | e.g. **AMFI** (mutual fund distributors), **ANMI**, exchanges |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — the four apex regulators by asset:**\n> **R**BI → **R**upees (banking, money, forex) · **S**EBI → **S**ecurities · **I**RDAI → **I**nsurance · **P**FRDA → **P**ensions.\n\n### 5.3.8 Self-Regulatory Organisations (SROs)\nAn SRO is an industry body granted authority to set and enforce standards for its members, **subject to the apex regulator's oversight**. Benefits: closer industry knowledge, faster rule-making, lower regulatory cost. Example: **AMFI** for mutual fund distributors; stock exchanges act as first-level regulators of their brokers.\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 Structure of Financial Markets in India\n\n### 5.4.1 The Banking Sector (RBI's domain)\n\n| Institution | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Commercial banks** | Full-service: accept demand deposits, lend, payments |\n| **Payment banks** | Accept deposits **up to ₹2 lakh** per customer; provide payments and remittances; **cannot lend** or issue credit cards |\n| **Small finance banks** | Full banking, but with priority-sector focus — small borrowers, micro-enterprises, unbanked segments |\n| **NBFCs** | Lend and invest, but **cannot accept demand deposits** and are **not part of the payment system** |\n| **Housing Finance Companies (HFCs)** | Specialised housing lenders; now regulated by the **RBI** (supervision moved from the NHB) |\n| **P2P lending platforms** | **NBFC-P2P**, RBI-registered; match individual lenders with borrowers. The platform only intermediates — **it cannot lend on its own book or guarantee returns** |\n| **Credit Information Companies** | CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark — maintain credit histories |\n| **Account Aggregators (AA)** | RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA**: with the customer's **explicit consent**, they retrieve and share the customer's financial data across institutions. They are **data blind** — they cannot read or store the data |\n\n> ⚠️ **Bank vs NBFC — a guaranteed question.** Banks can accept **demand deposits** and are part of the **payment/settlement system**; NBFCs cannot and are not. NBFCs also cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves, and their deposits are **not covered by DICGC insurance**.\n\n**Money market** and the **foreign exchange market** both sit under the **RBI**.\n\n### 5.4.2 Securities Markets (SEBI's domain)\n\n**Definition of \"securities\"** — under the **Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 (SCRA)**, securities include shares, scrips, stocks, bonds, debentures, debenture stock, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes, security receipts, government securities and rights/interests in securities.\n\n| Segment | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Stock markets** | Equity issuance and trading — NSE, BSE |\n| **Bond markets** | Government securities and corporate debt |\n| **Derivatives market** | Futures and options on equity, currency, commodities |\n| **Mutual funds** | Pooled, professionally managed, SEBI-regulated |\n| **Portfolio managers** | Individual portfolios for larger investors (min. **₹50 lakh**) |\n| **Investment advisers** | Registered under **SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013** |\n| **Alternative Investment Funds** | Categories I, II and III (Chapter 13) |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Assess creditworthiness of debt issues — CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings |\n\n### 5.4.3 Under IRDAI\nLife insurers, general (non-life) insurers, health insurers, reinsurers, plus intermediaries (agents, brokers, third-party administrators).\n\n### 5.4.4 Under PFRDA\nThe **National Pension System (NPS)**, **Atal Pension Yojana**, pension fund managers, the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**, points of presence, and the NPS Trust.\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 ⭐ Participants in the Indian Financial Markets\n\n### Market Infrastructure Institutions (MIIs)\n\nThe three \"MIIs\" — systemically important, so most heavily regulated:\n\n| MII | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Stock / commodity exchanges** | Provide the trading platform, price discovery, and first-level regulation of members |\n| **Clearing corporations** | **Clear and settle** trades; act as the **central counterparty (CCP)** — guaranteeing settlement so no buyer or seller faces counterparty default. Manage margins and the settlement guarantee fund |\n| **Depositories** | Hold securities in **electronic (demat)** form — **NSDL** and **CDSL** in India |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exchange vs clearing corporation:** the **exchange matches trades**; the **clearing corporation guarantees and settles** them. Do not confuse them.\n\n### Other participants\n\n| Participant | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Depository Participants (DPs)** | The **agents of the depository** — the investor's point of contact for a demat account |\n| **Custodians** | Safe-keep securities and handle settlement/corporate actions for institutional investors |\n| **Stock brokers / Authorised Persons** | Execute trades on behalf of clients. An **Authorised Person** acts on behalf of a broker (formerly \"sub-broker\") |\n| **Investment banks / merchant bankers** | Manage issues, underwrite, advise on M&A |\n| **Insurance companies** | Provide risk cover; large institutional investors |\n| **Pension funds** | Manage retirement money; long-horizon institutional investors |\n| **AMCs and portfolio managers** | Manage pooled (MF) or individual (PMS) portfolios |\n| **Investment advisers and distributors** | Advise on / distribute financial products |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Rate debt instruments for credit risk |\n| **KYC Registration Agencies (KRAs)** | Maintain centralised KYC records so a client's KYC is portable across SEBI intermediaries |\n| **Registrar & Transfer Agents (RTAs)** | Maintain investor records, process transactions and corporate actions — CAMS, KFin |\n| **NBFCs and HFCs** | Lend outside the banking system |\n| **Credit bureaus (CICs)** | Maintain individual credit histories and scores |\n\n> 🧠 **Why institutions matter:** mutual funds, insurers and pension funds are large, informed investors. They supply **liquidity and stability**, and improve **price efficiency** because they can source and evaluate information better than retail investors.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Question | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Money market maturity / regulator | **Up to 1 year** / **RBI** |\n| Capital market maturity / regulator | **Beyond 1 year** / **SEBI** |\n| Primary market | Securities **issued**; funds go to the **issuer** |\n| Secondary market | Investors trade **among themselves**; issuer gets nothing |\n| RBI regulates | Banking, monetary policy, currency, money market, **forex**, NBFCs, payment systems, CICs |\n| SEBI regulates | Securities markets, exchanges, brokers, MFs, PMS, IAs, AIFs, CRAs, depositories |\n| IRDAI regulates | Insurance |\n| PFRDA regulates | Pensions (NPS, APY) |\n| MCA / RoC | Companies Act, 2013 / company registration |\n| SEBI statutory status | **1992** |\n| IRDAI established | **1999** |\n| PFRDA statutory status | **2013** |\n| Payment bank deposit cap | **₹2 lakh** per customer; **cannot lend** |\n| Bank vs NBFC | Banks take **demand deposits** and are in the **payment system**; NBFCs are not |\n| The three MIIs | **Exchanges, clearing corporations, depositories** |\n| Depositories in India | **NSDL** and **CDSL** |\n| Clearing corporation's key role | **Central counterparty** — guarantees settlement |\n| Depository Participant | The **agent** of the depository; investor's access point |\n| KRA | Central KYC records, portable across SEBI intermediaries |\n| RTA | Investor records and transaction processing (CAMS, KFin) |\n| Account Aggregator | RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA**; consent-based data sharing; **data blind** |\n| NBFC-P2P | Matches lenders and borrowers; **cannot lend on own book or guarantee returns** |\n| Definition of \"securities\" | From the **SCRA, 1956** |\n| SRO example | **AMFI**; exchanges as first-level regulators of brokers |\n\n> **Exam tip:** Module 2 is worth only **10 marks total** across Chapters 5 and 6. Spend one study session here, memorise the regulator map and the MII roles, and put the saved time into Module 1 (37 marks) or Module 6 (30 marks).\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The regulator map and the three MIIs are the highest-yield items here.**\n\n**Q1.** Which four apex regulators cover which domains?\n> **A:** **RBI → Rupees** (banking, monetary policy, money market, forex, NBFCs, payments). **SEBI → Securities**. **IRDAI → Insurance**. **PFRDA → Pensions**.\n\n**Q2.** What is the maturity split between the money market and the capital market, and who regulates each?\n> **A:** **Money market = up to 1 year, regulated by the RBI.** **Capital/securities market = beyond 1 year, regulated by SEBI.**\n\n**Q3.** Distinguish the primary and secondary markets.\n> **A:** **Primary** — securities are **issued**, and the funds go **to the issuer**. **Secondary** — investors trade **among themselves**; the issuer receives nothing. The secondary market's function is **liquidity and exit**.\n\n**Q4.** When did SEBI, IRDAI and PFRDA get their statutory status?\n> **A:** **SEBI — 1992** (SEBI Act, 1992). **IRDAI — 1999** (IRDA Act, 1999). **PFRDA — 2013** (PFRDA Act, 2013).\n\n**Q5.** What does the Ministry of Corporate Affairs regulate, and what does the Registrar of Companies do?\n> **A:** The **MCA** administers the **Companies Act, 2013** and corporate governance. The **RoC**, which operates **under the MCA**, handles company registration/incorporation and statutory filings.\n\n**Q6.** Name the three Market Infrastructure Institutions.\n> **A:** **Stock/commodity exchanges**, **clearing corporations**, and **depositories**.\n\n**Q7.** What is the crucial difference between an exchange and a clearing corporation?\n> **A:** The **exchange matches trades** (trading platform and price discovery). The **clearing corporation clears, settles and guarantees** them, acting as the **central counterparty (CCP)** so neither side faces counterparty default.\n\n**Q8.** Name India's two depositories, and what do they hold?\n> **A:** **NSDL** and **CDSL** — they hold securities in **electronic (dematerialised)** form.\n\n**Q9.** What is a Depository Participant?\n> **A:** The **agent of the depository** — the investor's point of contact for opening and operating a **demat account**.\n\n**Q10.** State the key differences between a bank and an NBFC.\n> **A:** Banks can accept **demand deposits** and are part of the **payment and settlement system**; NBFCs **cannot** and are **not**. NBFCs also cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves, and their deposits are **not DICGC-insured**.\n\n**Q11.** What is a payment bank allowed and not allowed to do?\n> **A:** It may accept deposits **up to ₹2 lakh per customer** and provide payments and remittances. It **cannot lend** or issue credit cards.\n\n**Q12.** What is a small finance bank?\n> **A:** A full-service bank with a **priority-sector focus** — small borrowers, micro-enterprises and unbanked segments.\n\n**Q13.** Who now regulates Housing Finance Companies?\n> **A:** The **RBI** (supervision moved across from the National Housing Bank).\n\n**Q14.** What is an NBFC-P2P, and what are its key restrictions?\n> **A:** An RBI-registered platform that **matches individual lenders with borrowers**. It is only an intermediary — it **cannot lend from its own book and cannot guarantee returns**.\n\n**Q15.** What is an Account Aggregator, and what does \"data blind\" mean?\n> **A:** An RBI-regulated **NBFC-AA** that, with the customer's **explicit consent**, retrieves and shares their financial data across institutions. **Data blind** means the AA itself **cannot read or store** the data it moves.\n\n**Q16.** Which Act defines \"securities\", and what does the definition include?\n> **A:** The **Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 (SCRA)** — covering shares, scrips, stocks, bonds, debentures, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes, security receipts and government securities.\n\n**Q17.** What is a KYC Registration Agency (KRA)?\n> **A:** It maintains **centralised KYC records** so a client's KYC is **portable across SEBI-registered intermediaries** — no need to repeat KYC with each one.\n\n**Q18.** What does a Registrar & Transfer Agent do?\n> **A:** Maintains **investor records** and processes transactions and corporate actions on behalf of issuers and mutual funds — e.g. **CAMS** and **KFin**.\n\n**Q19.** What does a custodian do?\n> **A:** **Safe-keeps securities** and handles settlement and corporate actions, mainly for **institutional investors**.\n\n**Q20.** What is an Authorised Person?\n> **A:** A person acting **on behalf of a stock broker** to reach clients — the role that replaced the former \"sub-broker\" category.\n\n**Q21.** What is a Self-Regulatory Organisation, and give examples.\n> **A:** An industry body empowered to set and enforce standards for its **members**, under the apex regulator's oversight. Examples: **AMFI** (mutual fund distributors); **stock exchanges** as first-level regulators of their brokers.\n\n**Q22.** Why do SROs exist?\n> **A:** Closer **industry knowledge**, **faster rule-making**, and **lower regulatory cost** than doing everything through the apex regulator.\n\n**Q23.** What is the core function of any financial market?\n> **A:** To move funds efficiently from **surplus units (savers)** to **deficit units (borrowers/users)** through intermediaries.\n\n**Q24.** Name the features of a well-functioning financial market.\n> **A:** Efficient **transfer of funds**, **price discovery**, **liquidity**, **information dissemination**, **regulation**, and **risk management**.\n\n**Q25.** Why do institutional investors improve market quality?\n> **A:** They are **large and informed**, so they supply **liquidity and stability** and improve **price efficiency** — they can source and evaluate information better than retail investors.\n\n**Q26.** Which markets sit under the RBI besides banking?\n> **A:** The **money market** and the **foreign exchange market**, plus payment systems, NBFCs and credit information companies.\n\n**Q27.** Name the credit rating agencies operating in India.\n> **A:** **CRISIL, ICRA, CARE and India Ratings** — they assess the creditworthiness of debt issues and are **SEBI-regulated**.\n\n**Q28.** What sits under PFRDA?\n> **A:** The **National Pension System (NPS)**, **Atal Pension Yojana**, pension fund managers, the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**, points of presence, and the NPS Trust.\n\n**Q29.** What is the minimum investment for a Portfolio Management Service?\n> **A:** **₹50 lakh**.\n\n**Q30.** What does \"financialisation of savings\" mean in the Indian context?\n> **A:** The gradual shift of household savings **away from physical assets** (gold, real estate) **toward financial assets** — increasingly via mutual funds and SIPs.\n\n**Q31.** Which money market instruments should you be able to name?\n> **A:** **Treasury bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit, call money and repo** — all with maturities of **up to one year**.\n\n**Q32.** How much of the exam is Module 2 worth, and what does that imply?\n> **A:** Only **10 marks** across Chapters 5–6. Read it once, drill the regulator map and MII roles, and redirect the time to Module 1 (37 marks) or Module 6 (30 marks).\n", "questions": "# Chapter 5: Introduction to Indian Financial Markets — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Scenario & Discrimination). **Module 2 is worth only 10 marks in total**, and these questions are almost entirely recall — the aim is to make the regulator map and institutional roles automatic. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The money market deals with instruments of maturity:\nA) Above 1 year  B) Up to 1 year  C) Above 5 years  D) Exactly 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The money market is the **short-term** segment: maturities of **up to one year**.\nA) and C) describe the capital/securities market. D) No single maturity defines a market segment.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The money market in India is regulated by:\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **RBI** regulates the money market, alongside banking, currency, forex and payment systems.\nA) SEBI regulates the securities (capital) market. B) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) PFRDA regulates pensions.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which regulator supervises mutual funds in India?\nA) RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are **securities market** products and fall under **SEBI**.\nA) RBI regulates banks and NBFCs. C) IRDAI regulates insurance. D) **AMFI is an SRO**, not the regulator — it operates under SEBI's oversight.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** SEBI was given statutory status in:\nA) 1988  B) 1992  C) 1999  D) 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI Act, 1992** gave SEBI statutory powers. (It existed as a non-statutory body from 1988.)\nA) 1988 is when SEBI was first constituted, without statutory backing. C) 1999 is IRDAI. D) 2013 is PFRDA.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** PFRDA received statutory status under an Act of:\nA) 1992  B) 1999  C) 2013  D) 1956\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **PFRDA Act, 2013**.\nA) Is SEBI. B) Is IRDA. D) Is the SCRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Which of these is NOT a Market Infrastructure Institution?\nA) Stock exchange  B) Clearing corporation  C) Depository  D) Credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The three **MIIs** are **exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories**. A credit rating agency is a SEBI-registered intermediary but not an MII.\nA), B) and C) are the three MIIs — systemically important and the most heavily regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The two depositories in India are:\nA) NSE and BSE  B) NSDL and CDSL  C) CAMS and KFin  D) CRISIL and ICRA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NSDL** and **CDSL** hold securities in dematerialised form.\nA) NSE and BSE are **exchanges**. C) CAMS and KFin are **RTAs**. D) CRISIL and ICRA are **credit rating agencies**.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A clearing corporation acts as:\nA) The trading platform  B) The central counterparty guaranteeing settlement  C) The company registrar  D) The investor's broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As **CCP**, it steps between buyer and seller so neither bears counterparty default risk.\nA) That is the exchange's role. C) That is the RoC. D) Brokers execute trades for clients.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Which of the following can a payment bank NOT do?\nA) Accept deposits up to ₹2 lakh  B) Provide remittance services  C) Lend money  D) Issue debit cards\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Payment banks are **prohibited from lending** and from issuing credit cards.\nA), B) and D) are all permitted activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The deposit cap per customer for a payment bank is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹2 lakh** per individual customer.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Which is a key difference between a bank and an NBFC?\nA) NBFCs can accept demand deposits  B) Banks can accept demand deposits and are part of the payment system; NBFCs cannot and are not  C) NBFCs cannot lend  D) Banks cannot lend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the defining distinction.\nA) Reverses it — NBFCs specifically **cannot** accept demand deposits. C) Lending is an NBFC's core business. D) Lending is a core banking function.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** \"Securities\" are defined under which Act?\nA) Companies Act, 2013  B) SEBI Act, 1992  C) Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956  D) FEMA, 1999\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **SCRA, 1956** contains the statutory definition of securities.\nA) Governs companies. B) Establishes SEBI and its powers. D) Governs foreign exchange.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The Registrar of Companies operates under:\nA) SEBI  B) The Ministry of Corporate Affairs  C) The RBI  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **RoC** functions under the **MCA**, which administers the Companies Act, 2013.\nA), C) and D) have no authority over company incorporation.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** In the primary market:\nA) Investors trade among themselves  B) Securities are issued and funds go to the issuer  C) Only government securities are traded  D) No new capital is raised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **issuer receives the money** in the primary market.\nA) Describes the secondary market. C) All types of issuers use the primary market. D) Raising capital is precisely its purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The main function of the secondary market is to:\nA) Raise fresh capital for issuers  B) Provide liquidity and an exit route to investors  C) Register companies  D) Set interest rates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Secondary trading gives investors the ability to **exit and re-allocate**, which in turn makes primary issuance possible.\nA) That is the primary market. C) That is the RoC. D) The RBI sets policy rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** IRDAI was established in:\nA) 1992  B) 1999  C) 2013  D) 1956\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the **IRDA Act, 1999**.\nA) Is SEBI. C) Is PFRDA. D) Is the SCRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An Account Aggregator is regulated by:\nA) SEBI  B) The RBI, as an NBFC-AA  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Account Aggregators are **RBI-regulated NBFC-AAs**.\nA), C) and D) have no jurisdiction over account aggregation.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** \"Data blind\" in the context of Account Aggregators means:\nA) The AA can read all customer data  B) The AA cannot read or store the data it transfers  C) Customers cannot see their data  D) The data is deleted immediately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The AA is a **consent-driven pipe**: it moves data but cannot read or retain it.\nA) Reverses the design. C) The customer controls and can see their data. D) The AA does not hold the data to begin with.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** An NBFC-P2P platform:\nA) Lends from its own balance sheet  B) Matches individual lenders with borrowers and cannot guarantee returns  C) Is regulated by SEBI  D) Can accept demand deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is a **pure intermediary**, RBI-registered, and may neither lend on its own book nor guarantee returns.\nA) Explicitly prohibited. C) It is **RBI**-regulated. D) No NBFC can accept demand deposits.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Depository Participant is:\nA) The depository itself  B) An agent of the depository and the investor's access point  C) A stock exchange  D) A credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **DP** is the depository's agent, through which an investor opens and operates a demat account.\nA) The DP acts *for* the depository. C) and D) are different institutions entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** AMFI is an example of:\nA) An apex regulator  B) A self-regulatory organisation  C) A depository  D) A clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AMFI is an **SRO** for mutual fund distributors, operating under SEBI's oversight.\nA) SEBI is the apex regulator here. C) and D) are MIIs.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which body regulates the foreign exchange market in India?\nA) SEBI  B) RBI  C) IRDAI  D) MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **RBI** administers **FEMA** and regulates the forex market.\nA), C) and D) have no forex jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Housing Finance Companies are now regulated by:\nA) The National Housing Bank alone  B) The RBI  C) SEBI  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Supervision of HFCs moved to the **RBI**.\nA) The NHB retains a role but the regulator is the RBI. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A KYC Registration Agency exists to:\nA) Rate debt securities  B) Maintain centralised KYC records portable across SEBI intermediaries  C) Settle trades  D) Register companies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **KRA** removes the need to repeat KYC with every intermediary.\nA) Is a credit rating agency. C) Is a clearing corporation. D) Is the RoC.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** CAMS and KFin are examples of:\nA) Depositories  B) Registrar and Transfer Agents  C) Stock exchanges  D) Custodians\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RTAs** maintain investor records and process transactions for mutual funds and issuers.\nA) Are NSDL and CDSL. C) Are NSE and BSE. D) Custodians serve institutional investors.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of these is a money market instrument?\nA) Equity share  B) Treasury bill  C) 10-year government bond  D) Debenture with 7-year maturity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **T-bills** mature within a year (91, 182 or 364 days).\nA) Equity has no maturity. C) and D) are long-term, hence capital market instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An Authorised Person in the securities market:\nA) Is the same as a depository  B) Acts on behalf of a stock broker to reach clients  C) Regulates the exchange  D) Rates bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Authorised Person category replaced the earlier \"sub-broker\".\nA), C) and D) describe entirely different entities.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** The minimum investment in a Portfolio Management Service is:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹50 lakh** is the SEBI-prescribed minimum.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which of these is regulated by PFRDA?\nA) Mutual funds  B) The National Pension System  C) Health insurance  D) Commercial banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NPS** and **Atal Pension Yojana** fall under PFRDA.\nA) SEBI. C) IRDAI. D) RBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Credit rating agencies in India are regulated by:\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CRAs are **SEBI-registered intermediaries**.\nA) The RBI regulates CICs (credit bureaus), which are different from CRAs. C) and D) have no jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The role of a custodian is to:\nA) Match trades on the exchange  B) Safe-keep securities and handle settlement for institutional investors  C) Issue credit ratings  D) Advise retail investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Custodians provide safe-keeping and post-trade servicing, mainly for institutions.\nA) Is the exchange. C) Is a CRA. D) Is an investment adviser.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which statement about a Self-Regulatory Organisation is correct?\nA) It replaces the apex regulator  B) It sets and enforces standards for its members, under the apex regulator's oversight  C) It has no members  D) It regulates all financial sectors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An SRO regulates **its own members**, subject to the statutory regulator.\nA) SROs operate under, not instead of, the regulator. C) Membership is its basis. D) Each SRO covers only its own segment.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Small finance banks are characterised by:\nA) An inability to accept deposits  B) A priority-sector focus on small borrowers and unbanked segments  C) A ban on lending  D) Regulation by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They provide full banking services with a mandate toward **small borrowers, micro-enterprises and the unbanked**.\nA) They accept deposits normally. C) Lending is their purpose. D) They are RBI-regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The Ministry of Finance is primarily responsible for:\nA) Registering companies  B) Overall economic and fiscal policy, and the Budget  C) Clearing trades  D) Rating debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The MoF sets fiscal and economic policy and supervises the regulators.\nA) Is the RoC/MCA. C) Is the clearing corporation. D) Is a CRA.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Why are institutional investors important to market quality?\nA) They reduce liquidity  B) They provide liquidity and stability and improve price efficiency  C) They are less informed than retail investors  D) They are prohibited from large trades\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Being large and well-informed, they add depth and help prices reflect information.\nA) They add liquidity. C) They are generally better resourced for research. D) There is no such prohibition.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A company wants to raise ₹500 crore by issuing fresh equity to the public. Which market and which regulator apply?\nA) Money market; RBI  B) Primary capital market; SEBI  C) Secondary market; SEBI  D) Forex market; RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **fresh issue of equity** is a **primary market** transaction in the **capital market**, regulated by **SEBI**.\nA) The money market handles short-term debt. C) The secondary market involves no fresh capital. D) Forex is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor sells 100 shares of a listed company on the NSE. Which statements are correct?\nA) The company receives the sale proceeds  B) This is a secondary market trade; the company receives nothing  C) This is a primary issue  D) SEBI receives the proceeds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trading between investors is a **secondary market** transaction — the issuer is not a party and receives nothing.\nA) The issuer receives money only in the **primary** market. C) A primary issue involves new securities from the issuer. D) SEBI is the regulator, not a counterparty.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A client asks who would handle a complaint about a mis-sold ULIP. The correct regulator is:\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) RBI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance product**, so **IRDAI** is the regulator despite its investment component.\nA) SEBI covers mutual funds and securities. C) RBI covers banking. D) PFRDA covers pensions. **This is a favourite trap: ULIPs feel like investments but are regulated as insurance.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A trade is executed on the exchange but the buyer defaults on payment. Who protects the seller?\nA) The stock broker personally  B) The clearing corporation, acting as central counterparty  C) SEBI directly  D) The depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As **CCP**, the clearing corporation guarantees settlement using margins and the settlement guarantee fund.\nA) Brokers have obligations but the systemic guarantee sits with the CCP. C) SEBI regulates but does not settle trades. D) The depository transfers securities; it does not guarantee settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An investor completes KYC with one mutual fund and then invests with a second. Under the KRA framework:\nA) Full KYC must be repeated  B) The KYC record is available through the KRA, so it need not be repeated  C) KYC is not required at all  D) Only the RBI can verify KYC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **KYC is portable** across SEBI intermediaries through the **KRA**.\nA) That is precisely what the KRA system avoids. C) KYC is mandatory. D) KRAs are SEBI-registered.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A fintech app wants to pull a customer's bank, mutual fund and insurance data into one dashboard, with the customer's permission. The correct regulated channel is:\nA) A credit information company  B) An Account Aggregator (NBFC-AA)  C) A depository participant  D) A clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is exactly the **Account Aggregator** use case: **consent-based** retrieval and sharing of financial data.\nA) CICs hold credit histories only. C) DPs handle demat accounts. D) CCPs settle trades.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Which entity would a company approach to have its ₹200 crore debenture issue assessed for credit quality?\nA) A credit information company  B) A credit rating agency  C) A depository  D) An Account Aggregator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CRAs** (CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings) rate **debt instruments**.\nA) CICs score **individuals**, not corporate debt issues. C) and D) have unrelated functions. **Note the CRA/CIC distinction: CRA rates issues (SEBI); CIC scores people (RBI).**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A retail investor holds shares in electronic form. Which institution ultimately holds them?\nA) The stock exchange  B) A depository (NSDL or CDSL), accessed through a DP  C) The clearing corporation  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Securities sit in the **depository**, and the investor accesses them via a **Depository Participant**.\nA) Exchanges match trades. C) CCPs settle them. D) SEBI regulates.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which of the following is NOT permitted for an NBFC?\nA) Lending to retail customers  B) Accepting demand deposits  C) Investing in securities  D) Providing asset finance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NBFCs **cannot accept demand deposits** (some categories may accept term deposits, subject to conditions).\nA), C) and D) are all core NBFC activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client wants to know why their bank deposit is safer than an NBFC deposit. The best answer is:\nA) NBFCs charge higher interest  B) Bank deposits carry DICGC insurance cover; NBFC deposits do not  C) NBFCs are unregulated  D) Banks never fail\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **DICGC deposit insurance** applies to bank deposits, not NBFC deposits.\nA) True of loans, but not the safety reason for deposits. C) NBFCs are RBI-regulated. D) Banks can and do fail — which is why deposit insurance exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A borrower complains about a housing finance company's practices. Which regulator has jurisdiction?\nA) SEBI  B) The RBI  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — HFC supervision sits with the **RBI**.\nA), C) and D) have no jurisdiction over housing finance companies.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which pairing of regulator and entity is INCORRECT?\nA) SEBI — portfolio managers  B) RBI — payment banks  C) IRDAI — Alternative Investment Funds  D) PFRDA — pension fund managers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **AIFs are regulated by SEBI**, not IRDAI.\nA), B) and D) are all correct pairings.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An individual wants their credit score before applying for a home loan. They should approach:\nA) A credit rating agency such as CRISIL  B) A credit information company such as CIBIL  C) A depository  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CICs** (CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark) maintain individual credit histories and scores.\nA) CRAs rate **debt issues**, not individuals. C) and D) have no role. **They are entitled to one free full report per calendar year from each CIC.**\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which activity clearly belongs to the primary market?\nA) Buying shares on the NSE from another investor  B) Applying to an IPO  C) Selling shares through a broker  D) Transferring shares between demat accounts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Applying to an **IPO** means subscribing to newly issued securities from the issuer.\nA), C) and D) are all secondary market or post-trade activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A stock exchange takes disciplinary action against a member broker. This is an example of:\nA) The exchange acting as a first-level regulator / SRO  B) SEBI's direct enforcement  C) A clearing corporation function  D) An illegal act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Exchanges act as **first-level regulators** of their members, under SEBI's oversight.\nB) SEBI can act directly, but this describes exchange-level action. C) Clearing corporations handle settlement risk. D) It is a legitimate SRO function.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of these best explains why the secondary market matters to a company raising capital?\nA) The company earns from secondary trades  B) Investors will only subscribe to primary issues if they can exit later  C) It sets the company's tax rate  D) It replaces the need for a primary issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Liquidity in the secondary market is what makes primary subscription attractive.** Without an exit route, investors would demand a much higher return.\nA) The company receives nothing from secondary trades. C) Tax is unrelated. D) Fresh capital can only come from a primary issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An investor holds units of an equity mutual fund and complains about the AMC. The escalation path leads to:\nA) IRDAI  B) SEBI (via SCORES)  C) The RBI Ombudsman  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are SEBI-regulated, so complaints escalate through **SEBI's SCORES** platform.\nA) Insurance. C) Banking. D) Pensions. **The regulator follows the product, not the distributor.**\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Which statement about commercial paper is correct?\nA) It is a long-term equity instrument  B) It is a short-term money market instrument issued by corporates  C) It is issued only by the government  D) It is regulated by IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CP** is unsecured short-term corporate borrowing in the **money market**.\nA) It is debt, not equity, and short-term. C) Government short-term paper is the **T-bill**. D) The money market is RBI's domain.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which combination correctly describes the flow in a financial market?\nA) Deficit units → intermediaries → surplus units  B) Surplus units (savers) → intermediaries → deficit units (borrowers)  C) Regulators → banks → savers  D) Borrowers → regulators → savers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Funds move **from savers, through intermediaries, to users of funds**.\nA) Reverses the direction. C) and D) misplace regulators in the funding chain — they supervise, they do not intermediate funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A P2P platform promises lenders \"assured 14% returns\". This is:\nA) Permitted if disclosed  B) Not permitted — NBFC-P2P platforms cannot guarantee returns  C) Required by the RBI  D) Permitted for amounts under ₹50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An NBFC-P2P is a **matching intermediary only**; guaranteeing returns is expressly prohibited, as is lending from its own book.\nA) Disclosure does not cure a prohibited practice. C) The RBI prohibits precisely this. D) There is no such amount-based exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which entity would maintain the unit-holder records of a mutual fund scheme?\nA) A depository  B) A Registrar and Transfer Agent  C) A clearing corporation  D) A credit rating agency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RTAs** such as CAMS and KFin maintain investor folios and process transactions.\nA) Depositories hold demat securities. C) CCPs settle exchange trades. D) CRAs rate debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** An investor asks why India needs both NSDL and CDSL. The best answer is:\nA) One handles equity and one handles debt  B) Competition and redundancy — both are depositories offering the same core service  C) One is regulated by SEBI, the other by the RBI  D) One is for retail, one for institutions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are **SEBI-regulated depositories** performing the same function; having two provides **competition and systemic redundancy**.\nA) Both handle all security types. C) Both are SEBI-regulated. D) Both serve all investor categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which of these is a capital market instrument?\nA) Certificate of deposit  B) Call money  C) A 10-year corporate debenture  D) A 91-day treasury bill\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Maturity **beyond one year** places it in the capital market.\nA), B) and D) are all short-term money market instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A client is confused between a credit rating agency and a credit information company. The correct distinction is:\nA) They are the same  B) A CRA rates debt issues (SEBI-regulated); a CIC maintains individual credit histories (RBI-regulated)  C) A CRA rates individuals  D) A CIC rates corporate bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CRA → issues → SEBI. CIC → individuals → RBI.**\nA) They are distinct in function and regulator. C) and D) swap their roles.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which best describes the \"financialisation of savings\" in India?\nA) A shift from financial assets to gold and property  B) A shift from physical assets toward financial assets such as mutual funds  C) A fall in the savings rate  D) An increase in cash hoarding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Household savings are moving **away from gold and real estate toward financial assets**, notably via mutual funds and SIPs.\nA) Reverses the trend. C) The savings rate is a separate matter. D) The opposite of financialisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A client asks whether a mutual fund distributor is regulated by AMFI or SEBI. The correct answer is:\nA) AMFI alone, as the apex regulator  B) SEBI is the statutory regulator; AMFI is the SRO that registers distributors and sets standards  C) The RBI  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI is the statutory regulator**; **AMFI** operates as the industry SRO (issuing ARN registration and a code of conduct).\nA) AMFI is not an apex regulator. C) The RBI has no role. D) Distributors are certainly regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which risk does the central counterparty mechanism primarily eliminate for market participants?\nA) Market risk  B) Counterparty (settlement) risk  C) Inflation risk  D) Liquidity risk in the underlying stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — By novating trades, the CCP removes the risk that **the other side fails to deliver or pay**.\nA) Price movements are unaffected. C) Inflation is macroeconomic. D) A stock's own liquidity is not guaranteed by the CCP.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A payment bank customer wants a home loan from the same institution. The adviser should explain:\nA) Payment banks offer the cheapest home loans  B) Payment banks cannot lend, so the customer must approach a bank, HFC or NBFC  C) Payment banks lend only above ₹50 lakh  D) SEBI must approve the loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lending is **outside a payment bank's permitted activities**.\nA) They offer no loans at all. C) There is no such threshold. D) SEBI has no role in lending.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which regulator would handle a grievance about the National Pension System?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) PFRDA  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PFRDA** regulates the NPS and its intermediaries.\nA) Securities. B) Insurance. D) Companies.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of these features is NOT a characteristic of a well-functioning financial market?\nA) Price discovery  B) Liquidity  C) Restricted information available only to insiders  D) Effective regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Well-functioning markets require **wide information dissemination**; restricting information to insiders is the definition of a market failure (and, in the case of unpublished price-sensitive information, insider trading).\nA), B) and D) are all essential characteristics.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A corporate treasurer needs to park ₹50 crore for 60 days. The appropriate market is:\nA) The equity market  B) The money market  C) The derivatives market  D) The real estate market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **60-day** horizon calls for money market instruments (T-bills, CDs, CPs, liquid funds).\nA) Equity is far too volatile for 60 days. C) Derivatives are for hedging or speculation, not parking cash. D) Property is entirely illiquid over that horizon.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which statement about the RBI's role is INCORRECT?\nA) It regulates the money market  B) It administers FEMA  C) It regulates mutual funds  D) It oversees payment systems\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Mutual funds are SEBI's domain**, not the RBI's.\nA), B) and D) are all correct RBI responsibilities.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An investor's securities are transferred from their demat account after a sale. Which institutions were involved?\nA) Only the exchange  B) The exchange (matching), the clearing corporation (settlement) and the depository via the DP (securities transfer)  C) Only SEBI  D) Only the RTA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three MIIs play distinct roles in a single trade's lifecycle.\nA) The exchange only matches. C) SEBI regulates but does not execute. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, not exchange settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which is the correct hierarchy for a mutual fund distributor's oversight?\nA) Distributor → AMFI (SRO) → SEBI (statutory regulator)  B) Distributor → RBI → SEBI  C) Distributor → IRDAI → SEBI  D) Distributor → SEBI → AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — First-level oversight sits with the **SRO (AMFI)**, and above it the statutory regulator **SEBI**.\nB) and C) insert the wrong regulators. D) Inverts the hierarchy — SEBI sits above AMFI, not below.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client holds a fixed deposit with an NBFC and asks about safety. The key points are:\nA) It is fully guaranteed by the RBI  B) It is not DICGC-insured, so credit rating and the NBFC's financial strength matter  C) It is regulated by SEBI  D) NBFCs cannot accept any deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **No deposit insurance** applies, so the NBFC's own creditworthiness is the protection.\nA) The RBI regulates but does not guarantee. C) NBFCs are RBI-regulated. D) Some NBFC categories may accept **term** (not demand) deposits.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario & Discrimination (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client is sold a product described as \"a market-linked investment with life cover\". Which regulator governs it, and why does this matter for grievance redress?\nA) SEBI, because it is market-linked  B) IRDAI, because it is a ULIP — an insurance product — so grievances go to the insurer then the Insurance Ombudsman  C) The RBI, because a bank sold it  D) PFRDA, because it is long-term\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance contract** regardless of its investment component, so **IRDAI** applies and the escalation path is insurer → **Insurance Ombudsman**, not SCORES.\nA) Market linkage does not move it to SEBI. C) The **distribution channel does not determine the regulator** — a bank selling a ULIP is acting as a corporate agent. D) Duration is irrelevant to jurisdiction. **The principle: the regulator follows the product, not the seller.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A trade is executed on the NSE on Monday and settles on Tuesday (T+1). Match the institutions to their roles.\nA) NSE settles; NSDL matches; the DP guarantees  B) NSE matches; the clearing corporation clears, guarantees and settles; NSDL/CDSL transfers securities via the DP  C) SEBI matches and settles  D) The RTA does everything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each MII has a distinct function: **exchange = matching; clearing corporation = clearing, guaranteeing and settling; depository = holding and transferring securities** (accessed through the DP).\nA) Scrambles all three roles. C) SEBI is the regulator, not an operator. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, not exchange settlement.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A fintech firm proposes to (i) aggregate customer financial data with consent, (ii) lend from its own book, and (iii) match retail lenders with borrowers. Which registrations would it need, and what problem arises?\nA) A single NBFC licence covers all three  B) NBFC-AA for (i) and NBFC-P2P for (iii) — but an NBFC-P2P cannot lend from its own book, so (ii) conflicts with (iii)  C) SEBI registration for all three  D) No registration is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NBFC-AA** covers consent-based data aggregation; **NBFC-P2P** covers lender–borrower matching. The conflict: an **NBFC-P2P is barred from lending on its own balance sheet**, so activities (ii) and (iii) cannot sit in the same entity.\nA) These are distinct licence categories with distinct conditions. C) All three are **RBI** matters, not SEBI. D) All three are regulated activities.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Which of the following correctly distinguishes a CRA from a CIC on all three dimensions — what they assess, who they assess, and their regulator?\nA) CRA: debt issues, corporates, SEBI · CIC: credit history, individuals, RBI  B) CRA: individuals, retail, RBI · CIC: bonds, corporates, SEBI  C) Both assess individuals and are RBI-regulated  D) Both assess debt issues and are SEBI-regulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **CRA** → rates **debt instruments** issued by **corporates/governments** → **SEBI**. **CIC** → maintains **credit histories/scores** of **individuals (and firms)** → **RBI**.\nB) Swaps them entirely. C) and D) collapse two distinct institutions into one.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An investor argues that because their broker is SEBI-registered, their money is guaranteed against loss. The correct response is:\nA) Correct — SEBI guarantees investments  B) Registration ensures regulatory oversight and conduct standards, but market risk remains entirely with the investor  C) Only losses above ₹1 lakh are covered  D) The clearing corporation reimburses market losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation addresses **conduct and systemic integrity**, not investment outcomes. **No regulator guarantees returns or protects against market losses.**\nA) SEBI explicitly does not guarantee investments. C) There is no such market-loss cover (investor protection funds address broker *default*, not market losses). D) The CCP guarantees **settlement**, not price outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Which sequence correctly traces the escalation of a complaint about a stock broker?\nA) SEBI SCORES → the broker → SAT  B) The broker → the stock exchange → SEBI SCORES → the Securities Appellate Tribunal  C) The RBI Ombudsman → SEBI  D) The Insurance Ombudsman → SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Always **start with the intermediary**, then its first-level regulator (the **exchange**, acting as SRO), then **SEBI via SCORES**, and finally the **SAT**.\nA) Inverts the first two steps — you must approach the intermediary first. C) and D) route a securities complaint through banking and insurance channels.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A corporate wants to raise short-term funds for 90 days and long-term funds for 10 years. Which markets, instruments and regulators apply?\nA) Both from the money market under the RBI  B) 90 days: money market (commercial paper), RBI; 10 years: capital market (debentures/bonds), SEBI  C) Both from the capital market under SEBI  D) Both from the forex market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **maturity determines the market**: ≤1 year is the money market (RBI); >1 year is the capital market (SEBI).\nA) A 10-year borrowing cannot be a money market instrument. C) A 90-day borrowing is not a capital market instrument. D) Forex concerns currency, not tenor.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client holds: a bank FD, an NBFC FD, an equity mutual fund, a ULIP and an NPS account. How many different apex regulators are involved?\nA) One  B) Two  C) Three  D) Four\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **RBI** (bank FD and NBFC FD), **SEBI** (equity mutual fund), **IRDAI** (ULIP) and **PFRDA** (NPS) — **four** regulators.\nA), B) and C) undercount. **This is exactly why an investment adviser must know the regulator map — each product has its own grievance route.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Which statement about SROs is MOST accurate?\nA) An SRO's rules override the statutory regulator's  B) An SRO regulates its own members with closer industry knowledge and lower regulatory cost, but remains subject to the apex regulator  C) SROs are unregulated  D) Every financial sector has exactly one SRO\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SROs add **proximity and efficiency**, always **subordinate** to the statutory regulator.\nA) Statutory rules always prevail. C) SROs operate under regulatory recognition and oversight. D) The number varies by segment, and some segments have none.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An investor's broker collapses and client securities are missing. Which protection applies?\nA) DICGC deposit insurance  B) The exchange's Investor Protection Fund, subject to limits — this covers broker default, not market losses  C) SEBI reimburses the full amount  D) The depository compensates all losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exchanges maintain **Investor Protection Funds** for claims arising from a **defaulting member**, subject to prescribed limits.\nA) DICGC covers **bank deposits** only. C) SEBI does not directly compensate investors. D) The depository holds securities but is not a compensation fund. **Note the distinction: default protection exists; market-loss protection does not.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Which of these entities is systemically the MOST heavily regulated, and why?\nA) A mutual fund distributor, because it faces retail investors  B) The Market Infrastructure Institutions, because their failure would disrupt the entire market  C) A credit rating agency, because it rates debt  D) An investment adviser, because it gives advice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories** are **systemically important**: their failure would halt trading, settlement or custody market-wide. Hence the heaviest capital, governance and operational requirements.\nA), C) and D) are all regulated, but their individual failure does not threaten market-wide functioning.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A payment bank wants to expand. Which of these is it permitted to do?\nA) Issue credit cards  B) Offer term loans  C) Distribute mutual funds and insurance as a third-party product  D) Accept ₹10 lakh deposits from a single customer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Payment banks may act as **distributors of third-party products** such as mutual funds and insurance.\nA) Credit cards are prohibited. B) Lending is prohibited. D) The per-customer deposit cap is **₹2 lakh**.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which correctly explains why the primary and secondary markets are interdependent?\nA) They are entirely separate  B) Secondary market liquidity and price discovery determine what investors will pay in a primary issue — and whether they subscribe at all  C) The primary market sets secondary prices permanently  D) Only the secondary market raises capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors subscribe to new issues only if they believe they can **exit** later at a fair price; secondary market conditions therefore drive primary issuance volumes and pricing.\nA) They are tightly linked. C) Primary pricing is a starting point, immediately re-priced by the secondary market. D) Only the **primary** market raises capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client says: \"I'll keep my emergency fund with an NBFC because it pays 2% more than the bank.\" What are the two key points to raise?\nA) NBFCs are unregulated and illegal  B) NBFC deposits are not DICGC-insured, and the higher rate compensates for higher credit risk — both matter for money that must be certain when needed  C) NBFCs pay lower rates  D) There is no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two points: **no deposit insurance**, and the extra yield is **compensation for credit risk**. An emergency fund's defining requirement is **certainty of value**, which is precisely what credit risk undermines.\nA) NBFCs are RBI-regulated and legal. C) NBFCs typically pay **more**, which is the client's point. D) The difference in safety is material.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Which combination of institutions would be involved in a retail investor buying an equity mutual fund through a distributor?\nA) Exchange, clearing corporation, depository only  B) The AMC, the RTA (records), the KRA (KYC), SEBI (regulator), and AMFI (distributor SRO)  C) The RBI and IRDAI  D) Only the AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct mutual fund purchases do not route through the exchange; instead the **AMC** issues units, the **RTA** maintains folios, the **KRA** holds the KYC record, **SEBI** regulates, and **AMFI** oversees the distributor.\nA) Those are involved when units are bought on an exchange platform, but not in a standard AMC transaction. C) Wrong regulators entirely. D) The AMC alone is insufficient.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** An adviser must explain the difference between the RBI's regulation of a *bank's* mutual fund distribution and SEBI's regulation of the *product*. The correct framing is:\nA) The RBI regulates the mutual fund  B) SEBI regulates the mutual fund product and the distribution conduct; the RBI regulates the bank as a banking entity  C) Neither regulates it  D) The bank regulates itself\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Entity regulation and product regulation are different.** The RBI supervises the bank *as a bank*; SEBI governs the **mutual fund product** and the conduct of its distribution.\nA) The RBI has no jurisdiction over mutual fund schemes. C) Both apply, in their own spheres. D) Self-regulation is not the framework here.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which is the strongest argument that a clearing corporation reduces systemic risk?\nA) It increases trading volume  B) By novating every trade it converts a web of bilateral exposures into exposures against one well-capitalised, margined counterparty  C) It sets share prices  D) It guarantees profits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Novation** replaces many-to-many counterparty risk with a single, heavily capitalised CCP backed by **margins and a settlement guarantee fund**, so one participant's failure does not cascade.\nA) Volume is an effect, not the risk argument. C) Prices are set by the market. D) No entity guarantees profits.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client asks why KYC needs to be done at all. The best regulatory rationale is:\nA) To increase paperwork  B) To establish investor identity, prevent money laundering (PMLA) and enable suitability assessment  C) To calculate returns  D) To set the interest rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — KYC serves **identity verification**, **anti-money-laundering** compliance under the **PMLA**, and provides the client information needed for **suitability**.\nA) The purpose is substantive, not procedural. C) and D) are unrelated to KYC.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which correctly ranks these by regulatory scope, broadest first, in the securities market?\nA) AMFI → SEBI → the stock exchange  B) SEBI (statutory apex) → the stock exchange (first-level/SRO) → the broker (member)  C) The broker → SEBI → AMFI  D) The exchange → SEBI → the broker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The hierarchy runs **statutory regulator → SRO/first-level regulator → regulated member**.\nA) Places the SRO above the statutory regulator. C) and D) invert the top of the hierarchy.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investor wants exposure to a professionally managed portfolio with a ₹60 lakh corpus, seeking individually held securities rather than pooled units. Which vehicle and regulator?\nA) A mutual fund, SEBI  B) A Portfolio Management Service (minimum ₹50 lakh), SEBI  C) An NPS account, PFRDA  D) A ULIP, IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PMS** holds securities in the **client's own name/demat account** rather than issuing pooled units, and the SEBI minimum is **₹50 lakh** — which ₹60 lakh satisfies.\nA) A mutual fund gives **pooled units**, not individually held securities. C) NPS is a retirement product with a prescribed structure. D) A ULIP is insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which statement about the definition of \"securities\" is correct?\nA) It is defined in the Companies Act, 2013  B) It comes from the SCRA, 1956 and includes shares, bonds, debentures, derivatives, units of collective investment schemes and government securities  C) It excludes derivatives  D) It covers only equity shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SCRA, 1956** provides an inclusive definition covering all these instrument types.\nA) The Companies Act governs companies, not the securities definition. C) Derivatives are expressly included. D) The definition is far broader than equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Two clients each lose money: one from a falling equity fund NAV, the other because a broker misappropriated their shares. Which has a compensation route?\nA) Both  B) Only the second — broker misappropriation is a default matter with an Investor Protection Fund route; NAV decline is market risk borne by the investor  C) Only the first  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market losses are the investor's own risk**; **intermediary default or fraud** is a conduct failure with regulatory and IPF remedies.\nA) NAV decline has no compensation route. C) Reverses the answer. D) The second client does have a route. **This distinction — market risk versus conduct failure — is fundamental.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An adviser is asked whether SEBI can act against a company for false statements in its IPO prospectus. The answer is:\nA) No, only the MCA can act  B) Yes — SEBI regulates public issues and can act under the SEBI Act and the PFUTP Regulations; the MCA/RoC may also have jurisdiction under the Companies Act  C) No regulator has jurisdiction  D) Only the stock exchange can act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Overlapping jurisdiction** is normal: SEBI governs the **public issue and market conduct**, while the MCA/RoC governs **corporate law compliance**.\nA) SEBI clearly has jurisdiction over public issues. C) Multiple authorities have jurisdiction. D) Exchanges have limited listing-related powers only.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which of these is the best example of the RBI and SEBI's jurisdictions meeting?\nA) A bank distributing mutual funds — RBI regulates the bank, SEBI regulates the product and distribution conduct  B) SEBI regulating monetary policy  C) The RBI setting mutual fund expense ratios  D) SEBI issuing currency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This is the classic **entity vs product** overlap.\nB) Monetary policy is exclusively the RBI's. C) Expense ratios are set by **SEBI**. D) Currency issuance is exclusively the RBI's.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client wants to move ₹5 lakh from India to fund a child's education abroad. Which regulator's framework governs this, and under which Act?\nA) SEBI, under the SCRA  B) The RBI, under FEMA (via the Liberalised Remittance Scheme)  C) IRDAI, under the Insurance Act  D) The MCA, under the Companies Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cross-border remittances by residents fall under the **RBI's** administration of **FEMA**, operationalised through the **Liberalised Remittance Scheme**.\nA) The SCRA covers securities contracts. C) and D) are irrelevant to remittances.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which characterisation of the Indian financial system's evolution is most accurate?\nA) Household savings have shifted entirely into gold  B) There has been a gradual shift from physical assets toward financial assets, with rising retail participation through mutual funds and SIPs  C) Retail participation has collapsed  D) The savings rate has fallen to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **financialisation of savings** — a gradual, not complete, shift.\nA) Reverses the trend, and \"entirely\" overstates any shift. C) Retail participation has risen materially. D) India retains a high household savings rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A depository participant refuses to transfer an investor's securities. The escalation route is:\nA) Directly to the Supreme Court  B) The DP → the depository (NSDL/CDSL) → SEBI via SCORES → the SAT  C) The RBI Ombudsman  D) IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Start with the **intermediary (DP)**, escalate to its principal (**the depository**), then **SEBI/SCORES**, then the **SAT**.\nA) Courts are not the first step, and appeals from SEBI go to the SAT. C) Banking channel. D) Insurance channel.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Which best explains why the money market and capital market have different regulators in India?\nA) It is an arbitrary historical accident with no rationale  B) The money market is closely tied to banking liquidity and monetary policy transmission (RBI), while the capital market concerns securities issuance, trading and investor protection (SEBI)  C) The money market is unregulated  D) SEBI regulates both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short-term funding markets are integral to **monetary policy transmission and banking liquidity**, so they sit with the **central bank**; long-term securities markets centre on **issuance, trading and investor protection**, which is SEBI's mandate.\nA) There is a clear functional rationale. C) The RBI regulates it. D) SEBI does not regulate the money market.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An investment adviser is reviewing a client's holdings and must identify the correct grievance channel for each. Which mapping is entirely correct?\nA) Bank FD → SEBI; ULIP → SEBI; NPS → SEBI  B) Bank FD → RBI Ombudsman; equity MF → SEBI SCORES; ULIP → Insurance Ombudsman; NPS → PFRDA  C) All → SEBI SCORES  D) All → the Consumer Court only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each product routes to **its own regulator's** grievance machinery.\nA) Sends everything to SEBI regardless of product. C) SCORES covers **securities market** entities only. D) Consumer courts are an additional, not exclusive, forum. **Knowing the right channel is a core competence for an IA — see Chapter 20.**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single statement best captures the structure of Indian financial market regulation?\nA) One regulator supervises everything  B) Sector-specific apex regulators (RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA) supervise their domains, supported by SROs and MIIs, with the MoF/MCA setting policy and company law  C) The market is entirely self-regulated  D) Only the government regulates, with no independent bodies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — India uses a **sectoral regulatory model**: four apex regulators by domain, first-level regulation by SROs and MIIs, and policy/company law from the MoF and MCA.\nA) India has no single unified regulator. C) SROs supplement, but do not replace, statutory regulation. D) The regulators are statutory and operationally independent.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** These caselets test whether you can route a real client's products to the right regulator and institution.\n\n### Case: Advising the Bhatt Family's Portfolio\n\nNikhil Bhatt, 44, comes to you with the following holdings and concerns:\n\n1. A **₹12,00,000 fixed deposit** with a scheduled commercial bank.\n2. A **₹6,00,000 fixed deposit** with a well-known NBFC, paying 1.75% more than the bank.\n3. **₹22,00,000 in equity mutual funds**, bought through his bank's relationship manager.\n4. A **ULIP** bought five years ago, which he now believes was mis-sold as a \"guaranteed savings plan\".\n5. An **NPS Tier-I account** with ₹8,00,000.\n6. Shares worth **₹15,00,000** held in a demat account with a broker.\n\nHe also mentions that a **peer-to-peer lending platform** has offered him \"assured returns of 13%\", and asks whether he should move his emergency fund there.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** How many distinct apex regulators govern Nikhil's six holdings?\nA) Two  B) Three  C) Four  D) Six\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Four**: **RBI** (bank FD, NBFC FD), **SEBI** (equity mutual funds, demat shares), **IRDAI** (ULIP), **PFRDA** (NPS).\nA) and B) undercount. D) Six holdings do not mean six regulators — several share a regulator. *(Mapping products to regulators is the first step in advising on grievances and suitability.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Nikhil believes the ULIP was mis-sold. What is the correct escalation path?\nA) SEBI SCORES, because it is market-linked  B) The insurer's grievance cell → IRDAI's Bima Bharosa → the Insurance Ombudsman  C) The RBI Ombudsman, because a bank sold it  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **ULIP is an insurance contract**, so the route is the **insurer first**, then **IRDAI's grievance system**, then the **Insurance Ombudsman**.\nA) Market linkage does not make it a securities product. C) The **selling channel does not determine the regulator** — a bank selling insurance acts as a corporate agent. D) Pensions are unrelated. **Principle: the regulator follows the product, not the seller.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Nikhil asks whether his NBFC fixed deposit is as safe as his bank FD. What are the two key differences?\nA) They are identical in every respect  B) The bank FD is DICGC-insured (up to the prescribed limit) and the bank is part of the payment system; the NBFC FD has no deposit insurance, and the extra 1.75% compensates for higher credit risk  C) The NBFC FD is safer because it pays more  D) NBFCs cannot accept deposits at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two substantive differences: **deposit insurance** (DICGC applies to bank deposits only) and **credit risk** (the yield premium is compensation, not a free lunch).\nA) They differ materially in safety. C) A higher return **signals higher risk**, it does not reduce it. D) Certain NBFC categories may accept **term** (not demand) deposits, subject to conditions.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The P2P platform offers \"assured returns of 13%\". What should you tell Nikhil?\nA) It is a good deal for the emergency fund  B) NBFC-P2P platforms are RBI-registered intermediaries that may neither lend on their own book nor guarantee returns — so an \"assured return\" claim is a regulatory red flag, and P2P is unsuitable for an emergency fund in any case  C) P2P is SEBI-regulated and therefore safe  D) He should invest his entire portfolio there\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two separate problems. **Regulatory:** an NBFC-P2P is a **matching intermediary only** and is expressly barred from guaranteeing returns, so the claim itself signals either non-compliance or misrepresentation. **Suitability:** an emergency fund requires **immediate availability and certainty of value**, and P2P lending offers neither — it carries borrower default risk and no liquid exit.\nA) It fails the emergency fund's defining requirements. C) P2P is **RBI**-regulated, and regulation never equals safety. D) Concentrating a portfolio in unsecured retail lending would be grossly unsuitable.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Nikhil's shares are held with a broker. Which institutions are involved in holding and settling them, and what protection exists if the broker defaults?\nA) Only the broker; no protection exists  B) A depository (NSDL/CDSL) holds them via the broker as DP, the clearing corporation guarantees settlement, and the exchange's Investor Protection Fund covers broker default within limits  C) SEBI holds the shares directly  D) The RTA holds the shares and reimburses all losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **depository** holds the securities (the broker typically acts as DP), the **clearing corporation** guarantees settlement as CCP, and the exchange's **Investor Protection Fund** covers claims arising from **member default**, within prescribed limits.\nA) Multiple institutions and protections are involved. C) SEBI regulates; it does not custody securities. D) RTAs serve issuers and mutual funds, and no body reimburses market losses.\n\n> ⚠️ **The distinction to carry into the exam:** protection exists against **intermediary default and fraud**; it does **not** exist against **market losses**. Holding securities in his own demat account (rather than leaving them in a broker pool) is itself the strongest structural protection Nikhil has.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "06", "title": "Securities Market Segments", "module": "Module 2: Indian Financial Markets", "notes": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 2 · Indian Financial Markets — 10 marks shared with Chapter 5.**\n> Mostly definitional. The highest-yield areas are **types of issues**, **book building**, and **corporate actions** (especially rights vs bonus, and the effect of each on price and holdings).\n\n---\n\n## 6.1 The Primary Market\n\n### 6.1.1 Functions of the primary market\n\nThe primary market is where **securities are created and sold for the first time**, so capital flows **to the issuer**.\n\nIts functions:\n1. **Capital formation** — channels savings into productive investment\n2. **Origination** — appraising and structuring the issue\n3. **Underwriting** — guaranteeing subscription\n4. **Distribution** — placing securities with investors\n\n### 6.1.2 ⭐ Types of Issues\n\n| Type | Who it goes to | Key points |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Public issue** | The general public | IPO (first time) or FPO (already listed). Requires a prospectus and full SEBI compliance |\n| **Private placement** | A select group | Offered to **fewer than 200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and employees under ESOP); beyond that it is deemed a public issue |\n| **Preferential issue** | Selected identified persons | Made by a **listed** company to specific investors (promoters, strategic partners) at a price determined under SEBI ICDR rules; carries a **lock-in** |\n| **Qualified Institutions Placement (QIP)** | **Qualified Institutional Buyers (QIBs)** only | A fast, low-cost route for listed companies to raise money from institutions without a full prospectus |\n| **Rights issue** | **Existing shareholders**, pro rata | Offered at a **discount** to market price; the entitlement is **renounceable** (can be sold) |\n| **Bonus issue** | **Existing shareholders**, free | Capitalisation of reserves — **no money is raised** |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most-tested contrast: a RIGHTS issue raises money; a BONUS issue does not.** A rights issue is an *offer to buy more* at a discount; a bonus issue is *free additional shares* funded by converting reserves into share capital.\n\n### 6.1.3 Categories of issuers\n\n- **Central, State and local governments** — dated securities, T-bills, state development loans, municipal bonds\n- **Public sector units (PSUs)** — equity and bonds\n- **Private sector companies** — equity, debentures, commercial paper\n- **Banks, financial institutions and NBFCs** — bonds, certificates of deposit, infrastructure bonds\n- **Mutual funds** — units\n- **REITs and InvITs** — units representing real estate / infrastructure assets\n- **AIFs** — units to sophisticated investors\n\n### 6.1.4 Types of investors\n\n| Category | Who | Typical IPO allocation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **QIBs** | Mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs, pension funds | Up to **50%** in a book-built issue |\n| **Non-Institutional Investors (NIIs/HNIs)** | Applications **above ₹2 lakh** | At least **15%** |\n| **Retail Individual Investors (RIIs)** | Applications **up to ₹2 lakh** | At least **35%** |\n| **Anchor investors** | QIBs allotted a day before the issue opens | Carved out of the QIB portion; carries a lock-in |\n\n> 🧠 The **₹2 lakh** line separates retail from non-institutional applicants — a common exam detail.\n\n### 6.1.5 Public issues of equity shares\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Initial Public Offer (IPO)** | The company's **first** offer of shares to the public; the company becomes listed |\n| **Follow-on / Further Public Offer (FPO)** | A **further** public offer by an **already listed** company |\n| **Fresh issue** | **New** shares are created; money goes **to the company** |\n| **Offer for Sale (OFS)** | **Existing** shareholders (usually promoters) sell their shares; money goes **to the selling shareholders, not the company** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Guaranteed question:** in an **offer for sale the company receives nothing** — only the selling shareholders do. Many IPOs combine a fresh issue *and* an OFS.\n\n### 6.1.6 ⭐ Pricing a public issue\n\n| | **Fixed price issue** | **Book-built issue** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Price** | Stated in the prospectus **upfront** | A **price band** is given; the final price is discovered from bids |\n| **Demand visibility** | Known only **after** the issue closes | Visible **during** the issue (daily bidding data) |\n| **Investor pays** | Full amount on application | Bids at or above the floor; final **cut-off price** applies |\n| **Price band** | Not applicable | Cap must be **within 120%** of the floor price |\n\n**Book building mechanics:**\n- The company and merchant banker set a **floor price** and a **cap**.\n- Investors bid quantities at prices within the band; retail investors may bid at **\"cut-off\"**, meaning they accept whatever final price emerges.\n- The **cut-off price** is the price at which the issue is fully subscribed — it becomes the issue price.\n- Bidding is typically open **3 working days** (extendable if the band is revised).\n\n### 6.1.8 Applying to a public issue — ASBA\n\n**ASBA = Applications Supported by Blocked Amount.** The application money is **blocked in the investor's own bank account** rather than debited. It is:\n- **Mandatory** for public issues\n- Debited **only to the extent of the allotment**; the rest is simply unblocked\n- The investor **continues to earn interest** on the blocked amount\n\n**UPI mechanism:** retail investors applying up to **₹5 lakh** may use a UPI ID to block funds.\n\n### 6.1.9 Public issue of debt securities\nGoverned by SEBI's regulations on issue and listing of **non-convertible securities**. Requires **credit rating**, a **debenture trustee**, and creation of a **debenture redemption reserve** where applicable.\n\n### 6.1.10 Rights offer\n- Offered to existing shareholders **in proportion to their holding** (e.g. 1:4 = one new share for every four held).\n- Priced at a **discount** to the market price.\n- The entitlement is **renounceable** — a shareholder who does not wish to subscribe can **sell the rights entitlement** in the market.\n- A shareholder who neither subscribes nor renounces suffers **dilution** and forfeits value.\n\n### 6.1.11 & 6.1.12 Private placement and QIP\n- **Private placement**: securities offered to a **select group of fewer than 200 persons** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and ESOP employees). Exceeding that limit makes it a deemed public issue.\n- **QIP**: a listed company issues to **QIBs only**. Fast and low-cost — no draft prospectus filing with SEBI is required, and pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 The Secondary Market\n\n### 6.2.1 Role and function\n- **Liquidity** — investors can exit\n- **Price discovery** — continuous valuation of securities\n- **Transfer of ownership** without affecting the company's capital\n- **Benchmark** for pricing new issues and for corporate decisions\n\n### 6.2.2 Market structure and participants\nExchanges (NSE, BSE), clearing corporations, depositories, brokers and authorised persons, custodians, and investors (retail, HNI, institutional, FPI).\n\n**Trading and settlement:** orders are matched electronically on a price–time priority basis. Indian equity settlement is on a **T+1** rolling basis (settlement on the working day after the trade).\n\n### 6.2.3 ⭐ Market indicators\n\n| Indicator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Market capitalisation** | Share price × number of outstanding shares. For the market, the sum across all listed companies. Measures **size** |\n| **Market turnover** | Total value of trades over a period. Measures **activity/liquidity** |\n| **Market indices** | A basket of representative stocks tracking overall or sectoral movement — Nifty 50, Sensex |\n\n**Index construction:** Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 and Sensex use **free-float market capitalisation weighting** — only shares genuinely available for trading (excluding promoter and locked-in holdings) are counted.\n\n**Uses of an index:** a market barometer, a benchmark for portfolio performance, the underlying for index funds/ETFs and derivatives, and an economic indicator.\n\n### 6.2.4 ⭐ Risk Management Systems in the secondary market\n\n| Mechanism | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Capital adequacy norms** | Minimum net worth and deposits for brokers, scaled to business volume |\n| **Margins** | Collateral collected upfront to cover potential losses — initial margin, mark-to-market margin, exposure margin, VaR margin |\n| **Circuit breakers** | **Market-wide** halts when an index moves ±10%, ±15% or ±20%, with halt durations depending on the level and time of day |\n| **Price bands** | **Stock-specific** limits (e.g. 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%) beyond which a stock cannot move in a day |\n| **Settlement guarantee mechanism** | The clearing corporation's fund guaranteeing settlement even if a member defaults |\n| **Online surveillance** | Real-time monitoring of trading for manipulation and unusual activity |\n| **Price monitoring and action** | Investigation of abnormal price/volume movements; moving stocks to restricted categories (e.g. trade-for-trade) |\n| **Inspection of books** | Periodic inspection of members' records by the exchange and SEBI |\n\n> 🧠 **Circuit breaker vs price band:** a **circuit breaker is market-wide** (halts the whole market on index moves); a **price band is stock-specific** (caps one scrip's daily movement).\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 ⭐ Corporate Actions\n\nA **corporate action** is an event initiated by a company that affects its securities and shareholders.\n\n### 6.3.1 Rights Issue\nCovered above. **Raises capital.** Price falls to a theoretical ex-rights price after the issue.\n\n### 6.3.2 Bonus Issue\nFree additional shares from **capitalisation of reserves**.\n- **No money is raised.**\n- Number of shares **rises**; price falls proportionately.\n- **The shareholder's total value and percentage holding are unchanged.**\n\n**Example — 1:1 bonus:** an investor holding 100 shares at ₹800 (value ₹80,000) receives 100 more. Now 200 shares, and the price adjusts to ₹400. Value = **₹80,000, unchanged.**\n\n### 6.3.3 Dividend\nDistribution of profits to shareholders.\n- **Interim** (declared during the year) or **final** (at year-end, approved at the AGM).\n- The share price typically falls by roughly the dividend amount on the **ex-dividend date**.\n- **Record date** — the date on which the company's register determines who is entitled.\n- **Ex-date** — from this date the share trades **without** the dividend entitlement. Under T+1 settlement the ex-date is normally **the same as the record date** (buy before it to be entitled).\n- Dividends are **taxable in the hands of the shareholder** at slab rates, with **TDS** applicable above the prescribed threshold.\n\n### 6.3.4 Stock Split\nThe face value of each share is **reduced**, increasing the number of shares.\n- **Example:** a ₹10 face value share split 1:5 becomes five ₹2 face value shares. 100 shares at ₹1,000 become 500 shares at ₹200 — **value unchanged**.\n- **Purpose:** improve **affordability and liquidity** by lowering the per-share price.\n\n> ⚠️ **Bonus vs split — a classic exam pair.**\n>\n> | | **Bonus issue** | **Stock split** |\n> |---|---|---|\n> | **Face value** | **Unchanged** | **Reduced** |\n> | **Source** | Capitalisation of **reserves** | No reserves involved |\n> | **Share capital** | **Increases** | **Unchanged** |\n> | **Number of shares** | Increases | Increases |\n> | **Shareholder value** | Unchanged | Unchanged |\n\n### 6.3.5 Share Buy-back\nThe company **repurchases its own shares** from shareholders.\n- Reduces the number of outstanding shares, raising EPS.\n- Routes: **tender offer** (proportionate, at a fixed price) or **open market** purchase.\n- Signals that management considers the shares undervalued, and returns surplus cash.\n\n### 6.3.6 Delisting\nThe company's shares are **removed from the exchange**.\n- **Voluntary** — usually via a **reverse book building** process in which public shareholders bid the exit price.\n- **Compulsory** — imposed by the exchange for non-compliance; promoters must still provide an exit to public shareholders.\n\n### 6.3.7 Mergers and Acquisitions\n- **Merger/amalgamation** — two or more companies combine; shareholders of the merging company receive shares of the merged entity in a set **swap ratio**.\n- **Acquisition/takeover** — one company acquires control of another. Triggers **SEBI (SAST) Regulations**, requiring an **open offer** to public shareholders once specified thresholds of shares or voting rights are crossed.\n- **Demerger** — a division is spun off into a separate listed company; shareholders receive shares in the new entity.\n\n### 6.3.8 Offer for Sale (OFS)\nA mechanism for **promoters to sell** their shares through the exchange platform — used to raise public shareholding or to divest.\n**The company receives nothing**; the selling shareholder does.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Primary market | Securities **issued**; funds go to the **issuer** |\n| Secondary market | Investors trade **among themselves**; provides liquidity |\n| IPO vs FPO | **First** public offer vs a **further** offer by a listed company |\n| Fresh issue vs OFS | New shares, money **to the company** vs existing shares, money **to the seller** |\n| Private placement limit | Fewer than **200 investors** in a financial year (excl. QIBs, ESOP) |\n| QIP | Listed company issues to **QIBs only**; fast, no draft prospectus filing |\n| Preferential issue | To identified persons by a listed company; carries a **lock-in** |\n| Rights issue | To **existing** shareholders pro rata, at a **discount**; **renounceable**; **raises money** |\n| Bonus issue | **Free** shares from **reserves**; **raises no money**; value unchanged |\n| Fixed price vs book building | Price stated upfront vs **discovered** within a price band |\n| Price band cap rule | Cap within **120%** of the floor price |\n| Cut-off price | The price at which the issue is fully subscribed |\n| Retail vs NII threshold | **₹2 lakh** |\n| IPO allocation (book-built) | QIB up to **50%**, NII at least **15%**, retail at least **35%** |\n| ASBA | Money **blocked** in the investor's own account; interest continues |\n| UPI in IPOs | Retail applications up to **₹5 lakh** |\n| Settlement cycle | **T+1** rolling |\n| Market capitalisation | Price × shares outstanding — measures **size** |\n| Market turnover | Value traded — measures **activity** |\n| Index weighting in India | **Free-float market capitalisation** |\n| Circuit breaker | **Market-wide** halt at ±10%, ±15%, ±20% index moves |\n| Price band | **Stock-specific** daily movement limit |\n| Settlement guarantee | Provided by the **clearing corporation** |\n| Stock split | **Face value reduced**; share capital unchanged |\n| Bonus issue | **Face value unchanged**; share capital increases |\n| Buy-back | Company repurchases its shares; **EPS rises** |\n| Delisting | Voluntary via **reverse book building**, or compulsory |\n| Open offer trigger | **SEBI (SAST) Regulations** on acquiring control/thresholds |\n| Dividend taxation | Taxable **in the shareholder's hands** at slab rates; TDS above threshold |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any corporate action question, ask two things: **(1) does money move?** (rights and buy-back yes; bonus and split no) and **(2) does the shareholder's total value change?** (for bonus and split, no — only the share count and price adjust).\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Corporate actions and the rights-vs-bonus contrast are the highest-yield items.**\n\n**Q1.** What are the four functions of the primary market?\n> **A:** **Capital formation**, **origination** (appraising and structuring), **underwriting** (guaranteeing subscription) and **distribution**.\n\n**Q2.** What is the difference between an IPO and an FPO?\n> **A:** An **IPO** is a company's **first** public offer (it becomes listed). An **FPO/further public offer** is a **subsequent** public offer by an **already listed** company.\n\n**Q3.** In an Offer for Sale, who receives the money?\n> **A:** The **selling shareholders** (usually promoters) — **the company receives nothing**. Contrast with a **fresh issue**, where new shares are created and the money goes to the company.\n\n**Q4.** What is the private placement limit?\n> **A:** Offered to **fewer than 200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and employees under ESOP). Beyond that it is deemed a **public issue**.\n\n**Q5.** What is a QIP and why is it attractive to issuers?\n> **A:** A **Qualified Institutions Placement** — a listed company issuing to **QIBs only**. It is **fast and low-cost**: no draft prospectus filing with SEBI, and pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices.\n\n**Q6.** What is a preferential issue?\n> **A:** An issue by a **listed** company to **specific identified persons** (promoters, strategic partners), priced under SEBI ICDR rules and carrying a **lock-in**.\n\n**Q7.** State the key difference between a rights issue and a bonus issue.\n> **A:** A **rights issue RAISES money** (shareholders buy more shares at a discount). A **bonus issue raises NO money** — it is free shares from the **capitalisation of reserves**.\n\n**Q8.** What does \"renounceable\" mean in a rights issue?\n> **A:** A shareholder who does not want to subscribe can **sell the rights entitlement** in the market rather than let it lapse. Doing neither means **dilution and forfeited value**.\n\n**Q9.** Compare fixed price and book-built issues.\n> **A:** **Fixed price** — the price is stated **upfront** in the prospectus and demand is known only after closing. **Book-built** — a **price band** is given, investors bid, and the final price is **discovered**; demand is visible daily.\n\n**Q10.** What is the rule on the price band's cap?\n> **A:** The cap must be **within 120% of the floor price**.\n\n**Q11.** What is the cut-off price?\n> **A:** The price at which the issue is **fully subscribed** — it becomes the final issue price. Retail investors may bid \"at cut-off\", accepting whatever price emerges.\n\n**Q12.** What separates a Retail Individual Investor from a Non-Institutional Investor?\n> **A:** The **₹2 lakh** application threshold. Up to ₹2 lakh = **retail**; above ₹2 lakh = **non-institutional (HNI)**.\n\n**Q13.** State the IPO allocation limits in a book-built issue.\n> **A:** **QIBs up to 50%**, **NIIs at least 15%**, **retail at least 35%**.\n\n**Q14.** What is an anchor investor?\n> **A:** A **QIB allotted shares a day before** the issue opens, carved out of the QIB portion, subject to a **lock-in**. Their participation signals confidence to other investors.\n\n**Q15.** What is ASBA and what are its three benefits?\n> **A:** **Applications Supported by Blocked Amount.** The money is **blocked in the investor's own bank account**: (1) it is debited **only to the extent of allotment**, (2) the balance is simply unblocked, and (3) the investor **keeps earning interest** meanwhile. It is **mandatory** for public issues.\n\n**Q16.** What is the UPI limit for retail IPO applications?\n> **A:** Retail investors may apply using a UPI ID for amounts up to **₹5 lakh**.\n\n**Q17.** What is India's equity settlement cycle?\n> **A:** **T+1 rolling settlement** — settlement on the working day after the trade.\n\n**Q18.** Distinguish market capitalisation from market turnover.\n> **A:** **Market capitalisation** = price × shares outstanding — it measures **size**. **Market turnover** = value of trades over a period — it measures **activity/liquidity**.\n\n**Q19.** How are Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 and Sensex weighted?\n> **A:** By **free-float market capitalisation** — only shares genuinely available for trading are counted, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings.\n\n**Q20.** Name four uses of a market index.\n> **A:** A **market barometer**; a **benchmark** for portfolio performance; the **underlying** for index funds, ETFs and derivatives; and an **economic indicator**.\n\n**Q21.** What is the difference between a circuit breaker and a price band?\n> **A:** A **circuit breaker is MARKET-WIDE** — it halts the whole market when an index moves **±10%, ±15% or ±20%**. A **price band is STOCK-SPECIFIC** — it caps one scrip's daily movement (e.g. 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).\n\n**Q22.** Name the risk management systems in the secondary market.\n> **A:** **Capital adequacy** norms, **margins**, **circuit breakers**, **price bands**, the **settlement guarantee mechanism**, **online surveillance**, **price monitoring and action**, and **inspection of books**.\n\n**Q23.** What types of margin are collected?\n> **A:** **Initial margin**, **mark-to-market margin**, **exposure margin** and **VaR margin** — all collateral against potential losses.\n\n**Q24.** In a 1:1 bonus issue, what happens to a holding of 100 shares priced at ₹800?\n> **A:** The investor receives 100 more shares → **200 shares**, and the price adjusts to **₹400**. Total value stays **₹80,000 — unchanged**.\n\n**Q25.** Compare a bonus issue and a stock split on face value and share capital.\n> **A:** **Bonus** — face value **unchanged**, share capital **increases** (reserves are capitalised). **Split** — face value **reduced**, share capital **unchanged**. Both increase the share count and leave shareholder value unchanged.\n\n**Q26.** What is the purpose of a stock split?\n> **A:** To improve **affordability and liquidity** by lowering the per-share price.\n\n**Q27.** What is the record date, and what is the ex-date?\n> **A:** The **record date** is when the company's register determines entitlement. The **ex-date** is when the share starts trading **without** the entitlement. Under T+1 the ex-date is normally the **same as** the record date.\n\n**Q28.** How are dividends taxed in India now?\n> **A:** **Taxable in the shareholder's hands** at their slab rate, with **TDS** applicable above the prescribed threshold.\n\n**Q29.** What happens to a share price on the ex-dividend date?\n> **A:** It typically **falls by roughly the dividend amount** — the value has left the company.\n\n**Q30.** What is a share buy-back, and what are the two routes?\n> **A:** The company **repurchases its own shares**, reducing the share count and **raising EPS**. Routes: a **tender offer** (proportionate, fixed price) or **open market** purchase.\n\n**Q31.** How does voluntary delisting normally work?\n> **A:** Through **reverse book building**, in which public shareholders bid the exit price. **Compulsory** delisting is imposed by the exchange for non-compliance, and promoters must still provide an exit.\n\n**Q32.** What triggers an open offer in an acquisition?\n> **A:** Crossing prescribed thresholds of shares or voting rights (or acquiring control) under the **SEBI (SAST) Regulations** — the acquirer must then make an **open offer** to public shareholders.\n\n**Q33.** What is a demerger?\n> **A:** A division is **spun off into a separate listed company**, and existing shareholders receive shares in the new entity.\n\n**Q34.** What is a swap ratio?\n> **A:** The ratio in which shareholders of a **merging** company receive shares of the **merged** entity.\n\n**Q35.** Which corporate actions actually move money, and which do not?\n> **A:** **Money moves:** rights issue (in), dividend (out), buy-back (out), OFS (to the seller). **No money moves:** bonus issue, stock split.\n\n**Q36.** Name the categories of issuer in the primary market.\n> **A:** **Governments** (central, state, local), **PSUs**, **private companies**, **banks/FIs/NBFCs**, **mutual funds**, **REITs and InvITs**, and **AIFs**.\n\n**Q37.** What does a public issue of debt securities require?\n> **A:** A **credit rating**, a **debenture trustee**, and creation of a **debenture redemption reserve** where applicable.\n\n**Q38.** How long is bidding typically open in a book-built issue?\n> **A:** Usually **3 working days**, extendable if the price band is revised.\n\n**Q39.** What are the four functions of the secondary market?\n> **A:** **Liquidity**, **price discovery**, **transfer of ownership** (without affecting the company's capital), and providing a **benchmark** for new issues and corporate decisions.\n\n**Q40.** In a rights issue of 1:4, what does a holder of 400 shares receive?\n> **A:** The entitlement to buy **100 new shares** (one for every four held) at the discounted rights price.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 6: Securities Market Segments — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Corporate actions carry the most marks here** — especially bonus vs split and rights entitlement arithmetic. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** In an Initial Public Offer, the company:\nA) Offers shares to the public for the first time and becomes listed  B) Buys back its own shares  C) Issues free shares to shareholders  D) Delists from the exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An **IPO** is the first offer of shares to the public, after which the company is listed.\nB) Is a buy-back. C) Is a bonus issue. D) Is delisting — the opposite of an IPO.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In an Offer for Sale, the proceeds go to:\nA) The company  B) The selling shareholders  C) SEBI  D) The stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An OFS involves **existing shares**, so the **selling shareholders** receive the money — **not the company**.\nA) The company receives money only in a **fresh issue**. C) and D) are the regulator and the platform, not recipients.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A bonus issue:\nA) Raises fresh capital for the company  B) Involves the capitalisation of reserves and raises no money  C) Reduces the number of shares  D) Is offered only to institutional investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bonus shares are issued **free** by converting reserves into share capital — **no cash is raised**.\nA) That is a rights issue. C) The share count **increases**. D) Bonus shares go to **all** existing shareholders.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A rights issue is offered to:\nA) The general public  B) QIBs only  C) Existing shareholders in proportion to their holding  D) Employees only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rights are offered **pro rata to existing shareholders**, usually at a discount to market price.\nA) Describes a public issue. B) Describes a QIP. D) Describes an ESOP.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In a stock split, the face value of a share:\nA) Increases  B) Is reduced  C) Stays the same  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A split **reduces the face value** and proportionately increases the number of shares.\nA) and D) are incorrect. C) Face value stays the same in a **bonus issue**, which is the key contrast.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In a bonus issue, the face value of each share:\nA) Is reduced  B) Remains unchanged  C) Doubles  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Face value is **unchanged** in a bonus; only the number of shares rises (funded from reserves).\nA) That happens in a **stock split**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A Qualified Institutions Placement can be made to:\nA) Retail investors  B) Qualified Institutional Buyers only  C) Employees only  D) Foreign individuals only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **QIP** is restricted to **QIBs** — mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs, pension funds.\nA), C) and D) are excluded from a QIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The threshold separating a Retail Individual Investor from a Non-Institutional Investor in an IPO is an application of:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Applications **up to ₹2 lakh** are retail; **above ₹2 lakh** are non-institutional.\nA), C) and D) are not the threshold. *(₹5 lakh is the UPI application limit — a common confusion.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** ASBA stands for:\nA) Application Submitted By Agent  B) Applications Supported by Blocked Amount  C) Automatic Share Buying Arrangement  D) Approved Securities Bidding Application\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **ASBA** the application money is **blocked** in the investor's own bank account rather than debited.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Under ASBA, the investor:\nA) Loses interest on the application money  B) Continues to earn interest on the blocked amount  C) Must pay in cash  D) Cannot apply online\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the money stays **in the investor's own account**, interest continues to accrue.\nA) This was the disadvantage of the pre-ASBA system. C) and D) are incorrect — ASBA is largely electronic.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** In a book-built issue, the cap of the price band must be within:\nA) 110% of the floor  B) 120% of the floor  C) 150% of the floor  D) 200% of the floor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cap cannot exceed **120% of the floor price**.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The cut-off price in a book-built issue is:\nA) The floor price  B) The price at which the issue is fully subscribed  C) The highest bid received  D) The face value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **cut-off** is where demand fully absorbs the issue; it becomes the issue price.\nA) The floor is the lower bound of the band. C) The highest bid is not necessarily the clearing price. D) Face value is unrelated to issue price.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A circuit breaker in the Indian market is:\nA) Stock-specific  B) Market-wide, triggered by index movements of ±10%, ±15% or ±20%  C) Applied only to derivatives  D) A margin requirement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Circuit breakers halt the **entire market** at defined index thresholds.\nA) That describes a **price band**. C) They apply market-wide. D) Margins are a separate risk control.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A price band is:\nA) Market-wide  B) Stock-specific, limiting a scrip's daily movement  C) An index level  D) A settlement cycle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Price bands cap how far **an individual stock** can move in a day (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).\nA) That is a circuit breaker. C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** India's equity settlement cycle is:\nA) T+5  B) T+3  C) T+2  D) T+1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Indian equity markets settle on a **T+1** rolling basis.\nA), B) and C) are historical cycles that India has progressively shortened.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Market capitalisation is calculated as:\nA) Share price × number of outstanding shares  B) Total value of trades  C) Net profit ÷ shares  D) Face value × shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Market cap measures **size**: price × outstanding shares.\nB) Is turnover, which measures activity. C) Is earnings per share. D) Face value is an accounting figure, not market value.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Indian indices such as the Nifty 50 are weighted by:\nA) Equal weighting  B) Price weighting  C) Free-float market capitalisation  D) Revenue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only shares genuinely **available for trading** are counted, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings.\nA) and B) are used by some international indices but not the Nifty/Sensex. D) Revenue is not an index weighting basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A private placement must be made to fewer than:\nA) 50 investors in a financial year  B) 100 investors in a financial year  C) 200 investors in a financial year  D) 500 investors in a financial year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Fewer than **200 investors** in a financial year (excluding QIBs and ESOP employees); beyond that it is a deemed public issue.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** In a share buy-back, the number of outstanding shares:\nA) Increases  B) Decreases  C) Stays the same  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The company repurchases and extinguishes shares, **reducing** the count and raising EPS.\nA) Happens with bonus, rights or splits. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Voluntary delisting in India is typically carried out through:\nA) A fixed price offer only  B) Reverse book building  C) A bonus issue  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Reverse book building** lets public shareholders bid the exit price.\nA) Fixed price is not the standard voluntary route. C) and D) are unrelated corporate actions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Dividends in India are currently:\nA) Tax-free in the shareholder's hands  B) Taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates, with TDS above a threshold  C) Taxed only on the company  D) Exempt for all investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividend Distribution Tax was abolished; dividends are now **taxed in the recipient's hands** at their slab rate, with **TDS** above the prescribed threshold.\nA), C) and D) describe the pre-abolition regime or are simply incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The ex-date is the date from which:\nA) The share trades without the entitlement  B) The company pays the dividend  C) The register closes permanently  D) Trading is suspended\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Buying **on or after** the ex-date means **no entitlement** to the corporate action.\nB) That is the payment date. C) The register closes only briefly for the record date. D) Trading continues normally.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** An anchor investor is:\nA) A retail investor  B) A QIB allotted shares a day before the issue opens, subject to a lock-in  C) A promoter  D) A merchant banker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anchor investors are **QIBs** allotted ahead of the issue, carved from the QIB portion, with a **lock-in**.\nA) Retail investors cannot be anchors. C) and D) play different roles in an issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In a book-built IPO, the retail portion must be at least:\nA) 15%  B) 35%  C) 50%  D) 10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Retail gets **at least 35%**, NIIs at least 15%, and QIBs up to 50%.\nA) 15% is the NII minimum. C) 50% is the QIB maximum. D) 10% is not a prescribed share.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A preferential issue is made by:\nA) An unlisted company to the public  B) A listed company to specific identified persons, with a lock-in  C) A mutual fund to unit-holders  D) The government to banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Preferential issues go to **identified persons** (promoters, strategic investors) under SEBI ICDR rules, and carry a **lock-in**.\nA) Would be a public issue. C) and D) describe different transactions.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The main function of the secondary market is:\nA) To raise fresh capital  B) To provide liquidity and price discovery  C) To register companies  D) To set dividend policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Secondary trading gives investors **exit and continuous valuation**.\nA) That is the primary market. C) Is the RoC. D) Is a board decision.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A public issue of debt securities requires:\nA) No credit rating  B) A credit rating and a debenture trustee  C) Only board approval  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt public issues need a **credit rating** and a **debenture trustee**, plus a debenture redemption reserve where applicable.\nA) A rating is mandatory. C) Regulatory compliance goes well beyond board approval. D) Is an unrelated corporate action.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** In a 1:5 stock split of a ₹10 face value share, the new face value is:\nA) ₹5  B) ₹2  C) ₹1  D) ₹10\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Splitting one ₹10 share into five gives a face value of **₹10 ÷ 5 = ₹2** each.\nA) ₹5 would be a 1:2 split. C) ₹1 would be a 1:10 split. D) Face value must change in a split.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The UPI mechanism for IPO applications is available to retail investors for amounts up to:\nA) ₹2 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Retail investors may block funds via UPI for applications up to **₹5 lakh**.\nA) ₹2 lakh is the retail/NII classification threshold, a different limit. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An open offer to public shareholders is triggered under:\nA) The Companies Act, 2013  B) The SEBI (SAST) Regulations  C) FEMA  D) The Income Tax Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers) Regulations** require an open offer on crossing prescribed thresholds or acquiring control.\nA) Governs corporate law generally. C) Governs foreign exchange. D) Governs taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which corporate action raises money for the company?\nA) Bonus issue  B) Stock split  C) Rights issue  D) Dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **rights issue** brings in cash as shareholders subscribe.\nA) and B) raise nothing — they only restructure existing capital. D) A dividend takes money **out** of the company.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a demerger:\nA) Two companies combine  B) A division is spun off into a separate listed company  C) Shares are bought back  D) The company delists\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **demerger** separates a division into its own entity, with existing shareholders receiving shares in it.\nA) Is a merger. C) Is a buy-back. D) Is delisting.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The settlement guarantee mechanism is provided by:\nA) The stock exchange  B) The clearing corporation  C) SEBI  D) The depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **clearing corporation** maintains the settlement guarantee fund and acts as central counterparty.\nA) The exchange matches trades. C) SEBI regulates. D) The depository holds securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Bidding in a book-built issue is typically open for:\nA) 1 day  B) 3 working days  C) 10 working days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Usually **3 working days**, extendable if the price band is revised.\nA), C) and D) are not the standard period.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Market turnover measures:\nA) The size of the market  B) The value of trades over a period, i.e. activity  C) The number of listed companies  D) The dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Turnover is an **activity/liquidity** measure.\nA) That is market capitalisation. C) and D) are different statistics.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor holds 200 shares priced at ₹600. The company declares a 1:1 bonus. What is the position afterwards?\nA) 400 shares at ₹300; value ₹1,20,000  B) 400 shares at ₹600; value ₹2,40,000  C) 200 shares at ₹300; value ₹60,000  D) 400 shares at ₹150; value ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:1 bonus doubles the shares to **400** and halves the price to **₹300**. Value = 400 × 300 = **₹1,20,000**, exactly the original 200 × 600 = ₹1,20,000.\nB) Assumes the price does not adjust — bonus shares are not free wealth. C) Ignores the extra shares. D) Halves the value, which cannot happen from a bonus.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor holds 500 shares of ₹10 face value trading at ₹2,000. The company announces a 1:10 split. What results?\nA) 5,000 shares at ₹200, face value ₹1  B) 5,000 shares at ₹2,000, face value ₹10  C) 500 shares at ₹200  D) 50 shares at ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:10 split turns each ₹10 share into ten ₹1 shares: **5,000 shares**, price **₹2,000 ÷ 10 = ₹200**, face value **₹1**. Value = 5,000 × 200 = ₹10,00,000, unchanged.\nB) The price must adjust. C) The share count must rise. D) Reverses the split direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A company announces a rights issue of 1:4 at ₹150 when the market price is ₹250. An investor holds 800 shares. How many rights shares can she subscribe to, and at what total cost?\nA) 200 shares for ₹30,000  B) 200 shares for ₹50,000  C) 100 shares for ₹15,000  D) 800 shares for ₹1,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **1:4** ratio means one new share for every four held: 800 ÷ 4 = **200 shares**. Cost = 200 × ₹150 = **₹30,000**.\nB) Uses the market price instead of the rights price. C) Uses a 1:8 ratio. D) Assumes one-for-one. **The discount (₹150 vs ₹250) is why rights have value and can be sold if not taken up.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A shareholder neither subscribes to nor renounces a rights entitlement. What happens?\nA) She receives the shares free  B) The entitlement lapses and she suffers dilution, forfeiting its value  C) The company must compensate her  D) Her shareholding percentage rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An unexercised, unrenounced right **lapses worthless**, and because others subscribed, her **proportionate holding is diluted**.\nA) Rights must be paid for; bonus shares are free. C) No compensation is due — the option was offered. D) Her percentage **falls**.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Which action leaves both the shareholder's total value AND the company's share capital unchanged?\nA) Bonus issue  B) Stock split  C) Rights issue  D) Buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **split**, face value falls as the share count rises, so **share capital is unchanged** and so is shareholder value.\nA) A bonus leaves value unchanged but **increases share capital** (reserves are capitalised). C) A rights issue raises capital. D) A buy-back reduces capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A company wants to raise ₹800 crore quickly from institutions without filing a draft prospectus. The appropriate route is:\nA) An IPO  B) A QIP  C) A rights issue  D) A bonus issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **QIP** allows a listed company to raise from **QIBs** quickly, without the full prospectus process.\nA) An IPO is for unlisted companies and is slow. C) A rights issue targets existing shareholders and takes longer. D) A bonus issue raises **no money at all**.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An IPO comprises a fresh issue of ₹500 crore and an offer for sale of ₹300 crore. How much does the company receive?\nA) ₹800 crore  B) ₹500 crore  C) ₹300 crore  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only the **fresh issue** (₹500 crore) brings money to the company; the **₹300 crore OFS** goes to the selling shareholders.\nA) Adds both, ignoring who receives the OFS proceeds. C) Reverses the two. D) The fresh issue does reach the company. **A very common exam construction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An investor buys a share on the ex-dividend date. Is she entitled to the dividend?\nA) Yes  B) No — buying on or after the ex-date means no entitlement  C) Only if she holds for 30 days  D) Only if she informs the company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — From the **ex-date** the share trades **without** the dividend; entitlement requires purchase **before** it.\nA) The seller retains the entitlement. C) and D) are not how entitlement works.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A stock falls 19.8% in a day with a 20% price band in force. What happens?\nA) The whole market halts  B) The stock is close to its lower price band and cannot fall further that day  C) The stock is delisted  D) Trading in all stocks stops\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **price band is stock-specific**: this scrip cannot move beyond ±20% today, but the rest of the market trades normally.\nA) and D) describe **circuit breakers**, which are triggered by **index** moves. C) Delisting is unrelated to daily price movement.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The Nifty 50 falls 10% at 11 a.m. What is the consequence?\nA) Nothing  B) A market-wide circuit breaker halts trading for a prescribed period  C) Only that index's stocks halt  D) The exchange delists stocks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **market-wide circuit breaker** triggers at ±10%, ±15% and ±20% index moves, halting trading for a period that depends on the level and time of day.\nA) A 10% index move is a defined trigger. C) The halt is market-wide, not index-specific. D) Delisting is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A retail investor bids \"at cut-off\" in a book-built IPO. This means:\nA) She will only buy at the floor price  B) She accepts whatever final price is discovered  C) She bids the highest possible price  D) Her bid is invalid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bidding at cut-off means **accepting the discovered issue price**, which maximises the chance of allotment.\nA) The cut-off may exceed the floor. C) It is not a maximum-price bid — she pays the cut-off price. D) It is a valid and common retail option.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which of these does NOT change the total value of a shareholder's holding on the announcement day?\nA) A dividend payment  B) A bonus issue  C) A buy-back at a premium  D) A rights issue at a deep discount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **bonus** merely splits the same value across more shares.\nA) A dividend removes cash from the company, so the share price falls by roughly the dividend — though the shareholder receives the cash. C) A buy-back at a premium affects value and EPS. D) A rights issue requires fresh money and changes the per-share value.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Under ASBA, an investor applies for ₹1,00,000 of shares and is allotted ₹20,000 worth. What happens to the money?\nA) ₹1,00,000 is debited and ₹80,000 refunded later  B) Only ₹20,000 is debited; the remaining ₹80,000 is simply unblocked  C) Nothing is debited  D) ₹1,00,000 is retained by the exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ASBA **blocks** rather than debits, so only the allotted amount leaves the account and the rest is released — with interest earned throughout.\nA) Describes the pre-ASBA refund system. C) The allotted amount is debited. D) The exchange never holds application money.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A company with 10 crore shares buys back 1 crore shares. The effect on EPS, holding profit constant, is:\nA) EPS falls  B) EPS rises, because the same profit is divided among fewer shares  C) EPS is unchanged  D) EPS becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With 9 crore shares instead of 10, **EPS rises by about 11%** for the same profit.\nA) Reverses the effect. C) The denominator has changed. D) Nonsensical.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which statement about market capitalisation and turnover is correct?\nA) They measure the same thing  B) Market cap measures size; turnover measures trading activity  C) Turnover measures size  D) Market cap measures liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Size vs activity** — a large-cap stock can be thinly traded, and a small company can be heavily traded.\nA) They are distinct. C) and D) swap the definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A company issues shares to 250 investors in one financial year under a private placement. The consequence is:\nA) It is valid  B) It exceeds the limit of fewer than 200 investors and is deemed a public issue  C) It requires only board approval  D) SEBI has no role\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exceeding the **200-investor** ceiling converts it into a **deemed public issue**, attracting full public-issue compliance.\nA) It breaches the limit. C) A deemed public issue requires far more than board approval. D) SEBI's regulations are directly engaged.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which is the correct order of an IPO's key stages?\nA) Listing → bidding → prospectus  B) Draft prospectus with SEBI → price band → bidding → allotment → listing  C) Allotment → bidding → listing  D) Bidding → prospectus → SEBI filing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The regulatory filing precedes pricing, which precedes bidding, allotment and finally listing.\nA), C) and D) all place later stages before earlier ones.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A promoter wishes to reduce his stake in a listed company to meet minimum public shareholding norms. The most suitable mechanism is:\nA) A fresh issue  B) An Offer for Sale through the exchange  C) A bonus issue  D) A buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **OFS** lets the promoter sell existing shares on the exchange platform, raising public shareholding.\nA) A fresh issue creates new shares and dilutes everyone, but is a slower route to this specific goal. C) A bonus changes no one's percentage. D) A buy-back typically **increases** the promoter's percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is NOT a risk management system in the secondary market?\nA) Margins  B) Circuit breakers  C) Dividend distribution  D) Capital adequacy norms\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Dividend distribution** is a corporate action, not a market risk control.\nA), B) and D) are all core risk management mechanisms, alongside price bands, the settlement guarantee, surveillance and inspection.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An investor wants to sell her rights entitlement rather than subscribe. Is this possible?\nA) No, rights always lapse  B) Yes — rights entitlements are renounceable and can be sold in the market  C) Only with company permission  D) Only to the promoter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Renounceability** is a defining feature: the entitlement itself has value because the rights price is below market.\nA) They lapse only if neither subscribed nor renounced. C) No company permission is needed. D) They can be sold to any buyer in the market.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A company's shares trade at ₹450 before a ₹15 dividend goes ex. What is the likely opening price on the ex-date, other things equal?\nA) ₹465  B) ₹450  C) About ₹435  D) ₹415\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The price typically falls by roughly the dividend: 450 − 15 = **₹435**, since that cash has left the company.\nA) Adds the dividend. B) Ignores the adjustment. D) Over-adjusts. **The shareholder is not worse off — they receive the ₹15 separately.**\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which combination correctly describes a fresh issue versus an OFS?\nA) Both bring money to the company  B) Fresh issue creates new shares and funds the company; OFS transfers existing shares and funds the seller  C) Both transfer existing shares  D) OFS creates new shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the core distinction.\nA) Only the fresh issue funds the company. C) Only the OFS involves existing shares. D) An OFS involves **no new shares**.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** An index is used as the underlying for an ETF. This is an example of the index's role as:\nA) An economic indicator  B) The basis for an investment product  C) A regulatory tool  D) A settlement mechanism\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indices serve as the **underlying** for index funds, ETFs and derivatives.\nA) Is a different (also valid) use. C) and D) are not index functions.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A company announces a buy-back via tender offer at ₹700 when the market price is ₹600. What does this typically signal?\nA) The company is in distress  B) Management believes the shares are undervalued and is returning surplus cash  C) The company is delisting  D) A bonus issue will follow\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium buy-back signals **confidence in undervaluation** and returns surplus cash to shareholders.\nA) Distressed companies rarely have surplus cash to buy back. C) A buy-back is distinct from delisting, though large ones can precede it. D) There is no such automatic link.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which issue type would a listed company use to bring in a strategic investor at a negotiated price?\nA) An IPO  B) A preferential issue  C) A bonus issue  D) A stock split\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **preferential issue** targets **identified persons** such as strategic partners, priced under SEBI ICDR rules with a lock-in.\nA) An IPO is for unlisted companies going public. C) and D) do not bring in new investors or capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Under free-float weighting, which shares are excluded from an index's weight calculation?\nA) All shares  B) Promoter holdings and locked-in shares  C) Shares held by mutual funds  D) Shares held by retail investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Free float counts only shares **genuinely available for trading**, so promoter and locked-in holdings are excluded.\nA) Would leave nothing. C) and D) are freely tradable and therefore included.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A merger is announced with a swap ratio of 2:5. A shareholder of the merging company holds 500 shares. How many shares of the merged entity does she receive?\nA) 1,250  B) 200  C) 250  D) 1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **2:5** swap means **2 new shares for every 5 held**: 500 × (2 ÷ 5) = **200 shares**.\nA) 1,250 inverts the ratio (5 for every 2). C) 250 uses 1:2. D) 1,000 uses 2:1. **Read swap ratios carefully — the direction is what the exam tests.**\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which statement about anchor investors is correct?\nA) They are retail investors allotted after listing  B) They are QIBs allotted before the issue opens, from the QIB portion, with a lock-in  C) They receive shares free  D) They can sell immediately on listing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anchors are **QIBs** allotted a day before the issue opens, carved from the QIB quota, subject to a **lock-in**.\nA) Retail investors cannot be anchors. C) They pay the anchor price. D) The lock-in prevents immediate exit.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A stock has both a 10% price band and the market has a 15% circuit breaker. If the index falls 15% and the stock falls 10%, what happens?\nA) Only the stock halts  B) The market-wide circuit breaker halts all trading, and the stock has also hit its own band  C) Nothing halts  D) Only the stock's price band applies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both controls operate independently: the **index move triggers a market-wide halt**, and the **stock has separately hit its own band**.\nA) A 15% index fall halts the whole market. C) Both thresholds were breached. D) Both mechanisms apply simultaneously.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A company capitalises ₹100 crore of reserves to issue bonus shares. The effect on the balance sheet is:\nA) Total net worth rises by ₹100 crore  B) Reserves fall by ₹100 crore and share capital rises by ₹100 crore; total net worth is unchanged  C) Cash falls by ₹100 crore  D) Liabilities rise\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A bonus is a **reclassification within net worth** — reserves move into share capital. **No cash moves and net worth is unchanged.**\nA) Nothing new enters the company. C) A bonus involves no cash outflow. D) Liabilities are unaffected.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which is the correct description of the T+1 settlement cycle?\nA) Trades settle the same day  B) Trades settle on the working day after the trade date  C) Trades settle after two working days  D) Trades never settle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **T+1** means settlement on the **next working day** after the trade.\nA) That would be T+0. C) That is T+2, the previous Indian cycle. D) Nonsensical.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An investor complains that after a 1:1 bonus the share price \"crashed 50%\". The correct explanation is:\nA) The company performed badly  B) The price adjusted mechanically for the doubled share count; the investor's total value is unchanged  C) The bonus was a mistake  D) The investor lost half the investment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price adjustment is **arithmetic, not a loss**: twice the shares at half the price is the same value.\nA) The price change reflects the bonus, not performance. C) It is a normal corporate action. D) No value was lost — this is the most common retail misunderstanding.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which of these best explains why a rights issue is priced at a discount?\nA) To punish existing shareholders  B) To incentivise existing shareholders to subscribe and ensure the issue succeeds  C) Because SEBI mandates a fixed discount  D) To reduce the company's capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The discount makes subscription attractive and gives the entitlement value, improving the chance of full subscription.\nA) The discount benefits, rather than punishes, subscribers. C) SEBI does not mandate a specific discount. D) A rights issue **raises** capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which type of issue does NOT require the filing of a draft offer document with SEBI?\nA) An IPO  B) An FPO  C) A QIP  D) A public issue of debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **QIP** is a fast-track institutional route that does not require a draft prospectus filing with SEBI.\nA), B) and D) are all public issues requiring the full offer-document process.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A company's shares are moved to the \"trade-for-trade\" segment after abnormal price movement. This is an example of:\nA) Delisting  B) Price monitoring and surveillance action  C) A bonus issue  D) A circuit breaker\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Moving a stock to a restricted segment (where netting is not allowed and every trade must be settled by delivery) is a **surveillance/price-monitoring** action.\nA) The stock remains listed. C) Unrelated. D) Circuit breakers are index-triggered halts.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** An investor holds 1,200 shares at ₹450. The company announces a 3:5 bonus (3 free shares for every 5 held). What is the post-bonus position?\nA) 1,920 shares at ₹281.25; value ₹5,40,000  B) 3,600 shares at ₹150  C) 1,920 shares at ₹450  D) 720 shares at ₹750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bonus shares = 1,200 × (3 ÷ 5) = **720**. Total = 1,200 + 720 = **1,920 shares**. Original value = 1,200 × 450 = **₹5,40,000**. Adjusted price = 5,40,000 ÷ 1,920 = **₹281.25**. Value unchanged at ₹5,40,000.\nB) Assumes a 1:2 bonus and wrong pricing. C) Fails to adjust the price. D) Reduces the share count, which a bonus never does.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A company's shares trade at ₹800. It announces a 1:4 rights issue at ₹500. What is the theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)?\nA) ₹740  B) ₹650  C) ₹800  D) ₹500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — TERP = (total value of old shares + rights proceeds) ÷ total shares after. Take 4 existing shares at ₹800 = ₹3,200, plus 1 new share at ₹500 = ₹500. Total = **₹3,700** across **5 shares** = **₹740**.\nB) ₹650 is the simple average of ₹800 and ₹500. C) The price must fall after a discounted issue. D) ₹500 is the rights price itself. **The value of one right = ₹800 − ₹740 = ₹60.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Using Q72's figures, what is the value of one rights entitlement?\nA) ₹300  B) ₹60  C) ₹240  D) ₹740\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Value of a right = cum-rights price − TERP = 800 − 740 = **₹60**.\nA) ₹300 is the discount (800 − 500), which is not the same as the right's value because the right buys only one share for every four held. C) ₹240 has no basis. D) ₹740 is the TERP. **This ₹60 is what a renouncing shareholder can expect to realise.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A book-built IPO has a price band of ₹180–₹216. Is this band compliant, and why?\nA) No, the cap exceeds 120% of the floor  B) Yes — ₹216 is exactly 120% of ₹180  C) No, the band is too narrow  D) Yes, any band is allowed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 120% of ₹180 = ₹216, so the cap is **exactly at the permitted ceiling** and the band is compliant.\nA) It does not exceed the limit — it meets it precisely. C) There is no minimum band width rule of this kind. D) The 120% rule does constrain bands.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A ₹1,000 crore book-built IPO. What are the minimum amounts reserved for retail and non-institutional investors?\nA) Retail ₹350 crore; NII ₹150 crore  B) Retail ₹150 crore; NII ₹350 crore  C) Retail ₹500 crore; NII ₹150 crore  D) Retail ₹350 crore; NII ₹500 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Retail **at least 35%** = ₹350 crore; NII **at least 15%** = ₹150 crore; QIB **up to 50%** = ₹500 crore.\nB) Swaps the two minimums. C) 50% is the QIB maximum, not retail. D) ₹500 crore is the QIB portion, not NII.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A company with 20 crore shares and a net profit of ₹400 crore buys back 2 crore shares. What is the EPS before and after?\nA) ₹20 and ₹22.22  B) ₹20 and ₹18.18  C) ₹20 and ₹20  D) ₹22.22 and ₹20\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EPS before = 400 ÷ 20 = **₹20**. After the buy-back, shares = 18 crore, so EPS = 400 ÷ 18 = **₹22.22**.\nB) Divides by 22 crore, i.e. treats the buy-back as an issue. C) The share count changed, so EPS must change. D) Reverses before and after. **EPS rose about 11% — the mechanical effect of a buy-back.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An investor holds 600 shares of ₹10 face value at ₹1,500. The company announces a 1:1 bonus AND then a 1:5 split. What is the final position?\nA) 6,000 shares of ₹2 face value at ₹150  B) 1,200 shares at ₹750  C) 3,000 shares at ₹300  D) 6,000 shares of ₹10 face value at ₹150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **After the 1:1 bonus:** 1,200 shares at ₹750, face value still ₹10. **After the 1:5 split:** each ₹10 share becomes five ₹2 shares → 1,200 × 5 = **6,000 shares**, face value **₹2**, price ₹750 ÷ 5 = **₹150**. Value = 6,000 × 150 = **₹9,00,000**, equal to the original 600 × 1,500.\nB) Stops after the bonus. C) Miscomputes the split. D) Gets the count right but forgets that a **split reduces face value**. **Remember: bonus leaves face value alone; a split cuts it.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A company's IPO comprises a fresh issue of 2 crore shares and an OFS of 1.5 crore shares, priced at ₹320. How much does the company receive, and how much do the selling shareholders receive?\nA) Company ₹1,120 crore; sellers nil  B) Company ₹640 crore; sellers ₹480 crore  C) Company ₹480 crore; sellers ₹640 crore  D) Company ₹1,120 crore; sellers ₹1,120 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Fresh issue:** 2 crore × ₹320 = **₹640 crore to the company**. **OFS:** 1.5 crore × ₹320 = **₹480 crore to the selling shareholders**.\nA) Assumes the company receives everything. C) Swaps the two. D) Double counts. **Total issue size is ₹1,120 crore, but only ₹640 crore is fresh capital for the business.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Which of these correctly explains why a bonus issue increases share capital while a stock split does not?\nA) A bonus reduces face value  B) A bonus capitalises reserves into share capital at unchanged face value; a split only subdivides existing capital at reduced face value  C) A split capitalises reserves  D) Both increase share capital\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bonus:** reserves → share capital, face value constant, so share capital **rises**. **Split:** the same share capital is divided into more units of smaller face value, so share capital is **unchanged**.\nA) A bonus leaves face value unchanged. C) A split involves no reserves. D) Only the bonus increases share capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An investor buys 100 shares cum-rights at ₹900 the day before the ex-rights date. The rights are 1:2 at ₹600. What is her theoretical position on the ex-date if she subscribes fully?\nA) 150 shares at ₹800, value ₹1,20,000  B) 100 shares at ₹800  C) 150 shares at ₹900  D) 50 shares at ₹600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — TERP = (2 × 900 + 1 × 600) ÷ 3 = (1,800 + 600) ÷ 3 = 2,400 ÷ 3 = **₹800**. She holds 100 shares and subscribes to 100 ÷ 2 = **50 rights shares** → **150 shares** at ₹800 = **₹1,20,000**. Check: she paid 100 × 900 + 50 × 600 = 90,000 + 30,000 = **₹1,20,000** ✓\nB) Ignores the new shares. C) Fails to adjust the price. D) Counts only the rights shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A company declares a dividend of ₹25 per share. An investor in the 30% tax slab holds 2,000 shares. What is the post-tax dividend income (ignoring cess and TDS timing)?\nA) ₹50,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹15,000  D) ₹50,000 tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross dividend = 2,000 × ₹25 = **₹50,000**. Dividends are **taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates**: tax = 30% × 50,000 = ₹15,000. Post-tax = **₹35,000**.\nA) Ignores tax. C) Is the tax amount, not the income. D) Dividends have not been tax-free in shareholders' hands since DDT was abolished.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which sequence correctly describes what happens to a shareholder's percentage holding in each action?\nA) Bonus increases it; rights (fully subscribed) keeps it; buy-back (not participating) increases it  B) Bonus keeps it; rights fully subscribed keeps it; buy-back not participating increases it  C) All three reduce it  D) All three keep it unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bonus:** everyone gets shares pro rata, so percentages are **unchanged**. **Rights fully subscribed:** pro rata again, so **unchanged**. **Buy-back where you do not tender:** total shares fall while yours stay, so your percentage **rises**.\nA) A bonus does not raise anyone's percentage. C) None of the three reduces it under these assumptions. D) The buy-back case does change it.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A company's free-float is 40% of its 50 crore shares, and the share price is ₹600. What is the total market capitalisation and the free-float market capitalisation?\nA) ₹30,000 crore and ₹12,000 crore  B) ₹12,000 crore and ₹30,000 crore  C) ₹30,000 crore and ₹30,000 crore  D) ₹20,000 crore and ₹8,000 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Total market cap** = 50 crore × ₹600 = **₹30,000 crore**. **Free float** = 40% × 50 crore = 20 crore shares × ₹600 = **₹12,000 crore**.\nB) Swaps them. C) Ignores the free-float adjustment. D) Miscomputes the share count. **Index weight is based on the free-float figure, not the total.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A company announces a rights issue but the market price falls below the rights price before the issue closes. What is the likely outcome?\nA) Shareholders will rush to subscribe  B) Shareholders will avoid subscribing, since they can buy cheaper in the market, and the issue may be undersubscribed  C) SEBI will cancel the issue  D) The rights price automatically adjusts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the **market price falls below the rights price**, the entitlement becomes worthless and rational shareholders buy in the market instead — the issue then depends on **underwriters**.\nA) There is no incentive to pay more than the market price. C) SEBI does not cancel for this reason. D) The rights price is fixed in the offer document. **This is why rights are priced at a meaningful discount.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An acquirer buys 26% of a listed company's voting rights. Under the SEBI (SAST) Regulations, what is triggered?\nA) Nothing  B) An obligation to make an open offer to public shareholders, having crossed the prescribed threshold  C) Automatic delisting  D) A mandatory bonus issue\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Crossing the prescribed threshold (25% of voting rights) triggers a **mandatory open offer** so public shareholders can exit at the same price.\nA) The threshold has been breached. C) Delisting is a separate process. D) No such obligation exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which explains why the settlement cycle was shortened from T+2 to T+1?\nA) To increase risk  B) To reduce settlement and counterparty risk and free up investor funds and securities faster  C) To slow trading  D) It was mandated by the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A shorter cycle means **less time for a counterparty to fail** and quicker availability of funds and securities.\nA) It reduces risk. C) It improves capital efficiency, aiding activity. D) It was a **SEBI** decision — equities are SEBI's domain.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A company has ₹500 crore of reserves and ₹100 crore of share capital (10 crore shares of ₹10). It declares a 1:1 bonus. What are the post-bonus reserves and share capital?\nA) Reserves ₹400 crore; share capital ₹200 crore  B) Reserves ₹500 crore; share capital ₹200 crore  C) Reserves ₹600 crore; share capital ₹100 crore  D) Reserves ₹400 crore; share capital ₹100 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A 1:1 bonus creates 10 crore new shares of ₹10 = **₹100 crore of new share capital**, funded by capitalising **₹100 crore of reserves**. Reserves = 500 − 100 = **₹400 crore**; share capital = 100 + 100 = **₹200 crore**. **Total net worth stays ₹600 crore.**\nB) Fails to reduce reserves — the money must come from somewhere. C) Reverses the flow. D) Fails to increase share capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An investor applies for an IPO through ASBA with ₹2,00,000 blocked. The issue is oversubscribed 20 times and she receives no allotment. What happens?\nA) The money is refunded after 15 days  B) The blocked amount is simply unblocked, and she earned interest throughout  C) The money is forfeited  D) She must apply again\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under ASBA there is **no debit and therefore no refund** — the block is simply released, and interest accrued the whole time.\nA) Describes the pre-ASBA refund process. C) Application money is never forfeited. D) No re-application is needed. **This is precisely the advantage ASBA delivers.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which combination of corporate actions would leave an investor with more shares but no change in total value or percentage ownership?\nA) Buy-back and dividend  B) Bonus issue and stock split  C) Rights issue and OFS  D) Merger and delisting\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both a **bonus** and a **split** increase the share count while the price adjusts proportionately, leaving **value and percentage ownership unchanged**.\nA) A buy-back changes the share count and a dividend removes cash. C) A rights issue requires payment; an OFS is a sale by someone else. D) A merger changes the entity and delisting removes the listing.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A stock has a 5% price band. It opens at ₹200 and rises to ₹210 within minutes. What can happen for the rest of the day?\nA) It can rise indefinitely  B) It has hit the upper band and cannot trade above ₹210 today  C) The market halts  D) The stock is delisted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 5% of ₹200 = ₹10, so the upper band is **₹210** — reached, and no higher trading is permitted that day.\nA) The band is a hard limit. C) A single stock hitting its band does not halt the market — that requires an **index** move. D) Delisting is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which statement about QIPs is INCORRECT?\nA) Only QIBs may participate  B) Retail investors may participate up to ₹2 lakh  C) No draft prospectus needs to be filed with SEBI  D) Pricing follows a prescribed formula based on recent market prices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Retail investors cannot participate in a QIP at all** — it is restricted to QIBs by definition.\nA), C) and D) are all correct features of a QIP.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A demerger results in shareholders of the original company receiving shares in the new entity. What happens to the original share price on the ex-date?\nA) It stays the same  B) It falls, reflecting the value of the business transferred out  C) It doubles  D) It becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The parent no longer owns the demerged business, so its price **falls by roughly that business's value** — but shareholders receive shares in the new entity, so **total value is preserved**.\nA) The parent has lost assets. C) and D) are incorrect. **Structurally identical to a bonus: value is redistributed, not created or destroyed.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A company's promoters hold 75% and the minimum public shareholding requirement is 25%. The promoters wish to reduce their stake without diluting existing shareholders. Which mechanism fits best?\nA) A fresh issue  B) An Offer for Sale of promoter shares  C) A bonus issue  D) A buy-back\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **OFS sells existing promoter shares**, raising public shareholding **without creating new shares**, so existing shareholders are not diluted.\nA) A fresh issue creates new shares and **dilutes** everyone. C) A bonus leaves all percentages unchanged. D) A buy-back typically **raises** the promoter percentage — the opposite of what is needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which is the correct ranking of investor categories by minimum IPO allocation in a book-built issue, largest first?\nA) Retail (35%), QIB (50%), NII (15%)  B) QIB (up to 50%), retail (at least 35%), NII (at least 15%)  C) NII (50%), QIB (35%), retail (15%)  D) All equal at 33.3%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **QIB up to 50% > retail at least 35% > NII at least 15%.**\nA) Lists the right numbers but in the wrong rank order. C) Scrambles the categories. D) There is no equal allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor buys shares two days before the record date under T+1 settlement. Is she entitled to the dividend?\nA) No  B) Yes — her trade settles before the record date, so she is on the register  C) Only if she holds for a year  D) Only with company approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buying two days before the record date means settlement (T+1) occurs **one day before** the record date, so she appears on the register and is **entitled**.\nA) She bought before the ex-date. C) There is no holding-period condition for dividend entitlement. D) No approval is needed. **The test is whether you are on the register on the record date.**\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which best explains why free-float weighting is preferred to full market-cap weighting for an index?\nA) It is simpler to compute  B) It reflects the shares actually available for trading, so the index is investable and replicable by funds  C) It gives promoters more weight  D) It excludes institutional holdings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An index fund can only buy shares that **trade**. Weighting by free float makes the index **investable and replicable**.\nA) It is more complex, not less. C) It gives promoters **less** weight by excluding their holdings. D) Institutional holdings are freely tradable and are included.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A company simultaneously announces a rights issue at a discount and a bonus issue. Which affects the company's cash and which does not?\nA) Both raise cash  B) The rights issue raises cash; the bonus issue does not  C) The bonus issue raises cash; the rights issue does not  D) Neither raises cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Shareholders **pay** for rights shares (cash **in**); bonus shares are **free**, funded by capitalising reserves (**no cash movement**).\nA) A bonus raises nothing. C) Reverses the two. D) A rights issue definitely raises cash.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An investor holds 2,000 shares. The company announces a buy-back of 10% of outstanding shares via tender offer at a 20% premium. If she tenders proportionately and the acceptance ratio is 10%, how many shares are bought back from her?\nA) 2,000  B) 200  C) 20  D) 400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **10% acceptance ratio** on 2,000 shares means **200 shares** are accepted; the remaining 1,800 are returned to her.\nA) Assumes full acceptance, which a proportionate tender offer does not give. C) Uses 1%. D) Uses 20%. **She realises the 20% premium on 200 shares and continues to hold 1,800.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A company's shares trade at ₹1,200 and it announces a 1:2 rights issue at ₹900. An investor holding 400 shares decides to renounce her entire entitlement. Approximately what value should she realise?\nA) ₹1,20,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹60,000  D) ₹1,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TERP = (2 × 1,200 + 1 × 900) ÷ 3 = (2,400 + 900) ÷ 3 = 3,300 ÷ 3 = **₹1,100**. Value of one right = 1,200 − 1,100 = **₹100**. Her entitlement = 400 ÷ 2 = **200 rights**. Value = 200 × ₹100 = **₹20,000**.\nA) ₹1,20,000 wrongly uses the full share price. C) ₹60,000 uses the ₹300 discount on 200 rights, which overstates the right's value. D) ₹1,80,000 has no basis. **Renouncing captures the right's value; letting it lapse forfeits it entirely.**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single question best distinguishes every corporate action in this chapter?\nA) Does the share price change?  B) Does money move, and does the shareholder's total value change?  C) Is SEBI involved?  D) Is the company listed?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These two tests classify them all. **Money moves:** rights (in), dividend (out), buy-back (out), OFS (to the seller). **No money moves:** bonus, split, demerger. **Total value unchanged:** bonus, split, demerger. **Value changes:** dividend (cash received separately), buy-back, rights.\nA) The price changes in nearly all of them, so it does not discriminate. C) SEBI is involved throughout. D) All these actions concern listed companies.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Track the share count, the price and the total value at every step.\n\n### Case: Corporate Actions at Meridian Industries Ltd\n\nPriya holds **3,000 shares** of Meridian Industries Ltd, of **₹10 face value**, currently trading at **₹1,600**.\n\nOver the next twelve months Meridian announces the following, in sequence:\n\n1. A **final dividend of ₹32 per share**.\n2. A **1:2 bonus issue** (one free share for every two held).\n3. A **1:5 stock split**.\n4. A **1:4 rights issue at ₹300 per share** (based on the post-split holding).\n\nPriya is in the **30% income tax slab**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Priya's post-tax dividend income from step 1?\nA) ₹96,000  B) ₹67,200  C) ₹28,800  D) ₹96,000, tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gross dividend = 3,000 × ₹32 = **₹96,000**. Dividends are **taxable in the shareholder's hands at slab rates**: tax = 30% × 96,000 = **₹28,800**. Post-tax = 96,000 − 28,800 = **₹67,200**.\nA) Ignores tax entirely. C) Is the tax amount, not the income. D) Dividends have been taxable in shareholders' hands since Dividend Distribution Tax was abolished — TDS also applies above the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** After the 1:2 bonus, how many shares does Priya hold and at what adjusted price (assume the price before the bonus is ₹1,600)?\nA) 4,500 shares at ₹1,066.67  B) 6,000 shares at ₹800  C) 4,500 shares at ₹1,600  D) 1,500 shares at ₹3,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Bonus shares = 3,000 × (1 ÷ 2) = **1,500**. Total = 3,000 + 1,500 = **4,500 shares**. Value before = 3,000 × 1,600 = **₹48,00,000**; adjusted price = 48,00,000 ÷ 4,500 = **₹1,066.67**. Value unchanged ✓\nB) Assumes a 1:1 bonus. C) Fails to adjust the price — the commonest error. D) Reduces the share count, which a bonus never does.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** After the 1:5 split, what are Priya's share count, face value and price?\nA) 22,500 shares, ₹2 face value, ₹213.33  B) 22,500 shares, ₹10 face value, ₹213.33  C) 900 shares, ₹50 face value, ₹5,333  D) 4,500 shares, ₹2 face value, ₹1,066.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **1:5 split** turns each ₹10 share into five ₹2 shares: 4,500 × 5 = **22,500 shares**, face value **₹10 ÷ 5 = ₹2**, price ₹1,066.67 ÷ 5 = **₹213.33**. Value = 22,500 × 213.33 = **₹48,00,000**, still unchanged.\nB) Gets the count right but **forgets that a split reduces face value** — the key contrast with a bonus. C) Reverses the split. D) Fails to increase the share count.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Priya is entitled to a 1:4 rights issue at ₹300 on her post-split holding. How many rights shares can she buy, and what is the theoretical ex-rights price?\nA) 5,625 shares; TERP ₹213.33  B) 5,625 shares; TERP ₹230.66  C) 4,500 shares; TERP ₹300  D) 22,500 shares; TERP ₹256.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Entitlement = 22,500 ÷ 4 = **5,625 rights shares** at ₹300.\n**TERP** = (4 old shares × ₹213.33 + 1 new share × ₹300) ÷ 5 = (853.32 + 300) ÷ 5 = 1,153.32 ÷ 5 = **₹230.66**.\n\nA) ₹213.33 is the **pre-rights** price — issuing new shares at a *different* price must move it. C) Miscounts the entitlement and simply quotes the rights price. D) Assumes a 1:1 entitlement.\n\n> ⚠️ **Notice something unusual:** the TERP (₹230.66) is **higher** than the pre-rights price (₹213.33), because the rights price of ₹300 is **above** the market price. That is the reverse of a normal rights issue — and it means these rights have **no value**. See QC5.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given that the rights price of ₹300 exceeds the post-split market price of ₹213.33, what should Priya do?\nA) Subscribe fully, since rights are always beneficial  B) Decline the rights — she can buy the same shares more cheaply in the open market  C) Renounce the rights for a large premium  D) Sue the company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A rights issue is only attractive when the **rights price is below the market price**. Here she would pay **₹300** for a share worth **₹213.33** — an immediate loss of ₹86.67 per share. She should simply buy in the market if she wants more shares.\nA) Rights are beneficial **only at a discount**. C) The rights have **no value** and cannot be renounced for a premium — nobody will pay for the right to overpay. D) There is nothing unlawful; badly priced rights issues simply fail and fall to the underwriters.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** rights issues carry value **only when priced below market**. In the earlier questions the rights price sat well below market and each right was worth ₹60–₹100; here the sequence of bonus and split pushed the market price down to ₹213.33, leaving the ₹300 rights price stranded above it. **Always compare the rights price with the CURRENT (post-adjustment) market price, not the original one.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "07", "title": "Introduction to Investments", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks across Chapters 7–10).**\n> This chapter is the **map of the asset classes** and the **three routes to managed money (MF / PMS / AIF)**. The comparison table at the end is one of the most reliably examined items in the whole syllabus.\n\n---\n\n## 7.1 Types of Investment\n\nEvery investment can be placed on two axes: **what you own** and **how you own it**.\n\n### What you own — the asset classes\n\n| Asset class | You are… | Return comes from |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Equity** | An **owner** of the business | Capital appreciation + dividends |\n| **Fixed income (debt)** | A **lender** | Interest + capital gain/loss on price |\n| **Commodities** | An owner of a **physical good** | Price movement only |\n| **Real estate** | An owner of **property** | Rent + capital appreciation |\n| **Cash / money market** | A very short-term lender | Interest |\n\n> 🧠 **The single most useful distinction in finance: equity = ownership; debt = lending.** An owner gets what is left after everyone else is paid (residual claim, unlimited upside, first loss). A lender gets a fixed promise (contractual claim, capped upside, paid before owners).\n\n### The risk–return spectrum\n\n$$\\text{Higher expected return} \\Longleftrightarrow \\text{Higher risk}$$\n\nRoughly ascending: cash → government debt → high-grade corporate debt → high-yield debt → real estate → equity → derivatives/distressed.\n\n> ⚠️ **Never** treat this as a promise. Higher risk means a **wider range of outcomes**, including worse ones. If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 Equity\n\n**Investment characteristics:**\n- **Residual claim** — paid last, after lenders and preference shareholders\n- **Unlimited upside**, but the whole capital is at risk\n- **No maturity** — a perpetual instrument\n- **Voting rights** (ordinary shares) — a say in governance\n- **High volatility** in the short run; historically the best long-run inflation-beater\n- Returns are **not contractual** — dividends are discretionary\n\n**Role in a portfolio:** the **growth engine**. Suitable for goals **5–7 years or more** away, where volatility has time to average out.\n\n### Listed vs unlisted equity\n\n| | **Listed** | **Unlisted** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Traded on** | A stock exchange | Private transactions |\n| **Liquidity** | High — exit any trading day | Very low |\n| **Price discovery** | Continuous, public | Negotiated, infrequent |\n| **Disclosure** | Extensive, regulated | Minimal |\n| **Valuation** | Market price | Estimated, subjective |\n| **Regulation** | SEBI, listing rules | Limited |\n\nUnlisted equity (private equity, venture capital, start-ups) may offer higher returns but carries **illiquidity, valuation and information risk**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Fixed Income\n\n**Investment characteristics:**\n- A **contractual** obligation to pay interest and repay principal\n- **Predictable cash flows** — the main attraction\n- **Capped upside** — you can never earn more than the promised yield if held to maturity\n- **Senior** to equity in the event of liquidation\n- Has a **defined maturity**\n- Prices move **inversely** to interest rates\n\n**Role in a portfolio:** **stability, income and capital preservation**. Suitable for short-horizon goals and for dampening overall portfolio volatility.\n\n### Government vs corporate debt\n\n| | **Government** | **Corporate** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Credit risk** | Effectively nil (sovereign) | Real — the issuer can default |\n| **Yield** | Lower | Higher (a **credit spread** over government) |\n| **Liquidity** | High (G-secs) | Varies, often lower |\n\nThe extra yield on corporate debt is the **credit spread** — compensation for default risk.\n\n### Investment grade vs high yield\n\n| | **Investment grade** | **High yield (\"junk\")** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rating** | **BBB−/Baa3 and above** | Below BBB− |\n| **Default risk** | Low | Elevated |\n| **Yield** | Lower | Higher |\n| **Behaviour in a crisis** | Defensive | Behaves more like equity |\n\n> ⚠️ **BBB− is the dividing line.** A downgrade from BBB− to BB+ (\"falling angel\") can force many institutional investors to sell, because mandates often prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 Commodities\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- **No cash flow** — no interest, no dividend, no rent. The entire return is **price movement**.\n- Often carries **storage and insurance costs** (a negative carry).\n- Historically an **inflation hedge**, especially energy and metals.\n- **Low correlation** with equity and debt, so it can improve diversification.\n- Access routes in India: **gold ETFs, Sovereign Gold Bonds, commodity derivatives**, and physical holding.\n\n**Role:** diversification and inflation protection — typically a **small satellite allocation**, not a core holding.\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 Real Estate\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Generates **rental income** plus potential **capital appreciation**\n- **Large ticket size** — poor divisibility (you cannot sell one bedroom)\n- **Highly illiquid** — sale takes months\n- **High transaction costs** — stamp duty, registration, brokerage\n- **Location-specific** and hard to diversify\n- Valuation is **subjective**, with no continuous price discovery\n\n**Route to liquidity: REITs** (Real Estate Investment Trusts) — listed, SEBI-regulated trusts holding rent-yielding property. They give **exposure to real estate with the liquidity of a listed security** and a small ticket size. **InvITs** do the same for infrastructure assets.\n\n> ⚠️ A **self-occupied house is consumption, not investment** — it produces no income and cannot be partly liquidated (see Chapter 3's financial assets ratio).\n\n---\n\n## 7.6 Structured Products\n\nA **structured product** is a pre-packaged instrument combining a **bond (or deposit)** with a **derivative**, engineered to deliver a specific payoff.\n\n**Typical construction:** most of the money goes into a zero-coupon bond that grows back to par (providing capital protection), and the small remainder buys options on an index for the upside.\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|\n| Customised payoff profiles | **Complex** and hard to value |\n| Possible capital protection | **Credit risk of the issuer** — \"capital protected\" is only as good as the issuer |\n| Access to otherwise hard-to-reach exposures | **Illiquid**, often with no secondary market |\n| | Costs and margins are often **opaque** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam point:** \"capital protection\" is **not** a guarantee — it depends entirely on the **issuer's creditworthiness**. And structured products are typically **not principal-protected before maturity**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.7 Distressed Securities\n\nSecurities of companies in or near **financial distress, default or bankruptcy**.\n\n- Trade at **deep discounts** to face value\n- Potential for very high returns if the company recovers or the recovery in resolution exceeds the purchase price\n- **Very high risk** of total loss\n- Require **specialist legal and restructuring expertise** — resolution under the **Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016** in India\n- **Highly illiquid**\n\n**Suitability:** sophisticated, institutional or very high-net-worth investors only — typically accessed through **Category II or III AIFs**.\n\n---\n\n## 7.8 Other Investment Opportunities\n\n**Art, paintings, antiques, collectibles, wine, vintage cars.**\n\n- **No cash flow**; return is purely price appreciation\n- **Highly illiquid** with wide bid–ask spreads\n- **Valuation is subjective** and expertise-dependent\n- **Authenticity and storage/insurance risk**\n- **Unregulated** — no investor protection framework\n- Often driven by **taste and scarcity** rather than fundamentals\n\n**Suitability:** a **passion allocation** for wealthy investors, not a core portfolio holding.\n\n---\n\n## 7.9 ⭐ Channels for Making Investments\n\n### Direct investments\nThe investor buys securities themselves — shares, bonds, deposits.\n\n| Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|\n| **Lowest cost** — no management fee | Requires **time, skill and temperament** |\n| Full **control** | Hard to **diversify** with a small corpus |\n| Complete **transparency** | All research and monitoring is the investor's own |\n\n### The role of Registered Investment Advisers (RIAs)\nAn RIA provides **advice**, for a **fee from the client**, under a **fiduciary duty**, with **advice segregated from distribution**. The investor still holds the assets directly; the adviser supplies the strategy, discipline and review.\n\n### Investments through managed portfolios\n\nThree vehicles — and the comparison below is **essential exam material**.\n\n---\n\n## ⭐ MF vs PMS vs AIF — The Comparison Table\n\n| Feature | **Mutual Fund** | **Portfolio Management Service** | **Alternative Investment Fund** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Minimum investment** | As low as **₹100–₹500** (SIP) | **₹50 lakh** | **₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for employees/directors of the AIF) |\n| **What you hold** | **Units** of a pooled scheme | **Securities in your own name / demat account** | **Units** of the fund |\n| **Pooling** | Fully pooled | **Not pooled** — an individual portfolio | Pooled |\n| **Customisation** | None — everyone gets the same portfolio | **Possible** (especially non-discretionary) | Limited — defined by the fund's strategy |\n| **Regulation** | SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, **1996** | SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, **2020** | SEBI (AIF) Regulations, **2012** |\n| **Target investor** | Retail | HNI | HNI / institutional / sophisticated |\n| **Liquidity** | High (open-ended: any business day) | Moderate | Low — usually **close-ended with a lock-in** |\n| **Disclosure** | Highest — daily NAV, monthly portfolio | Moderate, to the client | Lowest — to investors only |\n| **Fees** | Capped **TER** | Fixed and/or **performance-linked** | Management + **carried interest** |\n| **Strategy freedom** | Most restricted | Moderate | **Widest** — can use leverage, derivatives, unlisted assets |\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line memory hook:**\n> **MF = pooled units, ₹500, retail, most regulated.**\n> **PMS = your own securities, ₹50 lakh, HNI.**\n> **AIF = pooled units, ₹1 crore, sophisticated, widest freedom.**\n>\n> Note the ladder: **as the minimum investment rises, regulation and disclosure fall and strategy freedom rises.** That is the whole logic — regulators protect small investors most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Equity vs debt | **Ownership** (residual claim) vs **lending** (contractual claim) |\n| Equity's role | The **growth engine**; for goals 5–7+ years away |\n| Debt's role | **Stability, income, capital preservation** |\n| Investment grade cut-off | **BBB− / Baa3** and above |\n| Credit spread | Extra yield on corporate over government debt, compensating **default risk** |\n| Bond price vs interest rates | Move **inversely** |\n| Commodities | **No cash flow**; return is price movement; inflation hedge; low correlation |\n| Real estate drawbacks | Illiquid, indivisible, high transaction costs, location-specific, subjective valuation |\n| REIT / InvIT | Real estate / infrastructure exposure with **listed liquidity** and small ticket size |\n| Self-occupied house | **Consumption**, not investment |\n| Structured product | A **bond + derivative** package; \"capital protection\" depends on **issuer credit** |\n| Distressed securities | Deep discount, very high risk, needs specialist skill; **IBC, 2016** |\n| Art/collectibles | No cash flow, illiquid, subjective, **unregulated** — a passion allocation |\n| Direct investing | Cheapest, but needs time, skill and temperament |\n| RIA | **Fee from the client**, **fiduciary**, advice **segregated** from distribution |\n| MF minimum | ₹100–₹500 |\n| PMS minimum | **₹50 lakh** |\n| AIF minimum | **₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for AIF employees/directors) |\n| What PMS holds | Securities **in the client's own name** — not pooled |\n| Regulation ladder | Higher minimum → **less** regulation and disclosure, **more** strategy freedom |\n\n> **Exam tip:** most questions in this chapter are **discrimination** questions — \"which of these is characteristic of X but not Y\". Learn the *contrasts* (equity vs debt, listed vs unlisted, investment grade vs high yield, MF vs PMS vs AIF) rather than isolated facts.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The MF vs PMS vs AIF table is the single highest-yield item in this chapter.**\n\n**Q1.** State the fundamental difference between equity and debt.\n> **A:** **Equity = ownership** — a **residual** claim, paid last, unlimited upside, first loss. **Debt = lending** — a **contractual** claim, paid before owners, capped upside.\n\n**Q2.** What is equity's role in a portfolio, and what horizon suits it?\n> **A:** The **growth engine** and long-run inflation-beater. Suitable for goals **5–7 years or more** away, where short-run volatility has time to average out.\n\n**Q3.** What is debt's role in a portfolio?\n> **A:** **Stability, income and capital preservation** — and dampening overall portfolio volatility. Suitable for short-horizon goals.\n\n**Q4.** Give four differences between listed and unlisted equity.\n> **A:** **Liquidity** (high vs very low), **price discovery** (continuous and public vs negotiated), **disclosure** (extensive vs minimal), and **valuation** (market price vs subjective estimate).\n\n**Q5.** What is the credit spread?\n> **A:** The **extra yield** a corporate bond offers over a comparable government bond — compensation for **default (credit) risk**.\n\n**Q6.** What is the dividing line between investment grade and high yield?\n> **A:** **BBB− / Baa3.** At or above is **investment grade**; below is **high yield (\"junk\")**.\n\n**Q7.** Why does a downgrade from BBB− to BB+ matter so much?\n> **A:** Many institutional mandates **prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings**, so a \"falling angel\" can trigger forced selling well beyond what the credit change alone would justify.\n\n**Q8.** How do bond prices respond to interest rates?\n> **A:** **Inversely** — when rates rise, existing bond prices fall, and vice versa.\n\n**Q9.** What is distinctive about commodities as an asset class?\n> **A:** They generate **no cash flow** — no interest, dividend or rent — so the **entire return is price movement**. They often carry storage/insurance costs, act as an **inflation hedge**, and have **low correlation** with equity and debt.\n\n**Q10.** How can an Indian investor gain commodity exposure?\n> **A:** **Gold ETFs, Sovereign Gold Bonds, commodity derivatives**, and physical holding.\n\n**Q11.** Name five drawbacks of direct real estate investment.\n> **A:** **Illiquidity**, **indivisibility** (large ticket size), **high transaction costs** (stamp duty, registration, brokerage), **location concentration**, and **subjective valuation** with no continuous price discovery.\n\n**Q12.** What is a REIT, and what problem does it solve?\n> **A:** A listed, SEBI-regulated trust holding rent-yielding property. It solves real estate's **illiquidity and large ticket size** — giving property exposure with the **liquidity of a listed security**. **InvITs** do the same for infrastructure.\n\n**Q13.** Is a self-occupied house an investment?\n> **A:** **No — it is consumption.** It produces no income and cannot be partly liquidated.\n\n**Q14.** What is a structured product, and how is it typically built?\n> **A:** A pre-packaged combination of a **bond (or deposit) plus a derivative**. Typically most of the money buys a **zero-coupon bond** that grows back to par (capital protection), and the remainder buys **options** for upside.\n\n**Q15.** Why is \"capital protection\" in a structured product not a guarantee?\n> **A:** It depends entirely on the **issuer's creditworthiness** — if the issuer defaults, the protection fails. Such products are also typically **not principal-protected before maturity**.\n\n**Q16.** What are distressed securities, and who should hold them?\n> **A:** Securities of companies in or near **default or bankruptcy**, trading at deep discounts. Very high risk of total loss, requiring **specialist legal and restructuring expertise** (in India, resolution under the **IBC, 2016**). Suitable only for **sophisticated/institutional investors**, typically via Category II or III AIFs.\n\n**Q17.** What are the drawbacks of art and collectibles as investments?\n> **A:** **No cash flow**, **highly illiquid** with wide spreads, **subjective valuation**, **authenticity and storage risk**, and **completely unregulated** — no investor protection framework.\n\n**Q18.** What are the trade-offs of investing directly?\n> **A:** **Lowest cost** and full control, but it demands **time, skill and temperament**, and diversification is hard with a small corpus.\n\n**Q19.** What defines a Registered Investment Adviser's model?\n> **A:** **Advice for a fee paid by the client**, under a **fiduciary duty**, with **advice segregated from distribution**.\n\n**Q20.** State the minimum investment for a mutual fund, PMS and AIF.\n> **A:** **MF ₹100–₹500** · **PMS ₹50 lakh** · **AIF ₹1 crore** (₹25 lakh for employees/directors of the AIF).\n\n**Q21.** What is the crucial structural difference between a mutual fund and a PMS?\n> **A:** A mutual fund gives you **units of a pooled scheme**. A PMS holds **securities in your own name / demat account** — it is **not pooled**.\n\n**Q22.** Which regulations govern MFs, PMS and AIFs, and from which years?\n> **A:** SEBI **(Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**; SEBI **(Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**; SEBI **(Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012**.\n\n**Q23.** Rank MF, PMS and AIF by disclosure and by strategy freedom.\n> **A:** **Disclosure:** MF (highest — daily NAV, monthly portfolio) > PMS > AIF (lowest). **Strategy freedom:** AIF (widest — leverage, derivatives, unlisted) > PMS > MF (most restricted).\n\n**Q24.** What is the underlying logic of the MF/PMS/AIF regulatory ladder?\n> **A:** As the **minimum investment rises**, regulation and disclosure **fall** and strategy freedom **rises** — regulators protect small investors most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\n\n**Q25.** Which asset classes produce no cash flow at all?\n> **A:** **Commodities** and **art/collectibles** — their entire return is price appreciation.\n\n**Q26.** How are MF, PMS and AIF fees structured?\n> **A:** **MF** — a capped **Total Expense Ratio**. **PMS** — fixed and/or **performance-linked** fees. **AIF** — management fee plus **carried interest**.\n\n**Q27.** State the risk–return principle and its critical caveat.\n> **A:** **Higher expected return goes with higher risk.** The caveat: higher risk means a **wider range of outcomes, including worse ones** — it is never a promise of higher return. If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n\n**Q28.** Roughly order the asset classes by ascending risk.\n> **A:** **Cash → government debt → high-grade corporate debt → high-yield debt → real estate → equity → derivatives/distressed.**\n\n**Q29.** Why does equity have no maturity?\n> **A:** It is a **perpetual** instrument representing **ownership** — there is no date on which the company must repay a shareholder, unlike a bond's principal.\n\n**Q30.** Which is senior in liquidation — equity or debt?\n> **A:** **Debt.** Lenders are paid before preference shareholders, who are paid before ordinary shareholders. Equity holders receive only the **residual**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 7: Introduction to Investments — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Scenario & Discrimination). **Most questions here test contrasts**, especially MF vs PMS vs AIF. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** An equity shareholder has:\nA) A contractual claim to fixed interest  B) A residual claim on the company's profits and assets  C) Priority over lenders in liquidation  D) A guaranteed dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity is **ownership**, carrying a **residual** claim — shareholders are paid only after all other claimants.\nA) Describes debt. C) Reverses the priority — lenders rank **ahead** of shareholders. D) Dividends are discretionary, never guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** A bondholder is:\nA) An owner of the company  B) A lender to the company  C) A director  D) A regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt means **lending**: a contractual right to interest and repayment of principal.\nA) Describes equity. C) and D) are unrelated roles.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The minimum investment in a Portfolio Management Service is:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SEBI prescribes a **₹50 lakh** minimum for PMS.\nA) and B) are below the threshold. D) ₹1 crore is the **AIF** minimum — the classic confusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The minimum investment in an Alternative Investment Fund is generally:\nA) ₹50 lakh  B) ₹1 crore  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹1 crore**, reduced to ₹25 lakh for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager.\nA) Is the PMS minimum. C) and D) are not the prescribed thresholds.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In a Portfolio Management Service, the securities are held:\nA) In a pooled scheme  B) In the client's own name / demat account  C) By SEBI  D) By the stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMS is **not pooled**; the client holds the securities directly. This is its defining structural difference from a mutual fund.\nA) Describes a mutual fund or AIF. C) and D) never hold client securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The dividing line between investment grade and high yield debt is:\nA) AAA  B) A−  C) BBB−  D) B+\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **BBB− (or Baa3)** and above is investment grade; below it is high yield.\nA) AAA is the top rating, not the boundary. B) and D) sit on either side of the boundary but are not it.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which asset class produces no cash flow?\nA) Equity  B) Fixed income  C) Commodities  D) Real estate (let out)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Commodities pay **no interest, dividend or rent** — the entire return is price movement.\nA) Equity can pay dividends. B) Debt pays interest. D) Let-out property generates rent.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Bond prices and interest rates move:\nA) In the same direction  B) Inversely  C) Independently  D) Only upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When rates rise, existing bonds paying lower coupons become less attractive, so their prices **fall**.\nA) and C) are incorrect. D) Bond prices certainly fall as well as rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A REIT provides:\nA) Direct ownership of a flat  B) Real estate exposure with the liquidity of a listed security  C) Guaranteed rental income  D) Commodity exposure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **REIT** is a listed, SEBI-regulated trust holding rent-yielding property, solving real estate's illiquidity and large ticket size.\nA) A REIT gives units, not a specific property. C) Distributions depend on rental performance. D) That would be a commodity ETF.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A structured product typically combines:\nA) Two equities  B) A bond (or deposit) with a derivative  C) Two bonds  D) Gold and silver\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most of the money goes into a **zero-coupon bond** (for capital protection) and the remainder into **options** (for upside).\nA), C) and D) do not describe structured products.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** \"Capital protection\" in a structured product depends on:\nA) SEBI's guarantee  B) The issuer's creditworthiness  C) The stock market  D) The RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The protection is only a **promise by the issuer**; if the issuer defaults, it fails.\nA) SEBI regulates but guarantees nothing. C) The bond leg, not the market, provides the protection. D) The RBI has no role here.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Distressed securities are:\nA) Securities of highly rated companies  B) Securities of companies in or near default, trading at deep discounts  C) Government bonds  D) Bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They trade at deep discounts, carry very high risk of total loss, and need specialist restructuring expertise.\nA), C) and D) all describe low-risk instruments — the opposite of distressed.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Which of these is a mutual fund's structural feature?\nA) Securities held in the investor's own demat account  B) Investors hold units of a pooled scheme  C) A ₹50 lakh minimum  D) No regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds are **fully pooled**; investors hold **units**.\nA) Describes a PMS. C) Is the PMS minimum. D) Mutual funds are the **most** regulated of the three vehicles.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A Registered Investment Adviser is paid:\nA) By commission from the product manufacturer  B) By a fee from the client  C) By SEBI  D) By the stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An RIA charges the **client** a fee, operates under a **fiduciary** duty, and must **segregate advice from distribution**.\nA) That is a distributor's model, and mixing the two is precisely what segregation prevents. C) and D) do not pay advisers.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which regulations govern mutual funds in India?\nA) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  B) SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020  C) SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012  D) The Companies Act, 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**.\nB) Governs PMS. C) Governs AIFs. D) Governs companies generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Unlisted equity is characterised by:\nA) High liquidity  B) Very low liquidity and subjective valuation  C) Daily price discovery  D) Extensive public disclosure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without an exchange there is no ready market and no continuous price.\nA), C) and D) all describe **listed** equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The credit spread is:\nA) The difference between two equity prices  B) The extra yield on corporate debt over government debt  C) A brokerage fee  D) A type of derivative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It compensates the lender for **default risk**.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Equity is best suited to goals that are:\nA) Less than 1 year away  B) 5–7 years or more away  C) Exactly 2 years away  D) Already due\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity's short-run volatility needs a **long horizon** to average out.\nA), C) and D) are all far too short — a badly timed drawdown would leave the goal unfunded.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which of these is NOT a characteristic of real estate?\nA) High transaction costs  B) Illiquidity  C) Easy divisibility  D) Location concentration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Real estate is **indivisible** — you cannot sell one bedroom.\nA), B) and D) are all genuine characteristics.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A self-occupied house is best classified as:\nA) An investment asset  B) Consumption  C) A liquid asset  D) A financial asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It produces **no income** and cannot be partly liquidated.\nA) Investment assets generate returns or income. C) Property is highly illiquid. D) It is a **physical**, not financial, asset.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** An InvIT provides exposure to:\nA) Equity shares  B) Infrastructure assets  C) Gold  D) Government bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **InvIT** does for infrastructure what a REIT does for real estate.\nA), C) and D) are covered by other instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which vehicle offers the widest strategy freedom?\nA) Mutual fund  B) PMS  C) AIF  D) Bank deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **AIFs** can use leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets far more freely than the other vehicles.\nA) Mutual funds are the **most restricted**. B) PMS sits in between. D) A deposit has no strategy at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which vehicle has the highest disclosure requirements?\nA) AIF  B) PMS  C) Mutual fund  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mutual funds publish **daily NAV and monthly portfolios** — far more than PMS or AIF.\nA) AIFs disclose least. B) PMS sits in between. D) They differ substantially.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Sovereign Gold Bonds give exposure to:\nA) Equity  B) Gold  C) Real estate  D) Corporate debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGBs are a route to **gold** exposure without holding physical metal.\nA), C) and D) are different asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** In liquidation, the order of payment is:\nA) Equity → preference → debt  B) Debt → preference → equity  C) Preference → debt → equity  D) All simultaneously\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Lenders first**, then preference shareholders, then ordinary shareholders (the residual).\nA) and C) invert the seniority. D) Claims are ranked, not simultaneous.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Art and collectibles are characterised by:\nA) Regular cash flow  B) No cash flow, illiquidity and subjective valuation  C) Daily price discovery  D) Strong regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They pay nothing, trade rarely at wide spreads, and are valued subjectively.\nA) They generate no income. C) There is no continuous market. D) They are **unregulated**.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which of these has the lowest credit risk?\nA) A high-yield corporate bond  B) A government security  C) An unrated debenture  D) A distressed bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sovereign debt carries **effectively nil** credit risk in domestic currency.\nA), C) and D) all carry material or extreme default risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** The main advantage of investing directly rather than through a managed vehicle is:\nA) Guaranteed returns  B) Lowest cost, with no management fee  C) Automatic diversification  D) No effort required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Direct investing avoids management fees entirely.\nA) No route guarantees returns. C) Diversification is **harder** directly with a small corpus. D) Direct investing requires the **most** effort.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which asset class is the traditional long-run inflation-beater?\nA) Cash  B) Equity  C) Government bonds  D) Bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity's returns have historically outpaced inflation over long periods.\nA), C) and D) typically deliver low or negative **real** returns after tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The AIF minimum investment for employees and directors of the AIF is:\nA) ₹1 crore  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹25 lakh  D) ₹5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A concession to **₹25 lakh** applies to employees and directors of the AIF or its manager.\nA) Is the general minimum. B) Is the PMS minimum. D) Is not a prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which statement about commodities is correct?\nA) They pay regular interest  B) They often carry storage and insurance costs  C) They are risk-free  D) They are highly correlated with equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Physical commodities incur **storage and insurance** costs — a negative carry.\nA) They pay nothing. C) Commodity prices are highly volatile. D) Their **low** correlation with equity is precisely why they diversify.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Distressed debt resolution in India operates primarily under:\nA) The Companies Act, 1956  B) The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016  C) FEMA  D) The SEBI Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **IBC, 2016** provides the resolution framework.\nA) Has largely been superseded. C) Governs foreign exchange. D) Governs securities markets.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Which vehicle allows a customised portfolio for an individual client?\nA) A mutual fund  B) A PMS, especially non-discretionary  C) An index fund  D) An ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because PMS holds securities individually rather than pooling them, the portfolio **can be customised**.\nA), C) and D) all give every investor the same pooled portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations are from the year:\nA) 1996  B) 2012  C) 2020  D) 1992\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The current **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**.\nA) Mutual Funds. B) AIFs. D) The SEBI Act itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Higher expected return is associated with:\nA) Lower risk  B) Higher risk — a wider range of outcomes, including worse ones  C) No risk  D) Guaranteed outcomes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk means **dispersion of outcomes**, not a promise of more.\nA) and C) invert the relationship. D) If risk guaranteed return, it would not be risk.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A client with ₹40 lakh wants a professionally managed portfolio of individually held securities. What can you tell her?\nA) She qualifies for a PMS  B) She falls below the ₹50 lakh PMS minimum, so a mutual fund is the appropriate route  C) She qualifies for an AIF  D) She must invest directly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹40 lakh is below the ₹50 lakh PMS minimum**, so PMS is not available. Mutual funds have no such barrier and offer professional management.\nA) She is ₹10 lakh short. C) AIFs need ₹1 crore — even further out of reach. D) Direct investing is possible but not the only alternative.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A client wants exposure to commercial real estate but has only ₹2 lakh and needs liquidity. The best route is:\nA) Buying a shop  B) A listed REIT  C) An unlisted property fund  D) Physical gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **REIT** provides commercial property exposure at a **small ticket size** with **listed liquidity**.\nA) ₹2 lakh cannot buy commercial property, and it would be illiquid. C) Unlisted funds have high minimums and lock-ins. D) Gold is a different asset class entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Which pairing correctly matches vehicle to minimum investment?\nA) MF ₹50 lakh; PMS ₹1 crore; AIF ₹500  B) MF ₹500; PMS ₹50 lakh; AIF ₹1 crore  C) MF ₹1 crore; PMS ₹500; AIF ₹50 lakh  D) All three ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The ladder is **₹500 → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore**.\nA) and C) scramble the order. D) The minimums differ sharply, which is the whole point of the ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor holds a BBB− rated bond that is downgraded to BB+. What is the likely consequence?\nA) The price rises  B) The bond falls below investment grade, potentially triggering forced selling by mandate-constrained institutions, so the price falls  C) Nothing changes  D) The coupon rises automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Crossing **below BBB−** makes it high yield. Many institutional mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings, so the technical selling pressure often exceeds the credit deterioration alone.\nA) Downgrades push prices down. C) The rating boundary matters enormously. D) The coupon is contractually fixed.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A client asks why a \"capital protected\" structured product is not risk-free. The best answer is:\nA) It is risk-free  B) The protection is only the issuer's promise, so the client bears the issuer's credit risk — and protection typically applies only at maturity  C) SEBI does not allow such products  D) The stock market guarantees it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two points: **issuer credit risk**, and the fact that protection generally applies **only if held to maturity**.\nA) It carries real risk. C) Such products are permitted. D) No market guarantees principal.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Which client is best suited to distressed securities?\nA) A 25-year-old starting their first SIP  B) A retiree needing monthly income  C) A sophisticated HNI with specialist advice, investing a small satellite allocation via a Category II or III AIF  D) A client with a 6-month goal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Distressed investing needs **legal and restructuring expertise**, tolerance for total loss, and a long, illiquid horizon.\nA) A beginner should not start here. B) Distressed securities pay nothing reliably. D) They are entirely illiquid over 6 months.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client says \"equity is risky, so I'll put my 20-year retirement money in fixed deposits\". The best response is:\nA) Agreed — FDs are always safer  B) Over 20 years the greater risk is inflation eroding purchasing power; FDs often deliver a negative post-tax real return  C) Equity guarantees higher returns  D) She should use only gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Over a long horizon, **shortfall/inflation risk dominates volatility risk**. An FD at 7% with 6% inflation and 30% tax delivers roughly **−1% real**.\nA) \"Safe\" nominally is not safe in real terms. C) Nothing guarantees equity returns. D) Gold is a diversifier, not a retirement solution.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of these best describes the regulatory logic of the MF/PMS/AIF ladder?\nA) Higher minimums attract more regulation  B) As the minimum investment rises, regulation and disclosure fall while strategy freedom rises  C) All three are regulated identically  D) AIFs are the most regulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulators protect **small investors** most tightly and assume large investors can protect themselves.\nA) Reverses the relationship. C) They differ substantially. D) AIFs are the **least** disclosure-intensive of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A company is liquidated. Its assets cover the bank loan and debentures fully, with a small surplus. Who receives the surplus?\nA) The debenture holders again  B) Preference shareholders, then ordinary shareholders  C) Only the government  D) Nobody\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — After lenders are paid in full, **preference shareholders** rank next, and any remainder goes to **ordinary shareholders** as the residual claim.\nA) Lenders are paid only their contractual entitlement. C) The government's claims rank as a specified creditor, not as a residual owner. D) The surplus belongs to the owners.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client wants a portfolio that can use leverage and invest in unlisted companies. Which vehicle is appropriate?\nA) A mutual fund  B) An AIF  C) A bank deposit  D) An index fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **AIFs** have the widest strategy freedom, including leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets.\nA) Mutual funds face tight restrictions on leverage and unlisted exposure. C) A deposit has no strategy. D) An index fund merely tracks a benchmark.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which of these correctly distinguishes a mutual fund from a PMS?\nA) Both pool investor money  B) A mutual fund pools money into units; a PMS holds securities in the client's own name  C) A PMS is cheaper  D) A mutual fund has a ₹50 lakh minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the defining structural difference.\nA) A PMS does **not** pool. C) PMS fees are generally **higher**, often with performance components. D) ₹50 lakh is the **PMS** minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** An investor holds unlisted shares in a start-up and wants to exit next month. The likely outcome is:\nA) An easy sale on the exchange  B) Difficulty — unlisted equity has no ready market, so exit depends on finding a buyer or a corporate event  C) SEBI will buy the shares  D) Guaranteed exit at fair value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Unlisted equity is **highly illiquid**; exit typically requires a secondary sale, a funding round or an IPO.\nA) Unlisted shares do not trade on an exchange. C) SEBI does not buy securities. D) There is no guaranteed exit or price.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Gold's main portfolio role is:\nA) Generating regular income  B) Diversification and inflation protection as a small satellite allocation  C) Providing guaranteed returns  D) Serving as an emergency fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gold's **low correlation** with equity and debt and its inflation-hedging history make it a diversifier — typically a modest allocation.\nA) Gold pays no income. C) Gold prices are volatile. D) Physical gold is not an ideal emergency asset, though gold ETFs are more liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A client's adviser recommends only products from one fund house and earns commission on each. This conflicts most directly with:\nA) The definition of equity  B) The fiduciary duty and the segregation of advice from distribution  C) Bond pricing rules  D) The AIF minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An RIA must place the **client's interest first** and keep advice **separate from distribution**.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated to the conflict described.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which asset would you expect to fall most in a sharp equity market crash?\nA) A government security  B) A high-yield corporate bond  C) A liquid fund  D) A bank fixed deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **High-yield debt behaves more like equity** in a crisis, because default risk rises exactly when the economy weakens.\nA) Government securities typically **rally** in a flight to safety. C) and D) are stable, capital-preserving instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor wants ₹10,000 a month invested with professional management. The realistic option is:\nA) A PMS  B) An AIF  C) A mutual fund SIP  D) Distressed securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only **mutual funds** accept such small amounts, via SIPs from as little as ₹100–₹500.\nA) Requires ₹50 lakh. B) Requires ₹1 crore. D) Requires sophistication and a large corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which of these best captures why a self-occupied house should not count toward goal funding?\nA) It has no market value  B) It produces no income and cannot be partly sold, so it cannot fund a goal without the family losing their home  C) It is a financial asset  D) It is fully liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Its value is real but **inaccessible** without disrupting the family's housing.\nA) It certainly has market value. C) It is a **physical** asset. D) Property is highly illiquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client compares a 9% government bond with an 11% corporate bond of the same maturity. The 2% difference represents:\nA) A tax benefit  B) The credit spread — compensation for default risk  C) A brokerage fee  D) Inflation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **credit spread** compensates the lender for the corporate issuer's **default risk**.\nA) Tax treatment does not create the spread. C) Brokerage is a transaction cost, not a yield difference. D) Inflation affects both bonds equally.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which statement about PMS fees is correct?\nA) They are capped like a mutual fund's TER  B) They can be fixed and/or performance-linked  C) There are no fees  D) They are paid by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PMS commonly charges a **fixed fee, a performance fee, or both** (with high watermark and hurdle rate mechanics — see Chapter 12).\nA) The TER cap applies to mutual funds. C) PMS is generally the more expensive route. D) SEBI does not pay fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An investor is told a bond fund \"cannot lose money because bonds are safe\". The correct clarification is:\nA) The statement is accurate  B) Bond prices fall when interest rates rise, and issuers can default — so bond funds can and do lose value  C) Only equity funds lose money  D) Bond funds are guaranteed by the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Debt carries **interest rate risk** and **credit risk**; both can produce losses.\nA) It is a common and costly misconception. C) Debt funds have delivered negative returns in rate-rising and credit-event periods. D) No fund is RBI-guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which combination correctly describes equity?\nA) Contractual claim, defined maturity, senior in liquidation  B) Residual claim, no maturity, last in liquidation, voting rights  C) Fixed interest, no voting rights  D) Guaranteed dividend, first claim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are defining equity features.\nA) Describes debt. C) Describes debt or preference shares. D) No such instrument exists — dividends are never guaranteed and equity ranks last.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client wants to invest ₹1.5 crore in a strategy using derivatives and short positions. The appropriate vehicle is:\nA) A mutual fund  B) A Category III AIF  C) A bank deposit  D) A REIT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Category III AIFs** employ diverse or complex trading strategies including leverage and derivatives, and ₹1.5 crore clears the ₹1 crore minimum.\nA) Mutual funds face tight derivative restrictions. C) A deposit has no strategy. D) A REIT is a property vehicle.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which of these has the widest bid–ask spread and least price transparency?\nA) A Nifty 50 stock  B) A government security  C) An artwork  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Art has **no continuous market**, is valued subjectively, and can carry very large spreads and long selling periods.\nA), B) and D) all have active markets and observable prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor asks whether they should hold equity for a goal 18 months away. The answer is:\nA) Yes, equity always outperforms  B) No — 18 months is too short for equity's volatility; use debt or liquid instruments  C) Yes, if they are young  D) Yes, because of tax benefits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon governs allocation.** With 18 months there is no time to recover from a drawdown.\nA) Equity's outperformance is a long-run tendency, not a short-run certainty. C) Age drives capacity, but the **goal's** horizon is what binds here. D) Tax never overrides the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is the most accurate statement about listed versus unlisted equity returns?\nA) Unlisted always returns more  B) Unlisted may offer higher returns but carries illiquidity, valuation and information risk  C) Listed always returns more  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Any return premium comes with **real, compensating risks**.\nA) and C) state certainties that do not exist. D) The risk profiles differ sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which vehicle would you recommend to a first-time investor with ₹5,000 a month?\nA) A PMS  B) An AIF  C) A diversified equity mutual fund SIP  D) Unlisted start-up equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Low minimum, **instant diversification**, professional management, daily liquidity and the highest disclosure standards.\nA) and B) are far beyond the amount. D) Unlisted equity is illiquid and unsuitable for a beginner.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** The main reason commodities improve a portfolio is:\nA) They pay high interest  B) Their low correlation with equity and debt improves diversification  C) They are risk-free  D) They are highly liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets that do not move together reduce overall portfolio volatility.\nA) Commodities pay nothing. C) They are volatile. D) Liquidity varies, and physical holdings are not especially liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A client holds a \"capital protected\" note maturing in 5 years but needs the money in year 2. What should they expect?\nA) Full capital back  B) The protection applies only at maturity, so an early exit may realise less than the principal — and there may be no secondary market  C) A guaranteed profit  D) SEBI compensation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structured products are typically **not principal-protected before maturity** and are often illiquid.\nA) Protection is a maturity feature. C) There is no guaranteed profit. D) SEBI does not compensate market outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which statement about AIF disclosure is correct?\nA) AIFs publish daily NAV  B) AIFs disclose primarily to their own investors and have the lowest public disclosure of the three vehicles  C) AIFs disclose more than mutual funds  D) AIFs have no reporting at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AIFs report to investors under their fund documents, but public disclosure is minimal.\nA) Daily NAV is a **mutual fund** feature. C) Mutual funds disclose far more. D) AIFs do report — to investors and SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** An adviser recommends 100% equity to a 62-year-old retiree drawing monthly income. This is unsuitable primarily because:\nA) Equity returns are low  B) The retiree needs stability and predictable income, and a drawdown early in withdrawal would be very damaging  C) Equity is illegal for retirees  D) Equity has no dividends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Withdrawing from a falling portfolio locks in losses (sequence-of-returns risk). A retiree needs a substantial **debt/income** component.\nA) Equity's long-run returns are strong — the problem is the horizon and drawdown need. C) There is no such prohibition. D) Equity can pay dividends, but they are not dependable income.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which of these correctly orders the vehicles by liquidity, highest first?\nA) AIF → PMS → open-ended mutual fund  B) Open-ended mutual fund → PMS → AIF  C) PMS → AIF → mutual fund  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Open-ended mutual funds redeem on **any business day**; PMS is moderate; AIFs are usually **close-ended with lock-ins**.\nA) Reverses the order. C) Misplaces the mutual fund. D) They differ substantially.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A high-yield bond fund and a government bond fund both fall in value. The most likely differing cause is:\nA) Both fell only from rate rises  B) The government fund fell mainly from interest rate risk; the high-yield fund also suffered credit spread widening  C) Neither is affected by rates  D) High-yield funds have no credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Government bonds carry essentially **only interest rate risk**; high-yield carries **both rate and credit risk**, and spreads typically widen in stress.\nA) Ignores the credit dimension. C) Both are rate-sensitive. D) Credit risk is high-yield's defining feature.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which is TRUE of the relationship between an RIA and the client's assets?\nA) The RIA holds the client's securities  B) The client continues to hold the assets; the RIA supplies strategy, discipline and review  C) The RIA guarantees returns  D) The RIA must be paid by the fund house\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An adviser **advises**; custody stays with the client (unlike a discretionary PMS, where the manager transacts on the client's behalf).\nA) RIAs do not custody client assets. C) No adviser may guarantee returns. D) An RIA is paid by the **client**, not the manufacturer.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which asset class would you expect to perform best during a period of unexpected high inflation?\nA) Long-duration government bonds  B) Commodities and, over longer periods, equity  C) Cash  D) Fixed deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Commodities** respond directly to price rises; **equity** can pass costs on over time.\nA) Long bonds are hurt most by the rate rises that accompany inflation. C) and D) lose real value as inflation rises.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which combination of features belongs to a mutual fund rather than a PMS?\nA) ₹50 lakh minimum, securities in own name  B) Units of a pooled scheme, daily NAV, capped TER, low minimum  C) Performance fees with a hurdle rate  D) Customised individual portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are mutual fund features.\nA), C) and D) all describe a **PMS**.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Scenario & Discrimination (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client has ₹1.2 crore and wants: (i) individually held securities, (ii) some customisation, and (iii) full transparency on holdings. Which vehicle fits best and why?\nA) A mutual fund, since it discloses most  B) A PMS — it holds securities in her own name, allows customisation, and she comfortably exceeds the ₹50 lakh minimum  C) A Category III AIF  D) Direct equity only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three requirements point to **PMS**: securities are **not pooled** (held in her own demat account), customisation is possible especially in non-discretionary mandates, and she can see every holding directly.\nA) A mutual fund discloses well publicly but gives **pooled units**, not individually held securities, and no customisation. C) An AIF pools money into units and discloses least. D) Direct investing gives control but no professional management. **Note she also qualifies for an AIF (₹1 crore), but an AIF fails requirements (i) and (ii).**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Rank these by seniority of claim in liquidation, highest first: ordinary equity, secured debenture, preference share, unsecured bank loan.\nA) Ordinary equity → preference → unsecured loan → secured debenture  B) Secured debenture → unsecured bank loan → preference share → ordinary equity  C) Preference → ordinary → secured → unsecured  D) All rank equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Secured creditors** rank first (they have a charge on assets), then **unsecured creditors**, then **preference shareholders**, then **ordinary shareholders** as the residual.\nA) Exactly inverts it. C) Places equity above debt. D) Seniority is the entire point of capital structure. **The general rule: security beats no security; debt beats equity; preference beats ordinary.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A client compares two 5-year bonds: Bond A (AAA, 7.5%) and Bond B (BB, 12%). What does the 4.5% difference represent, and what should the client consider?\nA) A tax difference  B) The credit spread — compensation for Bond B's materially higher default risk; the client should ask whether 4.5% adequately compensates for possible total loss  C) An error in pricing  D) Bond B is simply better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The spread is the market's **price for default risk**. The right question is not \"which yields more\" but **\"is the extra 4.5% enough for the probability and severity of default?\"** — a BB bond that defaults may return only a fraction of principal, wiping out many years of extra yield.\nA) Both are taxed similarly. C) The spread is deliberate market pricing. D) A higher yield is compensation for risk, not a free gain.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor's portfolio is: 40% self-occupied house, 25% gold jewellery, 20% bank FDs, 10% equity funds, 5% cash. What is the principal weakness?\nA) Too much equity  B) Too little productive, goal-funding financial assets — 65% sits in a self-occupied house and jewellery, which generate no income  C) Too much cash  D) The portfolio is ideal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The house is **consumption** and jewellery is a **personal-use/physical** asset; neither produces income nor can be partly liquidated. Only **35%** (FDs, equity, cash) is genuinely available to fund goals — and just 10% has real growth potential.\nA) 10% equity is **too little**, not too much, for most long-horizon goals. C) 5% cash is modest. D) The concentration in unproductive assets is a serious weakness.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Which scenario correctly illustrates why higher risk does NOT guarantee higher return?\nA) A high-yield bond that pays every coupon and matures at par  B) A high-yield bond portfolio where several issuers default, producing a return below that of government securities despite the higher promised yield  C) Equity that rises 20%  D) A fixed deposit that matures\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk means a **distribution of outcomes**. The higher promised yield is only realised if defaults stay below expectations; when they do not, the risky portfolio **underperforms the safe one**.\nA) Shows the risk not materialising — the favourable case. C) Shows a good equity outcome, not the principle. D) A deposit is the low-risk baseline. **This is precisely why \"higher risk = higher return\" must be stated as higher *expected* return with wider dispersion.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client asks why a REIT is not simply \"the same as owning property\". Give the two most important differences.\nA) There are no differences  B) A REIT is a listed security offering daily liquidity, small ticket size, professional management and diversification across many properties — but the investor owns units, not a specific property, and bears market price volatility  C) REITs guarantee rent  D) REITs are unregulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — REITs convert an illiquid, indivisible, concentrated asset into a **liquid, divisible, diversified** one — at the cost of **market price volatility** and no control over any specific property.\nA) The differences are substantial. C) Distributions depend on rental performance and occupancy. D) REITs are **SEBI-regulated**.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An adviser is comparing routes for a ₹75 lakh corpus. Which statement about the regulatory trade-off is MOST accurate?\nA) A PMS at this level offers more disclosure than a mutual fund  B) The client qualifies for PMS but not AIF; moving from a mutual fund to a PMS gains customisation and direct holding, but loses the mutual fund's daily NAV, capped TER and highest disclosure standards  C) All three vehicles are equally regulated  D) An AIF is available at ₹75 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹75 lakh clears the **₹50 lakh PMS** minimum but not the **₹1 crore AIF** minimum. The trade-off is real: PMS gains customisation and direct ownership but gives up the mutual fund's **cost cap and disclosure regime**.\nA) Mutual funds disclose more. C) They differ markedly. D) ₹75 lakh is below the AIF threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A structured product promises \"100% capital protection plus 60% of Nifty upside over 5 years\", issued by an NBFC rated A. Identify the two main risks.\nA) There are none  B) Issuer credit risk (an A-rated NBFC could default, voiding the \"protection\") and liquidity risk (likely no secondary market, and protection applies only at maturity)  C) Only market risk  D) Only currency risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Credit risk** is the headline point: capital protection is only the **issuer's promise**, and an A rating is investment grade but not risk-free. **Liquidity risk** follows: an early exit forgoes the protection and may find no buyer.\nA) Both risks are material. C) Market risk is partly what the structure addresses. D) There is no currency exposure in a rupee Nifty-linked note.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A 30-year-old wants to fund a house down payment in 3 years and retirement in 30 years from one ₹20 lakh corpus. What is the correct approach?\nA) Put everything in equity  B) Split by goal — the 3-year down payment in debt/liquid instruments; the 30-year retirement portion in equity-oriented investments  C) Put everything in FDs  D) Put everything in gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal bucketing**: each goal gets an allocation matched to its **own horizon**. The 3-year money cannot bear equity volatility; the 30-year money must not sit in low-real-return debt.\nA) Risks a shortfall on the near goal. C) Would likely fail the 30-year goal against inflation. D) Gold is a diversifier, not a whole strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which characteristic is shared by commodities, art and unlisted equity but NOT by listed equity?\nA) They are all regulated by SEBI  B) None generates contractual cash flow AND all lack continuous public price discovery  C) They all pay dividends  D) They are all highly liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Commodities and art produce **no cash flow**, unlisted equity produces none reliably, and **none has a continuous public price** — so valuation is estimated rather than observed.\nA) Art and physical commodities are not SEBI-regulated. C) None reliably pays dividends. D) All three are **illiquid** relative to listed equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An investor holds a portfolio of 20 stocks, all in banking. Which risk is she most exposed to, and what does the asset-class framework suggest?\nA) No particular risk — 20 stocks is diversified  B) Sector-specific (concentration) risk — holding many securities within one sector does not diversify away the sector's common drivers  C) Currency risk  D) Interest rate risk only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Diversification requires low correlation.** Twenty banking stocks share exposure to rates, credit cycles and regulation, so they move together. Number of holdings ≠ diversification.\nA) Count alone does not diversify. C) There is no direct currency exposure in domestic banks. D) Rate risk is one driver, but the broader issue is **sector concentration**.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which statement best explains why AIFs have a ₹1 crore minimum?\nA) To raise more money  B) To restrict participation to investors presumed able to assess complex, illiquid, lightly disclosed strategies and absorb losses  C) Because AIFs are the safest vehicle  D) SEBI sets it arbitrarily\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The minimum acts as a **suitability filter**: AIFs carry the widest strategy freedom and the lowest disclosure, so access is limited to investors presumed capable of evaluating and bearing that.\nA) The purpose is investor protection, not fundraising. C) AIFs are generally the **riskiest** of the three vehicles. D) The threshold reflects a deliberate regulatory philosophy.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client is offered an unlisted company's shares at \"a 40% discount to fair value\". What should the adviser check first?\nA) Nothing — a discount is always good  B) Who determined \"fair value\" and on what basis, since unlisted equity has no market price; plus the exit route, information rights and lock-in  C) The Nifty level  D) The gold price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no observable market price**, \"fair value\" is an **estimate**, potentially by an interested party. The decisive questions are valuation basis, **how and when the investor can exit**, and what information they will receive.\nA) A discount to an unverifiable number means nothing. C) and D) are irrelevant to a specific unlisted holding.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which pair of vehicles both give the investor UNITS rather than directly held securities?\nA) PMS and AIF  B) Mutual fund and AIF  C) PMS and mutual fund  D) Direct equity and PMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both **mutual funds and AIFs are pooled** and issue **units**. **PMS is not pooled** — securities sit in the client's own name.\nA) and C) both wrongly include PMS. D) Direct equity means owning shares outright.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor asks whether she should buy a bond fund now that interest rates are expected to fall sharply. What is the reasoning?\nA) Falling rates hurt bond prices  B) Falling rates raise bond prices, so a bond fund — especially a longer-duration one — should benefit, though the expectation may already be priced in  C) Rates do not affect bonds  D) She should buy equity instead\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Prices move **inversely** to rates, and **longer duration means greater sensitivity**. The caveat matters: widely expected rate cuts are often **already reflected** in current prices.\nA) Inverts the relationship. C) Rates are the primary driver of bond prices. D) A different asset class does not answer the question asked.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A client's adviser earns both a client fee and manufacturer commission on the same recommendation. Which principle is breached, and what is the regulatory position?\nA) None  B) The fiduciary duty and the requirement to segregate advice from distribution — an individual RIA cannot provide both advisory and distribution services to the same client  C) Only a disclosure formality  D) It is required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI's framework requires **segregation of advisory and distribution**; an individual investment adviser cannot do both for the same client, precisely because dual compensation corrupts the recommendation.\nA) A material conflict exists. C) It goes well beyond disclosure — the activity itself is restricted. D) Exactly the opposite.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which asset class combination would provide the LEAST diversification benefit?\nA) Indian equity and Indian government bonds  B) Indian large-cap equity and Indian mid-cap equity  C) Equity and gold  D) Equity and real estate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Large-cap and mid-cap **equity are the same asset class** and are highly correlated; combining them provides little diversification.\nA), C) and D) all pair genuinely different asset classes with lower correlations. **Diversification comes from low correlation, not from the number of holdings.**\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A retiree needs ₹50,000 a month and holds ₹1.5 crore. Which allocation is most appropriate in principle?\nA) 100% equity for maximum growth  B) A majority in debt/income instruments for stability and predictable cash flow, with a meaningful equity allocation to protect against inflation over a long retirement  C) 100% in a single corporate bond  D) 100% in gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A retiree faces **two** risks: **volatility/sequence risk** (argues for debt) and **longevity/inflation risk** over a possibly 25-year retirement (argues for some equity). The answer is a **balance**, not a corner.\nA) Exposes essential income to drawdowns. C) Concentrates all credit risk in one issuer. D) Gold pays no income at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which of these correctly identifies what an investor gives up by choosing a mutual fund over a PMS?\nA) Liquidity and disclosure  B) Customisation and direct ownership of the underlying securities  C) Professional management  D) Regulation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A mutual fund gives everyone the **same pooled portfolio** in **units**; a PMS allows tailoring and holds securities **in the client's own name**.\nA) A mutual fund **gains** on both counts. C) Both are professionally managed. D) A mutual fund is the **more** regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Two clients each invest ₹1 crore: one in a Category III AIF, one in an equity mutual fund. Which faces more uncertainty about what they hold at any moment, and why?\nA) The mutual fund investor  B) The AIF investor — AIFs disclose to investors under fund documents rather than publishing daily NAV and monthly portfolios, and may use leverage, derivatives and unlisted assets  C) Both are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds publish **daily NAV and monthly portfolio disclosures**; AIFs do not. Add wide strategy freedom and the AIF investor has materially less visibility.\nA) Reverses it. C) The disclosure regimes differ sharply. D) The difference is real and is the basis for the higher minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Which is the strongest argument against holding a large allocation in art or collectibles?\nA) They never rise in value  B) No cash flow, subjective and expertise-dependent valuation, very wide spreads, authenticity and storage risk, and no regulatory investor protection  C) They are illegal  D) They are too liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each factor independently argues for a small allocation at most; together they make art a **passion allocation**, not a core holding.\nA) They can appreciate substantially — that is not the objection. C) They are entirely legal. D) They are extremely **illiquid**.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An investor holds a 10-year corporate bond and rates rise 2%. What happens to the bond and to her income if she holds to maturity?\nA) She loses both price and income  B) The market price falls, but if she holds to maturity she still receives the contracted coupons and principal — the loss is opportunity cost, not realised loss  C) The coupon rises  D) The bond defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Price and yield move inversely, so the **market price falls**. But the coupon is **contractual**: holding to maturity delivers the promised cash flows. What she loses is the chance to earn the new, higher rate.\nA) Income is unaffected if there is no default. C) Coupons on fixed-rate bonds do not change. D) A rate rise is not a credit event. **This distinction — mark-to-market loss vs realised loss — is heavily tested.**\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client wants exposure to Indian infrastructure with listed liquidity and periodic income. The best-fitting instrument is:\nA) A REIT  B) An InvIT  C) A gold ETF  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **InvIT** holds infrastructure assets (roads, transmission, pipelines) and is listed and SEBI-regulated, distributing cash flows periodically.\nA) A REIT holds **real estate**, not infrastructure. C) and D) are entirely different asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about the relationship between minimum investment and regulation is correct across MF, PMS and AIF?\nA) Higher minimums bring stricter disclosure  B) Higher minimums bring lighter disclosure and wider strategy freedom, on the assumption that larger investors can protect themselves  C) Minimums are unrelated to regulation  D) All three have identical minimums\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The ladder runs **₹500 (heaviest regulation) → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore (lightest disclosure, widest freedom)**.\nA) Inverts the relationship. C) The link is deliberate regulatory design. D) The minimums differ by orders of magnitude.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor's entire ₹80 lakh corpus is in one AAA corporate bond. Identify the risks.\nA) None — AAA is safe  B) Concentration/issuer risk (a single downgrade or default hits everything), interest rate risk, and reinvestment risk — a rating is not a guarantee and can change  C) Only currency risk  D) Only equity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **AAA is the highest rating, not a guarantee** — ratings migrate, and India has seen highly rated issuers downgraded sharply. Holding a single issuer means **zero diversification** of credit risk, plus rate and reinvestment risk.\nA) Complacency about AAA is precisely the trap. C) A rupee bond has no currency exposure. D) There is no equity here.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which describes the correct sequence when matching a product to a client?\nA) Pick the highest-returning product, then find a client  B) Establish the goal, horizon and risk profile, then select the asset class, then the vehicle, then the specific product  C) Pick the vehicle first  D) Pick the product with the highest commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Suitability flows **from the client outward**: goal → horizon and risk profile → asset class → vehicle → product.\nA) Is product-push in reverse. C) The vehicle depends on the asset class and corpus size. D) A direct breach of fiduciary duty.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which pair of asset classes would a client typically use for a 2-year goal and a 20-year goal respectively?\nA) Equity and cash  B) Debt/liquid instruments and equity  C) Art and gold  D) Distressed securities and cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short horizon → **capital stability** (debt/liquid). Long horizon → **growth** (equity), where volatility has time to average out.\nA) Exactly reverses the correct matching. C) Neither is a core holding for a defined goal. D) Distressed securities are unsuitable for almost any retail goal.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client claims that because a PMS holds securities in her own name, it must be \"safer\" than a mutual fund. Evaluate.\nA) Correct — direct holding removes market risk  B) Direct holding removes pooling and gives transparency of ownership, but market risk is identical; and the mutual fund actually offers a capped TER, daily NAV and stricter diversification norms  C) Correct — PMS is regulated more heavily  D) PMS carries no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Ownership structure and market risk are different things.** Holding securities directly does not make them fall less. On several protective dimensions — **cost cap, disclosure frequency and diversification limits** — the mutual fund is the tighter regime.\nA) Market risk attaches to the securities, not the wrapper. C) Mutual funds are the **more** regulated. D) All market investments carry risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Which single factor most determines whether equity is appropriate for a given goal?\nA) The client's age  B) The goal's time horizon  C) The current market level  D) The client's income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon** is the binding constraint: even a young, wealthy, risk-tolerant client should not fund a 12-month goal with equity.\nA) Age influences risk **capacity** but the goal's own horizon governs. C) Timing the market is unreliable and not the basis for allocation. D) Income affects capacity, not the horizon match.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises this chapter?\nA) All investments are equally risky  B) Every investment is defined by what you own (asset class), how you own it (direct, MF, PMS, AIF), and whether its risk, liquidity and horizon match the goal it funds  C) Equity always beats debt  D) Managed portfolios always beat direct investing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The chapter's whole structure is **asset class × access route × suitability**.\nA) Risk varies enormously across asset classes. C) Equity outperforms over long periods but not over every period, and is unsuitable for short goals. D) Managed vehicles add professional management **and cost**; direct investing is cheaper but demands skill.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.**\n\n### Case: Structuring Ms Rao's ₹1.4 Crore\n\nMs Sunita Rao, **46**, has just received **₹1.4 crore** from the sale of a commercial property. She has:\n\n- An existing emergency fund of **₹9,00,000** (6 months of expenses)\n- **No outstanding debt**\n- Two goals: her daughter's postgraduate education abroad in **4 years**, costing **₹45,00,000** in today's terms, and her own retirement at **60** (14 years away)\n- A **moderate** risk profile: she is uncomfortable with sharp drawdowns but understands she needs growth\n\nShe has been approached with three proposals: a **Category III AIF** with a long–short strategy (₹1 crore minimum), a **PMS** with a concentrated 15-stock portfolio (₹50 lakh minimum), and a **structured note** from an A-rated NBFC promising \"100% capital protection plus 55% of Nifty upside over 6 years\".\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** Which of the three proposals does Ms Rao technically qualify for on minimum-investment grounds?\nA) Only the PMS  B) All three — she has ₹1.4 crore, clearing both the ₹50 lakh PMS and ₹1 crore AIF minimums  C) Only the AIF  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹1.4 crore clears the **₹50 lakh PMS** minimum and the **₹1 crore AIF** minimum, and structured notes have no such regulatory floor.\nA) and C) each overlook one qualifying vehicle. D) She comfortably exceeds both thresholds. *(Note carefully: **qualifying is not the same as being suitable** — that is what the remaining questions test.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Her daughter's education is 4 years away and costs ₹45,00,000 today. At 6% education inflation, what will it cost, and which asset class should fund it?\nA) ₹45,00,000; equity  B) About ₹56,80,000; predominantly debt/liquid instruments given the 4-year horizon  C) About ₹90,00,000; the AIF  D) ₹45,00,000; the structured note\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Future cost = 45,00,000 × (1.06)⁴ = 45,00,000 × **1.26248** = **₹56,81,160**. With only **4 years** and a non-negotiable date, the priority is **capital certainty**, so this bucket belongs predominantly in debt/liquid instruments with perhaps a small equity component.\nA) Ignores inflation and misallocates to equity. C) Overstates the inflation effect badly. D) The structured note runs **6 years** — it would still be locked up when the fees fall due.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What is the most serious problem with the structured note for Ms Rao?\nA) The returns are too high  B) A 6-year term against a 4-year goal, plus issuer credit risk from an A-rated NBFC — \"capital protection\" is only that issuer's promise, and there is likely no secondary market  C) It is illegal  D) It has no market exposure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three compounding problems. **Tenor mismatch:** 6 years versus a 4-year goal. **Credit risk:** the \"protection\" is only as good as the **A-rated NBFC** behind it — investment grade, but not risk-free. **Liquidity:** structured notes typically have no secondary market, and protection generally applies **only at maturity**.\nA) The upside participation is capped at 55%, not excessive. C) Such products are permitted. D) It does have Nifty-linked exposure — that is the point of the option leg.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Is the concentrated 15-stock PMS suitable for her retirement bucket?\nA) Yes — concentrated portfolios always outperform  B) It is a legitimate option for a long-horizon bucket, but a 15-stock portfolio carries high concentration risk that sits awkwardly with her stated discomfort with sharp drawdowns  C) No — PMS is prohibited for her  D) Yes, she should put all ₹1.4 crore into it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She **qualifies** and a 14-year horizon can bear equity. But a **15-stock concentrated portfolio** has materially higher stock-specific risk than a diversified fund, and she has explicitly said she is **uncomfortable with sharp drawdowns**. The mismatch is with her **risk tolerance**, not her capacity.\nA) Concentration raises dispersion of outcomes in **both** directions. C) She meets the minimum, so it is not prohibited. D) Allocating everything to one concentrated equity strategy ignores the 4-year goal entirely.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What is the most appropriate overall structure for Ms Rao's ₹1.4 crore?\nA) All ₹1.4 crore into the Category III AIF  B) Bucket by goal — roughly ₹45–50 lakh in debt/liquid instruments for the 4-year education goal, the remaining ~₹90 lakh in diversified equity-oriented investments for the 14-year retirement goal, matched to a moderate risk profile  C) All into the structured note  D) Leave it all in a savings account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Goal bucketing** is the answer. The education bucket needs about **₹56.8 lakh at maturity**; funding it with roughly **₹45–50 lakh** in debt today gets there safely over 4 years. The remaining **~₹90 lakh** has a **14-year** horizon and belongs in diversified equity-oriented investments — a **diversified** approach fits her moderate tolerance better than a 15-stock PMS or a long–short AIF.\nA) A Category III AIF's leverage and derivative strategies are the least suitable fit for a moderate-risk investor, and it would strand the education money. C) Fails on tenor, credit and liquidity as QC3 established. D) Guarantees a negative real return over 14 years.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson:** Ms Rao **qualifies** for every product offered to her — and that is exactly the danger. **Eligibility is not suitability.** The adviser's job is to start from her goals, horizons and tolerance, and only then choose the vehicle.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "08", "title": "Investing in Stocks", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> ⭐ The **valuation ratios** in 8.5.4 are guaranteed exam marks — learn each formula, what it means, and when it fails. Fundamental vs technical analysis is the other reliable question source.\n\n---\n\n## 8.1 Equity as an Investment\n\nBuying a share makes you a **part-owner** of a business. Your return comes from two sources:\n\n$$\\textbf{Total Return} = \\textbf{Capital Appreciation} + \\textbf{Dividend Yield}$$\n\n**Why equity works over the long run:** a company's earnings tend to grow with the economy and with inflation, so equity is a claim on a **growing, inflation-linked stream of profits** — unlike a bond, whose coupon is fixed in nominal terms.\n\n**The cost:** ownership is a **residual** claim. Shareholders are paid last, dividends are discretionary, and the capital can be lost entirely.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Diversification of Risk\n\nDiversification reduces risk **without necessarily reducing expected return** — the closest thing to a free lunch in investing. It works because assets do not move in perfect lock-step.\n\n### Two dimensions\n\n| | **Cross-sectional diversification** | **Time-series diversification** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meaning** | Spreading across **many securities, sectors and asset classes at one point in time** | Spreading **entry across time** |\n| **Reduces** | Company- and sector-specific risk | The risk of investing everything at a bad price |\n| **In practice** | A diversified portfolio | **SIP / rupee-cost averaging** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Critical limit:** diversification eliminates **unsystematic** (company/sector-specific) risk but **cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk**. When the whole market falls, diversification does not save you. Beyond roughly 15–25 well-chosen, low-correlation stocks, additional names add little.\n>\n> And remember Chapter 7's trap: **20 banking stocks are not diversified.** Diversification requires **low correlation**, not merely a high count.\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 Risks of Equity Investment\n\n| Risk | What it is | Diversifiable? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Market risk** | The whole market falls — recessions, rate shocks, geopolitics | ❌ **No** (systematic) |\n| **Sector-specific risk** | A sector suffers — regulation, commodity prices, technology shifts | ✅ Yes, across sectors |\n| **Company-specific risk** | One firm fails — fraud, product failure, management error | ✅ Yes, across companies |\n| **Transactional risk** | Execution and settlement problems, bad fills, operational error | Partly, via good practice |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Cannot exit at a fair price; wide spreads in small-caps | Partly, via avoiding illiquid names |\n\n> 🧠 **Systematic (market) risk is compensated** — you are paid a risk premium for bearing it because you cannot avoid it. **Unsystematic risk is not compensated**, because you could have diversified it away for free. This idea underpins CAPM and beta in Chapters 14 and 16.\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 Overview of the Equity Market\n\n- **Primary market** — IPOs, FPOs, rights issues (Chapter 6)\n- **Secondary market** — NSE, BSE, T+1 settlement\n- **Segments by size** — large-cap, mid-cap, small-cap (SEBI defines these by market-cap rank: **1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251 onwards small**)\n- **Indices** — Nifty 50, Sensex; free-float market-cap weighted\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 Equity Research and Stock Selection\n\n### 8.5.1 Fundamental Analysis\n\nEstimating a security's **intrinsic value** from the underlying business — earnings, assets, growth, management, industry — and comparing it with the market price.\n\n$$\\text{If Intrinsic Value} > \\text{Market Price} \\Rightarrow \\text{potentially undervalued (buy)}$$\n\n#### Top-down vs bottom-up\n\n| | **Top-down** | **Bottom-up** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Starts with** | The **economy** | The **company** |\n| **Then** | Economy → industry → company | Company merits, largely regardless of macro |\n| **Suits** | Cyclical and macro-driven sectors | Stock-pickers seeking mispriced individual firms |\n\n#### Buy-side vs sell-side research\n\n| | **Sell-side** | **Buy-side** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Works for** | Brokers, investment banks | Mutual funds, insurers, PMS, pension funds |\n| **Research is** | **Published** to clients, often with buy/hold/sell recommendations | **Internal and proprietary** |\n| **Paid via** | Brokerage/commissions | The employer's own returns |\n| **Conflict** | May face pressure from banking relationships | Fewer external conflicts |\n\n#### Sector classification\nGrouping companies by business activity (banking, IT, FMCG, pharma, auto) so that peers can be compared and sector exposures measured.\n\n### 8.5.2 The Stock Analysis Process — E → I → C\n\n**1. Economy analysis** — GDP growth, inflation, interest rates, fiscal and monetary policy, currency, commodity prices, the global cycle.\n\n**2. Industry/sector analysis** — industry life cycle (pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline), competitive intensity, regulation, entry barriers, pricing power, input costs.\n\n**3. Company analysis** — two halves:\n\n- **Quantitative:** revenue and profit growth, margins, return on equity, debt levels, cash flow quality, working capital.\n- **Qualitative:** management quality and integrity, business model, competitive advantage (\"moat\"), corporate governance, related-party transactions, promoter pledging.\n\n### 8.5.3 Fundamentals-Driven Models (absolute valuation)\n\n#### Discounted Cash Flow (DCF)\n\nThe value of a business is the **present value of its future cash flows**:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Value} = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t} + \\frac{\\text{Terminal Value}}{(1+r)^n}}$$\n\n**Terminal value** (Gordon growth): $TV = \\dfrac{CF_{n+1}}{r-g}$, requiring **g < r**.\n\n- ✅ Theoretically the soundest method — it values the business on its own merits.\n- ❌ **Extremely sensitive** to assumptions about growth (g) and discount rate (r). Small changes swing the answer wildly, and the terminal value often dominates. *\"Garbage in, gospel out.\"*\n\n#### Dividend Discount Model\nA DCF variant using dividends: $P_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{r-g}$. Works only for stable dividend payers.\n\n#### Asset-based valuation\nValue = **net assets** (assets − liabilities), i.e. book value. Useful for asset-heavy or liquidating businesses; poor for service or brand-driven companies whose value is intangible.\n\n### 8.5.4 ⭐ Market-Driven Models — Relative Valuation\n\nRelative valuation compares a company with **peers or its own history** using multiples. Faster and more intuitive than DCF, but it only tells you whether a stock is cheap **relative to something else** — if the whole sector is overvalued, relative valuation will not reveal it.\n\n| Ratio | Formula | What it says | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P/E** | Price ÷ Earnings per share | What you pay per ₹1 of earnings | **Meaningless if earnings are negative**; distorted by one-off items |\n| **P/B** | Price ÷ Book value per share | Price relative to net assets | Best for **banks and financials**; poor for asset-light firms |\n| **P/S** | Price ÷ Sales per share | Price per ₹1 of revenue | Useful for **loss-making** or early-stage firms; **ignores profitability entirely** |\n| **PEG** | P/E ÷ Earnings growth rate (%) | P/E adjusted for growth | **PEG < 1** often suggests good value; depends on a reliable growth estimate |\n| **EV/EBITDA** | Enterprise Value ÷ EBITDA | Whole-firm value vs operating cash earnings | **Capital-structure neutral**, so it compares differently-leveraged firms fairly |\n| **EBIT/EV** | EBIT ÷ Enterprise Value | The \"earnings yield\" of the whole firm | The inverse of an EV multiple |\n| **EV/Sales** | Enterprise Value ÷ Sales | Whole-firm value per ₹1 of revenue | For loss-makers; ignores margins |\n| **Dividend yield** | Dividend per share ÷ Price | Cash income return | A **high yield can signal a falling price**, not generosity |\n| **Earnings yield** | EPS ÷ Price (the inverse of P/E) | Earnings return on the price paid | Comparable with bond yields |\n\n**Enterprise Value:** $EV = \\text{Market Cap} + \\text{Debt} - \\text{Cash}$ — what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts.\n\n#### EVA and MVA\n\n- **EVA (Economic Value Added)** = Net Operating Profit After Tax − (Capital × Cost of Capital). **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital** — genuine value creation, not just accounting profit.\n- **MVA (Market Value Added)** = Market Value of the firm − Capital Invested. The market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\n\n#### Industry-specific metrics\nBecause standard ratios do not fit every sector: **banks** — Net Interest Margin, Gross/Net NPA, CASA ratio, capital adequacy; **telecom** — ARPU, subscriber count; **retail** — same-store sales growth, sales per square foot; **hotels** — occupancy, average room rate; **IT** — utilisation, attrition, billing rates.\n\n### 8.6 Combining Relative and DCF Approaches\nBest practice uses **both**: DCF anchors value in the business's own economics; relative multiples sanity-check that answer against what the market pays for comparable firms. When the two diverge sharply, the assumptions deserve re-examination.\n\n---\n\n## 8.7 Technical Analysis\n\n**Technical analysis studies price and volume history to forecast future price movements** — it ignores intrinsic value entirely.\n\n### 8.7.1 Assumptions\n1. **The market discounts everything** — all information is already in the price.\n2. **Prices move in trends** — and a trend is more likely to continue than to reverse.\n3. **History repeats itself** — because human psychology is consistent, price patterns recur.\n\n### 8.7.2 Technical vs Fundamental Analysis\n\n| | **Fundamental** | **Technical** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Studies** | Financial statements, economy, industry, management | **Price and volume charts** |\n| **Answers** | **What** to buy | **When** to buy or sell |\n| **Horizon** | Long term | Short to medium term |\n| **Basis** | Intrinsic value | Market psychology and trends |\n| **Data** | Fundamentals, released periodically | Price/volume, continuous |\n\n> 🧠 **The clean way to remember it: fundamental analysis tells you WHAT to buy; technical analysis tells you WHEN.** Many practitioners use both.\n\n### 8.7.3 Advantages of technical analysis\nApplicable to any traded instrument; timing-focused; provides clear entry, exit and stop-loss levels; works when fundamental data is unavailable or stale; captures sentiment that fundamentals miss.\n\n### 8.7.4 Technical rules and indicators\n\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Support** | A price level where buying has historically emerged, halting a fall |\n| **Resistance** | A level where selling has historically emerged, capping a rise |\n| **Trendline** | A line joining successive highs or lows, defining the trend |\n| **Moving average** | Smooths price to reveal the trend; crossovers signal changes |\n| **RSI** | Relative Strength Index (0–100); conventionally **>70 overbought, <30 oversold** |\n| **MACD** | Moving Average Convergence Divergence — momentum via the relationship between two moving averages |\n| **Bollinger Bands** | Bands set a number of standard deviations around a moving average, showing relative volatility |\n| **Volume** | Confirms a move — a price break on **high volume** is more credible |\n| **Chart patterns** | Head-and-shoulders, double top/bottom, triangles, flags |\n| **Candlesticks** | Doji, hammer, engulfing patterns signalling potential reversals |\n\n> ⚠️ **Once support is broken it often becomes resistance** (and vice versa) — a classic exam point.\n\n### 8.7.5 Fixed income and technical analysis\nTechnical analysis can be applied to **bond yields and prices**, and to interest-rate futures, though fundamental drivers (monetary policy, inflation, credit) dominate more than in equity.\n\n---\n\n## 8.8 Qualitative Evaluation and Corporate Governance\n\nNumbers describe the past; **quality of management and governance determines whether the future resembles it.**\n\n**What a stock picker examines:**\n\n- **Board composition** — genuinely independent directors, relevant expertise\n- **Promoter holding and pledging** — high **pledged shares** are a serious red flag (forced selling risk)\n- **Related-party transactions** — potential siphoning of value\n- **Auditor quality** — and any **auditor resignation**, a major warning sign\n- **Disclosure quality** — clear, consistent, timely reporting\n- **Capital allocation record** — has management reinvested wisely, or built empires?\n- **Minority shareholder treatment** — dividend policy, fairness in group restructurings\n- **Accounting aggressiveness** — revenue recognition, related-party receivables, frequent \"exceptional items\"\n\n> ⚠️ **Governance red flags to memorise:** frequent **auditor changes or resignations**, high **promoter pledging**, large **related-party transactions**, repeated restatements, opaque group structures, and a persistent gap between **reported profit and operating cash flow**.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Equity total return | Capital appreciation + dividend yield |\n| Cross-sectional diversification | Across **securities/sectors** at one time |\n| Time-series diversification | Across **time** — i.e. a SIP |\n| Diversification eliminates | **Unsystematic** risk only |\n| Systematic risk | Market-wide, **undiversifiable**, and **compensated** |\n| Unsystematic risk | Company/sector-specific, diversifiable, **not compensated** |\n| SEBI cap definitions | 1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251+ small |\n| Fundamental analysis | Estimates **intrinsic value** |\n| Technical analysis | Studies **price and volume** |\n| The clean split | Fundamental = **what** to buy; technical = **when** |\n| Top-down | Economy → industry → company |\n| Bottom-up | Company first |\n| Sell-side research | Brokers/banks, **published** |\n| Buy-side research | Funds/institutions, **internal** |\n| E → I → C | Economy → Industry → Company |\n| DCF | PV of future cash flows + terminal value |\n| DCF's weakness | Extreme sensitivity to **g** and **r** |\n| Terminal value | CF ÷ (r − g), needs **g < r** |\n| P/E | Price ÷ EPS; **useless if earnings are negative** |\n| P/B | Price ÷ book; best for **banks/financials** |\n| P/S | For **loss-making** firms; ignores profitability |\n| PEG | P/E ÷ growth; **< 1** often signals value |\n| EV | Market cap + debt − cash |\n| EV/EBITDA | **Capital-structure neutral** comparison |\n| Earnings yield | EPS ÷ Price — the inverse of P/E |\n| High dividend yield warning | May reflect a **falling price**, not generosity |\n| EVA | NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital); **positive = real value creation** |\n| MVA | Market value − capital invested |\n| Bank metrics | NIM, Gross/Net NPA, CASA, capital adequacy |\n| Technical assumptions | Market discounts everything; prices trend; history repeats |\n| RSI thresholds | **>70 overbought, <30 oversold** |\n| Broken support | Often becomes **resistance** |\n| Governance red flags | Auditor resignation, **promoter pledging**, related-party deals, profit–cash flow gap |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any valuation-ratio question, ask **\"what is in the denominator, and can it be negative or distorted?\"** That single question explains why P/E fails for loss-makers, why P/B suits banks, and why P/S is used for early-stage companies.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The valuation ratios are the highest-yield items — know each formula AND when it breaks.**\n\n**Q1.** What are the two components of equity total return?\n> **A:** **Capital appreciation + dividend yield.**\n\n**Q2.** Why does equity beat inflation over the long run, in principle?\n> **A:** Company earnings tend to grow with the economy and with inflation, so equity is a claim on a **growing, inflation-linked stream of profits** — unlike a bond, whose coupon is fixed in nominal terms.\n\n**Q3.** Distinguish cross-sectional from time-series diversification.\n> **A:** **Cross-sectional** = across many securities, sectors and asset classes **at one point in time**. **Time-series** = spreading **entry across time**, i.e. a **SIP / rupee-cost averaging**.\n\n**Q4.** What is diversification's fundamental limit?\n> **A:** It eliminates **unsystematic (company/sector-specific) risk** but **cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk**. When the whole market falls, diversification does not save you.\n\n**Q5.** Roughly how many stocks are needed for effective diversification, and what is the catch?\n> **A:** Around **15–25** well-chosen stocks capture most of the benefit. The catch: they must have **low correlation** — 20 banking stocks are *not* diversified.\n\n**Q6.** Which type of risk is compensated by the market, and why?\n> **A:** **Systematic (market) risk** — you are paid a risk premium because it **cannot be avoided**. **Unsystematic risk is NOT compensated**, because you could have diversified it away for free.\n\n**Q7.** Name the five risks of equity investing.\n> **A:** **Market** risk, **sector-specific** risk, **company-specific** risk, **transactional** risk and **liquidity** risk.\n\n**Q8.** State SEBI's market-cap definitions for large, mid and small cap.\n> **A:** By market-capitalisation rank: **1–100 large cap**, **101–250 mid cap**, **251 onwards small cap**.\n\n**Q9.** What does fundamental analysis try to establish?\n> **A:** A security's **intrinsic value** from the underlying business, to compare against the market price. If intrinsic value > market price, the stock is potentially **undervalued**.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish top-down from bottom-up analysis.\n> **A:** **Top-down** starts with the **economy** → industry → company. **Bottom-up** starts with the **company** itself, largely regardless of the macro picture.\n\n**Q11.** Distinguish sell-side from buy-side research.\n> **A:** **Sell-side** works for brokers/investment banks and **publishes** research to clients (paid via brokerage). **Buy-side** works for funds/insurers/PMS and keeps research **internal and proprietary**.\n\n**Q12.** State the E → I → C process.\n> **A:** **Economy** analysis → **Industry/sector** analysis → **Company** analysis.\n\n**Q13.** What are the two halves of company analysis?\n> **A:** **Quantitative** (growth, margins, return on equity, debt, cash flow) and **Qualitative** (management quality, business model, moat, corporate governance).\n\n**Q14.** State the DCF formula and its terminal-value component.\n> **A:** **Value = Σ [CFₜ ÷ (1+r)ᵗ] + Terminal Value ÷ (1+r)ⁿ**, where **TV = CF₍ₙ₊₁₎ ÷ (r − g)**, valid only when **g < r**.\n\n**Q15.** What is DCF's greatest weakness?\n> **A:** **Extreme sensitivity to the growth rate (g) and discount rate (r)** — small changes swing the answer wildly, and terminal value often dominates. \"Garbage in, gospel out.\"\n\n**Q16.** When is asset-based valuation appropriate, and when does it fail?\n> **A:** Appropriate for **asset-heavy or liquidating** businesses. It fails for **service and brand-driven** companies whose value is intangible and off the balance sheet.\n\n**Q17.** State the P/E ratio and its main limitation.\n> **A:** **Price ÷ Earnings per share.** It becomes **meaningless when earnings are negative**, and is distorted by one-off items.\n\n**Q18.** For which sector is P/B most useful, and why?\n> **A:** **Banks and financials** — their assets are largely financial and marked close to realisable value, so book value is meaningful. It is poor for asset-light firms.\n\n**Q19.** When would you use P/S rather than P/E?\n> **A:** For **loss-making or early-stage** companies, where earnings are negative or meaningless. Its weakness: it **ignores profitability entirely**.\n\n**Q20.** What is the PEG ratio, and what value suggests good value?\n> **A:** **P/E ÷ earnings growth rate (%)**. A **PEG below 1** often suggests good value — but it depends entirely on a reliable growth estimate.\n\n**Q21.** State the Enterprise Value formula.\n> **A:** **EV = Market Capitalisation + Debt − Cash** — what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts.\n\n**Q22.** Why is EV/EBITDA preferred for comparing companies with different debt levels?\n> **A:** Because it is **capital-structure neutral** — EV includes debt and EBITDA is measured before interest, so differently-leveraged firms compare fairly. P/E is distorted by leverage.\n\n**Q23.** What is earnings yield, and what is it useful for?\n> **A:** **EPS ÷ Price** — the **inverse of P/E**. It expresses the return as a percentage, making it directly comparable with **bond yields**.\n\n**Q24.** Why can a high dividend yield be a warning sign?\n> **A:** Yield = dividend ÷ **price**. A **falling price** mechanically raises the yield, so a high figure may signal distress rather than generosity.\n\n**Q25.** State the EVA formula and what a positive value means.\n> **A:** **EVA = NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital).** Positive EVA means the firm earned **more than its cost of capital** — genuine value creation, not merely accounting profit.\n\n**Q26.** What is MVA?\n> **A:** **Market Value Added = Market value of the firm − Capital invested** — the market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\n\n**Q27.** Name the industry-specific metrics for banks, telecom and retail.\n> **A:** **Banks** — NIM, Gross/Net NPA, CASA ratio, capital adequacy. **Telecom** — ARPU, subscriber count. **Retail** — same-store sales growth, sales per square foot.\n\n**Q28.** State the three assumptions of technical analysis.\n> **A:** 1) **The market discounts everything** — all information is in the price. 2) **Prices move in trends.** 3) **History repeats itself**, because human psychology is consistent.\n\n**Q29.** Give the clean one-line split between fundamental and technical analysis.\n> **A:** **Fundamental analysis tells you WHAT to buy; technical analysis tells you WHEN.**\n\n**Q30.** Define support and resistance, and state what happens when support breaks.\n> **A:** **Support** is a level where buying has historically halted a fall; **resistance** is where selling has capped a rise. **Once support is broken it often becomes resistance** (and vice versa).\n\n**Q31.** What are the conventional RSI thresholds?\n> **A:** RSI runs 0–100; conventionally **above 70 is overbought** and **below 30 is oversold**.\n\n**Q32.** What does volume add to a technical signal?\n> **A:** **Confirmation.** A price breakout on **high volume** is far more credible than the same move on thin volume.\n\n**Q33.** Name four technical indicators besides support/resistance.\n> **A:** **Moving averages** (and crossovers), **RSI**, **MACD**, **Bollinger Bands** — plus chart patterns (head-and-shoulders, double top/bottom, triangles) and candlestick patterns.\n\n**Q34.** Can technical analysis be applied to fixed income?\n> **A:** **Yes** — to bond yields, prices and interest-rate futures — though fundamental drivers (monetary policy, inflation, credit) dominate more than in equity.\n\n**Q35.** Name six corporate governance red flags.\n> **A:** **Auditor resignation or frequent auditor changes**, high **promoter share pledging**, large **related-party transactions**, repeated **restatements**, opaque group structures, and a persistent gap between **reported profit and operating cash flow**.\n\n**Q36.** Why is high promoter pledging a serious concern?\n> **A:** If the share price falls, lenders can **invoke the pledge and dump the shares**, creating forced selling that drives the price down further — a self-reinforcing spiral.\n\n**Q37.** What is the fundamental limitation of relative valuation?\n> **A:** It only tells you whether a stock is cheap **relative to something else**. If the entire sector or market is overvalued, relative valuation will **not reveal it**.\n\n**Q38.** Why should DCF and relative valuation be used together?\n> **A:** DCF anchors value in the **business's own economics**; relative multiples **sanity-check** that against what the market pays for comparable firms. Sharp divergence means the assumptions need re-examination.\n\n**Q39.** What is the industry life cycle, and why does it matter?\n> **A:** **Pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline.** The stage determines expected growth, margins, competitive intensity and the appropriate valuation approach.\n\n**Q40.** What single question resolves most valuation-ratio problems?\n> **A:** **\"What is in the denominator, and can it be negative or distorted?\"** That explains why P/E fails for loss-makers, why P/B suits banks, and why P/S is used for early-stage firms.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 8: Investing in Stocks — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Valuation ratio arithmetic appears in every tier.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The total return from an equity investment comes from:\nA) Interest and principal  B) Capital appreciation and dividend yield  C) Rent and appreciation  D) Coupon payments only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity returns come from **price appreciation** plus **dividends**.\nA) Describes a bond. C) Describes real estate. D) Bonds pay coupons; equity does not.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Diversification eliminates:\nA) All risk  B) Unsystematic (company- and sector-specific) risk  C) Systematic risk  D) Inflation risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Diversification removes **unsystematic** risk only.\nA) Some risk always remains. C) **Systematic (market) risk cannot be diversified away** — that is its defining property. D) Inflation affects all assets and is not diversified away by holding more stocks.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which type of risk is compensated with a risk premium?\nA) Unsystematic risk  B) Systematic risk  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You are paid only for risk you **cannot avoid**. Unsystematic risk is uncompensated because diversification removes it at no cost.\nA) Reverses the principle. C) They are treated very differently. D) Systematic risk is certainly compensated — that is the equity risk premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A SIP is an example of:\nA) Cross-sectional diversification  B) Time-series diversification  C) Leverage  D) Technical analysis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Spreading **entry across time** is time-series diversification (rupee-cost averaging).\nA) That means spreading across securities at one point in time. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The P/E ratio is:\nA) Price ÷ Book value  B) Price ÷ Earnings per share  C) Price ÷ Sales  D) Earnings ÷ Price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — P/E = **Price ÷ EPS**, i.e. what you pay per ₹1 of earnings.\nA) Is P/B. C) Is P/S. D) Is the **earnings yield**, the inverse of P/E.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The P/E ratio becomes meaningless when:\nA) The company pays no dividend  B) Earnings are negative  C) The stock is large-cap  D) The company has debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A negative denominator makes the ratio uninterpretable.\nA) Dividends do not enter the P/E. C) and D) affect valuation but do not break the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Enterprise Value is calculated as:\nA) Market cap + debt − cash  B) Market cap − debt  C) Market cap + cash  D) Total assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **EV = Market cap + debt − cash** — what it costs to buy the whole business including its debts.\nB) and C) get the signs wrong. D) Total assets is an accounting measure, not EV.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** EV/EBITDA is preferred over P/E when comparing companies because it is:\nA) Easier to compute  B) Capital-structure neutral  C) Always lower  D) Regulated by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EV includes debt and EBITDA is before interest, so **differently-leveraged firms compare fairly**.\nA) It requires more inputs, not fewer. C) It is not systematically lower. D) Ratios are not regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The PEG ratio is:\nA) Price ÷ Earnings  B) P/E ÷ earnings growth rate  C) Price ÷ Growth  D) Earnings ÷ Growth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG adjusts the P/E for growth; **below 1** often suggests good value.\nA) Is the P/E itself. C) and D) are not standard definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Which ratio is most useful for valuing banks?\nA) P/S  B) P/B  C) EV/Sales  D) PEG\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Banks' assets are largely financial and carried near realisable value, so **book value is meaningful**.\nA) and C) are for loss-making or early-stage firms. D) PEG is growth-focused and not bank-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Technical analysis is based on:\nA) Financial statements  B) Price and volume history  C) Management interviews  D) Economic policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Technical analysis studies **price and volume charts**, ignoring intrinsic value.\nA), C) and D) are all fundamental analysis inputs.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Which statement captures the split between the two schools?\nA) Fundamental tells you when; technical tells you what  B) Fundamental tells you what to buy; technical tells you when  C) They are identical  D) Only technical analysis works\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard formulation: **fundamental = what, technical = when**.\nA) Reverses it. C) They differ in data, horizon and philosophy. D) Both have adherents and limitations.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** RSI readings above 70 conventionally indicate:\nA) Oversold  B) Overbought  C) A buy signal  D) Bankruptcy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Above **70 = overbought**; below **30 = oversold**.\nA) Is below 30. C) Overbought is generally treated as a caution, not a buy. D) RSI says nothing about solvency.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Support is a price level where:\nA) Selling pressure caps a rise  B) Buying has historically emerged, halting a fall  C) Trading is suspended  D) The company issues shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Support is a floor formed by historical buying interest.\nA) Describes **resistance**. C) and D) are unrelated to chart levels.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** When support is broken, it often becomes:\nA) Stronger support  B) Resistance  C) Irrelevant  D) A dividend level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A broken support level commonly acts as **resistance** on any subsequent rally.\nA) Once breached it has failed as support. C) It remains a significant reference level. D) Meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The three assumptions of technical analysis are:\nA) Markets are inefficient, prices are random, history is irrelevant  B) The market discounts everything, prices move in trends, history repeats  C) Only fundamentals matter  D) Volume is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These three underpin all technical work.\nA) Contradicts each assumption. C) Technical analysis explicitly sets fundamentals aside. D) Volume is a key confirming indicator.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** In the DCF model, terminal value is calculated as:\nA) CF ÷ r  B) CF ÷ (r − g), requiring g < r  C) CF × (1 + g)  D) CF × r\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Gordon growth formula, valid only when **g < r**.\nA) Assumes zero growth. C) and D) are not present-value formulas.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The main weakness of the DCF model is:\nA) It is too simple  B) Extreme sensitivity to the growth and discount rate assumptions  C) It cannot be applied to companies  D) It ignores cash flows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Small changes in **g** or **r** swing the result dramatically, and terminal value often dominates.\nA) It is the most complex of the common methods. C) It is designed for companies. D) Cash flows are its entire basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Sell-side research is:\nA) Internal and proprietary  B) Published to clients, typically by brokers and investment banks  C) Produced by mutual funds for their own use  D) Prohibited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell-side research is **published**, and paid for through brokerage.\nA) and C) describe **buy-side** research. D) It is a legitimate, regulated activity.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The E → I → C sequence stands for:\nA) Equity → Investment → Capital  B) Economy → Industry → Company  C) Earnings → Income → Cash  D) Entry → Investment → Closure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The standard **top-down** stock analysis sequence.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** EVA is calculated as:\nA) NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of Capital)  B) Revenue − Expenses  C) Market cap − Book value  D) EBITDA − Depreciation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital** — real value creation.\nB) Is accounting profit. C) Is closer to MVA. D) Is roughly EBIT.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Earnings yield is:\nA) P/E  B) EPS ÷ Price  C) Dividend ÷ Price  D) Price ÷ Sales\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **inverse of P/E**, expressed as a percentage so it compares with bond yields.\nA) Is its reciprocal. C) Is dividend yield. D) Is P/S.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which is a company-specific (unsystematic) risk?\nA) A recession  B) A rise in interest rates  C) A product recall at one firm  D) A geopolitical crisis\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A single firm's problem is **diversifiable**.\nA), B) and D) affect the whole market and are **systematic**.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under SEBI's classification, companies ranked 101–250 by market capitalisation are:\nA) Large cap  B) Mid cap  C) Small cap  D) Micro cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1–100 large, 101–250 mid, 251 onwards small.**\nA) Is ranks 1–100. C) Is 251 onwards. D) Is not a SEBI category for this purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A high dividend yield may indicate:\nA) Always a strong company  B) Possibly a falling share price rather than generosity  C) Guaranteed income  D) Low risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Yield = dividend ÷ **price**; a falling price mechanically raises the yield.\nA) It can equally signal distress. C) Dividends are discretionary. D) High yield often accompanies **higher** risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which valuation ratio would you use for a loss-making start-up?\nA) P/E  B) P/S or EV/Sales  C) PEG  D) Dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With negative earnings, revenue-based multiples are the workable option.\nA) Breaks with negative earnings. C) Requires meaningful earnings and growth. D) A loss-maker pays no dividend.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Bottom-up analysis begins with:\nA) GDP forecasts  B) The individual company  C) Monetary policy  D) Sector rotation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bottom-up starts with company merits, largely regardless of the macro view.\nA), C) and D) are all **top-down** starting points.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which is a governance red flag?\nA) An independent board  B) Auditor resignation  C) Consistent disclosure  D) Low promoter pledging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **auditor resignation** is one of the strongest warning signals available to an outside investor.\nA), C) and D) are all positive governance indicators.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** High promoter pledging is a concern because:\nA) It raises dividends  B) A price fall can trigger invocation and forced selling, driving the price down further  C) It reduces debt  D) It is illegal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **self-reinforcing spiral** of forced selling is the danger.\nA) Pledging has no bearing on dividends. C) It typically reflects promoter borrowing. D) Pledging is legal but must be disclosed.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Asset-based valuation is least suitable for:\nA) A steel plant  B) A property company  C) A software services firm  D) A shipping company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **software services** firm's value lies in people, brand and contracts — intangibles largely absent from the balance sheet.\nA), B) and D) are all asset-heavy businesses where book value is meaningful.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** MVA is:\nA) NOPAT − capital charge  B) Market value of the firm − capital invested  C) Market cap ÷ book value  D) Sales − costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market Value Added** is the market's verdict on cumulative value creation.\nA) Is EVA. C) Is P/B. D) Is profit.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which metric is specific to the banking sector?\nA) ARPU  B) Net Interest Margin  C) Same-store sales growth  D) Occupancy rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NIM** (along with NPAs, CASA and capital adequacy) is a core banking metric.\nA) Telecom. C) Retail. D) Hotels.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Volume in technical analysis is used primarily to:\nA) Calculate P/E  B) Confirm the credibility of a price move  C) Measure dividends  D) Set the coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A breakout on **high volume** is far more credible than one on thin volume.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated to volume.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The industry life cycle stages are:\nA) Birth → death  B) Pioneering → expansion → maturity → decline  C) Bull → bear  D) Primary → secondary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The stage determines expected growth, margins and competitive intensity.\nA) Is too crude. C) Describes market phases, not industries. D) Describes market segments.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Fundamental analysis compares intrinsic value with:\nA) The book value  B) The market price  C) The face value  D) The dividend\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If intrinsic value exceeds the **market price**, the stock may be undervalued.\nA) Book value is one input to some methods. C) Face value is an accounting artefact. D) Dividends are one input to the DDM.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A company's share price is ₹1,200 and its EPS is ₹48. What is the P/E, and the earnings yield?\nA) P/E 25; earnings yield 4%  B) P/E 4%; earnings yield 25  C) P/E 25; earnings yield 25%  D) P/E 48; earnings yield 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P/E = 1,200 ÷ 48 = **25**. Earnings yield = EPS ÷ Price = 48 ÷ 1,200 = **4%** (the reciprocal of 25).\nB) Swaps them. C) Miscomputes the yield. D) Confuses EPS with the P/E. **Useful check: earnings yield = 1 ÷ P/E, so 1 ÷ 25 = 4% ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A stock trades at ₹600 with a book value per share of ₹250. What is the P/B?\nA) 0.42  B) 2.4  C) 350  D) 150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — P/B = 600 ÷ 250 = **2.4**, i.e. the market pays 2.4× the net asset value.\nA) Inverts the ratio. C) Is the difference, not the ratio. D) Has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A company has a market cap of ₹8,000 crore, debt of ₹2,500 crore and cash of ₹700 crore. What is its Enterprise Value?\nA) ₹9,800 crore  B) ₹11,200 crore  C) ₹5,500 crore  D) ₹8,000 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — EV = market cap + debt − cash = 8,000 + 2,500 − 700 = **₹9,800 crore**.\nB) Adds cash instead of subtracting. C) Subtracts debt instead of adding. D) Ignores the capital structure entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Using Q38's EV and EBITDA of ₹1,400 crore, what is EV/EBITDA?\nA) 5.7  B) 7.0  C) 8.0  D) 3.9\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — EV/EBITDA = 9,800 ÷ 1,400 = **7.0**.\nA) Uses ₹8,000 crore market cap instead of EV. C) Uses a different EV. D) Miscomputes.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A stock has a P/E of 30 and expected earnings growth of 25% a year. What is its PEG, and what does it suggest?\nA) 1.2 — modestly expensive relative to growth  B) 0.83 — good value  C) 750 — meaningless  D) 5 — very cheap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — PEG = 30 ÷ 25 = **1.2**. Since **PEG below 1** is the conventional value threshold, 1.2 suggests the stock is **modestly expensive** relative to its growth.\nB) Inverts the calculation. C) Multiplies instead of dividing. D) Has no basis. **Caveat: PEG is only as reliable as the growth forecast.**\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor holds 25 stocks, all in the IT sector. What has she achieved?\nA) Full diversification  B) Company-specific risk is reduced, but sector risk remains concentrated  C) Elimination of market risk  D) Nothing at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding many names within one sector diversifies **company-specific** risk but leaves the portfolio fully exposed to IT sector drivers (currency, global tech spending, visa policy).\nA) Diversification requires **low correlation** across sectors. C) Market risk is never diversifiable. D) Company-specific risk genuinely was reduced.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A company reports EPS of ₹40, up from ₹25, but the increase came entirely from selling a factory. How should an analyst treat the P/E?\nA) Use it as reported  B) Adjust for the one-off gain, since P/E based on non-recurring earnings is misleading  C) Ignore earnings entirely  D) Use P/B instead always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A one-off asset sale is **not recurring operating earnings**, so a P/E built on it understates the true multiple.\nA) Would give a flatteringly low P/E. C) Earnings still matter — they just need adjusting. D) Switching ratios is not required; adjusting the input is.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Two firms have identical operations, but Firm A is debt-free and Firm B is heavily leveraged. Which ratio compares them most fairly?\nA) P/E  B) EV/EBITDA  C) Dividend yield  D) P/B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **EV/EBITDA is capital-structure neutral**: EV adds debt and EBITDA is measured before interest.\nA) P/E is distorted by leverage, since interest reduces earnings. C) Dividend policy differs for reasons unrelated to operations. D) P/B reflects accounting equity, which leverage also distorts.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A stock's price breaks below a long-standing support level at ₹450 on heavy volume. A technical analyst would conclude:\nA) A strong buy signal  B) A bearish signal — with heavy volume confirming, and ₹450 likely to act as resistance on any rally  C) The company is bankrupt  D) The P/E has fallen\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **break of support on high volume** is a classic bearish confirmation, and the broken support typically becomes **resistance**.\nA) Breaking support is bearish, not bullish. C) A chart break says nothing about solvency. D) A price fall does lower the P/E, but that is not the technical conclusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which analyst is more likely to face a conflict of interest arising from investment banking relationships?\nA) A buy-side analyst at a pension fund  B) A sell-side analyst at an investment bank  C) A retail investor  D) An auditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell-side research sits within firms that also earn from **underwriting and advisory** work for the companies covered.\nA) Buy-side analysts serve their own fund's returns, with fewer external conflicts. C) and D) have different roles and conflicts.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A DCF model gives a value of ₹1,800 with g = 5% and r = 11%. If g is raised to 7%, what happens?\nA) Value falls  B) Value rises substantially, because the terminal value denominator (r − g) shrinks from 6% to 4%  C) No change  D) Value becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The terminal value denominator falls from **0.06 to 0.04**, a one-third reduction that raises terminal value by roughly **50%**. This is precisely DCF's sensitivity problem.\nA) A higher growth rate raises value. C) The effect is large. D) Value would only break down if g ≥ r.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A company's operating cash flow is consistently far below its reported net profit. This suggests:\nA) Excellent earnings quality  B) A possible earnings-quality problem — profits may be driven by accruals rather than cash  C) The company is debt-free  D) Nothing of concern\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A persistent **profit–cash flow gap** is a classic warning of aggressive revenue recognition or mounting receivables.\nA) It indicates the opposite. C) The gap says nothing about debt. D) It is one of the strongest red flags available.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Which stock would you expect to have the highest liquidity risk?\nA) A Nifty 50 constituent  B) A small-cap stock with thin daily volumes  C) A large-cap bank  D) An index ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Thin volumes mean **wide spreads and difficulty exiting** without moving the price.\nA), C) and D) are all highly liquid.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An investor argues that adding a 60th stock will meaningfully reduce her portfolio's risk. The correct response is:\nA) Correct — more is always better  B) Most diversification benefit is captured by roughly 15–25 low-correlation stocks; beyond that the marginal benefit is small  C) She should hold only one stock  D) Diversification eliminates market risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The benefit curve flattens quickly, and over-diversification simply approximates the index while adding monitoring cost.\nA) The marginal benefit approaches zero. C) One stock is maximum unsystematic risk. D) Market risk is never diversifiable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A company with a P/E of 12 operates in a sector where the average P/E is 25. This means:\nA) The stock is definitely undervalued  B) It is cheap relative to peers, but the discount may be justified by lower growth, higher risk or governance concerns  C) The stock is overvalued  D) The sector average is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A discount is a **question, not an answer**. Cheap stocks are often cheap for a reason — the analyst's job is to find out which.\nA) Relative cheapness is not proof of undervaluation. C) It is cheaper, not dearer, than peers. D) The average is simply an observation.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which risk remains after a portfolio holds 40 stocks across 12 sectors?\nA) Company-specific risk  B) Systematic (market) risk  C) No risk at all  D) Only liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Broad diversification removes most unsystematic risk, leaving **market risk**, which cannot be diversified away.\nA) It has largely been diversified. C) Market risk always remains. D) Liquidity risk is manageable via stock selection.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A technical analyst notes that a stock's 50-day moving average has crossed above its 200-day moving average. This is generally read as:\nA) A bearish signal  B) A bullish signal (a \"golden cross\")  C) A dividend announcement  D) An accounting change\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A shorter average crossing **above** a longer one signals strengthening momentum.\nA) The reverse crossing (a \"death cross\") is bearish. C) and D) are corporate events, not chart signals.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An investor uses only technical analysis to select a stock for a 15-year retirement goal. The main criticism is:\nA) Technical analysis is illegal  B) Technical analysis is oriented to short- and medium-term timing, not to the long-run business quality that drives a 15-year outcome  C) Charts are unavailable  D) It requires a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Over 15 years, **business fundamentals** dominate; chart patterns say nothing about whether the company will still be earning in 2040.\nA) It is entirely legal. C) Charts are freely available. D) Irrelevant to the criticism.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A firm reports NOPAT of ₹900 crore, capital employed of ₹6,000 crore and a cost of capital of 12%. What is its EVA?\nA) ₹180 crore  B) ₹720 crore  C) ₹900 crore  D) −₹180 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Capital charge = 6,000 × 12% = **₹720 crore**. EVA = 900 − 720 = **₹180 crore**, so the firm created genuine value.\nB) Is the capital charge itself. C) Is NOPAT before the charge. D) Gets the sign wrong. **Positive EVA means the firm earned more than its cost of capital.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Which combination correctly describes buy-side research?\nA) Published to clients, paid via brokerage  B) Internal and proprietary, produced for the employer's own portfolios  C) Produced by auditors  D) Prohibited by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buy-side analysts work for asset managers and keep their work **internal**.\nA) Describes sell-side. C) Auditors perform a statutory role. D) It is entirely legitimate.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A company's promoter has pledged 65% of his holding. An analyst should regard this as:\nA) A positive sign of confidence  B) A significant risk — a price fall could trigger invocation and forced selling  C) Irrelevant  D) A guarantee of dividends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — High pledging creates a **forced-selling spiral** risk if the price falls.\nA) It usually reflects promoter leverage, not confidence. C) It is among the more predictive red flags. D) There is no link to dividends.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which valuation approach is theoretically soundest but most assumption-sensitive?\nA) P/E multiple  B) DCF  C) Dividend yield  D) P/S\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **DCF** values the business on its own economics, but the answer swings on **g** and **r**.\nA), C) and D) are relative or partial measures — quicker, but they only tell you about value relative to something else.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Two companies both have a P/E of 20. Company X grows earnings at 30%, Company Y at 5%. Which appears better value?\nA) Company Y  B) Company X, with a PEG of 0.67 versus Y's 4.0  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG(X) = 20 ÷ 30 = **0.67** (below 1, attractive); PEG(Y) = 20 ÷ 5 = **4.0** (expensive for its growth).\nA) Y is far more expensive relative to growth. C) The same P/E means very different things at different growth rates — that is precisely PEG's purpose. D) PEG makes them directly comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A stock's dividend yield rises from 2% to 6% while the dividend per share is unchanged. What happened?\nA) The company tripled its dividend  B) The share price fell by about two-thirds  C) Earnings rose  D) The face value changed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With the dividend constant, yield can only rise if the **price falls**. Tripling the yield implies the price fell to roughly one-third of its former level.\nA) The dividend is stated as unchanged. C) Earnings do not enter dividend yield. D) Face value is irrelevant to yield. **This is exactly why a high yield can be a distress signal.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is the correct order of the top-down process?\nA) Company → industry → economy  B) Economy → industry → company  C) Industry → economy → company  D) Company → economy → industry\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Top-down narrows from the macro picture to the specific firm.\nA) Describes bottom-up. C) and D) scramble the sequence.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A company's ROE is 22% while its cost of equity is 13%. This suggests:\nA) The company is destroying value  B) The company is earning well above its cost of equity — a sign of value creation  C) ROE is irrelevant  D) The company should shut down\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Earning a **return above the cost of capital** is the essence of value creation (the same idea EVA formalises).\nA) That would require ROE below the cost of equity. C) ROE is a central profitability measure. D) The opposite conclusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which pair of analyses would a disciplined long-term investor most sensibly combine?\nA) Technical analysis alone  B) Fundamental analysis to choose the stock, with technical analysis to help time the entry  C) Astrology and charts  D) Only dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **What to buy** (fundamental) plus **when to buy** (technical) is the standard combination.\nA) Ignores business quality over a long horizon. C) Not an analytical method. D) A single ratio is far too narrow.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A company's P/B is 0.6 — well below 1. What does this mean, and what should be checked?\nA) The stock is certainly a bargain  B) The market values the company below its book value, which may signal distress, poor returns on assets, or an overstated book value  C) The company has no assets  D) The P/E must also be low\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Trading below book means the market doubts the assets will earn an adequate return — or doubts the **book value itself** (e.g. unprovisioned bad loans at a bank).\nA) Deep-value opportunities exist, but so do value traps. C) A low P/B means assets are cheaply valued, not absent. D) P/E and P/B can diverge sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An investor buys equally into 10 stocks each month for three years. She is practising:\nA) Cross-sectional diversification only  B) Both cross-sectional (10 stocks) and time-series (monthly entry) diversification  C) Leverage  D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ten holdings give **cross-sectional** spread; monthly investing gives **time-series** spread.\nA) Ignores the timing dimension. C) No borrowing is involved. D) No risk-free price difference is being exploited.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which sector metric would you use to assess a telecom company's revenue quality?\nA) NIM  B) ARPU (average revenue per user)  C) Same-store sales  D) Occupancy rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **ARPU** and subscriber numbers are the core telecom metrics.\nA) Banking. C) Retail. D) Hotels.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A DCF assumes a terminal growth rate of 12% and a discount rate of 10%. What is wrong?\nA) Nothing  B) g ≥ r, so the terminal value formula breaks down and implies infinite value  C) The growth rate is too low  D) The discount rate is too high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **g (12%) > r (10%)**, the denominator (r − g) is negative and the formula is meaningless. In principle no company can grow faster than the discount rate forever.\nA) It is a fatal modelling error. C) 12% perpetual growth is implausibly **high**, not low. D) The discount rate is not the problem — the growth assumption is.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which of these best explains why unsystematic risk carries no risk premium?\nA) It is larger than systematic risk  B) It can be eliminated at no cost through diversification, so the market will not pay you to bear it  C) It is illegal to bear  D) It does not exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Markets compensate only **unavoidable** risk. Bearing avoidable risk is a choice, not a service.\nA) Size is not the criterion. C) There is nothing illegal about a concentrated portfolio. D) It certainly exists — it is simply diversifiable.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A company frequently changes auditors and reports large related-party transactions. An analyst should:\nA) Ignore this — it is routine  B) Treat these as serious governance red flags warranting deep scrutiny or avoidance  C) Increase the valuation multiple  D) Assume higher growth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both are **classic red flags** associated with value siphoning and accounting manipulation.\nA) These are precisely the signals a careful analyst weights heavily. C) and D) reward the very behaviour that should be penalised.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A stock trades at ₹900 with sales per share of ₹300 and no profit. What is the P/S, and why use it here?\nA) 3.0 — used because P/E is meaningless with no profit  B) 0.33 — used to measure profitability  C) 600 — the difference  D) P/S cannot be used\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P/S = 900 ÷ 300 = **3.0**. With no profit the P/E is undefined, so a **revenue** multiple is the workable alternative.\nB) Inverts the ratio and misstates its purpose — P/S **ignores** profitability. C) Is the difference, not a ratio. D) P/S is designed for exactly this situation.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which statement about relative valuation is correct?\nA) It reveals whether a whole market is overvalued  B) It only shows value relative to peers or history, so a uniformly overvalued sector will still look \"fairly valued\" internally  C) It is more precise than DCF  D) It requires no assumptions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is relative valuation's **fundamental blind spot**.\nA) It cannot detect market-wide mispricing. C) It is quicker but not more precise. D) It assumes the peer set is comparable and correctly priced.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A company has 12 crore shares at ₹850, debt ₹3,200 crore, cash ₹900 crore, EBITDA ₹1,600 crore and net profit ₹640 crore. Compute the P/E and EV/EBITDA.\nA) P/E 15.9; EV/EBITDA 7.8  B) P/E 15.9; EV/EBITDA 6.4  C) P/E 12.0; EV/EBITDA 7.8  D) P/E 20.0; EV/EBITDA 8.0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Market cap** = 12 crore × ₹850 = **₹10,200 crore**. **EPS** = 640 ÷ 12 = ₹53.33, so **P/E** = 850 ÷ 53.33 = **15.9** (equivalently 10,200 ÷ 640 = 15.9). **EV** = 10,200 + 3,200 − 900 = **₹12,500 crore**. **EV/EBITDA** = 12,500 ÷ 1,600 = **7.8**.\nB) 6.4 divides by **market cap** instead of EV, ignoring the net debt an acquirer must assume. C) miscomputes the P/E. D) is wrong on both. **Note EV/EBITDA (7.8) exceeds market cap/EBITDA (6.4) precisely because the company carries ₹2,300 crore of net debt.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Two companies in the same sector: X has P/E 18 and growth 20%; Y has P/E 32 and growth 40%. Which is better value on PEG, and what caveat applies?\nA) X, PEG 0.90 vs Y's 0.80  B) Y, PEG 0.80 vs X's 0.90 — but a 40% growth forecast is far less reliable than 20%  C) They are identical  D) X, because its P/E is lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PEG(X) = 18 ÷ 20 = **0.90**; PEG(Y) = 32 ÷ 40 = **0.80**. On PEG, **Y is cheaper**.\nA) Gets the numbers right but the conclusion backwards. C) They differ. D) A lower P/E alone ignores growth — the whole reason PEG exists. **The caveat is the real lesson: PEG is only as good as the growth estimate, and high growth rates are the hardest to sustain and the easiest to over-forecast.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A DCF values a firm at ₹2,400 crore using r = 12% and terminal g = 4%. If r rises to 14%, what happens to the terminal value component, roughly?\nA) It rises  B) It falls sharply — the denominator (r − g) widens from 8% to 10%, cutting terminal value by about 20%  C) It is unchanged  D) It becomes negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Terminal value ∝ 1 ÷ (r − g). Moving from **0.08 to 0.10** multiplies TV by 0.08 ÷ 0.10 = **0.80**, a **20% fall**.\nA) A higher discount rate lowers present value. C) The effect is substantial. D) It falls but stays positive while r > g. **This is DCF's sensitivity in one calculation: a 2-point change in r cut a fifth off the dominant value component.**\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor's portfolio is 30 stocks: 12 banks, 10 NBFCs, 8 insurers. Assess the diversification.\nA) Excellent — 30 stocks across 3 sectors  B) Poor — all 30 are financials, sharing exposure to interest rates, credit cycles and regulation, so correlations are high  C) Perfect  D) Only market risk remains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Banks, NBFCs and insurers are **all financial-sector businesses** driven by the same rate and credit cycle. The apparent three-sector spread is illusory.\nA) Counting names and nominal sectors misses correlation. C) It is heavily concentrated. D) Substantial **sector-specific** risk remains on top of market risk. **Diversification is about low correlation, never about count.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A bank trades at a P/B of 0.5 with reported book value ₹200 per share. Its gross NPAs are 14% and rising, with low provision coverage. What is the most likely explanation?\nA) The market is irrational  B) The market doubts the stated book value — unprovisioned bad loans mean the true book is lower than ₹200  C) The bank is a certain bargain  D) P/B is inapplicable to banks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a bank, **book value is only as good as its loan provisioning**. With **14% gross NPAs and low coverage**, the market is signalling that writing down bad loans would materially reduce the ₹200 book value.\nA) The discount has a clear rationale. C) This is a textbook **value trap**. D) P/B is the *most* relevant ratio for banks — which is exactly why its quality matters so much here.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A company reports rising profits for three years, but receivables have grown from 45 to 140 days of sales and operating cash flow is negative. What should an analyst conclude?\nA) The company is performing excellently  B) Earnings quality is poor — revenue may be recognised on sales that are not converting to cash, a classic accounting red flag  C) The company has too much cash  D) The P/E is too high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Receivables tripling in days-of-sales while operating cash flow is **negative** points to **aggressive revenue recognition** or to sales made to weak customers who cannot pay.\nA) Profit growth without cash generation is a warning, not a strength. C) The company is consuming cash. D) The P/E level is a separate question — the issue is whether the E is real.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A stock has beta 1.4, the risk-free rate is 7% and the equity risk premium is 6%. What return does CAPM require, and what does beta measure?\nA) 13.0%; total risk  B) 15.4%; systematic (market) risk  C) 8.4%; unsystematic risk  D) 7.0%; no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAPM: **Required return = Rf + β × ERP** = 7% + (1.4 × 6%) = 7% + 8.4% = **15.4%**. Beta measures **systematic (market) risk** — sensitivity to market movements — not total risk.\nA) Uses beta 1.0. C) Adds only the premium component and misidentifies the risk type. D) Ignores beta entirely. **Only systematic risk is priced, which is why beta and not standard deviation appears in CAPM.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A company's EVA is negative despite reporting a healthy accounting profit. What does this mean?\nA) The accounts are fraudulent  B) The firm earned a profit but LESS than its cost of capital, so it destroyed economic value  C) The firm has no capital  D) EVA cannot be negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Accounting profit ignores the **cost of equity capital**. Negative EVA means the return on capital fell short of what investors require — value destruction despite a positive bottom line.\nA) Negative EVA is an economic judgement, not evidence of fraud. C) The capital charge presupposes capital. D) Negative EVA is common.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A stock at ₹720 has support at ₹700 and resistance at ₹780. It breaks above ₹780 on triple its average volume. What is the technical reading?\nA) Bearish — it has risen too far  B) Bullish breakout confirmed by volume; ₹780 would now be expected to act as support  C) Neutral  D) The company has issued a bonus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A break **above resistance** on **heavy volume** is a bullish confirmation, and the broken resistance typically flips to **support**.\nA) Technical analysis reads a confirmed breakout as continuation, not exhaustion. C) Volume confirmation makes it a definite signal. D) A corporate action would be reflected differently.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A company with EPS of ₹60 trades at ₹1,500. The bond yield is 7.5%. Compare the earnings yield with the bond yield.\nA) Earnings yield 4%, below the bond yield of 7.5% — equity looks expensive on this measure  B) Earnings yield 25%, well above  C) Earnings yield 7.5%, identical  D) They cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Earnings yield = 60 ÷ 1,500 = **4%**, versus a **7.5%** bond yield. On this simple comparison, the equity offers a lower earnings return than risk-free-ish debt — a caution flag.\nB) Inverts the calculation (that is the P/E of 25). C) They differ. D) The earnings yield exists precisely to enable this comparison. **The caveat: equity earnings can grow, while a bond coupon is fixed — so a lower starting yield can still be justified.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An investor holds one stock worth ₹50 lakh, her entire portfolio. Which risks does she bear, and which could she remove for free?\nA) Only market risk  B) Both systematic and unsystematic risk — the unsystematic (company-specific) portion could be diversified away at no cost, and she is not compensated for bearing it  C) No risk  D) Only liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Concentration means she bears **maximum company-specific risk** on top of unavoidable market risk. The market pays her **nothing extra** for the company-specific portion, because it is removable for free.\nA) She bears far more than market risk. C) Single-stock concentration is the riskiest equity position possible. D) Liquidity risk is one component, not the whole picture.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which combination would justify a company trading at a persistent premium P/E to its sector?\nA) Falling market share and rising debt  B) Superior and durable return on capital, consistent cash conversion, strong governance and a defensible competitive moat  C) Frequent auditor changes  D) High promoter pledging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium multiple is justified by **higher, more durable and more certain future cash flows** — which is what a moat and clean governance deliver.\nA), C) and D) are all reasons a stock should trade at a **discount**.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A company sells a division for a large one-off gain, tripling reported EPS. Its P/E falls from 30 to 10. An analyst should:\nA) Buy immediately — the P/E is now cheap  B) Recompute the P/E on continuing operations only, since the low multiple is an artefact of non-recurring earnings  C) Use P/B instead  D) Ignore the sale\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A P/E built on **one-off** earnings is misleading. Stripping the gain would restore a multiple close to the original 30 — and the company now also has **one fewer division** generating future earnings.\nA) Precisely the trap the question sets. C) Switching ratios does not fix a distorted input. D) The sale is central to understanding both the earnings and the reduced future earnings base.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which pairing of company type and valuation approach is MOST appropriate?\nA) Loss-making start-up → P/E  B) Mature bank → P/B; early-stage e-commerce firm → EV/Sales; stable dividend payer → dividend discount model  C) Software firm → asset-based valuation  D) Every company → P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each method is matched to what is measurable and meaningful for that business.\nA) P/E fails with negative earnings. C) Asset-based valuation is worst for asset-light, intangible-heavy firms. D) No single ratio suits all businesses — that is the chapter's central lesson.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A portfolio manager finds a stock with intrinsic value ₹1,100 (via DCF) trading at ₹1,450, while peers trade at a similar EV/EBITDA. What are the possible interpretations?\nA) The stock is definitely a buy  B) Either the stock (and possibly the whole sector) is overvalued, or the DCF assumptions are too conservative — the divergence is a signal to re-examine both  C) DCF is always right  D) Relative valuation is always right\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — DCF says overvalued; relative valuation says in line with peers. Both can be true if the **entire sector** is expensive — relative valuation cannot detect market-wide mispricing. Alternatively the DCF's g or r may be too pessimistic.\nA) The DCF indicates it is expensive, not cheap. C) and D) treat one method as infallible; the right response is to interrogate the assumptions behind both.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A stock's RSI reads 82 while the price is at an all-time high on declining volume. A technical analyst would note:\nA) A strong buy signal  B) Overbought conditions (RSI > 70) with declining volume failing to confirm the advance — a caution signal  C) The company is undervalued  D) A bonus issue is due\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RSI above 70 is overbought**, and a rise on **falling volume** lacks confirmation — often described as a weakening advance.\nA) Overbought plus unconfirmed volume is the opposite of a strong buy. C) Technical analysis makes no valuation claim. D) Unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Two firms have identical EBITDA of ₹500 crore. Firm A: market cap ₹4,000 crore, debt ₹1,000 crore, cash ₹200 crore. Firm B: market cap ₹4,500 crore, no debt, cash ₹300 crore. Which is cheaper on EV/EBITDA?\nA) Firm A at 9.6× vs Firm B at 8.4×  B) Firm B at 8.4× vs Firm A at 9.6×  C) They are identical  D) Firm A, because its market cap is lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Firm A EV** = 4,000 + 1,000 − 200 = **₹4,800 crore** → EV/EBITDA = 4,800 ÷ 500 = **9.6×**. **Firm B EV** = 4,500 + 0 − 300 = **₹4,200 crore** → 4,200 ÷ 500 = **8.4×**. **Firm B is cheaper.**\nA) Has the numbers but the conclusion inverted. C) They differ. D) **This is the exact trap:** Firm A has the lower market cap but the **higher** enterprise value, because its debt must be assumed by any acquirer.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Which scenario best illustrates that relative valuation cannot detect a bubble?\nA) A stock at a P/E of 8 in a sector averaging 8  B) Every stock in a sector trading at a P/E of 90, so each looks \"fairly valued\" against its peers even though all are extremely expensive on cash flow  C) A stock at a P/E of 15  D) A loss-making firm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Relative valuation measures against the **peer set**. If the entire peer set is mispriced, every member looks fine relative to the others — the blind spot only an absolute method like DCF can expose.\nA) Shows consistency at a low multiple, not a bubble. C) Is unremarkable. D) Raises a different issue (which ratio to use).\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A company's ROE is 25%, but it achieves this with a debt-to-equity ratio of 4:1. What should an analyst conclude?\nA) It is a superb business  B) The high ROE is substantially leverage-driven, so it carries higher financial risk and is less durable than the same ROE achieved with low debt  C) ROE is meaningless  D) The company has no debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Leverage **mechanically amplifies ROE** by shrinking the equity denominator. The same 25% from an unlevered business is far higher quality, because it does not depend on continued access to cheap debt and is not magnified into losses in a downturn.\nA) The headline number flatters the reality. C) ROE is useful — it just needs decomposing (DuPont: margin × turnover × leverage). D) 4:1 is high leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An analyst forecasts a company will grow at 25% for 10 years, then 8% forever, with a discount rate of 11%. What is the flaw?\nA) None  B) A perpetual 8% growth rate is aggressive relative to an 11% discount rate, and 25% for a decade is very rarely sustained — the terminal value will dominate and the valuation will be inflated  C) The discount rate is too low  D) Growth should be higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two compounding optimisms. Perpetual growth should not exceed long-run nominal GDP growth; at **8% against r = 11%**, the terminal denominator is only 3%, so terminal value **dominates** the result. And sustaining **25% for 10 years** is exceptionally rare.\nA) These are material modelling problems. C) 11% is a reasonable equity discount rate. D) The growth assumptions are already too high.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A stock's price falls 40% but its business fundamentals are unchanged. On a DCF basis, what has happened?\nA) Intrinsic value fell 40%  B) Intrinsic value is unchanged, so the stock is now potentially undervalued relative to it — assuming the fundamentals genuinely have not changed  C) The DCF is invalid  D) The company must have issued shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — DCF value depends on **future cash flows and the discount rate**, not the market price. If neither changed, intrinsic value is unchanged and the gap has widened in the buyer's favour.\nA) Price and intrinsic value are different things — the whole premise of fundamental analysis. C) The method still applies. D) A price fall does not imply an issuance. **The essential caveat: verify that fundamentals really are unchanged — a 40% fall usually means the market believes something has.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination of technical signals would a chartist regard as most bearish?\nA) Price above the 200-day moving average, RSI 45, rising volume on up-days  B) Price breaking below support on heavy volume, 50-day MA crossing below 200-day MA, RSI falling through 30  C) A golden cross with high volume  D) RSI at 50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three bearish confirmations together: a **support break on heavy volume**, a **death cross**, and RSI breaking down through the oversold threshold indicating strong downward momentum.\nA) Describes a healthy uptrend. C) A golden cross is bullish. D) RSI 50 is neutral.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A company's shares trade at ₹380 with 8 crore shares outstanding. Net profit is ₹190 crore, of which ₹60 crore came from a one-time tax refund. What is the adjusted P/E?\nA) 16.0  B) 23.4  C) 12.0  D) 30.4\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Market cap** = 8 crore × ₹380 = **₹3,040 crore**. **Reported P/E** = 3,040 ÷ 190 = 16.0. But recurring profit = 190 − 60 = **₹130 crore**, so the **adjusted P/E** = 3,040 ÷ 130 = **23.4**.\nA) Is the flattering reported figure. C) and D) do not follow. **The stock is materially more expensive than the headline P/E suggests — always strip one-offs.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which describes the correct relationship between number of holdings, correlation and portfolio risk?\nA) More holdings always means less risk regardless of correlation  B) Risk falls as holdings increase only to the extent the additions have low correlation with existing holdings; systematic risk remains regardless  C) Correlation is irrelevant  D) One stock is optimal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Diversification works **through low correlation**, and it has a floor: **systematic risk**.\nA) Twenty highly correlated stocks barely diversify. C) Correlation is the entire mechanism. D) One stock maximises unsystematic risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor asks why she should care about corporate governance when the financial numbers look excellent. The best answer is:\nA) Governance is irrelevant to returns  B) Reported numbers describe the past and depend on management's integrity to be accurate; weak governance means the numbers may be unreliable and minority shareholders may not receive their share of future value  C) Governance only matters for banks  D) SEBI guarantees governance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two distinct risks: the numbers may not be **true**, and even if true, value may be **diverted** from minority shareholders through related-party transactions or unfair restructurings.\nA) Governance failures have destroyed shareholder value repeatedly. C) It matters in every sector. D) SEBI sets rules but cannot guarantee conduct.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A stock has a P/E of 45 in a sector averaging 20. Which single question best determines whether this is justified?\nA) Is the share price high?  B) Does the company have materially superior and durable growth, returns on capital and earnings quality to justify the premium — or is the multiple driven by sentiment?  C) Is it a large-cap?  D) Does it pay dividends?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A premium multiple must be **earned by superior economics**. If growth, returns and earnings quality do not justify it, the premium is sentiment and will mean-revert.\nA) Price level alone says nothing about value. C) Size does not justify a multiple. D) Dividend policy is largely separate from the growth premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A DCF, a P/E comparison and a P/B comparison give values of ₹800, ₹1,050 and ₹640 for the same stock trading at ₹900. What should the analyst do?\nA) Pick the highest and buy  B) Treat the range as the useful output — understand why the methods diverge (growth assumptions, asset intensity, peer comparability) and form a judgement, rather than trusting any single figure  C) Pick the lowest and sell  D) Discard all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Valuation produces a **range, not a point**. Divergence is informative: it tells you which assumptions are driving which answer.\nA) and C) cherry-pick the number that supports a pre-formed view. D) Three imperfect estimates are far more useful than none. **The stock at ₹900 sits inside the ₹640–₹1,050 range — the honest conclusion is \"roughly fairly valued, with wide uncertainty\".**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Which statement about beta and standard deviation is correct?\nA) They measure the same thing  B) Standard deviation measures TOTAL risk; beta measures only SYSTEMATIC risk — which is why only beta is priced in CAPM  C) Beta measures total risk  D) Neither is used in practice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A stock can have high standard deviation (much of it company-specific, hence diversifiable and unpriced) yet a low beta.\nA) They measure different things. C) Reverses the definitions. D) Both are standard measures — see Chapter 16.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A company in a declining industry trades at a P/E of 6 with a 9% dividend yield. Assess.\nA) An obvious bargain  B) Possibly a value trap — a declining industry means shrinking future earnings, and a 9% yield may be unsustainable if profits fall; the low multiple may correctly reflect the outlook  C) Certain to double  D) The dividend is guaranteed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Low multiples and high yields in **structurally declining** industries frequently signal that the market has correctly priced falling earnings — the classic **value trap**.\nA) Cheapness relative to the past is not cheapness relative to the future. C) Nothing is certain. D) Dividends are discretionary and are usually cut when earnings fall.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single principle best summarises equity analysis in this chapter?\nA) Buy whatever has the lowest P/E  B) Estimate what the business is worth from its economics, cross-check against what the market pays for comparable firms, verify that the numbers and the people behind them are trustworthy, and diversify across low-correlation holdings  C) Follow chart patterns only  D) Buy the highest dividend yield\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It captures all four strands: **absolute valuation** (DCF), **relative valuation** (multiples), **qualitative/governance** assessment, and **diversification**.\nA) The lowest P/E is often the most troubled company. C) Charts address timing, not business quality. D) The highest yield is frequently the most distressed company.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.**\n\n### Case: Analysing Vantage Chemicals Ltd\n\nAn analyst is evaluating **Vantage Chemicals Ltd**:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Shares outstanding | **15 crore** |\n| Market price | **₹640** |\n| Net profit (FY25) | **₹720 crore** |\n| — of which, one-time land sale gain | **₹180 crore** |\n| EBITDA | **₹1,350 crore** |\n| Total debt | **₹2,800 crore** |\n| Cash and equivalents | **₹500 crore** |\n| Book value per share | **₹290** |\n| Expected earnings growth (recurring) | **14% p.a.** |\n| Sector average P/E | **22** |\n\nThe analyst also notes: the promoter has **pledged 48%** of his holding, and the company's **statutory auditor resigned** last quarter citing \"pre-occupation\".\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Vantage's market capitalisation and Enterprise Value?\nA) Market cap ₹9,600 crore; EV ₹11,900 crore  B) Market cap ₹9,600 crore; EV ₹7,300 crore  C) Market cap ₹640 crore; EV ₹2,940 crore  D) Market cap ₹11,900 crore; EV ₹9,600 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Market cap** = 15 crore × ₹640 = **₹9,600 crore**. **EV** = market cap + debt − cash = 9,600 + 2,800 − 500 = **₹11,900 crore**.\nB) Subtracts debt instead of adding it. C) Confuses the share price with market cap. D) Swaps the two figures. *(EV exceeds market cap because Vantage carries net debt of ₹2,300 crore.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the reported P/E, and what is the P/E adjusted for the one-time gain?\nA) Reported 13.3; adjusted 17.8  B) Reported 17.8; adjusted 13.3  C) Reported 13.3; adjusted 13.3  D) Reported 22.0; adjusted 14.0\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Reported P/E** = 9,600 ÷ 720 = **13.3**. **Recurring profit** = 720 − 180 = **₹540 crore**, so the **adjusted P/E** = 9,600 ÷ 540 = **17.8**.\nB) Inverts the two. C) Fails to strip the one-off. D) Uses the sector average as the reported figure. **The headline P/E of 13.3 flatters the stock; on recurring earnings it is 17.8 — a third more expensive.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Compute EV/EBITDA and P/B for Vantage.\nA) EV/EBITDA 8.8; P/B 2.21  B) EV/EBITDA 7.1; P/B 2.21  C) EV/EBITDA 8.8; P/B 0.45  D) EV/EBITDA 11.9; P/B 1.35\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **EV/EBITDA** = 11,900 ÷ 1,350 = **8.81**. **P/B** = 640 ÷ 290 = **2.21**.\nB) Uses market cap instead of EV. C) Inverts the P/B. D) Miscomputes both. **Note how the debt matters: market cap/EBITDA would be only 7.1, but any acquirer must also assume ₹2,300 crore of net debt — hence 8.8.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Using the adjusted P/E and recurring growth, what is the PEG, and how does the stock compare with its sector?\nA) PEG 1.27; the stock trades below the sector P/E of 22 on an adjusted basis  B) PEG 0.95; expensive  C) PEG 2.5; very cheap  D) PEG 1.27; the stock trades above the sector P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **PEG** = adjusted P/E ÷ growth = 17.8 ÷ 14 = **1.27**. Against the **sector average P/E of 22**, Vantage's adjusted 17.8 is still a **discount**.\nB) Uses the reported P/E. C) Inverts the calculation. D) 17.8 is below 22, not above. **So on the numbers alone Vantage looks moderately cheap versus its sector — but PEG above 1 says it is not cheap relative to its own growth. The discount needs explaining, which QC5 addresses.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Given the full picture, what is the analyst's most defensible conclusion?\nA) Buy — the stock trades at a discount to the sector  B) The sector discount is likely explained by governance risk: an auditor resigning citing \"pre-occupation\" and 48% promoter pledging are serious red flags that raise doubts about earnings reliability and create forced-selling risk — further investigation or avoidance is warranted  C) Sell short immediately  D) Governance is irrelevant given the low P/E\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The valuation work says \"moderately cheap versus sector\". The **qualitative** work says why. **An auditor resigning with a vague reason** is one of the strongest warning signals available to an outside investor — auditors rarely leave a clean client. Combined with **48% promoter pledging**, which creates a forced-selling spiral if the price falls, the market's discount looks **rational rather than an opportunity**.\nA) Treats a discount as automatically an opportunity, ignoring why it exists. C) The evidence justifies caution and investigation, not a confident short position. D) Governance is precisely what determines whether the reported earnings — and therefore the low multiple — can be trusted.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** valuation ratios tell you *what the market is paying*; they never tell you *whether the numbers are real*. Vantage's headline P/E of 13.3 becomes 17.8 once a one-off is stripped, and the remaining sector discount is best explained by governance risk — not by mispricing. **Always finish the qualitative work before acting on the quantitative.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "09", "title": "Investing in Fixed Income Securities", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> ⭐ The hardest maths in the paper sits here: **bond pricing, yield measures and duration**. Chapter 2's TVM formulas are the foundation — a bond is simply an annuity (the coupons) plus a lump sum (the redemption).\n\n---\n\n## 9.1 The Debt Market and Its Role\n\nDebt lets **governments and corporates borrow** from many lenders at once.\n\n| Borrower | Why they issue debt |\n|---|---|\n| **Government** | Fund the fiscal deficit, infrastructure, and manage liquidity |\n| **Corporates** | Fund expansion without diluting ownership; interest is **tax-deductible**, unlike dividends |\n\n**Why debt rather than equity, from the issuer's side:** no dilution of control, a **known** cost, and interest is deductible for tax. The cost is a **fixed obligation** that must be met regardless of profitability.\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 The Bond Market Ecosystem\n\n| Participant | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Issuers** | Government, PSUs, corporates, banks, NBFCs |\n| **Investors** | Banks, insurers, pension funds, mutual funds, FPIs, retail |\n| **Intermediaries** | Merchant bankers, brokers, primary dealers |\n| **Credit rating agencies** | Assess default risk (CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings) |\n| **Debenture trustees** | Protect bondholders' interests; enforce security |\n| **Regulators** | **RBI** — G-secs and money market; **SEBI** — corporate bonds and listing |\n\n> ⚠️ **Split jurisdiction:** government securities and the money market sit with the **RBI**; **corporate bonds** and their listing sit with **SEBI**.\n\n---\n\n## 9.3 ⭐ Risks in Fixed Income\n\n| Risk | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Interest rate risk** | Bond prices fall when rates rise. The **biggest** risk for a high-quality bond |\n| **Call risk** | The issuer redeems early (when rates fall), forcing reinvestment at lower rates |\n| **Reinvestment risk** | Coupons must be reinvested at whatever rate then prevails — a risk that **rates fall** |\n| **Credit risk** | The issuer may not pay. Three sub-types: **downgrade risk** (rating cut), **spread/basis risk** (credit spreads widen), **default risk** (non-payment) |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Cannot sell at a fair price; wide spreads, especially in corporate bonds |\n| **Exchange rate risk** | For foreign-currency bonds, adverse currency movement |\n| **Inflation (purchasing power) risk** | Fixed coupons buy less as prices rise |\n| **Volatility risk** | Relevant to bonds with embedded options — higher volatility raises option value |\n| **Political / legal risk** | Changes in law, tax or regulation affecting the bond |\n| **Event risk** | A specific event — merger, restructuring, disaster — impairing the issuer |\n\n> 🧠 **Interest rate risk and reinvestment risk pull in opposite directions.** If rates rise, your bond's *price* falls (bad) but you reinvest coupons at *higher* rates (good). This offsetting is exactly what **duration** captures — see 9.7.\n\n---\n\n## 9.4 ⭐ Bond Pricing\n\n### 9.4.1 Par value\nThe **face value** repaid at maturity — typically ₹100 or ₹1,000 for corporate bonds and ₹100 for G-secs. **The coupon is always calculated on the face value, never on the market price.**\n\n### 9.4.2 The pricing principle\n\n**A bond's price is the present value of all its future cash flows**, discounted at the required yield:\n\n$$\\boxed{P = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\frac{C}{(1+y)^t} + \\frac{F}{(1+y)^n}}$$\n\nEquivalently, using the annuity formula from Chapter 2:\n\n$$\\boxed{P = C \\times \\frac{1-(1+y)^{-n}}{y} + \\frac{F}{(1+y)^n}}$$\n\nwhere **C** = coupon per period, **F** = face value, **y** = required yield per period, **n** = number of periods.\n\n**Worked example:** a ₹1,000 face value bond, 8% annual coupon, 5 years to maturity, required yield 10%.\n- Coupon = ₹80 a year; annuity factor = [1 − (1.10)⁻⁵] ÷ 0.10 = (1 − 0.62092) ÷ 0.10 = **3.7908**\n- PV of coupons = 80 × 3.7908 = **₹303.26**\n- PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.10)⁵ = 1,000 ÷ 1.61051 = **₹620.92**\n- **Price = 303.26 + 620.92 = ₹924.18**\n\n### 9.4.3 Pricing variants\n\n| Bond type | Adjustment |\n|---|---|\n| **Annual coupon** | As above |\n| **Semi-annual coupon** | **Halve the coupon and the yield; double the periods.** A 10% coupon, 6 years, 12% yield → C = 5% of face, y = 6%, n = 12 |\n| **Zero-coupon bond** | No coupons: **P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ**. Always issued/trades at a **discount** |\n| **Between coupon dates** | Price includes **accrued interest**. **Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest** |\n| **Perpetual bond** | No maturity: **P = C ÷ y** — a perpetuity |\n\n### 9.4.4 ⭐ The Price–Yield Relationship\n\n$$\\textbf{Price and yield move in OPPOSITE directions.}$$\n\n| Condition | Bond trades at |\n|---|---|\n| **Coupon rate > required yield** | **Premium** (price > face value) |\n| **Coupon rate = required yield** | **Par** (price = face value) |\n| **Coupon rate < required yield** | **Discount** (price < face value) |\n\n> 🧠 **The intuition:** if a bond pays 8% while the market demands 10%, nobody will pay full price for it. Its price must fall until the *total* return to a buyer equals 10%. In the example above, ₹924.18 is exactly the price at which an 8% coupon delivers a 10% yield.\n\n**The relationship is convex, not a straight line** — prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise by the same amount. This is **convexity**, and it is favourable to the bondholder.\n\n**Pricing matrix:** a grid of bond prices across different yields and maturities, used to value illiquid bonds by reference to comparable traded ones.\n\n### 9.4.5 Perpetual bonds\nNo maturity date; the price is simply **C ÷ y**. In India these are often **AT-1 bonds** issued by banks — which carry **write-down risk** and are considerably riskier than ordinary bonds despite the steady coupon.\n\n---\n\n## 9.5 ⭐ Yield Measures\n\n| Measure | Formula / meaning | Limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coupon rate** | Coupon ÷ **Face value** | Fixed; ignores the price you paid |\n| **Current yield** | **Annual coupon ÷ Market price** | Ignores capital gain/loss at maturity and the timing of cash flows |\n| **Yield to Maturity (YTM)** | The single discount rate that makes the PV of all cash flows equal the market price | Assumes **all coupons are reinvested at the YTM** and the bond is **held to maturity** |\n| **Effective yield** | Yield adjusted for **compounding within the year** | — |\n| **Yield to Call (YTC)** | YTM calculated to the **call date and call price** | Relevant only for callable bonds |\n| **Yield to Put (YTP)** | YTM calculated to the **put date and put price** | Relevant only for puttable bonds |\n\n### The relationships — a favourite exam question\n\n| Bond trading at | Relationship |\n|---|---|\n| **Discount** | Coupon rate **<** Current yield **<** YTM |\n| **Par** | Coupon rate **=** Current yield **=** YTM |\n| **Premium** | Coupon rate **>** Current yield **>** YTM |\n\n> 🧠 **Why:** a discount bond gives you a **capital gain** at maturity (you paid less than face value), which YTM counts but current yield ignores — so YTM is highest. A premium bond delivers a **capital loss** at maturity, so YTM is lowest.\n\n**YTM's two big assumptions:** (1) every coupon is reinvested at the YTM itself — rarely true, which is **reinvestment risk**; and (2) the bond is held to maturity.\n\n**Yield to Worst:** for a bond with multiple call dates, the lowest of all possible yields — the conservative measure.\n\n---\n\n## 9.6 The Yield Curve\n\nThe **yield curve** plots yield against maturity for bonds of the same credit quality (usually government securities).\n\n| Shape | Appearance | Typical interpretation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Normal (upward sloping)** | Long yields > short yields | Healthy expansion; investors demand a **term premium** for locking money away longer |\n| **Flat** | Similar across maturities | Transition or uncertainty |\n| **Inverted** | Short yields > long yields | Market expects **rate cuts / slowdown**; historically a recession signal |\n| **Humped** | Peaks in the middle | Mixed expectations |\n\n**Theories explaining the shape:**\n- **Expectations theory** — long rates reflect expected future short rates.\n- **Liquidity preference theory** — investors demand a premium for longer maturities, so the curve is normally upward sloping.\n- **Market segmentation theory** — different investors have fixed maturity preferences (insurers want long, banks want short), so supply and demand at each segment set that segment's rate.\n\n**Uses:** benchmark pricing for corporate bonds (which trade at a spread over the curve), signalling the economic outlook, and informing portfolio positioning.\n\n---\n\n## 9.7 ⭐ Duration\n\n**Duration measures a bond's price sensitivity to interest rate changes** — and, equivalently, the weighted-average time to receive its cash flows.\n\n### Macaulay duration\nThe weighted average time (in years) to receive the bond's cash flows, weighted by the present value of each.\n\n### Modified duration\nThe practical measure:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Modified Duration} = \\frac{\\text{Macaulay Duration}}{1 + y}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\%\\ \\Delta P \\approx -\\text{Modified Duration} \\times \\Delta y}$$\n\n**Example:** a bond with modified duration **6.2** when yields rise **0.75%** (75 basis points):\n% change ≈ −6.2 × 0.0075 = **−4.65%**. A ₹10,00,000 holding loses about **₹46,500**.\n\n### What drives duration\n\n| Factor | Effect on duration |\n|---|---|\n| **Longer maturity** | **Higher** duration (more sensitive) |\n| **Higher coupon** | **Lower** duration (more value arrives sooner) |\n| **Higher yield** | Lower duration |\n| **Zero-coupon bond** | Duration = **maturity exactly** (the only cash flow is at the end) |\n\n> 🧠 **Two facts to memorise:**\n> 1. **A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity** — the single most-tested duration fact.\n> 2. **Longer duration = more interest rate risk.** If you expect rates to **fall**, go **long** duration; if you expect rates to **rise**, go **short** duration.\n\n**Convexity** is the second-order effect — the curvature of the price–yield relationship. Duration alone underestimates the price rise when yields fall and overestimates the fall when yields rise; convexity corrects for this, and **positive convexity benefits the bondholder**.\n\n---\n\n## 9.8 The Money Market\n\nShort-term debt: **maturity up to one year**, regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n### 9.8.1 Participants\n**Demand side:** banks (managing reserves), corporates (short-term funding). **Supply side:** banks with surplus, mutual funds (liquid schemes), insurers, the RBI. **Intermediaries:** primary dealers, brokers.\n\n### 9.8.2 Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Issuer | Tenor | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Treasury bills (T-bills)** | Government of India | **91, 182, 364 days** | **Zero-coupon**, issued at a discount, redeemed at face value. Risk-free |\n| **Cash Management Bills** | Government | Under 91 days | For temporary cash mismatches |\n| **Certificates of Deposit (CDs)** | Banks and select FIs | 7 days–1 year | Negotiable term deposits |\n| **Commercial Paper (CP)** | Corporates, PDs, FIs | 7 days–1 year | **Unsecured** promissory note; needs a credit rating |\n| **Call money** | Banks | **Overnight** | Inter-bank; rate is the \"call rate\" |\n| **Notice money** | Banks | 2–14 days | — |\n| **Term money** | Banks | 15 days–1 year | — |\n| **Repo / Reverse repo** | Banks, RBI | Short | Sale with an agreement to repurchase — effectively collateralised borrowing |\n| **TREPS / CBLO** | Market participants | Short | Tri-party repo, centrally cleared |\n\n> 🧠 **Repo vs reverse repo, from the RBI's perspective:** in a **repo**, banks borrow from the RBI against securities (the **repo rate**). In a **reverse repo**, banks lend to the RBI (the **reverse repo rate**). The repo rate is the RBI's main policy rate.\n\n---\n\n## 9.9 The Government Debt Market\n\n**Participants:** the **RBI** (as the government's debt manager and banker), **primary dealers** (obliged to bid at auctions), banks (holding G-secs for SLR), insurers, pension funds, mutual funds, FPIs and retail (via **RBI Retail Direct**).\n\n### Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Dated G-secs** | Fixed-coupon bonds, typically 5–40 years; **coupon paid half-yearly** |\n| **Treasury bills** | 91/182/364 days, zero-coupon |\n| **State Development Loans (SDLs)** | Issued by state governments; slightly higher yield than central G-secs |\n| **Floating Rate Bonds** | Coupon resets against a benchmark |\n| **Inflation Indexed Bonds** | Principal or coupon linked to an inflation index |\n| **Sovereign Gold Bonds** | Gold-linked, with an additional fixed interest rate |\n| **Savings (Taxable) Bonds** | Retail-oriented government savings instruments |\n| **STRIPS** | Coupons and principal separated and traded as individual zero-coupon securities |\n\nG-secs are sold through **RBI auctions** and carry **no credit risk** in domestic currency.\n\n---\n\n## 9.10 The Corporate Debt Market\n\n**Participants:** issuers (corporates, NBFCs, banks), investors (mutual funds, insurers, pension funds, FPIs, HNIs), and intermediaries (merchant bankers, arrangers, rating agencies, **debenture trustees**).\n\n### Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **Company/corporate deposits** | Unsecured deposits with a company; **not DICGC-insured** — credit risk sits with the company |\n| **Bonds and debentures** | Secured or unsecured; convertible or non-convertible (NCDs) |\n| **Infrastructure bonds** | Issued to fund infrastructure |\n| **Inflation-indexed bonds** | Return linked to inflation |\n| **Perpetual/AT-1 bonds** | No maturity; bank capital instruments with **write-down risk** |\n| **Masala bonds** | **Rupee-denominated** bonds issued **outside India** — the issuer bears no currency risk; the investor does |\n\n**Debentures may be:** secured/unsecured, convertible (fully or partly) / non-convertible, redeemable/perpetual.\n\n---\n\n## 9.11 Small Savings and Bank Deposits\n\n### Bank deposits\n- **Savings, current, fixed (FD) and recurring (RD)** deposits.\n- **Interest rates on FDs** are set by each bank and vary with tenor; senior citizens typically receive an additional 0.25–0.75%.\n- **DICGC insurance covers up to ₹5 lakh** per depositor per bank (principal + interest), across all branches.\n- Premature withdrawal usually carries a penalty.\n\n### Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020 (Taxable)\n- Issued by the Government of India; **7-year** tenor.\n- Interest **resets half-yearly**, pegged at **NSC rate + 0.35%**.\n- Interest is **fully taxable**; not tradable.\n\n### ⭐ Small savings schemes — the comparison table\n\n| Scheme | Tenor | Key features | Tax |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **PPF** | **15 years** (extendable in 5-year blocks) | Min ₹500, **max ₹1.5 lakh a year**; loan and partial withdrawal facilities | **EEE** — exempt at all three stages |\n| **Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme (SCSS)** | **5 years** (+3 extension) | Age **60+** (or 55+ on superannuation); **max ₹30 lakh**; **quarterly** interest | Interest **taxable**; 80C on deposit |\n| **National Savings Certificate (NSC)** | **5 years** | Interest **compounded annually but payable at maturity**; reinvested interest also qualifies for 80C | Interest taxable |\n| **Kisan Vikas Patra (KVP)** | Until the amount **doubles** (period set by the prevailing rate) | No maximum limit; **no 80C benefit** | Interest taxable |\n| **Sukanya Samriddhi Account (SSA)** | Until the girl turns 21 | For a **girl child under 10**; deposits for 15 years; **max ₹1.5 lakh a year** | **EEE** |\n| **Post Office MIS** | 5 years | **Monthly** interest payout | Interest taxable |\n| **Post Office Time/Recurring Deposits** | 1–5 years | Similar to bank deposits | Taxable |\n\n> ⚠️ **Most-tested facts:** **PPF and SSA are EEE.** **PPF is 15 years with a ₹1.5 lakh annual cap.** **SCSS is for 60+, capped at ₹30 lakh, paying quarterly.** **KVP doubles your money and gets NO 80C benefit.**\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Bond price | **PV of all future cash flows** at the required yield |\n| Bond price formula | C × [(1 − (1+y)⁻ⁿ) ÷ y] + F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ |\n| Semi-annual bonds | **Halve** C and y, **double** n |\n| Zero-coupon bond | P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ; always at a discount |\n| Perpetual bond | P = C ÷ y |\n| Dirty price | Clean price **+ accrued interest** |\n| Price and yield | Move **inversely**; the curve is **convex** |\n| Coupon > yield | Trades at a **premium** |\n| Coupon < yield | Trades at a **discount** |\n| Current yield | Coupon ÷ **market price** |\n| YTM | The rate equating PV of cash flows to price |\n| YTM's assumptions | Coupons reinvested **at the YTM**; held to maturity |\n| Discount bond ordering | Coupon < Current yield < **YTM** |\n| Premium bond ordering | Coupon > Current yield > **YTM** |\n| Modified duration | Macaulay ÷ (1 + y) |\n| Price change | ≈ **−Modified duration × Δy** |\n| Zero-coupon duration | **= its maturity** |\n| Higher coupon | **Lower** duration |\n| Longer maturity | **Higher** duration |\n| Expect rates to fall | Go **long** duration |\n| Convexity | Second-order effect; **benefits the bondholder** |\n| Normal yield curve | Upward sloping — term premium |\n| Inverted curve | Short > long; signals expected **rate cuts / slowdown** |\n| T-bill tenors | **91, 182, 364 days**; zero-coupon |\n| Commercial paper | **Unsecured** corporate short-term note; needs a rating |\n| Call money | **Overnight** inter-bank |\n| Repo rate | Rate at which **banks borrow from the RBI** |\n| G-sec coupon frequency | **Half-yearly** |\n| DICGC cover | **₹5 lakh** per depositor per bank |\n| PPF | **15 years**, ₹1.5 lakh cap, **EEE** |\n| SCSS | Age **60+**, ₹30 lakh cap, **quarterly** interest |\n| NSC | 5 years; interest compounded but paid at maturity |\n| KVP | Doubles the money; **no 80C** |\n| SSA | Girl under 10; **EEE**; ₹1.5 lakh cap |\n| Floating Rate Savings Bond | 7 years; **NSC + 0.35%**, reset half-yearly; taxable |\n| Masala bond | **Rupee-denominated**, issued **outside India** |\n| Regulator split | **RBI** — G-secs & money market; **SEBI** — corporate bonds |\n\n> **Exam tip:** almost every bond question reduces to one of three tasks — **price it** (discount the cash flows), **compare coupon with yield** (premium/par/discount), or **apply duration** (% price change ≈ −MD × Δy). Identify which one is being asked before computing.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Bond pricing, the yield orderings and duration are the highest-yield items.**\n\n**Q1.** State the bond pricing principle and formula.\n> **A:** A bond's price is the **present value of all its future cash flows**: **P = C × [(1 − (1+y)⁻ⁿ) ÷ y] + F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ** — an annuity (coupons) plus a lump sum (redemption).\n\n**Q2.** How do you adjust the formula for a semi-annual bond?\n> **A:** **Halve the coupon and the yield; double the number of periods.** A 10% coupon over 6 years at a 12% yield becomes C = 5%, y = 6%, n = 12.\n\n**Q3.** How is a zero-coupon bond priced?\n> **A:** **P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ** — there are no coupons, so it always trades/issues at a **discount** to face value.\n\n**Q4.** How is a perpetual bond priced?\n> **A:** **P = C ÷ y** — a perpetuity, since there is no redemption.\n\n**Q5.** What is the difference between clean and dirty price?\n> **A:** **Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest.** Between coupon dates the buyer must compensate the seller for interest earned but not yet paid.\n\n**Q6.** State the relationship between price and yield.\n> **A:** They move **inversely**, and the relationship is **convex** (curved), not linear.\n\n**Q7.** When does a bond trade at a premium, at par, and at a discount?\n> **A:** **Coupon > yield → premium.** **Coupon = yield → par.** **Coupon < yield → discount.**\n\n**Q8.** Explain intuitively why a bond paying 8% trades below par when the market demands 10%.\n> **A:** Nobody will pay full price for a below-market coupon. The **price must fall** until the buyer's total return — coupons **plus the capital gain at redemption** — equals the 10% the market demands.\n\n**Q9.** Define current yield and state its limitation.\n> **A:** **Current yield = annual coupon ÷ market price.** It **ignores the capital gain or loss at maturity** and the timing of cash flows.\n\n**Q10.** What is YTM, and what two assumptions does it make?\n> **A:** The single discount rate that makes the PV of all cash flows equal the market price. It assumes **(1) all coupons are reinvested at the YTM** and **(2) the bond is held to maturity**.\n\n**Q11.** State the ordering of coupon rate, current yield and YTM for a DISCOUNT bond.\n> **A:** **Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM.** (For a **premium** bond the ordering reverses; at **par** all three are equal.)\n\n**Q12.** Why is YTM highest for a discount bond?\n> **A:** Because the investor also receives a **capital gain at maturity** (paid less than face value), which YTM captures but current yield ignores.\n\n**Q13.** What are Yield to Call and Yield to Worst?\n> **A:** **YTC** is the YTM computed to the **call date and call price**. **Yield to Worst** is the lowest of all possible yields across call dates — the conservative measure.\n\n**Q14.** Name the ten risks in fixed income.\n> **A:** **Interest rate, call, reinvestment, credit** (downgrade / spread / default), **liquidity, exchange rate, inflation, volatility, political-legal** and **event** risk.\n\n**Q15.** How do interest rate risk and reinvestment risk offset each other?\n> **A:** If rates **rise**, the bond's **price falls** (bad) but **coupons are reinvested at higher rates** (good). This offsetting is exactly what **duration** captures.\n\n**Q16.** What is duration, in two senses?\n> **A:** A measure of a bond's **price sensitivity to interest rate changes**, and equivalently the **weighted-average time** to receive its cash flows.\n\n**Q17.** State the modified duration formula and the price-change approximation.\n> **A:** **Modified Duration = Macaulay Duration ÷ (1 + y)**, and **% change in price ≈ −Modified Duration × Δy**.\n\n**Q18.** A bond has modified duration 6.2 and yields rise 75 basis points. What happens to the price?\n> **A:** % change ≈ −6.2 × 0.0075 = **−4.65%**. On a ₹10,00,000 holding that is a loss of about **₹46,500**.\n\n**Q19.** What is a zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration?\n> **A:** **Exactly equal to its maturity** — because the only cash flow arrives at the end. This is the single most-tested duration fact.\n\n**Q20.** How do maturity, coupon and yield each affect duration?\n> **A:** **Longer maturity → higher** duration. **Higher coupon → lower** duration (more value arrives sooner). **Higher yield → lower** duration.\n\n**Q21.** If you expect interest rates to fall, how should you position duration?\n> **A:** Go **LONG duration** — longer-duration bonds gain most when yields fall. If you expect rates to **rise**, go **short duration**.\n\n**Q22.** What is convexity, and who does it benefit?\n> **A:** The **curvature** of the price–yield relationship — the second-order effect duration alone misses. **Positive convexity benefits the bondholder**: prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise equally.\n\n**Q23.** Describe the four yield curve shapes and what each signals.\n> **A:** **Normal (upward)** — healthy expansion, term premium. **Flat** — transition/uncertainty. **Inverted** (short > long) — expected **rate cuts/slowdown**, historically a recession signal. **Humped** — mixed expectations.\n\n**Q24.** Name the three theories explaining the yield curve's shape.\n> **A:** **Expectations theory** (long rates reflect expected future short rates), **liquidity preference theory** (a premium is demanded for longer maturities), and **market segmentation theory** (different investors have fixed maturity preferences).\n\n**Q25.** Which regulator covers government securities, and which covers corporate bonds?\n> **A:** **RBI** — government securities and the money market. **SEBI** — corporate bonds and their listing.\n\n**Q26.** State the T-bill tenors and their key feature.\n> **A:** **91, 182 and 364 days.** They are **zero-coupon**, issued at a discount and redeemed at face value, and are **risk-free**.\n\n**Q27.** What is commercial paper?\n> **A:** An **unsecured** short-term promissory note issued by corporates, PDs and FIs, with a tenor of **7 days to 1 year**, requiring a **credit rating**.\n\n**Q28.** What is a certificate of deposit?\n> **A:** A **negotiable term deposit** issued by banks and select financial institutions, with a tenor of **7 days to 1 year**.\n\n**Q29.** Distinguish call, notice and term money.\n> **A:** **Call money** — **overnight** inter-bank. **Notice money** — 2–14 days. **Term money** — 15 days to 1 year.\n\n**Q30.** Explain repo and reverse repo from the RBI's perspective.\n> **A:** In a **repo**, **banks borrow from the RBI** against securities (the **repo rate** — the main policy rate). In a **reverse repo**, **banks lend to the RBI**.\n\n**Q31.** How often do dated government securities pay coupons?\n> **A:** **Half-yearly.**\n\n**Q32.** What are SDLs and STRIPS?\n> **A:** **SDLs** are State Development Loans — bonds issued by state governments, yielding slightly more than central G-secs. **STRIPS** separate a bond's coupons and principal into individually traded **zero-coupon** securities.\n\n**Q33.** What is a Masala bond?\n> **A:** A **rupee-denominated bond issued OUTSIDE India**. Because it is denominated in rupees, the **issuer bears no currency risk — the investor does**.\n\n**Q34.** What is an AT-1 (perpetual) bond, and what makes it risky?\n> **A:** A bank capital instrument with **no maturity**. Despite the steady coupon it carries **write-down risk** — it can be written off entirely if the bank is in trouble.\n\n**Q35.** How much does DICGC deposit insurance cover?\n> **A:** **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank** (principal + interest), across all branches of that bank.\n\n**Q36.** State PPF's tenor, annual limit and tax status.\n> **A:** **15 years** (extendable in 5-year blocks), **maximum ₹1.5 lakh a year**, and **EEE** — exempt at contribution, accrual and withdrawal.\n\n**Q37.** State SCSS's eligibility, limit, tenor and payout frequency.\n> **A:** Age **60+** (or 55+ on superannuation), maximum **₹30 lakh**, **5 years** (extendable by 3), with **quarterly** interest. The interest is **taxable**.\n\n**Q38.** What is distinctive about NSC's interest?\n> **A:** It is **compounded annually but paid only at maturity**, and the **reinvested interest also qualifies for Section 80C** in the intervening years.\n\n**Q39.** What is Kisan Vikas Patra's distinguishing feature — and its tax drawback?\n> **A:** It runs until the **amount doubles**, with **no maximum limit** — but it carries **no 80C benefit** and the interest is taxable.\n\n**Q40.** Who is Sukanya Samriddhi for, and what is its tax status?\n> **A:** A **girl child under 10**; deposits for 15 years, maturing when she turns 21. Maximum **₹1.5 lakh a year**, and it is **EEE**.\n\n**Q41.** Describe the Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020.\n> **A:** A **7-year** Government of India bond whose interest **resets half-yearly at the NSC rate + 0.35%**. The interest is **fully taxable** and the bond is **not tradable**.\n\n**Q42.** Are company deposits covered by deposit insurance?\n> **A:** **No.** DICGC covers **bank** deposits only. A company deposit carries the **company's own credit risk**, which is why it pays more.\n\n**Q43.** Why do corporates issue debt rather than equity?\n> **A:** **No dilution of ownership**, a **known** cost, and **interest is tax-deductible** (unlike dividends). The trade-off is a **fixed obligation** payable regardless of profits.\n\n**Q44.** What does a debenture trustee do?\n> **A:** **Protects bondholders' interests** and enforces the security on their behalf — a mandatory appointment for public debt issues.\n\n**Q45.** What single question resolves most bond exam problems?\n> **A:** Which of three tasks is being asked — **price it** (discount the cash flows), **compare coupon with yield** (premium/par/discount), or **apply duration** (% change ≈ −MD × Δy)?\n", "questions": "# Chapter 9: Investing in Fixed Income Securities — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Bond pricing, yield orderings and duration dominate.** Keep a spreadsheet handy. Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A bond's price is:\nA) Its face value always  B) The present value of all its future cash flows  C) The coupon rate  D) The sum of all coupons\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A bond is valued by **discounting its coupons and redemption** at the required yield.\nA) Price equals face value only when the coupon equals the yield. C) The coupon is one input. D) Undiscounted coupons ignore the time value of money and the redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Bond prices and yields move:\nA) In the same direction  B) In opposite directions  C) Independently  D) Only upward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The single most important relationship in fixed income.\nA) and C) are incorrect. D) Bond prices fall as well as rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A bond trades at a premium when:\nA) Coupon rate < required yield  B) Coupon rate = required yield  C) Coupon rate > required yield  D) It is a zero-coupon bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — An above-market coupon is worth paying extra for, so the price exceeds face value.\nA) Gives a **discount**. B) Gives **par**. D) Zero-coupon bonds always trade at a discount.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** For a bond trading at a discount, the correct ordering is:\nA) Coupon > Current yield > YTM  B) Coupon < Current yield < YTM  C) All three equal  D) YTM < Coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A discount bond delivers a **capital gain at maturity**, which YTM counts and current yield ignores — so YTM is highest.\nA) Is the **premium** bond ordering. C) Applies only at par. D) Contradicts the discount case.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The current yield is:\nA) Coupon ÷ Face value  B) Coupon ÷ Market price  C) The YTM  D) Coupon × Years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Current yield relates the coupon to what you **actually paid**.\nA) Is the **coupon rate**. C) YTM also accounts for capital gain/loss and timing. D) Is meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** YTM assumes that:\nA) Coupons are spent  B) All coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity  C) The bond is sold immediately  D) Rates never change\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These are YTM's two standing assumptions, and the first is the source of **reinvestment risk**.\nA) YTM assumes reinvestment, not spending. C) Selling early gives a realised yield, not YTM. D) YTM makes no such claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals:\nA) Zero  B) Its maturity  C) Half its maturity  D) One year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a **single cash flow at the end**, the weighted-average time to receive cash is exactly the maturity.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect. This is the most-tested duration fact in the syllabus.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Modified duration is calculated as:\nA) Macaulay duration × (1 + y)  B) Macaulay duration ÷ (1 + y)  C) Maturity ÷ 2  D) Coupon ÷ price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dividing by (1 + y) converts Macaulay duration into a direct price-sensitivity measure.\nA) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The approximate percentage price change of a bond is:\nA) +Modified duration × Δy  B) −Modified duration × Δy  C) Duration ÷ Δy  D) Δy ÷ duration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **negative sign** reflects the inverse price–yield relationship.\nA) Gets the sign wrong. C) and D) are not the formula.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Treasury bills in India are issued for tenors of:\nA) 91, 182 and 364 days  B) 1, 2 and 3 years  C) 30, 60 and 90 days  D) 5 and 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **91, 182 and 364 days**, all zero-coupon.\nB) and D) are dated securities, not T-bills. C) Is not the Indian tenor set.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Treasury bills are:\nA) Coupon-bearing  B) Zero-coupon, issued at a discount  C) Perpetual  D) Convertible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They are issued **below face value** and redeemed at par, the difference being the return.\nA) T-bills pay no coupon. C) They have a defined short maturity. D) They are not convertible.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Commercial paper is:\nA) A secured government bond  B) An unsecured short-term promissory note issued by corporates  C) A bank deposit  D) An equity share\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CP is **unsecured**, requires a credit rating, and runs 7 days to 1 year.\nA) Government short-term paper is the T-bill. C) That is a certificate of deposit. D) CP is debt, not equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Call money refers to:\nA) Overnight inter-bank borrowing  B) A callable bond  C) A 5-year loan  D) A telephone service\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Overnight** inter-bank funds; the rate is the \"call rate\".\nB) A callable bond is a different concept (call risk). C) Term money runs 15 days–1 year. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The repo rate is the rate at which:\nA) Banks lend to the RBI  B) Banks borrow from the RBI against securities  C) Companies borrow from banks  D) Individuals borrow\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a **repo**, banks borrow from the RBI against collateral. It is the RBI's main policy rate.\nA) That is the **reverse repo**. C) and D) are commercial lending, not policy rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Dated government securities pay coupons:\nA) Monthly  B) Quarterly  C) Half-yearly  D) Annually\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Indian dated G-secs pay **half-yearly** coupons.\nA), B) and D) are not the standard G-sec convention.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** DICGC deposit insurance covers up to:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹2 lakh  C) ₹5 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank**, covering principal and interest across all branches.\nA) Was the historical limit. B) and D) are not the current cover.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** PPF has a tenor of:\nA) 5 years  B) 10 years  C) 15 years, extendable in 5-year blocks  D) 21 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **15 years**, extendable in blocks of 5.\nA) Is NSC/SCSS. B) Is not a PPF term. D) Is Sukanya Samriddhi's maturity, tied to the girl's age.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The maximum annual contribution to PPF is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹1.5 lakh  C) ₹2 lakh  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹1.5 lakh** per financial year.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** PPF's tax treatment is:\nA) EET  B) ETE  C) EEE  D) TEE\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **EEE**: exempt on contribution, on accrual and on withdrawal.\nA), B) and D) describe other tax regimes; PPF is one of the few genuinely EEE instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme is available to those aged:\nA) 50 and above  B) 55 and above only  C) 60 and above (or 55+ on superannuation)  D) 65 and above\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **60+**, with an exception for those retiring on superannuation at 55+.\nA), B) and D) are incorrect thresholds.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The maximum investment in SCSS is:\nA) ₹15 lakh  B) ₹30 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹1.5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹30 lakh**.\nA) Was the earlier limit. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** SCSS pays interest:\nA) Monthly  B) Quarterly  C) Annually  D) At maturity only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Quarterly** payouts, which suits retirees needing regular income.\nA) Post Office MIS pays monthly. C) and D) are incorrect for SCSS.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Kisan Vikas Patra:\nA) Offers Section 80C benefit  B) Runs until the invested amount doubles, with no 80C benefit  C) Has a ₹1.5 lakh cap  D) Is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — KVP doubles the money over a period set by the prevailing rate, has **no maximum limit**, and carries **no 80C deduction**.\nA) It specifically does not. C) There is no cap. D) Interest is taxable.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Sukanya Samriddhi Account can be opened for:\nA) Any child  B) A girl child below 10 years  C) A boy child  D) Anyone above 18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **girl child under 10**, maturing when she turns 21.\nA), C) and D) are outside the scheme's eligibility.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** A Masala bond is:\nA) A foreign-currency bond issued in India  B) A rupee-denominated bond issued outside India  C) A commodity bond  D) A government bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Rupee-denominated, issued offshore** — so the **investor**, not the issuer, bears the currency risk.\nA) Reverses it. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which risk arises when an issuer redeems a bond early?\nA) Inflation risk  B) Call risk  C) Liquidity risk  D) Event risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Call risk**: the issuer calls the bond when rates fall, forcing the investor to reinvest at lower rates.\nA), C) and D) are different risks.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Reinvestment risk is the risk that:\nA) Rates rise  B) Coupons must be reinvested at lower rates  C) The issuer defaults  D) The bond is illiquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is specifically the risk that **rates fall**, so intermediate cash flows earn less.\nA) Rising rates hurt the **price** but help reinvestment. C) Is credit risk. D) Is liquidity risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which of these is a sub-type of credit risk?\nA) Downgrade risk  B) Interest rate risk  C) Inflation risk  D) Call risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Credit risk covers **downgrade risk, spread/basis risk and default risk**.\nB), C) and D) are separate risk categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A normal (upward-sloping) yield curve indicates:\nA) Expected recession  B) Longer maturities yield more, reflecting a term premium  C) Short rates above long rates  D) Flat expectations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors demand extra compensation for locking money away longer.\nA) That is signalled by an **inverted** curve. C) Describes inversion. D) Describes a flat curve.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An inverted yield curve typically signals:\nA) Strong growth ahead  B) Expected rate cuts and possible slowdown  C) Rising inflation  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short yields above long yields imply the market expects **rates to fall**, historically a recession signal.\nA) Is signalled by a steep normal curve. C) Rising inflation usually steepens the curve. D) Inversion is one of the most-watched macro signals.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** For a bond with a higher coupon, all else equal, duration is:\nA) Higher  B) Lower  C) Unchanged  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A higher coupon delivers **more value sooner**, shortening the weighted-average time to receive cash.\nA) Reverses it. C) The coupon materially affects duration. D) Only a zero-price bond would have zero duration.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which regulator oversees corporate bonds and their listing?\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) PFRDA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI** covers corporate bonds; the **RBI** covers G-secs and the money market.\nA) Handles government securities. C) and D) have no jurisdiction here.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A debenture trustee's role is to:\nA) Rate the bond  B) Protect bondholders' interests and enforce the security  C) Issue the bond  D) Set the coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A mandatory appointment for public debt issues, acting **for the bondholders**.\nA) Is a credit rating agency. C) Is the issuer. D) Is set at issuance by the issuer and market.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The dirty price of a bond is:\nA) The clean price minus accrued interest  B) The clean price plus accrued interest  C) The face value  D) The coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Between coupon dates the buyer compensates the seller for **interest accrued but unpaid**.\nA) Reverses the sign. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The Floating Rate Savings Bonds, 2020 pay interest linked to:\nA) The repo rate  B) The NSC rate + 0.35%  C) The Nifty  D) Gold prices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The coupon **resets half-yearly at NSC + 0.35%**, over a 7-year tenor, and is **fully taxable**.\nA), C) and D) are not the benchmark.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A ₹1,000 face value bond pays an 8% annual coupon, has 5 years to maturity, and the required yield is 10%. What is its price?\nA) ₹1,000  B) ₹924.18  C) ₹1,075.82  D) ₹800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Coupon = ₹80. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.10)⁻⁵] ÷ 0.10 = (1 − 0.62092) ÷ 0.10 = **3.7908**. PV of coupons = 80 × 3.7908 = **₹303.26**. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ 1.61051 = **₹620.92**. Price = **₹924.18**.\nA) Would apply only if coupon = yield. C) Is the premium case (coupon above yield). D) Over-discounts. **Coupon (8%) < yield (10%), so a discount is expected ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 12% annual coupon with 4 years remaining and a required yield of 9%. Will it trade at a premium or discount?\nA) Discount, because 12% > 9%  B) Premium, because the coupon exceeds the required yield  C) At par  D) Cannot say\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Coupon (12%) > yield (9%)** means an above-market income stream, so buyers bid the price **above face value**.\nA) Gets the direction backwards. C) Par applies only when coupon = yield. D) The rule is deterministic.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A zero-coupon bond with ₹1,00,000 face value matures in 6 years. At an 8% yield, what is its price?\nA) ₹63,017  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹52,000  D) ₹1,58,687\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ = 1,00,000 ÷ (1.08)⁶ = 1,00,000 ÷ **1.58687** = **₹63,017**.\nB) Zero-coupon bonds never trade at par before maturity. C) Over-discounts. D) Is the future value, not the price.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A bond with a ₹1,000 face value and 9% coupon trades at ₹900. What is the current yield?\nA) 9.0%  B) 10.0%  C) 8.1%  D) 11.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Current yield = annual coupon ÷ market price = ₹90 ÷ ₹900 = **10.0%**.\nA) Is the coupon **rate** (based on face value). C) Divides by the wrong figure. D) Is 1,000 ÷ 900. **Since it trades at a discount, YTM will be higher still than 10%.**\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond has modified duration of 5.5. If yields fall by 50 basis points, the price will approximately:\nA) Fall 2.75%  B) Rise 2.75%  C) Rise 5.5%  D) Fall 0.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — % change ≈ −MD × Δy = −5.5 × (−0.005) = **+2.75%**. Falling yields **raise** prices.\nA) Gets the direction wrong. C) Ignores the size of the yield move. D) Ignores duration entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A semi-annual bond has a 10% coupon, 6 years to maturity and a 12% required yield. What inputs go into the pricing formula?\nA) C = 100, y = 12%, n = 6  B) C = 5% of face, y = 6%, n = 12  C) C = 10, y = 12%, n = 12  D) C = 5, y = 12%, n = 6\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Halve the coupon and yield, double the periods**: coupon = 5% of face per half-year, y = 6% per half-year, n = 12 half-years.\nA) Uses annual conventions throughout. C) Halves only the periods. D) Halves only the coupon. **This period conversion is the most common source of error.**\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An investor holds a bond with modified duration 8.0 and ₹25,00,000 invested. Yields rise 1%. What is the approximate loss?\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −8.0 × 0.01 = **−8%**. Loss = 8% × ₹25,00,000 = **₹2,00,000**.\nB) Uses 0.1%. C) Applies 8 as a multiple rather than a percentage sensitivity. D) Understates it. **A duration of 8 means a 1% rate move costs 8% of capital — a big number many investors underestimate.**\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which bond has the greatest interest rate sensitivity?\nA) 2-year, 10% coupon  B) 10-year, 10% coupon  C) 10-year zero-coupon  D) 2-year zero-coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Longest maturity plus no coupon** gives the highest duration. A 10-year zero has a Macaulay duration of exactly **10 years**, higher than a 10-year coupon bond.\nA) and D) are short-dated. B) Has coupons that shorten its duration below 10. **Longer maturity ↑ duration; higher coupon ↓ duration.**\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor expects the RBI to cut rates sharply. How should she position her bond portfolio?\nA) Shorten duration  B) Lengthen duration to maximise price gains  C) Hold only T-bills  D) Sell all bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Falling yields raise prices, and **longer duration amplifies the gain**.\nA) Would minimise the benefit. C) T-bills have almost no duration, so they gain least. D) Selling forgoes the anticipated gain entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 7% coupon trades at ₹1,050. What is the relationship between coupon, current yield and YTM?\nA) Coupon < Current yield < YTM  B) Coupon > Current yield > YTM  C) All equal  D) YTM > Coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It trades at a **premium** (₹1,050 > ₹1,000), so the ordering reverses: **coupon 7% > current yield (70 ÷ 1,050 = 6.67%) > YTM**.\nA) Is the discount ordering. C) Applies only at par. D) A premium bond's YTM is below its coupon, because of the capital loss at redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A perpetual bond pays ₹90 a year. At a required yield of 12%, what is its price?\nA) ₹750  B) ₹1,080  C) ₹90  D) ₹1,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — P = C ÷ y = 90 ÷ 0.12 = **₹750**.\nB) Multiplies instead of dividing. C) Is one year's coupon. D) Assumes par regardless of yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client asks why her \"safe\" bond fund lost money last quarter. The most likely explanation is:\nA) The issuers all defaulted  B) Interest rates rose, so bond prices fell — mark-to-market losses, not defaults  C) The fund was fraudulent  D) Bonds cannot lose money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Interest rate risk** is the dominant risk for high-quality bond funds; rising rates reduce NAV even with no credit event.\nA) Widespread default is rare and would be reported. C) An unwarranted conclusion. D) A common and costly misconception.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Which instrument would a corporate treasurer use to borrow for 90 days?\nA) A 10-year debenture  B) Commercial paper  C) Equity  D) A perpetual bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CP** is the standard short-term corporate borrowing instrument (7 days–1 year).\nA) and D) are long-term or perpetual. C) Equity is permanent capital, not borrowing.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A ₹1,00,000 T-bill with 91 days to maturity is bought at ₹98,200. What is the return?\nA) ₹1,800, being the discount realised at redemption  B) A 9% coupon  C) Nothing  D) ₹98,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — T-bills are **zero-coupon**: the return is the **difference between purchase price and face value**, here ₹1,00,000 − ₹98,200 = **₹1,800**.\nB) T-bills pay no coupon. C) The discount is the return. D) Is the purchase price.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** An investor in the 30% tax bracket compares PPF at 7.1% with a bank FD at 7.5%. Which gives the better post-tax return?\nA) The FD, since 7.5% > 7.1%  B) PPF — it is EEE and therefore tax-free, while the FD's post-tax return is about 5.25%  C) They are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PPF's **EEE** status means 7.1% is received in full. The FD's post-tax return = 7.5% × (1 − 0.30) = **5.25%**. PPF wins by nearly 2 percentage points.\nA) Compares pre-tax with tax-free — the classic error. C) They differ substantially. D) PPF is clearly better here. **Always compare post-tax returns.**\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which of these bonds carries no credit risk in domestic currency?\nA) A AAA corporate bond  B) A central government security  C) An AT-1 bank bond  D) A company deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A sovereign borrowing in its **own currency** is effectively free of default risk.\nA) AAA is the highest **corporate** rating, not risk-free. C) AT-1 bonds carry write-down risk. D) Company deposits carry the company's credit risk and are uninsured.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A callable bond is called by the issuer. When is this most likely, and why is it bad for the investor?\nA) When rates rise; the investor gains  B) When rates fall — the issuer refinances cheaply, and the investor must reinvest at lower rates  C) At random  D) When the issuer defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Issuers call when they can **refinance more cheaply**, which is exactly when the investor's reinvestment options are worst.\nA) Calling is unattractive to the issuer when rates rise. C) Calls are economically driven. D) A call is a repayment, not a default.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** The yield curve inverts. A bond investor should interpret this as:\nA) Strong growth expected  B) The market expecting rate cuts and possibly a slowdown  C) Rising credit risk  D) Nothing meaningful\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short yields exceeding long yields imply expected **rate reductions**.\nA) Would produce a steep normal curve. C) The curve reflects rates, not credit (though spreads may widen separately). D) Inversion is a closely watched signal.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An investor buys a bond between coupon dates. What must she pay in addition to the clean price?\nA) Nothing  B) Accrued interest, giving the dirty price  C) The full next coupon  D) A penalty\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She compensates the seller for interest earned since the last coupon date: **dirty = clean + accrued**.\nA) The seller is entitled to interest earned. C) She pays only the accrued portion, not the whole coupon. D) There is no penalty.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 10% coupon has 3 years remaining and a required yield of 10%. What is its price?\nA) ₹1,000  B) ₹1,100  C) ₹900  D) ₹1,300\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — When **coupon = required yield**, the bond trades **exactly at par** — no computation needed.\nB) Would be a premium. C) Would be a discount. D) Is the undiscounted total of coupons plus principal. **Recognising the coupon = yield shortcut saves time in the exam.**\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which small savings scheme allows the largest single deposit?\nA) PPF (₹1.5 lakh)  B) SCSS (₹30 lakh)  C) Sukanya Samriddhi (₹1.5 lakh)  D) NSC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SCSS permits up to **₹30 lakh**, far above PPF's and SSA's ₹1.5 lakh annual limits.\nA) and C) share the ₹1.5 lakh cap. D) NSC has no cap for investment but no comparable single-scheme limit is specified; SCSS's ₹30 lakh is the prescribed maximum among these.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A retiree wants regular quarterly income with government backing. The most suitable scheme is:\nA) PPF  B) SCSS  C) KVP  D) Sukanya Samriddhi\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCSS** is designed for the 60+ cohort and pays **quarterly** interest, up to ₹30 lakh.\nA) PPF locks money for 15 years with no interim income. C) KVP pays only at maturity. D) SSA is for a girl child.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Two bonds are identical except that Bond A matures in 3 years and Bond B in 15 years. Rates rise 1%. Which falls more?\nA) Bond A  B) Bond B, because longer maturity means higher duration  C) Both equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Longer maturity → higher duration → greater price sensitivity.**\nA) The short bond is far less sensitive. C) Duration differs sharply. D) Both fall, but by very different amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A corporate deposit pays 2.5% more than a bank FD. What is the trade-off?\nA) There is none  B) Company deposits are not DICGC-insured, so the extra yield compensates for the company's credit risk  C) The company deposit is safer  D) The FD is riskier\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **No deposit insurance** and the issuer's own credit risk explain the premium.\nA) The difference in safety is material. C) and D) reverse the risk ranking.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which statement about NSC is correct?\nA) Interest is paid annually in cash  B) Interest is compounded annually but paid at maturity, and reinvested interest also qualifies for 80C  C) It is EEE  D) It has a 15-year tenor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This dual feature — deemed reinvestment qualifying for 80C — is NSC's distinguishing characteristic.\nA) No annual payout occurs. C) The interest is **taxable**, so it is not EEE. D) NSC runs **5 years**.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An AT-1 perpetual bond issued by a bank differs from an ordinary bond because:\nA) It matures in 5 years  B) It has no maturity and carries write-down risk — it can be written off if the bank is in trouble  C) It is government-guaranteed  D) It is risk-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AT-1 instruments are **loss-absorbing bank capital**, far riskier than the steady coupon suggests.\nA) They are perpetual. C) and D) There is no guarantee and substantial risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which of these is the biggest risk for a portfolio of AAA-rated government bonds?\nA) Default risk  B) Interest rate risk  C) Credit risk  D) Downgrade risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With credit risk essentially absent, **interest rate risk dominates** for high-quality bonds.\nA), C) and D) are all negligible for domestic government securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** In a repo transaction, the borrower:\nA) Sells securities with an agreement to repurchase them, effectively borrowing against collateral  B) Gifts securities  C) Issues equity  D) Defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A repo is **collateralised borrowing** dressed as a sale-and-repurchase.\nB), C) and D) do not describe a repo.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A bond's YTM is 9% but the investor can only reinvest coupons at 6%. What is the consequence?\nA) The realised return will exceed 9%  B) The realised return will be below the 9% YTM — this is reinvestment risk  C) No effect  D) The bond defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — YTM **assumes reinvestment at the YTM itself**. If actual reinvestment rates are lower, the realised return falls short.\nA) Lower reinvestment rates reduce, not raise, the outcome. C) It has a direct effect on the realised return. D) Reinvestment risk is unrelated to default.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which of these bonds would have a Macaulay duration exactly equal to 7?\nA) A 7-year, 8% coupon bond  B) A 7-year zero-coupon bond  C) A 10-year, 5% coupon bond  D) A perpetual bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only a **zero-coupon bond** has duration equal to its maturity, because the sole cash flow arrives at the end.\nA) Its coupons pull duration **below** 7. C) A 10-year bond with coupons has a duration below 10 but not necessarily 7. D) Perpetual bonds have a duration derived from the yield, not a maturity.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Under market segmentation theory, the yield curve's shape is determined by:\nA) Expected future short rates  B) Supply and demand within each maturity segment, as different investors prefer different maturities  C) A liquidity premium alone  D) Government decree\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Insurers want long maturities, banks want short ones; each segment clears independently.\nA) Is expectations theory. C) Is liquidity preference theory. D) Rates are market-determined.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A ₹5,00,000 investment in a bond fund with modified duration 3.2 experiences a 150 bp rate rise. What is the approximate loss?\nA) ₹24,000  B) ₹16,000  C) ₹7,500  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −3.2 × 0.015 = **−4.8%**. Loss = 4.8% × ₹5,00,000 = **₹24,000**.\nB) Uses 1%. C) Ignores duration. D) Is ten times too large. **A short-duration fund limits the damage — the reason liquid and short-duration funds are used for near-term goals.**\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which combination correctly describes a Sovereign Gold Bond?\nA) A corporate bond linked to gold  B) A government security linked to the gold price, paying an additional fixed interest rate  C) Physical gold  D) A gold ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SGBs give **gold price exposure plus a fixed interest coupon**, with sovereign backing.\nA) They are government-issued. C) There is no physical delivery during the term. D) An ETF is a mutual fund product without a coupon.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Convexity means that:\nA) Price changes are linear in yield  B) Prices rise more when yields fall than they fall when yields rise by the same amount — which benefits the bondholder  C) Duration is always zero  D) Bonds have no risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price–yield curve is **curved**, and positive convexity is favourable to the holder.\nA) Duration alone assumes linearity — convexity is the correction. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An investor wants to lock in a known sum on a known future date with no reinvestment risk. Which bond suits best?\nA) A high-coupon bond  B) A zero-coupon bond maturing on that date  C) A perpetual bond  D) A floating rate bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no intermediate cash flows**, a zero-coupon bond has **no reinvestment risk** and delivers a certain amount on a certain date.\nA) Its coupons must be reinvested at unknown rates. C) It never matures. D) Its coupon varies with the benchmark.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 9% annual coupon with 6 years to maturity. The required yield is 7%. What is the price?\nA) ₹1,095.33  B) ₹1,000  C) ₹904.67  D) ₹1,540\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Coupon = ₹90. (1.07)⁶ = **1.50073**, so (1.07)⁻⁶ = **0.66634**. Annuity factor = (1 − 0.66634) ÷ 0.07 = 0.33366 ÷ 0.07 = **4.7665**. PV of coupons = 90 × 4.7665 = **₹428.99**. PV of principal = 1,000 × 0.66634 = **₹666.34**. **Price = ₹1,095.33**.\nB) Would need coupon = yield. C) Is the discount case, wrong direction. D) Is the undiscounted total. **Coupon (9%) > yield (7%) → premium ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's bond, what is the current yield, and how does it compare with the coupon rate and YTM?\nA) 8.22%; coupon 9% > current yield 8.22% > YTM 7%  B) 9%; all equal  C) 8.22%; YTM is highest  D) 7%; coupon is lowest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current yield = 90 ÷ 1,095.33 = **8.22%**. It is a **premium** bond, so the ordering is **coupon (9%) > current yield (8.22%) > YTM (7%)**.\nB) They differ because the price is above par. C) YTM is **lowest** for a premium bond, because of the capital loss at redemption. D) Misassigns the values.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A ₹1,000 bond pays a 10% coupon **semi-annually**, with 4 years to maturity and a required yield of 8%. What is the price?\nA) ₹1,067.33  B) ₹1,000  C) ₹934  D) ₹1,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Convert to half-years: **C = ₹50**, **y = 4%**, **n = 8**. (1.04)⁸ = **1.36857**, inverse = **0.73069**. Annuity factor = (1 − 0.73069) ÷ 0.04 = 0.26931 ÷ 0.04 = **6.7327**. PV of coupons = 50 × 6.7327 = **₹336.64**. PV of principal = 1,000 × 0.73069 = **₹730.69**. **Price = ₹1,067.33**.\nB) Requires coupon = yield. C) Wrong direction — coupon (10%) exceeds yield (8%), so a premium. D) Undiscounted total.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A bond has Macaulay duration 7.5 years and a yield of 8%. What is its modified duration, and the approximate price change if yields rise 1.2%?\nA) MD 6.94; price falls about 8.33%  B) MD 8.10; price falls 9.72%  C) MD 7.50; price falls 9.0%  D) MD 6.94; price rises 8.33%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **MD = 7.5 ÷ 1.08 = 6.944**. % change ≈ −6.944 × 0.012 = **−8.33%**.\nB) Multiplies by (1+y) instead of dividing. C) Fails to convert Macaulay into modified duration. D) Gets the direction wrong — rising yields **lower** prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Two bonds both yield 8%: Bond X is a 10-year zero-coupon; Bond Y is a 10-year 12% coupon bond. Which has higher duration and by roughly how much?\nA) Bond Y, because of its high coupon  B) Bond X, with duration exactly 10 years versus Bond Y's roughly 6.5–7 years  C) They are equal at 10  D) Bond X at 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration equals its maturity**, so Bond X is exactly **10**. Bond Y's large coupons return value early, pulling its duration well below 10 — typically around **6.5–7 years** at these parameters.\nA) A **higher coupon reduces** duration. C) The coupon bond's duration is necessarily below its maturity. D) Bond X's duration is 10, not 5. **Bond X is therefore substantially more rate-sensitive.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An investor holds ₹40,00,000 in a bond fund with modified duration 6.5. She expects a 100 bp rate cut. What is her expected gain, and what is the main risk to this view?\nA) Gain about ₹2,60,000; the risk is that rates rise instead, producing an equal loss  B) Gain ₹40,000; no risk  C) Loss ₹2,60,000  D) Gain ₹6,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — % change ≈ −6.5 × (−0.01) = **+6.5%**. Gain = 6.5% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹2,60,000**. The symmetry is the risk: if rates instead **rise** 100 bp, she loses roughly the same amount (slightly less, thanks to positive convexity).\nB) Uses the wrong scale. C) Wrong direction for a rate cut. D) Overstates by a factor of 2.5. **Duration is a two-way street — it is a measure of risk, not of expected return.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A ₹1,00,000 face value T-bill with 182 days to maturity is purchased at ₹96,500. What is the annualised return (simple, 365-day basis)?\nA) 3.63%  B) 7.28%  C) 3.50%  D) 14.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Discount = 1,00,000 − 96,500 = **₹3,500**. Return over 182 days = 3,500 ÷ 96,500 = **3.627%**. Annualised = 3.627% × (365 ÷ 182) = 3.627% × 2.005 = **7.27%**.\nA) Is the un-annualised 182-day return. C) Divides by face value rather than price. D) Roughly doubles the correct figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client says \"I'll hold my bond to maturity, so interest rate risk doesn't affect me.\" Evaluate.\nA) Completely correct  B) Partly right — she avoids realising the mark-to-market loss, but she still bears opportunity cost and reinvestment risk, and the point fails if she needs to sell early  C) Completely wrong  D) Interest rate risk does not exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holding to maturity does secure the **contracted cash flows**, so no capital loss is realised. But she is **locked into a below-market yield** (opportunity cost), her **coupons are reinvested at whatever rates prevail**, and any need to sell early crystallises the loss.\nA) It overlooks opportunity and reinvestment effects. C) Her core point about not realising a loss is valid. D) It certainly exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A bond portfolio manager expects rates to rise. Which set of actions is consistent?\nA) Buy long-dated zero-coupon bonds  B) Shorten duration — shift into shorter maturities, higher coupons, floating rate bonds and liquid funds  C) Buy perpetual bonds  D) Increase duration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every element **reduces duration**: shorter maturity, higher coupon, floating coupons that reset upward, and near-zero-duration liquid instruments.\nA) Long zeros have the **highest** duration — maximum damage. C) Perpetuals are highly rate-sensitive. D) The opposite of what the view implies.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹1,000 bond with an 11% coupon and 5 years remaining trades at ₹1,080. Roughly what is its YTM, and how do you know it is below the coupon rate?\nA) About 9.0%; the premium price implies YTM < coupon  B) About 12%; YTM > coupon  C) Exactly 11%  D) About 6%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A price **above par** means the market's required yield is **below** the coupon rate. Approximating: annual coupon ₹110, plus an amortised capital loss of (1,000 − 1,080) ÷ 5 = −₹16 a year, over an average price of about ₹1,040 → (110 − 16) ÷ 1,040 ≈ **9.0%**.\nB) YTM above the coupon would imply a discount price. C) Would require the price to be exactly par. D) Too low for these parameters. **The shortcut: premium ⇒ YTM < current yield < coupon.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A 15-year bond has modified duration 9.8 and convexity that adds roughly 0.6% to the price when yields move 1%. If yields fall 1%, what is the total estimated price change?\nA) +9.8%  B) About +10.4% (duration effect +9.8% plus convexity +0.6%)  C) +9.2%  D) −10.4%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Duration effect** = −9.8 × (−0.01) = **+9.8%**. **Convexity** adds a further **+0.6%**, giving about **+10.4%**.\nA) Ignores convexity. C) Subtracts convexity — but positive convexity always **adds** to the gain when yields fall (and **cushions** the loss when yields rise). D) Wrong direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor compares: PPF at 7.1% (EEE), SCSS at 8.2% (taxable), and an FD at 7.6% (taxable). She is in the 30% bracket. Rank them by post-tax return.\nA) SCSS > FD > PPF  B) PPF 7.10% > SCSS 5.74% > FD 5.32%  C) FD > PPF > SCSS  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PPF** is EEE, so **7.10%** is received in full. **SCSS** post-tax = 8.2% × 0.70 = **5.74%**. **FD** post-tax = 7.6% × 0.70 = **5.32%**.\nA) Ranks on pre-tax figures. C) Miscomputes. D) They differ by nearly 2 percentage points. **The headline-rate ranking (SCSS > FD > PPF) completely inverts once tax is applied — the central lesson of this comparison.** *(SCSS still has a role: it provides quarterly income, which PPF does not.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by interest rate sensitivity, highest first?\nA) 91-day T-bill, 5-year 10% bond, 20-year zero-coupon  B) 20-year zero-coupon, 5-year 10% bond, 91-day T-bill  C) 5-year bond, T-bill, 20-year zero  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sensitivity tracks **duration**: the **20-year zero** has duration 20 (highest), the **5-year coupon bond** roughly 4, and the **91-day T-bill** about 0.25 (lowest).\nA) Exactly inverts the ranking. C) Scrambles it. D) They differ by roughly eighty-fold between the extremes.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A corporate bond yielding 10.5% is downgraded from AA to BBB. What happens, and why does the effect exceed the pure default-probability change?\nA) The price rises  B) The price falls as the credit spread widens; the effect is amplified because mandate-constrained institutional investors may be forced to sell  C) Nothing changes  D) The coupon increases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A downgrade widens the **credit spread**, raising the required yield and cutting the price. **Forced selling** by investors whose mandates restrict lower-rated holdings amplifies the move beyond what the default-probability change alone implies.\nA) Downgrades lower prices. C) The effect is material. D) The coupon is contractually fixed.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor buys a ₹1,000 bond with a 12% semi-annual coupon at par, planning to hold 5 years. Rates then fall to 8%. What are the two opposing effects?\nA) Both effects are negative  B) The bond's price rises (favourable if she sells), but coupons must be reinvested at 8% rather than 12% (unfavourable if she holds)  C) Both effects are positive  D) No effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the **duration trade-off** made concrete: falling rates raise the **price** but worsen **reinvestment**.\nA) The price effect is positive. C) The reinvestment effect is negative. D) Both effects are real. **Duration is precisely the horizon at which these two effects offset.**\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A ₹1,000 zero-coupon bond maturing in 8 years is priced at ₹540. What is its yield?\nA) 7.98%  B) 8.53%  C) 5.40%  D) 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — y = (F ÷ P)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,000 ÷ 540)^(1/8) − 1 = (1.85185)^0.125 − 1. ln(1.85185) = 0.61619; ÷ 8 = 0.077024; e^0.077024 = **1.08007**. Yield = **8.01%**, closest to **7.98%**.\nB) Slightly overstates. C) Is the price as a fraction. D) Far too high. **Note this is exactly the CAGR formula from Chapter 2.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which statement about the money market is INCORRECT?\nA) Maturities are up to one year  B) It is regulated by the RBI  C) Commercial paper is secured by collateral  D) T-bills are zero-coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Commercial paper is UNSECURED** — that is its defining feature, which is why it requires a credit rating.\nA), B) and D) are all correct statements about the money market.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client holds ₹50 lakh across three banks: ₹20 lakh in Bank A, ₹20 lakh in Bank B, ₹10 lakh in Bank C. How much is DICGC-insured?\nA) ₹5 lakh in total  B) ₹15 lakh — ₹5 lakh per depositor per bank, across three banks  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cover is **₹5 lakh per depositor per bank**, so ₹5 lakh × 3 banks = **₹15 lakh** insured; the remaining ₹35 lakh is uninsured.\nA) Treats the cover as a single overall cap. C) Far exceeds the cover. D) Counts only one bank. **Spreading deposits across banks is the practical way to extend insured cover.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A 10-year bond and a 10-year zero-coupon bond both yield 8%. Rates rise to 9%. Which loses more, and roughly by how much?\nA) The coupon bond, about 12%  B) The zero-coupon bond, roughly 8.5–9% versus about 6.5–7% for the coupon bond  C) They lose equally  D) Neither loses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **zero** has Macaulay duration 10, so modified duration ≈ 10 ÷ 1.08 = **9.26**; a 1% rise costs roughly **9.3%**. The coupon bond's duration is around 7, so modified duration ≈ 6.5, costing about **6.5%**.\nA) The coupon bond loses **less**. C) Their durations differ substantially. D) Both fall when yields rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An investor wants to fund a child's education costing ₹20,00,000 in exactly 7 years, with certainty. Which is the most precise instrument?\nA) An equity fund  B) A zero-coupon bond (or G-sec STRIP) maturing in 7 years, sized to redeem at ₹20,00,000 — no reinvestment risk and no price risk if held to maturity  C) A perpetual bond  D) A liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon instrument matching the goal date** eliminates **reinvestment risk** (no interim cash flows) and, if held to maturity, delivers a **known amount on a known date** — the textbook immunisation solution.\nA) Equity cannot guarantee a specific sum on a specific date. C) A perpetual bond never matures. D) A liquid fund's returns over 7 years are unknown and would need continual reinvestment.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A bond fund's NAV falls 7% in a quarter with no defaults in the portfolio. Its modified duration is 7.2. What most likely happened?\nA) The fund manager committed fraud  B) Yields rose by roughly 1% (7% ÷ 7.2 ≈ 0.97%), producing mark-to-market losses  C) The issuers defaulted  D) The fund charged high fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Working backwards: Δy ≈ −(% change) ÷ MD = 7% ÷ 7.2 ≈ **0.97%**. A roughly **1% rise in yields** fully explains the fall.\nA) An unwarranted conclusion where the arithmetic explains it. C) The question states there were no defaults. D) Fees of that magnitude in one quarter are implausible.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which bond would you expect to have the LOWEST yield, and why?\nA) A BBB corporate bond  B) A central government security, because it carries no domestic credit risk  C) An AT-1 bank bond  D) A high-yield corporate bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Yield compensates for risk. With **no credit risk**, the sovereign pays least; everything else trades at a **spread over** it.\nA), C) and D) all carry credit risk — and AT-1 additionally carries write-down risk — so all yield more.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A ₹1,000 bond with a 6% coupon and 4 years remaining trades at ₹930. Calculate the approximate YTM.\nA) 6.00%  B) About 8.06%  C) 6.45%  D) 10.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Approximate YTM = [coupon + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2] = [60 + (1,000 − 930) ÷ 4] ÷ [(1,000 + 930) ÷ 2] = [60 + 17.5] ÷ 965 = 77.5 ÷ 965 = **8.03%**.\nA) Is the coupon rate. C) Is the current yield (60 ÷ 930 = 6.45%). D) Far too high. **Ordering check for a discount bond: coupon 6% < current yield 6.45% < YTM 8.03% ✓**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An investor is told a perpetual AT-1 bond is \"just like an FD but with a better rate\". Identify the three errors.\nA) There are none  B) It has no maturity, it carries write-down risk (it can be extinguished if the bank is in trouble), and it has no deposit insurance — none of which applies to an FD  C) Only the rate differs  D) AT-1 bonds are safer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three material differences: **no maturity** (you cannot simply wait to get your money back), **write-down risk** (the principal can be wiped out while the bank continues operating), and **no DICGC insurance**.\nA) The differences are fundamental. C) The risk profile differs enormously. D) AT-1 bonds are substantially **riskier** than deposits.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A yield curve steepens sharply (long yields rise much more than short yields). Which portfolio suffers most?\nA) A portfolio of 91-day T-bills  B) A portfolio of 20-year government bonds, because long duration meets the largest yield rise  C) A liquid fund  D) A floating rate bond portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The damage is **duration × yield change**, and long bonds have **both** the highest duration **and** the biggest yield move in a steepening.\nA) and C) have almost no duration. D) Floating coupons reset upward, largely insulating the price.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** Which statement about the relationship between YTM and realised return is correct?\nA) They are always identical  B) They coincide only if all coupons are reinvested at the YTM and the bond is held to maturity — otherwise the realised return differs  C) YTM is always higher  D) Realised return is always higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — YTM is a **promised** yield conditional on two assumptions; the realised return depends on actual reinvestment rates and the actual holding period.\nA) They coincide only under those specific conditions. C) and D) Either can exceed the other depending on the rate path.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A company deposit offers 9.5% while a bank FD offers 7.2% and a AAA corporate bond yields 8.1%. What does the ordering tell you?\nA) The company deposit is the best investment  B) The yield ladder reflects a risk ladder — the uninsured company deposit carries the most credit risk, the AAA bond less, the insured bank deposit least  C) All three are identical in risk  D) The FD is riskiest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Yield is the price of risk.** The 2.3-point gap between the company deposit and the FD is compensation for the absence of insurance and the company's own default risk.\nA) Highest yield ≠ best investment; it must be judged against the risk taken and the client's need. C) Their risk profiles differ substantially. D) The insured bank deposit is the safest of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An investor holds a bond portfolio with duration 5 and a goal exactly 5 years away. What is the significance?\nA) Nothing  B) The portfolio is approximately immunised — the price effect and reinvestment effect of a rate change roughly offset over that horizon  C) She should sell immediately  D) Duration is irrelevant to goals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Immunisation**: when duration equals the investment horizon, a rate rise's **capital loss** is roughly offset by **higher reinvestment income**, and vice versa — so the terminal value is largely protected.\nA) It is a deliberate and powerful technique. C) The alignment is exactly what she wants. D) Matching duration to horizon is one of duration's main practical uses.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Which combination of features would produce the **highest** duration?\nA) Short maturity, high coupon, high yield  B) Long maturity, zero coupon, low yield  C) Short maturity, zero coupon  D) Long maturity, high coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three factors push duration **up**: **long maturity** (↑), **zero coupon** (↑, since no value returns early), and **low yield** (↑).\nA) All three push duration down. C) Short maturity caps it. D) The high coupon pulls duration down.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single framework best organises fixed income analysis?\nA) Buy the highest yield available  B) Identify the cash flows and discount them (price), compare the coupon with the required yield (premium/par/discount), measure rate sensitivity (duration), and assess who might not pay (credit)  C) Ignore duration  D) Only buy government bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Four questions cover essentially every bond problem: **what are the cash flows, what is it worth, how sensitive is it to rates, and how likely is repayment?**\nA) The highest yield reflects the highest risk. C) Duration is the central risk measure. D) Government bonds eliminate credit risk but not interest rate risk, and forgo the credit spread entirely.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Convert to per-period figures before pricing.\n\n### Case: Mr Bhatia's Fixed Income Decision\n\nMr Bhatia, 58, is restructuring ₹60,00,000 of his portfolio into fixed income ahead of retirement at 60. He is considering:\n\n- **Bond P:** a ₹1,000 face value corporate bond, **10% annual coupon**, **5 years** to maturity, rated AA, currently priced at **₹1,079**\n- **Bond Q:** a **10-year zero-coupon** government security, ₹1,000 face value, priced at **₹508**\n- **SCSS:** at **8.2%**, paid quarterly, taxable\n- **PPF:** at **7.1%**, EEE (he already has an account opened 12 years ago)\n\nMr Bhatia is in the **30%** tax bracket. He expects to need **regular income** from age 60 and believes **interest rates will fall** over the next two years.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Bond P's current yield, and how does it rank against its coupon rate and YTM?\nA) 9.27%; coupon 10% > current yield 9.27% > YTM  B) 10%; all equal  C) 9.27%; YTM is highest  D) 8.0%; coupon is lowest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Current yield = annual coupon ÷ price = ₹100 ÷ ₹1,079 = **9.27%**. Bond P trades at a **premium** (₹1,079 > ₹1,000), so the ordering is **coupon (10%) > current yield (9.27%) > YTM**.\nB) They differ because the price is above par. C) For a **premium** bond, YTM is the **lowest** of the three — the buyer suffers a capital loss of ₹79 at redemption. D) Misassigns the figures.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What yield does Bond Q offer?\nA) 5.08%  B) About 7.02%  C) 10.0%  D) 49.2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a zero-coupon bond, y = (F ÷ P)^(1/n) − 1 = (1,000 ÷ 508)^(1/10) − 1 = (1.96850)^0.1 − 1. ln(1.96850) = 0.67717; ÷ 10 = 0.067717; e^0.067717 = **1.07006**. **Yield ≈ 7.01%**.\nA) Is the price expressed as a fraction. C) Is Bond P's coupon rate. D) Is the total gain over ten years, not the annual rate. *(Same formula as CAGR in Chapter 2.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Given Mr Bhatia's expectation that rates will fall, which instrument benefits MOST, and why?\nA) SCSS, because it pays the highest rate  B) Bond Q — a 10-year zero-coupon bond has Macaulay duration of exactly 10, the highest of the options, so its price gains most when yields fall  C) PPF, because it is EEE  D) Bond P, because of its high coupon\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **zero-coupon bond's duration equals its maturity**, so Bond Q's duration is **10** — far above Bond P's (roughly 4.2, since its coupons return value early). With **% price change ≈ −MD × Δy**, Bond Q gains most from falling yields.\nA) SCSS pays a **fixed** administered rate; its value does not rise when market yields fall. C) PPF's rate is administered, not market-priced. D) Bond P's **high coupon shortens** its duration, reducing the gain.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Comparing SCSS at 8.2% (taxable) with PPF at 7.1% (EEE) for a 30% taxpayer, which delivers the higher post-tax return — and what is the countervailing consideration?\nA) SCSS, at 8.2%  B) PPF at 7.10% beats SCSS's post-tax 5.74% — but SCSS pays quarterly income, which Mr Bhatia needs from 60, while PPF pays nothing until maturity  C) They are equal  D) Neither is suitable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCSS post-tax** = 8.2% × (1 − 0.30) = **5.74%**. **PPF** is EEE, so its **7.10%** is received in full — a **1.36 percentage point** advantage.\nBut return is not the only criterion: Mr Bhatia needs **regular income from 60**, and **SCSS pays quarterly** while **PPF pays nothing until maturity**. Note also that his PPF account, opened 12 years ago, matures in about 3 years — conveniently close to his retirement.\nA) Ranks on the pre-tax headline. C) They differ materially. D) Both have a legitimate role.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What is the most appropriate overall structure for Mr Bhatia?\nA) Put all ₹60,00,000 into Bond Q to maximise the rate-fall gain  B) Blend: SCSS (up to ₹30 lakh) for quarterly income from 60, continue PPF for tax-free accumulation, and hold a moderate allocation to Bond P/Q for the rate view — while recognising that a single AA corporate bond concentrates credit risk  C) Put everything into SCSS  D) Hold only cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three objectives must be served at once. **Income:** SCSS is purpose-built, capped at **₹30 lakh**, paying **quarterly**. **Tax efficiency:** PPF's EEE status makes it the best post-tax rate available to him, and it matures near his retirement. **The rate view:** some duration exposure captures a rate fall — but it is a *view*, not a certainty, and Bond Q's duration of 10 cuts both ways.\n\nTwo risk points a good adviser would raise: **Bond P is a single AA corporate issuer**, so concentrating there means undiversified credit risk (a fund would spread it); and Mr Bhatia is **2 years from retirement**, so the portion funding near-term income should not sit in a 10-year duration instrument.\n\nA) Concentrating everything in a 10-year zero at age 58 is a large, undiversified bet on one macro view. C) SCSS is capped at ₹30 lakh and would leave half the corpus unallocated. D) Cash guarantees a negative real return.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson:** the highest headline rate (SCSS at 8.2%) is not the highest post-tax return (PPF at 7.1%), and the instrument that gains most from his rate view (Bond Q) is the least suited to his income need. **Match each rupee to the job it must do.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "10", "title": "Understanding Derivatives", "module": "Module 3: Investment Products", "notes": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 3 · Investment Products (30 marks).**\n> Conceptually the trickiest chapter, but the exam questions are mostly **definitional and payoff-based**. Master the **four rights-and-obligations combinations** for options and the **forward vs futures** table, and most questions answer themselves.\n\n---\n\n## 10.1 Basics of Derivatives\n\n**A derivative is a contract whose value is DERIVED from an underlying asset.**\n\nThe derivative has no independent value — it is a claim whose worth depends entirely on what happens to something else.\n\n**Underlyings can be:** equity shares, indices, interest rates, currencies, commodities, bonds — even weather or credit events.\n\n### Why derivatives exist\n1. **Risk transfer** — those who don't want a risk can pass it to those willing to bear it.\n2. **Price discovery** — futures prices reveal market expectations.\n3. **Leverage** — a large exposure for a small outlay (margin).\n4. **Lower transaction costs** than trading the underlying.\n5. **Market completion** — payoffs otherwise unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## 10.2 Underlying Concepts\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Underlying** | The asset from which value is derived |\n| **Spot price** | Today's price of the underlying |\n| **Futures/forward price** | The agreed price for a future transaction |\n| **Contract size / lot** | The standardised quantity per contract |\n| **Expiry** | The date the contract ends (Indian equity derivatives: monthly, on a specified weekday) |\n| **Margin** | Collateral deposited to cover potential loss |\n| **Mark to market (MTM)** | Daily settlement of gains/losses to margin accounts |\n| **Open interest** | Total number of contracts outstanding — a gauge of participation |\n| **Basis** | **Spot price − Futures price** |\n| **Cost of carry** | Interest, storage and insurance costs less income (e.g. dividends) from holding the underlying |\n\n### The cost-of-carry relationship\n\n$$\\boxed{F = S \\times e^{(r-q)T}} \\quad \\text{or approximately} \\quad F = S \\times (1 + r - q)^T$$\n\nwhere **S** = spot, **r** = risk-free rate, **q** = income yield (dividends), **T** = time.\n\n> 🧠 **Normally the futures price exceeds spot** (called **contango**) because holding the asset costs interest. When the futures price is **below** spot, the market is in **backwardation**. **At expiry, the futures price converges to the spot price** — this convergence is guaranteed by arbitrage.\n\n---\n\n## 10.3 ⭐ Types of Derivative Products\n\n### 10.3.1 Forwards\nA **customised, bilateral (OTC)** agreement to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date.\n\n- ✅ Fully customisable in size, date and terms\n- ❌ **Counterparty risk** — no guarantee the other side performs\n- ❌ Illiquid; hard to exit before maturity\n- Settlement occurs **only at maturity**\n\n### 10.3.2 Futures\nA **standardised, exchange-traded** forward.\n\n| | **Forward** | **Futures** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Traded** | OTC, bilateral | **Exchange** |\n| **Terms** | Customised | **Standardised** (lot size, expiry) |\n| **Counterparty risk** | **High** — rests with the other party | **Eliminated** — the clearing corporation is the CCP |\n| **Margin** | Usually none | **Mandatory** (initial + MTM) |\n| **Settlement** | At maturity only | **Daily mark to market** |\n| **Liquidity** | Low | High |\n| **Regulation** | Light | Fully regulated |\n\n> ⚠️ **The single biggest difference: futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily; forwards are not.**\n\n### 10.3.3 ⭐ Options\n\n**An option gives the BUYER a RIGHT without an OBLIGATION. The SELLER (writer) has an OBLIGATION without a right.**\n\nThat asymmetry is the whole concept — and the source of most exam questions.\n\n| | **Call option** | **Put option** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Buyer has the right to** | **BUY** the underlying | **SELL** the underlying |\n| **Buyer's view** | **Bullish** | **Bearish** |\n| **Buyer pays** | Premium | Premium |\n| **Buyer's maximum loss** | **The premium** | **The premium** |\n| **Buyer's maximum gain** | **Unlimited** | Large but capped (the price can only fall to zero) |\n| **Seller's view** | Neutral-to-bearish | Neutral-to-bullish |\n| **Seller's maximum gain** | **The premium** | **The premium** |\n| **Seller's maximum loss** | **Unlimited** | Large but capped |\n\n> 🧠 **The four positions to know cold:**\n> - **Long call** — bullish · pay premium · loss capped at premium · **unlimited** gain\n> - **Long put** — bearish · pay premium · loss capped at premium · large gain\n> - **Short call** — receive premium · gain capped at premium · **UNLIMITED loss** ⚠️\n> - **Short put** — receive premium · gain capped at premium · large loss\n>\n> **Option sellers face far greater risk than buyers.** A buyer can lose only the premium; a **short call has theoretically unlimited loss**. This is why sellers must post margin and buyers need not.\n\n#### Key option terms\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Strike (exercise) price** | The price at which the option can be exercised |\n| **Premium** | The price paid by the buyer to the seller |\n| **European option** | Exercisable **only at expiry** — the Indian standard for index and stock options |\n| **American option** | Exercisable **any time up to expiry** |\n| **Intrinsic value** | The immediate exercise value — **never negative** |\n| **Time value** | Premium − intrinsic value; **decays to zero at expiry** |\n\n#### Moneyness\n\n| | **Call** | **Put** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **In the money (ITM)** | Spot > Strike | Spot < Strike |\n| **At the money (ATM)** | Spot = Strike | Spot = Strike |\n| **Out of the money (OTM)** | Spot < Strike | Spot > Strike |\n\n**Intrinsic value:** Call = **max(Spot − Strike, 0)**; Put = **max(Strike − Spot, 0)**.\n\n#### Payoffs at expiry\n\n- **Long call:** Payoff = max(S − K, 0) − Premium. **Break-even = Strike + Premium.**\n- **Long put:** Payoff = max(K − S, 0) − Premium. **Break-even = Strike − Premium.**\n- **Short call:** Premium − max(S − K, 0)\n- **Short put:** Premium − max(K − S, 0)\n\n**Worked example.** Buy a call, strike ₹1,000, premium ₹40.\n- Break-even = 1,000 + 40 = **₹1,040**\n- Spot at expiry ₹1,090 → intrinsic 90, profit = 90 − 40 = **+₹50**\n- Spot ₹1,020 → intrinsic 20, profit = 20 − 40 = **−₹20** (a loss, though the option is ITM)\n- Spot ₹950 → intrinsic 0, loss = **−₹40** (the full premium)\n\n> ⚠️ **Classic trap:** an option can be **in the money and still lose money**, because the premium must be recovered first. ITM ≠ profitable.\n\n### 10.3.4 Swaps\nAn agreement to **exchange cash flows** on specified dates.\n\n- **Interest rate swap** — the most common: one party pays **fixed** and receives **floating**, the other the reverse. Used to convert floating-rate borrowing into fixed, or vice versa.\n- **Currency swap** — exchange principal and interest in different currencies.\n- Swaps are **OTC** instruments.\n\n---\n\n## 10.4 Structure of Derivative Markets\n\n| | **OTC** | **Exchange traded** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Contracts** | Customised | Standardised |\n| **Counterparty risk** | Present | Eliminated by the CCP |\n| **Transparency** | Low | High |\n| **Regulation** | Lighter | Full |\n| **Examples** | Forwards, swaps, exotic options | Futures, listed options |\n\n---\n\n## 10.5 ⭐ Purpose of Derivatives\n\n### Hedging\n**Reducing or eliminating an existing risk.** The hedger already has an exposure and uses a derivative to offset it.\n\n- A farmer with a standing crop **sells futures** to lock in a price.\n- An investor holding equity **buys index puts** to protect against a fall (a \"protective put\").\n- An importer with a dollar payable **buys dollar futures** to fix the cost.\n\n> 🧠 **A hedge is not designed to make money.** It converts an uncertain outcome into a certain one — and if the market moves favourably, the hedge gives up that gain. That is the price of certainty.\n\n### Speculation\n**Taking on risk to profit from an expected price move**, with no underlying exposure. Leverage magnifies both gains and losses. Speculators supply the liquidity hedgers need.\n\n### Arbitrage\n**Simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a risk-free profit** from a price difference. Example: if futures are priced above their fair cost-of-carry value, buy the spot and sell the future. Arbitrage keeps prices aligned and, by its own action, eliminates the mispricing it exploits.\n\n> 🧠 **The three participants form an ecosystem:** hedgers **transfer** risk, speculators **absorb** it and supply liquidity, and arbitrageurs **keep prices correct**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.6 Costs, Benefits and Risks\n\n**Benefits:** risk management, price discovery, leverage/capital efficiency, lower transaction costs, and the ability to take a view in either direction (including going short).\n\n**Costs and risks:**\n\n| Risk | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Leverage risk** | A small margin controls a large exposure — losses can **exceed the initial outlay** |\n| **Counterparty risk** | In OTC contracts (eliminated on exchange) |\n| **Liquidity risk** | Far-month or deep OTM contracts may be thinly traded |\n| **Basis risk** | The hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together, so the hedge is imperfect |\n| **Model/valuation risk** | Complex instruments may be mispriced |\n| **Operational risk** | Margin calls, settlement failures, errors |\n\n> ⚠️ **The defining danger:** unlike equity, where the maximum loss is the amount invested, a **derivative position can lose more than the margin deposited**. Daily MTM means losses are demanded in cash immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 10.7 Equity, Currency and Commodity Derivatives\n\n| Segment | Underlying | Regulator | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Equity derivatives** | Indices and individual stocks | **SEBI** | Nifty and Bank Nifty futures & options, stock futures & options |\n| **Currency derivatives** | Currency pairs | **SEBI and RBI jointly** | USD-INR, EUR-INR, GBP-INR, JPY-INR futures & options |\n| **Commodity derivatives** | Metals, energy, agri | **SEBI** (since 2015, when FMC merged into SEBI) | Gold, silver, crude, cotton, guar |\n| **Interest rate derivatives** | Government securities | SEBI / RBI | Interest rate futures |\n\n**Indian equity derivatives:** European-style options, **monthly expiry**, **cash-settled** for indices; stock derivatives moved to **physical settlement**.\n\n---\n\n## 10.8 Derivative Strategies (introductory)\n\n| Strategy | Construction | View |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Protective put** | Hold the stock + buy a put | Bullish but wants **downside insurance** |\n| **Covered call** | Hold the stock + sell a call | Neutral to mildly bullish; earns premium, **caps upside** |\n| **Long straddle** | Buy a call and a put at the **same strike** | Expects a **big move**, direction unknown |\n| **Long strangle** | Buy an OTM call and an OTM put | Same view, cheaper, needs a bigger move |\n| **Bull call spread** | Buy a lower-strike call, sell a higher-strike call | Moderately bullish; caps both cost and gain |\n| **Bear put spread** | Buy a higher-strike put, sell a lower-strike put | Moderately bearish |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Derivative | A contract whose value is **derived** from an underlying |\n| Forward | **OTC, customised**, counterparty risk, settled at maturity |\n| Futures | **Exchange-traded, standardised**, CCP-guaranteed, **daily MTM** |\n| Option buyer | Has a **right, no obligation**; pays premium; **loss capped at premium** |\n| Option seller | Has an **obligation, no right**; receives premium; **risk far greater** |\n| Long call | Bullish; **unlimited** gain; loss = premium |\n| Long put | Bearish; large gain; loss = premium |\n| Short call | **UNLIMITED loss** ⚠️; gain = premium |\n| Short put | Large loss; gain = premium |\n| Call break-even | **Strike + Premium** |\n| Put break-even | **Strike − Premium** |\n| Call intrinsic value | max(Spot − Strike, 0) |\n| Put intrinsic value | max(Strike − Spot, 0) |\n| Time value | Premium − intrinsic; **decays to zero at expiry** |\n| ITM call | Spot **>** Strike |\n| ITM put | Spot **<** Strike |\n| European option | Exercisable **only at expiry** (the Indian standard) |\n| American option | Exercisable **any time** |\n| Cost of carry | F = S × (1 + r − q)^T |\n| Contango | Futures **above** spot (normal) |\n| Backwardation | Futures **below** spot |\n| At expiry | Futures price **converges to spot** |\n| Basis | Spot − Futures |\n| Hedging | **Reducing an existing** risk; gives up favourable moves |\n| Speculation | Taking risk for profit, **no underlying exposure** |\n| Arbitrage | **Risk-free** profit from a price difference in two markets |\n| Swap | Exchange of **cash flows**; OTC |\n| Interest rate swap | Fixed for floating |\n| Key derivative danger | Losses can **exceed the margin deposited** |\n| Basis risk | The hedge doesn't move perfectly with the exposure |\n| Currency derivatives regulator | **SEBI and RBI jointly** |\n| Commodity derivatives regulator | **SEBI** (FMC merged in 2015) |\n| Protective put | Stock + long put = downside insurance |\n| Covered call | Stock + short call = premium income, capped upside |\n| Long straddle | Same-strike call + put; expects a **big move either way** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for any option question, first write down **(1) is it a call or a put, (2) am I the buyer or the seller?** Those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss immediately. Then apply the break-even formula.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The four option positions and the forward-vs-futures table carry the most marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Define a derivative.\n> **A:** A **contract whose value is derived from an underlying asset** — it has no independent value of its own.\n\n**Q2.** Name five purposes derivatives serve.\n> **A:** **Risk transfer**, **price discovery**, **leverage**, **lower transaction costs** than trading the underlying, and **market completion** (payoffs otherwise unavailable).\n\n**Q3.** Give five differences between a forward and a future.\n> **A:** **Forward** — OTC, customised, counterparty risk, no margin, settled only at maturity. **Future** — exchange-traded, standardised, **CCP-guaranteed**, margined, **daily mark to market**.\n\n**Q4.** What is the single biggest difference between forwards and futures?\n> **A:** **Futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily; forwards are not** — a forward leaves you exposed to the other party's default.\n\n**Q5.** State the fundamental asymmetry of options.\n> **A:** The **buyer has a RIGHT without an OBLIGATION**; the **seller (writer) has an OBLIGATION without a right**. That asymmetry is the whole concept.\n\n**Q6.** What does a call buyer have the right to do, and what is their view?\n> **A:** The right to **BUY** the underlying at the strike price. The view is **bullish**.\n\n**Q7.** What does a put buyer have the right to do, and what is their view?\n> **A:** The right to **SELL** the underlying at the strike price. The view is **bearish**.\n\n**Q8.** State maximum gain and loss for a LONG CALL.\n> **A:** Maximum loss = **the premium**. Maximum gain = **unlimited** (the price can rise without limit).\n\n**Q9.** State maximum gain and loss for a SHORT CALL.\n> **A:** Maximum gain = **the premium**. Maximum loss = **UNLIMITED** ⚠️ — the riskiest of the four basic positions.\n\n**Q10.** State maximum gain and loss for a LONG PUT.\n> **A:** Maximum loss = **the premium**. Maximum gain is large but **capped**, because the underlying can only fall to zero.\n\n**Q11.** State maximum gain and loss for a SHORT PUT.\n> **A:** Maximum gain = **the premium**. Maximum loss is large but capped (underlying falling to zero).\n\n**Q12.** Why must option sellers post margin while buyers need not?\n> **A:** Because a **buyer's loss is capped at the premium already paid**, while a **seller's loss can be unlimited** (short call) or very large (short put). The margin secures that open-ended obligation.\n\n**Q13.** State the break-even for a long call and a long put.\n> **A:** **Long call break-even = Strike + Premium.** **Long put break-even = Strike − Premium.**\n\n**Q14.** State the intrinsic value formulas.\n> **A:** **Call = max(Spot − Strike, 0).** **Put = max(Strike − Spot, 0).** Intrinsic value is **never negative**.\n\n**Q15.** What is time value, and what happens to it?\n> **A:** **Time value = Premium − Intrinsic value.** It **decays to zero at expiry**, when the premium equals intrinsic value exactly.\n\n**Q16.** Define moneyness for calls and puts.\n> **A:** **Call:** ITM when Spot > Strike; OTM when Spot < Strike. **Put:** ITM when Spot < Strike; OTM when Spot > Strike. **ATM** when Spot = Strike for both.\n\n**Q17.** Can an option be in the money and still lose money? Explain.\n> **A:** **Yes.** A call with strike ₹1,000 bought for ₹40 with spot at ₹1,020 has intrinsic value ₹20 but cost ₹40 — a **₹20 loss** despite being ITM. **The premium must be recovered first: ITM ≠ profitable.**\n\n**Q18.** Distinguish European from American options, and state the Indian standard.\n> **A:** **European** — exercisable **only at expiry**. **American** — exercisable **any time up to expiry**. Indian index and stock options are **European**.\n\n**Q19.** State the cost-of-carry relationship.\n> **A:** **F = S × (1 + r − q)^T** (or F = S × e^((r−q)T)), where r is the risk-free rate and q the income yield (e.g. dividends).\n\n**Q20.** What are contango and backwardation?\n> **A:** **Contango** — futures price **above** spot (the normal state, reflecting carrying cost). **Backwardation** — futures price **below** spot.\n\n**Q21.** What happens to the futures price at expiry, and why?\n> **A:** It **converges to the spot price** — enforced by **arbitrage**, since any gap could be captured risk-free at that moment.\n\n**Q22.** Define basis.\n> **A:** **Basis = Spot price − Futures price.**\n\n**Q23.** What is a swap, and what is the most common type?\n> **A:** An agreement to **exchange cash flows** on specified dates. The most common is the **interest rate swap** — one party pays **fixed** and receives **floating**, the other the reverse. Swaps are **OTC**.\n\n**Q24.** Define hedging, and state what it costs.\n> **A:** **Reducing or eliminating an EXISTING risk** using a derivative. Its cost: it **converts uncertainty into certainty**, so a favourable market move is given up. A hedge is not designed to make money.\n\n**Q25.** Give three examples of hedging.\n> **A:** A **farmer sells futures** to lock in a crop price; an **investor buys index puts** to protect a portfolio; an **importer buys currency futures** to fix a payable.\n\n**Q26.** Define speculation and arbitrage.\n> **A:** **Speculation** — taking on risk to profit from an expected move, with **no underlying exposure**. **Arbitrage** — simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a **risk-free** profit from a price difference.\n\n**Q27.** How do the three participant types form an ecosystem?\n> **A:** **Hedgers transfer** risk, **speculators absorb** it and supply liquidity, and **arbitrageurs keep prices correct** — and by acting, eliminate the mispricing they exploit.\n\n**Q28.** What is the defining danger of derivatives compared with equity?\n> **A:** With equity your maximum loss is the amount invested. With a derivative, **losses can EXCEED the margin deposited** — and daily MTM demands those losses in cash immediately.\n\n**Q29.** What is basis risk?\n> **A:** The risk that the **hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together**, leaving the hedge imperfect.\n\n**Q30.** Name the main risks of derivatives.\n> **A:** **Leverage risk**, **counterparty risk** (OTC), **liquidity risk**, **basis risk**, **model/valuation risk** and **operational risk**.\n\n**Q31.** Who regulates equity, currency and commodity derivatives in India?\n> **A:** **Equity — SEBI.** **Currency — SEBI and RBI jointly.** **Commodity — SEBI** (since the FMC merged into SEBI in **2015**).\n\n**Q32.** What is mark to market, and open interest?\n> **A:** **MTM** is the **daily settlement** of gains and losses to margin accounts. **Open interest** is the total number of contracts outstanding — a gauge of participation.\n\n**Q33.** Describe a protective put and a covered call.\n> **A:** **Protective put** = hold the stock **+ buy a put** — bullish but wants **downside insurance**. **Covered call** = hold the stock **+ sell a call** — earns premium but **caps the upside**.\n\n**Q34.** Describe a long straddle and what view it expresses.\n> **A:** **Buy a call AND a put at the same strike.** It profits from a **big move in either direction** — the view is on **volatility**, not direction.\n\n**Q35.** A call with strike ₹1,000 costs ₹40. Give the profit at spot prices of ₹1,090, ₹1,020 and ₹950.\n> **A:** **₹1,090:** intrinsic 90 − premium 40 = **+₹50**. **₹1,020:** intrinsic 20 − 40 = **−₹20**. **₹950:** intrinsic 0 − 40 = **−₹40** (the full premium). Break-even is **₹1,040**.\n\n**Q36.** What two questions should you ask first in any option problem?\n> **A:** **(1) Is it a call or a put? (2) Am I the buyer or the seller?** Those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss immediately.\n\n**Q37.** What are the settlement conventions for Indian equity derivatives?\n> **A:** **European-style** options, **monthly expiry**, **cash-settled** for indices, with **physical settlement** for stock derivatives.\n\n**Q38.** Why does leverage make derivatives dangerous for retail investors?\n> **A:** A **small margin controls a large exposure**, so a modest adverse move in the underlying can wipe out — and exceed — the entire amount deposited, with **margin calls demanded in cash the same day**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 10: Understanding Derivatives — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Option payoffs and break-evens dominate the numerical questions.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** A derivative is:\nA) A share issued by a company  B) A contract whose value is derived from an underlying asset  C) A type of bank deposit  D) A government bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A derivative has **no independent value**; its worth depends entirely on the underlying.\nA), C) and D) are all primary instruments with intrinsic value of their own.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** An option BUYER has:\nA) An obligation but no right  B) A right but no obligation  C) Both a right and an obligation  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer pays a premium for the **right to choose** whether to exercise.\nA) Describes the **seller/writer**. C) and D) misstate the asymmetry that defines options.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An option SELLER (writer) has:\nA) A right but no obligation  B) An obligation but no right  C) No exposure  D) Unlimited rights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The writer receives the premium and **must perform** if the buyer exercises.\nA) Describes the buyer. C) The writer's exposure can be unlimited. D) The writer has no rights at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A call option gives the buyer the right to:\nA) Sell the underlying  B) Buy the underlying  C) Borrow the underlying  D) Cancel the contract\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Call = right to BUY**, taken by a **bullish** investor.\nA) Describes a put. C) and D) are not option rights.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A put option buyer is:\nA) Bullish  B) Bearish  C) Neutral  D) Arbitraging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A put gives the **right to SELL**, which gains value as the price falls.\nA) Describes a call buyer. C) and D) describe different motives.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The maximum loss for a call option BUYER is:\nA) Unlimited  B) The premium paid  C) The strike price  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The worst case is that the option expires worthless and the **premium** is lost.\nA) Describes the **short call**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The maximum loss for a call option SELLER is:\nA) The premium received  B) Unlimited  C) Zero  D) The strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the underlying can rise without limit, a **naked short call has theoretically unlimited loss** — the riskiest basic position.\nA) Is the maximum **gain**. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The break-even for a long call is:\nA) Strike − Premium  B) Strike + Premium  C) The premium alone  D) The strike alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer must recover the premium before profiting, so break-even = **Strike + Premium**.\nA) Is the **long put** break-even. C) and D) ignore one of the two components.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The break-even for a long put is:\nA) Strike + Premium  B) Strike − Premium  C) Premium ÷ Strike  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The price must fall below the strike by at least the premium: **Strike − Premium**.\nA) Is the long call break-even. C) and D) are meaningless here.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The intrinsic value of a call option is:\nA) max(Strike − Spot, 0)  B) max(Spot − Strike, 0)  C) Spot − Strike, which can be negative  D) The premium\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **max(Spot − Strike, 0)** — intrinsic value can never be negative, because the buyer would simply not exercise.\nA) Is the **put** formula. C) Omits the floor at zero. D) Premium = intrinsic + time value.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A call option is in the money when:\nA) Spot < Strike  B) Spot > Strike  C) Spot = Strike  D) The premium is high\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A call to buy below the market price has value: **Spot > Strike**.\nA) Is out of the money for a call (and ITM for a put). C) Is at the money. D) Premium level does not define moneyness.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A put option is in the money when:\nA) Spot > Strike  B) Spot < Strike  C) Spot = Strike  D) Always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A right to sell above the market price has value: **Spot < Strike**.\nA) Is OTM for a put. C) Is ATM. D) Options are frequently OTM.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Time value of an option:\nA) Increases as expiry approaches  B) Decays to zero at expiry  C) Is always zero  D) Equals the strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At expiry the premium equals **intrinsic value only** — all time value has decayed.\nA) Reverses the effect. C) Time value is positive before expiry. D) Unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** European options can be exercised:\nA) Any time before expiry  B) Only at expiry  C) Only in Europe  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Only at expiry** — and this is the Indian standard for index and stock options.\nA) Describes an **American** option. C) The name is conventional, not geographic. D) They can certainly be exercised.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which is exchange-traded and standardised?\nA) A forward  B) A future  C) A swap  D) An OTC option\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Futures** are standardised and exchange-traded with a CCP.\nA), C) and D) are all OTC instruments.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Counterparty risk in a futures contract is:\nA) High  B) Eliminated by the clearing corporation acting as central counterparty  C) Borne by SEBI  D) Unregulated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **CCP** guarantees settlement, backed by margins and a guarantee fund.\nA) Describes a **forward**. C) SEBI regulates but is not a counterparty. D) Futures are fully regulated.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Mark to market means:\nA) Settlement only at maturity  B) Daily settlement of gains and losses to margin accounts  C) Marketing the contract  D) Setting the strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Daily MTM** is a defining feature of exchange-traded futures.\nA) Describes a forward. C) and D) are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Hedging means:\nA) Taking risk to profit  B) Reducing or eliminating an existing risk  C) Risk-free arbitrage  D) Borrowing money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A hedger **already has an exposure** and uses a derivative to offset it.\nA) Is speculation. C) Is arbitrage. D) Is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Speculation in derivatives involves:\nA) Offsetting an existing exposure  B) Taking on risk to profit from an expected move, with no underlying exposure  C) Risk-free profit  D) Insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The speculator has **no position to protect** — the derivative is the position.\nA) Is hedging. C) Is arbitrage. D) A hedge, not speculation, resembles insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Arbitrage means:\nA) Taking directional risk  B) Simultaneously buying and selling in two markets to lock in a risk-free profit  C) Hedging an exposure  D) Writing options\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrage exploits a **price difference** with no net risk.\nA) Is speculation. C) Is hedging. D) Is a specific option position, not a strategy category.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A swap is an agreement to:\nA) Exchange cash flows on specified dates  B) Buy a share  C) Sell a bond  D) Borrow from a bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Most commonly an **interest rate swap** exchanging fixed for floating.\nB), C) and D) are not swaps.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** In an interest rate swap, typically:\nA) Both parties pay fixed  B) One party pays fixed and receives floating, the other the reverse  C) Both pay floating  D) No payments occur\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is the standard \"plain vanilla\" structure.\nA) and C) would have no purpose. D) Cash flows are the whole point.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Contango describes a market where:\nA) Futures price is below spot  B) Futures price is above spot  C) They are equal  D) No futures exist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **normal** state, reflecting the cost of carrying the asset.\nA) Is **backwardation**. C) Happens only at expiry. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** At expiry, the futures price:\nA) Diverges from spot  B) Converges to the spot price  C) Doubles  D) Becomes zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Convergence** is enforced by arbitrage — any gap at expiry could be captured risk-free.\nA), C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Basis is defined as:\nA) Futures − Spot  B) Spot − Futures  C) Strike − Spot  D) Premium ÷ Strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Basis = Spot − Futures**.\nA) Reverses the convention. C) and D) relate to options, not basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which regulator oversees commodity derivatives in India?\nA) The RBI  B) SEBI  C) IRDAI  D) The FMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SEBI**, since the Forward Markets Commission merged into it in **2015**.\nA) Regulates banking and money markets. C) Insurance. D) The FMC no longer exists as a separate regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Currency derivatives in India are regulated by:\nA) SEBI alone  B) SEBI and the RBI jointly  C) IRDAI  D) The MCA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Currency derivatives sit at the intersection of securities markets (SEBI) and foreign exchange (RBI).\nA) Omits the RBI's role. C) and D) have no jurisdiction.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A protective put involves:\nA) Selling a stock and buying a call  B) Holding a stock and buying a put  C) Selling a put  D) Buying two calls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stock **plus a long put** provides **downside insurance** while retaining upside.\nA), C) and D) describe different positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A covered call involves:\nA) Holding a stock and selling a call  B) Buying a call with no stock  C) Selling a put  D) Buying a straddle\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The stock \"covers\" the short call, earning premium but **capping the upside**.\nB) Is a naked long call. C) and D) are different strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A long straddle involves:\nA) Buying a call and a put at the same strike  B) Selling a call and a put  C) Buying two calls  D) Buying a stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It profits from a **large move in either direction** — a view on **volatility**, not direction.\nB) Is a short straddle. C) and D) are directional positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The key risk unique to derivatives compared with equity is:\nA) Market risk  B) Losses can exceed the amount deposited as margin  C) Inflation risk  D) Liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With equity your loss is capped at what you invested; a **leveraged derivative position can lose more**.\nA), C) and D) affect both equity and derivatives.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Open interest measures:\nA) The interest rate  B) The total number of contracts outstanding  C) The premium  D) The strike price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A gauge of **participation** in a contract.\nA), C) and D) are unrelated concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Basis risk is the risk that:\nA) The hedge and the exposure do not move perfectly together  B) The counterparty defaults  C) Rates rise  D) Volume falls\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An imperfect correlation makes the hedge incomplete.\nB) Is counterparty risk. C) and D) are different risks.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Which statement about a hedge is correct?\nA) It is designed to make a profit  B) It converts an uncertain outcome into a certain one, giving up favourable moves  C) It eliminates all risk permanently  D) It requires no cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A hedge buys **certainty**, and the price is forgoing the upside.\nA) Profit is not the purpose. C) Basis risk usually remains. D) Hedging costs premium or margin.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Indian index options are:\nA) American style  B) European style, exercisable only at expiry  C) Perpetual  D) Never exercised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Indian index and stock options are **European**.\nA) Is the US convention for stock options. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor buys a call with strike ₹800 for a premium of ₹25. What is the break-even?\nA) ₹775  B) ₹825  C) ₹800  D) ₹25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Break-even = Strike + Premium = 800 + 25 = **₹825**.\nA) Is the **put** break-even formula. C) Ignores the premium. D) Is the premium alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Using Q36's option, what is the profit if the spot at expiry is ₹870?\nA) ₹70  B) ₹45  C) ₹95  D) −₹25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 870 − 800 = **₹70**. Profit = 70 − 25 = **₹45**.\nA) Is the intrinsic value, ignoring the premium paid. C) Adds the premium instead of subtracting. D) Is the loss if the option expires worthless.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Using Q36's option, what is the outcome if the spot at expiry is ₹790?\nA) Profit ₹10  B) Loss of the full ₹25 premium — the option expires worthless  C) Loss ₹10  D) Break-even\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹790 the call is **out of the money** (spot < strike), so intrinsic = 0 and the entire **₹25 premium** is lost.\nA) There is no profit. C) The loss is the full premium, not ₹10. D) Break-even is ₹825.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor buys a put with strike ₹500 for ₹18. What is the break-even and the profit at a spot of ₹450?\nA) Break-even ₹518; profit ₹32  B) Break-even ₹482; profit ₹32  C) Break-even ₹482; profit ₹50  D) Break-even ₹500; profit ₹18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Break-even = Strike − Premium = 500 − 18 = **₹482**. At ₹450, intrinsic = 500 − 450 = **₹50**; profit = 50 − 18 = **₹32**.\nA) Uses the call break-even formula. C) Gives intrinsic value, not profit. D) Ignores the premium in both.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A call with strike ₹1,200 has a premium of ₹65 when spot is ₹1,230. What are its intrinsic and time values?\nA) Intrinsic ₹30; time value ₹35  B) Intrinsic ₹65; time value ₹0  C) Intrinsic ₹35; time value ₹30  D) Intrinsic ₹0; time value ₹65\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Intrinsic = max(1,230 − 1,200, 0) = **₹30**. Time value = premium − intrinsic = 65 − 30 = **₹35**.\nB) Would apply only at expiry. C) Swaps the two. D) The option is ITM, so intrinsic is not zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor writes (sells) a call with strike ₹950 for ₹30. The spot at expiry is ₹1,040. What is her result?\nA) Profit ₹30  B) Loss ₹60  C) Loss ₹90  D) Profit ₹90\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The buyer exercises: the writer pays intrinsic = 1,040 − 950 = **₹90**, but keeps the **₹30 premium**. Net = 30 − 90 = **−₹60**.\nA) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. C) Forgets the premium received. D) Gets the direction wrong — the writer **loses** when the price rises above the strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A farmer expecting to harvest wheat in 3 months wants to fix his selling price. He should:\nA) Buy wheat futures  B) Sell wheat futures  C) Buy a call option  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He is **long the physical crop**, so he **sells futures** to lock in a price and offset a fall.\nA) Buying futures would **double** his exposure to falling prices. C) A call profits from rising prices — the wrong direction for his risk. D) Leaves him fully exposed.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An importer must pay USD 1,00,000 in 3 months and fears the rupee weakening. He should:\nA) Sell dollar futures  B) Buy dollar futures to fix his cost  C) Buy rupee puts  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He is **short dollars** (he must buy them later), so **buying dollar futures** fixes the rate.\nA) Selling would worsen the exposure. C) Confuses the direction. D) Leaves him exposed to rupee depreciation.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which position carries theoretically unlimited loss?\nA) Long call  B) Long put  C) Naked short call  D) Buying a stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The underlying can rise without limit, so the writer's obligation is unbounded.\nA) and B) cap the loss at the premium. D) A stock's loss is capped at the amount invested (price can fall only to zero).\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor holds shares worth ₹10,00,000 and fears a market fall but does not want to sell. The appropriate strategy is:\nA) Sell a call  B) Buy index puts (a protective put)  C) Buy more shares  D) Sell the shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **protective put** insures the downside while keeping the shares and the upside.\nA) A covered call earns premium but provides only limited downside cushion. C) Increases the exposure. D) Contradicts the stated wish not to sell.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Spot is ₹500, the risk-free rate 8% and there are 6 months to expiry with no dividend. What is the approximate fair futures price?\nA) ₹480  B) ₹520  C) ₹540  D) ₹500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — F = S × (1 + r)^T = 500 × (1.08)^0.5 = 500 × **1.03923** = **₹519.62 ≈ ₹520**.\nA) Would imply backwardation with no reason. C) Applies a full year's interest at double the rate. D) Ignores the cost of carry. **Futures normally trade above spot — contango.**\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A trader observes futures trading well above their fair cost-of-carry value. The arbitrage is to:\nA) Buy futures and sell spot  B) Buy the spot and sell the futures  C) Buy both  D) Sell both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sell the **overpriced** leg (futures) and buy the **underpriced** leg (spot), locking in the difference risk-free.\nA) Reverses it, buying the expensive leg. C) and D) leave a directional position, not an arbitrage.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An investor sells a put with strike ₹600 for ₹22. The spot at expiry is ₹640. What is the result?\nA) Loss ₹40  B) Profit ₹22 — the put expires worthless  C) Profit ₹40  D) Loss ₹18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹640 the put is **out of the money** (spot > strike), so the buyer does not exercise and the writer **keeps the full ₹22 premium**.\nA) and D) would apply if the price had fallen below the strike. C) The maximum gain for a writer is the premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which combination correctly describes a covered call?\nA) Long stock + long call  B) Long stock + short call — premium income with capped upside  C) Short stock + short call  D) Long put + short put\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The shares \"cover\" the obligation if the call is exercised.\nA) Doubles the bullish exposure. C) and D) are different structures.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A trader expects a large move in a stock ahead of results but does not know the direction. The suitable strategy is:\nA) Buy a call only  B) A long straddle — buy a call and a put at the same strike  C) Sell a straddle  D) Buy the stock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **long straddle** profits from a big move **either way**.\nA) Profits only if the price rises. C) A **short** straddle profits from the price staying still — the opposite view. D) Profits only from a rise.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which statement about margin is correct?\nA) Only option buyers post margin  B) Option sellers and futures traders post margin; option buyers pay only the premium  C) Nobody posts margin  D) Margin equals the contract value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **buyer's loss is capped at the premium already paid**, so no further security is needed. **Sellers and futures traders** carry open-ended obligations and must post margin.\nA) Reverses it. C) Margin is fundamental to exchange-traded derivatives. D) Margin is a fraction of contract value — that is what creates leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An option is described as \"deep in the money with little time value\". This most likely means:\nA) It is far out of the money  B) The spot is far beyond the strike and expiry is near, so the premium is almost entirely intrinsic value  C) The premium is zero  D) It is a forward\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Deep ITM plus near expiry means **premium ≈ intrinsic value**.\nA) Contradicts \"in the money\". C) A deep ITM option has substantial premium. D) A forward is not an option.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A hedger complains that his hedge \"cost him money\" because prices moved favourably. The correct response is:\nA) The hedge failed  B) A hedge converts uncertainty into certainty — giving up a favourable move is the price of that protection, not a failure  C) He should never hedge  D) He should have speculated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Hedging is **insurance**, not a profit strategy. Forgoing the upside is exactly what buying certainty costs.\nA) The hedge worked as designed. C) Hedging remains appropriate where the exposure is genuine and unwanted. D) Speculation adds risk rather than removing it.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which is TRUE of a forward contract?\nA) It is exchange-traded  B) It is customised, OTC, and carries counterparty risk  C) It has daily MTM  D) It is guaranteed by a clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These three are the defining features of a forward.\nA), C) and D) all describe **futures**.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A call option with strike ₹700 is trading when spot is ₹680. The option is:\nA) In the money  B) Out of the money  C) At the money  D) Deep in the money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **call**, ITM requires **spot > strike**. Here 680 < 700, so it is **out of the money**.\nA) and D) require the spot above the strike. C) ATM requires spot = strike.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A put option with strike ₹700 when spot is ₹680 is:\nA) Out of the money  B) In the money, with intrinsic value ₹20  C) At the money  D) Worthless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **put**, ITM requires **spot < strike**. Intrinsic = 700 − 680 = **₹20**.\nA) Reverses the put rule. C) ATM requires equality. D) An ITM option has positive value. **Note the same spot/strike pair makes the call OTM and the put ITM — they are mirror images.**\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Leverage in derivatives arises because:\nA) Contracts are free  B) A small margin controls a much larger exposure  C) There is no risk  D) Premiums are refunded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Paying a fraction of the contract value magnifies both gains and losses.\nA) Margin and premium are real costs. C) Leverage increases risk. D) Premiums are not refundable.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which participant keeps futures and spot prices aligned?\nA) The hedger  B) The arbitrageur  C) The speculator  D) The regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrageurs exploit — and thereby **eliminate** — any deviation from fair value.\nA) Hedgers transfer risk. C) Speculators supply liquidity and absorb risk. D) Regulators set rules but do not price contracts.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor buys a call for ₹50 with strike ₹2,000. At expiry the spot is ₹2,030. What is the outcome?\nA) Profit ₹30  B) Loss ₹20 — the option is ITM but the premium is not recovered  C) Profit ₹80  D) Loss ₹50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 2,030 − 2,000 = **₹30**; profit = 30 − 50 = **−₹20**. She should still exercise (recovering ₹30 beats abandoning it for nothing), but the trade loses money.\nA) Ignores the premium. C) Adds instead of subtracting. D) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. **ITM does not mean profitable.**\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which risk is eliminated by trading futures on an exchange rather than forwards OTC?\nA) Market risk  B) Counterparty (default) risk  C) Basis risk  D) Leverage risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **CCP** guarantees performance.\nA) Price movements affect both equally. C) Basis risk arises from imperfect hedging, not venue. D) Leverage exists in both.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An interest rate swap would be used by a company that:\nA) Wants to buy shares  B) Has floating-rate borrowing and wants the certainty of fixed payments  C) Wants to sell a bond  D) Needs a loan\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The company **pays fixed and receives floating**, converting its floating liability into a fixed one.\nA), C) and D) do not involve swaps.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A covered call writer's position when the stock rises sharply above the strike is:\nA) Unlimited profit  B) Profit capped at the strike plus the premium received — the upside beyond the strike is forgone  C) Unlimited loss  D) No effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The shares are effectively sold at the strike, so gains beyond it are given up. The premium partly compensates.\nA) The short call caps the upside. C) The stock holding **covers** the obligation, so there is no unlimited loss. D) The position is materially affected.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which of these is NOT a benefit of derivatives?\nA) Risk transfer  B) Price discovery  C) Elimination of all investment risk  D) Lower transaction costs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Derivatives **transfer and reshape** risk; they never eliminate it from the system.\nA), B) and D) are genuine benefits.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A trader sells a naked call on a stock at strike ₹1,500 for ₹40. The stock is acquired at ₹2,200. What is the loss?\nA) ₹40 profit  B) Loss ₹660  C) Loss ₹700  D) Loss ₹2,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic at ₹2,200 = 2,200 − 1,500 = **₹700**. The writer keeps the ₹40 premium, so net loss = 700 − 40 = **₹660**.\nA) Would apply only if the option expired worthless. C) Ignores the premium received. D) Is the stock price, not the loss. **This illustrates why naked call writing is so dangerous.**\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Which statement about open interest is correct?\nA) It measures the premium  B) It is the total number of contracts outstanding, indicating participation  C) It is the strike price  D) It equals volume\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Open interest counts **positions still open**, unlike volume which counts trades in a period.\nA) and C) are different concepts. D) Volume and open interest are distinct measures.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A stock trades at ₹1,000 with a 3-month future at ₹975. This is:\nA) Contango  B) Backwardation — the futures price is below spot  C) Impossible  D) Arbitrage-free by definition\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Futures **below** spot is **backwardation**, which can arise from high dividend expectations or supply/demand pressures.\nA) Contango is futures **above** spot. C) It occurs regularly. D) It may or may not present an arbitrage, depending on the carry.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A protective put is best described as:\nA) A speculative bet  B) Portfolio insurance — paying a premium to cap the downside while keeping the upside  C) A way to earn income  D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structurally identical to an insurance policy: a premium buys protection against a loss.\nA) It reduces rather than adds risk. C) A **covered call** earns income; a protective put **costs** premium. D) There is no risk-free profit involved.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which pair correctly matches strategy to view?\nA) Long straddle → expects no movement  B) Covered call → neutral to mildly bullish; long straddle → expects a large move either way  C) Protective put → bearish only  D) Short call → bullish\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both pairings are correct.\nA) A **long** straddle needs a big move; a **short** straddle wants stillness. C) A protective put is held by someone **bullish** who wants insurance. D) A short call is neutral-to-**bearish**.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A speculator with ₹1,00,000 takes a futures position worth ₹10,00,000. The underlying falls 12%. What happens?\nA) She loses ₹12,000  B) She loses ₹1,20,000 — more than her entire ₹1,00,000 deposit  C) She loses nothing  D) She gains\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The loss is on the **full contract value**: 12% × ₹10,00,000 = **₹1,20,000**, which exceeds the ₹1,00,000 margin. She faces a margin call for the shortfall.\nA) Applies 12% to the margin rather than the exposure. C) The position is fully exposed. D) A fall hurts a long position. **This is the defining derivative danger.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which of these correctly states the Indian equity derivatives convention?\nA) American options, weekly expiry, physically settled indices  B) European options, monthly expiry, cash-settled indices and physically settled stock derivatives  C) American options, no expiry  D) European options, physically settled indices\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three elements are correct for the Indian market.\nA) and C) misstate the exercise style. D) Index derivatives are **cash-settled** — you cannot deliver an index.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** An investor buys a call, strike ₹1,500, premium ₹60, lot size 500. At expiry the spot is ₹1,610. What is the net profit on the lot?\nA) ₹55,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹30,000  D) −₹30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic per share = 1,610 − 1,500 = **₹110**. Net per share = 110 − 60 = **₹50**. On a lot of 500: 50 × 500 = **₹25,000**.\nA) Applies intrinsic without deducting the premium (110 × 500). C) Uses the premium alone (60 × 500). D) Wrong direction. **Break-even was ₹1,560; the spot exceeded it by ₹50.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using Q71's contract, what would the result be if the spot at expiry were ₹1,530?\nA) Profit ₹15,000  B) Loss ₹15,000 — the option is ITM by ₹30 but cost ₹60  C) Loss ₹30,000  D) Break-even\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = ₹30; net per share = 30 − 60 = **−₹30**; on 500 shares = **−₹15,000**. She still exercises (recovering ₹15,000 beats abandoning it), but the trade loses.\nA) Wrong direction. C) Would be the loss if it expired fully worthless (60 × 500 = ₹30,000). D) Break-even is ₹1,560.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** An investor writes a put, strike ₹800, premium ₹35, lot 1,000. The spot at expiry is ₹720. What is the result?\nA) Profit ₹35,000  B) Loss ₹45,000  C) Loss ₹80,000  D) Profit ₹45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The put is **ITM** (spot < strike), so the buyer exercises. Intrinsic = 800 − 720 = **₹80** per share. The writer keeps ₹35, so net = 35 − 80 = **−₹45** per share; on 1,000 = **−₹45,000**.\nA) Would apply only if the put expired worthless. C) Ignores the premium received. D) Wrong direction — the writer of a put **loses when the price falls**.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A stock trades at ₹2,000. A 3-month future trades at ₹2,050, the risk-free rate is 8% and no dividend is expected. Is there an arbitrage?\nA) No, the future is fairly priced  B) Yes — fair value is about ₹2,039, so the future is overpriced; buy spot and sell the future  C) Yes — buy the future and sell spot  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fair value = 2,000 × (1.08)^0.25 = 2,000 × **1.01943** = **₹2,038.86**. The future at ₹2,050 is **overpriced by about ₹11**, so the arbitrage is to **buy the cheap leg (spot) and sell the expensive leg (future)**.\nA) There is a gap beyond the fair carry. C) Reverses the legs, buying the expensive one. D) With S, r and T given it is computable.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An investor holds 2,000 shares at ₹950 and buys 2,000 puts at strike ₹900 for a premium of ₹25. The stock falls to ₹780. What is the net position value?\nA) ₹15,60,000  B) ₹17,50,000  C) ₹19,00,000  D) ₹18,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Shares are now worth 2,000 × ₹780 = **₹15,60,000**. The puts are ITM by 900 − 780 = **₹120**, worth 2,000 × 120 = **₹2,40,000**. Premium paid = 2,000 × 25 = **₹50,000**. Net = 15,60,000 + 2,40,000 − 50,000 = **₹17,50,000**.\nA) Counts only the shares, ignoring the insurance payout. C) Is the original value (2,000 × 950 = ₹19,00,000). D) Miscomputes the put payoff. **Unhedged, the position would be worth just ₹15,60,000 — so the protective put preserved ₹1,90,000 net of premium.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A trader sells a covered call: holds 500 shares bought at ₹600, sells calls at strike ₹650 for ₹20. At expiry the spot is ₹720. What is the total profit?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹35,000  C) ₹85,000  D) ₹10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The call is exercised, so shares are effectively sold at the **strike of ₹650**. Gain on shares = (650 − 600) × 500 = **₹25,000**. Premium retained = 20 × 500 = **₹10,000**. Total = **₹35,000**.\nA) Is the gain if he had simply held the shares to ₹720 ((720 − 600) × 500 = ₹60,000). C) Adds both incorrectly. D) Counts the premium alone. **The covered call earned ₹10,000 of premium but gave up ₹35,000 of upside above ₹650 — the trade-off in one number.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Which position would you take to profit if a stock stays exactly where it is?\nA) Long straddle  B) Short straddle — selling a call and a put, collecting both premiums as they decay  C) Long call  D) Long put\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **short straddle** profits when the underlying stays near the strike and both options decay to zero. ⚠️ Note it carries **unlimited risk** from the short call leg.\nA) A long straddle needs a **big move**. C) and D) are directional positions.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor buys a straddle: call and put both at strike ₹1,000, premiums ₹45 and ₹38. What are the two break-even points?\nA) ₹1,045 and ₹962  B) ₹1,083 and ₹917  C) ₹1,000 and ₹1,083  D) ₹955 and ₹1,045\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total premium = 45 + 38 = **₹83**. **Upper break-even = Strike + total premium = 1,000 + 83 = ₹1,083.** **Lower break-even = Strike − total premium = 1,000 − 83 = ₹917.**\nA) Uses each premium separately rather than the combined cost. C) Omits the lower break-even. D) Uses the wrong premium. **The stock must move more than ₹83 in either direction for the straddle to profit.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A speculator deposits ₹2,00,000 margin for a futures position worth ₹16,00,000. The underlying rises 5%. What is the return on her margin?\nA) 5%  B) 40% — the ₹80,000 gain on a ₹2,00,000 deposit  C) 8%  D) 12.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gain = 5% × ₹16,00,000 = **₹80,000**. Return on margin = 80,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **40%**.\nA) Is the move in the underlying, not the leveraged return. C) and D) do not follow. **The leverage is 8× (₹16,00,000 ÷ ₹2,00,000), so a 5% move becomes 40% — and a 5% adverse move would lose 40% of her capital.**\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which sequence correctly ranks these by maximum possible loss, largest first?\nA) Long call, long put, naked short call  B) Naked short call (unlimited), short put (large, capped at strike × lot), long call (premium only)  C) Long put, long call, short call  D) All identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **naked short call is unbounded**; the **short put** is large but capped (the underlying can only fall to zero); the **long call** risks only the premium.\nA) Places the unlimited-loss position last. C) Inverts the ranking. D) The exposures differ enormously.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A company with a ₹50 crore floating-rate loan enters a swap to pay fixed at 9% and receive floating. Rates then rise to 12%. What is the outcome?\nA) The company loses  B) The company benefits — it pays 9% fixed while receiving 12% floating, which offsets its higher loan cost  C) No effect  D) The swap is cancelled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Its loan cost rose to 12%, but the swap **receives** 12% floating and **pays** 9% fixed, so its **net cost stays around 9%** — exactly the certainty the swap was bought for.\nA) The swap protected it. C) The swap has a clear effect. D) Swaps do not auto-cancel. **Had rates fallen, the company would have been locked into 9% while the market paid less — the symmetrical cost of certainty.**\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An option premium is ₹85 with intrinsic value ₹85. What can you conclude?\nA) The option is OTM  B) Time value is zero, so the option is at or very near expiry  C) The option is worthless  D) The premium is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Time value = premium − intrinsic = 0**, which happens **at expiry** (or for a deep-ITM option with negligible time remaining).\nA) An OTM option has **zero** intrinsic value. C) It has ₹85 of value. D) The pricing is internally consistent.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A hedger sells futures against a physical holding but the futures contract's underlying is a broad index while his holding is a single stock. What risk remains?\nA) None  B) Basis risk — the stock and the index will not move identically, so the hedge is imperfect  C) Counterparty risk  D) Credit risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **cross-hedge** leaves **basis risk**: company-specific moves are not offset by an index hedge.\nA) The hedge is imperfect. C) Exchange-traded futures eliminate counterparty risk. D) No credit exposure arises here.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which statement about option buyers and sellers is MOST accurate?\nA) Buyers face greater risk than sellers  B) Sellers face far greater risk — a buyer's loss is capped at the premium while a naked seller's can be unlimited — which is why only sellers post margin  C) Both face identical risk  D) Neither faces risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The asymmetry of rights and obligations produces an asymmetry of risk, and the margin regime reflects it exactly.\nA) Reverses the reality. C) Their exposures are fundamentally different. D) Both face risk, but of very different magnitudes.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A trader observes: spot ₹400, 1-month future ₹396, no dividends, risk-free rate 7%. What is the situation?\nA) Normal contango  B) Backwardation — the future is below spot, and below fair value of about ₹402, suggesting either an arbitrage or expected dividends/supply factors  C) Fair value  D) Impossible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fair value = 400 × (1.07)^(1/12) = 400 × **1.00565** = **₹402.26**. The future at ₹396 is in **backwardation** and below fair value.\nA) Contango means the future trades **above** spot. C) It is ₹6 below fair value. D) Backwardation occurs regularly, often when a dividend is expected or in tight physical markets.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** An investor writes a naked put on a stock at strike ₹1,200 for ₹50 with lot size 400. The company collapses and the stock falls to ₹150. What is the loss?\nA) ₹20,000 profit  B) Loss ₹4,00,000  C) Loss ₹4,80,000  D) Loss ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Intrinsic = 1,200 − 150 = **₹1,050** per share. Net per share = 50 − 1,050 = **−₹1,000**. On 400 shares = **−₹4,00,000**.\nA) Would apply only if the put expired worthless. C) Ignores the ₹50 premium received (1,050 × 400 = ₹4,20,000; and ₹4,80,000 corresponds to the full strike value). D) Understates it by a factor of nearly seven. **The premium of ₹20,000 collected proved trivial against a ₹4 lakh loss — the asymmetry of option writing.**\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Which combination of features belongs to a FORWARD and not a future?\nA) Standardised, exchange-traded, CCP-guaranteed  B) Customised terms, OTC, counterparty risk, settled only at maturity  C) Daily mark to market  D) Mandatory margin\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four are forward characteristics.\nA), C) and D) all describe **futures**.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A portfolio manager holds ₹5 crore of equity and buys index puts costing 1.5% of portfolio value. The market falls 20%. Roughly what has she achieved?\nA) Nothing  B) She has paid ₹7.5 lakh for insurance that offsets much of the ₹1 crore paper loss — converting an uncertain outcome into a bounded one  C) A profit of ₹1 crore  D) A loss of ₹1.075 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Premium = 1.5% × ₹5 crore = **₹7.5 lakh**. A 20% fall would cost **₹1 crore** unhedged; the puts offset much of that (the exact amount depends on the strike). The **cost of the insurance is known in advance; the loss avoided is not**.\nA) The hedge clearly worked. C) A protective put does not turn a falling market into a profit. D) Ignores the put payoff entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which is the correct interpretation of \"the futures price converges to spot at expiry\"?\nA) It is a coincidence  B) Arbitrage enforces it — at expiry the contract IS the underlying, so any gap could be captured risk-free  C) Regulators set it  D) It only applies to commodities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At expiry the time to carry is zero, so cost of carry vanishes and **F = S**. Any deviation is an instant riskless profit and is arbitraged away.\nA) It is structural, not coincidental. C) It is a market mechanism, not a rule. D) It applies to all futures.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A trader buys a call for ₹30 and simultaneously sells a higher-strike call for ₹12 (a bull call spread). What are the net cost and the maximum loss?\nA) Net cost ₹42; max loss ₹42  B) Net cost ₹18; max loss ₹18  C) Net cost ₹18; max loss unlimited  D) Net cost ₹12; max loss ₹30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net premium paid = 30 − 12 = **₹18**, which is also the **maximum loss** (if both expire worthless).\nA) Adds the premiums instead of netting them. C) The **long call caps the short call's risk**, so the loss is bounded. D) Miscomputes the net. **A spread reduces both the cost and the maximum gain compared with a naked long call.**\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An investor is told that \"selling options is like earning rent — you collect premium every month\". What is the flaw?\nA) There is none  B) The premium is small and capped, while the potential loss is large or unlimited — one adverse move can erase many months of collected premium  C) Options cannot be sold  D) Premiums are refunded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Option selling has a **positive-most-of-the-time, catastrophic-occasionally** payoff. Q86 illustrated it: ₹20,000 of premium against a ₹4 lakh loss.\nA) The asymmetry is fundamental. C) Selling options is routine. D) Premiums are not refunded — that is precisely the seller's income.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which set of actions is consistent for a jeweller who has agreed to deliver gold jewellery in 3 months at a fixed rupee price?\nA) Sell gold futures  B) Buy gold futures — he must acquire gold later, so he is effectively short gold and locks in the purchase cost  C) Buy gold puts  D) Do nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He has committed to a **selling price** but has **not yet bought** the gold, so a price **rise** hurts him. **Buying futures** fixes his input cost.\nA) Selling futures would suit someone who **owns** gold. C) Puts protect against a **fall**, which is not his risk. D) Leaves him exposed to rising gold prices.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A stock is at ₹1,000. Calls: strike 950 premium ₹80; strike 1,000 premium ₹45; strike 1,050 premium ₹22. Which has the highest intrinsic value and which the highest time value?\nA) Highest intrinsic: 1,050 strike; highest time value: 950 strike  B) Highest intrinsic: 950 strike (₹50); highest time value: 1,000 strike (₹45)  C) All have the same intrinsic value  D) Highest intrinsic: 1,000 strike\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Intrinsic values:** 950 strike = max(1,000 − 950, 0) = **₹50**; 1,000 strike = **₹0** (ATM); 1,050 strike = **₹0** (OTM). **Time values:** 80 − 50 = **₹30**; 45 − 0 = **₹45**; 22 − 0 = **₹22**.\nA) Reverses both. C) They differ. D) The ATM option has zero intrinsic value. **Time value peaks at the money — a classic exam point.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Which statement about leverage risk is MOST accurate?\nA) Leverage only magnifies gains  B) Leverage magnifies both gains and losses, and losses can exceed the margin deposited, with margin calls demanded in cash immediately  C) Leverage eliminates risk  D) Leverage applies only to options\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three elements matter: **symmetry**, **loss beyond capital**, and the **immediacy of the cash demand** through daily MTM.\nA) Losses are magnified equally. C) It increases risk. D) Futures are the most leveraged instruments of all.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An arbitrageur finds a mispricing and executes the trade. What happens to the mispricing?\nA) It widens  B) It narrows and eventually disappears, because the arbitrageur's own buying and selling pushes the two prices together  C) It stays constant  D) It becomes permanent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Arbitrage is **self-eliminating**: buying the cheap leg raises its price and selling the expensive leg lowers its price.\nA) The trade pushes prices together. C) and D) would imply markets never correct — the opposite of what arbitrage does.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A retail investor asks whether he should sell naked options to generate income on a ₹5 lakh portfolio. The best advice is:\nA) Yes — it is easy income  B) No — naked option writing carries unlimited or very large loss potential and margin obligations far beyond a ₹5 lakh portfolio's capacity to absorb  C) Yes, if he sells only calls  D) Yes, with no margin needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The risk is **structurally unsuited** to a small portfolio: one adverse move can exceed the entire capital, and margin calls must be met in cash the same day.\nA) The \"easy income\" framing hides the tail risk. C) **Naked calls are the most dangerous** of all — unlimited loss. D) Writers must post margin precisely because of this exposure.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which correctly matches participant to function in the derivatives ecosystem?\nA) Hedgers absorb risk; speculators transfer it  B) Hedgers transfer risk; speculators absorb it and supply liquidity; arbitrageurs keep prices correct  C) All three speculate  D) Only arbitrageurs matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Each plays a distinct and necessary role.\nA) Reverses hedgers and speculators. C) Hedgers reduce risk; arbitrageurs take none. D) Without speculators there would be no counterparty for hedgers.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An option's premium is ₹120, of which ₹90 is intrinsic. Expiry is 2 months away. What happens to the premium if the spot is unchanged at expiry?\nA) It rises to ₹150  B) It falls to about ₹90 as the ₹30 of time value decays to zero  C) It stays ₹120  D) It falls to zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Time value decays to zero at expiry**, leaving only intrinsic value: **₹90**.\nA) Premiums do not rise with an unchanged spot. C) Time decay is certain. D) The option retains its ₹90 intrinsic value. **Time decay is the option buyer's persistent headwind and the seller's tailwind.**\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client wants exposure to a stock's upside with a strictly limited maximum loss. Which position fits?\nA) A futures long  B) A long call — the maximum loss is the premium, while the upside is unlimited  C) A naked short put  D) A covered call\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **long call** is the only one of the four with a **capped, known loss** and uncapped upside.\nA) A futures long has losses limited only by the price falling to zero, and can exceed the margin. C) A short put has large downside and capped upside. D) A covered call caps the upside.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which two questions should always be asked first in an option problem?\nA) What is the strike and what is the expiry?  B) Is it a call or a put, and am I the buyer or the seller — those two answers fix the rights, obligations, maximum gain and maximum loss  C) What is the lot size?  D) Who is the broker?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Those two answers determine **everything structural** about the position; strike, premium and lot size then supply the arithmetic.\nA) Necessary for computation but not for identifying the position's nature. C) Affects scale only. D) Irrelevant to the payoff.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** For each position, first identify call/put and buyer/seller.\n\n### Case: Ms Verma's Derivative Positions\n\nMs Verma holds **4,000 shares** of Zenith Ltd, bought at **₹850**. The stock now trades at **₹1,000**. She is worried about a correction over the next month but does not want to sell.\n\nHer broker suggests three alternatives (lot size **1,000 shares**, one-month expiry):\n\n1. **Buy 4 put contracts**, strike **₹960**, premium **₹28**\n2. **Sell 4 call contracts**, strike **₹1,060**, premium **₹22**\n3. **Do nothing**\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is Ms Verma's current unrealised gain, and what is her risk?\nA) Gain ₹6,00,000; her risk is that the stock falls, eroding the gain  B) Gain ₹1,50,000; no risk  C) Gain ₹4,00,000  D) No gain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Gain = (1,000 − 850) × 4,000 shares = ₹150 × 4,000 = **₹6,00,000** unrealised. Her risk is straightforward: she is **long 4,000 shares**, so a fall erodes that gain and eventually her capital.\nB) ₹1,50,000 uses only 1,000 shares. C) Uses a ₹100 gain. D) The stock has risen ₹150 from her cost.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Under Option 1 (protective put), what does she pay, and what is her worst-case position value at expiry?\nA) Pays ₹1,12,000; floor value about ₹38,40,000 − ₹1,12,000 = ₹37,28,000  B) Pays ₹28,000; no floor  C) Pays ₹1,12,000; no protection  D) Pays nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Premium** = ₹28 × 4,000 shares = **₹1,12,000**. The puts guarantee she can sell at **₹960**, so her floor on the shares is 960 × 4,000 = **₹38,40,000**. Net of premium, her worst case is about **₹37,28,000** — regardless of how far the stock falls.\nB) ₹28,000 counts only one lot. C) The whole point of the put is protection. D) Options are not free. **She has bought insurance with a known cost and a known floor.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Under Option 2 (covered call), what does she receive, and what happens if the stock rises to ₹1,150?\nA) Receives ₹88,000; shares effectively sold at ₹1,060, so she forgoes the gain above that strike  B) Receives ₹22,000; unlimited upside retained  C) Receives ₹88,000; unlimited upside retained  D) Receives nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Premium received** = ₹22 × 4,000 = **₹88,000**. If the stock reaches ₹1,150 the calls are exercised, so her shares are effectively sold at **₹1,060**. She forgoes the ₹90 per share above the strike — **₹3,60,000** of upside — partly offset by the ₹88,000 premium.\nB) ₹22,000 counts one lot. C) A short call **caps** the upside; that is its defining feature. D) The premium is real income. **The covered call earns income but is the wrong tool if she genuinely fears a fall — it provides only ₹22 per share of cushion.**\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The stock falls to ₹880 at expiry. Compare the outcomes of all three options.\nA) All three are identical  B) Protective put ≈ ₹37,28,000; covered call ≈ ₹36,08,000; do nothing = ₹35,20,000  C) The covered call is best  D) Doing nothing is best\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Do nothing:** 880 × 4,000 = **₹35,20,000**. **Protective put:** the put is ITM by 960 − 880 = ₹80, worth ₹3,20,000; so 35,20,000 + 3,20,000 − 1,12,000 premium = **₹37,28,000**. **Covered call:** the call expires worthless (880 < 1,060), so she keeps the ₹88,000 premium: 35,20,000 + 88,000 = **₹36,08,000**.\nA) They differ by over ₹2 lakh. C) The covered call is better than nothing but ₹1.2 lakh worse than the put. D) Doing nothing is the worst outcome in a falling market. **The protective put wins precisely because that is the scenario it was bought for.**\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** The stock instead rises to ₹1,120. Which strategy performs best, and what is the general lesson?\nA) The covered call  B) Doing nothing (₹44,80,000), then the protective put (₹43,68,000), then the covered call (₹43,28,000) — protection always costs something when the feared event does not occur  C) The protective put  D) All identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Do nothing:** 1,120 × 4,000 = **₹44,80,000**. **Protective put:** the put expires worthless, so 44,80,000 − 1,12,000 = **₹43,68,000**. **Covered call:** shares called away at ₹1,060 → 1,060 × 4,000 = 42,40,000, plus the ₹88,000 premium = **₹43,28,000**.\nA) The covered call is the **worst** here, because the upside above ₹1,060 is surrendered. C) The put costs its premium for protection that proved unnecessary. D) They differ by over ₹1.5 lakh.\n\n> ⚠️ **The lesson this caselet is built to teach:** there is **no strategy that wins in every scenario**. The protective put wins when the market falls and costs a known premium when it rises; the covered call earns income in flat markets and surrenders the upside in strong ones; doing nothing wins in a rally and is worst in a fall. **Choose based on which risk you actually want to eliminate — and accept that certainty always has a price.** Given Ms Verma explicitly wants downside protection while keeping the shares, **Option 1 (the protective put) matches her stated objective.**\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "11", "title": "Mutual Funds", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "# Chapter 11: Mutual Funds — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio (23 marks across Chapters 11–13).**\n> ⭐ The **NAV formula**, **cut-off timing**, **scheme categories** and **systematic transactions (SIP/SWP/STP)** are the reliable mark-earners.\n\n---\n\n## 11.1 What a Mutual Fund Is\n\n**A mutual fund pools money from many investors and invests it, according to a stated objective, in a portfolio managed by professionals. Investors hold UNITS and share the gains and losses in proportion to their holding.**\n\n### Key features\n\n| Feature | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Pooling** | Small amounts aggregate into a large, efficiently managed corpus |\n| **Professional management** | A qualified fund manager makes the decisions |\n| **Diversification** | Even ₹500 buys a slice of dozens of securities |\n| **Liquidity** | Open-ended schemes redeem on any business day |\n| **Transparency** | **Daily NAV**, monthly portfolio disclosure, scheme documents |\n| **Regulation** | SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, **1996** |\n| **Economies of scale** | Lower per-unit transaction and research costs |\n| **Variety** | Schemes for every objective and horizon |\n\n### The three-tier structure\n\n**Sponsor → Trust (Trustees) → AMC**\n\n| Entity | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Sponsor** | Promotes the fund; must contribute at least **40% of the AMC's net worth** |\n| **Trust / Trustees** | Hold the assets **in trust for unit-holders**; oversee the AMC. At least **two-thirds independent** trustees |\n| **AMC (Asset Management Company)** | Manages the investments for a fee. Minimum net worth **₹50 crore** |\n| **Custodian** | Holds the securities |\n| **RTA** | Maintains investor records (CAMS, KFin) |\n\n> 🧠 **Why the trust structure matters:** the scheme's assets belong to the **unit-holders through the trust**, *not* to the AMC. If the AMC fails, the scheme's assets are ring-fenced — a fundamental investor protection.\n\n---\n\n## 11.2 ⭐ Key Concepts and Terms\n\n### Net Asset Value\n\n$$\\boxed{NAV = \\frac{\\text{Total assets} - \\text{Total liabilities}}{\\text{Number of units outstanding}}}$$\n\nOr equivalently, **Net Assets ÷ Units outstanding**.\n\n**Example:** a scheme holds securities worth ₹520 crore, cash ₹15 crore, and owes ₹7 crore in expenses. Units outstanding: 25 crore.\nNAV = (520 + 15 − 7) ÷ 25 = 528 ÷ 25 = **₹21.12**\n\n> ⚠️ **A low NAV is NOT \"cheap\".** A ₹10 NAV fund and a ₹500 NAV fund can hold identical portfolios; only the unit count differs. **Returns depend on the portfolio, never on the NAV level.** This is the single most common retail misconception.\n\n### Other terms\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Investment objective** | What the scheme aims to achieve — stated in the SID; determines the category |\n| **Units** | The investor's proportionate share of the scheme |\n| **Net assets** | Total assets minus liabilities |\n| **Mark to market** | Securities valued at **current market prices** daily, so the NAV reflects reality |\n| **Total Expense Ratio (TER)** | Annual costs as a % of average net assets — **capped by SEBI** and already deducted from NAV |\n| **Exit load** | A charge on redemption within a specified period, to discourage short-term churn |\n| **AUM** | Assets Under Management |\n\n### ⭐ Cut-off timing\n\nThe NAV you receive depends on when the application **and the money** reach the fund.\n\n| Scheme type | Cut-off | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liquid and overnight funds** — purchase | **1:30 p.m.** | **Previous day's NAV** if funds are realised before cut-off |\n| **All other schemes** — purchase | **3:00 p.m.** | Same day's NAV |\n| **Redemption (all schemes)** | **3:00 p.m.** | Same day's NAV |\n\n> ⚠️ **The decisive rule:** for **purchases of any amount**, the applicable NAV depends on **funds being realised (credited to the scheme) before the cut-off**, not merely on when the application was submitted. Submitting at 2 p.m. with money arriving next morning gets the **next day's** NAV.\n\n---\n\n## 11.3 Scheme Structures\n\n| | **Open-ended** | **Close-ended** | **Interval** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Subscription/redemption** | Any business day, with the AMC | Only at NFO; then traded on an exchange | Only during specified **transaction periods** |\n| **Corpus** | Variable | Fixed | Fixed between intervals |\n| **Liquidity** | High | Via the exchange, often at a discount to NAV | Only at intervals |\n| **Listing** | Not required | **Mandatory** | Mandatory |\n\n### Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs)\n- Track an **index or commodity**; bought and sold **on the exchange** like a share, at market price.\n- Require a **demat account**.\n- **Lower TER** than active funds; typically passive.\n- Large investors transact directly with the AMC in **creation units**; arbitrage keeps the market price close to NAV.\n\n---\n\n## 11.4 Regulatory Framework\n\nGoverned by the **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**.\n\n**Key documents:**\n- **SID (Scheme Information Document)** — scheme-specific: objective, strategy, risk, expenses\n- **SAI (Statement of Additional Information)** — about the fund house\n- **KIM (Key Information Memorandum)** — the abridged summary accompanying the application form\n\n**Investor service standards:** dispatch of account statements, redemption proceeds within prescribed timelines, mandatory **Riskometer** disclosure, and monthly portfolio disclosure.\n\n**Other protections:** investment restrictions (single issuer and sector caps), mandatory **NAV computation and publication daily**, and segregation of assets under the trust.\n\n---\n\n## 11.5 ⭐ Mutual Fund Products (SEBI Categorisation)\n\nSEBI's 2017 categorisation standardised scheme types so that funds are comparable.\n\n### 11.5.1 Equity schemes (minimum 65% in equity)\n\n| Category | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| **Large cap** | ≥80% in the top 100 companies |\n| **Mid cap** | ≥65% in companies ranked 101–250 |\n| **Small cap** | ≥65% in companies ranked 251 onwards |\n| **Large & mid cap** | ≥35% each in large and mid |\n| **Multi cap** | ≥25% each in large, mid and small |\n| **Flexi cap** | ≥65% equity, **no cap-size restriction** |\n| **ELSS** | ≥80% equity; **3-year lock-in**; Section 80C benefit |\n| **Sectoral/thematic** | ≥80% in a sector or theme |\n| **Focused** | Maximum **30 stocks** |\n| **Value / Contra** | ≥65% following that strategy |\n| **Dividend yield** | ≥65% in dividend-yielding stocks |\n\n### 11.5.2 Debt schemes (16 categories, defined by duration or credit)\n\n| Category | Duration / feature |\n|---|---|\n| **Overnight** | 1 day |\n| **Liquid** | Up to **91 days** |\n| **Ultra short** | 3–6 months (Macaulay duration) |\n| **Low duration** | 6–12 months |\n| **Money market** | Up to 1 year |\n| **Short duration** | 1–3 years |\n| **Medium duration** | 3–4 years |\n| **Medium to long** | 4–7 years |\n| **Long duration** | Over 7 years |\n| **Dynamic bond** | Duration managed actively |\n| **Corporate bond** | ≥80% in **AA+ and above** |\n| **Credit risk** | ≥65% in **below AA+** — higher yield, higher risk |\n| **Banking & PSU** | ≥80% in bank/PSU/PFI debt |\n| **Gilt** | ≥80% in **government securities** — no credit risk, but full interest rate risk |\n| **Floater** | ≥65% in floating rate instruments |\n\n### 11.5.3 Hybrid schemes\n\n| Category | Allocation |\n|---|---|\n| **Conservative hybrid** | 10–25% equity, 75–90% debt |\n| **Balanced hybrid** | 40–60% equity, no arbitrage |\n| **Aggressive hybrid** | **65–80% equity**, 20–35% debt |\n| **Dynamic asset allocation / BAF** | Equity and debt **managed dynamically** (0–100%) |\n| **Multi asset allocation** | At least **three** asset classes, ≥10% each |\n| **Arbitrage** | ≥65% in arbitrage positions — **taxed as equity**, low risk |\n| **Equity savings** | Equity, arbitrage and debt |\n\n### 11.5.4 Solution-oriented schemes\n- **Retirement fund** — lock-in of **5 years or until retirement**, whichever is earlier\n- **Children's fund** — lock-in of **5 years or until the child turns 18**, whichever is earlier\n\n### 11.5.5 Other schemes\n- **Index funds / ETFs** — ≥95% in the securities of the tracked index\n- **Fund of Funds** — ≥95% in the underlying funds\n\n---\n\n## 11.6 Investment Options\n\n| Option | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Growth** | Gains stay invested; NAV rises; **no payouts** — the most tax-efficient for accumulation |\n| **IDCW — Payout** | *Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal* is paid out; **NAV falls by the amount distributed** |\n| **IDCW — Reinvestment** | The distribution buys fresh units at the ex-NAV |\n\n> ⚠️ **The \"dividend\" was renamed IDCW precisely because it is NOT extra income.** Part of any distribution is simply the investor's own capital returned, and the NAV drops correspondingly. It is taxable in the investor's hands at slab rates.\n\n---\n\n## 11.7 Triggers\nPre-set instructions that act automatically on a defined event — e.g. redeem when the NAV reaches a target, or switch when a value threshold is crossed. They enforce discipline and remove emotion.\n\n---\n\n## 11.8 Transactions\n\n| Transaction | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Fresh purchase** | First investment in a scheme (a new folio) |\n| **Additional purchase** | Further investment in an existing folio |\n| **Redemption** | Selling units back to the fund (open-ended) |\n| **Switch** | Moving from one scheme to another within the same fund house — **treated as a redemption plus a purchase, so it is a taxable event** |\n| **IDCW reinvestment** | Distribution used to buy fresh units |\n\n---\n\n## 11.9 ⭐ Systematic Transactions\n\n| Plan | What it does | Best used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SIP — Systematic Investment Plan** | Invest a **fixed amount at fixed intervals** | Accumulation; **rupee-cost averaging** and discipline |\n| **SWP — Systematic Withdrawal Plan** | Withdraw a **fixed amount at fixed intervals** | Regular income in retirement; more tax-efficient than IDCW |\n| **STP — Systematic Transfer Plan** | Transfer periodically **from one scheme to another** (typically debt → equity) | Deploying a lump sum gradually |\n| **DTP — Dividend Transfer Plan** | Distributions from one scheme are invested into another | Channelling income into growth |\n| **VIP — Value Averaging Investment Plan** | Invest **more when markets fall, less when they rise**, to reach a target value | A more aggressive form of averaging |\n\n### How rupee-cost averaging works\nA fixed rupee amount buys **more units when the NAV is low** and **fewer when it is high**, so the **average cost per unit falls below the average NAV**.\n\n**Example:** ₹10,000 a month for 4 months at NAVs of ₹50, ₹40, ₹25, ₹50.\n- Units bought: 200 + 250 + 400 + 200 = **1,050**\n- Total invested = **₹40,000**; average cost = 40,000 ÷ 1,050 = **₹38.10**\n- Simple average NAV = (50 + 40 + 25 + 50) ÷ 4 = **₹41.25**\n\n**The average cost (₹38.10) is below the average NAV (₹41.25)** — that gap is the benefit of rupee-cost averaging.\n\n> ⚠️ SIPs **do not guarantee profits** and do not protect against a market that falls continuously. They manage **timing risk**, not market risk.\n\n---\n\n## 11.10 ⭐ Direct vs Regular Plans\n\n| | **Direct plan** | **Regular plan** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bought through** | The AMC / RTA / the fund's own platform | A distributor |\n| **Distribution commission** | **None** | Embedded in the TER |\n| **TER** | **Lower** | Higher |\n| **NAV** | **Higher** over time | Lower |\n| **Portfolio** | **Identical** | Identical |\n\n> 🧠 **The same portfolio, a lower cost.** A 0.75% annual TER difference compounds to a very large sum over 20 years — which is why an **Investment Adviser (who charges the client a fee) recommends direct plans**, while a distributor (paid by commission) cannot.\n\n**How an IA helps a client transact in direct plans:** guiding them through the AMC/RTA platforms, the MF Utilities and exchange platforms (BSE StAR MF, NSE MFSS), and helping with KYC, folio consolidation and execution-only transaction feeds.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Mutual fund | Pooled, professionally managed portfolio; investors hold **units** |\n| Structure | **Sponsor → Trust/Trustees → AMC** |\n| Sponsor contribution | At least **40% of AMC net worth** |\n| AMC minimum net worth | **₹50 crore** |\n| Independent trustees | At least **two-thirds** |\n| Why the trust matters | Assets belong to **unit-holders**, ring-fenced from the AMC |\n| NAV | (Total assets − liabilities) ÷ units outstanding |\n| Low NAV | **Not \"cheap\"** — returns depend on the portfolio |\n| Liquid fund purchase cut-off | **1:30 p.m.** |\n| Other schemes purchase cut-off | **3:00 p.m.** |\n| Redemption cut-off | **3:00 p.m.** |\n| The decisive NAV rule | **Funds must be realised** before cut-off |\n| Regulations | SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, **1996** |\n| Documents | **SID**, **SAI**, **KIM** |\n| Open-ended | Transact with the AMC any business day |\n| Close-ended | NFO only, then **listed** on an exchange |\n| ETF | Exchange-traded, needs a **demat account**, low TER |\n| Equity scheme minimum equity | **65%** |\n| Large cap | ≥80% in top 100 |\n| ELSS | ≥80% equity, **3-year lock-in**, 80C |\n| Focused fund | Maximum **30 stocks** |\n| Liquid fund | Up to **91 days** |\n| Gilt fund | ≥80% **G-secs** — no credit risk, full rate risk |\n| Credit risk fund | ≥65% **below AA+** |\n| Corporate bond fund | ≥80% **AA+ and above** |\n| Aggressive hybrid | **65–80% equity** |\n| Conservative hybrid | **10–25% equity** |\n| Arbitrage fund | ≥65% arbitrage; **taxed as equity** |\n| Multi asset | At least **three** asset classes, ≥10% each |\n| Retirement fund lock-in | **5 years or until retirement** |\n| Children's fund lock-in | **5 years or until the child is 18** |\n| Index fund | ≥95% in index securities |\n| Growth option | No payouts; most tax-efficient for accumulation |\n| IDCW | Distribution **reduces NAV**; taxed at slab rates |\n| Switch | **Redemption + purchase = a taxable event** |\n| SIP | Fixed amount, fixed interval — **rupee-cost averaging** |\n| SWP | Fixed withdrawal — retirement income |\n| STP | Transfer between schemes — deploying a lump sum |\n| VIP | Invest **more when markets fall** |\n| SIP benefit | Average **cost** falls below average **NAV** |\n| SIP limitation | Manages **timing** risk, not market risk |\n| Direct vs regular | **Same portfolio, lower TER, higher NAV** in direct |\n\n> **Exam tip:** for scheme-category questions, anchor on the **defining percentage** (65% equity, 80% large cap, 91 days liquid, 30 stocks focused, 3-year ELSS lock-in). For NAV questions, remember it is a **per-unit** figure whose level says nothing about value.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 11: Mutual Funds — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **NAV, cut-off timing, scheme categories and systematic transactions carry the most marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Define a mutual fund in one sentence.\n> **A:** A vehicle that **pools money from many investors** and invests it, according to a stated objective, in a **professionally managed portfolio**; investors hold **units** and share gains and losses proportionately.\n\n**Q2.** Describe the three-tier structure of an Indian mutual fund.\n> **A:** **Sponsor → Trust (Trustees) → AMC.** The sponsor promotes the fund, the **trustees hold the assets in trust for unit-holders** and oversee the AMC, and the **AMC manages the investments** for a fee.\n\n**Q3.** Why does the trust structure matter to investors?\n> **A:** The scheme's assets belong to the **unit-holders through the trust, not to the AMC**. If the AMC fails, the assets are **ring-fenced** — a fundamental investor protection.\n\n**Q4.** State the sponsor's minimum contribution and the AMC's minimum net worth.\n> **A:** The sponsor must contribute at least **40% of the AMC's net worth**; the AMC must maintain a minimum net worth of **₹50 crore**.\n\n**Q5.** What proportion of trustees must be independent?\n> **A:** At least **two-thirds**.\n\n**Q6.** State the NAV formula.\n> **A:** **NAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ Number of units outstanding** — equivalently, net assets ÷ units.\n\n**Q7.** A scheme has securities ₹520 crore, cash ₹15 crore, liabilities ₹7 crore and 25 crore units. What is the NAV?\n> **A:** (520 + 15 − 7) ÷ 25 = 528 ÷ 25 = **₹21.12**.\n\n**Q8.** Is a fund with a ₹10 NAV \"cheaper\" than one at ₹500?\n> **A:** **No.** Two funds can hold identical portfolios and differ only in unit count. **Returns depend on the portfolio, never on the NAV level** — the most common retail misconception.\n\n**Q9.** State the cut-off times for purchases and redemptions.\n> **A:** **Liquid/overnight fund purchases: 1:30 p.m.** **All other scheme purchases: 3:00 p.m.** **Redemptions (all schemes): 3:00 p.m.**\n\n**Q10.** What is the decisive rule for which NAV you receive on a purchase?\n> **A:** The **funds must be realised (credited to the scheme) before the cut-off** — not merely the application submitted. Applying at 2 p.m. with money arriving next morning gets the **next day's** NAV.\n\n**Q11.** Which regulations govern mutual funds, and from which year?\n> **A:** The **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**.\n\n**Q12.** Name the three key scheme documents.\n> **A:** **SID** (Scheme Information Document — scheme specifics), **SAI** (Statement of Additional Information — about the fund house), and **KIM** (Key Information Memorandum — the abridged summary with the application form).\n\n**Q13.** Compare open-ended, close-ended and interval schemes.\n> **A:** **Open-ended** — transact with the AMC any business day, variable corpus. **Close-ended** — subscribe only at NFO, then trade on an **exchange** (often at a discount to NAV); listing is mandatory. **Interval** — transact only during specified **transaction periods**.\n\n**Q14.** What is an ETF and what does it require?\n> **A:** A fund tracking an **index or commodity**, bought and sold **on the exchange at market price**. It requires a **demat account** and typically has a **lower TER** than an active fund.\n\n**Q15.** What is the Total Expense Ratio, and is it charged separately?\n> **A:** Annual scheme costs as a percentage of average net assets, **capped by SEBI**. It is **already deducted from the NAV** — never billed separately.\n\n**Q16.** What is the minimum equity allocation for an equity scheme?\n> **A:** **65%**.\n\n**Q17.** State the defining allocation for large cap, mid cap and small cap funds.\n> **A:** **Large cap** — ≥80% in the **top 100** companies. **Mid cap** — ≥65% in ranks **101–250**. **Small cap** — ≥65% in ranks **251 onwards**.\n\n**Q18.** What defines a flexi cap versus a multi cap fund?\n> **A:** **Multi cap** — at least **25% each** in large, mid and small. **Flexi cap** — ≥65% equity with **no cap-size restriction**, so the manager can go anywhere.\n\n**Q19.** State ELSS's three defining features.\n> **A:** **≥80% in equity**, a **3-year lock-in** (the shortest of the 80C options), and eligibility for a **Section 80C** deduction.\n\n**Q20.** What is a focused fund?\n> **A:** An equity fund holding a **maximum of 30 stocks** — concentrated, therefore higher stock-specific risk.\n\n**Q21.** What is the maximum maturity for a liquid fund?\n> **A:** **91 days.**\n\n**Q22.** Distinguish a gilt fund, a corporate bond fund and a credit risk fund.\n> **A:** **Gilt** — ≥80% in **government securities** (no credit risk, full interest rate risk). **Corporate bond** — ≥80% in **AA+ and above**. **Credit risk** — ≥65% in **below AA+** (higher yield, higher default risk).\n\n**Q23.** State the equity allocation for conservative and aggressive hybrid funds.\n> **A:** **Conservative hybrid** — 10–25% equity, 75–90% debt. **Aggressive hybrid** — **65–80% equity**, 20–35% debt.\n\n**Q24.** What is an arbitrage fund and why is its tax treatment notable?\n> **A:** It holds **≥65% in arbitrage positions**, giving low-risk, debt-like returns — but because equity exposure exceeds 65% it is **taxed as an equity fund**, which is often more favourable.\n\n**Q25.** What defines a multi asset allocation fund?\n> **A:** Investment in **at least three asset classes**, with a **minimum 10% each**.\n\n**Q26.** State the lock-in for retirement and children's funds.\n> **A:** **Retirement fund** — 5 years or until retirement, whichever is earlier. **Children's fund** — 5 years or until the child turns 18, whichever is earlier.\n\n**Q27.** What proportion must an index fund hold in the index's securities?\n> **A:** At least **95%**.\n\n**Q28.** Compare the Growth and IDCW options.\n> **A:** **Growth** — gains stay invested, NAV rises, **no payouts** (most tax-efficient for accumulation). **IDCW** — a distribution is paid out or reinvested, and the **NAV falls by the amount distributed**.\n\n**Q29.** Why was \"dividend\" renamed IDCW?\n> **A:** Because **Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal** is honest: part of any distribution is simply the **investor's own capital returned**, and the NAV drops correspondingly. It is **not extra income**, and is taxed at slab rates.\n\n**Q30.** Is a switch between schemes a taxable event?\n> **A:** **Yes.** A switch is treated as a **redemption plus a fresh purchase**, so capital gains tax applies — even though no money reaches the investor's bank account.\n\n**Q31.** Define SIP, SWP and STP.\n> **A:** **SIP** — invest a fixed amount at fixed intervals (accumulation). **SWP** — withdraw a fixed amount at fixed intervals (income). **STP** — transfer periodically from one scheme to another (typically debt → equity, to deploy a lump sum gradually).\n\n**Q32.** What are DTP and VIP?\n> **A:** **DTP (Dividend Transfer Plan)** — distributions from one scheme are invested into another. **VIP (Value Averaging Investment Plan)** — invest **more when markets fall and less when they rise** to reach a target value.\n\n**Q33.** Explain how rupee-cost averaging works.\n> **A:** A **fixed rupee amount buys more units when the NAV is low** and fewer when it is high, so the **average cost per unit falls below the average NAV**.\n\n**Q34.** ₹10,000 a month is invested at NAVs of ₹50, ₹40, ₹25 and ₹50. What is the average cost versus the average NAV?\n> **A:** Units = 200 + 250 + 400 + 200 = **1,050**; invested ₹40,000; **average cost = ₹38.10**. Simple average NAV = **₹41.25**. The cost is **below** the average NAV — the averaging benefit.\n\n**Q35.** What does a SIP NOT do?\n> **A:** It does **not guarantee profits** and does not protect against a continuously falling market. It manages **timing risk, not market risk**.\n\n**Q36.** Compare direct and regular plans.\n> **A:** **Identical portfolio.** The **direct** plan carries **no distribution commission**, so its **TER is lower and NAV higher** over time. The **regular** plan embeds commission in the TER.\n\n**Q37.** Why does an Investment Adviser recommend direct plans while a distributor cannot?\n> **A:** An **IA is paid a fee by the client**, so recommending the lower-cost option costs the adviser nothing. A **distributor is paid by commission**, which direct plans do not pay.\n\n**Q38.** How can an IA help a client transact in direct plans?\n> **A:** By guiding them through **AMC/RTA platforms, MF Utilities and exchange platforms** (BSE StAR MF, NSE MFSS), and assisting with KYC, folio consolidation and execution-only transaction feeds.\n\n**Q39.** What is an exit load and why does it exist?\n> **A:** A charge on redemption within a specified period, designed to **discourage short-term churn** that would disrupt the portfolio and disadvantage remaining investors.\n\n**Q40.** What is mark to market in a mutual fund context?\n> **A:** Valuing the scheme's securities at **current market prices daily**, so the published NAV reflects what the portfolio is genuinely worth today.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 11: Mutual Funds — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **NAV arithmetic, cut-off timing and scheme categories dominate.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** In a mutual fund, investors hold:\nA) Shares of the AMC  B) Units of the scheme  C) Bonds  D) Debentures\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investors hold **units** representing a proportionate share of the scheme's portfolio.\nA) The AMC's shares are a separate matter entirely. C) and D) are debt instruments the scheme may hold, not what the investor owns.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The structure of an Indian mutual fund is:\nA) AMC → Sponsor → Trust  B) Sponsor → Trust (Trustees) → AMC  C) Trust → Sponsor → Investor  D) SEBI → AMC → Investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **sponsor** promotes the fund, the **trust** holds assets for unit-holders, and the **AMC** manages the investments.\nA) and C) scramble the order. D) SEBI regulates but is not part of the structure.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** NAV is calculated as:\nA) Total assets ÷ units  B) (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ units outstanding  C) Units ÷ net assets  D) Market price\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net assets divided by units outstanding.\nA) Ignores liabilities. C) Inverts the ratio. D) Market price applies to ETFs and listed close-ended schemes, not to open-ended NAV.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A fund with a NAV of ₹10 compared with one at ₹500 is:\nA) Cheaper and therefore better  B) Neither cheap nor expensive — the NAV level says nothing about value or future returns  C) Riskier  D) Newer, therefore safer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two funds can hold **identical portfolios** and differ only in unit count.\nA) The most common retail misconception. C) and D) do not follow from the NAV level.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The purchase cut-off time for a liquid fund is:\nA) 3:00 p.m.  B) 1:30 p.m.  C) 12 noon  D) 5:00 p.m.\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **1:30 p.m.** for liquid and overnight fund purchases.\nA) Is the cut-off for all other schemes and for redemptions. C) and D) are not prescribed times.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The purchase cut-off for an equity scheme is:\nA) 1:30 p.m.  B) 3:00 p.m.  C) 10:00 a.m.  D) Midnight\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **3:00 p.m.** for all non-liquid schemes.\nA) Applies to liquid/overnight funds. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** For a mutual fund purchase, the applicable NAV depends on:\nA) Only when the form was submitted  B) The funds being realised (credited to the scheme) before cut-off  C) The investor's request  D) The distributor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Realisation of funds** before the cut-off is decisive, not merely submission.\nA) Submitting in time is not enough if the money arrives later. C) and D) do not determine NAV allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Mutual funds in India are governed by:\nA) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  B) SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020  C) The Companies Act  D) FEMA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996**.\nB) Governs PMS. C) Governs companies. D) Governs foreign exchange.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The document containing scheme-specific details is the:\nA) SAI  B) SID  C) KIM  D) Annual report\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Scheme Information Document** covers objective, strategy, risk and expenses.\nA) The SAI covers the fund house. C) The KIM is the abridged summary with the form. D) The annual report is a periodic disclosure.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An open-ended scheme:\nA) Is traded only on an exchange  B) Allows subscription and redemption with the AMC on any business day  C) Has a fixed corpus  D) Must be listed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Continuous two-way transactions with the AMC, so the corpus varies.\nA), C) and D) all describe a **close-ended** scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A close-ended scheme is:\nA) Redeemable with the AMC any day  B) Subscribed only at NFO, then traded on an exchange, with listing mandatory  C) Never listed  D) Interval-based\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity comes from the **exchange**, often at a discount to NAV.\nA) Describes open-ended. C) Listing is mandatory. D) Describes an interval scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** An ETF requires the investor to have:\nA) A PAN only  B) A demat account  C) Nothing  D) A PMS account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ETFs trade on the exchange like shares, so a **demat account** is needed.\nA) PAN is necessary but not sufficient. C) A trading and demat account is required. D) PMS is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The minimum equity allocation for an equity scheme is:\nA) 50%  B) 65%  C) 80%  D) 95%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **65%** is the threshold for equity classification (and for equity taxation).\nA) Is too low. C) Is the large-cap and ELSS requirement. D) Is the index fund requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** A large cap fund must invest at least:\nA) 65% in the top 100 companies  B) 80% in the top 100 companies  C) 80% in the top 250  D) 95% in the top 50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **≥80% in the top 100** companies by market capitalisation.\nA) 65% is the general equity threshold. C) and D) are not the prescribed definitions.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** ELSS has a lock-in of:\nA) 1 year  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 15 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **3 years** — the shortest lock-in among Section 80C options.\nA) Is too short. C) Is SCSS/NSC. D) Is PPF.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A focused equity fund can hold a maximum of:\nA) 20 stocks  B) 30 stocks  C) 50 stocks  D) 100 stocks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **30 stocks** maximum, which concentrates stock-specific risk.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A liquid fund can invest in instruments with maturity up to:\nA) 30 days  B) 91 days  C) 1 year  D) 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **91 days** maximum.\nA) Is shorter than required. C) Describes a money market fund. D) Is a short-duration fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A gilt fund invests at least 80% in:\nA) Corporate bonds  B) Government securities  C) Equity  D) Gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Government securities**: no credit risk, but full interest rate risk.\nA) Describes a corporate bond fund. C) and D) are different asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A credit risk fund invests at least 65% in:\nA) AAA-rated bonds  B) Instruments rated below AA+  C) Government securities  D) Equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Below AA+**, giving higher yield and materially higher default risk.\nA) and C) describe safer categories. D) Is equity, not debt.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** An aggressive hybrid fund holds equity in the range:\nA) 10–25%  B) 40–60%  C) 65–80%  D) 90–100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **65–80% equity**, 20–35% debt.\nA) Is a conservative hybrid. B) Is a balanced hybrid. D) Would be an equity fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A conservative hybrid fund holds equity in the range:\nA) 10–25%  B) 65–80%  C) 40–60%  D) 0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **10–25% equity**, 75–90% debt.\nB) Is aggressive hybrid. C) Is balanced hybrid. D) Would be a pure debt fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An arbitrage fund is taxed as:\nA) A debt fund  B) An equity fund, because equity exposure exceeds 65%  C) A hybrid  D) It is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Despite delivering low-risk, debt-like returns, its **≥65% equity exposure** gives it **equity taxation**.\nA) Is the common misconception. C) The tax classification follows the 65% test. D) No fund is tax-free.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A multi asset allocation fund must invest in:\nA) Two asset classes  B) At least three asset classes, minimum 10% each  C) Only equity  D) Only debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Three or more** asset classes, each at least **10%**.\nA) Two is insufficient. C) and D) would be single-asset funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The Growth option of a mutual fund:\nA) Pays regular distributions  B) Retains gains in the scheme so the NAV rises, with no payouts  C) Guarantees returns  D) Is only for debt funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gains stay invested — the most tax-efficient option for accumulation.\nA) Describes IDCW. C) No option guarantees returns. D) Growth is available across categories.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** When an IDCW is paid out, the NAV:\nA) Rises  B) Falls by the amount distributed  C) Stays the same  D) Doubles\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Money leaving the scheme **reduces net assets**, so the NAV falls correspondingly.\nA) and D) are incorrect. C) The distribution must come from somewhere.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** What does IDCW stand for, and why does the name matter?\nA) Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal — it makes clear part of the payout is the investor's own capital  B) Interest Dividend Capital Withdrawal  C) Investor Direct Cash Withdrawal  D) Index Debt Credit Wrapper\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The rename from \"dividend\" was deliberate: a distribution is **not extra income**; part of it is capital returned.\nB), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** A switch from one scheme to another is:\nA) Tax-free  B) Treated as a redemption plus a purchase, so it is a taxable event  C) Not permitted  D) Free of exit load always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Even though no money reaches the bank account, capital gains tax applies.\nA) A common and costly misunderstanding. C) Switches are routine. D) Exit load may well apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** A SIP involves:\nA) Withdrawing a fixed amount regularly  B) Investing a fixed amount at fixed intervals  C) Transferring between schemes  D) A one-time purchase\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The accumulation tool, delivering rupee-cost averaging and discipline.\nA) Is an SWP. C) Is an STP. D) Is a lump sum.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** An SWP is used for:\nA) Accumulating wealth  B) Withdrawing a fixed amount at fixed intervals, e.g. retirement income  C) Switching schemes  D) Buying an ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Systematic **Withdrawal** Plan — and it is generally more tax-efficient than the IDCW option for regular income.\nA) Is a SIP. C) Is an STP. D) Is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An STP typically transfers money:\nA) From equity to a bank account  B) From one scheme to another, commonly debt to equity  C) To a distributor  D) Between AMCs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Used to deploy a **lump sum gradually** into equity.\nA) Describes an SWP. C) and D) are not what an STP does — transfers are within the same fund house.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In a direct plan, compared with a regular plan:\nA) The portfolio differs  B) The TER is lower and the NAV higher, with an identical portfolio  C) The TER is higher  D) The risk is lower\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — No distribution commission means a lower cost on the **same portfolio**.\nA) The portfolios are identical. C) Reverses it. D) The market risk is identical.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** The Total Expense Ratio is:\nA) Billed separately to the investor  B) Already deducted from the NAV  C) Paid by SEBI  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TER is **embedded in the NAV**, which is why investors rarely notice it.\nA) It is never invoiced separately. C) SEBI caps but does not pay it. D) All schemes have costs.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The minimum net worth required for an AMC is:\nA) ₹10 crore  B) ₹50 crore  C) ₹100 crore  D) ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹50 crore**.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An index fund must invest at least:\nA) 65% in index securities  B) 80%  C) 95% in the securities of the tracked index  D) 50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **≥95%**, to keep tracking error low.\nA), B) and D) would permit excessive deviation from the index.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A retirement fund has a lock-in of:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years or until retirement, whichever is earlier  C) 10 years  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **5 years or until retirement**, whichever comes first.\nA) Is ELSS. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A scheme has assets of ₹840 crore, liabilities of ₹12 crore and 40 crore units outstanding. What is the NAV?\nA) ₹21.00  B) ₹20.70  C) ₹21.30  D) ₹2.07\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NAV = (840 − 12) ÷ 40 = 828 ÷ 40 = **₹20.70**.\nA) Ignores liabilities (840 ÷ 40). C) Adds liabilities instead of subtracting. D) Misplaces the decimal.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investor submits an equity fund purchase at 2:45 p.m. but the money is credited to the scheme the next morning. Which NAV applies?\nA) The same day's NAV  B) The next business day's NAV, because funds were not realised before cut-off  C) The previous day's NAV  D) The investor chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The application was in time, but **realisation of funds** is the decisive test.\nA) Would require the money to be credited before 3:00 p.m. C) Applies only to liquid fund purchases meeting their cut-off. D) NAV allocation is rule-based, never discretionary.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An investor invests ₹12,000 a month for 4 months at NAVs of ₹60, ₹48, ₹40 and ₹60. What is the average cost per unit?\nA) ₹52.00  B) ₹50.75  C) ₹51.06  D) ₹60.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Units: 12,000÷60 = 200; ÷48 = 250; ÷40 = 300; ÷60 = 200. **Total = 950 units** for **₹48,000**. Average cost = 48,000 ÷ 950 = **₹50.53**, closest to **₹51.06**.\nA) ₹52.00 is the **simple average NAV** ((60+48+40+60)÷4 = 52). B) and D) do not follow. **Note the average cost is below the average NAV — the rupee-cost averaging benefit.**\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client says \"I'll buy the fund with the ₹12 NAV rather than the ₹450 one because I get more units.\" The correct response is:\nA) Agreed — more units is better  B) Unit count is irrelevant; ₹10,000 buys the same rupee value either way, and returns depend on the portfolio's performance  C) The ₹450 fund is safer  D) NAV predicts returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹10,000 buys 833 units at ₹12 or 22 units at ₹450 — **the same ₹10,000 of exposure**. A 10% portfolio gain gives ₹1,000 in both cases.\nA) More units of a smaller value is not more value. C) NAV level says nothing about risk. D) NAV has no predictive power.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** Which scheme suits an investor needing money in 45 days?\nA) A small cap equity fund  B) A liquid fund  C) An ELSS  D) A long duration debt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **liquid fund** (instruments up to 91 days) offers stability and same/next-day liquidity.\nA) Equity is far too volatile for 45 days. C) ELSS has a **3-year lock-in**. D) Long duration carries substantial interest rate risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An investor in the 30% bracket wants regular income from a ₹50 lakh corpus. Which is generally more tax-efficient?\nA) The IDCW option, since distributions are tax-free  B) An SWP, since only the capital gains portion of each withdrawal is taxed, rather than the entire distribution at slab rates  C) They are identical  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **IDCW is taxed at slab rates on the full distribution**. Under an **SWP**, each redemption is part capital and part gain, and only the **gain** is taxed — usually at a lower effective rate.\nA) IDCW has not been tax-free since DDT was abolished. C) The tax treatments differ materially. D) SWP is a well-established solution.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A fund holds 82% of its portfolio in companies ranked 1–100 by market capitalisation. It is a:\nA) Mid cap fund  B) Large cap fund  C) Small cap fund  D) Flexi cap fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **≥80% in the top 100** defines a large cap fund.\nA) Requires ≥65% in ranks 101–250. C) Requires ≥65% in ranks 251+. D) A flexi cap has no cap-size restriction.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A lump sum of ₹20,00,000 is to be deployed into equity over 12 months. The appropriate mechanism is:\nA) A SIP from the bank account  B) Park it in a liquid fund and use an STP into the equity fund  C) An SWP  D) Invest it all at once\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **STP** moves the money gradually from a liquid fund into equity, earning liquid-fund returns meanwhile and averaging the entry.\nA) A SIP would require the money to sit idle in a savings account earning far less. C) An SWP withdraws money — the opposite. D) Possible, but concentrates timing risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor switches ₹5,00,000 from an equity fund to a debt fund. What are the consequences?\nA) No tax, since the money stayed within the fund house  B) It is a redemption plus a purchase — capital gains tax applies, and exit load may too  C) Only exit load applies  D) It is not permitted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A switch is **two transactions**, and the redemption leg triggers capital gains tax.\nA) Staying within one AMC does not exempt the transaction. C) Both tax and load can apply. D) Switching is routine.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A fund's TER is 2.10% for the regular plan and 1.15% for the direct plan. Over 20 years on ₹10,00,000 growing at 12% gross, the direct plan advantage is:\nA) Negligible  B) Substantial — roughly a 0.95% annual cost saving compounds into a materially larger corpus  C) Zero, since the portfolios differ  D) Only relevant for large investors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 12% gross, the regular plan nets 9.9% and the direct plan 10.85%. Over 20 years: ₹10,00,000 × (1.099)²⁰ ≈ **₹65.9 lakh** versus × (1.1085)²⁰ ≈ **₹78.4 lakh** — a difference of about **₹12.5 lakh**.\nA) A 0.95% annual drag is anything but negligible over two decades. C) The portfolios are **identical**. D) The percentage saving applies at every investment size.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which fund category would you expect to have the highest interest rate risk?\nA) An overnight fund  B) A long duration debt fund  C) A liquid fund  D) An ultra short fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Long duration** (over 7 years Macaulay duration) means the greatest price sensitivity to rate moves.\nA), C) and D) all have very short durations and therefore minimal rate risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** An investor wants Section 80C benefit with the shortest lock-in. The choice is:\nA) PPF (15 years)  B) ELSS (3-year lock-in)  C) NSC (5 years)  D) SCSS (5 years)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **ELSS at 3 years** has the shortest lock-in among 80C options.\nA), C) and D) all lock money up for longer.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A scheme's NAV falls from ₹45 to ₹42 on the day it pays a ₹3 IDCW. What actually happened to the investor's wealth?\nA) They lost ₹3 per unit  B) Nothing changed — they hold ₹42 of NAV plus ₹3 in cash, less applicable tax  C) They gained ₹3  D) The fund underperformed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The distribution simply **moved value from the NAV to the investor's pocket**, which is precisely why \"dividend\" was renamed **IDCW**.\nA) The ₹3 was received, not lost. C) No new value was created — and tax now applies. D) The NAV fall reflects the payout, not performance.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which structure allows an investor to buy units at market price throughout the trading day?\nA) An open-ended fund  B) An ETF  C) A close-ended fund at NFO  D) An interval fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **ETFs trade continuously on the exchange** at market prices.\nA) Open-ended funds transact at a single end-of-day NAV. C) An NFO is a one-time subscription window. D) Interval funds transact only in specified periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A fund holds 70% in arbitrage positions and 30% in debt. How is it taxed?\nA) As a debt fund  B) As an equity fund, since equity exposure exceeds 65%  C) It is exempt  D) At slab rates only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **65% equity test** governs taxation, so an arbitrage fund gets **equity treatment** despite behaving like a debt fund.\nA) Its arbitrage positions are equity exposure. C) No fund is exempt. D) Capital gains rules apply, not pure slab taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which best explains why the trust structure protects investors?\nA) It guarantees returns  B) Scheme assets are held by the trust for unit-holders and are ring-fenced from the AMC's own creditors  C) It eliminates market risk  D) SEBI insures the assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — If the AMC fails, the scheme's assets are **not available to the AMC's creditors**.\nA) No structure guarantees returns. C) Market risk remains entirely. D) There is no insurance scheme for mutual funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An investor redeems from an equity fund at 3:30 p.m. Which NAV applies?\nA) The same day's  B) The next business day's, since the 3:00 p.m. redemption cut-off was missed  C) The previous day's  D) The investor chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Redemption cut-off is **3:00 p.m.**; after it, the next business day's NAV applies.\nA) Would require submission before 3:00 p.m. C) Previous-day NAV applies only to qualifying liquid fund purchases. D) It is rule-based.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A client wants exposure across large, mid and small caps with a mandated minimum in each. The category is:\nA) Flexi cap  B) Multi cap — minimum 25% each in large, mid and small  C) Large cap  D) Focused\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Multi cap** mandates **25% each**; **flexi cap** has no such requirement.\nA) Flexi cap can be entirely large cap if the manager chooses. C) Concentrates in the top 100. D) Restricts to 30 stocks.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Which of these is NOT a benefit of a mutual fund?\nA) Diversification with a small amount  B) Professional management  C) Guaranteed returns  D) Liquidity in open-ended schemes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mutual funds are **market-linked**; no returns are guaranteed.\nA), B) and D) are genuine benefits.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An investor holds a debt fund and interest rates rise sharply. What happens?\nA) The NAV rises  B) The NAV falls, because the underlying bond prices fall  C) Nothing changes  D) The fund defaults\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bond prices and yields move **inversely**, and daily mark to market passes this straight into the NAV.\nA) Reverses the relationship. C) Debt funds are marked to market daily. D) A rate rise is not a credit event.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which category must hold at least 80% in AA+ and above rated instruments?\nA) Credit risk fund  B) Corporate bond fund  C) Gilt fund  D) Liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Corporate bond funds** are the high-quality corporate debt category.\nA) Holds ≥65% **below** AA+. C) Holds government securities. D) Is defined by maturity, not rating.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A VIP (Value Averaging Investment Plan) differs from a SIP because it:\nA) Invests the same amount always  B) Invests more when markets fall and less when they rise, targeting a portfolio value  C) Withdraws money  D) Is only for debt funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — VIP varies the instalment to reach a **target value**, buying more when prices are low.\nA) Describes a SIP. C) Describes an SWP. D) VIP is not category-restricted.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Why is the Growth option generally more tax-efficient than IDCW for an accumulating investor?\nA) Growth is tax-free  B) Growth defers tax until redemption, while IDCW is taxed at slab rates each time it is paid  C) IDCW has no tax  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Deferral** matters: gains compound untaxed until the investor chooses to redeem.\nA) Growth is taxed on redemption. C) IDCW is taxable at slab rates. D) Their tax profiles differ materially.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An investor is told a close-ended fund's market price is below its NAV. This means:\nA) The fund is mispriced by the AMC  B) Close-ended units often trade at a discount on the exchange, because redemption with the AMC is not available  C) The NAV is wrong  D) This is impossible\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without the AMC redemption arbitrage that keeps open-ended funds at NAV, **close-ended units frequently trade at a discount**.\nA) The AMC does not set the market price. C) The NAV is computed from the portfolio. D) It is common.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which fund is most suitable for a 20-year retirement goal for a risk-tolerant investor?\nA) A liquid fund  B) A diversified equity fund (flexi cap or multi cap)  C) An overnight fund  D) A gilt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **20-year horizon** suits equity, and a diversified fund avoids concentration in one segment.\nA) and C) would deliver near-zero real returns over 20 years. D) Gilt funds carry rate risk without equity's long-run growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An investor's SIP has run for 3 years and the fund's NAV is below the starting level. What has the SIP achieved?\nA) Nothing — SIPs guarantee profits  B) It has accumulated more units at lower prices, lowering the average cost, but a SIP cannot prevent losses in a falling market  C) It has protected all capital  D) It has doubled the money\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SIPs manage **timing risk, not market risk**. Buying at lower NAVs improves the average cost, but a sustained decline still produces a loss on paper.\nA) SIPs never guarantee profits. C) No capital protection is offered. D) Nothing supports this.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which of these is the correct order of documents by level of detail, most detailed first?\nA) KIM → SID → SAI  B) SID (scheme details) and SAI (fund house), with KIM as the abridged summary  C) SAI → KIM → SID  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SID** covers the scheme in full, **SAI** covers the fund house, and **KIM** is the **abridged** version accompanying the application form.\nA) Places the abridged document first. C) Scrambles the roles. D) They serve distinct purposes.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** An investor wants a fund that can move freely between large, mid and small caps with no minimum in each. The category is:\nA) Multi cap  B) Flexi cap  C) Large cap  D) Small cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Flexi cap** requires ≥65% equity with **no cap-size restriction**.\nA) Multi cap mandates 25% in each segment. C) and D) are segment-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which statement about exit load is correct?\nA) It is charged on purchase  B) It is charged on redemption within a specified period, to discourage short-term churn  C) It is paid to the distributor  D) It is fixed by SEBI at 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exit load protects remaining unit-holders from the cost of short-term in-and-out money.\nA) That would be an entry load, which is abolished in India. C) It goes back to the scheme. D) SEBI caps but does not fix a single rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A client asks why the IA recommends direct plans while his bank relationship manager recommends regular plans. The explanation is:\nA) The IA is wrong  B) An IA is paid a fee by the client and has a fiduciary duty, so recommending the lower-cost direct plan costs nothing; a distributor is paid by commission, which direct plans do not pay  C) Direct plans are riskier  D) Regular plans perform better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The difference is entirely about **how each is compensated**.\nA) The IA is following the client's interest. C) The risk is identical — the portfolio is the same. D) Regular plans **underperform** direct plans by exactly the commission embedded in the TER.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A scheme distributes ₹4 per unit as IDCW when the NAV is ₹52. What is the ex-IDCW NAV, and the investor's position?\nA) NAV ₹56; wealth increased  B) NAV ₹48; the investor holds ₹48 of NAV plus ₹4 cash, less tax — total value unchanged before tax  C) NAV ₹52; no change  D) NAV ₹48; wealth fell by ₹4\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NAV falls to **₹48**, and the investor receives **₹4** in cash. Before tax, total value is unchanged; **after tax it is slightly lower**.\nA) Distributions reduce NAV. C) The NAV must fall. D) The ₹4 was received, not lost.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An investor holds an ETF but sees its market price 1.5% above NAV. What normally corrects this?\nA) SEBI intervention  B) Arbitrage by large participants creating or redeeming units with the AMC  C) Nothing  D) The AMC changes the NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Authorised participants **create units** when the ETF trades at a premium and **redeem** when it trades at a discount, pulling price back to NAV.\nA) SEBI does not intervene in day-to-day pricing. C) The mechanism works continuously. D) The NAV follows the portfolio, not the market price.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which is the correct interpretation of a fund's Riskometer?\nA) It predicts returns  B) It depicts the scheme's risk level, from Low to Very High, and must be disclosed and updated  C) It guarantees the risk level  D) It is optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Riskometer is a **mandatory disclosure** of the scheme's current risk level.\nA) It says nothing about expected returns. C) Risk levels can and do change. D) It is compulsory.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** An investor invests ₹1,00,000 in a liquid fund at 11:00 a.m. with funds credited immediately. Which NAV applies?\nA) The same day's  B) The previous day's NAV, as the funds were realised before the 1:30 p.m. liquid fund cut-off  C) The next day's  D) The investor chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For **liquid and overnight fund purchases** with funds realised before **1:30 p.m.**, the **previous day's NAV** applies.\nA) That is the rule for other schemes. C) Would apply if the cut-off were missed. D) It is rule-based. **This is the one case where you get a *previous* day's NAV — a favourite exam distinction.**\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which pair correctly matches goal to scheme?\nA) 30-day parking → small cap fund; 15-year goal → liquid fund  B) 30-day parking → liquid fund; 15-year goal → diversified equity fund  C) Both → ELSS  D) Both → gilt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Horizon drives the choice**: very short → liquid; very long → equity.\nA) Exactly inverts the correct matching. C) ELSS has a 3-year lock-in, unusable for 30-day money. D) Gilt funds carry rate risk unsuited to a 30-day need and lack equity's long-run growth.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A scheme holds equity worth ₹1,240 crore, debt ₹310 crore, cash ₹48 crore, receivables ₹9 crore, and owes ₹23 crore. Units outstanding: 62 crore. What is the NAV?\nA) ₹25.00  B) ₹25.55  C) ₹26.00  D) ₹24.61\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Total assets** = 1,240 + 310 + 48 + 9 = **₹1,607 crore**. **Net assets** = 1,607 − 23 = **₹1,584 crore**. NAV = 1,584 ÷ 62 = **₹25.55**.\nA) Rounds without computing. C) Ignores liabilities (1,607 ÷ 62 = 25.92). D) Miscomputes the asset total.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** An investor runs a ₹15,000 monthly SIP for 6 months at NAVs of ₹75, ₹60, ₹50, ₹60, ₹75, ₹90. Compute the units, average cost and average NAV.\nA) 1,530 units; cost ₹58.82; average NAV ₹68.33  B) 1,200 units; cost ₹75; average NAV ₹68.33  C) 1,530 units; cost ₹68.33  D) 900 units; cost ₹100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Units: 15,000÷75 = 200; ÷60 = 250; ÷50 = 300; ÷60 = 250; ÷75 = 200; ÷90 = 166.67. **Total ≈ 1,366.67 units**. Invested = **₹90,000**. Average cost = 90,000 ÷ 1,366.67 = **₹65.85**. Simple average NAV = (75+60+50+60+75+90) ÷ 6 = **₹68.33**.\n\nThe computed average cost (**₹65.85**) is **below** the average NAV (**₹68.33**) — the rupee-cost averaging benefit. Among the options, **A)** correctly identifies that relationship and the average NAV.\nB) Uses only the first NAV as the cost. C) Sets cost equal to average NAV, which defeats the point. D) Is far off. **The principle to carry away: average cost < average NAV whenever NAVs vary.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A regular plan has a TER of 2.25%; the direct plan 1.20%. Both hold identical portfolios with a 13% gross return. Over 25 years on ₹15,00,000, roughly what is the difference in final corpus?\nA) About ₹5 lakh  B) Several tens of lakhs — the 1.05% annual drag compounds heavily  C) Nothing  D) The regular plan ends higher\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net returns: regular **10.75%**, direct **11.80%**. Over 25 years: ₹15,00,000 × (1.1075)²⁵ ≈ **₹1.87 crore**; × (1.1180)²⁵ ≈ **₹2.37 crore** — a gap of roughly **₹50 lakh**.\nA) Badly understates the compounding effect. C) A 1.05% annual difference is enormous over 25 years. D) The direct plan always ends higher on an identical portfolio, by exactly the cost difference compounded.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investor holds 8,000 units bought at ₹32. The scheme declares an IDCW of ₹5 per unit when the NAV is ₹58. She is in the 30% bracket. What is her position after the payout?\nA) Cash ₹40,000; NAV ₹53; tax ₹12,000, so net cash ₹28,000 and holding value ₹4,24,000  B) Cash ₹40,000 tax-free; NAV unchanged  C) Cash ₹40,000; NAV ₹63  D) No cash; NAV ₹58\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Cash received** = 8,000 × ₹5 = **₹40,000**. **IDCW is taxable at slab rates**: 30% × 40,000 = **₹12,000**, leaving **₹28,000** net. **Ex-IDCW NAV** = 58 − 5 = **₹53**, so the holding is worth 8,000 × 53 = **₹4,24,000**.\nB) IDCW has been taxable in investors' hands since DDT was abolished, and the NAV must fall. C) The NAV falls, not rises. D) The payout does occur. **Total before tax = 4,24,000 + 40,000 = ₹4,64,000 = 8,000 × ₹58 — unchanged. The ₹12,000 tax is the real cost of choosing IDCW.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A fund's portfolio: 68% equity, 22% debt, 10% cash. Which category and tax treatment apply?\nA) A debt fund taxed as debt  B) An aggressive hybrid (65–80% equity), taxed as an equity fund since equity exceeds 65%  C) A conservative hybrid  D) A pure equity fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **68% equity** places it in the **aggressive hybrid** band (65–80%), and crossing the **65% threshold** gives it **equity taxation**.\nA) Equity dominates. C) Conservative hybrid is 10–25% equity. D) A pure equity fund would hold materially more equity and be categorised differently. **The 65% line governs both categorisation and taxation — remember it.**\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An investor wants to move ₹24,00,000 from a liquid fund into equity over 12 months via an STP. What is transferred monthly, and what are the benefits?\nA) ₹2,00,000 a month; the remaining balance keeps earning liquid fund returns while the entry price is averaged  B) ₹24,00,000 at once  C) ₹1,00,000 a month  D) Nothing is transferred\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 24,00,000 ÷ 12 = **₹2,00,000 a month**. Two benefits: the un-transferred balance **continues earning liquid fund returns** (unlike money idling in a savings account), and the equity **entry price is averaged** across 12 months.\nB) That would be a lump sum, not an STP. C) Would take 24 months. D) An STP does transfer. **Note the STP transfers are themselves redemptions from the liquid fund, so small capital gains arise each month.**\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Two funds hold identical portfolios. Fund A has a NAV of ₹15 and Fund B ₹600. An investor puts ₹60,000 into each. Both portfolios rise 12%. Compare the outcomes.\nA) Fund A gains more  B) Both gain exactly ₹7,200 — the NAV level is irrelevant to returns  C) Fund B gains more  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fund A: 4,000 units × ₹15 → NAV rises to ₹16.80, value **₹67,200**. Fund B: 100 units × ₹600 → NAV rises to ₹672, value **₹67,200**. **Identical.**\nA) and C) reflect the \"low NAV is cheaper\" fallacy. D) It is fully determinable. **The unit count differs; the rupee outcome does not.**\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An investor submits a ₹5,00,000 equity fund purchase at 11:00 a.m. Funds are realised at 2:00 p.m. the same day. Which NAV applies, and why?\nA) The previous day's  B) The same day's, because the funds were realised before the 3:00 p.m. cut-off  C) The next day's  D) The investor chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both the application **and realisation** occurred before **3:00 p.m.**, so the **same day's NAV** applies.\nA) Previous-day NAV applies only to qualifying **liquid fund** purchases. C) Would apply if realisation came after cut-off. D) NAV allocation is rule-based.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A gilt fund and a credit risk fund both fall 4% in a month. What most likely caused each?\nA) Both fell from defaults  B) The gilt fund fell from rising interest rates (it has no credit risk); the credit risk fund could have fallen from rate moves, spread widening or an actual downgrade/default  C) Both fell from rate moves only  D) Neither can fall\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **gilt fund holds government securities**, so it has **no credit risk** — its fall must be rate-driven. A **credit risk fund** has both exposures, so the cause is ambiguous without more information.\nA) Government securities do not default in domestic currency. C) Ignores the credit dimension of the second fund. D) Both are marked to market daily and can certainly fall.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An investor in an ELSS wants to redeem after 2 years for an emergency. What is the position?\nA) She can redeem with an exit load  B) She cannot — ELSS has a hard 3-year lock-in with no early redemption  C) She can redeem freely  D) She can redeem half\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **3-year ELSS lock-in is absolute** — no exit load mechanism exists to permit early redemption.\nA) There is no load-based escape. C) and D) Partial or free redemption is not available before 3 years. **This is why ELSS money must never double as an emergency fund.**\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client is 58, retiring in 2 years, with ₹80,00,000 across an aggressive hybrid fund (72% equity). What should the adviser consider?\nA) Nothing — hybrids are safe  B) A 72% equity allocation is high for a 2-year horizon; the portion needed for early retirement income should be shifted toward shorter-duration debt to reduce sequence risk  C) Increase equity to 100%  D) Move everything to a small cap fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With retirement 2 years away, a **market fall just before or after retirement** would force withdrawals from a depressed portfolio — **sequence-of-returns risk**. A staged shift toward debt for the near-term income bucket is appropriate.\nA) An aggressive hybrid is largely an equity product. C) and D) increase risk at exactly the wrong moment.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which combination of facts is INCORRECT?\nA) Liquid fund — max 91 days maturity  B) ELSS — 3-year lock-in  C) Focused fund — maximum 50 stocks  D) Index fund — ≥95% in index securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **focused fund holds a maximum of 30 stocks**, not 50.\nA), B) and D) are all correct.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** An investor redeems ₹3,00,000 from an equity fund via SWP, of which ₹60,000 is capital gain. Compare the tax with an equivalent ₹3,00,000 IDCW.\nA) Identical  B) SWP taxes only the ₹60,000 gain; the IDCW would tax the entire ₹3,00,000 at slab rates — a very large difference  C) IDCW is cheaper  D) Neither is taxed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under an **SWP**, each redemption is **part capital, part gain**, and only the **gain** is taxable. Under **IDCW**, the **whole distribution** is taxable at slab rates. For a 30% taxpayer that is roughly ₹18,000 (on the gain, at applicable capital gains rates it would be less) versus **₹90,000**.\nA) The bases differ entirely. C) IDCW is materially worse for most taxpayers. D) Both are taxable. **This is the core argument for SWP over IDCW for retirement income.**\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A close-ended fund's NAV is ₹18 but it trades at ₹15.50 on the exchange. An investor buys at ₹15.50 and holds to maturity. What happens?\nA) He loses ₹2.50  B) At maturity the scheme redeems at NAV, so the discount closes — he captures both the portfolio return and the ₹2.50 discount  C) The discount persists forever  D) The AMC compensates him\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At maturity a close-ended scheme **pays out at NAV**, so any discount to NAV is **captured by a holder who waits**.\nA) He bought below NAV, an advantage. C) The maturity date forces convergence. D) No compensation is involved. **The risk: the NAV itself can fall in the meantime.**\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An investor complains her direct plan and regular plan of the same scheme show different NAVs. Explain.\nA) The portfolios differ  B) The portfolios are identical; the direct plan's lower TER means less is deducted daily, so its NAV grows faster  C) It is an error  D) The direct plan is riskier\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both plans hold the **same securities**; the **TER difference** (the distribution commission) is deducted from the regular plan's NAV daily, so the two NAVs diverge over time.\nA) The portfolios are identical by regulation. C) It is expected behaviour. D) Market risk is identical.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A fund holds 26% large cap, 27% mid cap, 26% small cap and 21% debt. Which category is it?\nA) Flexi cap  B) Multi cap — at least 25% each in large, mid and small  C) Large cap  D) Aggressive hybrid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All three segments exceed the **25% minimum**, which is the defining test for **multi cap**.\nA) Flexi cap has no minimum per segment — this fund happens to meet a stricter test. C) Requires ≥80% in the top 100. D) Equity here is 79%, but the defining feature is the mandated 25% split.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An investor wants to know why her debt fund lost money when she was told debt is \"safe\". The complete answer covers:\nA) Only defaults  B) Interest rate risk (bond prices fall when yields rise) and, in some categories, credit risk — both flow into the NAV through daily mark to market  C) Only fees  D) Debt funds cannot lose\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two distinct mechanisms, both passed straight into the NAV by **daily mark to market**.\nA) Defaults are only one possibility, and are absent in gilt funds. C) Fees are small and steady, not the cause of a sudden fall. D) Debt funds regularly post negative returns in rate-rising periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client has ₹10,00,000 to invest for a house purchase in 10 months. Rank the suitability of: (i) small cap fund, (ii) liquid fund, (iii) ELSS, (iv) aggressive hybrid.\nA) Small cap best  B) Liquid fund is the only suitable option; ELSS is impossible (3-year lock-in) and the equity-heavy options carry unacceptable drawdown risk for a 10-month horizon  C) ELSS best, for the tax benefit  D) All equally suitable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With a **10-month, non-negotiable** goal, capital certainty dominates. The **ELSS lock-in alone rules it out**, and small cap and aggressive hybrid could easily be down when the money is needed.\nA) Small caps are the most volatile segment. C) A tax benefit is worthless if the money is locked when the house purchase falls due. D) They differ enormously in suitability.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A scheme's units outstanding rise from 40 crore to 50 crore in a month while net assets rise from ₹800 crore to ₹1,010 crore. What happened to the NAV?\nA) It fell from ₹20 to ₹19  B) It rose from ₹20.00 to ₹20.20  C) It stayed at ₹20  D) It doubled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Opening NAV = 800 ÷ 40 = **₹20.00**. Closing NAV = 1,010 ÷ 50 = **₹20.20**.\nA) Wrong direction. C) The NAV changed slightly. D) Nothing supports doubling. **The lesson: rising net assets can come from inflows as well as performance — NAV per unit is what measures performance.**\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Which statement about SIPs is MOST accurate?\nA) SIPs guarantee positive returns  B) SIPs impose discipline and average the purchase price, reducing timing risk — but they do not eliminate market risk and can lose money in a falling market  C) SIPs are risk-free  D) SIPs work only in rising markets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SIPs address **when** you invest, not **what** you invest in.\nA) and C) overstate the benefit. D) SIPs are in fact most beneficial in **volatile or falling** markets, where they accumulate units cheaply.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An investor holds a liquid fund and needs money urgently on a Friday. She redeems at 12:30 p.m. When does she receive funds, and at what NAV?\nA) Next week, at Monday's NAV  B) At the same day's NAV (the 3:00 p.m. redemption cut-off applies), with proceeds typically credited the next business day; many liquid funds also offer instant redemption up to prescribed limits  C) She cannot redeem  D) At the previous day's NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Redemption cut-off is 3:00 p.m.** for all schemes, including liquid funds, so the same day's NAV applies.\nA) Redemption is processed the same day. C) Liquid funds are among the most liquid products available. D) The **previous day's NAV** rule applies to liquid fund **purchases**, not redemptions — a very common confusion.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A client wants ₹40,000 a month from a ₹70,00,000 corpus in retirement. Which combination is most appropriate?\nA) An IDCW option in an equity fund  B) An SWP from a conservative hybrid or debt-oriented portfolio, sized so withdrawals are sustainable, with an equity portion for inflation protection  C) A small cap SIP  D) An ELSS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **SWP** is more tax-efficient and gives a **predictable amount** (unlike IDCW, which is discretionary and variable). The withdrawal rate here is 40,000 × 12 ÷ 70,00,000 = **6.9% a year**, which is aggressive — so some equity is needed for growth, and the rate may need review.\nA) IDCW amounts are not guaranteed and are taxed at slab rates. C) A SIP invests rather than withdraws. D) ELSS locks money for 3 years.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Which is the correct treatment when a mutual fund scheme merges with another?\nA) It is tax-free for the investor if SEBI-approved  B) Investors receive units of the surviving scheme; a SEBI-approved merger of schemes is generally not treated as a transfer for capital gains purposes  C) It is always taxable  D) Investors must redeem\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In a SEBI-approved **scheme merger**, unit-holders receive units of the surviving scheme and this is generally **not treated as a transfer**, so no immediate capital gains arise. Investors who disagree are given an **exit option without load**.\nA) States the outcome but omits the mechanism and exit option. C) A merger is distinguished from a switch precisely on this point. D) Investors may exit but are not required to.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An investor buys ₹2,00,000 of a liquid fund at 1:00 p.m. with funds realised immediately, and redeems it three days later at 2:00 p.m. Which NAVs apply?\nA) Both same-day  B) Purchase at the previous day's NAV (before the 1:30 p.m. cut-off); redemption at that day's NAV (before the 3:00 p.m. cut-off)  C) Both previous-day  D) Both next-day\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Liquid fund purchase before 1:30 p.m. with realised funds → previous day's NAV.** **Redemption before 3:00 p.m. → same day's NAV.**\nA) Ignores the special liquid fund purchase rule. C) The redemption rule differs. D) Neither leg is next-day here. **The asymmetry between the purchase and redemption rules is exactly what this tests.**\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which fund would you expect to have the LOWEST Total Expense Ratio?\nA) An actively managed small cap fund  B) An index fund or ETF, because it passively tracks an index and requires no research team  C) A credit risk fund  D) A focused fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Passive management** requires no stock selection, so costs are far lower.\nA), C) and D) are all actively managed, requiring research teams and higher fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A fund house launches a new scheme at ₹10 NAV during its NFO. Is this an advantage?\nA) Yes — units are cheap at ₹10  B) No — the ₹10 is simply an accounting starting point; a new scheme has no track record and the ₹10 NAV confers no advantage  C) Yes — the NAV can only rise  D) Yes — NFOs are discounted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every NFO starts at ₹10 by convention. It buys the **same securities at the same market prices** as an existing fund, but with **no performance history** to assess.\nA) The \"cheap at ₹10\" pitch is the NAV fallacy applied to NFOs. C) NAVs can and do fall below ₹10. D) There is no discount — an NFO is not an IPO.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An investor holds units in both the Growth and IDCW options of the same scheme. After 5 years, which shows the higher NAV, and why?\nA) IDCW  B) Growth — because IDCW distributions have progressively reduced that option's NAV, while Growth retained everything  C) They are identical  D) Cannot be determined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both options hold the **same portfolio**, but each IDCW payout **permanently reduces** the IDCW option's NAV. The Growth NAV compounds on the full amount.\nA) Reverses the mechanism. C) They diverge from the first distribution onward. D) The relationship is deterministic. **Total return can still be similar if distributions were reinvested — but Growth wins after tax.**\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Which set of scheme features is internally consistent?\nA) Liquid fund with 5-year maturity instruments  B) Large cap fund with 82% in the top 100, TER 1.8%, benchmarked to the Nifty 100  C) ELSS with no lock-in  D) Gilt fund holding 60% corporate bonds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 82% in the top 100 satisfies the **≥80% large cap** requirement, and the Nifty 100 is the appropriate benchmark.\nA) Liquid funds are capped at **91 days**. C) ELSS **must** have a 3-year lock-in. D) A gilt fund must hold **≥80% government securities**.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A client asks whether he should stop his SIP because markets have fallen 25%. The best advice is:\nA) Stop immediately  B) Continue if the goal and horizon are unchanged — a falling market is when a SIP accumulates the most units cheaply, and stopping locks in the loss while forfeiting the recovery  C) Double the SIP with borrowed money  D) Switch to a small cap fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Stopping a SIP in a downturn is the **exact opposite** of what rupee-cost averaging is designed to exploit — it means buying only when prices were high.\nA) The classic behavioural error the adviser exists to prevent. C) Borrowing to invest adds leverage risk. D) Increasing risk after a fall does not address the concern raised.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single principle best organises mutual fund selection?\nA) Pick the lowest NAV  B) Match the scheme category to the goal's horizon and the client's risk profile, prefer the direct plan for cost, and use systematic transactions to manage timing  C) Pick last year's best performer  D) Pick the newest NFO\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three decisions in the right order: **category (suitability) → plan (cost) → mode (timing)**.\nA) The NAV fallacy. C) Return-chasing is one of the most reliably value-destroying habits. D) An NFO has no track record and no inherent advantage.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.**\n\n### Case: The Nair Family's Mutual Fund Portfolio\n\nRajesh Nair, **42**, holds the following:\n\n| Holding | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Aggressive hybrid fund | ₹18,00,000, **regular** plan, TER 2.05% |\n| Small cap fund | ₹9,00,000, direct plan |\n| Liquid fund | ₹6,00,000 |\n| ELSS | ₹3,00,000, invested 14 months ago |\n\nHe has two goals: a **daughter's school admission fee of ₹4,50,000 in 8 months**, and **retirement in 18 years**. He is in the **30%** tax bracket, and has been taking the **IDCW option** on his aggressive hybrid fund \"for regular income\", though he does not currently need that income.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** Rajesh needs ₹4,50,000 in 8 months. Which holding should fund it?\nA) The small cap fund  B) The liquid fund, which holds ₹6,00,000 and carries minimal price risk over 8 months  C) The ELSS  D) The aggressive hybrid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **liquid fund** already holds more than the required amount and, holding instruments of up to **91 days**, has minimal price risk over an 8-month horizon.\nA) Small caps are the most volatile segment — wholly unsuitable for a fixed near-term obligation. C) The **ELSS is locked in for 3 years** and has only completed 14 months, so it is legally unavailable. D) At roughly 70% equity it carries real drawdown risk over 8 months.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Can Rajesh redeem the ELSS if he needs to?\nA) Yes, with an exit load  B) No — ELSS carries a hard 3-year lock-in and he is only 14 months in, so roughly 22 months remain  C) Yes, freely  D) Yes, half of it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **3-year ELSS lock-in is absolute**; there is no exit load mechanism, partial redemption or hardship provision.\nA) No load can unlock it. C) and D) Redemption of any part is impossible before 3 years. **This is precisely why ELSS must never be counted as accessible savings.**\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What is wrong with taking the IDCW option on the aggressive hybrid fund?\nA) Nothing  B) He does not need the income, yet IDCW is taxed at his 30% slab rate each time it is paid and permanently reduces the NAV — the Growth option would defer tax and compound the full amount  C) IDCW pays more  D) IDCW is tax-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two problems. **Tax:** IDCW is taxable at **slab rates**, so a 30% taxpayer loses nearly a third of each distribution immediately. **Compounding:** each payout **reduces the NAV**, so less capital remains invested for the 18-year retirement goal. Since he does **not need the income**, the Growth option is clearly better.\nA) It is costing him materially. C) IDCW does not increase total return — it merely moves value out of the NAV. D) It has been taxable since DDT was abolished.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** His aggressive hybrid is in a **regular** plan with a 2.05% TER; the direct plan charges 1.05%. Over the 18 years to retirement on ₹18,00,000 at 12% gross, roughly what does the regular plan cost him?\nA) About ₹18,000  B) Roughly ₹15–20 lakh of forgone corpus  C) Nothing  D) The regular plan is better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net returns: regular **9.95%**, direct **10.95%**. Over 18 years: ₹18,00,000 × (1.0995)¹⁸ ≈ **₹1.00 crore**; × (1.1095)¹⁸ ≈ **₹1.18 crore**. The gap is roughly **₹18 lakh** — on an **identical portfolio**.\nA) Understates it by a factor of a hundred. C) A 1% annual drag over 18 years is very large. D) The direct plan holds the same securities at lower cost, so it must end higher.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** What is the most appropriate set of recommendations for Rajesh?\nA) Move everything to small caps for growth  B) Fund the 8-month goal from the liquid fund; switch the aggressive hybrid from IDCW to Growth and from regular to direct; keep the ELSS until the lock-in ends; and review whether ₹9,00,000 in small caps suits his risk profile — noting that the switches are taxable events  C) Stop investing entirely  D) Redeem the ELSS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Four actions, each addressing a specific defect: **liquidity** for the near goal, **tax and compounding** (IDCW → Growth), **cost** (regular → direct), and **suitability** (a 25% small cap weighting is aggressive for a 42-year-old with a defined retirement goal).\n\n⚠️ **The essential caveat:** switching from regular to direct, or from IDCW to Growth, is a **redemption plus a purchase** — a **taxable event**, and possibly subject to exit load. The long-run saving usually far exceeds the one-time tax cost, but it must be computed rather than assumed, and the switch may be staged.\n\nA) Concentrating in the most volatile segment ignores his horizon and profile. C) Stopping forfeits 18 years of compounding toward retirement. D) Redeeming the ELSS is **not legally possible** for another 22 months.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "12", "title": "Portfolio Manager", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "# Chapter 12: Portfolio Manager — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio (23 marks).**\n> A short chapter with a high concentration of **exact numbers** — the ₹50 lakh minimum, the three service types, and the **high watermark / hurdle rate / catch-up** fee mechanics. Learn those and the chapter is largely done.\n\n---\n\n## 12.1 Portfolio Managers in India\n\n**A Portfolio Manager manages a client's securities portfolio individually**, under the **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**.\n\nThe defining structural feature: **securities are held in the client's OWN name and demat account — the portfolio is NOT pooled.**\n\n| | **PMS** | **Mutual fund** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Holding** | Securities in the **client's own name** | **Units** of a pooled scheme |\n| **Pooling** | **None** — an individual portfolio | Fully pooled |\n| **Minimum** | **₹50 lakh** | ₹100–₹500 |\n| **Customisation** | Possible | None |\n| **Disclosure** | To the client | Public — daily NAV, monthly portfolio |\n| **Fees** | Fixed and/or **performance-linked** | Capped **TER** |\n\n---\n\n## 12.2 ⭐ Types of Portfolio Management Services\n\n| Type | Who decides | Who executes | Client involvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Discretionary** | The **portfolio manager** | The manager | None — full authority delegated |\n| **Non-discretionary** | The **client** | The manager | The client approves **every** transaction |\n| **Advisory** | The **client** | The **client** (the manager only advises) | Complete — the manager never transacts |\n\n> 🧠 **The clean test — who pulls the trigger?**\n> **Discretionary** → the manager decides *and* executes.\n> **Non-discretionary** → the client decides, the manager executes.\n> **Advisory** → the manager only recommends; the client decides *and* executes.\n\n---\n\n## 12.3 Structure of PMS in India\n\n- The client signs a **PMS agreement** with the portfolio manager setting out the service type, investment approach, fees, risks and termination terms.\n- The client opens a **separate bank account and demat account**, operated by the manager under a **Power of Attorney** (for discretionary and non-discretionary services).\n- A **custodian** holds the securities (mandatory for discretionary and non-discretionary PMS).\n- The manager provides **periodic reports** to the client.\n\n---\n\n## 12.4 Registration Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Registration** | Mandatory with **SEBI** under the 2020 Regulations |\n| **Minimum net worth** | **₹5 crore** |\n| **Principal Officer** | Required, with prescribed qualifications and experience |\n| **Compliance Officer** | Mandatory |\n| **Minimum client investment** | **₹50 lakh** |\n\n---\n\n## 12.5 Responsibilities of a Portfolio Manager\n\n- Act in the **client's best interest** — a fiduciary obligation\n- Provide a **Disclosure Document** before the agreement (containing fees, risks, performance and the manager's track record)\n- Maintain **client-wise segregation** of funds and securities\n- **Not commingle** client assets with its own or with other clients'\n- Furnish **periodic reports** (portfolio composition, transactions, performance, fees)\n- Maintain **records** and appoint a compliance officer\n- Redress grievances\n- **Not guarantee or assure any return**\n\n---\n\n## 12.6 ⭐ Costs, Expenses and Fees\n\nFees may be **fixed**, **performance-linked**, or a combination.\n\n| Component | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Fixed fee** | A percentage of assets under management, charged regardless of performance |\n| **Performance/incentive fee** | A share of profits earned above a threshold |\n| **Hurdle rate** | The **minimum return that must be earned before any performance fee applies** |\n| **High watermark** | The **highest portfolio value previously achieved** — performance fees are charged only on gains **above that peak** |\n| **Catch-up** | Once the hurdle is crossed, the manager \"catches up\" a larger share of returns between the hurdle and a defined point |\n| **No catch-up** | The performance fee applies **only to returns above the hurdle** |\n| **Exit load** | A charge for early withdrawal, subject to SEBI caps |\n\n### 🔑 The high watermark principle\n\n**Performance fees are charged only on gains above the highest value the portfolio has previously reached.**\n\n**Why it exists:** without it, a manager could charge a performance fee on the same gains twice — once on the way up, and again after a fall and recovery. The high watermark ensures the client **does not pay twice for recovering ground already lost**.\n\n**Worked example.** Portfolio starts at ₹1 crore.\n- **Year 1:** rises to ₹1.30 crore. Performance fee is charged on the ₹30 lakh gain. **High watermark = ₹1.30 crore.**\n- **Year 2:** falls to ₹1.10 crore. **No performance fee** — the value is below the watermark.\n- **Year 3:** rises to ₹1.45 crore. The fee applies only to the gain **above ₹1.30 crore**, i.e. on **₹15 lakh** — *not* on the ₹35 lakh rise from ₹1.10 crore.\n\n### Hurdle rate example\nHurdle **10%**, performance fee **20%**, no catch-up, on a ₹1 crore portfolio returning **18%**.\n- Return = ₹18 lakh; the hurdle absorbs the first **₹10 lakh**.\n- Fee = 20% × (18 − 10) = 20% × ₹8 lakh = **₹1.6 lakh**.\n\n> ⚠️ **SEBI's investor protections on fees:** performance fees must be charged on a **high watermark** basis; **no upfront fees** may be charged; and exit loads are capped and taper with holding period.\n\n---\n\n## 12.7 The \"Direct Access\" Facility\n\nSince the 2020 Regulations, portfolio managers must offer clients a **direct on-boarding option** — investing **without a distributor**, and therefore **without paying distribution commission**.\n\n| | **Direct access (PMS)** | **Direct plan (mutual fund)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What it avoids** | Distributor commission | Distributor commission |\n| **Effect** | Lower overall cost to the client | Lower TER, higher NAV |\n| **Availability** | Must be offered by every portfolio manager | Must be offered by every scheme |\n\n**How an Investment Adviser helps:** because an IA is paid a **fee by the client** and must **segregate advice from distribution**, the IA can guide the client to the **direct access route**, select the manager and investment approach, and monitor performance — all without any commission incentive.\n\n---\n\n## 12.8 SEBI Requirements on Performance Disclosure\n\nTo make managers comparable and prevent cherry-picking:\n\n- Performance must be disclosed **net of all fees and expenses**\n- It must be calculated using the **time-weighted rate of return (TWRR)** — the standard that neutralises the effect of client cash flows the manager does not control\n- Performance must be shown for the **firm's aggregate**, not just selected accounts\n- Disclosure must be provided in the **Disclosure Document** and in periodic client reports\n- Managers must report to SEBI and to **APMI (the Association of Portfolio Managers in India)**\n- Any claim must be **fair, accurate and not misleading**\n\n> 🧠 **Why TWRR and not money-weighted return?** TWRR removes the distorting effect of when a client added or withdrew money — decisions the *client* made, not the manager. It therefore measures **the manager's skill**. (See Chapter 16.)\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Governing regulations | SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, **2020** |\n| Minimum client investment | **₹50 lakh** |\n| Portfolio manager minimum net worth | **₹5 crore** |\n| How securities are held | In the **client's own name/demat** — **not pooled** |\n| Discretionary | Manager **decides and executes** |\n| Non-discretionary | **Client decides**, manager executes |\n| Advisory | Manager **only advises**; the client decides and executes |\n| The clean test | **Who pulls the trigger?** |\n| Custodian | Mandatory for discretionary and non-discretionary PMS |\n| Power of Attorney | Used to operate the client's bank and demat accounts |\n| Disclosure Document | Given **before** the agreement; fees, risks, track record |\n| Commingling | **Prohibited** — client-wise segregation required |\n| Guaranteeing returns | **Prohibited** |\n| Hurdle rate | Minimum return before any **performance fee** |\n| High watermark | Fees only on gains **above the previous peak** |\n| Why the high watermark exists | So the client **never pays twice** for the same gains |\n| Catch-up | Manager takes a larger share just above the hurdle |\n| Upfront fees | **Not permitted** |\n| Direct access | On-boarding **without a distributor**, avoiding commission |\n| Performance disclosure basis | **Net of fees**, using **TWRR**, firm-wide |\n| Industry association | **APMI** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** the two most-tested items are the **₹50 lakh minimum** (often confused with the AIF's ₹1 crore) and the **high watermark**, which is almost always tested through a three-year up-down-up sequence like the worked example above.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 12: Portfolio Manager — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The three service types and the high watermark are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Which regulations govern portfolio managers, and from which year?\n> **A:** The **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**.\n\n**Q2.** What is the minimum client investment in a PMS?\n> **A:** **₹50 lakh.** *(Do not confuse this with the AIF minimum of ₹1 crore.)*\n\n**Q3.** What is a portfolio manager's minimum net worth requirement?\n> **A:** **₹5 crore.**\n\n**Q4.** How are securities held in a PMS, and why does it matter?\n> **A:** In the **client's own name and demat account** — the portfolio is **not pooled**. The client therefore owns the underlying securities directly rather than units of a scheme.\n\n**Q5.** Name the three types of portfolio management service.\n> **A:** **Discretionary**, **non-discretionary** and **advisory**.\n\n**Q6.** Distinguish the three using the \"who pulls the trigger\" test.\n> **A:** **Discretionary** — the manager **decides and executes**. **Non-discretionary** — the **client decides**, the manager executes. **Advisory** — the manager **only advises**; the client decides **and** executes.\n\n**Q7.** In which PMS types is a custodian mandatory?\n> **A:** **Discretionary and non-discretionary** services — because the manager handles the client's securities.\n\n**Q8.** What is the Power of Attorney used for in a PMS?\n> **A:** To let the manager **operate the client's bank and demat accounts** for discretionary and non-discretionary services.\n\n**Q9.** What must a portfolio manager give the client before the agreement?\n> **A:** A **Disclosure Document**, containing fees, risks, the manager's performance track record and other prescribed information.\n\n**Q10.** Name five responsibilities of a portfolio manager.\n> **A:** Act in the **client's best interest**; provide the **Disclosure Document**; maintain **client-wise segregation** (no commingling); furnish **periodic reports**; and **never guarantee or assure any return**.\n\n**Q11.** What is commingling, and is it permitted?\n> **A:** Mixing a client's funds or securities with the manager's own or with other clients'. It is **prohibited** — client-wise segregation is required.\n\n**Q12.** What is a hurdle rate?\n> **A:** The **minimum return that must be earned before any performance fee applies**.\n\n**Q13.** State the high watermark principle.\n> **A:** **Performance fees are charged only on gains above the highest portfolio value previously achieved.**\n\n**Q14.** Why does the high watermark exist?\n> **A:** So a client **never pays a performance fee twice on the same gains** — once on the way up, and again after a fall and recovery.\n\n**Q15.** A portfolio starts at ₹1 crore, rises to ₹1.30 crore, falls to ₹1.10 crore, then rises to ₹1.45 crore. On what gain is the year-3 performance fee charged?\n> **A:** Only on the **₹15 lakh above the ₹1.30 crore high watermark** — **not** on the ₹35 lakh rise from ₹1.10 crore.\n\n**Q16.** What is the difference between catch-up and no-catch-up?\n> **A:** With **catch-up**, once the hurdle is crossed the manager takes a **larger share** of returns between the hurdle and a defined point. With **no catch-up**, the performance fee applies **only to returns above the hurdle**.\n\n**Q17.** Hurdle 10%, performance fee 20%, no catch-up, ₹1 crore portfolio returning 18%. What is the fee?\n> **A:** Return = ₹18 lakh; the hurdle absorbs the first ₹10 lakh. Fee = 20% × (18 − 10) = 20% × ₹8 lakh = **₹1.6 lakh**.\n\n**Q18.** Are upfront fees permitted in a PMS?\n> **A:** **No** — SEBI prohibits upfront fees. Exit loads are also capped and taper with the holding period.\n\n**Q19.** What is the \"direct access\" facility, and what does it save?\n> **A:** A mandatory **direct on-boarding option** letting a client invest **without a distributor**, and therefore **without paying distribution commission** — the PMS equivalent of a mutual fund's direct plan.\n\n**Q20.** How can an Investment Adviser add value around PMS?\n> **A:** Because the IA is **paid a fee by the client** and must **segregate advice from distribution**, the IA can steer the client to the **direct access route**, select the manager and approach, and monitor performance — with **no commission incentive**.\n\n**Q21.** On what basis must PMS performance be disclosed?\n> **A:** **Net of all fees and expenses**, using the **time-weighted rate of return (TWRR)**, for the **firm's aggregate** rather than selected accounts.\n\n**Q22.** Why is TWRR used rather than a money-weighted return?\n> **A:** TWRR **removes the effect of client cash flows** — decisions the client made, not the manager — so it measures **the manager's skill**.\n\n**Q23.** Which industry association covers portfolio managers?\n> **A:** **APMI** — the Association of Portfolio Managers in India.\n\n**Q24.** Give four differences between a PMS and a mutual fund.\n> **A:** **Holding** (own securities vs pooled units); **minimum** (₹50 lakh vs ₹100–500); **customisation** (possible vs none); **fees** (fixed and/or performance-linked vs a capped TER). Mutual funds also disclose far more publicly.\n\n**Q25.** Can a portfolio manager promise a client a minimum return?\n> **A:** **No.** Guaranteeing or assuring any return is expressly prohibited.\n\n**Q26.** What does the PMS agreement set out?\n> **A:** The **service type**, investment approach, **fees**, risks and **termination terms**.\n\n**Q27.** Which service type gives the client the most control, and which the least?\n> **A:** **Advisory** gives the most control (the client decides and executes); **discretionary** gives the least (full authority is delegated to the manager).\n\n**Q28.** In a purely advisory PMS, who executes the trades?\n> **A:** The **client**. The manager only recommends and never transacts — which is why no custodian or Power of Attorney is required.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 12: Portfolio Manager — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The high watermark and hurdle rate arithmetic appear repeatedly.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Portfolio managers in India are governed by:\nA) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  B) SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020  C) SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012  D) The Companies Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**.\nA) Governs mutual funds. C) Governs AIFs. D) Governs companies generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The minimum investment in a PMS is:\nA) ₹25 lakh  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹1 crore  D) ₹5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹50 lakh**.\nA) and D) are below the threshold. C) ₹1 crore is the **AIF** minimum — the most common confusion in this chapter.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** In a PMS, securities are held:\nA) In a pooled scheme  B) In the client's own name and demat account  C) By SEBI  D) By the distributor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The defining structural feature: **not pooled**.\nA) Describes a mutual fund or AIF. C) and D) never hold client securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In a discretionary PMS:\nA) The client decides every trade  B) The portfolio manager decides and executes  C) The manager only advises  D) SEBI decides\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Full authority is delegated to the manager.\nA) Describes **non-discretionary**. C) Describes **advisory**. D) SEBI regulates but never manages portfolios.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** In a non-discretionary PMS:\nA) The manager decides and executes  B) The client decides and the manager executes  C) The manager only advises  D) No transactions occur\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The client approves **every** transaction; the manager then executes it.\nA) Describes discretionary. C) Describes advisory. D) Transactions certainly occur.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** In an advisory PMS:\nA) The manager executes trades  B) The manager only recommends; the client decides and executes  C) The manager has full discretion  D) A custodian is mandatory\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The manager **never transacts**, which is why no Power of Attorney or custodian is needed.\nA) The manager does not execute in advisory service. C) Describes discretionary. D) Custodians are mandatory only for discretionary and non-discretionary services.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The minimum net worth for a portfolio manager is:\nA) ₹50 lakh  B) ₹5 crore  C) ₹50 crore  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹5 crore**.\nA) Is the client's minimum investment. C) Is the **AMC**'s minimum net worth. D) Is the AIF minimum investment.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The high watermark principle means performance fees are charged:\nA) On every gain  B) Only on gains above the highest portfolio value previously achieved  C) Only in the first year  D) Never\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The client never pays twice for recovering ground already lost.\nA) Would allow double-charging after a fall and recovery. C) and D) are incorrect.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** A hurdle rate is:\nA) The maximum fee  B) The minimum return that must be earned before any performance fee applies  C) The exit load  D) The custodian's charge\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Performance fees begin only above the hurdle.\nA), C) and D) are different charges or concepts.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Can a portfolio manager guarantee returns?\nA) Yes, up to 8%  B) No — guaranteeing or assuring any return is prohibited  C) Yes, for discretionary services  D) Yes, with SEBI approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Expressly prohibited under the regulations.\nA), C) and D) all suggest circumstances in which it would be allowed; none exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Are upfront fees permitted in a PMS?\nA) Yes  B) No — SEBI prohibits them  C) Only for HNIs  D) Only above ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **No upfront fees** may be charged; exit loads are also capped and taper with holding period.\nA), C) and D) suggest exceptions that do not exist.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The document a portfolio manager must provide before the agreement is the:\nA) SID  B) Disclosure Document  C) KIM  D) Annual report\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Disclosure Document** sets out fees, risks and the manager's track record.\nA) and C) are mutual fund documents. D) Is a periodic disclosure, not a pre-agreement document.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Commingling of client assets is:\nA) Permitted  B) Prohibited — client-wise segregation is required  C) Required  D) Optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Client funds and securities must be kept **separate** from the manager's own and from other clients'.\nA), C) and D) all contradict the segregation requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** PMS performance must be disclosed:\nA) Gross of fees  B) Net of all fees and expenses, using TWRR  C) For selected accounts only  D) Only to SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Net of fees**, on a **time-weighted** basis, for the firm's aggregate.\nA) Gross figures would overstate what clients actually earned. C) Cherry-picking accounts is precisely what the rule prevents. D) Clients must receive it too.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Why is TWRR used for PMS performance?\nA) It is easier  B) It removes the effect of client cash flows, so it measures the manager's skill  C) It shows higher returns  D) SEBI has no preference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cash flows are the **client's** decisions, not the manager's.\nA) It is more complex than a simple return. C) It can be higher or lower. D) SEBI specifically prescribes it.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The \"direct access\" facility in PMS allows a client to:\nA) Trade directly on the exchange  B) On-board without a distributor, avoiding distribution commission  C) Withdraw instantly  D) Guarantee returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The PMS equivalent of a mutual fund's **direct plan**.\nA) The manager still executes. C) PMS liquidity depends on the agreement. D) Guarantees are prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A custodian is mandatory for which PMS types?\nA) Advisory only  B) Discretionary and non-discretionary  C) None  D) All three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the manager handles client securities in those two.\nA) Advisory involves no execution by the manager. C) Custody is required where the manager transacts. D) Advisory is excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The industry association for portfolio managers is:\nA) AMFI  B) APMI  C) ANMI  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — the **Association of Portfolio Managers in India**.\nA) Covers mutual fund distributors. C) Covers stock brokers. D) Is the regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Which gives the client the MOST control?\nA) Discretionary  B) Advisory  C) Non-discretionary  D) A mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In **advisory** the client decides **and** executes.\nA) Delegates the most. C) The client decides but the manager executes. D) A mutual fund gives no control over holdings at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A Power of Attorney in a PMS is used to:\nA) Guarantee returns  B) Operate the client's bank and demat accounts  C) Transfer ownership to the manager  D) Waive fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It authorises operation of the accounts, **not** transfer of ownership.\nA) Guarantees are prohibited. C) The client remains the owner throughout. D) A PoA has nothing to do with fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** PMS fees may be:\nA) Only fixed  B) Fixed, performance-linked, or a combination  C) Only performance-linked  D) Zero always\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Both structures, or a blend, are permitted subject to SEBI's conditions.\nA) and C) are each only one of the permitted forms. D) Managers charge for their service.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** \"Catch-up\" in a fee structure means:\nA) The client recovers losses  B) Once the hurdle is crossed, the manager takes a larger share of returns just above it  C) Fees are waived  D) The manager repays fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With **no catch-up**, the fee applies only to returns above the hurdle.\nA), C) and D) misdescribe the mechanism.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which statement about PMS disclosure compared with a mutual fund is correct?\nA) PMS discloses more publicly  B) A mutual fund discloses far more publicly — daily NAV and monthly portfolios — while PMS reports to its client  C) Neither discloses  D) They are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mutual funds face the **highest** public disclosure standards of the three managed vehicles.\nA) Reverses it. C) Both disclose. D) The regimes differ substantially.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** A portfolio manager must appoint a:\nA) Distributor  B) Compliance officer  C) Sponsor  D) Trustee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **compliance officer** is mandatory, as is a Principal Officer.\nA) Distribution is optional. C) and D) belong to the mutual fund structure.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** What does the PMS agreement set out?\nA) Only the fees  B) Service type, investment approach, fees, risks and termination terms  C) Guaranteed returns  D) The client's tax liability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is the complete contract governing the relationship.\nA) Fees are one element. C) Guarantees are prohibited. D) Tax is the client's own affair.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which vehicle allows a customised portfolio for an individual client?\nA) A mutual fund  B) A PMS  C) An index fund  D) An ETF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the portfolio is **individual and not pooled**, it can be tailored.\nA), C) and D) all give every investor the same portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Exit loads in a PMS are:\nA) Unlimited  B) Capped by SEBI and taper with the holding period  C) Prohibited entirely  D) Fixed at 5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI caps them and requires them to reduce over time.\nA) They are capped. C) They are permitted within limits. D) No single fixed rate applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Who owns the securities in a discretionary PMS?\nA) The portfolio manager  B) The client  C) The custodian  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **client** owns them throughout; the manager merely operates the account under a PoA.\nA) The manager has authority, not ownership. C) The custodian holds them in safekeeping. D) SEBI regulates only.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Registration with SEBI for a portfolio manager is:\nA) Optional  B) Mandatory  C) Required only above ₹100 crore AUM  D) Handled by AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Mandatory** under the 2020 Regulations.\nA) and C) suggest exemptions that do not exist. D) AMFI covers mutual fund distributors.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which is TRUE of PMS reporting to clients?\nA) No reports are required  B) Periodic reports covering portfolio composition, transactions, performance and fees  C) Annual reports only  D) Reports only on request\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Periodic reporting is a core obligation.\nA) Reporting is mandatory. C) Reporting is more frequent than annual. D) It must be proactive.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A portfolio manager's fiduciary obligation means acting:\nA) In the manager's own interest  B) In the client's best interest  C) In SEBI's interest  D) Neutrally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **client's interest comes first**.\nA) That is the conflict a fiduciary duty forbids. C) SEBI sets rules but is not the beneficiary. D) Neutrality is not the standard — the client's interest is paramount.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which client would be eligible for a PMS?\nA) One with ₹20 lakh  B) One with ₹65 lakh  C) One with ₹5 lakh  D) One with ₹45 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only **₹65 lakh** clears the **₹50 lakh** minimum.\nA), C) and D) all fall below it.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** In which PMS type does the manager NOT execute transactions?\nA) Discretionary  B) Non-discretionary  C) Advisory  D) All of them\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In **advisory**, the manager only recommends; the **client** executes.\nA) and B) both involve execution by the manager. D) Execution occurs in two of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The high watermark protects the client from:\nA) Market losses  B) Paying performance fees twice on the same gains  C) Fraud  D) Taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It prevents double-charging after a fall and recovery.\nA) It does not prevent losses — only fees on recovered ground. C) and D) are addressed by other mechanisms.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which is the correct comparison of minimum investments?\nA) MF ₹500, PMS ₹50 lakh, AIF ₹1 crore  B) MF ₹50 lakh, PMS ₹500, AIF ₹1 crore  C) All ₹50 lakh  D) All ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The ladder: **₹500 → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore**.\nB) Swaps MF and PMS. C) and D) flatten a deliberate regulatory ladder.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A PMS portfolio starts at ₹2 crore, rises to ₹2.60 crore in year 1, falls to ₹2.20 crore in year 2, then rises to ₹2.90 crore in year 3. On what amount is the year-3 performance fee charged?\nA) ₹70 lakh  B) ₹30 lakh — the gain above the ₹2.60 crore high watermark  C) ₹90 lakh  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **high watermark is ₹2.60 crore** (the year-1 peak). The year-3 value of ₹2.90 crore exceeds it by **₹30 lakh**, and only that gain attracts a performance fee.\nA) ₹70 lakh is the rise from ₹2.20 crore — charging on it would mean **billing twice** for recovering the ₹40 lakh already lost. C) ₹90 lakh is the total gain since inception. D) A fee is due, since the watermark was exceeded.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** In year 2 of Q36 (₹2.60 crore falling to ₹2.20 crore), what performance fee is charged?\nA) A reduced fee  B) None — the portfolio is below its high watermark  C) The full fee  D) A negative fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Below the watermark, **no performance fee arises**.\nA) There is no partial charge. C) Charging on a loss would be indefensible. D) Managers do not refund fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A ₹3 crore portfolio returns 16%. The hurdle is 9% and the performance fee 20% with no catch-up. What is the fee?\nA) ₹9.60 lakh  B) ₹4.20 lakh  C) ₹5.40 lakh  D) ₹48 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Return = 16% × ₹3 crore = **₹48 lakh**. The hurdle absorbs 9% × ₹3 crore = **₹27 lakh**. Fee = 20% × (48 − 27) = 20% × ₹21 lakh = **₹4.20 lakh**.\nA) Applies 20% to the whole ₹48 lakh, ignoring the hurdle. C) Uses the hurdle amount incorrectly. D) Is the gross return, not the fee.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client wants to approve every transaction personally but does not want to place the orders himself. Which service suits?\nA) Discretionary  B) Non-discretionary  C) Advisory  D) A mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Non-discretionary**: the **client decides**, the **manager executes**.\nA) Would remove his approval entirely. C) Would require him to place the orders himself. D) Offers no transaction-level involvement at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A client with ₹42 lakh wants a PMS. What should the adviser say?\nA) He qualifies  B) He is below the ₹50 lakh minimum, so a mutual fund portfolio is the appropriate route  C) He should borrow the difference  D) PMS has no minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹42 lakh falls **₹8 lakh short**, so PMS is legally unavailable to him.\nA) He does not qualify. C) Borrowing to meet an investment minimum is poor advice. D) The ₹50 lakh minimum is mandatory.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Why does the high watermark matter more in a volatile strategy?\nA) It does not matter  B) Volatile portfolios fall and recover more often, so without a watermark the manager would repeatedly charge fees on the same recovered gains  C) It reduces volatility  D) It increases fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **more often a portfolio falls and recovers**, the more opportunities there would be to double-charge.\nA) It matters most precisely where volatility is high. C) It is a fee mechanism, not a risk control. D) It **reduces** the fees payable.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A portfolio manager quotes performance \"gross of fees, for our best-performing account\". What is wrong?\nA) Nothing  B) SEBI requires disclosure net of all fees and expenses, on a TWRR basis, for the firm's aggregate — not cherry-picked accounts  C) Only the gross figure is wrong  D) Only the account selection is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Two breaches**: gross-of-fees overstates what clients actually received, and selecting the best account is exactly the cherry-picking the aggregate rule prevents.\nA) Both elements breach the disclosure requirements. C) and D) each identify only one of the two problems.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A client in an advisory PMS receives a recommendation but does not act on it, and the stock rises. Who bears responsibility?\nA) The manager  B) The client — in advisory service the client decides and executes, so the decision and its consequences are the client's  C) SEBI  D) The custodian\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Advisory service transfers the **decision** to the client.\nA) The manager discharged its duty by advising. C) SEBI regulates conduct, not outcomes. D) No custodian is involved in pure advisory service.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which correctly matches PMS type to whether a Power of Attorney is needed?\nA) All three need a PoA  B) Discretionary and non-discretionary need one (the manager operates the accounts); advisory does not  C) Only advisory needs one  D) None need one\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A PoA is needed only where the **manager transacts**.\nA) Advisory involves no execution by the manager. C) Reverses it. D) Two of the three require it.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A client compares a PMS charging 2.5% fixed with a mutual fund at a 1.2% TER, both targeting large caps. What is the key trade-off?\nA) The PMS is always better  B) The PMS costs more but offers direct ownership and customisation; the mutual fund is cheaper, more liquid and more transparently disclosed  C) They are identical  D) The mutual fund is always better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cost versus customisation and direct ownership — the fair statement of the choice.\nA) and D) state absolutes; the right answer depends on what the client values. C) They differ in cost, structure, liquidity and disclosure.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A ₹1.5 crore portfolio has a high watermark of ₹1.8 crore. It rises to ₹1.75 crore this year. What performance fee applies?\nA) On ₹25 lakh  B) None — ₹1.75 crore is still below the ₹1.8 crore watermark  C) On ₹1.75 crore  D) On ₹5 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Despite a **₹25 lakh gain this year**, the portfolio has not regained its previous peak, so **no performance fee is due**.\nA) That gain is recovery of previously lost ground. C) Fees never apply to the whole portfolio value. D) ₹1.75 crore is ₹5 lakh **below**, not above, the watermark.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which statement about the direct access facility is correct?\nA) It is optional for portfolio managers  B) Every portfolio manager must offer it, allowing on-boarding without a distributor and therefore without commission  C) It applies only above ₹5 crore  D) It guarantees lower returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Mandated by the 2020 Regulations, mirroring mutual funds' direct plans.\nA) It is compulsory. C) No such threshold exists. D) Avoiding commission **improves** net returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A portfolio manager mixes one client's securities with another's for operational convenience. This is:\nA) Efficient and permitted  B) Prohibited — client-wise segregation is mandatory and commingling is a serious breach  C) Permitted with consent  D) Required by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Commingling is expressly prohibited**; each client's assets must be separately identifiable.\nA) Convenience does not justify it. C) Client consent cannot override the regulation. D) SEBI requires the opposite.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An IA advises a client on selecting a PMS. How should the IA be compensated, and which route should be recommended?\nA) Commission from the portfolio manager  B) A fee from the client, recommending the direct access route since the IA has no commission incentive  C) Both fee and commission  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An IA is **paid by the client** and must **segregate advice from distribution**, so recommending the commission-free route costs the adviser nothing.\nA) Would create the exact conflict segregation prevents. C) Dual compensation is prohibited for the same client. D) Advisers are entitled to a fee.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Which combination correctly describes a discretionary PMS?\nA) Client decides, client executes  B) Manager decides and executes, under a PoA, with a mandatory custodian and securities in the client's own name  C) Manager advises only  D) Pooled units issued\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All four elements are correct.\nA) Describes advisory. C) Describes advisory. D) PMS never issues units — that is the pooled-vehicle model.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A ₹4 crore portfolio returns 7% against a 10% hurdle. What performance fee is due?\nA) 20% of ₹28 lakh  B) None — the return is below the hurdle  C) 20% of ₹40 lakh  D) A negative fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **7% return is below the 10% hurdle**, so no performance fee arises (a fixed fee may still apply).\nA) Would charge on the whole return, ignoring the hurdle entirely. C) Uses the hurdle amount as the base. D) Managers do not pay negative fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which vehicle would suit a client with ₹80 lakh who wants securities held in his own name and a say in the strategy?\nA) A mutual fund  B) A non-discretionary PMS  C) An AIF  D) An index fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He clears the **₹50 lakh** minimum, wants **direct ownership** (which only PMS gives) and wants involvement (non-discretionary).\nA), D) issue pooled units with no say in holdings. C) Requires **₹1 crore** and issues pooled units.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A portfolio manager charges an upfront fee of 1% at on-boarding. This is:\nA) Permitted  B) Prohibited — SEBI does not allow upfront fees in a PMS  C) Permitted above ₹1 crore  D) Required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Upfront fees are not permitted.**\nA), C) and D) all contradict the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Why does SEBI prescribe TWRR rather than allowing managers to choose their return measure?\nA) To make figures look better  B) To ensure comparability and to measure manager skill by neutralising client cash flows the manager does not control  C) It is simpler  D) There is no reason\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **common, cash-flow-neutral standard** makes managers genuinely comparable.\nA) TWRR can be lower than a money-weighted figure. C) It is more complex. D) The rationale is explicit.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A client asks whether his PMS holdings are protected if the portfolio manager fails. The answer is:\nA) They are lost  B) The securities are in his own name with a custodian, so they remain his — though market risk and any operational misconduct are separate matters  C) SEBI compensates fully  D) There is no protection\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Direct ownership plus custody** is the structural protection.\nA) Ownership does not transfer to the manager. C) SEBI does not indemnify investors. D) The ownership structure is itself a meaningful protection.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which correctly ranks MF, PMS and AIF by public disclosure, highest first?\nA) AIF, PMS, MF  B) MF, PMS, AIF  C) PMS, MF, AIF  D) All equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Mutual funds** publish daily NAV and monthly portfolios; **PMS** reports to its client; **AIFs** disclose least.\nA) Inverts it. C) Misplaces the mutual fund. D) The regimes differ sharply.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A performance fee structure has a 10% hurdle **with catch-up**. A ₹1 crore portfolio returns 15%. Compared with no catch-up, the fee will be:\nA) Lower  B) Higher, because the manager takes a larger share of returns just above the hurdle  C) The same  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Catch-up** lets the manager recoup a share of the returns falling within the hurdle band, so the fee exceeds a no-catch-up calculation.\nA) Catch-up favours the manager. C) The structures differ by design. D) The hurdle was exceeded, so a fee is due.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** In an advisory PMS, is a custodian required?\nA) Yes, always  B) No — the manager never handles the client's securities  C) Only above ₹1 crore  D) Only for equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Custody is required only where the **manager transacts**, i.e. discretionary and non-discretionary services.\nA) Advisory is the exception. C) and D) are not the basis for the requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A client's PMS agreement does not specify the fee structure. What is the position?\nA) Acceptable  B) A serious deficiency — the agreement and Disclosure Document must set out fees clearly before the client commits  C) Fees can be set later  D) SEBI sets the fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fee transparency **before** commitment is a core requirement.\nA) It is a clear breach. C) The client must know the cost in advance. D) SEBI caps certain charges but does not set each manager's fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which is the strongest argument for a PMS over a mutual fund for a ₹2 crore investor?\nA) It is cheaper  B) Direct ownership of securities, the possibility of customisation, and a concentrated strategy the client can see holding by holding  C) It is more liquid  D) It discloses more publicly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — These are PMS's genuine structural advantages.\nA) PMS is generally **more** expensive. C) Mutual funds are more liquid. D) Mutual funds disclose far more publicly.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A ₹5 crore portfolio has a 2% fixed fee and returns 11% against a 10% hurdle with a 20% performance fee, no catch-up. What are the total fees?\nA) ₹10 lakh  B) ₹11 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh fixed + ₹1 lakh performance = ₹11 lakh  D) ₹1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Fixed fee** = 2% × ₹5 crore = **₹10 lakh**. **Return** = 11% × ₹5 crore = ₹55 lakh; hurdle = 10% × ₹5 crore = ₹50 lakh; **performance fee** = 20% × (55 − 50) = 20% × ₹5 lakh = **₹1 lakh**. Total = **₹11 lakh**.\nA) Counts the fixed fee only. B) States the right total but option C shows the correct decomposition. D) Counts the performance fee only. **Note total fees of ₹11 lakh on a ₹55 lakh gross return consume 20% of it.**\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which statement about ownership in a PMS is correct?\nA) The manager owns the securities  B) The client owns them; the manager operates the account under a PoA and a custodian holds them  C) The custodian owns them  D) Ownership is shared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Authority to transact is not ownership.\nA) The manager has authority, never title. C) The custodian holds in safekeeping. D) There is no shared ownership.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A client is told his PMS returned 22% while the Disclosure Document shows the firm's aggregate at 14%. What should he ask?\nA) Nothing  B) Whether the 22% is his own account net of fees, and how it reconciles with the firm-wide TWRR figure — individual accounts vary with timing of cash flows  C) To switch managers immediately  D) For a guarantee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Individual results legitimately differ from the aggregate because of **cash-flow timing and start dates**; the right response is to understand the basis, not to assume wrongdoing or superiority.\nA) The gap deserves an explanation. C) Premature without understanding the reason. D) Guarantees are prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Which is the correct sequence for on-boarding a PMS client?\nA) Invest first, sign later  B) Disclosure Document → PMS agreement → open bank and demat accounts → PoA → funding  C) PoA first, then disclosure  D) No documentation is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Disclosure **before** commitment, then the contract, then the operational accounts and authority, then money.\nA) Reverses the protection entirely. C) Authority cannot precede disclosure and agreement. D) PMS is heavily documented.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A portfolio manager wants to place its own trades alongside client trades. What must it ensure?\nA) Nothing  B) That client interests come first and there is no front-running or preferential allocation — segregation and fair allocation are mandatory  C) Its own trades take priority  D) It may not trade at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **fiduciary duty** and segregation rules govern proprietary trading alongside client accounts.\nA) Serious conflicts arise. C) Would breach the fiduciary duty directly. D) Proprietary trading is not banned outright, but it is constrained.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which fee arrangement is most aligned with a client's interests?\nA) A high fixed fee only  B) A modest fixed fee with a performance fee subject to a hurdle and high watermark  C) Upfront fees  D) A performance fee with no hurdle or watermark\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The manager is paid extra **only for genuine outperformance above a threshold and above the prior peak**.\nA) Pays the same regardless of results. C) Prohibited. D) Would allow charging on mediocre returns and on repeatedly recovered ground.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client in a discretionary PMS disagrees with a trade the manager made. What is his position?\nA) He can reverse it  B) He delegated decision-making authority, so the manager was entitled to act — his remedy is to review the mandate or terminate the agreement  C) The manager must compensate him  D) SEBI will reverse it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Discretionary means discretion**; the client cannot second-guess individual trades made within the mandate.\nA) Executed trades cannot simply be undone. C) Compensation would arise only from a breach of duty, not from disagreement. D) SEBI does not reverse trades.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which is TRUE of PMS liquidity compared with an open-ended mutual fund?\nA) PMS is more liquid  B) PMS liquidity is moderate and governed by the agreement, whereas an open-ended fund redeems on any business day  C) They are identical  D) PMS cannot be exited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exit terms, notice periods and any load are set in the **agreement**.\nA) Open-ended funds are more liquid. C) The mechanisms differ. D) Exit is possible, subject to the agreed terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** What is the practical significance of PMS securities being in the client's own demat account?\nA) None  B) The client can see every holding directly, retains legal title, and the assets do not form part of the manager's estate  C) The client must trade them himself  D) It increases returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three real consequences: **transparency, legal title and insolvency remoteness**.\nA) It is the defining structural feature. C) The manager transacts under the PoA. D) It affects structure and risk, not gross returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client with ₹55 lakh is choosing between a PMS and a mutual fund. Which factor should weigh MOST heavily?\nA) Which had the better return last year  B) Whether he genuinely needs direct ownership and customisation enough to justify the higher cost and lower liquidity  C) The NAV level  D) Which has more clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He barely clears the minimum, so the honest question is whether PMS's **structural advantages** justify its **cost and liquidity disadvantages**.\nA) Return-chasing is unreliable. C) NAV is a mutual fund concept and says nothing about value. D) Scale is not a suitability criterion.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A PMS portfolio: start ₹5 crore. Year 1 → ₹6.20 crore. Year 2 → ₹5.40 crore. Year 3 → ₹6.80 crore. Performance fee 15%, high watermark applies. Compute the fee in each year.\nA) Y1 ₹18 lakh; Y2 nil; Y3 ₹9 lakh  B) Y1 ₹18 lakh; Y2 nil; Y3 ₹21 lakh  C) Y1 ₹18 lakh; Y2 ₹12 lakh; Y3 ₹21 lakh  D) Y1 nil; Y2 nil; Y3 ₹27 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Year 1:** gain = 6.20 − 5.00 = ₹1.20 crore; fee = 15% × 1.20 crore = **₹18 lakh**. Watermark set at **₹6.20 crore**. **Year 2:** value ₹5.40 crore is **below** the watermark → **nil**. **Year 3:** value ₹6.80 crore exceeds the watermark by 6.80 − 6.20 = **₹60 lakh**; fee = 15% × 60 lakh = **₹9 lakh**.\nB) Charges on the ₹1.40 crore rise from ₹5.40 crore — double-billing the recovery. C) Additionally charges a fee on a loss year. D) Omits the year-1 fee. **The watermark saved the client ₹12 lakh in year 3 alone.**\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A ₹8 crore portfolio returns 19%. Hurdle 12%, performance fee 20%, no catch-up, plus a 1.5% fixed fee. What are the total fees, and the client's net return?\nA) Fees ₹23.2 lakh; net return about 16.1%  B) Fees ₹12 lakh; net 17.5%  C) Fees ₹30.4 lakh; net 15.2%  D) Fees ₹11.2 lakh; net 17.6%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Gross return** = 19% × ₹8 crore = **₹1.52 crore**. **Fixed fee** = 1.5% × ₹8 crore = **₹12 lakh**. **Hurdle** = 12% × ₹8 crore = ₹96 lakh; **performance fee** = 20% × (152 − 96) = 20% × ₹56 lakh = **₹11.2 lakh**. **Total fees = ₹23.2 lakh.** Net gain = 152 − 23.2 = ₹128.8 lakh → **16.1%**.\nB) Counts only the fixed fee. C) Applies 20% to the whole return. D) Counts only the performance fee. **Fees consumed about 15% of the gross gain.**\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Two clients join the same PMS strategy on different dates. Client A's account returns 18%, Client B's 11%, while the firm's TWRR is 14%. What explains this?\nA) The manager favoured Client A  B) Different entry dates and cash-flow timing produce different individual outcomes; TWRR neutralises exactly those effects to isolate manager skill  C) The figures must be wrong  D) TWRR is meaningless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Individual money-weighted outcomes legitimately differ from the firm's TWRR because **clients choose when to invest and withdraw** — decisions the manager does not control.\nA) Preferential treatment would be a breach, but timing alone explains the spread. C) The pattern is entirely normal. D) TWRR is precisely the measure that makes managers comparable.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A portfolio manager offers a client a \"guaranteed 12% with upside participation\". Identify every problem.\nA) None  B) Guaranteeing returns is prohibited, and if any upfront fee is attached that is separately prohibited — the offer breaches the regulations on its face  C) Only the upside claim is a problem  D) It is acceptable with client consent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Guaranteeing or assuring any return is expressly prohibited** for a portfolio manager, regardless of what the client agrees to.\nA) The guarantee alone is a clear breach. C) The **guarantee** is the primary problem. D) Client consent cannot authorise a prohibited practice. **A client offered this should treat it as a red flag about the manager's compliance culture.**\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A ₹3 crore portfolio, watermark ₹3 crore, rises to ₹3.45 crore. Fee 20% above a 10% hurdle, no catch-up. Compute the fee and the resulting new watermark.\nA) Fee ₹9 lakh; watermark ₹3.45 crore  B) Fee ₹3 lakh; watermark ₹3.45 crore  C) Fee ₹9 lakh; watermark ₹3 crore  D) Fee nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Gain** = 3.45 − 3.00 = ₹45 lakh (15%). **Hurdle** = 10% × ₹3 crore = ₹30 lakh. **Fee** = 20% × (45 − 30) = 20% × ₹15 lakh = **₹3 lakh**. The **new watermark is ₹3.45 crore**.\nA) Applies 20% to the whole ₹45 lakh gain, ignoring the hurdle. C) Fails to update the watermark. D) The hurdle was exceeded, so a fee is due.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Which set of facts describes a NON-discretionary PMS correctly?\nA) Manager decides and executes; no custodian  B) Client approves each transaction; manager executes; custodian mandatory; PoA required; securities in the client's name  C) Manager only advises  D) Pooled units, ₹1 crore minimum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — All five elements are correct for non-discretionary service.\nA) Describes discretionary, and a custodian **is** required. C) Describes advisory. D) Describes an AIF.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A client complains that his PMS charged a performance fee in a year the portfolio fell. Assuming a high watermark applies, what should have happened?\nA) The fee is correct  B) No performance fee should arise below the watermark — the client should seek an explanation and, if unresolved, escalate to SEBI/SCORES  C) He should pay it  D) He should switch to an AIF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Charging a performance fee on a **loss year below the watermark** contradicts the mechanism.\nA) It contradicts the high watermark principle. C) He should query it first. D) Switching product type does not address a possible fee breach.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Compare total costs: PMS at 2% fixed + 20% above a 10% hurdle, versus a mutual fund at a 1.1% TER, on ₹1 crore returning 18% gross. Which costs less and by how much?\nA) The PMS  B) The mutual fund — ₹1.1 lakh versus PMS ₹3.6 lakh, a difference of ₹2.5 lakh  C) They are equal  D) Cannot be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PMS:** fixed 2% × ₹1 crore = ₹2 lakh; performance = 20% × (18 − 10) lakh = 20% × ₹8 lakh = ₹1.6 lakh; **total ₹3.6 lakh**. **Mutual fund:** 1.1% × ₹1 crore = **₹1.1 lakh**. Difference = **₹2.5 lakh**.\nA) The PMS costs more than three times as much here. C) They differ materially. D) Both are computable. **The PMS must outperform by more than 2.5 percentage points just to break even on cost.**\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client asks why PMS minimums are ₹50 lakh while mutual funds start at ₹500. The best explanation is:\nA) PMS is safer  B) The minimum acts as a suitability filter — PMS carries lighter disclosure and higher costs, so access is limited to investors presumed able to evaluate and bear that  C) It is arbitrary  D) Mutual funds are riskier\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **regulatory ladder** (₹500 → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore) reflects a deliberate philosophy: protect small investors most tightly.\nA) PMS is not inherently safer — often the reverse, given concentration. C) The threshold is a deliberate policy choice. D) Mutual funds are the **most** regulated and disclosed of the three.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A portfolio manager's Disclosure Document shows a 3-year annualised return of 21%, but a footnote says it excludes accounts closed during the period. What is the concern?\nA) None  B) Survivorship bias — excluding closed accounts (often the worst performers) inflates the reported figure, which conflicts with the firm-aggregate disclosure requirement  C) The return is too low  D) Footnotes are always fine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Survivorship bias** systematically overstates performance, which is exactly why SEBI requires **firm-wide aggregate** disclosure.\nA) It materially misrepresents the track record. C) 21% is high, not low. D) A footnote does not cure a biased calculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Which combination of client circumstances best justifies a discretionary PMS?\nA) ₹20 lakh, wants full control, needs daily liquidity  B) ₹2 crore, no time or expertise to make decisions, comfortable delegating, long horizon, wants direct ownership  C) ₹5 lakh, first-time investor  D) ₹60 lakh, needs the money in 6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Clears the minimum comfortably, **genuinely wants delegation**, has the horizon to bear a concentrated strategy, and values direct ownership.\nA) Below the minimum and contradicts delegation. C) Far below the minimum and unsuitable for a first-timer. D) A 6-month horizon suits liquid instruments, not a PMS.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A ₹10 crore portfolio has a watermark of ₹11 crore. Over two years it goes ₹9 crore then ₹11.5 crore. What performance fee arises across both years (fee 15%)?\nA) ₹37.5 lakh  B) ₹7.5 lakh — only on the ₹50 lakh above the ₹11 crore watermark in year 2  C) ₹15 lakh  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Year 1:** ₹9 crore is below the ₹11 crore watermark → **nil**. **Year 2:** ₹11.5 crore exceeds it by **₹50 lakh**; fee = 15% × 50 lakh = **₹7.5 lakh**.\nA) Charges on the ₹2.5 crore rise from ₹9 crore — double-billing the recovery of ₹2 crore. C) Uses the wrong base. D) The watermark **was** exceeded in year 2.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Which is the correct hierarchy of protections for a PMS client?\nA) SEBI guarantees the portfolio  B) Direct ownership + custodian + segregation + mandatory disclosure + fiduciary duty — but market risk remains entirely with the client  C) There are no protections  D) The manager insures losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structural, operational and conduct protections exist; **investment outcome is not among them**.\nA) No regulator guarantees portfolios. C) Substantial protections exist. D) Guaranteeing or insuring returns is prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A client in an advisory PMS wants the manager to place orders as well. What must change?\nA) Nothing  B) The service must be re-documented as non-discretionary or discretionary, requiring a PoA and a custodian  C) The manager can simply start executing  D) He must switch to a mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Execution by the manager requires a **different service type** with its own documentation, **PoA and custody** arrangements.\nA) The service type would change materially. C) Executing without authority would be a serious breach. D) A different vehicle is not required.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A portfolio manager reports performance of 16% and mentions this is \"before fees\". If total fees are 3%, what should the client understand?\nA) He earned 16%  B) His actual return is closer to 13%, and SEBI requires disclosure net of all fees precisely to prevent this ambiguity  C) Fees do not matter  D) He earned 19%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Net of fees** is what the client actually keeps, and it is the prescribed disclosure basis.\nA) Gross return is not what reaches him. C) A 3% drag is nearly a fifth of a 16% return. D) Fees reduce, never add to, returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Which statement about the catch-up mechanism is MOST accurate?\nA) It benefits the client  B) It benefits the manager — once the hurdle is crossed, the manager recoups a share of the returns within the hurdle band, raising the total fee above a no-catch-up structure  C) It has no effect  D) It is prohibited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Catch-up is a **manager-favourable** feature that a client should identify and price when comparing offers.\nA) It increases the fee payable. C) The effect can be substantial. D) It is permitted, subject to disclosure.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client with ₹1.2 crore is offered both a PMS (₹50 lakh minimum) and an AIF (₹1 crore minimum). Which structural difference should drive the choice?\nA) Cost alone  B) PMS gives direct ownership of securities in his own name; an AIF gives pooled units with wider strategy freedom but lower disclosure and liquidity  C) There is no difference  D) The AIF is always better\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He qualifies for both, so the decision turns on **ownership structure, disclosure and liquidity** versus **strategy freedom**.\nA) Cost matters but is not the structural difference. C) The structures differ fundamentally. D) Suitability, not product tier, decides.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Over five years a portfolio goes: ₹2cr → ₹2.4cr → ₹2.1cr → ₹2.35cr → ₹2.9cr. With a high watermark and a 20% fee, in which years is a fee charged?\nA) Every year  B) Years 1 and 4 only — the watermark is ₹2.4cr after year 1, and only year 4's ₹2.9cr exceeds it  C) Years 1, 3 and 4  D) No years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Y1:** ₹2.4cr, gain ₹40 lakh → fee, watermark **₹2.4cr**. **Y2:** ₹2.1cr, below → nil. **Y3:** ₹2.35cr, still below ₹2.4cr → **nil** (despite a ₹25 lakh gain). **Y4:** ₹2.9cr exceeds by ₹50 lakh → fee.\nA) Would charge on losses and on recovered ground. C) Year 3 remains below the watermark. D) Two years did exceed it. **Year 3 is the instructive case: a real gain, but no fee, because the peak was not regained.**\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which is the strongest reason SEBI mandates the direct access facility?\nA) To reduce manager profits  B) To let clients avoid distribution commission — the same rationale as mutual fund direct plans, ensuring cost is not inflated by an intermediary the client may not need  C) To increase disclosure  D) To raise minimums\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Cost transparency and choice** — clients who do not use a distributor should not pay for one.\nA) The purpose is client benefit, not punishing managers. C) Disclosure is addressed by separate rules. D) Minimums are unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A client sees his PMS holds 12 stocks while his mutual fund holds 55. What should he understand?\nA) The PMS is safer  B) The PMS is more concentrated, so stock-specific risk is materially higher — potentially higher returns but also higher dispersion  C) The mutual fund is worse  D) Concentration has no effect\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Fewer holdings = more unsystematic risk**, in both directions.\nA) Concentration increases risk. C) Broader diversification is a genuine advantage of the fund. D) It is one of the most significant portfolio characteristics.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A portfolio manager charges the performance fee annually without a high watermark. Over three years the portfolio goes ₹1cr → ₹1.3cr → ₹1.0cr → ₹1.3cr. How much MORE does the client pay than under a watermark (fee 20%)?\nA) Nothing  B) ₹6 lakh more — without a watermark, year 3's ₹30 lakh recovery is charged again  C) ₹12 lakh more  D) ₹3 lakh more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Without a watermark:** Y1 fee = 20% × ₹30 lakh = ₹6 lakh; Y2 nil (loss); Y3 fee = 20% × ₹30 lakh = **₹6 lakh**. Total **₹12 lakh**. **With a watermark:** Y3's ₹1.3 crore only equals the ₹1.3 crore peak, so **no year-3 fee** — total **₹6 lakh**. The client pays **₹6 lakh more** without the watermark, for a portfolio that ended exactly where it stood after year 1.\nA) The difference is real and material. C) Overstates it. D) Understates it. **This is why SEBI mandates the high watermark basis.**\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Which combination of PMS features would most concern an adviser reviewing an offer document?\nA) High watermark, hurdle rate, TWRR disclosure  B) No high watermark, an upfront fee, performance shown gross of fees for selected accounts  C) Custodian appointed, compliance officer named  D) Client-wise segregation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Three separate breaches or red flags**: no watermark (double-charging possible), an **upfront fee** (prohibited), and **gross, cherry-picked performance** (contrary to the disclosure rules).\nA), C) and D) are all proper practice.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client wants to terminate his discretionary PMS. What governs the process?\nA) He cannot terminate  B) The PMS agreement's termination terms, including notice period and any exit load, which is why those clauses must be read before signing  C) SEBI approval is needed  D) The manager decides\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exit is a **contractual matter** set out in the agreement.\nA) Termination is always possible on the agreed terms. C) No regulatory approval is required for a client to exit. D) The client, not the manager, initiates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Comparing an IA-advised direct-access PMS with a distributor-sold PMS, the difference over 10 years on ₹1 crore with a 1% commission drag is roughly:\nA) Negligible  B) Substantial — a 1% annual drag compounds to a large sum, which is precisely why the direct route is mandated  C) Nothing  D) The distributor route is cheaper\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At a 12% gross return, 1% a year compounds to roughly **₹25–30 lakh** of difference on ₹1 crore over 10 years.\nA) A 1% annual drag over a decade is anything but negligible. C) The commission is a real cost. D) Paying commission cannot be cheaper than not paying it.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** Which correctly states what a portfolio manager may and may not do?\nA) May guarantee returns; may commingle  B) May charge fixed and performance fees, exercise discretion under a mandate, and hold client securities via a custodian — but may NOT guarantee returns, charge upfront fees or commingle assets  C) May charge upfront fees  D) May pool client portfolios\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A complete and accurate statement of the permissions and prohibitions.\nA), C) and D) each assert something expressly prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client's account underperforms the firm's aggregate TWRR by 5 percentage points. What is the most likely benign explanation?\nA) Fraud  B) His cash flows were timed differently — money added before a fall or withdrawn before a rise — which TWRR deliberately excludes  C) The manager is incompetent  D) TWRR is wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Money-weighted outcomes** for individual accounts routinely differ from the firm's TWRR because of **client-controlled cash-flow timing**.\nA) An unwarranted leap. C) TWRR measures the manager; a personal shortfall from cash-flow timing does not indict skill. D) TWRR is the prescribed and appropriate measure.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which single feature most distinguishes a PMS from every pooled vehicle?\nA) Its fees  B) Securities are held in the client's own name and demat account rather than as units of a pooled scheme  C) Its returns  D) Its regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Direct ownership rather than pooled units** is the defining structural difference.\nA) Fees differ but pooled vehicles also vary. C) Returns depend on strategy, not structure. D) SEBI regulates all three vehicles.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An adviser is asked whether PMS \"beats\" mutual funds. The professional answer is:\nA) Yes, always  B) Neither structure guarantees better returns — the choice depends on the minimum, the need for ownership and customisation, cost tolerance and liquidity needs  C) No, never  D) It depends on the NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structure does not determine performance; **suitability** determines the right choice.\nA) and C) both state absolutes. D) NAV is a mutual fund concept and irrelevant to the comparison.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A ₹6 crore PMS charges 2.25% fixed only (no performance fee) and returns 9% gross. What is the net return in rupees and percent?\nA) ₹54 lakh; 9%  B) ₹40.5 lakh; 6.75%  C) ₹13.5 lakh; 2.25%  D) ₹67.5 lakh; 11.25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Gross return** = 9% × ₹6 crore = **₹54 lakh**. **Fixed fee** = 2.25% × ₹6 crore = **₹13.5 lakh**. **Net** = 54 − 13.5 = **₹40.5 lakh**, i.e. **6.75%**.\nA) Ignores fees. C) Is the fee itself. D) Adds the fee instead of deducting it. **A 2.25% fee consumed a quarter of a 9% gross return — the arithmetic that makes cost so decisive in low-return years.**\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which principle best summarises this chapter?\nA) PMS is superior to mutual funds  B) A PMS gives direct, customisable ownership for ₹50 lakh and up, at a higher cost — and its fee mechanics (hurdle, high watermark, no catch-up, no upfront fee) determine how much of the return the client actually keeps  C) Fees do not matter  D) All managed vehicles are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It captures the **structure** (direct ownership, ₹50 lakh), the **trade-off** (higher cost), and the **fee mechanics** that decide the client's net outcome.\nA) Neither structure is inherently superior. C) Fee structure is arguably the most consequential item in this chapter. D) MF, PMS and AIF differ fundamentally in structure, minimum, disclosure and freedom.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Track the high watermark carefully across years.\n\n### Case: Mr Kapoor's Portfolio Management Service\n\nMr Kapoor invests **₹4,00,00,000** in a discretionary PMS. The fee structure is:\n\n- **Fixed fee: 1.75%** of assets per year\n- **Performance fee: 20%** of returns above a **10% hurdle**, **no catch-up**\n- **High watermark** applies\n\nPortfolio values at each year-end (before fees):\n\n| Year | Value |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | ₹4,72,00,000 |\n| 2 | ₹4,28,00,000 |\n| 3 | ₹5,16,00,000 |\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the year-1 performance fee, and what is the new high watermark?\nA) Fee ₹14.4 lakh; watermark ₹4.72 crore  B) Fee ₹6.4 lakh; watermark ₹4.72 crore  C) Fee ₹14.4 lakh; watermark ₹4 crore  D) No fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Gain** = 4,72,00,000 − 4,00,00,000 = **₹72,00,000** (18%). **Hurdle** = 10% × ₹4 crore = **₹40,00,000**. **Performance fee** = 20% × (72 − 40) lakh = 20% × **₹32,00,000** = **₹6,40,000**. The **new high watermark is ₹4,72,00,000**.\nA) Applies 20% to the full ₹72 lakh gain, ignoring the hurdle. C) Fails to update the watermark. D) The hurdle was comfortably exceeded.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the year-1 fixed fee, and the total year-1 fee?\nA) Fixed ₹7 lakh; total ₹13.4 lakh  B) Fixed ₹8.26 lakh; total ₹14.66 lakh  C) Fixed ₹7 lakh; total ₹7 lakh  D) Fixed ₹6.4 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Fixed fee** = 1.75% × ₹4,00,00,000 = **₹7,00,000** (charged on the opening/average asset base). **Total** = 7,00,000 + 6,40,000 = **₹13,40,000**.\nB) Applies the fixed fee to the year-end value — a defensible variant, but the question's base is the ₹4 crore invested. C) Omits the performance fee. D) Is the performance fee alone. *(In practice the fixed fee is usually charged on average daily assets; the exam convention is the stated base.)*\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** In year 2 the portfolio falls to ₹4,28,00,000. What performance fee is due?\nA) A reduced fee  B) None — the value is below the ₹4.72 crore high watermark  C) 20% of the loss  D) The full fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹4,28,00,000 is **below** the ₹4,72,00,000 watermark, so **no performance fee arises**. The **fixed fee of about ₹7.49 lakh** (1.75% × ₹4.28 crore) would still be charged.\nA) There is no partial performance fee. C) Fees are never charged on losses. D) Would contradict the high watermark entirely.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** In year 3 the portfolio reaches ₹5,16,00,000. On what gain is the performance fee charged, and how much is it?\nA) On ₹88 lakh; fee ₹17.6 lakh  B) On ₹44 lakh above the watermark, less the hurdle — fee about ₹0.35 lakh  C) On ₹1.16 crore; fee ₹23.2 lakh  D) No fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **high watermark is ₹4,72,00,000**. Year 3's ₹5,16,00,000 exceeds it by **₹44,00,000**. The **hurdle** for the year is 10% of the opening value (₹4,28,00,000) = **₹42,80,000**. Performance fee = 20% × (44,00,000 − 42,80,000) = 20% × **₹1,20,000** = **₹24,000**.\n\nSo the fee is very small — roughly **₹0.24–0.35 lakh** depending on the exact hurdle base — because the gain above the watermark barely exceeded the hurdle.\nA) ₹88 lakh is the rise from ₹4.28 crore, which would **double-charge** the ₹44 lakh recovery. C) ₹1.16 crore is the total gain since inception. D) A small fee is due, since both the watermark and the hurdle were (just) exceeded.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Over the three years, what has the high watermark saved Mr Kapoor, and what is the wider lesson?\nA) Nothing  B) Without the watermark, year 3's fee would have been charged on the ₹88 lakh rise from ₹4.28 crore rather than on the ₹44 lakh above the peak — roughly ₹8.8 lakh more in fees for gains he had already been charged on once  C) It cost him money  D) It guaranteed his returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without a watermark, the year-3 fee would be computed on the **₹88 lakh rise from the year-2 trough** (less the hurdle), instead of on the **₹44 lakh above the previous peak**. The difference is on the order of **₹8–9 lakh** — a fee for **recovering ground the client had already paid a fee on in year 1**.\n\n**The wider lesson:** the high watermark is the single most important fee protection in a PMS, and its value rises with volatility. When comparing managers, a client should check **three things together — the hurdle rate, whether a high watermark applies, and whether there is a catch-up** — because those three, not the headline percentage, determine how much of the return the client actually keeps.\n\nA) The saving is substantial. C) It reduces fees, never increases them. D) No fee mechanism guarantees returns — and guaranteeing them is prohibited.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "13", "title": "Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)", "module": "Module 4: Investment Through Managed Portfolio", "notes": "# Chapter 13: Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 4 · Investment Through Managed Portfolio (23 marks across Chapters 11–13).**\n> This chapter is almost pure **numbers and categories**. If you can place any fund into **Category I, II or III** and recite the **₹1 crore / ₹25 lakh / ₹20 crore / 1000 investors / 2.5%-₹5 crore vs 5%-₹10 crore** set, you have most of the marks. The single sharpest trap in the whole module is **pass-through taxation for Categories I and II but NOT Category III**.\n\n---\n\n## 13.1 What \"Alternative Investments\" Are\n\n**An alternative investment is anything outside the three traditional asset classes — listed equity, debt and cash.**\n\nTypical alternatives: **private equity, venture capital, hedge funds, real estate, infrastructure, commodities, structured products, distressed assets, art and collectibles.**\n\n### How alternatives differ from traditional investments\n\n| Feature | Traditional (equity/debt/cash) | Alternative |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liquidity** | High — daily exit | **Low** — multi-year lock-in |\n| **Minimum ticket** | ₹100–₹5,000 | **₹1 crore** (AIF) |\n| **Investor base** | Retail and everybody | **Sophisticated / HNI** only |\n| **Regulation & disclosure** | Heavy, public, standardised | **Lighter, private, negotiated** |\n| **Valuation** | Daily market price | **Periodic, often by an independent valuer** |\n| **Fees** | Capped TER | **Fixed + performance/carry** |\n| **Strategy** | Long-only, simple | **Complex — leverage, derivatives, unlisted deals** |\n| **Correlation with markets** | High | **Typically lower** |\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line definition to remember:** an alternative investment is *illiquid, expensive, lightly disclosed, sophisticated-investor-only* — and that is precisely why SEBI sets a **₹1 crore minimum**: it is a filter, not a fee.\n\n---\n\n## 13.2 Role of Alternative Investments in Portfolio Management\n\nWhy would anyone accept a 7–10 year lock-in?\n\n| Reason | The logic |\n|---|---|\n| **Diversification** | Returns are driven by deal-specific factors, not the Nifty. **Low correlation** with listed markets lowers total portfolio risk. |\n| **Illiquidity premium** | The investor is *paid* for giving up liquidity — an extra expected return for accepting a lock-in. |\n| **Access to a bigger opportunity set** | Most Indian companies are **unlisted**. Listed equity is only a slice of the economy; PE/VC reaches the rest. |\n| **Higher return potential** | Early-stage and special-situation deals can compound far faster than an index — with a far higher chance of total loss. |\n| **Inflation hedge** | Real assets — infrastructure, real estate — carry revenue that reprices with inflation. |\n| **Manager skill (alpha)** | Complex strategies (Category III) aim to earn returns independent of market direction. |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the benefit of an alternative is **diversification through low correlation**, *not* \"higher return with lower risk\". Alternatives are **higher risk**; they improve the *portfolio's* risk-return trade-off, not their own.\n\n**Where they sit in a portfolio:** alternatives are a **satellite**, added on top of a completed **core** of equity, debt and cash — never a substitute for the core, and never funded out of money the client may need.\n\n---\n\n## 13.3 Evolution and Growth of AIFs in India\n\n| Phase | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| **Pre-2012** | Venture capital funds registered under the **SEBI (Venture Capital Funds) Regulations, 1996**. Many other private pools — PE, real estate, hedge-style funds — operated with **no dedicated framework**. |\n| **May 2012** | SEBI notified the **SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012**, bringing *every* privately pooled vehicle under one umbrella and creating **Categories I, II and III**. |\n| **After 2012** | The 1996 VCF Regulations were **repealed**; funds already registered under them continue until wound up, but **all new funds must register under the AIF Regulations**. |\n| **Since** | Commitments raised have grown from a small base to **several lakh crore**, driven by rising HNI wealth, a maturing start-up ecosystem, family offices and institutional allocations. **Category II is by far the largest** category by commitments. |\n\n> 🧠 **The four-word history:** *\"1996 VCF → 2012 AIF.\"* The AIF Regulations replaced a narrow venture-capital rulebook with a broad, three-category one.\n\n---\n\n## 13.4 ⭐ What an AIF Is — the SEBI Definition\n\n**An AIF is a privately pooled investment vehicle, established in India as a trust, company, LLP or body corporate, which collects funds from sophisticated investors — Indian or foreign — and invests them according to a defined investment policy for the benefit of those investors.**\n\nBreak that definition into its five load-bearing words:\n\n| Word | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| **Privately pooled** | Money may be raised **only by private placement** — never by a public advertisement or public issue. |\n| **Established in India** | An offshore fund is not an AIF. |\n| **Trust / company / LLP / body corporate** | Most Indian AIFs are structured as **trusts**. |\n| **Defined investment policy** | Stated up front in the **placement memorandum** and binding on the manager. |\n| **For the benefit of investors** | A fiduciary standard. |\n\n**What is NOT an AIF:** mutual funds (SEBI MF Regulations, 1996), collective investment schemes (SEBI CIS Regulations, 1999), and any other vehicle already regulated as a fund-management activity. Also excluded: **family trusts, ESOP and employee-welfare trusts, gratuity trusts, holding companies and securitisation trusts** — pools that are not really commercial investment funds.\n\n### The three parties\n\n| Party | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Sponsor** | The person(s) who **set up** the AIF (promoter of a company, designated partner of an LLP). |\n| **Manager** | Appointed by the AIF to **manage its investments**. |\n| **Trustee** | For a trust structure, holds the assets for the investors' benefit. |\n\n---\n\n## 13.5 ⭐⭐ The Three Categories\n\n### Category I — the \"socially and economically desirable\" bucket\n\nFunds that invest in **start-ups, early-stage ventures, SMEs, social ventures, infrastructure, or other sectors that the government or regulators consider socially or economically desirable.** Because these areas are policy priorities, Category I funds are the ones on which the government or SEBI **may grant incentives or concessions**.\n\n**The four named sub-categories:**\n\n| Sub-category | Invests in |\n|---|---|\n| **Venture Capital Fund** (includes **Angel Funds**) | Early-stage, unlisted, high-growth ventures |\n| **SME Fund** | Small and medium enterprises, including SME-exchange listings |\n| **Social Venture Fund** | Social ventures — enterprises with a stated social objective |\n| **Infrastructure Fund** | Infrastructure companies, projects and SPVs |\n\n### Category II — the residual bucket\n\n**Funds that do not fall in Category I or Category III, and which do not undertake leverage or borrowing other than to meet day-to-day operational requirements.**\n\nTypical members: **private equity funds, debt funds, real estate funds, distressed-asset funds, fund-of-funds.** No specific government incentives.\n\n> 🧠 **Category II is defined by subtraction.** \"Not I, not III, and no leverage.\" If a fund invests in ordinary unlisted companies and does not trade derivatives, it lands here by default.\n\n### Category III — the complex-strategy bucket\n\n**Funds that employ diverse or complex trading strategies and may employ leverage, including through investment in listed or unlisted derivatives.**\n\nTypical members: **hedge funds, PIPE funds** (private investment in public equity). No government incentives. **Only Category III may use leverage.**\n\n---\n\n## 13.6 The Eight Fund Types You Must Be Able to Place\n\n| Fund type | Category | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Venture Capital Fund** | **I** | Equity in **early-stage, unlisted** high-growth ventures; long horizon; exits by IPO or strategic sale |\n| **Angel Fund** | **I** (sub-category of VCF) | Very early \"seed\" capital raised from **angel investors**; special relaxed rules |\n| **Private Equity Fund** | **II** | Larger, **later-stage** unlisted companies — growth capital, buyouts, mezzanine |\n| **Debt Fund** | **II** | **Debt or debt securities** of listed/unlisted investee companies |\n| **Infrastructure Fund** | **I** | Roads, power, ports, telecom — long-gestation projects and SPVs |\n| **SME Fund** | **I** | Small and medium enterprises |\n| **Hedge Fund** | **III** | Complex, leveraged, derivative-based strategies; **may be open-ended** |\n| **Social Venture Fund** | **I** | Social ventures; investors may accept **\"muted\" (below-market) returns** |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"VASI in I\":** **V**enture capital, **A**ngel, **S**ME + **S**ocial venture, **I**nfrastructure are **Category I**. **PE and Debt are II. Hedge is III.**\n\n### A few defining details\n\n**Venture Capital Fund.** Invests in unlisted early-stage ventures. Very high failure rate at the individual deal level — returns come from a small number of large winners. Illiquid; the exit route (IPO, strategic sale, buyback) is decided at the time of entry.\n\n**Private Equity Fund.** Invests across stages — **seed, early/start-up, expansion (second and third stage), mezzanine, and buyouts/M&A.** The manager typically receives **carried interest of up to about 20% of profits**, payable only after a **minimum rate of return (hurdle)** is achieved; the remaining ~80% goes to investors. Risk rises the earlier the stage.\n\n**Debt Fund.** A **Category II** AIF investing in debt securities of investee companies. Because an AIF is a *pooled investment vehicle*, it invests in **debt instruments** — it cannot be used to carry on a lending business with investors' money. Debt funds receive **no special concessions** from the regulator.\n\n**Social Venture Fund.** May **accept grants** and may **make grants** to social ventures; investors expressly accept that returns may be **muted** because the objective is measurable social impact alongside a financial return.\n\n**Hedge Fund.** In India a hedge fund *is* a **Category III AIF**. May be **open-ended or close-ended**, may use **leverage** with investor consent and within the SEBI-specified limit, and is the **only category taxed at the fund level**.\n\n### Angel funds — the special rules\n\nAngel funds are a **sub-category of venture capital funds** (therefore **Category I**) that raise money from **angel investors**.\n\n| Item | Angel fund rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Who is an angel investor** | An **individual with net tangible assets of at least ₹2 crore** (excluding the principal residence) *plus* early-stage investment experience / serial-entrepreneur experience / senior-management experience; **or a body corporate**; **or a registered AIF/VCF** |\n| **Minimum corpus** | **₹10 crore** (against ₹20 crore for other AIFs) |\n| **Maximum investors per scheme** | **49** (against 1000) |\n| **Minimum investment per angel investor** | **₹25 lakh**, which may be drawn down over a **maximum of 5 years** — the ₹1 crore minimum does **not** apply |\n| **Sponsor/manager continuing interest** | Lower of **2.5% of corpus or ₹50 lakh** |\n| **Investee company must be** | Incorporated within **3 years**, turnover under **₹25 crore**, and not part of an industrial group with turnover above **₹300 crore** |\n| **Size of each investment** | Between **₹50 lakh and ₹5 crore** |\n| **Lock-in on each investment** | **3 years** |\n| **Listing** | Angel funds **shall not be listed** |\n| **Scheme document** | A **scheme memorandum** is filed with SEBI and comments incorporated before launch |\n\n> ⚠️ Angel-fund thresholds are the ones SEBI has amended most often over the years. **Answer with the workbook figures above** — they are what the examination tests.\n\n---\n\n## 13.7 ⭐⭐ SEBI Requirements on AIFs — the Number Set\n\n### Money\n\n| Requirement | Figure |\n|---|---|\n| **Minimum corpus per scheme** | **₹20 crore** (**₹10 crore** for an angel fund) |\n| **Minimum investment per investor** | **₹1 crore** |\n| **Minimum for employees / directors of the AIF or of the Manager** | **₹25 lakh** |\n| **Minimum for an angel investor** | **₹25 lakh**, over a maximum of 5 years |\n| **Maximum investors per scheme** | **1000** (**49** for an angel fund scheme) |\n| **Minimum tradable lot if units are listed** | **₹1 crore** |\n\n**Why ₹25 lakh for employees and directors?** They already know the strategy, the manager and the risks from the inside — the ₹1 crore filter exists to keep the uninformed out, and insiders are not uninformed. It also lets the investment team invest alongside clients.\n\n**Why 1000 investors?** The AIF Regulations permit fund-raising **only by private placement**. A cap on investor numbers is what keeps a \"private\" placement genuinely private rather than a disguised public issue.\n\n### ⭐ Sponsor / Manager continuing interest (\"skin in the game\")\n\n**The sponsor or manager must invest its own money in the fund and keep it there.**\n\n| Category | Continuing interest |\n|---|---|\n| **Category I and II** | Lower of **2.5% of corpus** or **₹5 crore** |\n| **Category III** | Lower of **5% of corpus** or **₹10 crore** |\n| **Angel fund** | Lower of **2.5% of corpus** or **₹50 lakh** |\n\nThis interest **may not be met by waiving management fees** — it must be real money at risk.\n\n> 🧠 **Why Category III is double:** Category III can use **leverage** and complex strategies, so the manager can lose investors' money faster. SEBI therefore doubles the alignment requirement — **5% / ₹10 crore instead of 2.5% / ₹5 crore.**\n\n**Worked example.** A Category II fund with a corpus of **₹300 crore**: 2.5% × 300 = **₹7.5 crore**, but the rule is the **lower** of that and ₹5 crore → the sponsor commits **₹5 crore**.\nA Category II fund with a corpus of **₹120 crore**: 2.5% × 120 = **₹3 crore**, lower than ₹5 crore → the sponsor commits **₹3 crore**.\nThe same ₹300 crore corpus in a **Category III** fund: 5% × 300 = ₹15 crore vs ₹10 crore → the sponsor commits **₹10 crore**.\n\n### ⭐ Structure and tenure\n\n| Category | Open or close-ended | Minimum tenure |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Category I** | **Close-ended** | **3 years** |\n| **Category II** | **Close-ended** | **3 years** |\n| **Category III** | **Open OR close-ended** | No minimum tenure if open-ended |\n\n- The tenure of a close-ended fund may be **extended by up to 2 years** with the consent of **two-thirds of unit holders by value** of their investment.\n- If that consent is not obtained, the fund must **fully liquidate within one year** of the end of its tenure.\n- Units of a **close-ended** AIF **may be listed** on a stock exchange, with a **minimum tradable lot of ₹1 crore** — and only after the final close of the scheme. **Angel funds may not be listed.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Listing is not liquidity.** A ₹1 crore minimum lot on an exchange with almost no buyers is a legal right to sell, not a practical one. Never tell a client an AIF is liquid because it is listed.\n\n### Fund-raising and documents\n\n- Funds are raised **only through private placement** using a **placement memorandum** containing all material information an investor needs.\n- The placement memorandum must be **filed with SEBI at least 30 days before the launch** of the scheme, and SEBI's comments incorporated before launch.\n- Every AIF must set up a **procedure to resolve disputes** between investors, the fund, the manager and the sponsor.\n\n### Eligibility of the fund house\n\n- The **constitution document** (trust deed / MoA / LLP agreement) must permit the activity of an AIF.\n- The **sponsor, manager and key personnel** must be **fit and proper persons**.\n- At least **one key person must have at least 5 years' experience** in asset/portfolio management, advisory, securities trading or dealing in other financial assets.\n- Adequate **infrastructure** must exist to manage the fund.\n\n### Registration fees (payable to SEBI)\n\n| Item | Fee |\n|---|---|\n| **Application fee** | **₹1 lakh** |\n| **Category I registration** | **₹5 lakh** |\n| **Angel fund registration** | **₹2 lakh** |\n| **Category II registration** | **₹10 lakh** |\n| **Category III registration** | **₹15 lakh** |\n\n> 🧠 The registration fee **rises with the freedom the category enjoys**: I (₹5 lakh) → II (₹10 lakh) → III (₹15 lakh), with the small angel fund cheapest at ₹2 lakh.\n\n### Investment conditions\n\n| Condition | Category I & II | Category III |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Maximum in one investee company** | **25% of investable funds** | **10% of investable funds** |\n| **Leverage / borrowing** | **Not permitted**, except temporary funding — not more than **30 days**, on not more than **4 occasions a year**, and not more than **10% of investable funds** | **Permitted**, with investor consent, disclosure in the placement memorandum, and within the SEBI-specified limit |\n| **Overseas investment** | Permitted, subject to RBI/SEBI conditions | Permitted, subject to RBI/SEBI conditions |\n| **Investment in associates** | Requires approval of **75% of investors by value** | Same |\n| **Un-invested money** | May be parked in liquid mutual funds, bank deposits, T-bills and other high-quality liquid assets | Same |\n\n### Valuation and reporting\n\n| Category | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| **Category I and II** | Investments valued by an **independent valuer at least once every six months** |\n| **Category III — close-ended** | **NAV disclosed to investors at least once every quarter** |\n| **Category III — open-ended** | **NAV disclosed to investors at least once every month** |\n\n> 🧠 **Why the difference?** Categories I and II hold **unlisted** assets with no market price, so a valuer is needed — and twice a year is as often as it is meaningful. Category III trades **listed** securities and derivatives that price daily, so frequent NAV disclosure is both possible and necessary.\n\n---\n\n## 13.8 ⭐⭐ Taxation — the Highest-Yield Fact in the Chapter\n\n| | **Category I & II** | **Category III** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pass-through status** | **Yes** — granted under the Income Tax Act | **No** |\n| **Who pays the tax** | **The investor**, on his share of the income | **The fund**, before distributing |\n| **Character of income** | **Preserved** — capital gains stay capital gains, interest stays interest, dividend stays dividend | Lost — the investor receives income already taxed |\n| **Rate applied** | The **investor's own** applicable rate | Generally the **maximum marginal rate** at the fund level |\n| **Exception** | **Business income** of the fund is taxed **at the fund level**, not passed through | — |\n| **TDS** | The fund deducts tax at source on income credited or paid to resident investors | — |\n\n**What \"pass-through\" actually means.** The fund is treated as **transparent**: income is deemed to arise directly to the investor, **as if he had made the investment himself**. There is no tax at the fund level, so there is no double taxation, and a low-slab investor pays a low rate while a high-slab investor pays a high one.\n\n> ⚠️ **The exam trap, stated plainly:** *pass-through applies to Category I and Category II — never to Category III.* A hedge fund (Category III) is taxed **at the fund level**, so every investor effectively bears the **same** rate regardless of his own slab. This makes Category III **relatively less tax-efficient for an investor in a low tax bracket** — an important suitability point.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook:** *\"One and Two pass it through; Three pays for you.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 13.9 ⭐ Comparison of the Three Categories\n\n| Feature | **Category I** | **Category II** | **Category III** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Core idea** | Socially/economically desirable sectors | Everything else, no leverage | Complex trading strategies |\n| **Examples** | VC, angel, SME, social venture, infrastructure | PE, debt, real estate, distressed, fund-of-funds | Hedge funds, PIPE funds |\n| **Structure** | **Close-ended** | **Close-ended** | **Open or close-ended** |\n| **Minimum tenure** | **3 years** | **3 years** | None if open-ended |\n| **Leverage** | **No** (temporary borrowing only) | **No** (temporary borrowing only) | **Yes**, within SEBI limits |\n| **Concessions from government/SEBI** | **May be given** | **None** | **None** |\n| **Sponsor continuing interest** | Lower of **2.5% / ₹5 crore** | Lower of **2.5% / ₹5 crore** | Lower of **5% / ₹10 crore** |\n| **Max in one investee company** | **25%** of investable funds | **25%** | **10%** |\n| **Valuation / NAV** | Independent valuer **every 6 months** | Every 6 months | NAV **quarterly** (close) / **monthly** (open) |\n| **Registration fee** | **₹5 lakh** (angel ₹2 lakh) | **₹10 lakh** | **₹15 lakh** |\n| **Taxation** | **Pass-through** | **Pass-through** | **Taxed at the fund level** |\n\n### AIF vs PMS vs mutual fund\n\n| | **Mutual fund** | **PMS** | **AIF** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Regulation** | SEBI (MF) Regulations, **1996** | SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, **2020** | SEBI (AIF) Regulations, **2012** |\n| **Minimum investment** | ₹100–₹5,000 | **₹50 lakh** | **₹1 crore** |\n| **Pooling** | **Pooled** — investor holds units | **Not pooled** — securities in the client's own name | **Pooled** — investor holds units |\n| **Offered to** | The public | Individually | **Private placement only**, max 1000 per scheme |\n| **Liquidity** | High (open-ended) | Moderate | **Low** — multi-year lock-in |\n| **Leverage** | Not permitted | Not permitted | **Category III only** |\n| **Disclosure** | Daily NAV, public | To the client | To investors, **private** |\n| **Customisation** | None | **Possible** | None — a common pool |\n\n> 🧠 **The three minimums in one line: ₹100 → ₹50 lakh → ₹1 crore.** Mutual fund, PMS, AIF. Getting these three the right way round answers a surprising number of questions across Chapters 11, 12 and 13.\n\n---\n\n## 13.10 Suitability and Enablers\n\n### Who an AIF suits\n\n| Suitable when the client has | Because |\n|---|---|\n| A **large investible surplus** — well above ₹1 crore of *spare* money | The ₹1 crore is a **commitment**, drawn down over time by capital calls |\n| A **long horizon** — typically **7–10 years** | Close-ended, minimum 3-year tenure, extendable by 2 more |\n| **No need for liquidity** from this money | There is effectively no exit before maturity |\n| **High risk tolerance** and the ability to absorb a total loss on a deal | Unlisted, concentrated, sometimes leveraged |\n| An **already complete core portfolio** | Alternatives are a **satellite**, never the core |\n| The **sophistication** to understand the strategy, the fee structure and the valuation basis | Disclosure is lighter than in a mutual fund |\n\n### Who it does NOT suit\n\n- Anyone who may need the money back — **there is no redemption on demand**\n- Anyone seeking **regular income** or **capital protection**\n- Anyone whose entire portfolio is small enough that a ₹1 crore commitment would be a concentrated bet\n- Anyone for whom the **fee load** (fixed management fee + carry, plus set-up costs) would swallow the extra return\n\n### Enablers that have driven AIF growth in India\n\n- A clear, single **regulatory framework** since 2012 and the credibility that brings\n- **Pass-through taxation** for Categories I and II\n- Rapid growth in **HNI and UHNI wealth**, and the rise of **family offices**\n- A maturing **start-up and unlisted-company ecosystem** needing risk capital\n- The ability to **list close-ended units** and the move to holding **units in demat form**\n- **GIFT City / IFSC** structures making it easier to pool domestic and offshore money\n- Growing professional distribution and advice, including **fee-only investment advisers**\n\n> ⚠️ **Adviser's duty:** an AIF pays no commission to a SEBI-registered investment adviser — the IA is paid by the client. So the recommendation must rest **only on suitability**: horizon, liquidity need, risk capacity and the client's existing asset allocation. Recommending an AIF to a client who cannot tolerate a decade-long lock-in is a **suitability failure**, however good the fund.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Governing regulations | **SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012** |\n| Regulations they replaced | SEBI (Venture Capital Funds) Regulations, **1996** |\n| Definition of an AIF | **Privately pooled** vehicle in India — trust/company/LLP/body corporate — with a **defined investment policy** |\n| Permitted structures | Trust, company, LLP or body corporate (**most are trusts**) |\n| Not an AIF | Mutual funds, CIS, family trusts, ESOP/gratuity trusts, holding companies, securitisation trusts |\n| Fund-raising mode | **Private placement only** — never a public issue |\n| Category I | Start-ups, SMEs, social ventures, infrastructure — **may get incentives** |\n| Category I sub-types | **VCF (incl. angel), SME, social venture, infrastructure** |\n| Category II | **Not I, not III, no leverage** — PE, debt, real estate, distressed, fund-of-funds |\n| Category III | **Complex strategies + leverage** — hedge funds, PIPE funds |\n| Close vs open-ended | **I and II close-ended; III may be open or close-ended** |\n| Minimum tenure (I & II) | **3 years** |\n| Tenure extension | Up to **2 years**, with **two-thirds of unit holders by value** |\n| Liquidation deadline | Within **1 year** of the end of the tenure |\n| Minimum corpus per scheme | **₹20 crore** (**₹10 crore** angel fund) |\n| Minimum investment | **₹1 crore** |\n| Employees / directors minimum | **₹25 lakh** |\n| Angel investor minimum | **₹25 lakh**, over up to **5 years** |\n| Maximum investors per scheme | **1000** (**49** for an angel fund) |\n| Sponsor interest — Cat I & II | Lower of **2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore** |\n| Sponsor interest — Cat III | Lower of **5% of corpus or ₹10 crore** |\n| Sponsor interest — angel fund | Lower of **2.5% of corpus or ₹50 lakh** |\n| Continuing interest may not be | Met by **waiving management fees** |\n| Listing | **Close-ended** units may be listed; minimum lot **₹1 crore**; **angel funds may not list** |\n| Placement memorandum | Filed with SEBI **at least 30 days before launch** |\n| Key personnel experience | At least one person with **5 years'** relevant experience |\n| Registration fees | Application **₹1 lakh**; Cat I **₹5 lakh**; angel **₹2 lakh**; Cat II **₹10 lakh**; Cat III **₹15 lakh** |\n| Max in one investee company | **25%** (Cat I & II) / **10%** (Cat III) of investable funds |\n| Temporary borrowing (I & II) | ≤ **30 days**, ≤ **4 times a year**, ≤ **10%** of investable funds |\n| Leverage | **Category III only**, with investor consent and within SEBI limits |\n| Valuation — Cat I & II | **Independent valuer every 6 months** |\n| NAV — Cat III | **Quarterly** (close-ended) / **monthly** (open-ended) |\n| Taxation — Cat I & II | **Pass-through** — taxed in the **investor's** hands |\n| Taxation — Cat III | **No pass-through** — taxed **at the fund level** |\n| Pass-through exception | **Business income** is taxed at the fund level even for Cat I & II |\n| Angel investor test | Individual with net tangible assets **≥ ₹2 crore** + relevant experience; or a body corporate; or a registered AIF/VCF |\n| Angel investee company | Incorporated within **3 years**, turnover < **₹25 crore**, group turnover ≤ **₹300 crore** |\n| Angel investment size / lock-in | **₹50 lakh to ₹5 crore**, locked in for **3 years** |\n| PE carried interest | Typically up to **20%** of profits, after a **hurdle** is met; ~80% to investors |\n| Social venture fund | Cat I; may **accept and give grants**; investors accept **muted returns** |\n| Debt fund | **Category II**; invests in debt securities; **no special concessions** |\n| Hedge fund | **Category III**; leverage permitted; **no pass-through** |\n| The three minimums | MF **₹100–5,000** → PMS **₹50 lakh** → AIF **₹1 crore** |\n| Suitability | **HNI, 7–10 year horizon, no liquidity need, high risk tolerance, core portfolio already built** |\n| Role in a portfolio | A **satellite** for diversification through **low correlation** — never the core |\n\n> **Exam tip:** three items produce the majority of Chapter 13 questions — **(1)** placing a named fund in the right category (*VASI in I, PE & debt in II, hedge in III*); **(2)** the sponsor's continuing interest, where the trick is always the word **\"lower of\"** and the doubling for Category III; and **(3)** **pass-through for I and II but not III**. Learn those three cold before worrying about anything else.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 13: Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The category placement, the \"lower of\" sponsor interest, and pass-through taxation for I and II but not III are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Which regulations govern AIFs in India, and from which year?\n> **A:** The **SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012**.\n\n**Q2.** Which earlier regulations did the AIF Regulations replace?\n> **A:** The **SEBI (Venture Capital Funds) Regulations, 1996**. Funds already registered under them continue until wound up, but **all new funds must register under the AIF Regulations**.\n\n**Q3.** Define an AIF.\n> **A:** A **privately pooled investment vehicle established in India** as a **trust, company, LLP or body corporate**, which collects funds from sophisticated investors — Indian or foreign — and invests them according to a **defined investment policy** for the benefit of those investors.\n\n**Q4.** Name four kinds of pool that are expressly NOT AIFs.\n> **A:** **Mutual funds** (SEBI MF Regulations, 1996) and **collective investment schemes** (CIS Regulations, 1999), plus **family trusts, ESOP/employee-welfare and gratuity trusts, holding companies and securitisation trusts**.\n\n**Q5.** How may an AIF raise money?\n> **A:** **Only by private placement**, using a **placement memorandum** — never by public issue or public advertisement.\n\n**Q6.** When must the placement memorandum be filed with SEBI?\n> **A:** **At least 30 days before the launch** of the scheme, with SEBI's comments incorporated before launch.\n\n**Q7.** What defines a Category I AIF?\n> **A:** It invests in **start-ups, early-stage ventures, SMEs, social ventures, infrastructure** or other sectors the government or regulators consider **socially or economically desirable** — and is therefore the only category on which **incentives or concessions may be granted**.\n\n**Q8.** Name the four sub-categories of Category I.\n> **A:** **Venture Capital Funds (including Angel Funds), SME Funds, Social Venture Funds and Infrastructure Funds.**\n\n**Q9.** What defines a Category II AIF?\n> **A:** A fund that **does not fall in Category I or III** and **does not undertake leverage or borrowing** other than for day-to-day operational requirements. Examples: **private equity, debt, real estate, distressed-asset and fund-of-funds**.\n\n**Q10.** What defines a Category III AIF?\n> **A:** A fund employing **diverse or complex trading strategies**, which **may employ leverage** including through listed or unlisted **derivatives**. Examples: **hedge funds and PIPE funds**.\n\n**Q11.** Place these in categories: venture capital, private equity, debt, infrastructure, SME, hedge, social venture, angel.\n> **A:** **Category I** — venture capital, angel, SME, social venture, infrastructure. **Category II** — private equity, debt. **Category III** — hedge. *(Memory hook: \"VASI in I\".)*\n\n**Q12.** Which categories must be close-ended, and which may be open-ended?\n> **A:** **Categories I and II must be close-ended** with a **minimum tenure of 3 years**. **Category III may be open-ended or close-ended.**\n\n**Q13.** By how much may the tenure of a close-ended AIF be extended, and with whose consent?\n> **A:** By up to **2 years**, with the consent of **two-thirds of unit holders by value** of their investment.\n\n**Q14.** What happens if that consent is not obtained?\n> **A:** The fund must **fully liquidate its assets within one year** of the end of its tenure.\n\n**Q15.** What is the minimum corpus of an AIF scheme?\n> **A:** **₹20 crore** — and **₹10 crore** for an angel fund.\n\n**Q16.** What is the minimum investment by an investor in an AIF?\n> **A:** **₹1 crore.** *(Do not confuse this with the PMS minimum of ₹50 lakh.)*\n\n**Q17.** Who may invest less than ₹1 crore, and how much less?\n> **A:** **Employees and directors of the AIF, and employees and directors of the Manager** — for them the minimum is **₹25 lakh**.\n\n**Q18.** Why does SEBI allow employees and directors a lower minimum?\n> **A:** The ₹1 crore is a **sophistication filter** to keep uninformed investors out; insiders already understand the strategy and the risks. It also lets the investment team **invest alongside clients**.\n\n**Q19.** What is the maximum number of investors in an AIF scheme?\n> **A:** **1000** — and only **49** in an angel fund scheme.\n\n**Q20.** Why is there a cap on investor numbers at all?\n> **A:** Because an AIF may raise money **only by private placement**. The cap is what keeps the placement genuinely private rather than a disguised public issue.\n\n**Q21.** State the sponsor/manager continuing interest for each category.\n> **A:** **Category I and II** — lower of **2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore**. **Category III** — lower of **5% of corpus or ₹10 crore**. **Angel fund** — lower of **2.5% of corpus or ₹50 lakh**.\n\n**Q22.** Why is Category III's continuing interest double that of Categories I and II?\n> **A:** Category III may use **leverage and complex strategies**, so investors' money can be lost faster. SEBI **doubles the alignment requirement** to keep more of the manager's own money at risk.\n\n**Q23.** A Category II AIF has a corpus of ₹300 crore. What continuing interest must the sponsor hold?\n> **A:** 2.5% × ₹300 crore = ₹7.5 crore, but the rule is the **lower of** that and ₹5 crore → **₹5 crore**.\n\n**Q24.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of ₹120 crore. What continuing interest applies?\n> **A:** 5% × ₹120 crore = **₹6 crore**, which is **lower than ₹10 crore** → **₹6 crore**.\n\n**Q25.** May the sponsor meet the continuing-interest requirement by waiving management fees?\n> **A:** **No.** It must be **real money invested and kept in the fund**.\n\n**Q26.** Can AIF units be listed, and what is the minimum tradable lot?\n> **A:** Units of a **close-ended** AIF **may** be listed after the final close, with a **minimum tradable lot of ₹1 crore**. **Angel funds may not be listed.**\n\n**Q27.** Does listing make an AIF liquid?\n> **A:** **No.** A ₹1 crore minimum lot with almost no buyers is a legal right to sell, not a practical one. Never describe a listed AIF as liquid.\n\n**Q28.** Which category may use leverage, and on what conditions?\n> **A:** **Category III only** — with **investor consent**, **disclosure in the placement memorandum**, and within the **maximum limit specified by SEBI**.\n\n**Q29.** What temporary borrowing is allowed to Categories I and II?\n> **A:** Only to meet temporary funding needs — for **not more than 30 days**, on **not more than 4 occasions a year**, and **not more than 10% of investable funds**.\n\n**Q30.** What is the maximum an AIF may invest in a single investee company?\n> **A:** **25% of investable funds** for **Categories I and II**; **10%** for **Category III**.\n\n**Q31.** How often must Category I and II investments be valued?\n> **A:** By an **independent valuer at least once every six months**.\n\n**Q32.** How often must a Category III AIF disclose NAV?\n> **A:** **At least quarterly** if close-ended and **at least monthly** if open-ended.\n\n**Q33.** Why do Categories I and II value only twice a year while Category III reports monthly or quarterly?\n> **A:** Categories I and II hold **unlisted assets with no market price**, so an independent valuer is needed and more frequent valuation would not be meaningful. Category III trades **listed securities and derivatives** that price daily.\n\n**Q34.** Which AIF categories enjoy tax pass-through status?\n> **A:** **Categories I and II only.** **Category III does NOT** get pass-through.\n\n**Q35.** What does \"pass-through\" actually mean for the investor?\n> **A:** The fund is treated as **transparent** — income is deemed to arise **directly to the investor as if he had invested himself**. There is **no tax at the fund level**, the **character of the income is preserved** (capital gain stays a capital gain), and the investor pays at **his own rate**.\n\n**Q36.** What is the exception to pass-through for Categories I and II?\n> **A:** **Business income** of the fund is taxed **at the fund level**; only the other income passes through. The fund also **deducts tax at source** on income credited or paid to resident investors.\n\n**Q37.** How is a Category III AIF taxed, and what is the suitability consequence?\n> **A:** **At the fund level**, generally at the **maximum marginal rate**, with investors receiving income already taxed. So every investor bears effectively the **same** rate — making Category III **relatively less tax-efficient for an investor in a low tax bracket**.\n\n**Q38.** Which category may receive incentives or concessions from the government or SEBI?\n> **A:** **Category I only** — because it funds sectors regarded as socially or economically desirable. Categories II and III receive **none**.\n\n**Q39.** What is an angel fund, and which category is it in?\n> **A:** A **sub-category of venture capital funds** — therefore **Category I** — that raises money from **angel investors** to fund very early-stage ventures.\n\n**Q40.** Who qualifies as an angel investor?\n> **A:** An **individual with net tangible assets of at least ₹2 crore** (excluding the principal residence) **plus** early-stage investment experience, serial-entrepreneur experience or senior-management experience; **or a body corporate**; **or a registered AIF/VCF**.\n\n**Q41.** What must an angel fund's investee company look like?\n> **A:** Incorporated **within 3 years**, turnover **below ₹25 crore**, and **not** part of an industrial group with turnover above **₹300 crore**.\n\n**Q42.** What is the size and lock-in of an angel fund's investment in a venture?\n> **A:** Between **₹50 lakh and ₹5 crore**, locked in for **3 years**.\n\n**Q43.** What is the minimum commitment by an angel investor?\n> **A:** **₹25 lakh**, which may be drawn down over a **maximum of 5 years**. The ₹1 crore AIF minimum does **not** apply to angel funds.\n\n**Q44.** How does a private equity fund's manager get paid out of profits?\n> **A:** Through **carried interest** — typically up to **20% of profits**, payable only after a **minimum rate of return (hurdle)** is achieved. The remaining roughly **80%** goes to the investors.\n\n**Q45.** Name the financing stages a PE fund may participate in.\n> **A:** **Seed**, **start-up/early**, **expansion (second and third stage)**, **mezzanine** (for companies expected to go public), and **buyouts / mergers and acquisitions**.\n\n**Q46.** How does a PE or VC fund exit an investment?\n> **A:** By an **IPO**, a **sale to a financial or strategic investor**, or a **buyback by the company or promoters** — agreed with the promoters, and vulnerable to weak equity markets at exit time.\n\n**Q47.** What is a debt fund AIF, and why can't it simply lend?\n> **A:** A **Category II** AIF investing in **debt or debt securities** of investee companies. It is a **pooled investment vehicle**, so it invests in instruments — it cannot run a lending business with investors' money. It receives **no special concessions**.\n\n**Q48.** What is distinctive about a social venture fund?\n> **A:** It is **Category I**; it may **accept grants and make grants** to social ventures; and its investors expressly accept **muted (below-market) returns** in exchange for measurable social impact.\n\n**Q49.** What is a hedge fund in the Indian regulatory framework?\n> **A:** A **Category III AIF** — complex strategies, **leverage permitted**, **open or close-ended**, and **taxed at the fund level with no pass-through**.\n\n**Q50.** Give three reasons to add alternatives to a portfolio.\n> **A:** **Diversification** from **low correlation** with listed markets; an **illiquidity premium** for accepting a lock-in; and **access to the unlisted opportunity set**, which is most of the Indian economy. Real assets also act as an **inflation hedge**.\n\n**Q51.** What is the classic wrong reason for buying alternatives?\n> **A:** \"Higher return with lower risk.\" Alternatives are **higher risk on their own**; they improve the **portfolio's** risk-return trade-off through low correlation. They belong as a **satellite**, never the core.\n\n**Q52.** For whom is an AIF suitable?\n> **A:** An investor with a **large investible surplus**, a **7–10 year horizon**, **no liquidity need** from that money, **high risk tolerance**, an **already complete core portfolio**, and the sophistication to understand the strategy and fees.\n\n**Q53.** Why is the ₹1 crore best described as a commitment rather than a payment?\n> **A:** Because AIFs **draw down capital over time through capital calls** as deals are found — the investor must keep the committed money available, not merely have it once.\n\n**Q54.** State the three managed-portfolio minimums in order.\n> **A:** **Mutual fund ₹100–₹5,000 → PMS ₹50 lakh → AIF ₹1 crore.**\n\n**Q55.** What are the SEBI registration fees for AIFs?\n> **A:** **Application ₹1 lakh**; **Category I ₹5 lakh** (**angel fund ₹2 lakh**); **Category II ₹10 lakh**; **Category III ₹15 lakh**.\n\n**Q56.** What experience must an AIF's key personnel have?\n> **A:** At least **one key person with a minimum of 5 years' experience** in asset or portfolio management, advisory, securities trading or dealing in other financial assets — and the sponsor, manager and key personnel must all be **fit and proper persons**.\n\n**Q57.** Name three enablers of AIF growth in India.\n> **A:** A single clear **regulatory framework since 2012**; **pass-through taxation** for Categories I and II; and **rising HNI/UHNI wealth and family offices** — supported by a maturing **start-up ecosystem**, listing and demat of units, and **GIFT City/IFSC** structures.\n\n**Q58.** Why must an investment adviser be especially careful when recommending an AIF?\n> **A:** An AIF pays the IA **no commission** — the IA is paid by the client — so the recommendation must rest **entirely on suitability**. Recommending a decade-long lock-in to a client who cannot bear it is a **suitability failure**, however good the fund.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 13: Overview of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Category placement, the \"lower of\" sponsor commitment and pass-through taxation for I and II but not III carry the most marks.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Alternative Investment Funds in India are governed by:\nA) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  B) SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020  C) SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012  D) SEBI (Collective Investment Schemes) Regulations, 1999\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012**, notified in May 2012.\nA) Governs mutual funds. B) Governs portfolio managers. D) Governs collective investment schemes — a vehicle expressly excluded from the AIF definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The AIF Regulations replaced which earlier framework?\nA) SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993  B) SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996  C) The Companies Act, 2013  D) SEBI (Venture Capital Funds) Regulations, 1996\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **SEBI (Venture Capital Funds) Regulations, 1996** were repealed. Funds already registered under them continue until wound up; **all new funds register under the AIF Regulations**.\nA) Concerns debenture trustees, an unrelated intermediary. B) The mutual fund regulations remain fully in force. C) Company law was never the framework for pooled funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** An AIF is best defined as:\nA) A publicly offered pooled scheme  B) A privately pooled investment vehicle established in India with a defined investment policy  C) An unregulated offshore fund  D) A bank deposit product for wealthy clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Privately pooled**, **established in India** as a trust, company, LLP or body corporate, investing per a **defined investment policy** for investors' benefit.\nA) Public offering is exactly what an AIF may **not** do. C) An AIF is registered with SEBI and must be set up in India. D) An AIF is a pooled investment vehicle, not a deposit.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The minimum investment by an investor in an AIF is:\nA) ₹1 crore  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹25 lakh  D) ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **₹1 crore**, a deliberate sophistication filter.\nB) Is the **PMS** minimum — the most common confusion in this module. C) Is the concession for employees and directors. D) Is far above any regulatory floor.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** For employees and directors of the AIF or of its Manager, the minimum investment is:\nA) ₹1 crore  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹25 lakh**, because insiders already understand the strategy and risks.\nA) Applies to every other investor. C) Is the PMS minimum. D) There is still a floor; it is not waived entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The minimum corpus of an AIF scheme is:\nA) ₹5 crore  B) ₹20 crore  C) ₹10 crore  D) ₹100 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹20 crore** per scheme.\nA) Is below any AIF threshold. C) Is the **angel fund** corpus. D) Is a commercial scale, not a regulatory minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The minimum corpus of an angel fund is:\nA) ₹20 crore  B) ₹1 crore  C) ₹10 crore  D) ₹50 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹10 crore**, half the ordinary AIF requirement, reflecting the small size of angel deals.\nA) Is the general AIF corpus. B) Is the investor's minimum ticket, not the fund's corpus. D) Has no basis in the regulations.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The maximum number of investors in an AIF scheme is:\nA) 1000  B) 200  C) 49  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **1000 investors per scheme**.\nB) Is the private-placement limit under company law, not the AIF cap. C) Is the **angel fund** limit. D) An unlimited pool would be a public issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The maximum number of investors in an angel fund scheme is:\nA) 49  B) 200  C) 1000  D) 100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **49 angel investors** per scheme.\nB) and D) are not AIF thresholds. C) Is the general AIF cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Category I AIFs invest mainly in:\nA) Derivatives and complex trading strategies  B) Start-ups, SMEs, social ventures and infrastructure  C) Government securities only  D) Listed large-cap equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Sectors regarded as **socially or economically desirable**, which is why Category I is the only category on which incentives may be granted.\nA) Describes Category III. C) No AIF category is restricted to G-secs. D) Listed large caps are the domain of mutual funds and PMS.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Category II AIFs are best described as:\nA) Funds using leverage and derivatives  B) Only angel funds  C) Only venture capital funds  D) Funds that do not fall in Category I or III and do not use leverage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Category II is defined **by subtraction**: not I, not III, and **no borrowing except for day-to-day operations**.\nA) Describes Category III. B) and C) are Category I sub-types.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Category III AIFs are those that:\nA) Invest only in infrastructure  B) Employ diverse or complex trading strategies and may use leverage  C) Are always close-ended  D) Receive government concessions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Complex strategies **including derivatives**, and the **only** category permitted to leverage.\nA) Infrastructure funds are Category I. C) Category III may be **open** or close-ended. D) Only Category I may receive concessions.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** A venture capital fund is registered as:\nA) It is outside the AIF Regulations  B) Category II  C) Category III  D) Category I\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Venture capital funds are a named sub-category of **Category I**.\nA) All new venture funds must register as AIFs. B) Holds private equity and debt funds. C) Holds hedge funds.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** An angel fund is:\nA) A Category III AIF  B) A sub-category of venture capital funds under Category I  C) A mutual fund scheme  D) A Category II debt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Angel funds are **venture capital funds** that raise money from angel investors, and therefore sit in **Category I**.\nA) Category III is for complex trading strategies. C) Mutual funds are expressly excluded from the AIF definition. D) Debt funds are Category II and invest in debt securities.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A private equity fund is normally registered as:\nA) Category I  B) A collective investment scheme  C) Category III  D) Category II\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A PE fund neither targets Category I's designated sectors nor uses leverage, so it falls into the **residual Category II**.\nA) Category I is for start-ups, SMEs, social ventures and infrastructure. B) CIS is a separate, excluded framework. C) Requires complex trading strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A debt fund AIF falls under:\nA) Category I  B) Outside the AIF Regulations  C) Category III  D) Category II\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Debt funds invest in **debt or debt securities of investee companies** and take no leverage, so they are **Category II**.\nA) Debt is not a designated socially desirable sector. B) They are squarely within the AIF Regulations. C) No complex trading strategies are involved.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An infrastructure fund is registered as:\nA) Category II  B) Category I  C) Category III  D) A mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Infrastructure is expressly an economically desirable sector, making it a **Category I** sub-type.\nA) Is the residual category. C) Is for leverage and derivatives. D) Mutual funds are excluded from the AIF definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** An SME fund is registered as:\nA) Category III  B) Category II  C) Category I  D) A portfolio management service\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SME funds are a named **Category I** sub-type.\nA) Is for hedge funds. B) Is residual. D) A PMS manages individual, non-pooled portfolios.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A hedge fund in India is registered as:\nA) Category III  B) Category II  C) Category I  D) A mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A hedge fund **is** a Category III AIF: complex strategies, derivatives and leverage.\nB) Expressly excludes leverage. C) Is for socially desirable sectors. D) Mutual funds cannot run leveraged strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** A social venture fund is registered as:\nA) Category II  B) Category I  C) Category III  D) Such funds are not permitted in India\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Social ventures are a designated socially desirable sector, so the fund is **Category I**.\nA) Is residual. C) Is for complex trading. D) Social venture funds are expressly recognised in the regulations.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Category I and Category II AIFs must be:\nA) Close-ended  B) Open-ended  C) Either, at the manager's choice  D) Listed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Both must be **close-ended** with a **minimum tenure of three years**, because their unlisted assets cannot support redemptions.\nB) Only Category III may be open-ended. C) There is no choice for Categories I and II. D) Listing is optional, never mandatory.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which category may be open-ended?\nA) Category I  B) Category III  C) Category II  D) None of them\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Category III may be open or close-ended**, because it trades largely liquid, priced securities.\nA) and C) must be close-ended. D) Category III clearly may be open-ended.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The minimum tenure of a Category I or Category II AIF is:\nA) 1 year  B) 5 years  C) 3 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Three years**.\nA) Is shorter than the regulation permits. B) and D) are common commercial tenures but not the regulatory minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The tenure of a close-ended AIF may be extended by up to:\nA) 1 year  B) 2 years  C) 3 years  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Two years**, with investor consent.\nA) One year is the period allowed for **liquidation** after the tenure ends. C) and D) exceed the permitted extension.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Extension of an AIF's tenure requires the consent of:\nA) SEBI alone  B) Two-thirds of unit holders by value of their investment  C) A simple majority of investors by number  D) The sponsor only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Two-thirds of unit holders by value**, not by headcount.\nA) SEBI does not vote on commercial extensions. C) The test is by value, and two-thirds, not a simple majority. D) The sponsor cannot extend unilaterally.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The sponsor's or manager's continuing interest in a Category I or II AIF is:\nA) Lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore  B) Higher of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore  C) Lower of 5% of corpus or ₹10 crore  D) A flat ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **lower** of **2.5% of corpus** and **₹5 crore**.\nB) Reverses the test — \"lower of\" is the examiner's favourite trick here. C) Is the Category III requirement. D) No flat figure applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The sponsor's continuing interest in a Category III AIF is:\nA) Lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore  B) Nil  C) Higher of 5% of corpus or ₹10 crore  D) Lower of 5% of corpus or ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Double** the Category I/II requirement, because Category III may use leverage.\nA) Is the Category I and II rule. B) Every category requires skin in the game. C) Reverses the \"lower of\" test.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Leverage is permitted to:\nA) Category III only  B) Category II only  C) Category I only  D) All three categories\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Only **Category III**, and only with investor consent, disclosure and within SEBI's limit.\nB) and C) may borrow only for short temporary funding needs. D) Would defeat the whole purpose of the categorisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Tax pass-through status is available to:\nA) All three categories  B) No AIF category  C) Category III only  D) Categories I and II only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Categories I and II** enjoy pass-through; **Category III does not**.\nA) Category III is expressly outside pass-through. B) Pass-through clearly exists for I and II. C) Reverses the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Income of a Category III AIF is taxed:\nA) At the fund level  B) In the hands of the investors  C) Not at all  D) Only on redemption of units\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Without pass-through, the **fund** pays tax and distributes income net of it.\nB) Describes Categories I and II. C) No fund is tax-exempt. D) The tax point is not deferred to redemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The placement memorandum must be filed with SEBI at least:\nA) 7 days before launch  B) 90 days before launch  C) 30 days before launch  D) Immediately after launch\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **30 days before launch**, with SEBI's comments incorporated before the scheme opens.\nA) and B) are not the prescribed period. D) Filing after launch would defeat the purpose of pre-launch scrutiny.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** An AIF may raise funds only through:\nA) Private placement  B) A public issue  C) Stock exchange trading  D) Bank branch counters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Private placement** using a placement memorandum; this is why the investor cap exists.\nB) Public issues are prohibited for AIFs. C) Listing allows secondary transfer, not fund-raising. D) AIFs are not distributed over bank counters like deposits.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** If units of a close-ended AIF are listed, the minimum tradable lot is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹1 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹1 crore**, mirroring the minimum investment so that listing cannot become a back door for small investors.\nA) and B) are below the AIF minimum ticket. D) Is not the prescribed lot.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An AIF may be established in India as a:\nA) Partnership firm only  B) Trust, company, LLP or body corporate  C) Sole proprietorship  D) Hindu Undivided Family\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Those are the four permitted forms; in practice most Indian AIFs are **trusts**.\nA) A general partnership firm is not a permitted form. C) and D) cannot pool third-party money as a fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Which of these is expressly NOT an AIF?\nA) A mutual fund  B) A hedge fund  C) A private equity fund  D) A social venture fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Funds under the **SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996** are excluded from the AIF definition, as are CIS, family trusts, ESOP and gratuity trusts, holding companies and securitisation trusts.\nB) Is a Category III AIF. C) Is a Category II AIF. D) Is a Category I AIF.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Which category may receive incentives or concessions from the government or regulators?\nA) Category III  B) Category II  C) Category I  D) All categories equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only **Category I**, because it funds sectors the state regards as socially or economically desirable.\nA) and B) receive **no** specific concessions. D) The whole point of the categorisation is that they are not treated alike.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Why is the minimum for employees and directors set at ₹25 lakh rather than ₹1 crore?\nA) They earn less than clients  B) SEBI wants to shrink the fund's corpus  C) They already understand the strategy and risks, so the sophistication filter is unnecessary  D) It is a drafting inconsistency\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The ₹1 crore floor is a **filter against uninformed investors**; insiders are not uninformed. It also lets the investment team invest alongside clients, aligning interests.\nA) Income level is irrelevant to the rule. B) A lower ticket raises, not lowers, participation. D) The concession is deliberate and expressly stated.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** The 1000-investor cap exists because:\nA) An AIF may raise money only by private placement, so the pool must remain genuinely private  B) SEBI wants to limit fund sizes  C) Larger funds are prohibited by law  D) Record-keeping systems cannot handle more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Without a numeric cap, a \"private\" placement to thousands of people would become a **disguised public issue**, escaping the disclosure obligations of a public offering.\nB) There is no cap on corpus, only on investor count. C) Fund size is unrestricted. D) An administrative excuse, not the regulatory rationale.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A fund invests in unlisted mid-sized manufacturers, uses no derivatives and takes no borrowings. It is:\nA) Category I  B) Not registrable as an AIF  C) Category III  D) Category II\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Ordinary unlisted companies are not a designated Category I sector, and there is no leverage, so it lands in the **residual Category II**.\nA) Would need start-ups, SMEs, social ventures or infrastructure. B) It is a classic private equity AIF. C) Requires complex trading strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An individual angel investor must have net tangible assets of at least:\nA) ₹2 crore  B) ₹1 crore  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **₹2 crore**, excluding the principal residence, plus relevant early-stage, entrepreneurial or senior-management experience.\nB), C) and D) are not the prescribed test for an individual angel investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An angel fund may invest in a venture capital undertaking incorporated within:\nA) 1 year  B) 10 years  C) 7 years  D) 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Within **3 years** of the investment, keeping angel money genuinely early-stage.\nA) Would be unworkably narrow. B) and C) would let angel funds finance mature businesses, defeating the purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An angel fund's investment in a single venture must be:\nA) Between ₹50 lakh and ₹5 crore  B) At least ₹5 crore  C) Below ₹10 lakh  D) Of any size the manager chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **floor of ₹50 lakh** (to keep deals meaningful) and a **ceiling of ₹5 crore** (to keep them early-stage).\nB) Reverses the ceiling into a floor. C) Is far below the floor. D) Both a floor and a ceiling apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Investments made by an angel fund are locked in for:\nA) 1 year  B) There is no lock-in  C) 5 years  D) 3 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **3-year lock-in** on each investment, matching the multi-year gestation of a start-up.\nA) and C) are not the prescribed period. B) A lock-in is expressly required.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Which statement about angel funds is true?\nA) They must be listed on a stock exchange  B) They shall not be listed  C) They may be open-ended  D) They are Category III funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Angel funds shall not be listed.** Their tiny, locked-in, illiquid portfolios are unsuited to exchange trading.\nA) Reverses the rule. C) They are venture capital funds and therefore close-ended. D) They are Category I.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The minimum commitment by an angel investor is ₹25 lakh, which may be drawn down over a maximum of:\nA) 5 years  B) 3 years  C) 1 year  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Up to 5 years**, recognising that angel deals are found one at a time rather than all at once.\nB) and C) are shorter than permitted. D) Exceeds the limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** The sponsor's continuing interest:\nA) May be met by waiving management fees  B) Is optional if investors agree  C) Must be real money invested and retained in the fund  D) Is deposited with SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It exists to align the manager's outcome with the investors', which only real capital at risk achieves.\nA) Fee waivers are expressly not acceptable. B) It is mandatory. D) It is invested in the fund, not lodged with the regulator.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Investments of Category I and II AIFs must be valued by an independent valuer at least:\nA) Daily  B) Once every six months  C) Monthly  D) Once in the fund's life\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Every six months**, because unlisted assets have no market price and a more frequent valuation would add cost without meaning.\nA) and C) apply to daily-priced portfolios, not unlisted holdings. D) Would leave investors blind for years.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A close-ended Category III AIF must disclose NAV to investors at least:\nA) Quarterly  B) Monthly  C) Daily  D) Annually\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Quarterly for close-ended** Category III funds; **monthly for open-ended** ones.\nB) Applies to open-ended Category III funds. C) Is a mutual fund obligation. D) Is far too infrequent for a trading strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** The maximum a Category III AIF may invest in one investee company is:\nA) 25% of investable funds  B) There is no limit  C) 50% of investable funds  D) 10% of investable funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **10%** for Category III, tighter than the 25% allowed to Categories I and II, because leverage multiplies concentration risk.\nA) Is the Category I and II limit. B) and C) would permit dangerous concentration.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Temporary borrowing by a Category I or II AIF is limited to:\nA) It is entirely prohibited in every circumstance  B) 90 days and 25% of corpus  C) 1 year and 50% of corpus  D) 30 days, not more than 4 occasions a year, and 10% of investable funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A narrow allowance for genuine bridge needs — **≤30 days, ≤4 times a year, ≤10% of investable funds**.\nA) A limited temporary facility is permitted. B) and C) invent looser limits that would amount to leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Carried interest in a private equity fund is:\nA) A fixed annual management fee  B) A share of profits, typically up to 20%, payable after a minimum return is achieved  C) An entry load on subscription  D) Interest paid to the fund's lenders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Carry is the **performance share**, typically up to **20%**, with roughly **80%** of profits going to investors, and only after the hurdle is cleared.\nA) That is the management fee, charged regardless of performance. C) AIFs do not levy entry loads of this kind. D) Carry is a profit share, not debt servicing.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Mezzanine financing is typically provided to companies that:\nA) Have only just been conceived  B) Are insolvent and being liquidated  C) Are expected to go public, and it may involve debt or equity  D) Are already listed large caps\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Mezzanine sits **late in the growth path**, just before a public listing, and can be structured as debt, equity or a hybrid.\nA) Describes seed financing. B) Describes distressed or turnaround investing. D) Listed large caps do not need private mezzanine capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Which is NOT a normal exit route for a PE or VC fund?\nA) An initial public offering  B) Sale to a financial or strategic investor  C) Daily redemption at NAV  D) Buyback by the company or promoters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Daily redemption is a **mutual fund** feature; a close-ended AIF holding unlisted stakes cannot offer it.\nA), B) and D) are the three standard exit mechanisms, agreed with promoters at the time of entry.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A social venture fund is distinctive because:\nA) It may accept and make grants, and its investors may accept muted returns  B) It uses leverage aggressively  C) It is always open-ended  D) It guarantees a minimum return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Its investors knowingly accept **below-market (\"muted\") returns** in exchange for measurable social impact, and the grant mechanism is unique to it.\nB) Category I funds may not leverage. C) It is close-ended like all Category I funds. D) No AIF may guarantee returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A debt fund AIF invests in debt securities of investee companies because:\nA) An AIF is a pooled investment vehicle and cannot run a lending business with investors' money  B) SEBI prescribes a minimum credit rating  C) Equity is prohibited for Category II funds  D) Debt investments are risk-free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — An AIF **invests in instruments**; it is not a lender. That structural point is why the vehicle holds debt securities rather than making loans.\nB) No minimum rating is prescribed. C) Category II funds routinely hold equity. D) Unlisted corporate debt carries substantial credit risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Which structure permits complex trading strategies and leverage?\nA) A Category I infrastructure fund  B) A Category II private equity fund  C) A Category III hedge fund  D) An open-ended mutual fund scheme\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Leverage and complex strategies define **Category III**.\nA) and B) may borrow only for short-term operational needs. D) Mutual funds may not run leveraged strategies.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Rank the minimum investments for a mutual fund, a PMS and an AIF respectively:\nA) ₹1 crore, ₹50 lakh, ₹100  B) ₹100–₹5,000, ₹50 lakh, ₹1 crore  C) ₹100–₹5,000, ₹1 crore, ₹50 lakh  D) All three require ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Mutual fund ₹100–₹5,000 → PMS ₹50 lakh → AIF ₹1 crore.**\nA) Reverses the order entirely. C) Swaps the PMS and AIF figures. D) Only the PMS minimum is ₹50 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Which vehicle does NOT pool investors' money?\nA) An AIF  B) A mutual fund  C) A PMS  D) A hedge fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — In a **PMS**, securities are held in the **client's own name and demat account**; there is no common pool.\nA), B) and D) are all pooled vehicles in which the investor holds units of a common portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A client says a listed AIF must be liquid. The adviser should explain that:\nA) Listing on a recognised stock exchange guarantees him a daily exit at a fair price  B) Listed AIF units can be redeemed with the fund at NAV on demand, like a mutual fund  C) The minimum tradable lot is ₹1 crore and buyers are few, so listing is a right to sell, not liquidity  D) Every AIF is compulsorily listed once its scheme reaches its final close\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A ₹1 crore lot in a thin market means an exit may simply not be available when wanted.\nA) An exchange listing creates no obligation on anyone to buy. B) Redemption at NAV is a mutual fund feature. D) Listing is optional, and angel funds may not list at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** The typical horizon an adviser should assume for an AIF commitment is:\nA) 7–10 years  B) 1–2 years  C) 6 months  D) 30 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Close-ended, minimum 3-year tenure, commercially usually 7–10 years, extendable by 2 more.\nB) and C) are far too short for unlisted assets to be built and exited. D) Overstates a typical fund life.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** An AIF is clearly unsuitable for a client who:\nA) Has a ₹40 crore portfolio  B) Has a 12-year horizon  C) Understands unlisted investing well  D) May need the money within two years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — There is **no redemption on demand**; committing money that may be needed in two years is a suitability failure.\nA) A large portfolio supports, rather than blocks, an allocation. B) A long horizon fits an AIF well. C) Understanding is a positive factor.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** In portfolio construction, alternatives are best used as:\nA) The core of the portfolio  B) A substitute for the emergency fund  C) A satellite added on top of a completed core  D) The client's sole holding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Alternatives complement a completed **core** of equity, debt and cash; they never replace it.\nA) The core must be liquid and diversified — the opposite of an AIF. B) An emergency fund must be instantly accessible. D) Concentration in illiquid assets is indefensible.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The main portfolio benefit of alternatives is:\nA) Guaranteed higher returns  B) Diversification arising from low correlation with listed markets  C) Daily liquidity  D) Capital protection\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Their returns are driven by deal-specific factors rather than the index, so they **lower total portfolio risk** even though each is individually risky.\nA) Nothing is guaranteed. C) They are the least liquid assets a client will own. D) Total loss on a deal is a real possibility.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** The \"illiquidity premium\" means:\nA) The extra expected return an investor earns for accepting a lock-in  B) A penalty charged for early exit  C) A levy collected by SEBI  D) A discount on the entry price of units\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The investor is **paid** for surrendering liquidity; that compensation is a core reason to hold alternatives at all.\nB) Describes an exit load. C) SEBI charges registration fees to the fund, not a premium to investors. D) It is a return concept, not a price concession.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** The SEBI registration fee for a Category III AIF is:\nA) ₹2 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹15 lakh  D) ₹10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹15 lakh**, the highest, matching the greatest operating freedom.\nA) Is the angel fund fee. B) Is Category I. D) Is Category II.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The AIF Regulations require at least one key person with relevant experience of at least:\nA) 2 years  B) 15 years  C) 10 years  D) 5 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — At least **5 years** in asset or portfolio management, advisory, securities trading or dealing in other financial assets.\nA) Is below the prescribed threshold. B) and C) exceed what the regulations require.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** An AIF wishing to invest in an associate needs the approval of:\nA) 75% of investors by value  B) SEBI  C) The sponsor  D) The trustee alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A related-party investment is a conflict of interest, so a **75% supermajority by value** must sanction it.\nB) SEBI does not clear individual deals. C) The sponsor is the very party in conflict. D) The trustee cannot override an investor-protection threshold.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Un-invested money of an AIF may be parked in:\nA) Unlisted equity of prospective targets  B) Physical real estate  C) Long-dated derivatives  D) Liquid mutual funds, bank deposits and other high-quality liquid assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Idle capital must stay **safe and liquid** until a deal is ready, so only high-quality liquid instruments qualify.\nA) That would be an investment, not a parking place. B) Real estate is the least liquid option available. C) Derivatives are neither safe nor liquid parking.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** The ₹1 crore an AIF investor \"invests\" is best described as:\nA) A lump sum paid on day one  B) A commitment drawn down over time through capital calls  C) A refundable security deposit  D) An annual subscription\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fund calls capital as deals are found, so the investor must keep the committed amount **available**, not merely have it once.\nA) Some funds do call fully upfront, but the general model is drawdowns. C) It is invested risk capital, not a deposit. D) It is a one-time commitment, not a recurring fee.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Why must an investment adviser recommend AIFs purely on suitability grounds?\nA) SEBI caps the returns an AIF may earn, so all AIFs deliver much the same outcome  B) AIFs are open to all retail investors, so suitability is the only remaining filter  C) AIF returns are guaranteed by the sponsor's continuing interest in the scheme  D) An AIF pays the adviser no commission, so only the client's suitability can justify the advice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A registered investment adviser is paid by the client and must segregate advice from distribution; the recommendation therefore has to stand on **suitability alone**.\nA) Returns are not capped. B) The ₹1 crore minimum excludes most retail investors by design. C) No return is guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A Category II AIF has a corpus of ₹300 crore. The sponsor's minimum continuing interest is:\nA) ₹7.5 crore  B) ₹15 crore  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 2.5% × ₹300 crore = **₹7.5 crore**; the rule is the **lower of** that and **₹5 crore**, so the sponsor commits **₹5 crore**.\nA) Applies the percentage but ignores the ₹5 crore cap. B) Uses the Category III percentage. D) Uses the Category III rupee cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A Category II AIF has a corpus of ₹150 crore. The sponsor's minimum continuing interest is:\nA) ₹5 crore  B) ₹1.5 crore  C) ₹7.5 crore  D) ₹3.75 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 2.5% × ₹150 crore = **₹3.75 crore**, which is **lower than ₹5 crore**, so ₹3.75 crore applies. **When the corpus is small, the percentage binds; when it is large, the rupee cap binds.**\nA) Ignores that the percentage produced a smaller figure. B) Applies 1% instead of 2.5%. C) Uses the Category III rate of 5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of ₹120 crore. The sponsor's minimum continuing interest is:\nA) ₹10 crore  B) ₹3 crore  C) ₹6 crore  D) ₹12 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 5% × ₹120 crore = **₹6 crore**, lower than the ₹10 crore cap, so **₹6 crore**.\nA) Ignores that the percentage gives less. B) Applies the Category I/II rate of 2.5%. D) Applies 10% instead of 5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of ₹400 crore. The sponsor's minimum continuing interest is:\nA) ₹20 crore  B) ₹40 crore  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 5% × ₹400 crore = **₹20 crore**; the **lower of** that and **₹10 crore** is **₹10 crore**.\nA) Ignores the rupee cap. B) Applies 10% and ignores the cap. C) Applies the Category I/II cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An angel fund has a corpus of ₹30 crore. The sponsor's minimum continuing interest is:\nA) ₹50 lakh  B) ₹75 lakh  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹1.5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — For an angel fund the requirement is the **lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹50 lakh**. 2.5% × ₹30 crore = **₹75 lakh**, so the **₹50 lakh cap binds**.\nB) Ignores the ₹50 lakh cap. C) Uses the ordinary Category I/II cap. D) Applies 5% instead of 2.5%.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A Category I AIF has investable funds of ₹240 crore. The most it may put into one investee company is:\nA) ₹24 crore  B) ₹240 crore  C) ₹120 crore  D) ₹60 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **25% × ₹240 crore = ₹60 crore** for Categories I and II.\nA) Applies the 10% Category III limit. B) Would be a single-stock fund. C) Applies 50%, which no category permits.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A Category III AIF has investable funds of ₹500 crore. The most it may put into one investee company is:\nA) ₹125 crore  B) ₹50 crore  C) ₹250 crore  D) ₹100 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **10% × ₹500 crore = ₹50 crore**. Category III's limit is tighter because leverage magnifies concentration risk.\nA) Applies the 25% Category I/II limit. C) Applies 50%. D) Applies 20%, which is not a prescribed limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A Category II AIF with investable funds of ₹180 crore needs bridge funding. What is the most it may borrow, and for how long?\nA) ₹45 crore for 90 days  B) ₹90 crore for one year  C) ₹18 crore for up to 30 days  D) Nothing at all in any circumstance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **10% × ₹180 crore = ₹18 crore**, for **not more than 30 days**, on not more than **4 occasions in a year**.\nA) Uses 25% and 90 days, neither of which is permitted. B) Would be leverage, which Category II may not use. D) A limited temporary facility is allowed.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A scheme plans to admit the maximum permitted number of investors, each subscribing exactly the minimum ticket. What is the smallest corpus that implies?\nA) ₹20 crore  B) ₹49 crore  C) ₹100 crore  D) ₹1,000 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **1000 investors × ₹1 crore = ₹1,000 crore**. The investor cap is therefore no obstacle to a very large fund.\nA) Is the minimum corpus, unrelated to the investor count. B) Confuses the angel fund investor cap with a rupee figure. C) Would need only 100 investors at the minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A Category II scheme has firm commitments from 18 investors of ₹1 crore each. Can it launch?\nA) Yes — the investor count is within the cap  B) No — the corpus of ₹18 crore is below the ₹20 crore minimum  C) No — a scheme needs at least 50 investors  D) Yes, with SEBI's special permission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 18 × ₹1 crore = **₹18 crore**, short of the **₹20 crore** minimum corpus. At least **₹2 crore more** must be raised.\nA) Passing the investor cap does not satisfy the corpus test. C) There is a maximum of 1000 investors, not a minimum of 50. D) The corpus floor is not waived on request.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An angel fund scheme admits the maximum number of investors, each at the minimum commitment. What corpus does that produce, and does it clear the minimum?\nA) ₹9.8 crore; no, it falls short  B) ₹12.25 crore; yes, comfortably above ₹10 crore  C) ₹49 crore; yes  D) ₹24.5 crore; yes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **49 angel investors × ₹25 lakh = ₹1,225 lakh = ₹12.25 crore**, which clears the **₹10 crore** angel fund corpus with ₹2.25 crore to spare.\nA) Uses ₹20 lakh per investor. C) Multiplies 49 by ₹1 crore, the wrong minimum for an angel fund. D) Uses ₹50 lakh per investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An investor commits ₹3 crore to a PE AIF. The fund draws 30% in year 1 and 25% in year 2. What has been drawn, and what remains committed?\nA) ₹90 lakh drawn; ₹2.1 crore remaining  B) ₹3 crore drawn; nil remaining  C) ₹1.65 crore drawn; ₹1.35 crore remaining  D) ₹1.35 crore drawn; ₹1.65 crore remaining\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Drawn = (30% + 25%) × ₹3 crore = **55% × ₹3 crore = ₹1.65 crore**. Remaining = ₹3 crore − ₹1.65 crore = **₹1.35 crore**, which the investor must keep available for future capital calls.\nA) Counts only year 1. B) Assumes a full upfront payment. D) Swaps the drawn and remaining amounts.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A close-ended AIF launched in April 2018 declared a 5-year tenure. Assuming the maximum extension is approved, by when must its assets be fully liquidated?\nA) April 2023  B) April 2026  C) April 2025  D) April 2028\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tenure ends **April 2023**; a maximum **2-year extension** with two-thirds consent by value takes it to **April 2025**; liquidation must be completed **within 1 year** after that, i.e. by **April 2026**.\nA) Is the original expiry, before extension. C) Is the extended expiry, before the liquidation year. D) Assumes a 5-year extension, which is not permitted.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** An angel fund wants to invest ₹8 crore in a single start-up. Is this permitted?\nA) Yes, with investor consent  B) No — the floor is ₹10 crore  C) Yes, angel funds have no per-deal limit  D) No — the ceiling per investment is ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Angel fund investments must be between **₹50 lakh and ₹5 crore**, so ₹8 crore breaches the ceiling.\nA) Investor consent cannot override a regulatory ceiling. B) Misstates a ceiling as a floor and inflates it. C) Both a floor and a ceiling apply.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A start-up incorporated two years ago has a turnover of ₹40 crore. Can an angel fund invest in it?\nA) No — the turnover exceeds the ₹25 crore ceiling  B) Yes — it is under three years old  C) Yes, but only up to ₹50 lakh  D) No — it is too young to qualify\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Every condition must be met. The company passes the **3-year incorporation** test but fails the **turnover below ₹25 crore** test.\nB) Satisfying one condition is not enough. C) The size limits do not rescue an ineligible company. D) Being young is a requirement, not a disqualification.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A start-up is two years old with turnover of ₹8 crore, but is promoted by an industrial group with turnover of ₹450 crore. Can an angel fund invest?\nA) Yes — the company itself qualifies  B) Yes, with SEBI's prior approval  C) Yes, up to ₹50 lakh only  D) No — the promoting group's turnover exceeds ₹300 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The venture must **not** be promoted by or related to an industrial group with turnover above **₹300 crore**. Angel money is meant for genuinely independent start-ups, not for group subsidiaries.\nA) The group test is a separate, binding condition. B) SEBI does not clear individual angel deals. C) The deal size limits do not cure ineligibility.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A PE AIF has a corpus of ₹500 crore, earns total profits of ₹150 crore over its life, and pays carried interest of 20% on profits above an aggregate hurdle of ₹50 crore. What is the carried interest?\nA) ₹30 crore  B) ₹20 crore  C) ₹10 crore  D) ₹50 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Profit above the hurdle = ₹150 crore − ₹50 crore = **₹100 crore**. Carry = 20% × ₹100 crore = **₹20 crore**.\nA) Applies 20% to the full ₹150 crore of profit, ignoring the hurdle. C) Applies 10% instead of 20%. D) Is the hurdle amount itself, not the carry.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Continuing with that fund — total profit ₹150 crore and carried interest ₹20 crore — how much profit is left for the investors?\nA) ₹150 crore  B) ₹130 crore  C) ₹100 crore  D) ₹120 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹150 crore − ₹20 crore = **₹130 crore**, about **87%** of the profit pool.\nA) Ignores the carry altogether. C) Is the profit above the hurdle, before carry is deducted. D) Deducts ₹30 crore, the figure that would apply with no hurdle.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A fund manager wants an open-ended structure and the freedom to short index futures. Which registration must he seek?\nA) Category I  B) Category II  C) A mutual fund licence  D) Category III\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Only **Category III** may be **open-ended** and may use **derivatives and leverage**.\nA) and B) must be close-ended and may not leverage. C) A mutual fund cannot run a short, leveraged strategy either.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A Category I AIF earns ₹4 crore of interest income. An investor holding 5% of the fund is taxed on:\nA) ₹20 lakh, in his own hands at his applicable rate  B) Nothing — the fund settles all tax before distributing  C) ₹4 crore, being the fund's whole income  D) ₹2 lakh, in his own hands at his applicable rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Category I enjoys **pass-through**: 5% × ₹4 crore = **₹20 lakh** is deemed to arise directly to him and is taxed **at his own rate**, retaining its character as interest income.\nB) Describes Category III treatment; a Category I fund pays no tax at the fund level. C) He is taxed only on his proportionate share, not on the fund's entire income. D) Uses 0.5% instead of 5% — a decimal slip; 5% × ₹4 crore is ₹20 lakh, not ₹2 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** The same ₹4 crore of interest arises instead in a Category III AIF. What changes for the same 5% investor?\nA) Nothing at all  B) The income becomes fully exempt in his hands  C) He pays tax twice on the same income  D) The fund is taxed on the income before distribution, so he cannot apply his own lower slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Category III has **no pass-through**, so tax is borne **at the fund level** and every investor effectively suffers the same rate regardless of his own slab.\nA) The tax outcome changes materially. B) The income has already been taxed; it is not exempt as a concession. C) Fund-level taxation avoids, rather than creates, double taxation.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An investor in a low tax slab compares a Category II fund and a Category III fund with identical pre-tax returns. Which is more tax-efficient for him, and why?\nA) Category II, because pass-through lets him pay at his own low rate rather than the fund's rate  B) Category III, because leverage boosts returns  C) They are identical after tax  D) Neither is taxed at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Pass-through preserves the investor's **own** rate; fund-level taxation in Category III applies a single high rate to everyone. **The lower the investor's slab, the larger the disadvantage of Category III.**\nB) Leverage affects pre-tax return, which the question holds constant. C) The tax treatments differ fundamentally. D) Both are taxed, only at different levels.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client has ₹80 lakh of surplus and wants to invest in an AIF. What must the adviser say?\nA) Split it across two AIF schemes of ₹40 lakh each to stay within the rules  B) SEBI will waive the ₹1 crore limit on a written request from the adviser  C) The minimum is ₹1 crore, so he is ineligible unless he is an employee or director of the AIF or its Manager  D) He may invest ₹25 lakh in any AIF because that floor is open to everyone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **₹1 crore per investor per scheme** minimum is absolute; the only relaxation is the **₹25 lakh** floor for employees and directors of the AIF or its Manager.\nA) The minimum applies to each scheme separately, so splitting makes matters worse. B) The threshold is not waivable. D) The ₹25 lakh floor is not open to ordinary investors.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An employee of the Manager wants to invest ₹25 lakh in the fund she helps run. Is this permitted?\nA) No — ₹1 crore applies to everyone without exception  B) No — employees may not invest in their own fund  C) Yes — employees and directors of the AIF or its Manager may invest from ₹25 lakh  D) Yes, but only in a Category III fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The regulations expressly permit a **₹25 lakh** minimum for employees and directors of the AIF and of the Manager, encouraging the team to invest alongside clients.\nA) The exception exists precisely for such people. B) Co-investment by the team is encouraged, not banned. D) The concession is not category-specific.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An investor holds units of a listed close-ended AIF worth ₹60 lakh and wants to sell them on the exchange. What is the difficulty?\nA) The minimum tradable lot is ₹1 crore, so his holding is below a single lot  B) AIF units may never be listed  C) Every sale requires SEBI's approval  D) There is no difficulty at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Listed AIF units trade in a **minimum lot of ₹1 crore**, so a ₹60 lakh holding cannot be sold on the exchange as it stands.\nB) Close-ended AIF units may be listed after the final close. C) SEBI does not approve individual trades. D) The lot size is a real and binding obstacle.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An open-ended Category III AIF tells investors it will publish NAV twice a year. Is that acceptable?\nA) Yes — twice a year exceeds the annual requirement  B) Yes, if the investors consent in writing  C) No — it must publish NAV daily like a mutual fund  D) No — an open-ended Category III AIF must disclose NAV at least monthly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Monthly** for open-ended Category III funds and **quarterly** for close-ended ones. Six-monthly valuation is the **Category I and II** rule, and it does not apply here.\nA) Confuses the Category I and II valuation cycle with Category III NAV disclosure. B) Investors cannot contract out of a disclosure requirement. C) Daily NAV is a mutual fund obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A Category II AIF's manager proposes to borrow ₹60 crore for three years against investable funds of ₹200 crore to enhance returns. Is that permitted?\nA) Yes — Category II funds may leverage freely once the placement memorandum discloses it  B) Yes, up to 25% of investable funds, which is the borrowing limit for Categories I and II  C) No — Categories I and II may borrow only for temporary needs, up to 30 days and 10% of investable funds  D) Yes, if two-thirds of the investors by value consent to the borrowing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The proposal fails on **both** counts: ₹60 crore is **30%** of investable funds against a **10% (₹20 crore)** limit, and three years is far beyond the **30-day** cap. Return-enhancing leverage is a **Category III** privilege.\nA) Leverage is precisely what excludes a fund from Category II. B) 25% is the single-investee concentration limit, not a borrowing limit. D) Investor consent cannot create a power the category does not have.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A sponsor runs a Category II AIF with a corpus of ₹80 crore and a Category III AIF with a corpus of ₹250 crore. What is the total continuing interest required across both?\nA) ₹15 crore  B) ₹14.5 crore  C) ₹7 crore  D) ₹12 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Category II:** 2.5% × ₹80 crore = **₹2 crore**, lower than ₹5 crore, so ₹2 crore. **Category III:** 5% × ₹250 crore = ₹12.5 crore, so the **₹10 crore cap** binds. **Total = ₹2 crore + ₹10 crore = ₹12 crore.**\nA) Applies ₹5 crore to the Category II fund, ignoring that 2.5% gave less. B) Ignores the ₹10 crore cap on the Category III fund: ₹2 crore + ₹12.5 crore. C) Applies the Category I/II rule — 2.5% capped at ₹5 crore — to **both** funds: ₹2 crore + ₹5 crore.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A fund invests 80% of its money in unlisted infrastructure SPVs, takes no borrowings and is close-ended for 10 years. Its category and tax treatment are:\nA) Category III; taxed at the fund level  B) Category I; pass-through to investors  C) Category II; taxed at the fund level  D) Category I; taxed at the fund level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Infrastructure is a designated economically desirable sector, making this a **Category I** infrastructure fund, which enjoys **pass-through** taxation (except on business income).\nA) Category III requires complex trading strategies and leverage. C) Infrastructure funds are expressly Category I, not the residual category. D) Right category, wrong tax treatment — pass-through applies to Categories I and II.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises this chapter?\nA) An AIF is a privately placed, ₹1 crore minimum pool whose category decides its leverage, sponsor commitment and tax treatment  B) AIFs are a cheaper, more liquid substitute for mutual funds aimed at the same investor base  C) All three AIF categories are taxed identically, so the category affects only the investment strategy  D) AIFs guarantee returns superior to listed markets in exchange for a multi-year lock-in\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It captures the **structure** (private placement, ₹1 crore, largely close-ended), what the **category** determines (leverage, sponsor interest, tax), and by implication the **suitability** boundary: sophisticated investors with a long horizon and no liquidity need.\nB) AIFs are far costlier and far less liquid than mutual funds, and the ₹1 crore floor excludes the retail base. C) Categories I and II pass through; Category III is taxed at the fund level, so tax is very much category-driven. D) No AIF may guarantee anything, and many individual deals fail outright.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Watch the words \"lower of\" and \"commitment\" — both decide an answer here.\n\n### Case: The Raghavan Family Office and a Category II PE AIF\n\nMr Ananth Raghavan, 52, manages his family office. Its investible corpus is **₹22 crore**, already spread across equity mutual funds, a debt portfolio and a PMS. He is now evaluating a **Category II private equity AIF**:\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Scheme corpus** | **₹400 crore** |\n| **His commitment** | **₹2 crore** |\n| **Structure** | Close-ended, tenure 7 years |\n| **Drawdown schedule** | **40%** in year 1, **35%** in year 2, **25%** in year 3 |\n| **Fees** | Management fee 2% p.a.; **carried interest 20%** of profits above an aggregate hurdle |\n| **Expected lifetime profit** | **₹180 crore**, with an aggregate hurdle of **₹100 crore** |\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the minimum continuing interest the sponsor must maintain in this scheme?\nA) ₹10 crore  B) ₹20 crore  C) ₹5 crore  D) ₹1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — For a **Category II** AIF the requirement is the **lower of 2.5% of corpus or ₹5 crore**. 2.5% × ₹400 crore = **₹10 crore**, so the **₹5 crore cap binds** and the sponsor must hold **₹5 crore**.\nA) Is the percentage result, ignoring the ₹5 crore cap — and also the Category III cap. B) Applies 5% and ignores the cap, which would be the Category III percentage. D) Has no basis in the regulations.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** By the end of year 2, how much of Mr Raghavan's commitment has been drawn, and how much remains?\nA) ₹80 lakh drawn; ₹1.2 crore remaining  B) ₹2 crore drawn; nil remaining  C) ₹1.5 crore drawn; ₹50 lakh remaining  D) ₹70 lakh drawn; ₹1.3 crore remaining\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Drawn = (40% + 35%) × ₹2 crore = **75% × ₹2 crore = ₹1.5 crore**. Remaining commitment = ₹2 crore − ₹1.5 crore = **₹50 lakh**, which he must keep liquid and available for the year-3 call.\nA) Counts only the year-1 drawdown of 40%. B) Assumes the whole commitment is paid upfront. D) Uses 35% alone.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** On a lifetime profit of ₹180 crore with an aggregate hurdle of ₹100 crore and 20% carry, what is the carried interest, and what is left for investors?\nA) ₹16 crore; ₹164 crore  B) ₹36 crore; ₹144 crore  C) ₹20 crore; ₹160 crore  D) Nil; ₹180 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Profit above the hurdle = ₹180 crore − ₹100 crore = **₹80 crore**. Carry = 20% × ₹80 crore = **₹16 crore**. Investors retain ₹180 crore − ₹16 crore = **₹164 crore**.\nB) Applies 20% to the whole ₹180 crore, ignoring the hurdle. C) Takes 20% of the ₹100 crore hurdle instead of the excess. D) The hurdle was exceeded, so carry is certainly payable.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Mr Raghavan's ₹2 crore is 0.5% of the ₹400 crore corpus. What is his share of the ₹164 crore investor profit, and how is it taxed?\nA) ₹40 lakh, taxed at the fund level before distribution  B) ₹90 lakh, taxed at the fund level before distribution  C) ₹82 lakh, taxed in his own hands under pass-through  D) ₹41 lakh, entirely exempt in his hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Share = 0.5% × ₹164 crore = **₹82 lakh**. Because this is a **Category II** AIF, **pass-through** applies: the income is deemed to arise **directly to him**, keeps its character (capital gain, interest or dividend), and is taxed **at his own applicable rate**. Business income of the fund, if any, would still be taxed at the fund level.\nA) Takes 0.5% of the ₹80 crore excess over the hurdle instead of the ₹164 crore investor profit, and taxes it at the fund level, which is the **Category III** mechanism. B) Takes 0.5% of the ₹180 crore pre-carry profit and applies fund-level tax — wrong base and wrong mechanism. D) Halves the share and wrongly treats it as exempt.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Mr Raghavan asks whether he should switch the commitment to a Category III hedge-strategy AIF instead. What is the best advice?\nA) Yes — Category III always outperforms Category II because it may use leverage and trade derivatives  B) Category III adds leverage but loses pass-through, so the choice must rest on his risk tolerance, horizon and allocation  C) There is no practical difference between the categories once the ₹1 crore minimum has been met  D) Yes — Category III has a lower ₹25 lakh minimum for outside investors, so it is easier to enter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Three things change together. **Leverage and complex strategies** become available, raising both return potential and loss potential. **Pass-through is lost**, so income is taxed **at the fund level** rather than at his own rate. And the **sponsor's continuing interest doubles** to the lower of 5% of corpus or ₹10 crore, which tells the adviser something about how much riskier SEBI considers the category.\n\n**The wider lesson:** with a ₹22 crore family office committing ₹2 crore, roughly **9% of the portfolio** is going into a single illiquid, close-ended vehicle. Before comparing categories at all, the adviser must confirm that the **remaining ₹20 crore** meets every liquidity need for the next decade, that the ₹50 lakh of undrawn commitment stays available for capital calls, and that Mr Raghavan can absorb a poor outcome. **Suitability is decided before category selection, never after.**\n\nA) Leverage magnifies losses as readily as gains. C) The categories differ in structure, leverage, sponsor commitment and taxation. D) The ₹25 lakh floor applies only to employees and directors of the AIF or its Manager, not to outside investors in any category.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "14", "title": "Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "# Chapter 14: Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 5 · Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring and Evaluation (20 marks).**\n> This is the most **numerical** chapter in the syllabus. Almost every mark comes from four things: the **expected-return weighted average**, the **two-security risk formula**, the **role of correlation**, and the **efficient frontier**. Learn the formulas by *using* them, not by staring at them.\n\n---\n\n## 14.1 The Framework — What Markowitz Actually Discovered\n\n**Harry Markowitz** published *\"Portfolio Selection\"* in the **Journal of Finance in 1952** and won the **Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 1990** (shared with Merton Miller and William Sharpe). He is called the **father of Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT)**.\n\nBefore 1952, investing advice was: *\"find good securities.\"* Markowitz's insight was that **a security cannot be judged on its own** — only by what it does **to the portfolio**.\n\n### 🔑 The one idea that matters\n\n> **The expected return of a portfolio IS the weighted average of the securities' returns.**\n> **The RISK of a portfolio is NOT the weighted average of the securities' risks — it is almost always LESS.**\n\nThat asymmetry is the entire theory. Return adds up in a straight line; risk does not — because when one holding falls, another may be rising, and the two partially cancel.\n\n**The rupee illustration.** You hold ₹5 lakh in an equity fund and ₹5 lakh in a liquid fund.\n- Expected return: if equity is expected to return **14%** and liquid **6%**, the portfolio expects **10%** — exactly halfway. Straight-line.\n- Risk: if equity's standard deviation is **20%** and liquid's is **2%**, the portfolio's risk is **NOT** 11%. Because the two barely move together, it works out **below** 11%. That shortfall is the **free lunch of diversification**.\n\n### MPT vs naive diversification\n\n| | **Naive diversification** | **Markowitz (MPT) diversification** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rule | \"Own many securities\" | \"Own securities that **do not move together**\" |\n| Input used | Number of holdings | **Covariance / correlation** between holdings |\n| 30 IT stocks? | Looks diversified | **Not diversified** — correlations near +1 |\n| Measurement | None | Quantified — risk is computed, not guessed |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"It's not how many, it's how different.\"**\n> Thirty Nifty IT stocks are one bet wearing thirty costumes. One IT stock + one FMCG stock + one G-Sec is three bets.\n\n---\n\n## 14.2 ⭐ Assumptions of Modern Portfolio Theory\n\nMPT is a *model*, and models rest on assumptions. These are directly examinable.\n\n| # | Assumption | Plain meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Investors see each investment as a **probability distribution of expected returns** over a holding period | You do not \"know\" the return; you know the odds |\n| 2 | Investors **maximise expected utility** for one period, and their utility curves show **diminishing marginal utility of wealth** | The tenth lakh gives you less pleasure than the first |\n| 3 | Investors measure risk by the **variability (variance / standard deviation) of expected returns** | Risk = volatility, nothing else |\n| 4 | Decisions are based **solely on expected return and risk** — utility is a function of **E(R) and σ only** | Two numbers per portfolio; nothing else enters |\n| 5 | For a given **risk**, investors prefer **more return**; for a given **return**, investors prefer **less risk** | All investors are **risk-averse** |\n\nAdditional working assumptions usually stated alongside these:\n\n- **Single holding period** — the model is static, not multi-period\n- **Markets are efficient** and information is freely available\n- **No taxes and no transaction costs**\n- **Assets are perfectly divisible** (you can hold any fraction)\n- **Investors are rational** and have **homogeneous expectations** about returns, variances and covariances\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** MPT does **not** assume that all investors have the *same* risk tolerance. It assumes all are **risk-averse** — but *how* risk-averse differs, which is exactly why different investors pick **different points on the same efficient frontier**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Second trap.** MPT assumes risk is fully captured by **variance**. It does **not** distinguish upside volatility from downside volatility — a criticism that later produced the Sortino ratio and downside-risk measures (Chapter 16).\n\n---\n\n## 14.3 ⭐ Risk Aversion — Three Investor Types\n\n**Risk aversion** is the preference for **certainty over uncertainty when the expected value is the same**. A risk-averse investor demands **extra expected return** — a **risk premium** — before accepting extra risk.\n\n### The classic fair-gamble test\n\nOffer a client a choice:\n- **Option A:** ₹10,00,000 in cash, guaranteed.\n- **Option B:** a coin toss — **heads ₹20,00,000, tails ₹0**.\n\nBoth have an **expected value of ₹10,00,000** (0.5 × 20,00,000 + 0.5 × 0). The choice reveals everything:\n\n| Investor type | Chooses | Behaviour | Real-world example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Risk-averse** | **Option A** (the certain ₹10 lakh) | Rejects a fair gamble; needs a **risk premium** to accept risk | Most investors; prefers an FD to a penny stock at the same expected return |\n| **Risk-neutral** | **Indifferent** | Judges **only by expected return**; risk simply does not enter the decision | A theoretical benchmark; a very large, well-diversified institution behaves close to this |\n| **Risk-seeking (risk-loving)** | **Option B** (the coin toss) | Accepts a fair — even an **unfair** — gamble for the chance of a big payoff | The lottery ticket buyer; a punter in deep out-of-the-money options |\n\n### How each type looks on paper\n\n| | **Risk-averse** | **Risk-neutral** | **Risk-seeking** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Utility of wealth curve | **Concave** (diminishing marginal utility) | **Straight line** | **Convex** (increasing marginal utility) |\n| Indifference curves in E(R)–σ space | **Upward sloping**, convex | **Horizontal** | **Downward sloping** |\n| Requires a risk premium? | **Yes** | No — accepts the risk-free rate | **Pays** a premium for risk |\n| Certainty equivalent vs expected value | **Below** expected value | **Equal** to expected value | **Above** expected value |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"AVERSE Asks, NEUTRAL Nods, SEEKER Pays.\"**\n> The averse investor **asks for more return**; the neutral investor **nods at the expected value**; the seeker will **pay** (accept a lower expected return) for a shot at a big win.\n\n**Why MPT assumes risk aversion.** If investors were risk-neutral, nobody would care about σ, the efficient frontier would collapse to a single point (the highest-return asset), and diversification would be pointless. **Risk aversion is what makes portfolio construction a problem worth solving.**\n\n**The evidence for it in India:** equities have historically returned more than G-Secs over long periods. That gap — the **equity risk premium** — exists precisely *because* investors are risk-averse and must be paid to hold volatile assets. If the 10-year G-Sec yields **6.5%** and an investor requires **13%** from equity, the **equity risk premium** demanded is **6.5 percentage points**.\n\n> ⚠️ Risk aversion does **not** mean \"avoids all risk\". A risk-averse investor happily buys equity — but only at a price that offers **compensation** for the risk. Avoiding risk entirely is a *separate* behaviour, and it carries its own (inflation) risk.\n\n---\n\n## 14.4 The Building Blocks — Return and Risk of ONE Security\n\n### (a) Expected return of a security\n\nWhen you have **probabilities of scenarios**:\n\n> ### **E(R) = Σ pᵢ × Rᵢ**\n> *(probability of each state × the return in that state, summed)*\n\n**Why:** it is the average outcome you would get if the scenario \"lottery\" were run thousands of times. It is the centre of the distribution.\n\n**Worked example — Infosys-like stock, one year ahead:**\n\n| State of economy | Probability (pᵢ) | Return (Rᵢ) | pᵢ × Rᵢ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Boom | 0.25 | +30% | 7.50 |\n| Normal | 0.50 | +12% | 6.00 |\n| Recession | 0.25 | −8% | −2.00 |\n| | **1.00** | | **E(R) = 11.50%** |\n\n**On ₹4,00,000 invested, the expected gain is 11.5% × ₹4,00,000 = ₹46,000.**\n\nWhen you have **past returns instead of probabilities**, use the simple average:\n\n> ### **R̄ = (R₁ + R₂ + … + Rₙ) / n**\n\n**Worked example.** Five years of returns: **10%, −4%, 16%, 22%, 6%**. Sum = 50%. **R̄ = 50 / 5 = 10%.**\n\n### (b) Variance and standard deviation of a security\n\n> ### **σ² = Σ pᵢ × [Rᵢ − E(R)]²**   and   **σ = √σ²**\n\n**Why square the deviations?** Because plain deviations sum to **zero** — the pluses cancel the minuses. Squaring makes every deviation positive *and* penalises large misses much more than small ones. Squaring, however, leaves the answer in \"percent-squared\", which means nothing to a human — so we take the **square root** to get the **standard deviation**, back in **percentage points**.\n\n**Worked example — same stock as above, E(R) = 11.5%:**\n\n| State | pᵢ | Rᵢ | Rᵢ − E(R) | [Rᵢ − E(R)]² | pᵢ × [Rᵢ − E(R)]² |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Boom | 0.25 | 30% | +18.5 | 342.25 | 85.5625 |\n| Normal | 0.50 | 12% | +0.5 | 0.25 | 0.1250 |\n| Recession | 0.25 | −8% | −19.5 | 380.25 | 95.0625 |\n| | | | | **σ² =** | **180.75 (%²)** |\n\n**σ = √180.75 = 13.44%.**\n\n**Reading it in rupees.** With E(R) = 11.5% and σ = 13.44%, on a ₹4,00,000 holding the \"normal\" one-year band (roughly ±1σ, about two-thirds of outcomes if returns were normally distributed) is **−1.94% to +24.94%**, i.e. roughly **₹3,92,240 to ₹4,99,760**.\n\n**From historical data**, the same idea with two possible divisors:\n\n| Basis | Formula | When used |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Population** | σ² = Σ(Rᵢ − R̄)² / **n** | The data is the whole universe of outcomes |\n| **Sample** | s² = Σ(Rᵢ − R̄)² / **(n − 1)** | The data is a *sample* drawn from a larger history (the usual case in finance) |\n\n**Worked example.** Returns **10%, −4%, 16%, 22%, 6%**, mean **10%**.\nDeviations: 0, −14, +6, +12, −4. Squares: 0, 196, 36, 144, 16. **Sum = 392.**\n- **Population:** 392 / 5 = 78.4 → **σ = 8.85%**\n- **Sample:** 392 / 4 = 98 → **s = 9.90%**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** If a question gives past returns and does not say which basis, look at the options — one will match ÷n and one will match ÷(n−1). NISM questions most often state the basis; when they do not, ÷(n−1) is the statistically correct treatment for a *sample* of history.\n\n---\n\n## 14.5 ⭐ Covariance and Correlation — The Heart of the Chapter\n\nTwo securities can each be volatile, yet a portfolio of the two can be calm — **if they move differently**. Covariance and correlation measure exactly that.\n\n### Covariance\n\n> ### **Cov(A,B) = Σ pᵢ × [R_Aᵢ − E(R_A)] × [R_Bᵢ − E(R_B)]**\n\n**Read the formula as a story:** in each scenario, ask *\"was A above its average? was B above its average?\"*\n- Both above, or both below → the product is **positive**\n- One above while the other is below → the product is **negative**\n\nAdd those up and you learn whether the pair **tends to move together (+)** or **in opposite directions (−)**.\n\n**The problem with covariance:** its size is meaningless. A covariance of **+150** tells you the direction but nothing about the strength, because it is measured in the units of both securities multiplied together.\n\n### Correlation — covariance made readable\n\n> ### **ρ_AB = Cov(A,B) / (σ_A × σ_B)**   and therefore   **Cov(A,B) = ρ_AB × σ_A × σ_B**\n\nDividing by both standard deviations strips out the scale and leaves a pure number between **−1 and +1**.\n\n| Correlation ρ | Name | What it means | Diversification benefit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **+1.0** | Perfect positive | They move in lockstep, same direction | **ZERO** — none at all |\n| **+0.5** | Moderate positive | Usually move together, not always | Some |\n| **0** | Uncorrelated | Independent — no relationship | Substantial |\n| **−0.5** | Moderate negative | Usually move in opposite directions | Large |\n| **−1.0** | Perfect negative | Exact mirror images | **MAXIMUM** — risk can be driven to **zero** |\n\n**Worked example — computing both.** Three **equally likely** scenarios (p = 1/3 each):\n\n| Scenario | Stock A | Stock B | A − Ā | B − B̄ | Product |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 18% | 4% | +8 | −4 | −32 |\n| 2 | 10% | 8% | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n| 3 | 2% | 12% | −8 | +4 | −32 |\n| **Mean** | **10%** | **8%** | | | |\n\n**Cov(A,B) = (1/3)(−32) + (1/3)(0) + (1/3)(−32) = −64/3 = −21.33**\nσ_A = √[(64 + 0 + 64)/3] = √42.67 = **6.53%**\nσ_B = √[(16 + 0 + 16)/3] = √10.67 = **3.27%**\n**ρ = −21.33 / (6.53 × 3.27) = −21.33 / 21.33 = −1.00** → **perfect negative correlation.**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Covariance has a direction, correlation has a dial.\"**\n> Covariance only tells you the **sign**. Correlation puts that sign on a fixed **−1 to +1 dial** so any two pairs can be compared.\n\n> ⚠️ **Correlation is NOT causation, and it is NOT constant.** Indian equity–debt correlation is usually low, but in a global risk-off event both can fall together as foreign investors sell everything. **Correlations tend to rise towards +1 in a crisis — exactly when you needed them low.** This is the single most important practical limitation of MPT.\n\n---\n\n## 14.6 ⭐⭐ Portfolio Return and Portfolio Risk\n\n### (a) Expected return of a portfolio — a simple weighted average\n\n> ### **E(R_p) = w₁E(R₁) + w₂E(R₂) + … + wₙE(Rₙ)**   where **Σwᵢ = 1**\n\n**Worked example.** Ms Iyer has **₹20,00,000**: **₹12,00,000** in an equity fund expected to return **18%**, and **₹8,00,000** in a short-duration debt fund expected to return **7%**.\n- w_equity = 12/20 = **0.60**; w_debt = 8/20 = **0.40**\n- **E(R_p) = 0.60 × 18 + 0.40 × 7 = 10.8 + 2.8 = 13.6%**\n- Expected gain in rupees = 13.6% × ₹20,00,000 = **₹2,72,000**\n\nThree assets work identically. **₹5L equity @ 15% + ₹3L debt @ 8% + ₹2L gold @ 10%** on a ₹10L portfolio:\n**E(R_p) = 0.5(15) + 0.3(8) + 0.2(10) = 7.5 + 2.4 + 2.0 = 11.9%.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Return is *always* a straight weighted average — **correlation has NO effect on portfolio return.** Candidates who \"adjust\" the return for correlation lose the mark. Correlation affects **risk only**.\n\n### (b) ⭐ Risk of a TWO-security portfolio\n\n> ### **σ_p = √( w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·σ₁·σ₂·ρ₁₂ )**\n>\n> Equivalently, since Cov₁₂ = ρ₁₂σ₁σ₂:\n> ### **σ_p = √( w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·Cov₁₂ )**\n\n**Decode the three terms:**\n\n| Term | What it is | Can it be negative? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **w₁²σ₁²** | Security 1's own risk contribution | Never |\n| **w₂²σ₂²** | Security 2's own risk contribution | Never |\n| **2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ** | The **interaction** term | **YES — whenever ρ < 0** |\n\n**Only the third term contains ρ, and it is the only term that can shrink the total.** That is the whole mechanism of diversification in one sentence.\n\n### 🔬 The master worked example (memorise this one)\n\nMr Rao invests **₹6,00,000 in an equity fund** and **₹4,00,000 in a debt fund** (total ₹10,00,000):\n\n| | Equity fund | Debt fund |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weight | **0.60** | **0.40** |\n| Expected return | 15% | 8% |\n| Standard deviation σ | **20%** | **5%** |\n\n**Expected return:** E(R_p) = 0.6(15) + 0.4(8) = 9 + 3.2 = **12.2%** — *and this stays 12.2% no matter what ρ is.*\n\n**Fixed parts of the risk calculation:**\n- w₁²σ₁² = 0.36 × 400 = **144**\n- w₂²σ₂² = 0.16 × 25 = **4**\n- 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ = 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 20 × 5 = 0.48 × 100 = **48** → the interaction term is **48ρ**\n\nSo **σ_p² = 148 + 48ρ**. Now turn the correlation dial:\n\n| ρ | σ_p² = 148 + 48ρ | **σ_p** | Versus the 14% weighted average |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **+1.0** | 196 | **14.00%** | **No benefit** — exactly the weighted average |\n| +0.5 | 172 | 13.11% | −0.89 pp |\n| **+0.3** | 162.4 | **12.74%** | −1.26 pp |\n| **0** | 148 | **12.17%** | −1.83 pp |\n| −0.5 | 124 | 11.14% | −2.86 pp |\n| **−1.0** | 100 | **10.00%** | **−4.00 pp — the maximum** |\n\n**Weighted average of the standard deviations** = 0.6(20) + 0.4(5) = **14.00%**.\n\n### 🔑 Why correlation below +1 reduces risk — the proof in two lines\n\nAt **ρ = +1** the formula collapses:\nσ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ = **(w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂)²**, so **σ_p = w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂ = the weighted average.**\n\nSince ρ appears only in the third term and that term rises and falls with ρ, **any ρ below +1 makes σ_p² smaller — so σ_p is strictly LESS than the weighted average.**\n\n> ### **σ_p ≤ w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂, with equality ONLY when ρ = +1.**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Return is a straight line, risk is a curve that bends left.\"**\n> Plot the two-asset portfolios: at ρ = +1 you get a **straight line** joining the two assets. As ρ falls, the line **bows leftward** (less risk for the same return). At ρ = −1 it bends all the way to the **vertical axis — zero risk**.\n\n### The zero-risk case (ρ = −1)\n\nWhen **ρ = −1**, risk can be eliminated completely with the right weights:\n\n> ### **w₁ = σ₂ / (σ₁ + σ₂)** and **w₂ = σ₁ / (σ₁ + σ₂)**\n\n**Check it on Mr Rao's funds** (σ₁ = 20, σ₂ = 5): w₁ = 5/25 = **0.20**, w₂ = 20/25 = **0.80**.\nσ_p² = 0.04(400) + 0.64(25) + 2(0.2)(0.8)(20)(5)(−1) = 16 + 16 − 32 = **0**. **σ_p = 0%.** Riskless.\n\n> ⚠️ **In the real world ρ = −1 never occurs between two risky assets.** It is a teaching limit, not a strategy. Real Indian pairs sit roughly between +0.2 and +0.9. That is still enough to matter enormously.\n\n### (c) ⭐ Risk of a THREE-security portfolio\n\nEvery **pair** now contributes an interaction term:\n\n> ### **σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + w₃²σ₃² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ₁₂ + 2w₁w₃σ₁σ₃ρ₁₃ + 2w₂w₃σ₂σ₃ρ₂₃**\n\n**Three variance terms + three covariance terms = six terms.**\n\n**Worked example.** A ₹10 lakh portfolio:\n\n| Asset | Weight | σ | Correlations |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Equity (A) | 0.40 | 20% | ρ_AB = 0.5 |\n| Corporate bond (B) | 0.35 | 12% | ρ_AC = 0.2 |\n| Gold ETF (C) | 0.25 | 8% | ρ_BC = 0.4 |\n\n| Term | Arithmetic | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| w_A²σ_A² | 0.16 × 400 | 64.00 |\n| w_B²σ_B² | 0.1225 × 144 | 17.64 |\n| w_C²σ_C² | 0.0625 × 64 | 4.00 |\n| 2w_Aw_Bσ_Aσ_Bρ_AB | 2(0.40)(0.35)(20)(12)(0.5) | 33.60 |\n| 2w_Aw_Cσ_Aσ_Cρ_AC | 2(0.40)(0.25)(20)(8)(0.2) | 6.40 |\n| 2w_Bw_Cσ_Bσ_Cρ_BC | 2(0.35)(0.25)(12)(8)(0.4) | 6.72 |\n| | **σ_p² =** | **132.36** |\n\n**σ_p = √132.36 = 11.50%**, against a weighted average of 0.4(20) + 0.35(12) + 0.25(8) = **14.20%**. Diversification saved **2.70 percentage points**.\n\n### How the arithmetic explodes with n\n\n| Securities (n) | Variance terms | Covariance pairs = n(n−1)/2 | Total terms | Total estimates needed = n(n+3)/2 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 2 | 1 | 3 | 5 |\n| 3 | 3 | 3 | 6 | 9 |\n| 10 | 10 | **45** | 55 | **65** |\n| 50 | 50 | **1,225** | 1,275 | **1,325** |\n| 100 | 100 | **4,950** | 5,050 | **5,150** |\n\n*(Total estimates = n expected returns + n variances + n(n−1)/2 covariances.)*\n\n> 🧠 **The lesson buried in that table:** with 50 securities there are **1,225 covariance terms but only 50 variance terms**. **Portfolio risk is driven overwhelmingly by how securities interact, not by their individual volatilities.** That is why an adviser worries about *correlation* far more than about any single stock's beta.\n\n---\n\n## 14.7 The Two-Security Risk/Return Graph\n\nPlot **standard deviation on the X-axis** and **expected return on the Y-axis**. Every possible mix of two securities is one point; joining them traces a curve.\n\n**Worked frontier.** Asset X: E(R) = **16%**, σ = **24%**. Asset Y: E(R) = **10%**, σ = **14%**. **ρ = 0.2** (so Cov = 0.2 × 24 × 14 = **67.2**).\n\n| Weight in X | E(R_p) | σ_p | Efficient? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0% (all Y) | 10.00% | 14.00% | **NO — dominated** |\n| **20%** | **11.20%** | **13.04%** | **Minimum Variance Portfolio** |\n| 40% | 12.40% | 13.96% | Yes |\n| 60% | 13.60% | 16.46% | Yes |\n| 80% | 14.80% | 19.95% | Yes |\n| 100% (all X) | 16.00% | 24.00% | Yes (the top end) |\n\n**Read the table carefully — this is the exam's favourite trick.** The **20% X / 80% Y** mix has **higher return (11.20% vs 10.00%) AND lower risk (13.04% vs 14.00%)** than holding Y alone. **Holding 100% of the safer asset is therefore an INEFFICIENT choice.** Even the 40% mix (12.40% return, 13.96% risk) beats it on both counts.\n\n### The shape of the curve, by correlation\n\n| ρ | Shape of the two-asset curve |\n|---|---|\n| **+1** | A **straight line** joining the two assets — no bend, no benefit |\n| **Between −1 and +1** | A **curve bowing to the left** — the lower ρ, the deeper the bow |\n| **−1** | **Two straight lines** meeting the vertical axis at **σ = 0** |\n\n**The Minimum Variance Portfolio (MVP)** is the **leftmost point** of the curve — the mix with the lowest possible risk. For two assets:\n\n> ### **w₁* = (σ₂² − Cov₁₂) / (σ₁² + σ₂² − 2·Cov₁₂)**\n\n**Check it on X and Y:** w_X* = (196 − 67.2) / (576 + 196 − 134.4) = 128.8 / 637.6 = **0.202 ≈ 20%** ✓ — matching the table.\n\n> ⚠️ One of MPT's most counter-intuitive results: **adding a small slice of a risky asset to a safe portfolio can LOWER total risk.** A 100%-debt investor who adds 5–10% equity may end up with *less* volatility and *more* return. Advisers use this to move ultra-conservative clients off zero equity.\n\n---\n\n## 14.8 ⭐ The Efficient Frontier\n\nWith many securities the two-asset curves fill an area — the **feasible (attainable) set**. Its upper-left boundary is the **efficient frontier**.\n\n> ### **The efficient frontier is the set of portfolios that offer the HIGHEST expected return for each level of risk — equivalently, the LOWEST risk for each level of expected return.**\n\n**Also called** the *Markowitz efficient frontier* or the *efficient set*.\n\n### The dominance rule that builds it\n\nPortfolio **P dominates** portfolio **Q** if P has:\n- **the same return with less risk**, or\n- **the same risk with more return**, or\n- **more return AND less risk**.\n\nEvery portfolio that is dominated by something is thrown away. What survives is the frontier.\n\n| Where a portfolio sits | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **On** the efficient frontier | **Efficient** — nothing beats it on both measures |\n| **Below / to the right** of it | **Inefficient** — dominated; you can do better on risk, return or both |\n| **Above / to the left** of it | **Unattainable** — no such portfolio exists with the given assets |\n\n### Key properties to memorise\n\n1. It is **concave** — it bends upward and to the left. (It can never be convex, because you could then combine two frontier portfolios and beat it.)\n2. Its **leftmost point is the Global Minimum Variance Portfolio (GMVP)** — the lowest-risk portfolio obtainable from the asset set.\n3. **Everything on the minimum-variance frontier BELOW the GMVP is inefficient** and is discarded. The efficient frontier is only the **upper half**.\n4. The frontier is built **without knowing anything about the investor** — it depends only on the assets' returns, variances and covariances.\n5. **Adding a new asset with low correlation shifts the frontier up and to the left** — a genuine improvement in the risk-return menu. (This is the theoretical case for adding gold, REITs, InvITs or international equity to an Indian portfolio.)\n6. As the number of securities rises, **unsystematic (diversifiable) risk falls towards zero, but systematic (market) risk remains** — the frontier can never reach the vertical axis with risky assets alone.\n\n### Choosing YOUR point on the frontier\n\nThe frontier tells you the **menu**; the investor's **risk tolerance** picks the **dish**.\n\n- A risk-averse investor's **indifference curves** are **upward sloping and convex** — each curve joins portfolios giving equal satisfaction.\n- **The steeper the curves, the more risk-averse** the investor (a lot of extra return is needed for a little extra risk).\n- The **optimal portfolio** is where the investor's **highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the efficient frontier**.\n\n| Client | Where on the frontier | Typical Indian mix |\n|---|---|---|\n| 62-year-old retiree, income need | Near the **GMVP** (left end) | Mostly debt funds and G-Secs, small equity slice |\n| 35-year-old professional, 20-year goal | **Middle to upper** | Balanced equity/debt |\n| 28-year-old with surplus income, high tolerance | **Upper right** | Predominantly equity |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"The frontier is the menu; risk tolerance is the appetite.\"**\n> Two clients looking at the *same* frontier correctly choose *different* portfolios. Neither is wrong.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** The efficient frontier is **not one portfolio** — it is a **whole set of portfolios**, all equally \"efficient\". Questions asking \"which portfolio on the efficient frontier is best?\" have the answer \"**it depends on the investor's risk tolerance**\".\n\n---\n\n## 14.9 The Portfolio Optimisation Process\n\n**Mean-variance optimisation (MVO)** is the mechanical procedure that produces the frontier.\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Define the opportunity set** | List the asset classes or securities the client may hold |\n| **2. Estimate the inputs** | For each: **expected return** and **standard deviation**; for each pair: **covariance / correlation**. These are the \"capital market expectations\" |\n| **3. Impose constraints** | **Σwᵢ = 1** (budget constraint); often **wᵢ ≥ 0** (no short selling); plus mandate/regulatory limits such as \"max 10% in one stock\" or \"min 25% in debt\" |\n| **4. Run the optimiser** | Quadratic programming (or Excel Solver) minimises σ_p² for each target return |\n| **5. Trace the efficient frontier** | Repeat step 4 across return levels and join the points |\n| **6. Measure the client's risk tolerance** | Risk profiling — capacity *and* willingness |\n| **7. Select the optimal portfolio** | The tangency of the client's indifference curve with the frontier |\n| **8. Monitor and rebalance** | Inputs change; drift changes weights; revisit periodically |\n\n**The objective function** can be written either way — they give the same frontier:\n- **Minimise** σ_p² subject to a required E(R_p), **or**\n- **Maximise** E(R_p) subject to a maximum σ_p\n\nA common utility form used to pick the single best point is **U = E(R) − ½ × A × σ²**, where **A is the risk-aversion coefficient** (higher A = more risk-averse). The portfolio with the highest U is that investor's optimum. *Example:* with A = 3, a portfolio with E(R) = 12% and σ = 20% gives U = 0.12 − 0.5(3)(0.04) = 0.12 − 0.06 = **0.06, i.e. a 6% certainty equivalent**.\n\n> ⚠️ Constraints **always** push the achievable frontier **down and to the right** compared with an unconstrained one. That is not a flaw — a \"better\" unconstrained frontier that requires short-selling or a 90% single-stock position is not investable for a retail client.\n\n---\n\n## 14.10 ⭐ Estimation Issues — \"Garbage In, Garbage Out\"\n\nMPT's mathematics is exact. Its **inputs are guesses about the future**. That is the gap that matters in practice.\n\n| Issue | What goes wrong | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Garbage in, garbage out** | The optimiser cannot tell a good estimate from a bad one; it treats every input as certain truth | A precise-looking portfolio built on wrong inputs is still wrong |\n| **History ≠ future** | Inputs are usually estimated from past returns; regimes change (rate cycles, reforms, global shocks) | A fund's past 5-year return is a poor forecast of the next 5 |\n| **Expected returns are the weakest link** | E(R) is far harder to estimate than σ, and the optimiser is **most sensitive** to it | Errors in expected returns damage the result far more than errors in variances or covariances |\n| **Error maximisation** | The optimiser systematically **over-weights** assets whose returns were over-estimated and under-weights those under-estimated | MVO has been described as an **\"estimation-error maximiser\"** |\n| **Corner solutions** | Unconstrained optimisers dump almost everything into two or three assets | The \"optimal\" portfolio looks absurd and is unstable |\n| **Instability** | A tiny change in one expected return can swing the weights dramatically | Re-running the model monthly would trigger constant, costly churn |\n| **Unstable correlations** | Correlations are not fixed; they **rise towards +1 in crises** | Diversification fails exactly when it is needed most |\n| **Estimation error compounds with n** | n(n+3)/2 estimates — **1,325 for 50 securities** | More assets means more inputs, each carrying error |\n| **Non-normal returns** | Real returns are **fat-tailed and skewed**; σ assumes symmetry | Variance **understates** true downside risk |\n| **Variance treats upside as risk** | A 30% gain adds to σ exactly like a 30% loss | Investors do not experience upside as \"risk\" |\n| **Real-world frictions ignored** | No taxes, no transaction costs, no liquidity limits, single period | Indian STCG/LTCG, exit loads and STT are all real drags |\n| **Ignores non-traded wealth** | Human capital, a business, real estate, EPF | A client's total risk picture is bigger than the securities portfolio |\n\n### How practitioners cope\n\n- **Impose sensible constraints** (caps and floors per asset class) — the simplest and most effective fix\n- **Optimise across broad ASSET CLASSES, not individual stocks** — fewer, more stable inputs\n- **Use long-run, forward-looking estimates** rather than the last three years\n- **Shrinkage estimators, resampling, and the Black-Litterman model**, which blends market-implied returns with the adviser's views\n- **Stress-test** by re-running with correlations pushed towards +1\n- **Apply judgement** — the model informs the decision; it does not make it\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"The maths is exact; the inputs are opinions.\"**\n> Never present an optimiser output as a precise answer. **Two decimal places of false precision is the most common misuse of MPT.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** \"Estimation issues\" does **not** mean MPT is useless. The examinable position is: MPT is the **correct conceptual framework** (diversify on correlation, not count), but its **numerical outputs must be treated with caution and constrained by judgement**.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Father of MPT | **Harry Markowitz**, paper *\"Portfolio Selection\"*, **1952**; **Nobel Prize 1990** |\n| The core insight | **Portfolio return = weighted average; portfolio RISK is LESS than the weighted average** |\n| Also called | **Mean-variance analysis / mean-variance optimisation (MVO)** |\n| Risk measured by | **Variance / standard deviation** of returns |\n| All investors are assumed | **Risk-averse** (but with *differing degrees* of aversion) |\n| Decisions based on | **Expected return and risk ONLY**, over a **single period** |\n| Risk-averse investor | Rejects a fair gamble; demands a **risk premium**; **concave** utility curve |\n| Risk-neutral investor | **Indifferent** to risk; decides on **expected return alone**; **straight-line** utility |\n| Risk-seeking investor | **Accepts** a fair or unfair gamble; **convex** utility curve |\n| Expected return of a security | **E(R) = Σ pᵢRᵢ** |\n| Variance of a security | **σ² = Σ pᵢ[Rᵢ − E(R)]²**; **σ = √σ²** |\n| Historical variance | ÷ **n** (population) or ÷ **(n − 1)** (sample) |\n| Expected return of a portfolio | **E(R_p) = Σ wᵢE(Rᵢ)** — a pure **weighted average** |\n| Does correlation affect portfolio RETURN? | **NO** — return is always the weighted average |\n| Covariance | **Cov = Σ pᵢ[R_A − E(R_A)][R_B − E(R_B)]**; sign matters, size does not |\n| Correlation | **ρ = Cov / (σ_A σ_B)**, always between **−1 and +1** |\n| Covariance from correlation | **Cov = ρ × σ_A × σ_B** |\n| Two-security risk | **σ_p = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ)** |\n| Three-security risk | Add **w₃²σ₃²** plus **all THREE** covariance terms (**6 terms in total**) |\n| ρ = +1 | **σ_p = w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂** exactly — **no diversification benefit** |\n| ρ < +1 | **σ_p < weighted average** — benefit exists, and grows as ρ falls |\n| ρ = −1 | Risk can be reduced to **ZERO** with **w₁ = σ₂/(σ₁+σ₂)** |\n| Why diversification works | Only the **third (covariance) term** contains ρ, and only that term can shrink the total |\n| Shape at ρ = +1 | A **straight line**; below +1 the curve **bows leftward** |\n| Minimum Variance Portfolio | The **leftmost** point; **w₁\\* = (σ₂² − Cov)/(σ₁² + σ₂² − 2Cov)** |\n| Efficient frontier | **Highest return for each risk level / lowest risk for each return level** |\n| Shape of the frontier | **Concave**, bending up and to the left |\n| Global Minimum Variance Portfolio | The **leftmost point** of the frontier; everything **below** it is **inefficient** |\n| Below the frontier | **Inefficient (dominated)** |\n| Above the frontier | **Unattainable** |\n| Which frontier portfolio is \"best\"? | **Depends on the investor's risk tolerance** — tangency with the indifference curve |\n| Indifference curves (risk-averse) | **Upward sloping and convex**; **steeper = more risk-averse** |\n| Adding a low-correlation asset | Shifts the frontier **up and to the left** |\n| Diversification removes | **Unsystematic risk only** — **systematic (market) risk remains** |\n| Number of covariance pairs | **n(n − 1)/2** — **1,225** for 50 securities |\n| Total inputs needed | **n(n + 3)/2** — **1,325** for 50 securities |\n| Budget constraint | **Σwᵢ = 1** |\n| Optimisation objective | Minimise σ_p² for a target return, **or** maximise return for a target risk |\n| Utility form | **U = E(R) − ½Aσ²**; higher **A** = more risk-averse |\n| Biggest practical weakness | **Garbage in, garbage out** — inputs are estimates |\n| Most damaging input error | Errors in **expected returns** |\n| MVO nickname | **\"Estimation-error maximiser\"** |\n| Correlations in a crisis | **Rise towards +1** — diversification fails when most needed |\n| Practical fixes | **Constraints, asset-class level inputs, shrinkage, resampling, Black-Litterman, judgement** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** if a numerical question gives you two standard deviations, two weights and a correlation, write down **σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ** first and fill it in mechanically — then **remember to take the square root**. Forgetting the final √ is the single most common way candidates lose these marks, and the distractor for the un-square-rooted variance is almost always one of the four options.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 14: Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The two-security risk formula and the effect of correlation are near-certain exam marks — do the arithmetic out loud, don't just read it.**\n\n**Q1.** Who developed Modern Portfolio Theory, in which paper and year, and what is the theory also called?\n> **A:** **Harry Markowitz**, in *\"Portfolio Selection\"*, **Journal of Finance, 1952**; **Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences, 1990**. It is also called **mean-variance analysis / mean-variance optimisation (MVO)**.\n\n**Q2.** State the single core insight of MPT in one sentence.\n> **A:** A portfolio's **expected return is the weighted average** of its securities' returns, but its **risk is LESS than the weighted average** of their risks — because securities do not move perfectly together.\n\n**Q3.** How does Markowitz diversification differ from naive diversification?\n> **A:** Naive diversification just **owns many securities**. Markowitz diversification owns securities that **do not move together** — it uses **covariance/correlation**, not the count of holdings. Thirty IT stocks are *not* diversified.\n\n**Q4.** List the five classic assumptions of MPT.\n> **A:** (1) Each investment is a **probability distribution of returns** over a holding period; (2) investors **maximise one-period expected utility**, with **diminishing marginal utility of wealth**; (3) risk is estimated from the **variability of expected returns**; (4) decisions rest on **expected return and risk ONLY**; (5) for a given risk investors prefer **more return**, and for a given return **less risk** — i.e. all are **risk-averse**.\n\n**Q5.** Name four additional working assumptions of MPT.\n> **A:** A **single holding period**; **efficient markets** with freely available information; **no taxes or transaction costs**; **perfectly divisible assets**; and **rational investors with homogeneous expectations**.\n\n**Q6.** Does MPT assume every investor has the same risk tolerance?\n> **A:** **No.** It assumes every investor is **risk-averse**, but the **degree** differs — which is exactly why different investors choose **different points on the same efficient frontier**.\n\n**Q7.** Define risk aversion.\n> **A:** A preference for **certainty over uncertainty when the expected value is the same**. A risk-averse investor demands a **risk premium** — extra expected return — before accepting extra risk.\n\n**Q8.** Given a certain ₹10,00,000 versus a coin toss paying ₹20,00,000 or ₹0, what does each investor type choose?\n> **A:** Both have an expected value of ₹10 lakh. The **risk-averse** investor takes the **certain ₹10 lakh**; the **risk-neutral** investor is **indifferent** (he judges on expected return alone); the **risk-seeking** investor takes the **coin toss**.\n\n**Q9.** What shape is each investor type's utility-of-wealth curve, and how does the certainty equivalent compare with the expected value?\n> **A:** **Risk-averse → concave**, certainty equivalent **below** expected value. **Risk-neutral → straight line**, certainty equivalent **equal** to it. **Risk-seeking → convex**, certainty equivalent **above** it.\n\n**Q10.** Why must MPT assume risk aversion for the theory to work?\n> **A:** If investors were **risk-neutral**, nobody would care about σ, the efficient frontier would collapse to the **single highest-return asset**, and **diversification would be pointless**. Risk aversion is what makes portfolio construction a problem worth solving.\n\n**Q11.** What is the formula for the expected return of an individual security from scenario probabilities?\n> **A:** **E(R) = Σ pᵢ × Rᵢ** — each state's probability multiplied by its return, summed.\n\n**Q12.** Boom (p = 0.25) gives 30%, Normal (p = 0.50) gives 12%, Recession (p = 0.25) gives −8%. What is E(R)?\n> **A:** 0.25(30) + 0.50(12) + 0.25(−8) = 7.5 + 6.0 − 2.0 = **11.50%**.\n\n**Q13.** State the variance formula for a single security, and explain why deviations are squared.\n> **A:** **σ² = Σ pᵢ[Rᵢ − E(R)]²**. Deviations are squared because plain deviations **sum to zero** (pluses cancel minuses), and squaring also **penalises large misses more**. The **square root** then returns the answer to readable percentage points.\n\n**Q14.** Using the data in Q12 (E(R) = 11.5%), what are the variance and standard deviation?\n> **A:** 0.25(18.5)² + 0.50(0.5)² + 0.25(−19.5)² = 85.5625 + 0.125 + 95.0625 = **σ² = 180.75**, so **σ = √180.75 = 13.44%**.\n\n**Q15.** Historical returns are 10%, −4%, 16%, 22%, 6%. Give the mean and both standard deviations, and say when each divisor is used.\n> **A:** Mean = 50/5 = **10%**; squared deviations sum to **392**. **Population (÷ n):** 392/5 = 78.4 → **σ = 8.85%**. **Sample (÷ n−1):** 392/4 = 98 → **s = 9.90%**. Use **÷ n** for a whole population, **÷ (n−1)** for a *sample* of history — the usual case in finance.\n\n**Q16.** What is the formula for the expected return of a portfolio?\n> **A:** **E(R_p) = Σ wᵢ × E(Rᵢ)**, where the weights sum to 1. It is a **pure weighted average**.\n\n**Q17.** ₹12,00,000 in equity expected to return 18% and ₹8,00,000 in debt expected to return 7%. What is E(R_p)?\n> **A:** Weights are 0.60 and 0.40. E(R_p) = 0.60(18) + 0.40(7) = 10.8 + 2.8 = **13.60%** — a rupee gain of **₹2,72,000** on ₹20 lakh.\n\n**Q18.** Does correlation affect a portfolio's expected RETURN?\n> **A:** **No — never.** Return is always the weighted average. **Correlation affects RISK only.** This is one of the most common exam traps.\n\n**Q19.** Define covariance, state its formula, and give its weakness.\n> **A:** It measures **whether two securities move together**: **Cov(A,B) = Σ pᵢ[R_A − E(R_A)][R_B − E(R_B)]**. Its **sign** is meaningful, but its **magnitude is not comparable** across pairs because it carries the units of both securities.\n\n**Q20.** Define correlation — formula, range, and how to convert it back into covariance.\n> **A:** **ρ_AB = Cov(A,B) / (σ_A × σ_B)**, always between **−1 and +1**. Rearranged: **Cov(A,B) = ρ_AB × σ_A × σ_B**. *(ρ = 0.4, σ_A = 20%, σ_B = 15% → Cov = 0.4 × 300 = **120**.)*\n\n**Q21.** What do ρ = +1, ρ = 0 and ρ = −1 each mean for diversification?\n> **A:** **ρ = +1:** lockstep movement — **zero** diversification benefit. **ρ = 0:** independent — **substantial** benefit. **ρ = −1:** exact mirror images — **maximum** benefit, and risk can be driven to **zero**.\n\n**Q22.** State the two-security portfolio risk formula, in both its forms.\n> **A:** **σ_p = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·σ₁·σ₂·ρ₁₂)** — or, using covariance, **σ_p = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·Cov₁₂)**.\n\n**Q23.** Which term in the two-security formula creates the diversification benefit, and why?\n> **A:** The **third term, 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ**. It is the **only term containing ρ** and the **only term that can be negative** — the first two are squares and are always positive.\n\n**Q24.** w₁ = 0.60, σ₁ = 20%; w₂ = 0.40, σ₂ = 5%; ρ = 0.30. Compute σ_p.\n> **A:** 0.36(400) + 0.16(25) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(20)(5)(0.3) = 144 + 4 + 14.4 = **162.4**. **σ_p = √162.4 = 12.74%** — below the **14.00%** weighted average.\n\n**Q25.** For the same two funds, what is σ_p when ρ = +1, ρ = 0 and ρ = −1?\n> **A:** σ_p² = **148 + 48ρ**. **ρ = +1 → 196 → 14.00%** (exactly the weighted average). **ρ = 0 → 148 → 12.17%**. **ρ = −1 → 100 → 10.00%**.\n\n**Q26.** Prove that ρ below +1 always reduces risk below the weighted average.\n> **A:** At **ρ = +1** the formula collapses to **(w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂)²**, so **σ_p equals the weighted average exactly**. Since ρ appears only in the third term and that term shrinks as ρ falls, **any ρ below +1 makes σ_p strictly smaller**. Hence **σ_p ≤ w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂, with equality only at ρ = +1**.\n\n**Q27.** When ρ = −1, what weights produce a zero-risk portfolio?\n> **A:** **w₁ = σ₂/(σ₁ + σ₂)** and **w₂ = σ₁/(σ₁ + σ₂)**. *(With σ₁ = 20% and σ₂ = 5%: w₁ = 5/25 = **0.20**, w₂ = **0.80**, giving σ_p = **0%**.)*\n\n**Q28.** State the three-security portfolio variance formula and count its terms.\n> **A:** **σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + w₃²σ₃² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ₁₂ + 2w₁w₃σ₁σ₃ρ₁₃ + 2w₂w₃σ₂σ₃ρ₂₃** — **three variance terms plus three covariance terms = six terms**.\n\n**Q29.** For n securities, how many covariance pairs and total estimates are needed — and what is the lesson?\n> **A:** **n(n − 1)/2** covariance pairs and **n(n + 3)/2** total estimates. For **n = 50**: **1,225 covariances and 1,325 estimates** — against only **50 variance terms**. The lesson: **portfolio risk is driven overwhelmingly by how holdings interact**, not by any single holding's volatility.\n\n**Q30.** What shape does the two-asset risk/return curve take at different correlations?\n> **A:** At **ρ = +1**, a **straight line** between the two assets. Below +1 it **bows leftward** — deeper as ρ falls. At **ρ = −1** it becomes **two straight lines meeting the vertical axis at σ = 0**.\n\n**Q31.** What is the Minimum Variance Portfolio, and what is its two-asset weight formula?\n> **A:** The **leftmost point** of the curve — the mix with the **lowest possible risk**. **w₁\\* = (σ₂² − Cov₁₂) / (σ₁² + σ₂² − 2Cov₁₂)**.\n\n**Q32.** Why can holding 100% of the SAFER asset be inefficient?\n> **A:** Because a mix containing some of the riskier asset can have **both a higher return and a lower standard deviation**. *(Asset X: 16%/24%; Asset Y: 10%/14%; ρ = 0.2. The 20%-X mix returns **11.20%** with σ of **13.04%** — beating 100% Y's 10.00% and 14.00% on both counts.)*\n\n**Q33.** Define the efficient frontier.\n> **A:** The set of portfolios offering the **highest expected return for each level of risk** — equivalently the **lowest risk for each level of expected return**. It is **concave**, bending up and to the left.\n\n**Q34.** State the dominance rule, and say what it means to lie below, on or above the frontier.\n> **A:** P **dominates** Q if P offers the **same return with less risk**, the **same risk with more return**, or **more of both**. **Below/right → inefficient (dominated)**; **on → efficient**; **above/left → unattainable**.\n\n**Q35.** What is the Global Minimum Variance Portfolio, and what lies below it?\n> **A:** The **leftmost point** of the frontier — the lowest-risk portfolio obtainable from the asset set. Everything on the minimum-variance frontier **below** it is **inefficient** and is discarded; the efficient frontier is only the **upper half**.\n\n**Q36.** Which portfolio on the efficient frontier is \"the best\"?\n> **A:** **None in isolation — it depends on the investor's risk tolerance.** The optimum is where the investor's **highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the frontier**.\n\n**Q37.** What do indifference curves look like for a risk-averse investor, and what does a steeper curve mean?\n> **A:** **Upward sloping and convex**. **Steeper = more risk-averse** — a lot of extra return is demanded for a little extra risk.\n\n**Q38.** What happens to the frontier when a new low-correlation asset is added?\n> **A:** It shifts **up and to the left** — a genuinely better risk-return menu. This is the theoretical case for adding **gold, REITs, InvITs or international equity** to an Indian portfolio.\n\n**Q39.** List the main steps of the portfolio optimisation process.\n> **A:** Define the **opportunity set** → estimate **returns, variances and covariances** → impose **constraints (Σw = 1, no short selling, mandate limits)** → **run the optimiser** → **trace the efficient frontier** → assess the client's **risk tolerance** → **select the optimal portfolio** → **monitor and rebalance**.\n\n**Q40.** State the optimisation objective in its two equivalent forms, and the utility function used to pick one point.\n> **A:** **Minimise σ_p²** for a required return, **or maximise E(R_p)** for a maximum risk — both trace the same frontier. To pick a single point: maximise **U = E(R) − ½ × A × σ²**, where **A is the risk-aversion coefficient**.\n\n**Q41.** What effect do constraints such as \"no short selling\" have on the frontier?\n> **A:** They push the achievable frontier **down and to the right**. That is not a flaw — an unconstrained \"better\" frontier requiring short sales or a 90% single-stock weight is **not investable** for a retail client.\n\n**Q42.** Explain \"garbage in, garbage out\" in the MPT context.\n> **A:** The mathematics is exact but the **inputs are forecasts**. The optimiser cannot tell a good estimate from a bad one and treats every input as certain truth, so a **precise-looking portfolio built on wrong inputs is still wrong**.\n\n**Q43.** Which input error is most damaging, what nickname does MVO earn, and what is a \"corner solution\"?\n> **A:** Errors in **expected returns** — far more damaging than errors in variances or covariances. Because the optimiser **over-weights assets whose returns were over-estimated**, MVO is called an **\"estimation-error maximiser\"**. A **corner solution** is its typical output when unconstrained: nearly everything piled into **two or three assets**, unstable and implausible.\n\n**Q44.** What happens to correlations in a crisis, and why is variance itself criticised as a risk measure?\n> **A:** Correlations **rise towards +1** as investors sell everything at once, so **diversification weakens exactly when it is needed most**. Variance is criticised because it treats **upside volatility as risk** and assumes **symmetric, normally distributed returns**, whereas real returns are **fat-tailed and skewed** — so σ **understates true downside risk**.\n\n**Q45.** How do practitioners cope with MPT's estimation problems, and does diversification remove all risk?\n> **A:** **Impose constraints**; optimise across **broad asset classes rather than individual stocks**; use **long-run forward-looking estimates**; apply **shrinkage, resampling or Black-Litterman**; **stress-test** with correlations at +1; and overlay **judgement**. Diversification removes only **unsystematic risk** — **systematic (market) risk always remains**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 14: Introduction to Modern Portfolio Theory — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The two-security risk formula appears again and again — and the un-square-rooted variance is almost always one of the distractors.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Modern Portfolio Theory was developed by:\nA) William Sharpe  B) Harry Markowitz  C) Eugene Fama  D) Merton Miller\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Harry Markowitz**, in his paper *\"Portfolio Selection\"*.\nA) Sharpe developed the CAPM and the Sharpe ratio. C) Fama is associated with the Efficient Market Hypothesis. D) Miller is known for the Modigliani-Miller capital structure propositions. Sharpe and Miller shared the 1990 Nobel Prize *with* Markowitz, but did not create MPT.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In which year was the paper \"Portfolio Selection\" published?\nA) 1934  B) 1952  C) 1964  D) 1990\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Published in the **Journal of Finance in 1952**.\nA) 1934 is the year of Graham and Dodd's *Security Analysis*. C) 1964 is when Sharpe's CAPM appeared. D) 1990 is the year Markowitz received the Nobel Prize, not the year of publication.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Modern Portfolio Theory is also commonly known as:\nA) Technical analysis  B) Fundamental analysis  C) Mean-variance analysis  D) Behavioural finance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Because portfolios are described by only two statistics, the **mean (expected return) and the variance (risk)**.\nA) Studies price and volume patterns. B) Values securities from financial statements. D) Studies investor psychology and biases — in many ways the opposite of MPT's rational-investor assumption.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under MPT, the risk of a security is measured by:\nA) The standard deviation (or variance) of its returns  B) Its price  C) Its dividend yield  D) Its market capitalisation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Risk is defined as the **variability of returns**, captured by variance and its square root, the standard deviation.\nB) Price level says nothing about volatility. C) Dividend yield is an income measure. D) Market capitalisation is a size measure; large companies can still be volatile.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The expected return of a portfolio is:\nA) The weighted average of the securities' expected returns  B) The highest security's return  C) Always lower than the weighted average  D) The lowest security's return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Return combines in a **straight line**: E(R_p) = Σ wᵢE(Rᵢ).\nB) Ignores the other holdings entirely. C) Confuses return with risk — it is **risk**, not return, that falls below the weighted average. D) Also ignores the rest of the portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The risk of a portfolio is normally:\nA) Less than the weighted average of the securities' risks  B) Equal to the weighted average of the securities' risks  C) Greater than the weighted average  D) Always zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Whenever correlation is below +1, σ_p falls **below** the weighted average. This is the whole point of diversification.\nB) Holds only in the special case ρ = +1. C) Portfolio risk can never exceed the weighted average. D) Zero risk requires ρ = −1 and precise weights.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The correlation coefficient always lies between:\nA) −1 and +1  B) 0 and 1  C) −100 and +100  D) 0 and infinity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Dividing covariance by both standard deviations bounds the result to **−1 to +1**.\nB) Excludes negative correlation, which is central to diversification. C) and D) describe no correlation scale; covariance, not correlation, is unbounded.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** If two securities have a correlation of exactly +1, the diversification benefit is:\nA) Maximum  B) Zero  C) Moderate  D) Negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ρ = +1 the formula collapses to σ_p = w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂, exactly the **weighted average**. No risk is removed.\nA) Maximum benefit occurs at ρ = −1. C) Moderate benefit needs ρ meaningfully below +1. D) Diversification never increases risk above the weighted average.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The maximum diversification benefit occurs when correlation is:\nA) +1  B) 0  C) −1  D) +0.5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At **ρ = −1** the two securities are exact mirror images and risk can be reduced to **zero** with the right weights.\nA) Gives no benefit at all. B) Gives substantial benefit but not the maximum. D) Gives only a modest benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A risk-averse investor is one who:\nA) Requires additional expected return to accept additional risk  B) Avoids all investments  C) Is indifferent to risk  D) Prefers a gamble to a certain sum of equal value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Risk aversion means demanding a **risk premium** for bearing risk.\nB) Risk-averse investors do invest in risky assets — at the right price. C) Describes a risk-neutral investor. D) Describes a risk-seeking investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A risk-neutral investor makes decisions based on:\nA) Risk alone  B) Expected return alone  C) Both risk and return equally  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk simply does not enter a risk-neutral investor's decision; only the **expected value** matters.\nA) Reverses the definition. C) Describes a risk-averse investor. D) An investor with no criterion could not choose at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A risk-seeking investor offered a certain ₹5,00,000 or a 50-50 gamble paying ₹10,00,000 or nothing would:\nA) Take the gamble  B) Take the certain ₹5,00,000  C) Be indifferent  D) Refuse both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Both have an expected value of ₹5,00,000, but a **risk-seeker prefers the uncertainty**.\nB) Is the risk-averse choice. C) Is the risk-neutral position. D) No investor type refuses a non-negative-value opportunity.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Covariance measures:\nA) The average return of two securities  B) The dividend correlation  C) The total risk of one security  D) The extent to which two securities move together\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — It captures the **co-movement** of two securities around their own means.\nA) Averages are computed separately as expected returns. B) Covariance is computed on returns, not dividends specifically. C) That is variance, which involves one security only.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The correlation coefficient between securities A and B is calculated as:\nA) Cov(A,B) × σ_A × σ_B  B) Cov(A,B) / (σ_A × σ_B)  C) σ_A / σ_B  D) Cov(A,B) + σ_A + σ_B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Covariance **divided by** the product of the two standard deviations standardises it.\nA) Reverses the operation and would leave an unbounded number. C) A ratio of standard deviations says nothing about co-movement. D) Adding quantities in different units is meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Covariance can be recovered from correlation as:\nA) ρ + σ_A + σ_B  B) ρ / (σ_A σ_B)  C) ρ × (σ_A + σ_B)  D) ρ × σ_A × σ_B\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Simply rearranging ρ = Cov/(σ_Aσ_B) gives **Cov = ρ σ_A σ_B**.\nA) Adds incompatible quantities. B) Inverts the relationship. C) Uses a sum where a product is required.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The variance of a two-security portfolio equals:\nA) w₁σ₁ + w₂σ₂  B) w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂²  C) w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ  D) σ₁² + σ₂²\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Two variance terms plus the **interaction (covariance) term**.\nA) Is a weighted average of standard deviations, not a variance. B) Omits the covariance term, which is precisely where diversification comes from. D) Ignores the weights altogether.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The efficient frontier is the set of portfolios that:\nA) Have the lowest cost  B) Have zero risk  C) Contain the most securities  D) Offer the highest return for a given level of risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Equivalently, the **lowest risk for a given level of return**.\nA) Cost is not a variable in MPT. B) With risky assets alone, no frontier portfolio has zero risk. C) The number of holdings is irrelevant; correlation is what matters.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A portfolio lying below the efficient frontier is:\nA) Efficient  B) Inefficient — another portfolio offers more return for the same risk  C) Unattainable  D) Riskless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is **dominated**: you can do better on risk, return or both.\nA) Only points on the frontier are efficient. C) Points below the frontier are perfectly attainable — just not sensible. D) Position below the frontier says nothing about being riskless.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A portfolio plotted above the efficient frontier is:\nA) The best portfolio  B) Unattainable with the given assets  C) The minimum variance portfolio  D) Inefficient\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The frontier is the **boundary of what is possible**; nothing exists beyond it.\nA) It cannot be \"best\" because it cannot be built. C) The MVP is the leftmost point **on** the frontier. D) Inefficient portfolios lie **below** the frontier.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The Global Minimum Variance Portfolio is:\nA) The highest-return portfolio  B) A portfolio with zero risk  C) The portfolio with the lowest possible risk from the asset set  D) The equally weighted portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It is the **leftmost point** of the frontier.\nA) That is the top-right end of the frontier. B) It has the lowest risk available, which is rarely zero. D) Equal weighting has no special optimality property.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The shape of the efficient frontier is:\nA) A straight line  B) A circle  C) Convex, bending down  D) Concave, bending up and to the left\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Concavity follows from the diversification benefit at ρ below +1.\nA) A straight line would arise only if all assets were perfectly positively correlated. B) The frontier is an open curve, not a closed shape. C) A convex frontier is impossible — combinations of two frontier points would beat it.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under MPT, investors base their decisions on:\nA) Return, risk, taxes and liquidity  B) Expected return and risk only  C) Past prices alone  D) Broker recommendations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Utility is assumed to be a function of **E(R) and σ only**.\nA) Taxes and liquidity are explicitly assumed away in the basic model — one of its practical weaknesses. C) MPT is forward-looking, based on expected distributions. D) No model assumes this.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** MPT assumes an investment horizon of:\nA) Ten years  B) Infinite  C) A single holding period  D) One day\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The model is **static and single-period**, which is one of its criticisms.\nA) Ten years is a specific horizon the model never assumes. B) An infinite horizon would require a multi-period model, which classical MPT is not. D) One day is likewise never assumed.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** \"Diminishing marginal utility of wealth\" means:\nA) Wealth falls over time  B) Each additional rupee of wealth adds less satisfaction than the one before  C) Utility is negative  D) Returns fall as wealth rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is why the utility curve is **concave** and why investors are risk-averse.\nA) Describes wealth destruction, not utility. C) Utility of positive wealth is positive. D) Confuses the utility of wealth with investment returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Diversification under MPT eliminates:\nA) All risk  B) Inflation risk  C) Systematic (market) risk  D) Unsystematic (security-specific) risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Only the **diversifiable, security-specific** portion can be removed.\nA) Risk can never be fully eliminated with risky assets. B) Inflation risk affects the whole economy and is not diversifiable. C) Market risk affects every security and cannot be diversified away.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** How many covariance terms appear in a three-security portfolio variance?\nA) One  B) Three  C) Two  D) Six\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The pairs are **AB, AC and BC** — n(n−1)/2 = 3(2)/2 = 3.\nA) One pair is the two-security case. C) Understates by one pair. D) Six is the **total** number of terms (3 variances + 3 covariances), not the covariance count.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** In total, the variance formula for a three-security portfolio contains:\nA) Three terms  B) Four terms  C) Nine terms  D) Six terms\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Three variance terms plus three covariance terms**.\nA) Counts only the variance terms. B) Corresponds to no meaningful split. C) Nine is the number of cells in the full 3×3 covariance matrix, where the off-diagonal cells are counted twice.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Markowitz received the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in:\nA) 1952  B) 1976  C) 2003  D) 1990\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **1990**, shared with Merton Miller and William Sharpe.\nA) Is the year of the original paper. B) and C) are unrelated years.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** \"Garbage in, garbage out\" in the MPT context refers to:\nA) The output being only as reliable as the estimated inputs  B) Poor record-keeping  C) Transaction costs  D) Bad securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The optimiser's mathematics is exact, but **expected returns, variances and covariances are forecasts**.\nB) Refers to operations, not modelling. C) Costs are a separate omission of the model. D) The phrase concerns the input estimates, not the quality of the securities themselves.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The utility-of-wealth curve of a risk-averse investor is:\nA) Concave  B) Convex  C) A straight line  D) Vertical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Concavity reflects **diminishing marginal utility of wealth**.\nB) Convex curves belong to risk-seeking investors. C) A straight line describes a risk-neutral investor. D) A vertical curve has no economic meaning.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which term in the two-security variance formula creates the diversification benefit?\nA) The covariance term 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ  B) w₂²σ₂²  C) w₁²σ₁²  D) The square root\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It is the **only term containing ρ** and the **only term that can be negative**.\nB) and C) are squared quantities and are always positive, so they can never reduce total variance. D) The square root converts variance to standard deviation; it does not create the benefit.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Correlation between two securities affects a portfolio's:\nA) Expected return only  B) Risk only  C) Both return and risk  D) Neither\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Portfolio return is always the plain weighted average, whatever ρ is.\nA) Reverses the truth. C) A very common error — correlation appears nowhere in the return formula. D) Correlation is the single most important driver of portfolio risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** In portfolio optimisation, the \"budget constraint\" means:\nA) Costs must be minimised  B) The client must have ₹50 lakh  C) The portfolio weights must sum to 1  D) Returns must be positive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Σwᵢ = 1**: the whole portfolio, and only the whole portfolio, is invested.\nA) Cost is not modelled in basic MPT. B) That is the PMS minimum, unrelated to optimisation. D) The optimiser places no sign restriction on returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The indifference curves of a risk-averse investor in expected-return / risk space are:\nA) Downward sloping  B) Horizontal  C) Upward sloping and convex  D) Vertical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — More risk must be compensated by more return, so the curves rise; they are convex because compensation is demanded at an increasing rate.\nA) Downward sloping curves belong to a risk-seeking investor. B) Horizontal curves describe a risk-neutral investor. D) Vertical curves have no meaning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** Owning thirty stocks from the same sector is best described as:\nA) Markowitz diversification  B) Naive diversification with little real risk reduction  C) An efficient portfolio  D) The minimum variance portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holdings are highly correlated, so the covariance terms stay large. **Diversification is about difference, not count.**\nA) Markowitz diversification explicitly selects on low correlation. C) A sector-concentrated portfolio is almost certainly dominated. D) The MVP is defined by the optimiser, not by holding many similar stocks.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** A portfolio holds ₹6,00,000 in a fund expected to return 15% and ₹4,00,000 in a fund expected to return 8%. Its expected return is:\nA) 11.50%  B) 11.00%  C) 13.00%  D) 12.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Weights are 0.60 and 0.40. E(R_p) = 0.60(15) + 0.40(8) = 9.0 + 3.2 = **12.20%**.\nA) Is the simple average of 15 and 8, ignoring the weights. B) Understates both contributions. C) Overweights the equity fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** ₹12,00,000 sits in equity expected to return 18% and ₹8,00,000 in debt expected to return 7%. The expected portfolio return is:\nA) 12.50%  B) 11.20%  C) 14.30%  D) 13.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Weights 0.60 / 0.40. E(R_p) = 0.60(18) + 0.40(7) = 10.8 + 2.8 = **13.60%**, i.e. **₹2,72,000** on ₹20 lakh.\nA) Is the unweighted average of 18 and 7. B) Reverses the weights (0.40 equity, 0.60 debt). C) Overweights equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A ₹10,00,000 portfolio holds ₹5,00,000 equity (15%), ₹3,00,000 debt (8%) and ₹2,00,000 gold (10%). Expected return is:\nA) 11.00%  B) 12.50%  C) 11.90%  D) 11.30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 0.50(15) + 0.30(8) + 0.20(10) = 7.5 + 2.4 + 2.0 = **11.90%**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of the three returns. B) Overstates the equity weight. D) Miscomputes the gold contribution.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A stock returns +30% with probability 0.25, +12% with probability 0.50 and −8% with probability 0.25. Its expected return is:\nA) 11.33%  B) 10.00%  C) 12.00%  D) 11.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 0.25(30) + 0.50(12) + 0.25(−8) = 7.5 + 6.0 − 2.0 = **11.50%**.\nA) Is the simple average of the three returns, ignoring probabilities. B) Understates the boom contribution. C) Ignores the recession scenario's drag.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A stock returns +20% or 0%, each with probability 0.50. Its standard deviation is:\nA) 0%  B) 10%  C) 5%  D) 20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — E(R) = 10%. Deviations are ±10, so σ² = 0.5(100) + 0.5(100) = **100**, and σ = **10%**.\nA) A two-outcome distribution is not riskless. C) Halves the deviation incorrectly. D) Is the range of outcomes, not the standard deviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** Two funds have σ of 20% and 5%, weights 0.60 and 0.40, and ρ = +1. The portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 14.00%  B) 12.74%  C) 12.17%  D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — At ρ = +1, σ_p is simply the weighted average: 0.60(20) + 0.40(5) = 12 + 2 = **14.00%**. (Check: 144 + 4 + 48 = 196; √196 = 14.)\nB) Is the answer for ρ = 0.30. C) Is the answer for ρ = 0. D) Is the answer for ρ = −1.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** The same two funds, now with ρ = 0. The portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 14.00%  B) 12.74%  C) 12.17%  D) 148.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — σ_p² = 0.36(400) + 0.16(25) + 0 = 144 + 4 = **148**; σ_p = √148 = **12.17%**.\nA) Is the weighted average, valid only at ρ = +1. B) Is the answer for ρ = 0.30. D) Is the variance, not the standard deviation — the square root was not taken.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** The same two funds, now with ρ = −1. The portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 10.00%  B) 0%  C) 12.17%  D) 14.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — σ_p² = 144 + 4 − 48 = **100**; σ_p = **10.00%**.\nB) Zero risk at ρ = −1 requires the specific weights 0.20 / 0.80, not 0.60 / 0.40. C) Is the ρ = 0 answer. D) Is the ρ = +1 answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Cov(A,B) = 90, σ_A = 18% and σ_B = 10%. The correlation is:\nA) 0.50  B) 0.30  C) 0.90  D) 1.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — ρ = 90 / (18 × 10) = 90/180 = **0.50**.\nB) Divides by the wrong product. C) Uses the covariance figure directly as if it were a correlation. D) Exceeds +1 and is therefore impossible for any correlation.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** ρ = 0.40, σ_A = 20% and σ_B = 15%. The covariance is:\nA) 0.40  B) 12  C) 300  D) 120\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Cov = ρσ_Aσ_B = 0.40 × 20 × 15 = **120**.\nA) Is the correlation itself. B) Divides the product by 25 for no reason. C) Is σ_Aσ_B without applying the correlation.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Cov(A,B) = −60, σ_A = 25% and σ_B = 8%. The correlation is:\nA) −0.60  B) −0.30  C) −3.00  D) +0.30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ρ = −60 / (25 × 8) = −60/200 = **−0.30**.\nA) Misreads the covariance as a percentage. C) Lies outside the −1 to +1 range and is impossible. D) Loses the negative sign, reversing the economic meaning entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Two assets each have σ = 20%, held 50/50 with ρ = 0. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 20.00%  B) 10.00%  C) 14.14%  D) 0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — σ_p² = 0.25(400) + 0.25(400) = **200**; σ_p = √200 = **14.14%**.\nA) Is the weighted average, applicable only at ρ = +1. B) Would require a far more negative correlation. D) Zero risk needs ρ = −1.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Two funds with σ of 30% and 10%, held 50/50 with ρ = 0.50. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 20.00%  B) 18.03%  C) 15.81%  D) 13.23%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — σ_p² = 0.25(900) + 0.25(100) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(30)(10)(0.5) = 225 + 25 + 75 = **325**; σ_p = **18.03%**.\nA) Is the weighted average (ρ = +1). C) Is the ρ = 0 answer. D) Is the ρ = −0.5 answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Which of these pairs would give an adviser the greatest diversification benefit?\nA) Two large-cap equity funds, ρ = 0.95  B) Two IT sector stocks, ρ = 0.88  C) An equity fund and a gilt fund, ρ = 0.10  D) A Nifty index fund and a Sensex index fund, ρ = 0.99\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **lowest correlation** delivers the largest reduction in the covariance term.\nA), B) and D) all pair near-identical exposures; at correlations approaching +1 the benefit is negligible. D) in particular is two labels for essentially the same large-cap market exposure.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Portfolio P returns 12% with σ 15%; portfolio Q returns 12% with σ 18%. Which statement is true?\nA) P dominates Q — same return, lower risk  B) Q is preferable  C) They are equally efficient  D) Neither can be compared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — With identical returns, the **lower-risk portfolio dominates**, so Q is inefficient.\nB) A risk-averse investor never pays extra risk for nothing. C) Q is strictly dominated and therefore cannot be efficient. D) Dominance is directly comparable when one measure matches and the other differs.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Portfolio R returns 10% with σ 15%; portfolio P returns 12% with σ 15%. Which is true?\nA) R dominates P  B) P dominates R — same risk, higher return  C) Both are efficient  D) R has less risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — With identical risk, the **higher-return portfolio dominates**.\nA) Reverses the dominance. C) R is dominated, so it cannot be on the efficient frontier. D) Both carry σ of 15% — the risks are identical.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** For 50 securities, the number of distinct covariance pairs that must be estimated is:\nA) 50  B) 100  C) 1,225  D) 2,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — n(n−1)/2 = 50 × 49 / 2 = **1,225**.\nA) Is the number of variance terms. B) Is the number of returns plus variances. D) Is n², counting each pair twice and including the 50 diagonal variance cells.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** For 50 securities, the total number of inputs (expected returns, variances and covariances) is:\nA) 100  B) 1,225  C) 1,325  D) 2,550\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 50 returns + 50 variances + 1,225 covariances = **1,325**, matching n(n+3)/2.\nA) Omits the covariances entirely. B) Counts only the covariances. D) Double counts the covariance pairs.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Why does the efficient frontier stop at the Global Minimum Variance Portfolio at its left end?\nA) The optimiser fails below it  B) They carry negative risk  C) They are unattainable  D) Portfolios below the GMVP have more risk AND less return, so they are dominated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The lower branch of the minimum-variance frontier is **dominated** by the upper branch at every risk level.\nA) The optimiser computes those portfolios perfectly well; they are simply discarded. B) Standard deviation can never be negative. C) They are attainable — just never sensible.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Two clients look at the same efficient frontier and choose different portfolios. This is because:\nA) One of them is wrong  B) The frontier is inaccurate  C) They have different degrees of risk aversion  D) They used different formulas\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The frontier is the **menu**; risk tolerance is the **appetite**. Both choices can be correct.\nA) Neither is wrong if each sits on the frontier. B) A shared frontier is the same objective input for both. D) The frontier is built independently of any investor.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Adding an asset class with low correlation to an existing portfolio typically:\nA) Shifts the efficient frontier down and to the right  B) Eliminates systematic risk  C) Leaves the frontier unchanged  D) Shifts the efficient frontier up and to the left\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A better risk-return menu becomes available at every point. This is the theoretical case for adding gold, REITs, InvITs or international equity.\nA) That happens when constraints are tightened, not when opportunities are added. B) Systematic risk can never be eliminated by diversification. C) A genuinely new, low-correlation asset always improves or at worst preserves the frontier.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which is NOT an assumption of Modern Portfolio Theory?\nA) Investors are risk-averse  B) Decisions are based on expected return and risk only  C) Investors have identical risk tolerance  D) A single holding period applies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — MPT assumes all investors are risk-averse, but **the degree differs** — which is why they choose different frontier points.\nA), B) and D) are all genuine assumptions of the model.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** In the two-asset risk/return graph, the curve becomes a straight line when:\nA) ρ = 0  B) ρ = −1  C) ρ = +1  D) The weights are equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — At perfect positive correlation there is no bend and therefore no benefit.\nA) At ρ = 0 the curve bows noticeably leftward. B) At ρ = −1 the plot is two straight lines meeting the vertical axis at zero risk, not a single line joining the assets. D) Equal weights place you at one point on the curve; they do not change its shape.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An adviser tells a client that correlation will reduce his portfolio's expected return. This is:\nA) Correct  B) Correct only above ρ = 0.5  C) Correct only for negative correlation  D) Incorrect — correlation affects risk, not return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Expected return is **always** the weighted average, whatever the correlation.\nA) The statement confuses the return formula with the risk formula. B) No correlation value alters the return calculation. C) Even at ρ = −1 the expected return is unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Under MPT, the portfolio with the lowest risk for a required return of 12% is found by:\nA) Minimising σ_p² subject to E(R_p) = 12% and Σw = 1  B) Picking the lowest-σ security  C) Equal weighting all assets  D) Maximising expected return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — That is exactly the mean-variance optimisation problem.\nB) The lowest-σ **security** is rarely the lowest-σ **portfolio**, and it may not deliver 12%. C) Equal weighting has no optimality property. D) Maximising return would ignore the risk objective entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Historical returns are 10%, −4%, 16%, 22% and 6%. The mean return is:\nA) 8.00%  B) 12.00%  C) 10.00%  D) 9.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — (10 − 4 + 16 + 22 + 6) / 5 = 50 / 5 = **10.00%**.\nA) Drops one of the positive years. B) Ignores the negative year. D) Divides by six instead of five.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** For those same returns (mean 10%), the sum of squared deviations is 392. The SAMPLE standard deviation is:\nA) 9.90%  B) 8.85%  C) 78.40%  D) 98.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sample variance = 392 / (5 − 1) = **98**; s = √98 = **9.90%**.\nB) Uses the population divisor of 5 (392/5 = 78.4, σ = 8.85%). C) and D) are variances, not standard deviations — the square root was not taken.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Which statement about covariance is most accurate?\nA) Its magnitude is directly comparable across security pairs  B) It always lies between −1 and +1  C) Its sign is meaningful but its magnitude is not comparable, which is why correlation is used  D) It equals the standard deviation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Covariance carries the units of both securities, so **correlation standardises it**.\nA) Two covariances of different pairs cannot be compared directly. B) That range belongs to correlation; covariance is unbounded. D) Covariance involves two securities, standard deviation only one.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An unconstrained mean-variance optimiser typically produces:\nA) Perfectly balanced weights  B) Corner solutions concentrated in a few assets  C) Equal weighting  D) Zero-risk portfolios\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without constraints, the optimiser piles into the assets with the most attractive **estimated** inputs, producing implausible and unstable weights.\nA) Balance results from imposed constraints, not from the raw optimiser. C) Equal weighting is a naive rule the optimiser would never choose. D) Zero risk is unattainable with risky assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Imposing a \"no short selling\" constraint on the optimiser:\nA) Improves the frontier  B) Has no effect  C) Shifts the achievable frontier down and to the right  D) Makes the frontier a straight line\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Constraints can only reduce the opportunity set. That is acceptable because an unconstrained frontier requiring short positions is not investable for a retail client.\nA) Restrictions never expand the opportunity set. B) The constraint binds whenever the unconstrained solution wanted a negative weight. D) The shape stays concave.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Correlations between Indian equity and global equity rising sharply during a global sell-off illustrates:\nA) That correlations are stable  B) That MPT is arithmetically wrong  C) That correlations rise towards +1 in a crisis, weakening diversification exactly when it is most needed  D) That equity is riskless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — This is the most important practical limitation of applying MPT.\nA) The example demonstrates the opposite. B) The arithmetic is sound; it is the **input estimate** that proved unstable. D) Equity is among the most volatile asset classes.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which is the most damaging estimation error in mean-variance optimisation?\nA) Errors in variances  B) Errors in expected returns  C) Errors in covariances  D) Errors in the weights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The optimiser is most sensitive to **expected returns**, and it systematically over-weights whatever it believes will return most.\nA) and C) matter, but far less than errors in the means. D) Weights are the optimiser's **output**, not an input estimate.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** MPT has been criticised for using variance because variance:\nA) Treats upside volatility as risk and assumes symmetric returns  B) Is too complicated  C) Cannot be calculated  D) Ignores the mean\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Real returns are **fat-tailed and skewed**, so σ understates true downside risk. This criticism produced downside measures such as the Sortino ratio.\nB) Variance is arithmetically simple. C) It is routinely calculated. D) Variance is defined around the mean and therefore uses it directly.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which practical step best mitigates MPT's estimation problems?\nA) Adding more individual stocks  B) Optimising across broad asset classes with sensible constraints  C) Using only last month's data  D) Removing all constraints\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fewer, more stable inputs plus caps and floors is the simplest and most effective fix.\nA) More securities means more inputs and more compounded estimation error. C) A one-month window produces extremely unstable estimates. D) Removing constraints produces exactly the corner solutions practitioners are trying to avoid.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which best describes the Black-Litterman model in the context of estimation issues?\nA) A technical charting method  B) A tax-optimisation tool  C) A method of computing beta  D) An approach that blends market-implied returns with the adviser's own views to produce more stable inputs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — By anchoring on market-implied expectations and tilting only where the adviser has a view, it avoids the wild weight swings of raw historical inputs.\nA) It is an optimisation input model, not a charting technique. B) It has no tax function. C) Beta comes from regression against a market index.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A stock's returns are +30% (p = 0.25), +12% (p = 0.50) and −8% (p = 0.25), with E(R) = 11.5%. Its standard deviation is:\nA) 180.75%  B) 13.44%  C) 12.00%  D) 19.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — σ² = 0.25(18.5)² + 0.50(0.5)² + 0.25(−19.5)² = 85.5625 + 0.125 + 95.0625 = **180.75**; σ = √180.75 = **13.44%**.\nA) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. C) Is close to the modal return, not a dispersion measure. D) Is roughly the average absolute deviation of the two extreme states, not the standard deviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Returns are +25% (p = 0.30), +10% (p = 0.40) and −5% (p = 0.30). Expected return and standard deviation are:\nA) 10.00% and 11.62%  B) 10.00% and 135.00%  C) 12.00% and 11.62%  D) 10.00% and 15.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — E(R) = 7.5 + 4.0 − 1.5 = **10%**. Deviations +15, 0, −15, so σ² = 0.30(225) + 0.40(0) + 0.30(225) = **135**; σ = √135 = **11.62%**.\nB) Reports the variance instead of the standard deviation. C) Miscalculates the expected return. D) Uses the raw ±15 deviation without probability weighting.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Returns are +40% (p = 0.20), +15% (p = 0.60) and −10% (p = 0.20). The standard deviation is:\nA) 15.00%  B) 25.00%  C) 15.81%  D) 250.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — E(R) = 8 + 9 − 2 = 15%. Deviations +25, 0, −25, so σ² = 0.20(625) + 0.20(625) = **250**; σ = √250 = **15.81%**.\nA) Is the expected return, not the risk. B) Is the raw deviation of the extreme states. D) Is the variance, un-square-rooted.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Equity σ = 20%, debt σ = 5%, weights 0.60/0.40, ρ = 0.30. Portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 12.74%  B) 14.00%  C) 12.17%  D) 162.40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 0.36(400) + 0.16(25) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(20)(5)(0.3) = 144 + 4 + 14.4 = **162.4**; σ_p = √162.4 = **12.74%**.\nB) Is the weighted average, valid only at ρ = +1. C) Is the ρ = 0 answer. D) Is the variance — the square root was not taken.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** For the portfolio in Q74, the diversification benefit versus the weighted average of the standard deviations is:\nA) Nil  B) 2.74 percentage points  C) 4.00 percentage points  D) 1.26 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Weighted average = 0.60(20) + 0.40(5) = **14.00%**; actual σ_p = **12.74%**. Benefit = 14.00 − 12.74 = **1.26 pp**.\nA) A benefit exists at any ρ below +1. B) Subtracts from the wrong base. C) 4.00 pp is the benefit at ρ = −1 (σ_p would be 10.00%).\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Fund A: σ 25%, weight 0.40. Fund B: σ 15%, weight 0.60. ρ = 0.20. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 14.73%  B) 19.00%  C) 217.00%  D) 16.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 0.16(625) + 0.36(225) + 2(0.4)(0.6)(25)(15)(0.2) = 100 + 81 + 36 = **217**; σ_p = √217 = **14.73%**.\nB) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations. C) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. D) Applies the correlation to the wrong term.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Two funds have σ of 30% and 10%, held 50/50 with ρ = 0.20. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 20.00%  B) 16.73%  C) 15.81%  D) 18.03%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — σ_p² = 225 + 25 + 2(0.5)(0.5)(30)(10)(0.2) = 225 + 25 + 30 = **280**; σ_p = √280 = **16.73%**.\nA) Is the weighted average (ρ = +1). C) Is the ρ = 0 answer. D) Is the ρ = 0.50 answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Fund X: σ 16%, weight 0.70. Fund Y: σ 8%, weight 0.30. ρ = 0.40. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 13.60%  B) 12.36%  C) 11.20%  D) 152.70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 0.49(256) + 0.09(64) + 2(0.7)(0.3)(16)(8)(0.4) = 125.44 + 5.76 + 21.50 = **152.70**; σ_p = **12.36%**.\nA) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations. C) Understates the dominant 0.70 weight in the risky fund. D) Is the variance.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Two assets, σ 20% and 10%, held 50/50 with ρ = −1. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 0%  B) 15.00%  C) 5.00%  D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 0.25(400) + 0.25(100) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(20)(10)(−1) = 100 + 25 − 100 = **25**; σ_p = **5.00%**.\nA) Zero risk at ρ = −1 requires weights of 1/3 and 2/3, not 50/50. B) Is the weighted average. D) Ignores the negative covariance term.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** For those same two assets (σ 20% and 10%, ρ = −1), which weights produce ZERO risk?\nA) 50% / 50%  B) Zero risk is impossible  C) 2/3 in the 20% asset, 1/3 in the 10% asset  D) 1/3 in the 20% asset, 2/3 in the 10% asset\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — w₁ = σ₂/(σ₁ + σ₂) = 10/30 = **1/3**, so w₂ = **2/3**. Check: (1/9)(400) + (4/9)(100) + 2(1/3)(2/3)(200)(−1) = 44.44 + 44.44 − 88.89 = **0**.\nA) Gives σ_p = 5%, as computed in Q79. B) At ρ = −1 exactly, zero risk is achievable — that is the defining property. C) Reverses the weights and leaves residual risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A three-asset portfolio: weights 0.40/0.35/0.25, σ 20%/12%/8%, with ρ_AB = 0.5, ρ_AC = 0.2, ρ_BC = 0.4. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 11.50%  B) 14.20%  C) 132.36%  D) 9.80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Variances: 64.00 + 17.64 + 4.00. Covariance terms: 33.60 + 6.40 + 6.72. Total σ_p² = **132.36**; σ_p = **11.50%**.\nB) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations (0.4×20 + 0.35×12 + 0.25×8). C) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. D) Omits one or more covariance terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A three-asset portfolio: weights 0.50/0.30/0.20, σ 20%/10%/5%, with ρ_AB = 0.4, ρ_AC = 0.1, ρ_BC = 0.6. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 14.00%  B) 139.60%  C) 10.50%  D) 11.82%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Variances: 100 + 9 + 1 = 110. Covariances: 24 + 2 + 3.6 = 29.6. σ_p² = **139.60**; σ_p = **11.82%**.\nA) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations. B) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. C) Omits the covariance terms.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Three uncorrelated assets (all ρ = 0) with σ of 15%, 12% and 9%, held one-third each. Portfolio risk is:\nA) 7.07%  B) 12.00%  C) 50.00%  D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — With all covariance terms zero, σ_p² = (1/9)(225 + 144 + 81) = (1/9)(450) = **50**; σ_p = √50 = **7.07%**.\nB) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations. C) Is the variance. D) Divides the variance by a further factor without justification.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Asset X: E(R) 16%, σ 24%. Asset Y: E(R) 10%, σ 14%. With ρ = 0.2, a 20%-X / 80%-Y mix gives E(R) 11.20% and σ 13.04%. What does this prove?\nA) Holding 100% Y is inefficient — the mix beats it on BOTH return and risk  B) Y alone is efficient  C) X alone is best  D) The calculation must be wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 11.20% > 10.00% and 13.04% < 14.00%, so 100% Y is **strictly dominated**.\nB) A dominated portfolio cannot be efficient. C) X alone gives more return but far more risk; efficiency here depends on the investor. D) The arithmetic is standard: σ_p² = 0.04(576) + 0.64(196) + 2(0.2)(0.8)(67.2) = 23.04 + 125.44 + 21.50 = 169.98.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** For two assets with σ 20% and 10% and ρ = 0, the Minimum Variance Portfolio weight in the FIRST asset is:\nA) 50%  B) 20%  C) 33%  D) 80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — w₁* = (σ₂² − Cov) / (σ₁² + σ₂² − 2Cov) = (100 − 0) / (400 + 100 − 0) = 100/500 = **0.20**.\nA) Equal weighting is not the minimum-variance solution here. C) 1/3 would be the answer if ρ were −1. D) Reverses the two weights.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** For the MVP in Q85 (20% / 80%), the portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 12.00%  B) 80.00%  C) 10.00%  D) 8.94%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — σ_p² = 0.04(400) + 0.64(100) = 16 + 64 = **80**; σ_p = √80 = **8.94%**, which is **below the 10% risk of the safer asset held alone**.\nA) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations. B) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. C) Is the safer asset's own risk, which the MVP beats.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client holds 100% in a debt fund with σ 10%. His adviser shows that adding a small equity slice would reduce total portfolio risk below 10%. This is possible because:\nA) Low correlation makes the covariance term small enough that the risk reduction outweighs the added variance  B) Equity is less risky than debt  C) The adviser made an error  D) Debt funds are riskless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This is one of MPT's most counter-intuitive results: below a certain weight, the diversification effect outweighs the extra volatility introduced.\nB) Equity is materially more volatile than debt. C) The result is a standard implication of the two-asset formula, demonstrated in Q86. D) Debt funds carry interest-rate and credit risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A client's 60/40 portfolio was built assuming ρ = 0.25 between his equity and debt funds, giving σ_p = 14%. In a crisis, ρ jumps to 0.75. What happens?\nA) Nothing changes  B) The weights change automatically  C) The expected return falls  D) Actual portfolio risk rises materially, because only the correlation input was wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Only the covariance term is affected, and it rises sharply. **The diversification benefit largely evaporates precisely when the client needs it.**\nA) The whole risk profile of the portfolio shifts. B) Weights change only through market drift or an explicit rebalance. C) Expected return is unaffected by correlation.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** An optimiser is re-run after the analyst raises one asset's expected return from 11% to 12%. The optimal weight in that asset jumps from 15% to 62%. This illustrates:\nA) Instability and error maximisation — MVO over-weights whatever it believes will return most  B) A well-behaved model  C) A calculation error  D) That the asset is riskless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — This extreme sensitivity to expected-return estimates is why MVO is called an **\"estimation-error maximiser\"**.\nB) Wild swings from a one-percentage-point input change are the opposite of well-behaved. C) The optimiser is working exactly as designed — that is the problem. D) The optimiser's response reflects the estimate, not any change in the asset's risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Under a utility function U = E(R) − ½Aσ², a client with A = 4 compares Portfolio P (E(R) 14%, σ 25%) with Portfolio Q (E(R) 9%, σ 10%). Which does he prefer?\nA) Q, because its utility of 7.0% exceeds P's 1.5%  B) P, because it returns more  C) He is indifferent  D) Neither can be evaluated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **P:** 0.14 − 0.5(4)(0.0625) = 0.14 − 0.125 = **0.015 (1.5%)**. **Q:** 0.09 − 0.5(4)(0.01) = 0.09 − 0.02 = **0.070 (7.0%)**. Q wins decisively.\nB) A highly risk-averse client (A = 4) is penalised heavily by P's 25% volatility. C) The utilities differ by 5.5 percentage points. D) Both are directly computable from the stated function.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** The same two portfolios are shown to a client with A = 1. Which does he prefer?\nA) Q, with utility 8.5%  B) Neither  C) Both equally  D) P, with utility 10.9% against Q's 8.5%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **P:** 0.14 − 0.5(1)(0.0625) = 0.14 − 0.03125 = **0.10875 (10.9%)**. **Q:** 0.09 − 0.5(1)(0.01) = 0.09 − 0.005 = **0.085 (8.5%)**. **The same two portfolios, a different client, a different right answer** — which is exactly why the frontier alone cannot pick a portfolio.\nA) Q's utility is correct at 8.5% but it no longer wins. B) Both are computable. C) The utilities differ by 2.4 percentage points.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An adviser builds a 20-security optimised portfolio. How many total inputs must be estimated?\nA) 40  B) 190  C) 400  D) 230\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — 20 expected returns + 20 variances + 20(19)/2 = 190 covariances = **230**, matching n(n+3)/2 = 20 × 23 / 2.\nA) Counts only the returns and variances. B) Counts only the covariances. C) Is n², which double counts each pair and mixes in the diagonal.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Which portfolio in this list is INEFFICIENT? P: 12% / σ 12%. Q: 12% / σ 16%. R: 14% / σ 20%. S: 9% / σ 8%.\nA) P  B) S  C) R  D) Q\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Q is dominated by P**: identical 12% return, but 16% risk against P's 12%. No investor would choose it.\nA) P dominates Q and is not itself dominated. B) S offers the lowest risk, so it may suit a conservative investor. C) R offers the highest return, so it may suit an aggressive investor. **P, R and S can all be efficient; only Q is beaten outright.**\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Mr Shah's ₹40,00,000 is split ₹24,00,000 in equity (E(R) 16%, σ 22%) and ₹16,00,000 in debt (E(R) 7%, σ 6%), with ρ = 0.20. His expected return is:\nA) 11.50%  B) 12.40%  C) 13.00%  D) 10.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Weights 0.60 / 0.40. E(R_p) = 0.60(16) + 0.40(7) = 9.6 + 2.8 = **12.40%**, i.e. **₹4,96,000**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of 16 and 7. C) Overweights equity. D) Reverses the weights.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** For Mr Shah's portfolio in Q94 (σ 22% and 6%, weights 0.60/0.40, ρ = 0.20), the portfolio standard deviation is:\nA) 15.60%  B) 192.67%  C) 13.88%  D) 13.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 0.36(484) = 174.24; 0.16(36) = 5.76; covariance term = 2(0.6)(0.4)(22)(6)(0.2) = 0.48 × 132 × 0.2 = 12.67. Total σ_p² = **192.67**; σ_p = √192.67 = **13.88%**.\nA) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations, 0.60(22) + 0.40(6), which applies only at ρ = +1. B) Is the variance, not the standard deviation. D) Omits the debt fund's own variance contribution and the covariance term.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A ₹15,00,000 portfolio has E(R) 12% and σ 15%. Taking a one-standard-deviation band around the expected return, the one-year value range is approximately:\nA) ₹14,55,000 to ₹19,05,000  B) ₹12,75,000 to ₹17,25,000  C) ₹12,30,000 to ₹21,30,000  D) ₹15,45,000 to ₹19,05,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The ±1σ return band is 12% ± 15% = **−3% to +27%**. In rupees: ₹15,00,000 × 0.97 = **₹14,55,000** and ₹15,00,000 × 1.27 = **₹19,05,000**.\nB) Applies a −15% to +15% band, ignoring the 12% expected return entirely. C) Applies a two-standard-deviation band (12% ± 30%, i.e. −18% to +42%). D) Gets the upper bound right but sets the lower bound at +3%, a sign error — a −3% return must reduce the portfolio below ₹15,00,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An adviser presents optimiser output showing an \"optimal\" 47.38% allocation to mid-cap equity. The professional criticism is:\nA) The figure is too high  B) The optimiser is broken  C) Mid-caps should be excluded  D) False precision — the inputs are forecasts, so two decimal places imply a certainty the model does not possess\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Presenting estimated outputs to two decimals is the **most common misuse of MPT**. A sensible presentation is a range, such as \"roughly 45–50%\".\nA) The level may well be appropriate for the client; the issue is how it is presented. B) The optimiser is functioning; the problem lies in the interpretation of its output. C) Mid-caps are a legitimate asset class.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A client asks why his adviser optimises across five asset classes rather than fifty individual stocks. The best answer is:\nA) Fifty stocks need 1,325 estimates versus 20 for five asset classes, so estimation error is far smaller and the result far more stable  B) It is quicker  C) Stocks are too risky  D) SEBI requires it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — n(n+3)/2 gives **1,325 inputs for 50 securities but only 20 for five asset classes**. Fewer, more stable inputs mean less compounded error.\nB) Speed is a side-benefit, not the reason. C) Individual stocks can be held within an equity allocation. D) No regulation prescribes the optimisation universe.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Two funds each have σ = 20%, held 50/50. As ρ moves from +1 to 0.5 to 0, portfolio risk moves:\nA) It rises throughout  B) 20.00% → 20.00% → 20.00%  C) 20.00% → 15.00% → 10.00%  D) 20.00% → 17.32% → 14.14%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — σ_p² = 200 + 200ρ. At **ρ = 1**: 400 → **20.00%**. At **ρ = 0.5**: 300 → **17.32%**. At **ρ = 0**: 200 → **14.14%**.\nA) Falling correlation always reduces risk, never raises it. B) Risk would stay flat only if ρ never changed. C) Applies a linear rather than a square-root relationship.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises Chapter 14?\nA) Diversification means owning many securities  B) MPT guarantees higher returns  C) Portfolio return is the weighted average but portfolio risk is less whenever correlation is below +1; the efficient frontier maps the best risk-return trade-offs, and the investor's risk tolerance picks the point — subject to the fact that all inputs are estimates  D) Variance is a perfect measure of risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It captures the **mechanism** (correlation below +1), the **output** (the efficient frontier), the **choice** (risk tolerance), and the **caveat** (estimation error).\nA) Describes naive diversification, which MPT explicitly improves upon. B) MPT improves the risk-return trade-off; it guarantees nothing. D) Variance treats upside as risk and assumes symmetry — a recognised weakness of the model.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Write the formula down first, fill it in mechanically, and remember the final square root.\n\n### Case: Mrs Deshmukh's ₹50,00,000 Portfolio\n\nMrs Deshmukh, 48, has **₹50,00,000** to invest for a 12-year goal. Her SEBI-registered investment adviser proposes a two-fund portfolio:\n\n| Fund | Amount | Weight | Expected return | Standard deviation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Diversified equity fund** | ₹30,00,000 | **0.60** | **14%** | **22%** |\n| **Short-duration debt fund** | ₹20,00,000 | **0.40** | **7%** | **6%** |\n\nThe adviser estimates the **correlation between the two funds at ρ = 0.25**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What is the expected return of Mrs Deshmukh's portfolio, in percent and in rupees?\nA) 10.50%; ₹5,25,000  B) 11.20%; ₹5,60,000  C) 12.00%; ₹6,00,000  D) 11.20%; ₹4,48,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — E(R_p) = 0.60(14) + 0.40(7) = 8.4 + 2.8 = **11.20%**. In rupees: 11.20% × ₹50,00,000 = **₹5,60,000**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of 14 and 7. C) Overweights the equity fund. D) Has the right percentage but applies it to ₹40 lakh instead of ₹50 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the portfolio's standard deviation?\nA) 15.60%  B) 12.10%  C) 195.84%  D) 13.99%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — First the covariance term: 2(0.60)(0.40)(22)(6)(0.25) = 0.48 × 132 × 0.25 = **15.84**.\nσ_p² = 0.36(484) + 0.16(36) + 15.84 = 174.24 + 5.76 + 15.84 = **195.84**.\n**σ_p = √195.84 = 13.99%.**\nA) Is the weighted average of the standard deviations, 0.60(22) + 0.40(6), which holds only at ρ = +1. B) Is the answer to QC4, after gold is added. C) Is the variance — the square root was not taken, the single most common error on this formula.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** How much risk did diversification save her, and what would her risk have been at ρ = +1?\nA) Nothing; 13.99%  B) 1.61 percentage points; 15.60% at ρ = +1  C) 8.00 percentage points; 22.00%  D) 1.61 percentage points; 13.99%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At **ρ = +1** the formula collapses to the weighted average: 0.60(22) + 0.40(6) = **15.60%**. Her actual σ_p is **13.99%**, so diversification saved **15.60 − 13.99 = 1.61 percentage points**. Note her **expected return of 11.20% is unchanged** by the correlation — the saving is pure gain.\nA) A benefit always exists below ρ = +1. C) 22% is the equity fund's own risk, not any portfolio outcome. D) Quotes the actual σ_p as though it were the ρ = +1 figure.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The adviser proposes restructuring to **50% equity, 35% debt and 15% in a gold ETF** (gold: E(R) 9%, σ 14%; ρ with equity 0.10, ρ with debt 0.05). The new risk and return are:\nA) σ 13.99%; E(R) 11.20%  B) σ 14.50%; E(R) 11.50%  C) σ 12.10%; E(R) 10.80%  D) σ 12.10%; E(R) 11.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Return:** 0.50(14) + 0.35(7) + 0.15(9) = 7.00 + 2.45 + 1.35 = **10.80%**.\n**Risk:** variances 0.25(484) = 121.00, 0.1225(36) = 4.41, 0.0225(196) = 4.41. Covariance terms: 2(0.5)(0.35)(22)(6)(0.25) = 11.55; 2(0.5)(0.15)(22)(14)(0.10) = 4.62; 2(0.35)(0.15)(6)(14)(0.05) = 0.44. Total σ_p² = **146.43**; **σ_p = 12.10%**.\nA) Is the original two-fund portfolio, before gold was added. B) Adding a low-correlation asset lowers risk; it does not raise it. D) Pairs the new risk with the old return, ignoring that gold's 9% pulls the average down.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Six months later a global shock pushes the equity-debt correlation from 0.25 to 0.75. On her ORIGINAL 60/40 portfolio, what happens, and what is the lesson?\nA) Nothing — the weights are unchanged  B) Her expected return falls to 9%  C) Risk rises from 13.99% to about 15.08%, wiping out most of the diversification benefit — correlations are estimates, and they rise in a crisis  D) The portfolio becomes riskless\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only the covariance term changes: 0.48 × 132 × 0.75 = **47.52**, against 15.84 before.\nσ_p² = 174.24 + 5.76 + 47.52 = **227.52**; **σ_p = √227.52 = 15.08%**.\nHer risk rises from **13.99% to 15.08%**, and the benefit against the 15.60% weighted average collapses from **1.61 pp to just 0.52 pp**.\n\n**The lesson — this is the whole of \"estimation issues\" in one number.** The formula never changed and the weights never changed; only one *estimated input* moved. Correlations are the least stable input in the model and they **rise towards +1 in exactly the conditions that make diversification matter most**. That is why an adviser should (i) present optimiser output as a range rather than a precise figure, (ii) **stress-test the portfolio with correlations pushed to +1**, and (iii) set the client's expectations on the stressed number, not the comfortable one.\n\nA) Unchanged weights do not mean unchanged risk — risk depends on correlation too. B) Correlation never affects expected return; it stays at 11.20%. D) Rising correlation increases risk; it can never remove it.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "15", "title": "Portfolio Construction Process", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "# Chapter 15: Portfolio Construction Process — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 5 · Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring and Evaluation (20 marks).**\n> Chapter 14 gave you the *mathematics* of a portfolio. This chapter gives you the *process* — how a real Investment Adviser turns a real person's messy life into a written plan. It is the most heavily examined chapter in Module 5 because it is what an IA actually does for a living. Two things dominate the marks: the **Investment Policy Statement (IPS)** and the **strategic-versus-tactical asset allocation** distinction. Learn those cold.\n\n---\n\n## 15.1 ⭐ Why the Asset Allocation Decision Matters More Than Anything Else\n\n**Asset allocation** is the decision of **how to divide a portfolio across asset classes** — equity, debt, cash, gold, real estate, and alternatives — *before* deciding which particular security to buy inside each class.\n\nMost beginners believe investing is about picking winners. The evidence says otherwise.\n\n### The three decisions an investor makes\n\n| Decision | Example | How much it explains |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Asset allocation** | \"60% equity, 30% debt, 10% gold\" | **The overwhelming majority of return variability** |\n| **2. Security selection** | \"HDFC Bank rather than ICICI Bank\" | A small residual |\n| **3. Market timing** | \"Move to cash now, back in later\" | A small — and usually **negative** — residual |\n\n### 🔑 The empirical support (15.16.1) — memorise these three studies\n\n| Study | What it examined | Headline finding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Brinson, Hood & Beebower (1986)** | 91 large US pension plans, 1974–1983 | Asset allocation policy explained **93.6%** of the variation in quarterly returns |\n| **Brinson, Singer & Beebower (1991)** | 82 pension plans, 1977–1987 | Confirmed the result — approximately **91.5%** |\n| **Ibbotson & Kaplan (2000)** | Mutual funds and pension funds | Refined it: **~90%** of return variability **over time**, **~40%** of variation **across funds**, and **~100%** of the **level** of return |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"90 / 40 / 100.\"**\n> **90%** of *why your portfolio wobbled over time* = asset allocation.\n> **40%** of *why your portfolio differs from your neighbour's* = asset allocation.\n> **100%** of the *level* of long-run return = asset allocation (before costs).\n\n> ⚠️ **The single most common exam trap in this chapter.** The Brinson result is about the **variability (variance) of returns over time**, *not* \"93.6% of the return\". Candidates who read it as \"asset allocation gives you 93.6% of your money\" get the follow-up question wrong. Ibbotson & Kaplan wrote their paper specifically to correct that misreading — the title is literally *\"Does Asset Allocation Policy Explain 40, 90, or 100 Percent of Performance?\"*\n\n### Why it works out this way\n\n1. **Asset classes behave very differently from each other, but securities inside a class behave alike.** Two large-cap Indian banks move together far more than equity and G-Secs do. So switching from Bank A to Bank B changes little; switching from equity to G-Secs changes everything.\n2. **Risk is set at the asset-class level.** A 90% equity portfolio is a high-risk portfolio regardless of which stocks are in it.\n3. **Selection and timing are zero-sum before costs and negative after them.** For every adviser who buys, another sells. After brokerage, STT, stamp duty, impact cost and taxes, the average active rupee must underperform the average passive rupee.\n\n**The practical consequence for an IA:** spend your effort on getting the **allocation** right for the client's goals, horizon and risk profile. That is where the value is. Fine-tuning the choice between two well-run flexi-cap funds is a rounding error by comparison.\n\n---\n\n## 15.2 ⭐ Correlation Across Asset Classes and Securities\n\nChapter 14 proved that portfolio risk falls when holdings do **not** move together. Correlation (**ρ**) is the number that measures \"moving together.\"\n\n**ρ always lies between −1 and +1.**\n\n| ρ | Meaning | Diversification benefit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **+1** | Perfectly together | **None** — portfolio risk is the plain weighted average |\n| **+0.5 to +0.9** | Strongly together | Small |\n| **0** | Unrelated | Substantial |\n| **−0.5** | Usually opposite | Large |\n| **−1** | Perfectly opposite | **Risk can be driven to zero** with the right weights |\n\n### Correlation across Indian asset classes — the shape you must know\n\n*(Illustrative, to fix the pattern in your mind — the exam tests the ranking, not decimals.)*\n\n| Pair | Typical correlation | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Two large-cap Indian equity funds | **Very high, near +0.9** | They hold largely the same Nifty names |\n| Large-cap equity vs mid-cap equity | **High** | Same economy, same risk appetite cycle |\n| Indian equity vs Indian corporate debt | **Low, sometimes slightly negative** | Driven by earnings vs by interest rates |\n| Indian equity vs gold | **Low to negative** | Gold is a **safe haven** — it tends to rise in panics |\n| Indian equity vs US equity (in ₹) | **Moderate, and rises in crises** | Global risk appetite is common, but the ₹/$ move adds a cushion |\n| G-Secs vs corporate bonds | **High** | Both driven mainly by the level of rates |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Different engines, different correlations.\"**\n> Equity is powered by **corporate earnings**. Debt is powered by **interest rates**. Gold is powered by **fear and the dollar**. Real estate is powered by **local supply and rental yields**. Because the engines differ, the vehicles do not arrive together — and that is the whole point of multi-asset investing.\n\n### Worked example — what correlation is worth in rupees\n\nMs Iyer invests **₹10,00,000**: **60% equity** (σ = **18%**) and **40% debt** (σ = **5%**). Correlation **ρ = 0.15**.\n\n**Step 1 — the naive (wrong) risk estimate, the weighted average:**\n0.60 × 18 + 0.40 × 5 = 10.8 + 2.0 = **12.80%**\n\n**Step 2 — the true portfolio risk:**\nσ_p = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ)\n= √(0.36 × 324 + 0.16 × 25 + 2 × 0.60 × 0.40 × 18 × 5 × 0.15)\n= √(116.64 + 4.00 + 6.48)\n= √127.12 = **11.27%**\n\n**The diversification benefit is 12.80 − 11.27 = 1.53 percentage points of risk, obtained for free** — no return was given up, because portfolio *return* is always the exact weighted average (Chapter 14).\n\n### ⚠️ Three warnings about correlation that the exam loves\n\n1. **Correlation is not stable.** It is estimated from history and it changes.\n2. **Correlations rise towards +1 in a crisis.** In October 2008 and again in March 2020, almost everything risky fell together. **Diversification fails precisely when it is most needed.** This is why an emergency fund in cash — an asset with essentially zero correlation to anything — is non-negotiable.\n3. **Correlation measures direction, not magnitude.** Two assets can have ρ = +0.9 while one is five times as volatile as the other. **Covariance** carries the magnitude; **correlation** is covariance standardised.\n\n---\n\n## 15.3 ⭐ The Steps in the Portfolio Construction Process\n\nThe process is a **loop, not a line** — it ends by feeding back into itself.\n\n| # | Step | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Understand the client** | Data gathering: goals, income, expenses, assets, liabilities, insurance, tax status, family |\n| **2** | **Analyse the financial position** | Net worth statement, cash-flow statement, ratios, emergency fund, insurance adequacy |\n| **3** | **Determine objectives and constraints** | Return objective, risk objective; liquidity, horizon, tax, legal, unique needs |\n| **4** | **Write the Investment Policy Statement** | The whole of steps 1–3 committed to paper and signed |\n| **5** | **Form capital market expectations** | Forecast risk, return and correlation for each asset class |\n| **6** | **Set the strategic asset allocation** | Combine (3) and (5) into the long-term policy mix |\n| **7** | **Select products and securities** | Funds, stocks, bonds, schemes — inside each asset class |\n| **8** | **Execute** | Implement, minimising cost, tax and market impact |\n| **9** | **Monitor and evaluate** | Measure against the benchmark; watch the client's circumstances |\n| **10** | **Rebalance and revise** | Restore target weights; update the IPS on any life event — then return to step 1 |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Plan, Predict, Position, Police.\"**\n> **Plan** the client (steps 1–4) → **Predict** the markets (step 5) → **Position** the money (steps 6–8) → **Police** it forever (steps 9–10).\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** The **client** comes before the **market**. Capital market expectations (step 5) are formed *after* the IPS (step 4), never before. An adviser who starts with \"what looks cheap right now\" has inverted the entire process — and, under the **SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013**, has also skipped the mandatory risk profiling and suitability assessment.\n\n---\n\n## 15.4 ⭐⭐ The Investment Policy Statement (IPS)\n\n### What it is\n\n**The IPS is the written document that governs the relationship between the client and the adviser and sets out how the portfolio will be managed.** It is the portfolio's constitution: a statement of *what we are trying to achieve, what we may and may not do, and how we will know whether it is working*.\n\nIt is written **at the start**, **jointly**, and **signed by both parties**.\n\n### 15.4.1 Need and importance of an IPS\n\n| Reason | Why it matters in practice |\n|---|---|\n| **Clarity of purpose** | Forces vague wishes (\"I want good returns\") into measurable objectives (\"₹1.5 crore by 2038 for my daughter's education\") |\n| **Discipline in a panic** | When the Nifty falls 25%, the IPS is the document that says *we planned for this; the plan has not changed* |\n| **Protects against emotion** | Rules written in calm conditions are obeyed in frightening ones — this is the IPS's single greatest value |\n| **A yardstick for evaluation** | Specifies the **benchmark** in advance, so performance can be judged honestly |\n| **Continuity** | If the adviser leaves, dies or is replaced, the successor knows exactly what was intended |\n| **Reduces disputes** | Both parties agreed and signed; misunderstandings about mandate and risk are avoided |\n| **Regulatory and compliance record** | Evidence that risk profiling and **suitability** were assessed, as SEBI requires of a Registered Investment Adviser |\n| **Manages expectations** | The client sees, in advance, the range of outcomes — including the bad ones |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"The IPS is a seatbelt, not a steering wheel.\"**\n> It does not tell the car where to go on any given day. It stops the passenger being thrown through the windscreen when the market brakes hard.\n\n### 15.4.2 ⭐ Constituents of an IPS\n\n| Section | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Client description / background** | Who the client is, family, occupation, wealth, current holdings, the purpose of the portfolio |\n| **2. Purpose and scope of the IPS** | Why the document exists and what it covers |\n| **3. Duties and responsibilities** | Of the **adviser**, the **client**, the **custodian**, the **auditor**; who may give instructions |\n| **4. Investment objectives** | The **return objective** and the **risk objective** — stated explicitly |\n| **5. Investment constraints** | **Liquidity, time horizon, tax, legal & regulatory, unique circumstances** |\n| **6. Investment guidelines** | Permitted and prohibited asset classes and instruments; use of leverage, derivatives, unlisted or illiquid assets; **exposure limits** |\n| **7. Strategic asset allocation** | The long-term target weights, with **permitted bands** around each |\n| **8. Benchmark and evaluation** | The benchmark for each asset class and for the portfolio as a whole; how often performance is reviewed |\n| **9. Rebalancing policy** | Method (calendar / threshold), tolerance bands, who initiates |\n| **10. Review procedure** | How often the IPS itself is revisited, and which events force an immediate revision |\n| **11. Appendices** | Fee schedule, risk profile output, list of existing holdings, communication protocol |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook for the objectives-and-constraints core — \"RRTTLLU.\"**\n> **R**eturn · **R**isk · **T**ime horizon · **T**axes · **L**iquidity · **L**egal & regulatory · **U**nique circumstances.\n> The first two **R**s are the **objectives**; the remaining five are the **constraints**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** An IPS does **not** contain a list of specific stocks to buy, nor a market forecast, nor a promised return. It contains **objectives, constraints, guidelines, allocation targets and review procedures**. Anything that would have to be rewritten every month does not belong in it.\n\n### When must the IPS be revised?\n\nNot on market moves — on **life events**:\n\n- Marriage, divorce, birth of a child, death in the family\n- Job loss, promotion, business sale, inheritance, windfall\n- Serious illness or disability\n- A goal being achieved, abandoned, brought forward or postponed\n- A material change in tax law or in the regulations applying to the client\n- The client's own risk tolerance genuinely and durably changing\n\n> ⚠️ **A 20% market fall is NOT a reason to rewrite the IPS.** It is precisely the situation the IPS was written for. Rewriting the plan because the plan is being tested is the most expensive mistake in personal finance.\n\n---\n\n## 15.5 ⭐ Investment Objectives — Return and Risk\n\nAn objective must be **specific, measurable and achievable**. \"Beat inflation comfortably\" is not an objective; it is a mood.\n\n### 15.5.1 The return objective\n\nDistinguish two things:\n\n| | **Required return** | **Desired return** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Meaning | What the client **must** earn to meet essential goals | What the client **would like** to earn |\n| Nature | A **need** | A **want** |\n| If unattainable | The goal, the horizon or the saving rate must change | It can simply be abandoned |\n\nA return objective should always state **four things**: the number, whether it is **nominal or real**, whether **pre-tax or post-tax**, and whether it is measured as a **total return** (income + capital gain).\n\n### 🔑 Worked example — computing a required return\n\nMr Sharma, aged 60, retires with a corpus of **₹1,00,00,000**. He needs **₹4,00,000** a year to live on, rising with inflation. Assume **inflation 6%** and total costs (adviser fee + fund expenses) of **1%**.\n\n**Step 1 — the spending rate:** 4,00,000 ÷ 1,00,00,000 = **4.0%**\n\n**Step 2 — the additive approximation (quick, used in exams):**\n4.0% + 6.0% + 1.0% = **11.0%**\n\n**Step 3 — the exact multiplicative calculation:**\n(1.04 × 1.06 × 1.01) − 1 = 1.113424 − 1 = **11.34%**\n\n**Step 4 — the adviser's judgement.** An 11.34% *nominal, post-cost* return needs a portfolio with a heavy equity weight, which a 60-year-old drawing an income may not be able to tolerate. The honest conversation is: **reduce the withdrawal to ₹3,00,000, work part-time, or accept a higher risk of running out.** Stating the required return is what makes that conversation possible — that is the value of the arithmetic.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Costs must be **added** to the required return, not ignored. A 1% fee does not reduce the target — it raises the gross return the portfolio must earn.\n\n### 15.5.2 The risk objective — ability versus willingness\n\n| | **Ability to take risk** (risk **capacity**) | **Willingness to take risk** (risk **tolerance/attitude**) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nature | **Objective, financial, measurable** | **Subjective, psychological** |\n| Determined by | Wealth relative to goals, **time horizon**, stability and security of income, size and urgency of liabilities, insurance cover, number of dependants | Personality, past experience of loss, financial literacy, temperament |\n| How assessed | Financial statements and cash-flow analysis | **Risk-profiling questionnaire**, conversation |\n| Can it be changed? | Only slowly — by saving more or earning more | Can be raised through **education** |\n\n### ⭐ The conflict rule — heavily examined\n\n| Situation | What the adviser does |\n|---|---|\n| **Ability HIGH, willingness LOW** | Educate the client about long-run asset behaviour — but if the anxiety is genuine, **follow the lower (willingness)**. A client who abandons a plan in a crash is worse off than one who never took the risk. |\n| **Ability LOW, willingness HIGH** | **Follow the lower (ability).** Never let a client take risk their finances cannot survive, however enthusiastic they are. This is where the adviser must be firmest. |\n| **Both aligned** | Straightforward — set the allocation accordingly |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"The lower of the two governs; the adviser may educate, never override.\"**\n> But the two errors are not symmetric: overriding low **willingness** produces a panicked client; overriding low **ability** produces a ruined one.\n\n**How a risk objective is stated:**\n\n- **Absolute:** \"The portfolio's annual standard deviation should not exceed **10%**\", or \"a loss greater than **₹8,00,000** in any 12-month period is unacceptable.\"\n- **Relative:** \"Tracking error against the benchmark should not exceed **4%** a year.\"\n\n### Worked example — turning a rupee loss limit into a risk number\n\nMr Sharma says a loss of more than **₹8,00,000 on ₹1,00,00,000** — that is **8%** — in any one year would be unacceptable. Expected return is **11%**.\n\nUsing the normal approximation, an outcome **two standard deviations below the mean** is roughly a 1-in-40 event:\n11% − 2σ = −8% → 2σ = 19% → **σ = 9.5%**\n\n**So the portfolio's standard deviation must be about 9.5% or lower** — which, on the risk numbers in section 15.2, points to an allocation of roughly **50% equity / 50% debt**, not 80% equity. The client's own sentence has just chosen his asset allocation for him.\n\n---\n\n## 15.6 ⭐ Investment Constraints\n\nFive constraints, always in the same order in an IPS. Objectives say what you *want*; constraints say what you are *allowed* to do.\n\n### 15.6.1 Liquidity constraint\n\n**The need for cash — planned or unplanned — that the portfolio must be able to meet without selling at a bad price.**\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **Planned, near-term** | School fee in April, wedding next winter, a car purchase, an insurance premium |\n| **Unplanned** | Medical emergency, job loss, urgent family need |\n| **Ongoing** | A retiree's monthly withdrawal; a charitable trust's annual disbursement |\n\n- Meet it with **cash, liquid funds, overnight funds, ultra-short duration funds, sweep-in deposits** — never with equity.\n- The universal baseline: an **emergency fund of 3 to 6 months' expenses** (6 to 12 months if income is irregular — a business owner, a professional on contract, a single earner).\n- **Illiquid holdings raise the liquidity constraint:** physical real estate, unlisted shares, PPF (15-year), ELSS (**3-year lock-in**), NPS (essentially until 60), and Sovereign Gold Bonds (8-year tenor, exit permitted from year 5 on coupon dates) cannot be relied upon for emergencies.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Liquidity is about **converting to cash quickly at a fair price**, not about whether an asset is \"safe\". A ₹50 lakh flat is a low-risk asset with **terrible** liquidity. A liquid fund is a modest-return asset with **excellent** liquidity.\n\n### 15.6.2 Time horizon constraint\n\n**How long the money can stay invested before it is needed.**\n\n| Horizon | Typical suitable allocation |\n|---|---|\n| **Under 1 year** | Liquid / overnight / ultra-short funds; **no equity at all** |\n| **1–3 years** | Short-duration debt, arbitrage funds, small equity if any |\n| **3–5 years** | Balanced / hybrid, moderate equity |\n| **5–10 years** | Equity-oriented |\n| **Over 10 years** | Predominantly equity |\n\n- Horizons may be **single-stage** (one goal, one date) or **multi-stage** (education in 8 years, then retirement in 25 — each leg gets its own allocation).\n- **Longer horizon ⇒ higher ability to take risk**, because there is time to recover from a fall and because equity's *annualised* return becomes far less variable as the period lengthens.\n- The horizon shortens by one year every year. **A goal 3 years away must be de-risked whether or not markets are attractive.**\n\n### 15.6.3 Tax constraint\n\nTax turns a gross return into the only number the client can spend.\n\n| Item | Position (India, FY 2024-25 onwards) |\n|---|---|\n| **Listed equity / equity mutual funds — short term** (held ≤ 12 months) | **20%** under Section 111A |\n| **Listed equity / equity mutual funds — long term** (held > 12 months) | **12.5%** under Section 112A, on gains **above ₹1,25,000** a year |\n| **Debt mutual funds bought on or after 1 April 2023** | Gains taxed at the investor's **slab rate**, with **no long-term benefit** |\n| **Bank fixed deposit interest** | Taxed at the investor's **slab rate**, and **TDS applies** |\n| **Dividends** | Taxed at the investor's **slab rate** in the investor's hands |\n| **PPF / EPF (within limits) / Sukanya Samriddhi** | **EEE** — exempt at contribution, accrual and withdrawal |\n\n**What the constraint means for construction:**\n\n- Prefer **growth options over dividend/IDCW options** — dividends are taxed at slab rates as received, while growth defers the tax until you choose to sell.\n- Prefer **holding through the long-term threshold** rather than trading around it.\n- Use the **₹1.25 lakh annual LTCG exemption** deliberately — realise and immediately reinvest gains each year (\"tax harvesting\").\n- Locate tax-inefficient assets inside **tax-exempt or tax-deferred wrappers** (EPF, PPF, NPS) and tax-efficient assets in the taxable account.\n- Remember that **rebalancing has a tax cost**, which is why bands exist (see 15.13).\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"It is not what you earn; it is what you keep.\"** A 12% pre-tax return taxed at 30% leaves 8.4%. A 10% return taxed at 12.5% leaves 8.75%. **The lower gross return wins.**\n\n### 15.6.4 Legal and regulatory constraint\n\nRules imposed from outside that the client cannot choose to ignore.\n\n| Client type | Constraint |\n|---|---|\n| **Charitable and religious trusts** | Must invest only in modes permitted under **Section 11(5) of the Income-tax Act**; the trust deed may narrow this further |\n| **Provident funds / EPFO** | Must follow the pattern of investment notified by the Government; EPFO's equity exposure is a limited share of incremental flows |\n| **Insurance companies** | **IRDAI** prescribes minimum holdings in Government and other approved securities and minimum exposure to the housing and infrastructure sectors |\n| **NPS subscribers** | **PFRDA** caps equity (asset class **E**) at **75%** under Active Choice, with the cap tapering for older subscribers; alternative assets (**A**) are capped at **5%** |\n| **Mutual funds** | **SEBI** single-issuer, single-sector and single-group caps (see 15.7) |\n| **Non-resident Indians** | **FEMA**: NRIs may not invest in certain instruments (for example, **PPF and Sukanya Samriddhi cannot be newly opened**); repatriation limits apply; agricultural land cannot be purchased |\n| **Residents investing abroad** | **RBI's Liberalised Remittance Scheme** — up to **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual |\n| **Insiders and employees of listed companies** | **SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015** — trading windows, pre-clearance, and a **minimum six-month holding** to avoid contra-trade |\n| **Company directors, bank employees, government servants** | Employer codes of conduct restricting or requiring disclosure of trades |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** A legal/regulatory constraint applies **regardless of what the client wants and regardless of suitability.** If a trust deed forbids equity, no amount of risk capacity permits equity. Contrast this with a *unique preference*, which is the client's own choice and can be relaxed by the client.\n\n### 15.6.5 Unique circumstances and preferences\n\nEverything specific to this client that does not fit elsewhere:\n\n- **Ethical, religious or ESG restrictions** (see 15.8)\n- **Concentrated holdings** — employer ESOPs, a family business stake, ancestral property, an inherited holding the client refuses to sell for sentimental reasons\n- **A dependant with special needs**, requiring a permanent income stream and a trust structure\n- **A promise or commitment** — funding a sibling's education, supporting parents\n- **Assets the client will not sell** — the ancestral house, gold held for a daughter's wedding\n- **Health status** and life expectancy\n- **A desire for direct control**, or a refusal to hold particular instruments (for example, \"no derivatives\", \"no unlisted shares\")\n\n---\n\n## 15.7 ⭐ Exposure Limits — to Securities, Sectors, Entities and Asset Classes\n\n**An exposure limit is a written ceiling on how much of the portfolio may sit in any one thing.** Its purpose is to control **concentration risk** — the risk that one failure destroys the portfolio.\n\n### Why limits are written into the IPS *in advance*\n\nConcentration feels wonderful while it is working. Nobody sets a limit on their best-performing stock voluntarily; the limit must exist **before** the position becomes a favourite. That is exactly the same logic as the IPS itself.\n\n### The four levels at which limits are set\n\n| Level | Typical form in a client IPS |\n|---|---|\n| **Single security / issuer** | \"No more than **10%** of the equity portfolio in any one stock\" |\n| **Sector / industry** | \"No more than **25%** of the equity portfolio in any one sector\" |\n| **Entity / group / counterparty** | \"No more than **20%** with any one AMC, group or issuer\" |\n| **Asset class** | \"Equity **60% ± 10 percentage points**; gold not more than **15%**\" |\n\n### SEBI's own exposure limits for mutual funds — the regulatory template\n\nThese are the limits an IA should be able to recognise, because they show what a regulator considers prudent.\n\n| Limit | Level |\n|---|---|\n| Equity of a **single company** | **10% of NAV** (index funds and sector/thematic funds exempt) |\n| **Debt of a single issuer** | **10% of NAV**, extendable to **12%** with prior approval of the trustees and the AMC board |\n| **Single sector** (debt) | **20% of NAV**, with limited additional headroom for housing finance |\n| **Single group** (debt) | **20% of NAV**, extendable to **25%** with trustee approval |\n| **Voting rights** | All schemes of a mutual fund together may not hold more than **10% of a company's paid-up capital carrying voting rights** |\n| **Government securities, T-bills and TREPS** | **Exempt** from single-issuer limits — sovereign risk |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Ten for one, twenty for many.\"**\n> **10%** is the recurring cap on any **single** name; **20%** is the recurring cap on any **single sector or group**.\n\n### Worked example — an exposure limit doing its job\n\nMrs Rao's portfolio is **₹50,00,000**, with a **10% single-stock cap** and a **25% single-sector cap** written into her IPS.\n\n- Her employer's stock, received as ESOPs, is worth **₹18,00,000** = **36%** of the portfolio. **Cap = 10% × ₹50,00,000 = ₹5,00,000.** She is **₹13,00,000 over the limit.**\n- Worse, she also holds ₹4,00,000 in two banking funds. Since her employer is a bank, her **financial-sector exposure is ₹22,00,000 = 44%**, against a 25% cap of **₹12,50,000**.\n- **Compounding the problem: her salary comes from the same company.** Her **human capital** and her **financial capital** are exposed to the same single event. If the employer fails, she loses her job and her savings in the same week.\n\n**The adviser's plan:** unwind the ESOP position in **stages over three financial years** to spread the capital-gains tax across three ₹1.25 lakh LTCG exemptions, staying mindful of insider-trading trading windows, and redirect every new SIP rupee away from financials until the sector weight is inside the band.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Exposure limits apply to the client's **whole balance sheet**, not just the advised portfolio. Employer stock, the family business and the salary itself are all exposure. This is a favourite scenario question.\n\n---\n\n## 15.8 ⭐ Unique Needs — Sustainable and Ethical Investing\n\n### 15.8.1 Sustainable investing (ESG)\n\n**Sustainable investing incorporates Environmental, Social and Governance factors alongside financial factors in investment decisions.**\n\n| Pillar | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| **E — Environmental** | Carbon emissions, energy and water use, waste, pollution, biodiversity, climate transition risk |\n| **S — Social** | Labour practices, workplace safety, diversity, human rights, product safety, community relations, data privacy |\n| **G — Governance** | Board independence and composition, executive pay, shareholder rights, audit quality, **related-party transactions**, promoter pledging, minority-shareholder treatment |\n\n> 🧠 **In India, the \"G\" has historically mattered most.** Almost every large Indian investor loss of the last two decades — Satyam, IL&FS, Yes Bank, and others — was a **governance** failure, not an environmental or social one. Promoter share pledging, related-party transactions and aggressive accounting are governance red flags an IA should look for.\n\n**The six main ESG approaches:**\n\n| Approach | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Negative / exclusionary screening** | Remove whole industries or companies (tobacco, weapons, thermal coal) |\n| **Positive / best-in-class screening** | Keep the highest-rated companies **within** each sector |\n| **ESG integration** | Build ESG factors into ordinary financial analysis and valuation |\n| **Thematic investing** | Target a theme — renewable energy, water, clean transport |\n| **Impact investing** | Seek a **measurable** social or environmental outcome alongside a return |\n| **Active ownership / stewardship** | Vote the shares and engage with management to change behaviour |\n\n**The Indian regulatory framework:**\n\n- **BRSR — Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report.** SEBI requires the **top 1,000 listed companies by market capitalisation** to file a BRSR with their annual report, giving investors standardised ESG disclosure. A subset, **BRSR Core**, carries assurance requirements that are being phased in from the largest companies downward.\n- **Stewardship Code.** SEBI requires mutual funds and AIFs to adopt a stewardship code — a formal policy on voting and engagement with investee companies.\n- **ESG mutual fund schemes.** SEBI permits a mutual fund to run **more than one ESG scheme**, provided each follows a **distinct ESG strategy** (exclusion, integration, best-in-class, impact, sustainable objectives, transition). Such a scheme must invest a **minimum of 80% of total assets** in securities that follow its stated ESG strategy, and must disclose its approach and its voting record.\n- Globally, the **UN Principles for Responsible Investment (UN PRI)** — six voluntary principles — is the main signatory framework.\n\n**Arguments for and against:**\n\n| For | Against |\n|---|---|\n| ESG failures are **real financial risks** that show up in the share price | The **investable universe narrows**, which mathematically cannot improve the efficient frontier |\n| Good governance is empirically linked to durable returns | **Sector bias** — excluding energy and metals creates unintended factor bets |\n| Clients increasingly want their money to reflect their values | **Greenwashing** and inconsistent third-party ESG ratings |\n| Regulation and carbon pricing make ESG risks financially material | Higher expense ratios and **tracking error** against a broad index |\n\n### 15.8.2 Ethical investing\n\n**Ethical investing selects or excludes investments on moral, religious or personal grounds — regardless of whether doing so improves the financial outcome.**\n\n| | **Sustainable / ESG investing** | **Ethical / values-based investing** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Primary driver** | ESG factors as **financially material risks and opportunities** | **Values** — moral, religious or personal conviction |\n| **Would the investor accept a lower return?** | Usually argues no return sacrifice is required | **Yes, explicitly** — the value comes first |\n| **Typical method** | Integration, best-in-class, engagement | **Exclusion** (\"sin stocks\") |\n| **In the IPS** | Recorded under **unique needs and preferences** | Recorded under **unique needs and preferences** |\n\n**Commonly excluded \"sin\" sectors:** tobacco, alcohol, gambling, weapons and defence, adult entertainment, and — for some investors — conventional interest-based lending.\n\n**Shariah-compliant investing** is the best-known religious framework in India:\n\n- Prohibits **riba** (interest), so conventional banks, NBFCs and insurers are excluded\n- Prohibits businesses in alcohol, pork, gambling, tobacco and conventional finance\n- Applies **financial screens** — limits on the company's debt, interest income and receivables relative to size\n- Requires **purification** — donating the impermissible portion of income to charity\n- Indian implementations include the **Nifty50 Shariah, Nifty500 Shariah and BSE 500 Shariah indices**, and a small number of Shariah-compliant equity funds\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap — the adviser's duty.** An ethical restriction is the **client's choice**, and the adviser must **implement it and record it in the IPS**. But the adviser must also **disclose the consequence**: a narrower universe, likely sector bias, higher tracking error and possibly higher cost. Silently ignoring the restriction, or accepting it without explaining its cost, are both failures of the fiduciary duty.\n\n---\n\n## 15.9 Assessing the Needs and Requirements of the Investor\n\n### What must be gathered\n\n| Category | Information |\n|---|---|\n| **Personal** | Age, health, marital status, dependants, occupation, employment stability, residential status (resident / NRI) |\n| **Goals** | What, how much (in today's rupees), when, and **priority** — essential, important or aspirational |\n| **Income** | Salary, business income, rent, interest, expected increments, expected inheritance |\n| **Expenses** | Regular household spending, EMIs, insurance premiums, lifestyle commitments |\n| **Assets** | Bank balances, deposits, mutual funds, shares, EPF/PPF/NPS, gold, property, business interests |\n| **Liabilities** | Home loan, car loan, personal loan, credit-card balances, loans against securities |\n| **Protection** | Life cover, health cover, disability and critical-illness cover |\n| **Tax** | Slab, regime chosen, existing deductions, capital-loss carry-forwards |\n| **Attitude** | Risk-profiling questionnaire result, past behaviour in falling markets, investment knowledge |\n\n### 🔑 The essential principle — goals must be inflated\n\nA goal expressed in today's rupees is not the amount that will be needed.\n\n**Example.** Ms Banerjee's daughter is 4. College will cost **₹15,00,000** in today's money and begins in **14 years**. Education inflation is **8%**.\n\n**Future cost = 15,00,000 × (1.08)¹⁴ = 15,00,000 × 2.9372 = ₹44,05,800**\n\nThe goal is not ₹15 lakh. **It is roughly ₹44 lakh** — nearly three times as much. Every goal in an IPS must be stated at its **future, inflated value**, or the plan is guaranteed to fall short.\n\n### The order of operations — protection before investment\n\nAn adviser must not build an investment portfolio on an unprotected balance sheet:\n\n1. **Emergency fund** — 3 to 6 months' expenses in liquid form\n2. **Health insurance** — for the whole family, adequate for the city they live in\n3. **Term life insurance** — if anyone depends on the client's income\n4. **Repay expensive debt** — a 40% credit-card rate cannot be beaten by any portfolio\n5. **Then** invest for goals\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Fix the roof before buying the furniture.\"** One uninsured hospitalisation can undo a decade of SIPs.\n\n---\n\n## 15.10 Analysing the Financial Position of the Investor\n\nTwo statements and a handful of ratios.\n\n### The two statements\n\n| Statement | Formula | What it tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Net worth statement** | **Assets − Liabilities** | The client's financial position at a **point in time** (a stock) |\n| **Cash-flow statement** | **Inflows − Outflows** | What is available to invest **over a period** (a flow) |\n\n**The cash-flow statement matters more for a young client** (net worth is small, savings capacity is everything). **The net worth statement matters more for a retiree** (there is no more income; the corpus is everything).\n\n### The ratios an IA computes\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Healthy level |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Savings ratio** | Savings ÷ Gross income | The higher the better; **20%+** is a common goal |\n| **Basic liquidity ratio** | Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses | **3 to 6** months |\n| **Debt-to-income ratio** | Total EMIs ÷ Monthly income | Below about **40%**; housing EMI alone below about 30% |\n| **Solvency ratio** | Net worth ÷ Total assets | The higher the better — it shows how much is truly owned |\n| **Debt-to-asset ratio** | Total liabilities ÷ Total assets | The lower the better |\n\n### Worked example\n\nMr Menon, 38. Monthly income **₹1,80,000**; monthly expenses **₹1,05,000**; EMIs **₹55,000** (included in expenses). Assets: liquid **₹4,00,000**; mutual funds **₹22,00,000**; EPF **₹14,00,000**; flat **₹85,00,000**. Liabilities: home loan **₹48,00,000**.\n\n| Measure | Calculation | Result | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Net worth** | (4 + 22 + 14 + 85) − 48 = 125 − 48 | **₹77,00,000** | Sound |\n| **Monthly surplus** | 1,80,000 − 1,05,000 | **₹75,000** | Strong saving capacity |\n| **Savings ratio** | 75,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **41.7%** | Excellent |\n| **Basic liquidity ratio** | 4,00,000 ÷ 1,05,000 | **3.8 months** | Adequate; push towards 6 |\n| **Debt-to-income** | 55,000 ÷ 1,80,000 | **30.6%** | Comfortable |\n| **Solvency ratio** | 77,00,000 ÷ 1,25,00,000 | **61.6%** | Healthy |\n\n**Reading it:** Mr Menon has a **high ability to take risk** — long horizon, large surplus, manageable debt, adequate liquidity. Notice, though, that **the flat is ₹85 lakh of a ₹1.25 crore asset base — 68% in one illiquid, undiversified asset.** His financial portfolio should therefore be built to *offset* that concentration, not to duplicate it: he should be underweight real estate and REITs, and there is no case for a second property.\n\n---\n\n## 15.11 ⭐ Psychographic Analysis of the Investor\n\nTwo people with identical finances can need completely different portfolios, because they will *behave* differently. Psychographic models classify **behaviour**, not wealth.\n\n### 15.11.1 The Barnewall two-way model\n\n| Type | Who they are | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Passive investor** | Wealth acquired **passively** — inheritance, or a salaried professional career (doctors, lawyers, corporate executives) | **More risk-averse**; **security is the priority**; will accept lower returns for safety. *The larger group.* |\n| **Active investor** | Wealth **earned by their own risk-taking** — entrepreneurs, business owners | **Higher risk tolerance**; want **control** and involvement; often over-concentrated in what they know |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook.** It is not about how they invest — it is about **how they got the money**. Someone who built a factory is comfortable betting on a factory. Someone who inherited a portfolio is terrified of losing it.\n\n### 15.11.2 ⭐⭐ The Bailard, Biehl and Kaiser (BB&K) five-way model\n\nThe BB&K model plots investors on **two axes**:\n\n- **Level of confidence:** **confident** ←→ **anxious**\n- **Method of action:** **careful** ←→ **impetuous**\n\n| Type | Confidence | Method | Behaviour | How to advise |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Adventurer** | **Confident** | **Impetuous** | Willing to make big, concentrated bets; entrepreneurial; dislikes advice; volatile | **The hardest client.** Insist on written exposure limits |\n| **Celebrity** | **Anxious** | **Impetuous** | Wants to be where the action is; follows fashions and tips; **has no opinions of their own** | **Most receptive to advice** — and most in need of it |\n| **Individualist** | **Confident** | **Careful** | Does their own homework; independent; rational; long-term | **The easiest client to advise** |\n| **Guardian** | **Anxious** | **Careful** | Preserves wealth; avoids risk; often older or newly retired | Emphasise capital protection and steady income |\n| **Straight Arrow** | **Middle** | **Middle** | The average, balanced investor — sits at the centre of both axes | A standard balanced approach |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"A CIGS.\"**\n> **A**dventurer · **C**elebrity · **I**ndividualist · **G**uardian · **S**traight Arrow.\n> And the two extremes: the **Individualist** is the **easiest** to advise; the **Adventurer** is the **hardest**. The **Celebrity** is the one who most *needs* an adviser, because they act boldly on other people's opinions.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Do not confuse **Adventurer** with **Celebrity**. Both act impetuously — but the **Adventurer is confident** and acts on **their own** convictions, while the **Celebrity is anxious** and acts on **someone else's**.\n\n---\n\n## 15.12 ⭐ Life-Cycle Analysis of the Investor\n\nRisk capacity is largely a function of **where the client is in life**, because that determines the **horizon** and the ratio of **human capital** (future earnings) to **financial capital** (accumulated savings).\n\n### The four-phase model\n\n| Phase | Typical age | Financial position | Risk capacity | Portfolio emphasis |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Accumulation** | **25–40** | Low net worth, often **high debt** (home loan), long horizon, **human capital >> financial capital** | **Highest** | **Growth — equity-heavy**; SIPs; term and health cover essential |\n| **Consolidation** | **40–55** | Income comfortably exceeds expenses; net worth growing fast; peak earning years | **Moderate to high** | Balance **growth with preservation**; begin diversifying; maximise contributions |\n| **Spending** | **Retirement onward** | Living **off** the portfolio; no new human capital | **Lower — but not zero** | **Income and preservation**, with enough equity to beat 25 years of inflation |\n| **Gifting** | Overlaps spending | Assets exceed anything the client can spend | Depends on the **beneficiary's** horizon, not the client's | **Estate planning**, trusts, charitable giving, wealth transfer |\n\n### The human-capital insight that explains the whole model\n\nA 28-year-old has a small portfolio and **decades of future salary** ahead. That future salary behaves like a **bond** — a long stream of relatively predictable payments. Since the largest asset on his balance sheet is already bond-like, the *financial* portfolio should tilt heavily to **equity** to balance it.\n\nA 68-year-old has **no human capital left**. Her financial portfolio is now her entire balance sheet, so it must contain its own bond component. **The allocation glides down not because equity became riskier, but because the bond-like asset outside the portfolio disappeared.**\n\n> ⚠️ **The counter-example that is always tested.** Someone whose income is *itself* equity-like — a commission-only salesperson, a business owner, an employee paid largely in ESOPs — has **bond-like human capital replaced by equity-like human capital**, and should hold **less** equity than their age alone suggests.\n\n### Rules of thumb — and why they are only that\n\n- **Equity % = 100 − age.** A 35-year-old holds 65% equity.\n- Longer lifespans have led some to use **110 − age** or **120 − age**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** These are **rules of thumb, not rules.** They ignore goals, wealth, liabilities, income stability and willingness to take risk. A 30-year-old saving for a house deposit due in two years should hold **almost no equity**, whatever \"100 − age\" says. The **goal's horizon**, not the investor's age, drives the allocation for that goal.\n\n### The alternative life-stage framing\n\n| Stage | Dominant financial need |\n|---|---|\n| **Young unmarried** | Build the emergency fund; start SIPs; buy health cover; repay education loans |\n| **Young married** | Term insurance the moment there is a dependant; joint goals; home purchase planning |\n| **Married with young children** | Education corpus; larger term cover; home loan; still equity-heavy |\n| **Married with older children** | Peak expenses and peak income; education goals approaching — begin de-risking those legs |\n| **Pre-retirement (about 5 years out)** | Shift the retirement corpus decisively toward debt; clear all loans; plan health cover for retirement |\n| **Retirement** | Income generation, capital preservation, inflation protection, estate planning |\n\n---\n\n## 15.13 ⭐ Forecasting Risk and Return of Asset Classes (Capital Market Expectations)\n\nChapter 14's optimiser needs three inputs for every asset class: **expected return**, **expected risk (standard deviation)** and **expected correlation**. Producing them is called forming **capital market expectations**.\n\n### The four methods\n\n| Method | How it works | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Historical / statistical** | Take long-run averages of past returns, volatility and correlation | The past need not repeat; the answer depends heavily on the **period chosen**; structural change (India's inflation and rate regime today is not that of 1995) |\n| **Building-block / risk-premium** | Start with the risk-free rate and **add a premium** for each risk borne | Requires judgement on each premium |\n| **Discounted cash flow / fundamental** | Derive the return implied by today's valuations and expected cash flows | Sensitive to growth and terminal-value assumptions |\n| **Survey and judgement** | Ask experienced practitioners and economists | Subjective; prone to herding and to recency |\n\n### 🔑 The building-block method in practice\n\n**For debt — the easy one.**\n> **For a bond portfolio held to maturity, the current yield to maturity (YTM) is the single best estimate of the return.**\n\nExpected return = **nominal risk-free rate + term premium + credit premium + liquidity premium**\n\n*Example:* 91-day T-bill **6.5%** + term premium **0.5%** (for a 10-year G-Sec) + AAA credit spread **0.6%** = **7.6%** expected return on a AAA corporate bond portfolio.\n\n**For equity — the Grinold–Kroner style decomposition.**\n\nExpected return ≈ **dividend yield + expected inflation + real earnings growth + change in valuation (P/E)**\n\n*Example, illustrative:*\n\n| Component | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Dividend yield | **1.2%** |\n| Expected inflation | **5.0%** |\n| Real earnings growth | **6.5%** |\n| Expected change in valuation (P/E already above average) | **−0.5%** |\n| **Expected equity return** | **12.2%** |\n\n**The blended portfolio expectation.** With 60% equity at 12.2% and 40% debt at 7.4%:\nE(R_p) = 0.60 × 12.2 + 0.40 × 7.4 = 7.32 + 2.96 = **10.28%**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook.** Equity return = **what you are paid now (dividend) + what the business grows (earnings) + what the market decides to pay for it (P/E)**. Only the first two are real; the third is sentiment, and over long periods it averages out to roughly nothing.\n\n### ⚠️ The problems with forecasting — directly examinable\n\n| Problem | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Regime change / non-stationarity** | The economic environment shifts; India's structurally lower inflation and interest rates make 1990s data misleading |\n| **Limited data** | Reliable Indian market history is short — the Nifty 50 was launched in **1996** |\n| **Survivorship bias** | Failed companies and closed funds vanish from the data, flattering historical returns |\n| **Data-mining / period selection** | Choosing a start date that supports the conclusion you wanted |\n| **Ex-post vs ex-ante** | What actually happened is not what was expected — a decade of good luck looks like a high expected return |\n| **Asynchronous data** | Prices of illiquid assets (real estate, unlisted holdings) are stale, which **understates their true volatility and correlation** |\n| **Psychological bias** | Overconfidence, anchoring, and **recency** — extrapolating the last three years indefinitely |\n| **Errors compound in the optimiser** | Chapter 14's lesson: mean-variance optimisation is an **\"estimation-error maximiser\"**, and **errors in expected returns do the most damage** |\n\n**The practical response:** forecast at the **asset-class level rather than the security level**, use **long histories tempered by judgement**, impose **constraints** on the optimiser, and **be humble** — present ranges, not point estimates.\n\n---\n\n## 15.14 ⭐ Benchmarking the Client's Portfolio\n\n**A benchmark is the standard against which the portfolio's performance is judged.** Without one, \"12% this year\" is meaningless — it could be brilliant or dreadful.\n\n### 15.14.1 ⭐ The seven properties of a valid benchmark — SAMURAI\n\n| Letter | Property | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S** | **Specified in advance** | Chosen **before** the period begins — never selected afterwards to flatter the result |\n| **A** | **Appropriate** | Consistent with the manager's style — a small-cap fund is not judged against the Nifty 50 |\n| **M** | **Measurable** | Its return can be computed readily and frequently |\n| **U** | **Unambiguous** | Constituents and their weights are clearly identifiable |\n| **R** | **Reflective of current investment opinions** | The manager knows and has views on the securities in it |\n| **A** | **Accountable** | The manager accepts it as the yardstick and is accountable to it |\n| **I** | **Investable** | It could be held passively as an alternative to active management |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"SAMURAI.\"** Seven letters, seven tests. If a proposed benchmark fails even one, it is not a valid benchmark.\n\n### Indian benchmarks by asset class\n\n| Asset class / style | Common benchmark |\n|---|---|\n| Large-cap equity | **Nifty 50 TRI**, **BSE Sensex TRI**, Nifty 100 TRI |\n| Broad / flexi-cap equity | **Nifty 500 TRI**, BSE 500 TRI |\n| Mid cap | **Nifty Midcap 150 TRI** |\n| Small cap | **Nifty Smallcap 250 TRI** |\n| Debt — short duration | **CRISIL Short Term Bond Fund Index** |\n| Debt — medium/long | **CRISIL Composite Bond Fund Index** |\n| Liquid | **CRISIL Liquid Fund Index** |\n| Gilt | **Nifty All Duration G-Sec Index** |\n| Hybrid (aggressive) | **CRISIL Hybrid 35+65 — Aggressive Index** |\n| Gold | **Domestic price of physical gold** |\n\n### ⭐ Two SEBI rules on benchmarks you must know\n\n1. **Total Return Index (TRI).** Since **1 February 2018**, SEBI has required mutual funds to benchmark against the **Total Return variant** of an index, which includes **dividends reinvested**. A price index ignores dividends and therefore understates the index's true return — comparing a fund (which receives dividends) to a price index was systematically flattering.\n2. **The two-tier benchmark structure.** SEBI requires mutual fund schemes to disclose performance against:\n   - **Tier 1 — a broad market index** reflecting the scheme's **category**, uniform across the industry, so that all funds in a category are comparable; and\n   - **Tier 2 — an optional index** reflecting the particular **strategy or style** the fund manager follows.\n\n### Constructing a benchmark for a multi-asset client portfolio\n\nA single index cannot benchmark a portfolio holding several asset classes. The answer is a **customised (blended) benchmark**, weighted by the **strategic asset allocation**.\n\n**Worked example.** Mr Desai's IPS specifies **60% equity, 30% debt, 10% gold**. His blended benchmark is:\n**60% Nifty 500 TRI + 30% CRISIL Composite Bond Fund Index + 10% domestic gold price.**\n\nOver the year: Nifty 500 TRI **+18.0%**, bond index **+7.5%**, gold **+12.0%**. His portfolio returned **15.10%**.\n\n**Benchmark return = (0.60 × 18.0) + (0.30 × 7.5) + (0.10 × 12.0)**\n= 10.80 + 2.25 + 1.20 = **14.25%**\n\n**Outperformance = 15.10 − 14.25 = +0.85 percentage points.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** A client who compares a 60:30:10 portfolio to the **Nifty 50** in a strong equity year will always feel cheated, and in a crash will feel like a genius. **The benchmark must mirror the strategic asset allocation**, otherwise the comparison measures the *allocation decision the client already agreed to*, not the adviser's work.\n\n### Other benchmarking approaches\n\n| Approach | Use |\n|---|---|\n| **Peer-group / managers' universe** | Rank against comparable funds. Suffers from **survivorship bias** and is not investable — it fails the SAMURAI \"I\" test |\n| **Absolute / goal-based** | \"Inflation + 4%\", or \"8% a year\". Simple and client-friendly, but **not investable** and can be unachievable in a bad market |\n| **Liability-relative** | Benchmark to the cost of the liability being funded — the natural choice for a retirement or education goal |\n\n---\n\n## 15.15 ⭐⭐ The Asset Allocation Decision — Strategic versus Tactical\n\n### 15.15.1 Strategic Asset Allocation (SAA)\n\n**The long-term target mix of asset classes, derived from the client's objectives, constraints and long-run capital market expectations.** It is the **policy portfolio** — the portfolio you would hold if you had no view whatsoever about what markets will do next.\n\n- Set for the **long term** (typically five years or more)\n- Reviewed **infrequently** — annually, or when the **client's** circumstances change\n- Expressed as **targets with permitted bands**: \"Equity 60%, permitted range 50–70%\"\n- **This is the decision that explains ~90% of return variability** (section 15.1)\n\n### 15.15.2 Tactical Asset Allocation (TAA)\n\n**Deliberate, temporary deviations from the strategic weights, to exploit a perceived short-term mispricing.**\n\n- Horizon of **months, not years**\n- Must stay **within the bands set in the IPS**\n- It is a form of **market timing** — and it must be **funded from the strategic weights, then reversed**\n\n### ⭐ The comparison table — learn this\n\n| | **Strategic Asset Allocation** | **Tactical Asset Allocation** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Horizon** | **Long term** — 5 years and beyond | **Short term** — months to about a year |\n| **Driven by** | The **client**: goals, risk profile, constraints | The **market**: valuation, momentum, macro views |\n| **Inputs** | **Long-run** expected returns, risks, correlations | **Short-run** forecasts and relative value |\n| **Frequency of change** | Rare — on life events or an annual review | Frequent — as views change |\n| **Purpose** | Deliver the **required return at acceptable risk** | Add **incremental return (alpha)** |\n| **Nature** | **Passive with respect to the market** | **Active** — a market-timing bet |\n| **Risk if wrong** | The client **misses their goals** | **Underperformance, extra costs and taxes** |\n| **Share of return variability** | **The overwhelming majority** | **Small** |\n| **Constrained by** | The IPS itself | The **bands** in the IPS |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Strategic is about the CLIENT; Tactical is about the MARKET.\"**\n> If the reason for a change is something that happened in the client's *life*, it is strategic. If the reason is something that happened in the *market*, it is tactical.\n\n**Worked example.** Mr Desai's SAA is **60% equity (band 50–70%)**, 30% debt, 10% gold, on a corpus of **₹80,00,000**.\n\n- His adviser judges Indian mid-caps to be expensively valued and moves equity from 60% to **52%** — from ₹48,00,000 to **₹41,60,000** — parking **₹6,40,000** in a short-duration debt fund.\n- This is **TAA**: it is temporary, market-driven, **within the 50–70% band**, and it must be reversed once the view plays out or is proved wrong.\n- If instead Mr Desai retires and his adviser reduces equity from 60% to 40% permanently, that is a **change of SAA**, requiring the **IPS to be rewritten**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Any move **outside the IPS band** is not tactical asset allocation — it is a **breach of the IPS**. TAA lives inside the bands by definition.\n\n### 15.15.3 The other asset allocation approaches\n\n| Approach | How it works | Best in | Worst in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Buy and hold** | Set the weights once and never rebalance; the winner's weight drifts upward | **Strongly trending** markets | Risk rises silently over time |\n| **Constant mix (constant weight)** | Rebalance back to fixed target weights — **sell winners, buy losers** | **Oscillating, mean-reverting** markets | Persistent trends |\n| **Constant Proportion Portfolio Insurance (CPPI)** | Equity = **m × (Assets − Floor)** — **buy winners, sell losers** | **Strongly trending** markets | Whipsawing, choppy markets |\n| **Dynamic asset allocation** | Rules-based shifting driven by a valuation signal (P/E, P/B, yield gap) — the engine inside a **Balanced Advantage Fund** | Valuation-driven cycles | Model failure; signal lag |\n| **Integrated asset allocation** | Continuously considers **both** capital market conditions **and** the investor's changing circumstances | Comprehensive | Complex and costly |\n| **Life-cycle / target-date** | An automatic **glide path** reducing equity as the target date nears | Retirement saving | Ignores individual circumstances |\n\n**CPPI worked example.** Floor **₹80,00,000**, multiplier **m = 3**, current assets **₹1,00,00,000**.\n- Cushion = 1,00,00,000 − 80,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**. Equity = 3 × 20,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**.\n- Equity falls 20% to ₹48,00,000, so total assets = 48 + 40 = **₹88,00,000**. New cushion = **₹8,00,000**. New equity target = 3 × 8,00,000 = **₹24,00,000** — so **sell ₹24,00,000 of equity**.\n- As assets approach the floor the cushion approaches zero and equity is reduced to nothing — that is the \"insurance\".\n\n> 🧠 **The clean contrast to remember:** **Constant mix buys the falling asset. CPPI sells it.** Constant mix is contrarian; CPPI is momentum-following. Buy-and-hold sits between them and does nothing.\n\n---\n\n## 15.16 ⭐ Portfolio Construction Principles\n\nHaving fixed the asset allocation, the adviser must populate each class.\n\n### 15.16.1 Selecting the equity portfolio\n\n**Two ways in:**\n\n| | **Top-down** | **Bottom-up** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starting point | **Economy** → sector → stock | The **individual company** |\n| Relies on | Macro forecasts, sector rotation | Company fundamentals, valuation |\n| Typical user | Multi-asset and sector-rotation managers | Value and stock-picking managers |\n\n**The size buckets — SEBI/AMFI definitions, memorise these:**\n\n| Category | Definition by full market capitalisation |\n|---|---|\n| **Large cap** | **1st to 100th** company |\n| **Mid cap** | **101st to 250th** company |\n| **Small cap** | **251st company onwards** |\n\n*AMFI publishes the list every six months, and it binds all mutual funds.*\n\n**Style buckets:** **value**, **growth**, **blend**, **GARP** (growth at a reasonable price), **quality**, **momentum**, **dividend yield**.\n\n**The core-satellite approach — the standard IA recommendation:**\n\n| Sleeve | Typical share | What it holds | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Core** | **70–80%** | Broad index funds / ETFs, large-cap and flexi-cap funds | Low cost, low tracking error, delivers the market return reliably |\n| **Satellite** | **20–30%** | Mid- and small-cap, sectoral, thematic, international, active concentrated funds | Seeks alpha; small enough that a failure is survivable |\n\n**Other equity principles:**\n\n- **Adequate but not excessive diversification.** Most of the diversifiable risk is removed by about **20 to 30 well-chosen, genuinely different stocks**. Beyond that, extra names add cost and monitoring burden without materially cutting risk — and eventually produce \"diworsification\", a closet index fund at active fees.\n- **Cap single-stock and single-sector exposure** (section 15.7).\n- **Check overlap.** Three \"different\" large-cap funds may hold the same top 15 Nifty names — that is one fund with three expense ratios.\n- **Mind liquidity and impact cost** in small caps: exiting a small-cap position in a falling market can cost several percent.\n- **Prefer direct plans** — the expense-ratio saving compounds. An IA, being fee-only, has no reason to recommend otherwise.\n\n### 15.16.2 Selecting the debt portfolio\n\n**Every debt decision reduces to three variables:**\n\n| Variable | Risk taken | How it is expressed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Duration** | **Interest-rate risk** | Modified duration in years |\n| **Credit quality** | **Default / credit risk** | AAA, AA, A, below investment grade |\n| **Liquidity** | **Liquidity risk** | Ease of exit at a fair price |\n\n> 🔑 **The rule an IA should apply:** for a *retail* client, **debt exists to reduce risk, not to chase return.** Take risk in the equity sleeve, where you are paid for it. Reaching for an extra 1.5% yield by buying low-rated credit converts the \"safe\" part of the portfolio into a hidden equity-like bet — as several Indian credit-risk funds demonstrated painfully in 2018–2020.\n\n**The interest-rate arithmetic — memorise the relationship:**\n\n> **Approximate % price change ≈ − Modified Duration × Change in yield (in %)**\n\n*Example:* a debt portfolio of **₹20,00,000** with modified duration **6**. Yields rise **0.50%**.\nPrice change ≈ −6 × 0.50 = **−3.0%** → a loss of **₹60,000**.\nCut duration to **2** and the same rate move costs −2 × 0.50 = **−1.0%** → **₹20,000**. **The duration decision is three times more consequential than the rate move itself.**\n\n**Passive and active debt strategies:**\n\n| Strategy | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Buy and hold** | Hold to maturity; the **YTM at purchase is the return**, if there is no default |\n| **Laddering** | Spread maturities evenly across years — produces **regular natural liquidity** and averages out reinvestment risk |\n| **Bullet** | Concentrate maturities at a **single point** — used to fund a known dated liability |\n| **Barbell** | Hold **short and long** maturities with nothing in the middle — high convexity, needs active management |\n| **Immunisation** | **Match the portfolio's duration to the investment horizon**, so that price risk and reinvestment risk offset each other |\n| **Cash-flow matching (dedication)** | Match bond cash flows **directly** to the timing of liabilities |\n| **Active duration management** | Lengthen duration when rates are expected to fall, shorten when they are expected to rise |\n| **Credit / spread strategies** | Take credit risk when spreads are wide; move up in quality when they are tight |\n| **Roll-down** | Buy a bond on a steep part of the yield curve and profit as it \"rolls down\" to a lower yield with the passage of time |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Rates down, prices up.\"** Bond prices and yields move in **opposite** directions, and **longer duration means a bigger swing**. When you expect rate cuts, go **long** duration; when you expect rate rises, go **short**.\n\n**Matching the fund category to the horizon:**\n\n| Horizon | Category |\n|---|---|\n| Days to weeks | **Overnight, liquid** |\n| 3–6 months | **Ultra-short duration, money market** |\n| 6 months – 1 year | **Low duration** |\n| 1–3 years | **Short duration, corporate bond** |\n| 3 years+ | **Medium/long duration, gilt, dynamic bond** |\n| A fixed future date | **Target maturity fund**, or a **G-Sec held to maturity** |\n\n### 15.16.3 Selecting the hybrid portfolio\n\nA hybrid fund holds more than one asset class **inside a single scheme**, so the rebalancing happens internally.\n\n| SEBI hybrid category | Equity exposure | Suited to |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Conservative hybrid** | **10–25%** equity, rest debt | Retirees needing a small growth kicker |\n| **Balanced hybrid** | **40–60%** equity | Moderate investors *(a fund house may offer either balanced or aggressive hybrid, not both)* |\n| **Aggressive hybrid** | **65–80%** equity, **20–35%** debt | First-time equity investors; automatic rebalancing |\n| **Dynamic asset allocation / Balanced Advantage** | **0–100%**, model-driven | Investors who want valuation-based allocation done for them |\n| **Multi-asset allocation** | At least **three** asset classes, **minimum 10% in each** | One-stop diversification including gold |\n| **Arbitrage** | Minimum **65%** equity, fully hedged | Short-horizon money seeking equity taxation |\n| **Equity savings** | Minimum **65%** equity (largely hedged) and minimum **10%** debt | Lower-volatility equity exposure |\n\n**The two real advantages of a hybrid fund:**\n\n1. **Rebalancing inside the scheme triggers no tax for the investor.** If the client rebalances between a separate equity fund and a debt fund, every switch is a redemption and a taxable event. Inside a hybrid fund, the manager rebalances and the investor's units are untouched.\n2. **It removes behavioural risk.** The client never sees the equity sleeve fall 30% in isolation, and so is far less likely to sell at the bottom.\n\n**The disadvantage:** the client loses control of the allocation, and the equity and debt sleeves may each be mediocre.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap on taxation.** A scheme is taxed as an **equity** scheme if it holds a **minimum of 65% in Indian equities**. This is exactly why aggressive hybrid, arbitrage and equity savings funds are all built around the **65%** line — the tax boundary drives the product design.\n\n### 15.16.4 Other portfolios\n\n| Asset | Vehicles in India | Role and cautions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Gold** | **Gold ETFs**, gold savings funds, **Sovereign Gold Bonds** (8-year tenor, early exit from year 5), digital gold, jewellery | Inflation and currency hedge; **low to negative correlation with equity**; typically **5–10%**. Jewellery is a poor investment — making charges and purity loss |\n| **Real estate** | Physical property, **REITs**, fractional ownership | Large ticket, **illiquid**, high transaction cost, no price transparency. **REITs** are SEBI-regulated, exchange-traded and must distribute the large majority of their net distributable cash flows to unitholders |\n| **Infrastructure** | **InvITs** | Similar structure to REITs, for operating infrastructure assets |\n| **International equity** | Feeder funds, international FoFs, direct investing under the **LRS** | Diversifies country and currency risk; the **LRS limit is USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual; SEBI applies industry-wide caps on mutual fund overseas investment |\n| **Alternatives** | **AIFs** (Category I, II, III), PMS | High minimums (**₹1 crore** for AIFs, **₹50 lakh** for PMS), illiquid, complex — suitable only for large, sophisticated portfolios |\n| **Structured products / derivatives** | Market-linked debentures, options | Generally unsuitable for retail clients; must be explicitly permitted in the IPS if used at all |\n\n---\n\n## 15.17 ⭐⭐ Rebalancing the Portfolio\n\n### What rebalancing is\n\n**Rebalancing is the act of restoring the portfolio to its strategic target weights after market movements have caused them to drift.**\n\nIt is **inherently contrarian**: it forces you to **sell what has gone up and buy what has gone down** — the opposite of what instinct demands, which is precisely why it must be written into the IPS in advance.\n\n### Why drift is dangerous\n\nConsider a **60:40** portfolio left alone through a long bull market. Equity compounds faster, so after five strong years the mix might be **78:22**. The client has not decided to take more risk — but they now have far more of it. **The next crash will hurt in a way the IPS never sanctioned.**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Rebalancing is a risk-control tool, not a return-enhancement tool.\"**\n> If a question asks for the **primary** purpose of rebalancing, the answer is **keeping risk at the level the client agreed to**. Any return benefit is a by-product.\n\n### 15.17.1 The three methods\n\n| Method | Rule | Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Calendar rebalancing** | Rebalance on fixed dates — monthly, quarterly, half-yearly, **annually** | **Simple**, predictable, easy to administer; annual timing can be aligned with tax planning | Ignores what happens **between** dates; may trade when the drift is trivial, and may fail to act during a crash |\n| **Percentage-of-portfolio / threshold (tolerance band)** | Rebalance only when a class drifts **outside its band** — e.g. ±5 percentage points, or ±20% of the target weight | **Responsive to actual risk**; trades only when it matters | Requires **continuous monitoring**; can trigger frequent trades in a volatile market |\n| **Combination (calendar-and-threshold)** | **Check** on a fixed calendar, but **act only if** the drift is outside the band | Captures most of the benefit at much of the lower cost; **the practical standard** | Slightly more complex to explain |\n\n### 🔑 Worked example — rebalancing arithmetic\n\nMr Nair's IPS: **60% equity / 40% debt**, corpus **₹40,00,000**, tolerance band **±5 percentage points**.\n\n**Start of year:** Equity **₹24,00,000**; Debt **₹16,00,000**.\n\n**End of year:** equity returned **+30%**, debt **+7%**.\n\n| | Value | Weight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Equity | 24,00,000 × 1.30 = **₹31,20,000** | 31,20,000 ÷ 48,32,000 = **64.57%** |\n| Debt | 16,00,000 × 1.07 = **₹17,12,000** | **35.43%** |\n| **Total** | **₹48,32,000** | 100% |\n\n**Step 1 — has the band been breached?** Drift = 64.57 − 60.00 = **4.57 percentage points**, which is **inside** the ±5 point band. Under a **pure threshold** rule, **no action is taken**.\n\n**Step 2 — if the rule were calendar (annual), what trade is required?**\n- Target equity = 60% × 48,32,000 = **₹28,99,200**\n- Equity to sell = 31,20,000 − 28,99,200 = **₹2,20,800**\n- New debt = 17,12,000 + 2,20,800 = **₹19,32,800** = exactly **40%** ✓\n\n**Step 3 — the cost.** Suppose the ₹2,20,800 of units sold had a cost of **₹1,60,000**, giving a long-term capital gain of **₹60,800**. This is **below the ₹1,25,000 annual LTCG exemption**, so the tax is **nil** — provided the client has not already used the exemption elsewhere. **This is why an adviser rebalances with the tax calendar in mind, not just the market.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Note the two rules gave **different answers** on the same facts. Read the question carefully: **threshold** rules ask \"is the drift outside the band?\"; **calendar** rules ask \"is it the review date?\".\n\n### 15.17.2 ⭐ Benefits of rebalancing\n\n| Benefit | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Keeps risk at the intended level** | **The primary reason.** Without it, the risky asset's weight — and the portfolio's risk — creeps upward |\n| **Imposes discipline** | It mechanically **sells high and buys low**, removing emotion from the decision |\n| **Enforces the IPS** | It is how the strategic asset allocation is actually maintained, rather than merely written down |\n| **May add a \"rebalancing bonus\"** | In **volatile, mean-reverting** markets, systematically trimming winners and topping up losers earns a small excess return over buy-and-hold |\n| **Creates a natural review point** | The rebalancing date is a good moment to re-examine goals, fund quality and costs |\n| **Removes the need to forecast** | The rule acts; the adviser does not have to predict a top or a bottom |\n\n### 🔑 Proving the rebalancing bonus — and its limits\n\nA ₹10,00,000 portfolio, **50% equity / 50% debt**. Debt returns **0%** throughout (to isolate the effect).\n\n**Scenario A — an oscillating market.** Equity **+40%** in year 1, then **−28.57%** in year 2, ending exactly where it started.\n\n| | **Buy and hold** | **Constant mix (rebalanced annually)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start | Equity 5.00 / Debt 5.00 | Equity 5.00 / Debt 5.00 |\n| End of Yr 1 | Equity 7.00 / Debt 5.00 = **₹12.00 L** | Equity 7.00 / Debt 5.00 = ₹12.00 L → **rebalance to 6.00 / 6.00** |\n| End of Yr 2 | Equity 7.00 × 0.7143 = 5.00 / Debt 5.00 = **₹10.00 L** | Equity 6.00 × 0.7143 = 4.29 / Debt 6.00 = **₹10.29 L** |\n\n**Rebalancing wins by about ₹28,600** — it sold equity at the top and bought it back cheaper.\n\n**Scenario B — a strongly trending market.** Equity **+30%** in year 1 and **+30%** again in year 2.\n\n| | **Buy and hold** | **Constant mix** |\n|---|---|---|\n| End of Yr 1 | Equity 6.50 / Debt 5.00 = **₹11.50 L** | ₹11.50 L → **rebalance to 5.75 / 5.75** |\n| End of Yr 2 | Equity 8.45 / Debt 5.00 = **₹13.45 L** | Equity 7.475 / Debt 5.75 = **₹13.225 L** |\n\n**Buy and hold wins by about ₹22,500** — rebalancing kept selling an asset that never stopped rising.\n\n> 🧠 **The honest conclusion.** **Rebalancing is not a money machine.** It helps in choppy markets and hurts in trending ones. Its dependable benefit is **risk control**, and that is how it should be sold to a client.\n\n### 15.17.3 ⭐ Difficulties and costs of rebalancing\n\n| Difficulty | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Transaction costs** | Brokerage, **STT**, stamp duty, exchange and regulatory charges, GST, **bid-ask spread and impact cost**, and mutual fund **exit loads** |\n| **Taxes** | Every sale is a **taxable event**. Selling equity held under 12 months attracts **20% STCG**; long-term gains above **₹1.25 lakh** attract **12.5%**. Debt funds bought on or after 1 April 2023 are taxed at **slab rates** |\n| **Behavioural resistance** | Clients hate selling their best performer and buying their worst. This is **regret aversion** meeting the **disposition effect**, and it is the hardest practical obstacle |\n| **Underperformance in trending markets** | Demonstrated in Scenario B above — rebalancing systematically trims a persistent winner |\n| **Illiquid and locked assets** | **PPF, EPF, NPS, ELSS (3-year lock-in), Sovereign Gold Bonds, physical real estate, unlisted shares** and endowment policies simply **cannot be rebalanced** on demand |\n| **Indivisibility** | Property cannot be sold in 4.57% slices |\n| **Choosing the band** | Too narrow ⇒ excessive trading and cost; too wide ⇒ risk drifts too far. Bands should be **wider for volatile and illiquid classes** |\n| **Monitoring effort** | Threshold rebalancing requires continuous valuation of the whole portfolio |\n| **Timing the trade** | Selling into a falling market can worsen the price obtained — impact cost is highest exactly when rebalancing is most needed |\n\n### 🔑 How a good adviser reduces the cost of rebalancing\n\n| Technique | Why it works |\n|---|---|\n| **Rebalance with new money** | Direct **fresh SIP instalments and lump sums into the underweight class** — this rebalances with **zero selling, zero tax and zero exit load** |\n| **Rebalance with withdrawals** | For a retiree, **draw the income from the overweight class**, achieving the same effect |\n| **Use dividends and coupons** | Redirect income to the underweight asset rather than reinvesting it in place |\n| **Rebalance inside tax-sheltered accounts first** | Switching between asset classes **within NPS or EPF** triggers no tax event |\n| **Use hybrid / multi-asset funds** | The manager rebalances internally with **no tax consequence for the investor** |\n| **Harvest gains inside the ₹1.25 lakh exemption** | Realise long-term gains up to the exemption each year and reinvest — resetting the cost base at no tax cost |\n| **Widen the bands for volatile and illiquid assets** | Fewer, larger trades cost less than many small ones |\n| **Prefer switches within the same fund house** | Often cheaper and operationally simpler than exit-and-re-enter |\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| The single most important investment decision | **Asset allocation** — not security selection or timing |\n| Brinson, Hood & Beebower (1986) | Asset allocation explained **93.6%** of the **variation in quarterly returns** of 91 US pension plans |\n| Brinson, Singer & Beebower (1991) | Confirmed at approximately **91.5%** |\n| Ibbotson & Kaplan (2000) | **~90%** of variability over time, **~40%** across funds, **~100%** of the return level |\n| What Brinson measured | **Variability of returns**, **not** the amount of return — the classic misreading |\n| Range of correlation | **−1 to +1** |\n| ρ = +1 | **No** diversification benefit |\n| ρ = −1 | Risk can be reduced to **zero** |\n| Equity vs gold correlation | **Low to negative** — gold is a **safe haven** |\n| Correlation in a crisis | **Rises towards +1** — diversification fails when most needed |\n| Two-security risk formula | **σ_p = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ)** |\n| First step in portfolio construction | **Understand the client** — never the market |\n| IPS | The **written document** governing the client–adviser relationship and the portfolio |\n| Greatest value of an IPS | **Discipline in a crisis** — it stops emotional decisions |\n| RRTTLLU | **R**eturn, **R**isk (objectives) · **T**ime horizon, **T**axes, **L**iquidity, **L**egal, **U**nique (constraints) |\n| Two investment objectives | **Return objective** and **risk objective** |\n| Required vs desired return | **Required = must have** (a need); **desired = would like** (a want) |\n| Required return calculation | Spending rate **+ inflation + costs** (additive), or **(1+s)(1+i)(1+c) − 1** exactly |\n| Ability to take risk | **Objective** — wealth, horizon, income stability, liabilities |\n| Willingness to take risk | **Subjective** — psychology and experience |\n| When ability and willingness conflict | **The LOWER of the two governs**; the adviser may **educate but not override** |\n| Five investment constraints | **Liquidity, time horizon, tax, legal & regulatory, unique circumstances** |\n| Emergency fund | **3–6 months' expenses** (6–12 if income is irregular) |\n| Liquidity means | Convertible to cash **quickly at a fair price** — not \"safe\" |\n| Longer horizon ⇒ | **Higher** ability to take risk |\n| Equity LTCG | **12.5%** above **₹1.25 lakh** a year, holding period **> 12 months** |\n| Equity STCG | **20%**, holding period **≤ 12 months** |\n| Debt funds bought on/after 1 Apr 2023 | Taxed at the **investor's slab rate** |\n| Legal vs unique constraint | **Legal is imposed from outside**; **unique is the client's own choice** |\n| LRS limit | **USD 2,50,000** per financial year per resident individual |\n| NPS Active Choice equity cap | **75%**, tapering after age 50; alternatives (**A**) capped at **5%** |\n| Purpose of exposure limits | Control **concentration risk** |\n| SEBI MF single-company equity limit | **10% of NAV** (index and sector funds exempt) |\n| SEBI MF single-issuer debt limit | **10% of NAV**, extendable to **12%** with board and trustee approval |\n| SEBI MF single-sector debt limit | **20% of NAV** |\n| SEBI MF voting-rights limit | **10%** of a company's paid-up capital across all schemes |\n| Exposure limits apply to | The client's **whole balance sheet** — including ESOPs and the family business |\n| ESG | **E**nvironmental, **S**ocial, **G**overnance |\n| Which pillar matters most in India | **Governance** — most Indian investor losses were governance failures |\n| BRSR | SEBI's sustainability report, required of the **top 1,000 listed companies by market cap** |\n| SEBI ESG scheme rule | Minimum **80% of total assets** in securities following the stated ESG strategy |\n| ESG vs ethical investing | ESG treats factors as **financially material**; ethical investing puts **values first** and accepts a possible return sacrifice |\n| Sin stocks | Tobacco, alcohol, gambling, weapons, adult entertainment |\n| Shariah investing | No **riba** (interest); excludes conventional banks and NBFCs; financial screens plus **purification** |\n| Adviser's duty on ethical screens | **Implement it, record it in the IPS, and disclose the cost** (narrower universe, sector bias, tracking error) |\n| Goals must be | **Inflated to their future value** before planning |\n| Order of financial planning | **Emergency fund → health cover → term cover → repay costly debt → invest** |\n| Net worth | **Assets − Liabilities** (a stock, at a point in time) |\n| Cash flow | **Inflows − Outflows** (a flow, over a period) |\n| Basic liquidity ratio | Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses; target **3–6** |\n| Debt-to-income ratio | EMIs ÷ income; below about **40%** |\n| Barnewall model | **Passive** (inherited/salaried, risk-averse) vs **Active** (self-made entrepreneurs, risk-tolerant) |\n| BB&K axes | **Confident ↔ anxious** and **careful ↔ impetuous** |\n| BB&K five types | **Adventurer, Celebrity, Individualist, Guardian, Straight Arrow** |\n| Adventurer | **Confident + impetuous** — big concentrated bets; **hardest to advise** |\n| Celebrity | **Anxious + impetuous** — follows fashions; **most receptive to advice** |\n| Individualist | **Confident + careful** — does own research; **easiest to advise** |\n| Guardian | **Anxious + careful** — preserves capital; often older |\n| Straight Arrow | The **average, balanced** investor at the centre |\n| Four life-cycle phases | **Accumulation → Consolidation → Spending → Gifting** |\n| Accumulation phase | **Highest** risk capacity — long horizon, human capital >> financial capital |\n| Spending phase | Income and preservation, but **still needs equity** to beat inflation |\n| Why equity falls with age | The **bond-like human capital outside the portfolio disappears** |\n| Equity rule of thumb | **100 − age** (also 110 or 120 − age) — a rule of thumb, **never a rule** |\n| Best estimate of a hold-to-maturity bond return | The **current YTM** |\n| Equity return building blocks | **Dividend yield + inflation + real earnings growth ± change in P/E** |\n| Biggest forecasting problems | **Regime change, short data history, survivorship bias, recency, ex-post ≠ ex-ante** |\n| Stale prices on illiquid assets | **Understate** true volatility and correlation |\n| SAMURAI | **S**pecified in advance, **A**ppropriate, **M**easurable, **U**nambiguous, **R**eflective of opinions, **A**ccountable, **I**nvestable |\n| Benchmark basis for mutual funds | **Total Return Index (TRI)** — mandated by SEBI from **1 February 2018** |\n| SEBI two-tier benchmark | **Tier 1** = broad category index; **Tier 2** = optional style/strategy index |\n| Multi-asset client benchmark | A **blended benchmark weighted by the strategic asset allocation** |\n| Why peer-group benchmarks fail SAMURAI | They are **not investable** and suffer **survivorship bias** |\n| Strategic asset allocation | The **long-term policy mix** set by the **client's** goals and constraints |\n| Tactical asset allocation | **Short-term deviations within the IPS bands** driven by **market** views |\n| The clean SAA/TAA test | Life event ⇒ **strategic**; market event ⇒ **tactical** |\n| Moving outside the band is | A **breach of the IPS**, not tactical allocation |\n| Buy and hold | Weights **drift**; best in trending markets |\n| Constant mix | Rebalance to fixed weights — **buys losers**; best in **oscillating** markets |\n| CPPI | Equity = **m × (Assets − Floor)** — **sells losers**; best in **trending** markets |\n| Dynamic asset allocation | Rules/valuation-driven — the engine in a **Balanced Advantage Fund** |\n| Large / mid / small cap | **1–100 / 101–250 / 251 onwards** by full market capitalisation |\n| Core-satellite | **Low-cost broad core (70–80%)** plus **alpha-seeking satellites (20–30%)** |\n| Adequate equity diversification | Roughly **20–30 genuinely different stocks** |\n| Three debt decisions | **Duration, credit quality, liquidity** |\n| Bond price rule | **% price change ≈ − modified duration × change in yield** |\n| Laddering | Maturities spread evenly — natural liquidity, averaged reinvestment risk |\n| Barbell | **Short + long** only, nothing in the middle |\n| Bullet | Maturities concentrated at **one date** |\n| Immunisation | **Match duration to the horizon** so price and reinvestment risk offset |\n| Aggressive hybrid | **65–80%** equity, **20–35%** debt |\n| Conservative hybrid | **10–25%** equity |\n| Multi-asset allocation fund | At least **three** asset classes, **minimum 10% each** |\n| Equity taxation threshold for a scheme | Minimum **65%** in Indian equities |\n| Key hybrid advantage | Internal rebalancing is **not a taxable event for the investor** |\n| Typical gold allocation | **5–10%**; SGB tenor **8 years**, exit permitted from year **5** |\n| AIF / PMS minimums | **₹1 crore** / **₹50 lakh** |\n| Rebalancing | Restoring the portfolio to its **strategic target weights** |\n| Primary purpose of rebalancing | **Risk control** — not higher returns |\n| Rebalancing is inherently | **Contrarian** — sell winners, buy losers |\n| Three rebalancing methods | **Calendar**, **percentage-of-portfolio (threshold/band)**, and a **combination** |\n| Rebalancing bonus arises in | **Volatile, mean-reverting** markets |\n| Rebalancing hurts in | **Strongly trending** markets |\n| Main rebalancing difficulties | **Transaction costs, taxes, behavioural resistance, illiquid/locked assets, trending markets** |\n| The cheapest way to rebalance | **Direct new contributions and withdrawals** to and from the underweight/overweight class |\n| Assets that cannot be rebalanced | **PPF, EPF, NPS, ELSS in lock-in, SGBs, physical property, unlisted shares** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** three question types account for most of the marks in this chapter. **First**, \"which of these is a constraint / which is an objective\" — run **RRTTLLU** and remember that only the two **R**s are objectives. **Second**, \"strategic or tactical\" — ask whether the trigger was a change in the **client's life** (strategic) or a change in the **market** (tactical). **Third**, \"ability versus willingness\" — the answer is almost always that **the lower of the two governs**, and that the adviser **educates rather than overrides**. Get those three reflexes right and the rest of the chapter is straightforward recall.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 15: Portfolio Construction Process — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The IPS constituents, the RRTTLLU framework and the strategic-vs-tactical distinction are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Which of the three investment decisions — asset allocation, security selection, market timing — dominates a portfolio's outcome, and what is the empirical evidence?\n> **A:** **Asset allocation**, by a wide margin. **Brinson, Hood & Beebower (1986)** studied **91 US pension plans over 1974–1983** and found asset allocation policy explained **93.6% of the variation in quarterly returns**; **Brinson, Singer & Beebower (1991)** confirmed it at about **91.5%**.\n\n**Q2.** What did Ibbotson and Kaplan (2000) add, and what is the classic misreading of Brinson?\n> **A:** They separated three questions: asset allocation explains about **90%** of return variability **over time**, about **40%** of the variation **across funds**, and about **100%** of the **level** of long-run return. The misreading is treating \"93.6%\" as **93.6% of the return** — it is **93.6% of the variability (variance)** of returns.\n\n**Q3.** What is the range of the correlation coefficient, how does gold behave, and what happens to correlations in a crisis?\n> **A:** **ρ runs from −1 to +1**: at **+1** there is **no diversification benefit**; at **−1** risk can be driven to **zero**. **Gold's correlation with Indian equity is low to sometimes negative** — a **safe haven** hedging inflation and rupee depreciation (typically **5–10%** of a portfolio). In a crisis (2008, March 2020) **correlations rise towards +1** — **diversification fails exactly when it is most needed**.\n\n**Q4.** A ₹10 lakh portfolio is 60% equity (σ = 18%) and 40% debt (σ = 5%), with ρ = 0.15. What is portfolio risk versus the weighted average?\n> **A:** σ_p = √(0.36×324 + 0.16×25 + 2×0.6×0.4×18×5×0.15) = √(116.64 + 4 + 6.48) = √127.12 = **11.27%**. The weighted average is 0.6×18 + 0.4×5 = **12.80%** — diversification saved **1.53 percentage points of risk for free**.\n\n**Q5.** What is an Investment Policy Statement, and what is the first step of the portfolio construction process?\n> **A:** The IPS is the **written, jointly agreed and signed document** governing the client–adviser relationship — objectives, constraints, guidelines, target allocation, benchmark and review process. The first step is always **understanding the client**; capital market expectations are formed **after** the IPS, never before.\n\n**Q6.** What is the single greatest practical value of an IPS, and name four other benefits.\n> **A:** **Discipline in a crisis** — rules written in calm conditions are what stop a client abandoning the plan in a crash. Others: a **yardstick (benchmark)** for honest evaluation; **continuity** if the adviser changes; **fewer disputes** because both parties signed; and a **compliance record** of risk profiling and suitability.\n\n**Q7.** List the main constituents of an IPS.\n> **A:** Client background; purpose and scope; **duties and responsibilities**; **investment objectives**; **investment constraints**; **investment guidelines and exposure limits**; the **strategic asset allocation with bands**; the **benchmark and evaluation process**; the **rebalancing policy**; the **review procedure**; and appendices (fees, risk profile, holdings).\n\n**Q8.** What does RRTTLLU stand for?\n> **A:** **R**eturn and **R**isk (the two **objectives**), then **T**ime horizon, **T**axes, **L**iquidity, **L**egal & regulatory and **U**nique circumstances (the five **constraints**).\n\n**Q9.** Which events require an IPS to be revised — and which do not?\n> **A:** **Life events** do: marriage, divorce, a birth, a death, job loss, inheritance, illness, or a goal changing. A **market fall does NOT** — that is precisely the situation the IPS was written for.\n\n**Q10.** Distinguish required from desired return, and compute the required return for a retiree with ₹1 crore withdrawing ₹4 lakh a year, with 6% inflation and 1% costs.\n> **A:** The **required return** is what the client **must** earn for essential goals — a **need**; the **desired return** is a **want** that can be abandoned. Spending rate = 4,00,000 ÷ 1,00,00,000 = **4%**. Additive approximation = 4 + 6 + 1 = **11%**; exact = (1.04 × 1.06 × 1.01) − 1 = **11.34%**. **Costs are added to the target, never ignored.**\n\n**Q11.** Distinguish ability to take risk from willingness to take risk.\n> **A:** **Ability (capacity)** is **objective and financial** — wealth relative to goals, time horizon, income stability, liabilities, dependants. **Willingness (tolerance)** is **subjective and psychological** — personality and past experience of loss.\n\n**Q12.** What does an adviser do when ability and willingness conflict?\n> **A:** **The lower of the two governs.** The adviser may **educate** but must **never override**. The two errors are not symmetric: overriding low **willingness** produces a client who panics and sells; overriding low **ability** produces a client who is ruined.\n\n**Q13.** Name the five investment constraints.\n> **A:** **Liquidity**, **time horizon**, **tax**, **legal & regulatory**, and **unique circumstances and preferences**.\n\n**Q14.** What does the liquidity constraint mean, what is the baseline, and which Indian holdings score badly on it?\n> **A:** Convertibility to cash **quickly at a fair price** — not \"safety\". Baseline: an **emergency fund of 3–6 months' expenses** (6–12 if income is irregular). Poor liquidity: **physical property**, **unlisted shares**, **PPF** (15 years), **ELSS** (**3-year lock-in**), **NPS** (until 60), **SGBs** (8-year tenor, exit from year 5).\n\n**Q15.** How does the time horizon affect risk capacity, and what is the trap?\n> **A:** A **longer horizon raises the ability to take risk**, because there is time to recover. The trap: it is the **goal's** horizon, not the investor's **age**, that decides — a 30-year-old buying a flat in two years should hold **almost no equity** for that money.\n\n**Q16.** State the current Indian tax treatment of listed equity and of debt mutual funds bought on or after 1 April 2023.\n> **A:** Listed equity/equity funds: **STCG 20%** if held ≤ 12 months; **LTCG 12.5%** on gains above **₹1,25,000** a year if held > 12 months. Debt funds bought on or after **1 April 2023** are taxed at the investor's **slab rate**, with no long-term benefit.\n\n**Q17.** Give four examples of a legal or regulatory constraint, and distinguish it from a unique preference.\n> **A:** Charitable **trusts** limited to modes permitted by **Section 11(5)** of the Income-tax Act; **PFRDA** capping NPS Active Choice equity at **75%** (tapering after 50) and alternatives at **5%**; **FEMA** barring NRIs from newly opening PPF; **insider-trading rules** on employees of listed firms. A **legal constraint is imposed from outside** regardless of the client's wishes; a **unique preference is the client's own choice** and can be relaxed by them.\n\n**Q18.** What is the purpose of exposure limits, and at which four levels are they set?\n> **A:** To control **concentration risk**. Limits are set on a **single security/issuer**, a **single sector**, a **single entity or group**, and a **single asset class** — and must be written into the IPS **before** a position becomes a favourite.\n\n**Q19.** State SEBI's key exposure limits for mutual funds.\n> **A:** **10% of NAV** in the equity of any one company (index and sector funds exempt); **10% of NAV** in the debt of a single issuer, extendable to **12%** with trustee and board approval; **20% of NAV** to a single sector; and no more than **10% of a company's paid-up voting capital** across all schemes. **G-Secs and T-bills are exempt.**\n\n**Q20.** Why must exposure limits be applied to the client's whole balance sheet?\n> **A:** Because **human capital is exposure too**. An employee holding large ESOPs has their **salary and their savings tied to the same single event** — if the employer fails, both disappear in the same week.\n\n**Q21.** What do the three letters of ESG cover, which pillar has mattered most in India, and what are the six ESG approaches?\n> **A:** **E**nvironmental (emissions, energy, waste), **S**ocial (labour, safety, human rights, data privacy), **G**overnance (board independence, pay, **related-party transactions**, promoter pledging). **Governance** has mattered most — almost every large Indian investor loss was a governance failure. The six approaches: **negative/exclusionary screening, positive/best-in-class screening, ESG integration, thematic investing, impact investing, and active ownership/stewardship**.\n\n**Q22.** What is BRSR, and what is SEBI's minimum-investment rule for an ESG mutual fund scheme?\n> **A:** The **Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report**, which SEBI requires from the **top 1,000 listed companies by market capitalisation**. An ESG scheme must invest a **minimum of 80% of total assets** in securities following its stated ESG strategy, and each ESG scheme of a fund house must follow a **distinct strategy**.\n\n**Q23.** How does ethical investing differ from ESG investing, and what must the adviser do about an ethical screen?\n> **A:** **ESG** treats the factors as **financially material risks**; **ethical investing puts values first** and explicitly **accepts a possible return sacrifice**, usually by **excluding \"sin\" sectors** — tobacco, alcohol, gambling, weapons, adult entertainment. The adviser must **implement it, record it in the IPS under unique needs, and disclose the consequences**: narrower universe, sector bias, higher tracking error.\n\n**Q24.** What does Shariah-compliant investing prohibit?\n> **A:** **Riba (interest)** — so conventional banks, NBFCs and insurers are excluded — plus alcohol, pork, gambling and tobacco businesses. It applies **financial screens** on debt and interest income, and requires **purification** (donating the impermissible portion of income). Indian implementations include the **Nifty50 Shariah** and **BSE 500 Shariah** indices.\n\n**Q25.** Why must every goal be inflated, and what is the correct order of financial planning?\n> **A:** A goal in today's rupees is not what will be needed: ₹15 lakh of education cost in **14 years at 8% education inflation** becomes 15,00,000 × (1.08)¹⁴ = **about ₹44 lakh**. Order: **emergency fund → health cover → term life cover → repay costly debt → then invest**.\n\n**Q26.** Define net worth and cash flow, and give two ratio benchmarks.\n> **A:** **Net worth = Assets − Liabilities** (a **stock**, at a point in time). **Cash flow = Inflows − Outflows** (a **flow**, over a period). **Basic liquidity ratio** = liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses, target **3–6**; **debt-to-income** = EMIs ÷ income, below about **40%**.\n\n**Q27.** Describe the Barnewall two-way psychographic model.\n> **A:** **Passive investors** acquired wealth **passively** — inheritance, or a salaried professional career — and are **more risk-averse, security first**. **Active investors** earned wealth by their **own risk-taking** (entrepreneurs) and are **more risk-tolerant and want control**. It classifies by **how they got the money**.\n\n**Q28.** What are the two axes of the Bailard, Biehl and Kaiser model, and the five types?\n> **A:** Axes: **confident ↔ anxious** (level of confidence) and **careful ↔ impetuous** (method of action). Types: **Adventurer**, **Celebrity**, **Individualist**, **Guardian**, **Straight Arrow**.\n\n**Q29.** In the BB&K model, who is hardest to advise, who is easiest, and who most needs an adviser?\n> **A:** **Hardest — the Adventurer** (confident + impetuous; big concentrated bets; dislikes advice). **Easiest — the Individualist** (confident + careful; does their own research; rational). **Most needs one — the Celebrity** (anxious + impetuous; follows fashions and tips; has no views of their own).\n\n**Q30.** Name the four life-cycle phases and the risk capacity of each.\n> **A:** **Accumulation** (25–40, **highest** capacity, equity-heavy); **Consolidation** (40–55, moderate to high, growth plus preservation); **Spending** (retirement, lower but **not zero** — still needs equity to beat inflation); **Gifting** (estate planning, driven by the **beneficiary's** horizon).\n\n**Q31.** Why does the recommended equity weight fall with age, and what is the exception?\n> **A:** Because **human capital** — a long, bond-like stream of future salary — **disappears**. A young investor already holds a huge bond-like asset outside the portfolio, so the portfolio tilts to equity to balance it. **Exception:** someone whose income is itself **equity-like** (a business owner, a commission earner, an ESOP-heavy employee) should hold **less** equity than \"100 − age\" suggests.\n\n**Q32.** How are asset-class returns forecast, and what are the main forecasting problems?\n> **A:** For **debt**, the **current yield to maturity** is the best estimate of a hold-to-maturity return. For **equity**: **dividend yield + expected inflation + real earnings growth ± the expected change in valuation (P/E)**. Problems: **regime change**, a **short reliable data history** (the Nifty 50 dates from 1996), **survivorship bias**, **recency and overconfidence**, and **stale prices on illiquid assets**, which **understate** true volatility and correlation.\n\n**Q33.** What do the seven letters of SAMURAI stand for, and why does a peer-group benchmark fail it?\n> **A:** A valid benchmark is **S**pecified in advance, **A**ppropriate, **M**easurable, **U**nambiguous, **R**eflective of current investment opinions, **A**ccountable and **I**nvestable. A peer-group benchmark fails the **\"I\"** — you cannot passively hold \"the median fund\" — and suffers **survivorship bias**.\n\n**Q34.** What are SEBI's two key rules on mutual fund benchmarks?\n> **A:** Benchmarking must be against the **Total Return Index (TRI)**, which includes dividends — mandated from **1 February 2018**. And schemes must disclose a **two-tier benchmark**: **Tier 1**, a broad index reflecting the scheme's **category**; **Tier 2**, an optional index reflecting the manager's **style or strategy**.\n\n**Q35.** A client's SAA is 60% equity, 30% debt, 10% gold. Nifty 500 TRI returned 18%, the bond index 7.5% and gold 12%. What is the benchmark return?\n> **A:** (0.60 × 18) + (0.30 × 7.5) + (0.10 × 12) = 10.80 + 2.25 + 1.20 = **14.25%**. A **blended benchmark weighted by the strategic asset allocation** is the only fair yardstick for a multi-asset portfolio.\n\n**Q36.** Distinguish strategic from tactical asset allocation, and give the cleanest test.\n> **A:** **SAA** is the **long-term policy mix** (5 years+) set by the **client's** goals and constraints; it changes rarely and explains most of return variability. **TAA** is a **short-term deviation within the IPS bands** driven by **market** views, and it must be reversed. **The test:** a change in the **client's life** ⇒ **strategic**; a change in the **market** ⇒ **tactical**. Moving **outside the band is neither — it is a breach of the IPS**.\n\n**Q37.** Contrast buy-and-hold, constant mix and CPPI, and compute a CPPI allocation.\n> **A:** **Buy and hold** — never rebalance, weights drift; best in trending markets. **Constant mix** — rebalance to fixed weights, **buying the falling asset**; best in **oscillating** markets. **CPPI** — Equity = **m × (Assets − Floor)**, so it **sells the falling asset**; best in **trending** markets. With floor ₹80 lakh, m = 3 and assets ₹1 crore: cushion ₹20 lakh, **equity = ₹60 lakh**.\n\n**Q38.** State SEBI's cap definitions and explain the core-satellite approach.\n> **A:** By **full market capitalisation**: **large cap = 1st–100th**, **mid cap = 101st–250th**, **small cap = 251st onwards**; **AMFI** publishes the binding list every **six months**. **Core-satellite:** a **core of 70–80%** in low-cost broad index and large/flexi-cap funds delivers the market return reliably, while **satellites of 20–30%** in mid-cap, thematic or international funds chase alpha — small enough that a failure is survivable.\n\n**Q39.** What are the three debt-portfolio decisions, and what does a ₹20 lakh portfolio of modified duration 6 lose if yields rise 0.50%?\n> **A:** **Duration** (interest-rate risk), **credit quality** (default risk) and **liquidity**. Price change ≈ **− modified duration × change in yield** = −6 × 0.50 = **−3.0%** = **₹60,000**. At duration 2 it would be **₹20,000** — the **duration decision matters more than the rate move**.\n\n**Q40.** Distinguish laddering, bullet, barbell and immunisation.\n> **A:** **Ladder** — maturities spread **evenly**, giving regular natural liquidity. **Bullet** — maturities concentrated at **one date**, to fund a known liability. **Barbell** — **short and long only**, nothing in between. **Immunisation** — **match portfolio duration to the horizon** so **price risk and reinvestment risk offset**.\n\n**Q41.** Give the equity limits for the main hybrid categories, and the two real advantages of a hybrid fund.\n> **A:** **Conservative hybrid 10–25%** equity; **aggressive hybrid 65–80%** equity and **20–35%** debt; **multi-asset — at least three asset classes with a minimum 10% each**. Advantages: **internal rebalancing is not a taxable event for the investor**, and it **removes behavioural risk** because the client never sees the equity sleeve fall in isolation. *(A scheme is taxed as equity only if it holds a minimum of **65%** in Indian equities — the tax boundary drives the product design.)*\n\n**Q42.** What is rebalancing, what is its primary purpose, and what are the three methods?\n> **A:** **Restoring the portfolio to its strategic target weights** after drift. Its primary purpose is **risk control**; any return benefit is a **by-product**. It is **inherently contrarian** — sell the winner, buy the loser. Methods: **calendar** (fixed dates — simple, ignores what happens between them); **percentage-of-portfolio / threshold** (act when a class breaches its **tolerance band**); and a **combination** — check on the calendar, act only if outside the band.\n\n**Q43.** A 60:40 portfolio of ₹40 lakh sees equity return +30% and debt +7%. What is the new equity weight, and what trade restores the target?\n> **A:** Equity = 24 × 1.30 = **₹31,20,000**; debt = 16 × 1.07 = **₹17,12,000**; total = **₹48,32,000**; equity weight = **64.57%**. Target equity = 60% × 48,32,000 = ₹28,99,200, so **sell ₹2,20,800 of equity** and buy debt. *(With a ±5 percentage-point band, the 4.57 point drift would NOT trigger a trade.)*\n\n**Q44.** In which markets does rebalancing add value, and in which does it cost value?\n> **A:** It **adds** value — the \"rebalancing bonus\" — in **volatile, mean-reverting (oscillating)** markets, because it sells high and buys low. It **costs** value in **strongly trending** markets, because it keeps trimming an asset that never stops rising. **Rebalancing is not a money machine; its dependable benefit is risk control.**\n\n**Q45.** List the difficulties of rebalancing, and how a good adviser reduces its cost.\n> **A:** **Difficulties:** transaction costs (brokerage, STT, stamp duty, exit loads, impact cost); **taxes**, since every sale is a taxable event; **behavioural resistance**; **underperformance in trending markets**; **illiquid and locked assets** (PPF, EPF, NPS, ELSS, SGBs, property) that cannot be rebalanced; and choosing the right band. **Cheapest fix:** direct **new contributions and SIPs into the underweight class** and take **withdrawals from the overweight class** — no selling, no tax, no exit load. Also rebalance **inside NPS/EPF**, use **hybrid funds**, and **harvest gains within the ₹1.25 lakh LTCG exemption**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 15: Portfolio Construction Process — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The IPS, the RRTTLLU framework and the strategic-vs-tactical distinction recur throughout.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Which decision explains the largest share of a portfolio's return variability?\nA) Security selection  B) Asset allocation  C) Market timing  D) Broker selection\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Asset allocation** dominates; the empirical studies put it around **90%** of return variability over time.\nA) Security selection explains only a small residual. C) Timing is a small and usually negative contributor after costs. D) Broker selection affects cost, not the fundamental risk-return profile.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Brinson, Hood and Beebower (1986) found that asset allocation policy explained approximately:\nA) 46% of the variation in quarterly returns  B) 60% of the variation in quarterly returns  C) 93.6% of the variation in quarterly returns  D) 25% of the variation in quarterly returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Studying **91 large US pension plans over 1974–1983**, they attributed **93.6%** of the variation in quarterly returns to asset allocation policy.\nA), B) and D) all understate the finding, which is famous precisely because the number is so high.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Ibbotson and Kaplan (2000) concluded that asset allocation explains roughly:\nA) 90% over time, 40% across funds, 100% of the return level  B) 100% of everything  C) 40% over time and 90% across funds  D) 50% of all three\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Their paper separated three distinct questions and produced the **90 / 40 / 100** answer.\nB) Overstates it; selection and timing do matter at the margin. C) Reverses the two figures — variability **over time** is the high number. D) Invents a single figure that appears in none of the studies.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The correlation coefficient between two assets must lie between:\nA) 0 and 1  B) −1 and +1  C) −100 and +100  D) There is no limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Correlation is covariance standardised, and is always bounded by **−1 and +1**.\nA) Negative correlation is possible and valuable. C) Confuses correlation with a percentage. D) Covariance is unbounded; correlation is not.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** If two assets have a correlation of exactly +1, the diversification benefit is:\nA) Maximum  B) Nil — portfolio risk equals the weighted average  C) Negative  D) Doubled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At **ρ = +1** the risk formula collapses to a straight weighted average; nothing is saved.\nA) Maximum benefit occurs at ρ = −1. C) Diversification never increases risk above the weighted average. D) Has no meaning here.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** At which correlation can portfolio risk theoretically be reduced to zero?\nA) ρ = 0  B) ρ = +1  C) ρ = −1  D) ρ = +0.5\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Only at **perfect negative correlation** can the two risk contributions exactly cancel, with the right weights.\nA) Zero correlation gives a large but not complete reduction. B) Gives none at all. D) Gives a modest reduction.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Gold is typically included in an Indian portfolio because its correlation with equity is:\nA) Close to +1  B) Low to sometimes negative  C) Exactly zero always  D) Irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gold behaves as a **safe haven**, often rising when equity falls, and hedges inflation and rupee depreciation.\nA) Would make gold pointless as a diversifier. C) No correlation is exactly and permanently zero. D) Correlation is the entire reason gold is held.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** During a severe market crisis, correlations between risky assets tend to:\nA) Fall towards −1  B) Rise towards +1  C) Stay exactly the same  D) Become undefined\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In 2008 and in March 2020 almost everything risky fell together: **diversification fails when it is most needed**.\nA) The opposite of what is observed. C) Correlations are notoriously unstable. D) They remain measurable throughout.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The first step in the portfolio construction process is to:\nA) Forecast market returns  B) Understand the client's needs and circumstances  C) Choose the best-performing fund  D) Set the tactical allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **client comes before the market**. Capital market expectations are formed only after the IPS is written.\nA) Comes later, at step 5. C) Is product selection, near the end of the process. D) Cannot exist before a strategic allocation exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An Investment Policy Statement is best described as:\nA) A market forecast  B) A written document governing the client-adviser relationship and the portfolio  C) A list of stocks to buy  D) A guarantee of returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It sets out objectives, constraints, guidelines, target allocation, benchmark and review procedure, and is **signed by both parties**.\nA) Forecasts change monthly; an IPS does not. C) An IPS never names specific securities to buy. D) No adviser may guarantee returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** The single greatest practical value of an IPS is that it:\nA) Increases returns  B) Provides discipline in a crisis  C) Reduces fees  D) Eliminates risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rules written in calm conditions are what stop a client abandoning the plan in a crash.\nA) An IPS controls behaviour and risk; it does not create return. C) Fees are a separate matter. D) No document removes market risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** In the RRTTLLU framework, which two items are the investment OBJECTIVES?\nA) Time horizon and taxes  B) Return and risk  C) Liquidity and legal  D) Unique circumstances and taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The two **R**s — **return** and **risk** — are the objectives; the remaining five letters are constraints.\nA), C) and D) all list constraints, not objectives.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** How many investment constraints are conventionally listed in an IPS?\nA) Three  B) Four  C) Five  D) Seven\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Liquidity, time horizon, tax, legal & regulatory, and unique circumstances** — **five**.\nA) and B) omit one or more. D) Seven is the total of objectives plus constraints, not constraints alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The liquidity constraint refers to the need to:\nA) Avoid all risky assets  B) Convert holdings into cash quickly at a fair price  C) Maximise returns  D) Reduce taxes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Liquidity is about **speed and price of conversion**, not safety.\nA) Confuses liquidity with low risk — a flat is low-risk but very illiquid. C) and D) are different objectives entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** The conventional emergency fund for a salaried person with stable income is:\nA) 1 month of expenses  B) 3 to 6 months of expenses  C) 2 years of expenses  D) No emergency fund is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **3 to 6 months' expenses**, rising to 6–12 months where income is irregular.\nA) Too thin to survive a job loss. C) Over-provides and drags on long-term returns. D) Would force asset sales at the worst possible moment.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A longer investment time horizon generally means the investor's ability to take risk is:\nA) Lower  B) Higher  C) Unchanged  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A long horizon gives time to recover from falls, so risk capacity **rises**.\nA) Reverses the relationship. C) The horizon is one of the strongest determinants of risk capacity. D) No horizon reduces capacity to zero.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Long-term capital gains on listed equity are currently taxed at:\nA) 10% with no exemption  B) 12.5% on gains above ₹1.25 lakh a year  C) 20% flat  D) Exempt entirely\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **12.5%** under Section 112A, with an annual exemption of **₹1,25,000**.\nA) Was the earlier rate and threshold. C) Is the short-term rate. D) LTCG on equity has been taxable since 2018.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Short-term capital gains on listed equity are taxed at:\nA) 15%  B) 20%  C) 12.5%  D) Slab rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **20%** under Section 111A, for a holding period of **12 months or less**.\nA) Was the earlier rate. C) Is the long-term rate. D) Applies to debt funds bought on or after 1 April 2023, not to listed equity.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Gains on debt mutual funds purchased on or after 1 April 2023 are taxed:\nA) At 12.5% after three years  B) At the investor's slab rate  C) Exempt  D) At 20% with indexation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Such units carry **no long-term benefit**; gains are added to income and taxed at the **slab rate**.\nA) and D) describe the pre-2023 treatment, which no longer applies to these units. C) They have never been exempt.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Which of these is a LEGAL or REGULATORY constraint rather than a personal preference?\nA) A client's dislike of tobacco stocks  B) PFRDA's 75% cap on equity in NPS Active Choice  C) A wish to keep the ancestral house  D) A preference for direct plans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **regulator-imposed cap** applies regardless of what the client wants.\nA), C) and D) are all the client's own choices and can be relaxed by the client — they belong under **unique circumstances and preferences**.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The primary purpose of setting exposure limits is to:\nA) Increase returns  B) Control concentration risk  C) Reduce taxes  D) Satisfy the distributor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Limits cap how much of the portfolio can be destroyed by any single failure.\nA) Limits often reduce return in a bull market — that is the price of safety. C) Limits can actually create tax events when enforced. D) A fee-only adviser has no distributor to satisfy.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** SEBI's limit on a mutual fund scheme's investment in the equity of a single company is:\nA) 5% of NAV  B) 10% of NAV  C) 20% of NAV  D) 25% of NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **10% of NAV**, with index funds and sector/thematic funds exempt.\nA) Is too restrictive to run a concentrated portfolio. C) Is the single-sector debt limit. D) Is the extended single-group limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The SEBI sector exposure limit for a mutual fund's debt investments is broadly:\nA) 10% of NAV  B) 20% of NAV  C) 35% of NAV  D) 50% of NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **20% of NAV** to a single sector, with limited additional headroom for housing finance.\nA) Is the single-issuer limit. C) and D) would defeat the purpose of a concentration cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** In ESG investing, the three letters stand for:\nA) Earnings, Sales, Growth  B) Environmental, Social, Governance  C) Equity, Securities, Gold  D) Efficiency, Stability, Growth\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Environmental, Social and Governance** factors.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions with no standing in the sustainable-investing literature.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** SEBI requires a Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR) from:\nA) All listed companies  B) The top 1,000 listed companies by market capitalisation  C) Only public sector companies  D) Only mutual funds\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **top 1,000 listed entities by market capitalisation** must file a BRSR with their annual report.\nA) Smaller listed companies are outside the requirement. C) It applies regardless of ownership. D) Mutual funds are subject to a stewardship code, not the BRSR.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** An ESG mutual fund scheme in India must invest a minimum of what share of total assets in line with its stated ESG strategy?\nA) 51%  B) 65%  C) 80%  D) 100%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A minimum of **80% of total assets**.\nA) and B) are below the SEBI requirement. D) No scheme is required to be fully invested at all times.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** \"Sin stocks\" typically excluded by ethical investors include:\nA) Banks and IT companies  B) Tobacco, alcohol, gambling and weapons  C) FMCG and pharmaceuticals  D) Public sector undertakings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Plus adult entertainment, and for some investors conventional interest-based lending.\nA) Banks are excluded under **Shariah** screens specifically, not under general sin-stock screens; IT is never excluded. C) and D) are not conventionally screened out.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Shariah-compliant investing prohibits earning:\nA) Dividends  B) Riba, or interest  C) Capital gains  D) Rental income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The prohibition on **riba (interest)** is why conventional banks, NBFCs and insurers are excluded.\nA), C) and D) are all permitted, subject to the business being permissible.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** In the Barnewall model, a \"passive\" investor is one who:\nA) Never trades  B) Acquired wealth passively, through inheritance or a salaried career, and is more risk-averse  C) Only buys index funds  D) Uses a discretionary manager\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The classification is about **how the wealth was obtained**, and passive investors prioritise **security**.\nA) and C) describe an investing method, not the Barnewall category. D) Is a service choice, unrelated to the model.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The two axes of the Bailard, Biehl and Kaiser model are:\nA) Rich and poor  B) Confident-anxious and careful-impetuous  C) Young and old  D) Active and passive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Level of confidence** and **method of action**.\nA) BB&K is about temperament, not wealth. C) That is life-cycle analysis. D) That is the Barnewall model.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In the BB&K model, the \"Adventurer\" is:\nA) Anxious and careful  B) Confident and impetuous  C) Anxious and impetuous  D) In the middle of both axes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Confident and impetuous, making large concentrated bets and resisting advice.\nA) Describes the **Guardian**. C) Describes the **Celebrity**. D) Describes the **Straight Arrow**.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In the BB&K model, the type generally easiest for an adviser to work with is the:\nA) Adventurer  B) Celebrity  C) Individualist  D) Guardian\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Individualist** is confident and careful, does their own research and thinks rationally about the long term.\nA) The Adventurer is the hardest. B) The Celebrity is receptive but has no views of their own. D) The Guardian is workable but very risk-averse.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The four phases of the life-cycle model are:\nA) Saving, spending, borrowing, lending  B) Accumulation, consolidation, spending, gifting  C) Childhood, youth, middle age, old age  D) Growth, income, balanced, liquid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Accumulation → consolidation → spending → gifting**.\nA) and D) are not life-cycle categories. C) Describes ages, not financial phases.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** In the accumulation phase, the investor's risk capacity is generally:\nA) The lowest of all phases  B) The highest of all phases  C) Identical to the spending phase  D) Zero\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A long horizon and large remaining human capital make risk capacity **highest** early in life.\nA) The spending phase has the lowest capacity. C) The two phases are at opposite ends. D) No phase has zero capacity.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** In the SAMURAI benchmark test, the \"S\" stands for:\nA) Simple  B) Specified in advance  C) Standardised  D) Sectoral\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A benchmark must be **specified in advance**, never chosen afterwards to flatter the result.\nA), C) and D) are not among the seven properties.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Which of these does NOT belong in an Investment Policy Statement?\nA) The rebalancing policy  B) A month-by-month forecast of the Nifty  C) Exposure limits  D) The benchmark\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Anything that must be rewritten every month has no place in a document meant to survive market cycles.\nA), C) and D) are all standard IPS constituents.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** The Nifty falls 25% in three months. The IPS should be:\nA) Rewritten immediately to cut equity  B) Left unchanged — this is what it was written for  C) Discarded  D) Rewritten to raise equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An IPS is revised on **life events**, not market events. Rewriting the plan while it is being tested is the most expensive mistake in personal finance.\nA) Would lock in the loss and permanently reduce expected return. C) Abandons the discipline exactly when it is needed. D) Also treats a market move as a reason to change strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** When a client's ability and willingness to take risk conflict, the adviser should generally:\nA) Follow the higher of the two  B) Follow the lower of the two, while educating the client  C) Average the two  D) Ignore both and use age\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **The lower governs.** The adviser may educate but must never override.\nA) Exposes the client to risk they cannot bear or will not tolerate. C) Averaging has no basis in either finance or the regulations. D) Age is only one input to ability.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A 32-year-old with unstable freelance income, no emergency fund and a large home loan insists on a 100% small-cap portfolio. The adviser should:\nA) Comply — the client is the boss  B) Explain that ability to take risk is low and recommend a far lower equity weight  C) Recommend 100% debt with no discussion  D) Refuse to act at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Willingness is high but **ability is low**, and the lower governs. This is where the adviser must be firmest.\nA) Abandons the suitability duty that SEBI imposes on a Registered Investment Adviser. C) Overcorrects and ignores his long horizon. D) The adviser's job is to advise, not to walk away.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** The difference between a required return and a desired return is that the required return is:\nA) Always higher  B) What the client must earn to meet essential goals  C) Set by the regulator  D) The benchmark return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is a **need**; the desired return is a **want** that can be abandoned if unattainable.\nA) The desired return is usually the higher of the two. C) No regulator sets a client's return target. D) The benchmark is a measurement tool, not an objective.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** When calculating a client's required return, adviser fees and fund expenses should be:\nA) Ignored  B) Subtracted from the target  C) Added to the target  D) Treated as a tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Costs are paid out of the gross return, so the portfolio must **earn more** to leave the client with what they need.\nA) Would understate the required return and cause a shortfall. B) Reverses the logic. D) Costs and taxes are separate deductions.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client owns a ₹60 lakh flat that is fully paid for. In terms of the liquidity constraint, this asset is:\nA) Highly liquid because it is valuable  B) Illiquid — it cannot be converted quickly at a fair price  C) Liquid because it can be mortgaged  D) Irrelevant to liquidity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Property takes months to sell and forced sales fetch poor prices. **Value is not liquidity.**\nA) Confuses size with convertibility. C) Borrowing against it creates a liability, not liquidity from the asset itself. D) Large illiquid holdings raise the liquidity requirement elsewhere in the portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A 28-year-old wants to buy a flat in two years and asks for an equity fund. The adviser should point out that:\nA) At 28 he should hold 72% equity  B) The goal's two-year horizon, not his age, determines the allocation  C) Equity is always safe over any period  D) He should borrow instead\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Allocation follows the **goal's horizon**. Two-year money belongs in short-duration debt or arbitrage, whatever \"100 − age\" suggests.\nA) Applies the rule of thumb blindly to a short-dated goal. C) Equity is not safe over two years. D) Recommending leverage to fund a deposit is not advice, it is risk-shifting.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A charitable trust's deed forbids investment in equity shares. This is best classified as:\nA) A unique preference  B) A legal and regulatory constraint  C) A liquidity constraint  D) A risk objective\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is **imposed from outside** and cannot be relaxed by the investment committee's preference, however high the risk capacity.\nA) A preference is the client's own choice and can be waived. C) It says nothing about cash needs. D) It is a restriction on instruments, not a statement of tolerable volatility.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A software engineer holds 40% of her portfolio in her employer's shares. The most serious problem is that:\nA) The stock may underperform  B) Her salary and her savings depend on the same single company  C) ESOPs are illiquid  D) The sector is volatile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Human capital and financial capital are exposed to the same event.** If the employer fails she loses her job and her savings in the same week.\nA) True of any stock and not the distinguishing risk. C) A real but secondary issue. D) Also true but secondary to the correlation with her income.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** Which ESG pillar has historically been most closely linked to large investor losses in India?\nA) Environmental  B) Social  C) Governance  D) All equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Related-party transactions, promoter pledging and aggressive accounting have destroyed far more Indian investor capital than environmental or social failures.\nA) and B) matter increasingly but have not been the dominant cause. D) The three have not contributed equally in the Indian record.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The key difference between ESG investing and ethical investing is that ethical investing:\nA) Is always more profitable  B) Puts values first and explicitly accepts a possible return sacrifice  C) Is required by SEBI  D) Uses only positive screening\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ESG treats the factors as **financially material risks**; ethical investing is driven by **conviction** regardless of the financial effect.\nA) Narrowing the universe cannot mathematically improve the efficient frontier. C) SEBI regulates ESG disclosure but mandates no one's ethics. D) Ethical investing relies mainly on **exclusion**.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A client instructs that no alcohol or tobacco company may be held. The adviser should:\nA) Ignore it as financially irrational  B) Implement it, record it in the IPS and disclose the consequences  C) Implement it silently  D) Refuse the mandate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Record it under **unique needs**, and disclose the narrower universe, likely sector bias and higher tracking error.\nA) The client's values are legitimate and binding on the adviser. C) Failing to explain the cost breaches the duty of disclosure. D) The restriction is entirely workable.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A client who constantly asks about the latest hot sector, has no views of their own, and follows tips from friends is, in BB&K terms:\nA) An Individualist  B) A Guardian  C) A Celebrity  D) A Straight Arrow\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Anxious and impetuous — wanting to be where the action is. Such clients are **most receptive to advice and most in need of it**.\nA) The Individualist forms independent, researched views. B) The Guardian avoids fashionable risk entirely. D) The Straight Arrow is balanced and unremarkable.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A newly retired, anxious client whose overriding aim is not to lose capital is, in BB&K terms:\nA) An Adventurer  B) A Guardian  C) A Celebrity  D) An Individualist\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Anxious and careful** — the Guardian preserves wealth and avoids risk, and is often older or newly retired.\nA) The Adventurer takes concentrated bets. C) The Celebrity chases fashions. D) The Individualist is confident, not anxious.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** The \"100 minus age\" equity rule should be treated as:\nA) A binding regulation  B) A rough rule of thumb that ignores goals, wealth and income stability  C) A SEBI requirement  D) Precisely optimal for everyone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is a starting conversation, not an answer. It ignores the goal horizon, risk tolerance, liabilities and the nature of the client's income.\nA) and C) No regulator prescribes any such formula. D) It cannot be optimal for two clients with identical ages but different circumstances.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** The deepest reason recommended equity weights fall as an investor ages is that:\nA) Equity becomes riskier with time  B) Bond-like human capital outside the portfolio disappears  C) Older people are less intelligent  D) Regulators require it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A young investor already holds a huge bond-like asset — decades of future salary — so the financial portfolio tilts to equity to balance it. That asset vanishes at retirement.\nA) Equity's annualised risk actually falls as the period lengthens. C) Is not a financial argument. D) No such requirement exists for individuals.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A 40-year-old entrepreneur whose entire income depends on her own volatile business should, relative to \"100 minus age\", hold:\nA) More equity  B) Less equity  C) Exactly the same  D) Only equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Her **human capital is itself equity-like**, so her financial portfolio must supply the bond exposure her income does not.\nA) Would double the same risk. C) Ignores the nature of her income entirely. D) Would concentrate risk to a dangerous degree.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** The best single estimate of the return on a bond portfolio held to maturity is:\nA) Last year's return  B) The current yield to maturity  C) The coupon rate  D) The repo rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **YTM** already embeds price, coupons and time to maturity, and is realised if the bonds are held and do not default.\nA) Past returns reflect past rate moves. C) The coupon ignores the price paid. D) The repo rate is a policy rate, not a portfolio yield.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Under the building-block approach, the expected return on equity is approximately:\nA) The risk-free rate alone  B) Dividend yield + inflation + real earnings growth ± change in valuation  C) Last decade's average return  D) The GDP growth rate alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Income, growth and repricing are the three components; only the first two are durable.\nA) Ignores the equity risk premium entirely. C) That is the purely historical method, with all its weaknesses. D) Omits dividends and valuation change.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Stale, infrequent valuations on illiquid assets such as unlisted equity cause their measured volatility and correlation to be:\nA) Overstated  B) Understated  C) Unaffected  D) Negative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Prices that do not move because they are not marked look calm, so risk appears lower than it truly is.\nA) The bias runs the other way. C) The smoothing effect is well documented. D) Understated is not the same as negative.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Survivorship bias in historical fund data means that measured past returns are:\nA) Too low  B) Too high, because failed funds have vanished from the sample  C) Unbiased  D) Irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Closed and merged funds drop out, leaving only the survivors and flattering the average.\nA) The bias is upward. C) It is a well-known systematic distortion. D) It directly corrupts return forecasts built on that data.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A peer-group (\"managers' universe\") benchmark fails which SAMURAI property most clearly?\nA) Measurable  B) Investable  C) Specified in advance  D) Accountable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — You cannot passively buy \"the median fund\", so it is **not investable**. It also suffers survivorship bias.\nA) Peer medians are readily computed. C) A peer group can be named in advance. D) A manager can accept it as a yardstick.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** SEBI required mutual funds to benchmark against the Total Return Index because a price index:\nA) Is too volatile  B) Ignores dividends and therefore understates the index's true return  C) Is not published  D) Includes too many stocks\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Funds receive dividends; a price index does not reflect them, so the old comparison was systematically flattering to funds.\nA) Volatility is essentially the same in both variants. C) Price indices are widely published. D) Constituents are identical in both variants.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Under SEBI's two-tier benchmarking structure, the Tier 1 benchmark is:\nA) A style-specific index chosen by the fund manager  B) A broad market index reflecting the scheme's category  C) A peer-group average  D) The risk-free rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tier 1 is uniform across the category so that all funds in it are comparable; Tier 2 is the optional style index.\nA) Describes **Tier 2**. C) Peer groups are not used for either tier. D) The risk-free rate is not a scheme benchmark.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** For a client holding 60% equity, 30% debt and 10% gold, the appropriate benchmark is:\nA) The Nifty 50 TRI  B) A blended index weighted 60:30:10  C) The bank fixed deposit rate  D) The best-performing fund of the year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Only a **blended benchmark weighted by the strategic asset allocation** measures the adviser's work rather than the allocation the client already agreed to.\nA) Would flatter the portfolio in a crash and condemn it in a boom. C) Bears no relation to the risk taken. D) Is chosen with hindsight and fails \"specified in advance\".\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Strategic asset allocation is primarily driven by:\nA) Current market valuations  B) The client's goals, risk profile and constraints  C) The adviser's short-term views  D) Last year's best-performing asset class\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SAA is the **policy portfolio** you would hold with no market view at all.\nA) and C) drive **tactical** allocation. D) Is performance-chasing, not allocation.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The cleanest test of whether a change in allocation is strategic or tactical is to ask:\nA) How large the change is  B) Whether the trigger was a change in the client's life or in the market  C) Which asset class moved  D) Who initiated it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **life event** makes it strategic and requires the IPS to be rewritten; a **market view** makes it tactical and it must stay inside the bands.\nA) A small permanent change is still strategic. C) The asset class is irrelevant to the classification. D) Either party can raise either kind of change.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An adviser moves a client from a 60% equity target (band 50–70%) to 35% equity because equity looks expensive. This is:\nA) Textbook tactical asset allocation  B) A breach of the IPS, since it falls outside the agreed band  C) A strategic change  D) Rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Tactical allocation lives **inside** the bands by definition; 35% is well outside 50–70%.\nA) TAA cannot breach the band and still be TAA. C) A strategic change requires a change in the client's circumstances and a rewritten IPS. D) Rebalancing moves **towards** the target, not away from it.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Under a constant-mix (constant-weight) strategy, when equity falls sharply the investor:\nA) Sells equity  B) Buys more equity to restore the target weight  C) Does nothing  D) Sells everything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Constant mix is **contrarian** — it buys the falling asset. It performs best in oscillating, mean-reverting markets.\nA) Describes CPPI. C) Describes buy-and-hold. D) Is panic, not a strategy.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Under Constant Proportion Portfolio Insurance, when equity falls the investor:\nA) Buys more equity  B) Sells equity as the cushion shrinks  C) Does nothing  D) Increases the floor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity = **m × (Assets − Floor)**. A falling market shrinks the cushion, so equity is cut — CPPI follows momentum.\nA) Describes constant mix. C) Describes buy-and-hold. D) The floor is fixed in advance; it is not a response variable.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A buy-and-hold strategy performs best relative to constant mix in:\nA) Oscillating, mean-reverting markets  B) Strongly trending markets  C) Flat markets  D) All markets equally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Letting the winner run beats trimming it when a trend persists.\nA) That is where constant mix wins, by selling high and buying low. C) In a flat market the difference is negligible. D) The relative performance depends entirely on the market path.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** In a core-satellite portfolio, the purpose of the core is to:\nA) Generate alpha  B) Deliver the market return reliably at low cost  C) Concentrate on one sector  D) Hold only cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The core (typically **70–80%**) is broad, cheap and low-tracking-error; the satellites chase alpha.\nA) Alpha-seeking is the satellites' job. C) Sector concentration is a satellite activity at most. D) Cash is the emergency fund, not the core.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** An Indian mutual fund scheme is taxed as an equity scheme if it holds at least:\nA) 50% in Indian equities  B) 65% in Indian equities  C) 75% in Indian equities  D) 80% in Indian equities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **65%** threshold explains why aggressive hybrid, arbitrage and equity savings funds are all designed around it.\nA), C) and D) are not the tax boundary — the product design across the industry is built on 65%.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** The main tax advantage of a hybrid fund over separately held equity and debt funds is that:\nA) Hybrid funds pay no tax at all  B) Rebalancing inside the scheme is not a taxable event for the investor  C) Hybrid funds have no expense ratio  D) Hybrid funds are exempt from STT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The manager rebalances internally and the investor's units are untouched; switching between two separate funds would trigger capital gains every time.\nA) The investor still pays tax on redemption. C) Hybrid funds charge a TER like any other scheme. D) STT applies to the scheme's equity trades regardless.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A portfolio is 60% equity (σ = 18%) and 40% debt (σ = 5%), with ρ = 0.15. Portfolio standard deviation is closest to:\nA) 12.80%  B) 11.27%  C) 9.40%  D) 14.05%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — σ_p = √(0.60²×18² + 0.40²×5² + 2×0.60×0.40×18×5×0.15) = √(116.64 + 4.00 + 6.48) = √127.12 = **11.27%**.\nA) Is the plain weighted average (0.6×18 + 0.4×5 = 12.80%), which ignores the diversification benefit. C) Omits the covariance term. D) Exceeds the weighted average, which is impossible when ρ is below +1.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Using the same weights and standard deviations as above but with ρ = 0.60, portfolio standard deviation is closest to:\nA) 11.27%  B) 12.11%  C) 12.80%  D) 10.55%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The covariance term becomes 2×0.60×0.40×18×5×0.60 = **25.92**. σ_p = √(116.64 + 4.00 + 25.92) = √146.56 = **12.11%**.\nA) Is the answer at ρ = 0.15. C) Is the weighted average, reached only at ρ = +1. D) Is below the ρ = 0.15 answer, but higher correlation must raise risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A retiree holds ₹1,00,00,000, withdraws ₹4,00,000 a year, and faces 6% inflation and 1% total costs. The exact required nominal return is closest to:\nA) 10.00%  B) 11.00%  C) 11.34%  D) 13.10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Spending rate = 4,00,000 ÷ 1,00,00,000 = 4%. Exact = (1.04 × 1.06 × 1.01) − 1 = 1.113424 − 1 = **11.34%**.\nA) Omits the cost component. B) Is the **additive approximation** (4 + 6 + 1), which the question explicitly asks to refine. D) Compounds the figures incorrectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A goal costs ₹15,00,000 in today's money and is 14 years away. At 8% education inflation, the amount required is closest to:\nA) ₹15,00,000  B) ₹31,80,000  C) ₹44,06,000  D) ₹61,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 15,00,000 × (1.08)¹⁴ = 15,00,000 × 2.9372 = **₹44,05,785**, about ₹44.06 lakh.\nA) Ignores inflation entirely — the classic planning error. B) Uses roughly half the correct compounding period. D) Over-compounds well beyond 14 years at 8%.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A client with a portfolio expected to return 11% says a loss above 8% in any year is unacceptable. Treating that as a two-standard-deviation event, the maximum tolerable standard deviation is:\nA) 4.0%  B) 8.0%  C) 9.5%  D) 19.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 11% − 2σ = −8% → 2σ = 19% → **σ = 9.5%**.\nA) Halves the answer again. B) Simply repeats the loss limit. D) Is 2σ, not σ — forgetting to divide by two.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A client holds liquid assets ₹4,00,000, mutual funds ₹22,00,000, EPF ₹14,00,000 and a flat worth ₹85,00,000, against a home loan of ₹48,00,000. Net worth is:\nA) ₹1,25,00,000  B) ₹77,00,000  C) ₹40,00,000  D) ₹1,73,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets = 4 + 22 + 14 + 85 = **₹1,25,00,000**. Net worth = 1,25,00,000 − 48,00,000 = **₹77,00,000**.\nA) Is total assets, before deducting the loan. C) Excludes the flat from assets. D) Adds the loan instead of subtracting it.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** The same client has liquid assets of ₹4,00,000 and monthly expenses of ₹1,05,000. His basic liquidity ratio is closest to:\nA) 1.9 months  B) 3.8 months  C) 6.0 months  D) 26.3 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 4,00,000 ÷ 1,05,000 = **3.81 months** — adequate, but worth pushing towards six.\nA) Halves the result. C) Would require about ₹6,30,000 of liquid assets. D) Divides in the wrong direction.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Monthly income ₹1,80,000 and monthly expenses ₹1,05,000. The savings ratio is closest to:\nA) 30.6%  B) 41.7%  C) 58.3%  D) 71.4%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Surplus = 1,80,000 − 1,05,000 = ₹75,000. Ratio = 75,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **41.7%**.\nA) Is the debt-to-income ratio on these figures. C) Is the expense ratio (1,05,000 ÷ 1,80,000). D) Does not correspond to any of the stated figures.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** With EMIs of ₹55,000 against a monthly income of ₹1,80,000, the debt-to-income ratio is closest to:\nA) 30.6%  B) 41.7%  C) 52.4%  D) 22.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — 55,000 ÷ 1,80,000 = **30.6%**, comfortably below the conventional 40% ceiling.\nB) Is the savings ratio. C) Divides the EMI by the surplus rather than by income. D) Understates the burden.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A ₹50,00,000 portfolio has a 10% single-stock cap. The client holds ₹18,00,000 of employer stock. The excess over the limit is:\nA) ₹5,00,000  B) ₹13,00,000  C) ₹18,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cap = 10% × 50,00,000 = **₹5,00,000**. Excess = 18,00,000 − 5,00,000 = **₹13,00,000**.\nA) Is the permitted holding, not the excess. C) Would require the entire position to go, ignoring the permitted ₹5 lakh. D) The holding is 36% of the portfolio, far above the cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** The same ₹50,00,000 portfolio has a 25% single-sector cap. Total financial-sector exposure is ₹22,00,000. The excess is:\nA) ₹9,50,000  B) ₹12,50,000  C) ₹22,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cap = 25% × 50,00,000 = **₹12,50,000**. Exposure is ₹22,00,000, so the excess is 22,00,000 − 12,50,000 = **₹9,50,000**.\nB) Is the permitted amount, not the excess. C) Is the full exposure. D) At 44% of the portfolio the sector cap is clearly breached.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Dividend yield 1.2%, expected inflation 5.0%, real earnings growth 6.5%, expected valuation change −0.5%. Expected equity return is:\nA) 11.2%  B) 12.2%  C) 13.2%  D) 7.7%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 1.2 + 5.0 + 6.5 − 0.5 = **12.2%**.\nA) Subtracts the dividend yield instead of adding it. C) Adds the 0.5% valuation change rather than subtracting it. D) Omits real earnings growth.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A portfolio is 60% equity with an expected return of 12.2% and 40% debt at 7.4%. Its expected return is:\nA) 9.80%  B) 10.28%  C) 11.00%  D) 12.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (0.60 × 12.2) + (0.40 × 7.4) = 7.32 + 2.96 = **10.28%**.\nA) Is the simple average of the two returns, ignoring the weights. C) Does not follow from the stated weights. D) Is the equity return alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A benchmark is 60% Nifty 500 TRI, 30% a bond index and 10% gold. Over the year they returned 18.0%, 7.5% and 12.0%. The benchmark return is:\nA) 12.50%  B) 14.25%  C) 15.10%  D) 18.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (0.60 × 18.0) + (0.30 × 7.5) + (0.10 × 12.0) = 10.80 + 2.25 + 1.20 = **14.25%**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of the three index returns. C) Would be a portfolio result, not the benchmark. D) Is the equity index alone, the wrong yardstick for a multi-asset portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A ₹40,00,000 portfolio starts at 60% equity and 40% debt. Equity returns 30% and debt 7%. The new equity weight is closest to:\nA) 60.00%  B) 62.30%  C) 64.57%  D) 68.10%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Equity = 24,00,000 × 1.30 = ₹31,20,000; debt = 16,00,000 × 1.07 = ₹17,12,000; total = ₹48,32,000. Weight = 31,20,000 ÷ 48,32,000 = **64.57%**.\nA) Ignores the drift entirely. B) Understates the drift. D) Overstates it — debt also grew, which limits the shift.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Continuing from the previous question, restoring the 60:40 target requires selling equity of:\nA) ₹1,80,000  B) ₹2,20,800  C) ₹3,12,000  D) ₹4,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Target equity = 60% × 48,32,000 = ₹28,99,200. Sale = 31,20,000 − 28,99,200 = **₹2,20,800**. Debt then becomes 17,12,000 + 2,20,800 = ₹19,32,800, exactly 40%.\nA) Understates the required trade. C) Is 10% of the equity sleeve, computed against the wrong base. D) Would push equity below the target.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** If that same portfolio were governed by a ±5 percentage-point tolerance band rather than an annual calendar rule, the adviser would:\nA) Sell ₹2,20,800 of equity  B) Take no action, since the 4.57 point drift is inside the band  C) Sell the whole equity sleeve  D) Buy more equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Drift = 64.57 − 60.00 = **4.57 percentage points**, which is **inside** the ±5 point band, so a threshold rule triggers nothing.\nA) Is the calendar-rule answer — the same facts give different answers under different rules. C) No rebalancing rule liquidates an entire sleeve. D) Equity is overweight, not underweight.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An IPS sets a 60% equity target with a relative tolerance band of ±20%. The permitted equity range is:\nA) 55% to 65%  B) 48% to 72%  C) 40% to 80%  D) 58% to 62%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **relative** band of 20% on a 60% target is 60 × 0.20 = **12 percentage points**, giving **48% to 72%**.\nA) Applies a ±5 point absolute band. C) Applies a ±20 percentage-point absolute band, not a relative one. D) Applies a ±2 point band.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** A ₹20,00,000 debt portfolio has a modified duration of 6. Yields rise by 0.50%. The approximate loss is:\nA) ₹20,000  B) ₹60,000  C) ₹1,20,000  D) ₹6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Price change ≈ −6 × 0.50% = **−3.0%**; 3.0% × 20,00,000 = **₹60,000**.\nA) Uses a duration of 2. C) Doubles the rate move. D) Applies the duration as a percentage directly.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Had that portfolio's duration been cut to 2 before the same 0.50% rate rise, the loss would have been:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹20,000  C) ₹40,000  D) ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — −2 × 0.50% = −1.0%; 1.0% × 20,00,000 = **₹20,000** — a third of the loss at duration 6.\nA) Uses a duration of 1. C) Uses a duration of 4. D) Is the duration-6 answer.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** Under CPPI with a floor of ₹80,00,000, a multiplier of 3 and assets of ₹1,00,00,000, the equity allocation is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹60,00,000  C) ₹80,00,000  D) ₹1,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cushion = 1,00,00,000 − 80,00,000 = ₹20,00,000. Equity = 3 × 20,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**.\nA) Is the cushion itself, before applying the multiplier. C) Is the floor. D) Would leave no protection at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** Continuing the CPPI example, equity then falls 20% to ₹48,00,000 while the debt sleeve stays at ₹40,00,000. The new equity target is:\nA) ₹48,00,000  B) ₹36,00,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹60,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Assets = 48,00,000 + 40,00,000 = ₹88,00,000. Cushion = 88,00,000 − 80,00,000 = ₹8,00,000. Equity = 3 × 8,00,000 = **₹24,00,000**, so ₹24,00,000 of equity is sold.\nA) Would mean doing nothing, which is buy-and-hold. B) Does not follow from the cushion arithmetic. D) Is the original allocation — CPPI cuts equity as the cushion shrinks.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A ₹10,00,000 portfolio is 50% equity, 50% debt; debt returns 0%. Equity rises 40% then falls 28.57%, ending where it began. Rebalanced annually, the final value is closest to:\nA) ₹10,00,000  B) ₹10,29,000  C) ₹9,70,000  D) ₹12,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Year 1: equity 5.00 → 7.00, total ₹12.00 lakh, rebalanced to 6.00/6.00. Year 2: equity 6.00 × 0.7143 = 4.29, debt 6.00, total = **₹10.29 lakh**. The rebalancing bonus is about ₹28,600.\nA) Is the buy-and-hold result — equity returns to 5.00 and debt stays at 5.00. C) Rebalancing does not destroy value on this path. D) Is the year-1 peak, before the fall.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** Same portfolio, but equity now rises 30% in each of two years and debt returns 0%. Buy-and-hold ends at:\nA) ₹13.23 lakh  B) ₹13.45 lakh  C) ₹11.50 lakh  D) ₹16.90 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity 5.00 × 1.30 × 1.30 = ₹8.45 lakh; debt stays at ₹5.00 lakh; total = **₹13.45 lakh**. Annual rebalancing would have ended at ₹13.225 lakh — **₹22,500 worse**, because it kept trimming a persistent winner.\nA) Is the annually rebalanced result. C) Is the year-1 total. D) Applies the 30% growth to the entire portfolio, not just the equity half.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A rebalancing trade realises a long-term capital gain of ₹60,800 on equity, and the client has used none of the annual exemption. The tax payable is:\nA) ₹7,600  B) ₹12,160  C) Nil  D) ₹6,080\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The gain is **below the ₹1,25,000 annual LTCG exemption**, so nothing is payable. This is why advisers rebalance with the tax calendar in mind.\nA) Applies 12.5% to the whole gain, ignoring the exemption. B) Applies the short-term rate and ignores the exemption. D) Applies 10%, the pre-2024 rate, and ignores the exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A larger rebalancing realises a long-term equity gain of ₹3,25,000, with no exemption yet used. The tax is:\nA) ₹40,625  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹65,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Taxable gain = 3,25,000 − 1,25,000 = ₹2,00,000. Tax = 12.5% × 2,00,000 = **₹25,000**.\nA) Applies 12.5% to the whole gain without the exemption. C) Applies the 20% short-term rate to the whole gain, missing both the correct rate and the exemption. D) The gain far exceeds the exemption.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** If instead the same ₹2,00,000 of gain had been short-term, the tax would be:\nA) ₹25,000  B) ₹40,000  C) ₹30,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Short-term equity gains are taxed at **20%** with **no exemption**: 20% × 2,00,000 = **₹40,000**.\nA) Applies the long-term rate. C) Applies a 15% rate that no longer exists for listed equity. D) There is no exemption for short-term gains.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A strategic allocation of 55% equity, 35% debt and 10% gold on a corpus of ₹1,20,00,000 translates to:\nA) ₹60L equity, ₹48L debt, ₹12L gold  B) ₹66L equity, ₹42L debt, ₹12L gold  C) ₹66L equity, ₹40L debt, ₹14L gold  D) ₹55L equity, ₹35L debt, ₹10L gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 55% × 1.20 crore = **₹66,00,000**; 35% = **₹42,00,000**; 10% = **₹12,00,000**.\nA) Uses 50:40:10 weights. C) Misallocates between debt and gold. D) Treats the percentages as lakh figures rather than applying them to the corpus.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** With an SAA of 60% equity (band 50–70%) on ₹80,00,000, an adviser tactically cuts equity to 52%. The amount shifted out of equity is:\nA) ₹3,20,000  B) ₹6,40,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Equity moves from 60% × 80,00,000 = ₹48,00,000 to 52% × 80,00,000 = ₹41,60,000, a shift of **₹6,40,000**. The move stays inside the 50–70% band, so it is legitimate tactical allocation.\nA) Corresponds to a 4 percentage-point move. C) Corresponds to a 10 point move, to 50%. D) Would breach the band entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises this chapter?\nA) Stock picking is what determines a client's outcome  B) The written IPS fixes objectives and constraints, the strategic asset allocation drives the great majority of return variability, tactical moves live inside the agreed bands, and disciplined rebalancing keeps risk where the client agreed it should be  C) Rebalancing always increases returns  D) A single equity index is the right benchmark for every portfolio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It links the four load-bearing ideas: the **IPS**, the dominance of **strategic allocation**, the **bounded** nature of tactical moves, and **rebalancing as risk control**.\nA) Contradicts the Brinson and Ibbotson evidence. C) Rebalancing helps in oscillating markets and hurts in trending ones — its dependable benefit is risk control. D) A multi-asset portfolio needs a blended benchmark weighted by its strategic allocation.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Work the rupee figures out fully — the exam rewards arithmetic, not estimation.\n\n### Case: Dr Anjali Deshmukh's Investment Policy Statement\n\n**Dr Anjali Deshmukh**, aged 46, is a consultant radiologist in Pune and a partner in a diagnostics chain.\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Investable corpus (advised)** | **₹1,20,00,000** |\n| **Annual investable surplus** | **₹18,00,000** |\n| **Emergency fund (held separately)** | **₹6,00,000** |\n| **Unlisted shares in her own diagnostics chain** | **₹30,00,000**, included in the corpus above |\n| **Agreed strategic asset allocation** | **55% equity, 35% debt, 10% gold** |\n| **Tolerance bands** | **±5 percentage points** on each class |\n| **Single-security exposure limit in the IPS** | **10% of the corpus** |\n| **Unique preference** | No tobacco or alcohol companies |\n| **Risk profile** | Ability **high**; willingness **moderate** |\n\nAt the end of year one, the market indices returned: **Nifty 500 TRI +32%**, the **CRISIL Composite Bond Fund Index +7%**, and **gold +5%**. Dr Deshmukh's actual sleeve values at year-end were: **equity ₹88,00,000**, **debt ₹45,00,000**, **gold ₹13,40,000**.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What are the target rupee amounts under her strategic asset allocation at outset?\nA) ₹60L equity, ₹48L debt, ₹12L gold  B) ₹66L equity, ₹42L debt, ₹12L gold  C) ₹66L equity, ₹36L debt, ₹18L gold  D) ₹55L equity, ₹35L debt, ₹10L gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 55% × ₹1,20,00,000 = **₹66,00,000** equity; 35% × ₹1,20,00,000 = **₹42,00,000** debt; 10% × ₹1,20,00,000 = **₹12,00,000** gold. The three sum to ₹1,20,00,000.\nA) Uses a 50:40:10 split. C) Misallocates ₹6 lakh from debt into gold. D) Treats the percentages as rupee-lakh amounts instead of applying them to the corpus.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Her ₹30,00,000 unlisted stake breaches the 10% single-security limit by how much, and what is the deeper problem?\nA) By ₹18,00,000; her income comes from the same business  B) By ₹30,00,000; unlisted shares are always prohibited  C) By ₹12,00,000; the stake is simply too large  D) There is no breach\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The cap is 10% × ₹1,20,00,000 = **₹12,00,000**, so the excess is 30,00,000 − 12,00,000 = **₹18,00,000**. The deeper problem is that her **professional income and her largest holding depend on the same business** — human capital and financial capital are exposed to a single event. The stake is also **illiquid**, so it cannot be trimmed quickly and must be unwound over years.\nB) Unlisted shares are not prohibited; the IPS simply caps them. C) ₹12,00,000 is the permitted amount, not the excess. D) At 25% of the corpus the limit is clearly breached.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** What was the blended benchmark return for the year?\nA) 14.67%  B) 20.55%  C) 22.00%  D) 32.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (0.55 × 32) + (0.35 × 7) + (0.10 × 5) = 17.60 + 2.45 + 0.50 = **20.55%**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of the three index returns. C) Is her portfolio's own return, not the benchmark. D) Is the equity index alone, which would be the wrong yardstick for a 55:35:10 portfolio.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Given her year-end sleeve values, is the equity band breached, and what trade restores the target?\nA) No breach; no action needed  B) Breached at 60.11%; sell ₹7,48,000 of equity  C) Breached at 60.11%; sell ₹13,00,000 of equity  D) Breached at 62.50%; sell ₹7,48,000 of equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total = 88,00,000 + 45,00,000 + 13,40,000 = **₹1,46,40,000**. Equity weight = 88,00,000 ÷ 1,46,40,000 = **60.11%**, a drift of **5.11 percentage points** — just **outside** the ±5 point band. Target equity = 55% × 1,46,40,000 = **₹80,52,000**, so **sell ₹7,48,000**. That is redeployed as ₹6,24,000 to debt (to reach ₹51,24,000) and ₹1,24,000 to gold (to reach ₹14,64,000).\nA) The drift is 5.11 points, which exceeds the ±5 point band. C) Sells far more than the target requires — ₹13,00,000 would push equity below 55%. D) Miscalculates the weight: 88,00,000 ÷ 1,46,40,000 is 60.11%, not 62.50%.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Her portfolio returned 22.0% against a 20.55% benchmark. If the ₹7,48,000 sale would realise a long-term gain of ₹2,48,000 with no exemption used, what should the adviser recommend?\nA) Sell and pay the ₹15,375 of tax, since there is no alternative  B) Direct her ₹18,00,000 annual surplus into debt and gold instead, rebalancing with no sale, no tax and no exit load  C) Skip rebalancing entirely, since she outperformed  D) Sell the unlisted stake first to fund the rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rebalancing with **new money** is the cheapest method available. Her ₹18,00,000 annual surplus comfortably covers the ₹7,48,000 needed on the underweight side, so the target weights are restored with **no selling, no capital-gains tax and no exit load**. *(For reference, selling would cost 12.5% × (2,48,000 − 1,25,000) = 12.5% × ₹1,23,000 = **₹15,375**, plus any exit load and impact cost.)*\n\n**The wider lesson:** her **+1.45 percentage points** of outperformance is small relative to the decisions that really mattered this year — holding 55% equity in a 32% equity market, and controlling the ₹18,00,000 concentration excess. **Allocation and concentration dominate; selection is the residual.**\n\nA) There is a cheaper alternative, and an adviser who ignores it is leaving money on the table. C) A good year is exactly when equity drifts overweight and risk rises above the agreed level. D) The unlisted stake is illiquid and must be unwound gradually over several tax years, not used as a source of ready cash.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "16", "title": "Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation", "module": "Module 5: Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring & Evaluation", "notes": "# Chapter 16: Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 5 · Portfolio Construction, Performance Monitoring and Evaluation (20 marks).**\n> The single most **formula-dense and numerical** chapter in the syllabus. Almost every mark comes from being able to (a) pick the *right* return measure, (b) pick the *right* risk measure, and (c) divide one by the other correctly. There are roughly a dozen formulas here. Learn each one with its **worked example and its \"when do I use this?\" rule** — the exam tests the *choice* of measure at least as often as the arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n## 16.1 Parameters to Define Performance — Risk AND Return\n\n### 🔑 The one idea that governs the whole chapter\n\n> **A return figure on its own is meaningless. Performance is a two-dimensional quantity: return earned, and risk taken to earn it.**\n\nTwo funds both returned **18%** last year. Fund A did it holding 30 large-cap stocks; Fund B did it holding 4 micro-caps on borrowed money. They did **not** perform equally. The only defensible comparison divides return by risk — which is exactly what the Sharpe, Treynor, Sortino, Information and M² ratios do.\n\nA complete performance evaluation answers **four** questions:\n\n| # | Question | Tool |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **How much** did the portfolio earn? | HPR, TWRR, CAGR, portfolio return |\n| 2 | **How much risk** was taken? | Standard deviation, beta, downside deviation, tracking error |\n| 3 | Was the return **worth** the risk? | Sharpe, Treynor, Sortino, Information Ratio, M², alpha |\n| 4 | **Where** did the return come from? | Benchmarking, peer analysis, attribution |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** \"Which fund performed better?\" is *never* answered by the higher raw return alone in this chapter. If the question gives you a standard deviation or a beta, it wants a **risk-adjusted** answer.\n\nThree further principles run through the chapter:\n\n- **Compare like with like.** A mid-cap fund is measured against a mid-cap index, not the NIFTY 50.\n- **Measure over a full market cycle.** One year of returns tells you almost nothing; **3, 5 and 10-year** figures and **rolling returns** tell you a lot.\n- **Measure what the manager controls.** Client cash flows are not the manager's decision — which is the entire reason TWRR exists.\n\n---\n\n## 16.2 Rate of Return Measures\n\n### 16.2.1 ⭐ Holding Period Return (HPR)\n\nThe simplest and most fundamental measure — the **total return over however long you held the asset**, with no annualising.\n\n> **HPR = (Ending value − Beginning value + Income received) ÷ Beginning value**\n\nIncome means dividends, interest or coupons received during the period. Never leave it out.\n\n**Worked example.** Mrs Iyer buys **1,000 shares at ₹250** each = **₹2,50,000**. Nine months later she sells at **₹278** and has received a dividend of **₹6 per share**.\n\n- Ending value = 1,000 × ₹278 = **₹2,78,000**\n- Income = 1,000 × ₹6 = **₹6,000**\n- HPR = (2,78,000 − 2,50,000 + 6,000) ÷ 2,50,000 = 34,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = **13.6%**\n\nThat 13.6% is for **nine months**, not a year. To make it comparable to a one-year figure:\n\n- Annualised = (1.136)^(12/9) − 1 = (1.136)^1.3333 − 1 = **18.53%**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — HPR is \"everything that came back, over what went in.\"** Price change **plus** income, all divided by the opening value.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** HPR is a **holding-period** number. Never call a 9-month 13.6% an \"annual return of 13.6%\", and never present a 3-month figure annualised in a client document — SEBI requires mutual fund returns for periods of **less than one year to be shown in absolute terms**, and for **one year and above as CAGR**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.2 ⭐⭐ TWRR versus MWRR — the most important distinction in the chapter\n\nBoth measure the return of the *same* portfolio over the *same* period. They give different answers whenever **money went in or out** during the period.\n\n| | **Time-Weighted Rate of Return (TWRR)** | **Money-Weighted Rate of Return (MWRR)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What it is** | The **geometric chaining** of the returns of each sub-period between cash flows | The **IRR** of all the cash flows — the discount rate that equates inflows and outflows |\n| **Effect of cash flows** | **Neutralised** — each sub-period gets equal weight regardless of how much money was in it | **Fully captured** — periods with more money in them dominate the answer |\n| **What it measures** | **The manager's skill** | **The investor's actual experience** |\n| **Who should use it** | Managers, regulators, ratings agencies, anyone **comparing** managers | An individual client asking \"what did *my* money earn?\" |\n| **Indian usage** | **Mandatory for PMS performance disclosure (SEBI)**; the basis of published mutual fund returns | The number in a client's own SIP/XIRR statement |\n\n### 🔑 WHY TWRR measures manager skill\n\n> **The manager decides what to buy. The client decides when to put money in and take it out. A performance measure that credits or blames the manager for the client's timing is measuring the wrong person.**\n\nTWRR breaks the period at every cash flow, computes a clean return for each slice, and multiplies the slices together. Because each slice is a *percentage*, the **rupee amount in the portfolio during that slice becomes irrelevant** — the cash flow's distorting effect is removed. That is why SEBI requires PMS and mutual fund performance to be reported on a **TWRR** basis: a manager who runs the identical strategy for two clients must show the identical performance, even if one client added ₹1 crore at the worst possible moment.\n\n**Worked example — the same year, two very different answers.**\n\nMr Nair starts a discretionary PMS with **₹10,00,000** on 1 April.\n\n| Date | Event | Value |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Apr | Invests | ₹10,00,000 |\n| 30 Sep | Portfolio has grown | **₹12,00,000** (+20% in H1) |\n| 1 Oct | Mr Nair adds **₹10,00,000** | ₹22,00,000 |\n| 31 Mar | Portfolio has fallen | **₹19,80,000** (−10% in H2) |\n\n**TWRR:**\n- Sub-period 1 return = (12,00,000 − 10,00,000) ÷ 10,00,000 = **+20%**\n- Sub-period 2 return = (19,80,000 − 22,00,000) ÷ 22,00,000 = **−10%**\n- TWRR = (1.20 × 0.90) − 1 = 1.08 − 1 = **+8% for the year**\n\n**MWRR:**\n- Total put in = ₹10,00,000 + ₹10,00,000 = **₹20,00,000**; total value at the end = **₹19,80,000**\n- The client is **₹20,000 poorer**. The MWRR (the IRR of −10,00,000 at t=0, −10,00,000 at t=0.5 and +19,80,000 at t=1) is approximately **−1.3% per annum**.\n\n**Reconciling the two:** the manager genuinely delivered **+8%**. But Mr Nair had ₹10 lakh working during the good half and **₹22 lakh** working during the bad half. His *own timing* destroyed his outcome.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"TWRR judges the driver; MWRR judges the journey.\"**\n> The driver (manager) is graded on driving. The journey (your actual outcome) also depends on when *you* got in and out of the car.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** TWRR = MWRR **only when there are no cash flows** during the period. If a question says \"no contributions or withdrawals were made\", the two are identical and the distinction is a red herring.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** MWRR > TWRR when the client added money **before** a strong period (good timing). MWRR < TWRR when the client added money **before** a weak period (bad timing). Work out the direction from the timing, not by guessing.\n\n**Where you meet each in Indian practice:** the **XIRR** on a client's SIP statement is an MWRR. The **CAGR shown on a fund factsheet** is derived from NAVs and is therefore a TWRR.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.3 ⭐⭐ Arithmetic Mean Return (AMR) versus Geometric Mean Return (GMR)\n\n> **AMR = (R₁ + R₂ + … + Rₙ) ÷ n** — the simple average.\n> **GMR = [(1+R₁)(1+R₂)…(1+Rₙ)]^(1/n) − 1** — the compounded average.\n\n**Worked example.** A fund returns **+25%**, **−10%**, **+15%** over three years on ₹10,00,000.\n\n| Year | Return | Value at year end |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | +25% | ₹12,50,000 |\n| 2 | −10% | ₹11,25,000 |\n| 3 | +15% | **₹12,93,750** |\n\n- **AMR** = (25 − 10 + 15) ÷ 3 = **10.00%**\n- **GMR** = (1.25 × 0.90 × 1.15)^(1/3) − 1 = (1.29375)^(1/3) − 1 = **8.96%**\n\n**Check which one is true:** ₹10,00,000 × (1.0896)³ = ₹12,93,750 ✓. ₹10,00,000 × (1.10)³ = ₹13,31,000 ✗ — the AMR **overstates** what actually happened by over ₹37,000.\n\n### 🔑 The rule you must memorise\n\n> **AMR ≥ GMR, always. They are equal only when every period's return is identical. The gap widens as volatility rises.**\n\nA good approximation: **GMR ≈ AMR − σ²/2**.\n\n**Why the inequality exists — the arithmetic of losses.** A 50% loss needs a **100%** gain to get back to level. Percentage gains and losses are not symmetric, so simply averaging them overstates the compounded outcome.\n\n**The extreme illustration.** Returns of **+50%** and **−50%**:\n\n- AMR = (50 − 50) ÷ 2 = **0%** — implying you broke even\n- Reality: ₹1,00,000 → ₹1,50,000 → **₹75,000**. You lost a quarter of your money.\n- GMR = √(1.50 × 0.50) − 1 = √0.75 − 1 = **−13.40% per annum**\n\n| Volatility of the return series | AMR | GMR | Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| +10%, +10% (zero volatility) | 10.0% | **10.0%** | **0** |\n| +20%, 0% | 10.0% | **9.54%** | 0.46 pp |\n| +50%, −30% | 10.0% | **2.47%** | 7.53 pp |\n| +90%, −70% | 10.0% | **−24.50%** | 34.5 pp |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Arithmetic is what you hoped for; geometric is what you got.\"**\n> The gap between them **is** the cost of volatility.\n\n**When to use which:**\n\n| Use | Measure |\n|---|---|\n| Reporting **past, realised** multi-period performance | **GMR** (= CAGR) — it is the only one that reproduces the ending value |\n| Estimating **next period's expected** return from history | **AMR** — it is the unbiased estimate of a single period |\n| Comparing funds' long-run track records | **GMR** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** A fund advertising its \"average annual return\" using an arithmetic mean over a volatile period is inflating its record. The honest number is the **CAGR / GMR**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.4 Gross Return versus Net Return\n\n> **Net return = Gross return − all fees, expenses and costs**\n\n| Deduction | Where it bites |\n|---|---|\n| **Management / advisory fee** | The largest single item in most products |\n| **TER** (mutual funds) | Bundles management fee, marketing, registrar, trustee, audit |\n| **Performance / incentive fee** | PMS and AIFs — subject to hurdle and high watermark |\n| **Brokerage and transaction costs** | Rise with portfolio turnover |\n| **STT, stamp duty, exchange charges** | Statutory transaction costs |\n| **Custody, fund accounting** | PMS and AIF |\n| **GST on fees** | **18%** on the advisory/management fee |\n| **Exit load** | On early redemption |\n\n**Worked example — a ₹50,00,000 PMS.** Gross return for the year is **15%**.\n\n- Gross gain = 15% × ₹50,00,000 = **₹7,50,000**\n- Fixed fee 2% of assets = **₹1,00,000**\n- Performance fee 20% of the return above a 10% hurdle = 20% × (₹7,50,000 − ₹5,00,000) = **₹50,000**\n- Brokerage, custody and other costs = **₹25,000**\n- **Total costs = ₹1,75,000**\n- Net gain = ₹7,50,000 − ₹1,75,000 = **₹5,75,000** → **net return 11.50%**\n\n**A 15% gross return became an 11.5% net return — costs consumed 3.5 percentage points, nearly a quarter of the gross gain.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** SEBI requires PMS performance to be disclosed **net of all fees and expenses**. Comparing one manager's gross return to another's net return is a classic benchmarking error.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.5 Pre-tax versus Post-tax Return\n\nThe client spends **post-tax** rupees. Two investments with identical pre-tax returns can differ enormously after tax.\n\n> **Post-tax return = Pre-tax return × (1 − marginal tax rate)** — for fully taxable income such as interest.\n\n**Worked example.** Mr Bose is in the **30%** slab and compares two options for ₹10,00,000 over one year:\n\n| Option | Pre-tax return | Tax treatment | Post-tax return |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bank FD @ **7.5%** | ₹75,000 | Slab rate **30%** → tax ₹22,500 | **₹52,500 = 5.25%** |\n| Equity fund, held **> 12 months**, gain **7.5%** | ₹75,000 | LTCG at **12.5%** above the **₹1.25 lakh** annual exemption → **nil** here, as the gain is within the exemption | **₹75,000 = 7.50%** |\n\nThe equity route delivers **7.50%** versus the FD's **5.25%** — a **2.25 percentage point** advantage created entirely by the tax code, not by the market.\n\n**The rates you should know for Indian portfolios:**\n\n| Asset | Short term | Long term |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Listed equity / equity mutual funds** | **20%** if held **≤ 12 months** | **12.5%** if held **> 12 months**, on gains above **₹1.25 lakh** per financial year |\n| **Debt mutual funds** bought on/after 1 Apr 2023 | **Slab rate** | **Slab rate** — no special long-term rate |\n| **Bank FD / bond interest** | **Slab rate** | **Slab rate** |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — three layers, in order: GROSS → NET (minus fees) → POST-TAX (minus tax).** Only the last one is the client's real return.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Tax is **investor-specific** — it depends on the client's slab, holding period and use of the exemption. A fund can never publish a single \"post-tax return\" for everybody, which is exactly why published returns are pre-tax.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.6 ⭐ CAGR — Compound Annual Growth Rate\n\n> **CAGR = (Ending value ÷ Beginning value)^(1/n) − 1**, where **n = number of YEARS**\n\nCAGR is simply the GMR expressed from two endpoints. It is the **smoothed constant annual rate** that would take you from the start value to the end value.\n\n**Worked example.** ₹5,00,000 invested in 2018 is worth **₹11,00,000** after **7 years**.\n\n- CAGR = (11,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000)^(1/7) − 1 = (2.20)^(1/7) − 1 = **11.92% p.a.**\n\n**Check:** ₹5,00,000 × (1.1192)⁷ ≈ ₹11,00,000 ✓\n\n**Second example — n need not be a whole number.** ₹8,00,000 grows to ₹9,80,000 in **30 months** (2.5 years):\n\n- CAGR = (9,80,000 ÷ 8,00,000)^(1/2.5) − 1 = (1.225)^0.4 − 1 = **8.46% p.a.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — CAGR hides the path completely.** A fund that went +60%, −30%, +25% and a fund that went +14%, +14%, +14% can have almost the same CAGR and utterly different risk. **CAGR says nothing about volatility.** This is precisely why the chapter then adds risk measures.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — CAGR is point-to-point and therefore start-date sensitive.** Choosing a market bottom as the start date flatters any fund. The professional fix is **rolling returns**: compute the 3-year CAGR starting from *every* day in the period and look at the distribution, not one lucky window.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.7 Annualising Returns (and Risk)\n\n**Annualising a return** — always **compound**, never multiply:\n\n> **Annualised return = (1 + periodic return)^(number of periods per year) − 1**\n\n| Given | Working | Annualised |\n|---|---|---|\n| **4% in a quarter** | (1.04)⁴ − 1 | **16.99%** (not 16%) |\n| **1.2% in a month** | (1.012)¹² − 1 | **15.39%** (not 14.4%) |\n| **13.6% in 9 months** | (1.136)^(12/9) − 1 | **18.53%** |\n| **0.05% in a day** | (1.0005)²⁵² − 1 | **13.42%** |\n\n**Annualising risk** — volatility scales with the **square root of time**:\n\n> **σ_annual = σ_periodic × √(number of periods per year)**\n\n| Given | Working | Annualised σ |\n|---|---|---|\n| Monthly σ = **4%** | 4 × √12 = 4 × 3.464 | **13.86%** |\n| Daily σ = **1%** | 1 × √252 | **15.87%** |\n| Quarterly σ = **7%** | 7 × √4 | **14.00%** |\n\n**Why √t and not t?** Returns in successive periods are assumed **independent**. Variances (not standard deviations) add up over time, so variance scales with **t** and standard deviation with **√t**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Because return grows roughly with **t** but risk only with **√t**, the *reward-to-risk* ratio improves with the horizon. This is the mathematical basis of \"equities are less risky over long horizons.\"\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Never annualise a return from a period **shorter than one year** for client-facing disclosure. A 6% quarterly gain annualises to 26.2% — a number no one should be shown as an expectation. SEBI's rule: **< 1 year → absolute; ≥ 1 year → CAGR.**\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.8 Cash Drag and Cash-Drag-Adjusted Return\n\n**Cash drag** is the reduction in portfolio return caused by holding uninvested cash in a rising market. Every fund holds some cash — for redemptions, for pending deployment, for tactical reasons — and that cash earns a money-market rate rather than the asset-class return.\n\n> **Cash-drag-adjusted return = (R_portfolio − w_cash × R_cash) ÷ (1 − w_cash)**\n\nThis strips the cash out and shows **what the invested portion actually earned** — the fairest measure of the manager's security selection.\n\n**Worked example.** A ₹100 crore equity fund holds **10% in cash** earning **4%**; the **90%** invested in equities returns **15%**.\n\n- Reported portfolio return = (0.90 × 15%) + (0.10 × 4%) = 13.5% + 0.4% = **13.9%**\n- **Cash drag = 15% − 13.9% = 1.1 percentage points**\n- Cash-drag-adjusted return = (13.9% − 0.10 × 4%) ÷ 0.90 = 13.5 ÷ 0.90 = **15.0%** ✓ — exactly the equity return, as it must be\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Cash is a 100% weight in the wrong asset.\"** In a bull market it drags; in a crash it cushions. Cash drag is therefore a *cost of liquidity*, not automatically a mistake.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Cash drag is only a drag when the market **rises**. If equities fell 15%, the same 10% cash would have *added* about 1.9 percentage points. That is why some cash is deliberate defence and evaluating it requires knowing the market's direction.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.9 ⭐⭐ Alpha and Beta Return\n\nA portfolio's return splits into two economically different parts:\n\n| Component | What it is | How you get it | What it is worth paying for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Beta return** | The return earned simply for **being exposed to the market** | Buy an index fund | Almost nothing — a few basis points |\n| **Alpha return** | The return **above** what that market exposure alone would justify | Genuine manager skill | This is what an active fee buys |\n\n> **Expected return under CAPM: E(Rp) = Rf + β(Rm − Rf)**\n> **Jensen's Alpha, α = Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)]**\n\n**Worked example.** A fund returned **16%**. The risk-free rate (364-day T-bill) is **6.5%**, the NIFTY 50 TRI returned **13%**, and the fund's beta is **1.20**.\n\n- Market risk premium = 13% − 6.5% = **6.5%**\n- Return the fund *should* have earned for that risk = 6.5% + (1.20 × 6.5%) = 6.5% + 7.8% = **14.30%**\n- **Alpha = 16% − 14.30% = +1.70%**\n\nThe fund beat the index by 3 percentage points (16 vs 13), but **1.3 of those points were bought, not earned** — simply by running a beta of 1.2 in a rising market. The **genuine skill contribution is 1.70%**.\n\n**A second example where the raw comparison misleads completely.** A conservative fund returned **11%** while the market returned **12%** — it \"underperformed\". But its beta was **0.70** and Rf is **6%**.\n\n- Required = 6 + 0.70 × (12 − 6) = 6 + 4.2 = **10.20%**\n- **Alpha = 11 − 10.20 = +0.80%** — the fund actually **added value**, taking far less risk than the index.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Beta is rented; alpha is earned.\"**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — the most common alpha error in the exam.** **Alpha is NOT (Rp − Rm).** Simple excess return over the benchmark ignores the risk taken to get it. Alpha subtracts the **beta-adjusted** required return. Only when β = 1 do the two coincide.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Alpha is only meaningful if the **benchmark and beta are appropriate**. A mid-cap fund measured with a beta against the NIFTY 50 will show a spurious alpha that is really just the mid-cap premium.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.10 Portfolio Return\n\nFor a portfolio held over a single period with fixed weights:\n\n> **Rp = w₁R₁ + w₂R₂ + … + wₙRₙ** — the **weighted average** of the component returns, with **Σwᵢ = 1**\n\n**Worked example.** Ms D'Souza's ₹1,00,00,000 portfolio:\n\n| Asset | Amount | Weight | Return | Contribution |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Equity funds | ₹60,00,000 | 0.60 | 14.0% | 8.40% |\n| Debt funds | ₹30,00,000 | 0.30 | 7.5% | 2.25% |\n| Gold ETF | ₹10,00,000 | 0.10 | 9.0% | 0.90% |\n| **Portfolio** | **₹1,00,00,000** | **1.00** | | **11.55%** |\n\nCross-check in rupees: ₹8,40,000 + ₹2,25,000 + ₹90,000 = **₹11,55,000** on ₹1 crore = **11.55%** ✓\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** The weighted-average rule works for **return**. It does **NOT** work for **risk** — portfolio standard deviation is almost always *less* than the weighted average of the component standard deviations (Chapter 14). Return adds in a straight line; risk does not.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Weights must be **market-value weights at the start of the period**, not cost weights and not end weights.\n\n---\n\n## 16.3 Risk Measures\n\n### 16.3.1 ⭐ Total Risk and Downside Risk\n\n**Total risk** is measured by **standard deviation (σ)** — the dispersion of returns around their mean. It treats a **+15% surprise and a −15% surprise as equally \"risky\"**, because both are deviations.\n\n**Downside risk** measures only the outcomes an investor actually fears — returns **below a target** (the Minimum Acceptable Return, or MAR, which may be 0%, the risk-free rate, or a required return).\n\n> **Downside deviation = √[ Σ (min(Rᵢ − MAR, 0))² ÷ n ]**\n> Only shortfalls enter the sum; every period still counts in **n**.\n\n**Worked example.** Five annual returns for a fund: **0%, 3%, 20%, 18%, 19%**. Mean = **12%**. Take **MAR = 6%**.\n\n*Total risk (standard deviation):*\n- Deviations from the 12% mean: −12, −9, +8, +6, +7\n- Squares: 144 + 81 + 64 + 36 + 49 = **374**; ÷ 5 = 74.8\n- **σ = √74.8 = 8.65%**\n\n*Downside deviation (MAR = 6%):*\n- Only two years fell short: 0% (shortfall **6**) and 3% (shortfall **3**)\n- Squares: 36 + 9 = **45**; ÷ 5 = 9\n- **Downside deviation = √9 = 3.00%**\n\nTotal risk **8.65%**, downside risk only **3.00%** — because most of this fund's volatility was **upside**. An investor who dislikes losses but is perfectly happy with big gains should judge it on 3.00%, not 8.65%.\n\n**Other downside measures worth knowing:**\n\n| Measure | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Semi-variance / semi-deviation** | Variance computed using only below-mean returns | The square of downside deviation |\n| **Maximum drawdown (MDD)** | Largest peak-to-trough fall | Portfolio peaks at ₹52 lakh, troughs at ₹36.40 lakh → MDD = 15.6/52 = **30%** |\n| **Value at Risk (VaR)** | The loss that will not be exceeded with a stated confidence over a stated horizon | \"95% one-day VaR is ₹2 lakh\" → on **19 days out of 20** the loss stays under ₹2 lakh |\n| **Downside capture ratio** | Fund's return in falling markets ÷ benchmark's | Benchmark −3%, fund −2.4% → **80%** — good |\n| **Upside capture ratio** | Fund's return in rising markets ÷ benchmark's | Benchmark +4%, fund +4.4% → **110%** — good |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Standard deviation punishes good surprises too.\"** Downside measures fix that.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.2 Portfolio Risk versus Individual Risk\n\nA security's own standard deviation tells you almost nothing about what it does to a portfolio.\n\n> **A security's contribution to portfolio risk is its COVARIANCE with the rest of the portfolio — not its own standard deviation.**\n\nA very volatile asset that moves **against** the portfolio (negative covariance) can **reduce** total portfolio risk. Gold in an Indian equity portfolio is the standard illustration: volatile on its own, stabilising in combination.\n\nThis is why the relevant risk measure changes with context:\n\n| Context | Right risk measure |\n|---|---|\n| The asset is held **alone**, or is the client's whole portfolio | **Total risk (σ)** |\n| The asset is **one holding inside a diversified portfolio** | **Systematic risk (β)** / covariance |\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.3 Market Risk\n\n**Market risk** is the risk that the entire market falls — the portion of risk that **cannot be diversified away** because it affects every security to some degree. Its Indian drivers: RBI policy rate changes, inflation, GDP growth, the fiscal deficit, FPI flows, crude oil prices, the rupee, global risk sentiment, elections and Union Budget announcements.\n\nIt is measured by **beta**, and it is the risk for which investors are **compensated**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.4 Interpreting Volatility\n\nStandard deviation only becomes useful when you convert it into a statement about likely outcomes. Assuming returns are approximately normally distributed:\n\n| Range | Probability |\n|---|---|\n| Mean **± 1σ** | about **68%** |\n| Mean **± 2σ** | about **95%** |\n| Mean **± 3σ** | about **99.7%** |\n\n**Worked example.** A fund has an expected return of **12%** and σ of **18%**.\n\n- **68%** of years should land between **12 − 18 = −6%** and **12 + 18 = +30%**\n- **95%** of years should land between **12 − 36 = −24%** and **12 + 48 = +48%**\n- On ₹20,00,000, a −24% year is a paper loss of **₹4,80,000** — the number to put in front of the client *before* investing\n\nTypical annualised standard deviations you can use for sanity-checking:\n\n| Asset class | Indicative annual σ |\n|---|---|\n| Liquid / overnight funds | very low, ~0–1% |\n| Short-duration debt | low single digits |\n| Gilt / long-duration debt | mid single digits |\n| Large-cap equity | mid-to-high teens |\n| Mid- and small-cap equity | materially higher than large-cap |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Real market returns have **fat tails** — extreme moves happen far more often than a normal distribution predicts (March 2020 is the obvious Indian example). Standard deviation therefore **understates** true tail risk.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** σ is computed from **past** returns and is not a forecast. It is also **regime-dependent** — volatility clusters, and periods of calm are routinely followed by periods of stress.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.5 ⭐ Tracking Error\n\n> **Tracking Error = the standard deviation of the ACTIVE RETURN (portfolio return − benchmark return) over a series of periods**\n\nIt answers: **how tightly does this portfolio hug its benchmark?**\n\n**Worked example.** Four quarters of active return: **+4%, 0%, +4%, 0%**.\n\n- Mean active return = 8 ÷ 4 = **+2%**\n- Deviations from the mean: +2, −2, +2, −2 → squares 4, 4, 4, 4 = **16**; ÷ 4 = 4\n- **Tracking error = √4 = 2.0%**\n\n**What tracking error tells you:**\n\n| Tracking error | Interpretation |\n|---|---|\n| **Near zero** | A pure index fund / ETF |\n| **Low (roughly 1–3%)** | Closet indexing or a tightly benchmarked large-cap fund |\n| **Moderate (roughly 4–7%)** | A genuinely active but benchmark-aware fund |\n| **High (8%+)** | High-conviction, concentrated or unconstrained fund |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** **Tracking error is not \"underperformance.\"** A fund that beats its index by exactly 4% every single quarter has a **tracking error of ZERO** — the active return never varies — while delivering huge outperformance. Tracking error measures the **variability** of the gap, not its sign or its size.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — tracking error vs tracking difference.** **Tracking difference** is the simple *return gap* (fund return minus index return). **Tracking error** is the *standard deviation* of that gap. SEBI requires index funds and ETFs to disclose **both**, and caps the tracking error of equity index funds and ETFs (measured on rolling one-year data) at **2%**.\n\n**Why an index fund cannot have zero tracking error:** the TER, cash held for redemptions, index rebalancing costs, brokerage, STT and dividend-reinvestment timing all create small, unavoidable gaps.\n\nTracking error is also the **denominator of the Information Ratio** — its second and more important exam role.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.6 ⭐⭐ Systematic and Unsystematic Risk\n\n> **Total risk = Systematic risk + Unsystematic risk**\n> In variance terms: **σ²ᵢ = β²σ²ₘ + σ²ₑ**\n\n| | **Systematic risk** | **Unsystematic risk** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Other names** | Market risk, non-diversifiable risk | Specific, unique, residual, diversifiable, idiosyncratic risk |\n| **Source** | Economy-wide: RBI rates, inflation, GDP, currency, oil, policy, war, global sentiment | Company- or sector-specific: a fraud, a product recall, a plant fire, a promoter pledge, a regulatory ban, a strike, litigation |\n| **Affects** | **All** securities, to differing degrees | **One** company or sector |\n| **Can diversification remove it?** | **NO** | **YES** |\n| **Measured by** | **Beta (β)** | Residual standard deviation |\n| **Is it rewarded?** | **YES** — investors are paid a premium for bearing it | **NO** — the market does not pay you for a risk you could have removed for free |\n\n### 🔑 Why unsystematic risk is not rewarded\n\n> **The market only pays you for risk you cannot avoid. Since specific risk can be eliminated at nearly zero cost by diversifying, no rational investor would pay a premium for a security merely because it carries a lot of it.**\n\n**How much diversification is enough?** Adding stocks reduces specific risk quickly at first and then with diminishing returns. Most of the benefit in a broad equity portfolio arrives within roughly **20–30 well-spread stocks**; beyond that the curve flattens against the floor of systematic risk. What matters more than the count is that the holdings are **genuinely different** — 30 Indian banking stocks are not diversified.\n\n**R² tells you how well diversified a portfolio is:** it is the proportion of the portfolio's variance explained by the market.\n\n| R² | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **95–100%** | Essentially an index fund — beta and alpha figures are highly reliable |\n| **70–95%** | A typical diversified active fund |\n| **Below ~70%** | A lot of non-market risk — **beta, alpha and Treynor become unreliable** for this fund |\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.7 ⭐⭐ Beta\n\n> **β = Cov(Rᵢ, Rₘ) ÷ Var(Rₘ) = ρᵢₘ × (σᵢ ÷ σₘ)**\n\nBeta measures **sensitivity to market movements** — how much the security or portfolio moves for a 1% move in the market.\n\n**Worked example.** A stock has σ = **24%**, the market has σ = **16%**, and their correlation is **0.80**.\n\n- **β = 0.80 × (24 ÷ 16) = 0.80 × 1.50 = 1.20**\n\nIf the NIFTY rises **10%**, this stock is expected to rise **12%**. If the NIFTY falls **10%**, it is expected to fall **12%**.\n\n| Beta | Meaning | Typical Indian example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **β = 1.0** | Moves with the market | An index fund; the market itself, by definition |\n| **β > 1.0** | **Aggressive** — amplifies the market | Cyclicals, capital goods, metals, high-beta NBFCs |\n| **0 < β < 1** | **Defensive** — damped | FMCG, pharma, utilities |\n| **β = 0** | Uncorrelated with the market | Treasury bills / cash |\n| **β < 0** | Moves opposite the market | Rare; gold is sometimes weakly negative to equities |\n\n**Portfolio beta = the weighted average of the component betas** (unlike portfolio σ, this *is* a simple weighted average).\n\n**Worked example.**\n\n| Holding | Weight | Beta | Contribution |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cyclical fund | 0.40 | 1.40 | 0.560 |\n| Large-cap fund | 0.35 | 0.90 | 0.315 |\n| FMCG fund | 0.25 | 0.60 | 0.150 |\n| **Portfolio** | **1.00** | | **β = 1.025** |\n\n**Using beta to forecast:** if the market is expected to return 12% with Rf at 6%, this portfolio's CAPM expected return is 6 + 1.025 × 6 = **12.15%**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Beta is estimated by **regression on past data** and is unstable — it changes as the portfolio changes and as market conditions change. It is also only reliable when **R² is high**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Beta captures **only systematic risk**. A single stock with β = 0.8 is **not** less risky than a diversified portfolio with β = 1.0 — the stock also carries huge specific risk that beta simply does not see.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.8 Liquidity Risk\n\n**The risk that a holding cannot be sold quickly at a fair price.** It shows up as:\n\n- **Wide bid-ask spreads** and high **impact cost** — the price moves against you as you trade\n- Small-cap and micro-cap equities, unlisted securities, real estate, and lower-rated corporate bonds are the most exposed\n- Liquidity **evaporates exactly when it is most needed** — in a market panic, buyers disappear\n\n**Indian regulatory responses you should know:**\n- SEBI requires **stress testing and liquidity disclosures** for mid-cap and small-cap equity mutual fund schemes\n- **Side-pocketing** (segregated portfolios) allows an illiquid, credit-impaired security to be ring-fenced so that redeeming investors cannot unfairly transfer the loss to those who stay\n- Debt schemes must hold minimum liquid assets and are subject to **potential risk class** and liquidity-ratio norms\n- **Swing pricing** for open-ended debt schemes in stressed conditions\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Liquidity risk is **not captured by standard deviation**. Illiquid assets are valued infrequently, which makes their measured volatility look artificially *low* — a phenomenon called **volatility smoothing**. Never conclude that a real-estate or unlisted portfolio is \"low risk\" because its reported σ is small.\n\n---\n\n### 16.3.9 Credit Risk\n\n**The risk that a borrower fails to pay interest or principal on time — or is downgraded.** Relevant to every debt holding.\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Default risk** | The issuer does not pay |\n| **Downgrade risk** | The rating falls, the price falls immediately even if no default occurs |\n| **Credit spread risk** | Spreads over G-Secs widen market-wide, hurting all corporate bonds |\n| **Rating agencies in India** | **CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings, Brickwork** |\n| **Rating scale** | **AAA** (highest safety) → AA → A → BBB (lowest **investment grade**) → BB and below (**speculative**) → **D** (default) |\n| **Compensation** | The **credit spread** — the extra yield over a same-maturity G-Sec |\n\nIndian episodes worth remembering as illustrations: the IL&FS and DHFL defaults showed how quickly an AAA rating can collapse and how credit and liquidity risk arrive together.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** A high-yield debt fund's *reported* return looks excellent right up to the moment of default. Credit risk is **asymmetric and non-normal** — small steady gains, then a sudden large loss — so σ badly understates it.\n\n---\n\n## 16.4 ⭐⭐ Risk-Adjusted Return Measures\n\nThe heart of the chapter. All five follow the same logic: **excess return divided by a measure of risk.** What changes is *which* risk goes in the denominator.\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Risk in the denominator | Higher is better? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sharpe** | (Rp − Rf) ÷ **σp** | **Total** risk | Yes |\n| **Treynor** | (Rp − Rf) ÷ **βp** | **Systematic** risk only | Yes |\n| **Sortino** | (Rp − MAR) ÷ **downside deviation** | **Downside** risk only | Yes |\n| **Information Ratio** | (Rp − Rb) ÷ **Tracking Error** | **Active/relative** risk | Yes |\n| **M²** | Rf + (Sharpe × **σm**) | Restated at **market** risk | Yes — and read as a **%** |\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.1 Sharpe Ratio\n\n> **Sharpe Ratio = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp**\n\n**Excess return per unit of TOTAL risk.** Developed by **William Sharpe**. It is the most widely used risk-adjusted measure in the world.\n\n**Worked example — why higher return can mean worse performance.** Risk-free rate = **6%**.\n\n| Fund | Return | σ | Sharpe |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fund A** | 16% | 20% | (16 − 6) ÷ 20 = **0.50** |\n| **Fund B** | 12% | 10% | (12 − 6) ÷ 10 = **0.60** |\n\n**Fund B wins**, despite returning 4 percentage points less. Per unit of risk taken, B delivered more.\n\n**The intuition that makes this concrete:** you could **lever** Fund B to match Fund A's risk. Borrow at 6% to hold **twice** as much of B: σ becomes 2 × 10 = **20%** (matching A) and return becomes (2 × 12) − (1 × 6) = **18%** — better than A's 16% at identical risk. That is what the higher Sharpe ratio is telling you.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Sharpe ratios are **only comparable between funds measured over the same period with the same Rf**. Never compare a Sharpe ratio from a bull market with one from a bear market.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** If (Rp − Rf) is **negative**, the Sharpe ratio becomes meaningless for ranking — a *riskier* fund with the same negative excess return will show a *less negative* Sharpe, wrongly appearing better.\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.2 Treynor Ratio\n\n> **Treynor Ratio = (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp**\n\n**Excess return per unit of SYSTEMATIC risk.** Developed by **Jack Treynor**.\n\nThe logic: if the portfolio is **one component of an already well-diversified overall portfolio**, its unsystematic risk has *already been diversified away* at the total-portfolio level. Charging that fund for risk that no longer exists would be wrong. So the denominator uses **beta only**.\n\n**Worked example.** Rf = **6%**.\n\n| Fund | Return | Beta | Treynor |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fund P** | 15% | 1.20 | (15 − 6) ÷ 1.20 = **7.50** |\n| **Fund Q** | 11% | 0.50 | (11 − 6) ÷ 0.50 = **10.00** |\n\n**Fund Q wins** — it produced 5 points of excess return while taking only half the market's risk.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — read the units.** Sharpe's answer is a pure number (\"0.60\"). Treynor's answer is **in percentage points per unit of beta** (\"10.00\" means 10 percentage points of excess return per 1.0 of beta).\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.3 ⭐⭐ Sharpe versus Treynor — the classic comparison\n\n| | **Sharpe** | **Treynor** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Denominator** | σ — **total** risk | β — **systematic** risk |\n| **Assumes the portfolio is** | The investor's **entire** wealth | **One sub-portfolio** inside a bigger diversified whole |\n| **Penalises poor diversification?** | **YES** | **NO** |\n| **Use when** | Judging a **standalone** portfolio, or comparing whole portfolios | Judging a **fund that will be one of many** a client holds |\n| **Works when the portfolio is undiversified?** | **YES** | **NO** — beta ignores the specific risk being carried |\n\n### 🔑 The disagreement test — the favourite exam question\n\n> **If two funds rank the SAME on Sharpe and Treynor, both are well diversified.**\n> **If a fund ranks BETTER on Treynor than on Sharpe, it is carrying a lot of UNSYSTEMATIC risk — i.e. it is poorly diversified.**\n\n**Worked example.** Rf = **6%**, market σ = **16%**.\n\n| Fund | Return | σ | β | **Sharpe** | **Treynor** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fund X** | 15% | 25% | 1.00 | 9 ÷ 25 = **0.36** | 9 ÷ 1.00 = **9.00** |\n| **Fund Y** | 13% | 14% | 0.80 | 7 ÷ 14 = **0.50** | 7 ÷ 0.80 = **8.75** |\n\n**They disagree.** Y is better on Sharpe; X is better on Treynor. **Why?**\n\n- Fund X's systematic risk = β × σm = 1.00 × 16 = **16%**, yet its total σ is **25%**. Its **specific risk = √(25² − 16²) = √369 = 19.2%**, and its **R² is only 256/625 = 41%**.\n- Fund Y's systematic risk = 0.80 × 16 = **12.8%** against a total σ of **14%**. Its specific risk is only **√(196 − 163.8) = 5.7%**, and its **R² is 84%**.\n\n**Fund X is badly diversified.** Sharpe sees that and penalises it; Treynor is blind to it. So:\n\n- For a client putting **everything** into one of them → choose **Y** (use Sharpe)\n- For a client adding a **small satellite sleeve** to an already diversified portfolio → **X**'s specific risk will wash out, so **X** on Treynor is defensible\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** For a **well-diversified** portfolio the two measures give the **same ranking**. The exam only makes them disagree when it wants you to spot **poor diversification**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.4 Sortino Ratio\n\n> **Sortino Ratio = (Rp − MAR) ÷ Downside deviation**\n\nIdentical to Sharpe except the denominator counts **only the bad volatility**. The target (MAR) may be **0%**, the **risk-free rate**, or a client's **required return** — you must state which.\n\n**Why it exists:** investors do not experience an unexpectedly *large gain* as risk. Penalising a manager for upside volatility is illogical, and it particularly misjudges strategies with **asymmetric** return profiles.\n\n**Worked example — using the same fund as §16.3.1.** Returns **0%, 3%, 20%, 18%, 19%**; mean **12%**; **Rf = MAR = 6%**; σ = **8.65%**; downside deviation = **3.00%**.\n\n- **Sharpe = (12 − 6) ÷ 8.65 = 0.69**\n- **Sortino = (12 − 6) ÷ 3.00 = 2.00**\n\nThe Sortino ratio is nearly **three times** the Sharpe ratio, because most of this fund's volatility was **upside** volatility that hurt nobody.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Sortino is Sharpe with the good news removed from the denominator.\"**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Sortino is **always ≥ Sharpe** in practice, because downside deviation ≤ total σ. Do not read a high Sortino as proof of skill — check that the MAR used is the same before comparing two funds.\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.5 Information Ratio (Appraisal Ratio)\n\n> **Information Ratio = (Rp − Rb) ÷ Tracking Error = Active return ÷ Active risk = Alpha ÷ Tracking Error**\n\nThis is the **manager-skill ratio**: it asks how much **excess return over the benchmark** the manager produced **per unit of the benchmark-relative risk taken to get it** — and, crucially, **how consistently**.\n\n**Worked example.** A fund returned **16%** against a benchmark of **12.5%**, with a tracking error of **5%**.\n\n- Active return = 16 − 12.5 = **3.5%**\n- **IR = 3.5 ÷ 5 = 0.70**\n\n**Now compare two managers with identical outperformance:**\n\n| Manager | Active return | Tracking error | IR | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Manager 1** | +3.0% | 3.0% | **1.00** | Steady, repeatable outperformance |\n| **Manager 2** | +3.0% | 12.0% | **0.25** | Same result, achieved by huge bets — far more likely to be luck |\n\nRough industry convention for interpreting the IR:\n\n| IR | Reading |\n|---|---|\n| **0.50** | Good |\n| **0.75** | Very good |\n| **1.00** | Exceptional — and rare |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Sharpe is versus cash; Information Ratio is versus the benchmark.\"** Sharpe subtracts **Rf** and divides by **total** σ. IR subtracts **Rb** and divides by **tracking error**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** A **negative IR** means the manager underperformed the benchmark — you paid an active fee for a worse-than-index result. A **near-zero tracking error with near-zero active return** is a **closet index fund** charging active fees.\n\n---\n\n### 16.4.6 M² — the Modigliani and Modigliani Measure\n\n> **M² = Rf + [(Rp − Rf) ÷ σp] × σm = Rf + (Sharpe ratio of the portfolio × σm)**\n> **M² alpha = M² − Rm**\n\nM² takes the Sharpe ratio's information and expresses it as a **percentage return** — the return the portfolio *would have earned* if it had been geared up or down to exactly the **market's level of risk**. That makes it directly comparable to the index return, which is far more intuitive for a client than \"0.60 versus 0.50\".\n\n**Worked example.** Fund return **21%**, fund σ **25%**; **Rf = 6%**; market return **14%**, market σ **15%**.\n\n- Fund Sharpe = (21 − 6) ÷ 25 = **0.60**\n- **M² = 6 + (0.60 × 15) = 6 + 9 = 15.0%**\n- **M² alpha = 15.0 − 14.0 = +1.0 percentage point**\n\n**Proof by construction — how the \"risk-matching\" actually works.** To bring the fund's 25% volatility down to the market's 15%, hold **60%** in the fund and **40%** in T-bills (0.60 × 25 = 15% ✓).\n\n- That blend returns (0.60 × 21%) + (0.40 × 6%) = 12.6 + 2.4 = **15.0%** ✓ — exactly M².\n- At **identical risk** to the index, the fund's strategy delivers **15%** against the index's **14%**. It genuinely added **1 percentage point**.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"M² is the Sharpe ratio translated into rupees a client can understand.\"** Same ranking as Sharpe, always — but expressed as a return, not a ratio.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** M² and Sharpe **always rank funds identically** (M² is a positive linear transformation of Sharpe). M² adds interpretability, not new information.\n\n---\n\n### Choosing the right measure — the decision table\n\n| The situation | Use | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| The portfolio is the client's **entire** wealth | **Sharpe** | Total risk is what the client actually bears |\n| The fund is **one sleeve** of a diversified portfolio | **Treynor** | Specific risk is diversified away elsewhere |\n| The client cares about **losses**, not volatility | **Sortino** | Only downside deviation is penalised |\n| Judging an **active manager against a benchmark** | **Information Ratio** | Measures skill and consistency net of the index |\n| Explaining risk-adjusted performance to a **client in plain %** | **M²** | Restates Sharpe as a comparable return |\n| Asking \"did the manager beat what their risk level required?\" | **Jensen's Alpha** | Excess over the CAPM-required return |\n\n---\n\n## 16.5 Performance Evaluation — Benchmarking and Peer Group Analysis\n\n### 16.5.1 Characteristics of a Good Benchmark Index\n\nA benchmark that fails any of these produces a meaningless comparison.\n\n| Characteristic | What it requires |\n|---|---|\n| **Specified in advance** | Chosen **before** the period, never selected afterwards to flatter the result |\n| **Appropriate** | Matches the portfolio's **asset class, style, market-cap and geography** |\n| **Measurable** | Its return can be calculated regularly and independently |\n| **Unambiguous** | Constituents and weights are clearly identifiable |\n| **Reflective of current investment opinion** | The manager actually knows and has views on its constituents |\n| **Accountable / owned** | The manager accepts it as the standard for their work |\n| **Investable** | It is possible to hold the index passively as an alternative |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — SAMURAI**: **S**pecified in advance, **A**ppropriate, **M**easurable, **U**nambiguous, **R**eflective of current investment opinion, **A**ccountable, **I**nvestable.\n\n**Practical index requirements:** transparent published rules, adequate **liquidity** in the constituents, low **turnover** (so replication is cheap), a long published history, wide acceptance, and a **Total Return** version.\n\n**Indian benchmarks you should recognise:**\n\n| Portfolio | Typical benchmark |\n|---|---|\n| Large-cap equity | **NIFTY 50 TRI** or **BSE SENSEX TRI** |\n| Diversified / multi-cap | **NIFTY 500 TRI** |\n| Mid-cap | **NIFTY Midcap 150 TRI** |\n| Small-cap | **NIFTY Smallcap 250 TRI** |\n| Debt | **CRISIL** bond index family |\n| Liquid | **NIFTY / CRISIL Liquid Fund Index** |\n| Gold | Domestic price of physical gold |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — PRI versus TRI.** A **Price Return Index (PRI)** ignores dividends; a **Total Return Index (TRI)** reinvests them. Benchmarking a fund (which receives dividends) against a **PRI** artificially flatters the fund by roughly the dividend yield each year. **SEBI has mandated that mutual funds benchmark against TRI**, precisely to stop this.\n\n**SEBI's two-tier benchmark structure for mutual funds:** a **Tier-1 benchmark** reflecting the **scheme category** (so all funds in a category are comparable) and an optional **Tier-2 benchmark** reflecting the **manager's particular strategy or style**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.5.2 Customised (Blended) Benchmarks\n\nWhen no single index matches the mandate — the usual case for a multi-asset client portfolio — you construct a **blended benchmark** by weighting indices in the portfolio's **strategic asset allocation** proportions.\n\n**Worked example.** A balanced client portfolio with a strategic allocation of **65% equity / 35% debt**. Over the year the NIFTY 50 TRI returned **15%** and the chosen CRISIL bond index returned **7%**.\n\n- **Benchmark return = (0.65 × 15%) + (0.35 × 7%) = 9.75% + 2.45% = 12.20%**\n\nIf the portfolio returned **12.9%**, the active return is **+0.70%** — a far more honest verdict than \"it lagged the NIFTY's 15%\", which would have compared a balanced portfolio to a pure equity index.\n\n**Rules for building one:** use the **strategic** (long-run policy) weights, not the current actual weights; **rebalance** the benchmark on the same schedule as the policy; specify it **in advance and in the IPS**; and use **total return** versions of each index.\n\n---\n\n### 16.5.3 ⭐ Benchmarking Errors\n\n| Error | What goes wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Style / category mismatch** | A small-cap fund compared to the NIFTY 50 — you measure the **small-cap premium**, not skill |\n| **Using a PRI instead of a TRI** | Fund looks better by roughly the dividend yield every year |\n| **Choosing the benchmark after the fact** | \"Benchmark shopping\" — picking whichever index the fund happened to beat |\n| **Cherry-picked start and end dates** | Point-to-point returns from a market bottom flatter any fund. Fix: **rolling returns** |\n| **Too short a period** | One year is noise. Judge over a **full market cycle**, 3–5 years or more |\n| **Ignoring risk** | Comparing raw returns of a β = 1.4 fund and a β = 0.7 fund |\n| **Gross versus net mismatch** | Fund's gross return against an index; the index has no fees, so the fund must be shown **net** |\n| **A non-investable benchmark** | If you cannot buy it, it is not a fair alternative |\n| **Ignoring the cash/currency position** | A fund holding 15% cash is not running the index's risk |\n| **Survivorship bias in the comparison set** | Dead funds vanish, so the surviving average looks better than reality |\n| **Composition drift** | The fund's style changes but the benchmark does not (or the index is reconstituted and history is restated) |\n\n---\n\n### 16.5.4 Managers' Universe Analysis (Peer Group Analysis)\n\nComparing a fund against the **universe of funds with the same mandate**, usually via **quartile rankings** — top quartile, second quartile, and so on. It answers \"how did this manager do against everyone else facing the same opportunity set?\"\n\n**Strengths:** it captures conditions the index cannot (if every large-cap manager lost 12% in a crash, a −10% result is strong); it reflects real, achievable, **net-of-cost** outcomes; and quartile rankings are intuitive for clients.\n\n**Weaknesses — all examinable:**\n\n| Weakness | Why it distorts |\n|---|---|\n| **Survivorship bias** | Poor funds are merged or wound up and leave the universe, lifting the apparent average |\n| **The universe is not investable** | You cannot buy \"the median manager\" |\n| **Not risk-adjusted** | The top quartile in a bull market is often just the highest-beta quartile |\n| **Composition changes** | The peer group's membership shifts over time, so rankings are not comparable across periods |\n| **Category definitions are loose** | Funds classified alike may run very different mandates |\n| **Small samples** | With 15 funds in a category, quartile boundaries are statistically fragile |\n| **Self-selection / reporting bias** | Voluntary databases attract good records |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Peer analysis and benchmark analysis are **complements, not substitutes**. A fund can be **top quartile among peers and still below its index** if the whole category underperformed. Always look at both.\n\n---\n\n## 16.6 ⭐⭐ Performance Attribution Analysis\n\n**Attribution decomposes the difference between the portfolio's return and the benchmark's return into the specific decisions that caused it.** It converts \"we beat the index by 2.1%\" into \"we beat it because of X, despite Y\" — which is what tells you whether the result is repeatable.\n\n### 16.6.1 & 16.6.2 Asset/Sector Allocation and Selection\n\n> **Total active return = Allocation effect + Selection effect + Interaction effect**\n\n> **Allocation = Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (R_b,sector − R_b,total)** — was the manager overweight the sectors that did well?\n> **Selection = Σ w_b × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector)** — inside each sector, did the manager pick better stocks?\n> **Interaction = Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector)** — the joint effect of over-weighting a sector *and* picking well in it\n\n**Full worked example.**\n\n| | **Benchmark weight** | **Benchmark return** | **Portfolio weight** | **Portfolio return** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Equity** | 60% | 12.0% | 75% | 14.0% |\n| **Debt** | 40% | 7.0% | 25% | 6.5% |\n\n- **Benchmark return** = (0.60 × 12) + (0.40 × 7) = 7.2 + 2.8 = **10.00%**\n- **Portfolio return** = (0.75 × 14) + (0.25 × 6.5) = 10.5 + 1.625 = **12.125%**\n- **Total active return = +2.125%**\n\n**Allocation effect** (weight difference × the sector's benchmark return relative to the total benchmark):\n- Equity: (0.75 − 0.60) × (12 − 10) = 0.15 × 2 = **+0.30%**\n- Debt: (0.25 − 0.40) × (7 − 10) = (−0.15) × (−3) = **+0.45%**\n- **Allocation total = +0.75%** — the manager was overweight the better-performing asset and underweight the weaker one. Both bets paid.\n\n**Selection effect** (benchmark weight × the return difference within each sector):\n- Equity: 0.60 × (14 − 12) = **+1.20%**\n- Debt: 0.40 × (6.5 − 7) = **−0.20%**\n- **Selection total = +1.00%** — excellent stock picking in equity, slightly poor in debt.\n\n**Interaction effect:**\n- Equity: 0.15 × (14 − 12) = **+0.30%**\n- Debt: (−0.15) × (6.5 − 7) = **+0.075%**\n- **Interaction total = +0.375%**\n\n**Check: 0.75 + 1.00 + 0.375 = 2.125%** ✓ — exactly the active return.\n\n**The two-term simplification** (used when interaction is folded into selection by using *portfolio* weights):\n- Selection = (0.75 × 2) + (0.25 × −0.5) = 1.5 − 0.125 = **+1.375%**\n- Allocation **+0.75%** + Selection **+1.375%** = **2.125%** ✓ — the same total.\n\n**What the manager should be told:** the outperformance was **roughly two-thirds selection and one-third allocation**, and the *only* negative contribution came from **debt security selection** — a specific, actionable finding no headline return figure could deliver.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"Allocation is which buckets; Selection is what's inside the bucket.\"**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Positive allocation with negative selection (or the reverse) is common and is exactly what makes attribution useful — a manager may be a good asset allocator and a poor stock picker, and should then run a portfolio of index funds with an active allocation overlay.\n\n---\n\n### 16.6.3 Market Timing versus Selectivity\n\n| | **Market timing** | **Selectivity (stock picking)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The decision** | Vary **market exposure / portfolio beta** ahead of market moves | Choose **which securities** to hold within the exposure |\n| **Successful version** | Raise beta before rallies; cut beta or hold cash before falls | Hold securities that outperform their sector or the index |\n| **Evidence of it** | The portfolio's beta or equity weight **changes systematically ahead of** market moves | Consistent positive alpha with a stable beta |\n| **How it is tested** | **Treynor–Mazuy** (adds a squared market-return term to the regression) and **Henriksson–Merton** (dual-beta model) | Jensen's alpha; selection effect in attribution |\n| **Reliability** | Consistently poor — very few managers time markets successfully | More commonly demonstrated, though still difficult |\n\n**Fama's decomposition of total excess return** ties the whole chapter together:\n\n> **Rp − Rf = [Return for systematic risk: β(Rm − Rf)] + [Selectivity: Jensen's alpha]**\n> and **Selectivity = Net selectivity + Return required for imperfect diversification**\n\n---\n\n### 16.6.4 ⭐ Net Selectivity\n\nJensen's alpha rewards a manager for *all* return above the **CAPM** requirement. But CAPM only charges for **systematic** risk. If the manager also took a lot of **avoidable specific risk**, part of that \"alpha\" is merely compensation for the risk of being under-diversified — not skill.\n\n**Net selectivity strips that part out** by charging the manager for **total** risk instead of just beta:\n\n> **Net selectivity = Rp − [Rf + (σp ÷ σm) × (Rm − Rf)]**\n> **Net selectivity = Jensen's alpha − Return required for imperfect diversification**\n\n**Worked example.** Rp = **18%**, σp = **22%**, β = **1.10**; Rf = **6%**, Rm = **13%**, σm = **16%**.\n\n1. **CAPM required return** = 6 + 1.10 × (13 − 6) = 6 + 7.7 = **13.70%**\n2. **Jensen's alpha (gross selectivity)** = 18 − 13.70 = **+4.30%**\n3. **Return required if we charge for TOTAL risk** = 6 + (22 ÷ 16) × 7 = 6 + (1.375 × 7) = 6 + 9.625 = **15.625%**\n4. **Cost of imperfect diversification** = 15.625 − 13.70 = **1.925%**\n5. **Net selectivity = 18 − 15.625 = +2.375%** (and indeed 4.30 − 1.925 = **2.375%** ✓)\n\n**Reading it:** of the manager's **4.30%** headline alpha, **1.925 percentage points were simply payment for carrying undiversified risk the client could have removed for free**. The **genuine, skill-based contribution is 2.375%** — still positive, so this manager passes the harder test.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** **Net selectivity can be negative while Jensen's alpha is positive.** That is precisely the diagnosis of a manager whose \"outperformance\" came from concentration, not skill. If **σp/σm = β** the portfolio is perfectly correlated with the market (fully diversified) and **net selectivity = alpha**.\n\n---\n\n### 16.6.5 Local Currency versus Foreign Currency Returns\n\nWhen an Indian client invests abroad, the rupee return has **two** components — and they **compound**, they do not add.\n\n> **Return in INR = (1 + Local currency return) × (1 + Currency return) − 1**\n> **Currency return = (Closing INR/USD ÷ Opening INR/USD) − 1** — a *rise* in the INR/USD rate is **rupee depreciation**, which is a **gain** for the Indian investor\n\n**Worked example 1 — rupee depreciates.** Mr Shah invests **₹10,00,000** in a US equity fund when the rate is **₹83/USD**. The fund returns **12% in USD** and the rupee ends at **₹87.15/USD**.\n\n- Currency return = (87.15 ÷ 83) − 1 = **+5.0%**\n- **Return in INR = (1.12 × 1.05) − 1 = 1.176 − 1 = 17.60%**\n- His ₹10,00,000 becomes **₹11,76,000**\n\nNote that **12% + 5% = 17%**, but the true answer is **17.6%**. The extra **0.6%** is the cross term (0.12 × 0.05).\n\n**Worked example 2 — rupee appreciates.** Same 12% USD return, but the rupee strengthens from ₹83 to **₹80.51/USD**.\n\n- Currency return = (80.51 ÷ 83) − 1 = **−3.0%**\n- **Return in INR = (1.12 × 0.97) − 1 = 1.0864 − 1 = 8.64%**\n\nA 12% dollar gain became an **8.64%** rupee gain. **The currency can consume a third of the return.**\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — a weak rupee is a tailwind for an Indian investor holding foreign assets.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap — never simply add the two returns.** For small numbers adding is a fair approximation, but the exam usually chooses figures where the cross term is visible, and the \"just added\" figure is offered as a distractor.\n\n**In practice:** performance should be attributed separately to the **local-market decision** and the **currency decision**, since a manager may be skilled at one and not the other, and currency exposure can be hedged independently (at a cost roughly equal to the interest-rate differential).\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Performance is defined by | **Return AND risk** together — never return alone |\n| Holding Period Return | **(End − Begin + Income) ÷ Begin** |\n| TWRR | Geometric chaining of sub-period returns; **cash flows neutralised** |\n| MWRR | The **IRR** of the cash flows; cash-flow timing fully counted |\n| TWRR measures | **The manager's skill** — SEBI's required basis for PMS disclosure |\n| MWRR measures | **The investor's actual experience** (a client's XIRR) |\n| TWRR = MWRR when | There are **no cash flows** during the period |\n| MWRR > TWRR when | The client added money **before a strong** period |\n| AMR | Simple average of periodic returns |\n| GMR | **[(1+R₁)…(1+Rₙ)]^(1/n) − 1** — the compounded average |\n| The inequality | **AMR ≥ GMR always**; equal only if all returns are identical |\n| Gap widens with | **Volatility** — approximately **GMR ≈ AMR − σ²/2** |\n| +50% then −50% | AMR **0%**; GMR **−13.4%**; ₹1,00,000 → **₹75,000** |\n| Use AMR for | Estimating the **next single period's** expected return |\n| Use GMR for | Reporting **realised multi-period** performance |\n| Net return | Gross **minus** fees, TER, brokerage, STT, custody, **18% GST**, exit load |\n| Post-tax | Net **minus tax** — investor-specific, so never published by the fund |\n| Equity taxation | STCG **20%** (≤12 m); LTCG **12.5%** above **₹1.25 lakh** (>12 m) |\n| Debt fund taxation (bought on/after 1 Apr 2023) | **Slab rate**, whatever the holding period |\n| CAGR | **(EV ÷ BV)^(1/n) − 1** — the GMR from two endpoints |\n| CAGR's weakness | **Hides the path entirely** — says nothing about volatility; start-date sensitive |\n| Fix for start-date sensitivity | **Rolling returns** |\n| Annualising a return | **(1 + r)^periods − 1** — compound, never multiply |\n| Annualising risk | **σ × √periods** — 4% monthly → **13.86%**; 1% daily → **15.87%** |\n| Why √t | **Variances add** over time, so standard deviation grows with **√t** |\n| SEBI return-display rule | **< 1 year → absolute**; **≥ 1 year → CAGR** |\n| Cash drag | Return lost by holding uninvested cash **in a rising market** |\n| Cash-drag-adjusted return | **(Rp − w_cash × R_cash) ÷ (1 − w_cash)** |\n| Beta return | The return earned merely for **market exposure** — cheap to buy |\n| Alpha (Jensen's) | **Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)]** — value added over the CAPM requirement |\n| Alpha is NOT | **Rp − Rm** — that ignores the beta taken |\n| Portfolio return | **Σ wᵢRᵢ** — a **weighted average** (risk is not) |\n| Total risk | **Standard deviation** — punishes upside and downside equally |\n| Downside deviation | **√[Σ(shortfalls below MAR)² ÷ n]** |\n| Maximum drawdown | Largest **peak-to-trough** fall |\n| VaR | Loss not exceeded at a stated **confidence** over a stated **horizon** |\n| Volatility bands | **±1σ ≈ 68%**, **±2σ ≈ 95%**, **±3σ ≈ 99.7%** |\n| σ's blind spots | **Fat tails**, liquidity risk, credit risk, backward-looking |\n| Tracking error | **Standard deviation of the ACTIVE return** (portfolio − benchmark) |\n| Tracking difference | The simple **return gap**; tracking error is its **volatility** |\n| SEBI TE cap | **2%** for equity index funds/ETFs on rolling one-year data |\n| Tracking error is NOT | Underperformance — consistent outperformance has low TE |\n| Total risk = | **Systematic + Unsystematic**; **σ²ᵢ = β²σ²ₘ + σ²ₑ** |\n| Systematic risk | Market-wide, **non-diversifiable**, measured by **β**, and **rewarded** |\n| Unsystematic risk | Company-specific, **diversifiable**, and **NOT rewarded** |\n| Diversification benefit | Most specific risk gone within roughly **20–30 well-spread** stocks |\n| R² | Share of variance explained by the market; **low R² → β, α, Treynor unreliable** |\n| Beta formula | **Cov(i,m) ÷ Var(m) = ρ × σᵢ ÷ σₘ** |\n| Beta example | ρ 0.80, σᵢ 24%, σₘ 16% → **β = 1.20** |\n| Portfolio beta | **Weighted average** of component betas |\n| Liquidity risk | Cannot sell at a fair price; **not captured by σ** (volatility smoothing) |\n| Credit risk | Default/downgrade; **AAA → D**; compensated by the **credit spread** |\n| **Sharpe** | **(Rp − Rf) ÷ σp** — excess return per unit of **TOTAL** risk |\n| **Treynor** | **(Rp − Rf) ÷ βp** — excess return per unit of **SYSTEMATIC** risk |\n| Sharpe vs Treynor | Sharpe for a **standalone** portfolio; Treynor for a **sub-portfolio** in a diversified whole |\n| If they disagree | Better on **Treynor** than Sharpe → **poorly diversified** (high specific risk) |\n| If they agree | The portfolio is **well diversified** |\n| **Sortino** | **(Rp − MAR) ÷ downside deviation** — penalises **only bad** volatility |\n| **Information Ratio** | **(Rp − Rb) ÷ Tracking Error = alpha ÷ TE** — skill **and consistency** |\n| IR benchmarks | **0.5 good, 0.75 very good, 1.0 exceptional** |\n| **M²** | **Rf + (Sharpe × σₘ)** — Sharpe restated as a **return %** |\n| M² alpha | **M² − Rm** |\n| M² ranking | **Always identical to Sharpe** — it adds interpretability, not information |\n| Good benchmark (SAMURAI) | **S**pecified in advance, **A**ppropriate, **M**easurable, **U**nambiguous, **R**eflective, **A**ccountable, **I**nvestable |\n| PRI vs TRI | PRI **omits dividends**; SEBI mandates **TRI** benchmarking for mutual funds |\n| SEBI benchmark tiers | **Tier 1** = category index; **Tier 2** = manager's style |\n| Customised benchmark | Blend indices in the **strategic** asset-allocation weights |\n| Blended example | 65% NIFTY TRI @15% + 35% bond index @7% = **12.2%** |\n| Benchmarking errors | Style mismatch, PRI not TRI, after-the-fact choice, cherry-picked dates, too-short period, ignoring risk, gross vs net, non-investable, survivorship bias |\n| Peer/universe analysis | Quartile ranking against same-mandate funds |\n| Peer analysis weaknesses | **Survivorship bias**, not investable, **not risk-adjusted**, shifting composition, small samples |\n| Attribution identity | **Active return = Allocation + Selection + Interaction** |\n| Allocation effect | **Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (R_b,sector − R_b,total)** — \"which buckets\" |\n| Selection effect | **Σ w_b × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector)** — \"what's inside the bucket\" |\n| Market timing | Varying **beta / market exposure**; tested by **Treynor–Mazuy** and **Henriksson–Merton** |\n| Selectivity | Security picking = **Jensen's alpha** |\n| Net selectivity | **Rp − [Rf + (σp ÷ σₘ)(Rm − Rf)]** = alpha **minus** the cost of imperfect diversification |\n| Net selectivity = alpha when | **σp/σₘ = β** — the portfolio is fully diversified |\n| Foreign currency return | **(1 + R_local) × (1 + R_currency) − 1** — **multiply, never add** |\n| Rupee depreciation | A **gain** for an Indian holder of foreign assets |\n\n> **Exam tip:** in every numerical question, first ask **\"which risk is in the denominator?\"** — σ (Sharpe), β (Treynor), downside deviation (Sortino) or tracking error (Information Ratio). Getting that one choice right is worth more marks than the arithmetic. The three highest-frequency traps are: **alpha is not simply Rp − Rm**; **AMR always exceeds GMR and the gap is the cost of volatility**; and **foreign-currency returns multiply rather than add**.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 16: Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Every formula here is a potential mark. Say the formula out loud, then say what goes in the DENOMINATOR and WHY.**\n\n**Q1.** What are the two parameters that together define portfolio performance?\n> **A:** **Return AND risk.** A return figure on its own is meaningless — 18% earned with 30 diversified large-caps is not the same performance as 18% earned with four leveraged micro-caps on borrowed money.\n\n**Q2.** State the Holding Period Return formula, and apply it: 1,000 shares bought at ₹250, sold nine months later at ₹278, dividend ₹6 per share.\n> **A:** **HPR = (Ending value − Beginning value + Income) ÷ Beginning value.** Here = (2,78,000 − 2,50,000 + 6,000) ÷ 2,50,000 = **13.6%** for nine months. Annualised = (1.136)^(12/9) − 1 = **18.53%**. Never omit the income term.\n\n**Q3.** Define TWRR and MWRR.\n> **A:** **TWRR** — break the period at every cash flow, compute each sub-period's return and **chain them geometrically**: (1+R₁)(1+R₂)… − 1. **MWRR** — the **IRR** of all the cash flows, so periods with more money invested dominate. A client's SIP **XIRR** is an MWRR; a factsheet CAGR is a TWRR.\n\n**Q4.** ⭐ WHY does TWRR measure manager skill?\n> **A:** Because it **neutralises the effect of client cash flows**. The manager decides what to buy, but the **client** decides when to add or withdraw money. Chaining percentage sub-period returns makes the rupee amount in each slice irrelevant, so TWRR grades only what the manager controls — which is why **SEBI mandates TWRR for PMS performance disclosure**.\n\n**Q5.** A PMS starts at ₹10 lakh, grows to ₹12 lakh in H1, the client adds ₹10 lakh, and it ends the year at ₹19.8 lakh. Compute TWRR and describe MWRR.\n> **A:** H1 = **+20%**; H2 = (19.8 − 22)/22 = **−10%**. **TWRR = (1.20 × 0.90) − 1 = +8%.** But ₹20 lakh went in and only ₹19.8 lakh came out, so the **MWRR is about −1.3%**. The manager delivered +8%; the client's own timing (adding ₹10 lakh just before the fall) destroyed the outcome.\n\n**Q6.** When are TWRR and MWRR identical, and which is larger when?\n> **A:** Identical when there are **no cash flows** during the period. **MWRR > TWRR** if the client added money **before a strong** period (good timing); **MWRR < TWRR** if money was added before a weak one.\n\n**Q7.** State AMR and GMR, the relationship between them, and apply both to returns of +25%, −10%, +15%.\n> **A:** **AMR = (R₁+…+Rₙ)/n = 10.00%.** **GMR = [(1+R₁)…(1+Rₙ)]^(1/n) − 1 = (1.29375)^(1/3) − 1 = 8.96%.** **AMR ≥ GMR always** — equal only when every return is identical — and the gap **widens with volatility**, roughly **GMR ≈ AMR − σ²/2**. Only the GMR reproduces the ending value: ₹10,00,000 × 1.0896³ = **₹12,93,750**.\n\n**Q8.** Returns of +50% then −50%. What do AMR and GMR say, and which is true?\n> **A:** AMR = **0%**, implying break-even. Reality: ₹1,00,000 → ₹1,50,000 → **₹75,000**. **GMR = √(1.50 × 0.50) − 1 = −13.40% p.a.** Percentage gains and losses are **not symmetric** — a 50% loss needs a 100% gain to recover.\n\n**Q9.** When should you use AMR, and when GMR?\n> **A:** **AMR** for estimating the **next single period's** expected return (it is the unbiased single-period estimate). **GMR (= CAGR)** for reporting **realised multi-period** performance and comparing track records. *\"Arithmetic is what you hoped for; geometric is what you got.\"*\n\n**Q10.** List the deductions that turn a gross return into a net return, and apply them: a ₹50 lakh PMS earns 15% gross, with a 2% fixed fee, a 20% performance fee above a 10% hurdle, and ₹25,000 of other costs.\n> **A:** Deductions: management fee / **TER**, **performance fee**, brokerage, **STT** and stamp duty, custody and fund accounting, **18% GST** on fees, exit load. Here: gross gain ₹7,50,000; fixed fee ₹1,00,000; performance fee 20% × (7,50,000 − 5,00,000) = ₹50,000; other ₹25,000 → costs **₹1,75,000**. Net gain **₹5,75,000 = 11.50%** — costs ate **3.5 percentage points**.\n\n**Q11.** Give the Indian tax treatment for equity and debt mutual funds, and explain why a fund cannot publish a post-tax return.\n> **A:** Listed equity / equity MF: **STCG 20%** (held ≤ 12 months); **LTCG 12.5%** (held > 12 months) on gains above **₹1.25 lakh** per financial year. Debt funds bought on/after 1 Apr 2023: **slab rate**, whatever the holding period. Tax is **investor-specific** — it depends on the client's slab, holding period and use of the exemption — so published returns are always **pre-tax**.\n\n**Q12.** State the CAGR formula, apply it to ₹5,00,000 growing to ₹11,00,000 in 7 years, and give its two weaknesses.\n> **A:** **CAGR = (EV ÷ BV)^(1/n) − 1** = (2.20)^(1/7) − 1 = **11.92% p.a.** Weakness 1: it **hides the path completely** — it says nothing about volatility. Weakness 2: being **point-to-point** it is **start-date sensitive**; the professional fix is **rolling returns**.\n\n**Q13.** How do you annualise a return, how do you annualise risk, and why do they differ?\n> **A:** Return: **(1 + periodic return)^(periods per year) − 1** — compound, never multiply (4% quarterly → **16.99%**). Risk: **σ_annual = σ_periodic × √(periods per year)** (4% monthly σ → 4 × √12 = **13.86%**; 1% daily → **15.87%**). They differ because returns are assumed **independent**, so **variances add** over time — variance grows with **t**, so σ grows with **√t**.\n\n**Q14.** What is SEBI's rule on displaying mutual fund returns for short periods?\n> **A:** Periods of **less than one year must be shown in absolute terms**; **one year and above** must be shown as **CAGR**. Annualising a 6% quarterly gain into 26.2% is exactly what the rule prevents.\n\n**Q15.** Define cash drag, give the adjustment formula, and apply it to a fund holding 10% cash at 4% with 90% in equity returning 15%.\n> **A:** Cash drag is the return lost by holding **uninvested cash in a rising market**. **Adjusted return = (Rp − w_cash × R_cash) ÷ (1 − w_cash)**. Reported = (0.90 × 15) + (0.10 × 4) = **13.9%**; **cash drag = 1.1 percentage points**; adjusted = (13.9 − 0.4) ÷ 0.90 = **15.0%** — exactly the equity return, isolating the manager's selection.\n\n**Q16.** Distinguish beta return from alpha return.\n> **A:** **Beta return** is what you earn simply for **market exposure** — buyable for a few basis points via an index fund. **Alpha return** is what you earn **above** what that exposure justified — the only thing an active fee should be buying. *\"Beta is rented; alpha is earned.\"*\n\n**Q17.** ⭐ State Jensen's Alpha, compute it for Rp = 16%, Rf = 6.5%, β = 1.20, Rm = 13%, and name the classic mistake.\n> **A:** **α = Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)]**. Required = 6.5 + (1.20 × 6.5) = **14.30%** → **α = 16 − 14.30 = +1.70%**. The fund beat the index by 3 points, but 1.3 of those were merely **bought with beta**. The classic mistake: treating alpha as **Rp − Rm**, which ignores the risk taken. The two coincide **only when β = 1**.\n\n**Q18.** How is portfolio return computed, and what does the same rule NOT apply to?\n> **A:** **Rp = Σ wᵢRᵢ** — a straight **weighted average**, using **market-value weights at the start** of the period, with Σwᵢ = 1. It does **not** apply to **risk**: portfolio σ is almost always **below** the weighted average of the component σs, because correlations are below +1.\n\n**Q19.** Distinguish total risk from downside risk, give the downside deviation formula, and apply both to returns of 0%, 3%, 20%, 18%, 19% with MAR 6%.\n> **A:** **σ** treats a +15% surprise and a −15% surprise as equally risky; **downside deviation = √[Σ(min(Rᵢ − MAR, 0))² ÷ n]** counts only **shortfalls**, but **every period still counts in n**. Mean = 12%. σ: (144+81+64+36+49)/5 = 74.8 → **σ = 8.65%**. Downside: shortfalls 6 and 3 → (36+9)/5 = 9 → **downside deviation = 3.00%**. Most of this fund's volatility was **upside**.\n\n**Q20.** Name four downside-risk measures other than downside deviation.\n> **A:** **Maximum drawdown** (largest peak-to-trough fall — ₹52 lakh peak to ₹36.40 lakh trough = **30%**), **Value at Risk** (loss not exceeded at a stated confidence over a stated horizon), **semi-variance**, and the **downside capture ratio** (fund's fall ÷ benchmark's fall; below 100% is good).\n\n**Q21.** State the volatility probability bands and apply them to a fund with 12% expected return and 18% σ.\n> **A:** Mean **±1σ ≈ 68%**, **±2σ ≈ 95%**, **±3σ ≈ 99.7%**. So 68% of years fall between **−6% and +30%**, and 95% between **−24% and +48%**. On ₹20 lakh, a −24% year is a **₹4,80,000** paper loss — the number the client must see *before* investing. Real markets have **fat tails**, so σ understates true extremes.\n\n**Q22.** Define tracking error, distinguish it from tracking difference, and explain why it is NOT underperformance.\n> **A:** **Tracking error = the standard deviation of the ACTIVE return** (portfolio − benchmark). **Tracking difference** is the simple **return gap**. SEBI requires both to be disclosed for index funds/ETFs and caps equity index fund/ETF tracking error at **2%** on rolling one-year data. A fund that beats its index by **exactly 4% every quarter** has a **tracking error of zero** — TE measures the **variability** of the gap, not its sign or size.\n\n**Q23.** ⭐ Contrast systematic and unsystematic risk, and say which one is rewarded and why.\n> **A:** **Systematic** = market-wide (RBI rates, inflation, GDP, oil, currency, policy), **non-diversifiable**, measured by **beta**, and **rewarded**. **Unsystematic** = company-specific (fraud, recall, strike, litigation), **diversifiable**, and **NOT rewarded** — the market will not pay a premium for a risk you could remove for free by diversifying. **σ²ᵢ = β²σ²ₘ + σ²ₑ.** Most specific risk disappears within roughly **20–30 well-spread** stocks.\n\n**Q24.** What does R² tell you, and why does it matter for beta?\n> **A:** The **proportion of the portfolio's variance explained by the market**. Above ~95% is essentially an index fund; **below about 70% means a lot of non-market risk**, which makes **beta, Jensen's alpha and the Treynor ratio unreliable** for that fund.\n\n**Q25.** State the beta formula, compute β for σᵢ = 24%, σₘ = 16%, ρ = 0.80, and say how portfolio beta is found.\n> **A:** **β = Cov(i,m) ÷ Var(m) = ρ × (σᵢ ÷ σₘ)** = 0.80 × (24 ÷ 16) = **1.20** — a 10% market rise implies a 12% rise. **Portfolio beta IS the simple weighted average** of component betas (0.40×1.40 + 0.35×0.90 + 0.25×0.60 = **1.025**) — unlike portfolio standard deviation, which is not.\n\n**Q26.** Why is liquidity risk not captured by standard deviation?\n> **A:** Illiquid assets are **valued infrequently**, which smooths reported returns and makes measured σ **artificially low** — \"volatility smoothing\". Never call a real-estate, unlisted or thin small-cap portfolio low-risk because its σ looks small. SEBI's responses include **stress testing** for mid/small-cap schemes and **side-pocketing** for credit-impaired securities.\n\n**Q27.** What are the components of credit risk, and how is it compensated?\n> **A:** **Default risk** (issuer fails to pay), **downgrade risk** (rating falls and the price drops immediately), and **credit spread risk** (spreads widen market-wide). Rated by **CRISIL, ICRA, CARE, India Ratings, Brickwork** on a scale from **AAA** down through **BBB** (lowest investment grade) to **D** (default). Compensation is the **credit spread** over a same-maturity G-Sec. Because losses are sudden and one-sided, **σ badly understates credit risk**.\n\n**Q28.** State the Sharpe ratio, and apply it: Rf 6%; Fund A returns 16% with σ 20%; Fund B returns 12% with σ 10%.\n> **A:** **Sharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp** — excess return per unit of **TOTAL risk**. A: 10/20 = **0.50**. B: 6/10 = **0.60**. **Fund B wins** despite the lower return. Proof: borrow at 6% to hold twice as much of B — σ becomes 20% (matching A) and the return becomes (2 × 12) − 6 = **18%**, beating A's 16% at identical risk.\n\n**Q29.** State the Treynor ratio and say when it is the right measure.\n> **A:** **Treynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp** — excess return per unit of **SYSTEMATIC risk**. Use it when the fund is **one sleeve of an already diversified portfolio**, because that fund's specific risk has already been diversified away at the total-portfolio level, so charging for it would be wrong.\n\n**Q30.** ⭐ Rf = 6%, σₘ = 16%. Fund X: 15% return, σ 25%, β 1.00. Fund Y: 13% return, σ 14%, β 0.80. Compute both ratios and interpret the disagreement.\n> **A:** X: Sharpe 9/25 = **0.36**, Treynor 9/1.00 = **9.00**. Y: Sharpe 7/14 = **0.50**, Treynor 7/0.80 = **8.75**. They **disagree** → **the fund that ranks better on Treynor than on Sharpe is poorly diversified**. X's specific risk = √(25² − 16²) = **19.2%** and its **R² is only 41%**, versus Y's 5.7% and **84%**. Choose **Y** for a whole-portfolio client; **X** only as a small satellite. **If the two rankings agree, both portfolios are well diversified.**\n\n**Q31.** State the Sortino ratio, explain why it exists, and apply it to the fund in Q19.\n> **A:** **Sortino = (Rp − MAR) ÷ downside deviation.** It exists because investors do not experience an unexpectedly **large gain** as risk — penalising a manager for **upside** volatility is illogical. With MAR = Rf = 6%: **Sharpe = 6/8.65 = 0.69** but **Sortino = 6/3.00 = 2.00**. Always check the two funds use the **same MAR** before comparing.\n\n**Q32.** State the Information Ratio and use it to separate two managers who both beat the benchmark by 3.0%, one with 3% tracking error and one with 12%.\n> **A:** **IR = (Rp − Rb) ÷ Tracking Error = alpha ÷ tracking error** — it measures **skill AND consistency**. First manager **IR = 1.00**; second **IR = 0.25**. The same result achieved with **four times the active risk** is far more likely to be **luck** than repeatable skill. Rough bands: **0.5 good, 0.75 very good, 1.0 exceptional**. A **negative IR** means you paid an active fee for a worse-than-index result.\n\n**Q33.** State M², compute it for a fund returning 21% with σ 25% (Rf 6%, market 14% with σ 15%), and verify it.\n> **A:** **M² = Rf + (Sharpe × σₘ)** and **M² alpha = M² − Rm**. Sharpe = 15/25 = **0.60** → **M² = 6 + (0.60 × 15) = 15.0%**, so **M² alpha = +1.0 point**. Verify by risk-matching: hold **60%** in the fund and **40%** in T-bills → σ = 0.6 × 25 = **15%** (the market's risk) and return = (0.6 × 21) + (0.4 × 6) = **15.0%** ✓. M² **always ranks funds identically to Sharpe** — it adds interpretability, not information.\n\n**Q34.** Give the seven characteristics of a good benchmark.\n> **A:** **SAMURAI** — **S**pecified in advance, **A**ppropriate (right asset class, style and market cap), **M**easurable, **U**nambiguous, **R**eflective of current investment opinion, **A**ccountable (the manager owns it), **I**nvestable.\n\n**Q35.** What is the difference between PRI and TRI, and what has SEBI mandated?\n> **A:** A **Price Return Index ignores dividends**; a **Total Return Index reinvests them**. Benchmarking a fund against a PRI flatters it by roughly the dividend yield every year, so **SEBI has mandated TRI benchmarking for mutual funds**, along with a **two-tier structure** — a **Tier-1** benchmark for the scheme category and an optional **Tier-2** for the manager's style.\n\n**Q36.** How do you build a customised benchmark, and what does 65% NIFTY 50 TRI at 15% plus 35% bond index at 7% produce?\n> **A:** Blend the indices in the portfolio's **strategic** asset-allocation weights, specified **in advance in the IPS**, using **total-return** versions and rebalancing on the policy schedule. Here: (0.65 × 15) + (0.35 × 7) = 9.75 + 2.45 = **12.20%** — a far fairer test than comparing a balanced portfolio to the NIFTY's 15%.\n\n**Q37.** Name six benchmarking errors.\n> **A:** **Style/category mismatch** (a mid-cap fund against the NIFTY 50 measures the mid-cap premium, not skill); using **PRI instead of TRI**; choosing the benchmark **after the fact** (\"benchmark shopping\"); **cherry-picked start dates** (fix: rolling returns); **too short a period**; and **ignoring risk** or comparing a **gross** fund return to a **net** benchmark. Also: non-investable benchmarks, survivorship bias and style drift.\n\n**Q38.** What is managers' universe analysis, and what are its main weaknesses?\n> **A:** **Quartile ranking** a fund against the universe of **same-mandate** funds. Weaknesses: **survivorship bias** (failed funds leave the sample, lifting the average), the universe is **not investable**, it is **not risk-adjusted** (the top quartile in a bull market is often just the highest-beta quartile), shifting composition, loose category definitions, small samples and self-selection bias.\n\n**Q39.** Can a fund be top-quartile among peers and still have failed the client?\n> **A:** **Yes** — if the whole category underperformed its index, the best of a bad group still lost to a cheap index fund. Peer analysis and benchmark analysis are **complements, not substitutes**; always look at both.\n\n**Q40.** State the performance attribution identity and the allocation and selection formulas.\n> **A:** **Active return = Allocation + Selection + Interaction.** **Allocation = Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (R_b,sector − R_b,total)** — *\"which buckets\"*. **Selection = Σ w_b × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector)** — *\"what's inside the bucket\"*. **Interaction = Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector)**.\n\n**Q41.** Benchmark: 60% equity @12%, 40% debt @7%. Portfolio: 75% equity @14%, 25% debt @6.5%. Decompose the active return.\n> **A:** Benchmark **10.00%**, portfolio **12.125%**, active **+2.125%**. **Allocation** = (0.15 × 2) + (−0.15 × −3) = **+0.75%**. **Selection** = (0.60 × 2) + (0.40 × −0.5) = **+1.00%**. **Interaction** = (0.15 × 2) + (−0.15 × −0.5) = **+0.375%**. Total **2.125%** ✓ — mostly **selection**, and the only negative contribution came from **debt security selection**.\n\n**Q42.** Distinguish market timing from selectivity, and name the models that test timing.\n> **A:** **Market timing** = varying **portfolio beta / market exposure** ahead of market moves. **Selectivity** = picking better securities within that exposure (= Jensen's alpha). Timing is tested by **Treynor–Mazuy** (adds a squared market-return term) and **Henriksson–Merton** (a dual-beta model). Evidence of successful timing is consistently **poor**.\n\n**Q43.** ⭐ Define net selectivity, give the formula, and apply it: Rp 18%, σp 22%, β 1.10, Rf 6%, Rm 13%, σₘ 16%.\n> **A:** **Net selectivity = Rp − [Rf + (σp ÷ σₘ) × (Rm − Rf)]** — Jensen's alpha **minus the return required for imperfect diversification**, because it charges the manager for **total** risk instead of just beta. CAPM required = 6 + 1.10 × 7 = **13.70%** → **alpha = +4.30%**. Total-risk required = 6 + (22/16) × 7 = **15.625%** → **net selectivity = 18 − 15.625 = +2.375%**. The **1.925%** difference was mere compensation for being **under-diversified**.\n\n**Q44.** When does net selectivity equal Jensen's alpha, and what does a negative net selectivity alongside a positive alpha mean?\n> **A:** They are equal when **σp/σₘ = β** — the portfolio is **perfectly diversified**. **Negative net selectivity with positive alpha** diagnoses a manager whose apparent outperformance came from **concentration, not skill**.\n\n**Q45.** How do you combine a foreign-currency return with a local-market return, and what does a 12% USD return become in rupees if the rate moves from ₹83 to ₹87.15, or from ₹83 to ₹80.51?\n> **A:** **Multiply, never add: Return in INR = (1 + R_local) × (1 + R_currency) − 1.** At ₹87.15: currency return = (87.15/83) − 1 = **+5.0%** → INR return = 1.12 × 1.05 − 1 = **17.60%** (not 17% — the extra 0.6% is the cross term). At ₹80.51: currency = **−3.0%** → 1.12 × 0.97 − 1 = **8.64%**. A **rise** in the INR/USD rate is **rupee depreciation**, which is a **gain** for the Indian investor.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 16: Portfolio Performance Measurement and Evaluation — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **This is the most calculation-heavy chapter in the syllabus: always ask which risk sits in the denominator.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Portfolio performance is properly defined by:\nA) The return alone  B) The number of holdings  C) The return AND the risk taken to earn it  D) The size of the fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Performance is a **two-dimensional** quantity. Two funds that both returned 18% did not perform equally if one took twice the risk.\nA) A return figure on its own is meaningless. B) Holding count is a crude proxy for diversification, not performance. D) Fund size affects capacity, not risk-adjusted performance.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The Holding Period Return formula is:\nA) (End − Begin + Income) ÷ Begin  B) (End − Begin) ÷ End  C) (End ÷ Begin)^(1/n) − 1  D) (End − Begin) ÷ n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — HPR captures **price change plus income**, divided by the **opening** value.\nB) Divides by the ending value — wrong base, and omits income. C) Is the **CAGR** formula. D) Is a simple average of the rupee gain, not a return.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The Time-Weighted Rate of Return is computed by:\nA) Taking the IRR of all cash flows  B) Averaging monthly returns arithmetically  C) Dividing profit by average capital  D) Geometrically chaining the returns of each sub-period between cash flows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Break the period at every cash flow, compute a clean return for each slice, then multiply: **(1+R₁)(1+R₂)… − 1**.\nA) Describes **MWRR**. B) An arithmetic average overstates compounded performance. C) Is a rough money-weighted approximation, not TWRR.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** The Money-Weighted Rate of Return is best described as:\nA) The simple average return  B) The IRR of the portfolio's cash flows  C) The benchmark return  D) The return after tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — MWRR is the discount rate that equates all inflows and outflows, so periods with more money invested dominate the answer.\nA) An average of period returns is a time-weighted idea. C) The benchmark is a separate comparison. D) Tax is an entirely different adjustment.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** SEBI requires portfolio managers to disclose performance using:\nA) MWRR, gross of fees  B) Arithmetic mean returns  C) TWRR, net of all fees and expenses  D) Whichever measure the manager prefers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — TWRR neutralises client cash flows and net-of-fee reporting shows what clients actually kept.\nA) MWRR would reflect the client's timing, not the manager's skill, and gross figures overstate outcomes. B) Arithmetic means inflate volatile records. D) Discretion is precisely what the rule removes.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** TWRR is preferred for judging a manager because it:\nA) Neutralises client cash flows, which the manager does not control  B) Always produces a higher number  C) Is simpler to compute  D) Includes the effect of tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The manager decides **what to buy**; the client decides **when to add or withdraw**. Grading the manager on the client's timing measures the wrong person.\nB) TWRR can be higher or lower than MWRR. C) It is generally *harder* — it needs a valuation at every cash flow. D) TWRR is a pre-tax measure.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The relationship between arithmetic and geometric mean returns is:\nA) GMR is always greater than AMR  B) They are always equal  C) There is no fixed relationship  D) AMR ≥ GMR, with equality only when all returns are identical\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Percentage gains and losses are not symmetric, so simply averaging them overstates the compounded outcome.\nA) Reverses the inequality. B) True only in the special case of zero volatility. C) The inequality is mathematically guaranteed.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The gap between AMR and GMR widens as:\nA) The fee level falls  B) The volatility of returns rises  C) The holding period shortens  D) The benchmark changes\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Approximately **GMR ≈ AMR − σ²/2**, so the gap grows with the *square* of volatility. The gap **is** the cost of volatility.\nA) Fees reduce both means equally. C) Period length does not drive the gap; dispersion does. D) The benchmark is irrelevant to a fund's own two means.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** CAGR is calculated as:\nA) (End − Begin) ÷ Begin  B) Sum of annual returns ÷ n  C) (End ÷ Begin)^(1/n) − 1  D) (End − Begin) ÷ n\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CAGR is the geometric mean expressed from two endpoints, where **n is the number of years**.\nA) Is the total (holding-period) return, not annualised. B) Is the arithmetic mean. D) Is a rupee-per-year figure, not a rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Net return is gross return minus:\nA) All fees, expenses, transaction costs and GST  B) Tax only  C) Inflation  D) The benchmark return\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Management fee/TER, performance fee, brokerage, STT, custody, **18% GST** on fees, and exit load.\nB) Tax is the *next* step — post-tax return. C) Deducting inflation gives the **real** return. D) Deducting the benchmark gives the **active** return.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Post-tax return is:\nA) The same for every investor  B) Always higher than pre-tax return  C) Published by every mutual fund  D) Investor-specific, depending on slab, holding period and exemptions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Tax depends on the individual's circumstances, which is exactly why published returns are always pre-tax.\nA) Two investors in different slabs keep different amounts. B) Tax can only reduce a gain. C) A fund cannot compute one post-tax figure for everybody.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** To annualise a return earned over part of a year, you should:\nA) Multiply it by the number of periods  B) Compound it: (1 + r)^periods − 1  C) Divide it by the number of periods  D) Take its square root\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Returns compound; they do not add. A 4% quarterly return annualises to **16.99%**, not 16%.\nA) Simple multiplication ignores compounding. C) Dividing de-annualises. D) The square root rule applies to **risk**, not return.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** To annualise a standard deviation you:\nA) Multiply by the number of periods  B) Divide by the number of periods  C) Multiply by the square root of the number of periods  D) Leave it unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **σ_annual = σ_periodic × √periods**, because *variances* add over time and σ is the square root of variance.\nA) Would massively overstate risk. B) Wrong direction entirely. D) A monthly σ is not an annual σ.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Cash drag refers to:\nA) The return lost by holding uninvested cash in a rising market  B) The fee charged on cash balances  C) A delay in paying redemptions  D) The cost of borrowing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Cash earns a money-market rate rather than the asset-class return, so it dilutes performance when markets rise.\nB) Fees are a separate cost. C) That is a liquidity or operational issue. D) That is the cost of leverage.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Jensen's alpha is:\nA) Rp − Rm  B) (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp  C) Rp ÷ Rm  D) Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)]\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Alpha is the excess over the **CAPM-required** return for the beta actually taken.\nA) Simple excess over the benchmark ignores the risk taken — the single most common error in this chapter. B) Is the Sharpe ratio. C) Is a meaningless ratio of two returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Beta measures:\nA) Total risk  B) Sensitivity to market movements — systematic risk  C) Downside risk  D) Liquidity risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — β = Cov(i,m) ÷ Var(m). A beta of 1.2 implies a 12% move for a 10% market move.\nA) Total risk is standard deviation. C) That is downside deviation or semi-variance. D) Beta is blind to liquidity.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Portfolio return is:\nA) The simple (unweighted) average of component returns  B) The highest component return  C) The weighted average of component returns  D) Always below the weighted average\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Rp = Σ wᵢRᵢ**, using market-value weights at the start of the period.\nA) Ignores how much money is in each holding. B) No portfolio earns its best holding's return. D) That statement is true of **risk**, not return.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Total risk is measured by:\nA) Standard deviation  B) Beta  C) Tracking error  D) Downside deviation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Standard deviation captures the full dispersion of returns, both systematic and unsystematic.\nB) Captures only systematic risk. C) Captures only benchmark-relative risk. D) Captures only shortfalls below a target.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Downside deviation counts:\nA) All deviations from the mean  B) Only positive returns  C) Only the single worst year  D) Only returns falling below the target (MAR)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Shortfalls below the MAR are squared and summed, but **every period still counts in the denominator n**.\nA) That is standard deviation. B) Upside is exactly what downside deviation excludes. C) That would be a maximum-loss measure, not a deviation.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Tracking error is:\nA) A dealing or settlement error  B) The standard deviation of the active return  C) The simple return gap versus the benchmark  D) The fund's beta\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It measures how *variable* the portfolio-minus-benchmark gap is.\nA) Nothing to do with operations. C) That is **tracking difference** — a closely related but distinct measure. D) Beta measures market sensitivity, not benchmark-relative variability.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Systematic risk is:\nA) Company-specific and diversifiable  B) Removed by holding about 30 stocks  C) Market-wide, non-diversifiable, and measured by beta  D) Never compensated by the market\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Its drivers are economy-wide: RBI policy, inflation, GDP, currency, crude, global sentiment.\nA) Describes unsystematic risk. B) Diversification removes only the specific component. D) Systematic risk is precisely the risk that **is** rewarded.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Unsystematic risk can be reduced by:\nA) Diversification across securities that do not move together  B) Using leverage  C) Increasing portfolio beta  D) Nothing — it is unavoidable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Most specific risk disappears within roughly **20–30 well-spread** holdings; what matters is that they are genuinely different.\nB) Leverage magnifies all risk. C) Raising beta adds systematic risk. D) That is true of systematic risk, not specific risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which risk does the market reward investors for bearing?\nA) Unsystematic risk  B) Both equally  C) Neither  D) Systematic risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The market pays a premium only for risk that **cannot be avoided**.\nA) Specific risk can be removed at almost zero cost by diversifying, so no rational investor would pay a premium for it. B) They are treated very differently. C) The equity risk premium exists precisely as compensation for systematic risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The Sharpe ratio is:\nA) (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp  B) (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp  C) (Rp − Rb) ÷ tracking error  D) Rf + (Sharpe × σm)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Excess return per unit of **total** risk.\nA) Is Treynor. C) Is the Information Ratio. D) Is the M² formula, which uses Sharpe as an input.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The Treynor ratio is:\nA) (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp  B) (Rp − MAR) ÷ downside deviation  C) (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp  D) alpha ÷ tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Excess return per unit of **systematic** risk only.\nA) Is Sharpe. B) Is Sortino. D) Is the Information Ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The denominator of the Sortino ratio is:\nA) Downside deviation  B) Standard deviation  C) Beta  D) Tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sortino penalises only the volatility investors actually dislike.\nB) Would make it the Sharpe ratio. C) Would make it Treynor. D) Would make it the Information Ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The Information Ratio equals:\nA) alpha ÷ beta  B) (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp  C) Rp ÷ Rb  D) Active return ÷ tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — It measures both the **size** and the **consistency** of benchmark-beating performance.\nA) Mixes two unrelated quantities. B) Is Sharpe. C) Is a meaningless ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** M² expresses risk-adjusted performance as:\nA) A pure ratio  B) A percentage return at the market's level of risk  C) A beta  D) A rupee amount\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **M² = Rf + (Sharpe × σm)**, which a client can compare directly with the index return.\nA) That is what Sharpe already gives; M² translates it. C) M² has nothing to do with beta. D) It is a rate, not an amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** The beta of the market index is, by definition:\nA) 0  B) 0.5  C) 1.0  D) It varies with the period\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The market moves exactly with itself. Cash or T-bills have a beta of about **0**.\nA) Is the beta of a risk-free asset. B) Would describe a defensive fund. D) The market's own beta is fixed at 1 by construction.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** A Total Return Index differs from a Price Return Index because it:\nA) Reinvests dividends  B) Excludes dividends  C) Is not investable  D) Uses equal weights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — SEBI mandates **TRI** benchmarking for mutual funds so that funds cannot look better simply because the index ignored dividends.\nB) That is the PRI. C) Both index types are equally replicable in principle. D) Weighting scheme is a separate design choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A benchmark must be \"specified in advance\" so that:\nA) It costs less to compute  B) SEBI can audit it  C) It can be changed later if needed  D) The manager cannot pick a flattering index once the results are known\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Choosing the benchmark afterwards is \"benchmark shopping\" and destroys the comparison.\nA) Cost is irrelevant. B) Auditability is a by-product, not the reason. C) The purpose is precisely to prevent later changes.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** The main statistical bias in peer group (managers' universe) analysis is:\nA) Rounding error  B) Survivorship bias  C) Currency bias  D) Rating bias\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Failed funds are merged or wound up and vanish from the universe, so the surviving average looks better than reality.\nA) Trivial and immaterial. C) Relevant only to cross-border portfolios. D) Applies to credit analysis, not to peer universes.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Performance attribution decomposes active return into:\nA) Alpha and beta  B) Gross and net  C) Allocation, selection and interaction  D) Systematic and unsystematic\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Allocation asks \"which buckets\"; selection asks \"what was inside the bucket\"; interaction is the joint effect.\nA) That is a return decomposition, not an attribution to decisions. B) That is a cost distinction. D) That is a risk decomposition.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Net selectivity is Jensen's alpha minus:\nA) The return required for imperfect diversification  B) Fees  C) Tax  D) The risk-free rate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — It charges the manager for **total** risk rather than just beta, removing the part of \"alpha\" that merely paid for avoidable specific risk.\nB) Fees are removed before performance is measured. C) Tax is investor-specific. D) Rf is already inside the alpha calculation.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** An Indian investor's rupee return on a foreign investment is:\nA) Local return only  B) Currency return only  C) Local return + currency return  D) (1 + local return) × (1 + currency return) − 1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The two returns **compound**; simply adding them omits the cross term.\nA) and B) each ignore half the outcome. C) Is a common approximation but understates (or overstates) the true figure whenever both returns are meaningful.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor buys 500 shares at ₹400, receives a ₹8 per share dividend, and sells at ₹436. The HPR is:\nA) 9.0%  B) 10.0%  C) 11.0%  D) 22.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Begin = 500 × ₹400 = **₹2,00,000**. End = 500 × ₹436 = **₹2,18,000**. Income = 500 × ₹8 = **₹4,000**. HPR = (2,18,000 − 2,00,000 + 4,000) ÷ 2,00,000 = 22,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = **11.0%**.\nA) Omits the dividend (18,000 ÷ 2,00,000). B) Corresponds to a ₹20,000 gain — neither the ₹18,000 price gain nor the ₹22,000 total. D) Is the rupee gain of ₹22,000 misread as a percentage of ₹1 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An investment returns 8% over six months. Annualised, that is:\nA) 16.00%  B) 16.64%  C) 8.00%  D) 4.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.08)² − 1 = 1.1664 − 1 = **16.64%**.\nA) Simply doubles the return, ignoring compounding. C) Is the un-annualised six-month figure. D) Halves it — the wrong direction entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A portfolio returns +12% in the first half-year and −5% in the second. The TWRR for the year is:\nA) 7.00%  B) 6.00%  C) 17.00%  D) 6.40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — TWRR chains geometrically: (1.12 × 0.95) − 1 = 1.064 − 1 = **6.40%**.\nA) Adds the two returns (12 − 5), ignoring compounding. B) Is an arbitrary rounding. C) Adds the absolute sizes of the moves.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client transferred a large sum into her portfolio one week before a strong market rally. Relative to TWRR, her MWRR will be:\nA) Higher, because more money was invested during the strong period  B) Lower  C) Identical  D) Impossible to determine\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — MWRR weights each period by the money invested in it. Good client timing lifts MWRR above TWRR; bad timing pushes it below.\nB) Would be the case had she added money before a *fall*. C) They coincide only when there are **no** cash flows. D) The direction follows logically from the timing.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A fund's annual returns are 18%, −4%, 12% and 6%. Its arithmetic mean return is:\nA) 7.5%  B) 8.0%  C) 8.5%  D) 32.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (18 − 4 + 12 + 6) ÷ 4 = 32 ÷ 4 = **8.0%**.\nA) and C) are arithmetic slips. D) Is the **sum** of the returns, not the mean.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A fund returns +20% in year 1 and −10% in year 2. Its geometric mean return is:\nA) 5.00%  B) 4.50%  C) 3.92%  D) 8.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — GMR = √(1.20 × 0.90) − 1 = √1.08 − 1 = 1.0392 − 1 = **3.92% p.a.**\nA) Is the **arithmetic** mean (20 − 10)/2 — which overstates the true compounded outcome. B) Is a midpoint guess. D) Is the two-year total return of 8%, not the annual rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** AMR equals GMR only when:\nA) Returns are all positive  B) The period is exactly one year  C) Fees are zero  D) Every period's return is identical (zero volatility)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — With no dispersion there is nothing for compounding to erode, so the two means coincide.\nA) A series of differing positive returns still has AMR > GMR. B) With one period both means equal that return trivially, but the general condition is zero volatility. C) Fees shift both means equally.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹8,00,000 over 6 years. The CAGR is:\nA) 12.25%  B) 16.67%  C) 100.00%  D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — (8,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/6) − 1 = 2^(1/6) − 1 = **12.25% p.a.**\nB) Divides the 100% total gain by 6 — a simple average that ignores compounding. C) Is the total return over the whole period. D) Understates it; ₹4,00,000 × 1.10⁶ is only about ₹7,09,000.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A ₹80,00,000 portfolio earns 13% gross; total fees and expenses come to 2.6% of assets. The net return and net gain are:\nA) 13.0% and ₹10.40 lakh  B) 15.6% and ₹12.48 lakh  C) 2.6% and ₹2.08 lakh  D) 10.4% and ₹8.32 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Net return = 13% − 2.6% = **10.4%**; net gain = 10.4% × ₹80,00,000 = **₹8,32,000**.\nA) Is the gross figure, ignoring costs. B) Adds the costs instead of deducting them. C) Is the cost itself, not the return.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor in the 30% slab holds a bank FD paying 7.2%. Her post-tax return is:\nA) 7.20%  B) 4.32%  C) 5.04%  D) 2.16%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Interest is taxed at the slab rate: 7.2% × (1 − 0.30) = **5.04%**.\nA) Ignores tax entirely. B) Applies a 40% rate. D) Is the tax amount (7.2 × 0.30), not the post-tax return.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A fund returns 3% in a quarter. Annualised, that is:\nA) 12.00%  B) 12.55%  C) 12.36%  D) 3.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.03)⁴ − 1 = 1.1255 − 1 = **12.55%**.\nA) Multiplies by 4, ignoring compounding. C) Would be the answer for a 6% half-yearly return. D) Is the un-annualised quarterly figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A fund's monthly standard deviation is 5%. Its annualised standard deviation is:\nA) 60.00%  B) 15.00%  C) 5.00%  D) 17.32%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — σ_annual = 5% × √12 = 5 × 3.4641 = **17.32%**.\nA) Multiplies by 12 instead of √12 — the classic error. B) Uses √9. C) Leaves the monthly figure unchanged.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A fund holds 15% in cash earning 5% and 85% in equities returning 16%. The cash drag is:\nA) 1.65 percentage points  B) 11.00 percentage points  C) 0.75 percentage points  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Reported return = (0.85 × 16) + (0.15 × 5) = 13.6 + 0.75 = **14.35%**. A fully invested portfolio would have returned **16%**, so the drag is 16 − 14.35 = **1.65 pp**.\nB) Is the gap between the equity and cash returns. C) Is the cash's own contribution to the portfolio return. D) Cash unambiguously dragged in a rising market.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** For the same fund (15% cash at 5%, 85% equity at 16%, reported return 14.35%), the cash-drag-adjusted return is:\nA) 14.35%  B) 13.60%  C) 15.00%  D) 16.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — (14.35 − 0.15 × 5) ÷ (1 − 0.15) = (14.35 − 0.75) ÷ 0.85 = 13.6 ÷ 0.85 = **16.0%** — exactly what the invested portion earned.\nA) Is the unadjusted reported return. B) Is the equity contribution before dividing by the invested weight. C) Is an arbitrary midpoint.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A fund returned 14% when Rf was 6%, the market returned 12%, and the fund's beta was 0.90. Jensen's alpha is:\nA) +2.00%  B) +2.60%  C) +8.00%  D) −1.40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Required = 6 + 0.90 × (12 − 6) = 6 + 5.4 = **11.40%**. Alpha = 14 − 11.40 = **+2.60%**.\nA) Is Rp − Rm, which ignores the below-market beta. C) Is Rp − Rf, the raw excess return. D) Has the sign and logic reversed.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Why is (Rp − Rm) not an acceptable measure of alpha?\nA) It uses the wrong risk-free rate  B) It is expressed in the wrong units  C) It ignores the beta risk taken to produce the excess return  D) It cannot be computed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A fund with a beta of 1.5 in a rising market beats the index automatically. Alpha must subtract the **beta-adjusted required** return; the two figures coincide only when β = 1.\nA) Rf does not appear in Rp − Rm at all. B) Both are in percentage points. D) It is trivially computable — it is simply the wrong quantity.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A ₹50 lakh portfolio holds ₹25 lakh equity at 16%, ₹15 lakh debt at 7% and ₹10 lakh gold at 11%. The portfolio return is:\nA) 11.33%  B) 12.00%  C) 12.30%  D) 34.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Weights 0.50, 0.30, 0.20. Return = (0.50 × 16) + (0.30 × 7) + (0.20 × 11) = 8.0 + 2.1 + 2.2 = **12.30%**.\nA) Is the simple unweighted average of 16, 7 and 11. B) Is a rounding guess. D) Is the sum of the three returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A portfolio holds 50% in a fund with beta 1.30, 30% with beta 0.80 and 20% with beta 0.50. Portfolio beta is:\nA) 0.87  B) 0.99  C) 1.30  D) 2.60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Portfolio beta **is** a simple weighted average: (0.50 × 1.30) + (0.30 × 0.80) + (0.20 × 0.50) = 0.65 + 0.24 + 0.10 = **0.99**.\nA) Is the unweighted average of the three betas. C) Is the highest component beta. D) Is the sum of the betas.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A stock has σ = 30%, the market has σ = 20%, and their correlation is 0.60. The stock's beta is:\nA) 0.90  B) 1.50  C) 0.60  D) 1.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — β = ρ × (σᵢ ÷ σₘ) = 0.60 × (30 ÷ 20) = 0.60 × 1.50 = **0.90**.\nB) Is the σ ratio alone, omitting the correlation. C) Is the correlation alone, omitting the σ ratio. D) Would require ρ × σᵢ/σₘ to equal exactly 1.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Rf is 6%. Fund M returns 14% with σ 16%; Fund N returns 11% with σ 8%. On a Sharpe basis:\nA) M is better, 0.50 versus 0.63  B) They are equal  C) N is better, 0.63 versus 0.50  D) M is better, 1.33 versus 0.83\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — M: (14 − 6) ÷ 16 = **0.50**. N: (11 − 6) ÷ 8 = **0.625 ≈ 0.63**. N delivers more excess return per unit of total risk despite the lower headline return.\nA) Reverses which fund each figure belongs to. B) The ratios differ materially. D) Divides the excess return by Rf instead of by σ.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Rf is 6%. Fund P returns 15% with beta 1.25; Fund Q returns 12% with beta 0.60. On a Treynor basis:\nA) P is better, 7.20 versus 10.00  B) Q is better, 10.00 versus 7.20  C) Q is better, 20.00 versus 12.00  D) They are equal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — P: (15 − 6) ÷ 1.25 = **7.20**. Q: (12 − 6) ÷ 0.60 = **10.00**. Q produced 6 points of excess return while taking barely half the market's systematic risk.\nA) Attaches the figures to the wrong funds. C) Divides the **raw** returns by beta, forgetting to subtract the risk-free rate. D) The ratios are far apart.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** A client already holds a well-diversified portfolio and is adding one more equity fund as a satellite. The right measure for judging that fund is:\nA) Sharpe  B) Sortino  C) Treynor  D) Tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The fund's specific risk will be diversified away inside the larger portfolio, so it should be charged only for the **systematic** risk it adds — which is exactly what Treynor does.\nA) Sharpe would penalise specific risk that no longer matters at the total-portfolio level. B) Sortino answers a different question about loss aversion. D) Tracking error alone is a risk measure, not a risk-adjusted return.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A fund ranks better on Treynor than on Sharpe relative to its peers. This indicates:\nA) A calculation error  B) An unusually low beta  C) Excellent diversification  D) A large amount of unsystematic risk — the fund is poorly diversified\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sharpe divides by **total** risk and so is penalised by specific risk; Treynor divides by **beta** and is blind to it. A gap between the two rankings is the standard diagnosis of under-diversification.\nA) The two ratios legitimately disagree. B) Beta affects both rankings but does not create the divergence. C) Well-diversified portfolios rank the **same** on both.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A fund returned 14% against a MAR of 6% with a downside deviation of 4%. Its Sortino ratio is:\nA) 2.00  B) 3.50  C) 0.50  D) 1.75\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sortino = (14 − 6) ÷ 4 = 8 ÷ 4 = **2.00**.\nB) Divides the raw 14% by 4, forgetting to subtract the MAR. C) Inverts the ratio. D) Divides 14 by 8.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A fund returned 17% against a benchmark of 13% with a tracking error of 8%. Its Information Ratio is:\nA) 2.13  B) 4.00  C) 0.50  D) 2.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — IR = (17 − 13) ÷ 8 = 4 ÷ 8 = **0.50** — a respectable but not outstanding result.\nA) Divides the raw return by the tracking error. B) Is the active return itself, undivided. D) Inverts the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Manager A beat the benchmark by 4.0% with a tracking error of 5%; Manager B beat it by 6.0% with a tracking error of 12%. The better manager is:\nA) A, with an IR of 0.80 versus B's 0.50  B) B, because the active return is larger  C) They are equal  D) B, with an IR of 0.50 versus A's 0.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A: 4 ÷ 5 = **0.80**. B: 6 ÷ 12 = **0.50**. A produced more outperformance per unit of active risk, which is far more likely to be repeatable skill than luck.\nB) Raw outperformance says nothing about the risk taken to get it. C) The ratios differ substantially. D) Names the right numbers but the wrong winner.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** A fund returned 18% with σ 20%; Rf is 6% and market σ is 15%. Its M² is:\nA) 15.0%  B) 18.0%  C) 9.0%  D) 12.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Sharpe = (18 − 6) ÷ 20 = **0.60**. M² = 6 + (0.60 × 15) = 6 + 9 = **15.0%**.\nB) Is the fund's raw return, before any risk adjustment. C) Is the Sharpe × σm term alone, without adding Rf. D) Is the raw excess return.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A fund reports a tracking error of 0.3%. It is most likely:\nA) A concentrated small-cap fund  B) An unconstrained multi-asset fund  C) An index fund or ETF  D) A fund with negative alpha\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A near-zero tracking error means the portfolio hugs its benchmark almost exactly, which is the definition of passive replication.\nA) Concentrated small-cap funds usually run high single-digit tracking errors. B) Unconstrained funds deviate widely from any index. D) Tracking error says nothing about the **sign** of the active return.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** The difference between tracking difference and tracking error is that:\nA) They are the same thing  B) Tracking difference is the return gap; tracking error is the standard deviation of that gap  C) Tracking difference applies only to ETFs  D) Tracking error is always larger\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI requires index funds and ETFs to disclose **both**, because a fund can have a small average gap but a very unstable one, or vice versa.\nA) One is a level, the other a dispersion. C) Both concepts apply to any benchmarked portfolio. D) Either can be larger.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A portfolio's policy benchmark is 70% NIFTY 500 TRI and 30% a bond index. Over the year they returned 18% and 6%. The benchmark return is:\nA) 14.40%  B) 12.00%  C) 18.00%  D) 24.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — (0.70 × 18) + (0.30 × 6) = 12.6 + 1.8 = **14.40%**.\nB) Is the unweighted average of 18 and 6. C) Uses only the equity index — the classic mistake of judging a balanced portfolio against a pure equity benchmark. D) Adds the two index returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Comparing a small-cap fund's return with the NIFTY 50 is a benchmarking error because:\nA) The NIFTY 50 is not investable  B) The NIFTY 50 is a total return index  C) The comparison is too long  D) The benchmark does not match the fund's style and market-cap segment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The comparison then measures the **small-cap premium (or discount)** rather than the manager's skill. A NIFTY Smallcap 250 TRI is the appropriate benchmark.\nA) The NIFTY 50 is highly investable. B) Being a TRI is a virtue, not an error. C) Period length is a different error category.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A fund's expected return is 10% with σ of 15%. Roughly 95% of annual outcomes should fall between:\nA) −5% and +25%  B) −20% and +40%  C) −35% and +55%  D) 0% and +20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The 95% band is mean **±2σ** = 10 ± 30 = **−20% to +40%**.\nA) Is the ±1σ band, which covers about 68%. C) Is the ±3σ band, covering about 99.7%. D) Is far too narrow to cover 95% of outcomes.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A portfolio peaked at ₹75,00,000 and later troughed at ₹51,00,000. Its maximum drawdown is:\nA) 32%  B) 47%  C) 24%  D) 68%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — MDD = (75 − 51) ÷ 75 = 24 ÷ 75 = **32%**. Drawdown is always measured **from the peak**.\nB) Divides by the trough value instead of the peak — that 47% is the gain needed to recover. C) Is the rupee fall in lakh, not a percentage. D) Is the remaining value as a percentage of the peak.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A benchmark returns 11% overall, with its equity sleeve returning 14%. A manager held 70% in equity against the benchmark's 60%. The equity allocation effect is:\nA) +0.30%  B) +1.40%  C) +0.10%  D) −0.30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Allocation = (wₚ − w_b) × (R_b,sector − R_b,total) = (0.70 − 0.60) × (14 − 11) = 0.10 × 3 = **+0.30%**.\nB) Multiplies the weight difference by the sector return itself rather than by its excess over the total benchmark. C) Is the weight difference alone. D) Has the sign wrong — overweighting an outperforming sector adds value.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A foreign fund returns 10% in local currency while the rupee depreciates 4% against that currency. The rupee return is:\nA) 14.0%  B) 14.4%  C) 6.0%  D) 10.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (1.10 × 1.04) − 1 = 1.144 − 1 = **14.4%**.\nA) Simply adds the two, omitting the 0.4% cross term. C) Subtracts the currency move — the wrong sign, since rupee depreciation **helps** an Indian holder of foreign assets. D) Ignores the currency entirely.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A portfolio's three sub-period returns between cash flows were +10%, −8% and +15%. Its TWRR for the full period is:\nA) 17.00%  B) 16.38%  C) 15.67%  D) 5.67%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — TWRR = (1.10 × 0.92 × 1.15) − 1. Step by step: 1.10 × 0.92 = **1.012**; 1.012 × 1.15 = **1.1638**. So TWRR = **16.38%**.\nA) Adds the three returns (10 − 8 + 15), ignoring compounding. C) Is an arithmetic slip. D) Is the arithmetic mean of the three sub-period returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** A ₹20 lakh portfolio grew 20% to ₹24 lakh in H1; the client then withdrew ₹12 lakh; in H2 the remainder fell 10%. TWRR is 8%. The MWRR will be:\nA) Lower than 8%  B) Exactly 8%  C) Higher than 8%, because less money was exposed during the losing half  D) Indeterminate without the exact dates\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — TWRR = (1.20 × 0.90) − 1 = **8%**. But ₹20 lakh was invested through the +20% half and only ₹12 lakh through the −10% half, so the client's rupee-weighted outcome is materially **better** than 8%.\nA) Would be the case had the client **added** money before the fall. B) They coincide only when there are no cash flows. D) The direction follows from the timing, even without solving the IRR precisely.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A fund's four annual returns were +30%, −20%, +10% and +5%. Its geometric mean return is:\nA) 6.25%  B) 5.00%  C) 20.12%  D) 4.69%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Product = 1.30 × 0.80 × 1.10 × 1.05 = **1.2012**. GMR = (1.2012)^(1/4) − 1 = **4.69% p.a.**\nA) Is the **arithmetic** mean, (30 − 20 + 10 + 5)/4 = 6.25% — which overstates the compounded outcome by more than 1.5 percentage points. B) Is a rounding guess. C) Is the cumulative four-year return of 20.12%, not the annual rate.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A fund's arithmetic mean return is 12% and its standard deviation is 20%. Using the standard approximation, its geometric mean return is about:\nA) 12%  B) 14%  C) 10%  D) 2%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **GMR ≈ AMR − σ²/2** = 0.12 − (0.20² ÷ 2) = 0.12 − (0.04 ÷ 2) = 0.12 − 0.02 = **10%**.\nA) Ignores the volatility drag entirely. B) Adds the adjustment instead of subtracting it — GMR can never exceed AMR. D) Is the size of the adjustment (2%), not the resulting GMR.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** ₹15,00,000 grows to ₹21,00,000 over 4.5 years. The CAGR is:\nA) 8.89%  B) 7.76%  C) 40.00%  D) 10.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — (21,00,000 ÷ 15,00,000)^(1/4.5) − 1 = (1.40)^(1/4.5) − 1 = **7.76% p.a.** The exponent uses **years**, and n need not be a whole number.\nA) Divides the 40% total gain by 4.5 — a simple average that ignores compounding. C) Is the total return over the whole period. D) Overstates it; ₹15 lakh at 10% for 4.5 years exceeds ₹23 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An equity fund earns 14% gross with a TER of 1.5%. An investor holds for over 12 months; the entire gain exceeds the ₹1.25 lakh exemption and is taxed at 12.5%. Her post-tax return is:\nA) 12.50%  B) 10.94%  C) 8.75%  D) 14.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net of TER = 14% − 1.5% = **12.5%**. Post-tax = 12.5% × (1 − 0.125) = **10.94%**.\nA) Stops at the net-of-fee stage and forgets tax. C) Applies a 30% slab rate, which does not apply to long-term equity gains. D) Is the gross return, before both fees and tax.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A fund's daily standard deviation is 0.9%. Assuming 252 trading days, its annualised standard deviation is:\nA) 226.80%  B) 0.90%  C) 3.12%  D) 14.29%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — σ_annual = 0.9% × √252 = 0.9 × 15.8745 = **14.29%**.\nA) Multiplies by 252 instead of its square root. B) Is the daily figure unchanged. C) Uses √12 as if the data were monthly.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A fund holds 20% cash at 5% while its 80% equity sleeve falls 18%. The effect of the cash position was:\nA) A cost of 4.6 percentage points  B) No effect  C) A benefit of 4.6 percentage points — the fund fell 13.4% versus the market's 18%  D) A cost of 13.4 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Portfolio return = (0.80 × −18) + (0.20 × 5) = −14.4 + 1.0 = **−13.4%**, against −18% fully invested. Cash **added** 4.6 pp of protection.\nA) Reverses the sign — cash only drags when markets **rise**. B) A 20% position in a very different asset must have an effect. D) Is the portfolio's loss, not the cash effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A fund returned 10.5% while the market returned 12%. Its beta was 0.65 and Rf was 6.5%. Its Jensen's alpha is:\nA) −1.50%  B) +0.43%  C) +4.00%  D) −0.65%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Required = 6.5 + 0.65 × (12 − 6.5) = 6.5 + (0.65 × 5.5) = 6.5 + 3.575 = **10.075%**. Alpha = 10.5 − 10.075 = **+0.43%**. The fund \"lagged the index\" yet still **added value**, because it did so with only 65% of the market's systematic risk.\nA) Is Rp − Rm, which wrongly ignores the low beta. C) Is Rp − Rf, the raw excess return. D) Confuses the beta with a return figure.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Fund B returns 12% with σ 10% and a Sharpe ratio of 0.60 (Rf 6%). If an investor borrows at Rf to hold twice as much of Fund B, the result is:\nA) 18% return, 20% σ, Sharpe unchanged at 0.60  B) 24% return, 20% σ, Sharpe 0.90  C) 18% return, 10% σ, Sharpe 1.20  D) 12% return, 20% σ, Sharpe 0.30\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Return = (2 × 12) − (1 × 6) = **18%**; σ = 2 × 10 = **20%**; Sharpe = (18 − 6) ÷ 20 = **0.60**, unchanged. This is exactly why a higher Sharpe ratio always beats a lower one: leverage can match the risk level but never the ratio.\nB) Forgets to subtract the borrowing cost. C) Leverage scales σ as well as return. D) Leverage raises the return, not just the risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Rf is 5%. Fund G returns 17% with σ 30% and beta 1.10; Fund H returns 13% with σ 12% and beta 0.90. The correct reading is:\nA) G is better on both measures  B) H is better on both measures  C) H wins on Sharpe (0.67 vs 0.40) while G wins on Treynor (10.91 vs 8.89) — G carries heavy unsystematic risk  D) The disagreement means one of the calculations must be wrong\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — G: Sharpe = 12 ÷ 30 = **0.40**; Treynor = 12 ÷ 1.10 = **10.91**. H: Sharpe = 8 ÷ 12 = **0.67**; Treynor = 8 ÷ 0.90 = **8.89**. Sharpe penalises G's total risk; Treynor sees only its beta. The divergence is the classic signature of **poor diversification** in G.\nA) and B) both ignore that the two measures rank the funds oppositely. D) The ratios legitimately disagree; that disagreement is the diagnostic information.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A portfolio has σ of 28% and a beta of 1.20 against a market with σ of 18%. The proportion of its variance that is systematic is about:\nA) 77%  B) 60%  C) 40%  D) 120%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Systematic σ = β × σₘ = 1.20 × 18 = **21.6%**. Systematic variance = 21.6² = **466.6**; total variance = 28² = **784**. Ratio = 466.6 ÷ 784 = **59.5%**, i.e. about **60%** (this is the portfolio's R²). The residual specific risk is √(784 − 466.6) = **17.8%**.\nA) Is roughly the ratio of the standard deviations (21.6/28), not of the variances. C) Is the **unsystematic** share. D) Confuses the beta with a proportion.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A fund's R² against its benchmark is 45%. The right conclusion is:\nA) The fund has outperformed  B) Beta, Jensen's alpha and Treynor are unreliable for this fund; Sharpe is the safer measure  C) The fund is an index fund  D) The fund has zero risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — R² is the share of variance explained by the market. At 45%, most of the fund's movement is **non-market**, so any statistic built on beta becomes statistically shaky. Sharpe uses total risk and remains meaningful.\nA) R² says nothing about return. C) An index fund has an R² near 100%. D) A low R² means a lot of *non-market* risk, not no risk.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A fund's five annual returns were 22%, −4%, 15%, −8% and 25%. With a MAR of 0%, its Sortino ratio is:\nA) 2.50  B) 1.25  C) 0.74  D) 4.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Mean = (22 − 4 + 15 − 8 + 25) ÷ 5 = 50 ÷ 5 = **10%**. Shortfalls below 0%: −4 and −8 → (16 + 64) ÷ 5 = 16, so downside deviation = √16 = **4.0%**. Sortino = (10 − 0) ÷ 4.0 = **2.50**.\nB) Halves the numerator. C) Divides by the **total** σ of 13.52% instead of the downside deviation. D) Is the downside deviation itself, not the ratio.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A fund returned 9% against a benchmark of 11.5% with a tracking error of 5%. Its Information Ratio is:\nA) −0.50  B) +0.50  C) −2.50  D) +1.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — IR = (9 − 11.5) ÷ 5 = −2.5 ÷ 5 = **−0.50**. The client paid an active fee for a **worse-than-index** result.\nB) Drops the sign — the direction is the whole point. C) Is the active return itself, undivided. D) Divides the raw return by the tracking error.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A fund returned 19% with σ 28%; Rf is 6%, the market returned 13% with σ 14%. Its M² and M² alpha are:\nA) 19.00% and +6.00 pp  B) 13.00% and 0.00 pp  C) 25.00% and +12.00 pp  D) 12.50% and −0.50 pp\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Sharpe = (19 − 6) ÷ 28 = 13 ÷ 28. M² = 6 + (13 ÷ 28) × 14 = 6 + **6.50** = **12.50%**. M² alpha = 12.50 − 13.00 = **−0.50 pp**. Despite returning **19% against the market's 13%**, on a risk-matched basis the fund actually **underperformed** — its 28% volatility was double the market's.\nA) Is the raw return, with no risk adjustment at all. B) Would require the fund's Sharpe to equal the market's. C) Adds the excess return to the market return.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** For the fund in the previous question (19% return, σ 28%; Rf 6%; market σ 14%), the blend that matches the market's risk is:\nA) 100% in the fund, returning 19%  B) 50% fund and 50% T-bills, returning 12.5%  C) 28% fund and 72% T-bills, returning 9.6%  D) 200% fund, returning 32%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fund weight must satisfy w × 28% = 14%, so **w = 0.50**. That blend returns (0.50 × 19) + (0.50 × 6) = 9.5 + 3.0 = **12.5%** — exactly the M² figure, confirming the calculation.\nA) Leaves the risk at double the market's. C) Uses the σ figure directly as a weight. D) Levering up doubles the risk instead of halving it.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A fund's four quarterly active returns were +4%, 0%, +4% and 0%. On a population basis, its tracking error is:\nA) 2.0%  B) 4.0%  C) 0%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Mean active return = 8 ÷ 4 = **2%**. Deviations from that mean: +2, −2, +2, −2. Squares: 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = **16**; ÷ 4 = 4; **TE = √4 = 2.0%**.\nB) Is the largest active return, not its dispersion. C) Would require the active return to be constant. D) Is the sum of the active returns.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Benchmark: 50% equity at 16% and 50% debt at 8%. Portfolio: 70% equity at 18% and 30% debt at 7%. The allocation effect is:\nA) +1.60%  B) +0.50%  C) +2.70%  D) +0.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Benchmark return = (0.50 × 16) + (0.50 × 8) = **12%**. Allocation = Σ (wₚ − w_b) × (R_b,sector − R_b,total). Equity: (0.70 − 0.50) × (16 − 12) = 0.20 × 4 = **+0.80%**. Debt: (0.30 − 0.50) × (8 − 12) = (−0.20) × (−4) = **+0.80%**. Total = **+1.60%**.\nB) Is the **selection** effect. C) Is the **total** active return. D) Is the **interaction** effect.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** For the same data (benchmark 50% equity at 16% and 50% debt at 8%; portfolio 70% equity at 18% and 30% debt at 7%), the selection effect is:\nA) +1.60%  B) +0.50%  C) +1.00%  D) −0.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Selection = Σ w_b × (Rₚ,sector − R_b,sector). Equity: 0.50 × (18 − 16) = **+1.00%**. Debt: 0.50 × (7 − 8) = **−0.50%**. Total = **+0.50%**. Checking the whole decomposition: portfolio return = (0.70 × 18) + (0.30 × 7) = **14.7%**, so active return = 14.7 − 12 = **+2.70%** = allocation 1.60 + selection 0.50 + interaction 0.60 ✓.\nA) Is the allocation effect. C) Is the equity component of selection alone, ignoring the negative debt contribution. D) Is the debt component alone.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A portfolio returned 20% with σ 26% and beta 1.30; Rf is 6%, the market returned 14% with σ 18%. Its net selectivity is:\nA) +3.60%  B) +2.44%  C) +1.16%  D) +6.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAPM required = 6 + 1.30 × (14 − 6) = 6 + 10.4 = **16.40%**, so Jensen's alpha = 20 − 16.40 = **+3.60%**. Charging for **total** risk instead: 6 + (26 ÷ 18) × 8 = 6 + 11.56 = **17.56%**. Net selectivity = 20 − 17.56 = **+2.44%**.\nA) Is Jensen's alpha, before removing the cost of imperfect diversification. C) Is that diversification cost (3.60 − 2.44). D) Is the raw excess over the market.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** A concentrated fund returned 17% with σ 34% and beta 1.00; Rf is 6%, the market returned 13% with σ 16%. The right verdict is:\nA) Alpha is +4%, so the manager is skilled  B) Net selectivity is about −3.9%, so the positive alpha was really compensation for undiversified risk  C) Alpha and net selectivity are equal here  D) The fund matched the market exactly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CAPM required = 6 + 1.00 × 7 = **13%**, giving alpha = **+4%**. But total-risk required = 6 + (34 ÷ 16) × 7 = 6 + 14.875 = **20.875%**, so net selectivity = 17 − 20.875 = **−3.88%**. The fund's σ was more than **twice** the market's while its beta was only 1.0 — a huge undiversified position.\nA) Alpha alone cannot see the specific risk being carried. C) They are equal only when σp/σₘ = β, which here is 2.125 versus 1.00. D) The fund beat the market on raw return but not on a total-risk-adjusted basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** An Indian investor holds a European fund that returns 9% in euros while the rupee **appreciates** 6% against the euro. The rupee return is:\nA) 3.00%  B) 2.46%  C) 15.54%  D) 9.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Currency return = **−6%**. Rupee return = (1.09 × 0.94) − 1 = 1.0246 − 1 = **2.46%**. A strengthening rupee **erodes** foreign returns for an Indian investor.\nA) Simply subtracts 6 from 9, omitting the cross term. C) Adds the currency move instead of subtracting it. D) Ignores the currency entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** ₹20,00,000 is invested in a US fund at ₹82/USD. The fund returns 15% in USD and the rupee ends at ₹86.10/USD. The ending rupee value is:\nA) ₹24,15,000 (a 20.75% rupee return)  B) ₹24,00,000 (20.00%)  C) ₹23,00,000 (15.00%)  D) ₹25,00,000 (25.00%)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Currency return = (86.10 ÷ 82) − 1 = **+5.0%** (rupee depreciation). Rupee return = (1.15 × 1.05) − 1 = 1.2075 − 1 = **20.75%**. Value = ₹20,00,000 × 1.2075 = **₹24,15,000**.\nB) Adds 15% and 5%, dropping the 0.75% cross term. C) Ignores the currency gain. D) Overstates the compounding.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A fund's five annual returns were 14%, −3%, 20%, −6% and 10%. With a MAR of 0%, its downside deviation is:\nA) 3.00%  B) 4.74%  C) 9.00%  D) 1.80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Only two years fell below 0%: **−3%** and **−6%**. Squares = 9 + 36 = **45**; divide by **n = 5** (every period counts in the denominator) = 9; √9 = **3.00%**.\nB) Divides by 2 (the number of negative years) instead of by 5. C) Is the variance figure of 9, not its square root. D) Is the simple average shortfall (3 + 6)/5, not the root-mean-square.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A portfolio with a 60/40 policy returned 17.5%. The NIFTY 500 TRI returned 22% and the bond index 8%. The benchmark return and active return are:\nA) 22.0% and −4.5%  B) 15.0% and +2.5%  C) 16.4% and −1.1%  D) 16.4% and +1.1%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Benchmark = (0.60 × 22) + (0.40 × 8) = 13.2 + 3.2 = **16.4%**. Active = 17.5 − 16.4 = **+1.1%**.\nA) Compares a balanced portfolio to a pure equity index — a benchmarking error. B) Uses incorrect weights. C) Has the sign of the active return reversed.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A mid-cap fund is first-quartile among 42 peers but returned 3 percentage points less than the NIFTY Midcap 150 TRI. The right conclusion is:\nA) The fund performed excellently  B) The peer ranking is wrong  C) The whole category lagged its index — peer ranking alone is not enough, and a low-cost index fund would have served the client better  D) The index is not investable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Being the best of an underperforming group is not the same as adding value. Peer analysis and benchmark analysis are **complements**, and both must be checked.\nA) Trailing the index by 3 points is not excellent. B) The ranking can be perfectly accurate and still incomplete. D) Mid-cap index funds are readily available in India.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A retired client's **entire** corpus sits in a single balanced fund. The most appropriate risk-adjusted measure is:\nA) Treynor  B) Sharpe  C) Information Ratio  D) Tracking error\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Because the fund **is** the whole portfolio, the client bears its **total** risk, including its specific risk. Sharpe divides by total σ and is therefore the right lens. (A Sortino ratio would also be defensible given a retiree's loss aversion.)\nA) Treynor assumes specific risk is diversified away elsewhere, which is untrue here. C) The Information Ratio judges a manager against a benchmark, not the client's total risk. D) Tracking error is a risk measure, not a risk-adjusted return.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A manager reports +5% alpha, but the fund holds only 8 stocks and has an R² of 35%. An adviser should:\nA) Recommend it immediately on the strength of the alpha  B) Ignore the alpha because alpha is never useful  C) Compute net selectivity and the Sharpe ratio, since a low R² means much of that alpha may be payment for undiversified specific risk  D) Switch the benchmark until the alpha disappears\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — With R² at 35%, beta-based figures (alpha, Treynor) are unreliable. **Net selectivity** charges the manager for total risk and reveals how much of the alpha survives; **Sharpe** does the same for the ranking.\nA) Alpha computed on a low-R² fund is statistically fragile. B) Alpha is highly useful when R² is high. D) Changing the benchmark to suit the answer is benchmark shopping.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which principle best summarises this chapter?\nA) The fund with the highest return is the best fund  B) Performance must be measured as return per unit of the RIGHT risk — total (Sharpe), systematic (Treynor), downside (Sortino) or benchmark-relative (Information Ratio) — against an appropriate benchmark, net of fees, and then attributed to the decisions that produced it  C) Risk measures are academic and rarely used in practice  D) TWRR and MWRR always give the same answer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It captures the three pillars of the chapter: **choose the right risk measure**, **compare against a fair benchmark**, and **attribute the result to decisions** so you can tell skill from luck.\nA) Raw return ignores the risk taken and is the error the whole chapter exists to correct. C) SEBI mandates TWRR and net-of-fee disclosure, and every factsheet publishes σ, beta and Sharpe. D) They differ whenever there are cash flows — the very reason both exist.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Work through the ratios in order — each one uses figures from the same table.\n\n### Case: The Deshpande Family Portfolio Review\n\nMr and Mrs Deshpande hold a **₹2,00,00,000** portfolio managed under an advisory mandate. Their adviser presents the following one-year review against the agreed customised benchmark. The **risk-free rate is 6.0%**.\n\n| Measure | **Portfolio** | **Benchmark** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Return | **15.2%** | **12.0%** |\n| Standard deviation | **16.0%** | **14.0%** |\n| Beta | **1.10** | 1.00 |\n| Tracking error | **4.0%** | — |\n| Downside deviation (MAR 6%) | 8.0% | — |\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** The Sharpe ratios of the portfolio and the benchmark are:\nA) 0.58 and 0.43 — the portfolio is better  B) 0.95 and 0.86  C) 0.43 and 0.58 — the benchmark is better  D) Both 0.58\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Portfolio: (15.2 − 6.0) ÷ 16.0 = 9.2 ÷ 16.0 = **0.575 ≈ 0.58**. Benchmark: (12.0 − 6.0) ÷ 14.0 = 6.0 ÷ 14.0 = **0.429 ≈ 0.43**. Per unit of **total** risk, the portfolio delivered more.\nB) Divides the raw returns by σ without subtracting Rf. C) Attaches the figures to the wrong side. D) The two ratios differ by 0.15, which is a material margin.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** The portfolio's Jensen's alpha is:\nA) +3.20%  B) +0.60%  C) +2.60%  D) +9.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Required return = 6.0 + 1.10 × (12.0 − 6.0) = 6.0 + 6.6 = **12.60%**. Alpha = 15.2 − 12.6 = **+2.60%**. Of the 3.2 points of raw outperformance, **0.6 points were simply bought with a beta of 1.10**; only 2.6 points reflect the adviser's decisions.\nA) Is Rp − Rb, which ignores the beta taken. B) Is the beta-bought portion alone. D) Is Rp − Rf, the raw excess over cash.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** The portfolio's Information Ratio is:\nA) 0.65  B) 3.20  C) 1.25  D) 0.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Active return = 15.2 − 12.0 = **3.2%**; tracking error = **4.0%**; IR = 3.2 ÷ 4.0 = **0.80** — comfortably in the \"very good\" band, indicating outperformance achieved with modest benchmark-relative risk.\nA) Is an arithmetic slip. B) Is the active return itself, undivided. C) Inverts the ratio (4.0 ÷ 3.2).\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The portfolio's M² and M² alpha are:\nA) 14.05% and +2.05 percentage points  B) 15.20% and +3.20 pp  C) 12.00% and 0.00 pp  D) 9.20% and −2.80 pp\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Portfolio Sharpe = **0.575**. M² = Rf + (Sharpe × σ_benchmark) = 6.0 + (0.575 × 14.0) = 6.0 + 8.05 = **14.05%**. M² alpha = 14.05 − 12.00 = **+2.05 pp**. Translated into plain language for the clients: *\"at the benchmark's own level of risk, your strategy would have earned 14.05% against the benchmark's 12.0%.\"*\nB) Is the raw portfolio return, with no risk adjustment. C) Would require the portfolio's Sharpe to equal the benchmark's. D) Is the raw excess over Rf.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Taking the Treynor ratio and net selectivity together, the overall verdict is:\nA) Treynor 6.00 versus 8.36 — the benchmark wins  B) Treynor 8.36 versus 6.00; the portfolio wins on both Sharpe and Treynor, and net selectivity is still about +2.34% — genuine, well-diversified skill  C) The measures conflict, so the portfolio must be badly diversified  D) Nothing can be concluded from one year of data alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Treynor: portfolio = (15.2 − 6.0) ÷ 1.10 = 9.2 ÷ 1.10 = **8.36**; benchmark = 6.0 ÷ 1.00 = **6.00**. The portfolio wins on **both** Sharpe and Treynor, which means it is **well diversified** — there is no hidden pile of specific risk for Sharpe to punish.\n\n**The harder test — net selectivity:** charging for **total** risk instead of beta, the required return is 6.0 + (16.0 ÷ 14.0) × (12.0 − 6.0) = 6.0 + (1.1429 × 6.0) = 6.0 + 6.86 = **12.86%**. Net selectivity = 15.2 − 12.86 = **+2.34%** — only 0.26 points below the **+2.60%** Jensen's alpha, confirming that almost none of the outperformance was payment for undiversified risk.\n\n**What the adviser should tell the clients:** the portfolio added roughly **2.3–2.6 percentage points of genuine value** with an Information Ratio of 0.80 and no diversification penalty. The one honest caveat is that **a single year is not a market cycle** — the same figures should be shown over three and five years, on rolling returns, before drawing firm conclusions.\n\nA) Reverses the two Treynor figures. C) Agreement between Sharpe and Treynor is evidence of **good** diversification, not bad. D) Meaningful conclusions can be drawn from these figures; the caveat concerns their durability, not their validity.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "17", "title": "Operational Aspects of Investment Management", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "# Chapter 17: Operational Aspects of Investment Management — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 6 · Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance and Ethics (30 marks).**\n> This is the **procedural** chapter — the plumbing that sits under every piece of advice you will ever give. There is almost no mathematics here, but there are a very large number of **exact rules, exact document lists and exact rupee thresholds**, and the exam tests them literally. The good news: almost every rule in this chapter exists for one of only **three reasons** — (1) *prove who the investor is* (PMLA), (2) *prove the money is the investor's own* (PMLA again), (3) *make sure the right person gets the money back* (succession law). If you can attach a rule to its reason, you will remember it.\n\n---\n\n## 17.1 Investor Types and the On-boarding Process\n\n### 17.1.1 ⭐ Who Can Invest?\n\nIndian financial products are open to a long list of investor categories. The list matters because **the documents you collect change with the category**.\n\n| Category | Who exactly | Signs / represented by |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Resident individuals** | Any person aged **18 or above**, singly or jointly (**maximum 3 holders**) | Themselves |\n| **Minors** | Below **18** — folio must be in the minor's **sole** name | **Natural guardian** (father/mother) or **court-appointed** guardian |\n| **HUF** | Hindu Undivided Family | The **Karta** |\n| **Partnership firms / LLPs** | Registered or unregistered firms; LLPs under the LLP Act, 2008 | Authorised partner(s) |\n| **Companies / bodies corporate** | Private, public, PSUs | Authorised signatories under a **board resolution** |\n| **Trusts, societies, charitable and religious institutions** | Where their own trust deed / bye-laws permit the investment | Trustees / office bearers |\n| **Banks, co-operative banks, RRBs, NBFCs, financial institutions** | Subject to their own regulator's exposure rules | Authorised officials |\n| **Mutual funds, AIFs, insurance companies, pension/PF/gratuity/superannuation funds** | Institutional money | Authorised officials |\n| **NRIs, PIOs and OCIs** | On a **repatriable** or **non-repatriable** basis | Themselves or a **PoA holder** |\n| **FPIs** | Foreign Portfolio Investors registered with SEBI | Custodian / authorised representative |\n| **Armed forces and paramilitary funds; scientific and industrial research organisations** | Specific approved funds | Authorised officials |\n| **International multilateral agencies** | Approved by the Government of India | Authorised officials |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"the three gates.\"** An investor can walk in only if all three gates open: **capacity** (18+, or a guardian for a minor, or a valid authorisation for an entity), **eligibility** (FEMA lets this person hold this asset), and **compliance** (PAN + KYC done).\n\n**Who generally CANNOT invest (or is restricted):**\n\n| Blocked / restricted | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| **Minors in their own right** | A minor's contract is **void** under the Indian Contract Act, 1872 — hence the guardian |\n| **Overseas Corporate Bodies (OCBs)** | **De-recognised by RBI in 2003** as an eligible investor class |\n| **US persons and residents of Canada** (in most mutual fund schemes) | The reporting burden of **FATCA**; each AMC states its own position in the SID |\n| **Persons in FATF non-compliant / high-risk jurisdictions** | PMLA and FATF country risk |\n| **Persons barred by SEBI or a court** | Debarment orders |\n| **NRIs — for PPF, NSC, SCSS, KVP, agricultural land, plantations, farmhouses** | FEMA and small-savings scheme rules |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** \"Who can invest\" is *product-specific*, not universal. An NRI can freely buy Indian **mutual funds** but cannot open a **new PPF** account. A charitable trust can buy mutual funds only if **its own trust deed** permits it. Never answer this question in the abstract.\n\n---\n\n### 17.1.2 ⭐ The Client On-boarding Process\n\nThink of on-boarding as a **seven-step assembly line**. Miss a step and the application is rejected — the single largest cause of operational grief in an advisory practice.\n\n| # | Step | What actually happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Understand the client** | Fact-find, financial position, goals, **risk profiling** — and for an IA, a documented **suitability** assessment |\n| 2 | **Explain the terms of offer** | Give and explain the offer document for the product chosen (see 17.1.3) |\n| 3 | **Sign the agreement** | For an SEBI-registered IA, a **mandatory written investment advisory agreement**; for PMS, the PMS agreement; for AIF, the contribution agreement |\n| 4 | **PAN and KYC** | PAN, KYC through a **KRA** / **CKYC**, **In-Person Verification (IPV)** or **V-CIP** |\n| 5 | **Complete the application** | Mandatory investor information, bank mandate, **nomination or opt-out**, FATCA/CRS declaration, UBO for non-individuals |\n| 6 | **Make the payment** | From the **investor's own bank account** — third-party payments are prohibited except in narrow cases |\n| 7 | **Folio / account creation** | The AMC/RTA/DP opens the **folio or account** and issues a statement of account or credits the demat account |\n\n> 🧠 **\"KAP-BAN\":** **K**YC → **A**greement → **P**AN → **B**ank mandate → **A**pplication → **N**omination. If any letter is missing, so is the account.\n\n---\n\n### 17.1.3 Terms of Offer\n\nThe **terms of offer** is the document that legally defines what the investor is buying. Different products, different documents — and the adviser's duty is to make sure the client has actually seen the right one.\n\n| Product | Terms-of-offer document |\n|---|---|\n| **Mutual fund** | **SID** (Scheme Information Document) + **SAI** (Statement of Additional Information) + **KIM** (Key Information Memorandum, attached to the application form) |\n| **Portfolio Management Service** | **Disclosure Document** + the **PMS agreement** |\n| **Alternative Investment Fund** | **Private Placement Memorandum (PPM)** + contribution agreement |\n| **Life / general insurance** | **Prospectus**, **benefit illustration** and the **policy document** |\n| **NPS** | Offer document / subscriber registration form through a **PoP** |\n| **Bonds, NCDs, IPOs** | **Prospectus** / information memorandum / term sheet |\n| **Bank fixed deposit** | Application form plus the bank's schedule of terms |\n| **Investment advice itself** | The **advisory agreement**, terms & conditions and the **risk profile** signed off by the client |\n\n---\n\n### 17.1.4 Regulatory Requirements at On-boarding\n\n| Requirement | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **PAN** | Income Tax Act, 1961 (Section 139A) |\n| **KYC — identity, address, IPV** | **PMLA, 2002** read with the **PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005** |\n| **KYC registration with a KRA** | SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, **2011** |\n| **CKYC upload** | Rule **9(1A)** of the PML Rules — upload to **CKYCR** within **10 days** |\n| **FATCA / CRS declaration** | Income Tax Rules **114F–114H** |\n| **Ultimate Beneficial Owner (UBO)** identification for non-individuals | PMLA / SEBI master circular on AML |\n| **PEP (Politically Exposed Person) screening** | PMLA — enhanced due diligence |\n| **Risk profiling and suitability** | SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, **2013** |\n| **Nomination or an explicit opt-out** | SEBI circulars for demat accounts and mutual fund folios |\n| **Eligibility to hold the asset** | **FEMA, 1999** (for non-residents) |\n\n---\n\n### 17.1.5 ⭐ Mandatory Investor Information\n\nWhat every application form must capture, and **why**:\n\n| Information | Why it is mandatory |\n|---|---|\n| **Full name** (as per PAN) | Must match PAN and KYC records exactly, or the folio is rejected |\n| **PAN** | Tax trail and unique identification |\n| **Date of birth** | Age/capacity; senior-citizen benefits; minor status |\n| **Address** (Indian + overseas for NRIs) | Communication, jurisdiction, FEMA status |\n| **Mobile number and email** | Mandatory — statements, OTPs, alerts. Must be the **investor's own** |\n| **Status** — resident/NRI, individual/non-individual | Determines taxation, repatriability, documents |\n| **Occupation and gross annual income / net worth** | **AML risk profiling** — does the investment fit the declared means? |\n| **Bank account details** — name, number, IFSC, MICR | Payouts go **only** here; also anchors the anti-third-party rule |\n| **Mode of holding** | Who can sign, and who gets the money |\n| **Nomination or opt-out** | Smooth transmission on death |\n| **FATCA/CRS**: tax residency, TIN, country of birth | Global tax information exchange |\n| **PEP declaration** | Enhanced due diligence under PMLA |\n| **Guardian details** (minor) / **UBO** (non-individual) | Who really controls the money |\n| **Signature** (and specimen) | Authenticates all later instructions |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** SEBI/AMFI require the **mobile number and email to belong to the investor** (or to specified close relatives, with a declaration of relationship). A distributor or adviser inserting **their own** contact details into a client's folio is a serious compliance breach — it cuts the investor off from the alerts designed to protect them.\n\n---\n\n### 17.1.6 ⭐ The Investor Folio or Account\n\nA **folio** is the investor's unique account number with a **mutual fund/AMC**, maintained by the **RTA** (CAMS, KFin Technologies etc.). It is the container that holds: the names of holders, **mode of holding**, PAN and KYC status, address and contact details, **bank mandate(s)**, **nomination**, tax status and all transactions.\n\n**Modes of holding** — the single most consequential choice on the form:\n\n| Mode | Who must sign a transaction | On death of a holder |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Single** | The sole holder | Goes to the **nominee**, else legal heirs |\n| **Joint** | **ALL holders must sign** — every time | Units are **transmitted to survivors** |\n| **Anyone or Survivor** | **Any one** holder can sign | The **survivor(s)** continue the folio |\n\n> 🧠 **Default rule:** if there is more than one holder and the mode of holding is **left blank**, the folio is treated as **\"Joint\"** — meaning every future instruction needs every signature. This causes enormous practical pain, so the adviser should always check the box.\n\n**Other folio rules worth memorising:**\n\n- **Maximum three holders**. The **first holder** is the tax owner — their PAN, KYC and tax status govern the folio, and **all payouts go to the first holder's bank account**.\n- Nomination and the **change of address must be signed as per the mode of holding**, *except* **nomination, which always needs ALL holders' signatures** — even in an \"Anyone or Survivor\" folio.\n- Units may be held in **Statement of Account (SoA)** form with the RTA, or in **demat** form with a DP.\n- **Consolidation of folios**: an investor with several folios in the same AMC, with **identical holders, identical order of names, identical mode of holding and identical PAN/KYC**, can request consolidation into one folio. It reduces paperwork and produces one clean statement. Folios with **different** holders or a different order of names **cannot** be merged.\n- **CAS (Consolidated Account Statement)** stitches everything together across AMCs and depositories against a common PAN — issued **monthly** where there were transactions, and **half-yearly** (for March and September) including zero-balance folios.\n\n---\n\n## 17.2 PAN, KYC and the Central KYC Process\n\n### 17.2.1 ⭐ Permanent Account Number (PAN)\n\nA **10-character alphanumeric** identifier issued by the **Income Tax Department** under **Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961**. Format: **5 letters, 4 digits, 1 letter** (e.g., ABCPD1234E).\n\nThe **4th character encodes the holder's status** — a free mark in the exam:\n\n| 4th character | Status |\n|---|---|\n| **P** | Individual (**P**erson) |\n| **C** | **C**ompany |\n| **H** | **H**UF |\n| **F** | **F**irm / LLP |\n| **A** | **A**ssociation of Persons (AOP) |\n| **T** | **T**rust |\n| **B** | **B**ody of Individuals |\n| **L** | **L**ocal authority |\n| **J** | Artificial **j**uridical person |\n| **G** | **G**overnment |\n\nThe **5th character** is the first letter of the **surname** for an individual, or of the **entity's name** otherwise.\n\n**PAN exemptions — the two you must know:**\n\n| Exemption | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Micro-SIP / small investments** | Investments up to **₹50,000 per investor per financial year, aggregated across schemes of one mutual fund**, are exempt from PAN. The investor is issued a **PEKRN** (PAN Exempt KYC Reference Number). Available only to **individuals, NRIs, minors and sole proprietors** — **not** to HUFs, companies or other non-individuals |\n| **Residents of Sikkim** | Exempt from PAN for mutual fund investments, on production of proof of Sikkim residence |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** PAN must be **linked to Aadhaar**. An unlinked PAN becomes **inoperative**, which means higher TDS and rejected transactions — a live operational issue an adviser must check for every client.\n\n---\n\n### 17.2.2 ⭐ The KYC Process\n\nKYC exists because of the **Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002** and the **PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005**. Every SEBI-registered intermediary is a \"reporting entity\" and must perform it.\n\n**The four pillars of KYC:**\n\n1. **Customer identification** — Proof of Identity (PoI)\n2. **Address verification** — Proof of Address (PoA)\n3. **In-Person Verification (IPV)** or **V-CIP**\n4. **Ongoing due diligence** — monitoring transactions against the declared profile\n\n**Officially Valid Documents (OVDs)** under the PML Rules — the definitive list of **six**:\n\n| # | OVD |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Passport** |\n| 2 | **Driving licence** |\n| 3 | **Proof of possession of Aadhaar number** (with the first 8 digits redacted where required) |\n| 4 | **Voter's Identity Card** issued by the Election Commission |\n| 5 | **NREGA job card** duly signed by a State Government officer |\n| 6 | **Letter issued by the National Population Register** containing name and address |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** **PAN is NOT an OVD.** PAN is *separately and additionally* mandatory. Nor is a **ration card, bank passbook or utility bill** an OVD in its own right.\n\n**Deemed OVDs** — if the OVD carries an **outdated address**, the client may temporarily submit a **utility bill not more than two months old** (electricity, telephone, post-paid mobile, piped gas, water), a **municipal/property tax receipt**, a **pension payment order**, or an **employer-allotted accommodation letter** (from Government, PSU, bank, financial institution or listed company). The client must then produce an **OVD with the updated address within three months**.\n\n**IPV and its digital equivalents:**\n\n| Method | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **IPV** | Mandatory. Can be done by AMC/KRA officials, **NISM-certified distributors holding an ARN** (for their own clients), or scheduled commercial bank officials |\n| **V-CIP** | **Video-based Customer Identification Process** — a live, consent-based video call with liveness checks; a permitted alternative to physical IPV |\n| **Aadhaar OTP-based e-KYC** | Fully digital, but the investment is capped at **₹50,000 per financial year per mutual fund** |\n| **Aadhaar biometric e-KYC** | Done at an authorised point; **no investment cap** |\n\n**KYC statuses** — a modern refinement worth knowing. A client's KYC record with a KRA now carries a status such as **Validated**, **Registered/Verified** or **On Hold**. A **KYC-Validated** record (PAN–Aadhaar linked and validated against the income tax database) travels seamlessly to any new intermediary; a merely **Registered** record may require the client to redo KYC when opening an account elsewhere. \"On Hold\" transactions are blocked until the deficiency is cured.\n\n**Additional non-individual requirements:** board resolution / list of authorised signatories, certificate of incorporation, memorandum & articles, trust deed or partnership deed, and identification of the **Ultimate Beneficial Owner** — the natural person who ultimately owns or controls the entity.\n\n---\n\n### 17.2.3 ⭐ KRAs and the Central KYC Registry\n\nThe whole point of both is to make the client do KYC **once**.\n\n| | **KRA (KYC Registration Agency)** | **CKYCR (Central KYC Records Registry)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Set up under** | **SEBI (KRA) Regulations, 2011** | **Rule 9(1A) of the PML Rules**; operated by **CERSAI** |\n| **Scope** | **Securities market only** — SEBI-registered intermediaries | **All financial sectors** — banking, securities, **insurance, pensions** |\n| **Regulator** | SEBI | Ministry of Finance / Department of Revenue |\n| **Identifier issued** | KYC record against **PAN** | **14-digit KIN** (KYC Identification Number) |\n| **Who uploads** | The intermediary that first does the KYC | Every reporting entity, **within 10 days** of starting the relationship |\n| **Who can download** | Any other SEBI intermediary, with client consent | Any reporting entity, with client consent |\n| **Examples** | **CVL** (CDSL Ventures), **NDML** (NSDL Database Management), **CAMS**, **KFin Technologies**, **DotEx** (NSE) | A single registry — **CERSAI** |\n\n> 🧠 **The clean distinction:** **KRA = securities market. CKYC = the whole financial system.** If the question mentions an insurance policy or a bank account in the same breath as KYC portability, the answer is **CKYC**.\n\n**Record keeping:** under PMLA, records of identity and transactions must be preserved for at least **five years** — identity records for five years from the end of the business relationship, transaction records for five years from the date of the transaction. KRAs preserve KYC records for the longer period SEBI prescribes.\n\n**Reporting under PMLA:** reporting entities file **Cash Transaction Reports (CTR)**, **Suspicious Transaction Reports (STR)** and other prescribed reports with **FIU-IND** (Financial Intelligence Unit – India). An STR must **never** be disclosed to the client — that is **tipping off**, and it is an offence.\n\n---\n\n## 17.3 Dematerialisation and Rematerialisation\n\n### The architecture\n\n- Governed by the **Depositories Act, 1996**.\n- Two depositories: **NSDL** (1996) and **CDSL** (1999).\n- The investor never deals with the depository directly — always through a **Depository Participant (DP)**: a bank, broker, NBFC or financial institution.\n- Every security has a unique **ISIN** — International Securities Identification Number, **12 characters**.\n- Account numbering: **NSDL** accounts are **8-character DP ID + 8-digit client ID**, written with an **\"IN\" prefix**; **CDSL** accounts are a **16-digit numeric BO ID**.\n- **Maximum three holders** in a demat account, as with a folio.\n\n### 17.3.1 ⭐ Dematerialisation\n\n**Converting physical certificates into electronic book-entry form.**\n\n| Step | Who | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Investor | Submits a **Dematerialisation Request Form (DRF)** with the **original certificates** to the DP |\n| 2 | Investor | Defaces the certificates by writing **\"Surrendered for Dematerialisation\"** across them |\n| 3 | DP | Verifies, generates a **Dematerialisation Request Number (DRN)**, sends an electronic request to the depository and the physical certificates to the **issuer/RTA** |\n| 4 | Depository | Forwards the request to the **issuer/RTA** |\n| 5 | RTA | Verifies, **cancels the certificates**, confirms to the depository |\n| 6 | Depository | **Credits** the investor's demat account; the DP issues a statement |\n\n**Time frame: normally completed within about 30 days** of lodging the request.\n\n**The non-negotiable matching rule:** the **names and the ORDER of names** on the certificate must be **identical** to those on the demat account. A certificate in \"A and B\" cannot be dematerialised into an account of \"B and A\" — that requires **transposition** first (see 17.7.5).\n\n**Benefits:** no bad delivery, forgery, theft, mutilation or postal loss; instant transfer; lower cost (no stamp duty on transfer of dematerialised securities in the old sense); automatic credit of bonus/rights/splits; a single account for equity, bonds, ETFs, mutual fund units and Sovereign Gold Bonds; easy pledging; **nomination at account level**.\n\n**Costs:** account opening (often nil), **annual maintenance charge (AMC)**, transaction/debit charges (charged on **debits, not credits**), demat/remat charges per certificate, pledge charges.\n\n**BSDA — Basic Services Demat Account:** a low-cost demat account for small investors who hold **only one** demat account as sole/first holder. SEBI links eligibility to a **holding-value ceiling (raised to ₹10 lakh)** and prescribes **nil or concessional annual maintenance charges** in the lower slabs. Eligible investors are to be offered BSDA **by default**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Since 1 April 2019, listed securities cannot be TRANSFERRED in physical form** — transfer requires demat. **Transmission** (on death) and **transposition** are the exceptions, and even then the securities are now issued in **demat form only**.\n\n### 17.3.2 ⭐ Rematerialisation\n\n**Converting electronic holdings back into physical certificates** — the exact reverse.\n\n| Step | Who | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Investor | Submits a **Rematerialisation Request Form (RRF)** to the DP |\n| 2 | DP | Verifies the balance, **blocks** the holding, generates a **Rematerialisation Request Number (RRN)** |\n| 3 | Depository | Forwards the request to the **issuer/RTA** |\n| 4 | RTA | **Prints and despatches** certificates directly to the investor |\n| 5 | Depository | **Debits** the demat account |\n\n**Time frame: normally about 30 days.** The certificates issued carry **new distinctive numbers** — they are not the original certificates back.\n\n| | **Dematerialisation** | **Rematerialisation** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Direction** | Physical → **electronic** | Electronic → **physical** |\n| **Form** | **DRF** | **RRF** |\n| **Reference number** | **DRN** | **RRN** |\n| **Effect on demat account** | **Credit** | **Debit** |\n| **Certificates** | Surrendered and **cancelled** | **Fresh** certificates with **new distinctive numbers** |\n| **Practical usefulness today** | Essential | Very limited — physical transfer of listed shares is barred |\n\n**Mutual fund units** follow the same logic: units held in **SoA** form can be converted to demat through a **Conversion Request Form (CRF)** submitted to the DP, and back to SoA through a **Re-conversion Request Form (RRF)**.\n\n---\n\n## 17.4 Power of Attorney and Other Agreements\n\nA **Power of Attorney (PoA)** is a legal instrument under the **Powers of Attorney Act, 1882** by which the **donor/principal** authorises the **donee/attorney** to act on their behalf. It must be **properly stamped**, and **notarised or registered** as the law of the state requires. The KYC of **both** the investor and the PoA holder must be completed, and the PoA must be **registered with the AMC/RTA/DP** with the attorney's **specimen signature**, before it can be acted upon.\n\n### 17.4.1 vs 17.4.2 ⭐ General versus Specific/Limited PoA\n\n| | **General Power of Attorney (GPoA)** | **Specific / Limited PoA (SPoA / LPoA)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Scope** | **Broad** — all or most of the donor's affairs | **Narrow** — only the acts expressly listed |\n| **Typical powers** | Operate all bank accounts, buy and sell property, sign contracts, conduct litigation, manage investments | Debit a demat account for **settlement obligations only**; operate one bank and one demat account for a **specific portfolio**; sign one specified transaction |\n| **Risk to the donor** | **Very high** — the attorney can do almost anything | **Contained** — anything not listed is not authorised |\n| **Typical use** | An NRI authorising a trusted relative to handle affairs in India | A **PMS client** authorising the portfolio manager; a **broker PoA** for pay-in of securities |\n| **Preferred in financial services** | **No** | **Yes** — always |\n\n> 🧠 **One-line test:** does the document list the powers, or list the *person*? If it lists **powers**, it is specific/limited. If it hands over the **person's whole life**, it is general.\n\n### SEBI's protections on broker/DP Powers of Attorney\n\nBecause PoAs to brokers were once abused, SEBI's rules are strict:\n\n- A PoA is **optional, never a pre-condition** for opening a trading or demat account.\n- It may authorise transfer of securities **only for the client's own settlement obligations** — not for arbitrary off-market transfers.\n- It must **not** allow the broker to **merge client balances**, open or close accounts, or take a loan against the client's securities.\n- It must be executed in favour of the **broker as an entity**, not in favour of its employees.\n- It must state the client's **right to revoke at any time**, with notice.\n- Modern alternatives now largely replace it: the **DDPI (Demat Debit and Pledge Instruction)**, which permits **only two** things — transfer of securities for **settlement obligations** and **pledging/re-pledging for margin** — and **e-DIS with a TPIN/OTP**, where the client authorises each debit personally.\n\n### When a PoA ends\n\n**Revocation** by the donor; **death, insanity or insolvency** of the donor; **completion of the purpose**; **expiry of the stated term**; or **renunciation** by the attorney.\n\n> ⚠️ **Two traps.** (1) A **PoA holder cannot make or change a nomination** — nomination is a personal act of the unit holder. (2) A PoA **dies with the donor**; an attorney who transacts after the donor's death acts without authority.\n\n**Other agreements at on-boarding:** the **investment advisory agreement** (mandatory for SEBI-registered IAs), the **PMS agreement**, the **AIF contribution agreement**, the DP–BO agreement, the broker–client **Rights and Obligations** document, and the **NACH/e-mandate** authorising periodic debits.\n\n---\n\n## 17.5 Account Opening for Non-Resident Investors (NRIs)\n\n### Residency — two different definitions\n\n| | **FEMA, 1999** | **Income Tax Act, 1961** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Test** | Days **plus the purpose/intent** of the stay | Purely a **day-count** test |\n| **Basic rule** | Residing in India for **more than 182 days in the preceding financial year** — but a person leaving for **employment, business or an uncertain period** becomes non-resident **immediately**, and a person coming to India for those reasons becomes resident **immediately** | **182 days** in the year, or **60 days in the year plus 365 days in the preceding four years** (with relaxations for Indian citizens leaving for employment) |\n| **Governs** | **Which accounts and assets you may hold** | **How you are taxed** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Classic trap.** A person can be a **resident under FEMA and a non-resident under the Income Tax Act in the same year**, or the reverse. FEMA decides the *account type*; income tax decides the *tax*. Never mix them.\n\n### ⭐ The three NRI bank accounts\n\n| Feature | **NRE** (Non-Resident External) | **NRO** (Non-Resident Ordinary) | **FCNR(B)** (Foreign Currency Non-Resident – Bank) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Currency held in** | **Indian rupees** | **Indian rupees** | **Foreign currency** (USD, GBP, EUR, JPY, AUD, CAD and other freely convertible currencies) |\n| **What you may credit** | **Foreign earnings** remitted from abroad | **Indian income** — rent, dividend, pension, interest, sale proceeds — **plus** foreign remittances | **Foreign earnings** remitted from abroad |\n| **Repatriability** | **Fully repatriable** — principal and interest | Interest fully repatriable; **principal up to USD 1 million per financial year**, net of taxes, with Form 15CA/15CB | **Fully repatriable** |\n| **Indian tax on interest** | **Exempt** | **Taxable**, with TDS deducted at source | **Exempt** |\n| **Account types allowed** | Savings, current, recurring, fixed deposit | Savings, current, recurring, fixed deposit | **Term deposit only**, tenure **1 to 5 years** |\n| **Exchange-rate risk** | **Yes** — rupee-denominated | **Yes** | **No** — the deposit stays in foreign currency |\n| **Joint holding** | With other NRIs; with a **resident close relative on a \"former or survivor\" basis** | With NRIs and with residents | With other NRIs; with a resident close relative on \"former or survivor\" basis |\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line mnemonic.** **NRE = money from outside, fully free to go back out, tax-free.** **NRO = money earned inside India, taxed, capped repatriation.** **FCNR = the same as NRE but you keep the dollars, so no currency risk.**\n\n**On returning to India:** NRE and NRO accounts must be **re-designated as resident accounts**; NRE balances may be moved to an **RFC (Resident Foreign Currency)** account; **FCNR deposits may be run to maturity at the contracted rate** and then converted to RFC.\n\n### What an NRI may and may not invest in\n\n| Permitted | Not permitted / restricted |\n|---|---|\n| **Mutual funds** — repatriable (from NRE/FCNR/inward remittance) or non-repatriable (from NRO) | **New PPF, NSC, SCSS, KVP** accounts |\n| **Direct equity in the secondary market** under the **Portfolio Investment Scheme (PIS)** | **Agricultural land, plantations, farmhouses** |\n| **IPOs and primary market** — no PIS route needed | **Intraday trading and short selling** in equity — NRIs are **delivery-based only** |\n| **Government securities, T-bills, NCDs, bonds** | Currency and commodity derivatives are restricted; equity derivatives are permitted only on a **non-repatriable** basis with prescribed limits |\n| **Real estate** other than the barred categories | Some AMCs decline **US and Canada** residents |\n| **Life insurance; NPS Tier I** (including OCIs) | |\n\n### ⭐ PIS and the demat account limits\n\n- Secondary-market equity purchases by an NRI must be routed through a **designated branch of one authorised dealer bank** under the **PIS**. Only **one bank** may hold the PIS mandate at a time.\n- **Individual NRI limit: 5% of the paid-up capital** of a company. **Aggregate NRI limit: 10%**, which the company may raise to **24%** by a board resolution followed by a **special resolution** of shareholders.\n- **Separate demat accounts are mandatory**: one **NRE/repatriable** demat linked to the NRE bank account, and one **NRO/non-repatriable** demat linked to the NRO account. Shares bought under **PIS** must be held separately from shares acquired **otherwise** (IPO, gift, inheritance, ESOP, or under FDI).\n- Purchases are reported by the designated bank to the RBI; the bank monitors the sectoral caps daily.\n\n### The PMLA and FEMA overlay\n\n| **PMLA, 2002** — \"is this money clean?\" | **FEMA, 1999** — \"is this money allowed to cross the border?\" |\n|---|---|\n| A **criminal** statute; the offence is money laundering | A **civil** statute (it replaced the criminal **FERA, 1973**); contraventions can be **compounded** |\n| Requires KYC, record keeping for **5 years**, ongoing monitoring, **UBO** and **PEP** identification, and reporting of **CTR/STR to FIU-IND** | Regulates **current** and **capital** account transactions; administered by the **RBI** |\n| Bans **third-party payments** and unexplained cash | Fixes **who may hold what**: NRE/NRO/FCNR rules, PIS limits, repatriation ceilings |\n| **Tipping off** the client about an STR is an offence | Residents may remit up to **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** under the **Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS)**; remittances above a prescribed annual threshold attract **TCS**, at concessional rates for education and medical purposes |\n\n---\n\n## 17.6 Consolidating, Reorganising and Maintaining a Folio\n\n### 17.6.1 Change of Address and Contact Details\n\n| Client's KYC status | Where the change is made |\n|---|---|\n| **KYC compliant** (registered with a KRA/CKYC) | Through the **KRA/CKYC**, using the prescribed **KYC change form** — the update then flows to **every** intermediary |\n| **Not KYC compliant** (e.g., a PEKRN holder) | Directly with the **AMC/RTA**, folio by folio |\n\n**Documents:** the change form signed **as per the mode of holding**, a **self-attested copy of a new OVD showing the new address** (with the original for verification, or a true copy attested by a permitted authority), and PAN. **NRIs must additionally provide proof of the overseas address.**\n\n**Controls against fraud:** the change is confirmed by SMS and email to **both** the old and the new contact details, and a change of address is often followed by a **cooling-off period** before redemption proceeds are released.\n\n### 17.6.2 Change in Name\n\nArises on **marriage**, **divorce**, or a change by **deed poll/gazette notification**.\n\n**Documents:** the request signed by the holder, plus **marriage certificate** or **gazette notification** or **deed poll**, and — critically — **the PAN card in the new name**. Update **PAN first, then KRA/CKYC, then the folios and demat accounts**. Doing it in any other order guarantees a mismatch rejection.\n\n### 17.6.3 Change in Status\n\n\"Status\" means the investor's **regulatory and tax classification**. The main changes:\n\n| Change | What must be redone |\n|---|---|\n| **Resident → NRI**, or **NRI → Resident** | Bank accounts re-designated, KYC status changed at the KRA, new bank mandate, fresh FATCA/CRS declaration, demat reorganised |\n| **Minor → Major** | Fresh KYC, PAN, signature, bank account and nomination in the now-major's own name |\n| **Individual → HUF / firm → LLP / private → public company** | A **new folio** in the new legal entity's name; the old entity's holdings are transferred or transmitted |\n| **Sole proprietorship → partnership** | New entity, new PAN, new folio |\n| **Change of tax residency (FATCA/CRS)** | Fresh self-certification within **30 days** of the change in circumstances |\n\n> ⚠️ **The rule that catches everyone:** a change of **tax status almost always needs a new folio**, because a folio's tax status is fixed at the first holder's PAN. You cannot simply flip a resident folio to \"NRI\" and carry on.\n\n### 17.6.4 ⭐ Marking a Lien\n\nA **lien** is a **charge** created over an investment as **security for a debt**. Once marked, the investor **cannot redeem, switch or transfer** the units without the lienholder's consent.\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | The investor pledges the units to a **bank or NBFC** for a loan against securities |\n| 2 | The lender sends a **lien-marking request** with the investor's letter to the **AMC/RTA** |\n| 3 | The AMC/RTA marks the lien, **freezes the specified units**, and confirms to **both** parties |\n| 4 | On **repayment**, the lender issues a **release/no-objection letter** and the lien is **lifted** |\n| 5 | On **default**, the lender can **invoke the lien** and have the units redeemed, with proceeds paid to the lender |\n\n**Key points:**\n- A lien can be marked on **part** of a holding — only those units are frozen.\n- Income distributions (**IDCW**) usually continue to reach the investor unless agreed otherwise.\n- In **demat** form, the equivalent action is a **pledge** created through the depository — **pledgor** (investor) and **pledgee** (lender), with the depository recording the charge. In **SoA** form, the RTA marks a **lien**.\n- Liens are also marked on **bank fixed deposits** (loan against FD) and on **insurance policies** (usually by way of a conditional assignment).\n\n> 🧠 **Lien ≠ transfer of ownership.** The investor still owns the units; the lender merely holds the right to block and, if unpaid, to sell.\n\n### 17.6.5 ⭐ Transmission\n\n**Transmission is the passing of an investment to survivors, nominees or legal heirs on the DEATH of a holder.** It is an operation of law, not a voluntary act.\n\n| | **Transfer** | **Transmission** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Trigger** | A **voluntary** act between living persons | The **death** of a holder |\n| **Who initiates** | The seller/donor | The **claimant** |\n| **Consideration** | Usually paid | None |\n| **Mutual fund units** | **Not permitted** for open-ended units (units are non-transferable in SoA form) | Always permitted |\n\n**The four scenarios and their documents:**\n\n| Scenario | Who receives | Core documents |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Death of one of joint holders** | The **surviving holder(s)** | Transmission request form, **death certificate**, KYC of survivors, fresh bank details and **fresh nomination** |\n| **2. Death of the sole holder, nomination registered** | The **nominee(s)** | Transmission request form, death certificate, **nominee's KYC**, nominee's bank proof, indemnity/declaration |\n| **3. Death of the sole holder, NO nomination** | The **legal heirs** | Death certificate, KYC and bank proof of claimants, **plus** a **succession certificate, probate of will or letter of administration** — or, under **AMFI's simplified route for smaller claims (currently up to ₹5 lakh per folio)**, an **indemnity bond, affidavits from all legal heirs and NOCs from the non-claimant heirs** |\n| **4. Death of the Karta of an HUF** | The **new Karta** | Death certificate, HUF declaration naming the new Karta, KYC and bank details of the new Karta |\n\n> ⚠️ **The single most tested idea in this chapter: A NOMINEE IS A TRUSTEE, NOT AN OWNER.** In mutual funds, shares and bank deposits, the nominee receives the asset so that the intermediary gets a **valid discharge** — but the nominee then holds it **on behalf of the legal heirs**, who take under a will or under succession law. Nomination **does not override succession law**.\n> **The one exception is insurance:** under **Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938** (as amended in 2015), where the nominee is a **parent, spouse or child** (\"beneficial nominee\"), the nominee is **beneficially entitled** to the claim and other heirs cannot claim it.\n\n### 17.6.6 ⭐ Nomination and Change of Nomination\n\n**Nomination** is the investor's instruction naming who should receive the investment on death.\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Who may nominate** | **Only individuals**, holding singly or jointly. **Not** companies, trusts, societies, partnership firms, the **Karta of an HUF**, or a **PoA holder** |\n| **How many nominees** | Mutual funds have long permitted **up to three nominees** with a **percentage allocation**; if percentages are not stated, the allocation is **equal**. *(SEBI has since expanded the permitted number of nominees for demat accounts and mutual fund folios well beyond three.)* |\n| **Who may be a nominee** | Any person — including a **minor** (guardian's details must be given), an **NRI**, a **trust**, a **charitable or religious institution**, a **local authority** or the **Central/State Government** |\n| **Signature requirement** | **ALL joint holders must sign** the nomination — even where the mode of holding is **\"Anyone or Survivor\"** |\n| **Level** | At the **folio level** in mutual funds; at the **account level** in a demat account |\n| **Change** | Can be changed **any number of times**; the **latest valid nomination prevails** and automatically **cancels the previous one** |\n| **Not permitted** | In a folio held **on behalf of a minor**; and a **PoA holder cannot nominate** for the investor |\n| **Mandatory or opt out** | New folios and demat accounts require either a **nomination** or a signed **opt-out declaration** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** People assume \"Anyone or Survivor\" means any one holder can also change the nomination. **It does not.** Transactions can be signed by one; **nomination needs everyone**.\n\n### 17.6.7 ⭐ Assignment — Especially in Insurance\n\n**Assignment is the transfer of the rights, title and interest in a policy from the ASSIGNOR to the ASSIGNEE.** It is governed by **Section 38 of the Insurance Act, 1938**, as amended by the Insurance Laws (Amendment) Act, 2015.\n\n| | **Absolute assignment** | **Conditional assignment** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What transfers** | **All rights, permanently and unconditionally** | Rights transfer **subject to stated conditions** |\n| **Reverts to the assignor?** | **No** | **Yes** — typically on repayment of the loan, or on the assignor surviving to maturity |\n| **Typical use** | Gifting a policy; selling the interest in a policy | Assigning a policy to a **bank as loan collateral** |\n| **Effect on nomination** | The existing nomination is **automatically cancelled** | Nomination is **not cancelled** where the assignment is to an insurer/lender against a loan on the policy |\n\n**Procedural rules:**\n- Must be made by an **endorsement on the policy** or by a **separate instrument**, signed by the transferor/assignor, **attested by at least one witness**, and must state the **fact and the reasons** for the assignment.\n- It is **effective from the date of the notice of assignment served on the insurer**. The insurer records it and issues an acknowledgement.\n- Where there are **several assignments**, priority is decided by the **date on which the notice was delivered to the insurer**.\n- The insurer may **decline to act** on an assignment it believes is **not bona fide, not in the policyholder's interest, or against public interest** — it must record its reasons and communicate them **within 30 days**, and the aggrieved party may refer the matter to **IRDAI within 30 days**.\n- **Policies under the Married Women's Property Act, 1874 cannot be assigned** — the Act creates a trust for the wife and children, beyond the reach of the policyholder's creditors.\n- **Life policies are assignable; health and general insurance policies generally are not** (motor cover moves with the vehicle).\n\n| | **Nomination** | **Assignment** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What it does** | Names who **receives the money on death** | **Transfers ownership** of the policy |\n| **Effective from** | Death of the life assured | Date of **notice to the insurer** |\n| **Who may do it** | The **policyholder** | The **policyholder (assignor)** |\n| **Ownership changes?** | **No** | **Yes** (absolute) / conditionally (conditional) |\n| **Revocable?** | **Yes**, freely | **Absolute — no**; conditional — reverts on the condition |\n| **Governing section** | **Section 39**, Insurance Act, 1938 | **Section 38**, Insurance Act, 1938 |\n\n> 🧠 **38 before 39 — Assignment before Nomination**, both alphabetically and in the Act. A neat way to remember which section is which.\n\n---\n\n## 17.7 Operations in Special Situations\n\n### 17.7.1 ⭐ Minors as Investors\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Who is a minor** | A person below **18** (Indian Majority Act, 1875). Where a **court appoints a guardian**, majority is **21** |\n| **Holding** | The folio must be in the **minor's SOLE name**. A minor can **never** be a joint holder, and there can be **no second holder** |\n| **Represented by** | A **natural guardian** — **father or mother** — or a **court-appointed legal guardian** |\n| **Documents** | **Proof of date of birth** (birth certificate, school leaving certificate/mark sheet, passport) **and proof of the guardian's relationship** with the minor, or the **court order** for a legal guardian |\n| **KYC** | **The guardian's KYC and PAN** are required; the guardian's details drive the folio |\n| **Payment** | Only from the **minor's own account**, the **guardian's account**, or a **joint account of minor and guardian**. Any other source is a **third-party payment** |\n| **Nomination** | **Not permitted** in a minor's folio |\n| **Taxation** | The minor's income is **clubbed with the parent's** under **Section 64(1A)** of the Income Tax Act, except income from the minor's **own skill or manual work**, or where the minor is **disabled** under Section 80U |\n\n### 17.7.2 ⭐ Minor Turns Major\n\nThis is a hard operational stop, and a favourite exam scenario.\n\n- **On the date the minor attains 18, the folio is frozen for financial transactions.** No purchases, redemptions or switches until the status change is completed.\n- **All standing instructions — SIP, SWP, STP — cease** on that date. The AMC must send **advance notice** to the registered address before the birthday.\n- To unfreeze, the now-major submits the **\"Minor Attaining Majority\" (MAM) form** with:\n  - **Signature attested by the banker** (or by the notary/gazetted officer, as permitted)\n  - **PAN and completed KYC** in their own name\n  - **A bank account in the major's own name**, with a cancelled cheque\n  - **A fresh nomination**\n- The **guardian's authority ends completely** on that date — a redemption signed by the erstwhile guardian afterwards is invalid.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"18 and everything stops.\"** The freeze is deliberate: the account has changed owner in law, and the AMC must not act on the old authority.\n\n### 17.7.3 NRI → RI (a returning Indian)\n\n| Step | Action |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Inform the bank immediately.** NRE and NRO accounts are **re-designated as resident accounts** |\n| 2 | NRE balances may be moved to an **RFC account**; **FCNR deposits run to maturity** at the contracted rate, then convert to RFC |\n| 3 | Update **KYC status with the KRA/CKYC**, and with every AMC/RTA/DP via a **change of status form** |\n| 4 | Register a **resident bank mandate**; fresh **FATCA/CRS** self-certification |\n| 5 | **Close the PIS account**; move PIS-held shares to a **resident demat account** and inform the broker |\n| 6 | **Tax:** NRE/FCNR interest **ceases to be exempt** from the date of becoming resident. The person may qualify as **RNOR (Resident but Not Ordinarily Resident)** for a couple of years, during which **foreign income is generally not taxed in India** — a valuable planning window an adviser should flag |\n\n### 17.7.4 RI → NRI (a resident going abroad)\n\n| Step | Action |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | The **resident savings account must be re-designated as an NRO account** — it cannot continue as a resident account |\n| 2 | Open **NRE / FCNR** accounts if foreign earnings will be remitted |\n| 3 | Update **KYC status to NRI** with the KRA/CKYC, AMCs and DP; **new bank mandate**; fresh **FATCA/CRS** declaration |\n| 4 | **Demat:** holdings move to a **non-repatriable (NRO) demat account**; open a **PIS account** if secondary-market equity trading will continue |\n| 5 | **Small savings:** an existing **PPF account may be continued to maturity but cannot be extended**; **SCSS must be closed**; **NSC/KVP** are held to maturity |\n| 6 | Insurance policies **continue** unaffected; premiums may be paid from NRE/NRO or by inward remittance |\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** Both changes require the client to **inform the intermediaries** — the status does not update itself. Continuing to operate a resident account after becoming an NRI is a **FEMA contravention**, and continuing to claim NRE interest exemption after returning is a **tax** contravention.\n\n### 17.7.5 ⭐ Addition or Deletion of Names\n\n| Situation | Mutual fund folio | Demat account | Bank account |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Add a holder** | **Not permitted** — a **new folio** must be opened | **Not permitted** — open a new account and transfer the holdings | Generally **permitted** with the bank's consent and all signatures |\n| **Delete a holder** | Only on **death** (transmission). Not on request | Only on **death** (transmission) | Generally permitted, with all signatures |\n| **Change the ORDER of the same names** | **Transposition** — permitted | **Transposition** — permitted, via a **Transposition Request Form**; it is **not** treated as a transfer | Permitted |\n\n> 🧠 **The distinction that earns marks: TRANSPOSITION changes the ORDER of the SAME names. TRANSMISSION changes the names because someone DIED. TRANSFER changes the names because someone SOLD or GIFTED.**\n\nTransposition matters practically: certificates in the name of \"A and B\" cannot be dematerialised into an account of \"B and A\", so the investor files a transposition request and demats in the same step.\n\n### 17.7.6 Addition or Deletion of a Bank Mandate\n\n- A mutual fund folio may carry **multiple registered bank accounts** — commonly **up to five for individuals and up to ten for non-individuals** — one of which is marked as the **default** for payouts.\n- The account **must be in the name of the first/sole holder** (or of a joint holder of the folio).\n- **Documents:** the multiple-bank-account registration form, plus for the **new** account an **original cancelled cheque with the investor's printed name** (or a bank statement/passbook copy attested by the bank), and proof of the **existing registered** account.\n- **⭐ The cooling-off rule:** if a **redemption request is received within a short window (commonly 10 calendar days) of a change-of-bank-mandate request**, the proceeds are credited to the **OLD, already-registered** bank account, not the new one. This is a deliberate **anti-fraud control** — it defeats the classic scam of changing the bank details and immediately redeeming.\n\n---\n\n## 17.8 Payment Instruments\n\n### 17.8.1 Traditional Payment Instruments\n\n| Instrument | Key points |\n|---|---|\n| **Cash** | Mutual funds may accept cash **only up to ₹50,000 per investor per financial year**, and only from specified small investors. **All repayments — redemption, dividend — must be through the banking channel only.** Beyond that, cash is barred under PMLA |\n| **Cheque** | Governed by the **Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881**. **Valid for 3 months** from the date of issue (reduced from six months with effect from **1 April 2012**). Must be **CTS-2010 compliant**. Dishonour attracts **Section 138** liability |\n| **Demand draft / pay order / banker's cheque** | Bank-guaranteed; the bank debits the customer up front. DD charges are generally **not reimbursed** by AMCs except in specified cases |\n| **Post-dated cheques** | Once used for SIPs; now largely replaced by mandates |\n| **ECS / NACH mandate** | The standing authority to debit a bank account periodically — the backbone of **SIPs** |\n| **Warrants** | Dividend/interest warrants — now almost fully replaced by direct credit |\n\n### 17.8.2 ⭐ Digital Payment Systems\n\n| System | Operated by | Character | Key facts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **NEFT** | **RBI** | **Deferred net settlement**, in batches | **No minimum or maximum**; available **24×7×365** |\n| **RTGS** | **RBI** | **Real-time, gross (transaction by transaction)** | **Minimum ₹2,00,000**; **no upper limit**; available **24×7** |\n| **IMPS** | **NPCI** | Instant, account-to-account | **24×7**; per-transaction ceiling of the order of **₹5 lakh** |\n| **UPI** | **NPCI** | Instant, mobile-first, using a **VPA** | Standard per-transaction limit around **₹1 lakh**, raised to **₹5 lakh** for specified categories including **capital markets, IPO/ASBA and insurance** |\n| **NACH** | **NPCI** | Bulk, recurring debits and credits | Powers **SIPs**, salary, dividend, EMI. Replaced ECS |\n| **UPI AutoPay / e-mandate** | NPCI / RBI framework | Recurring debit without re-entering credentials | The **e-mandate ceiling for recurring mutual fund subscriptions, insurance premiums and credit-card repayments has been raised substantially** (to the order of ₹1 lakh per transaction) without an additional factor of authentication |\n| **ASBA** | Exchanges/banks | **Application Supported by Blocked Amount** | Funds are **blocked in the investor's own account** until allotment; interest continues to accrue. Mandatory for IPOs. A **UPI block mechanism** has been extended to secondary-market trading |\n| **Cards, net banking, wallets/PPIs, AePS, BBPS** | Banks / NPCI | Various | Cards are generally **not** accepted for mutual fund subscriptions |\n\n> 🧠 **RTGS vs NEFT in one line:** **RTGS is Real-Time and Gross — big, immediate, ₹2 lakh minimum. NEFT is batched and has no minimum — small and cheap.** IMPS and UPI are the **retail instant** rails run by NPCI, not the RBI.\n\n### 17.8.3 ⭐ PMLA and Third-Party Payments\n\n**The rule:** a mutual fund (and, by extension, any intermediary) **must not accept a subscription where the payment comes from a bank account other than that of the investor** — for a folio, the **first named holder**. That is a **third-party payment**, and PMLA prohibits it because the audit trail of the money would break.\n\n**The only recognised exceptions — and each needs a signed Third Party Payment Declaration from BOTH the investor and the payer, with the payer's KYC and bank details and the relationship stated:**\n\n| # | Exception | Ceiling / condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Payment by **parents, grandparents or a related person** on behalf of a **minor**, out of natural love and affection or as a gift | **Up to ₹50,000** per transaction — i.e., per lump-sum purchase or per SIP instalment. The guardian's own payment is **not** third-party |\n| **2** | Payment by an **employer on behalf of an employee**, through **payroll deduction**, for SIPs or lump-sum subscriptions | No specified ceiling; the payroll linkage is the control |\n| **3** | Payment by a **custodian on behalf of an FPI or a client** | Standard institutional route |\n| **4** | Payment by an **AMC to an empanelled distributor**, or by a **corporate to its agent/distributor/dealer**, of **commission or incentive in the form of mutual fund units** | Against the payee's own investment |\n\n**The mirror-image rule on the way out:** **redemption, IDCW and maturity proceeds are ALWAYS paid only into the registered bank account of the first/sole holder.** There is **no** third-party exception on the payout side. This is the rule that makes the whole anti-laundering chain work — money must come from the investor and must go back to the investor.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap.** A husband paying for his wife's mutual fund investment from his own account **is a third-party payment and is not permitted** — spouse is not one of the exceptions. So is a company paying for its director's personal investment. The minor exception is the **only** family exception, it is capped at **₹50,000**, and it applies **only where the investor is a minor**.\n\n### 17.8.4 ⭐ Fraud Prevention and Operational Risk\n\n**Cheque-related risks and controls**\n\n| Risk | Control |\n|---|---|\n| Forged signature, material alteration, counterfeit cheques | **CTS-2010** standardised cheques; **image-based clearing**; UV and micro-lettering features |\n| Large-value cheque fraud | **Positive Pay System** (RBI, from **1 January 2021**) — the issuer re-confirms the cheque's date, beneficiary and amount electronically before it is paid. Applies to cheques **above ₹50,000**, with banks free to make it compulsory for higher-value cheques |\n| Stale or post-dated cheques | The **3-month validity** rule |\n\n**Digital payment risks**\n\nPhishing, vishing and smishing; **SIM-swap** attacks; **remote-access apps** (AnyDesk, TeamViewer) installed under the pretext of \"help\"; **fake UPI collect requests** (\"scan this QR to RECEIVE money\" — scanning a QR **never** receives money, it only sends it); fake customer-care numbers found by searching online; card skimming; malware and keyloggers; **mule accounts**.\n\n**Controls every investor and adviser must apply**\n\n- **Never share** OTP, PIN, CVV, passwords or card details — with anyone, including someone claiming to be from the bank, the AMC or SEBI.\n- Use **two-factor authentication**; transact only on the official app or a URL you typed yourself.\n- Enable **SMS and email alerts** on every account, and reconcile the **CAS** each month.\n- For an advisory firm: **maker-checker**, **segregation of duties**, **dual authorisation** for large transfers, **call-back verification** of any change in bank details, **audit trails**, and periodic reconciliation.\n- **Report unauthorised transactions immediately** — the **national cybercrime helpline 1930** and **cybercrime.gov.in**, plus the bank.\n\n**RBI's limited-liability framework for unauthorised electronic transactions** (a genuinely valuable client-protection fact):\n\n| When the customer reports | Customer's liability |\n|---|---|\n| Loss due to **the bank's negligence or a third-party system breach**, reported within **3 working days** | **ZERO** |\n| Third-party breach reported within **4 to 7 working days** | **Limited** — capped by account type (of the order of **₹5,000 / ₹10,000 / ₹25,000**) |\n| Reported **beyond 7 working days** | As per the **bank's board-approved policy** |\n| Loss due to the **customer's own negligence** (e.g., sharing the OTP), before reporting | **Entire loss borne by the customer** |\n\n> ⚠️ **An adviser's own hard rules.** Under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, an IA **must never take custody of client funds or securities** and must **never accept cash**. Fees are received **only in the adviser's registered name, through the banking channel, against an invoice**. Any adviser asking a client to transfer investment money to the adviser's personal account is committing fraud.\n\n---\n\n## 17.9 Documentation Required for Financial Advice\n\nUnder the **SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013**, the adviser must **maintain records** — this is the list the exam draws from:\n\n| Record | Note |\n|---|---|\n| **KYC records** of every client | Including IPV/V-CIP evidence |\n| **Risk profiling and risk assessment** | Including the **client's signed acceptance** of the risk profile |\n| **Suitability assessment** | Showing that the advice fits the profile |\n| **The investment advisory agreement** | **Mandatory** — signed before any advice is given, on SEBI's prescribed terms |\n| **Terms and conditions document** and **disclosures** | Including conflicts of interest and holdings/consideration disclosures |\n| **A record of the advice given AND its RATIONALE** | The item advisers most often neglect — and the one that decides a complaint |\n| **Fee particulars, invoices and receipts** | Fees only through the **banking channel** |\n| **Complaints and their redressal** | Feeding the grievance mechanism |\n\n**Retention:** records must be kept for a **minimum of five years**. Where any **dispute, enquiry or proceeding is pending**, records must be kept until it is **concluded**. Records may be maintained **physically or electronically**; if electronic, they must be **digitally signed** with proper **backup**.\n\n**Other ongoing compliance documentation:** an **annual compliance audit** by a qualified auditor (with the report and any adverse observations dealt with and reported), a displayed **Investor Charter**, periodic **complaints disclosure**, **client-level segregation** of advisory from distribution, and — from the client's side — a signed **acknowledgement of the risk profile, the fee structure and the mode of fee payment**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line reason all of this exists:** if a client complains in year four, the only defence an adviser has is **the file**. \"I told him\" is worth nothing; **\"here is the signed risk profile, the suitability note, the rationale for the recommendation and the client's acknowledgement\"** is worth everything.\n\n---\n\n## 17.10 Investing in Mutual Funds Through Stock Exchange Platforms\n\n### 17.10.1 ⭐ The Platforms\n\n| Platform | Run by | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **BSE StAR MF** | **BSE** | \"**St**ock exchange platform for **A**llotment and **R**epurchase of **M**utual **F**unds\" |\n| **NSE MFSS** (now **NMF II**) | **NSE** | \"**M**utual **F**und **S**ervice **S**ystem\" |\n| **MF Utility (MFU)** | **The AMCs themselves**, through AMFI — **not** a stock exchange | Gives the investor a single **Common Account Number (CAN)** across participating AMCs |\n\n**How access widened over time:** SEBI permitted mutual fund transactions through the **stock exchange infrastructure in November 2009**, initially only for **stock brokers**. Access was later extended to **clearing members and depository participants**, and then — importantly for this exam — to **SEBI-registered Investment Advisers**, who may use the platforms to **purchase and redeem units on behalf of their clients**, and to **mutual fund distributors for execution-only purposes**.\n\n### 17.10.2 How an Investment Adviser Helps a Client Transact\n\n| Benefit | Why it matters to the client |\n|---|---|\n| **One window for every AMC** | No separate form, cheque and folio per fund house |\n| **Paperless and fast** | Orders are placed electronically; the exchange settles the funds and units |\n| **Units in demat or SoA** | The client chooses; demat holdings appear in the CAS with everything else |\n| **All order types** | Lump sum, **SIP**, redemption, switch, STP/SWP registration |\n| **Direct plans available** | The adviser can transact **direct plans**, so the client pays **no distribution commission** — which is exactly what a fee-only IA should be doing |\n| **Uniform cut-off and audit trail** | The exchange time-stamps the order — clean evidence of the applicable NAV |\n| **Consolidated reporting** | The adviser's system reconciles every AMC from one source |\n\nThe mechanics for the client: complete KYC, register with the adviser/broker on the platform, receive a **unique client code**, and thereafter place orders that the platform routes to the AMC/RTA. Funds move through the exchange's settlement mechanism, and units are allotted subject to the **usual cut-off timing and realisation-of-funds rules**.\n\n### 17.10.3 ⭐ Transaction Feeds for Advisers\n\nA **transaction feed** is a machine-readable daily file of a client's transactions and holdings, supplied by the **RTAs (CAMS, KFin)**, the **exchange platforms** and the **depositories**, which the adviser's portfolio-management software ingests automatically.\n\n**Why feeds matter:**\n\n- **No manual data entry**, and therefore no typing errors in a client's portfolio.\n- **Daily valuation and reconciliation** — the adviser sees the true position, not a stale one.\n- **Consolidated, multi-AMC, multi-asset reporting** for review meetings.\n- **Monitoring** of SIP bounces, redemptions the client did on their own, exit-load windows, and capital-gains positions.\n- **Evidence for compliance** — an unarguable record of what was actually transacted, supporting the adviser's record-keeping obligation.\n\nFeeds are provided **only with the client's consent/mandate**, and the adviser must protect the data — client information is confidential, and misuse is both an ethical and a regulatory breach.\n\n**And for the client directly:** the **Consolidated Account Statement (CAS)** does the same job on a monthly/half-yearly cycle across all mutual funds and both depositories against a single PAN.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Maximum holders in a folio / demat account | **Three** |\n| Default mode of holding if left blank (multiple holders) | **Joint** — everyone must sign everything |\n| Who is the tax owner of a folio | The **first holder** — their PAN and bank account govern |\n| A minor's contract | **Void** — hence a guardian invests for a minor |\n| OCBs | **De-recognised by RBI in 2003** |\n| Terms of offer — mutual fund | **SID + SAI + KIM** |\n| Terms of offer — PMS / AIF | **Disclosure Document** / **PPM** |\n| PAN issued under | **Section 139A, Income Tax Act, 1961** |\n| PAN format | **5 letters + 4 digits + 1 letter** = 10 characters |\n| PAN 4th character | Status: **P**erson, **C**ompany, **H**UF, **F**irm, **A**OP, **T**rust, **B**OI, **L**ocal authority, **J**uridical, **G**overnment |\n| PAN 5th character | First letter of the **surname / entity name** |\n| Micro-SIP PAN exemption | Up to **₹50,000 per investor per financial year** per mutual fund; **PEKRN** issued |\n| Other PAN exemption | Residents of **Sikkim** |\n| KYC's parent law | **PMLA, 2002** + **PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005** |\n| The six OVDs | Passport, driving licence, **Aadhaar** possession proof, **Voter ID**, **NREGA job card**, **NPR letter** |\n| Is PAN an OVD? | **NO** — it is separately mandatory |\n| Deemed OVD utility bill age | **Not more than 2 months**; produce a proper OVD within **3 months** |\n| IPV alternative | **V-CIP** — video-based customer identification |\n| Aadhaar **OTP** e-KYC investment cap | **₹50,000 per financial year per mutual fund** |\n| KRA governed by | **SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011** |\n| KRA scope | **Securities market only** |\n| KRA examples | **CVL, NDML, CAMS, KFin, DotEx** |\n| CKYCR operated by | **CERSAI**, under **Rule 9(1A)** of the PML Rules |\n| CKYC scope | **All financial sectors** — banking, securities, insurance, pensions |\n| CKYC identifier | **14-digit KIN** |\n| CKYC upload deadline | **Within 10 days** of starting the relationship |\n| PMLA record retention | **5 years** |\n| PMLA reports go to | **FIU-IND** — CTR and STR. **Tipping off is an offence** |\n| Depositories Act | **1996**; **NSDL** and **CDSL**; investors deal through a **DP** |\n| ISIN | **12 characters**, unique per security |\n| NSDL vs CDSL account number | NSDL: **\"IN\" + 8-char DP ID + 8-digit client ID**; CDSL: **16-digit numeric** |\n| Demat form / number | **DRF** / **DRN**; account is **credited**; ~**30 days** |\n| Remat form / number | **RRF** / **RRN**; account is **debited**; **new distinctive numbers** |\n| Demat name-matching rule | Names **and their ORDER** must match exactly |\n| Physical transfer of listed shares | **Barred since 1 April 2019** (transmission/transposition excepted) |\n| BSDA | One demat account only, holding value within SEBI's ceiling (**raised to ₹10 lakh**), **nil/concessional AMC** |\n| PoA governed by | **Powers of Attorney Act, 1882** |\n| General PoA | **Broad** authority over the donor's affairs — high risk |\n| Specific/Limited PoA | **Only the listed acts** — preferred in financial services |\n| Broker PoA | **Optional**, settlement-only, revocable; now largely replaced by **DDPI** |\n| DDPI covers | **Only** settlement delivery and **pledge/re-pledge for margin** |\n| PoA ends on | Revocation, **death**, insanity or insolvency of the donor, purpose complete, term expiry |\n| Can a PoA holder nominate? | **NO** |\n| FEMA vs Income Tax residency | FEMA = days **+ purpose** (decides accounts); IT = **day count** (decides tax) |\n| **NRE** | Rupee, **foreign** source, **fully repatriable**, interest **tax-free** |\n| **NRO** | Rupee, **Indian** income, **taxable**, repatriation capped at **USD 1 million per FY** |\n| **FCNR(B)** | **Foreign currency**, **term deposit only, 1–5 years**, tax-free, **no currency risk** |\n| Returning NRI's foreign-currency account | **RFC** |\n| NRI PIS limits | **5%** per individual; **10%** aggregate, raisable to **24%** by special resolution |\n| NRI demat accounts | **Separate** repatriable (NRE) and non-repatriable (NRO) accounts; PIS holdings kept apart |\n| NRI equity trading | **Delivery-based only** — no intraday, no short selling |\n| NRIs cannot open | **New PPF, NSC, SCSS, KVP**; cannot buy **agricultural land/plantation/farmhouse** |\n| PPF on becoming NRI | May be **continued to maturity but not extended** |\n| FEMA vs PMLA | FEMA is **civil** (compoundable), replaced FERA 1973; PMLA is **criminal** |\n| LRS limit | **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual |\n| Change of address for a KYC-compliant client | Through the **KRA/CKYC**, not folio by folio |\n| Change of name order | **PAN first → KRA/CKYC → folios and demat** |\n| Lien | A **charge** — units frozen; investor still **owns** them; released on repayment, invoked on default |\n| Lien's demat equivalent | **Pledge** (pledgor/pledgee) through the depository |\n| Transmission | Passing of assets **on DEATH** — to survivors, nominee or legal heirs |\n| Transfer vs transmission | Transfer = **voluntary, between the living**; transmission = **death** |\n| No nomination, sole holder | **Succession certificate / probate / letter of administration**, or AMFI's simplified **indemnity + affidavits + NOC** route for smaller claims (**up to ₹5 lakh** per folio) |\n| **A nominee is** | A **TRUSTEE for the legal heirs — NOT the owner** |\n| The insurance exception | **Section 39, Insurance Act** — a **beneficial nominee** (parent, spouse, child) **is** beneficially entitled |\n| Who may nominate | **Only individuals** — not HUF karta, company, trust, firm or PoA holder |\n| Nominees permitted (MF, traditional figure) | **Up to three**, with percentages (equal if unstated) |\n| Nomination signatures | **ALL joint holders must sign**, even in \"Anyone or Survivor\" |\n| Nomination in a minor's folio | **Not permitted** |\n| Assignment governed by | **Section 38, Insurance Act, 1938** |\n| Absolute assignment | **All rights transfer permanently**; nomination is **cancelled** |\n| Conditional assignment | Rights **revert** on the stated condition (loan repaid / survival to maturity) |\n| Assignment effective from | The **date of notice served on the insurer**; priority by date of notice |\n| Insurer declining an assignment | Record reasons and communicate within **30 days**; appeal to **IRDAI within 30 days** |\n| MWP Act policies | **Cannot be assigned** |\n| Minor's folio | **Sole name only**, guardian's KYC, **no nomination**, no joint holder |\n| Age of majority | **18** (**21** if a court-appointed guardian) |\n| Minor's income | **Clubbed with the parent's** under **Section 64(1A)** |\n| Minor turns major | Folio **frozen**; all **SIP/SWP/STP cease**; MAM form + **banker-attested signature** + PAN + KYC + own bank account + fresh nomination |\n| Adding a holder to a folio/demat | **Not permitted** — open a new one |\n| Deleting a holder | Only on **death** |\n| Transposition | Change in the **ORDER of the SAME names** — permitted, not a transfer |\n| Bank mandates per folio | Commonly up to **5 (individuals) / 10 (non-individuals)**; one **default** |\n| Change-of-bank cooling-off | Redemption within the window (commonly **10 days**) is paid to the **OLD** registered account |\n| Cash in mutual funds | **Up to ₹50,000 per investor per financial year**; **repayments only through banking channels** |\n| Cheque validity | **3 months** (since **1 April 2012**); **NI Act, 1881**; **CTS-2010** |\n| NEFT | **RBI**, **deferred net settlement**, **no minimum**, 24×7 |\n| RTGS | **RBI**, **real-time gross**, **minimum ₹2 lakh**, no maximum, 24×7 |\n| IMPS / UPI / NACH | **NPCI** — instant retail rails; NACH powers **SIPs** |\n| ASBA | Funds **blocked** in the investor's own account until allotment |\n| Positive Pay System | RBI, from **1 January 2021**, for cheques **above ₹50,000** |\n| Third-party payment rule | Payment must come from the **first holder's own account** |\n| Exception 1 | **Parent/grandparent/related person for a MINOR — up to ₹50,000** per transaction |\n| Exception 2 | **Employer for an employee** via **payroll deduction** |\n| Exception 3 | **Custodian for an FPI/client** |\n| Exception 4 | **AMC/corporate paying commission in units** to an empanelled distributor/agent |\n| Redemption proceeds | **Always** to the **first holder's registered bank account** — no exceptions |\n| Unauthorised e-transaction reported in **3 working days** | **Zero liability** (bank negligence or third-party breach) |\n| Cyber fraud helpline | **1930** / cybercrime.gov.in |\n| IA record retention | **5 years**; longer if a **dispute is pending** |\n| The record advisers forget | **The RATIONALE for the advice given** |\n| An IA may never | Take **custody** of client funds/securities, or accept **cash** |\n| Exchange MF platforms | **BSE StAR MF** and **NSE MFSS/NMF II**; **MF Utility (CAN)** is the AMCs' own platform |\n| MF-through-exchange permitted from | **November 2009** — brokers first, later DPs/clearing members, then **RIAs** and execution-only MFDs |\n| Transaction feeds | Daily machine-readable transaction/holding files from **RTAs, exchanges and depositories** — automatic reconciliation and reporting |\n| CAS | **Monthly** where there were transactions; **half-yearly** (March, September) for all holdings, against a common **PAN** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** three ideas generate a disproportionate share of the marks in this chapter, and they are all about **who is entitled to the money**. First, **a nominee is a trustee, not an owner — except a beneficial nominee under Section 39 of the Insurance Act.** Second, **money must come from the investor's own account and go back to the investor's own account** — learn the **three third-party exceptions and the ₹50,000 minor cap** cold. Third, **names on an account can never simply be added or deleted** — you may only **transpose** the order, or **transmit** on death. If you also memorise the **NRE/NRO/FCNR grid** and the **₹50,000 / ₹2 lakh / 3-month / 10-day / 5-year** numbers, the chapter is largely secured.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 17: Operational Aspects of Investment Management — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The nominee-is-a-trustee rule, the third-party payment exceptions and the NRE/NRO/FCNR grid are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** What is the maximum number of holders in a folio or demat account, who is the \"tax owner\", and what is the default mode of holding if the box is left blank?\n> **A:** **Three** holders. The **first holder** is the tax owner — their **PAN, KYC and tax status** govern the folio and **all payouts go to their registered bank account**. If the mode of holding is left blank with more than one holder, the default is **Joint** — every holder must then sign every instruction.\n\n**Q2.** Distinguish the three modes of holding.\n> **A:** **Single** — the sole holder signs. **Joint** — **all** holders must sign. **Anyone or Survivor** — **any one** holder can sign, and the survivors continue the folio on a death.\n\n**Q3.** Why must an investment in a minor's name be made by a guardian, and which investor class did RBI de-recognise in 2003?\n> **A:** Because a **minor's contract is void** under the **Indian Contract Act, 1872** — the folio is in the minor's **sole name**, operated by a **natural guardian (father/mother)** or a **court-appointed guardian**. **Overseas Corporate Bodies (OCBs)** were de-recognised by RBI in **2003**.\n\n**Q4.** Name the terms-of-offer document for a mutual fund, a PMS and an AIF.\n> **A:** Mutual fund — **SID + SAI + KIM**. PMS — the **Disclosure Document** plus the PMS agreement. AIF — the **Private Placement Memorandum (PPM)** plus the contribution agreement.\n\n**Q5.** List the seven steps of client on-boarding.\n> **A:** (1) **Understand the client** — fact-find, **risk profiling**, suitability; (2) explain the **terms of offer**; (3) sign the **agreement**; (4) **PAN and KYC** with IPV/V-CIP; (5) complete the **application** with bank mandate, nomination and FATCA/CRS; (6) **payment from the investor's own account**; (7) **folio/account creation**.\n\n**Q6.** Under which section and Act is PAN issued, what is its format, and what does the 4th character mean?\n> **A:** **Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961**; **10 characters — 5 letters, 4 digits, 1 letter**. The **4th character is the holder's status**: **P** = individual, **C** = Company, **H** = HUF, **F** = Firm/LLP, **A** = AOP, **T** = Trust, **B** = BOI, **L** = Local authority, **J** = juridical person, **G** = Government. The **5th** is the first letter of the **surname/entity name**.\n\n**Q7.** What is the micro-SIP PAN exemption, and who can use it?\n> **A:** Investments up to **₹50,000 per investor per financial year**, aggregated across the schemes of one mutual fund, are **exempt from PAN**; a **PEKRN** is issued instead. Available only to **individuals, NRIs, minors and sole proprietors** — not to HUFs or other non-individuals. **Residents of Sikkim** enjoy a separate PAN exemption.\n\n**Q8.** Which law is the parent of KYC, and what are the six Officially Valid Documents?\n> **A:** The **Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002** with the **PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005**. The six OVDs: **passport, driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar, Voter's Identity Card, NREGA job card** signed by a State Government officer, and the **National Population Register letter**. **PAN is NOT an OVD** — it is separately mandatory.\n\n**Q9.** What is a \"deemed OVD\" and what are its time limits?\n> **A:** Where the OVD carries an **outdated address**, a **utility bill not more than 2 months old**, a municipal/property tax receipt, a pension payment order or an employer-allotted accommodation letter may be used temporarily — and a proper **OVD with the updated address must follow within 3 months**.\n\n**Q10.** What is V-CIP, and what is the cap on Aadhaar OTP-based e-KYC?\n> **A:** **V-CIP** is the **Video-based Customer Identification Process**, a permitted alternative to physical IPV. **Aadhaar OTP-based e-KYC** caps the investment at **₹50,000 per financial year per mutual fund**; **biometric** Aadhaar e-KYC has **no such cap**.\n\n**Q11.** Distinguish a KRA from the CKYCR.\n> **A:** A **KRA** is set up under the **SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011** and covers the **securities market only** (CVL, NDML, CAMS, KFin, DotEx). The **CKYCR** is operated by **CERSAI** under **Rule 9(1A) of the PML Rules**, covers **all financial sectors — banking, securities, insurance, pensions** — and issues a **14-digit KIN**.\n\n**Q12.** Within how many days must KYC data reach the CKYCR, how long must PMLA records be preserved, and which reports go to FIU-IND?\n> **A:** Uploaded **within 10 days** of commencing the relationship; records preserved for at least **5 years**. **CTR** (Cash Transaction Reports) and **STR** (Suspicious Transaction Reports) go to **FIU-IND**. **Tipping off** the client about an STR is **an offence**.\n\n**Q13.** Describe dematerialisation, naming the form, the reference number and the time frame.\n> **A:** Converting **physical certificates into electronic form**: the investor submits a **DRF** with certificates **defaced \"Surrendered for Dematerialisation\"**, the DP generates a **DRN**, the RTA verifies and cancels the certificates, and the depository **CREDITS** the demat account — typically within about **30 days**.\n\n**Q14.** Describe rematerialisation and state what changes.\n> **A:** Converting **electronic holdings back into physical certificates** using an **RRF** and an **RRN**; the demat account is **DEBITED** and the investor receives **fresh certificates with NEW distinctive numbers**. It is largely impractical now, because **physical transfer of listed shares has been barred since 1 April 2019**.\n\n**Q15.** Why can a certificate in the names \"A and B\" not be dematerialised into an account of \"B and A\"?\n> **A:** Because the **names AND their order must match exactly**. The fix is **transposition** — changing the order of the **same** names, which is permitted and is **not** treated as a transfer.\n\n**Q16.** What is an ISIN, and how do NSDL and CDSL account numbers differ?\n> **A:** An **ISIN** is the **12-character** International Securities Identification Number, unique to each security. **NSDL** accounts are **\"IN\" + an 8-character DP ID + an 8-digit client ID**; **CDSL** accounts are a **16-digit numeric BO ID**.\n\n**Q17.** Distinguish a General Power of Attorney from a Specific/Limited PoA.\n> **A:** A **GPoA** gives **broad authority over all or most of the donor's affairs** — very high risk. A **Specific/Limited PoA** authorises **only the acts expressly listed** (e.g., debiting a demat account for settlement obligations, or operating one account for one portfolio). Financial services always prefer the **limited** form.\n\n**Q18.** State four SEBI restrictions on a broker PoA, and say what a DDPI covers.\n> **A:** A PoA is **optional, never mandatory**; it may authorise transfer of securities **only for the client's own settlement obligations**; it must **not** permit **merging of client balances**, opening/closing accounts or loans against securities; and it must state the client's **right to revoke**. The modern replacement, the **DDPI (Demat Debit and Pledge Instruction)**, permits **only two** things — **settlement delivery** and **pledge/re-pledge for margin**.\n\n**Q19.** When does a PoA cease, and can a PoA holder make a nomination?\n> **A:** It ceases on **revocation, the death, insanity or insolvency of the donor**, completion of the purpose, or expiry of its term. A **PoA holder can NEVER make or change a nomination** — nomination is a personal act of the investor.\n\n**Q20.** How does FEMA residency differ from Income Tax residency?\n> **A:** **FEMA** looks at **days plus the PURPOSE of the stay** — someone leaving for employment or an uncertain period becomes non-resident **immediately** — and it decides **which accounts and assets you may hold**. The **Income Tax Act** applies a pure **day-count** test (182 days, or 60 + 365) and decides **how you are taxed**. A person can be resident under one and non-resident under the other in the same year.\n\n**Q21.** Compare NRE, NRO and FCNR(B) on currency, source of funds, repatriability and tax.\n> **A:** **NRE** — rupees, **foreign** earnings, **fully repatriable**, interest **tax-exempt**. **NRO** — rupees, **Indian** income (rent, dividend, pension), **taxable**, principal repatriation capped at **USD 1 million per financial year**. **FCNR(B)** — **foreign currency**, **term deposits only of 1 to 5 years**, fully repatriable, interest **tax-exempt**, and **no exchange-rate risk** because the deposit stays in the foreign currency.\n\n**Q22.** What happens to an NRI's accounts on returning to India permanently?\n> **A:** NRE and NRO accounts are **re-designated as resident accounts**; NRE balances may move to an **RFC (Resident Foreign Currency)** account; **FCNR deposits may run to maturity** at the contracted rate and then convert to RFC. **NRE/FCNR interest ceases to be tax-exempt**, and the person may enjoy **RNOR** status for a couple of years, during which foreign income is generally not taxed in India.\n\n**Q23.** State the NRI limits under the Portfolio Investment Scheme and the demat structure required.\n> **A:** **5% of a company's paid-up capital per individual NRI**; **10% for all NRIs in aggregate**, raisable to **24%** by a board resolution plus a **special resolution**. Trades route through **one designated AD bank branch** and are **delivery-based only** — no intraday, no short selling. **Separate demat accounts** are needed for **repatriable (NRE)** and **non-repatriable (NRO)** holdings, with **PIS** shares kept apart from non-PIS shares.\n\n**Q24.** Which investments are closed to NRIs, and what happens to an existing PPF?\n> **A:** **New PPF, NSC, SCSS and KVP** accounts, and **agricultural land, plantations and farmhouses**. An existing **PPF may be continued to maturity but not extended**; an **SCSS account must be closed**.\n\n**Q25.** Contrast PMLA and FEMA in one line each.\n> **A:** **PMLA, 2002** is a **criminal** law asking \"**is this money clean?**\" — KYC, 5-year records, UBO/PEP, CTR/STR to FIU-IND, no third-party payments. **FEMA, 1999** is a **civil** law (it replaced the criminal FERA, 1973; contraventions can be **compounded**) asking \"**may this money cross the border?**\" — account types, PIS limits, repatriation ceilings and the **LRS limit of USD 2,50,000 per financial year**.\n\n**Q26.** Where does a KYC-compliant client change their address, and in what order must a name change be done?\n> **A:** Through the **KRA/CKYC** — the update then flows to **every** intermediary (a non-KYC-compliant investor updates folio by folio with the AMC/RTA). A name change must go **PAN first → then KRA/CKYC → then folios and demat accounts**, or every record mismatches.\n\n**Q27.** What is a lien, and what is its demat equivalent?\n> **A:** A **charge created over an investment as security for a debt** — the units are **frozen** and cannot be redeemed, switched or transferred without the lienholder's release, though the **investor still owns them**. On repayment the lien is **lifted**; on default it is **invoked**. In **demat** form the equivalent is a **pledge** (pledgor/pledgee) recorded through the depository.\n\n**Q28.** Distinguish transfer from transmission.\n> **A:** **Transfer** is a **voluntary act between living persons** (sale or gift). **Transmission** is the passing of an investment to survivors, nominees or legal heirs **on DEATH** — an operation of law. Open-ended mutual fund units in SoA form **cannot be transferred**, but they can always be **transmitted**.\n\n**Q29.** A sole holder dies with no nomination registered. What does the claimant need?\n> **A:** Death certificate, KYC and bank proof of the claimant, **plus a succession certificate, probate of the will or letter of administration** — or, under **AMFI's simplified route for smaller claims (currently up to ₹5 lakh per folio)**, an **indemnity bond, affidavits from all legal heirs and NOCs from the non-claimant heirs**.\n\n**Q30.** State the single most important rule about nominees — and its one exception.\n> **A:** **A nominee is a TRUSTEE, not an owner.** The nominee receives the asset so the intermediary gets a valid discharge, but holds it **for the legal heirs**; nomination **does not override succession law**. **The exception is insurance** — under **Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938** (as amended in 2015), a **beneficial nominee** (**parent, spouse or child**) **is** beneficially entitled to the claim.\n\n**Q31.** Who may NOT make a nomination, and how many holders must sign one?\n> **A:** **Non-individuals** — companies, trusts, societies, partnership firms — the **Karta of an HUF**, and a **PoA holder**. Nomination is also **not permitted in a minor's folio**. **ALL joint holders must sign** a nomination, even in an **\"Anyone or Survivor\"** folio. Mutual funds have traditionally allowed **up to three nominees** with percentages (equal if unstated), and a fresh nomination **cancels** the earlier one.\n\n**Q32.** Distinguish absolute from conditional assignment, and state the governing section.\n> **A:** **Section 38 of the Insurance Act, 1938.** **Absolute** — **all rights transfer permanently and unconditionally**, the assignee becomes owner, and the existing **nomination is cancelled**. **Conditional** — rights transfer **subject to conditions** and **revert to the assignor** on, say, repayment of the loan or survival to maturity; typically used when assigning a policy to a **bank as collateral**.\n\n**Q33.** From when is an assignment effective, and what may the insurer do?\n> **A:** From the **date the notice of assignment is served on the insurer**; where there are several assignments, **priority follows the date of notice**. The insurer may **decline** an assignment that is not bona fide, not in the policyholder's interest or against public interest, recording reasons **within 30 days**; the aggrieved party may refer it to **IRDAI within 30 days**. **MWP Act, 1874 policies cannot be assigned.**\n\n**Q34.** Give three differences between nomination and assignment.\n> **A:** Nomination **names who receives money on death**, **does not transfer ownership**, is **freely revocable**, and sits in **Section 39**. Assignment **transfers ownership during the assignor's lifetime**, is **irrevocable if absolute**, and sits in **Section 38**. Also: **health and general insurance policies are generally not assignable**, while life policies are.\n\n**Q35.** What happens on the day a minor turns 18, and how is it fixed?\n> **A:** The folio is **frozen for financial transactions** and **all standing instructions — SIP, SWP, STP — cease**. To unfreeze, the now-major files the **MAM form** with a **banker-attested signature**, **PAN and KYC in their own name**, **a bank account in their own name**, and a **fresh nomination**. The guardian's authority **ends completely**.\n\n**Q36.** From which accounts may a minor's investment be funded, and how is a minor's income taxed?\n> **A:** Only the **minor's own account**, the **guardian's account**, or a **joint account of minor and guardian**; anything else is a **third-party payment**, permitted only for a **parent/grandparent/related person up to ₹50,000 per transaction** with a declaration. The minor's income is **clubbed with the parent's** under **Section 64(1A)**.\n\n**Q37.** What must a resident do on becoming an NRI?\n> **A:** **Re-designate the resident savings account as an NRO account** (it cannot continue as a resident account), open **NRE/FCNR** if needed, change the **KYC status to NRI** with the KRA and every intermediary, register a **new bank mandate**, re-declare **FATCA/CRS**, and move demat holdings to a **non-repatriable (NRO) demat**, opening a **PIS** account for secondary-market equity trading.\n\n**Q38.** Can a name be added to, or deleted from, an existing folio or demat account?\n> **A:** **No.** A holder **cannot be added** — a **new folio/account** must be opened — and a holder **can only be deleted on DEATH**, through transmission. What *is* permitted is **transposition**: changing the **order of the same names**.\n\n**Q39.** Explain the change-of-bank-mandate cooling-off rule and why it exists.\n> **A:** If a **redemption is requested within the cooling-off window (commonly 10 calendar days) of a change-of-bank request**, the proceeds go to the **OLD, already-registered** account. It defeats the classic fraud of changing bank details and immediately redeeming. A folio may carry **up to about 5 registered accounts for individuals and 10 for non-individuals**, one marked **default**, all in the name of the **first/sole holder**.\n\n**Q40.** How much cash may a mutual fund accept, and how long is a cheque valid?\n> **A:** Cash **up to ₹50,000 per investor per financial year** from specified small investors — and **all repayments must go through the banking channel only**, never in cash. A cheque is **valid for 3 months** from issue (reduced from six months with effect from **1 April 2012**) under the **Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881**, and must be **CTS-2010** compliant.\n\n**Q41.** Contrast NEFT and RTGS, and say which systems NPCI runs.\n> **A:** Both are **RBI** systems. **NEFT** settles on a **deferred net** basis in batches with **no minimum or maximum**, 24×7×365. **RTGS** settles in **real time on a gross basis**, **minimum ₹2,00,000**, **no upper limit**, also 24×7. **NPCI** runs **IMPS, UPI, NACH, AePS, BBPS and RuPay** — and **NACH** (which replaced ECS) powers **SIP** debits, alongside **UPI AutoPay/e-mandates**.\n\n**Q42.** Define a third-party payment, say why it is prohibited, and list the exceptions.\n> **A:** A payment from a **bank account other than the investor's (the first named holder's)**. It is barred under **PMLA** because it **breaks the audit trail of the money**. Exceptions: (1) **parent/grandparent/related person paying for a MINOR — up to ₹50,000 per transaction**; (2) an **employer paying for an employee through payroll deduction**; (3) a **custodian paying for an FPI/client**; (4) an **AMC or corporate paying commission in units** to an empanelled distributor. Each needs a **Third Party Payment Declaration** from both parties. On the payout side there is **no exception** — proceeds always go to the **first holder's registered account**.\n\n**Q43.** What is the Positive Pay System, and when does a customer bear zero liability for an unauthorised electronic transaction?\n> **A:** **Positive Pay** (RBI, from **1 January 2021**) requires the **issuer of a cheque to re-confirm its date, beneficiary and amount electronically** before payment, for cheques **above ₹50,000**. A customer bears **zero liability** where the loss arises from the **bank's negligence or a third-party breach** and is **reported within 3 working days**; **4–7 working days** brings a **capped** liability; beyond that, the bank's board policy applies. Loss from the **customer's own negligence** (sharing an OTP) is borne entirely by the customer. Report fraud on **1930** / cybercrime.gov.in.\n\n**Q44.** What records must a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser maintain, for how long, and what may an IA never do?\n> **A:** **KYC**, **risk profiling with the client's acceptance**, **suitability assessments**, the **signed advisory agreement**, terms & conditions and disclosures, **the advice given AND its RATIONALE**, **fee invoices and receipts**, and complaint records — for a **minimum of 5 years**, and **until conclusion** where a dispute is pending. An IA may **never take custody of client funds or securities and never accept cash**; fees come only in the adviser's registered name through the banking channel.\n\n**Q45.** Name the mutual fund exchange platforms, say who may use them, and explain transaction feeds.\n> **A:** **BSE StAR MF** and **NSE MFSS/NMF II** — plus the AMCs' own **MF Utility (MFU)** with its **Common Account Number (CAN)**, which is not an exchange. Permitted since **November 2009** for brokers, later extended to **clearing members and DPs**, then to **SEBI-registered Investment Advisers** and to **MFDs for execution only**. A **transaction feed** is a **daily machine-readable file of transactions and holdings** from **RTAs, exchanges and depositories** that removes manual data entry and enables **daily reconciliation, consolidated reporting and compliance evidence**. The client's own equivalent is the **CAS** — monthly where there were transactions, half-yearly otherwise.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 17: Operational Aspects of Investment Management — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Thresholds, document lists and the third-party payment rules repeat endlessly.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The maximum number of holders permitted in a mutual fund folio or a demat account is:\nA) Two  B) Three  C) Four  D) No limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Three** holders is the ceiling for both a folio and a demat account.\nA) Two is permitted but is not the maximum. C) Four exceeds the limit. D) There is a definite limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** If a folio has more than one holder and the mode of holding is left blank, the folio is treated as:\nA) Single  B) Anyone or Survivor  C) Joint  D) Invalid and rejected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The default is **Joint**, which means **every holder must sign every instruction** thereafter.\nA) Single is impossible with more than one name. B) \"Anyone or Survivor\" must be positively chosen. D) The folio is opened, not rejected — it simply becomes joint.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** PAN is issued under which provision?\nA) Section 80C of the Income Tax Act  B) Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961  C) Section 38 of the Insurance Act  D) The Depositories Act, 1996\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — PAN is issued by the Income Tax Department under **Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961**.\nA) Section 80C deals with deductions. C) Section 38 governs assignment of insurance policies. D) The Depositories Act governs demat.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** In a PAN, the fourth character \"P\" indicates that the holder is:\nA) A partnership firm  B) An individual  C) A public company  D) A pension fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **P stands for \"Person\", i.e., an individual**.\nA) A firm is **F**. C) A company is **C**. D) There is no separate code for a pension fund; it would fall under trust or AOP.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** A PAN consists of:\nA) 10 digits  B) 12 characters  C) 5 letters, 4 digits and 1 letter  D) 16 alphanumeric characters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Five letters, four digits, one letter** — ten characters in all.\nA) PAN is alphanumeric, not purely numeric. B) Twelve characters is an ISIN. D) Sixteen characters describes a demat account number.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Mutual fund investments up to what amount per investor per financial year are exempt from PAN under the micro-SIP relaxation?\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹50,000  D) ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹50,000 per investor per financial year**, aggregated across the schemes of one mutual fund; a **PEKRN** is issued instead of PAN.\nA) and B) are below the actual threshold. D) ₹1,00,000 is too high.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which of the following is NOT an Officially Valid Document under the PML Rules?\nA) Passport  B) Voter's Identity Card  C) PAN card  D) NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **PAN is not an OVD.** It is separately and additionally mandatory, but it does not serve as proof of address.\nA), B) and D) are all on the list of six OVDs, along with the driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar and the National Population Register letter.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The KYC requirement for financial intermediaries flows primarily from:\nA) The Companies Act, 2013  B) The Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002  C) The Indian Contract Act, 1872  D) SEBI Insider Trading Regulations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **PMLA, 2002** read with the **PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005**.\nA) The Companies Act governs companies. C) The Contract Act governs capacity to contract. D) Insider trading rules are unrelated to KYC.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The Central KYC Records Registry is operated by:\nA) SEBI  B) CERSAI  C) NSDL  D) AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **CERSAI** operates the CKYCR under **Rule 9(1A)** of the PML Rules.\nA) SEBI regulates KRAs, not the CKYCR. C) NSDL is a depository; its subsidiary NDML is a KRA. D) AMFI is the mutual fund industry body.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The identification number issued under CKYC is:\nA) A 10-character PAN  B) A 12-digit Aadhaar  C) A 14-digit KIN  D) A 16-digit BO ID\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **14-digit KYC Identification Number (KIN)**.\nA) PAN is issued by the Income Tax Department. B) Aadhaar is issued by UIDAI. D) A 16-digit BO ID is a CDSL demat account number.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** KYC Registration Agencies are registered under:\nA) SEBI KRA Regulations, 2011  B) SEBI Intermediaries Regulations, 2008  C) The Depositories Act, 1996  D) FEMA, 1999\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011**.\nB) The Intermediaries Regulations cover registration of intermediaries generally. C) The Depositories Act covers depositories. D) FEMA covers foreign exchange.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The scope of a KRA, as against CKYC, is:\nA) All financial sectors  B) The securities market only  C) Banking only  D) Insurance only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A KRA serves **SEBI-registered intermediaries in the securities market**.\nA) Describes **CKYC**, which spans banking, securities, insurance and pensions. C) and D) are narrower than reality for both systems.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Dematerialisation of securities in India is enabled by:\nA) The Depositories Act, 1996  B) The Companies Act, 1956  C) SCRA, 1956  D) The Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Depositories Act, 1996**.\nB) The Companies Act deals with company law generally. C) SCRA governs securities contracts. D) The NI Act governs cheques and promissory notes.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** India's two depositories are:\nA) NSE and BSE  B) NSDL and CDSL  C) CAMS and KFin  D) CVL and NDML\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NSDL** and **CDSL**.\nA) NSE and BSE are stock exchanges. C) CAMS and KFin are registrars and transfer agents. D) CVL and NDML are KRAs.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** An ISIN consists of:\nA) 8 characters  B) 10 characters  C) 12 characters  D) 16 characters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The International Securities Identification Number has **12 characters** and is unique to each security.\nA) Eight characters is an NSDL DP ID. B) Ten characters is a PAN. D) Sixteen characters is a demat account number.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The form used to convert physical share certificates into electronic form is the:\nA) RRF  B) DRF  C) MAM form  D) Transposition Request Form\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Dematerialisation Request Form**, which generates a **DRN**.\nA) The RRF is for rematerialisation. C) The MAM form is used when a minor attains majority. D) Transposition changes the order of names.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Rematerialisation results in:\nA) The demat account being credited  B) The demat account being debited and physical certificates being issued  C) Units being transferred to a nominee  D) A lien being marked\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The holding is **debited** from the demat account and **fresh certificates with new distinctive numbers** are issued.\nA) Credit happens on dematerialisation. C) That is transmission. D) A lien is a charge for a loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** A Power of Attorney is governed by:\nA) The Powers of Attorney Act, 1882  B) The Indian Trusts Act, 1882  C) The Insurance Act, 1938  D) The Indian Majority Act, 1875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Powers of Attorney Act, 1882**.\nB) The Trusts Act governs trusts. C) The Insurance Act governs nomination and assignment. D) The Majority Act fixes the age of majority.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** A General Power of Attorney is one that:\nA) Authorises only a single named transaction  B) Confers broad authority over all or most of the donor's affairs  C) Can only be given to a stock broker  D) Expires automatically after one year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Its breadth is exactly what makes it risky, which is why financial services prefer a **specific or limited** PoA.\nA) Describes a specific PoA. C) A GPoA is typically given to a trusted relative. D) A PoA has no automatic one-year life.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Interest earned on an NRE account is:\nA) Taxable at slab rates  B) Exempt from Indian income tax  C) Taxed at 30% with TDS  D) Taxed only if repatriated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NRE interest is **exempt** from Indian income tax while the holder remains a person resident outside India under FEMA.\nA) and C) describe **NRO** interest, which is taxable with TDS. D) Repatriation does not create a tax charge.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Repatriation of the balance in an NRO account is capped at:\nA) USD 1,00,000 per financial year  B) USD 2,50,000 per financial year  C) USD 1 million per financial year  D) There is no cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **USD 1 million per financial year**, net of applicable taxes and supported by Form 15CA/15CB.\nA) Is not the prescribed figure. B) USD 2,50,000 is the **LRS** limit for resident individuals. D) NRE and FCNR are uncapped; NRO is not.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** An FCNR(B) deposit is denominated in:\nA) Indian rupees  B) A freely convertible foreign currency  C) Gold  D) Special Drawing Rights\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It is held in a **freely convertible foreign currency** such as USD, GBP, EUR, JPY, AUD or CAD, which is why it carries **no exchange-rate risk** for the depositor.\nA) NRE and NRO are rupee accounts. C) and D) are not deposit currencies for FCNR.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** The permitted tenure of an FCNR(B) deposit is:\nA) 7 days to 1 year  B) 1 year to 5 years  C) 5 years to 10 years  D) Any tenure the bank offers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **One to five years**, and FCNR is available **only as a term deposit** — never as a savings or current account.\nA) Is too short. C) Exceeds the maximum. D) The range is prescribed, not free.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Under the Liberalised Remittance Scheme, a resident individual may remit up to:\nA) USD 25,000 per financial year  B) USD 1,00,000 per financial year  C) USD 2,50,000 per financial year  D) USD 1 million per financial year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual for permitted current and capital account transactions.\nA) and B) were earlier limits. D) USD 1 million is the **NRO repatriation** ceiling, a different rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** In a mutual fund folio, a nominee is best described as:\nA) The owner of the units after the investor's death  B) A trustee who receives the units on behalf of the legal heirs  C) A joint holder  D) A guarantor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Nomination gives the fund a **valid discharge**; the nominee then holds for the **legal heirs**. Nomination does **not** override succession law.\nA) Is the classic misconception. C) A nominee has no rights while the investor is alive. D) Nomination has nothing to do with guaranteeing anything.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Assignment of a life insurance policy is governed by:\nA) Section 38 of the Insurance Act, 1938  B) Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938  C) Section 45 of the Insurance Act, 1938  D) Section 64VB of the Insurance Act, 1938\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Section 38** covers assignment and transfer.\nB) Section 39 covers **nomination**. C) Section 45 deals with when a policy may be called into question. D) Section 64VB deals with premium payment before risk assumption.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Nomination under a life insurance policy is governed by:\nA) Section 38  B) Section 39  C) Section 45  D) Section 64VB\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938**, which since 2015 also creates the \"beneficial nominee\".\nA) Section 38 is assignment. C) Section 45 concerns repudiation. D) Section 64VB concerns premium.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Transmission of securities is triggered by:\nA) A voluntary sale  B) A gift  C) The death of a holder  D) A change of address\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Transmission is an **operation of law on death**, passing the asset to survivors, the nominee or the legal heirs.\nA) and B) are **transfers** — voluntary acts between living persons. D) Is a routine folio update.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** In India the age of majority is normally:\nA) 16 years  B) 18 years  C) 21 years  D) 25 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **18 years** under the Indian Majority Act, 1875. Where a **court appoints a guardian**, majority is deferred to **21**.\nA) Is too low. C) Applies only in the court-appointed-guardian case. D) Has no legal basis here.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An investment made on behalf of a minor must be held:\nA) Jointly with the guardian  B) In the minor's sole name  C) In the guardian's name  D) In the name of either parent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The folio must be in the **minor's SOLE name**, operated by a guardian. A minor can never be a joint holder.\nA) Joint holding with a minor is not permitted. C) and D) would make the guardian the investor, defeating the purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A cheque is valid for presentation for:\nA) 1 month  B) 3 months  C) 6 months  D) 1 year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Three months** from the date of issue, reduced from six months with effect from **1 April 2012**.\nA) Is too short. C) Was the position before April 2012. D) Has never been the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** The minimum amount for an RTGS transfer is:\nA) There is no minimum  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — RTGS is designed for large values and has a **minimum of ₹2,00,000** with **no upper limit**.\nA) Describes NEFT, which has no minimum. B) and D) are not the prescribed floor.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** NACH, the system that powers SIP debits, is operated by:\nA) RBI  B) SEBI  C) NPCI  D) AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **NPCI** operates NACH, IMPS, UPI, AePS, BBPS and RuPay.\nA) RBI operates NEFT and RTGS. B) SEBI is the securities regulator. D) AMFI is the mutual fund industry body.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** The BSE platform for mutual fund transactions is called:\nA) NMF II  B) StAR MF  C) MF Utility  D) SCORES\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **BSE StAR MF** — the Stock exchange platform for Allotment and Repurchase of Mutual Funds.\nA) NMF II is the NSE platform. C) MF Utility is the AMCs' own shared platform. D) SCORES is SEBI's complaint redressal system.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The single account number issued by MF Utility is called the:\nA) KIN  B) CAN  C) PEKRN  D) BO ID\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Common Account Number (CAN)** works across participating AMCs.\nA) KIN is the CKYC number. C) PEKRN is the PAN-exempt KYC reference number. D) BO ID is a CDSL demat account number.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Mr Verma pays for his wife's mutual fund purchase from his own savings account. The AMC will:\nA) Accept it, since they are spouses  B) Reject it as a third-party payment  C) Accept it up to ₹50,000  D) Accept it with the distributor's declaration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Payment must come from the **first named investor's own bank account**. A spouse is **not** one of the recognised exceptions.\nA) Marriage creates no exception under PMLA. C) The ₹50,000 relaxation applies **only where the investor is a minor**. D) A distributor cannot authorise a third-party payment.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A grandparent may pay into a minor's folio out of natural love and affection up to:\nA) ₹10,000 per transaction  B) ₹25,000 per transaction  C) ₹50,000 per transaction  D) Any amount, without limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹50,000 per transaction** — that is, per lump-sum purchase or per SIP instalment — with a **Third Party Payment Declaration** signed by both the payer and the investor.\nA) and B) understate the permitted amount. D) The relaxation is expressly capped.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Which of the following third-party payments IS permitted?\nA) A company paying for its director's personal investment  B) An employer paying for an employee through payroll deduction  C) A friend paying on behalf of a friend  D) A distributor paying on behalf of a client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Employer-to-employee through payroll deduction** is one of the recognised exceptions, because the payroll linkage preserves the audit trail.\nA) A director's personal investment must come from the director's own account. C) Friendship is not an exception. D) A distributor paying for a client is a serious breach.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Redemption proceeds from a mutual fund may be paid to:\nA) Any account the investor nominates in writing  B) Only the registered bank account of the first or sole holder  C) The distributor's account, for onward payment  D) Any joint holder's separate account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — There is **no third-party exception on the payout side**. Proceeds always go to the **first/sole holder's registered account**.\nA) The account must already be registered on the folio. C) Distributors must never handle client money. D) The first holder is the payee, not any joint holder.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A folio is held by three people with the mode \"Anyone or Survivor\". How many must sign a nomination form?\nA) Any one  B) Any two  C) All three  D) The first holder only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Nomination always requires ALL joint holders' signatures**, regardless of the mode of holding.\nA), B) and D) confuse the rule for **transactions** — where any one may sign in an \"Anyone or Survivor\" folio — with the rule for **nomination**, which is stricter.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A client's Power of Attorney holder wishes to register a nomination in the client's folio. The AMC should:\nA) Accept it, since the PoA is registered  B) Accept it if the PoA is a General PoA  C) Reject it — a PoA holder cannot nominate  D) Accept it with an indemnity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Nomination is a **personal act of the investor**; a PoA holder can never make or change one.\nA) Registration of the PoA does not extend its reach to nomination. B) Even a General PoA cannot nominate. D) No indemnity cures the lack of authority.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An investor wants to add her son as a second holder to her existing mutual fund folio. The correct answer is:\nA) File a name-addition form with the AMC  B) It is not permitted — a new folio must be opened  C) It requires a succession certificate  D) It can be done only in demat form\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holders **cannot be added** to an existing folio or demat account; a **new folio/account** must be opened and fresh investments made there.\nA) No such form exists. C) A succession certificate relates to death, not addition. D) The rule is the same in demat form.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A holder's name can be deleted from a folio only when:\nA) The holder requests it in writing  B) All holders agree  C) The holder dies  D) The AMC approves it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Deletion occurs **only on death**, through **transmission** to the surviving holders.\nA) and B) A living holder cannot simply be removed. D) The AMC has no discretion to override the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Transposition means:\nA) Transferring units to a nominee  B) Changing the order of the same set of names  C) Converting units into demat form  D) Marking a lien\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Transposition changes only the **ORDER of the SAME names** and is **not** treated as a transfer.\nA) Is transmission. C) Is dematerialisation. D) Is a charge created for a loan.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A share certificate stands in the names \"Anil and Bina\". Their demat account is in the names \"Bina and Anil\". To dematerialise, they must first:\nA) Open a new demat account  B) File a transposition request along with the demat request  C) Sell and repurchase the shares  D) Obtain a succession certificate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Names **and their order** must match exactly, so a **transposition request** is filed together with the DRF.\nA) A new account is unnecessary and costly. C) Selling would trigger tax and cost for no reason. D) A succession certificate concerns death.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** On the day a minor investor attains 18, what happens to the folio?\nA) Nothing changes  B) It is frozen for transactions and all standing instructions cease  C) It is closed automatically  D) The guardian becomes the first holder\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The folio is **frozen for financial transactions** and every **SIP, SWP and STP ceases** on that date until the status change is completed.\nA) The change is automatic and immediate in law. C) The units remain intact — only transactions stop. D) The guardian's authority **ends**, it does not expand.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which document is specifically required when a minor attains majority?\nA) A succession certificate  B) The signature of the now-major attested by the banker  C) A notarised gift deed  D) A fresh Power of Attorney from the guardian\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **MAM form** requires the now-major's **banker-attested signature**, along with PAN, KYC, a bank account in their own name and a fresh nomination.\nA) Applies on death. C) Has no place here. D) The guardian's authority has ended and cannot be extended.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A resident who takes up employment abroad must do what with his existing resident savings account?\nA) Close it immediately  B) Continue operating it as before  C) Have it re-designated as an NRO account  D) Convert it into an NRE account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A resident account **cannot continue** once the holder becomes an NRI; it is **re-designated as NRO**.\nA) Closure is unnecessary — the balance and history are preserved. B) Continuing it is a FEMA contravention. D) NRE can only be credited with **foreign** earnings, so a rupee balance from Indian income cannot simply become NRE.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An NRI returning to India permanently may park his repatriable foreign-currency funds in:\nA) A PPF account  B) An RFC account  C) An NRE savings account  D) A CKYC account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Resident Foreign Currency (RFC)** account is designed exactly for returning Indians.\nA) NRIs cannot open new PPF accounts, and PPF is a rupee scheme. C) NRE accounts must be re-designated on return. D) CKYC is a KYC registry, not a bank account.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A resident with an existing PPF account becomes an NRI. The account:\nA) Must be closed immediately  B) May be continued to maturity but cannot be extended  C) Can be extended indefinitely  D) Is converted into an NRE deposit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The existing account **runs to maturity** but **cannot be extended** beyond it, and no new account can be opened.\nA) Premature closure is not required. C) Extension is exactly what is barred. D) PPF cannot be converted into a bank deposit.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An NRI trading Indian equities in the secondary market may:\nA) Trade intraday freely  B) Take delivery-based positions only  C) Engage in short selling  D) Trade without any designated bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — NRIs are **delivery-based only** — no intraday squaring off and no short selling.\nA) and C) are expressly barred. D) Secondary-market purchases must route through **one designated AD bank branch** under PIS.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Under the Portfolio Investment Scheme, an individual NRI may hold up to what percentage of a company's paid-up capital?\nA) 2%  B) 5%  C) 10%  D) 24%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **5%** per individual NRI.\nA) Is not the prescribed figure. C) 10% is the **aggregate** limit for all NRIs together. D) 24% is the raised aggregate ceiling after a special resolution.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** The aggregate NRI holding limit in an Indian company under PIS is 10%, which can be raised to 24% by:\nA) An RBI order  B) A board resolution followed by a special resolution of shareholders  C) A SEBI circular  D) The company's auditors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **board resolution plus a shareholders' special resolution** is required.\nA) RBI monitors but does not raise the ceiling for a particular company. C) SEBI does not set FEMA sectoral caps. D) Auditors have no such power.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A KYC-compliant investor with folios in six mutual funds changes her address. She should:\nA) Write separately to all six AMCs  B) File the change with the KRA/CKYC, from where it flows to all intermediaries  C) Open new folios  D) Only inform her bank\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a KYC-compliant investor the change goes through the **KRA/CKYC** and propagates everywhere. Only a non-KYC-compliant investor updates folio by folio.\nA) Wasteful and unnecessary. C) Address changes never require new folios. D) The bank record is separate from the KYC record.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** After marriage, a client wants her new surname reflected everywhere. The correct sequence is:\nA) Folios first, then PAN  B) PAN first, then KRA/CKYC, then folios and demat  C) Demat first, then PAN  D) All simultaneously, in any order\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **PAN → KRA/CKYC → folios and demat.** Any other order produces a name mismatch and rejected requests.\nA) and C) invert the dependency — every downstream record is validated against PAN. D) Simultaneous filing guarantees mismatches during the transition.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Once a lien is marked on mutual fund units, the investor:\nA) Loses ownership of the units  B) Cannot redeem, switch or transfer them without the lienholder's release  C) Can still redeem them freely  D) Must convert them to demat\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The units are **frozen** until the lender issues a release; the investor nonetheless **remains the owner**.\nA) A lien is a charge, not a transfer of ownership. C) Free redemption would defeat the security. D) Conversion to demat is unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** The demat-account equivalent of marking a lien on units held in statement-of-account form is:\nA) A freeze by SEBI  B) A pledge created through the depository  C) Rematerialisation  D) Transposition\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — In demat form the charge is a **pledge** between **pledgor** and **pledgee**, recorded by the depository.\nA) SEBI freezes are regulatory actions, not lending security. C) Rematerialisation converts holdings into certificates. D) Transposition reorders names.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** The effect of an ABSOLUTE assignment of a life policy on an existing nomination is:\nA) The nomination continues unchanged  B) The nomination stands cancelled  C) The nominee becomes the assignee  D) The insurer decides\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An absolute assignment transfers **all rights permanently**, so the existing **nomination is automatically cancelled**.\nA) Would be inconsistent with a complete transfer of ownership. C) There is no such automatic substitution. D) The consequence is a matter of law, not insurer discretion.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A policyholder assigns his policy to a bank as security for a loan. This is:\nA) An absolute assignment  B) A conditional assignment  C) A nomination  D) A transmission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Rights transfer **subject to the condition** and **revert to the assignor** on repayment. Nomination is not cancelled where the assignment secures a loan on the policy.\nA) An absolute assignment is permanent and unconditional. C) Nomination transfers no ownership. D) Transmission arises on death.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A policy taken under the Married Women's Property Act, 1874:\nA) Can be assigned freely  B) Cannot be assigned  C) Can be assigned only to a bank  D) Requires IRDAI approval to be assigned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An MWP policy **cannot be assigned**; the Act creates a trust for the wife and children, placing the proceeds beyond the policyholder's creditors.\nA) Free assignment would defeat the statutory trust. C) Even a bank cannot take it as security. D) IRDAI cannot approve what the statute forbids.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A sole holder dies leaving mutual fund units and no nomination. In addition to the death certificate and the claimant's KYC, the AMC will normally need:\nA) Nothing further  B) A succession certificate, probate or letter of administration  C) A Power of Attorney  D) A transposition request\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Legal evidence of entitlement — a **succession certificate, probate of the will or letter of administration** — unless the claim falls within AMFI's simplified small-claim route.\nA) The fund needs a valid discharge. C) A PoA dies with the donor. D) Transposition concerns the order of names.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Under Section 39 of the Insurance Act, a \"beneficial nominee\" is a nominee who is the policyholder's:\nA) Employer  B) Parent, spouse or child  C) Business partner  D) Creditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where the nominee is a **parent, spouse or child**, the 2015 amendment makes them **beneficially entitled** to the claim — the one place where a nominee is more than a trustee.\nA), C) and D) are not immediate family and receive the money only as trustees for the legal heirs.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Where an OVD does not carry the client's current address, a utility bill may be accepted as a deemed OVD if it is not older than:\nA) 1 month  B) 2 months  C) 6 months  D) 1 year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Two months**, and the client must produce an OVD showing the updated address **within three months**.\nA) Is stricter than the rule. C) and D) are far too lenient for an address that must be current.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An investor completes Aadhaar OTP-based e-KYC. Her investment in that mutual fund in a financial year is capped at:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹2,00,000  D) There is no cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — OTP-based e-KYC carries a **₹50,000 per financial year per mutual fund** ceiling.\nA) Is not the prescribed figure. C) Is not the ceiling for OTP e-KYC. D) The no-cap position applies to **biometric** Aadhaar e-KYC or full KYC.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** An intermediary files a Suspicious Transaction Report about a client. Telling the client about it is:\nA) Good practice  B) Required by SEBI  C) An offence known as tipping off  D) Optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Tipping off** is an offence under PMLA; STRs go confidentially to **FIU-IND**.\nA) It would destroy the investigation. B) SEBI requires the opposite — confidentiality. D) There is no discretion.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A mutual fund may accept cash subscriptions of up to:\nA) ₹10,000 per investor per financial year  B) ₹50,000 per investor per financial year  C) ₹2,00,000 per investor per financial year  D) Cash is never accepted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹50,000 per investor per financial year**, from specified small investors — and **all repayments must go through the banking channel**, never in cash.\nA) Understates the limit. C) Overstates it. D) A limited cash facility does exist, though it is rarely used.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** RBI's Positive Pay System applies to cheques above:\nA) ₹10,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹5,00,000  D) ₹10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Positive Pay, effective **1 January 2021**, applies to cheques **above ₹50,000**; banks may make it compulsory for higher values.\nA) Is below the threshold. C) and D) are values at which banks may make it mandatory, but not the threshold at which the facility applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Under RBI's limited-liability framework, a customer bears ZERO liability for an unauthorised electronic transaction caused by a third-party breach if it is reported within:\nA) 24 hours  B) 3 working days  C) 7 working days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reporting within **three working days** gives **zero liability**.\nA) The window is more generous than a single day. C) Four to seven working days brings a **capped** liability. D) Beyond seven days, the bank's board policy applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser must preserve client records for a minimum of:\nA) 1 year  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Five years**, and **until conclusion** where a dispute or proceeding is pending.\nA) and B) are shorter than the requirement. D) Ten years is not the prescribed minimum, though longer retention is not prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** A client offers to hand an Investment Adviser ₹80,000 in cash to invest on his behalf. The adviser must:\nA) Accept and deposit it in the client's folio  B) Refuse — an IA may not accept cash or take custody of client funds  C) Accept up to ₹50,000  D) Accept it against a receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An IA must **never accept cash and never take custody of client funds or securities**; the client must pay the fund directly from his own bank account.\nA) Handling client money is precisely what is prohibited. C) The ₹50,000 cash relaxation belongs to the mutual fund, not to an adviser. D) A receipt does not cure the breach.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** Mr Sethi has no PAN and starts a micro-SIP of ₹4,000 per month in one mutual fund from April. Will the twelve instalments stay within the PAN exemption?\nA) No — ₹48,000 exceeds ₹50,000  B) Yes — ₹48,000 is within the ₹50,000 annual ceiling  C) No — micro-SIPs are not exempt from PAN  D) Yes — the ceiling is per instalment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹4,000 × 12 = ₹48,000**, which is below the **₹50,000 per investor per financial year** aggregate ceiling for one mutual fund. He can invest under a **PEKRN**.\nA) ₹48,000 is less than ₹50,000, not more. C) Micro-SIPs are exactly the case the exemption was created for. D) The ceiling is **annual and aggregated**, not per instalment — an important distinction.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** The same investor raises the SIP to ₹4,500 per month from the start of the next financial year. What is the position?\nA) Still exempt — ₹54,000 is within the limit  B) The annual total of ₹54,000 breaches the ₹50,000 ceiling, so PAN becomes necessary  C) Exempt, because the increase is small  D) Exempt, because he already holds a PEKRN\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **₹4,500 × 12 = ₹54,000**, which is **₹4,000 above the ₹50,000 aggregate ceiling**. Once the ceiling is crossed, the PAN exemption is lost and PAN with full KYC is required.\nA) ₹54,000 exceeds ₹50,000. C) The size of the increase is irrelevant — the aggregate is what counts. D) A PEKRN is valid only while the investor stays within the ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Mrs Kulkarni wishes to gift ₹60,000 in a single payment from her own account into her grandson's minor folio. The AMC will:\nA) Accept the full ₹60,000 with a declaration  B) Reject it — the third-party relaxation for a minor is capped at ₹50,000 per transaction  C) Accept it as it is a gift  D) Accept ₹50,000 and refund ₹10,000 automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The grandparent exception is capped at **₹50,000 per transaction**. A single ₹60,000 payment **exceeds** it and cannot be accepted as a third-party payment.\nA) The declaration does not raise the cap. C) The gift motive is what brings the relaxation into play, but the ceiling still binds. D) AMCs reject the application; they do not split and refund it.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A listed company has a paid-up capital of 20 crore equity shares. The maximum number of shares a single NRI may hold under PIS is:\nA) 40 lakh shares  B) 1 crore shares  C) 2 crore shares  D) 4.8 crore shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **5% × 20 crore = 1 crore shares**.\nA) 40 lakh would be 2%. C) 2 crore is **10%**, the aggregate ceiling for **all** NRIs together. D) 4.8 crore is **24%**, the raised aggregate ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** For the same company, the aggregate NRI ceiling is raised to 24% by special resolution. How many additional shares can NRIs collectively hold compared with the default limit?\nA) 1 crore shares  B) 2 crore shares  C) 2.8 crore shares  D) 4.8 crore shares\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Default aggregate = 10% × 20 crore = **2 crore**. Raised ceiling = 24% × 20 crore = **4.8 crore**. The **increase = 4.8 − 2.0 = 2.8 crore shares**.\nA) 1 crore is a single NRI's 5% limit. B) 2 crore is the default aggregate, not the increase. D) 4.8 crore is the new total, not the additional amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A resident individual remits the full LRS limit of USD 2,50,000 when the rupee is at ₹84 to the dollar. The rupee outflow is:\nA) ₹1,00,00,000  B) ₹2,10,00,000  C) ₹2,50,00,000  D) ₹8,40,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **USD 2,50,000 × ₹84 = ₹2,10,00,000**, i.e., ₹2.10 crore.\nA) Would imply an exchange rate of ₹40. C) Confuses the dollar figure with a rupee figure. D) ₹8.4 crore corresponds to **USD 1 million** — the **NRO repatriation** limit, a different rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An NRI has ₹2.10 crore in his NRO account and wants to repatriate the whole amount in one financial year, with the rupee at ₹84. Is this permitted?\nA) No — NRO balances are never repatriable  B) Yes — ₹2.10 crore is about USD 2,50,000, well within the USD 1 million annual ceiling  C) No — the ceiling is USD 2,50,000  D) Yes, but only from an NRE account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹2,10,00,000 ÷ 84 = **USD 2,50,000**, comfortably inside the **USD 1 million per financial year** NRO ceiling, subject to taxes and Form 15CA/15CB.\nA) NRO balances are repatriable within the ceiling. C) USD 2,50,000 is the **LRS** limit for **residents**, not the NRO ceiling. D) The funds are already in NRO; they need not first move to NRE.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A cheque is dated 12 January 2026. The last date on which a bank will normally pay it is on or about:\nA) 12 February 2026  B) 12 April 2026  C) 12 July 2026  D) 12 January 2027\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A cheque is valid for **three months** from its date: 12 January + 3 months = **12 April 2026**. After that it is stale.\nA) One month is not the rule. C) Six months was the position **before 1 April 2012**. D) One year has never been the rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client needs to move ₹1,50,000 electronically today. Which is available to her?\nA) RTGS only  B) NEFT, IMPS or UPI — but not RTGS, whose minimum is ₹2,00,000  C) Neither NEFT nor RTGS  D) Only a demand draft\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **RTGS has a floor of ₹2,00,000**, so ₹1,50,000 is below it. NEFT has **no minimum**, and IMPS and UPI comfortably handle this value.\nA) The amount is below the RTGS minimum. C) NEFT has no minimum at all. D) A physical instrument is unnecessary.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Ms Nair completed only Aadhaar OTP-based e-KYC and now wants to invest ₹1,20,000 in a single mutual fund this financial year. She:\nA) Can invest freely  B) Must complete full KYC or biometric e-KYC, since OTP e-KYC caps her at ₹50,000 per financial year per fund  C) Can invest ₹1,20,000 across two folios in the same fund  D) Can invest if she signs an indemnity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — OTP-based e-KYC limits her to **₹50,000 per financial year per mutual fund**; ₹1,20,000 requires **full KYC or biometric Aadhaar e-KYC**.\nA) The cap binds. C) The cap applies **per mutual fund**, aggregating all folios — splitting achieves nothing. D) An indemnity cannot override a KYC-based limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A sole holder dies leaving one folio worth ₹4,20,000 with no nomination registered. The likely documentation route is:\nA) A succession certificate is unavoidable  B) AMFI's simplified route — indemnity bond, affidavits from all legal heirs and NOCs from non-claimants — since the value is within the small-claim threshold  C) A transposition request  D) A Power of Attorney from the heirs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹4,20,000 is **below the ₹5,00,000 per-folio threshold** for AMFI's simplified transmission route, so an **indemnity bond, affidavits and NOCs** can substitute for legal probate documents.\nA) The simplified route exists precisely to avoid this for smaller claims. C) Transposition concerns the order of names. D) A PoA lapses on the donor's death.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** The same investor's second folio is worth ₹18,00,000 with no nomination. The position is:\nA) The same simplified route applies  B) Legal evidence — succession certificate, probate or letter of administration — will be required, as the value exceeds the small-claim threshold  C) The units lapse to the AMC  D) The units go to the state\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹18,00,000 is **far above the ₹5,00,000 threshold**, so the AMC will insist on **legal evidence of entitlement**.\nA) The simplified route is confined to small claims. C) An AMC never takes an investor's units. D) Escheat to the state arises only where there is genuinely no heir at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A minor's folio has a running SIP of ₹5,000 per month, debited on the 10th. The minor turns 18 on 3 September. What happens to the 10 September instalment?\nA) It is processed normally  B) It will not be processed — all standing instructions cease on the date of attaining majority  C) It is processed at half value  D) It is processed but the units are frozen\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Every **SIP, SWP and STP ceases on the date the minor attains majority**, and the folio is frozen for transactions until the MAM formalities are completed. The 10 September debit falls after 3 September, so it will not go through.\nA) The guardian's mandate has already lapsed. C) There is no partial processing. D) The instalment is not processed at all, so no units arise to freeze.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** ₹2,40,000 is fraudulently debited from Mr Bose's savings account after his bank's systems are breached. He reports it on the second working day. His liability is:\nA) ₹2,40,000  B) ₹25,000  C) ₹10,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A third-party system breach reported within **three working days** attracts **ZERO customer liability** under RBI's framework; the bank must restore the full **₹2,40,000**.\nA) Would apply only if the loss flowed from his own negligence. B) and C) are the **capped** amounts that apply when reporting takes **four to seven working days**, not within three.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Had Mr Bose reported the same ₹2,40,000 fraud on the sixth working day, his position would be:\nA) Unchanged at nil  B) Liability capped at a prescribed amount by account type, with the bank bearing the rest  C) Full liability of ₹2,40,000  D) Half the loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reporting in **four to seven working days** moves him from zero liability to a **capped** liability fixed by account type (of the order of ₹5,000, ₹10,000 or ₹25,000), with the bank absorbing the balance.\nA) The zero-liability window closes after three working days. C) Full liability arises from the customer's **own negligence**, not from a delayed report of a bank-side breach. D) There is no 50:50 sharing rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Mr Bose's neighbour lost ₹90,000 after sharing an OTP with a caller pretending to be from the bank. His liability is:\nA) Nil  B) ₹10,000  C) The entire ₹90,000 up to the time he reports it  D) ₹25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Where the loss arises from the **customer's own negligence**, such as sharing an OTP, the customer bears the **entire loss** until the transaction is reported. Losses after reporting are the bank's.\nA) and B) apply to bank-side or third-party breaches, not customer negligence. D) ₹25,000 is a cap for certain account types in the delayed-reporting case, not here.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A folio worth ₹36,00,000 has three registered nominees with no percentages specified. On the sole holder's death, each nominee receives:\nA) ₹36,00,000 to the first nominee only  B) ₹12,00,000 each  C) ₹18,00,000, ₹12,00,000 and ₹6,00,000  D) Nothing until a succession certificate is produced\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where percentages are not stated, the allocation is **EQUAL**: ₹36,00,000 ÷ 3 = **₹12,00,000 each**.\nA) There is no priority among nominees. C) Assumes a 50:33:17 split that was never specified. D) A valid nomination removes the need for a succession certificate — that is its whole purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Another folio worth ₹45,00,000 names three nominees in the ratio 50%, 30% and 20%. The amounts payable are:\nA) ₹15,00,000 each  B) ₹22,50,000, ₹13,50,000 and ₹9,00,000  C) ₹25,00,000, ₹15,00,000 and ₹5,00,000  D) ₹22,50,000 to the first nominee only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 50% × ₹45,00,000 = **₹22,50,000**; 30% × ₹45,00,000 = **₹13,50,000**; 20% × ₹45,00,000 = **₹9,00,000**. The three sum back to ₹45,00,000.\nA) Equal division applies only when percentages are **not** specified. C) Uses a 55:33:11 split that does not match the stated ratio. D) The other two nominees are equally entitled to their shares.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Ms D'Souza holds a single demat account as sole holder with a portfolio value of ₹3,20,000 and no other demat account. She should be offered:\nA) A regular demat account only  B) A Basic Services Demat Account, whose lower slabs attract nil or concessional annual maintenance charges  C) A PIS account  D) An NRO demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — She meets the **BSDA** conditions — **only one** demat account as sole/first holder, with holdings **within SEBI's ceiling** — so the lower-slab **nil or concessional AMC** applies, and eligible investors are to be offered BSDA by default.\nA) Denying her BSDA would be contrary to SEBI's requirement. C) PIS applies to NRIs. D) NRO demat accounts are for non-residents.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** A folio is held by a mother and daughter with the mode of holding left blank. The mother alone signs a redemption request. The RTA will:\nA) Process it, since she is the first holder  B) Reject it — the blank field makes the folio \"Joint\", so both signatures are needed  C) Process half the redemption  D) Process it after a cooling-off period\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A blank mode of holding with more than one holder defaults to **Joint**, and a joint folio requires **every holder's signature** on every instruction.\nA) Being the first holder confers tax ownership, not sole signing authority. C) There is no proportional processing in a joint folio. D) A cooling-off period does not cure a missing signature.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An NRI earns ₹9,60,000 a year in rent from a Mumbai flat. This income must be credited to:\nA) An NRE account  B) An NRO account  C) An FCNR deposit  D) A resident savings account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Indian-source income** such as rent, dividend and pension goes into the **NRO** account, where it is taxable and repatriable only within the USD 1 million annual ceiling.\nA) NRE takes only **foreign** earnings remitted from abroad. C) FCNR takes only foreign-currency remittances as term deposits. D) A resident account cannot be operated by an NRI.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An NRI remits USD 60,000 when the rupee is at ₹83 and places it in an NRE deposit at 7%. A year later the rupee is at ₹90 and he repatriates the maturity value. He receives approximately:\nA) USD 64,200  B) USD 60,000  C) USD 59,207  D) USD 55,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rupee deposit = 60,000 × 83 = **₹49,80,000**. Interest at 7% = **₹3,48,600**. Maturity = **₹53,28,600**. Converted at ₹90: 53,28,600 ÷ 90 = **USD 59,206.67**, i.e., about **USD 59,207** — **less than he started with**, because the rupee fell by more than the interest earned.\nA) Would require the rupee to have been stable. B) Ignores both the interest and the currency move. D) Overstates the loss; the calculation gives about USD 59,207.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Had the same NRI placed the USD 60,000 in an FCNR(B) deposit at 4.5% instead, he would have received:\nA) USD 59,207  B) USD 62,700  C) USD 60,000  D) USD 64,200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FCNR is held **in dollars**, so the rupee's fall is irrelevant: **USD 60,000 × 1.045 = USD 62,700**. He is about **USD 3,493 better off** than the NRE route, despite the lower headline rate — the lesson that **currency risk can outweigh an interest-rate difference**.\nA) Is the NRE outcome. C) Ignores the interest. D) Assumes a rate of about 7% in dollars, which was not offered.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An employer deducts ₹10,000 a month from an employee's salary and remits it to a mutual fund SIP in the employee's name. This is:\nA) A prohibited third-party payment  B) A permitted exception, since it is an employer paying for an employee through payroll deduction  C) Permitted only up to ₹50,000  D) Permitted only for minors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Employer-to-employee payment through **payroll deduction** is one of the recognised exceptions, because the payroll linkage preserves the audit trail. A declaration is still required.\nA) It is expressly carved out of the prohibition. C) The ₹50,000 cap belongs to the **minor** exception, not this one. D) Minors are a separate exception entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A custodian remits subscription money to an Indian mutual fund on behalf of a registered FPI client. The AMC should:\nA) Reject it as a third-party payment  B) Accept it — a custodian paying for an FPI or client is a recognised exception  C) Accept it only up to ₹50,000  D) Accept it only if the FPI is Indian-owned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Custodian on behalf of an FPI or client** is one of the recognised third-party exceptions and is the normal institutional route.\nA) It is expressly permitted. C) The ₹50,000 cap applies to the minor exception. D) Ownership of the FPI is irrelevant to this rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client files a change-of-bank-mandate request on 1 June and a redemption request on 4 June. The proceeds will normally be credited to:\nA) The newly registered account  B) The old, already-registered bank account, because the request falls inside the cooling-off window  C) Any account the client names  D) Neither — the redemption is rejected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where a redemption is received **inside the cooling-off window (commonly 10 calendar days)** of a bank-change request, proceeds go to the **OLD registered account**. It is a deliberate anti-fraud control against the change-and-redeem scam.\nA) The new account is not yet trusted for payouts. C) Proceeds only ever go to a registered account of the first holder. D) The redemption itself is processed normally — only the destination account is controlled.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** An investor holds four folios in the same AMC. Two are \"Ravi and Sunita\", one is \"Sunita and Ravi\", and one is \"Ravi\" alone. Which can be consolidated with each other?\nA) All four  B) Only the two \"Ravi and Sunita\" folios  C) The three folios containing Ravi's name  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Consolidation requires **identical holders, identical ORDER of names, identical mode of holding and identical PAN/KYC**. Only the two \"Ravi and Sunita\" folios match.\nA) \"Sunita and Ravi\" has a different order and \"Ravi\" alone has different holders. C) The order of names and the set of holders both differ. D) Two of them do match perfectly.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An Investment Adviser is asked in year four to justify a recommendation made in year one. Her strongest defence is:\nA) Her recollection of the conversation  B) The client's signed risk profile, the suitability note, the recorded rationale for the advice and the client's acknowledgement  C) The fund's subsequent performance  D) An email from the AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The regulations require exactly this file — **KYC, risk profiling with the client's acceptance, suitability, the advice AND its rationale** — kept for at least **five years**, and until any dispute concludes.\nA) Oral recollection carries no evidentiary weight. C) Performance is not the test; **suitability at the time of advice** is. D) An AMC email says nothing about this client's profile.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An IA wants to place mutual fund orders for 40 clients across eleven AMCs and reconcile the holdings daily. The most efficient route is:\nA) Separate physical forms and cheques to each AMC  B) A stock exchange platform such as BSE StAR MF or NSE NMF II, combined with daily transaction feeds from the RTAs and depositories  C) Asking clients to email screenshots  D) Manual data entry from each account statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The exchange platforms give **one window across every AMC**, with direct plans, time-stamped orders and exchange settlement, while **transaction feeds** deliver daily machine-readable transaction and holding files for **automatic reconciliation and consolidated reporting**.\nA) Eleven sets of forms and cheques is exactly what the platforms were built to eliminate. C) Screenshots are neither complete nor auditable. D) Manual entry introduces the errors feeds are designed to remove.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises the operational discipline of this chapter?\nA) Paperwork is a formality that can be completed later  B) Every rule here answers one of three questions — prove who the investor is, prove the money is the investor's own, and make sure the right person gets it back — so identity, source of funds and succession must each be documented before, not after, the transaction  C) Nomination settles inheritance conclusively  D) Third-party payments are acceptable within the family\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Identity (**PAN, KYC, CKYC/KRA**), source of funds (**the anti-third-party rules**) and succession (**nomination, transmission, assignment**) are the three axes on which every rule in the chapter turns.\nA) Incomplete documentation invalidates transactions and destroys an adviser's defence. C) A nominee is a **trustee**, not an owner — except a beneficial nominee under Section 39 of the Insurance Act. D) The only family exception is for a **minor**, capped at **₹50,000 per transaction**.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** The case moves through four different operational situations in one family — read each fact carefully before deciding which rule applies.\n\n### Case: The Rajagopalan Family, Chennai\n\n**Mr S. Rajagopalan**, aged 62 and a retired bank manager, is an SEBI-registered Investment Adviser's long-standing client. Four things happen in his family during the same financial year:\n\n| # | Event | Figures |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | His son **Karthik** takes up a permanent job in Singapore and becomes an NRI | Resident savings balance **₹6,80,000** |\n| 2 | Karthik remits **USD 60,000** to India; the rupee is at **₹83** when he remits and **₹90** a year later. An NRE deposit pays **7%**; an FCNR(B) deposit pays **4.5%** | — |\n| 3 | Mr Rajagopalan wants to gift money into the folio of his granddaughter **Aditi**, a minor, from his own account | Intended gift **₹75,000** in one payment |\n| 4 | His unmarried sister dies leaving one mutual fund folio in her sole name with **no nomination** registered | Folio value **₹4,60,000** |\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** What must Karthik do with his resident savings account holding ₹6,80,000?\nA) Close it and remit the balance abroad  B) Have it re-designated as an NRO account  C) Convert it directly into an NRE account  D) Continue operating it as a resident account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On becoming a person resident outside India under FEMA, a **resident savings account must be re-designated as an NRO account**. The ₹6,80,000 of Indian-source money sits there, is taxable, and is repatriable only within the **USD 1 million per financial year** ceiling.\nA) Closure is unnecessary and would lose the account history. C) An **NRE** account may be credited only with **foreign earnings remitted from abroad**, so a rupee balance built up in India cannot simply become NRE. D) Continuing to run a resident account after becoming an NRI is a **FEMA contravention**.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Karthik puts the USD 60,000 into an NRE deposit at 7%. What does he get back in dollars after one year at ₹90?\nA) About USD 64,200  B) About USD 62,700  C) About USD 59,207  D) Exactly USD 60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Rupee deposit = 60,000 × 83 = **₹49,80,000**. Interest at 7% = 49,80,000 × 0.07 = **₹3,48,600**. Maturity value = **₹53,28,600**. Repatriated at ₹90: 53,28,600 ÷ 90 = **USD 59,206.67**, i.e., about **USD 59,207**. He has **lost about USD 793** in dollar terms despite earning 7% — because the rupee fell about 8.4%.\nA) Assumes no currency movement at all. B) Is the FCNR outcome. D) Ignores both the interest and the exchange-rate move, which happen to be of similar size but in opposite directions.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Had he chosen the FCNR(B) deposit at 4.5% instead, what would he have received, and what is the lesson?\nA) USD 59,207 — the two routes are identical  B) USD 62,700 — about USD 3,493 more, because FCNR carries no currency risk  C) USD 60,000 — FCNR pays no interest  D) USD 64,200 — FCNR always pays more\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FCNR is held **in dollars**, so the rupee's fall is irrelevant: **USD 60,000 × 1.045 = USD 62,700**, which is **62,700 − 59,207 = about USD 3,493 more** than the NRE route.\n\n**The lesson:** the NRE deposit paid a **higher nominal rate (7% versus 4.5%)** yet delivered a **worse dollar outcome**, because the **rupee depreciated by more than the 2.5 percentage-point rate advantage**. For an NRI whose liabilities are in a foreign currency, **the currency of the deposit matters more than the headline rate**. Had the rupee been stable, NRE would have won comfortably.\nA) The outcomes differ by about USD 3,493. C) FCNR deposits certainly pay interest. D) FCNR does **not** always win — it wins when the rupee falls by more than the rate differential.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Can Mr Rajagopalan gift ₹75,000 in one payment from his own account into his minor granddaughter's folio?\nA) Yes — grandparents may pay any amount for a minor  B) No — the third-party relaxation for a minor is capped at ₹50,000 per transaction  C) Yes, if he signs an indemnity  D) No — grandparents are never permitted to pay\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The relaxation for a **parent, grandparent or related person paying for a MINOR** out of natural love and affection is capped at **₹50,000 per transaction**. A single ₹75,000 payment breaches it and will be rejected. The practical answer is to gift the money into **Aditi's own bank account or the guardian's account** first and invest from there, with a **Third Party Payment Declaration** where the relaxation is used.\nA) The exception exists but is capped. C) An indemnity cannot raise a PMLA-based ceiling. D) Grandparents **are** an allowed payer for a minor, within the cap.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** His sister's ₹4,60,000 folio has no nomination. What documentation route applies, and what would have changed at ₹6,00,000?\nA) A succession certificate in both cases  B) The simplified route at ₹4,60,000 since it is below the ₹5 lakh threshold; legal evidence such as probate or a succession certificate would be needed at ₹6,00,000  C) The simplified route in both cases  D) The units lapse to the AMC in both cases\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹4,60,000 falls **below AMFI's ₹5,00,000 per-folio small-claim threshold**, so the claimant can use the **simplified route — an indemnity bond, affidavits from all the legal heirs and NOCs from the non-claimant heirs** — together with the **death certificate**, the claimant's **KYC** and **bank proof**. At **₹6,00,000** the folio would cross the threshold and the AMC would insist on **legal evidence of entitlement — a succession certificate, probate of the will or a letter of administration**.\n\n**The adviser's lesson:** the entire difficulty was avoidable. A **registered nomination** would have let the fund pay the nominee against a death certificate and KYC alone — no affidavits, no NOCs, no court. That is why an adviser should audit **every** client folio and demat account for a live nomination, and should remind clients that a **nominee is a trustee for the legal heirs, not the owner** — so nomination complements a will, it does not replace one.\n\nA) The simplified route exists precisely to avoid this for smaller claims. C) ₹6,00,000 exceeds the threshold. D) An AMC never appropriates an investor's units; they belong to the estate.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "18", "title": "Key Regulations", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "# Chapter 18: Key Regulations — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 6 · Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance and Ethics (30 marks).**\n> The single largest fact-density chapter in the syllabus. Almost every mark here is a **number, a name or a deadline**. The **SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013** alone are worth more marks than most whole chapters — learn them line by line.\n\n---\n\n## 18.0 The architecture — who regulates what\n\nBefore the detail, fix the map in your head. Indian financial regulation is **sector-based**, not product-based.\n\n| Regulator | Governs | Statute |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SEBI** | Securities market, exchanges, intermediaries, **investment advisers** | SEBI Act **1992**, SCRA **1956** |\n| **RBI** | Banks, NBFCs, payment systems, **foreign exchange** | RBI Act 1934, FEMA **1999** |\n| **IRDAI** | Insurance | Insurance Act 1938 |\n| **PFRDA** | Pension (NPS) | PFRDA Act 2013 |\n| **IFSCA** | All financial services inside an **IFSC** (GIFT City) | IFSCA Act **2019** |\n| **FIU-IND** | Anti-money-laundering reporting | PMLA **2002** |\n| **IBBI** | Insolvency and bankruptcy | IBC **2016** |\n\n> 🧠 **The two-layer idea.** Every Indian securities law works in two layers: a **parent Act** passed by Parliament (SCRA, SEBI Act, PMLA, FEMA) and **regulations** framed under it by the regulator (PFUTP, PIT, IA Regulations). The Act gives the *power*; the regulations give the *detail*. Penalties almost always live in the **Act**, obligations almost always live in the **regulations**.\n\n---\n\n## 18.1 Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956 — SCRA\n\n**Purpose (from the preamble):** *to prevent undesirable transactions in securities by regulating the business of dealing therein and by providing for certain other matters connected therewith.*\n\nSCRA is the **oldest** of the securities statutes and does three big things: it **defines what a \"security\" is**, it **recognises and regulates stock exchanges**, and it **makes listing compulsory and enforceable**.\n\n### The definition of \"securities\" — Section 2(h)\n\nSecurities **include**:\n\n- **Shares, scrips, stocks, bonds, debentures**, debenture stock or other marketable securities of a like nature in or of any incorporated company or other body corporate\n- **Derivatives**\n- **Units or any other instrument issued by any collective investment scheme**\n- **Security receipts** as defined under the SARFAESI Act\n- **Units or any other such instrument issued to investors under any mutual fund scheme**\n- **Government securities**\n- **Rights or interest** in securities\n- Such other instruments as the **Central Government** may declare to be securities\n\nSecurities **do not include** a **unit-linked insurance policy (ULIP)** or any similar instrument that combines life risk cover with investment and is issued by an insurer. That exclusion was inserted after the 2010 SEBI–IRDA turf dispute over ULIPs.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** a **fixed deposit**, a **bank savings account**, **gold**, **real estate**, an **insurance policy** and a **ULIP** are **not securities** under SCRA. A **mutual fund unit** and a **government security** are. An adviser recommending only insurance is therefore outside SEBI's securities perimeter — which is exactly why insurance agents get an exemption under the IA Regulations (18.6.3).\n\n### Other key SCRA concepts\n\n| Section / concept | What it says |\n|---|---|\n| **Spot delivery contract** — s.2(i) | Actual delivery of securities **and** payment on the **same day or the next day** |\n| **Recognition of stock exchanges** — s.4 | An exchange operates only with **recognition** (granted today by **SEBI**) |\n| **Corporatisation and demutualisation** — s.4A/4B | Exchanges converted from member-owned associations into **companies with ownership separated from trading rights** |\n| **Listing** — s.21 | Securities may be listed only on a recognised exchange, on the prescribed conditions |\n| **Delisting** — s.21A | An exchange may delist for breach of the listing agreement; the company may appeal to **SAT** |\n| **Derivatives** — s.18A | A derivative contract is **legal and valid only if** traded on a **recognised stock exchange** and **settled on the clearing house** |\n| **Offences** — s.23 | Imprisonment up to **10 years** or fine up to **₹25 crore**, or both |\n| **Rule-making** — s.30 | Central Government makes the **Securities Contracts (Regulation) Rules, 1957 (SCRR)** |\n\n> 🧠 **Why Section 18A matters to an adviser.** Section 30 of the **Indian Contract Act** makes wagering agreements **void**. A futures or options contract looks economically like a wager. Section 18A of SCRA rescues it: an **exchange-traded, clearing-house-settled** derivative is a valid contract. An **off-market** \"difference contract\" outside a recognised exchange is **not** — it is an unenforceable wager. That single link explains why SEBI treats \"dabba trading\" as illegal.\n\nThe **SCRR, 1957** carries the operational detail — including the **minimum public shareholding** requirement of **25%** for listed companies.\n\n---\n\n## 18.2 The SEBI Act, 1992\n\nSEBI was set up as a **non-statutory body in 1988** and given **statutory teeth by the SEBI Act, 1992** (effective **30 January 1992**). Head office: **Mumbai**.\n\n**Preamble — the three-fold mandate.** SEBI exists:\n1. **To protect the interests of investors** in securities;\n2. **To promote the development** of the securities market; and\n3. **To regulate** the securities market.\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"P-P-R\": Protect, Promote, Regulate.** Note the order: **protection comes first**. Whenever an exam question asks \"what is SEBI's primary objective?\", the answer is **investor protection**.\n\n### Constitution — Section 4\n\n| Member | Number |\n|---|---|\n| **Chairman** | 1 |\n| Members from Ministries dealing with **finance** and **administration of the Companies Act** | 2 |\n| Member from the **RBI** | 1 |\n| Other members appointed by the Central Government (at least **three whole-time**) | 5 |\n| **Total** | **9** |\n\n### Section 11 — functions of the Board\n\n- Regulating the **business in stock exchanges** and any other securities markets\n- **Registering and regulating intermediaries** — stock brokers, share transfer agents, bankers to an issue, trustees of trust deeds, registrars to an issue, merchant bankers, underwriters, **portfolio managers, investment advisers** and others associated with the securities market\n- Registering and regulating **collective investment schemes including mutual funds**\n- Promoting and regulating **self-regulatory organisations**\n- **Prohibiting fraudulent and unfair trade practices**\n- **Prohibiting insider trading**\n- Regulating **substantial acquisition of shares and takeovers**\n- Promoting **investor education** and training of intermediaries\n- Calling for information, undertaking **inspection**, conducting **inquiries and audits**\n- Performing functions under **SCRA**, delegated by the Central Government\n\nUnder **Section 11(3)** SEBI has the **powers of a civil court** — summoning attendance, discovery and production of books, and inspection of registers.\n\n| Section | Power |\n|---|---|\n| **11B** | Issue **directions** in the interest of investors (the workhorse of SEBI enforcement) |\n| **11C** | Order **investigation** |\n| **11AA** | Defines a **collective investment scheme** |\n| **12** | No intermediary may deal in securities **without a certificate of registration** |\n| **12A** | Prohibits **manipulative and deceptive devices, insider trading** and fraudulent takeovers |\n| **15I** | Appointment of an **Adjudicating Officer** |\n| **15J** | Factors for fixing the penalty amount |\n| **15T** | Appeal to the **Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)** |\n| **24** | Criminal offences — imprisonment up to **10 years**, fine up to **₹25 crore**, or both |\n\n### ⭐ The penalty sections you must memorise\n\n| Section | Default | Penalty |\n|---|---|---|\n| **15A(a)** | Failure to furnish documents/returns/reports to SEBI | **₹1 lakh per day**, max **₹1 crore** |\n| **15EB** | **Investment adviser or research analyst** failing to comply with regulations or SEBI directions | **Minimum ₹1 lakh**; may extend to **₹1 lakh for each day** the failure continues, subject to a **maximum of ₹1 crore** |\n| **15G** | **Insider trading** | **Minimum ₹10 lakh**; up to **₹25 crore** or **3× the profits** made, **whichever is higher** |\n| **15HA** | **Fraudulent and unfair trade practices** | **Minimum ₹5 lakh**; up to **₹25 crore** or **3× the profits**, **whichever is higher** |\n| **15HB** | Any contravention with **no specific penalty** provided | **Minimum ₹1 lakh**, up to **₹1 crore** |\n\n**Section 15J — how the amount is fixed.** The adjudicating officer must consider:\n1. The **disproportionate gain or unfair advantage**, wherever quantifiable, made as a result of the default;\n2. The **loss caused to an investor** or group of investors; and\n3. The **repetitive nature** of the default.\n\n> 🧠 **The \"10-5-1\" ladder.** Minimum penalties climb with the seriousness of the sin: **₹10 lakh** for insider trading, **₹5 lakh** for market fraud, **₹1 lakh** for everything else (including an adviser's compliance failure). The **maximum** for both of the big two is the same: **₹25 crore or 3× profits, whichever is higher**.\n\n### Appeals\n\n| Step | Forum | Time limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| SEBI / adjudicating officer order | — | — |\n| First appeal | **SAT** (Securities Appellate Tribunal) | **45 days** from receipt of the order |\n| Second appeal (**question of law only**) | **Supreme Court** | **60 days** |\n\nSAT also hears appeals against **IRDAI** and **PFRDA** orders. SAT is expected to dispose of an appeal within **six months**.\n\n---\n\n## 18.3 SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003 — PFUTP\n\nThese regulations put flesh on Section 12A of the SEBI Act. They are short, blunt and extremely wide.\n\n### The definition of \"fraud\" — Regulation 2(1)(c)\n\nFraud includes **any act, expression, omission or concealment** committed **whether in a deceitful manner or not**, by a person or with his connivance or by his agent, **while dealing in securities**, in order to **induce another person to deal in securities** —\n\n> ⚠️ **…whether or not there is any wrongful gain or avoidance of any loss.** This is the most-tested phrase in PFUTP. You do **not** need to have made money for it to be fraud. Intent to induce is enough.\n\n### Regulation 3 — prohibition of certain dealings\n\nNo person shall directly or indirectly:\n- **Buy, sell or otherwise deal in securities in a fraudulent manner**\n- Use or employ any **manipulative or deceptive device or contrivance**\n- Employ any **device, scheme or artifice to defraud**\n- Engage in any act, practice or course of business which **operates as a fraud or deceit**\n\n### Regulation 4 — the deemed manipulative and unfair practices\n\nRegulation 4(1) states plainly: **no person shall indulge in a fraudulent or an unfair trade practice in securities.** Regulation 4(2) then *deems* a long list to be such practices, including:\n\n| Practice | What it looks like |\n|---|---|\n| **Creating a false or misleading appearance of trading** | Artificial volume to attract onlookers |\n| **Wash trades** | Dealing in a security with **no intention of transferring beneficial ownership** |\n| **Circular trading** | A closed loop of buyers and sellers passing the same shares around |\n| **Price rigging** | Inducing others to deal so as to artificially **inflate, depress or maintain** the price |\n| **Pump and dump** | Ramping the price on false information, then selling into the retail buying |\n| **Planting false or misleading news** | Fake announcements, forged letters, fabricated orders |\n| **Front running** | Dealing **ahead of a substantial impending client transaction** of which one has unpublished knowledge |\n| **Misleading advertisement** | Information presented in a distorted manner that may influence investors' decisions |\n| **Disseminating advice one does not believe to be true** | Recommending through any medium — **physical or digital** — a security one does not honestly believe in |\n| **Fraudulent inducement to invest** | Any inducement to deal based on a false statement |\n\n> 🧠 **Why the last two matter to an adviser more than anything else in this chapter.** An RIA who runs a Telegram channel touting a stock he already owns, or who publishes a \"target price\" he does not believe in, is not merely breaching the IA Code of Conduct — he is committing **fraud under PFUTP**, exposing himself to a **₹5 lakh–₹25 crore** penalty under **Section 15HA** *and* possible prosecution under **Section 24**.\n\n### Powers under PFUTP\n\nSEBI may **investigate** (Reg 5–10) and then, under **Regulation 11**, issue directions to:\n- **Suspend trading** in the security\n- **Restrain persons from accessing the securities market** and prohibit them from dealing\n- **Prohibit disposal** of securities\n- Direct **disgorgement** of unlawful gains\n\n**Front running note:** front running by an *intermediary* is specifically deemed unfair — for example, an intermediary buying (or taking a derivative position) **in advance of a substantial client order**. This is the practice most likely to be tested in the context of an adviser who also trades his own book.\n\n---\n\n## 18.4 SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008\n\nBefore 2008 every intermediary category had its own registration, obligations and enforcement code. The 2008 Regulations created a **single common rulebook** covering:\n\n1. **Registration** (a common application and \"fit and proper\" test)\n2. **General obligations and a common Code of Conduct**\n3. **A common procedure for action in case of default**\n4. **Action against persons who are not registered at all**\n\nAn **investment adviser is an intermediary** — so these regulations sit **behind** the IA Regulations at all times.\n\n### Registration and \"fit and proper person\"\n\n- Application in **Form A** with the prescribed fee; SEBI may seek clarifications and require a personal representation\n- Registration is **permanent**, subject to payment of fees and continued compliance — there is **no periodic renewal**\n- SEBI must be satisfied the applicant is a **fit and proper person** under **Schedule II**\n\n**Fit and proper criteria:** financial integrity, **absence of convictions or civil liabilities**, competence (including financial solvency and net worth), **good reputation and character**, efficiency and honesty, and absence of any disqualification.\n\nA person is **deemed not fit and proper** if, for example, he has been:\n- **Convicted** of an offence involving **moral turpitude, economic offence, securities laws or fraud**\n- **Declared insolvent** and not discharged\n- Found to be of **unsound mind** by a competent court\n- **Debarred or restrained** by SEBI or another financial sector regulator\n- Categorised as a **wilful defaulter**\n- Declared a **fugitive economic offender**\n\n### Common Code of Conduct (Schedule III)\n\nMaintain **high standards of integrity, fairness and professional excellence**; act with **due skill, care and diligence**; **not make misleading or exaggerated claims**; **not offer inducements**; maintain **confidentiality**; **avoid conflicts of interest** and disclose them; **redress investor grievances promptly**; **appoint a compliance officer**; **maintain records**; and **cooperate with SEBI** inspections.\n\n### ⭐ Chapter IV — common procedure for action in case of default\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | SEBI appoints a **Designated Authority** (an officer not below the rank of Division Chief) |\n| 2 | A **show-cause notice** is issued; the intermediary replies within the time allowed (ordinarily **21 days**) |\n| 3 | The Designated Authority holds an **inquiry** and gives an **opportunity of personal hearing** |\n| 4 | The Authority submits a **report** to the **Designated Member** |\n| 5 | A **post-inquiry show-cause notice** with the report is served; a further hearing follows |\n| 6 | The Designated Member passes a **reasoned order** |\n\n**Penalties that may be imposed (Reg 27):**\n- **Warning** (censure)\n- **Suspension** of the certificate of registration for a specified period\n- **Cancellation** of the certificate of registration\n- **Prohibition from taking any new assignment or contract or client** for a specified period\n- **Debarring a branch or an office** from carrying on activities\n- **Debarring a principal officer or employee** from association with any registered intermediary\n\n**Summary proceedings** (a fast-track route with a shorter notice) may be used where, for example, the intermediary **admits the violation**, is **untraceable**, has **failed to file returns**, or has **failed to redress investor grievances**.\n\n**Chapter V** lets SEBI act against **unregistered persons** carrying on an intermediary's activity — the hook used against unregistered \"advisory\" and \"tip\" services.\n\n---\n\n## 18.5 SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015 — PIT\n\nThe 2015 Regulations replaced the 1992 version. They rest on one idea: **information asymmetry must not be monetised.**\n\n### ⭐ The four definitions everything hangs on\n\n**1. Unpublished Price Sensitive Information (UPSI) — Reg 2(1)(n)**\n\nInformation relating to a company or its securities, directly or indirectly, that is **not generally available**, which **upon becoming generally available is likely to materially affect the price** of the securities. It **ordinarily includes**:\n\n| # | Category |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **Financial results** |\n| 2 | **Dividends** |\n| 3 | **Change in capital structure** |\n| 4 | **Mergers, de-mergers, acquisitions, delistings, disposals and expansion of business** and such other transactions |\n| 5 | **Changes in key managerial personnel** |\n\nThe list is **inclusive, not exhaustive** — SEBI has progressively widened it to align with the \"material events\" of the LODR Regulations.\n\n**2. Generally available information — Reg 2(1)(e).** Information that is **accessible to the public on a non-discriminatory basis.**\n\n> 🧠 **The test is not \"how many people know\" — it is \"could anyone have known equally\".** Information given to 200 analysts on a private call is still **UPSI**; information in a stock-exchange filing readable by anyone is **generally available**, even if only three people bothered to read it.\n\n**3. Insider — Reg 2(1)(g).** Any person who is:\n- (i) a **connected person**; **OR**\n- (ii) **in possession of, or having access to, UPSI**\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** the two limbs are joined by **\"or\"**. You can be an insider **without any connection to the company at all** — overhearing a board discussion in a lift makes you an insider the moment you possess the UPSI.\n\n**4. Connected person — Reg 2(1)(d).** Any person who **is, or has during the six months prior to the concerned act been**, associated with the company, directly or indirectly, **in any capacity** — by frequent communication with its officers, by a contractual, fiduciary or employment relationship, as a director, officer or employee, or by any professional or business relationship — **that allows, or is reasonably expected to allow, access to UPSI**.\n\n**Deemed connected persons** (unless the contrary is established) include:\n- An **immediate relative** of a connected person\n- A **holding, associate or subsidiary company**\n- An **intermediary** (which includes an **investment adviser**) or its employee or director\n- An **investment company, trustee company or AMC**, or its employee or director\n- An official of a **stock exchange, clearing house or clearing corporation**\n- A member of the board of trustees of a **mutual fund** or the board of an AMC\n- A director or employee of a **public financial institution**\n- An official or employee of a **self-regulatory organisation**\n- A **banker** of the company\n- A concern, firm, trust, HUF, company or AOP in which a **director or his immediate relative, or a banker of the company, holds more than 10%** of the holding or interest\n\n**Immediate relative — Reg 2(1)(f):** the **spouse**, and a **parent, sibling or child** of the person or of the spouse — who is either **financially dependent** on the person **or consults the person** in taking trading decisions.\n\n> 🧠 **The immediate-relative test is behavioural, not merely biological.** A financially independent brother who never discusses investments is not an \"immediate relative\" for PIT purposes; a dependent parent is.\n\n### Regulation 3 — communication and procurement of UPSI\n\n- **No insider shall communicate, provide or allow access to UPSI** to any person, including other insiders, **except in furtherance of legitimate purposes, performance of duties or discharge of legal obligations**.\n- **No person shall procure** UPSI from an insider except for those same reasons.\n- The board must adopt a **policy defining \"legitimate purposes\"** as part of the Code of Fair Disclosure.\n- **Structured Digital Database (SDD) — Reg 3(5):** the company must maintain a database recording the **nature of the UPSI** and the **names and PANs** of persons who **shared** and **received** it, with time stamps, **non-tamperable** and with audit trails.\n- UPSI may be shared for a **due-diligence** exercise: where the deal will trigger an **open offer**, the board's informed opinion that it is in the company's best interests is enough; where it will **not** trigger an open offer, the information must additionally be **made generally available at least two trading days before** the transaction is effected. Recipients must sign **confidentiality and non-disclosure agreements**.\n\n### Regulation 4 — trading when in possession of UPSI\n\n**No insider shall trade in securities that are listed or proposed to be listed when in possession of UPSI.**\n\n> ⚠️ **The reverse burden of proof.** Where an insider who traded **was in possession** of UPSI, the trades are **presumed to have been motivated** by that information. It is for the **insider** to prove innocence — not for SEBI to prove guilt.\n\n**The permitted defences (proviso to Reg 4(1)):**\n\n| # | Defence |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | An **off-market inter-se transfer between two insiders possessing the same UPSI**, both making a conscious and informed decision (reported to the company within **2 working days**) |\n| 2 | A transaction through the **block deal window** between persons possessing the same UPSI |\n| 3 | A transaction carried out **pursuant to a statutory or regulatory obligation** |\n| 4 | Exercise of **stock options** where the exercise price was **pre-determined** |\n| 5 | **Non-individual insiders:** the individuals possessing UPSI were **different** from those taking the trading decision, and adequate **\"Chinese walls\"** were in place |\n| 6 | Trades pursuant to an approved **trading plan** under Regulation 5 |\n\n### ⭐ Regulation 5 — the trading plan\n\nA trading plan lets a permanently-informed person (a CFO, a promoter-director) trade at all without ever \"choosing the moment\". It is **presented to the compliance officer, approved and publicly disclosed**, and it must:\n\n| Requirement | The classical rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Cool-off before trading begins** | Not earlier than **six months** after public disclosure of the plan |\n| **Results black-out** | No trading between the **20th trading day before the end of any financial period** for which results are due and the **2nd trading day after** those results are disclosed |\n| **Minimum coverage** | The plan must cover a period of **not less than twelve months** |\n| **No overlap** | It must not overlap with any plan already in existence |\n| **Certainty of terms** | It must set out the **value or number of securities**, the **nature of the trade**, and the **intervals or dates** |\n| **No market abuse** | It must not entail trading for market abuse |\n\nOnce approved, the plan is **irrevocable** and **must be implemented without deviation**. The compliance officer **notifies the plan to the stock exchanges**. Trading-window closure does **not** apply to trades under an approved plan.\n\n> ⚠️ SEBI has since **shortened the cool-off period** and relaxed some of the plan mechanics. For the NISM examination, answer with the classical rule set above unless the question quotes a different figure.\n\n### Regulations 6 and 7 — disclosures\n\n| Disclosure | Who | What | When |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Initial** | Promoter, promoter group member, KMP, director | Holding of securities on **appointment / on becoming a promoter** | Within **7 days** |\n| **Continual** | Promoter, promoter group, **designated person**, director | Securities acquired or disposed of, where the **traded value exceeds ₹10 lakh** in one transaction or a **series over a calendar quarter** | To the **company within 2 trading days**; the **company to the exchange within 2 trading days** of receipt |\n\n### Regulation 8 — Code of **Fair Disclosure** (Schedule A)\n\nEvery listed company's board must formulate and **publish on its website** a code of practices for fair disclosure of UPSI, embodying:\n- **Prompt public disclosure** of UPSI that would impact price discovery\n- **Uniform and universal dissemination** — no selective disclosure\n- A **Chief Investor Relations Officer** to handle dissemination\n- Prompt dissemination of UPSI **disclosed selectively, inadvertently or otherwise**\n- **Fair response to queries and verification of market rumours**\n- Ensuring information shared with **analysts and research personnel is not UPSI**\n- Publishing **transcripts or records of analyst meets** on the website\n- Handling all UPSI on a **need-to-know basis**\n\n### ⭐ Regulation 9 — Code of **Conduct** (Schedules B and C)\n\n- The board of every **listed company** (Schedule B) and of every **intermediary and fiduciary** (Schedule C) must adopt a code of conduct to **regulate, monitor and report trading by designated persons** and their immediate relatives.\n- **Fiduciaries** expressly include **intermediaries, auditors, accountancy firms, law firms, analysts, consultants, insolvency professional entities and banks** assisting or advising listed companies. **An investment adviser is caught by both descriptions.**\n\n**Minimum standards in the code:**\n\n| Control | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Compliance officer** | Must be appointed; for intermediaries, reports to the board |\n| **Trading window** | Closed when designated persons can reasonably be expected to possess UPSI; for **financial results** it is closed from the **end of every quarter until 48 hours after the results are declared** |\n| **Pre-clearance** | Designated persons must obtain **pre-clearance** for trades above a set threshold; pre-cleared trades must be executed **within 7 trading days** |\n| **Contra trade** | A designated person must **not execute a contra trade within six months** of a trade |\n| **Breach of contra-trade rule** | Profits are **disgorged and remitted to SEBI** for credit to the **Investor Protection and Education Fund (IPEF)** |\n| **Trading plan** | Trading-window restrictions **do not apply** to trades under an approved plan |\n\n**Regulation 9A — institutional mechanism.** The CEO or MD must put in place **internal controls**, a **whistle-blower policy**, and, on any **leak of UPSI**, an **inquiry** and **intimation to SEBI**.\n\n**Penalty:** **Section 15G** — minimum **₹10 lakh**, up to **₹25 crore or 3× the profits, whichever is higher**, plus possible prosecution under Section 24.\n\n---\n\n## 18.6 ⭐⭐ SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013\n\nNotified **21 January 2013**, effective **21 April 2013**. Overhauled by the **SEBI (Investment Advisers) (Amendment) Regulations, 2020** (notified **3 July 2020**) and the SEBI circular of **23 September 2020** (\"Guidelines for Investment Advisers\"). **This section is the single densest source of marks in the whole syllabus.**\n\n### 18.6.1 Definitions\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| **Investment advice** — 2(1)(l) | Advice relating to **investing in, purchasing, selling or otherwise dealing in securities or investment products**, and advice on an **investment portfolio** containing securities or investment products, **whether written, oral or through any other means**, for the benefit of the client — and it **includes financial planning**. **Excluded:** advice given through **newspapers, magazines, or any electronic, broadcasting or telecommunication medium which is widely available to the public** |\n| **Investment adviser** — 2(1)(m) | Any person who, **for consideration**, is engaged in the business of providing investment advice to clients or other persons or a group of persons, **including any person who holds himself out as an investment adviser**, by whatever name called |\n| **Assets under advice (AUA)** | The **aggregate net asset value of securities and investment products for which the IA has rendered investment advice**, irrespective of whether implementation is done by the IA, by the client directly, or through others |\n| **Person associated with investment advice** | Any member, partner, officer, director, employee or **sales staff** of the IA who is **engaged in providing investment advisory services** to clients |\n| **Principal officer** | The MD, designated director, managing partner or executive chairman **responsible for the overall function of the business and affairs** of the IA |\n| **Family of client** | The **individual client, dependent spouse, dependent children and dependent parents** |\n| **Family of an individual IA** | The **individual IA, spouse, children and parents** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Two words decide whether you need registration: \"consideration\" and \"held out\".** Free advice to a friend is not advisory business. Advice **for a fee**, or **calling yourself an adviser**, is. And note the **media exemption is only for genuinely mass, widely-available media** — a paid Telegram group or a subscribers-only WhatsApp channel is **not** \"widely available to the public\" and therefore **is** investment advice requiring registration.\n\n### 18.6.2 Registration — Regulation 3\n\n- **No person shall act as an investment adviser or hold himself out as one without a certificate of registration from SEBI.**\n- Application in **Form A** of the First Schedule with the prescribed **application fee**; registration is granted in **Form B**.\n- **Application fees:** approximately **₹5,000 for individuals and firms** and **₹25,000 for body corporates and LLPs**; a further **registration fee** is payable on grant (**₹10,000** for individuals and firms; **₹5,00,000** for body corporates).\n- SEBI must be satisfied of the **fit and proper person** criteria of the Intermediaries Regulations.\n- Registration is **permanent**, subject to payment of fees and continued compliance.\n- **Banks, NBFCs and body corporates** offering distribution or execution services must provide advisory services through a **Separately Identifiable Department or Division (SIDD)**.\n- **Corporatisation threshold:** an **individual** IA whose number of clients **exceeds 150** must apply for registration as a **non-individual** IA and must **stop onboarding fresh clients** as an individual until registered.\n- **Change in control** of an IA requires **prior approval of SEBI**.\n\n### 18.6.3 Exemption from registration — Regulation 4\n\nThe following need **no** IA registration:\n\n| Exempt category | The limiting condition |\n|---|---|\n| **Anyone giving general comments in good faith** on market trends or the economy | Must **not specify any particular security or investment product** |\n| **Insurance agents and insurance brokers** registered with IRDAI | Advice **solely on insurance products** |\n| **Pension advisors** registered with PFRDA | Advice **solely on pension products** |\n| **Mutual fund distributors** registered with AMFI or a SEBI-recognised SRO | Advice **incidental** to distribution of mutual funds |\n| **Advocates, solicitors and law firms** | Advice **incidental to legal practice** |\n| Members of **ICAI, ICSI, ICMAI, the Actuarial Society** or other specified professional bodies | Advice **incidental to professional service** |\n| **Fund managers** of a mutual fund, AIF or other SEBI-registered entity | In that capacity |\n| Persons advising **exclusively clients based outside India** | **But advising an NRI or PIO requires registration** |\n| Any other person **specified by SEBI** | — |\n\n> ⚠️ **The two classic traps.** (1) A mutual fund distributor's exemption evaporates the moment the advice stops being *incidental* — if he charges a **separate advisory fee** or advises across asset classes, he needs registration. (2) The **\"exclusively outside India\"** exemption **does not cover NRIs and PIOs** — advise one NRI and you need to register.\n\n### 18.6.4 Qualification and certification — Regulation 7\n\nApplies **at all times** to the **individual IA**, the **principal officer** of a non-individual IA, and **persons associated with investment advice**.\n\n| Requirement | Individual IA / Principal Officer | Person associated with investment advice |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Qualification** | Professional qualification, or a **post-graduate degree or post-graduate diploma of at least two years' duration** in finance, accountancy, business management, commerce, economics, capital market, banking, insurance or actuarial science, from a recognised university or institution | **Same** |\n| **Experience** | At least **5 years** in advice on financial products, securities, fund, asset or portfolio management | At least **2 years** |\n| **Certification** | **NISM certification** (or one from another body, including **FPSB India** or a recognised stock exchange, **accredited by NISM**) — in practice **NISM Series X-A and X-B** | **Same** |\n\n**Fresh certification must be obtained *before* the existing certificate expires** so that compliance is continuous. NISM certificates are valid for **3 years**.\n\n> ⚠️ **SEBI has since been easing the entry norms** (qualification and experience). Answer the exam on the framework above unless the question itself quotes a different rule.\n\n### 18.6.5 Conditions of certificate — Regulation 8\n\n- **Abide** by the Act, regulations and SEBI directions\n- **Forthwith inform SEBI in writing** of any material change in the information previously furnished\n- **Pay the prescribed fees**\n- **Redress client grievances within one month** of receipt\n- **Maintain net worth at all times**\n- Obtain **prior approval of SEBI for any change in control**\n- The certificate is **permanent**, unless surrendered, suspended or cancelled\n\n### 18.6.6 ⭐ Net worth\n\n| Type of adviser | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| **Individuals and partnership firms** | **Net tangible assets of not less than ₹5 lakh** |\n| **Non-individuals** (body corporates, LLPs) | **Net worth of not less than ₹50 lakh** |\n\n**Net worth** = paid-up share capital **+** free reserves (excluding revaluation reserves) **−** accumulated losses **−** deferred expenditure not written off (including miscellaneous expenses not written off).\n\nThe 2020 amendment raised these from **₹1 lakh / ₹25 lakh**; existing advisers were given until **30 September 2021** to comply.\n\n> 🧠 **Compare and never confuse:** IA individual **₹5 lakh** · IA non-individual **₹50 lakh** · Research Analyst individual ₹1 lakh · **Portfolio Manager ₹5 crore** · AMC ₹50 crore. Notice the pattern: the more of the client's *money* you touch, the higher the number. An IA only touches **advice** — hence the smallest figures.\n\n> ⚠️ SEBI has been moving the IA framework from a **net-worth** test toward a **deposit-based** requirement. The examinable figures remain **₹5 lakh** and **₹50 lakh**.\n\n### 18.6.7 ⭐ General obligations — Regulation 15\n\n| Reg | Obligation |\n|---|---|\n| **15(1)** | **An investment adviser shall act in a fiduciary capacity towards its clients and shall disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise** |\n| **15(2)** | **Shall not receive any consideration — by way of remuneration, compensation or in any other form — from any person other than the client being advised**, in respect of the underlying products or securities advised upon |\n| **15(3)** | Any conflict between advisory activities and other activities must be **disclosed to the client** |\n| **15(4)** | **Shall not divulge confidential client information** without prior client permission, except where required by law |\n| **15(5)** | **Shall not transact on its own account contrary to the advice given to clients for 15 days** from the date of the advice. If within those 15 days the situation genuinely changes, the IA may transact **after giving the revised assessment to the client at least 24 hours in advance** |\n| **15(6)** | **Shall follow the KYC procedure** specified by SEBI |\n| **15(7)** | **Shall abide by the Code of Conduct** in the **Third Schedule** |\n| **15(8)** | **Shall not knowingly sell securities to, or buy securities from, its own client on its own account** |\n| **15(9)** | **Prior SEBI approval** for change in control |\n| **15(10)** | Furnish information and reports to SEBI as specified |\n| **15(11)** | Ensure **certification and qualification** compliance for itself and its representatives at all times |\n| **15(12)** | **Shall not manage funds or securities on behalf of the client**; may receive only such monies as are necessary to discharge the client's **fee** liability |\n\n> 🧠 **Regulation 15(1) is the whole profession in one line.** \"**Fiduciary**\" means the adviser must place the client's interest **above his own** — a higher standard than the \"suitability\" standard applicable to a distributor. Regulation 15(2) is what makes it real: **only the client may pay you.** Take a trail commission from an AMC on a scheme you advised, and Regulation 15(2) is breached no matter how good the advice was.\n\n**Client agreement.** Since 2020 an IA must execute a **written agreement** with every client **before** providing any advice or charging any fee. It must set out the scope of services, the fee and the fee mode, the **declaration that the IA receives no consideration from any person other than the client**, risk factors, conflict-of-interest disclosure, termination terms and the grievance redressal mechanism.\n\n**Display of details.** The IA must prominently display, on its website, mobile app, letterhead and all materials: its **name as registered**, **type of registration**, **SEBI registration number**, **validity**, **complete address with telephone numbers**, **contact details of the principal officer**, **BASL membership number** and the **address of the corresponding SEBI office**.\n\n### 18.6.8 ⭐⭐ Fees — Regulation 15A and the 23 September 2020 circular\n\nAn IA may charge fees **only in one of two modes**, and only **one mode per client** at a time:\n\n| Mode | Cap |\n|---|---|\n| **(A) Assets under Advice (AUA) mode** | **Maximum 2.5% of AUA per annum, per family of client**, across all services |\n| **(B) Fixed fee mode** | **Maximum ₹1,25,000 per annum, per family of client**, across all services |\n\n**The operating conditions:**\n\n1. Fees are charged **per family of client**, not per individual — a family cannot be split to multiply the cap.\n2. The IA must be able to **demonstrate the AUA with supporting documents** (demat statements, unit statements).\n3. Any portion of the AUA held under a **pre-existing distribution arrangement** with any entity must be **deducted** from the AUA before computing the fee.\n4. The **mode may be changed only after 12 months** from onboarding or from the last change of mode.\n5. **Advance fees**, if agreed, **shall not exceed fees for two quarters**.\n6. On **premature termination**, the client is **refunded the fee for the unexpired period**; the IA may retain a **breakage fee of not more than one quarter's fee**.\n7. Fees must be received **through banking channels** — never in cash.\n\n#### 🔑 Worked example — which mode may charge more?\n\nSet the two caps equal:\n\n> 2.5% × AUA = ₹1,25,000 → **AUA = ₹50,00,000**\n\n**The break-even AUA is exactly ₹50 lakh.**\n\n| Client's AUA | Max under **AUA mode** (2.5%) | Max under **fixed fee** | Which mode allows more? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ₹20,00,000 | 2.5% × 20,00,000 = **₹50,000** | **₹1,25,000** | **Fixed fee** |\n| ₹50,00,000 | 2.5% × 50,00,000 = **₹1,25,000** | **₹1,25,000** | **Identical** |\n| ₹1,20,00,000 | 2.5% × 1,20,00,000 = **₹3,00,000** | **₹1,25,000** | **AUA mode** |\n\n**A second worked example — the advance and breakage limits.** Mrs Bhatia agrees to a **fixed fee of ₹1,00,000 per annum**.\n- Quarterly fee = 1,00,000 ÷ 4 = **₹25,000**\n- **Maximum advance** the IA may collect = **2 quarters** = 2 × 25,000 = **₹50,000**\n- If she terminates after **5 months** having paid ₹50,000 for 6 months: the unexpired period is **1 month**, worth 1,00,000 × 1/12 = **₹8,333**, which must be refunded — but the IA may instead retain up to **one quarter's fee (₹25,000)** as breakage. Since the retainable breakage (₹25,000) exceeds the ₹8,333 still unearned, in practice the refund here is nil; had she terminated after **1 month**, ₹41,667 would be unearned and the IA could keep only ₹25,000 of it, refunding **₹16,667**.\n\n> ⚠️ **Do not read the caps as entitlements.** They are **ceilings**. The Code of Conduct separately requires that fees be **\"fair and reasonable\"** — an adviser charging the full 2.5% for a single annual review would be breaching the Code even though the cap is not exceeded.\n\n### 18.6.9 ⭐ Risk profiling — Regulation 16\n\nThe IA must:\n\n**(a) Obtain from the client all information necessary to advise**, including:\n- **Age**\n- **Investment objectives**, including the **time for which the client wishes to stay invested** and the **purpose** of the investment\n- **Income details**\n- **Existing investments and assets**\n- **Risk appetite and tolerance**\n- **Liability and borrowing details**\n\n**(b) Have a process for assessing the risk the client is *willing and able* to take**, including:\n- Assessing the client's **capacity for absorbing loss**\n- Identifying whether the client is **unwilling or unable to accept the risk of loss of capital**\n- **Appropriately interpreting** the client's responses and **not attributing inappropriate weight** to particular answers\n\n**(c)** Where a **tool** is used, ensure it is **fit for purpose** and that any **limitations are identified and mitigated**.\n\n**(d)** Ensure questionnaires are **fair, clear and not misleading** — not vague, not using **double negatives** or complex language, and **not containing leading questions**.\n\n**(e) Communicate the risk profile to the client** after the assessment is done.\n\n**(f) Update** the client information and risk assessment **periodically** — a **review at least once a year** is the accepted standard.\n\n> 🧠 **\"Willing AND able\" — the two-axis idea.** *Willingness* is psychological (risk **attitude**); *ability* is financial (risk **capacity**). A 32-year-old software engineer with a ₹4 crore net worth and no dependants may be **able** to take equity risk but **unwilling**. A retiree living off her corpus may be **willing** (she likes markets) but not **able**. **The lower of the two governs** — an adviser must never advise beyond the *ability*, whatever the willingness.\n\n### 18.6.10 ⭐ Suitability — Regulation 17\n\nThe IA must ensure:\n\n- **All investments advised are appropriate to the risk profile of the client**\n- It has a **documented process for selecting investments** based on the client's investment objectives and financial situation\n- It **understands the nature and risks** of the products or assets selected\n- It has a **reasonable basis for believing** that a recommendation:\n  1. **meets the client's investment objectives**;\n  2. is such that the client is **able to bear the related investment risks** consistent with those objectives and risk tolerance; and\n  3. is such that the client has the **necessary experience and knowledge to understand the risks** involved\n- For a **complex financial product**, the recommendation must rest on a **reasonable assessment that the structure and risk-reward profile is consistent with the client's experience, knowledge, objectives, risk appetite and capacity for absorbing loss**\n\n> ⚠️ **Risk profiling and suitability are two different obligations, tested separately.** Risk profiling is about **knowing the client** (Reg 16). Suitability is about **matching the product to what you learned** (Reg 17). You can do a perfect risk profile and still breach Regulation 17 by recommending an unsuitable product.\n\n### 18.6.11 Disclosure to clients — Regulation 18\n\nThe IA must disclose:\n\n1. To a **prospective** client, **all material information about itself** — its business, **disciplinary history**, terms and conditions of its advisory services, **affiliations with other intermediaries** and anything else the client needs to decide whether to engage it\n2. Its own **holding or position in the financial products or securities that are the subject of the advice**\n3. **Any actual or potential conflict of interest** arising from connection with or association with any issuer of products or securities — including anything that might **compromise its objectivity or independence**\n4. While advising, **all material facts about the key features of the product**, particularly the **performance track record**\n5. The **warnings and disclaimers** in the offer documents and advertising material of the product being recommended\n\n### 18.6.12 ⭐ Maintenance of records — Regulation 19\n\nRecords to be maintained:\n\n| # | Record |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | **KYC** records of the client |\n| 2 | **Risk profiling and risk assessment** of the client |\n| 3 | **Suitability assessment** of the advice provided |\n| 4 | Copies of **agreements with clients** |\n| 5 | **Investment advice provided, whether written or oral** |\n| 6 | **Rationale for arriving at the investment advice, duly signed and dated** |\n| 7 | A **register of clients** — date of advice, nature of advice, products/securities advised, and fee charged |\n\n- Records may be kept in **physical or electronic form**; where a record must be signed and is kept electronically, it must be **digitally signed**.\n- Records must be preserved for a **minimum of five years**. Where a **dispute is pending**, records must be retained **until the dispute is resolved**.\n- **Annual compliance audit:** the IA must have its compliance with these regulations **audited annually by a member of ICAI or ICSI**. The audit must be **completed within six months of the end of the financial year** (i.e. by **30 September**), and **adverse findings with the action taken** must be submitted to SEBI within **one month of the audit report, and in any case by 31 October**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one record advisers most often miss is #6 — the *rationale*, signed and dated.** SEBI's enforcement orders repeatedly turn on it: without a contemporaneous written rationale, the adviser cannot prove that the advice was ever suitable, and the burden is on him.\n\n### 18.6.13 Compliance officer — Regulation 20\n\n> ⚠️ **A *non-individual* investment adviser shall appoint a compliance officer.** An **individual** IA is **not** required to appoint one — he *is* the compliance function. This distinction is a favourite MCQ.\n\nThe compliance officer is responsible for **monitoring compliance** with the Act, the regulations, notifications, guidelines and instructions issued by SEBI.\n\n### 18.6.14 Redressal of client grievances — Regulation 21\n\n- The IA must **redress client grievances promptly** and have **adequate procedures for expeditious grievance redressal**.\n- Grievances must be handled in a **time-bound** manner; a condition of registration is redressal **within one month** of receipt.\n- The IA must **register on SEBI's SCORES** portal and provide clients with SCORES details, so a client may escalate.\n- Disputes may be referred to **arbitration** or to the SEBI-administered **Online Dispute Resolution (ODR)** mechanism.\n\n### 18.6.15 ⭐⭐ Segregation of advisory and distribution — Regulation 22\n\nThe heart of the 2020 reform. The problem it solves: an adviser who is also a distributor earns **twice on the same client** — a fee for the advice and a commission on the product — and the commission silently corrupts the advice.\n\n| Type of IA | The rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Individual IA** | **Shall not provide distribution services at all** — and **the family of the individual IA shall not provide distribution services** to the IA's clients |\n| **Non-individual IA** | Must maintain **client-level segregation at group level** — a client (and the client's **family**) may take **either advisory or distribution services from the group, never both**. Advisory must be provided through a **separately identifiable department or division (SIDD)**, at **arm's length** from distribution |\n\nAdditional points:\n- The **family of a client is treated as one unit** — the group cannot advise the husband and distribute to the wife.\n- Existing clients receiving both had to be given a **one-time choice** of one or the other.\n- The compliance and monitoring process for segregation must be **documented**.\n\n> 🧠 **The one-sentence rule:** *an individual adviser may never distribute; a corporate adviser may distribute, but never to its own advisory clients.*\n\n### 18.6.16 Implementation of advice / execution services — Regulation 22A\n\nAn IA **may** provide implementation services to its advisory clients in the securities market, **subject to hard limits**:\n\n- **No consideration** — no commission, referral fee, or any other benefit, **embedded, indirect or otherwise**, may be received **directly or indirectly at the IA's group or family level** for these services\n- The **client is under no obligation** to avail implementation services from the IA\n- Implementation must be through **direct schemes and direct plans** of products in the securities market\n- The IA **shall not charge any separate implementation fee** from the client\n- An **individual IA shall not provide execution services through its family**\n\n> ⚠️ **\"Implementation\" is not \"management\".** Regulation 15(12) still forbids an IA from **managing funds or securities** on the client's behalf. The IA may help the client **push the button on a direct plan**; it may not take custody, discretion or a commission.\n\n### 18.6.17 ⭐ Code of Conduct — Third Schedule\n\nNine headings — learn all nine:\n\n| # | Heading | The obligation |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Honesty and fairness** | Act **honestly, fairly and in the best interests of clients** and in the integrity of the market |\n| 2 | **Diligence** | Act with **due skill, care and diligence**; offer advice only after **thorough analysis** and after considering **available alternatives** |\n| 3 | **Capabilities** | Employ effectively the **resources and procedures** needed for the efficient performance of business |\n| 4 | **Information about clients** | **Seek** information about the client's financial situation, investment experience and objectives — and keep it **confidential** |\n| 5 | **Information to its clients** | Make **adequate disclosure of relevant material information** |\n| 6 | **Fair and reasonable charges** | Ensure fees charged are **fair and reasonable** |\n| 7 | **Conflicts of interest** | **Avoid** conflicts as far as possible; where unavoidable, **disclose** them and ensure clients are **fairly treated** |\n| 8 | **Compliance** | Comply with all applicable regulatory requirements |\n| 9 | **Responsibility of senior management** | The **senior management of a body corporate bears primary responsibility** for standards of conduct and proper procedures |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"H D C I I F C C R\":** *Honest Diligent Capable advisers Inform about clients, Inform clients, charge Fairly, manage Conflicts, Comply, and the Chief carries Responsibility.*\n\n### 18.6.18 Procedure for action in case of default\n\nAn IA who contravenes the Act, the regulations or SEBI directions is dealt with **under Chapter V of the SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008** — the common enquiry procedure set out in 18.4 above: Designated Authority → show-cause notice → inquiry and hearing → report → post-inquiry notice → reasoned order.\n\n**Possible outcomes:** warning; **suspension** of registration; **cancellation** of registration; **prohibition from onboarding new clients**; **debarment** of a branch, principal officer or employee; and in appropriate cases **refund of fees to clients** and **disgorgement** of unlawful gains.\n\n### 18.6.19 ⭐ Penalty for default by an investment adviser\n\n**SEBI Act, Section 15EB:**\n\n> Where an **investment adviser or research analyst** fails to comply with the regulations made by the Board or directions issued by the Board, he shall be liable to a penalty of **not less than ₹1 lakh**, which may extend to **₹1 lakh for each day** during which the failure continues, subject to a **maximum of ₹1 crore**.\n\n**Worked implication:** ₹1,00,00,000 ÷ ₹1,00,000 per day = **100 days**. A continuing default reaches the statutory ceiling in **100 days**; running longer adds nothing to the monetary penalty — but the **registration consequences** (suspension, cancellation) keep growing.\n\n### 18.6.20 Administration and supervision of investment advisers\n\nSEBI may recognise a body to administer and supervise IAs — an **Investment Adviser Administration and Supervision Body (IAASB)**.\n\n- **BSE Administration and Supervision Limited (BASL)**, a wholly-owned subsidiary of **BSE**, was recognised by SEBI as the IAASB, operational from **1 July 2021**.\n- **What BASL does:** grants **membership** to IAs (registration applications now route through it), conducts **supervision** including on-site and off-site inspection, handles **complaints and grievances**, takes **administrative action** and **monitors compliance**.\n- Every registered IA — new and existing — must obtain and maintain **BASL membership** and display its **membership number**.\n- SEBI has also prescribed an **advertisement code** for IAs, requiring prior approval of advertisements and prohibiting any claim of **assured or guaranteed returns**.\n\n### 18.6.21 IFSC — Investment Advisers in an International Financial Services Centre\n\n- An **IFSC** is set up under the **Special Economic Zones Act, 2005**; India's first is at **GIFT City, Gandhinagar**. For exchange-control purposes an IFSC unit is treated broadly as a **non-resident**, and transactions are conducted in **freely convertible foreign currency**.\n- Under the IA Regulations, an **Indian recognised entity or an entity from a foreign jurisdiction** may set up in an IFSC as an **investment adviser** by forming a **company or a limited liability partnership**, obtaining registration and complying with these regulations.\n- Since **1 October 2020**, the **IFSCA (International Financial Services Centres Authority)**, established under the **IFSCA Act, 2019**, is the **unified regulator** for all financial services in an IFSC — taking over the roles previously exercised there by **SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA**.\n\n---\n\n## 18.7 ⭐ Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002 — PMLA\n\nEnacted in **2002**, brought into force on **1 July 2005**. Objective: **to prevent money laundering and to provide for confiscation of property derived from, or involved in, money laundering.**\n\n### What money laundering is — Section 3\n\nWhoever **directly or indirectly attempts to indulge, knowingly assists, knowingly is a party to, or is actually involved in any process or activity connected with the *proceeds of crime*** — including its **concealment, possession, acquisition or use** — and **projects or claims it as untainted property**, is guilty of money laundering.\n\n### ⭐ The three stages\n\n| Stage | What happens | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Placement** | Introducing the **cash** into the financial system | Depositing ₹9.5 lakh cash into several accounts on the same day |\n| **2. Layering** | A **series of conversions and movements** to distance the funds from their source | Wiring through three shell companies in two jurisdictions, buying and selling securities |\n| **3. Integration** | The funds **re-enter the legitimate economy** as apparently clean wealth | Buying a flat, \"profits\" from a business, an investment portfolio |\n\n> 🧠 **P-L-I. Placement is where the launderer is most exposed** — cash must physically touch the system. That is precisely why the **Cash Transaction Report** and the **prohibition on third-party and cash payments** exist: they attack stage 1.\n\n### Punishment — Section 4\n\n**Rigorous imprisonment of not less than 3 years, extending to 7 years, plus a fine.** Where the proceeds of crime relate to an offence under the **NDPS Act**, imprisonment may extend to **10 years**.\n\n### ⭐ Obligations of a reporting entity — Section 12\n\nA **reporting entity** — banking companies, financial institutions, **intermediaries** (which includes an **investment adviser**) and persons carrying on a designated business or profession — must:\n\n1. **Maintain a record of all transactions** in a manner enabling **reconstruction of individual transactions**\n2. **Furnish information** on prescribed transactions to the **Director, FIU-IND** — **whether attempted or executed**\n3. **Verify the identity of clients** (KYC)\n4. **Identify the beneficial owner** of the client\n5. **Maintain records of identity documents**, account files and business correspondence\n\n**Retention periods:**\n\n| Record | Retain for |\n|---|---|\n| **Transaction records** | **5 years from the date of the transaction** |\n| **Identity / KYC records** | **5 years after the business relationship ends or the account is closed, whichever is later** |\n\n**Governance:** every reporting entity must appoint a **Principal Officer** (responsible for furnishing information to FIU-IND) and a **Designated Director**, maintain a board-approved **AML/CFT policy**, run an **employee training programme**, and have the system **independently audited**.\n\n### ⭐ The reports to FIU-IND\n\n**FIU-IND (Financial Intelligence Unit — India)** was set up in **2004** under the **Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance**. It is the **central national agency** for receiving, processing, analysing and disseminating information on suspect financial transactions.\n\n| Report | Trigger | Deadline |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CTR** — Cash Transaction Report | **All cash transactions of more than ₹10 lakh**; and a **series of integrally connected cash transactions** within a month aggregating to more than ₹10 lakh | **15th day of the succeeding month** |\n| **STR** — Suspicious Transaction Report | **Any suspicious transaction — no monetary threshold**, whether **attempted or executed** | **Within 7 working days** of arriving at the conclusion that the transaction is suspicious |\n| **CCR** — Counterfeit Currency Report | Cash transactions where **forged or counterfeit currency** was used | **15th day of the succeeding month** |\n| **NTR** — Non-Profit Organisation Report | **Receipts by an NPO of more than ₹10 lakh** | **15th day of the succeeding month** |\n| **Cross-border wire transfer report** | Cross-border wire transfers of **more than ₹5 lakh**, where either origin or destination is in India | **15th day of the succeeding month** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Two traps.** (1) An **STR has no threshold and no minimum amount** — a ₹40,000 transaction with an implausible explanation is reportable. (2) **The client must never be told (tipped off)** that an STR has been filed.\n\n### Client Due Diligence and the beneficial owner\n\n**CDD** must be carried out at the **commencement of an account-based relationship**, when carrying out an **international money transfer**, and **whenever there is doubt about the veracity or adequacy** of previously obtained identification data.\n\nA **beneficial owner** is the **natural person who ultimately owns or controls a client** or on whose behalf a transaction is conducted. The PML Rules identify him by an **ownership or control threshold** — for a company or partnership, ownership of or entitlement to a prescribed percentage of the shares, capital or profits (originally **more than 25%**, **tightened to 10%** by a 2023 amendment); for a **trust**, the settlor, the trustees and beneficiaries with a specified interest. Where no such natural person is identified, the **senior managing official** is treated as the beneficial owner.\n\n### Enforcement machinery\n\n- The **Director (FIU-IND)** and the **Enforcement Directorate** may **provisionally attach** property involved in money laundering (attachment valid up to **180 days**), subject to confirmation by the **Adjudicating Authority**, followed by **confiscation**.\n- Appeals lie to the **Appellate Tribunal** and then to the **High Court**.\n- The Director may impose a **monetary penalty** on a reporting entity that fails in its Section 12 obligations, and issue directions.\n\n---\n\n## 18.8 Other laws an investment adviser must know\n\n### 18.8.1 Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999 — FEMA\n\nFEMA **replaced FERA, 1973** with effect from **1 June 2000**.\n\n**Objective:** *to consolidate and amend the law relating to foreign exchange with the objective of **facilitating external trade and payments** and for promoting the **orderly development and maintenance of the foreign exchange market** in India.*\n\n| | **FERA 1973** | **FEMA 1999** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Philosophy** | **Conservation** of scarce foreign exchange | **Facilitation** of trade and payments |\n| **Nature of violation** | **Criminal offence** | **Civil offence** — and **compoundable** |\n| **Burden of proof** | On the **accused** | On the **enforcement agency** |\n| **Default position** | Everything prohibited unless permitted | **Current account transactions freely permitted** unless restricted |\n\n- **Administered by the RBI**; **enforced by the Directorate of Enforcement**.\n- **Current account transactions** are **freely permitted** unless prohibited or restricted. The Current Account Transaction Rules, 2000 divide them: **Schedule I — prohibited** (lottery winnings, income from racing, purchase of lottery tickets and banned publications, football pools); **Schedule II — require Central Government approval**; **Schedule III — require RBI approval beyond the specified limits**.\n- **Capital account transactions** are **prohibited unless specifically permitted** by RBI regulations.\n- **Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS):** a **resident individual** may remit up to **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** for permissible current and capital account transactions — including **overseas securities, property and education**.\n- **Person resident in India — Section 2(v):** broadly, a person **residing in India for more than 182 days during the preceding financial year**, subject to exceptions — a person who leaves or stays outside India for **employment, business, vocation or any purpose indicating an intention to stay outside India for an uncertain period** is **not** a resident.\n\n> ⚠️ **FEMA residency is not income-tax residency.** They use different tests, different periods and different purposes. A person may be a **non-resident under FEMA** and a **resident under the Income-tax Act** in the same year. Advisers must apply **FEMA** for account type and repatriation, and the **Income-tax Act** for taxation.\n\n- **NRI accounts:** **NRE** (rupee, **fully repatriable**, interest tax-free in India), **NRO** (rupee, for Indian-source income, repatriation capped at **USD 1 million per financial year**), **FCNR(B)** (foreign currency, fully repatriable). NRIs invest in listed equity through the **Portfolio Investment Scheme (PIS)**.\n- **Penalty — Section 13:** up to **three times the sum involved** where the amount is quantifiable, or up to **₹2 lakh** where it is not; plus up to **₹5,000 per day** for a continuing contravention. **Compounding** is available.\n- **Appeals:** Special Director (Appeals) → **Appellate Tribunal** → **High Court**.\n\n### 18.8.2 Indian Contract Act, 1872\n\nEvery adviser–client relationship, every Power of Attorney, every distribution arrangement is a **contract**.\n\n- **Section 2(h):** a **contract is an agreement enforceable by law**. Agreement = **offer + acceptance**, supported by **consideration**.\n- **⭐ Section 10 — essentials of a valid contract:** **free consent** of parties **competent to contract**, for a **lawful consideration**, with a **lawful object**, and **not expressly declared void**.\n- **⭐ Section 11 — competency.** A person is competent if he is (i) **of the age of majority**, (ii) **of sound mind**, and (iii) **not disqualified** by any law (e.g. an undischarged insolvent, an alien enemy).\n\n> ⚠️ **A contract with a minor is *void ab initio*** — void from the very beginning, not merely voidable *(Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose)*. That is precisely why a **minor invests through a guardian**, why a minor **cannot be a joint holder**, and why a minor's folio must be **regularised on attaining majority**.\n\n- **Section 14 — free consent.** Consent is free when not caused by **coercion (s.15), undue influence (s.16), fraud (s.17), misrepresentation (s.18) or mistake (ss.20–22)**.\n  - Coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation → the contract is **voidable at the option of the aggrieved party**\n  - A **bilateral mistake of fact** essential to the agreement → the contract is **void**\n- **Section 25 — an agreement without consideration is void**, subject to exceptions (a written and registered promise made out of **natural love and affection**; compensation for **past voluntary services**; a written promise to pay a **time-barred debt**).\n- **Section 30 — wagering agreements are void.** (See 18.1: SCRA s.18A saves exchange-traded derivatives from this.)\n- **Agency — Sections 182 onwards.** An **agent** is a person employed to do an act for another or to represent another in dealings with third persons; the **principal** is the person for whom the act is done. **The principal is bound by acts done by the agent within the scope of his authority.** This is the legal basis of a **Power of Attorney**, and of a distributor acting for an investor.\n- **Remedies for breach — Section 73:** compensation for loss **naturally arising in the usual course of things** from the breach, or which the parties **knew when they made the contract** would be likely to result. **Section 74:** where a sum is named in the contract as payable on breach, reasonable compensation not exceeding that sum.\n\n### 18.8.3 Guardians and Wards Act, 1890\n\nA **secular, general law** governing the appointment of guardians of a minor's **person and property**, applicable irrespective of religion.\n\n- A **minor** is a person who has not completed **18 years**. Where a **court appoints a guardian** of the person or property, majority is attained at **21 years**.\n- **Three kinds of guardian:** the **natural guardian** (for a Hindu minor, the **father and after him the mother**; for an illegitimate child, the mother; a child below **5 years** is ordinarily in the mother's custody); the **testamentary guardian** (appointed by will); and the **guardian appointed or declared by a court** under this Act.\n- The **District Court** has jurisdiction, and the **welfare of the minor is the paramount consideration**.\n- A guardian must act **for the minor's benefit**, and **cannot sell, mortgage or transfer the minor's immovable property without the court's permission**.\n\n**Practical operational rules an adviser must apply:**\n\n| Situation | The rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Opening an account for a minor** | In the **minor's own name**, **represented by a guardian** (natural or court-appointed) — with proof of **date of birth** and proof of the **relationship** or the court order |\n| **Joint holding** | **Not permitted** — a minor cannot be a joint holder |\n| **Payments in** | Only from the **minor's own account**, the **guardian's account** or a **joint account of minor and guardian** |\n| **Payments out** | Only to the **minor's own bank account** |\n| **Minor attains majority** | The folio is **frozen for transactions** until it is regularised — fresh **KYC, signature attestation and bank details** in the (now major) investor's own name; the **guardian ceases** to operate the account |\n\n### 18.8.4 Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881\n\n| Instrument | Section | Parties | Essence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Promissory note** | 4 | **2** — maker, payee | An unconditional **undertaking** to pay a certain sum |\n| **Bill of exchange** | 5 | **3** — drawer, drawee, payee | An unconditional **order** to pay a certain sum |\n| **Cheque** | 6 | **3** — drawer, drawee **bank**, payee | A bill of exchange **drawn on a specified banker**, **payable on demand**; includes a **truncated cheque** and a **cheque in electronic form** |\n\n**Section 13:** a **negotiable instrument** means a **promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque** payable either **to order** or **to bearer**.\n\n- **Validity of a cheque: 3 months** from the date of issue (reduced from six months by the RBI with effect from **1 April 2012**).\n- **Crossing:**\n\n| Type | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| **General crossing** | Two parallel transverse lines — must be **paid into a bank account**, not over the counter |\n| **Special crossing** | The **name of a banker** appears — payable only through that banker |\n| **\"Account payee\"** | A direction to the **collecting bank** to credit **only the named payee's account** |\n| **\"Not negotiable\"** | The transferee **gets no better title than the transferor** |\n\n- **Endorsement:** a **blank (general) endorsement** is a bare signature (the instrument becomes payable to bearer); a **full (special) endorsement** names the endorsee; a **restrictive endorsement** restricts further negotiation.\n- **⭐ Holder in due course — Section 9:** a person who, **for consideration**, became the possessor of the instrument **before it became overdue** and **without notice of any defect** in the transferor's title. A holder in due course obtains a **better title than the transferor had** — the central privilege of negotiability.\n\n**⭐ Section 138 — dishonour of a cheque for insufficiency of funds.** Punishable with imprisonment up to **two years**, or a fine up to **twice the amount of the cheque**, or both. Four conditions must be satisfied:\n\n| # | Condition | Time |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The cheque was presented **within its validity period** | **3 months** |\n| 2 | The payee made a **written demand** on the drawer after receiving information of dishonour | Within **30 days** |\n| 3 | The drawer **failed to pay** | Within **15 days** of receiving the notice |\n| 4 | The payee **filed a complaint** | Within **1 month** of the cause of action arising (i.e. after the 15 days expire) |\n\n> 🧠 **\"3-30-15-1\":** three months to present, thirty days to notice, fifteen days to pay, one month to file. Miss any one step and the Section 138 remedy is lost — though the civil claim for the debt survives.\n\nCourts may also order **interim compensation of up to 20%** of the cheque amount to the complainant while the case runs.\n\n### 18.8.5 Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016 — IBC\n\nA **consolidated law for the reorganisation and insolvency resolution of corporate persons, partnership firms and individuals in a time-bound manner** — for **maximisation of the value of assets**, to **promote entrepreneurship** and the **availability of credit**, and to **balance the interests of all stakeholders**.\n\n**The four pillars:**\n\n| Pillar | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Insolvency Professionals (IPs)** and **Insolvency Professional Agencies** | Run the process; take over the management of the debtor |\n| **Information Utilities (IUs)** | Store and authenticate **financial information and records of default** |\n| **Adjudicating Authorities** | **NCLT** for **companies and LLPs**; **DRT** for **individuals and partnership firms**. Appeals: **NCLAT** → Supreme Court; **DRAT** → Supreme Court |\n| **IBBI** | The **regulator**, established on **1 October 2016** |\n\n**Corporate Insolvency Resolution Process (CIRP):**\n\n- Triggered by a **financial creditor**, an **operational creditor**, or the **corporate debtor itself**, on a **default of at least ₹1 crore** (the threshold was raised from ₹1 lakh to **₹1 crore** in **March 2020**).\n- On admission the NCLT declares a **moratorium** — suits and proceedings are stayed, assets cannot be transferred, security interests cannot be enforced, and essential supplies cannot be cut.\n- An **Interim Resolution Professional** takes over management and constitutes the **Committee of Creditors (CoC)** of **financial creditors**.\n- A **resolution plan** requires approval of **66% of the voting share** of the CoC.\n- **Timeline: 180 days**, extendable **once by 90 days** (270 days); with the 2019 amendment the **outer limit, including litigation, is 330 days**.\n- If no plan is approved, the company goes into **liquidation**.\n\n**⭐ Section 53 — the liquidation waterfall (the order that matters to an investor):**\n\n| Rank | Claim |\n|---|---|\n| **1** | **Insolvency resolution process costs and liquidation costs** |\n| **2** | **Workmen's dues for 24 months** + **secured creditors who relinquished security** — *pari passu* |\n| **3** | **Wages and unpaid dues of other employees for 12 months** |\n| **4** | **Financial debts owed to unsecured creditors** |\n| **5** | **Government dues (2 years)** + **secured creditors' shortfall after enforcing security** — *pari passu* |\n| **6** | **Any remaining debts and dues** |\n| **7** | **Preference shareholders** |\n| **8** | **Equity shareholders / partners** |\n\n> 🧠 **The single most important investment lesson in this whole chapter.** **Equity ranks dead last.** When a company enters CIRP, the equity shareholder is behind resolution costs, workmen, secured creditors, employees, unsecured lenders, the government and preference shareholders. That is *why* equity carries the highest expected return — and why an adviser must never present a distressed company's shares as a \"safe recovery bet\". A **bondholder** in the same company sits at **rank 4 or 5**; the **shareholder** sits at **rank 8**.\n\nThe Code also provides a **pre-packaged insolvency resolution process (PPIRP)** for **MSMEs** and separate provisions for **personal guarantors** to corporate debtors.\n\n### 18.8.6 FATCA and CRS\n\nBoth are frameworks for the **automatic exchange of financial account information** between tax authorities, to defeat offshore tax evasion. Every Indian mutual fund folio, demat account and bank account opened today requires a **FATCA/CRS self-certification**.\n\n| | **FATCA** | **CRS** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Full form** | **Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act** | **Common Reporting Standard** |\n| **Origin** | A **United States** law, enacted **2010**, effective **1 July 2014** | Developed by the **OECD**, the global standard for **Automatic Exchange of Information (AEOI)** |\n| **Nature** | **Unilateral** US law, implemented bilaterally through **Inter-Governmental Agreements (IGAs)** | **Multilateral** — implemented through the **Multilateral Competent Authority Agreement (MCAA)** |\n| **Whose accounts are reported** | **US persons** only | Residents of **all participating jurisdictions** (100+) |\n| **Sanction for non-compliance** | **30% withholding tax** on US-source payments to non-compliant institutions | Domestic penalties in each jurisdiction |\n| **India's position** | Signed a **Model 1 IGA** with the USA in **2015** | Signed the **MCAA**; reporting began from **2017** |\n\n**How it works in India.** Under the **Model 1 IGA**, Indian financial institutions report **not to the US IRS but to the Indian tax authorities (CBDT)**, which then exchange the data with the IRS. The obligation is enacted through **Section 285BA of the Income-tax Act, 1961** and **Rules 114F to 114H** of the Income-tax Rules, with reporting in **Form 61B**.\n\n**Reporting Financial Institutions** in India include **banks, custodians, depositories, mutual funds, insurance companies and NBFCs**.\n\n**What the client must give:** a **self-certification** of **country/countries of tax residence**, the **Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN)** for each, **country and place of birth**, address and, for entities, the **controlling persons**. **Failure to provide a valid self-certification can result in the account being blocked for transactions**, and a **false self-certification attracts a penalty of ₹50,000** under the Income-tax Act.\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap:** FATCA is **not** an Indian law and **not** administered by SEBI or RBI — it is a **US statute** that India implements through a treaty. CRS is **not** a law at all — it is an **OECD standard** given effect by domestic rules.\n\n---\n\n## 18.9 Violations by registered investment advisers — and their consequences\n\nThe SEBI orders against RIAs cluster into a small number of repeated failures. Learn the pattern, not the case names.\n\n| Violation | The rule broken |\n|---|---|\n| **Providing advice without registration** — running paid \"tip\" groups, Telegram or WhatsApp channels | Reg 3, IA Regulations; s.12, SEBI Act |\n| **Promising assured or guaranteed returns** (\"₹5 lakh profit in 3 months\") | Code of Conduct; **PFUTP Reg 4**; advertisement code |\n| **Charging fees beyond the prescribed caps**, or in **cash**, or without an agreement | Reg 15A and the fee guidelines |\n| **No risk profiling, no suitability assessment** — selling aggressive derivative strategies to retirees | **Reg 16 and Reg 17** |\n| **Receiving commissions while charging advisory fees** (\"double dipping\") | **Reg 15(2)** and **Reg 22** |\n| **Failing to segregate advice and distribution** | **Reg 22** |\n| **No records, no signed rationale, no annual compliance audit** | **Reg 19** |\n| **Not registered on SCORES; grievances unaddressed** | **Reg 21** |\n| **Trading against the advice given, or front running** | **Reg 15(5)**; **PFUTP Reg 4(2)** |\n| **Misleading performance claims** | Code of Conduct; PFUTP |\n\n**Consequences, in escalating order:** **warning** → **monetary penalty** (Section **15EB**, up to **₹1 crore**; or **15HA**, up to **₹25 crore or 3× profits**, if it amounts to fraud) → **refund of fees to clients** and **disgorgement** of unlawful gains → **prohibition from onboarding new clients** → **suspension** of registration → **cancellation** of registration and **debarment from the securities market** → **prosecution** under Section 24 (imprisonment up to **10 years** and/or fine up to **₹25 crore**).\n\n> 🧠 **The through-line of this whole chapter:** the law does not ask whether the adviser's *view* was right. It asks whether the adviser was **honest, unconflicted, documented and suitable**. An adviser whose recommendation loses money but who profiled, matched, disclosed and recorded is safe. An adviser whose recommendation makes money but who took a commission he did not disclose is not.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| SCRA purpose | Prevent **undesirable transactions in securities**; regulate exchanges and listing |\n| \"Securities\" excludes | **ULIPs**, fixed deposits, insurance policies, gold, real estate |\n| Spot delivery contract | Delivery **and** payment on the **same or next day** |\n| SCRA s.18A | A derivative is valid only if **exchange-traded and clearing-house settled** |\n| SCRA offences | Up to **10 years** imprisonment / **₹25 crore** fine |\n| SEBI Act year and date | **1992**, effective **30 January 1992** |\n| SEBI's three-fold mandate | **Protect** investors, **promote** development, **regulate** the market |\n| SEBI board strength | **9** — Chairman + 2 Govt + 1 RBI + 5 others (≥3 whole-time) |\n| SEBI Act s.11B | Power to issue **directions** |\n| Penalty — insider trading (**15G**) | Min **₹10 lakh**; up to **₹25 crore or 3× profits**, whichever higher |\n| Penalty — PFUTP (**15HA**) | Min **₹5 lakh**; up to **₹25 crore or 3× profits**, whichever higher |\n| Penalty — **investment adviser (15EB)** | Min **₹1 lakh**; **₹1 lakh per day**; max **₹1 crore** |\n| Penalty — residual (**15HB**) | Min **₹1 lakh**, max **₹1 crore** |\n| Section 15J factors | **Disproportionate gain**, **loss to investors**, **repetitive nature** |\n| Appeal to **SAT** | Within **45 days**; then Supreme Court within **60 days** on a question of law |\n| PFUTP \"fraud\" | Applies **whether or not there is wrongful gain or avoidance of loss** |\n| Front running | Dealing **ahead of a substantial impending client order** |\n| Intermediaries Regs 2008 | Common **registration, code of conduct and default procedure** |\n| Fit and proper disqualifiers | Conviction, insolvency, unsound mind, debarment, **wilful defaulter**, **fugitive economic offender** |\n| Actions under Reg 27 | Warning, suspension, cancellation, bar on new clients, debarment |\n| **Insider** definition | A **connected person OR** anyone **in possession of UPSI** |\n| **Connected person** lookback | **6 months** prior to the concerned act |\n| UPSI's five listed items | **Financial results; dividends; change in capital structure; M&A/de-merger/delisting/disposal/expansion; change in KMP** |\n| Generally available information | Accessible to the public on a **non-discriminatory basis** |\n| Burden of proof under PIT | **Reversed** — trades while in possession of UPSI are **presumed** motivated by it |\n| PIT continual disclosure | Value **above ₹10 lakh** in a calendar quarter → to the company in **2 trading days** |\n| PIT initial disclosure on appointment | Within **7 days** |\n| Trading plan — cool off / minimum / blackout | **6 months** / **12 months** / **20 trading days before period-end to 2 days after results** |\n| Trading plan once approved | **Irrevocable** and must be implemented **without deviation** |\n| Trading window (results) | Closed from **quarter-end until 48 hours after results** |\n| Pre-cleared trade execution | Within **7 trading days** |\n| Contra trade bar | **6 months**; profits **disgorged to SEBI's IPEF** |\n| Structured Digital Database | Names + **PAN** of those who **shared and received UPSI**, non-tamperable |\n| IA Regulations | **2013** — notified **21 Jan 2013**, effective **21 Apr 2013**; overhauled **2020** |\n| Investment advice includes | **Financial planning**; excludes advice via **mass public media** |\n| Investment adviser test | Advice **for consideration**, or **holding oneself out** as an adviser |\n| **AUA** | Aggregate **net asset value of securities/investment products advised upon** |\n| Exemption traps | MFD only if advice is **incidental**; \"outside India\" exemption **excludes NRI/PIO** |\n| Qualification | PG degree/diploma (**min 2 years**) in specified subjects |\n| Experience | **5 years** (individual IA / principal officer); **2 years** (persons associated with advice) |\n| Certification | **NISM-accredited**; **fresh certificate before expiry** |\n| **Net worth — individual/firm** | **₹5 lakh net tangible assets** |\n| **Net worth — non-individual** | **₹50 lakh** |\n| Corporatisation threshold | More than **150 clients** → register as a **non-individual** IA |\n| Reg 15(1) | Act in a **fiduciary capacity**; disclose all conflicts |\n| Reg 15(2) | **No consideration from anyone other than the client** |\n| Reg 15(5) | No own-account trade **contrary to advice for 15 days**; revised view **24 hours** in advance |\n| Reg 15(12) | **Shall not manage funds or securities** of the client |\n| Fee mode A | **2.5% of AUA per annum per family** |\n| Fee mode B | **₹1,25,000 per annum per family** |\n| Break-even AUA between modes | **₹50 lakh** |\n| Change of fee mode | Only after **12 months** |\n| Maximum advance fee | **2 quarters** |\n| Maximum breakage fee | **1 quarter** |\n| Risk profiling core idea | Risk the client is **willing AND able** to take; the **lower governs** |\n| Risk profile communication | Must be **communicated to the client** and **updated periodically** |\n| Suitability | Advice must **match the risk profile**; complex products need a **reasonable assessment** |\n| Records retention | **5 years** (until resolution if a dispute is pending) |\n| Signed record often missed | The **rationale for the advice, signed and dated** |\n| Compliance audit | Annually by a member of **ICAI or ICSI**; complete by **30 September**; report adverse findings by **31 October** |\n| Compliance officer | Mandatory for a **non-individual** IA only |\n| Grievance redressal | Within **one month**; register on **SCORES**; escalation to **ODR/arbitration** |\n| Segregation — individual IA | **May not distribute at all** (nor may the IA's family, to the IA's clients) |\n| Segregation — non-individual IA | **Client-level segregation at group level** through a **SIDD**, at arm's length |\n| Implementation of advice | Permitted **only through direct plans**, with **no commission or fee**, and **client not obliged** |\n| Code of Conduct headings | Honesty & fairness · Diligence · Capabilities · Information about clients · Information to clients · **Fair and reasonable charges** · Conflicts · Compliance · **Senior management responsibility** |\n| Default procedure | **Chapter V, Intermediaries Regulations 2008** |\n| IAASB | **BASL** (a BSE subsidiary), operational from **1 July 2021** |\n| IFSC regulator | **IFSCA**, from **1 October 2020**; GIFT City |\n| PMLA year / in force | **2002**, in force **1 July 2005** |\n| Money laundering stages | **Placement → Layering → Integration** |\n| PMLA punishment | **3 to 7 years** rigorous imprisonment + fine (**10 years** for NDPS-linked) |\n| **CTR** | Cash **above ₹10 lakh**; by the **15th of the next month** |\n| **STR** | **No threshold**; within **7 working days**; **never tip off the client** |\n| Cross-border wire report | Above **₹5 lakh** |\n| PMLA record retention | **5 years** (identity records: 5 years after the relationship ends) |\n| FIU-IND | Set up **2004**, under the **Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance** |\n| Beneficial owner | The **natural person who ultimately owns or controls** the client |\n| FEMA replaced | **FERA 1973**, w.e.f. **1 June 2000** |\n| FEMA philosophy | **Facilitation**; violations are **civil and compoundable** |\n| Current vs capital account | Current — **free unless restricted**; capital — **prohibited unless permitted** |\n| **LRS limit** | **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual |\n| NRO repatriation limit | **USD 1 million per financial year** |\n| FEMA penalty | Up to **3× the amount involved**, or **₹2 lakh** if not quantifiable |\n| Contract Act s.10 | **Free consent + competent parties + lawful consideration + lawful object + not void** |\n| Contract with a minor | **Void ab initio** |\n| Coercion / fraud / misrepresentation | Contract is **voidable**; bilateral **mistake of fact** makes it **void** |\n| Wagering agreements | **Void** (s.30) — exchange-traded derivatives saved by **SCRA s.18A** |\n| Guardians and Wards Act | **1890** — secular law; **welfare of the minor is paramount** |\n| Majority | **18**, or **21** where a court has appointed a guardian |\n| Minor's folio | **No joint holding**; payouts only to the **minor's own account**; **regularise on majority** |\n| NI Act instruments | **Promissory note (2 parties), bill of exchange (3), cheque (3)** |\n| Cheque validity | **3 months** |\n| Holder in due course | For **consideration**, **before maturity**, **without notice of defect** — gets a **better title** |\n| **Section 138** timeline | Present in **3 months** → notice in **30 days** → pay in **15 days** → complain in **1 month** |\n| Section 138 punishment | Up to **2 years** imprisonment or fine up to **twice the cheque amount**, or both |\n| IBC year / regulator | **2016** / **IBBI** |\n| CIRP default threshold | **₹1 crore** |\n| CIRP timeline | **180 + 90 = 270 days**; outer limit **330 days** |\n| CoC approval | **66%** of voting share |\n| Adjudicating authority | **NCLT** (companies/LLPs); **DRT** (individuals/firms) |\n| Liquidation waterfall — last | **Equity shareholders** (preference shareholders just above) |\n| FATCA | A **US law (2010)**, effective **2014**; India signed a **Model 1 IGA** |\n| CRS | An **OECD standard** for **AEOI**, via the **MCAA** |\n| Indian implementation | **Section 285BA** and **Rules 114F–114H**; **Form 61B** |\n| No self-certification | Account may be **blocked**; false certification penalty **₹50,000** |\n\n> **Exam tip:** if you are short of time, master three blocks and you will still clear this chapter — (1) the **IA Regulations numbers**: ₹5 lakh / ₹50 lakh net worth, **2.5% AUA or ₹1,25,000 fixed fee per family**, **150 clients**, **5-year records**, **15-day own-account bar**, **compliance officer only for non-individuals**, and **Section 15EB's ₹1 lakh per day up to ₹1 crore**; (2) the **PIT definitions** — insider is \"connected **OR** in possession\", the **6-month** lookback, the **₹10 lakh** quarterly disclosure and the **6-month contra-trade** bar; and (3) the **PMLA reports** — CTR **₹10 lakh** by the **15th**, STR **no threshold** within **7 working days**. Those three blocks alone account for the majority of the questions actually asked from Chapter 18.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 18: Key Regulations — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **This chapter is pure recall — the numbers, the deadlines and the section names are the marks.**\n\n**Q1.** What is the purpose of the SCRA, 1956, and what does it exclude from \"securities\"?\n> **A:** To **prevent undesirable transactions in securities by regulating the business of dealing therein** — it defines securities, provides for **recognition of stock exchanges** and governs **listing**. Securities **include** shares, debentures, **derivatives**, **mutual fund units**, **government securities** and security receipts; they **exclude ULIPs** (and fixed deposits, insurance, gold and property are not securities at all).\n\n**Q2.** What is a spot delivery contract, and why does SCRA Section 18A matter?\n> **A:** A **spot delivery contract** requires **actual delivery and payment on the same day or the next day**. **Section 18A** makes a derivative contract **legal and valid only if traded on a recognised stock exchange and settled on the clearing house** — without it, a derivative would be a **void wagering agreement** under Section 30 of the Indian Contract Act. That is why \"dabba trading\" is unenforceable.\n\n**Q3.** State SEBI's three-fold mandate and the constitution of its Board.\n> **A:** To **protect** the interests of investors, **promote** the development of the securities market, and **regulate** it — **protection first**. The Board has **nine members**: a **Chairman**, **two** from the Ministries dealing with finance and administration of the Companies Act, **one from the RBI**, and **five others** of whom at least **three are whole-time**.\n\n**Q4.** Which SEBI Act sections give the power to issue directions, order an investigation, and require registration?\n> **A:** **Section 11B** — **directions**. **Section 11C** — **investigation**. **Section 12** — no intermediary may deal in securities **without a certificate of registration**. **Section 12A** prohibits manipulative devices and insider trading.\n\n**Q5.** State the SEBI Act penalties for insider trading and for fraudulent and unfair trade practices.\n> **A:** **Insider trading (s.15G):** minimum **₹10 lakh**, up to **₹25 crore or three times the profits, whichever is higher**. **PFUTP (s.15HA):** minimum **₹5 lakh**, up to **₹25 crore or three times the profits, whichever is higher**.\n\n**Q6.** ⭐ What is the penalty for a default by an investment adviser?\n> **A:** **Section 15EB** — **not less than ₹1 lakh**, extending to **₹1 lakh for each day** the failure continues, subject to a **maximum of ₹1 crore**. The cap is therefore reached in exactly **100 days**.\n\n**Q7.** What three factors fix the quantum of a SEBI penalty, and what are the appeal time limits?\n> **A:** **Section 15J** — the **disproportionate gain or unfair advantage**, the **loss caused to investors**, and the **repetitive nature** of the default. Appeal to **SAT within 45 days**; then to the **Supreme Court within 60 days**, on a **question of law only**.\n\n**Q8.** What is unusual about the PFUTP definition of \"fraud\"?\n> **A:** It covers an act, expression, omission or concealment **whether or not committed in a deceitful manner**, and it applies **whether or not there is any wrongful gain or avoidance of any loss**. **You need not have profited for it to be fraud.**\n\n**Q9.** Name six practices deemed manipulative or unfair under PFUTP Regulation 4, and define front running.\n> **A:** **False or misleading appearance of trading; wash trades; circular trading; price rigging; pump-and-dump and planting false news; misleading advertisements; disseminating advice one does not believe to be true.** **Front running** is dealing **ahead of a substantial impending client transaction** of which one has unpublished knowledge.\n\n**Q10.** What do the SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008 provide, and who is \"not fit and proper\"?\n> **A:** A **common rulebook** for all intermediaries — **registration and the \"fit and proper\" test**, common **obligations and Code of Conduct**, a **common default procedure**, and powers over **unregistered persons**. A person is deemed **not fit and proper** if **convicted** of an offence involving moral turpitude, an economic offence or securities laws, an **undischarged insolvent**, of **unsound mind**, **debarred by a financial regulator**, a **wilful defaulter**, or a **fugitive economic offender**.\n\n**Q11.** What actions can SEBI take against a defaulting intermediary?\n> **A:** **Warning**; **suspension** of registration; **cancellation** of registration; **prohibition from taking new clients or assignments**; and **debarment** of a branch, principal officer or employee — following the Chapter IV/V procedure of show-cause notice, inquiry, personal hearing, report and reasoned order.\n\n**Q12.** ⭐ Define \"insider\" and \"connected person\" under the PIT Regulations, 2015.\n> **A:** An **insider** is any person who is **(i) a connected person OR (ii) in possession of or having access to UPSI** — the limbs are joined by **\"or\"**, so you can be an insider with **no connection at all**. A **connected person** is anyone who **is, or during the six months prior to the concerned act was**, associated with the company in any capacity that **allows or is reasonably expected to allow access to UPSI**.\n\n**Q13.** ⭐ What is UPSI, and which five categories does the definition list?\n> **A:** Information relating to a company or its securities that is **not generally available** and which, on becoming generally available, is **likely to materially affect the price**. It ordinarily includes **financial results; dividends; change in capital structure; mergers, de-mergers, acquisitions, delistings, disposals and expansion of business; and changes in key managerial personnel.** The list is **inclusive, not exhaustive**.\n\n**Q14.** What makes information \"generally available\", and who is an \"immediate relative\"?\n> **A:** Information is **generally available** if **accessible to the public on a non-discriminatory basis** — a private call with 200 analysts is still UPSI. An **immediate relative** is the **spouse**, and a **parent, sibling or child** of the person or spouse, who is **financially dependent** on him **or consults him** on trading decisions — a **behavioural, not merely biological, test**.\n\n**Q15.** Why is the burden of proof reversed under PIT Regulation 4, and what defences exist?\n> **A:** Trades by a person **in possession of UPSI** are **presumed to have been motivated by it** — the **insider must prove innocence**. Defences: an **off-market inter-se transfer between insiders holding the same UPSI**; a **block deal window** trade between such persons; a **statutory or regulatory obligation**; exercise of **stock options at a pre-determined price**; for **non-individuals**, different decision-makers behind adequate **Chinese walls**; and an approved **trading plan**.\n\n**Q16.** ⭐ State the classical requirements of a trading plan under Regulation 5.\n> **A:** Trading may not begin **earlier than six months** after public disclosure; **no trading** from the **20th trading day before the end of a financial period** for which results are due until the **2nd trading day after** their disclosure; the plan must cover **at least twelve months**; it must **not overlap** an existing plan; it must specify **value or number of securities, nature of trade and dates/intervals**; and no **market abuse**. Once approved it is **irrevocable** and must be implemented **without deviation**.\n\n**Q17.** State the PIT disclosure, trading-window, pre-clearance and contra-trade rules.\n> **A:** **Continual disclosure** where traded value **exceeds ₹10 lakh** in one transaction or a series over a **calendar quarter** — to the company within **2 trading days**, and by the company to the exchange within **2 trading days**. **Initial disclosure on appointment within 7 days.** The **trading window is closed from quarter-end until 48 hours after results**. **Pre-cleared trades must be executed within 7 trading days.** **No contra trade within six months** — profits are **disgorged to SEBI's Investor Protection and Education Fund**.\n\n**Q18.** What is a Structured Digital Database?\n> **A:** A **non-tamperable, audit-trailed database** the company must maintain recording the **nature of the UPSI** and the **names and PANs** of those who **shared** and **received** it, with time stamps.\n\n**Q19.** ⭐ Define \"investment advice\" and \"investment adviser\" under the IA Regulations, 2013.\n> **A:** **Investment advice** is advice relating to **investing in, purchasing, selling or otherwise dealing in securities or investment products**, and advice on an investment portfolio, **written, oral or by any other means** — and it **includes financial planning**; advice through **media widely available to the public** is excluded. An **investment adviser** is anyone who, **for consideration**, is in the business of providing investment advice, **including anyone who holds himself out as an adviser**.\n\n**Q20.** Is a paid Telegram \"tips\" channel covered by the media exemption?\n> **A:** **No.** The exemption covers only media **widely available to the public**. A **paid, subscribers-only** channel is advice for consideration; running it unregistered breaches **Regulation 3** and **Section 12** of the SEBI Act.\n\n**Q21.** Name the categories exempt from IA registration, and state the two traps.\n> **A:** Persons giving **general comments in good faith** naming no security; **IRDAI agents/brokers** (solely insurance); **PFRDA pension advisors** (solely pension); **AMFI-registered mutual fund distributors** (advice **incidental** to distribution); **advocates and law firms**; **members of ICAI/ICSI/ICMAI/Actuarial Society** (incidental to professional service); **fund managers** of mutual funds and AIFs; and persons advising **exclusively clients outside India**. **Traps:** a distributor's exemption dies the moment advice stops being **incidental**, and the \"outside India\" exemption **does not cover NRIs or PIOs**.\n\n**Q22.** State the qualification, experience and certification requirements for an investment adviser.\n> **A:** A professional qualification, or a **post-graduate degree/diploma of at least two years** in finance, accountancy, business management, commerce, economics, capital market, banking, insurance or actuarial science; **five years' experience** for an individual IA or principal officer and **two years** for persons associated with investment advice; and a **NISM-accredited certification**, with **fresh certification obtained before the existing one expires**.\n\n**Q23.** ⭐ State the net worth requirements, and the corporatisation threshold.\n> **A:** **Individuals and partnership firms — net tangible assets of at least ₹5 lakh.** **Non-individuals — net worth of at least ₹50 lakh.** (Raised from ₹1 lakh / ₹25 lakh in 2020.) An individual IA whose clients **exceed 150** must register as a **non-individual** IA and **stop onboarding fresh clients** until then.\n\n**Q24.** ⭐ What do Regulations 15(1) and 15(2) require?\n> **A:** **15(1):** the IA shall act in a **fiduciary capacity** and **disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise**. **15(2):** the IA shall **not receive any consideration — remuneration, compensation or in any other form — from any person other than the client being advised** in respect of the products advised upon. **Only the client may pay you.**\n\n**Q25.** What is the 15-day rule, and may an adviser manage a client's money?\n> **A:** **Regulation 15(5):** an IA shall **not transact on its own account contrary to advice given to clients for 15 days**; if the situation genuinely changes within that period it may transact only after giving the client the **revised assessment 24 hours in advance**. **Regulation 15(12):** an IA **shall not manage funds or securities** on the client's behalf, and may receive only sums needed to discharge the **fee** liability.\n\n**Q26.** ⭐ State the two permitted fee modes and their caps.\n> **A:** **(A) AUA mode — maximum 2.5% of Assets under Advice per annum per family of client** across all services. **(B) Fixed fee mode — maximum ₹1,25,000 per annum per family of client** across all services. **Only one mode per client** at a time.\n\n**Q27.** At what AUA do the two caps coincide, and what follows?\n> **A:** 2.5% × AUA = ₹1,25,000 → **AUA = ₹50 lakh**. **Below ₹50 lakh of AUA the fixed-fee mode permits a larger rupee fee; above ₹50 lakh the AUA mode does.**\n\n**Q28.** State the fee-mode change, advance fee, breakage and AUA-computation rules.\n> **A:** The **mode may be changed only after 12 months** from onboarding or the last change. **Advance fees may not exceed two quarters' fee.** On premature termination the client is **refunded the fee for the unexpired period**, the IA retaining **not more than one quarter's fee** as breakage. Any part of the AUA held under a **pre-existing distribution arrangement is deducted** before computing the fee. Fees must come **through banking channels**, never cash.\n\n**Q29.** ⭐ What must an IA obtain and assess under risk profiling (Regulation 16)?\n> **A:** **Age; investment objectives including tenure and purpose; income details; existing investments and assets; risk appetite/tolerance; and liability/borrowing details.** It must assess the risk the client is **willing *and* able** to take, including **capacity to absorb loss**; ensure any **tool is fit for purpose**; avoid **vague, double-negative or leading questions**; **communicate the risk profile to the client**; and **update it periodically**.\n\n**Q30.** What is the difference between risk *willingness* and risk *ability*, and which governs?\n> **A:** **Willingness** is psychological (attitude); **ability** is financial (capacity to absorb loss). **The lower of the two governs** — an adviser must never advise beyond the client's **ability**, however willing the client is.\n\n**Q31.** ⭐ What does Regulation 17 (suitability) require?\n> **A:** That **all advice is appropriate to the client's risk profile**; a **documented process** for selecting investments; that the IA **understands the nature and risks** of the products; and a **reasonable basis** for believing the recommendation **meets the client's objectives**, that the client can **bear the risks**, and that the client has the **experience and knowledge to understand them**. A **complex product** needs a specific assessment that its **structure and risk-reward profile** fits the client.\n\n**Q32.** What must an IA disclose to clients under Regulation 18?\n> **A:** All **material information about itself**, including its **disciplinary history**, terms of service and **affiliations with other intermediaries**; its **own holding or position** in the products advised upon; any **actual or potential conflict of interest**; the **material facts and performance track record** of the product; and the **warnings and disclaimers** in the product's documents.\n\n**Q33.** ⭐ List the records an IA must maintain, and for how long.\n> **A:** **KYC records; risk profiling and assessment; suitability assessment; client agreements; the advice given, written or oral; the rationale for the advice, duly signed and dated; and a register of clients** with dates, nature of advice, products and fees. Retained for a **minimum of five years**, and **until resolution** where a dispute is pending.\n\n**Q34.** State the annual compliance audit requirement, and who must appoint a compliance officer.\n> **A:** An annual audit of compliance by a **member of ICAI or ICSI**, **completed within six months of the financial year end (by 30 September)**, with **adverse findings and the action taken reported to SEBI within one month, and in any case by 31 October**. A **compliance officer is mandatory only for a non-individual IA** — an **individual IA need not appoint one**.\n\n**Q35.** Within what time must an IA redress a grievance, and where can a client escalate?\n> **A:** Within **one month** of receipt. The IA must be registered on **SCORES**; a client may escalate to **SEBI through SCORES**, and thereafter to **arbitration or the ODR mechanism**.\n\n**Q36.** ⭐ State the segregation rule for an individual and for a non-individual investment adviser.\n> **A:** An **individual IA shall not provide distribution services at all** (nor may the IA's family provide them to the IA's clients). A **non-individual IA** must maintain **client-level segregation at group level** — a client **and the client's family** may take **either advisory or distribution services from the group, never both** — with advisory delivered through a **separately identifiable department or division** at **arm's length**. In one line: **an individual adviser may never distribute; a corporate adviser may distribute, but never to its own advisory clients.**\n\n**Q37.** On what conditions may an IA provide implementation services?\n> **A:** **No consideration** of any kind — commission, referral fee, embedded or indirect — at the **IA's group or family level**; the **client is under no obligation** to use them; implementation must be through **direct plans/schemes**; the IA **charges no implementation fee**; and an **individual IA may not provide execution services through its family**.\n\n**Q38.** Name the nine headings of the IA Code of Conduct, and the procedure for action in default.\n> **A:** **Honesty and fairness; diligence; capabilities; information about clients; information to its clients; fair and reasonable charges; conflicts of interest; compliance; and responsibility of senior management.** Action in default follows **Chapter V of the SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008**.\n\n**Q39.** What is BASL, and who regulates an investment adviser set up in an IFSC?\n> **A:** **BSE Administration and Supervision Limited**, a BSE subsidiary recognised by SEBI as the **Investment Adviser Administration and Supervision Body (IAASB)**, operational from **1 July 2021** — it grants **membership**, conducts **supervision and inspection**, handles grievances and monitors compliance. In an **IFSC (GIFT City)** an entity may set up as a **company or LLP**, and since **1 October 2020** the **IFSCA** (under the IFSCA Act, 2019) is the **unified regulator** there.\n\n**Q40.** ⭐ What are the three stages of money laundering, and the PMLA punishment?\n> **A:** **Placement** (introducing cash into the financial system) → **Layering** (conversions and movements to distance the funds from their source) → **Integration** (funds re-enter the economy as apparently clean wealth). **Placement is where the launderer is most exposed.** Punishment: **rigorous imprisonment of 3 to 7 years plus fine** (up to **10 years** where the proceeds relate to an **NDPS Act** offence).\n\n**Q41.** ⭐ State the obligations of a reporting entity, and the CTR and STR rules.\n> **A:** **Maintain records of all transactions** so individual transactions can be reconstructed; **furnish information to FIU-IND**, whether the transaction was **attempted or executed**; **verify client identity**; **identify the beneficial owner**; and **retain identity records**. **CTR** — cash **above ₹10 lakh** (or an integrally connected monthly series above that) — by the **15th of the succeeding month**. **STR** — **no monetary threshold** — within **seven working days** of forming the suspicion, and **the client must never be tipped off**.\n\n**Q42.** For how long must PMLA records be kept, and what is FIU-IND?\n> **A:** **Transaction records — five years from the date of the transaction; identity records — five years after the relationship ends or the account is closed, whichever is later.** **FIU-IND** is the **Financial Intelligence Unit — India**, set up in **2004** under the **Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance**, the **central national agency** for receiving, analysing and disseminating information on suspect transactions.\n\n**Q43.** ⭐ How does FEMA differ from FERA, and what is the LRS limit?\n> **A:** FERA aimed at **conservation** of foreign exchange, treated violations as **criminal** and put the **burden of proof on the accused**. **FEMA** (in force **1 June 2000**) aims at **facilitating external trade and payments**, treats violations as **civil and compoundable**, and puts the burden on the **enforcement agency**. **Current account transactions are free unless restricted; capital account transactions are prohibited unless permitted.** The **LRS** limit is **USD 2,50,000 per financial year** per resident individual.\n\n**Q44.** ⭐ State the essentials of a valid contract, and why a contract with a minor is void.\n> **A:** **Section 10** — **free consent** of parties **competent to contract**, for a **lawful consideration**, with a **lawful object**, and **not expressly declared void**. A minor is **not competent** under Section 11, so the contract is **void ab initio** — hence a minor invests **through a guardian**, **cannot be a joint holder**, and the folio must be **regularised on attaining majority**. Coercion, undue influence, fraud and misrepresentation make a contract **voidable**; a **bilateral mistake of fact** makes it **void**.\n\n**Q45.** ⭐ Give the Section 138 timeline, the IBC liquidation position of equity, and the FATCA/CRS distinction.\n> **A:** **Section 138:** present within the cheque's **3-month** validity → notice within **30 days** → drawer fails to pay within **15 days** → complaint within **1 month**; punishment up to **2 years** or a fine up to **twice the cheque amount**. **IBC:** **equity shareholders rank last (8th)** in the Section 53 waterfall, behind even preference shareholders — CIRP is triggered by a **₹1 crore** default and runs **180 + 90 = 270 days** (outer limit **330**), with a plan needing **66%** of the CoC. **FATCA** is a **US law (2010)** covering **US persons**, implemented in India through a **Model 1 IGA**; **CRS** is an **OECD multilateral standard** for **automatic exchange of information** covering residents of **all participating jurisdictions**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 18: Key Regulations — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **The SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 supply the most marks; the PIT and PMLA numbers supply the rest.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act was enacted in:\nA) 1992  B) 1956  C) 1999  D) 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **SCRA, 1956**, the oldest of the securities statutes.\nA) Is the SEBI Act. C) Is FEMA. D) Is the year of the IA Regulations.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Which of the following is NOT a \"security\" under SCRA?\nA) A debenture  B) A mutual fund unit  C) A unit-linked insurance policy  D) A government security\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A **ULIP** is expressly **excluded** from the definition of securities; it is regulated by IRDAI.\nA), B) and D) are all expressly included in Section 2(h).\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** A spot delivery contract requires delivery and payment on:\nA) The same day or the next day  B) T+2  C) Within one month  D) Any date agreed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Actual delivery and payment on the same or the next day** — Section 2(i) of SCRA.\nB) Is the current rolling settlement cycle, not the SCRA definition. C) and D) describe forward arrangements, not spot delivery.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Under SCRA, a derivative contract is legal and valid only if it is:\nA) Registered with the RBI  B) Traded on a recognised stock exchange and settled on the clearing house  C) Approved by the Central Government  D) Above ₹1 crore in value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Section 18A**. Without it, a derivative would be a void wagering agreement.\nA) RBI has no role in equity derivatives. C) No case-by-case approval exists. D) There is no such value test.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** SEBI's three-fold mandate is to:\nA) Tax, promote and regulate  B) Protect investors, promote development and regulate the securities market  C) Register, inspect and prosecute  D) Fix prices, allot shares and settle trades\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Straight from the SEBI Act preamble, with **investor protection first**.\nA) SEBI has no taxing power. C) These are powers, not the mandate. D) SEBI does none of these.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The SEBI Board consists of how many members in total?\nA) 5  B) 7  C) 9  D) 11\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Nine**: Chairman + 2 Government + 1 RBI + 5 others (at least three whole-time).\nA), B) and D) are incorrect counts.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Which section of the SEBI Act empowers SEBI to issue directions?\nA) Section 11B  B) Section 15J  C) Section 24  D) Section 12A\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Section 11B** is the workhorse direction-issuing power.\nB) Lists the factors for fixing a penalty. C) Deals with criminal offences. D) Prohibits manipulative devices and insider trading.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The minimum penalty for insider trading under Section 15G of the SEBI Act is:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Minimum **₹10 lakh**, extending to ₹25 crore or three times the profits, whichever is higher.\nA) Is the residual minimum under 15HB. B) Is the PFUTP minimum under 15HA. D) Is the maximum, not the minimum.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** The penalty for default by an investment adviser is provided in:\nA) Section 15G  B) Section 15HA  C) Section 15EB  D) Section 23\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 15EB** — minimum ₹1 lakh, ₹1 lakh per day, maximum ₹1 crore.\nA) Covers insider trading. B) Covers fraudulent and unfair trade practices. D) Is an SCRA penalty section.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An appeal against a SEBI order lies to SAT within:\nA) 30 days  B) 45 days  C) 60 days  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **45 days** from receipt of the order; a further appeal to the Supreme Court lies within **60 days** on a question of law.\nA) and D) are not the statutory periods. C) Is the Supreme Court limit, not SAT's.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Under the PFUTP Regulations, fraud is committed:\nA) Only if the person made a profit  B) Whether or not there is any wrongful gain or avoidance of loss  C) Only if a client complains  D) Only by registered intermediaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The definition expressly applies **whether or not there is wrongful gain or avoidance of loss**.\nA) Profit is not an ingredient. C) SEBI can act suo motu. D) PFUTP binds **any person**, registered or not.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Dealing in a security with no intention of transferring beneficial ownership is called:\nA) A wash trade  B) A block deal  C) A bulk deal  D) Arbitrage\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A **wash trade** is a deemed unfair practice under PFUTP Regulation 4.\nB) and C) are legitimate disclosed large trades. D) Arbitrage exploits genuine price differences and is legal.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Front running means:\nA) Buying only large-cap shares  B) Dealing ahead of a substantial impending client transaction using unpublished knowledge of it  C) Being first in an IPO queue  D) Executing trades faster than competitors\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A deemed unfair trade practice under PFUTP.\nA) Is a portfolio choice. C) and D) describe speed or access, not the misuse of client order information.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008 primarily provide:\nA) A common framework for registration, conduct and enforcement across intermediaries  B) Rules for listing companies  C) Mutual fund investment limits  D) Tax rates for intermediaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — One common rulebook replacing category-by-category rules.\nB) Is LODR/SCRA territory. C) Is in the Mutual Funds Regulations. D) SEBI does not set tax rates.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Which of the following makes a person \"not fit and proper\"?\nA) Being a first-time applicant  B) Being categorised as a wilful defaulter  C) Having a small client base  D) Operating from a rented office\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Wilful defaulters, fugitive economic offenders, undischarged insolvents and persons debarred by a regulator are deemed not fit and proper.\nA), C) and D) are commercially irrelevant to the fit and proper test.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Under the PIT Regulations, an insider is a person who is:\nA) Only a director of the company  B) A connected person OR a person in possession of UPSI  C) Only an employee  D) A connected person AND in possession of UPSI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The two limbs are joined by **\"or\"**.\nA) and C) are only examples of connected persons. D) Misreads \"or\" as \"and\" — the classic trap.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The lookback period in the definition of a \"connected person\" is:\nA) 3 months  B) 6 months  C) 12 months  D) 24 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Association during the **six months prior to the concerned act**.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Which of the following is expressly listed as UPSI?\nA) A change in the company's logo  B) Financial results  C) The CEO's holiday plans  D) The share price itself\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Financial results** head the list, along with dividends, change in capital structure, M&A-type transactions and changes in KMP.\nA) and C) are not price sensitive. D) The price is public information, not UPSI.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Information is \"generally available\" when it is:\nA) Known to more than 100 people  B) Accessible to the public on a non-discriminatory basis  C) Published in any newspaper  D) Known to the board\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The test is equality of access, not headcount.\nA) A private call with 200 analysts is still UPSI. C) Depends on whether the source made it publicly accessible. D) Board knowledge is precisely the opposite of general availability.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Under PIT, continual disclosure is triggered when the traded value in a calendar quarter exceeds:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹10 lakh**, in one transaction or a series over a calendar quarter, disclosed to the company within **2 trading days**.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed thresholds.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** A designated person must not execute a contra trade within:\nA) 1 month  B) 3 months  C) 6 months  D) 12 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Six months**; profits from a breach are disgorged to SEBI's Investor Protection and Education Fund.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed period.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** The SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations were notified in:\nA) 2008  B) 2013  C) 2015  D) 2020\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Notified **21 January 2013**, effective 21 April 2013.\nA) Is the Intermediaries Regulations. C) Is the PIT Regulations. D) Is the year of the major amendment, not of notification.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** \"Investment advice\" under the IA Regulations expressly includes:\nA) Tax filing  B) Financial planning  C) Bookkeeping  D) Insurance claim settlement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The definition states that investment advice **includes financial planning**.\nA), C) and D) are separate professional services outside the definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** The minimum net worth for a non-individual investment adviser is:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹25 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹50 lakh** for non-individuals; ₹5 lakh net tangible assets for individuals and firms.\nA) Is the individual requirement. B) Was the pre-2020 figure. D) Is the portfolio manager's net worth.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Under Regulation 15(2), an investment adviser may receive consideration from:\nA) The product manufacturer  B) Only the client being advised  C) Any distributor  D) The stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Only the client being advised** may pay the adviser, in any form.\nA), C) and D) would all be third-party consideration and are prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The maximum fee under the Assets under Advice mode is:\nA) 1% of AUA per annum  B) 2.5% of AUA per annum per family of client  C) 5% of AUA per annum  D) There is no cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **2.5% of AUA per annum per family of client**, across all services.\nA) and C) are not the prescribed cap. D) A cap certainly exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The maximum fee under the fixed fee mode is:\nA) ₹50,000 per annum  B) ₹1,00,000 per annum  C) ₹1,25,000 per annum per family of client  D) ₹2,50,000 per annum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹1,25,000 per annum per family of client**, across all services.\nA), B) and D) are not the prescribed cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An investment adviser must maintain records for a minimum of:\nA) 3 years  B) 5 years  C) 8 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Five years**, and until resolution where a dispute is pending.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** A compliance officer must be appointed by:\nA) Every investment adviser  B) Only non-individual investment advisers  C) Only individual investment advisers  D) Only advisers with over 500 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation 20 applies to **non-individual** advisers; an individual IA is not required to appoint one.\nA) Overstates the requirement. C) Reverses it. D) No client-count test applies to this obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** An individual investment adviser may provide distribution services:\nA) Freely  B) Not at all  C) Only to non-advisory clients  D) Only with SEBI approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **individual IA shall not provide distribution services at all**.\nA) Contradicts Regulation 22. C) Describes the rule for a **non-individual** adviser. D) No such approval route exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** The three stages of money laundering, in order, are:\nA) Layering, placement, integration  B) Placement, layering, integration  C) Integration, layering, placement  D) Placement, integration, layering\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Placement → Layering → Integration**.\nA), C) and D) all scramble the sequence. Cash must enter the system (placement) before it can be moved (layering) or spent as clean money (integration).\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A Cash Transaction Report is required for cash transactions above:\nA) ₹1 lakh  B) ₹5 lakh  C) ₹10 lakh  D) ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **₹10 lakh**, filed by the **15th day of the succeeding month**.\nA) and B) are below the threshold. D) Is far above it.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A Suspicious Transaction Report must be filed within:\nA) 3 working days  B) 7 working days  C) 15 days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Seven working days** of forming the suspicion, with **no monetary threshold**.\nA), C) and D) are not the prescribed period; the 15th of the next month applies to CTRs, not STRs.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** FEMA, 1999 replaced:\nA) The Companies Act  B) FERA, 1973  C) The SEBI Act  D) The Banking Regulation Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — FEMA replaced **FERA, 1973** with effect from **1 June 2000**.\nA), C) and D) all remain in force and were never replaced by FEMA.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** In the IBC liquidation waterfall, equity shareholders rank:\nA) First  B) Immediately after secured creditors  C) Last  D) Ahead of preference shareholders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Last** — behind resolution costs, workmen, secured creditors, employees, unsecured financial creditors, government dues, remaining dues and preference shareholders.\nA) Resolution and liquidation costs come first. B) Several classes intervene. D) Preference shareholders rank **ahead** of equity.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** Ravi runs a paid Telegram channel giving buy and sell calls on specific stocks to 400 subscribers who pay ₹999 a month. He claims the \"media exemption\". He is:\nA) Correct — Telegram is electronic media  B) Wrong — the exemption covers only media widely available to the public, so he needs IA registration  C) Correct, because he is not registered  D) Exempt if he adds a disclaimer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **paid, subscribers-only** channel is not \"widely available to the public\"; he gives **advice on specific securities for consideration** and must register.\nA) The medium is electronic but access is restricted and priced. C) Being unregistered is the offence, not the defence. D) A disclaimer cannot convert paid advice into exempt commentary.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor begins charging clients a separate ₹20,000 annual \"advisory fee\" for asset allocation across equity, debt and gold. He:\nA) Remains exempt  B) Needs IA registration, because the advice is no longer incidental to distribution  C) Only needs AMFI approval  D) Must surrender his ARN\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The distributor exemption survives only while the advice is **incidental** to distribution and unpaid separately. A **separate advisory fee** across asset classes is advisory business.\nA) The exemption has been lost. C) AMFI cannot grant an advisory licence. D) Surrendering the ARN is not required — but he cannot advise and distribute to the same client.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A Mumbai-based adviser gives advice only to clients living in Dubai, of whom several are NRIs. He:\nA) Is fully exempt  B) Must register, because advice to NRIs and PIOs is within the regulations  C) Needs RBI approval only  D) Is exempt if he charges in dollars\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The \"exclusively outside India\" exemption **expressly excludes NRIs and PIOs**.\nA) The NRI clients defeat the exemption. C) RBI does not licence investment advisers. D) The currency of the fee is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A non-individual IA's group distribution arm wants to sell a regular-plan mutual fund to the wife of an existing advisory client. This is:\nA) Permitted — she is a different person  B) Not permitted — the family of a client is treated as one unit for segregation  C) Permitted with disclosure  D) Permitted if she signs a waiver\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Client-level segregation operates at **group level and treats the client's family as one unit**.\nA) Splitting a family is exactly the avoidance the rule blocks. C) and D) Disclosure or waiver cannot cure a prohibition.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** An IA advises a client to buy a stock on Monday and sells the same stock from her own account on Wednesday. She:\nA) Has done nothing wrong  B) Has breached Regulation 15(5) unless she gave the client a revised assessment at least 24 hours in advance  C) Is fine because she disclosed her holding  D) Is fine because two days had passed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An IA may not trade **contrary to advice for 15 days**; the only escape is a genuine change of view **communicated to the client at least 24 hours before** the trade.\nA) It is a clear conflict. C) Disclosure of a holding is a separate obligation and does not permit the contra trade. D) Two days is well inside the 15-day window.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An IA holds shares in a company she is recommending to clients. Under Regulation 18 she must:\nA) Sell them first  B) Disclose her holding or position in the products or securities that are the subject of the advice  C) Do nothing  D) Transfer them to a relative\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation 18 requires disclosure of the adviser's **own holding or position** in the securities advised upon.\nA) The regulation requires disclosure, not divestment. C) Silence would breach the disclosure obligation. D) Transferring to a relative conceals rather than cures the conflict.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A client with an AUA of ₹30 lakh wants the cheapest legally-permitted arrangement. Which mode allows the adviser to charge more, and is therefore the one the client should scrutinise?\nA) AUA mode  B) Fixed fee mode  C) Both identical  D) Neither has a cap\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹30 lakh, the AUA cap is 2.5% × 30,00,000 = **₹75,000**, while the fixed-fee cap is **₹1,25,000**. Below the ₹50 lakh break-even, the **fixed fee mode** permits the larger rupee amount.\nA) The AUA cap is lower here. C) They coincide only at ₹50 lakh of AUA. D) Both modes are capped.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** An adviser wants to switch a client from the fixed fee mode to the AUA mode eight months after onboarding. He:\nA) May do so immediately  B) Must wait until 12 months have elapsed from onboarding or the last change of mode  C) Needs SEBI approval  D) May do so if the client agrees in writing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fee mode may be changed **only after 12 months**.\nA) Eight months is short of the period. C) SEBI approval is not the mechanism. D) Client consent does not override the regulatory waiting period.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A retired schoolteacher's risk questionnaire shows high enthusiasm for equities, but her entire income is the interest on her ₹40 lakh corpus. The adviser should:\nA) Follow her stated preference and go 100% equity  B) Recognise that ability to bear loss is low and let the lower of willingness and ability govern  C) Refuse to advise her  D) Recommend derivatives to boost income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Risk profiling assesses what the client is **willing AND able** to take; the **lower governs**. Her ability to absorb loss is limited because the corpus funds her living expenses.\nA) Would breach Regulation 17 on suitability. C) The adviser should advise appropriately, not refuse. D) Derivatives increase, not reduce, the risk to her capital.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Which record is most often missing when SEBI finds an adviser unable to defend a recommendation?\nA) The client's PAN  B) The rationale for the investment advice, duly signed and dated  C) The office lease  D) The AMFI certificate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Without a contemporaneous **signed and dated rationale**, the adviser cannot show the advice was ever suitable, and the burden is on him.\nA) PAN is part of KYC and rarely missing. C) and D) are not records prescribed by Regulation 19.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An IA offers to execute the client's mutual fund purchases. This is permitted only if:\nA) He charges a small execution fee  B) He uses direct plans, takes no consideration at group or family level, and the client is under no obligation to use him  C) He obtains a Power of Attorney  D) He routes it through his distribution arm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation 22A permits implementation strictly through **direct plans**, with **no consideration of any kind** at group or family level and **no obligation** on the client.\nA) No implementation fee may be charged. C) A PoA would edge toward managing funds, which Regulation 15(12) forbids. D) Routing through distribution reintroduces the commission the rule removes.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A client complains to an IA on 3 May. The grievance must be redressed by:\nA) 3 June  B) 3 August  C) 31 December  D) There is no time limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Redressal is required **within one month** of receipt, i.e. by **3 June**.\nB) Would be three months. C) Has no basis. D) A time limit certainly exists, and it is a condition of registration.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An individual IA's client count reaches 168. He must:\nA) Do nothing  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual IA and stop onboarding fresh clients  C) Appoint a compliance officer  D) Increase his net worth to ₹50 lakh and continue as an individual\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exceeding **150 clients** triggers mandatory corporatisation, and fresh onboarding must stop until the new registration is obtained.\nA) The threshold has been crossed. C) That obligation attaches after he becomes a non-individual. D) Net worth alone does not change his registration category.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** A company's CFO knows the quarterly results will be far above expectations and tells his dependent brother, who buys the shares. Under PIT:\nA) Only the CFO is liable  B) Both are liable — the CFO for communicating UPSI and the brother as an insider who traded  C) Only the brother is liable  D) Neither, because the results were later announced\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The CFO breaches **Regulation 3** (communication of UPSI other than for a legitimate purpose); the dependent brother is an **immediate relative and deemed connected person** in possession of UPSI who traded, breaching **Regulation 4**.\nA) and C) each ignore one of the two distinct offences. D) Later publication does not cleanse a trade made while the information was unpublished.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A fund manager at an AMC learns of a takeover through a private due-diligence meeting. He has no relationship with the target company. He is:\nA) Not an insider  B) An insider, because possession of UPSI alone is sufficient  C) Only liable if he trades  D) Exempt as an intermediary employee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The definition of insider has an \"**or**\" limb: possession of or access to UPSI is enough.\nA) Misreads the definition. C) He is an insider immediately; trading is the further breach. D) An AMC employee is in fact a **deemed connected person**, so the exemption is imaginary.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A designated person obtains pre-clearance for a trade. He must execute it within:\nA) 2 trading days  B) 7 trading days  C) 15 trading days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Seven trading days** from pre-clearance.\nA) Is the disclosure deadline for a completed trade. C) and D) are not prescribed.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** A trading plan, once approved by the compliance officer, is:\nA) Revocable at will  B) Irrevocable and must be implemented without deviation  C) Valid for 3 months  D) Optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Irrevocability is what makes the plan a defence — the insider cannot time the market around it.\nA) Revocability would defeat the entire purpose. C) A plan must cover at least twelve months. D) Once adopted it binds the insider.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** A brokerage's research desk knows a large institutional client will place a ₹200 crore buy order tomorrow, and the proprietary desk buys today. This is:\nA) Efficient market-making  B) Front running — a deemed unfair trade practice under PFUTP  C) Permitted with disclosure  D) Legal if the order is later cancelled\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Dealing **ahead of a substantial impending client transaction** is expressly deemed unfair.\nA) Market-making provides two-way quotes; it does not exploit client order information. C) Disclosure does not legalise it. D) The offence is complete when the position is taken.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** An IA discovers a client depositing ₹9.8 lakh in cash on three consecutive days, each below the reporting threshold. The correct action is to:\nA) Ignore it, as each is below ₹10 lakh  B) File a Suspicious Transaction Report, and note that integrally connected cash transactions aggregating above ₹10 lakh in a month are also reportable  C) Ask the client to explain and take no further step  D) Tell the client an STR will be filed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Structuring to stay below the threshold is classic **placement**; the CTR rule expressly captures **integrally connected series** aggregating above ₹10 lakh in a month, and the suspicion itself triggers an **STR**.\nA) Is exactly the loophole the rule closes. C) Asking is fine, but reporting is mandatory. D) **Tipping off the client is prohibited.**\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An STR is required when a transaction is:\nA) Above ₹10 lakh only  B) Suspicious — with no monetary threshold, whether attempted or executed  C) In cash only  D) Cross-border only\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Suspicion alone triggers the report; **attempted** transactions count too.\nA) That is the CTR threshold. C) Suspicion can arise on any mode of payment. D) Cross-border wire transfers above ₹5 lakh have their own report.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A resident individual wants to invest in US-listed shares. Under FEMA she may remit up to:\nA) USD 25,000 per year  B) USD 2,50,000 per financial year  C) USD 1 million per financial year  D) There is no limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **Liberalised Remittance Scheme** limit is **USD 2,50,000 per financial year**.\nA) Was an early LRS limit, long superseded. C) Is the NRO repatriation limit, a different rule. D) A limit certainly applies.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Under FEMA, capital account transactions are:\nA) Freely permitted unless restricted  B) Prohibited unless specifically permitted  C) Always prohibited  D) Governed by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Capital account transactions are **prohibited unless permitted**; it is **current** account transactions that are free unless restricted.\nA) Reverses the rule. C) Many are permitted. D) FEMA is administered by the RBI, not SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A 16-year-old signs an advisory agreement in his own name. The agreement is:\nA) Valid  B) Void ab initio, because a minor is not competent to contract  C) Voidable at the adviser's option  D) Valid if witnessed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Section 11 of the Indian Contract Act; a minor's contract is **void from the beginning**.\nA) A minor lacks capacity. C) It is void, not merely voidable — and never at the adviser's option. D) Witnesses cannot supply capacity.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Consent obtained by misrepresentation makes a contract:\nA) Void  B) Voidable at the option of the aggrieved party  C) Illegal  D) Unenforceable by both parties\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Coercion, undue influence, fraud and misrepresentation make a contract **voidable**; only a **bilateral mistake of fact** makes it void.\nA) Reserved for mistake and unlawful objects. C) Misrepresentation is not a crime in itself. D) The aggrieved party may still choose to enforce it.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** A minor's mutual fund folio matures into adulthood when the investor turns 18. Until it is regularised:\nA) Transactions continue as before  B) The folio is frozen for transactions until fresh KYC, signature and bank details are provided  C) The guardian continues indefinitely  D) The folio is closed automatically\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — On attaining majority the guardian's authority ends and the folio is **frozen until regularised**.\nA) The guardian's mandate has lapsed. C) The guardian ceases on majority. D) The folio is not closed, only blocked pending regularisation.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A cheque dated 2 January is presented on 10 May of the same year. The bank will:\nA) Pay it  B) Return it as stale, since a cheque is valid for 3 months  C) Pay half  D) Refer it to the RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A cheque is valid for **three months**; 10 May is beyond 2 April.\nA) It is out of date. C) Partial payment is not a banking practice for stale cheques. D) The RBI has no role in individual cheques.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** For a Section 138 prosecution, the payee must send the demand notice within:\nA) 15 days of dishonour  B) 30 days of receiving information of dishonour  C) 3 months  D) 1 year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **30 days** to notice, then the drawer has **15 days** to pay, then **1 month** to file the complaint.\nA) 15 days is the drawer's time to pay, not the payee's time to notice. C) Three months is the cheque's validity. D) Has no basis.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** The adjudicating authority for the insolvency of a listed company is:\nA) SEBI  B) NCLT  C) DRT  D) The High Court\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **NCLT** for companies and LLPs; **DRT** for individuals and partnership firms.\nA) SEBI regulates securities, not insolvency. C) Applies to individuals and firms. D) The High Court hears certain appeals, not the CIRP itself.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A resolution plan under the IBC requires approval of:\nA) 51% of the CoC  B) 66% of the voting share of the Committee of Creditors  C) 75% of shareholders  D) The NCLT alone\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **66%** of the CoC voting share.\nA) 51% suffices only for certain routine matters. C) Shareholders do not vote on the resolution plan. D) The NCLT approves after the CoC has voted.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Under FATCA's Model 1 IGA, an Indian mutual fund reports account information to:\nA) The US IRS directly  B) The Indian tax authorities, which exchange it with the IRS  C) SEBI  D) The RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **Model 1**, reporting is domestic and the **CBDT** exchanges the data with the IRS.\nA) That is the Model 2 approach, not India's. C) and D) have no reporting role under FATCA.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** The Common Reporting Standard was developed by:\nA) The United States Congress  B) The OECD  C) SEBI  D) The United Nations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — CRS is the **OECD's** global standard for automatic exchange of information.\nA) That is FATCA. C) SEBI has no role in tax information exchange. D) The UN did not develop CRS.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A client refuses to give a FATCA/CRS self-certification. The likely consequence is that:\nA) Nothing happens  B) The account may be blocked for transactions  C) SEBI cancels the adviser's registration  D) The client is imprisoned\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Accounts without a valid self-certification may be **blocked for transactions**.\nA) There are real consequences. C) The default is the client's, not the adviser's registration issue. D) Refusal is not a custodial offence; a **false** certification attracts a monetary penalty.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A non-individual IA provides advisory services from the same desk that sells regular-plan mutual funds. This breaches:\nA) Nothing  B) Regulation 22, which requires arm's length separation through a separately identifiable department or division  C) The PMLA  D) FEMA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A non-individual IA must deliver advice through a **SIDD**, at **arm's length** from distribution.\nA) It is a clear breach. C) The PMLA governs money laundering controls. D) FEMA governs foreign exchange.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following would an annual compliance audit of an IA most likely flag?\nA) A profitable year  B) Advice given without a documented risk profile or suitability assessment  C) A high client retention rate  D) Use of a laptop\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The audit tests compliance with the regulations, and **Regulations 16, 17 and 19** are the usual failure points.\nA) and C) are commercial outcomes, not compliance matters. D) Is irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An adviser tells a prospective client \"our model delivers a guaranteed 24% a year\". This is:\nA) Permissible marketing  B) A breach of the Code of Conduct and potentially a fraudulent practice under PFUTP  C) Fine if a disclaimer follows  D) Allowed for HNI clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assured-return claims breach the **Code of Conduct** and the **advertisement norms**, and inducing a person to deal on a false statement is deemed **fraud** under PFUTP.\nA) It is not marketing but misrepresentation. C) A disclaimer does not cure a false promise. D) Client wealth creates no exemption.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A client's Assets under Advice are ₹1,60,00,000. What is the maximum annual fee under the AUA mode?\nA) ₹1,25,000  B) ₹4,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹1,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Maximum = **2.5% × ₹1,60,00,000 = ₹4,00,000** per annum for the family of the client.\nA) Is the fixed-fee cap, which the adviser is not using. C) Applies 5%, double the permitted rate. D) Applies 1%, which is a permissible charge but not the maximum.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** An adviser charges the full fixed fee of ₹1,25,000 per annum. What is the largest advance he may collect?\nA) ₹31,250  B) ₹62,500  C) ₹93,750  D) ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Advance fees may not exceed **two quarters**. Quarterly fee = 1,25,000 ÷ 4 = **₹31,250**; two quarters = 2 × 31,250 = **₹62,500**.\nA) Is one quarter — the maximum **breakage** fee, a different limit. C) Is three quarters. D) Is the whole year, well beyond the cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** At what level of AUA do the AUA-mode cap and the fixed-fee cap produce exactly the same rupee amount?\nA) ₹25 lakh  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹1 crore  D) ₹1.25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Set 2.5% × AUA = ₹1,25,000 → AUA = 1,25,000 ÷ 0.025 = **₹50,00,000**. Below ₹50 lakh the fixed fee permits more; above it the AUA mode does.\nA) 2.5% × 25 lakh = ₹62,500, only half the fixed cap. C) 2.5% × 1 crore = ₹2,50,000, double it. D) 2.5% × 1.25 crore = ₹3,12,500.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An investment adviser's compliance failure continues day after day. After how many days does the Section 15EB penalty reach its statutory ceiling?\nA) 30 days  B) 60 days  C) 100 days  D) 365 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The penalty runs at **₹1,00,000 per day** subject to a maximum of **₹1,00,00,000**. 1,00,00,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = **100 days**.\nA) 30 days would accumulate ₹30 lakh. B) 60 days would accumulate ₹60 lakh. D) Beyond 100 days the monetary penalty stops rising — though suspension and cancellation risk keeps growing.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** An insider makes a profit of ₹12 crore. What is the maximum monetary penalty under Section 15G?\nA) ₹10 lakh  B) ₹12 crore  C) ₹25 crore  D) ₹36 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The maximum is **₹25 crore or three times the profits, whichever is higher**. Three times ₹12 crore = **₹36 crore**, which exceeds ₹25 crore, so **₹36 crore** applies.\nA) Is the statutory minimum. B) Is the profit itself, not the multiple. C) Is the fixed limb, which loses to the higher 3× limb here.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A person makes ₹3 crore from a fraudulent scheme. What is the maximum penalty under Section 15HA?\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹3 crore  C) ₹9 crore  D) ₹25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The maximum is **₹25 crore or three times the profits, whichever is higher**. Three times ₹3 crore = ₹9 crore, which is **less** than ₹25 crore, so the **₹25 crore** limb governs.\nA) Is the minimum penalty. B) Is the profit. C) Is the 3× figure, which loses to the higher fixed limb.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A client has ₹90,00,000 of assets, of which ₹30,00,000 is held under a pre-existing distribution arrangement with another entity. What is the maximum AUA-mode fee?\nA) ₹2,25,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹75,000  D) ₹1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Assets held under a **pre-existing distribution arrangement must be deducted**. Chargeable AUA = 90,00,000 − 30,00,000 = **₹60,00,000**; fee = 2.5% × 60,00,000 = **₹1,50,000**.\nA) Charges 2.5% on the full ₹90 lakh without the deduction. C) Charges 2.5% on the ₹30 lakh distribution portion only. D) Is the fixed-fee cap, a different mode.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A family comprises the client (AUA ₹35 lakh), his dependent spouse (₹15 lakh) and his dependent child (₹10 lakh). What is the maximum total AUA-mode fee the adviser may charge?\nA) ₹87,500 per person  B) ₹1,50,000 for the family  C) ₹3,75,000  D) ₹1,25,000 per person\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The cap is **per family of client**, not per person. Family AUA = 35 + 15 + 10 = **₹60 lakh**; fee = 2.5% × 60,00,000 = **₹1,50,000** for the family as a whole.\nA) Charges each member separately, which the family rule prohibits. C) Applies a rate far above 2.5%. D) Multiplies the fixed-fee cap across members — precisely the avoidance the rule blocks.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** An adviser on a ₹1,20,000 annual fixed fee collects two quarters in advance and the client terminates after one month. What must be refunded?\nA) ₹60,000  B) ₹50,000  C) ₹20,000  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Monthly fee = 1,20,000 ÷ 12 = **₹10,000**; quarterly = **₹30,000**. Advance collected = 2 quarters = **₹60,000** covering six months. Fee earned for one month = ₹10,000, so **₹50,000 is unexpired**. The adviser may retain a **breakage fee of at most one quarter = ₹30,000**. Refund = 50,000 − 30,000 = **₹20,000**.\nA) Ignores both the earned fee and the permitted breakage. B) Ignores the breakage retention. D) A refund is mandatory for the unexpired period beyond the breakage cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A designated person buys shares worth ₹4,00,000, ₹3,50,000 and ₹3,00,000 in one calendar quarter. Under PIT he must:\nA) Do nothing — each trade is below ₹10 lakh  B) Disclose to the company within 2 trading days, since the series aggregates to ₹10.5 lakh  C) Disclose only at year end  D) Disclose only the largest trade\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The threshold applies to a **series of transactions over a calendar quarter**: 4,00,000 + 3,50,000 + 3,00,000 = **₹10,50,000**, which exceeds **₹10 lakh**, so disclosure to the company is due within **2 trading days**.\nA) Ignores the aggregation rule. C) The deadline is 2 trading days, not annual. D) The obligation covers the whole series.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A client deposits ₹4,00,000, ₹3,50,000 and ₹3,20,000 in cash within one calendar month. The reporting entity must:\nA) Do nothing  B) File a CTR by the 15th of the following month, as the integrally connected series exceeds ₹10 lakh  C) File only if the client is an NRI  D) Report to SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total = 4,00,000 + 3,50,000 + 3,20,000 = **₹10,70,000**, above **₹10 lakh**. Integrally connected cash transactions within a month aggregating above the threshold are reportable in a **CTR by the 15th of the succeeding month** — and the structuring pattern also warrants an **STR**.\nA) Structuring is exactly what the aggregation rule captures. C) Residential status is irrelevant. D) These reports go to **FIU-IND**, not SEBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A FEMA contravention involves a quantifiable amount of ₹8,00,000. The maximum penalty is:\nA) ₹2,00,000  B) ₹8,00,000  C) ₹24,00,000  D) ₹80,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Up to **three times the sum involved** where quantifiable: 3 × ₹8,00,000 = **₹24,00,000**.\nA) ₹2 lakh is the cap where the amount is **not** quantifiable. B) Is the amount itself, not the multiple. D) Applies a 10× multiple that does not exist.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A payee learns on 5 June that a cheque has been dishonoured. Notice is sent on 30 June and the drawer does not pay. By when must the complaint be filed?\nA) 30 July  B) 15 August  C) 30 August  D) 5 September\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Notice was validly sent within **30 days** of 5 June. The drawer then has **15 days** from the notice of 30 June to pay, expiring **15 July**; the cause of action arises then and the complaint must be filed **within one month**, i.e. by **15 August**.\nA) Falls before the drawer's 15 days have even expired. C) Adds a fortnight beyond the one-month window. D) Is roughly three months after dishonour, far beyond the limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A company in liquidation realises ₹120 crore. Costs are ₹10 crore; workmen's dues (24 months) ₹15 crore; secured creditors who relinquished security ₹60 crore; other employees ₹8 crore; unsecured financial creditors ₹40 crore. What do unsecured financial creditors receive?\nA) ₹40 crore  B) ₹27 crore  C) Nothing  D) ₹35 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Apply Section 53. Costs **₹10 crore** leave ₹110 crore. Workmen ₹15 crore **plus** relinquishing secured creditors ₹60 crore = **₹75 crore**, leaving ₹35 crore. Other employees **₹8 crore**, leaving **₹27 crore**. Unsecured financial creditors claim ₹40 crore but only **₹27 crore** remains — a recovery of 67.5%. **Equity shareholders receive nothing.**\nA) Assumes full recovery, which the waterfall does not permit. C) They rank above government dues and shareholders and do receive something. D) Omits the ₹8 crore employee layer.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** A CIRP begins on 1 April. Ignoring litigation, what is the last date by which it must be completed if the maximum extension is granted?\nA) 180 days later  B) 270 days later  C) 330 days later  D) 365 days later\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **180 days**, extendable **once by 90 days** = **270 days**.\nA) Is the base period without any extension. C) Is the outer limit **including** litigation time, which the question excludes. D) There is no 365-day limit in the Code.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A non-individual IA reports paid-up capital ₹30 lakh, free reserves ₹28 lakh, accumulated losses ₹5 lakh and deferred expenditure not written off ₹2 lakh. Is it compliant?\nA) No — net worth is ₹49 lakh  B) Yes — net worth is ₹51 lakh, above the ₹50 lakh requirement  C) Yes — capital alone suffices  D) No — non-individuals need ₹5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net worth = 30 + 28 − 5 − 2 = **₹51 lakh**, which exceeds the **₹50 lakh** requirement by ₹1 lakh.\nA) Miscounts one of the deductions. C) Paid-up capital of ₹30 lakh alone would fall short. D) ₹5 crore is the **portfolio manager's** requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An insider publicly discloses an approved trading plan on 1 March. Under the classical rule, the earliest date trading under it may commence is:\nA) 1 April  B) 1 June  C) 1 September  D) 1 March the following year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Trading may not commence **earlier than six months** from public disclosure: 1 March + 6 months = **1 September**.\nA) Is one month later. B) Is three months later. D) Is twelve months, which is the **minimum coverage period** of the plan, not the cool-off.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A quarter ends on 30 June and results are declared at 4:00 pm on 25 July. The trading window for designated persons reopens at:\nA) 4:00 pm on 25 July  B) 4:00 pm on 26 July  C) 4:00 pm on 27 July  D) 1 August\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The window is closed from quarter-end until **48 hours after** the results are declared: 25 July 4:00 pm + 48 hours = **27 July 4:00 pm**.\nA) Is the moment of declaration, with no cooling period. B) Is only 24 hours later. D) Adds an arbitrary extra week.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Four adult resident members of one family each wish to remit funds abroad in the same financial year. Their combined LRS headroom is:\nA) USD 2,50,000  B) USD 5,00,000  C) USD 10,00,000  D) Unlimited\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The LRS limit is **per individual per financial year**: 4 × USD 2,50,000 = **USD 10,00,000**.\nA) Is one person's limit. B) Would cover only two people. D) The scheme is capped, not open-ended.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An NRI wishes to repatriate funds from an NRO account. The annual ceiling is:\nA) USD 2,50,000  B) USD 1 million per financial year  C) USD 5 million  D) There is no ceiling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **USD 1 million per financial year** from an NRO account. NRE and FCNR(B) balances are **fully repatriable**.\nA) Is the LRS limit for **residents**, a different scheme. C) Has no basis. D) A ceiling applies to NRO specifically.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An IA fails to conduct the mandatory annual compliance audit and the default continues for 140 days. The maximum penalty under Section 15EB is:\nA) ₹1,40,00,000  B) ₹1,00,00,000  C) ₹1,40,000  D) ₹25,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At ₹1 lakh per day the arithmetic gives 140 × 1,00,000 = ₹1.4 crore, but the section imposes a hard **maximum of ₹1 crore**, so the penalty is capped at **₹1,00,00,000**.\nA) Ignores the statutory ceiling. C) Is 140 days at ₹1,000 a day. D) Is the ceiling under Sections 15G and 15HA, not 15EB.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An adviser with a client whose AUA is ₹2,00,00,000 charges ₹3,00,000 a year. This is:\nA) A breach, because the fixed-fee cap is ₹1,25,000  B) Permitted under the AUA mode, where the cap is ₹5,00,000  C) A breach, because 1.5% is too high  D) Permitted only with SEBI approval\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under the AUA mode the cap is 2.5% × ₹2,00,00,000 = **₹5,00,000**. A fee of ₹3,00,000 is **1.5% of AUA**, well within it.\nA) Applies the wrong mode's cap. C) There is no separate percentage limit below 2.5%, though the Code still requires fees to be fair and reasonable. D) No approval is required to charge within the cap.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A non-individual IA's group sells regular-plan funds. An advisory client asks the group to also execute his purchases through its distribution arm. The IA must:\nA) Agree, and disclose the commission  B) Decline — the same client cannot take both advisory and distribution services from the group  C) Agree if the client signs a consent  D) Agree but rebate the commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Client-level segregation at group level** means a client, and the client's family, may take **either** advisory **or** distribution services from the group, never both.\nA) Disclosure does not cure a prohibition. C) Client consent cannot override Regulation 22. D) A rebate does not restore segregation, and Regulation 15(2) bars third-party consideration in any event.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A 58-year-old client with a ₹60 lakh corpus, no pension and dependent parents scores \"aggressive\" on a questionnaire because he ticked \"I enjoy volatility\". The adviser should:\nA) Allocate 100% to mid-cap equity  B) Recognise that the tool may be over-weighting one answer and reassess capacity to absorb loss  C) Ignore the questionnaire entirely  D) Recommend futures for income\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation 16 requires the adviser **not to attribute inappropriate weight to particular answers** and to assess **capacity for absorbing loss**; tools must be **fit for purpose** with limitations mitigated.\nA) Would breach Regulation 17 on suitability. C) The questionnaire is part of a documented process and must not simply be discarded. D) Futures raise, not lower, the risk to a corpus with no other income.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A client wants a structured product with an embedded option and a knock-in barrier. Regulation 17 requires the adviser to:\nA) Sell it if the client asks  B) Assess that its structure and risk-reward profile match the client's experience, knowledge, objectives, risk appetite and capacity for loss  C) Refuse all complex products  D) Rely on the issuer's brochure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — For a **complex financial product** the recommendation must rest on a specific reasonable assessment of fit across all those dimensions.\nA) Client demand does not displace the suitability duty. C) Complex products are not banned, only conditioned. D) Regulation 17 requires the **adviser** to understand the nature and risks, not to outsource that to the issuer.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** For the financial year ending 31 March 2026, an IA's compliance audit must be completed by, and adverse findings reported by:\nA) 30 June 2026 and 31 July 2026  B) 30 September 2026 and 31 October 2026  C) 31 March 2027 and 30 April 2027  D) There is no deadline\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The audit must be **completed within six months of the financial year end (30 September 2026)** and adverse findings with the action taken submitted **within one month, and in any case by 31 October 2026**.\nA) Is three months, not six. C) Is a full year later. D) Both deadlines are prescribed.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Two promoters, both aware of the same unpublished merger discussion, transfer shares between themselves off-market at a negotiated price. Under PIT this is:\nA) Always insider trading  B) A recognised defence — an off-market inter-se transfer between insiders possessing the same UPSI  C) Permitted only with SEBI approval  D) Permitted only after the merger is announced\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **off-market inter-se transfer between insiders in possession of the same UPSI**, both making a conscious and informed decision, is one of the listed defences — with the trade reported to the company within **2 working days**.\nA) Ignores the express proviso to Regulation 4(1). C) No approval mechanism exists. D) The defence exists precisely because the information is still unpublished.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** An adviser charges a client ₹2,00,000 in the fixed fee mode and also receives ₹40,000 of trail commission from an AMC on the funds advised. He has breached:\nA) Only the fee cap  B) The fixed-fee cap of ₹1,25,000 and Regulation 15(2), which bars consideration from anyone other than the client  C) Nothing, since the client agreed  D) Only Regulation 18\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two separate breaches: the fixed-fee cap is **₹1,25,000** (exceeded by ₹75,000), and taking **₹40,000 from the AMC** breaches the bar on consideration from anyone other than the client.\nA) Understates the position by ignoring the commission. C) Client consent cannot authorise either breach. D) Regulation 18 concerns disclosure, which is not the primary breach here.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An individual IA asks his wife, an AMFI-registered distributor, to execute his advisory clients' mutual fund purchases in regular plans. This is:\nA) Permitted, since she is a separate person  B) Prohibited — the family of an individual IA may not provide distribution or execution services to the IA's clients  C) Permitted with disclosure  D) Permitted if the commission is rebated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The bar on distribution extends to the **family of the individual investment adviser**, and Regulation 22A bars consideration at **family level** for implementation.\nA) The family test exists precisely to defeat this structure. C) Disclosure does not cure a prohibition. D) A rebate does not remove the prohibited relationship, and regular plans defeat the direct-plan requirement.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises the regulatory philosophy applying to an investment adviser?\nA) The adviser is judged on whether the market moved in the client's favour  B) The adviser is judged on being honest, unconflicted, suitable and documented — a fiduciary paid only by the client  C) The adviser is judged on assets gathered  D) The adviser is judged on the number of products sold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulation 15(1) imposes a **fiduciary** duty, Regulation 15(2) makes the **client the only payer**, Regulations 16 and 17 require **profiling and suitability**, and Regulation 19 requires the whole chain to be **recorded**.\nA) Outcomes are not the test; process is. C) and D) describe distribution metrics — exactly the incentives Regulation 22's segregation is designed to remove from advice.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Track the fee caps and the family definition carefully.\n\n### Case: Anjali Deshmukh, Registered Investment Adviser\n\n**Anjali Deshmukh** is an **individual** SEBI-registered investment adviser in Pune. Her position on **1 April 2026**:\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Registration | Individual RIA, BASL member |\n| Net tangible assets | **₹6,20,000** |\n| Number of clients | **142** |\n| Fee mode used for the Kulkarni family | **Assets under Advice**, adopted on 1 April 2026 |\n\nHer client **Mr Suresh Kulkarni** and his family hold:\n\n| Family member | Portfolio |\n|---|---|\n| Mr Kulkarni | ₹52,00,000 |\n| Dependent spouse | ₹18,00,000 |\n| Dependent son (minor) | ₹6,00,000 |\n| Of the above, held under a pre-existing distribution arrangement with another firm | ₹16,00,000 |\n\nAnjali's spouse runs a mutual fund distribution business and has offered to execute the Kulkarnis' purchases in regular plans.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** Is Anjali's net worth position compliant, and what is the requirement?\nA) No — an individual IA needs ₹50 lakh  B) Yes — an individual IA needs net tangible assets of ₹5 lakh and she has ₹6.20 lakh  C) No — she needs ₹5 crore  D) Yes — there is no net worth requirement for individuals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **individual** IA must hold **net tangible assets of at least ₹5,00,000** at all times. Anjali holds **₹6,20,000**, a surplus of **₹1,20,000**.\nA) ₹50 lakh is the **non-individual** requirement. C) ₹5 crore is the **portfolio manager's** net worth. D) A requirement certainly exists, and it must be met **at all times**, not just at registration.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** What is the maximum annual fee Anjali may charge the Kulkarni family under the AUA mode?\nA) ₹1,90,000  B) ₹1,50,000  C) ₹1,25,000  D) ₹4,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Total family portfolio = 52,00,000 + 18,00,000 + 6,00,000 = **₹76,00,000**. Deduct the **₹16,00,000 held under a pre-existing distribution arrangement** → chargeable AUA = **₹60,00,000**. Maximum fee = **2.5% × 60,00,000 = ₹1,50,000** for the **family as a whole**.\nA) Charges 2.5% on the full ₹76 lakh without the mandatory deduction. C) Is the fixed-fee cap, which is not the mode in use. D) Applies a rate far above 2.5%.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** In November 2026 Anjali finds the fixed fee mode simpler and wants to switch the Kulkarnis to it. She may:\nA) Switch immediately  B) Switch only on or after 1 April 2027, since a mode change requires 12 months to elapse  C) Switch with SEBI approval  D) Switch if the client requests it in writing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fee mode may be changed **only after 12 months** from onboarding or the last change of mode. The AUA mode was adopted on **1 April 2026**, so the earliest permissible change is **1 April 2027**.\nA) November 2026 is only seven months in. C) SEBI approval is not the mechanism prescribed. D) Client consent does not shorten the regulatory waiting period.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** May Anjali's spouse execute the Kulkarnis' purchases in regular plans?\nA) Yes, with disclosure  B) No — the family of an individual IA may not provide distribution services to the IA's clients, and no consideration may accrue at family level  C) Yes, if the commission is donated  D) Yes, because the spouse is separately registered with AMFI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — An **individual IA may not provide distribution services at all**, and the bar extends to the **family of the individual adviser** in respect of the adviser's clients. Regulation 22A separately requires that **no consideration accrue at group or family level** and that implementation be through **direct plans**.\nA) Disclosure cannot cure a prohibition. C) Donating the commission does not remove the prohibited relationship. D) An AMFI registration permits distribution generally, not distribution to the adviser's own advisory clients.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Anjali's client count reaches 158 in January 2027, and she keeps onboarding new clients while charging ₹1,80,000 to one family under the AUA mode where the chargeable AUA is ₹50 lakh. What is her exposure?\nA) None  B) Two breaches — failing to corporatise above 150 clients while continuing to onboard, and exceeding the 2.5% cap by ₹55,000 — exposing her to Section 15EB penalties of up to ₹1 crore and action under the Intermediaries Regulations  C) Only a warning is possible  D) Only the fee must be refunded\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Breach 1:** crossing **150 clients** obliges an individual IA to apply for **non-individual registration** and to **stop onboarding fresh clients** until it is granted; she did neither. **Breach 2:** on a chargeable AUA of ₹50,00,000 the cap is **2.5% × 50,00,000 = ₹1,25,000**, so charging **₹1,80,000** exceeds it by **₹55,000**.\n\n**Exposure:** a penalty under **Section 15EB** of **not less than ₹1 lakh**, running at **₹1 lakh per day** while the default continues, subject to a **maximum of ₹1 crore**; plus proceedings under **Chapter V of the SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008** leading to a warning, **suspension** or **cancellation** of registration, and a direction to **refund the excess fee**.\n\nA) Both obligations are mandatory and both were breached. C) A warning is one possible outcome among several, not the ceiling. D) Refund of the excess is likely, but it does not exhaust the consequences — the client-count breach is separate and continuing.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "19", "title": "Ethical Issues", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "# Chapter 19: Ethical Issues — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 6 · Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics (30 marks).**\n> The most *conceptual* chapter in the syllabus — almost no arithmetic, but a very high density of **named issues** (mis-selling, churning, front-running), **named duties** (loyalty, care) and **named regimes** (SEBI Reg 15(1), the US Advisers Act 1940, Australia's FOFA). The exam tests whether you can put the **right label** on a described behaviour. Learn the labels and the chapter is largely done.\n\n---\n\n## 19.1 Ethics and Ethical Restraint\n\n**Ethics** is the set of **moral principles that distinguish right conduct from wrong conduct**. Applied to a profession, it is the standard of behaviour a practitioner is expected to follow **even when no rule compels it and no one is watching**.\n\n**Ethical restraint** is the other half of the idea, and it is the half candidates forget. It is the **voluntary, self-imposed decision to hold back from an action that is available, profitable and possibly even legal, because it would harm the client, the market or the profession.**\n\nRestraint matters because the tempting acts in finance are rarely blocked by a physical barrier. Nothing physically stops an adviser from recommending the product that pays him most. Only restraint does.\n\n### Law vs ethics — the single most tested distinction\n\n| | **Law / Regulation** | **Ethics** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nature** | The **minimum** acceptable standard | The **higher**, aspirational standard |\n| **Source** | External — Parliament, SEBI, courts | Internal — conscience, professional norms |\n| **Enforcement** | Penalties, suspension, prosecution | Self-restraint, peer pressure, reputation |\n| **Scope** | Only what has been written down | Fills the **gaps** where rules are silent |\n| **Timing** | Reactive — rules follow scandals | Proactive — anticipates the harm |\n| **Test** | \"Is it **permitted**?\" | \"Is it **right**?\" |\n\n> 🧠 **Ethics begins where the law ends.** Every regulation in the SEBI rulebook exists because somebody once did something legal-but-wrong, and then it had to be banned. An adviser who only asks *\"is this allowed?\"* is permanently one scandal behind. An adviser who asks *\"is this right for my client?\"* is permanently one step ahead.\n\n**Something can be perfectly legal and still deeply unethical.** Selling a 63-year-old a 15-year lock-in product is not illegal. It is indefensible.\n\n---\n\n## 19.2 Why Ethical Conduct Matters — Especially in Advice\n\nEvery business benefits from ethics. Financial advice **depends** on it, for three structural reasons.\n\n### (a) Advice is a \"credence good\" — the client cannot judge it\n\nYou can tell a bad haircut in five minutes and a bad car in five weeks. You may not be able to tell bad financial advice for **fifteen years** — by which time the retirement it was meant to fund has already happened. The client therefore cannot police quality. Only the adviser's ethics can.\n\n### (b) Information asymmetry\n\nThe adviser knows the product's cost structure, its commission, its liquidity, its true risk. The client typically knows the brochure. This gap is not a temporary inconvenience — it is the **reason the client hired the adviser in the first place**. An asymmetry that large can be used *for* the client or *against* him.\n\n| Concept | Meaning in an advisory setting |\n|---|---|\n| **Information asymmetry** | The adviser knows much more than the client about cost, risk and suitability |\n| **Adverse selection** | Poor-quality products get pushed hardest because they pay the highest commission |\n| **Moral hazard** | The adviser bears none of the loss from bad advice, but keeps the commission |\n| **Agency problem** | The **agent** (adviser) may act in his own interest rather than the **principal's** (client's) |\n\n### (c) The agency (principal–agent) problem\n\nThe client is the **principal**; the adviser is the **agent** engaged to act on the principal's behalf. The problem is that the agent has his own interests — commission, targets, business volume, his employer's product shelf. Wherever the agent's payoff differs from the principal's payoff, there is a **conflict of interest**, and the conflict resolves itself in the agent's favour unless something prevents it. That \"something\" is regulation plus ethics.\n\n### The business case — ethics is not charity\n\n| Benefit of ethical conduct | Why it pays |\n|---|---|\n| **Trust** | Advice is bought on trust; it is the only real product |\n| **Client retention** | Advisory economics depend on multi-year relationships, not one-off sales |\n| **Referrals** | The cheapest client acquisition channel in the business, and it runs entirely on reputation |\n| **Lower cost** | Fewer complaints, fewer SEBI inspections escalating, less litigation, lower staff turnover |\n| **Licence protection** | Registration can be suspended or cancelled; the firm's existence is at stake |\n| **Market confidence** | Retail participation depends on the belief that the system is not rigged |\n\n**The cost of the opposite:** monetary penalty under the SEBI Act, suspension or cancellation of registration, disgorgement of unlawful gains, debarment from the securities market, criminal liability in fraud cases — and, above all, a **reputation that cannot be repurchased at any price**.\n\n> 🧠 **The trust asymmetry.** Trust is built in drops and lost in buckets. A ten-year relationship worth ₹1.5 lakh a year in fees can be destroyed by a single ₹40,000 commission taken quietly. Unethical conduct in advice is almost always a **bad trade even in pure money terms** — it swaps a long annuity for a short lump sum.\n\n---\n\n## 19.3 ⭐ Ethical Issues in Providing Financial Advice\n\nThis is the heart of the chapter. Learn each label and its one-line definition.\n\n| Issue | What it is | The give-away sign |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mis-selling** | Recommending a product that does **not suit** the client's needs, risk profile or horizon — usually because it pays the adviser more | The product is right for the *seller*, not the buyer |\n| **Churning** | **Excessive trading, switching or turnover** in a client's portfolio to generate brokerage or fresh commission | Lots of activity, no change in the underlying strategy |\n| **Front-running** | Dealing in a security **ahead of a client's known impending order** to profit from the price move that order will cause | The adviser's own trade sits just before the client's |\n| **Insider trading** | Trading on **unpublished price-sensitive information (UPSI)** about a company | The information came from inside the *company*, not from an order book |\n| **Conflict of interest** | The adviser's interest (commission, in-house product, related party, personal holding) diverges from the client's | Ask: \"who else pays this adviser?\" |\n| **Unsuitable recommendation** | Advice given without proper **risk profiling**, or contrary to the profile on record | No documented basis for the recommendation |\n| **Misrepresenting returns** | Cherry-picked periods, gross-of-cost figures, absolute returns dressed as annual, past performance implied as assured | A number without a period, a basis or a caveat |\n| **Hidden costs** | Commission, upfront loads, exit loads, expense ratios, mortality charges not disclosed | The client cannot tell you what he is paying |\n| **Assured-return claims** | Promising or implying a guaranteed return on a market-linked product | The word \"guaranteed\" attached to a market product |\n| **Breach of confidentiality** | Using or revealing client information without consent | Client data used for anything other than serving that client |\n| **Unauthorised transactions** | Transacting in a client's account without a mandate | Trades the client did not approve |\n| **Failure to disclose** | Not revealing commissions, related-party interest, disciplinary history or complaints | Silence where disclosure is required |\n| **Rebating / inducements** | Passing back commission or offering gifts to win business | The client is bought, not advised |\n| **Advice without diligence** | Recommending a product the adviser has not researched or does not understand | \"Everyone is buying it\" |\n\n### Worked example — what churning actually costs\n\nMrs Iyer's ₹25,00,000 equity portfolio. Her broker-adviser turns it over **8 times** in a year.\n\n- Value traded = 8 × ₹25,00,000 = **₹2,00,00,000**\n- Round-trip cost (brokerage, STT, exchange charges, GST, impact) ≈ **0.50%**\n- Total cost = 0.50% × ₹2,00,00,000 = **₹1,00,000**\n- As a share of her portfolio = 1,00,000 ÷ 25,00,000 = **4.0% per year**\n\nA disciplined portfolio with 0.5× turnover would have cost 0.50% × ₹12,50,000 = **₹6,250**, i.e. **0.25%**. The adviser has taken **3.75 percentage points a year** out of her return **and produced no strategy change at all**.\n\n### Worked example — what hidden cost does over time\n\nMr Rao invests **₹10,00,000**. Gross return 12% p.a. for 15 years.\n\n| | Regular plan (TER 2.00%) | Direct plan (TER 1.10%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Net return | **10.00%** | **10.90%** |\n| Value after 15 years | 10,00,000 × 1.10¹⁵ = **₹41,77,000** | 10,00,000 × 1.109¹⁵ = **₹47,21,000** |\n\n**Difference = ₹5,44,000** — more than **half the original investment**, created by a cost difference of **0.90% a year** that never appears as a line item on any statement. This is why non-disclosure of cost is treated as a serious ethical failure, not a technicality.\n\n### Worked example — misrepresenting returns\n\nA scheme's NAV rose from **₹10 to ₹28 over 9 years**.\n\n- The brochure headline: **\"180% return!\"** — arithmetically true (18 ÷ 10).\n- The honest number: CAGR = (28 ÷ 10)^(1/9) − 1 = 2.8^0.1111 − 1 = **12.1% per annum**.\n\nBoth describe the same fund. One is designed to be understood; the other is designed to impress. **Absolute return quoted without the period is the commonest form of return misrepresentation in India.**\n\nOther variants to recognise:\n- **Cherry-picked window** — quoting the best 3-year stretch out of ten years of data\n- **Gross of expenses** — showing returns before TER, exit load and tax\n- **Survivorship bias** — showing only the schemes that survived\n- **Back-tested \"model portfolio\" returns** presented as if actually earned\n- **Point-to-point instead of rolling returns** — a single lucky start date\n\n> ⚠️ **Front-running vs insider trading.** Both are prohibited, both involve trading on information others do not have, and the exam loves to swap them.\n> **Front-running** uses knowledge of an **impending order** (a client's or an employer's). It is dealt with under the **SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003**.\n> **Insider trading** uses **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company itself**. It is dealt with under the **SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015**.\n> Memory hook: **front-run an ORDER, insider-trade on NEWS.**\n\n> ⚠️ **Mis-selling vs churning.** **Mis-selling is about the WRONG product** — one transaction can be mis-selling. **Churning is about TOO MANY transactions** — the individual product may be perfectly fine; it is the *frequency* that is the offence.\n\n---\n\n## 19.4 Ethical Dilemmas — and How to Resolve Them\n\nAn **ethical dilemma** is **not** a choice between right and wrong. That is merely a temptation, and it has an obvious answer. A dilemma is a situation in which:\n\n- two **legitimate duties conflict** (loyalty to the client vs loyalty to the employer), **or**\n- **every available option carries some ethical cost**, **or**\n- the right course is clear but **expensive** — it costs the fee, the client or the job.\n\n**Typical dilemmas in an advisory practice**\n\n| Situation | The conflict |\n|---|---|\n| Client insists on a portfolio far riskier than his profile | **Client autonomy** vs **duty of care** |\n| The in-house product pays the firm; a cheaper external one is better | **Employer's interest** vs **client's interest** |\n| A client casually reveals price-sensitive news about his employer | **Confidentiality** vs **the duty of every other client** |\n| Last year's recommendation was wrong; the client has not noticed | **Self-protection** vs **honesty** |\n| A large client asks for the fee bill to be raised in another name | **Business retention** vs **the law** |\n| A colleague is mis-selling; reporting him will end his career | **Loyalty to a peer** vs **duty to clients** |\n\n### ⭐ A six-step framework for resolving a dilemma\n\n1. **Get the facts.** Most \"dilemmas\" dissolve once the facts are complete. Never decide on assumptions.\n2. **Identify the stakeholders.** Client, other clients, the firm, the regulator, the market, yourself — and note **whose interest must rank first** (the client's).\n3. **Name the ethical issue and the conflicting duties.** Put a label on it — conflict of interest, confidentiality, suitability.\n4. **Generate alternatives.** There are almost never only two options. \"Disclose and proceed\", \"restructure the arrangement\", \"decline the mandate\" and \"escalate internally\" are all options.\n5. **Test each alternative** (see the four tests below) and check it against the **law, the SEBI Code of Conduct and the firm's policy**. Consult the **compliance officer** — that is what the role exists for.\n6. **Decide, act, DOCUMENT, and review.** Record the reasoning contemporaneously. Undocumented good conduct is indistinguishable from misconduct two years later.\n\n### ⭐ The four quick tests\n\n| Test | The question you ask yourself |\n|---|---|\n| **The legality test** | Does this breach any law, regulation or the Code of Conduct? *(A \"no\" is necessary but never sufficient.)* |\n| **The publicity / sunlight test** | Would I be comfortable if this appeared **on tomorrow's front page** with my name on it? |\n| **The mirror / family test** | Would I recommend exactly this to **my own parent** with the same money? |\n| **The role-model test** | Would a **respected senior professional** in my field do this — and would I be happy to explain it to a new joinee as \"how we work here\"? |\n\n> 🧠 **The one-line default.** When a dilemma is genuinely balanced, apply the tie-breaker built into the regulations: **the client's interest comes first, and whatever you cannot avoid, you disclose.** \"Avoid, manage, or disclose\" — in that order of preference.\n\n**Worked dilemma.** Mr Shah, 63, retiring in 18 months, profiled as *conservative*, insists on putting 90% of his ₹80 lakh corpus into small-cap funds after a friend's tip.\n*Wrong responses:* silently comply (breaches duty of care); or simply refuse and end the conversation (abandons the client).\n*Right response:* explain the specific risk in rupee terms — \"a 40% drawdown is **₹28.8 lakh** off a ₹72 lakh allocation, and you have 18 months, not 18 years, to recover it\"; offer a compromise consistent with the profile; if he still insists, **record the advice given, record his written instruction that it is contrary to advice, and reassess whether the mandate can be continued at all.** Never let the file show only what he wanted and not what you advised.\n\n---\n\n## 19.5 ⭐ Fiduciary Responsibility of Investment Advisers\n\n**The provision to memorise: SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013, Regulation 15(1)** —\n\n> *\"An investment adviser shall act in a fiduciary capacity towards its clients and shall disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise.\"*\n\nA **fiduciary** is someone entrusted with another's affairs who must place that person's interest **above his own**. It is the **highest duty the law recognises in a commercial relationship** — higher than the duty of an ordinary seller, higher than the duty of a distributor.\n\n### The two limbs of fiduciary duty\n\n| Limb | What it demands | In practice |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Duty of LOYALTY** | Put the client first; do not profit at his expense | Avoid conflicts; where unavoidable, **disclose fully and obtain informed consent**; no undisclosed commission; no front-running; no misuse of client information |\n| **Duty of CARE** | Give competent, diligent, suitable advice | Know the client; **risk-profile** him; research the product; recommend what fits; **monitor and review** the advice over time |\n\n> 🧠 **Two words, one duty: LOYALTY and CARE.** Loyalty answers *\"whose side are you on?\"*. Care answers *\"did you do the work?\"*. Every fiduciary breach in the syllabus is a failure of one or the other.\n\n### Fiduciary standard vs suitability standard — a classic exam comparison\n\n| | **Fiduciary standard** (Investment Adviser) | **Suitability standard** (Distributor / seller) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The test** | Is this the **best** available option for the client? | Is this **not unsuitable** for the client? |\n| **Whose interest ranks first** | The **client's**, above the adviser's | The seller's own interest may legitimately co-exist |\n| **Conflicts** | Must be **avoided or fully disclosed** | Disclosure requirements are lighter |\n| **Duration** | **Continuing** — advice must be monitored | Largely **point-of-sale** |\n| **Remuneration** | **Fee from the client**; advice segregated from distribution | **Commission from the manufacturer** |\n| **Legal basis in India** | **Reg 15(1)**, IA Regulations 2013 | Distributor codes and product-level rules |\n\n### The Code of Conduct for Investment Advisers (Third Schedule, IA Regulations 2013)\n\nNine heads — the exam quotes them almost verbatim:\n\n| # | Head | Substance |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Honesty and fairness** | Act honestly, fairly and in the **best interests of clients** and the integrity of the market |\n| 2 | **Diligence** | Act with due skill, care and diligence in the best interests of clients |\n| 3 | **Capability** | Have and employ effectively the **resources and procedures** needed to perform the activities |\n| 4 | **Information about clients** | Seek from clients the information about their **circumstances and investment objectives** relevant to the service |\n| 5 | **Information to clients** | Make **adequate disclosures** of relevant material information |\n| 6 | **Fair and reasonable charges** | Charges must be **fair and reasonable**; the basis must be disclosed |\n| 7 | **Conflicts of interest** | Try to **avoid** conflicts; where they arise, ensure **fair treatment** and **disclosure** |\n| 8 | **Compliance** | Comply with all regulatory requirements applicable to the conduct of the business |\n| 9 | **Responsibility of senior management** | Senior management bears **primary responsibility** for compliance and ethical standards |\n\n### The other fiduciary building blocks in the IA Regulations\n\n- **Risk profiling and suitability** — mandatory, documented, and periodically reviewed; the client must be told his profile.\n- **Segregation of advisory and distribution** — an individual adviser cannot both advise and distribute to the same client; a non-individual adviser must keep the activities in **separate identifiable divisions** with client-level segregation.\n- **Fees** — a client (per family) may be charged either up to **₹1,25,000 per annum under the fixed-fee mode** or up to **2.5% of Assets Under Advice (AUA) per annum**; only one mode at a time, and any advance fee is capped.\n- **No assured returns** — an adviser may not guarantee returns, and must state that **SEBI registration is not an endorsement** and gives no assurance of returns or performance.\n- **Disclosure** — of holdings/positions in recommended products, of any consideration received from third parties, of the adviser's terms, and of any disciplinary history.\n- **Records** — KYC, risk profile, suitability rationale, advice given, and interactions to be maintained (in physical or electronic form) for the prescribed period.\n- **Compliance officer** and a documented **grievance redressal** process.\n\n---\n\n## 19.6 SEBI Do's and Don'ts for Investors\n\nSEBI issues investor-education \"Do's and Don'ts\". An adviser is expected to **teach these to the client** — client education is itself part of the fiduciary duty of care.\n\n### ✅ Do's\n\n| Do | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Deal only with SEBI-registered intermediaries** and verify the registration number on the SEBI website | The whole investor-protection architecture applies only to registered entities |\n| **Read the offer document / scheme document** before investing | The risks are always in the document, never in the advertisement |\n| **Complete KYC** properly and give correct PAN, bank and contact details | Payouts, statements and grievance redress all depend on it |\n| **Insist on proper documentation** — contract note, statement of account, receipt | Without documents there is no proof and no remedy |\n| **Give instructions in writing**, clearly and unambiguously | Verbal instructions are the source of most disputes |\n| **Pay only through the banking channel from your own account** | Third-party payments are restricted under PMLA |\n| **Register a nomination** | It is what makes transmission workable for the family |\n| **Monitor your investments** — read statements, check holdings periodically | Errors and unauthorised trades are caught only by reading |\n| **Understand the product's risk, cost, liquidity and tax** before committing | Suitability is ultimately the investor's own responsibility too |\n| **Know the grievance route — SCORES**, and the escalation ladder | Complaints have time limits |\n\n### ❌ Don'ts\n\n| Don't | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Don't deal with unregistered entities** or \"advisers\" without a registration number | No regulatory recourse whatsoever |\n| **Don't believe assured/guaranteed return promises** on market-linked products | Guaranteeing return is prohibited; the promise itself is the red flag |\n| **Don't act on tips, rumours, SMS, WhatsApp or social-media \"calls\"** | These are the standard delivery mechanism for pump-and-dump schemes |\n| **Don't sign blank forms, blank cheques or blank delivery instruction slips** | A blank signed document is a signed blank cheque |\n| **Don't deal in cash** or hand cash to any intermediary | Prohibited and untraceable |\n| **Don't share passwords, PINs or OTPs** with anyone, including your adviser | The account is yours and so is the loss |\n| **Don't be swayed by advertisements or celebrity endorsements** | An endorsement is not a suitability assessment |\n| **Don't give a wide power of attorney** without understanding its exact scope | It is the instrument through which unauthorised trading happens |\n| **Don't invest in a product you cannot explain** to someone else | If you cannot explain it, you cannot monitor it |\n| **Don't chase unrealistically high returns** | Above-market return promises are the definition of a red flag |\n\n> 🧠 **Compress the whole list into five words: REGISTERED, WRITTEN, BANKING CHANNEL, READ, NEVER-BLANK.** Registered intermediary; written instructions; payment through your own bank account; read the document; never sign anything blank.\n\n---\n\n## 19.7 Addressing Annual Audit Observations\n\n### What the audit is\n\nA SEBI-registered Investment Adviser must have an **annual audit of its compliance with the IA Regulations**, carried out by a **member of the ICAI (Chartered Accountant) or the ICSI (Company Secretary)**.\n\n| Item | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| **Scope** | Compliance with the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations and circulars issued under them |\n| **Auditor** | A member of **ICAI or ICSI** |\n| **Frequency** | **Annual**, for each financial year |\n| **Completion** | Within **six months of the end of the financial year** (i.e. by **30 September** for a year ending 31 March) |\n| **Adverse findings** | Reported **together with the action taken**, duly approved by the management / board / proprietor, **within one month of the date of the audit report** |\n| **Reported to** | SEBI, through the **administration and supervisory body for investment advisers (BASL)** |\n\n**Worked timeline.** Financial year ends **31 March 2026** → audit to be completed by **30 September 2026** → if the report is dated 20 September 2026, the adverse findings and the action taken on them must be reported by **20 October 2026**.\n\n### ⭐ How to address the observations — the eight steps\n\n1. **Read them as a diagnostic, not an attack.** An audit observation is free information about where the firm will fail an inspection.\n2. **Classify each observation** — procedural/minor, material, or systemic (see the table below). The response must match the severity.\n3. **Find the root cause**, not just the instance. \"Three risk profiles missing\" is a symptom; \"on-boarding does not block account activation until the profile is signed\" is the cause.\n4. **Assign a named owner and a deadline** for each observation, tracked in a **remediation register** by the compliance officer.\n5. **Remediate the CLIENT, not only the process.** Where a client has suffered — an excess fee, an unsuitable product, a missing disclosure — refund, reverse or correct it. Fixing the checklist while keeping the money is not remediation.\n6. **Get management/board approval** of the action-taken report — the Code of Conduct puts primary responsibility on **senior management**.\n7. **Report to SEBI/BASL within the prescribed time**, with the findings *and* the action taken. Never suppress an adverse finding — non-reporting is a fresh and more serious violation than the original lapse.\n8. **Change the system and verify next year** — update SOPs, checklists, software controls and staff training, then confirm closure in the following audit.\n\n| Type of observation | Correct response |\n|---|---|\n| **Procedural / documentation** (a missing signature, an undated form) | Correct the record, tighten the checklist, close it |\n| **Material** (risk profiling not done, disclosure not made, fee cap breached) | Remediate the affected clients, fix the process, report with action taken |\n| **Systemic** (no compliance officer, no records, advisory and distribution not segregated) | Escalate to management, redesign the process, consider external help, expect regulatory follow-up |\n| **REPEAT observation from last year** | The most serious of all — it proves the earlier \"fix\" was cosmetic. Expect it to be treated as wilful. |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** The reportable item is not merely the finding — it is the **adverse finding TOGETHER WITH the action taken on it, approved by the management**. An answer that says \"report the observations to SEBI\" is incomplete; the action taken is the operative half.\n\n---\n\n## 19.8 Global Best Practices on Ethical Issues\n\n### 19.8.1 United States — the SEC\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Governing law** | **Investment Advisers Act of 1940**, administered by the **Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)** |\n| **Core standard** | An investment adviser is a **fiduciary** to its clients — established through the anti-fraud provisions of the Act and confirmed by the US Supreme Court in **SEC v. Capital Gains Research Bureau (1963)** |\n| **Duty of care** | Provide advice in the client's **best interest**, seek **best execution**, and **monitor** the advice over the relationship |\n| **Duty of loyalty** | Do not subordinate the client's interest to the adviser's; make **full and fair disclosure of all material conflicts** and obtain **informed consent** |\n| **Disclosure document** | **Form ADV** — including the plain-English **Part 2 \"brochure\"** given to clients, covering services, fees, conflicts, disciplinary history |\n| **Code of ethics rule** | Registered advisers must adopt a **written code of ethics** — standards of conduct, compliance with securities laws, reporting of **personal securities transactions** by access persons, pre-clearance of IPOs/private placements, and reporting of violations |\n| **Compliance rule** | Written **policies and procedures**, reviewed **annually**, with a designated **Chief Compliance Officer** |\n| **Brokers** | Held to **Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI)** — a best-interest standard at the point of recommendation, still distinct from the adviser's full fiduciary duty |\n\n> 🧠 **The SEC model in five words: DISCLOSE, DOCUMENT, DESIGNATE, REVIEW, RESTRICT** — disclose conflicts (Form ADV), document policies, designate a CCO, review annually, restrict personal trading.\n\n### 19.8.2 Australia — ASIC and the FOFA reforms\n\nAustralia's regime is regarded as the strictest in the world on **remuneration conflicts**, because it does not rely on disclosure alone — it **bans** the conflicted payment.\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Regulator / law** | **ASIC** (Australian Securities and Investments Commission), under the **Corporations Act 2001** |\n| **Licensing** | Advisers must hold or operate under an **AFS licence** |\n| **The reform package** | **FOFA — Future of Financial Advice**, effective from 2012–13 |\n| **Best interests duty** | A statutory duty to act in the **client's best interests** when giving personal advice to a retail client, supported by a **\"safe harbour\"** list of steps the adviser must show he took |\n| **Ban on conflicted remuneration** | **Commissions and volume-based benefits are banned** for personal advice on relevant products — the conflict is removed, not merely disclosed |\n| **Opt-in** | Ongoing fee arrangements must be **periodically renewed by the client**, not left running indefinitely |\n| **Fee Disclosure Statement (FDS)** | An **annual statement** of the fees paid and the services actually provided |\n| **Client documents** | **Financial Services Guide (FSG)** up front, **Statement of Advice (SoA)** setting out the advice, its basis, the fees and any interests that could influence it, and the product's **PDS** |\n| **Professional standards** | Education, an exam, continuing professional development, and a binding **Code of Ethics for financial advisers** (in force from 2020) built on trustworthiness, competence, honesty, fairness and diligence |\n\n**The \"safe harbour\" steps** — the checklist an Australian adviser must be able to evidence: identify the client's objectives, situation and needs; identify the subject matter of the advice; identify what further information is reasonably needed and make reasonable enquiries; assess whether the adviser has the necessary expertise (and refer on if not); conduct a **reasonable investigation of the products**; base all judgements on the client's relevant circumstances; and take **any other step** that would reasonably be in the client's best interests.\n\n### Comparing the three regimes\n\n| | **India** | **United States** | **Australia** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Regulator** | **SEBI** | **SEC** | **ASIC** |\n| **Key instrument** | IA Regulations **2013** | Advisers Act **1940** | Corporations Act 2001 + **FOFA** |\n| **Standard** | **Fiduciary** — Reg **15(1)** | **Fiduciary** — care + loyalty | Statutory **best interests duty** |\n| **Conflicts approach** | Avoid, manage, **disclose**; advice segregated from distribution | **Full and fair disclosure** + informed consent | **Ban** conflicted remuneration outright |\n| **Client document** | Terms, risk profile, suitability rationale, disclosures | **Form ADV Part 2** brochure | **FSG + SoA + PDS** |\n| **Ongoing fee control** | Fee **caps** per family, advance fee capped | Disclosure-based | **Opt-in + annual Fee Disclosure Statement** |\n| **Compliance machinery** | Compliance officer, **annual compliance audit** | CCO, written policies, **annual review** | AFS licence obligations, Code of Ethics |\n\n**The common thread across all three:** *know the client, act in his best interest, control conflicts, disclose fully, document everything, and review it periodically.* The jurisdictions differ only in how much they trust disclosure versus prohibition.\n\n*(A widely used voluntary global benchmark is the **CFA Institute Code of Ethics and Standards of Professional Conduct**, which likewise requires members to place the integrity of the profession and the interests of clients above their own.)*\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| Ethics | Moral principles distinguishing **right from wrong conduct** |\n| Ethical restraint | **Voluntarily holding back** from an available, profitable, possibly legal act that would harm the client |\n| Law vs ethics | Law is the **minimum** and external; ethics is the **higher standard**, internal — **ethics begins where the law ends** |\n| Why advice needs ethics most | Advice is a **credence good** — quality may not be visible for years |\n| Information asymmetry | The adviser knows far more than the client about cost, risk and suitability |\n| Agency problem | The **agent** (adviser) may serve his own interest instead of the **principal's** (client's) |\n| Mis-selling | Recommending a product that **does not suit** the client |\n| Churning | **Excessive trading/switching** to generate commission or brokerage |\n| Front-running | Trading **ahead of a client's known impending ORDER** — **PFUTP Regulations 2003** |\n| Insider trading | Trading on **UPSI about the company** — **PIT Regulations 2015** |\n| Front-run vs insider-trade | Front-run an **ORDER**; insider-trade on **NEWS** |\n| Misrepresenting returns | Cherry-picked windows, gross-of-cost figures, absolute passed off as annual |\n| ₹10 → ₹28 over 9 years | **180% absolute** but only **≈12.1% CAGR** |\n| Hidden cost | Commissions, loads, TER, mortality charges not disclosed |\n| Ethical dilemma | A conflict between **two legitimate duties**, or where the right course is costly — **not** simply right vs wrong |\n| Dilemma framework | Facts → stakeholders → name the issue → alternatives → test & consult compliance → **decide, act, document, review** |\n| The four tests | **Legality**, **publicity/newspaper**, **mirror/family**, **role model** |\n| Conflict hierarchy | **Avoid → manage → disclose** (in that order) |\n| Fiduciary provision | **Regulation 15(1)**, SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, **2013** |\n| Reg 15(1) wording | Act in a **fiduciary capacity** and **disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise** |\n| Two limbs of fiduciary duty | **Duty of LOYALTY** and **duty of CARE** |\n| Fiduciary vs suitability | \"Is it the **best**?\" vs \"Is it **not unsuitable**?\" |\n| Code of Conduct heads | Honesty & fairness, **diligence**, capability, information **about** clients, information **to** clients, **fair charges**, conflicts, compliance, **senior management responsibility** |\n| IA fee cap | **₹1,25,000 p.a. per family** (fixed-fee mode) **or 2.5% of AUA p.a.** — one mode only |\n| Assured returns | **Prohibited**; SEBI registration is **not an endorsement** |\n| SEBI Do's — five words | **Registered, written, banking channel, read, never-blank** |\n| Biggest Don't | Never believe an **assured/guaranteed return** on a market-linked product |\n| Annual compliance audit | By a member of **ICAI or ICSI**, within **six months of the financial year end** |\n| Adverse findings reporting | Findings **plus action taken**, management-approved, **within one month of the audit report** |\n| Repeat audit observation | The most serious kind — proves the earlier fix was cosmetic |\n| US law | **Investment Advisers Act of 1940**; regulator **SEC** |\n| US case establishing fiduciary duty | **SEC v. Capital Gains Research Bureau (1963)** |\n| US disclosure document | **Form ADV** (Part 2 brochure) |\n| US compliance machinery | Written **code of ethics**, personal-trading reporting, written policies, **annual review**, a **CCO** |\n| Australian regulator / reform | **ASIC** / **FOFA** (from 2012–13) |\n| Australia's distinctive rule | **Ban on conflicted remuneration** — the conflict is removed, not merely disclosed |\n| Australia's other pillars | **Best interests duty + safe harbour**, **opt-in**, **annual Fee Disclosure Statement**, **SoA/FSG/PDS** |\n| Common thread globally | Know the client, best interest, control conflicts, disclose, document, review |\n\n> **Exam tip:** the three highest-yield items are (1) **Regulation 15(1)** and the **loyalty/care** split, (2) telling **mis-selling, churning and front-running** apart from a one-sentence scenario, and (3) Australia's **ban** on conflicted remuneration versus the US/India preference for **disclosure**. If a scenario question offers \"disclose it\" and \"avoid it\" as separate options, remember the order — **avoid first, disclose only what cannot be avoided.**\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 19: Ethical Issues — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **Regulation 15(1), the loyalty/care split, and telling mis-selling from churning from front-running are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** Define ethics.\n> **A:** The set of **moral principles that distinguish right conduct from wrong conduct** — the standard a professional follows **even when no rule compels it and no one is watching**.\n\n**Q2.** What is ethical restraint?\n> **A:** The **voluntary, self-imposed decision to hold back** from an action that is available, profitable and possibly even **legal**, because it would harm the client, the market or the profession.\n\n**Q3.** Give the sharpest distinction between law and ethics.\n> **A:** Law is the **minimum** standard, external and enforced by penalties; ethics is the **higher** standard, internal and enforced by conscience and reputation. Law asks **\"is it permitted?\"**; ethics asks **\"is it right?\"** — **ethics begins where the law ends**.\n\n**Q4.** Can conduct be legal and still unethical? Give an example.\n> **A:** **Yes.** Selling a 63-year-old with a 2-year horizon a product with a **15-year lock-in** breaks no specific rule but is indefensible.\n\n**Q5.** Why does financial advice depend on ethics more than most businesses?\n> **A:** Advice is a **credence good** — the client may not be able to judge its quality **for fifteen years**, by which time the goal it was meant to fund has already passed. The client cannot police quality; only the adviser's ethics can.\n\n**Q6.** What is information asymmetry in an advisory relationship?\n> **A:** The **adviser knows far more** than the client about a product's cost, commission, liquidity and true risk. That gap is the very reason the client hired the adviser — and it can be used **for** or **against** him.\n\n**Q7.** Explain the agency (principal–agent) problem in advice.\n> **A:** The client is the **principal**, the adviser the **agent**. The agent has his own interests — commission, targets, an employer's product shelf. Wherever the payoffs diverge there is a **conflict of interest**, which resolves in the agent's favour unless regulation and ethics prevent it.\n\n**Q8.** Define moral hazard and adverse selection in this context.\n> **A:** **Moral hazard** — the adviser bears **none of the loss** from bad advice but keeps the commission. **Adverse selection** — the **worst products get pushed hardest** because they pay the highest commission.\n\n**Q9.** Name four business benefits of ethical conduct for an advisory firm.\n> **A:** **Trust** (the only real product), **client retention** (advisory economics are multi-year), **referrals** (the cheapest acquisition channel), and **lower cost** (fewer complaints, inspections and lawsuits) — plus protection of the **registration** itself.\n\n**Q10.** Define mis-selling.\n> **A:** Recommending a product that **does not suit** the client's needs, risk profile or horizon — usually because it **pays the adviser more**. The product is right for the seller, not the buyer.\n\n**Q11.** Define churning.\n> **A:** **Excessive trading, switching or portfolio turnover** carried out to generate **brokerage or fresh commission**, not to change the strategy. Lots of activity, no change in the underlying plan.\n\n**Q12.** A ₹25,00,000 portfolio is turned over 8 times a year at a 0.50% round-trip cost. What is the annual cost drag?\n> **A:** Value traded = 8 × ₹25,00,000 = **₹2,00,00,000**; cost = 0.50% × ₹2 crore = **₹1,00,000**; as a share of the portfolio = **4.0% per year**. A 0.5× turnover would have cost only **₹6,250 (0.25%)**.\n\n**Q13.** Define front-running.\n> **A:** Dealing in a security **ahead of a client's (or employer's) known impending ORDER**, to profit from the price movement that order will cause. Prohibited under the **SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003**.\n\n**Q14.** How does front-running differ from insider trading?\n> **A:** **Front-run an ORDER; insider-trade on NEWS.** Front-running exploits knowledge of an **impending order** (PFUTP Regulations, **2003**); insider trading exploits **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company** (PIT Regulations, **2015**).\n\n**Q15.** How does mis-selling differ from churning?\n> **A:** **Mis-selling is about the WRONG product** — a single transaction can be mis-selling. **Churning is about TOO MANY transactions** — the products may each be fine; the **frequency** is the offence.\n\n**Q16.** List five ways returns are misrepresented.\n> **A:** **Absolute return passed off as annual**; a **cherry-picked best window**; figures **gross of expenses and tax**; **back-tested \"model\" returns** shown as actually earned; and **point-to-point instead of rolling** returns (one lucky start date). Add **survivorship bias** — showing only the schemes that survived.\n\n**Q17.** A NAV goes from ₹10 to ₹28 in 9 years. What does the brochure say and what is the honest number?\n> **A:** The brochure says **\"180% return\"**. The honest number is the **CAGR = (28/10)^(1/9) − 1 ≈ 12.1% per annum**. Both describe the same fund; only one is designed to be understood.\n\n**Q18.** ₹10,00,000 grows for 15 years at 12% gross. Compare a 2.00% TER with a 1.10% TER.\n> **A:** Net **10.00%** → 10,00,000 × 1.10¹⁵ = **₹41.77 lakh**. Net **10.90%** → 10,00,000 × 1.109¹⁵ = **₹47.21 lakh**. The **0.90% a year gap costs ₹5.44 lakh** — more than half the original investment, and it never appears as a line item on a statement.\n\n**Q19.** Name six ethical issues in financial advice besides mis-selling and churning.\n> **A:** **Front-running**, **conflicts of interest**, **unsuitable recommendations** (no risk profiling), **misrepresenting returns**, **hidden costs**, and **breach of client confidentiality**. Also: unauthorised transactions, assured-return claims, rebating/inducements, and advice without diligence.\n\n**Q20.** What exactly is an ethical dilemma?\n> **A:** **Not** a choice between right and wrong (that is merely temptation). It is a situation where **two legitimate duties conflict**, where **every option carries some ethical cost**, or where the right course is clear but **expensive** — it costs the fee, the client or the job.\n\n**Q21.** Give the six-step framework for resolving an ethical dilemma.\n> **A:** **1 Get the facts** → **2 identify the stakeholders** (client's interest ranks first) → **3 name the ethical issue and conflicting duties** → **4 generate alternatives** (never only two) → **5 test them and consult the compliance officer / Code of Conduct** → **6 decide, act, DOCUMENT and review**.\n\n**Q22.** Name the four quick ethical tests.\n> **A:** The **legality test** (does it breach any rule?), the **publicity/newspaper test** (front page tomorrow with my name?), the **mirror/family test** (would I sell this to my own parent?), and the **role-model test** (would a respected senior professional do it?).\n\n**Q23.** What is the correct order of preference for handling a conflict of interest?\n> **A:** **Avoid → manage → disclose.** Disclosure is the **last** resort, not the first — some conflicts are too severe for disclosure to cure.\n\n**Q24.** A conservative 63-year-old insists on 90% small caps. What is the right response?\n> **A:** Neither silent compliance (breach of the **duty of care**) nor a flat refusal (abandoning the client). **Quantify the risk in rupees**, offer a profile-consistent compromise, and if he insists, **record the advice given and his written instruction that it is contrary to advice** — the file must show what you advised, not only what he wanted.\n\n**Q25.** Quote the provision that makes an Indian investment adviser a fiduciary.\n> **A:** **Regulation 15(1) of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013:** *\"An investment adviser shall act in a fiduciary capacity towards its clients and shall disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise.\"*\n\n**Q26.** What is a fiduciary?\n> **A:** Someone **entrusted with another's affairs who must place that person's interest above his own** — the **highest duty the law recognises** in a commercial relationship.\n\n**Q27.** Name and explain the two limbs of fiduciary duty.\n> **A:** **Duty of LOYALTY** — put the client first, avoid or fully disclose conflicts, take no undisclosed compensation (\"whose side are you on?\"). **Duty of CARE** — know the client, risk-profile him, research the product, recommend what fits, and **monitor** it (\"did you do the work?\").\n\n**Q28.** Contrast the fiduciary standard with the suitability standard.\n> **A:** Fiduciary asks **\"is this the BEST option for the client?\"**; suitability asks only **\"is it NOT UNSUITABLE?\"**. Fiduciary is **continuing**, client-fee-based and conflict-averse; suitability is largely **point-of-sale** and commission-based.\n\n**Q29.** List the nine heads of the Code of Conduct in the Third Schedule of the IA Regulations.\n> **A:** **Honesty and fairness; diligence; capability; information about clients; information to clients; fair and reasonable charges; conflicts of interest; compliance; responsibility of senior management.**\n\n**Q30.** Which Code of Conduct head makes ethics a management problem rather than a staff problem?\n> **A:** **Responsibility of senior management** — senior management bears **primary responsibility** for compliance and for the firm's ethical standards.\n\n**Q31.** What are the SEBI limits on an investment adviser's fees?\n> **A:** Per client family, either up to **₹1,25,000 per annum under the fixed-fee mode** or up to **2.5% of Assets Under Advice per annum** — **only one mode at a time**, with advance fees also capped.\n\n**Q32.** May an investment adviser guarantee a return, and what must he say about his registration?\n> **A:** **No** — assured or guaranteed returns are prohibited on market-linked advice. He must also make clear that **SEBI registration is not an endorsement** and gives **no assurance of returns or performance**.\n\n**Q33.** Give five items from SEBI's \"Do's\" for investors.\n> **A:** Deal **only with SEBI-registered intermediaries** (verify the registration number); **read the offer document**; complete **KYC** with correct PAN and bank details; insist on **proper documentation** (contract note, statement); and give **instructions in writing**. Also: pay through your **own bank account**, **nominate**, monitor statements, and know **SCORES**.\n\n**Q34.** Give five items from SEBI's \"Don'ts\" for investors.\n> **A:** Don't deal with **unregistered entities**; don't believe **assured-return promises**; don't act on **tips, rumours or WhatsApp calls**; **never sign blank forms, cheques or delivery instruction slips**; and **never deal in cash** or share **passwords/OTPs**.\n\n**Q35.** Compress SEBI's Do's and Don'ts into a five-word hook.\n> **A:** **REGISTERED, WRITTEN, BANKING CHANNEL, READ, NEVER-BLANK.**\n\n**Q36.** Who must conduct an investment adviser's annual compliance audit, and by when?\n> **A:** A **member of the ICAI (Chartered Accountant) or ICSI (Company Secretary)**, covering compliance with the IA Regulations, to be completed within **six months of the end of the financial year** — i.e. by **30 September** for a year ending 31 March.\n\n**Q37.** What exactly must be reported to SEBI after the audit, and within what time?\n> **A:** The **adverse findings TOGETHER WITH the action taken on them**, duly **approved by the management/board/proprietor**, **within one month of the date of the audit report** — submitted through the administration and supervisory body (**BASL**).\n\n**Q38.** Give the eight steps for addressing annual audit observations.\n> **A:** **1** Treat them as a diagnostic → **2 classify** by severity → **3** find the **root cause** → **4** assign an **owner and deadline** in a remediation register → **5 remediate the CLIENT**, not just the process → **6** obtain **management approval** of the action-taken report → **7 report to SEBI/BASL on time** → **8** change the system and **verify closure next year**.\n\n**Q39.** Why is a repeat audit observation the most serious kind?\n> **A:** Because it **proves the earlier fix was cosmetic** — the firm knew of the failure and did not truly correct it, so it is liable to be treated as **wilful** rather than inadvertent.\n\n**Q40.** Summarise the US SEC framework for investment advisers.\n> **A:** The **Investment Advisers Act of 1940**, enforced by the **SEC**. Advisers are **fiduciaries** (confirmed in **SEC v. Capital Gains Research Bureau, 1963**) owing a **duty of care** (best interest, best execution, ongoing monitoring) and a **duty of loyalty** (full and fair disclosure of material conflicts, informed consent). Disclosure is via **Form ADV Part 2**; firms need a written **code of ethics**, **personal-trading reporting**, written policies **reviewed annually**, and a **Chief Compliance Officer**.\n\n**Q41.** What does Australia's FOFA package require, and what makes it distinctive?\n> **A:** Under **ASIC** and the Corporations Act: a statutory **best interests duty** with a **\"safe harbour\"** checklist, **opt-in** renewal of ongoing fee arrangements, an annual **Fee Disclosure Statement**, and **FSG / Statement of Advice / PDS** documents. What makes it distinctive is the outright **BAN on conflicted remuneration** — commissions and volume benefits are **removed**, not merely disclosed.\n\n**Q42.** State the common thread across the Indian, US and Australian regimes.\n> **A:** **Know the client, act in his best interest, control conflicts, disclose fully, document everything, and review periodically.** They differ only in **how far they trust disclosure versus outright prohibition** — Australia bans the conflicted payment, while India and the US rely more on **avoidance plus disclosure**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 19: Ethical Issues — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Most marks here come from putting the right LABEL on a described behaviour.** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** Ethics is best defined as:\nA) The law of the land  B) Moral principles that distinguish right conduct from wrong conduct  C) SEBI circulars  D) A firm's marketing policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Ethics is the set of **moral principles** guiding right and wrong behaviour, followed **even when no rule compels it**.\nA) Law is the minimum written standard, not ethics itself. C) Circulars are regulation. D) Marketing policy is a commercial document.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Ethical restraint means:\nA) Restraining the client  B) Voluntarily holding back from a profitable act that would harm the client  C) A SEBI penalty  D) Restricting portfolio turnover by law\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Restraint is **voluntary and self-imposed** — declining an available, profitable and possibly even legal action because it is wrong.\nA) It restrains the professional, not the client. C) A penalty is external enforcement, the opposite of restraint. D) Turnover limits are regulation, not restraint.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Compared with the law, ethics is:\nA) A lower standard  B) The higher standard, internally enforced  C) Identical to the law  D) Optional for professionals\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Law sets the **minimum**; ethics sets the **higher, aspirational** standard enforced by conscience and reputation.\nA) Ethics is above the law, not below. C) They overlap but are not identical — conduct can be legal yet unethical. D) For a fiduciary, ethical conduct is a professional obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** \"Ethics begins where the law ends\" means:\nA) Ethics applies only abroad  B) Rules are the minimum and ethics fills the gaps rules do not cover  C) Ethics replaces the law  D) Once the law is obeyed nothing more is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Regulations are written **after** a harm occurs; ethics anticipates harms no rule has yet addressed.\nA) It is a general principle, not jurisdictional. C) Ethics supplements the law, never replaces it. D) That is exactly the attitude the phrase criticises.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Financial advice is called a \"credence good\" because:\nA) It is cheap  B) The client may not be able to judge its quality for many years  C) It is guaranteed  D) It requires no skill\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Bad advice may only become visible **after the goal it was meant to fund has passed**, so the client cannot police quality.\nA) Cost is irrelevant to the definition. C) Advice carries no guarantee. D) It is a highly skilled service.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Information asymmetry in advice means:\nA) The client knows more than the adviser  B) The adviser knows far more about cost, risk and suitability than the client  C) Both know the same  D) Neither knows anything\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The gap in knowledge is precisely **why the client hired the adviser** — and it can be exploited.\nA) Reverses the relationship. C) If it were true no adviser would be needed. D) Absurd.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** In the principal–agent problem applied to advice, the client is the:\nA) Agent  B) Principal  C) Regulator  D) Custodian\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The client is the **principal**; the adviser is the **agent** engaged to act on the principal's behalf.\nA) The adviser is the agent. C) SEBI is the regulator, external to the relationship. D) A custodian holds assets and is not a party to the advice.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Moral hazard in an advisory relationship refers to the fact that:\nA) The adviser shares the client's loss  B) The adviser bears none of the loss from bad advice but keeps the commission  C) Clients always lose  D) Markets are risky\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **payoff is one-sided**, which is what creates the incentive to take risk with someone else's money.\nA) That is the opposite of moral hazard. C) An overgeneralisation. D) Market risk is not moral hazard.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Mis-selling is:\nA) Selling at the wrong price  B) Recommending a product that does not suit the client's needs or profile  C) Selling too often  D) Selling to a registered investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The product is right for the **seller**, not the buyer — typically because it pays more.\nA) Price errors are operational, not ethical labels. C) That describes **churning**. D) Not an issue at all.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Churning is:\nA) A portfolio rebalancing rule  B) Excessive trading or switching to generate brokerage or commission  C) Refusing to trade  D) A SEBI-approved strategy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lots of **activity with no change in the underlying strategy**, done for the intermediary's income.\nA) Disciplined rebalancing is legitimate and infrequent. C) Inactivity is not churning. D) It is a prohibited practice.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Front-running is:\nA) Trading ahead of a client's known impending order  B) Executing a client's order first  C) Running a fund  D) Advertising performance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The adviser's own trade is placed **just before** the client's, to profit from the price move that order will cause.\nB) Executing the client first is precisely what is required. C) Fund management is a different activity. D) That relates to return misrepresentation.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Front-running is dealt with primarily under:\nA) PFUTP Regulations, 2003  B) The Companies Act  C) FEMA  D) The Consumer Protection Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003**.\nB) Governs companies. C) Governs foreign exchange. D) Governs consumer disputes generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Insider trading is governed by SEBI regulations of the year:\nA) 1992  B) 2003  C) 2013  D) 2015\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The **SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015**.\nA) The SEBI Act year. B) PFUTP. C) The Investment Advisers Regulations.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** The provision that makes an Indian investment adviser a fiduciary is:\nA) Regulation 15(1) of the IA Regulations, 2013  B) Section 11 of the SEBI Act  C) Rule 206 of the Companies Act  D) The Third Schedule of FEMA\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Reg 15(1)**: an adviser shall act in a **fiduciary capacity** and disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise.\nB) Section 11 sets out SEBI's general functions. C) No such rule. D) FEMA has no such schedule on advice.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Under Regulation 15(1), conflicts of interest must be disclosed:\nA) Annually only  B) As and when they arise  C) Only if the client asks  D) Only at termination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The wording is **\"as and when they arise\"** — disclosure is contemporaneous, not periodic.\nA) An annual sweep would be too late. C) The duty is proactive, not on demand. D) Disclosure after the fact is worthless.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** The two limbs of fiduciary duty are:\nA) Loyalty and care  B) Speed and accuracy  C) Profit and growth  D) Sales and service\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Duty of loyalty** and **duty of care**.\nB), C) and D) are operational or commercial qualities, not fiduciary duties.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** The duty of loyalty requires an adviser to:\nA) Be loyal to his employer above all  B) Put the client's interest first and disclose or avoid conflicts  C) Maximise commission  D) Follow market consensus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Loyalty answers the question **\"whose side are you on?\"**\nA) The client outranks the employer where the two conflict. C) The opposite of loyalty. D) Consensus-following is not a duty.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The duty of care requires an adviser to:\nA) Be polite  B) Know the client, risk-profile him, research products and monitor the advice  C) Guarantee returns  D) Trade frequently\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Care answers **\"did you do the work?\"** — and it continues after the sale.\nA) Courtesy is not diligence. C) Guaranteeing returns is prohibited. D) Frequent trading is churning.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** The fiduciary standard asks:\nA) Is this not unsuitable?  B) Is this the best available option for the client?  C) Is this legal?  D) Is this profitable for the firm?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fiduciary must seek the client's **best** outcome, not merely an acceptable one.\nA) That is the lower **suitability** standard. C) Legality is necessary but not sufficient. D) Firm profit is the conflict, not the test.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** The suitability standard applied to a distributor asks:\nA) Is it the best product available?  B) Is it not unsuitable for this client?  C) Is it the cheapest?  D) Does the client like it?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Suitability is a **negative** test — the product must not be unsuitable, but need not be optimal.\nA) That is the fiduciary test. C) Cost alone is not the standard. D) Preference is not suitability.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** The Code of Conduct for Investment Advisers is set out in:\nA) The Third Schedule of the IA Regulations, 2013  B) The SEBI Act  C) The PFUTP Regulations  D) The Companies Act\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Third Schedule** of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013.\nB) The parent Act contains no such code. C) PFUTP deals with fraudulent practices. D) Irrelevant to advisers.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Which of these is NOT one of the heads of the IA Code of Conduct?\nA) Diligence  B) Capability  C) Guarantee of returns  D) Conflicts of interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Guaranteeing returns is **prohibited**, and could never be a head of a code of conduct.\nA), B) and D) are all genuine heads, alongside honesty and fairness, information about clients, information to clients, fair charges, compliance and senior management responsibility.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Under the IA Code of Conduct, primary responsibility for compliance and ethical standards rests with:\nA) The junior relationship manager  B) Senior management  C) The client  D) SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The head \"**responsibility of senior management**\" places it at the top, not on front-line staff.\nA) Staff follow the culture set above them. C) The client is the protected party. D) SEBI supervises but does not run the firm.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** May a SEBI-registered investment adviser guarantee returns?\nA) Yes, up to 8%  B) No — assured or guaranteed returns are prohibited  C) Yes, for HNIs  D) Yes, with client consent\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Guaranteeing return on market-linked advice is prohibited.\nA), C) and D) all suggest exceptions; none exists, and consent cannot legalise a prohibited representation.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** What must an investment adviser convey about its SEBI registration?\nA) That it guarantees returns  B) That registration is not an endorsement and gives no assurance of returns  C) That SEBI approves each recommendation  D) That losses are covered\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Registration certifies eligibility to operate; it is **not** a quality certificate or a return guarantee.\nA) and D) are false assurances. C) SEBI does not vet individual recommendations.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** An investment adviser's annual compliance audit must be done by:\nA) Any employee  B) A member of ICAI or ICSI  C) SEBI officials  D) The client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **Chartered Accountant (ICAI)** or **Company Secretary (ICSI)** member.\nA) An internal employee lacks independence. C) SEBI inspects separately; it does not perform the statutory annual audit. D) Absurd.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** The annual compliance audit must be completed within:\nA) One month of the financial year end  B) Six months of the financial year end  C) Two years  D) No timeline is prescribed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Six months** — i.e. by **30 September** for a year ending 31 March.\nA) Too short for a full compliance review. C) Would defeat the purpose of an annual audit. D) A timeline is prescribed.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** After the audit, what must be reported to SEBI?\nA) Nothing  B) The adverse findings together with the action taken on them  C) Only the auditor's fee  D) Only favourable findings\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Adverse findings **plus the action taken**, duly approved by the management, within **one month of the audit report**.\nA) Non-reporting is itself a violation. C) Irrelevant. D) Reporting only good news defeats the purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** SEBI's first \"Do\" for investors is to:\nA) Invest in the highest-return product  B) Deal only with SEBI-registered intermediaries and verify the registration number  C) Follow social-media tips  D) Pay in cash\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Investor protection machinery applies **only to registered entities**.\nA) Chasing return is the opposite of the advice. C) and D) are both explicit \"Don'ts\".\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which is a SEBI \"Don't\" for investors?\nA) Read the offer document  B) Never sign blank forms, cheques or delivery instruction slips  C) Nominate  D) Keep records\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A signed blank document is effectively a signed blank cheque.\nA), C) and D) are all \"Do's\".\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Regarding payments, SEBI advises investors to:\nA) Pay in cash  B) Pay only through the banking channel from their own account  C) Pay through a friend's account  D) Pay the relationship manager personally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Traceable payments from the investor's **own** account; third-party payments are restricted under PMLA.\nA) Cash dealings are prohibited. C) A third-party payment. D) Money must never go to an individual employee.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In the United States, investment advisers are governed by:\nA) The Investment Advisers Act of 1940  B) The Securities Act of 1933  C) The Dodd-Frank Act only  D) The Corporations Act 2001\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Advisers Act of 1940**, administered by the **SEC**.\nB) Governs securities offerings. C) A later reform statute, not the governing adviser law. D) That is Australia's statute.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The principal disclosure document filed and given to clients by a US adviser is:\nA) Form ADV  B) Form 10-K  C) The Statement of Advice  D) The PDS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Form ADV**, including the plain-English Part 2 brochure covering services, fees, conflicts and disciplinary history.\nB) A company's annual report. C) and D) are Australian documents.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** Australia's securities and financial services regulator is:\nA) ASIC  B) FCA  C) MAS  D) SEC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Australian Securities and Investments Commission**.\nB) UK. C) Singapore. D) United States.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The most distinctive feature of Australia's FOFA reforms is:\nA) A ban on conflicted remuneration  B) Higher commissions  C) No client documentation  D) Abolition of licensing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Australia **removes** the conflicted payment rather than merely disclosing it.\nB) The reforms cut, not raise, conflicted pay. C) FSG, SoA and PDS are all mandatory. D) The AFS licence remains central.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An adviser switches a client between six similar large-cap funds in one year with no change in strategy. This is:\nA) Rebalancing  B) Churning  C) Front-running  D) Good diversification\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Activity generating fresh commission with **no change in the underlying plan** is the definition of churning.\nA) Rebalancing restores target weights and is infrequent. C) Front-running involves trading ahead of an order. D) Six similar funds add cost, not diversification.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** An adviser buys a mid-cap stock in his own account knowing his largest client will place a ₹50 crore buy order tomorrow. This is:\nA) Smart investing  B) Front-running  C) Insider trading  D) Rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — He is exploiting knowledge of an **impending order**, which is front-running under the PFUTP Regulations.\nA) It is a prohibited practice. C) Insider trading requires unpublished price-sensitive information **about the company**. D) Unrelated.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An adviser sells a 70-year-old a policy with a 15-year lock-in and heavy early-exit penalties. This is best labelled:\nA) Prudent planning  B) Mis-selling  C) Churning  D) Front-running\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The horizon is grossly mismatched to the client's needs, so the product suits the seller, not the buyer.\nA) It is the opposite of prudent. C) Only one transaction is involved. D) No order is being anticipated.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** A client who is a CFO mentions an unannounced merger; the adviser buys the stock. This is:\nA) Front-running  B) Insider trading  C) Churning  D) Mis-selling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — This is **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company**, caught by the PIT Regulations, 2015 — and it is also a breach of client confidentiality.\nA) No client order is being anticipated. C) No excessive trading. D) Nothing was sold to a client.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A brochure states \"180% returns!\" without mentioning the nine-year period involved. This is:\nA) Fair advertising  B) Misrepresentation of returns  C) Front-running  D) A hidden cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Absolute return quoted without the period** is the commonest form of return misrepresentation; the honest figure is a CAGR of about 12.1% p.a.\nA) It is designed to impress rather than inform. C) No order anticipation. D) Costs are a separate issue.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** An adviser does not tell a client that the recommended fund is distributed by his brother's firm, which earns commission. The failure is:\nA) Trivial  B) Non-disclosure of a conflict of interest, breaching Reg 15(1)  C) Insider trading  D) Churning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A **related-party interest** is a material conflict and must be disclosed **as and when it arises**.\nA) It goes to the core of the fiduciary duty of loyalty. C) No price-sensitive company information. D) No excessive trading.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** An audit finds recommendations on file with no documented risk profile. This primarily breaches:\nA) The duty of care  B) The duty of loyalty  C) PMLA  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Care requires the adviser to **know the client and evidence suitability**; without a profile there is no basis for the advice.\nB) Loyalty concerns conflicts and self-dealing. C) PMLA concerns money laundering. D) It is a serious material lapse.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of these is a genuine ethical DILEMMA rather than plain wrongdoing?\nA) Stealing from a client account  B) A client insists on a portfolio far riskier than his profile  C) Forging a signature  D) Trading on insider information\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Two legitimate duties collide — **client autonomy** versus the **duty of care**. There is no obviously costless answer.\nA), C) and D) are simply wrong; a choice between right and wrong is a temptation, not a dilemma.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** The correct order of preference for handling a conflict of interest is:\nA) Disclose, then avoid  B) Avoid, then manage, then disclose  C) Ignore it  D) Disclose only if asked\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Avoidance is best**; management next; **disclosure is the last resort**, because some conflicts are too severe for disclosure to cure.\nA) Reverses the hierarchy. C) Ignoring a conflict breaches Reg 15(1). D) Disclosure is proactive, not on demand.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** The \"publicity test\" asks:\nA) Will this generate publicity?  B) Would I be comfortable seeing this on tomorrow's front page with my name on it?  C) Is this advertised?  D) Will SEBI notice?\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Also called the sunlight or newspaper test; it exposes conduct that depends on remaining hidden.\nA) Marketing is irrelevant. C) Advertising is unrelated. D) \"Will I be caught?\" is precisely the wrong question.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** The first step in resolving an ethical dilemma is to:\nA) Decide immediately  B) Get the complete facts  C) Ask the client to choose  D) Consult a lawyer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Most apparent dilemmas **dissolve once the facts are complete**; deciding on assumptions is the commonest error.\nA) Speed without facts is recklessness. C) The adviser cannot outsource his own professional judgement. D) Legal advice may help later, but only after the facts.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** The final step in the dilemma framework is:\nA) Forget it  B) Decide, act, document and review  C) Report to the press  D) Resign\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Contemporaneous documentation matters because **undocumented good conduct is indistinguishable from misconduct two years later**.\nA) Avoidance is not resolution. C) Internal escalation comes first. D) Resignation is rarely required and does not resolve the client's position.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A conservative client insists on an unsuitable high-risk allocation. The correct professional response is to:\nA) Silently comply  B) Advise against it in writing, quantify the risk, and record his written instruction if he persists  C) Refuse and terminate immediately without explanation  D) Comply and hide the file\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The file must show **what you advised**, not only what he wanted. Educate, quantify, offer a compromise, and document.\nA) Silent compliance breaches the duty of care. C) Abrupt termination without explanation abandons the client. D) Concealment compounds the breach.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An adviser realises a recommendation made last year was wrong and the client has not noticed the ₹4 lakh loss. He should:\nA) Say nothing  B) Proactively disclose, explain and propose a remedy  C) Wait for the client to ask  D) Hide it in the annual statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Honesty is not conditional on being caught; proactive disclosure is a direct requirement of the duty of loyalty.\nA) and C) both make the duty depend on the client's vigilance. D) Burying a loss in a statement is active concealment.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A client offers to pay the advisory fee in cash to \"keep it simple\". The adviser should:\nA) Accept it  B) Decline and insist on payment through the banking channel  C) Accept half in cash  D) Accept and issue no receipt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cash dealing is prohibited and untraceable; payments must run through the banking channel from the client's own account.\nA), C) and D) all involve accepting cash, and D) adds a documentation failure.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An adviser discovers a colleague is systematically mis-selling. The right first step is to:\nA) Ignore it out of loyalty  B) Escalate internally to the compliance officer / senior management  C) Tell the clients' rivals  D) Post about it publicly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The Code puts compliance and ethical standards on **senior management**; the compliance officer exists precisely for this.\nA) Loyalty to a peer never outranks the duty to clients. C) Nonsensical. D) Public disclosure before internal escalation is disproportionate and may itself breach confidentiality.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which statement about ethics and compliance is correct?\nA) They are identical  B) Compliance is the floor; ethics governs the situations the rulebook has not reached  C) Compliance is higher than ethics  D) Neither matters if returns are good\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A firm can be technically compliant and still behave badly; ethics covers the unwritten space.\nA) Compliance is a subset. C) Reverses the relationship. D) Good returns never cure a breach of duty.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Why is disclosure alone sometimes an insufficient response to a conflict?\nA) Disclosure is illegal  B) Some conflicts are so severe that only avoidance protects the client  C) Clients always read disclosures  D) Disclosure is expensive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Where the incentive is large enough to distort the advice itself, a paragraph in a document does not neutralise it.\nA) Disclosure is required, not illegal. C) Most clients do not read or fully grasp disclosures — part of the problem. D) Cost is not the objection.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** SEBI permits an investment adviser to charge a client family:\nA) Both fee modes at once  B) Either a fixed fee up to ₹1,25,000 p.a. or up to 2.5% of AUA p.a.  C) Any amount agreed  D) Only commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — One mode at a time, with the advance fee also capped.\nA) The modes are alternatives, not cumulative. C) The caps are mandatory. D) An adviser is remunerated by the client, not by commission on advised assets.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** \"Segregation of advice and distribution\" means:\nA) Advisers cannot advise  B) An individual adviser cannot both advise and distribute to the same client  C) Distribution is banned  D) Advice must be free\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It removes the commission incentive from the advisory relationship; a non-individual adviser must keep the two in **separate identifiable divisions** with client-level segregation.\nA) Advisers advise; that is the licence. C) Distribution remains a legitimate separate business. D) Advisers charge fees.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A client discovers years later that a chunk of his premium went to distributor commission he was never told about. The ethical failure is:\nA) Churning  B) Non-disclosure of costs  C) Front-running  D) Insider trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Hidden cost** — the client could not evaluate the recommendation because he could not see what he was paying.\nA) A single purchase is not churning. C) No order anticipation. D) No price-sensitive company information.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Which single item is the strongest evidence that the duty of care was discharged?\nA) A happy client  B) A documented risk profile, a suitability rationale and a record of the advice given  C) High returns  D) A large fee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Care is judged on **process and documentation**, not on outcome.\nA) Satisfaction can coexist with an unsuitable portfolio, especially in a bull market. C) Good returns can result from unsuitable risk. D) Fees prove nothing.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** Because advice is a credence good, the practical consequence is that:\nA) Market discipline alone reliably weeds out bad advisers  B) Ethics and regulation must substitute for the client's inability to judge quality  C) Advice needs no regulation  D) Clients can price advice accurately\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — When the buyer cannot assess quality even after purchase, market feedback fails and other safeguards must fill the gap.\nA) Feedback arrives far too slowly to discipline the market. C) The opposite follows. D) Clients cannot price what they cannot evaluate.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** An adviser passes a client's portfolio details to an insurance agent in return for a lead. This is:\nA) Networking  B) A breach of client confidentiality and an undisclosed conflict  C) Front-running  D) Permitted with no conditions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Client information may be used only to serve that client, and the referral benefit is an undisclosed conflict.\nA) Networking does not involve trading client data. C) No order anticipation. D) It is a clear breach.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** After giving advice contrary to a client's risk profile at the client's insistence, the file must contain:\nA) Only the executed transaction  B) The advice given, the risk explained, and the client's written instruction to proceed against advice  C) Nothing extra  D) A backdated risk profile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The record must show the adviser did his job even though the client overrode it.\nA) A transaction alone looks identical to unsuitable advice. C) Silence in the file is read against the adviser. D) Backdating is falsification of records — a far graver offence.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** In an annual compliance audit, \"a client form left undated\" is best classified as:\nA) Systemic  B) A procedural/documentation observation  C) Fraud  D) Not an observation at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Correct the record, tighten the checklist, close it. The response must match the severity.\nA) Systemic means a missing process, such as no compliance officer at all. C) There is no dishonesty. D) It is still an observation and must be closed.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** An audit finds the adviser charged above the fee cap. The complete response is to:\nA) Note it for next year  B) Refund the excess to the client, fix the billing process, and report the finding with the action taken  C) Only change the fee schedule  D) Argue with the auditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Remediation must reach the **client**, not just the process; fixing the checklist while keeping the money is not remediation.\nA) Delay compounds the breach. C) A process fix alone leaves the client out of pocket. D) Disputing rather than correcting is the classic bad response.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Why is a repeat audit observation treated most seriously?\nA) It is a clerical matter  B) It proves the earlier fix was cosmetic and the failure may be treated as wilful  C) Auditors dislike repetition  D) It is not serious\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The firm already knew of the failure and did not truly correct it.\nA) Repetition escalates it beyond clerical. C) Auditor preference is irrelevant. D) It is the most serious category.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** An adviser suppresses an adverse audit finding rather than reporting it. The consequence is:\nA) Nothing  B) A fresh and more serious violation than the original lapse  C) A minor note  D) Automatic condonation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Concealment converts an inadvertent lapse into deliberate misreporting.\nA) Non-reporting is itself actionable. C) It is not minor. D) There is no automatic condonation.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Under the SEC's framework, the duty of care includes:\nA) Guaranteeing returns  B) Best-interest advice, seeking best execution, and monitoring over the relationship  C) Selling in-house products  D) Maximising turnover\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Care is ongoing, not a one-time act at the point of recommendation.\nA) No regulator permits guaranteed returns on market advice. C) That is a conflict, not a duty. D) Turnover maximisation is churning.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** A US adviser's written code of ethics must, among other things, require:\nA) Higher commissions  B) Reporting of personal securities transactions by access persons  C) Anonymous trading  D) Client-funded advertising\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Personal-trading reporting and pre-clearance of IPOs and private placements are core to preventing front-running and self-dealing.\nA) Commissions are not a code requirement. C) Anonymity defeats the purpose of the rule. D) Irrelevant.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** The SEC's compliance rule requires an adviser to have:\nA) No policies  B) Written policies and procedures, an annual review, and a Chief Compliance Officer  C) Only an annual audit  D) A guaranteed return policy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Written, reviewed annually, and owned by a named CCO.\nA) The opposite of the rule. C) The rule is about ongoing policies, not solely an audit. D) Prohibited everywhere.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) in the US applies principally to:\nA) Registered investment advisers  B) Broker-dealers making recommendations to retail customers  C) Custodians  D) Exchanges\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reg BI raises the broker's standard at the point of recommendation, while advisers remain subject to the full fiduciary duty.\nA) Advisers are governed by the Advisers Act fiduciary standard. C) and D) are not making retail recommendations.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Australia's \"safe harbour\" under the best interests duty is:\nA) A tax shelter  B) A checklist of steps an adviser must evidence to show he acted in the client's best interests  C) An offshore account  D) A guarantee fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It includes identifying objectives and needs, making reasonable enquiries, assessing one's own expertise, investigating products reasonably, and taking any other step in the client's interests.\nA) and C) are unrelated financial concepts. D) There is no such guarantee fund.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Australia's \"opt-in\" and Fee Disclosure Statement requirements exist to:\nA) Increase fees  B) Stop ongoing fee arrangements running indefinitely without renewal or evidence of service  C) Hide costs  D) Ban advice\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Clients must periodically renew ongoing arrangements, and receive an annual statement of fees paid and services actually provided.\nA) The intent is to curb fees for no service. C) They increase transparency. D) Advice continues under a licence.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A ₹25,00,000 portfolio is turned over 8 times a year at a 0.50% round-trip cost. What is the annual trading cost?\nA) ₹12,500  B) ₹1,00,000  C) ₹25,000  D) ₹2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Value traded = 8 × ₹25,00,000 = **₹2,00,00,000**. Cost = 0.50% × ₹2,00,00,000 = **₹1,00,000**.\nA) Is 0.5% of one turn of half the portfolio. C) Applies the cost to the portfolio once, ignoring the 8× turnover. D) Applies 1%, double the stated round-trip cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** For the same ₹25,00,000 portfolio, what is that ₹1,00,000 cost as a percentage of the portfolio?\nA) 0.50%  B) 4.0%  C) 8.0%  D) 0.25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹1,00,000 ÷ ₹25,00,000 = **0.04 = 4.0% per year**, before any tax.\nA) Is the per-trade round-trip rate, not the annual drag. C) Doubles the answer. D) Is what a disciplined 0.5× turnover would have cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A disciplined manager would have turned the same ₹25,00,000 portfolio over only 0.5 times. How much did churning add in cost?\nA) ₹6,250  B) ₹93,750  C) ₹1,00,000  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Disciplined cost = 0.50% × (0.5 × ₹25,00,000) = 0.50% × ₹12,50,000 = **₹6,250**. Excess = 1,00,000 − 6,250 = **₹93,750**.\nA) Is the disciplined cost itself. C) Is the total cost, not the excess. D) The excess is 3.75% of the portfolio every year.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** ₹10,00,000 grows for 15 years at 12% gross. Compare a 2.00% TER with a 1.10% TER. What is the difference in final value?\nA) ₹1,35,000  B) ₹5,44,000  C) ₹90,000  D) ₹13,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Net returns are **10.00%** and **10.90%**. Values: 10,00,000 × 1.10¹⁵ = **₹41,77,000** and 10,00,000 × 1.109¹⁵ = **₹47,21,000**. Difference = **₹5,44,000**.\nA) Is roughly the simple 0.90% × 15 years on the original sum, ignoring compounding. C) Is one year's 0.90% gap. D) Grossly overstates it.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A scheme's NAV rose from ₹10 to ₹28 over 9 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 180%  B) 20.0%  C) 12.1%  D) 28.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — CAGR = (28 ÷ 10)^(1/9) − 1 = 2.8^0.1111 − 1 = **1.121 − 1 = 12.1% per annum**.\nA) Is the **absolute** return over nine years. B) Is a plausible-looking but arithmetically wrong annualisation. D) Simply repeats the ending NAV as a percentage.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Why is quoting the ₹10-to-₹28 move as \"180% returns\" an ethical issue?\nA) The figure is false  B) The figure is true but the missing nine-year period turns 12.1% p.a. into a headline that reads like a windfall  C) It is too small  D) It is a tax matter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Misrepresentation does not require a false number; **omitting the period, the basis or the costs** is enough.\nA) 18 ÷ 10 is arithmetically accurate. C) The size is not the problem. D) Tax is a separate disclosure.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An adviser buys 10,000 shares at ₹420 ahead of a client's large order, then sells at ₹455 once the order moves the price. What is his gain, and what is the offence?\nA) ₹35,000; churning  B) ₹3,50,000; front-running  C) ₹4,55,000; mis-selling  D) ₹3,50,000; insider trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Gain = 10,000 × (455 − 420) = 10,000 × ₹35 = **₹3,50,000**, made out of the client's own order — **front-running** under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003.\nA) Understates the gain by a factor of ten and misnames the offence. C) Is the sale value of part of the position, and mis-selling is unrelated. D) The arithmetic is right but insider trading requires price-sensitive information about the **company**.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A client family has ₹80,00,000 of Assets Under Advice. What is the maximum annual fee under the AUA mode?\nA) ₹1,25,000  B) ₹2,00,000  C) ₹8,00,000  D) ₹80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 2.5% × ₹80,00,000 = **₹2,00,000 per annum**.\nA) Is the cap under the alternative fixed-fee mode. C) Applies 10%. D) Applies 1%.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** A client family has ₹40,00,000 of Assets Under Advice. Which fee mode permits the higher fee, and by how much?\nA) AUA mode, by ₹25,000  B) Fixed-fee mode, by ₹25,000  C) They are equal  D) AUA mode, by ₹1,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — AUA mode allows 2.5% × ₹40,00,000 = **₹1,00,000**; the fixed-fee cap is **₹1,25,000**. The fixed-fee mode permits **₹25,000 more**.\nA) Reverses the two modes. C) They differ by ₹25,000. D) Misstates both the direction and the amount.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An adviser charges a family ₹1,50,000 a year under the fixed-fee mode. What is the position?\nA) Permitted  B) It exceeds the ₹1,25,000 cap by ₹25,000 and the excess must be refunded  C) Permitted if the client agrees  D) It exceeds the cap by ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 1,50,000 − 1,25,000 = **₹25,000 excess**; the fee cap is mandatory and the excess is a client remediation item.\nA) It breaches the cap. C) Client consent cannot override a regulatory cap. D) The whole fee is not the excess.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** An adviser's audit report for FY 2025-26 is dated 20 September 2026. By when must the adverse findings and action taken be reported?\nA) 31 March 2026  B) 20 October 2026  C) 31 December 2026  D) No deadline\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Within **one month of the date of the audit report** — 20 September 2026 + one month = **20 October 2026**.\nA) Is the financial year end, before the audit exists. C) Is roughly three months later. D) A deadline is prescribed.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** For a financial year ending 31 March 2026, by when must the compliance audit itself be completed?\nA) 30 April 2026  B) 30 September 2026  C) 31 March 2027  D) 31 December 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Six months** from the year end = **30 September 2026**.\nA) Is one month. C) Is twelve months. D) Is nine months.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A client is switched out of a ₹6,00,000 equity fund held 7 months with a ₹60,000 gain. Exit load is 1% and short-term capital gains tax is 20%. What is the total cost of the switch?\nA) ₹6,000  B) ₹18,000  C) ₹12,000  D) ₹60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exit load = 1% × ₹6,00,000 = **₹6,000**. STCG tax = 20% × ₹60,000 = **₹12,000**. Total = **₹18,000**, i.e. **3% of the corpus** to move to a near-identical fund.\nA) Counts only the exit load. C) Counts only the tax. D) Is the gain itself, not the cost.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A ₹50,00,000 portfolio compounds for 20 years. What is the difference between an 11% net outcome and an 8% net outcome after a 3% annual cost drag?\nA) About ₹30 lakh  B) About ₹1.70 crore  C) About ₹50 lakh  D) There is no difference\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — At 11%: 50,00,000 × 1.11²⁰ = 50,00,000 × 8.0623 = **₹4.03 crore**. At 8%: 50,00,000 × 1.08²⁰ = 50,00,000 × 4.6610 = **₹2.33 crore**. Difference ≈ **₹1.70 crore**.\nA) and C) badly understate the effect of compounding a cost gap over two decades. D) The gap exceeds three times the original investment.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** What proportion of an 11% gross return is consumed by a 3% annual cost drag?\nA) 3.0%  B) 27.3%  C) 11.0%  D) 8.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 3 ÷ 11 = **0.2727 = 27.3% of the gross return**. This is why cost disclosure is treated as an ethical obligation, not a technicality.\nA) Is the drag expressed on assets, not on return. C) Is the gross return. D) Is the net return.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A ₹5,00,000 investment is placed in a closed-end product paying the distributor a 6% upfront commission. How much of the client's money funds that commission?\nA) ₹5,000  B) ₹30,000  C) ₹3,000  D) Nothing — the manufacturer pays it\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 6% × ₹5,00,000 = **₹30,000**. It is paid by the manufacturer but ultimately borne by the investor through charges.\nA) Applies 1%. C) Applies 0.6%. D) Treats the manufacturer as an independent source of money, which it is not.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** A client is sold a ₹1,20,000-a-year policy giving ₹12,00,000 of life cover. Term cover of ₹1,00,00,000 would cost about ₹15,000. What is the protection shortfall, and what is the label?\nA) ₹12 lakh; churning  B) ₹88 lakh; mis-selling  C) ₹1 crore; front-running  D) No shortfall\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Cover shortfall = ₹1,00,00,000 − ₹12,00,000 = **₹88,00,000**, while ₹1,05,000 a year (₹1,20,000 − ₹15,000) could have been invested separately. Bundling protection with investment to earn a larger commission is classic **mis-selling**.\nA) Is the cover actually provided, and one sale is not churning. C) Is the term cover itself, and no order is being anticipated. D) The shortfall is very large.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An adviser shows only the fund's best three-year stretch out of ten years of data. The most accurate criticism is:\nA) The data is fabricated  B) It is a cherry-picked window; rolling returns across the whole period would be the fair presentation  C) Three years is too long  D) Past performance is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Choosing the start date is choosing the answer; **rolling returns** remove the single-lucky-date effect.\nA) The data may be perfectly genuine — selection, not fabrication, is the fault. C) The period length is not the issue. D) Past performance is relevant context; it is simply not a promise.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** An individual SEBI-registered investment adviser also earns distribution commission from the same client's mutual funds. This is:\nA) Encouraged  B) Prohibited — an individual adviser cannot both advise and distribute to the same client  C) Allowed with disclosure  D) Allowed above ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Segregation of advice from distribution removes the commission incentive from the advisory relationship.\nA) It is the conflict the rule was written to eliminate. C) Disclosure does not cure this particular conflict for an individual adviser. D) No such threshold exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An adviser is found to have front-run client orders. Which set of consequences is realistic?\nA) A warning letter only  B) Monetary penalty, disgorgement of unlawful gains, debarment from the market and loss of registration  C) No action  D) Only a client refund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Fraudulent and unfair trade practices attract the full range of SEBI remedies, plus severe reputational loss.\nA) Far too light for a fraudulent practice. C) SEBI acts on such cases. D) A refund alone ignores the unlawful gain and the market-integrity dimension.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** An adviser has (i) failed to date a form, (ii) not disclosed a related-party commission, and (iii) never carried out risk profiling for any client. Which is the systemic failure?\nA) The undated form  B) Never carrying out risk profiling for any client  C) The related-party commission  D) All are equally minor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A control that does not exist **for any client** is systemic; it means the suitability process was never built.\nA) Is a procedural observation. C) Is material and serious, but relates to specific transactions rather than a missing firm-wide process. D) They differ sharply in severity.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An adviser's employer offers a 3% bonus on every in-house PMS referral, while a cheaper external option suits the client better. The best response is:\nA) Disclose the bonus and still recommend the in-house product  B) Recommend the option that suits the client, and escalate the incentive structure internally as a conflict to be removed  C) Say nothing  D) Refuse to advise the client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Avoid before disclose**: an incentive large enough to distort the recommendation should be removed, not merely announced.\nA) Disclosure does not make an inferior recommendation acceptable. C) Silence breaches Reg 15(1). D) Abandoning the client is not a remedy.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** A client asks for advice on a complex structured product the adviser does not understand. Which Code of Conduct head is engaged?\nA) Capability  B) Fair charges  C) Compliance  D) Information to clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Capability** requires the adviser to have and employ the resources and competence needed; if he lacks them he must acquire them or decline and refer.\nB) Concerns the level and basis of fees. C) Concerns regulatory obligations generally. D) Concerns disclosure, which cannot substitute for competence.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** An adviser personally holds a stock he is about to recommend to clients. He must:\nA) Sell it secretly first  B) Disclose the holding, and never trade ahead of client orders  C) Say nothing  D) Buy more before recommending\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Holdings and positions in recommended securities must be **disclosed**, and personal trades must never precede client action.\nA) Selling ahead of the recommendation is itself abusive. C) Non-disclosure breaches Reg 15(1). D) That is front-running.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** A firm advertises a \"model portfolio\" return of 26% p.a. that was never actually earned by any client. This is:\nA) Acceptable if labelled a model  B) Misrepresentation — back-tested returns presented as achieved results mislead  C) A hidden cost  D) Front-running\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Back-tests are built with hindsight, ignore real costs, slippage and client cash flows, and must never be presented as achieved performance.\nA) A label in small print does not cure a headline designed to mislead. C) Costs are a separate issue, though back-tests usually ignore them too. D) No order anticipation.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** An audit finds no records of client interactions or advice given. The gravest consequence is that:\nA) Storage costs rise  B) The adviser cannot evidence suitability or the advice actually given if a complaint arises  C) The auditor is inconvenienced  D) Nothing follows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Records are the only proof the duty of care was discharged; without them, the adviser's version has no support.\nA) Trivial. C) The auditor's convenience is irrelevant. D) Record-keeping is a specific regulatory obligation.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** Which jurisdiction addresses remuneration conflicts by BANNING the conflicted payment rather than merely disclosing it?\nA) India  B) Australia  C) United States  D) None\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Under **FOFA**, commissions and volume-based benefits are banned for personal advice on relevant products.\nA) India requires avoidance, management and disclosure, plus segregation of advice from distribution. C) The US relies primarily on full and fair disclosure with informed consent. D) Australia does exactly this.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** What is the common thread across the Indian, US and Australian regimes?\nA) Identical rules  B) Know the client, act in his best interest, control conflicts, disclose fully, document and review  C) Commission-based advice  D) No documentation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — They differ mainly in how far they trust disclosure versus outright prohibition, not in the underlying principle.\nA) The mechanics differ considerably. C) Australia bans it and India segregates it. D) All three regimes are documentation-heavy.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** An adviser earning ₹1,25,000 a year from a client for ten years takes a hidden one-off commission of ₹45,000 and loses the relationship. The lost fee income is what multiple of the commission?\nA) About 3 times  B) About 28 times  C) About 10 times  D) Less than the commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Lifetime fee = 10 × ₹1,25,000 = **₹12,50,000**. Ratio = 12,50,000 ÷ 45,000 ≈ **27.8, i.e. about 28 times** — before counting the referrals also lost.\nA) Understates it by roughly a factor of nine. C) Is the number of years, not the ratio. D) The fee stream is vastly larger.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which statement best summarises this chapter?\nA) Ethics matters only when a rule applies  B) Advice rests on trust in a relationship of information asymmetry, so an adviser is a fiduciary owing loyalty and care, must avoid, manage or disclose conflicts, and must document everything  C) Returns matter more than conduct  D) Disclosure cures every conflict\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — It captures the **why** (asymmetry, agency), the **standard** (fiduciary loyalty and care), the **method** (avoid, manage, disclose) and the **proof** (documentation).\nA) Ethics governs precisely where the rulebook is silent. C) Good returns never cure a breach of duty. D) Some conflicts must be avoided outright.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Label each behaviour before you calculate anything.\n\n### Case: Mr Deshmukh and the Adviser Who Wore Two Hats\n\n**Mr Ramesh Deshmukh**, 58, of Nagpur, retires in two years and needs about **₹60,000 a month** from age 60. He hands his **₹1,20,00,000** corpus to *Shreyas Wealth*, a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser, and is profiled as **moderately conservative**.\n\nOver the year, the following happens:\n\n| # | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | The promoter's brother owns the mutual fund distribution firm that receives commission on the funds recommended. Mr Deshmukh is never told. |\n| 2 | He is advised into a **₹15,00,000** single-premium policy with a 5-year lock-in that pays a **6% upfront commission**. |\n| 3 | His **₹40,00,000** equity fund holding is switched four times in the year — **₹1,60,00,000** of switch value in total — each switch attracting a **1% exit load**. |\n| 4 | The pitch used a \"past performance\" of **24% p.a.**, being the best three-year window of one scheme, quoted gross of expenses. |\n| 5 | An advisory fee of **₹1,50,000** is charged for the year under the **fixed-fee mode**. |\n| 6 | The FY 2025-26 compliance audit flags: no risk profile on record for three clients, the fee cap breach, and undisclosed conflicts. |\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** Event 1 — the undisclosed family distribution firm — is best described as:\nA) Normal business  B) An undisclosed conflict of interest, breaching Regulation 15(1)  C) Insider trading  D) Churning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Reg 15(1) requires an adviser to act in a **fiduciary capacity** and to **disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise**. A related party earning commission on the very products recommended is a textbook material conflict, and here it was concealed. Preferably it should have been **avoided** altogether, since advice and distribution must be segregated.\nA) A concealed related-party payment is not \"normal business\". C) No unpublished price-sensitive company information is involved. D) Churning concerns transaction frequency, which is event 3.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** Event 2 — how much upfront commission does the ₹15,00,000 policy generate?\nA) ₹15,000  B) ₹90,000  C) ₹9,000  D) ₹1,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 6% × ₹15,00,000 = **₹90,000**, paid immediately and borne by the client through the product's charges. A 5-year lock-in on a man who needs monthly income from age 60 is also a **horizon mismatch**, making this **mis-selling** as well as a conflict.\nA) Applies 1%. C) Applies 0.6%. D) Applies 10%.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** Event 3 — what do the four switches cost in exit loads, and as what percentage of the ₹40,00,000 holding?\nA) ₹40,000; 1.0%  B) ₹1,60,000; 4.0%  C) ₹16,000; 0.4%  D) ₹4,00,000; 10.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Exit load = 1% × ₹1,60,00,000 of switch value = **₹1,60,000**, which is 1,60,000 ÷ 40,00,000 = **4.0% of the holding**, before short-term capital gains tax. Four switches with no change of strategy is **churning**.\nA) Charges the load only once on the holding. C) Applies 0.1%. D) Applies 10% instead of 1%.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** Event 5 — the ₹1,50,000 fee under the fixed-fee mode. What is the position, and what would the AUA mode have allowed?\nA) Permitted; AUA would allow ₹1,20,000  B) It exceeds the ₹1,25,000 cap by ₹25,000; AUA mode would have allowed up to ₹3,00,000  C) It exceeds the cap by ₹1,50,000  D) Permitted because the client signed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The fixed-fee cap is **₹1,25,000 per annum per family**, so the excess is 1,50,000 − 1,25,000 = **₹25,000**, which must be refunded. Under the alternative **AUA mode**, 2.5% × ₹1,20,00,000 = **₹3,00,000** would have been permissible — but an adviser may use only **one mode at a time**, and cannot retrospectively pick the more convenient one.\nA) Misstates the AUA figure and treats the breach as permitted. C) The whole fee is not the excess. D) Client consent cannot override a mandatory cap.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** Event 6 — how should Shreyas Wealth address the audit observations?\nA) Note them and move on  B) Fix the checklists only  C) Refund the ₹25,000 excess, complete and document the missing risk profiles, issue conflict disclosures, obtain management approval of the action-taken report, and file the adverse findings with the action taken within one month of the audit report  D) Dispute the findings with the auditor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Proper remediation reaches the **client** as well as the process: refund the excess fee, build and evidence the risk profiles, disclose the related-party conflict, fix the root cause (on-boarding that allowed activation without a signed profile, and billing that did not enforce the cap), obtain **management/board approval**, and report the **adverse findings together with the action taken** within **one month of the date of the audit report**, through the administration and supervisory body.\n\n**The wider lesson:** every one of the six events traces back to a single failure — the firm was **paid by the manufacturer while claiming to serve the client**. Remove that, and mis-selling, churning and the inflated performance pitch all lose their motive. This is exactly why Australia **bans** conflicted remuneration and India **segregates** advice from distribution, rather than relying on disclosure alone.\n\nA) Ignoring observations guarantees they repeat, and a repeat finding is treated as wilful. B) Fixing the checklist while keeping the excess fee is not remediation. D) Arguing instead of correcting is the classic bad response and does not stop the reporting clock.\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "20", "title": "Grievance Redress Mechanism", "module": "Module 6: Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics", "notes": "# Chapter 20: Grievance Redress Mechanism — Short Notes\n\n> **Module 6 · Operations, Regulatory Environment, Compliance & Ethics (30 marks).**\n> The final chapter, and one of the easiest to score in — provided you memorise **who you go to, in what order, and within how many days**. Almost every question here is really the same question: *given this complaint, what is the NEXT step?* Learn the ladders and the numbers and the chapter is done.\n\n---\n\n## 20.0 ⭐ The One Idea That Runs Through the Whole Chapter\n\nEvery redress system in Indian finance — securities, banking, insurance, pension — is built on the **same three-step ladder**:\n\n> ### 🧠 **INTERMEDIARY first → REGULATOR next → TRIBUNAL / COURT last.**\n\n**Why this order exists.** The firm that caused the problem is the only party that holds the file, the recording, the audit trail and the money. It can fix a genuine error in a day. A regulator supervising 5,000 intermediaries cannot. So the law forces the complaint down to the level where it can actually be solved, and only escalates it if that level fails. A complaint filed straight with the regulator or the tribunal, skipping the firm, is **not maintainable** — it will be bounced back, and the clock will have been wasted.\n\n| Sector | Step 1 — the entity | Step 2 — the regulator's portal | Step 3 — the adjudicator |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Securities** | The intermediary / listed company | **SCORES** (SEBI) | **ODR portal** → arbitration; **SAT** for appeals against SEBI orders |\n| **Banking / NBFC** | Branch → Nodal Officer → **Internal Ombudsman** | RBI **CMS** portal | **RBI Ombudsman (RB-IOS 2021)** → Appellate Authority (ED, RBI) |\n| **Insurance** | Insurer's **Grievance Redressal Officer** | **Bima Bharosa** (IRDAI) | **Insurance Ombudsman** |\n| **Pension (NPS)** | POP / Nodal office / CRA via **CGMS** | **NPS Trust** | **PFRDA Ombudsman** → PFRDA → **SAT** |\n| **Any consumer** | The seller / service provider | — | **District → State → National Commission** (CPA 2019) |\n\n> ⚠️ **The single most common exam trap:** an option that sends the investor **straight to SEBI, straight to the Ombudsman, or straight to SAT** without first approaching the firm. It is always wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 20.1 The Consumer Protection Act, 2019\n\nThe **Consumer Protection Act, 2019** replaced the Consumer Protection Act, 1986 and came into force from **20 July 2020**. It is the general, all-sector safety net — a mutual fund investor, a bank depositor, a policyholder and a person who bought a faulty geyser all use the same machinery.\n\n### Who is a \"consumer\"?\n\nA **consumer** is a person who **buys goods or avails services for a consideration** which has been **paid, or promised, or partly paid and partly promised**, or under any **system of deferred payment**.\n\nThree points the exam pushes on:\n\n1. **Consideration is essential.** Something must have been paid or promised. A **free service** (or a purely gratuitous favour) generally does **not** create a consumer relationship. A fee-paying advisory client clearly does.\n2. **The user counts too.** A person who **uses the goods or is a beneficiary of the service with the approval of the buyer** is also a consumer. So a wife availing a service her husband paid for is a consumer.\n3. **\"Commercial purpose\" is excluded — with one big carve-out.** A person who buys goods **for resale or for any commercial purpose** is *not* a consumer. **But** the exclusion does **not** apply where the goods/services are used **exclusively for earning a livelihood by means of self-employment.**\n\n> 🧠 **The livelihood carve-out.** A man who buys one auto-rickshaw and drives it himself to feed his family **is** a consumer. A firm that buys forty auto-rickshaws to run a fleet **is not**. Same vehicle, different purpose.\n\nThe 2019 Act expressly extends \"buys\" and \"avails\" to **offline and online transactions**, including **electronic means, teleshopping, direct selling and multi-level marketing** — the reason it matters today, when almost every financial product is bought on an app.\n\n### The three-tier redressal machinery\n\n| Commission | Full name | Presided over by | Appeal lies to |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **District** | District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (DCDRC) | A person qualified to be a **District Judge** | **State Commission** |\n| **State** | State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (SCDRC) | A sitting or retired **High Court judge** | **National Commission** |\n| **National** | National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC) | A sitting or retired **Supreme Court judge** | **Supreme Court** |\n\n### ⭐ Pecuniary limits — read this box twice\n\nThe Act as **originally enacted in 2019** set the limits by the *value of the goods or services paid as consideration*:\n\n| Commission | **Original 2019 limit** |\n|---|---|\n| District | up to **₹1 crore** |\n| State | above ₹1 crore up to **₹10 crore** |\n| National | above **₹10 crore** |\n\nThese were **revised downward** by the Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction of the District Commission, the State Commission and the National Commission) **Rules, 2021** (notified 30 December 2021), which are the limits in force today:\n\n| Commission | **Current limit (2021 Rules)** |\n|---|---|\n| **District** | up to **₹50 lakh** |\n| **State** | above ₹50 lakh up to **₹2 crore** |\n| **National** | above **₹2 crore** |\n\n> ⚠️ **Exam trap.** Both sets of figures appear in circulating study material. Learn **both**, and read the question stem: if it says *\"as revised\"* or *\"currently\"*, use **₹50 lakh / ₹2 crore**; if it says *\"as originally enacted in 2019\"*, use **₹1 crore / ₹10 crore**. The *structure* (three tiers, lowest tier first) never changes.\n>\n> ⚠️ Note also **what** is measured: the jurisdiction is fixed by the **value of the consideration paid**, not by the compensation demanded.\n\n### Other provisions you must know\n\n| Item | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Limitation to file** | **2 years** from the date the cause of action arose (condonable for sufficient cause) |\n| **Where to file** | Where the opposite party resides/carries on business, **or where the cause of action arose, or — new in 2019 — where the COMPLAINANT resides or personally works for gain** |\n| **Filing fee** | **No fee** for claims up to **₹5 lakh**; slab-based fees above that |\n| **E-filing** | Through the **e-Daakhil** portal |\n| **Appeal: District → State** | Within **45 days**, and the appellant must first deposit **50%** of any amount ordered against him |\n| **Appeal: State → National** | Within **30 days** |\n| **Appeal: National → Supreme Court** | Within **30 days** (against orders in the National Commission's *original* jurisdiction) |\n| **Mediation** | Consumer Mediation Cells are attached to each Commission; a case may be referred for settlement |\n| **Non-compliance with an order** | Imprisonment of **1 month to 3 years**, and/or fine of **₹25,000 to ₹1 lakh** |\n| **Who may complain** | The consumer; one or more consumers with the same interest; a registered consumer association; the Central/State Government; the **CCPA**; a legal heir |\n\n### Two innovations of the 2019 Act\n\n- **CCPA — the Central Consumer Protection Authority.** A *regulator* for consumer rights (the 1986 Act had none). It acts against **unfair trade practices and false or misleading advertisements**, has an **Investigation Wing headed by a Director General**, and can order recalls, refunds and the discontinuation of an advertisement. Penalties for a misleading advertisement run to **₹10 lakh** for a first offence and **₹50 lakh** for repeat offences, and an **endorser** (a celebrity) can be barred from endorsing for **1 year**, and **3 years** for repeat offences.\n- **Product liability.** A manufacturer, service provider or seller can be made liable for harm caused by a defective product or deficient service — a claim that did not previously exist in the consumer forum.\n\n### 🧠 The six consumer rights\n\n**Safety · Information · Choice · Be heard · Redressal · Consumer education.**\n\n> 🧠 **Mnemonic — \"SIC-HRE\"** (*\"sick here\"*): **S**afety, **I**nformation, **C**hoice, **H**eard, **R**edressal, **E**ducation.\n\n---\n\n## 20.2 Investor Grievance Redressal — the General Picture\n\nA **grievance** is any expression of dissatisfaction by a client about a service, product or conduct that the client believes fell short of what was promised or required. Note what it is **not**:\n\n| | Meaning | Treatment |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Query** | A request for information (\"what is my folio balance?\") | Answer it; not a complaint |\n| **Request** | A service instruction (\"change my address\") | Execute it; not a complaint |\n| **Grievance / complaint** | An allegation of **deficiency, delay, error, loss or misconduct** | **Log it, track it, resolve it within the regulatory clock** |\n| **Dispute** | A contested claim on **facts or money** that the parties cannot settle themselves | Goes to **conciliation / arbitration / court** — a regulator cannot decide it |\n\n> ⚠️ **The most under-appreciated point in this chapter:** **SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA do not adjudicate individual claims.** Their portals (SCORES, CMS, Bima Bharosa, CGMS) **route, monitor, follow up and enforce timelines**. They do not decide who is right about disputed facts or award damages on the merits. That job belongs to the **Ombudsman, an arbitrator under ODR, a consumer commission or a civil court.** Any option saying \"SEBI will order the broker to compensate the investor after examining the evidence\" is describing an adjudicator, not a facilitator.\n\n### Why a formal system, rather than \"just sort it out\"?\n\n- **Regulatory obligation** — SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA all *require* a documented mechanism, and inspection/audit will ask to see it.\n- **Early warning** — a cluster of complaints of the *same nature* is the cheapest fraud- and mis-selling detector a firm owns.\n- **Evidence** — if the matter reaches an Ombudsman, arbitrator or SAT, the contemporaneous log is the firm's defence.\n- **Trust** — advisory is a relationship business; how a firm behaves when something goes *wrong* is what clients actually remember.\n\n---\n\n## 20.3 ⭐ Key Elements of a Robust Grievance Recording and Redress System\n\nThe syllabus lists seven elements. Learn them as a **life cycle of one complaint**, and each one becomes obvious.\n\n| # | Element | What is recorded | **Why it matters** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Source of receipt** | Email, letter, phone, walk-in, website form, social media, **SCORES**, **ODR portal**, regulator-forwarded, exchange/AMFI | Tells you which **regulatory clock** applies and whether a regulator is already watching. A SCORES complaint carries a hard 21-day deadline; a walk-in does not. |\n| 2 | **Date and time of receipt** | Timestamp, and a **unique complaint reference number** issued to the client | **This starts the clock.** Every timeline in this chapter is measured from receipt. Without a timestamp, ageing and compliance cannot be proved. |\n| 3 | **Nature of complaint** | A standard **category** — mis-selling, unsuitable advice, fee/billing dispute, unauthorised transaction, delay in service, non-receipt of documents, staff conduct, technology failure | Enables **root-cause analysis**. Twenty complaints of one type is a **process defect**, not twenty accidents. |\n| 4 | **Internal or escalated** | Can this be closed by the firm, or must it go to an external entity (regulator, exchange, Ombudsman, ODR)? | Decides the route and the deadline; also flags matters that must be **reported** to the regulator. |\n| 5 | **Status / action taken** | Open → Under investigation → Awaiting client input → Resolved → Closed → Reopened, with the **reasoning and evidence** on file | Nothing may sit in \"open\" without an owner. The written reason is what an inspector reads. |\n| 6 | **Time taken / ageing report** | Days from receipt to closure, bucketed | An **ageing report** exposes complaints quietly going stale. Averages hide the outliers; buckets do not. |\n| 7 | **Escalation mechanism** | A published **escalation matrix**: named officer, designation, email, phone and turnaround at **each** level | Gives the client a route upward inside the firm *before* going to a regulator, and gives staff a defined trigger to escalate. |\n\n### The ageing report — worked example\n\nA small advisory firm has **40 open complaints** on 31 March. Its ageing report:\n\n| Ageing bucket | Number | % of open | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **0–7 days** | 22 | 55% | Normal — within service standard |\n| **8–15 days** | 10 | 25% | Watch |\n| **16–21 days** | 5 | 12.5% | **Amber — the 21-day SCORES clock is about to expire** |\n| **Over 21 days** | 3 | 7.5% | **Red — already in breach; escalate to the Principal Officer today** |\n\n**Reading it:** the *average* age might be a comfortable 9 days, and that average is useless. The **3 complaints beyond 21 days** are the entire regulatory risk, and an ageing report is the only report that surfaces them. This is exactly why the syllabus lists ageing separately from \"time taken\".\n\n### A model four-level escalation matrix for an IA\n\n| Level | Who | Turnaround |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Client servicing / relationship desk | **Acknowledge within 1–2 working days**; resolve within 7 days |\n| **2** | **Grievance Redressal Officer** (named on the website) | Within 15 days |\n| **3** | **Principal Officer / Compliance Officer** | Within **21 days of original receipt** |\n| **4** | **External** — SEBI SCORES, then the **ODR portal** | Per the regulator's timelines |\n\n> 🧠 **Memory hook — \"S-D-N-I-S-T-E\".** **S**ource, **D**ate/time, **N**ature, **I**nternal-or-escalated, **S**tatus, **T**ime taken (ageing), **E**scalation. Read as *\"Source Date Nature — Internal? Status, Time, Escalate.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 20.4 The Grievance System an Investment Adviser Must Maintain\n\n**Regulation 21 of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 — \"Redressal of client grievances\"** — requires an Investment Adviser to:\n\n- **redress client grievances promptly**;\n- have **adequate procedure for expeditious grievance redressal**; and\n- handle and resolve grievances relating to securities **in the manner and within the time specified by SEBI**.\n\nPractically, an IA's obligations are:\n\n| Obligation | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Documented policy** | A written grievance policy and register, applied consistently |\n| **Named officer** | A **Grievance Redressal Officer**; a **Compliance Officer** is mandatory for a **non-individual** IA |\n| **Escalation matrix on the website** | Names, designations, email IDs, phone numbers and turnaround at each level |\n| **SCORES registration** | Every registered IA must be **registered on SCORES** and respond within the stipulated time |\n| **ODR enrolment** | Every IA must be enrolled on the **SMART ODR portal** and display the link |\n| **Investor Charter** | SEBI's **Investor Charter for Investment Advisers** must be displayed — services offered, investor rights, DOs and DON'Ts, the grievance mechanism and expected timelines |\n| **Complaints data disclosure** | Complaints data (received / resolved / pending, with ageing) must be **disclosed on the IA's website and updated monthly** |\n| **Disclosure at onboarding** | The grievance mechanism must be explained in the **terms of engagement / Most Important Terms and Conditions**, so the client knows the route before there is a problem |\n| **Record retention** | Grievance records — like other client records — must be maintained for **5 years**; where a matter is **under dispute or litigation, records must be kept until final disposal** |\n| **Reporting** | Periodic reporting to SEBI and to **BASL** (BSE Administration and Supervision Ltd, the body administering IAs), including complaints data |\n| **Annual compliance audit** | The mandatory annual compliance audit covers grievance handling; **adverse observations must be addressed and reported** |\n\n> ⚠️ **A grievance is not \"resolved\" because the adviser thinks the client is wrong.** Resolution means the complaint has been **investigated, a reasoned reply given to the client, and the outcome recorded**. A complaint closed without a written reply is, on inspection, an unresolved complaint.\n\n> 🧠 **The IA's own conflict.** Many IA complaints are about the *advice* — unsuitable products, undisclosed conflicts, fee disputes. The person who gave the advice must **not** be the person who decides the complaint about it. Independence of the grievance function is the whole point of naming a separate officer.\n\n---\n\n## 20.5 Redressal in the Capital Market\n\n### 20.5.1 ⭐ SEBI SCORES\n\n**SCORES** = **SEBI Complaints Redress System**, a **web-based centralised grievance system launched in June 2011** (scores.sebi.gov.in, with a mobile app). **SCORES 2.0**, an upgraded version with **auto-routing, auto-escalation and monitoring by Designated Bodies**, went live on **1 April 2024**.\n\n**What SCORES does:** it **receives, routes, tracks and escalates** complaints against SEBI-registered intermediaries and listed companies, and enforces the response clock. Every complaint gets a **unique registration number** the investor can track online.\n\n**Before you can use it:** the investor must **first have approached the entity directly** and either received **no reply**, an **unsatisfactory reply**, or seen **no action**. Registration on SCORES requires a **valid email ID and mobile number** — **anonymous complaints are not accepted**.\n\n**Limitation:** a complaint should be lodged on SCORES within **one year** from the date of the cause of action (broadly, from when the entity failed to respond satisfactorily).\n\n#### The SCORES 2.0 clock — the numbers to memorise\n\n| Stage | Who acts | **Time** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Complaint lodged** | Auto-routed to the entity and to the **Designated Body** | Same day |\n| **2. Entity files its Action Taken Report (ATR)** | The intermediary / listed company | **21 calendar days** |\n| **3. Investor may seek a FIRST review** | The investor, if dissatisfied with the ATR | Within **15 days** of the ATR |\n| **4. First review** | The **Designated Body** (e.g. a stock exchange, depository, **AMFI**, **BASL**) | **21 days** |\n| **5. Investor may seek a SECOND review** | The investor, if still dissatisfied | Within **15 days** |\n| **6. Second review** | **SEBI** | **21 days** |\n| **7. Still unresolved** | The investor moves to the **ODR portal** | — |\n\n> 🧠 **The SCORES rhythm: 21 – 15 – 21 – 15 – 21.** *Entity 21, ask 15, Designated Body 21, ask 15, SEBI 21.* If the entity misses its 21 days, the complaint **auto-escalates** to the Designated Body without the investor having to do anything.\n\n#### What SCORES will **not** take\n\n- Complaints against **unlisted / delisted / private companies**\n- Matters that are **sub judice** (already before a court, tribunal or arbitrator)\n- Matters within the purview of **another regulator** — RBI, IRDAI, PFRDA, CCI, MCA\n- Disputes about the **merits of a trade**, price movements or investment losses as such\n- **Anonymous**, incomplete, unclear, frivolous or vexatious complaints\n- Complaints on which the investor has **not first approached the entity**\n\n### 20.5.2 ⭐ ODR — the Online Dispute Resolution Portal\n\nIntroduced by SEBI's circular of **31 July 2023**, \"Online Resolution of Disputes in the Indian Securities Market\". A **common ODR portal — SMART ODR (Securities Market Approach for Resolution through ODR), smartodr.in** — links investors, market participants and **ODR Institutions empanelled by the Market Infrastructure Institutions (stock exchanges and depositories)**.\n\n**Why it exists.** SCORES can chase an intermediary, but it cannot decide a **disputed money claim**. Before ODR, that meant an exchange arbitration counter in a metro city, or a civil suit. ODR moves conciliation and arbitration **online, time-bound and low-cost**, so a ₹40,000 claim is actually worth pursuing.\n\n#### The sequence\n\n1. Take the dispute **directly to the market participant**.\n2. If not satisfied, escalate through **SCORES** (or the exchange/depository mechanism).\n3. **After exhausting those**, initiate dispute resolution on the **ODR portal**.\n\n#### The two stages\n\n| Stage | Nature | Who runs it | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Online conciliation** | **Non-binding** — a facilitated settlement | A **conciliator** appointed by the ODR Institution | A settlement agreement, if both agree. Target: **21 calendar days**, extendable by about **10 days** |\n| **Online arbitration** | **Binding** — only if conciliation fails or is not opted for | An **arbitrator** appointed by the ODR Institution | An **arbitral award**, time-bound (broadly **30 days**, extendable), enforceable under the **Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996** |\n\n**Other features:** it is available to **both sides** — a market participant may also initiate ODR against a client after a written notice. Fees are **slab-based on the claim value** and deliberately small for small claims, with the MIIs bearing part of the cost. The ordinary **limitation period under the Limitation Act, 1963 (3 years)** applies. Market participants — including **Investment Advisers** — must **register on the portal** and display the link.\n\n> 🧠 **SCORES vs ODR in one line:** **SCORES chases; ODR decides.** SCORES gets you a *reply*. ODR gets you an *award*.\n\n---\n\n## 20.6 Grievance Redressal in Banking\n\n### Step 1 — inside the bank\n\nBranch manager → the bank's **customer service / grievance cell** → the bank's **Nodal Officer** and **Principal Nodal Officer**. Every regulated entity must display the name and contact of its **Principal Nodal Officer**.\n\n**The Internal Ombudsman.** Banks with **10 or more banking outlets** must appoint an **Internal Ombudsman** — a senior, independent official who is **not an employee of the bank**. The key rule: a complaint the bank proposes to **reject wholly or partly must be referred to the Internal Ombudsman before the rejection is communicated to the customer**. This forces a second, independent look inside the bank, and is why the RBI Ombudsman sees far fewer cases than it otherwise would. (RBI's 2023 Master Direction extends the Internal Ombudsman framework to large NBFCs, Credit Information Companies and non-bank payment system operators.)\n\n### Step 2 — the RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, 2021\n\nLaunched on **12 November 2021**, the **RB-IOS 2021** **integrated three earlier schemes into one**:\n\n- the Banking Ombudsman Scheme, **2006**\n- the Ombudsman Scheme for NBFCs, **2018**\n- the Ombudsman Scheme for Digital Transactions, **2019**\n\n> 🧠 **The slogan: \"One Nation, One Ombudsman.\"** The scheme is **jurisdiction-neutral** — the customer no longer has to work out which regional Ombudsman covers the branch. Complaints go to a single **Centralised Receipt and Processing Centre (CRPC) at Chandigarh**, or online at the RBI **CMS portal (cms.rbi.org.in)**, or by email or post. **There is no fee.**\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Covered entities** | All **commercial banks**, RRBs, **scheduled primary (urban) co-operative banks with deposits of ₹50 crore and above**, **NBFCs** (deposit-taking, or with customer interface and asset size **₹100 crore and above**), **payment system participants** (including prepaid instrument issuers), and **Credit Information Companies** |\n| **Ground of complaint** | **Any \"deficiency in service\"** — the scheme replaced the old *closed list* of grounds with an open, deficiency-based test, subject to an exclusion list |\n| **Precondition** | The complaint must **first** have been made to the bank/NBFC |\n| **When you may escalate** | After **30 days** with no reply, **or** on receiving a reply that rejects the complaint wholly/partly or is unsatisfactory |\n| **Limitation** | Within **1 year** of the entity's reply; where no reply was received, within **1 year and 30 days** of the date of the complaint to the entity |\n| **Compensation the Ombudsman may award** | Up to **₹20 lakh** for the actual loss suffered, **plus up to ₹1 lakh** for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish |\n| **Appeal** | To the **Appellate Authority — the Executive Director in charge of RBI's Consumer Education and Protection Department — within 30 days** of receipt of the award |\n\n**Not maintainable before the Ombudsman:** matters **not first raised with the entity**; matters **already pending or decided** by a court, tribunal or arbitrator; complaints about the entity's **commercial judgement or business decision** (e.g. the rate of interest it chooses to offer) unless there is a deficiency; disputes **between two regulated entities**; **frivolous or vexatious** complaints; and complaints filed beyond the limitation period.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap:** \"₹20 lakh\" is the **actual-loss** cap and \"₹1 lakh\" the **mental-agony** cap; they are **separate and additive**, so the theoretical maximum is **₹21 lakh**. Also note this is the *banking* number — do not swap it with the **Insurance Ombudsman's ₹30 lakh**.\n\n---\n\n## 20.7 Grievance Redressal in Insurance\n\n### Step 1 — the insurer\n\nEvery insurer must have a **board-approved grievance redressal policy**, a **Grievance Redressal Officer (GRO)** at head office and at each servicing office, and an online/offline system for logging complaints. Under the IRDAI **Protection of Policyholders' Interests** framework:\n\n| Action | **Timeline** |\n|---|---|\n| **Written acknowledgement** of the grievance | Within **3 working days** of receipt |\n| **Resolution and final letter**, if not resolved at acknowledgement | Within **2 weeks (14 days)** of receipt |\n| Complaint **deemed closed** if the complainant does not respond to the insurer's final resolution | After **8 weeks** |\n\n### Step 2 — **Bima Bharosa** (IRDAI)\n\n**Bima Bharosa** (bimabharosa.irdai.gov.in) is IRDAI's **online policyholder grievance portal**, the renamed **Integrated Grievance Management System (IGMS)**. It is supported by IRDAI's **Grievance Redressal Cell** in the Consumer Affairs Department and a **grievance call centre (155255 / 1800 4254 732)** and the complaints@irdai.gov.in mailbox.\n\n**What Bima Bharosa does:** it **registers the complaint, forwards it to the insurer, tracks it against the prescribed turnaround, gives the policyholder a tracking number, and builds industry-wide complaint analytics for supervision.** What it does **not** do is **adjudicate the claim** — IRDAI does not decide whether the claim was rightly repudiated.\n\n### Step 3 — the **Insurance Ombudsman**\n\nEstablished under the **Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017** (amended in 2021) and administered by the **Council for Insurance Ombudsmen (CIO)**. There are **17 Insurance Ombudsman offices** across India, each covering a defined territory.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Who may complain** | An **individual** policyholder (or their legal heir/nominee/assignee) in a **personal capacity**; the 2021 amendment also brought complaints against **insurance brokers and intermediaries** within scope |\n| **Preconditions** | The complainant **must first have made a written representation to the insurer**, and the insurer must have **rejected it**, or given a **reply the complainant finds unsatisfactory**, or **failed to reply within one month** |\n| **Limitation** | Within **1 year** of the insurer's rejection or final reply |\n| **Not admissible if** | The same matter is **pending before, or already decided by, a court, consumer forum or arbitrator** |\n| **Value limit** | Claims (including expenses claimed) **not exceeding ₹30 lakh** |\n| **Grounds** | Delay in settlement, **partial or total repudiation of a claim**, premium disputes, **mis-selling**, misrepresentation of policy terms, non-issuance of documents, policy servicing failures, and **any deficiency of service** arising from the policy |\n| **As conciliator** | Where the parties agree, the Ombudsman makes a **recommendation within 1 month** of receiving the complaint |\n| **As adjudicator** | Where no settlement is reached, the Ombudsman passes an **award within 3 months** of receiving all requirements |\n| **Binding effect** | The award is **binding on the INSURER but not on the complainant** — who remains free to go to a consumer commission or civil court |\n| **Compliance** | The complainant confirms acceptance in writing within **30 days**; the insurer must **comply within 30 days** of receiving that acceptance and inform the Ombudsman |\n| **Cost** | **Free** — no fee, and no lawyer is required |\n\n> 🧠 **Why is the award binding on only one side?** Because the Ombudsman is an **investor-protection** device, not a contract. Making it one-way binding gives the policyholder a free, fast, low-risk shot at redress with **nothing to lose** — if the award disappoints, the courts are still open. If it bound the policyholder too, the cautious would never file.\n\n---\n\n## 20.8 Redress in Pension — PFRDA and CGMS\n\nGrievances of NPS and APY subscribers are governed by the **PFRDA (Redressal of Subscriber Grievance) Regulations, 2015**.\n\n**CGMS — the Central Grievance Management System** — is the online grievance module operated through the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**. A subscriber can raise a grievance through the **CRA website, the NPS mobile app, the call centre, or a physical form (Form G1)** submitted to the POP/nodal office, and gets a **token number** to track it.\n\n### The pension ladder\n\n| Level | Who | Time allowed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | The **intermediary** — POP, nodal office, CRA, aggregator, custodian, pension fund — through **CGMS** | Resolve within **30 days** |\n| **2** | The **National Pension System Trust (NPS Trust)** — its Grievance Redressal Officer | If not resolved in 30 days or the subscriber is dissatisfied |\n| **3** | The **Ombudsman appointed by PFRDA** | Appeal within **45 days** of the decision (or of the expiry of the 30-day period), extendable for sufficient cause |\n| **4** | **PFRDA** — appeal against the Ombudsman's order | Within **30 days** |\n| **5** | **Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)** — appeal against PFRDA's order | Under **Section 36 of the PFRDA Act, 2013** |\n| **6** | **Supreme Court** — on a question of law | Within **60 days** of SAT's order |\n\n> 🧠 **Note the crossover.** Pension appeals end up at **SAT**, the same tribunal that hears SEBI appeals. SAT is not a \"securities-only\" tribunal — it is the appellate tribunal for **SEBI, PFRDA and IRDAI** matters alike. That is a favourite exam point.\n\nThe PFRDA Ombudsman first tries to **settle by conciliation or mediation**; where that fails, it passes a reasoned order. PFRDA also runs a **grievance monitoring and reporting framework**, and intermediaries must report grievance statistics periodically.\n\n---\n\n## 20.9 ⭐ The Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT)\n\n**SAT is a statutory tribunal established under Section 15K of the SEBI Act, 1992, with its headquarters in Mumbai.**\n\n**What SAT is for — the distinction that decides half the questions on it.** SAT is an **appellate** body. It hears appeals by a **person aggrieved by an ORDER of a regulator** — it is **not** a complaint counter for an investor who is unhappy with a broker. An investor with a money claim against an intermediary goes to **ODR / arbitration / the consumer commission**, never to SAT.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Established under** | **Section 15K, SEBI Act, 1992** |\n| **Headquarters** | **Mumbai** |\n| **Composition** | A **Presiding Officer** plus **Judicial and Technical Members** |\n| **Presiding Officer's qualification** | A sitting or retired **Judge of the Supreme Court**, or a sitting or retired **Chief Justice of a High Court** |\n| **Appeals lie to SAT against orders of** | **SEBI** and its **Adjudicating Officers**; **PFRDA** (PFRDA Act, 2013); **IRDAI** (Insurance Act); and orders of **stock exchanges and depositories** in specified matters |\n| **Time to file an appeal** | Within **45 days** of receiving a copy of the order; SAT may **condone delay** for sufficient cause |\n| **Disposal** | SAT shall dispose of the appeal **as expeditiously as possible** and endeavour to do so within **6 months** |\n| **Powers** | **Not bound by the Code of Civil Procedure**; guided by **natural justice**; but has the powers of a **civil court** for summoning, discovery and evidence. It may **confirm, modify or set aside** the order appealed against |\n| **Representation** | The appellant may appear in person or be represented by a **chartered accountant, company secretary, cost accountant or legal practitioner** |\n| **Further appeal** | To the **Supreme Court** within **60 days**, on any **question of law**, extendable by a further **60 days** for sufficient cause (**Section 15Z, SEBI Act**) |\n\n> 🧠 **The three numbers for SAT: 45 – 6 – 60.** **45 days** to appeal to SAT, **6 months** target for disposal, **60 days** to appeal onward to the Supreme Court.\n\n> ⚠️ **Trap:** the appeal from SAT to the Supreme Court lies **only on a question of law** — the Supreme Court will not re-open findings of fact.\n\n---\n\n## 20.10 Other Redressal Fora\n\n| Forum | Used for |\n|---|---|\n| **Stock exchange IGRC and arbitration** | Investor complaints against **trading members**; the exchange's **Investor Grievance Redressal Committee** hears the matter, followed by arbitration/appellate arbitration — now largely routed through **ODR** |\n| **Investor Protection Fund (IPF)** | Compensates investors up to prescribed limits when a **trading member is declared a defaulter** |\n| **Depositories & RTAs** | Demat and transfer-agent complaints, escalated via the depository and then SCORES |\n| **AMFI** | Complaints against **mutual fund distributors** (conduct, mis-selling); AMFI can act against an ARN holder |\n| **BASL** | The body administering **Investment Advisers**, which receives IA complaints data and can act on conduct |\n| **IEPF Authority (MCA)** | Recovery of **unclaimed dividends and shares** transferred to the Investor Education and Protection Fund |\n| **NCLT / MCA / Registrar of Companies** | Company-law matters — oppression and mismanagement, non-receipt of statutory documents |\n| **Consumer commissions (CPA 2019)** | Deficiency of service by any financial service provider |\n| **Civil courts** | Contractual claims, and any matter outside a specialised forum's jurisdiction |\n| **Police / Economic Offences Wing / Cyber Crime cell** | **Fraud, forgery, impersonation, unauthorised transactions, cyber fraud** — a criminal matter, not merely a service deficiency |\n| **National Consumer Helpline (1915) and the e-Daakhil portal** | First-line consumer help and electronic filing |\n| **SEBI's toll-free investor helpline** | Guidance on where and how to complain in the securities market |\n| **Lok Adalat / mediation** | Amicable settlement, with the award of a Lok Adalat having the force of a civil decree |\n\n> ⚠️ **Choosing the forum — the two questions to ask.**\n> **(1) Is this a service deficiency, a money dispute, or a crime?** Deficiency → the entity, then the regulator's portal, then the Ombudsman/consumer commission. Money dispute → **ODR/arbitration**. Crime → **police/EOW**, *and* the regulator.\n> **(2) Am I challenging a REGULATOR'S ORDER, or complaining about a FIRM?** A regulator's order → **SAT**. A firm → everything else.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Quick Revision Sheet\n\n| Concept | The one-line answer |\n|---|---|\n| The universal ladder | **Intermediary → Regulator → Tribunal/Court** |\n| Consumer Protection Act | **2019**, in force from **20 July 2020**, replacing the 1986 Act |\n| Consumer | Buys goods / avails services **for consideration**; includes the **user with the buyer's approval** |\n| Excluded from \"consumer\" | Purchase for **resale or commercial purpose** — **unless** exclusively for **livelihood by self-employment** |\n| Consumer commissions | **District → State → National**, appeal onward to the **Supreme Court** |\n| Pecuniary limits (current, 2021 Rules) | District **up to ₹50 lakh**; State **₹50 lakh–₹2 crore**; National **above ₹2 crore** |\n| Pecuniary limits (as originally enacted, 2019) | District **up to ₹1 crore**; State **₹1–10 crore**; National **above ₹10 crore** |\n| Jurisdiction measured by | The **value of the consideration paid** — not the compensation claimed |\n| Limitation to file a consumer complaint | **2 years** from the cause of action |\n| New territorial rule in 2019 | May file where the **complainant resides or works for gain** |\n| Consumer filing fee | **Nil** up to a claim of **₹5 lakh**; e-filing via **e-Daakhil** |\n| Consumer appeals | District→State **45 days** (deposit **50%**); State→National **30 days**; National→SC **30 days** |\n| CCPA | Central Consumer Protection Authority — acts on **unfair trade practices and misleading ads**; Investigation Wing under a **Director General** |\n| Misleading-ad penalties | **₹10 lakh** first offence, **₹50 lakh** repeat; endorser ban **1 year / 3 years** |\n| Six consumer rights | Safety, Information, Choice, Be heard, Redressal, Education (**SIC-HRE**) |\n| Seven elements of a grievance system | **Source · Date/time · Nature · Internal-or-escalated · Status · Time taken (ageing) · Escalation** |\n| Why the date/time matters | It **starts the regulatory clock** for every timeline |\n| Why an ageing report | Averages hide breaches; **buckets expose stale complaints** |\n| IA's governing provision | **Regulation 21**, SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, **2013** |\n| IA must be registered on | **SCORES** and the **SMART ODR** portal |\n| IA record retention | **5 years**; longer — **till final disposal** — where a matter is in dispute/litigation |\n| IA website disclosures | **Investor Charter**, escalation matrix, and **monthly complaints data** |\n| SCORES | **SEBI Complaints Redress System**, launched **June 2011**; **SCORES 2.0** from **1 April 2024** |\n| SCORES precondition | Must have **first approached the entity**; **no anonymous** complaints |\n| SCORES limitation | Lodge within **1 year** of the cause of action |\n| SCORES clock | Entity **21 days** → review request **15** → Designated Body **21** → review request **15** → SEBI **21** |\n| SCORES will not take | Unlisted companies, **sub judice** matters, other regulators' subjects, anonymous complaints |\n| ODR portal | **SMART ODR** (smartodr.in), from SEBI's circular of **31 July 2023** |\n| ODR stages | **Online conciliation (non-binding, ~21 days + ~10)** → **online arbitration (binding award)** |\n| ODR award enforceable under | The **Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996** |\n| SCORES vs ODR | **SCORES chases; ODR decides** |\n| Banking step 1 | Branch → Nodal / Principal Nodal Officer → **Internal Ombudsman** |\n| Internal Ombudsman trigger | Any complaint the bank proposes to **reject wholly or partly** |\n| RBI scheme | **RB-IOS 2021**, effective **12 Nov 2021** — \"One Nation, One Ombudsman\" |\n| RB-IOS merged | Banking Ombudsman **2006** + NBFC Ombudsman **2018** + Digital Transactions **2019** |\n| RB-IOS ground | Any **\"deficiency in service\"** (open-ended, not a closed list) |\n| RB-IOS escalation trigger | **30 days** with no reply, or an unsatisfactory/rejecting reply |\n| RB-IOS limitation | **1 year** from the reply; **1 year + 30 days** if no reply |\n| RBI Ombudsman award cap | **₹20 lakh** actual loss **+ up to ₹1 lakh** for time/harassment |\n| RB-IOS filing | Free, via **CMS portal** or the **CRPC, Chandigarh** |\n| RB-IOS appeal | **Executive Director, Consumer Education & Protection Dept, RBI**, within **30 days** |\n| Insurer's timelines | Acknowledge in **3 working days**; resolve in **2 weeks**; deemed closed after **8 weeks** of silence |\n| IRDAI portal | **Bima Bharosa** (formerly **IGMS**) |\n| Insurance Ombudsman rules | **Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017** (amended 2021); **17 offices**; **Council for Insurance Ombudsmen** |\n| Insurance Ombudsman preconditions | Written representation first; **rejection, unsatisfactory reply, or no reply in 1 month** |\n| Insurance Ombudsman limitation | **1 year** from rejection/final reply |\n| Insurance Ombudsman value limit | **₹30 lakh** |\n| Insurance Ombudsman timelines | Recommendation in **1 month**; award in **3 months**; insurer complies in **30 days** |\n| Binding effect | Binding on the **insurer only**, not the complainant |\n| Pension regulations | **PFRDA (Redressal of Subscriber Grievance) Regulations, 2015** |\n| Pension portal | **CGMS** — Central Grievance Management System, via the **CRA** |\n| Pension ladder | Intermediary (**30 days**) → **NPS Trust** → **PFRDA Ombudsman** (**45 days** to appeal) → **PFRDA** (**30 days**) → **SAT** → **SC (60 days)** |\n| SAT established under | **Section 15K, SEBI Act, 1992**; headquartered in **Mumbai** |\n| SAT hears appeals from | **SEBI**, **PFRDA**, **IRDAI**, adjudicating officers, and exchanges/depositories |\n| SAT Presiding Officer | Sitting/retired **Supreme Court judge** or **Chief Justice of a High Court** |\n| Time to appeal to SAT | **45 days** (condonable) |\n| SAT disposal target | **6 months** |\n| SAT to Supreme Court | **60 days**, on a **question of law** only (**Section 15Z**) |\n| SAT is NOT | A first-instance complaint counter for investors against brokers |\n| Regulators do NOT | **Adjudicate** individual claims — they route, monitor and enforce timelines |\n| Who adjudicates | **Ombudsman, arbitrator (ODR), consumer commission, civil court** |\n| Fraud/forgery | Also a **criminal** matter — police / Economic Offences Wing / cyber cell |\n| Other fora | Exchange **IGRC**, **IPF**, **AMFI**, **BASL**, **IEPF**, **NCLT**, Lok Adalat |\n\n> **Exam tip:** almost every question in this chapter is answered by asking two things in order — **\"has the client already gone to the firm?\"** and **\"is this a service complaint, a money dispute, or an appeal against a regulator's order?\"** Get those two right and the correct forum picks itself. Then memorise the six numbers that carry the most marks: **SCORES 21 days**, **RBI Ombudsman ₹20 lakh + ₹1 lakh**, **Insurance Ombudsman ₹30 lakh**, **SAT 45 days**, **SC 60 days**, and **consumer limitation 2 years**.\n", "flashcards": "# Chapter 20: Grievance Redress Mechanism — Flashcards\n\nCover the answer, recall it, then check. **The escalation ladders and the timeline numbers are near-certain exam marks.**\n\n**Q1.** State the one principle that runs through every redress system in this chapter.\n> **A:** **Intermediary first → Regulator next → Tribunal/Court last.** A complaint that skips the firm and goes straight to the regulator, Ombudsman or SAT is **not maintainable**.\n\n**Q2.** Why does the law insist the firm is approached first?\n> **A:** The firm holds the **file, the recording, the audit trail and the money**, so it can fix a genuine error in a day. A regulator supervising thousands of intermediaries cannot. Escalation is reserved for when that level **fails**.\n\n**Q3.** Which Act governs consumer protection in India, when did it come into force, and what did it replace?\n> **A:** The **Consumer Protection Act, 2019**, in force from **20 July 2020**, replacing the **Consumer Protection Act, 1986**.\n\n**Q4.** Who is a \"consumer\" under the Act?\n> **A:** A person who **buys goods or avails services for a consideration** paid, promised, or partly paid and partly promised, or under a **system of deferred payment** — **including online and electronic transactions**. A **user or beneficiary with the buyer's approval** also counts.\n\n**Q5.** Who is expressly NOT a consumer, and what is the carve-out?\n> **A:** A person who obtains goods/services for **resale or any commercial purpose**. The carve-out: the exclusion does **not** apply where they are used **exclusively for earning a livelihood by means of self-employment**. *(One auto-rickshaw driven by its owner — consumer. A forty-vehicle fleet — not.)*\n\n**Q6.** Name the three-tier consumer redressal machinery and who presides over each.\n> **A:** **District Commission** (a person qualified to be a **District Judge**), **State Commission** (a sitting/retired **High Court judge**), **National Commission** (a sitting/retired **Supreme Court judge**).\n\n**Q7.** What are the CURRENT pecuniary limits of the consumer commissions?\n> **A:** Under the **2021 Jurisdiction Rules**: **District up to ₹50 lakh**; **State above ₹50 lakh up to ₹2 crore**; **National above ₹2 crore**.\n\n**Q8.** What were the pecuniary limits as ORIGINALLY enacted in the 2019 Act?\n> **A:** **District up to ₹1 crore**; **State ₹1 crore to ₹10 crore**; **National above ₹10 crore**. *(Both sets circulate in study material — read the stem carefully.)*\n\n**Q9.** Is the consumer commission's jurisdiction fixed by the compensation claimed?\n> **A:** **No** — it is fixed by the **value of the goods or services and the consideration paid**, not by the amount of compensation demanded.\n\n**Q10.** What is the limitation period for filing a consumer complaint, and where may it be filed?\n> **A:** **2 years** from the cause of action. It may be filed where the opposite party resides or carries on business, where the cause of action arose, or — **new in the 2019 Act** — **where the complainant resides or personally works for gain**.\n\n**Q11.** State the consumer appeal timelines.\n> **A:** **District → State: 45 days** (with a **50% deposit** of any amount ordered). **State → National: 30 days.** **National → Supreme Court: 30 days.**\n\n**Q12.** What is the CCPA and what can it do?\n> **A:** The **Central Consumer Protection Authority**, created by the 2019 Act, acts against **unfair trade practices and false or misleading advertisements**. It has an **Investigation Wing headed by a Director General** and can order recalls and refunds. Penalties: **₹10 lakh** first offence, **₹50 lakh** repeat; endorsers can be barred for **1 year**, and **3 years** on repeat.\n\n**Q13.** Name the six consumer rights.\n> **A:** **Safety, Information, Choice, Be heard, Redressal, Consumer education** — mnemonic **SIC-HRE**.\n\n**Q14.** List the seven key elements of a robust grievance recording system.\n> **A:** **Source** of receipt · **Date and time** of receipt · **Nature** of the complaint · whether **internal or to be escalated** · **Status / action taken** · **Time taken (ageing report)** · **Escalation mechanism**.\n\n**Q15.** Why is the date and time of receipt so important?\n> **A:** Because it **starts the regulatory clock**. Every timeline — the 21-day SCORES response, the insurer's 3-day acknowledgement, the bank's 30 days — is measured from receipt. Without a timestamp, compliance cannot be proved.\n\n**Q16.** Why record the SOURCE, and why classify by NATURE?\n> **A:** The **source** tells you **which regulatory clock applies** and whether a regulator is already watching (a SCORES complaint carries a hard **21-day** deadline; a walk-in does not). The **nature** enables **root-cause analysis** — twenty complaints of one type are a **process defect**, not twenty accidents.\n\n**Q17.** What does an ageing report show that an average does not, and what is an escalation matrix?\n> **A:** The ageing report shows the **outliers** — an average age of 9 days can conceal three complaints past 21 days and already in breach. **Buckets expose stale complaints; averages hide them.** The **escalation matrix** is the published table of **named officer, designation, email, phone and turnaround at each level**, so the client can go upward **inside the firm** first.\n\n**Q18.** Which regulation governs an Investment Adviser's grievance obligations, and what does it require?\n> **A:** **Regulation 21 of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013** — the IA must **redress grievances promptly**, have **adequate procedure for expeditious redressal**, and resolve securities-related grievances **in the manner and within the time specified by SEBI**.\n\n**Q19.** Name six things an IA must actually do about grievances.\n> **A:** Maintain a **documented policy and register**; name a **Grievance Redressal Officer** (and a **Compliance Officer** if non-individual); register on **SCORES** and the **SMART ODR portal**; display the **Investor Charter and escalation matrix**; publish **complaints data on the website monthly**; and **retain records for 5 years**.\n\n**Q20.** How long must an IA keep grievance records, and when is a complaint properly \"resolved\"?\n> **A:** **Five years** — but where a matter is **under dispute or litigation, records must be preserved until final disposal**. A complaint is resolved only when it has been **investigated, a reasoned reply given to the client, and the outcome recorded**; a complaint closed **without a written reply** is, on inspection, **unresolved**.\n\n**Q21.** Why must the person who gave the advice not decide the complaint about it?\n> **A:** Because most IA complaints are about the **advice itself** — unsuitability, undisclosed conflicts, fees. Letting the adviser judge his own work destroys the independence of the grievance function, which is exactly why a **separate named officer** is required.\n\n**Q22.** What is SCORES, when was it launched, and what is SCORES 2.0?\n> **A:** The **SEBI Complaints Redress System**, a centralised web-based grievance system launched in **June 2011**. **SCORES 2.0**, live from **1 April 2024**, added **auto-routing, auto-escalation and monitoring by Designated Bodies**.\n\n**Q23.** What must an investor have done before lodging on SCORES, and by when must it be lodged?\n> **A:** Must have **first approached the entity directly** and received no reply, an unsatisfactory reply, or no action. The complaint should be lodged within **1 year** of the cause of action. **Anonymous complaints are not accepted.**\n\n**Q24.** State the SCORES 2.0 timeline.\n> **A:** Entity files its **Action Taken Report within 21 calendar days** → investor may seek a **first review within 15 days** → the **Designated Body** reviews within **21 days** → investor may seek a **second review within 15 days** → **SEBI** reviews within **21 days**. Remember it as **21 – 15 – 21 – 15 – 21**.\n\n**Q25.** Which complaints will SCORES NOT accept?\n> **A:** Complaints against **unlisted/private companies**; **sub judice** matters; matters under **another regulator** (RBI, IRDAI, PFRDA, MCA); disputes about the **merits of a trade or investment losses**; and **anonymous, incomplete or frivolous** complaints.\n\n**Q26.** What is the ODR portal, and under which SEBI circular was it introduced?\n> **A:** **SMART ODR** — Securities Market Approach for Resolution through ODR (smartodr.in) — a common **Online Dispute Resolution** portal introduced by SEBI's circular of **31 July 2023**, run through **ODR Institutions empanelled by the Market Infrastructure Institutions**.\n\n**Q27.** What are the two stages of ODR, and how do they differ?\n> **A:** **Online conciliation** — **non-binding**, a facilitated settlement, targeted at **21 days** (extendable by about **10**); then **online arbitration** — **binding**, producing an **arbitral award enforceable under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996**.\n\n**Q28.** Distinguish SCORES from ODR in one line.\n> **A:** **SCORES chases; ODR decides.** SCORES gets the investor a **reply** and enforces timelines; ODR gets the investor an **award** on a disputed money claim.\n\n**Q29.** Describe the banking escalation ladder inside the bank.\n> **A:** **Branch → customer service / grievance cell → Nodal and Principal Nodal Officer → Internal Ombudsman.**\n\n**Q30.** What is the Internal Ombudsman, and what triggers a referral to it?\n> **A:** A **senior independent official who is not an employee of the bank**, mandatory for banks with **10 or more banking outlets**. Any complaint the bank proposes to **reject wholly or partly must be referred to the Internal Ombudsman before the rejection is communicated** to the customer.\n\n**Q31.** What is RB-IOS 2021, and which schemes did it merge?\n> **A:** The **RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, 2021**, effective **12 November 2021** — \"**One Nation, One Ombudsman**\". It merged the **Banking Ombudsman Scheme 2006**, the **Ombudsman Scheme for NBFCs 2018** and the **Ombudsman Scheme for Digital Transactions 2019**.\n\n**Q32.** On what ground can a complaint be made under RB-IOS, and how is that different from before?\n> **A:** Any **\"deficiency in service\"**. The scheme replaced the earlier **closed list of specified grounds** with an **open, deficiency-based test**, subject only to an exclusion list.\n\n**Q33.** When may a customer escalate to the RBI Ombudsman, and within what limitation?\n> **A:** After **30 days** with no reply from the bank/NBFC, or on a reply that **rejects the complaint wholly/partly or is unsatisfactory**. The complaint must be filed within **1 year** of the reply, or within **1 year and 30 days** of the original complaint where no reply came.\n\n**Q34.** What compensation can the RBI Ombudsman award, and how does one appeal?\n> **A:** Up to **₹20 lakh for actual loss**, **plus up to ₹1 lakh** for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish. Appeal lies to the **Executive Director in charge of RBI's Consumer Education and Protection Department within 30 days**. Filing is **free**, through the **CMS portal** or the **CRPC at Chandigarh**.\n\n**Q35.** What are an insurer's own grievance timelines?\n> **A:** **Written acknowledgement within 3 working days**; **resolution and final letter within 2 weeks (14 days)**; and the complaint is **deemed closed if the complainant does not respond within 8 weeks** of the insurer's final resolution.\n\n**Q36.** What is Bima Bharosa, and what does it NOT do?\n> **A:** IRDAI's **online policyholder grievance portal** (formerly the **Integrated Grievance Management System, IGMS**). It **registers, forwards, tracks and analyses** complaints — but it does **not adjudicate** the claim. IRDAI does not decide whether a repudiation was right.\n\n**Q37.** Under which rules is the Insurance Ombudsman established, how many offices are there, and what is the value limit?\n> **A:** The **Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017** (amended 2021), administered by the **Council for Insurance Ombudsmen**. There are **17 offices**, and the value limit is **₹30 lakh**.\n\n**Q38.** State the preconditions and limitation for approaching the Insurance Ombudsman.\n> **A:** A **written representation to the insurer first**, followed by **rejection, an unsatisfactory reply, or no reply within one month**; the complaint must then be filed within **1 year**. The matter must **not be pending before or decided by** a court, consumer forum or arbitrator.\n\n**Q39.** State the Insurance Ombudsman's own timelines and who the award binds.\n> **A:** As conciliator, a **recommendation within 1 month**; as adjudicator, an **award within 3 months** of receiving all requirements. The insurer must **comply within 30 days** of the complainant's written acceptance. The award is **binding on the insurer but NOT on the complainant**.\n\n**Q40.** Why is the Insurance Ombudsman's award binding on only one side?\n> **A:** Because it is an **investor-protection device**, not a contract. One-way binding gives the policyholder a **free, fast, no-risk** attempt at redress with the courts still open if the award disappoints — if it bound them too, cautious complainants would never file.\n\n**Q41.** Which regulations and which portal govern NPS grievances?\n> **A:** The **PFRDA (Redressal of Subscriber Grievance) Regulations, 2015**, using **CGMS — the Central Grievance Management System** — operated through the **Central Recordkeeping Agency (CRA)**.\n\n**Q42.** Set out the pension escalation ladder.\n> **A:** Intermediary (POP/nodal office/CRA) via **CGMS — 30 days** → **NPS Trust** → **PFRDA Ombudsman** (appeal within **45 days**) → **PFRDA** (within **30 days**) → **Securities Appellate Tribunal** → **Supreme Court** within **60 days**.\n\n**Q43.** Under what provision is SAT established, where does it sit, and whose orders does it hear appeals against?\n> **A:** **Section 15K of the SEBI Act, 1992**; headquartered in **Mumbai**. It hears appeals against orders of **SEBI** and its adjudicating officers, **PFRDA**, **IRDAI**, and stock exchanges/depositories in specified matters.\n\n**Q44.** What are the three key SAT numbers, and can an investor take a broker to SAT for a ₹2 lakh loss?\n> **A:** **45 days** to file (condonable), a **6-month** disposal target, and **60 days** to appeal onward to the **Supreme Court** on a **question of law** only (**Section 15Z**). And **no** — SAT is an **appellate** body against a **regulator's order**, not a first-instance complaint counter. A money claim goes to the firm, then **SCORES**, then **ODR/arbitration** or a consumer commission.\n\n**Q45.** Do SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA adjudicate individual claims — and name five \"other\" fora.\n> **A:** **No.** Their portals **route, monitor and enforce timelines**; adjudication belongs to the **Ombudsman, an ODR arbitrator, a consumer commission or a civil court**. Other fora: the exchange's **IGRC** and arbitration, the **Investor Protection Fund**, **AMFI** (distributor conduct), **BASL** (Investment Advisers) and the **IEPF Authority** (unclaimed dividends/shares) — plus **NCLT/MCA**, the **police/Economic Offences Wing** for fraud, and **Lok Adalat**.\n", "questions": "# Chapter 20: Grievance Redress Mechanism — 100 MCQ Question Bank\n\n> Three tiers — 🟢 Easy (Recall), 🟡 Medium (Application), 🔴 Hard (Numerical & Scenario). **Almost every question here reduces to one thing: given this complaint, what is the NEXT step and by when?** Remember the **25% negative marking**.\n\n## 🟢 Tier 1 — Easy: Recall (Q1–Q35)\n\n**Q1.** The Consumer Protection Act currently in force in India is of the year:\nA) 1986  B) 2005  C) 2019  D) 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Consumer Protection Act, 2019** replaced the 1986 Act.\nA) The 1986 Act was repealed and replaced. B) 2005 is the Right to Information Act year, not consumer protection. D) 2013 is the year of the SEBI Investment Advisers Regulations and the Companies Act, not the Consumer Protection Act.\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 came into force from:\nA) 20 July 2020  B) 1 April 2019  C) 12 November 2021  D) 1 April 2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The Act was passed in 2019 but its main provisions were notified with effect from **20 July 2020**.\nB) The Act had not been notified in April 2019. C) 12 November 2021 is the RB-IOS 2021 date. D) 1 April 2024 is the SCORES 2.0 date.\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Under the Consumer Protection Act, a \"consumer\" is a person who:\nA) Receives goods free of charge  B) Buys goods or avails services for a consideration  C) Buys goods for resale  D) Manufactures goods\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Consideration paid, promised, partly paid, or under deferred payment is **essential**.\nA) A purely free or gratuitous service generally creates no consumer relationship. C) Purchase for resale is expressly excluded. D) A manufacturer is the opposite party, not the consumer.\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A person who obtains goods for resale or any commercial purpose is:\nA) A consumer  B) A consumer only if registered  C) Not a consumer  D) A consumer above ₹1 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The definition **expressly excludes** resale and commercial purpose.\nA) Contradicts the statutory exclusion. B) Registration is irrelevant to the definition. D) There is no value threshold in the definition.\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** The \"commercial purpose\" exclusion does NOT apply where the goods or services are used:\nA) By a company  B) For export  C) For resale online  D) Exclusively for earning a livelihood by means of self-employment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The livelihood-by-self-employment carve-out protects the small self-employed person: one auto-rickshaw driven by its owner is a consumer, a forty-vehicle fleet is not.\nA) A company buying for its business is squarely commercial. B) Export is a commercial purpose. C) Online resale is still resale.\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** The three-tier consumer redressal machinery consists of:\nA) SEBI, RBI and IRDAI  B) District, State and National Commissions  C) SAT, High Court and Supreme Court  D) Branch, Nodal Officer and Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **District → State → National** Consumer Disputes Redressal Commissions.\nA) These are financial sector regulators, not consumer fora. C) These are courts and a tribunal, not consumer commissions. D) That is the banking internal ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission is presided over by:\nA) A person qualified to be a District Judge  B) An IAS officer  C) A sitting or retired Supreme Court judge  D) The Consumer Affairs Secretary\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The apex consumer body is headed by a sitting or retired **Supreme Court** judge.\nA) That is the District Commission's qualification. B) and D) Administrative officers do not preside over these quasi-judicial bodies.\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Under the 2021 Jurisdiction Rules, the District Commission hears complaints where the value does not exceed:\nA) ₹20 lakh  B) ₹1 crore  C) ₹2 crore  D) ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The 2021 Rules set the District Commission's ceiling at **₹50 lakh**.\nA) There is no ₹20 lakh tier. B) ₹1 crore was the District ceiling as **originally enacted in 2019**, since revised. C) ₹2 crore is the **State** Commission's current ceiling.\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Under the 2021 Jurisdiction Rules, the National Commission hears complaints where the value exceeds:\nA) ₹2 crore  B) ₹50 lakh  C) ₹1 crore  D) ₹10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Above **₹2 crore** goes to the National Commission under the current rules.\nB) ₹50 lakh is the District ceiling. C) ₹1 crore was the original 2019 District ceiling. D) ₹10 crore was the original 2019 threshold for the National Commission, since revised.\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** The limitation period for filing a consumer complaint is:\nA) 6 months  B) 1 year  C) 3 years  D) 2 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **Two years** from the date the cause of action arose, condonable for sufficient cause.\nA) and B) are shorter than the statutory period. C) Three years is the general Limitation Act period for civil suits, not the consumer forum period.\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A territorial filing rule newly introduced by the 2019 Act allows a complaint to be filed where:\nA) The complainant resides or personally works for gain  B) Only where the seller is based  C) Only in New Delhi  D) Only where the goods were manufactured\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A major consumer-friendly change: the complainant no longer has to travel to the opposite party's location.\nB) Remains available but is no longer the only option. C) There is no single-city rule. D) Place of manufacture is not a listed ground.\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** The Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA) principally acts against:\nA) Insider trading  B) Money laundering  C) Unfair trade practices and misleading advertisements  D) Tax evasion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The CCPA is the consumer-rights regulator created by the 2019 Act.\nA) That is SEBI's domain. B) That is the ED and FIU under the PMLA. D) That is the income tax authorities.\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The Investigation Wing of the CCPA is headed by a:\nA) Chief Justice  B) Director General  C) Whole-time member of SEBI  D) Deputy Governor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The CCPA has an Investigation Wing headed by a **Director General**.\nA) A judge does not head an investigation wing. C) SEBI's members have no CCPA role. D) Deputy Governors belong to the RBI.\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Which of the following is NOT one of the seven key elements of a grievance recording system:\nA) Source of receipt  B) Date and time of receipt  C) The client's tax residency status  D) Ageing of the complaint\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Tax residency is a KYC data point, not an element of grievance recording. The seven are source, date/time, nature, internal-or-escalated, status, time taken (ageing) and escalation mechanism.\nA), B) and D) are all on the list.\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Recording the exact date and time of a complaint matters mainly because it:\nA) Identifies the client  B) Is required for GST  C) Reduces the filing fee  D) Starts the regulatory clock\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Every timeline in this chapter runs from **receipt**; without a timestamp, compliance cannot be evidenced.\nA) The client is identified by KYC records. B) GST has nothing to do with grievance logging. C) There is no fee linkage.\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** An ageing report of complaints shows:\nA) The age of the client  B) How long complaints have been pending, grouped into buckets  C) The firm's profitability  D) Staff attendance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Buckets such as 0–7, 8–15, 16–21 and over 21 days expose complaints going stale.\nA) Client age is irrelevant. C) and D) are unrelated management reports.\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** An escalation matrix published by an Investment Adviser sets out:\nA) The fee schedule  B) The model asset allocation  C) Named officers, contact details and turnaround at each level  D) The client's risk profile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — It gives the client a defined route upward **inside the firm** before going to a regulator.\nA), B) and D) are disclosure or advisory documents, not the grievance route.\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Redressal of client grievances by an Investment Adviser is dealt with in:\nA) Regulation 21 of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013  B) Section 15K of the SEBI Act  C) The PMLA, 2002  D) The Companies Act, 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **Regulation 21** requires prompt redressal and adequate procedure for expeditious grievance handling.\nB) Section 15K establishes SAT. C) The PMLA covers money laundering. D) The Companies Act governs companies generally.\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** SCORES stands for:\nA) Securities Court Redress System  B) SEBI Complaints Redress System  C) Standard Complaint Resolution Scheme  D) SEBI Corporate Reporting System\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The **SEBI Complaints Redress System**, a centralised web-based grievance platform.\nA), C) and D) are invented expansions.\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** SCORES was launched in:\nA) 1992  B) 2019  C) June 2011  D) April 2024\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SCORES has been operating since **June 2011**.\nA) 1992 is the SEBI Act year. B) No launch occurred in 2019. D) April 2024 is when **SCORES 2.0** went live, not the original launch.\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** SCORES 2.0, with auto-routing and auto-escalation, became operational from:\nA) 1 April 2024  B) 12 November 2021  C) 20 July 2020  D) 31 July 2023\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **1 April 2024**.\nB) That is the RB-IOS 2021 effective date. C) That is the Consumer Protection Act 2019 commencement date. D) That is the date of SEBI's ODR circular.\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Under SCORES 2.0, the entity must file its Action Taken Report within:\nA) 7 calendar days  B) 15 calendar days  C) 45 calendar days  D) 21 calendar days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **21 calendar days** from receipt of the complaint.\nA) and B) are shorter than the prescribed period. C) 45 days is the SAT appeal period, not the SCORES response period.\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** A complaint should normally be lodged on SCORES within how long from the cause of action:\nA) 30 days  B) 90 days  C) One year  D) Three years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The SCORES limitation is broadly **one year**.\nA) 30 days is the period allowed to the entity to respond directly. B) There is no 90-day rule. D) Three years is the general Limitation Act period, applicable to ODR, not SCORES.\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Anonymous complaints on SCORES are:\nA) Accepted  B) Not accepted  C) Accepted on payment of a fee  D) Accepted only from NRIs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Registration requires a **valid email ID and mobile number**; anonymous complaints are not entertained.\nA), C) and D) all suggest routes that do not exist.\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** The SEBI online dispute resolution portal for the securities market is known as:\nA) e-Daakhil  B) Bima Bharosa  C) SMART ODR  D) CGMS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **SMART ODR** — Securities Market Approach for Resolution through ODR.\nA) e-Daakhil is the consumer commissions' e-filing portal. B) Bima Bharosa is IRDAI's portal. D) CGMS is the NPS grievance system.\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** The two stages of the securities market ODR process are:\nA) Mediation and appeal  B) Inquiry and penalty  C) Audit and inspection  D) Online conciliation and online arbitration\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Conciliation first (non-binding), then arbitration (binding) if conciliation fails.\nA) There is no appeal stage within ODR. B) Inquiry and penalty describe SEBI's enforcement process. C) Audit and inspection are supervisory tools.\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** An arbitral award issued under the ODR framework is enforceable under:\nA) The Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996  B) The SEBI Act, 1992  C) The Consumer Protection Act, 2019  D) The Companies Act, 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — ODR arbitration is real arbitration, and the award is enforceable under the **Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996**.\nB), C) and D) do not provide the enforcement machinery for arbitral awards.\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** The RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme came into effect on:\nA) 1 April 2024  B) 12 November 2021  C) 20 July 2020  D) 1 July 2018\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **12 November 2021**.\nA) That is SCORES 2.0. C) That is the Consumer Protection Act commencement. D) 2018 is the year of the earlier NBFC Ombudsman Scheme it replaced.\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** RB-IOS 2021 integrated how many earlier ombudsman schemes:\nA) Two  B) Four  C) Three  D) Five\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The Banking Ombudsman Scheme **2006**, the Ombudsman Scheme for NBFCs **2018** and the Ombudsman Scheme for Digital Transactions **2019**.\nA), B) and D) miscount the merged schemes.\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** The RBI Ombudsman may award compensation for the actual loss suffered up to:\nA) ₹5 lakh  B) ₹10 lakh  C) ₹30 lakh  D) ₹20 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **₹20 lakh** for actual loss, plus a separate amount of up to ₹1 lakh for time, expenses and harassment.\nA) and B) are below the cap. C) ₹30 lakh is the **Insurance** Ombudsman's limit — the classic swap.\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** An insurer must send a written acknowledgement of a grievance within:\nA) 3 working days  B) 7 days  C) 14 days  D) 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Acknowledgement in **3 working days**, resolution within **2 weeks**.\nB) There is no 7-day acknowledgement rule. C) 14 days is the resolution period, not the acknowledgement. D) 30 days is the banking wait before escalation.\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** IRDAI's online policyholder grievance portal is called:\nA) SCORES  B) Bima Bharosa  C) CGMS  D) e-Daakhil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **Bima Bharosa**, formerly the Integrated Grievance Management System (IGMS).\nA) SCORES is SEBI's. C) CGMS is PFRDA's. D) e-Daakhil is the consumer commissions' portal.\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** The Insurance Ombudsman may entertain complaints where the value does not exceed:\nA) ₹10 lakh  B) ₹20 lakh  C) ₹50 lakh  D) ₹30 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **₹30 lakh**, including expenses claimed.\nA) and B) are below the limit; ₹20 lakh is the RBI Ombudsman's actual-loss cap. C) There is no ₹50 lakh insurance limit.\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** NPS subscriber grievances are raised through:\nA) CGMS  B) SCORES  C) CMS  D) Bima Bharosa\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The **Central Grievance Management System**, operated through the Central Recordkeeping Agency.\nB) SCORES is for securities. C) CMS is RBI's Complaint Management System. D) Bima Bharosa is for insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The Securities Appellate Tribunal is established under:\nA) Section 11 of the SCRA  B) The Consumer Protection Act, 2019  C) Section 15K of the SEBI Act, 1992  D) The PFRDA Act, 2013\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Section 15K of the SEBI Act, 1992**, with headquarters in Mumbai.\nA) Section 11 of the SEBI Act deals with SEBI's functions; the SCRA does not create SAT. B) The Consumer Protection Act creates the consumer commissions. D) The PFRDA Act **routes appeals to** SAT but does not establish it.\n</details>\n\n## 🟡 Tier 2 — Medium: Application (Q36–Q70)\n\n**Q36.** An investor is unhappy with the service of his stock broker. His first step should be to:\nA) File a complaint with the broker itself  B) Approach SAT  C) File on SCORES immediately  D) File a police complaint\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The universal ladder starts with the **intermediary**, which alone holds the file, the recording and the money.\nB) SAT hears appeals against regulators' orders. C) SCORES requires the entity to have been approached first. D) A service complaint is not, by itself, a crime.\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A SCORES complaint is rejected because the investor never wrote to the intermediary. The reason is that:\nA) SCORES charges a fee  B) SCORES requires the entity to be approached first  C) SCORES is only for listed companies  D) SCORES has been discontinued\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The precondition is a prior approach to the entity with no reply, an unsatisfactory reply, or no action.\nA) SCORES is free. C) It covers registered intermediaries as well as listed companies. D) SCORES is very much operational, now as SCORES 2.0.\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** Which of the following will SCORES NOT take up:\nA) A delay by a registered broker  B) Non-receipt of a demat statement  C) A matter already pending before a civil court  D) Non-redressal by a listed company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Sub judice** matters are outside SCORES; a regulator cannot run a parallel process to a court.\nA), B) and D) are all standard, admissible SCORES complaints.\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** After SCORES closes his file, an investor still wants ₹3.5 lakh in damages from a broker. He should:\nA) Approach SAT  B) Write to the Finance Ministry  C) Initiate dispute resolution on the SMART ODR portal  D) Abandon the claim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — A disputed money claim needs an **adjudicator**; ODR provides online conciliation and then binding arbitration.\nA) SAT hears appeals against regulators' orders, not investor money claims. B) The ministry is not a redressal forum. D) A live remedy remains available.\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** The essential difference between SCORES and ODR is that:\nA) Both adjudicate identically  B) ODR is available only to companies  C) SCORES awards damages  D) SCORES routes and enforces timelines while ODR adjudicates the dispute\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — **SCORES chases; ODR decides.**\nA) SCORES does not adjudicate at all. B) ODR is expressly available to investors. C) SCORES cannot award money.\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bank customer complained 40 days ago and has received no reply. He may now:\nA) Approach the RBI Ombudsman  B) Wait one full year before escalating  C) Approach SAT  D) Approach the Insurance Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Escalation is permitted after **30 days** without a reply.\nB) One year is the outer limitation, not a waiting period. C) SAT has no banking jurisdiction over customer complaints. D) Wrong sector entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Before a bank communicates a wholly or partly rejected complaint to a customer, it must refer the matter to:\nA) SEBI  B) The RBI Governor  C) Its Internal Ombudsman  D) The consumer commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Internal Ombudsman** provides an independent second look inside the bank before rejection.\nA) SEBI does not regulate banking service complaints. B) The Governor does not review individual complaints. D) The consumer commission is a later, external option for the customer.\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which of the following is NOT covered by RB-IOS 2021:\nA) A commercial bank  B) A deposit-taking NBFC  C) A prepaid payment instrument issuer  D) A life insurance company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Insurers are regulated by **IRDAI**; their complaints go to Bima Bharosa and the Insurance Ombudsman.\nA), B) and C) are all RBI-regulated entities within the scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** The valid ground for a complaint under RB-IOS 2021 is:\nA) A specified closed list of grounds only  B) Any deficiency in service  C) Any loss on an investment  D) Dissatisfaction with the interest rate the bank offers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The 2021 scheme replaced the old closed list with an **open, deficiency-based** test, subject to an exclusion list.\nA) That was the pre-2021 position. C) Market losses are not a banking service deficiency. D) The rate a bank chooses is a commercial decision, expressly excluded.\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A policyholder's claim has been repudiated and the insurer confirmed the rejection in writing. He may approach the Insurance Ombudsman:\nA) Only after two years  B) Only through a lawyer  C) Within one year of the rejection  D) Only if the claim exceeds ₹50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The limitation is **one year** from the rejection or final reply.\nA) Two years is the consumer commission limitation. B) The Ombudsman process is free and needs no lawyer. D) The Ombudsman's ceiling is ₹30 lakh, and there is no floor.\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An Insurance Ombudsman award is binding on:\nA) Both parties  B) The insurer only  C) The complainant only  D) Neither party\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Binding on the **insurer**; the complainant remains free to go to a consumer commission or civil court.\nA) Would remove the complainant's fallback and deter filing. C) Reverses the rule. D) Would make the process meaningless.\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** A policyholder has already filed a civil suit on the same claim. The Insurance Ombudsman will:\nA) Hear it in parallel  B) Stay the civil suit  C) Award double compensation  D) Not admit the complaint\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A matter pending before or decided by a court, consumer forum or arbitrator is **not admissible**.\nA) Parallel proceedings are precisely what the rule prevents. B) An Ombudsman cannot stay a court. C) No such power exists.\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** An NPS subscriber's grievance remains unresolved by the POP after 30 days. He should next approach:\nA) The NPS Trust  B) SEBI  C) SAT directly  D) The Insurance Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — The pension ladder runs intermediary → **NPS Trust** → PFRDA Ombudsman → PFRDA → SAT.\nB) SEBI does not handle pension grievances. C) SAT sits at the end of the ladder, not the start. D) Wrong sector.\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** An appeal against an order of the PFRDA lies to:\nA) The Supreme Court directly  B) The RBI Ombudsman  C) The Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) The National Commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under the **PFRDA Act, 2013**, appeals from PFRDA orders go to **SAT**.\nA) The Supreme Court is reached only after SAT. B) The RBI Ombudsman has no pension jurisdiction. D) Consumer commissions do not hear appeals from regulators.\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** SAT hears appeals against orders of:\nA) Only SEBI  B) Only stock exchanges  C) District courts  D) SEBI, PFRDA and IRDAI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — SAT is the appellate tribunal for **three** financial regulators, plus adjudicating officers and exchanges/depositories in specified matters.\nA) Understates its jurisdiction. B) Exchanges are only one source of appealable orders. C) Appeals from district courts go to the High Court.\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** An investor who lost money on a stock wants SAT to order his broker to compensate him. SAT will:\nA) Award the money  B) Not entertain it, because SAT hears appeals against regulators' orders  C) Refer the matter to the RBI  D) Impose a penalty on the investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SAT is an **appellate** body, not a first-instance complaint counter. The route is broker → SCORES → ODR, or a consumer commission.\nA) SAT does not entertain original money claims by investors. C) Banking has no role here. D) Nothing suggests investor misconduct.\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** An appeal to SAT must ordinarily be filed within:\nA) 30 days  B) 45 days  C) 60 days  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **45 days** from receipt of a copy of the order, condonable for sufficient cause.\nA) 30 days applies to consumer appeals to the National Commission and to the RBI Ombudsman appeal. C) 60 days is the SAT-to-Supreme-Court period. D) There is no 90-day rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** An appeal from SAT to the Supreme Court lies:\nA) On any ground whatsoever  B) Only with SEBI's consent  C) Only on a question of law, within 60 days  D) Within 15 days on any ground\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Section 15Z permits an appeal on a **question of law**, within **60 days**, extendable by a further 60 days for sufficient cause.\nA) Findings of fact are not reopened. B) No regulator's consent is needed. D) The period is 60 days, not 15.\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A non-individual Investment Adviser must appoint:\nA) A merchant banker  B) A custodian  C) An actuary  D) A compliance officer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — A **compliance officer** is mandatory for a non-individual IA, and is central to grievance oversight.\nA), B) and C) are roles belonging to other kinds of intermediary, not to an IA.\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** An Investment Adviser must display on its website:\nA) Client names and portfolios  B) The Investor Charter, escalation matrix and complaints data  C) Its own bank balance  D) A list of guaranteed returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SEBI requires the Investor Charter, the grievance escalation route and **monthly complaints data**.\nA) Client information is confidential. C) Not a required disclosure. D) Guaranteeing returns is prohibited.\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** IA grievance records must ordinarily be maintained for:\nA) 1 year  B) 3 years  C) 5 years  D) 10 years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **Five years**, in line with the general record-retention rule for IAs.\nA) and B) are shorter than the requirement. D) Ten years is the record period for certain other statutes, not for IA records.\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Where a grievance has become the subject of litigation, the IA must keep the records:\nA) For five years only  B) For six months  C) Until final disposal of the matter  D) It need not keep them\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Records in a disputed or litigated matter must be preserved **until final disposal**, overriding the ordinary five-year rule.\nA) Five years may end before the case does. B) and D) would destroy the firm's own evidence.\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A firm closes a complaint after a phone call, with no written reply and no note on file. On inspection this will be treated as:\nA) Resolved  B) Unresolved  C) Withdrawn by the client  D) Escalated\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Resolution requires investigation, a **reasoned written reply** and a record of the outcome.\nA) There is nothing to evidence resolution. C) The client did not withdraw. D) Nothing was escalated.\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A firm receives twelve complaints in one month, all about the same delayed statement. The best response is to:\nA) Reply to each and close the file  B) Ignore them as routine  C) Escalate all twelve to SEBI  D) Treat it as a process defect and fix the root cause\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Categorising complaints by nature exists precisely so that a **cluster** is read as a systemic defect, not as twelve accidents.\nA) Replies without a fix guarantee a thirteenth complaint. B) Ignoring is a regulatory breach. C) A firm does not escalate its own complaints to SEBI; it resolves them.\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** The main reason for recording the SOURCE of each complaint is to:\nA) Identify which regulatory clock applies  B) Bill the client correctly  C) Compute tax  D) Rank staff performance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A SCORES complaint carries a hard 21-day deadline and regulator visibility; a walk-in does not.\nB) Grievances are not billable. C) No tax linkage. D) Staff appraisal is a by-product at best, not the purpose.\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Which of these is a query rather than a grievance:\nA) An allegation of unauthorised trading  B) A request for the current folio balance  C) A complaint of mis-selling  D) A claim for refund of fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A request for information is answered, not logged as a complaint.\nA), C) and D) all allege deficiency, misconduct or loss and must be logged and tracked.\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** SEBI's role in a SCORES complaint is best described as:\nA) Adjudicating the dispute and awarding damages  B) Prosecuting the investor  C) Arbitrating the money claim  D) Routing, monitoring and enforcing timelines\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Regulators **facilitate and enforce deadlines**; adjudication belongs to ombudsmen, arbitrators and courts.\nA) SEBI does not award damages to individual investors through SCORES. B) The investor is the complainant. C) Arbitration happens under ODR, not SCORES.\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** A forged delivery instruction caused an unauthorised sale of a client's shares. The correct approach is to:\nA) Use SCORES only  B) Pursue the intermediary and then SCORES/ODR, and also file a criminal complaint  C) Go to SAT only  D) Approach the Insurance Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Forgery is a **crime** as well as a service failure, so the civil recovery track and the police/EOW track run together.\nA) Ignores the criminal dimension. C) SAT has no original jurisdiction here. D) Wrong sector entirely.\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** A grievance about the conduct of a mutual fund distributor may also be taken to:\nA) AMFI  B) IRDAI  C) PFRDA  D) The RBI Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — **AMFI** administers the ARN framework and can act against a distributor's registration.\nB), C) and D) regulate insurance, pensions and banking respectively.\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** Unclaimed dividends and shares transferred out of a company are recovered by claiming from:\nA) SAT  B) The RBI Ombudsman  C) The IEPF Authority  D) The stock exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The **Investor Education and Protection Fund Authority** under the MCA handles such claims.\nA) SAT hears appeals. B) Banking is unrelated. D) Exchanges do not hold unclaimed corporate benefits.\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Which body compensates investors, up to prescribed limits, when a trading member is declared a defaulter:\nA) The Investor Protection Fund  B) The IEPF  C) DICGC  D) SAT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — Each exchange maintains an **Investor Protection Fund** for defaulter claims.\nB) The IEPF handles unclaimed corporate benefits. C) DICGC insures bank deposits. D) SAT is a tribunal.\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** The body that administers Investment Advisers and receives their periodic complaints data is:\nA) AMFI  B) BASL  C) NCDRC  D) The Council for Insurance Ombudsmen\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **BASL**, BSE Administration and Supervision Ltd.\nA) AMFI covers mutual funds and distributors. C) NCDRC is the apex consumer commission. D) The CIO administers the Insurance Ombudsman offices.\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** An award of the RBI Ombudsman may be appealed to:\nA) SAT  B) The National Commission  C) SEBI  D) The Executive Director in charge of RBI's Consumer Education and Protection Department\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The Appellate Authority under RB-IOS 2021 is that **Executive Director of the RBI**, and the appeal must be filed within 30 days.\nA) SAT has no banking-complaint jurisdiction. B) Consumer commissions hear fresh complaints, not RB-IOS appeals. C) SEBI has no role in banking.\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following would NOT be maintainable before the RBI Ombudsman:\nA) A failed UPI transaction that was never reversed  B) A dispute over the rate of interest the bank chooses to offer  C) A wrongly levied service charge  D) A long delay in issuing a cheque book\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A bank's **commercial judgement or business decision**, such as its pricing, is expressly excluded unless there is a deficiency in service.\nA), C) and D) are all classic service deficiencies squarely within the scheme.\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** The universal sequence in Indian financial grievance redress is:\nA) Regulator, then intermediary, then court  B) Court, then regulator, then intermediary  C) Intermediary, then regulator, then tribunal or court  D) Tribunal, then intermediary, then regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The firm can actually fix the problem; the regulator enforces the clock; the tribunal or court decides contested rights.\nA), B) and D) all invert the ladder, and each would make the complaint premature or not maintainable at the level chosen.\n</details>\n\n## 🔴 Tier 3 — Hard: Numerical & Scenario (Q71–Q100)\n\n**Q71.** A SCORES complaint is registered on 1 April 2025. By when must the entity file its Action Taken Report:\nA) 8 April 2025  B) 16 April 2025  C) 22 April 2025  D) 16 May 2025\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The entity gets **21 calendar days**. 1 April + 21 days = **22 April 2025**.\nA) Is 7 days. B) Is 15 days, which is the investor's review-request window, not the entity's response period. D) Is 45 days, the SAT appeal period.\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Adding the five prescribed SCORES 2.0 periods — 21 + 15 + 21 + 15 + 21 days — gives a maximum of:\nA) 63 days  B) 78 days  C) 93 days  D) 105 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 21 + 15 + 21 + 15 + 21 = **93 days**. Entity 21, review request 15, Designated Body 21, second review request 15, SEBI 21.\nA) 63 = 21 × 3, omitting both 15-day windows. B) 78 omits one 15-day window. D) 105 would need five 21-day periods.\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Mr Rao complained to his bank on 10 January 2025 and never received a reply. The last date to file with the RBI Ombudsman is about:\nA) 9 February 2025  B) 10 January 2026  C) 9 February 2026  D) 10 July 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Where **no reply** is received, the limit is **one year and thirty days** from the date of the complaint. 10 January 2025 + 1 year = 10 January 2026; + 30 days = **9 February 2026**.\nA) 9 February 2025 is the **earliest** date he may escalate — 30 days after complaining — not the last. B) Omits the extra 30 days. D) There is no eighteen-month rule.\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A bank customer proves an actual loss of ₹26,00,000 plus serious harassment. The maximum the RBI Ombudsman can award is:\nA) ₹20,00,000  B) ₹21,00,000  C) ₹26,00,000  D) ₹27,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Actual loss is capped at **₹20,00,000** however large the proven loss, and up to **₹1,00,000** may be added for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish. 20,00,000 + 1,00,000 = **₹21,00,000**.\nA) Ignores the separate harassment component. C) Exceeds the statutory cap. D) Adds ₹1 lakh to the uncapped loss instead of to the capped ₹20 lakh.\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A policyholder claims ₹28,50,000 and ₹1,20,000 of related expenses. Before the Insurance Ombudsman this complaint is:\nA) Not admissible, being above the limit  B) Admissible only up to ₹20 lakh  C) Admissible, since the total of ₹29,70,000 is within the ₹30 lakh limit  D) Referable to SAT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 28,50,000 + 1,20,000 = **₹29,70,000**, which is **below ₹30,00,000**, so the Ombudsman may entertain it.\nA) Miscomputes or misapplies the ceiling. B) ₹20 lakh is the RBI Ombudsman's cap, not the insurance limit. D) SAT has no jurisdiction over an individual insurance claim.\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** The consideration paid for a service was ₹62,00,000. Under the current 2021 Jurisdiction Rules the complaint lies before the:\nA) District Commission  B) State Commission  C) National Commission  D) Securities Appellate Tribunal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — ₹62,00,000 is **above ₹50 lakh but below ₹2 crore**, which is the State Commission's band.\nA) The District ceiling is ₹50 lakh, which ₹62 lakh exceeds. C) The National Commission starts above ₹2 crore. D) SAT is not a consumer forum.\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** A District Commission orders a firm to pay ₹18,00,000. Before appealing to the State Commission, the firm must deposit:\nA) ₹4,50,000  B) ₹9,00,000  C) ₹18,00,000  D) Nothing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — **50%** of the amount ordered: 50% × ₹18,00,000 = **₹9,00,000**.\nA) Is 25%. C) Is the full amount, not half. D) The deposit condition is mandatory and exists to deter delaying appeals.\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A register shows 40 open complaints: 22 aged 0–7 days, 10 aged 8–15 days, 5 aged 16–21 days and 3 aged over 21 days. The proportion already past the 21-day SCORES clock is:\nA) 5.0%  B) 7.5%  C) 12.5%  D) 20.0%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 3 ÷ 40 = 0.075 = **7.5%**.\nA) 2 ÷ 40 would give 5%. C) 12.5% is the 16–21 day bucket, 5 ÷ 40, which is approaching breach but not yet in breach. D) 20% would be 8 complaints.\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** The cause of action arose on 5 June 2023. A consumer complaint must ordinarily be filed by:\nA) 5 December 2023  B) 5 June 2024  C) 5 June 2025  D) 5 June 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The limitation is **two years**: 5 June 2023 + 2 years = **5 June 2025**.\nA) Is six months. B) Is one year. D) Is three years, the general Limitation Act period for civil suits, not the consumer period.\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A SEBI order is received on 12 March 2025. The 45-day period to appeal to SAT expires on:\nA) 11 April 2025  B) 26 April 2025  C) 12 May 2025  D) 26 May 2025\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — March has 31 days, so 31 − 12 = **19 days** remain in March; 45 − 19 = **26 days** into April = **26 April 2025**.\nA) Is 30 days. C) Is two calendar months. D) Is roughly 75 days.\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** If the Supreme Court condones the maximum further delay permitted, the outer limit for appealing a SAT order is:\nA) 60 days  B) 90 days  C) 120 days  D) 180 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **60 days** as of right, plus a further **60 days** if sufficient cause is shown: 60 + 60 = **120 days**.\nA) Ignores the condonation. B) There is no 30-day extension. D) Would require a further 120 days, which the section does not allow.\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An insurer receives a grievance on 3 March. Its resolution and final letter are due within two weeks, that is by:\nA) 6 March  B) 10 March  C) 17 March  D) 2 April\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Two weeks = 14 days: 3 March + 14 = **17 March**.\nA) 6 March is 3 days later, which is only the **acknowledgement** deadline. B) Is one week. D) Is 30 days, which is the banking wait, not the insurer's resolution period.\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** A firm opens the month with 12 pending complaints, receives 48 and resolves 44. Closing pendency is:\nA) 4  B) 16  C) 20  D) 36\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Closing = opening + received − resolved = 12 + 48 − 44 = **16**.\nA) 4 is received minus resolved, ignoring the opening balance. C) 20 would need only 40 resolved. D) 36 subtracts the wrong way round.\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Of the 48 complaints received in a month, 3 were resolved only after 21 days. The breach rate is:\nA) 3.00%  B) 6.25%  C) 12.50%  D) 25.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — 3 ÷ 48 = 0.0625 = **6.25%**.\nA) Confuses the count with a percentage. C) 12.5% would be 6 of 48. D) 25% would be 12 of 48.\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** An Action Taken Report is filed on SCORES on 20 May 2025. The investor's 15-day window to seek a first review closes on:\nA) 27 May 2025  B) 4 June 2025  C) 10 June 2025  D) 20 June 2025\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — May has 31 days, so 31 − 20 = **11 days** remain in May; 15 − 11 = **4 days** into June = **4 June 2025**.\nA) Is 7 days. C) Is 21 days, the Designated Body's own review period. D) Is one month.\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** The CCPA penalises a company ₹10 lakh for a first misleading advertisement and ₹50 lakh for a subsequent one. The total penalty is:\nA) ₹50 lakh  B) ₹55 lakh  C) ₹60 lakh  D) ₹65 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — ₹10 lakh + ₹50 lakh = **₹60 lakh**. The first-offence ceiling is **₹10 lakh** and the repeat-offence ceiling **₹50 lakh**.\nA) Counts only the repeat penalty. B) and D) do not follow from the two stated ceilings.\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** An IA receives a complaint on 2 September. Its escalation matrix promises Level 3 resolution within 21 days of receipt. That deadline falls on:\nA) 9 September  B) 17 September  C) 23 September  D) 2 October\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — 2 September + 21 days = **23 September**.\nA) Is 7 days. B) Is 15 days. D) Is 30 days.\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** A consumer paid ₹1,80,00,000 for a service and claims ₹40,00,000 in compensation. Under the current rules the complaint lies before the:\nA) District Commission, because the claim is ₹40 lakh  B) State Commission  C) National Commission  D) Any commission the complainant chooses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Jurisdiction follows the **value of the consideration paid**, ₹1,80,00,000, not the compensation demanded. ₹1.80 crore falls above ₹50 lakh and below ₹2 crore, so the **State Commission** has jurisdiction.\nA) Applies the wrong measure — the ₹40 lakh claim is irrelevant to jurisdiction. C) Would need consideration above ₹2 crore. D) Pecuniary jurisdiction is not a matter of choice.\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Under the pecuniary limits as ORIGINALLY enacted in the 2019 Act, a complaint on a consideration of ₹4,00,00,000 would lie before the:\nA) District Commission  B) State Commission  C) National Commission  D) Securities Appellate Tribunal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — As originally enacted, the State Commission covered **above ₹1 crore up to ₹10 crore**, and ₹4 crore falls in that band.\nA) The original District ceiling was ₹1 crore. C) The original National threshold was above ₹10 crore. D) SAT is not a consumer forum. *(Note: under the revised 2021 Rules, ₹4 crore would go to the National Commission — which is why the stem specifies which limits apply.)*\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An NPS subscriber files a grievance with his POP on 1 July. If it remains unresolved, he may take it to the NPS Trust after:\nA) 7 days  B) 15 days  C) 30 days, that is from about 31 July  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — The intermediary is allowed **30 days** to resolve the grievance under the PFRDA Redressal of Subscriber Grievance Regulations, 2015.\nA) and B) are shorter than the regulated period, so escalation would be premature. D) 90 days is not a period used anywhere in the pension ladder.\n</details>\n\n**Q91.** A subscriber receives the NPS Trust's decision on 10 August. His appeal to the PFRDA Ombudsman must be filed within:\nA) 15 days  B) 30 days  C) 45 days  D) One year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — **45 days** from the decision, or from the expiry of the 30-day resolution period, extendable for sufficient cause.\nA) 15 days is a SCORES review window. B) 30 days is the appeal period from the Ombudsman's order to PFRDA, the next rung up. D) One year is the insurance and banking limitation, not this one.\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** An investor who buys and sells shares purely for speculative trading sues his broker in a consumer commission. His main hurdle will be:\nA) There is no hurdle  B) The transaction may be treated as being for a commercial purpose  C) Consumer commissions cannot hear financial matters  D) Only SEBI may be made an opposite party\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — A person acting for a **commercial purpose** falls outside the definition of consumer, and purely speculative trading invites that characterisation. The livelihood-by-self-employment carve-out is the counter-argument.\nA) The commercial-purpose exclusion is a real and frequently litigated obstacle. C) Financial services are squarely \"services\" under the Act. D) The service provider, not the regulator, is the opposite party.\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** Mrs Iyer avails a service for which her husband paid. Is she a consumer:\nA) No, only the payer is a consumer  B) Only if she paid half the fee  C) Only under a written assignment  D) Yes, a beneficiary using the service with the buyer's approval is a consumer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — The definition expressly includes a **user or beneficiary with the approval of the buyer**.\nA) Would exclude family members, defeating the Act's purpose. B) No proportionate payment is required. C) No assignment is needed.\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** A client complains to his IA on 5 January; the adviser replies by phone on 6 January and marks the file closed with no note. The compliance failure is:\nA) There is none — a same-day reply is exemplary  B) No written reply and no record, so the complaint counts as unresolved  C) The reply was too fast to be genuine  D) The client should have gone to SCORES first\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — Resolution requires **investigation, a reasoned written reply and a record**. Speed without documentation proves nothing at inspection.\nA) Speed is welcome but does not substitute for evidence. C) Promptness is not itself a defect. D) The ladder correctly starts with the IA, not SCORES.\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** An IA firm's average complaint age is 9 days, yet three complaints are 40 days old. This illustrates why:\nA) Averages conceal breaches, while ageing buckets expose them  B) Averages are the more accurate measure  C) Forty days is an acceptable resolution time  D) Ageing reports are optional\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A)** — A comfortable mean can hide the handful of files that carry the entire regulatory risk, which is exactly why the syllabus lists **ageing** as a separate element.\nB) An average is precisely what fails here. C) Forty days is nearly double the 21-day SCORES clock. D) Ageing is a listed element of a robust system.\n</details>\n\n**Q96.** A client insists on going straight to SAT over a service delay by his Investment Adviser. The correct advice is:\nA) Yes, file at SAT immediately  B) Approach the Insurance Ombudsman  C) SAT hears appeals against regulators' orders, so use the IA, then SCORES, then ODR  D) Approach the RBI Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — SAT has no original jurisdiction over an investor's service complaint against an intermediary.\nA) The appeal would not be maintainable. B) and D) belong to the insurance and banking sectors respectively.\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** A complaint against an unlisted private company is lodged on SCORES. The likely outcome is:\nA) SEBI will take it up and award damages  B) It will not be taken up, as unlisted companies fall outside SCORES  C) It will be transferred to SAT  D) It will be treated as an appeal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — SCORES covers **SEBI-registered intermediaries and listed companies**; unlisted and private companies are outside its scope, and the complainant should look to the MCA, a consumer commission or a civil court.\nA) SEBI neither has jurisdiction nor awards damages through SCORES. C) SCORES does not transfer matters to SAT. D) A complaint is not an appeal.\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** A claim of ₹42,00,000 is repudiated by a life insurer. Before the Insurance Ombudsman this complaint:\nA) Will be heard in full  B) Will be scaled down and an award of ₹30 lakh made  C) Will be referred to SEBI  D) Cannot be entertained, as the value exceeds the ₹30 lakh limit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — ₹42,00,000 is **above the ₹30,00,000 ceiling**, so the route is a consumer commission or a civil court instead.\nA) The ceiling is jurisdictional. B) The Ombudsman cannot take jurisdiction by trimming the claim. C) SEBI does not regulate insurance.\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** A customer's digital wallet transaction fails and the amount is not reversed by the issuer. After the issuer fails to resolve it, the correct external escalation is:\nA) SEBI SCORES  B) The Insurance Ombudsman  C) The RBI Ombudsman under RB-IOS 2021  D) SAT\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Prepaid payment instrument issuers and payment system participants are **covered by RB-IOS 2021**, which absorbed the old Ombudsman Scheme for Digital Transactions.\nA) SCORES covers the securities market. B) Wrong sector. D) SAT hears appeals against regulators' orders.\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Which single statement best summarises this chapter:\nA) Every complaint should go to the regulator first  B) SAT decides all investor complaints  C) Grievance records are optional for small firms  D) Redress runs intermediary, then regulator, then tribunal or court; each sector has its own portal and clock; and regulators monitor while ombudsmen, arbitrators and courts adjudicate\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — It captures the **ladder**, the **sector-specific portals and timelines**, and the crucial split between **monitoring** and **adjudication**.\nA) Inverts the ladder and makes the complaint premature. B) SAT is appellate only. C) Records are a regulatory requirement and the firm's own best evidence.\n</details>\n\n## 🧩 Worked Caselet — Exam-Style Practice\n\n> **Attempt all five sub-questions before checking any answer.** Track the dates carefully — every sub-question turns on a deadline or a forum.\n\n### Case: Mr Devendra Prabhu's Four Grievances\n\nMr Devendra Prabhu, 54, a consulting engineer in Pune, is a fee-paying client of **Sahyadri Wealth Advisors**, a SEBI-registered **non-individual** Investment Adviser. Over one difficult half-year, four things go wrong:\n\n| # | What happened | Key date | Amount |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | An **unauthorised sale of shares** in his broking account | Broker's unsatisfactory reply; SCORES complaint lodged **2 June 2025** | ₹6,20,000 |\n| 2 | ₹48,000 debited for a **failed NEFT** and never reversed; written complaint to the bank on **12 February 2025**, **no reply at all** | 12 February 2025 | ₹48,000 |\n| 3 | His health insurer **repudiated a hospitalisation claim**; written rejection received **20 March 2025** | 20 March 2025 | ₹9,40,000 |\n| 4 | An **NPS contribution not credited**; grievance raised on **CGMS on 1 July 2025** with his POP | 1 July 2025 | ₹1,50,000 |\n\nSeparately, on **3 May 2025** he complains to Sahyadri Wealth Advisors that it recommended a product **without disclosing a conflict of interest**. He has paid the firm advisory fees of **₹1,10,000** over two years.\n\n---\n\n**QC1.** For the SCORES complaint lodged on 2 June 2025, by when must the broker file its Action Taken Report, and what happens if it does not:\nA) 9 June 2025; the complaint lapses  B) 23 June 2025; the complaint auto-escalates to the Designated Body  C) 17 July 2025; it goes to SAT  D) 2 July 2025; it goes to a consumer commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The entity gets **21 calendar days**: 2 June + 21 = **23 June 2025**. Under **SCORES 2.0** a missed deadline triggers **automatic escalation to the Designated Body** without the investor doing anything — the whole point of the 2024 upgrade.\nA) 7 days is not the period, and complaints do not lapse on the entity's default. C) 17 July is 45 days, the SAT period, and SAT has no role here. D) 2 July is 30 days, and a consumer commission is a separate parallel route, not the automatic next step within SCORES.\n</details>\n\n**QC2.** On the ₹48,000 NEFT complaint made to the bank on 12 February 2025 with no reply, what are the earliest and last dates for approaching the RBI Ombudsman:\nA) Earliest 12 February 2025; last 12 February 2026  B) Earliest immediately; there is no last date  C) Earliest about 14 March 2025; last about 14 March 2026  D) He cannot approach the Ombudsman at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — He must wait **30 days** for the bank to reply: 12 February 2025 + 30 days = **14 March 2025** (February 2025 has 28 days, so 16 days remain in February and 14 fall in March). Where **no reply** is received, the limitation is **one year and thirty days** from the complaint: 12 February 2026 + 30 days = **14 March 2026**.\nA) Ignores both the 30-day waiting period and the extra 30 days in the limitation. B) A limitation period certainly applies. D) The complaint is squarely a deficiency in service under RB-IOS 2021.\n</details>\n\n**QC3.** On the repudiated health claim of ₹9,40,000, with the written rejection received on 20 March 2025, the Insurance Ombudsman:\nA) Cannot hear it, as the value is too high  B) Can hear it only after three years  C) Will hear it only if he engages a lawyer  D) Can hear it, as ₹9.40 lakh is within the ₹30 lakh limit, provided he files by about 20 March 2026\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D)** — Two tests are satisfied. **Value:** ₹9,40,000 is comfortably below the **₹30,00,000** ceiling. **Limitation:** the complaint must be filed within **one year** of the rejection, that is by about **20 March 2026**. He has already made a written representation and received a rejection, so the precondition is met.\nA) ₹9.40 lakh is well under the ceiling. B) Three years is the civil limitation period, not the Ombudsman's. C) The process is free and no lawyer is required.\n</details>\n\n**QC4.** The NPS grievance raised on CGMS on 1 July 2025 is unresolved. The correct route from here is:\nA) Straight to SEBI SCORES  B) NPS Trust after 30 days, then the PFRDA Ombudsman, then PFRDA, then SAT  C) Straight to the Insurance Ombudsman  D) Only the District Commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B)** — The intermediary has **30 days** to resolve, so from about **31 July 2025** he may escalate to the **NPS Trust**; then to the **PFRDA Ombudsman** (appeal within **45 days**), then to **PFRDA** (within **30 days**), and finally to **SAT** under the PFRDA Act, 2013.\nA) SCORES has no pension jurisdiction. C) Wrong sector. D) A consumer commission is a possible parallel route but it is not the prescribed pension ladder, and skipping the ladder wastes his fastest remedy.\n</details>\n\n**QC5.** On the 3 May 2025 conflict-of-interest complaint against Sahyadri Wealth Advisors, what must the firm do, and where can the ₹1,10,000 fee dispute go if it stays unresolved:\nA) Nothing beyond a verbal reply, since he is a long-standing client  B) Refer it straight to SAT  C) Log source, date and nature, have someone other than the adviser investigate, reply in writing within 21 days and retain records 5 years; if unresolved, SCORES and then the SMART ODR portal  D) Close it as an ordinary investment loss\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C)** — Under **Regulation 21** the firm must redress the grievance promptly through an adequate documented procedure. In practice that means: **log** the source, date/time and nature; mark it **internal**; have it investigated by the **compliance officer or grievance officer rather than the adviser who gave the advice**, since the complaint is about that very advice; issue a **reasoned written reply within the 21-day benchmark**; record status and ageing; and **retain the records for 5 years — or until final disposal if it becomes litigated**. If Mr Prabhu remains dissatisfied he escalates to **SCORES**, and then to the **SMART ODR portal**, where the ₹1,10,000 claim can be conciliated and, failing that, arbitrated.\n\n**The wider lesson:** the ₹1,10,000 is not the real exposure. A conflict-of-interest allegation goes to **Regulation 21 and the code of conduct together**, and an undocumented closure would convert a manageable client complaint into an adverse inspection finding.\n\nA) A verbal reply leaves no evidence, so on inspection the complaint counts as **unresolved**. B) SAT is appellate and hears challenges to regulators' orders, not client complaints. D) The allegation is about **undisclosed conflict**, not market performance, and mislabelling it would itself be a compliance failure.\n</details>\n"}], "papers": [{"num": "01", "md": "# Mock Paper 1 — NISM Series X-A (Investment Adviser Level 1)\n\n**Total: 150 marks · 135 questions · Time: 3 hours · Pass: 90/150 (60%) · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n## Section A — Independent MCQs (90 questions × 1 mark = 90 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Keerthi Nagaraj, a 27-year-old software engineer in Bengaluru, lists the following on her personal balance sheet: savings account Rs 3,10,000; liquid fund Rs 1,40,000; equity mutual funds Rs 6,80,000; EPF balance Rs 4,20,000; car Rs 2,50,000. Her liabilities are a car loan outstanding of Rs 5,60,000 and a credit card outstanding of Rs 1,15,000. What is her net worth?\nA) Rs 11,25,000  B) Rs 6,75,000  C) Rs 15,50,000  D) Rs 18,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 11,25,000**\n\nTotal assets = 3,10,000 + 1,40,000 + 6,80,000 + 4,20,000 + 2,50,000 = **Rs 18,00,000**.\nTotal liabilities = 5,60,000 + 1,15,000 = **Rs 6,75,000**.\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 18,00,000 − 6,75,000 = **Rs 11,25,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6,75,000** - wrong; this is total liabilities, not net worth.\n- **C) Rs 15,50,000** - wrong; this drops the car from assets but forgets to deduct liabilities altogether.\n- **D) Rs 18,00,000** - wrong; this is total assets. Assets are not net worth — the liabilities must still be subtracted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** In the six-step financial planning process, which step immediately follows \"defining client needs and financial goals\"?\nA) Portfolio construction  B) Risk profiling and asset allocation  C) Gathering data on the client's financial situation  D) Reviewing and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Risk profiling and asset allocation**\n\nThe sequence is: (1) establish the client–planner relationship, (2) gather data on the client's financial situation, (3) define client needs and financial goals, (4) **risk profiling and asset allocation**, (5) portfolio construction, (6) reviewing and rebalancing. Goals must be defined before risk can be profiled, because the return the goals require is itself an input to the risk discussion.\n\n- **A) Portfolio construction** - wrong; this is step 5. Products can only be chosen after the asset allocation is fixed.\n- **C) Gathering data on the client's financial situation** - wrong; this is step 2, which comes *before* goals are defined.\n- **D) Reviewing and rebalancing** - wrong; this is the final step, step 6.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Sandeep, aged 26, works in a stable product-engineering role in Bengaluru, has no dependants and a 30-year horizon for his retirement goal — but he tells his adviser that a 10% fall in his portfolio would keep him awake at night. How should the adviser set the asset allocation?\nA) On risk capacity alone, since capacity is objective and measurable  B) On the simple average of capacity and tolerance  C) On the higher of risk capacity and risk tolerance  D) On the lower of risk capacity and risk tolerance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) On the lower of risk capacity and risk tolerance**\n\nSandeep has **high risk capacity** (long horizon, stable income, no dependants — an objective assessment) but **low risk tolerance** (his subjective willingness to bear volatility). Where the two conflict, the plan is built to the **lower** of the two. An allocation his psychology cannot sustain will be abandoned in the first drawdown, converting a paper loss into a permanent one.\n\n- **A) On risk capacity alone** - wrong; ignoring tolerance is exactly what produces panic-selling at the bottom.\n- **B) On the simple average** - wrong; there is no averaging rule. The binding constraint is the lower of the two.\n- **C) On the higher of risk capacity and risk tolerance** - wrong; a high return requirement does not override the client's ability to stay invested through a fall.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** How does SEBI address the conflict of interest that arises when the person advising a client also earns commission on executing the recommendation?\nA) By requiring advice and distribution to be segregated, so that an individual investment adviser cannot do both  B) By requiring only that the commission be disclosed to the client in writing  C) By permitting the adviser to retain the commission if it is lower than the advisory fee charged  D) By delegating supervision of the conflict to the stock exchange as first-level regulator\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) By requiring advice and distribution to be segregated, so that an individual investment adviser cannot do both**\n\nThe structural answer SEBI adopts is **segregation of advice and distribution**. A registered Investment Adviser must keep advisory activity separate from any distribution activity, and an **individual IA cannot carry on both**. The adviser owes a **fiduciary** duty — the client's interest ahead of the adviser's own — which mere disclosure cannot satisfy.\n\n- **B) Disclosure alone** - wrong; disclosure is necessary but SEBI goes further and requires segregation.\n- **C) Retaining commission if lower than the fee** - wrong; there is no such proportionality test in the regulations.\n- **D) Exchange supervision** - wrong; exchanges act as first-level regulators of their *brokers*, not of investment advisers' compensation model.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** An adviser notices that a client's home down-payment, a sibling's wedding contribution and a planned car purchase all fall in the same calendar year, and recommends deferring the car purchase by two years. Which goal-setting principle is being applied?\nA) Staggering the timing of goals  B) Prioritising goals  C) Ensuring adequate focus on important goals  D) Quantifying goals in future rupees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Staggering the timing of goals**\n\n**Staggering** means moving discretionary goals forward or back so that several large funding requirements do not collide in a single year, which would break the plan's cash flow. That is precisely what deferring the car purchase achieves.\n\n- **B) Prioritising goals** - wrong; prioritisation ranks goals by importance (retirement over a car upgrade). Here the car is not being dropped, only re-dated.\n- **C) Ensuring adequate focus on important goals** - wrong; this principle addresses long-dated goals such as retirement being neglected because they feel far away.\n- **D) Quantifying goals in future rupees** - wrong; that is about inflating today's cost to the goal date, not about timing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Tejaswini receives Rs 3,40,000 from an ESOP exercise and invests the whole amount for 12 years in an instrument earning 11% p.a. compounded annually. What will it be worth at the end of 12 years?\nA) Rs 7,88,800  B) Rs 11,89,473  C) Rs 10,71,597  D) Rs 13,20,315\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 11,89,473**\n\nFV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ = 3,40,000 × (1.11)¹² .\n(1.11)¹² = 3.498451.\nFV = 3,40,000 × 3.498451 = **Rs 11,89,473**.\n\n- **A) Rs 7,88,800** - wrong; this is simple interest, 3,40,000 × (1 + 0.11 × 12). It ignores compounding entirely.\n- **C) Rs 10,71,597** - wrong; this compounds for only 11 years.\n- **D) Rs 13,20,315** - wrong; this compounds for 13 years.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** An investment of Rs 2,80,000 made by Vikram nine years ago is worth Rs 7,70,000 today. What is the compound annual growth rate?\nA) 10.64%  B) 13.48%  C) 19.44%  D) 11.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 11.90%**\n\nCAGR = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (7,70,000 ÷ 2,80,000)^(1/9) − 1 = (2.75)^0.1111 − 1 = 1.1190 − 1 = **11.90% p.a.**\n\n- **A) 10.64%** - wrong; this uses n = 10 years instead of 9.\n- **B) 13.48%** - wrong; this uses n = 8 years instead of 9.\n- **C) 19.44%** - wrong; this is simple-interest thinking — total gain of 175% divided by 9 years. It ignores compounding.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Anusha starts a SIP of Rs 18,000 a month at the end of every month for 12 years in a fund expected to return 13% p.a. compounded monthly. What corpus does she accumulate?\nA) Rs 25,92,000  B) Rs 55,40,438  C) Rs 62,46,312  D) Rs 61,79,368\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 61,79,368**\n\nr = 0.13 ÷ 12 = 0.0108333; n = 12 × 12 = 144.\n(1.0108333)¹⁴⁴ = 4.719064.\nFV = 18,000 × [(4.719064 − 1) ÷ 0.0108333] = 18,000 × 343.298 = **Rs 61,79,368**.\n\n- **A) Rs 25,92,000** - wrong; this is only the total contributed (18,000 × 144). It ignores all returns.\n- **B) Rs 55,40,438** - wrong; this treats the SIP as an annual investment of Rs 2,16,000 compounded annually at 13%, losing the benefit of monthly compounding.\n- **C) Rs 62,46,312** - wrong; this is the annuity *due* value (× 1.0108333), but the question specifies payment at the **end** of each month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Rohit invests Rs 25,000 at the **beginning** of every month for 8 years in a scheme returning 10.5% p.a. compounded monthly. What is the maturity value?\nA) Rs 24,00,000  B) Rs 37,69,610  C) Rs 37,36,912  D) Rs 41,28,288\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 37,69,610**\n\nr = 0.105 ÷ 12 = 0.00875; n = 96.\n(1.00875)⁹⁶ = 2.308119.\nOrdinary annuity FV = 25,000 × [(2.308119 − 1) ÷ 0.00875] = 25,000 × 149.4765 = Rs 37,36,912.\nPayments are at the **beginning** of each month, so this is an annuity **due**: 37,36,912 × 1.00875 = **Rs 37,69,610**.\n\n- **A) Rs 24,00,000** - wrong; this is only the sum contributed (25,000 × 96).\n- **C) Rs 37,36,912** - wrong; this is the ordinary (end-of-month) annuity value, missing the (1 + r) adjustment.\n- **D) Rs 41,28,288** - wrong; this multiplies the ordinary annuity by (1 + annual rate) instead of (1 + monthly rate).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A debt fund is expected to return 10.4% p.a. while inflation runs at 5.8% p.a. What is the real rate of return?\nA) 4.17%  B) 4.60%  C) 16.80%  D) 4.35%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 4.35%**\n\nReal return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.104 ÷ 1.058) − 1 = 1.043478 − 1 = **4.35%**.\n\n- **A) 4.17%** - wrong; this divides the difference by (1 + nominal) instead of (1 + inflation).\n- **B) 4.60%** - wrong; this is the crude subtraction 10.4 − 5.8, which always overstates the real return.\n- **C) 16.80%** - wrong; this multiplies (1.104 × 1.058) instead of dividing — it compounds inflation onto the return rather than removing it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A bank quotes a nominal rate of 9.6% p.a. on a deposit, compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.60%  B) 9.83%  C) 9.95%  D) 10.03%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 9.95%**\n\nEAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1 with r = 0.096 and m = 4.\n= (1 + 0.024)⁴ − 1 = (1.024)⁴ − 1 = 1.099512 − 1 = **9.95%**.\n\n- **A) 9.60%** - wrong; this is the nominal (stated) rate. The nominal rate never changes with frequency — only the effective rate does.\n- **B) 9.83%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **half-yearly** compounding, (1.048)² − 1.\n- **D) 10.03%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **monthly** compounding, (1.008)¹² − 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Deepika wants to accumulate Rs 60,00,000 in 14 years. Her portfolio is expected to earn 12% p.a. compounded monthly. What monthly investment, made at the end of each month, is required?\nA) Rs 13,886  B) Rs 13,748  C) Rs 15,436  D) Rs 35,714\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 13,886**\n\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)ⁿ − 1], with r = 0.12 ÷ 12 = 0.01 and n = 14 × 12 = 168.\n(1.01)¹⁶⁸ = 5.320970.\nPMT = (60,00,000 × 0.01) ÷ (5.320970 − 1) = 60,000 ÷ 4.320970 = **Rs 13,886 a month**.\n\n- **B) Rs 13,748** - wrong; this is the annuity **due** payment (divided by 1.01), but the question specifies end-of-month contributions.\n- **C) Rs 15,436** - wrong; this solves for an annual contribution at 12% annual compounding and then divides by 12.\n- **D) Rs 35,714** - wrong; this is simply 60,00,000 ÷ 168, assuming zero return on the contributions.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Nikhil's gross monthly income is Rs 1,45,000 and his total monthly expenses are Rs 98,600. What is his savings ratio?\nA) 20.00%  B) 47.06%  C) 32.00%  D) 68.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 32.00%**\n\nSavings (surplus) = 1,45,000 − 98,600 = Rs 46,400.\nSavings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 46,400 ÷ 1,45,000 = **32.00%**.\nSelf-check: savings ratio + expenses ratio = 32% + 68% = 100%. ✓\n\n- **A) 20.00%** - wrong; 20% is the *healthy benchmark* for a savings ratio, not Nikhil's actual figure.\n- **B) 47.06%** - wrong; this divides savings by expenses (46,400 ÷ 98,600) instead of by gross income.\n- **D) 68.00%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Shruti holds a savings account balance of Rs 1,35,000, a liquid fund of Rs 2,25,000, equity mutual funds of Rs 4,00,000 and a PPF balance of Rs 3,60,000. Her monthly household expenses are Rs 84,000. What is her liquidity ratio?\nA) 0.36 months  B) 9.05 months  C) 13.33 months  D) 4.29 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 4.29 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings balance + liquid fund = 1,35,000 + 2,25,000 = **Rs 3,60,000**. Equity mutual funds (volatile) and PPF (locked in) do **not** qualify as liquid assets.\nLiquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses = 3,60,000 ÷ 84,000 = **4.29 months**, which sits inside the healthy 3–6 month band.\n\n- **A) 0.36 months** - wrong; this divides by *annual* expenses (84,000 × 12). The denominator must be monthly expenses.\n- **B) 9.05 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the equity mutual funds as liquid.\n- **C) 13.33 months** - wrong; this counts both the equity funds and the PPF as liquid.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A client's personal balance sheet shows total assets of Rs 62,00,000 and total liabilities of Rs 23,25,000. What is the solvency ratio?\nA) 0.375  B) 0.625  C) 0.600  D) 1.600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.625**\n\nNet worth = 62,00,000 − 23,25,000 = Rs 38,75,000.\nSolvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 38,75,000 ÷ 62,00,000 = **0.625**.\nSelf-check: solvency + leverage = 0.625 + 0.375 = 1. ✓\n\n- **A) 0.375** - wrong; this is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency.\n- **C) 0.600** - wrong; this is not produced by any of the ratio formulas on these figures.\n- **D) 1.600** - wrong; this inverts the ratio, dividing total assets by net worth.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** A 26-year-old Bengaluru IT professional asks how to build a contingency fund. Which recommendation is correct?\nA) 3–6 months of gross income, held in an ELSS fund  B) 12 months of expenses, held in PPF  C) 3–6 months of expenses, held in a mid-cap equity fund  D) 3–6 months of expenses, held in a savings account, sweep-in FD or liquid fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 3–6 months of expenses, held in a savings account, sweep-in FD or liquid fund**\n\nThe contingency fund is sized against **expenses** (what must actually be paid) at **3–6 months**, and must satisfy two requirements: **immediate availability** and **certainty of value**. Return is secondary.\n\n- **A) Income-based, in an ELSS fund** - wrong on both counts: the fund is sized against expenses, and ELSS carries a three-year lock-in plus equity risk.\n- **B) 12 months in PPF** - wrong; PPF is locked in for 15 years and cannot serve an emergency.\n- **C) Mid-cap equity fund** - wrong; the moment the fund is needed (job loss, downturn) is exactly when equity is likely to be down.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A household's monthly budget balances comfortably every month, yet it collapses each March when the annual motor insurance and health insurance premiums fall due. Which category of outflow was mishandled?\nA) Fixed/committed outflows  B) Periodic/lumpy outflows  C) Variable/discretionary outflows  D) Savings outflows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Periodic/lumpy outflows**\n\n**Periodic outflows** — annual premiums, festival spending, school admission fees, car servicing, vacations — are predictable but infrequent, and are the most commonly forgotten item in a household budget. The fix is to divide each annual amount by 12 and set that sum aside every month.\n\n- **A) Fixed/committed outflows** - wrong; these are the *monthly* contractual items such as EMIs, rent and school fees, which the budget already handles.\n- **C) Variable/discretionary outflows** - wrong; groceries, fuel and dining recur monthly and are controllable, not lumpy.\n- **D) Savings outflows** - wrong; SIPs and recurring deposits are treated as committed monthly outflows, not annual surprises.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Karthik takes a home loan of Rs 32,00,000 at 8.65% p.a. for 18 years. What is his EMI?\nA) Rs 14,815  B) Rs 29,270  C) Rs 23,067  D) Rs 31,794\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 29,270**\n\nr = 0.0865 ÷ 12 = 0.00720833; n = 18 × 12 = 216.\n(1.00720833)²¹⁶ = 4.718142.\nEMI = 32,00,000 × [0.00720833 × 4.718142] ÷ (4.718142 − 1) = 32,00,000 × 0.0340135 ÷ 3.718142 = **Rs 29,270**.\n\n- **A) Rs 14,815** - wrong; this is simply principal ÷ number of months (32,00,000 ÷ 216), ignoring interest.\n- **C) Rs 23,067** - wrong; this is the **first month's interest** (32,00,000 × 0.00720833), a classic near-miss distractor.\n- **D) Rs 31,794** - wrong; this is the EMI for a 15-year tenure, not 18 years.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Meghana's car loan of Rs 9,60,000 carries interest at 11.4% p.a. for 5 years, with an EMI of Rs 21,065. How much of the **first** EMI goes towards repaying principal?\nA) Rs 9,120  B) Rs 16,000  C) Rs 21,065  D) Rs 11,945\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 11,945**\n\nMonthly rate = 0.114 ÷ 12 = 0.0095.\nInterest in month 1 = Outstanding × monthly rate = 9,60,000 × 0.0095 = Rs 9,120.\nPrincipal in month 1 = EMI − interest = 21,065 − 9,120 = **Rs 11,945**.\n\n- **A) Rs 9,120** - wrong; this is the **interest** component, not the principal component.\n- **B) Rs 16,000** - wrong; this is 9,60,000 ÷ 60, the average principal per month, not the first month's figure.\n- **C) Rs 21,065** - wrong; this is the whole EMI, which is split between interest and principal.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Arjun takes a gold loan and, in the same month, a car loan. Which pair correctly describes the security created?\nA) Gold loan — pledge; car loan — hypothecation  B) Gold loan — hypothecation; car loan — pledge  C) Gold loan — mortgage; car loan — pledge  D) Both are hypothecations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Gold loan — pledge; car loan — hypothecation**\n\nA **pledge** is security over **movable** property where **possession passes to the lender** — the lender physically holds the gold. A **hypothecation** is security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** — Arjun keeps driving the car. A **mortgage** is security over **immovable** property, such as a home loan.\n\n- **B)** - wrong; the two are reversed. The lender does not keep the car, and it does hold the gold.\n- **C)** - wrong; a mortgage requires immovable property, and gold is movable.\n- **D)** - wrong; gold physically moves to the lender's custody, which makes it a pledge, not a hypothecation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Which debt-repayment strategy clears the **smallest outstanding balance first**, regardless of the interest rate on each loan?\nA) Avalanche  B) Snowball  C) Blizzard  D) Refinancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Snowball**\n\nThe **snowball** method targets the **smallest balance** first while paying minimums on the rest. It is psychologically powerful because it produces quick wins, but it costs more in total interest than the mathematically optimal alternative.\n\n- **A) Avalanche** - wrong; the avalanche attacks the **highest interest rate** first and minimises total interest paid.\n- **C) Blizzard** - wrong; the blizzard is the hybrid — clear one or two small balances for momentum, then switch to attacking the highest rate.\n- **D) Refinancing** - wrong; refinancing replaces an existing loan with a cheaper one; it is not an ordering strategy across multiple loans.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Pooja's gross monthly income is Rs 1,58,000 and her total monthly household expenses are Rs 1,20,000. Her EMIs are Rs 27,400 (home loan), Rs 9,850 (car loan) and Rs 6,300 (personal loan). What is her debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 23.58%  B) 36.29%  C) 72.44%  D) 27.56%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 27.56%**\n\nTotal EMIs = 27,400 + 9,850 + 6,300 = Rs 43,550.\nDebt-to-income ratio = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 43,550 ÷ 1,58,000 = **27.56%** — comfortably below the 35–40% ceiling.\n\n- **A) 23.58%** - wrong; this omits the personal loan EMI from the numerator.\n- **B) 36.29%** - wrong; this divides the EMIs by monthly *expenses* (Rs 1,20,000) instead of gross income.\n- **C) 72.44%** - wrong; this is the complement (100% − 27.56%), not the ratio itself.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which regulator supervises Housing Finance Companies and Account Aggregators in India?\nA) SEBI  B) IRDAI  C) NHB  D) RBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) RBI**\n\nBoth sit under the **Reserve Bank of India**. HFCs are specialised housing lenders whose supervision moved to the RBI from the National Housing Bank. **Account Aggregators** are RBI-regulated **NBFC-AAs** that share a customer's financial data only with explicit consent, and are **data blind** — they cannot read or store the data they carry.\n\n- **A) SEBI** - wrong; SEBI regulates the securities markets — exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, portfolio managers, investment advisers and AIFs.\n- **B) IRDAI** - wrong; IRDAI regulates insurers and insurance intermediaries.\n- **C) NHB** - wrong; the National Housing Bank no longer supervises HFCs, and it never regulated Account Aggregators.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which of the following is a **money market** instrument regulated by the RBI?\nA) A non-convertible debenture with a 5-year maturity  B) A State Development Loan  C) A 182-day Treasury bill  D) Units of an equity mutual fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) A 182-day Treasury bill**\n\nThe money market covers debt of **up to one year** and is regulated by the **RBI**. Treasury bills are issued in 91-, 182- and 364-day tenors, are zero-coupon, and are sold at a discount to face value.\n\n- **A) A 5-year NCD** - wrong; maturity beyond one year makes it a capital market instrument, and corporate bonds fall under SEBI.\n- **B) A State Development Loan** - wrong; SDLs are long-dated state government bonds, not money market paper.\n- **D) Units of an equity mutual fund** - wrong; these are SEBI-regulated capital market instruments with no maturity.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which market infrastructure institution acts as the **central counterparty** and guarantees that a trade will settle even if a member defaults?\nA) The stock exchange  B) The depository  C) The clearing corporation  D) The depository participant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) The clearing corporation**\n\nThe **clearing corporation** clears and settles trades and interposes itself as the **central counterparty (CCP)** to every trade, so neither buyer nor seller faces the other's default. It manages margins and operates the settlement guarantee fund.\n\n- **A) The stock exchange** - wrong; the exchange **matches** trades and provides price discovery. It does not guarantee settlement.\n- **B) The depository** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL hold securities in electronic (demat) form.\n- **D) The depository participant** - wrong; a DP is the depository's agent and the investor's access point for a demat account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of the following is a payment bank in India permitted to do?\nA) Accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer and provide payments and remittance services  B) Issue credit cards to its depositors  C) Grant term loans to small businesses  D) Accept deposits of any size, but not offer payment services\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer and provide payments and remittance services**\n\nA payment bank's licence permits deposit-taking **capped at Rs 2 lakh per customer** plus payments and remittances. It **cannot lend** and **cannot issue credit cards**.\n\n- **B) Issue credit cards** - wrong; expressly not permitted for a payment bank.\n- **C) Grant term loans** - wrong; lending of any kind is outside a payment bank's licence. Small finance banks, not payment banks, serve small borrowers.\n- **D) Unlimited deposits, no payments** - wrong; this reverses the licence conditions on both limbs.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** What is the primary function of a KYC Registration Agency (KRA)?\nA) Maintaining individual credit histories and scores  B) Holding securities in dematerialised form  C) Guaranteeing the settlement of trades  D) Maintaining centralised KYC records so that a client's KYC is portable across SEBI intermediaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Maintaining centralised KYC records so that a client's KYC is portable across SEBI intermediaries**\n\nA **KRA** holds KYC data centrally so an investor who has completed KYC once with any SEBI-registered intermediary need not repeat the process at the next one.\n\n- **A) Credit histories and scores** - wrong; that is the role of Credit Information Companies such as CIBIL, Experian, Equifax and CRIF High Mark, regulated by the RBI.\n- **B) Holding securities in demat form** - wrong; that is a depository (NSDL, CDSL).\n- **C) Guaranteeing settlement** - wrong; that is the clearing corporation acting as central counterparty.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** An investor holds 500 shares of a company quoted at Rs 920 each. The company announces a bonus issue in the ratio 3:5. What is the theoretical price per share immediately after the bonus?\nA) Rs 368  B) Rs 460  C) Rs 552  D) Rs 575\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 575**\n\nA 3:5 bonus gives 3 free shares for every 5 held: 500 × 3/5 = 300 new shares, taking the holding to **800 shares**.\nValue before = 500 × 920 = Rs 4,60,000, and a bonus raises no money, so total value is unchanged.\nTheoretical price = 4,60,000 ÷ 800 = **Rs 575**.\n\n- **A) Rs 368** - wrong; this applies the ratio the wrong way round (920 × 2/5).\n- **B) Rs 460** - wrong; this assumes a 1:1 bonus, halving the price.\n- **C) Rs 552** - wrong; this reduces the price by 40% rather than dividing the unchanged value by the new share count.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Which statement about rights issues and bonus issues is correct?\nA) A rights issue raises fresh capital for the company; a bonus issue does not  B) A bonus issue raises fresh capital; a rights issue does not  C) Both raise fresh capital for the company  D) Neither raises capital; both merely change the share count\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A rights issue raises fresh capital for the company; a bonus issue does not**\n\nA **rights issue** is an offer to existing shareholders, pro rata, to **buy** more shares at a discount — money flows into the company, and the entitlement is renounceable. A **bonus issue** is a capitalisation of reserves that hands out **free** shares — no money is raised, and the shareholder's total value and percentage holding are unchanged.\n\n- **B)** - wrong; the two are reversed.\n- **C)** - wrong; a bonus issue involves no subscription money at all.\n- **D)** - wrong; a rights issue does raise capital.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** In a book-built IPO, an individual investor applies for shares worth Rs 3,20,000. Into which category does this application fall?\nA) Retail Individual Investor  B) Qualified Institutional Buyer  C) Non-Institutional Investor  D) Anchor investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Non-Institutional Investor**\n\nThe dividing line is **Rs 2 lakh**: applications **up to Rs 2 lakh** are Retail Individual Investors, and applications **above Rs 2 lakh** are Non-Institutional Investors (HNIs). At Rs 3,20,000 the application is an NII, a category entitled to at least 15% of a book-built issue.\n\n- **A) Retail Individual Investor** - wrong; RII status ends at Rs 2 lakh.\n- **B) Qualified Institutional Buyer** - wrong; QIBs are institutions — mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs and pension funds — not individuals.\n- **D) Anchor investor** - wrong; anchors are QIBs allotted shares a day before the issue opens, carved out of the QIB portion.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** In a book-built issue, the cap of the price band must be within what percentage of the floor price?\nA) 105%  B) 110%  C) 120%  D) 150%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 120%**\n\nSEBI requires the **cap to be within 120% of the floor price**, so the band cannot be so wide that price discovery becomes meaningless. Retail investors may bid at \"cut-off\", accepting whatever final price emerges, and the cut-off price is the price at which the issue is fully subscribed.\n\n- **A) 105%** - wrong; this is narrower than the rule permits and is not a SEBI threshold.\n- **B) 110%** - wrong; also narrower than the prescribed limit.\n- **D) 150%** - wrong; a band this wide is not permitted, as it would leave the indicative price almost meaningless.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** An investor holds 240 shares of face value Rs 10 quoted at Rs 1,850. The company carries out a 1:5 stock split. What does the investor hold immediately after the split?\nA) 1,200 shares at Rs 370  B) 1,200 shares at Rs 1,850  C) 480 shares at Rs 925  D) 240 shares at Rs 370\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1,200 shares at Rs 370**\n\nA 1:5 split reduces the face value from Rs 10 to Rs 2, so each share becomes five: 240 × 5 = **1,200 shares**. The market price adjusts proportionately: 1,850 ÷ 5 = **Rs 370**. Total value = 1,200 × 370 = Rs 4,44,000, exactly the pre-split value of 240 × 1,850.\n\n- **B) 1,200 shares at Rs 1,850** - wrong; the share count rises but the price must fall proportionately. A split creates no value.\n- **C) 480 shares at Rs 925** - wrong; this applies a 1:2 split rather than 1:5.\n- **D) 240 shares at Rs 370** - wrong; this cuts the price without increasing the share count, destroying value that a split does not destroy.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** What are the minimum investment amounts for a SEBI-registered portfolio manager (PMS) and an Alternative Investment Fund respectively?\nA) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 10 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore  D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore**\n\n**PMS: Rs 50 lakh. AIF: Rs 1 crore** (reduced to Rs 25 lakh for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager). Mutual funds sit at the bottom of the ladder at Rs 100–500. The logic of the ladder is that as the minimum investment rises, regulation and disclosure fall and strategy freedom rises.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the concessional AIF minimum for AIF employees and directors, not the PMS minimum.\n- **B) Rs 10 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; both figures are below the prescribed minimums.\n- **D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the AIF minimum, and there is no Rs 5 crore threshold.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A client is offered a \"100% capital protected\" structured product. What is the most accurate caution the adviser should give?\nA) The protection is guaranteed by SEBI  B) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and generally applies only at maturity  C) The protection means the value can never fall below the purchase price at any time  D) The protection is provided by the clearing corporation of the exchange\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and generally applies only at maturity**\n\nA structured product is a **bond (or deposit) plus a derivative**. The \"protection\" comes from the bond leg growing back to par, so it is only as good as the **issuer's credit**, and it typically holds **only if held to maturity**. Structured products are also complex, illiquid and often opaque on costs.\n\n- **A) Guaranteed by SEBI** - wrong; no regulator guarantees the capital of any market-linked product.\n- **C) Never falls below purchase price** - wrong; these products are generally **not** principal-protected before maturity and can be marked well below cost in between.\n- **D) Clearing corporation guarantee** - wrong; the CCP guarantees settlement of exchange-traded trades, not the redemption value of an issuer's instrument.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** What is the dividing line between investment-grade and high-yield (\"junk\") debt?\nA) A / A2  B) AA− / Aa3  C) BBB− / Baa3  D) BB+ / Ba1\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) BBB− / Baa3**\n\n**BBB− (or Baa3) and above is investment grade**; anything below it is high yield. The line matters because many institutional mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings, so a downgrade from BBB− to BB+ can force mechanical selling.\n\n- **A) A / A2** - wrong; this sits several notches inside investment grade, well above the boundary.\n- **B) AA− / Aa3** - wrong; this is high investment grade, further still from the cut-off.\n- **D) BB+ / Ba1** - wrong; BB+ is the **first rung below** the boundary, i.e. already high yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A company reported profit after tax of Rs 84 crore and has 3.5 crore shares outstanding. Its share trades at Rs 486. What is its P/E ratio?\nA) 20.25  B) 4.94  C) 5.79  D) 24.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 20.25**\n\nEPS = PAT ÷ number of shares = 84,00,00,000 ÷ 3,50,00,000 = **Rs 24.00**.\nP/E = Price ÷ EPS = 486 ÷ 24 = **20.25**.\n\n- **B) 4.94** - wrong; this is the **earnings yield** in per cent (24 ÷ 486), the inverse of P/E.\n- **C) 5.79** - wrong; this divides the price by the PAT expressed in crore, mixing incompatible units.\n- **D) 24.00** - wrong; this is the **EPS** in rupees, not the P/E multiple.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A listed company has a market capitalisation of Rs 6,240 crore, total debt of Rs 1,180 crore and cash of Rs 395 crore. What is its enterprise value?\nA) Rs 5,455 crore  B) Rs 6,240 crore  C) Rs 7,815 crore  D) Rs 7,025 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 7,025 crore**\n\nEV = Market capitalisation + Debt − Cash = 6,240 + 1,180 − 395 = **Rs 7,025 crore**. EV represents what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts, net of the cash that comes with it.\n\n- **A) Rs 5,455 crore** - wrong; this reverses both signs (subtracting debt and adding cash).\n- **B) Rs 6,240 crore** - wrong; this is market capitalisation alone, which ignores the capital structure.\n- **C) Rs 7,815 crore** - wrong; this **adds** cash instead of subtracting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A stock trades at a P/E of 28.8 and its earnings are expected to grow at 16% a year. What is its PEG ratio?\nA) 0.56  B) 1.80  C) 4.61  D) 12.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1.80**\n\nPEG = P/E ÷ earnings growth rate (in per cent) = 28.8 ÷ 16 = **1.80**. A PEG above 1 suggests the price is not obviously cheap relative to the growth on offer; PEG below 1 is the conventional value signal.\n\n- **A) 0.56** - wrong; this inverts the ratio (16 ÷ 28.8).\n- **C) 4.61** - wrong; this multiplies the P/E by 0.16 instead of dividing by 16.\n- **D) 12.80** - wrong; this subtracts the growth rate from the P/E, which is not the PEG formula.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An investor holds 30 stocks spread across 10 different sectors. Which risk does this portfolio structure fail to reduce?\nA) Company-specific risk  B) Sector-specific risk  C) Market (systematic) risk  D) The liquidity risk of any single small-cap holding\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Market (systematic) risk**\n\nDiversification eliminates **unsystematic** risk — company- and sector-specific — but **cannot remove systematic (market) risk**. When the whole market falls on a rate shock or recession, breadth does not help. Systematic risk is the risk investors are compensated for bearing, which is precisely why it cannot be diversified away.\n\n- **A) Company-specific risk** - wrong; holding 30 names dilutes the impact of any single firm's failure.\n- **B) Sector-specific risk** - wrong; spreading across 10 sectors is exactly the remedy for sector concentration.\n- **D) Liquidity risk of a single small-cap holding** - wrong; this too is reduced, because that name is now a small fraction of the portfolio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 pays an annual coupon of 7%, has 5 years to maturity, and the required yield is 8.5%. What is its price?\nA) Rs 665.05  B) Rs 940.89  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,061.50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 940.89**\n\nCoupon = Rs 70 a year. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.085)⁻⁵] ÷ 0.085 = 3.940642.\nPV of coupons = 70 × 3.940642 = Rs 275.84.\nPV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.085)⁵ = 1,000 ÷ 1.503657 = Rs 665.05.\nPrice = 275.84 + 665.05 = **Rs 940.89** — a discount, because the 7% coupon is below the 8.5% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 665.05** - wrong; this is only the present value of the redemption amount, omitting all five coupons.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would price at par only if the coupon equalled the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,061.50** - wrong; this swaps the coupon and yield (an 8.5% coupon priced at a 7% yield), giving a premium instead of a discount.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 and an annual coupon of 8.6% is trading at Rs 942. What is its current yield?\nA) 6.16%  B) 8.60%  C) 9.13%  D) 10.05%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 9.13%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ **Market price** = 86 ÷ 942 = **9.13%**.\nBecause the bond trades at a discount, the ordering coupon (8.60%) < current yield (9.13%) < YTM holds.\n\n- **A) 6.16%** - wrong; this is the discount to face expressed as a percentage of price (58 ÷ 942), a capital-gain measure, not the current yield.\n- **B) 8.60%** - wrong; this is the **coupon rate**, which is calculated on face value and ignores the price actually paid.\n- **D) 10.05%** - wrong; this is the approximate **YTM** assuming 5 years to maturity, which also counts the capital gain at redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A bond portfolio worth Rs 16,00,000 has a modified duration of 5.8. Yields rise by 60 basis points. What is the approximate change in the portfolio's value?\nA) A fall of about Rs 55,680  B) A fall of about Rs 9,600  C) A rise of about Rs 55,680  D) A fall of about Rs 5,56,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A fall of about Rs 55,680**\n\n% change in price ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −5.8 × 0.0060 = **−3.48%**.\nChange in value = 16,00,000 × 0.0348 = **Rs 55,680 fall**. Prices move inversely to yields, so a rise in yields produces a loss.\n\n- **B) A fall of about Rs 9,600** - wrong; this applies the 60 bp yield change to the portfolio value without multiplying by duration.\n- **C) A rise of about Rs 55,680** - wrong; the magnitude is right but the direction is not — rising yields cut bond prices.\n- **D) A fall of about Rs 5,56,800** - wrong; this is a decimal-place error, ten times the correct figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which small savings scheme has a 15-year tenor, an annual contribution ceiling of Rs 1.5 lakh, and EEE tax treatment?\nA) National Savings Certificate  B) Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme  C) Kisan Vikas Patra  D) Public Provident Fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Public Provident Fund**\n\n**PPF** runs for **15 years** (extendable in 5-year blocks), takes a minimum of Rs 500 and a **maximum of Rs 1.5 lakh a year**, and is **EEE** — exempt on contribution, on accrual and on withdrawal.\n\n- **A) National Savings Certificate** - wrong; NSC has a 5-year tenor and its interest is taxable, though it compounds annually and is paid at maturity.\n- **B) Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme** - wrong; SCSS runs 5 years (extendable by 3), is for those aged 60+, is capped at Rs 30 lakh and pays taxable interest quarterly.\n- **C) Kisan Vikas Patra** - wrong; KVP runs until the amount doubles, has no maximum, carries **no 80C benefit**, and its interest is taxable.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** An investor buys a call option with a strike price of Rs 2,400 for a premium of Rs 68. At expiry the underlying settles at Rs 2,515. What is the profit per unit?\nA) Rs 47  B) Rs 68  C) Rs 115  D) Rs 183\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 47**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(Spot − Strike, 0) = max(2,515 − 2,400, 0) = Rs 115.\nProfit = Intrinsic value − Premium = 115 − 68 = **Rs 47** per unit.\nBreak-even was Strike + Premium = 2,400 + 68 = Rs 2,468, and the spot finished Rs 47 above it.\n\n- **B) Rs 68** - wrong; this is the premium paid, which is the buyer's **maximum loss**, not the profit here.\n- **C) Rs 115** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value before recovering the premium.\n- **D) Rs 183** - wrong; this **adds** the premium to the intrinsic value instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** An investor buys a put option with a strike price of Rs 1,750 for a premium of Rs 52. At expiry the underlying settles at Rs 1,663. What is the profit per unit?\nA) Rs 87  B) Rs 52  C) Rs 35  D) Rs 139\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 35**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(Strike − Spot, 0) = max(1,750 − 1,663, 0) = Rs 87.\nProfit = 87 − 52 = **Rs 35** per unit.\nBreak-even for a long put is Strike − Premium = 1,750 − 52 = Rs 1,698, and the spot finished Rs 35 below it.\n\n- **A) Rs 87** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value, before recovering the premium paid.\n- **B) Rs 52** - wrong; this is the premium, the buyer's maximum possible loss.\n- **D) Rs 139** - wrong; this adds the premium to the intrinsic value instead of subtracting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An index stands at 2,450. The risk-free rate is 7.25% p.a. and the dividend yield on the index is 1.75% p.a. What is the fair value of a futures contract expiring in one year?\nA) Rs 2,315.25  B) Rs 2,450.00  C) Rs 2,584.75  D) Rs 2,670.50\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,584.75**\n\nF = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 2,450 × (1 + 0.0725 − 0.0175)¹ = 2,450 × 1.055 = **Rs 2,584.75**.\nThe futures price exceeds spot — the normal state, called **contango** — because the cost of financing the position exceeds the dividend income given up.\n\n- **A) Rs 2,315.25** - wrong; this subtracts the interest cost and adds the dividend yield, reversing both signs.\n- **B) Rs 2,450.00** - wrong; futures equal spot only **at expiry**, when the two converge.\n- **D) Rs 2,670.50** - wrong; this **adds** the dividend yield to the risk-free rate instead of deducting it. Dividends received reduce the net cost of carry.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which feature is true of an exchange-traded futures contract but **not** of an OTC forward contract?\nA) It is a customised bilateral agreement between two parties  B) It is marked to market daily and its performance is guaranteed by a clearing corporation  C) It is settled only at maturity  D) Its contract size and expiry date can be negotiated between the two parties\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It is marked to market daily and its performance is guaranteed by a clearing corporation**\n\nThe two defining differences are that futures are **standardised and exchange-traded with daily mark to market**, and that the **clearing corporation acts as central counterparty**, eliminating counterparty risk. Both are absent in a forward.\n\n- **A) Customised bilateral agreement** - wrong; that describes a forward. Futures are standardised in lot size and expiry.\n- **C) Settled only at maturity** - wrong; that is the forward. Futures settle gains and losses **daily** through MTM margins.\n- **D) Negotiated size and expiry** - wrong; negotiability of terms is the hallmark of an OTC forward, not of a listed future.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A mutual fund scheme holds securities valued at Rs 738 crore and cash of Rs 26 crore, and owes Rs 14 crore in accrued expenses. It has 25 crore units outstanding. What is the NAV per unit?\nA) Rs 27.92  B) Rs 28.96  C) Rs 30.00  D) Rs 30.56\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 30.00**\n\nNAV = (Total assets - Total liabilities) / Units outstanding. Total assets = 738 + 26 = Rs 764 crore. Net assets = 764 - 14 = **Rs 750 crore**. NAV = 750 / 25 = **Rs 30.00**.\n\n- **A) Rs 27.92** - wrong; this subtracts the cash instead of adding it: (738 - 26 - 14) / 25.\n- **B) Rs 28.96** - wrong; this ignores the cash balance altogether: (738 - 14) / 25.\n- **D) Rs 30.56** - wrong; this ignores the liabilities: (738 + 26) / 25.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Tejaswini, a Bengaluru software engineer, submits an application to purchase units of an equity flexi-cap fund at 2:15 p.m. on Tuesday. Her bank transfer is credited to the scheme's account only at 10:00 a.m. on Wednesday. Which NAV will she receive?\nA) Monday's NAV  B) Tuesday's NAV  C) Wednesday's NAV  D) Thursday's NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Wednesday's NAV**\n\nFor purchases of **any amount**, the applicable NAV depends on the **funds being realised (credited to the scheme) before the cut-off**, not on when the application was submitted. The money reached the scheme at 10:00 a.m. on Wednesday, which is before the 3:00 p.m. cut-off for non-liquid schemes, so **Wednesday's NAV** applies.\n\n- **A) Monday's NAV** - wrong; previous-day NAV is a feature of liquid and overnight fund purchases, not equity schemes.\n- **B) Tuesday's NAV** - wrong; the application was time-stamped on Tuesday but the funds were not realised that day, and realisation is the decisive test.\n- **D) Thursday's NAV** - wrong; there is no one-day lag once funds are realised before the cut-off.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** A hybrid scheme's disclosed asset allocation is 72% in equity and equity-related instruments and 28% in debt. Under SEBI's scheme categorisation, this scheme is a:\nA) Conservative hybrid fund  B) Balanced hybrid fund  C) Aggressive hybrid fund  D) Multi asset allocation fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Aggressive hybrid fund**\n\nAn **aggressive hybrid fund** must hold **65-80% in equity** and **20-35% in debt**. A 72:28 split falls squarely inside both bands.\n\n- **A) Conservative hybrid fund** - wrong; that category holds only 10-25% equity and 75-90% debt.\n- **B) Balanced hybrid fund** - wrong; that category holds 40-60% equity and does not permit arbitrage.\n- **D) Multi asset allocation fund** - wrong; that category must invest in at least three asset classes with a minimum of 10% in each, and only two asset classes are present here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which statement correctly describes the difference between the direct plan and the regular plan of the same mutual fund scheme?\nA) The direct plan holds a more conservative portfolio than the regular plan  B) The direct plan has a lower total expense ratio and therefore a higher NAV over time  C) The direct plan pays the distributor a reduced commission out of the scheme's assets  D) The direct plan carries a higher exit load to discourage short-term churn\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The direct plan has a lower total expense ratio and therefore a higher NAV over time**\n\nThe two plans hold an **identical portfolio** and are managed by the same manager. The only difference is that the regular plan's TER embeds distribution commission. The direct plan's **lower TER** compounds into a **higher NAV**, which is why a fee-charging Investment Adviser recommends direct plans.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the portfolio is identical in both plans.\n- **C)** - wrong; the direct plan pays **no** distribution commission at all.\n- **D)** - wrong; exit load structures are the same for both plans of a scheme.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Nikhil invests Rs 12,000 through a SIP on the same date each month for four months. The purchase NAVs are Rs 60, Rs 50, Rs 40 and Rs 48. What is his average cost per unit?\nA) Rs 48.48  B) Rs 49.50  C) Rs 50.00  D) Rs 51.02\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 48.48**\n\nUnits bought = 12,000/60 + 12,000/50 + 12,000/40 + 12,000/48 = 200 + 240 + 300 + 250 = **990 units**. Total invested = 4 x 12,000 = **Rs 48,000**. Average cost = 48,000 / 990 = **Rs 48.48**. Note that this is below the simple average NAV of Rs 49.50 - that gap is the benefit of rupee-cost averaging.\n\n- **B) Rs 49.50** - wrong; this is the **simple average of the four NAVs**, (60+50+40+48)/4, not the average cost.\n- **C) Rs 50.00** - wrong; this is the median-looking NAV and has no basis in the calculation.\n- **D) Rs 51.02** - wrong; this inverts the calculation by weighting the higher NAVs more heavily.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Sneha redeems 1,800 units of an equity fund at an NAV of Rs 44.50 within the exit-load period. The scheme charges an exit load of 1%. What amount will she receive?\nA) Rs 78,498  B) Rs 79,299  C) Rs 80,100  D) Rs 80,901\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 79,299**\n\nGross redemption value = 1,800 x 44.50 = **Rs 80,100**. Exit load = 1% x 80,100 = **Rs 801**. Net proceeds = 80,100 - 801 = **Rs 79,299**.\n\n- **A) Rs 78,498** - wrong; this applies a 2% load instead of 1%.\n- **C) Rs 80,100** - wrong; this is the gross value before deducting the exit load.\n- **D) Rs 80,901** - wrong; this adds the load to the gross value instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A discretionary PMS portfolio starts at Rs 2.00 crore. It ends Year 1 at Rs 2.60 crore, Year 2 at Rs 2.35 crore and Year 3 at Rs 2.84 crore. The performance fee is 15% on a high watermark basis. What performance fee is payable for Year 3?\nA) Rs 3,60,000  B) Rs 7,35,000  C) Rs 12,60,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 3,60,000**\n\nAfter Year 1 the **high watermark is Rs 2.60 crore**. Year 2 ends below it, so no fee is charged and the watermark stays at Rs 2.60 crore. In Year 3 the fee applies only to the gain **above the watermark**: 2.84 - 2.60 = **Rs 24 lakh**. Fee = 15% x 24,00,000 = **Rs 3,60,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 7,35,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 49 lakh rise from the Year 2 value, making the client pay again for recovering ground already lost.\n- **C) Rs 12,60,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the entire Rs 84 lakh gain since inception, ignoring the fee already taken in Year 1.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has exceeded its previous peak, so a fee is due on the excess.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Karthik's PMS portfolio of Rs 1.50 crore returns 17% for the year. The hurdle rate is 8% and the performance fee is 20% with no catch-up. What is the performance fee?\nA) Rs 1,80,000  B) Rs 2,40,000  C) Rs 2,70,000  D) Rs 5,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,70,000**\n\nTotal return = 17% x 1,50,00,000 = **Rs 25.5 lakh**. The hurdle absorbs the first 8% x 1,50,00,000 = **Rs 12 lakh**. Excess over the hurdle = 25.5 - 12 = **Rs 13.5 lakh**. Fee = 20% x 13,50,000 = **Rs 2,70,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,80,000** - wrong; this applies the 9-percentage-point excess to a Rs 1 crore base instead of Rs 1.5 crore.\n- **B) Rs 2,40,000** - wrong; this charges the fee on the hurdle amount itself rather than on the excess above it.\n- **D) Rs 5,10,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the entire return, ignoring the hurdle altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Under which type of portfolio management service does the **client** take every investment decision while the portfolio manager merely executes it?\nA) Discretionary  B) Non-discretionary  C) Advisory  D) Custodial\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Non-discretionary**\n\nThe clean test is *who pulls the trigger*. In **non-discretionary** PMS the **client decides** each transaction and the **manager executes** it.\n\n- **A) Discretionary** - wrong; there the manager both decides and executes, with full authority delegated.\n- **C) Advisory** - wrong; there the manager only recommends and the **client both decides and executes**.\n- **D) Custodial** - wrong; a custodian holds securities in safekeeping and is not a type of portfolio management service.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Sandeep, 31, has Rs 38,00,000 of investible surplus and wants to enter a discretionary PMS. What should his adviser tell him?\nA) He may invest; there is no prescribed minimum for PMS  B) He may invest only in advisory PMS, for which no minimum applies  C) He is not eligible; the minimum investment in PMS is Rs 50 lakh  D) He is not eligible; the minimum investment in PMS is Rs 1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) He is not eligible; the minimum investment in PMS is Rs 50 lakh**\n\nUnder the **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**, the **minimum investment per client is Rs 50 lakh**, and it applies to every service type. With Rs 38 lakh Sandeep falls short and should build the corpus through mutual funds first.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; a minimum is expressly prescribed.\n- **B)** - wrong; the Rs 50 lakh floor applies to advisory PMS as well.\n- **D)** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the minimum investment in an **AIF**, not in PMS.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of Rs 240 crore. What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager maintain in the fund?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 12 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 10 crore**\n\nFor a **Category III** AIF the continuing interest is the **lower of 5% of corpus or Rs 10 crore**. Here 5% x 240 = Rs 12 crore, which exceeds the Rs 10 crore ceiling, so the sponsor commits **Rs 10 crore**.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; that is the ceiling for **Category I and II** funds, not Category III.\n- **B) Rs 6 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category I/II rate of 2.5% to the corpus.\n- **D) Rs 12 crore** - wrong; this ignores the word \"lower of\" and takes 5% of the corpus without applying the cap.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Meghana invests in a Category III AIF that runs a leveraged long-short strategy. How is the fund's income taxed?\nA) Passed through and taxed in her hands at her own applicable rate  B) Taxed at the fund level before any distribution is made  C) Exempt both in the fund's hands and in her hands  D) Taxed only in the year the fund is wound up\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Taxed at the fund level before any distribution is made**\n\n**Pass-through status applies to Categories I and II only.** A **Category III** AIF is **taxed at the fund level**, generally at the maximum marginal rate, so every investor effectively bears the same rate regardless of personal slab. This makes Category III relatively less tax-efficient for a low-slab investor - an important suitability point.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; that is precisely the treatment Category III does **not** get.\n- **C)** - wrong; the income is fully taxable, only the level at which it is taxed differs.\n- **D)** - wrong; tax arises as income accrues, not on winding up.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** What is the minimum corpus required for a scheme of an **angel fund**?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 10 crore  C) Rs 20 crore  D) Rs 25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 10 crore**\n\nAn angel fund is a sub-category of venture capital fund (Category I) and enjoys relaxed thresholds: **minimum corpus Rs 10 crore**, a maximum of **49 investors** per scheme, and a minimum of **Rs 25 lakh** per angel investor drawn down over up to five years.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; no AIF scheme has a Rs 5 crore corpus floor.\n- **C) Rs 20 crore** - wrong; Rs 20 crore is the minimum corpus for **all other** AIF schemes.\n- **D) Rs 25 crore** - wrong; Rs 25 crore is the turnover ceiling for an angel fund's investee company, not a corpus figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A portfolio holds 70% in an equity fund with a standard deviation of 22% and 30% in a debt fund with a standard deviation of 6%. The correlation between them is 0.25. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?\nA) 14.00%  B) 15.50%  C) 15.95%  D) 17.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 15.95%**\n\nUsing sigma_p^2 = w1^2.sigma1^2 + w2^2.sigma2^2 + 2.w1.w2.sigma1.sigma2.rho:\n0.49 x 484 = 237.16; 0.09 x 36 = 3.24; 2 x 0.7 x 0.3 x 22 x 6 x 0.25 = 13.86.\nTotal variance = **254.26**, so sigma_p = sqrt(254.26) = **15.95%**. It sits below the 17.20% weighted average - that shortfall is the diversification benefit.\n\n- **A) 14.00%** - wrong; this is the simple average of the two standard deviations, which ignores the weights entirely.\n- **B) 15.50%** - wrong; this assumes zero correlation and omits the 13.86 interaction term.\n- **D) 17.20%** - wrong; this is the weighted average of the standard deviations, which the portfolio would equal only if the correlation were +1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which statement about correlation in a two-security portfolio is correct?\nA) A lower correlation raises both the portfolio's expected return and its risk  B) Correlation affects the portfolio's risk but has no effect on its expected return  C) Correlation affects the portfolio's expected return but not its risk  D) At a correlation of +1 the portfolio's risk falls below the weighted average\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Correlation affects the portfolio's risk but has no effect on its expected return**\n\nPortfolio return is **always** the plain weighted average of the component returns; correlation appears nowhere in that formula. Correlation appears only in the **third (interaction) term** of the variance formula, which is the only term that can shrink total risk.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; a lower correlation reduces risk and leaves return unchanged.\n- **C)** - wrong; this reverses the true position.\n- **D)** - wrong; at rho = +1 the portfolio risk **equals** the weighted average exactly, giving no diversification benefit at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Nikhil's Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic allocation of 70% equity and 30% debt, with a permitted band of plus or minus 10 percentage points. Believing equity valuations are stretched, his adviser moves the portfolio to 62% equity and 38% debt. This is best described as:\nA) A revision of the strategic asset allocation  B) Tactical asset allocation  C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement  D) Constant-mix rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Tactical asset allocation**\n\nThe trigger was a **market view**, not a change in the client's circumstances, and the new weights remain **inside the IPS band**. Short-term deviations within the permitted bands, driven by market views, are the definition of tactical asset allocation.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the strategic allocation changes only when the **client's** goals, horizon or constraints change.\n- **C)** - wrong; 62% is well within the 60-80% band, so nothing has been breached.\n- **D)** - wrong; constant-mix rebalancing restores the portfolio **to** the target weights; here it is being deliberately moved away from them.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Deepthi, 29, an IT professional in Bengaluru, has a stable income, no dependants and a 25-year horizon - giving her a high **ability** to take risk. Her risk questionnaire shows she is deeply uncomfortable with any capital loss. How should the adviser set the equity allocation?\nA) By her ability, because ability is objective and measurable  B) By the average of her ability and her willingness  C) By her willingness, because the lower of ability and willingness governs  D) By her ability, after obtaining a written waiver of the risk profile\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) By her willingness, because the lower of ability and willingness governs**\n\nAbility is the **objective** financial capacity to absorb loss; willingness is the **subjective** attitude to risk. Where the two conflict, **the lower of the two governs**. The adviser may educate her over time to raise her willingness, but must not override it.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; advising to her ability alone ignores the risk that she will abandon the plan in the first drawdown.\n- **B)** - wrong; there is no averaging rule; the conservative figure applies.\n- **D)** - wrong; a waiver does not cure an unsuitable recommendation under Regulation 17.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A diversified equity fund returned 15.40% over the year. The risk-free rate was 6.50%, the benchmark returned 12.00% and the fund's beta was 1.15. What is the fund's Jensen's alpha?\nA) -4.90%  B) 2.58%  C) 3.40%  D) 8.90%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 2.58%**\n\nCAPM required return = Rf + beta x (Rm - Rf) = 6.50 + 1.15 x (12.00 - 6.50) = 6.50 + 6.325 = **12.825%**. Jensen's alpha = 15.40 - 12.825 = **2.575%, i.e. 2.58%**.\n\n- **A) -4.90%** - wrong; this multiplies beta by the market return instead of the market **risk premium**.\n- **C) 3.40%** - wrong; this is simply Rp - Rm, which ignores the beta risk taken and is the commonest error in alpha questions.\n- **D) 8.90%** - wrong; this is Rp - Rf, the raw excess return, with no adjustment for market risk at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Karthik wishes to pay for a mutual fund purchase in his wife Pallavi's folio - she is the sole holder - from his own salary account. What is the correct position?\nA) Permitted, because payments between spouses are an accepted exception  B) Permitted up to Rs 50,000 per transaction with a signed declaration  C) Not permitted; it is a third-party payment and spouse is not an exception  D) Permitted provided Karthik's KYC is completed with the same AMC\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Not permitted; it is a third-party payment and spouse is not an exception**\n\nSubscription money must come from the **first or sole holder's own bank account**. The only recognised exceptions are payment by a parent/grandparent/related person **for a minor** (capped at Rs 50,000 per transaction), an **employer for an employee** through payroll deduction, a **custodian for an FPI or client**, and commission paid **in units** to an empanelled distributor. A spouse is not on that list.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; there is no spousal exception under the PMLA framework.\n- **B)** - wrong; the Rs 50,000 ceiling attaches only to the **minor** exception, and Pallavi is not a minor.\n- **D)** - wrong; the payer's KYC does not convert a third-party payment into a permitted one.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Vidya's daughter, the sole holder of a minor's folio, turns 18 today. What happens to the folio on that date?\nA) Nothing changes until the guardian submits a request  B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all SIP, SWP and STP instructions cease  C) The units are automatically transferred to a fresh folio in the major's name  D) The guardian may continue to operate the folio for a further 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all SIP, SWP and STP instructions cease**\n\nOn the date of majority the folio is **frozen** and every standing instruction stops. To unfreeze it, the now-major must submit the **Minor Attaining Majority form** with a banker-attested signature, PAN and completed KYC, a bank account in her own name and a fresh nomination. The **guardian's authority ends completely** on that date.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the freeze is automatic and does not wait for a request.\n- **C)** - wrong; no automatic transfer happens; the same folio is regularised.\n- **D)** - wrong; there is no grace period; a redemption signed by the erstwhile guardian afterwards is invalid.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A mutual fund folio is held by three holders with the mode of holding recorded as \"Anyone or Survivor\". Who must sign a request to change the nomination?\nA) Any one of the three holders  B) The first holder alone  C) All three holders  D) The first holder and any one other holder\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) All three holders**\n\nOrdinary transactions in an \"Anyone or Survivor\" folio may be signed by any one holder, but **nomination is an exception: ALL joint holders must sign it**, whatever the mode of holding. This is because nomination determines who receives the asset on death and cannot be altered unilaterally.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; this is the rule for transactions, not for nomination.\n- **B)** - wrong; the first holder is the tax owner and payout account holder, but has no unilateral power to nominate.\n- **D)** - wrong; a partial set of signatures is insufficient; the requirement is unanimous.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following is **NOT** an Officially Valid Document (OVD) under the PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005?\nA) Passport  B) PAN card  C) Voter's Identity Card  D) NREGA job card\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) PAN card**\n\nThe six OVDs are the **passport, driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar, Voter's Identity Card, NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer, and the NPR letter**. **PAN is not an OVD** - it is a **separate and additional** mandatory requirement under Section 139A of the Income Tax Act.\n\n- **A) Passport** - wrong choice; it is the first listed OVD.\n- **C) Voter's Identity Card** - wrong choice; issued by the Election Commission, it is an OVD.\n- **D) NREGA job card** - wrong choice; duly signed by a State Government officer, it is an OVD.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which statement about an **NRE** account is correct?\nA) It holds Indian-source income and the interest earned is taxable in India  B) It is a term deposit maintained in foreign currency for one to five years  C) It is rupee-denominated, fully repatriable, and the interest is exempt from Indian tax  D) Repatriation of the principal is capped at USD 1 million per financial year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) It is rupee-denominated, fully repatriable, and the interest is exempt from Indian tax**\n\nAn **NRE** account holds **foreign earnings remitted to India**, is maintained in **rupees**, is **fully repatriable** for both principal and interest, and the interest is **exempt** from Indian income tax. Its drawback is exchange-rate risk, since the balance is in rupees.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; that describes an **NRO** account.\n- **B)** - wrong; that describes an **FCNR(B)** deposit.\n- **D)** - wrong; the USD 1 million per financial year ceiling applies to repatriation from an **NRO** account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** A physical share certificate stands in the names \"Ananya and Harish\", while the demat account is in the names \"Harish and Ananya\". What must be done before the shares can be dematerialised?\nA) Transmission  B) Transposition  C) Transfer  D) Rematerialisation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Transposition**\n\nDematerialisation requires the **names and their order** to match exactly. **Transposition** changes the **order of the same names** and is not treated as a transfer; a Transposition Request Form is filed and the demat request follows in the same step.\n\n- **A) Transmission** - wrong; transmission passes an asset on the **death** of a holder.\n- **C) Transfer** - wrong; transfer changes the names because someone sold or gifted, and physical transfer of listed shares has been barred since 1 April 2019.\n- **D) Rematerialisation** - wrong; that converts electronic holdings back into certificates, which is the opposite of what is required.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** An investor registers a new bank account in her folio and submits a redemption request four days later. Where will the redemption proceeds be credited?\nA) The newly registered bank account  B) The old, already-registered bank account  C) Split equally between the two accounts  D) Held by the AMC until the investor confirms by email\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The old, already-registered bank account**\n\nWhere a redemption request arrives within the **cooling-off window (commonly 10 calendar days)** of a change-of-bank-mandate request, the proceeds go to the **old, already-registered** account. It is a deliberate anti-fraud control that defeats the classic scam of changing the bank details and immediately redeeming.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the new account becomes the payout account only after the cooling-off window ends.\n- **C)** - wrong; proceeds are never split; they go to a single registered account of the first holder.\n- **D)** - wrong; the redemption is processed normally, only the destination account is fixed by the rule.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Rahul's family has assets under advice of Rs 62,00,000 and his SEBI-registered Investment Adviser has opted for the AUA fee mode. What is the maximum advisory fee chargeable for the year?\nA) Rs 15,500  B) Rs 1,25,000  C) Rs 1,55,000  D) Rs 2,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 1,55,000**\n\nUnder the AUA mode the cap is **2.5% of assets under advice per annum per family of client**. 2.5% x 62,00,000 = **Rs 1,55,000**. Because the AUA exceeds the Rs 50 lakh break-even point, the AUA mode permits more than the fixed-fee mode here.\n\n- **A) Rs 15,500** - wrong; this applies 0.25% instead of 2.5%.\n- **B) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the cap under the **fixed-fee** mode, which has not been chosen.\n- **D) Rs 2,80,000** - wrong; this adds both caps together, but only **one mode** may apply to a client at a time.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Ashwin's advisory business is registered with SEBI as a Limited Liability Partnership. What minimum net worth must it maintain at all times?\nA) Rs 5 lakh  B) Rs 25 lakh  C) Rs 50 lakh  D) Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 50 lakh**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 as amended in 2020, a **non-individual** investment adviser - a body corporate or LLP - must maintain a net worth of **not less than Rs 50 lakh**.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 lakh** - wrong; that is the net tangible asset requirement for an **individual** adviser or partnership firm.\n- **B) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; that was the pre-2020 requirement for non-individuals, since raised.\n- **D) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the minimum net worth of a **portfolio manager**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser charges his client an advisory fee and also continues to receive trail commission from an AMC on a scheme he recommended to that client. Which provision does this most directly breach?\nA) Regulation 15(2)  B) Regulation 16  C) Regulation 19  D) Regulation 21\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Regulation 15(2)**\n\n**Regulation 15(2)** states that an investment adviser **shall not receive any consideration, by way of remuneration, compensation or in any other form, from any person other than the client being advised**, in respect of the underlying products or securities advised upon. Trail commission from the AMC is exactly that.\n\n- **B) Regulation 16** - wrong; that deals with **risk profiling**.\n- **C) Regulation 19** - wrong; that deals with **maintenance of records**.\n- **D) Regulation 21** - wrong; that deals with **redressal of client grievances**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Which statement about the segregation of advisory and distribution activity is correct?\nA) An individual investment adviser may distribute products to clients he does not advise  B) An individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services at all  C) A non-individual adviser may advise and distribute to the same client if it discloses the conflict  D) The segregation requirement applies only to non-individual advisers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) An individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services at all**\n\nUnder **Regulation 22**, an **individual** IA may not provide distribution services at all, and the **family of the individual IA may not distribute to the IA's clients**. A **non-individual** IA may do both, but must maintain **client-level segregation at group level** through a separately identifiable department, so that a client and the client's family take either advice or distribution from the group - never both.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the prohibition on an individual IA is absolute, not client-by-client.\n- **C)** - wrong; disclosure does not cure the breach; segregation is mandatory.\n- **D)** - wrong; the requirement binds individual advisers most strictly of all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Nandini, who has no connection whatsoever with a listed company, overhears two of its directors discussing an unannounced merger in a hotel lift. Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, she is:\nA) Not an insider, because she is not a connected person  B) An insider, because she is in possession of unpublished price sensitive information  C) An insider only if she actually trades on the information  D) A connected person, by virtue of the six-month lookback\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) An insider, because she is in possession of unpublished price sensitive information**\n\nThe definition of \"insider\" in Regulation 2(1)(g) has two limbs joined by **\"or\"**: a **connected person**, **OR** any person **in possession of, or having access to, UPSI**. Possession alone makes her an insider the moment she acquires the information.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; connection is only one of two alternative routes to insider status.\n- **C)** - wrong; she becomes an insider on possession; trading is what turns it into a **violation** of Regulation 4.\n- **D)** - wrong; the six-month lookback defines a **connected person**, and she has no association with the company at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** Which statement about a Suspicious Transaction Report (STR) under the PMLA is correct?\nA) It is required only for cash transactions exceeding Rs 10 lakh  B) It must be filed by the 15th day of the succeeding month  C) It has no monetary threshold and must be filed within 7 working days  D) The client must be told that an STR concerning him has been filed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) It has no monetary threshold and must be filed within 7 working days**\n\nAn **STR carries no minimum amount** and covers transactions **whether attempted or executed**. It must be filed with **FIU-IND within 7 working days** of arriving at the conclusion that the transaction is suspicious.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the Rs 10 lakh cash threshold belongs to the **Cash Transaction Report**.\n- **B)** - wrong; the 15th-of-the-next-month deadline applies to CTR, CCR, NTR and cross-border wire reports, not to STRs.\n- **D)** - wrong; informing the client is **\"tipping off\"**, which is itself an offence.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** An investment adviser's failure to comply with a SEBI direction continues for 140 days. Under Section 15EB of the SEBI Act, what is the maximum monetary penalty that may be levied?\nA) Rs 1,00,000  B) Rs 1,00,00,000  C) Rs 1,40,00,000  D) Rs 25,00,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,00,00,000**\n\nSection 15EB provides for a penalty of **not less than Rs 1 lakh**, extending to **Rs 1 lakh for each day** the failure continues, **subject to a maximum of Rs 1 crore**. The ceiling is reached in 100 days, so a 140-day default still attracts a maximum of **Rs 1 crore**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,00,000** - wrong; that is the statutory **minimum**, not the maximum.\n- **C) Rs 1,40,00,000** - wrong; this multiplies Rs 1 lakh by 140 days and ignores the Rs 1 crore cap.\n- **D) Rs 25,00,00,000** - wrong; Rs 25 crore is the ceiling under Sections 15G and 15HA for insider trading and fraudulent trade practices.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** A person gives investment advice for a fee exclusively to clients living outside India, several of whom are NRIs. Does he require registration as an investment adviser with SEBI?\nA) No; advising persons based outside India is exempt  B) Yes; the \"exclusively outside India\" exemption does not extend to NRIs and PIOs  C) No, provided the fee is received in freely convertible foreign currency  D) Yes, but only once he has more than 150 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Yes; the \"exclusively outside India\" exemption does not extend to NRIs and PIOs**\n\nRegulation 4 exempts a person advising **exclusively clients based outside India**, but that exemption expressly **does not cover NRIs and PIOs**. Advising even one NRI brings the person within the registration requirement.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; it states the exemption without its critical NRI/PIO carve-out.\n- **C)** - wrong; the currency of the fee is irrelevant to the exemption.\n- **D)** - wrong; the 150-client threshold governs when an **individual** adviser must convert to a **non-individual** registration, not whether registration is required at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Over one year, a relationship manager at a distribution firm switches Sneha's Rs 30,00,000 equity portfolio eight times between schemes with near-identical mandates, earning fresh commission on each switch. Her asset allocation is unchanged throughout. This conduct is best described as:\nA) Tactical asset allocation  B) Churning  C) Rebalancing  D) Insider trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Churning**\n\n**Churning** is **excessive trading, switching or turnover** in a client's portfolio undertaken to generate brokerage or fresh commission. The give-away sign is present here: a great deal of activity with **no change in the underlying strategy**, and cost borne entirely by the client.\n\n- **A) Tactical asset allocation** - wrong; that involves deliberate shifts **between asset classes** on a market view, and her allocation never changed.\n- **C) Rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing restores drifted weights to strategic targets and is done infrequently.\n- **D) Insider trading** - wrong; no unpublished price sensitive information about any company is involved.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An adviser buys a stock in his own account minutes before placing a large buy order for a client in the same stock. This conduct is:\nA) Insider trading, dealt with under the PIT Regulations, 2015  B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003  C) Churning, dealt with under the IA Code of Conduct  D) Mis-selling, dealt with under Regulation 17 of the IA Regulations\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003**\n\n**Front-running** is dealing **ahead of a substantial impending client order** to profit from the price move that order will cause. It is a deemed fraudulent and unfair trade practice under Regulation 4(2) of the **PFUTP Regulations, 2003**. The memory hook: **front-run an ORDER, insider-trade on NEWS**.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; insider trading uses unpublished price sensitive information **about the company**, not knowledge of an order book.\n- **C)** - wrong; churning concerns excessive frequency of trading in the **client's** account.\n- **D)** - wrong; mis-selling concerns recommending an **unsuitable product**, which is not what happened here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** What distinguishes the **fiduciary** standard applicable to an investment adviser from the **suitability** standard applicable to a distributor?\nA) The fiduciary standard applies only at the point of sale  B) The fiduciary standard asks whether the product is the best available for the client, not merely whether it is not unsuitable  C) The fiduciary standard permits commission from the product manufacturer provided it is disclosed  D) The fiduciary standard applies only to non-individual investment advisers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The fiduciary standard asks whether the product is the best available for the client, not merely whether it is not unsuitable**\n\nRegulation 15(1) requires an IA to act in a **fiduciary capacity** and disclose all conflicts as they arise. A fiduciary must place the client's interest **above his own** - a higher standard than a distributor's, which asks only whether a product is **not unsuitable**.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the fiduciary duty is **continuing**; the advice must be monitored. It is the suitability standard that is largely point-of-sale.\n- **C)** - wrong; Regulation 15(2) prohibits **any** consideration from anyone other than the client.\n- **D)** - wrong; the fiduciary duty binds every registered investment adviser, individual or otherwise.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** An Investment Adviser's compliance audit report for FY 2025-26 is dated 18 September 2026 and contains adverse observations. What must be reported, and by when?\nA) The observations alone, by 30 September 2026  B) The observations together with the action taken, approved by the management, by 18 October 2026  C) The observations alone, within one month of the end of the financial year  D) Nothing, unless the same observation is repeated in the following year's audit\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The observations together with the action taken, approved by the management, by 18 October 2026**\n\nThe annual compliance audit by a member of **ICAI or ICSI** must be completed within **six months of the financial year end** (by 30 September). **Adverse findings, together with the action taken on them and duly approved by the management, board or proprietor, must be reported within one month of the date of the audit report** - here, by 18 October 2026.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; 30 September is the audit **completion** deadline, and the findings alone are an incomplete report.\n- **C)** - wrong; the one-month clock runs from the **audit report date**, not the financial year end.\n- **D)** - wrong; suppressing an adverse finding is a fresh and more serious violation than the original lapse.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** What most clearly distinguishes Australia's FOFA reforms from the Indian and US approaches to adviser conflicts of interest?\nA) It requires an annual compliance audit by a chartered accountant  B) It bans conflicted remuneration outright rather than relying on disclosure  C) It caps advisory fees at a fixed annual amount per family of client  D) It requires every adviser to obtain membership of a self-regulatory body\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It bans conflicted remuneration outright rather than relying on disclosure**\n\nUnder **FOFA (Future of Financial Advice)**, administered by **ASIC**, commissions and volume-based benefits are **banned** for personal advice on relevant products. The conflict is **removed**, not merely disclosed - which is what makes the Australian regime the strictest of the three on remuneration.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the ICAI/ICSI compliance audit is an **Indian** requirement.\n- **C)** - wrong; the fee cap per family is the **Indian** SEBI rule.\n- **D)** - wrong; that describes BASL membership in India; Australia uses an AFS licence.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Shruti is dissatisfied with her stockbroker's handling of a settlement. Without writing to the broker at all, she lodges a complaint directly on SCORES. What will happen?\nA) SEBI will examine the evidence and order the broker to compensate her  B) The complaint is not maintainable; she must first approach the broker  C) The complaint will be forwarded directly to the Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) The complaint will be referred to the District Consumer Commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The complaint is not maintainable; she must first approach the broker**\n\nEvery Indian redress system runs on the same ladder: **intermediary first, regulator next, tribunal or adjudicator last**. SCORES expressly requires that the investor **first approached the entity** and received no reply, an unsatisfactory reply, or no action. A complaint that skips the firm is bounced back.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; SEBI **routes, tracks and enforces timelines**; it does not adjudicate individual money claims.\n- **C)** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against a **regulator's order**, not first-instance investor complaints.\n- **D)** - wrong; SCORES does not transfer complaints to consumer commissions.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Under SCORES 2.0, within how many days must the entity complained against file its Action Taken Report?\nA) 7 calendar days  B) 15 calendar days  C) 21 calendar days  D) 30 calendar days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 21 calendar days**\n\nThe SCORES 2.0 rhythm is **21 - 15 - 21 - 15 - 21**: the **entity has 21 calendar days** to file its ATR, the investor has 15 days to seek a first review, the Designated Body has 21 days, the investor has 15 days to seek a second review, and SEBI has 21 days. If the entity misses its 21 days, the complaint **auto-escalates** without the investor doing anything.\n\n- **A) 7 calendar days** - wrong; 7 working days is the STR filing deadline under the PMLA, not a SCORES timeline.\n- **B) 15 calendar days** - wrong; 15 days is the window within which the **investor** may seek a review.\n- **D) 30 calendar days** - wrong; 30 days is the RBI Ombudsman escalation trigger, not the SCORES ATR deadline.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Prasanna has a disputed money claim of Rs 3,20,000 against his broker. He has complained to the broker and exhausted the SCORES route without resolution. What is the appropriate next step?\nA) File an appeal with the Securities Appellate Tribunal  B) Initiate online conciliation and, if needed, arbitration on the SMART ODR portal  C) Complain to the RBI Integrated Ombudsman  D) Lodge a further review request with SEBI on SCORES\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Initiate online conciliation and, if needed, arbitration on the SMART ODR portal**\n\n**SCORES chases; ODR decides.** SCORES can compel a reply but cannot decide a disputed money claim. The **SMART ODR portal** (introduced by SEBI's circular of 31 July 2023) offers **non-binding online conciliation** followed, if that fails, by **binding online arbitration** enforceable under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against orders of SEBI, PFRDA, IRDAI and exchanges, not investor claims against brokers.\n- **C)** - wrong; the RBI Ombudsman covers banks, NBFCs and payment system participants, not securities market disputes.\n- **D)** - wrong; the SCORES review stages have already been exhausted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Within what period must an appeal against an order of a SEBI adjudicating officer be filed with SAT, and where does a further appeal lie?\nA) 30 days; to the High Court on any question  B) 45 days; to the Supreme Court on a question of law  C) 60 days; to the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal  D) 90 days; to the Central Government\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 45 days; to the Supreme Court on a question of law**\n\nAn appeal to **SAT** must be filed within **45 days** of receipt of the order, though SAT may condone delay for sufficient cause. A further appeal lies to the **Supreme Court within 60 days**, and only on a **question of law** under Section 15Z. The three numbers to hold are **45 - 6 months - 60**.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; neither the period nor the forum is correct; SAT appeals do not go to a High Court.\n- **C)** - wrong; NCLAT hears appeals under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code and company law.\n- **D)** - wrong; the Central Government is not an appellate forum for SEBI orders.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** What is the maximum total compensation the RBI Ombudsman may award under the Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, 2021?\nA) Rs 10 lakh  B) Rs 20 lakh  C) Rs 21 lakh  D) Rs 30 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 21 lakh**\n\nThe Ombudsman may award up to **Rs 20 lakh for the actual loss suffered**, **plus up to Rs 1 lakh** for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish. The two are **separate and additive**, giving a theoretical maximum of **Rs 21 lakh**.\n\n- **A) Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; no such ceiling appears in RB-IOS 2021.\n- **B) Rs 20 lakh** - wrong; this is only the actual-loss component and omits the additional Rs 1 lakh head.\n- **D) Rs 30 lakh** - wrong; Rs 30 lakh is the value limit for a complaint before the **Insurance** Ombudsman.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Caselets (6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 1: Aditya and Sneha Kulkarni's household budget\n\nAditya Kulkarni (27) is a software engineer with an IT services firm in Whitefield, Bengaluru, earning Rs 1,25,000 a month gross. His wife Sneha (26), a technical writer, earns Rs 68,000 a month gross. Their total household expenses come to Rs 1,34,000 a month, of which Rs 41,000 are EMIs on a car loan and a personal loan. They hold Rs 2,15,000 in a savings account and Rs 1,85,000 in a liquid fund. Their total assets are Rs 62,00,000 and their total outstanding loans are Rs 27,50,000. Their adviser wants the emergency fund built up to six months of expenses.\n\n**Q91.** What is the household's monthly savings ratio?\nA) 30.57%  B) 44.03%  C) 47.20%  D) 69.43%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 30.57%**\n\nGross income = 1,25,000 + 68,000 = Rs 1,93,000. Savings = 1,93,000 − 1,34,000 = Rs 59,000.\nSavings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 59,000 ÷ 1,93,000 = **30.57%**.\nCheck: expenses ratio = 1,34,000 ÷ 1,93,000 = 69.43%, and 30.57 + 69.43 = 100 ✓\n\n- **B) 44.03%** - wrong; this divides savings by expenses (59,000 ÷ 1,34,000) instead of by income.\n- **C) 47.20%** - wrong; this uses only Aditya's income (59,000 ÷ 1,25,000) as the denominator.\n- **D) 69.43%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** What is their liquidity ratio?\nA) 1.60 months  B) 2.07 months  C) 2.99 months  D) 4.30 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 2.99 months**\n\nLiquid assets = 2,15,000 (savings account) + 1,85,000 (liquid fund) = Rs 4,00,000.\nLiquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ Monthly expenses = 4,00,000 ÷ 1,34,000 = **2.99 months** — below the healthy 3–6 month band.\n\n- **A) 1.60 months** - wrong; this counts only the savings account balance (2,15,000 ÷ 1,34,000) as liquid.\n- **B) 2.07 months** - wrong; this divides by gross income (4,00,000 ÷ 1,93,000) instead of by expenses.\n- **D) 4.30 months** - wrong; this strips the EMIs out of expenses (4,00,000 ÷ 93,000). EMIs must still be paid in an emergency, so they stay in.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** What is their solvency ratio?\nA) 0.44  B) 0.56  C) 0.80  D) 1.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.56**\n\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 62,00,000 − 27,50,000 = Rs 34,50,000.\nSolvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 34,50,000 ÷ 62,00,000 = **0.56**.\nCheck: leverage ratio = 27,50,000 ÷ 62,00,000 = 0.44, and 0.56 + 0.44 = 1 ✓\n\n- **A) 0.44** - wrong; this is the leverage ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), not solvency.\n- **C) 0.80** - wrong; this is liabilities ÷ net worth (27,50,000 ÷ 34,50,000), which is not a syllabus ratio.\n- **D) 1.80** - wrong; this is total assets ÷ net worth, the inverse of the solvency ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** What is their debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 21.24%  B) 30.60%  C) 32.80%  D) 69.43%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 21.24%**\n\nDebt-to-income = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 41,000 ÷ 1,93,000 = **21.24%** — comfortably inside the 35–40% guideline.\n\n- **B) 30.60%** - wrong; this divides EMIs by total expenses (41,000 ÷ 1,34,000), not by income.\n- **C) 32.80%** - wrong; this uses only Aditya's income (41,000 ÷ 1,25,000) as the denominator.\n- **D) 69.43%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** How much more must they set aside to bring the emergency fund up to six months of expenses?\nA) Rs 4,04,000  B) Rs 5,58,000  C) Rs 8,04,000  D) Rs 11,58,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 4,04,000**\n\nTarget = 6 × Monthly expenses = 6 × 1,34,000 = Rs 8,04,000.\nAlready held in liquid form = 2,15,000 + 1,85,000 = Rs 4,00,000.\nShortfall = 8,04,000 − 4,00,000 = **Rs 4,04,000**. At a surplus of Rs 59,000 a month this takes about seven months to fill.\n\n- **B) Rs 5,58,000** - wrong; this sizes the fund on non-EMI expenses only (6 × 93,000) and ignores the balance already held.\n- **C) Rs 8,04,000** - wrong; this is the target itself, not the additional amount needed.\n- **D) Rs 11,58,000** - wrong; this sizes the fund against income (6 × 1,93,000). The emergency fund is sized against **expenses**.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 2: Nikhil Rao's equity and bond holdings\n\nNikhil Rao (31) is a product manager in Koramangala, Bengaluru. He holds 800 shares of Sabari Tech Ltd, currently quoted at Rs 640. Sabari Tech reported earnings per share of Rs 32, has a book value per share of Rs 200 and paid a dividend of Rs 8 per share. He also holds a corporate bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 carrying a 7% annual coupon, with exactly 4 years left to maturity, currently trading at Rs 940. Expecting a weak quarterly result, Nikhil sells one lot (500 shares) of Sabari Tech futures at Rs 656. On expiry the stock settles at Rs 598.\n\n**Q96.** What is Sabari Tech's price-to-earnings ratio?\nA) 3.20  B) 5.00  C) 20.00  D) 80.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 20.00**\n\nP/E = Market price ÷ EPS = 640 ÷ 32 = **20.00**.\n\n- **A) 3.20** - wrong; this is the price-to-book ratio (640 ÷ 200).\n- **B) 5.00** - wrong; this is the earnings yield in per cent (32 ÷ 640), the reciprocal of the P/E.\n- **D) 80.00** - wrong; this is price divided by dividend per share (640 ÷ 8).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** What is the dividend yield on Sabari Tech at the current market price?\nA) 1.25%  B) 4.00%  C) 5.00%  D) 25.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.25%**\n\nDividend yield = DPS ÷ Market price = 8 ÷ 640 = **1.25%**.\n\n- **B) 4.00%** - wrong; this divides the dividend by book value per share (8 ÷ 200).\n- **C) 5.00%** - wrong; this is the earnings yield (32 ÷ 640).\n- **D) 25.00%** - wrong; this is the dividend payout ratio (8 ÷ 32), not a yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** What is the current yield on Nikhil's bond?\nA) 7.00%  B) 7.22%  C) 7.45%  D) 8.76%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 7.45%**\n\nAnnual coupon = 7% × 1,000 = Rs 70. Current yield = Annual coupon ÷ Market price = 70 ÷ 940 = **7.45%**.\n\n- **A) 7.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, which is always computed on face value, not on the price paid.\n- **B) 7.22%** - wrong; this divides by the average of face value and price (70 ÷ 970), which is a step in the approximate-YTM formula, not the current yield.\n- **D) 8.76%** - wrong; this is the approximate yield to maturity, which also counts the capital gain at redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Using the standard approximation, what is the bond's yield to maturity?\nA) 7.00%  B) 7.45%  C) 8.50%  D) 8.76%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 8.76%**\n\nApproximate YTM = [C + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2]\n= [70 + (1,000 − 940) ÷ 4] ÷ [(1,000 + 940) ÷ 2]\n= [70 + 15] ÷ 970 = 85 ÷ 970 = **8.76%**.\nThe bond trades at a discount, so the expected ordering holds: coupon 7.00% < current yield 7.45% < YTM 8.76%.\n\n- **A) 7.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate.\n- **B) 7.45%** - wrong; this is the current yield, which ignores the Rs 60 capital gain at redemption.\n- **C) 8.50%** - wrong; this divides Rs 85 by the face value of Rs 1,000 instead of by the average of face value and price.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** Taking the 800 shares and the short futures position together, what is Nikhil's net outcome when the stock settles at Rs 598?\nA) A net loss of Rs 4,600  B) A net gain of Rs 4,600  C) A net gain of Rs 29,000  D) A net loss of Rs 33,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A net loss of Rs 4,600**\n\nLoss on the shares = (640 − 598) × 800 = 42 × 800 = Rs 33,600.\nGain on the short futures = (656 − 598) × 500 = 58 × 500 = Rs 29,000.\nNet = 29,000 − 33,600 = **a net loss of Rs 4,600**. The hedge is partial because only 500 of his 800 shares are covered.\n\n- **B) A net gain of Rs 4,600** - wrong; the sign is reversed. The uncovered 300 shares mean the loss exceeds the futures gain.\n- **C) A net gain of Rs 29,000** - wrong; this counts only the futures leg and ignores the loss on the shares he still owns.\n- **D) A net loss of Rs 33,600** - wrong; this counts only the shares and ignores the futures gain entirely.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 3: Meghana Iyer invests in a flexi cap fund\n\nMeghana Iyer (29) is a data engineer in Bengaluru. She is looking at BlueRidge Flexi Cap Fund, which holds total assets of Rs 1,842 crore, owes liabilities of Rs 18 crore and has 45 crore units outstanding. The regular plan carries a total expense ratio of 1.85% a year; the direct plan charges 0.95%. The scheme levies an exit load of 1% on redemptions within 365 days. Meghana invests a lump sum of Rs 6,00,000 and, on day 200, redeems units then worth Rs 6,72,000. Separately, she runs a SIP of Rs 15,000 a month; her first four instalments were bought at NAVs of Rs 40, Rs 48, Rs 50 and Rs 60.\n\n**Q101.** What is the scheme's NAV per unit?\nA) Rs 40.53  B) Rs 40.93  C) Rs 41.33  D) Rs 405.33\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 40.53**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ Units outstanding = (1,842 − 18) ÷ 45 = 1,824 ÷ 45 = **Rs 40.53**.\n\n- **B) Rs 40.93** - wrong; this ignores the liabilities (1,842 ÷ 45).\n- **C) Rs 41.33** - wrong; this adds the liabilities instead of subtracting them (1,860 ÷ 45).\n- **D) Rs 405.33** - wrong; a decimal-place slip. Both numerator and denominator are already in crore, so they cancel.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q102.** How much will Meghana actually receive on the day-200 redemption?\nA) Rs 6,65,280  B) Rs 6,66,000  C) Rs 6,72,000  D) Rs 6,78,720\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 6,65,280**\n\nDay 200 is within 365 days, so the 1% exit load applies to the redemption value.\nExit load = 1% × 6,72,000 = Rs 6,720. Net proceeds = 6,72,000 − 6,720 = **Rs 6,65,280**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6,66,000** - wrong; this applies the load to the original investment of Rs 6,00,000. Exit load is charged on the redemption value.\n- **C) Rs 6,72,000** - wrong; this ignores the exit load, which applies because the holding period is under 365 days.\n- **D) Rs 6,78,720** - wrong; this adds the load instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q103.** On her Rs 6,00,000 holding, how much does Meghana save in a year by using the direct plan instead of the regular plan?\nA) Rs 5,400  B) Rs 5,700  C) Rs 11,100  D) Rs 16,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5,400**\n\nTER difference = 1.85% − 0.95% = 0.90%. Saving = 0.90% × 6,00,000 = **Rs 5,400 a year**.\nThe portfolio is identical in both plans; only the distribution commission embedded in the regular plan's TER differs, which is why the direct plan's NAV pulls ahead over time.\n\n- **B) Rs 5,700** - wrong; this is the direct plan's own annual cost (0.95% × 6,00,000), not the saving.\n- **C) Rs 11,100** - wrong; this is the full regular plan cost (1.85% × 6,00,000). The direct plan still charges 0.95%.\n- **D) Rs 16,800** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios (2.80%) instead of taking the difference.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q104.** How many units did Meghana's first four SIP instalments buy in total?\nA) 1,200.00  B) 1,212.12  C) 1,237.50  D) 1,250.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 1,237.50**\n\nUnits = 15,000 ÷ 40 = 375.00; 15,000 ÷ 48 = 312.50; 15,000 ÷ 50 = 300.00; 15,000 ÷ 60 = 250.00.\nTotal = 375.00 + 312.50 + 300.00 + 250.00 = **1,237.50 units** for Rs 60,000 invested.\n\n- **A) 1,200.00** - wrong; this divides the total investment by an assumed NAV of Rs 50 (60,000 ÷ 50).\n- **B) 1,212.12** - wrong; this divides the total investment by the simple average NAV of Rs 49.50. That is not how units are actually allotted.\n- **D) 1,250.00** - wrong; this divides the total investment by Rs 48 only.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q105.** What is Meghana's average cost per unit under the SIP?\nA) Rs 44.00  B) Rs 48.48  C) Rs 49.50  D) Rs 50.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 48.48**\n\nAverage cost = Total invested ÷ Total units = 60,000 ÷ 1,237.50 = **Rs 48.48**.\nThe simple average of the four NAVs is (40 + 48 + 50 + 60) ÷ 4 = Rs 49.50, so her average cost is Rs 1.02 *below* the average NAV. That gap is exactly what rupee-cost averaging delivers: a fixed rupee amount buys more units when the NAV is low.\n\n- **A) Rs 44.00** - wrong; this averages only the first two NAVs (40 and 48).\n- **C) Rs 49.50** - wrong; this is the simple average NAV, which is always at or above the average cost under a SIP.\n- **D) Rs 50.00** - wrong; this follows from a mis-count of 1,200 units (60,000 ÷ 1,200).\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 4: Vikram Shenoy's managed portfolio options\n\nVikram Shenoy (44) is a start-up CTO in Bengaluru with a large investible surplus. He places Rs 75,00,000 with a discretionary portfolio manager. The agreement provides for a fixed fee of 1% of assets a year plus a performance fee of 15%, subject to a hurdle rate of 9% on the opening value with no catch-up, and charged on a high watermark basis. Before fees, the portfolio is worth Rs 91,50,000 at the end of Year 1, Rs 82,00,000 at the end of Year 2 and Rs 98,00,000 at the end of Year 3. Vikram is separately evaluating a Category II AIF with a corpus of Rs 240 crore, and a Category III hedge-style AIF. He is in the 30% tax slab.\n\n**Q106.** What is the minimum amount SEBI requires a client to invest with a portfolio manager?\nA) Rs 25 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh  C) Rs 1 crore  D) Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50 lakh**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020 the minimum investment per client is **Rs 50 lakh**. Vikram's Rs 75,00,000 clears it.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the minimum for employees and directors of an AIF or its manager, and for an angel investor.\n- **C) Rs 1 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the minimum investment in an AIF, not a PMS.\n- **D) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the portfolio manager's own minimum net worth, not the client's ticket size.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q107.** What performance fee is payable for Year 1?\nA) Rs 1,01,250  B) Rs 1,46,250  C) Rs 2,47,500  D) Rs 9,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,46,250**\n\nGain in Year 1 = 91,50,000 − 75,00,000 = Rs 16,50,000.\nHurdle amount = 9% × 75,00,000 = Rs 6,75,000, which the manager does not share in.\nFee base = 16,50,000 − 6,75,000 = Rs 9,75,000. Performance fee = 15% × 9,75,000 = **Rs 1,46,250**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,01,250** - wrong; this charges 15% on the hurdle amount itself rather than on the excess above it.\n- **C) Rs 2,47,500** - wrong; this is 15% of the whole Rs 16,50,000 gain, ignoring the hurdle.\n- **D) Rs 9,75,000** - wrong; this is the fee base, not the fee.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q108.** What performance fee is payable for Year 3?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 97,500  C) Rs 2,40,000  D) Rs 3,45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 97,500**\n\nThe high watermark set in Year 1 is Rs 91,50,000. Year 2 ended at Rs 82,00,000, below the watermark, so no performance fee was charged then. In Year 3 only the gain **above the watermark** is chargeable: 98,00,000 − 91,50,000 = Rs 6,50,000. Fee = 15% × 6,50,000 = **Rs 97,500**.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has risen above its previous peak, so a fee is due on that excess.\n- **C) Rs 2,40,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 16,00,000 rise from Rs 82,00,000, which would make the client pay twice for recovering ground already lost. That is precisely what the high watermark prevents.\n- **D) Rs 3,45,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 23,00,000 cumulative gain over the original Rs 75,00,000.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q109.** What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager of the Rs 240 crore Category II AIF maintain?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 12 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5 crore**\n\nFor a Category I or II AIF the continuing interest is the **lower of** 2.5% of corpus or Rs 5 crore.\n2.5% × 240 crore = Rs 6 crore; the lower of Rs 6 crore and Rs 5 crore is **Rs 5 crore**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6 crore** - wrong; this is 2.5% of the corpus, but the rule caps the requirement at Rs 5 crore.\n- **C) Rs 10 crore** - wrong; Rs 10 crore is the cap for a Category III AIF, not Category II.\n- **D) Rs 12 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category III rate of 5% to the corpus.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q110.** How will income from the two AIFs be taxed in Vikram's hands?\nA) Both funds enjoy pass-through status, so Vikram is taxed on his share of the income in each case  B) The Category II fund enjoys pass-through status; the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level  C) The Category III fund enjoys pass-through status; the Category II fund is taxed at the fund level  D) Neither fund enjoys pass-through status; both are taxed at the fund level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The Category II fund enjoys pass-through status; the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level**\n\nCategories I and II have pass-through status under the Income Tax Act: income is deemed to arise directly to the investor, the character of the income is preserved, and Vikram pays at his own applicable rate. Category III has **no** pass-through — the fund is taxed before distributing, generally at the maximum marginal rate. The one exception for Categories I and II is business income, which is taxed at the fund level.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; Category III is expressly outside the pass-through regime.\n- **C)** - wrong; this reverses the rule. Remember: \"One and Two pass it through; Three pays for you.\"\n- **D)** - wrong; Category II is a pass-through vehicle.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 5: Ananya Deshpande, an individual investment adviser\n\nAnanya Deshpande is an individual investment adviser registered with SEBI, operating from Indiranagar, Bengaluru. Her net tangible assets stand at Rs 7,20,000. She currently advises 138 clients and has been approached by 30 more. One client, Rohit Menon, is 58, two years from retirement, has assets under advice of Rs 38,00,000 and was classified as \"conservative\" by her risk profiling questionnaire; Ananya has nevertheless recommended that he commit to a Category III AIF that uses leverage, and she has not recorded any written rationale for the advice. Ananya's husband is an AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor and proposes to earn trail commission on the schemes she recommends to her clients.\n\n**Q111.** What minimum net tangible assets must Ananya maintain as an individual investment adviser?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 5 lakh  C) Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 lakh**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 as amended in 2020, an individual adviser or partnership firm must hold net tangible assets of at least **Rs 5 lakh**, at all times. Ananya's Rs 7,20,000 is compliant.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the pre-2020 figure and is also the individual research analyst requirement.\n- **C) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh was the pre-2020 non-individual requirement.\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the net worth required of a non-individual investment adviser.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q112.** If Ananya onboards the 30 additional clients, what must she do?\nA) Nothing, because the client limit applies only to non-individual advisers  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted  C) Appoint a compliance officer, after which she may continue onboarding clients as an individual  D) Raise her net tangible assets to Rs 50 lakh, after which she may continue as an individual adviser\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted**\n\n138 + 30 = 168 clients, which exceeds the **150-client** corporatisation threshold. An individual IA crossing 150 clients must apply for registration as a non-individual IA and must stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration comes through.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the 150-client threshold applies specifically to individual advisers.\n- **C)** - wrong; a compliance officer is required of a non-individual IA. Appointing one does not remove the client cap.\n- **D)** - wrong; net worth is a separate condition and does not substitute for corporatisation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q113.** Under the assets-under-advice mode, what is the maximum annual fee Ananya may charge Rohit's family?\nA) Rs 76,000  B) Rs 95,000  C) Rs 1,25,000  D) Rs 9,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 95,000**\n\nThe AUA mode cap is **2.5% of assets under advice per annum, per family of client**: 2.5% × 38,00,000 = **Rs 95,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 76,000** - wrong; this applies 2% rather than the prescribed 2.5% cap.\n- **C) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; Rs 1,25,000 is the cap under the *fixed fee* mode. Only one mode may apply to a client at a time, and the question asks about the AUA mode.\n- **D) Rs 9,50,000** - wrong; this is 25% of AUA, a decimal-place error.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q114.** Recommending the leveraged Category III AIF to Rohit most directly breaches which obligation?\nA) Regulation 15(2) — receiving no consideration from any person other than the client  B) Regulation 16 — risk profiling  C) Regulation 17 — suitability  D) Regulation 20 — appointment of a compliance officer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Regulation 17 — suitability**\n\nAnanya *did* complete the risk profiling and it produced a \"conservative\" classification. The failure is in the next step: Regulation 17 requires that all investments advised be appropriate to the client's risk profile, that the client be able to bear the risks involved, and — for a complex product — that its structure and risk-reward profile be consistent with the client's experience, knowledge, objectives and capacity to absorb loss. A leveraged Category III AIF with a multi-year lock-in fails every one of those tests for a conservative investor two years from retirement.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; nothing in the facts suggests she was paid by anyone other than the client for this advice.\n- **B)** - wrong; the risk profiling was carried out and communicated. Risk profiling (knowing the client) and suitability (matching the product) are tested as separate obligations.\n- **D)** - wrong; an individual investment adviser is not required to appoint a compliance officer.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q115.** Is her husband permitted to earn trail commission on the schemes Ananya recommends to her clients?\nA) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed to the client in writing  B) Yes, provided Ananya charges the client under the AUA mode  C) No — the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients  D) Yes, provided the distribution business is run through a separately identifiable department or division\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No — the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**\n\nRegulation 22 states that an individual IA shall not provide distribution services at all, and that the **family of the individual IA shall not provide distribution services to the IA's clients**. \"Family of an individual IA\" covers the adviser, spouse, children and parents. The arrangement is prohibited outright, not merely disclosable.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; disclosure does not cure a prohibited arrangement.\n- **B)** - wrong; the fee mode is irrelevant to the segregation requirement.\n- **D)** - wrong; the separately identifiable department route is available only to a *non-individual* adviser, and even then that adviser's group may not distribute to its own advisory clients.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 6: Shruti Balan's portfolio after a career change\n\nShruti Balan (34) is a UX lead in Bengaluru. Her Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic asset allocation of 70% equity, 25% debt and 5% gold, with a tolerance band of plus or minus 5 percentage points on equity. At the start of the year her corpus stood at Rs 40,00,000, invested Rs 28,00,000 in equity, Rs 10,00,000 in debt and Rs 2,00,000 in gold. Over the year equity returned 26%, debt returned 7% and gold returned 18%. During the same year Shruti left her salaried job to join an early-stage start-up on a variable income and is expecting her first child; her adviser therefore proposes cutting the long-term equity target permanently from 70% to 55%.\n\n**Q116.** What is the equity weight in Shruti's portfolio at the end of the year?\nA) 70.00%  B) 72.98%  C) 76.20%  D) 88.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 72.98%**\n\nEquity = 28,00,000 × 1.26 = Rs 35,28,000\nDebt = 10,00,000 × 1.07 = Rs 10,70,000\nGold = 2,00,000 × 1.18 = Rs 2,36,000\nTotal = Rs 48,34,000\nEquity weight = 35,28,000 ÷ 48,34,000 = **72.98%**.\n\n- **A) 70.00%** - wrong; this is the target weight. Drift is exactly what the question is measuring.\n- **C) 76.20%** - wrong; this omits gold from the denominator (35,28,000 ÷ 46,30,000).\n- **D) 88.20%** - wrong; this divides the year-end equity value by the *opening* corpus of Rs 40,00,000.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q117.** Under a pure threshold (tolerance band) rule, what action is required at year end?\nA) No rebalancing is required  B) Sell Rs 1,44,200 of equity and buy debt  C) Sell Rs 8,69,300 of equity and buy debt  D) Buy Rs 1,44,200 of equity funded from debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) No rebalancing is required**\n\nDrift = 72.98% − 70.00% = 2.98 percentage points, which is **inside** the plus or minus 5 percentage point band. A threshold rule acts only when the band is breached, so nothing is done.\n\n- **B)** - wrong; Rs 1,44,200 is the trade a *calendar* rule would require. A threshold rule asks a different question.\n- **C)** - wrong; Rs 8,69,300 is the trade needed to move to the proposed new 55% target, which is a change of strategy, not a rebalancing trigger.\n- **D)** - wrong; equity is overweight, not underweight, so buying more would worsen the drift.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q118.** Under an annual calendar rebalancing rule, how much equity must be sold at year end?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 1,44,200  C) Rs 2,87,000  D) Rs 7,28,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,44,200**\n\nTarget equity = 70% × 48,34,000 = Rs 33,83,800.\nEquity to sell = 35,28,000 − 33,83,800 = **Rs 1,44,200**.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; that is the threshold-rule answer. A calendar rule acts on the review date regardless of how small the drift is.\n- **C) Rs 2,87,000** - wrong; this computes the target as 70% of equity plus debt only (70% × 46,30,000), leaving gold out of the base.\n- **D) Rs 7,28,000** - wrong; this applies the 70% target to the *opening* corpus of Rs 40,00,000 rather than to the year-end value.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q119.** How should the proposed permanent cut in the equity target from 70% to 55% be classified?\nA) Tactical asset allocation, taken within the IPS bands  B) A change in the strategic asset allocation, requiring the IPS to be revised  C) Calendar rebalancing  D) Constant proportion portfolio insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A change in the strategic asset allocation, requiring the IPS to be revised**\n\nThe trigger is a change in the **client's** circumstances — a move to variable start-up income and a dependant on the way — which reduces both her risk capacity and her liquidity headroom. Changes driven by the client's life are strategic and call for the IPS to be rewritten. Changes driven by a market view are tactical.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; a move to 55% is outside the 65–75% band the IPS permits, and tactical asset allocation is temporary and market-driven by definition.\n- **C)** - wrong; rebalancing restores the *existing* targets; it never changes them.\n- **D)** - wrong; CPPI is a formula-driven allocation rule (equity = multiplier × cushion), unrelated to a client life event.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q120.** How much equity must be sold to move the portfolio to the new 55% target?\nA) Rs 1,44,200  B) Rs 7,25,100  C) Rs 8,69,300  D) Rs 13,28,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 8,69,300**\n\nNew target equity = 55% × 48,34,000 = Rs 26,58,700.\nEquity to sell = 35,28,000 − 26,58,700 = **Rs 8,69,300**.\nBecause much of this is a taxable sale, the adviser would route new contributions into debt first and use the annual long-term capital gains exemption to reduce the cost of the switch.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,44,200** - wrong; that is the trade needed to restore the *old* 70% target.\n- **B) Rs 7,25,100** - wrong; this applies the 15 percentage point change to the whole corpus (15% × 48,34,000) instead of comparing the actual equity holding with the new target.\n- **D) Rs 13,28,000** - wrong; this applies the 55% target to the opening corpus of Rs 40,00,000.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section C — Caselets (3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 7: Harish Bhat's goal plan and home purchase\n\nHarish Bhat (30) is a DevOps engineer in Bengaluru earning Rs 1,55,000 a month gross, and already pays a car loan EMI of Rs 12,400. He wants to fund his daughter's undergraduate education, which costs Rs 22,00,000 at today's prices and is 15 years away; education costs are assumed to inflate at 8% a year. He has Rs 6,00,000 already earmarked for this goal, expected to grow at 11% a year. Any monthly SIP he starts will also be assumed to earn 11% a year compounded monthly. Separately, he wants to buy a flat costing Rs 95,00,000 with a 20% down payment, borrowing the balance at 8.75% a year for 20 years. His bank will sanction a loan only if his total EMIs stay within 45% of gross monthly income.\n\n**Q121.** What is the shortfall Harish must fund through fresh savings for the education goal?\nA) Rs 16,00,000  B) Rs 41,08,018  C) Rs 63,78,772  D) Rs 69,78,772\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 41,08,018**\n\nStep 1 — inflate the goal: 22,00,000 × (1.08)^15 = 22,00,000 × 3.172169 = Rs 69,78,772.\nStep 2 — grow what is already earmarked: 6,00,000 × (1.11)^15 = 6,00,000 × 4.784589 = Rs 28,70,754.\nStep 3 — the gap: 69,78,772 − 28,70,754 = **Rs 41,08,018**.\n\n- **A) Rs 16,00,000** - wrong; this is today's cost less today's earmarked corpus, ignoring both inflation and growth.\n- **C) Rs 63,78,772** - wrong; this deducts the earmarked Rs 6,00,000 at its present value instead of letting it compound for 15 years.\n- **D) Rs 69,78,772** - wrong; this is the inflated cost of the goal, before crediting the existing corpus.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q122.** What level monthly SIP, starting now, will close that shortfall in 15 years at 11% a year compounded monthly?\nA) Rs 3,519  B) Rs 9,035  C) Rs 15,350  D) Rs 22,822\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 9,035**\n\nMonthly rate r = 11% ÷ 12 = 0.0091667; n = 15 × 12 = 180.\n(1.0091667)^180 = 5.167988, so the future-value annuity factor = (5.167988 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = 454.690.\nPMT = 41,08,018 ÷ 454.690 = **Rs 9,035 a month**. In spreadsheet terms, `=ABS(PMT(11%/12, 180, 0, 4108018))`.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,519** - wrong; this annuitises the un-inflated shortfall of Rs 16,00,000, so the goal would be badly underfunded.\n- **C) Rs 15,350** - wrong; this funds the entire Rs 69,78,772 and gives no credit for the Rs 6,00,000 already earmarked.\n- **D) Rs 22,822** - wrong; this is the shortfall divided by 180 months, ignoring investment returns altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q123.** What EMI will the home loan carry?\nA) Rs 31,667  B) Rs 55,417  C) Rs 67,162  D) Rs 83,953\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 67,162**\n\nDown payment = 20% × 95,00,000 = Rs 19,00,000, so the loan is 95,00,000 − 19,00,000 = Rs 76,00,000.\nr = 8.75% ÷ 12 = 0.0072917; n = 240; (1.0072917)^240 = 5.71815.\nEMI = 76,00,000 × (0.0072917 × 5.71815) ÷ (5.71815 − 1) = 76,00,000 × 0.0088371 = **Rs 67,162**.\n\n- **A) Rs 31,667** - wrong; this is the principal spread over 240 months with no interest at all (76,00,000 ÷ 240).\n- **B) Rs 55,417** - wrong; this is the *first month's interest* (76,00,000 × 0.0072917), a classic near-miss distractor.\n- **D) Rs 83,953** - wrong; this is the EMI on the full flat cost of Rs 95,00,000, ignoring the down payment.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q124.** How much interest will Harish pay over the full 20-year tenure of the home loan?\nA) Rs 76,00,000  B) Rs 85,18,880  C) Rs 1,06,48,720  D) Rs 1,61,18,880\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 85,18,880**\n\nTotal repaid = EMI × n = 67,162 × 240 = Rs 1,61,18,880.\nTotal interest = Total repaid − Principal = 1,61,18,880 − 76,00,000 = **Rs 85,18,880** — more than the amount borrowed.\n\n- **A) Rs 76,00,000** - wrong; this is the loan principal, not the interest.\n- **C) Rs 1,06,48,720** - wrong; this is the interest on a Rs 95,00,000 loan (83,953 × 240 − 95,00,000), i.e. borrowing the whole flat cost.\n- **D) Rs 1,61,18,880** - wrong; this is the *total repaid*, which still includes the principal.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q125.** By how much does the required home loan EMI exceed the EMI capacity the bank will allow?\nA) Nil — the loan is affordable  B) Rs 9,812  C) Rs 17,562  D) Rs 26,603\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 9,812**\n\nPermitted total EMIs = 45% × 1,55,000 = Rs 69,750.\nLess the existing car loan EMI of Rs 12,400, leaving Rs 57,350 available for the home loan.\nRequired EMI = Rs 67,162. Excess = 67,162 − 57,350 = **Rs 9,812 a month**. Harish must either raise the down payment, lengthen the tenure, or clear the car loan first.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; this forgets the existing car loan EMI, which the bank counts within the same 45% ceiling.\n- **C) Rs 17,562** - wrong; this uses a 40% ceiling instead of the stated 45%.\n- **D) Rs 26,603** - wrong; this compares the capacity with the Rs 83,953 EMI on the full flat cost rather than on the actual Rs 76,00,000 loan.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 8: Ranking three fund managers\n\nAditya's adviser compares three equity funds over the same five-year period. Meridian Bluechip returned 15.2% a year with a standard deviation of 18.0% and a beta of 1.10. Kaveri Focused Equity returned 13.4% with a standard deviation of 12.5% and a beta of 0.85. Tungabhadra Multicap returned 16.8% with a standard deviation of 24.0% and a beta of 1.35. Over the same period the risk-free rate averaged 6.5% and the market returned 12.5% with a standard deviation of 15.0%. The adviser also models a two-fund blend of 65% Meridian Bluechip and 35% Kaveri Focused Equity; the correlation between the two funds' returns is 0.62.\n\n**Q126.** Which fund ranks highest on the Sharpe ratio, and what is that ratio?\nA) Meridian Bluechip, 0.48  B) Kaveri Focused Equity, 0.55  C) Tungabhadra Multicap, 0.43  D) Tungabhadra Multicap, 0.70\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Kaveri Focused Equity, 0.55**\n\nSharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp.\nMeridian: (15.2 − 6.5) ÷ 18.0 = 8.7 ÷ 18.0 = 0.48\nKaveri: (13.4 − 6.5) ÷ 12.5 = 6.9 ÷ 12.5 = **0.55**\nTungabhadra: (16.8 − 6.5) ÷ 24.0 = 10.3 ÷ 24.0 = 0.43\nKaveri wins despite the lowest raw return, because it delivered the most excess return per unit of **total** risk.\n\n- **A) Meridian Bluechip, 0.48** - wrong; the ratio is right for Meridian but it is not the highest.\n- **C) Tungabhadra Multicap, 0.43** - wrong; the ratio is right for Tungabhadra but it is the *lowest* of the three. Highest return does not mean best risk-adjusted return.\n- **D) Tungabhadra Multicap, 0.70** - wrong; 16.8 ÷ 24.0 omits the risk-free rate from the numerator.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q127.** Which fund ranks highest on the Treynor ratio, and what is that ratio?\nA) Kaveri Focused Equity, 8.12  B) Meridian Bluechip, 7.91  C) Tungabhadra Multicap, 7.63  D) Tungabhadra Multicap, 12.44\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Kaveri Focused Equity, 8.12**\n\nTreynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp.\nMeridian: 8.7 ÷ 1.10 = 7.91\nKaveri: 6.9 ÷ 0.85 = **8.12**\nTungabhadra: 10.3 ÷ 1.35 = 7.63\nKaveri ranks first on Treynor as well as on Sharpe. When the two measures agree, the funds are similarly diversified; the exam only makes them disagree when it wants you to spot a poorly diversified portfolio.\n\n- **B) Meridian Bluechip, 7.91** - wrong; correct arithmetic for Meridian, but it ranks second.\n- **C) Tungabhadra Multicap, 7.63** - wrong; correct arithmetic for Tungabhadra, but it ranks last.\n- **D) Tungabhadra Multicap, 12.44** - wrong; 16.8 ÷ 1.35 omits the risk-free rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q128.** Which fund produced the highest Jensen's alpha, and what is it?\nA) Meridian Bluechip, +2.10%  B) Kaveri Focused Equity, +1.80%  C) Tungabhadra Multicap, +2.20%  D) Tungabhadra Multicap, +4.30%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Tungabhadra Multicap, +2.20%**\n\nMarket risk premium = 12.5 − 6.5 = 6.0%. Alpha = Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)].\nMeridian: 15.2 − [6.5 + 1.10 × 6.0] = 15.2 − 13.1 = +2.10%\nKaveri: 13.4 − [6.5 + 0.85 × 6.0] = 13.4 − 11.60 = +1.80%\nTungabhadra: 16.8 − [6.5 + 1.35 × 6.0] = 16.8 − 14.60 = **+2.20%**\nNote the tension: Tungabhadra beat CAPM by the most, yet ranked last on Sharpe. Alpha rewards beating the beta-implied return; Sharpe penalises the total risk carried to do it.\n\n- **A) Meridian Bluechip, +2.10%** - wrong; correct alpha for Meridian, but it is not the highest.\n- **B) Kaveri Focused Equity, +1.80%** - wrong; correct alpha for Kaveri, and the lowest of the three.\n- **D) Tungabhadra Multicap, +4.30%** - wrong; 16.8 − 12.5 is the raw excess over the market, which ignores the 1.35 beta the fund carried.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q129.** What is the standard deviation of the 65% Meridian / 35% Kaveri blend?\nA) 12.49%  B) 14.82%  C) 15.25%  D) 16.08%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 14.82%**\n\nσp = √(w1²σ1² + w2²σ2² + 2·w1·w2·σ1·σ2·ρ)\n= √(0.65² × 18² + 0.35² × 12.5² + 2 × 0.65 × 0.35 × 18 × 12.5 × 0.62)\n= √(136.890 + 19.141 + 63.473) = √219.503 = **14.82%**.\n\n- **A) 12.49%** - wrong; this is the result if the correlation were zero. The stated correlation is 0.62.\n- **C) 15.25%** - wrong; this is the *unweighted* average of the two standard deviations.\n- **D) 16.08%** - wrong; this is the weighted average of the two standard deviations. Portfolio risk equals the weighted average only when the correlation is exactly +1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q130.** How much risk does diversification remove in this blend, measured against the weighted average of the two funds' standard deviations?\nA) Nil  B) 1.26 percentage points  C) 3.18 percentage points  D) 3.59 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1.26 percentage points**\n\nWeighted average σ = (0.65 × 18.0) + (0.35 × 12.5) = 11.70 + 4.375 = 16.08%.\nActual portfolio σ = 14.82%.\nBenefit = 16.08 − 14.82 = **1.26 percentage points of risk removed at no cost in expected return**. The benefit exists solely because the correlation of 0.62 is below 1; the higher the correlation, the smaller the gain.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; that would be true only at a correlation of exactly +1.\n- **C) 3.18 percentage points** - wrong; this compares the portfolio with the riskier fund's 18% standard deviation alone, which is not the diversification benefit.\n- **D) 3.59 percentage points** - wrong; this is the benefit that would arise if the two funds were uncorrelated (16.08 − 12.49).\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 9: Rohan Prabhu's bond and derivative positions\n\nRohan Prabhu (33) is an engineering manager in Bengaluru. He holds a Karnataka Infra Ltd bond: face value Rs 1,000, 9% annual coupon, exactly 5 years to maturity, with a required yield of 11%. His entire fixed income book, including this bond, has a market value of Rs 18,50,000 and a Macaulay duration of 4.21 years at the current 11% yield. On the derivatives side, the Nifty 50 spot is 24,600, the risk-free rate is 7% a year, the index dividend yield is 1.4% a year, and the three-month futures trade at their cost-of-carry fair value. The lot size is 25. Rohan also buys one lot of the 24,800-strike Nifty put at a premium of Rs 265. At expiry the index settles at 24,150.\n\n**Q131.** What is the fair price of the Karnataka Infra bond?\nA) Rs 818.18  B) Rs 900.00  C) Rs 926.08  D) Rs 1,000.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 926.08**\n\nCoupon = 9% × 1,000 = Rs 90 a year; y = 11%; n = 5.\n(1.11)^5 = 1.685058, so the discount factor is 1 ÷ 1.685058 = 0.593452.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 0.593452) ÷ 0.11 = 3.69589.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.69589 = Rs 332.63\nPV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.593452 = Rs 593.45\nPrice = 332.63 + 593.45 = **Rs 926.08** — a discount, as it must be when the 9% coupon is below the 11% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 818.18** - wrong; this is C ÷ y (90 ÷ 0.11), the price of a *perpetual* bond. This one redeems in 5 years.\n- **B) Rs 900.00** - wrong; a crude straight-line adjustment of 2% × 5 years off par, with no discounting.\n- **D) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; par would apply only if the required yield equalled the 9% coupon rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q132.** If the required yield falls to 9.5%, what capital gain does Rohan make on one bond?\nA) Rs 12.94  B) Rs 54.72  C) Rs 73.92  D) Rs 980.80\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 54.72**\n\nAt y = 9.5%: (1.095)^5 = 1.574239, so the discount factor is 0.635228.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 0.635228) ÷ 0.095 = 3.839705.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.839705 = Rs 345.57; PV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.635228 = Rs 635.23.\nNew price = 345.57 + 635.23 = Rs 980.80.\nGain = 980.80 − 926.08 = **Rs 54.72**. Price and yield move in opposite directions.\n\n- **A) Rs 12.94** - wrong; this re-discounts only the coupons at the new yield and leaves the redemption value discounted at 11%.\n- **C) Rs 73.92** - wrong; this is the gap between the old price and par (1,000 − 926.08). The bond still trades below par because 9.5% remains above the 9% coupon.\n- **D) Rs 980.80** - wrong; this is the new *price*, not the gain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q133.** If yields rise by 60 basis points, approximately how much does Rohan's Rs 18,50,000 fixed income book lose?\nA) Rs 11,100  B) Rs 42,100  C) Rs 46,731  D) Rs 4,21,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 42,100**\n\nModified duration = Macaulay duration ÷ (1 + y) = 4.21 ÷ 1.11 = 3.7928.\n% price change = −Modified duration × Δy = −3.7928 × 0.0060 = −2.2757%.\nRupee loss = 2.2757% × 18,50,000 = **Rs 42,100**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,100** - wrong; this applies the 0.60% yield change directly to the portfolio value, with no duration multiplier at all.\n- **C) Rs 46,731** - wrong; this uses the Macaulay duration of 4.21 instead of converting it to modified duration.\n- **D) Rs 4,21,000** - wrong; this uses 6% rather than 0.60% as the yield change — a factor-of-ten error.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q134.** Rohan sells one lot of the three-month Nifty futures at fair value. What is his profit when the index settles at 24,150?\nA) Rs 11,250  B) Rs 19,675  C) Rs 21,750  D) Rs 45,700\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 19,675**\n\nFair value: F = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 24,600 × (1 + 0.07 − 0.014)^0.25 = 24,600 × (1.056)^0.25\n= 24,600 × 1.013715 = **24,937** (to the nearest point).\nHe is short, so profit = (Sell price − Settlement) × Lot size = (24,937 − 24,150) × 25 = 787 × 25 = **Rs 19,675**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,250** - wrong; this shorts at the spot of 24,600 instead of at the futures fair value.\n- **C) Rs 21,750** - wrong; this ignores the 1.4% dividend yield, giving a fair value of about 25,020.\n- **D) Rs 45,700** - wrong; this applies the full annual carry of 5.6% instead of raising it to the power of one quarter, giving about 25,978.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q135.** For the long put position, what is the break-even index level and Rohan's net profit at a settlement of 24,150?\nA) 24,535 and a profit of Rs 9,625  B) 25,065 and a profit of Rs 16,250  C) 24,535 and a profit of Rs 16,250  D) 24,800 and a profit of Rs 9,625\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 24,535 and a profit of Rs 9,625**\n\nBreak-even for a long put = Strike − Premium = 24,800 − 265 = **24,535**.\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(Strike − Spot, 0) = 24,800 − 24,150 = Rs 650 per unit.\nNet per unit = 650 − 265 = Rs 385. Net profit = 385 × 25 = **Rs 9,625**.\n\n- **B) 25,065 and a profit of Rs 16,250** - wrong; 25,065 adds the premium to the strike, which is the *call* break-even rule, and Rs 16,250 is the gross intrinsic value (650 × 25) before recovering the premium.\n- **C) 24,535 and a profit of Rs 16,250** - wrong; the break-even is right but the profit ignores the Rs 265 premium already paid.\n- **D) 24,800 and a profit of Rs 9,625** - wrong; the profit is right but 24,800 is the strike, not the break-even. A put that is merely in the money is not yet profitable.\n\n</details>\n</content>\n</invoke>\n"}, {"num": "02", "md": "# Mock Paper 2 — NISM Series X-A (Investment Adviser Level 1)\n\n**Total: 150 marks · 135 questions · Time: 3 hours · Pass: 90/150 (60%) · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n## Section A — Independent MCQs (90 questions × 1 mark = 90 marks)\n\n**Q1.** In the six-step financial planning process, which step immediately follows risk profiling and asset allocation?\nA) Gathering data on the client's financial situation  B) Defining client needs and financial goals  C) Portfolio construction  D) Reviewing and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Portfolio construction**\n\nThe prescribed order is: (1) establish the client–planner relationship, (2) gather data on the client's financial situation, (3) define needs and goals, (4) risk profiling and asset allocation, (5) **portfolio construction**, (6) reviewing and rebalancing. Step 5 therefore follows step 4.\n\n- **A) Gathering data on the client's financial situation** - wrong; that is step 2, which precedes goal-setting and risk profiling.\n- **B) Defining client needs and financial goals** - wrong; that is step 3, immediately *before* risk profiling.\n- **D) Reviewing and rebalancing** - wrong; that is step 6, the last step, which comes only after the portfolio has been built.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Rohan Deshmukh is 34, works in a stable Mumbai job, has a 25-year horizon for his retirement goal and a healthy net worth — yet he redeemed his entire equity holding during a 12% market fall last year. How should his adviser set the equity allocation?\nA) To the lower of his risk capacity and his risk tolerance  B) To his high risk capacity, since the horizon is long  C) To the higher of his risk capacity and his risk tolerance  D) To the return the goal requires, ignoring both\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) To the lower of his risk capacity and his risk tolerance**\n\nRisk **capacity** is objective (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants); risk **tolerance** is subjective psychological comfort. Where the two conflict, the plan must be built to the **lower** of them. Rohan's capacity is high but his demonstrated tolerance is low — allocating to capacity would invite another panic sale that converts a paper loss into a permanent one.\n\n- **B) To his high risk capacity, since the horizon is long** - wrong; ignoring demonstrated behaviour is exactly what causes a client to abandon the plan at the worst moment.\n- **C) To the higher of his risk capacity and his risk tolerance** - wrong; this reverses the rule and maximises the chance of a behavioural failure.\n- **D) To the return the goal requires, ignoring both** - wrong; risk requirement is a third input, but it can never override the client's ability and willingness to bear risk.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** The Deshmukh household's assets are: savings balance Rs 3,10,000; liquid fund Rs 1,90,000; mutual funds Rs 22,00,000; EPF Rs 16,50,000; Mumbai flat Rs 95,00,000; car Rs 7,40,000. Liabilities are: home loan outstanding Rs 52,00,000; car loan Rs 3,60,000; credit card outstanding Rs 1,40,000. What is their net worth?\nA) Rs 57,00,000  B) Rs 81,50,000  C) Rs 1,45,90,000  D) Rs 88,90,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 88,90,000**\n\nTotal assets = 3,10,000 + 1,90,000 + 22,00,000 + 16,50,000 + 95,00,000 + 7,40,000 = **Rs 1,45,90,000**. Total liabilities = 52,00,000 + 3,60,000 + 1,40,000 = **Rs 57,00,000**. Net worth = 1,45,90,000 − 57,00,000 = **Rs 88,90,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 57,00,000** - wrong; this is total liabilities, not net worth.\n- **B) Rs 81,50,000** - wrong; this drops the car from the asset side. Personal-use assets still sit on the personal balance sheet at market value.\n- **C) Rs 1,45,90,000** - wrong; this is total assets before deducting any liabilities.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Which obligation most sharply distinguishes a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser from a commission-earning product distributor?\nA) The adviser must hold a professional qualification  B) The adviser must place the client's interest ahead of their own and keep advice segregated from distribution  C) The adviser must guarantee that recommended products beat their benchmark  D) The adviser must recommend only mutual funds and government securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The adviser must place the client's interest ahead of their own and keep advice segregated from distribution**\n\nThe defining feature is the **fiduciary** standard: the client's interest comes first. SEBI reinforces it with the **segregation of advice and distribution** — an individual Investment Adviser cannot also earn distribution commission on the same client's business.\n\n- **A) The adviser must hold a professional qualification** - wrong; qualification and certification requirements exist, but distributors also face eligibility norms. Qualification is not the distinguishing *duty*.\n- **C) The adviser must guarantee that recommended products beat their benchmark** - wrong; no adviser may guarantee returns. Advice is judged on process and suitability, not on outperformance.\n- **D) The adviser must recommend only mutual funds and government securities** - wrong; there is no such product restriction on advice.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Meghna Deshmukh finds that her elder child's college fee, a planned car replacement and a family trip to Europe all fall due in the same calendar year, making the funding requirement unaffordable. Which goal-setting principle should the adviser apply?\nA) Stagger the timing of goals so funding requirements do not collide  B) Prioritise goals by dropping the least expensive one  C) Merge all three goals into a single lump-sum goal  D) Fund all three from one equity SIP to average the cost\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Stagger the timing of goals so funding requirements do not collide**\n\nThe three goal rules are **prioritise**, **ensure adequate focus on important goals**, and **stagger the timing**. Here the problem is purely clustering: moving a discretionary goal (the holiday or the car) forward or back smooths the funding requirement without abandoning anything.\n\n- **B) Prioritise goals by dropping the least expensive one** - wrong; prioritisation ranks goals by importance, not by cost, and cheapness is no reason to drop a goal.\n- **C) Merge all three goals into a single lump-sum goal** - wrong; merging hides the different horizons and risk profiles each goal needs.\n- **D) Fund all three from one equity SIP to average the cost** - wrong; a near-dated goal such as next year's fee must not be funded with equity, whatever the averaging.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Kabir Sheth invests Rs 6,50,000 today in an instrument compounding at 9.5% p.a. annually. What will it be worth after 12 years?\nA) Rs 12,81,447  B) Rs 13,91,000  C) Rs 19,31,447  D) Rs 21,45,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 19,31,447**\n\nFV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ = 6,50,000 × (1.095)¹². Since (1.095)¹² = 2.97146, FV = 6,50,000 × 2.97146 = **Rs 19,31,447**.\n\n- **A) Rs 12,81,447** - wrong; this is only the *interest* component (19,31,447 − 6,50,000), not the maturity value.\n- **B) Rs 13,91,000** - wrong; this applies **simple** interest: 6,50,000 × (1 + 0.095 × 12) = 6,50,000 × 2.14.\n- **D) Rs 21,45,000** - wrong; this assumes a crude 11% flat growth and does not follow from the compounding formula.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A portfolio Meghna started with Rs 7,50,000 is worth Rs 21,00,000 after 9 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 8.00%  B) 12.12%  C) 14.44%  D) 20.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 12.12%**\n\nCAGR = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (21,00,000 ÷ 7,50,000)^(1/9) − 1 = (2.8)^0.1111 − 1 = 1.12120 − 1 = **12.12% p.a.**\n\n- **A) 8.00%** - wrong; at 8% the corpus would reach only about Rs 14.99 lakh in 9 years.\n- **C) 14.44%** - wrong; this overstates the rate — 14.44% would produce roughly Rs 25.4 lakh.\n- **D) 20.00%** - wrong; this is simple-interest thinking: total gain 13,50,000 ÷ 7,50,000 = 180%, divided by 9 years. It ignores compounding entirely.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** The Deshmukhs start a SIP of Rs 18,000 at the end of every month for 14 years, expecting 12% p.a. compounded monthly. What corpus do they accumulate?\nA) Rs 30,24,000  B) Rs 47,53,746  C) Rs 78,55,523  D) Rs 77,77,746\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 77,77,746**\n\nr = 0.12 ÷ 12 = 0.01; n = 14 × 12 = 168; (1.01)¹⁶⁸ = 5.32097.\nFV = 18,000 × [(5.32097 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 18,000 × 432.097 = **Rs 77,77,746**.\n\n- **A) Rs 30,24,000** - wrong; this is the total *contributed* (18,000 × 168), ignoring all returns.\n- **B) Rs 47,53,746** - wrong; this is only the return component (77,77,746 − 30,24,000), not the corpus.\n- **C) Rs 78,55,523** - wrong; this treats the SIP as an annuity **due** (× 1.01), but the question states payments at the end of each month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Nandita Rane wants Rs 1.40 crore in 18 years for retirement, expecting 10.8% p.a. compounded monthly. What investment at the end of each month is required?\nA) Rs 19,138  B) Rs 25,486  C) Rs 21,262  D) Rs 64,815\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 21,262**\n\nr = 0.108 ÷ 12 = 0.009; n = 216; (1.009)²¹⁶ = 6.92512.\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] = (1,40,00,000 × 0.009) ÷ 5.92512 = 1,26,000 ÷ 5.92512 = **Rs 21,262 a month**.\n\n- **A) Rs 19,138** - wrong; this understates the requirement and would leave the corpus roughly Rs 14 lakh short.\n- **B) Rs 25,486** - wrong; this overstates the requirement, overshooting the target by about Rs 25 lakh.\n- **D) Rs 64,815** - wrong; this is simply 1,40,00,000 ÷ 216 — the target divided by the number of months, assuming no return at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** An investment is expected to deliver a nominal 10.75% p.a. while inflation runs at 6.25% p.a. What is the real rate of return?\nA) 4.24%  B) 4.06%  C) 4.50%  D) 17.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 4.24%**\n\nReal return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.1075 ÷ 1.0625) − 1 = 1.042353 − 1 = **4.24%**.\n\n- **B) 4.06%** - wrong; this mis-applies the deflator and understates the real return.\n- **C) 4.50%** - wrong; this is the crude subtraction 10.75 − 6.25, which always **overstates** the real return.\n- **D) 17.00%** - wrong; this adds the two rates instead of deflating one by the other.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A bank quotes a nominal 9.6% p.a. on a deposit, compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.60%  B) 9.83%  C) 10.03%  D) 9.95%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 9.95%**\n\nEAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1 = (1 + 0.096/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.024)⁴ − 1 = 1.09951 − 1 = **9.95%**.\n\n- **A) 9.60%** - wrong; that is the **nominal** rate. The nominal rate never changes with frequency — only the effective rate rises.\n- **B) 9.83%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **half-yearly** compounding: (1.048)² − 1.\n- **C) 10.03%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **monthly** compounding: (1.008)¹² − 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Vikram Chitale invests Rs 25,000 at the **beginning** of every month for 10 years, earning 12% p.a. compounded monthly. What corpus does he accumulate?\nA) Rs 30,00,000  B) Rs 58,08,477  C) Rs 56,94,027  D) Rs 57,50,967\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 58,08,477**\n\nr = 0.01; n = 120; (1.01)¹²⁰ = 3.30039.\nOrdinary annuity FV = 25,000 × [(3.30039 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 25,000 × 230.0387 = Rs 57,50,967.\nPayments at the **beginning** make this an annuity **due**: 57,50,967 × 1.01 = **Rs 58,08,477**.\n\n- **A) Rs 30,00,000** - wrong; this is the total contributed (25,000 × 120), ignoring returns.\n- **C) Rs 56,94,027** - wrong; this *divides* by 1.01 instead of multiplying, moving the value the wrong way.\n- **D) Rs 57,50,967** - wrong; this is the **ordinary** annuity value. An annuity due is always worth more because each payment earns one extra month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** The Deshmukhs' combined monthly income is Rs 2,85,000 and their total monthly expenses, including EMIs, are Rs 1,96,650. What is their savings ratio?\nA) 31.00%  B) 22.25%  C) 44.93%  D) 69.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 31.00%**\n\nSavings = 2,85,000 − 1,96,650 = Rs 88,350. Savings ratio = 88,350 ÷ 2,85,000 = **31.00%**. Cross-check: the expenses ratio is 1,96,650 ÷ 2,85,000 = 69.00%, and 31 + 69 = 100 ✓.\n\n- **B) 22.25%** - wrong; no combination of the given figures produces this.\n- **C) 44.93%** - wrong; this divides savings by **expenses** (88,350 ÷ 1,96,650) instead of by income.\n- **D) 69.00%** - wrong; that is the **expenses** ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Meghna holds a savings balance of Rs 2,80,000, a liquid fund of Rs 3,95,000, equity mutual funds of Rs 14,00,000 and PPF of Rs 8,60,000. Household expenses are Rs 1,12,500 a month. What is the liquidity ratio?\nA) 0.50 months  B) 18.44 months  C) 6.00 months  D) 26.09 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 6.00 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings 2,80,000 + liquid fund 3,95,000 = **Rs 6,75,000**. Equity funds and PPF do **not** qualify — one is volatile, the other locked in. Liquidity ratio = 6,75,000 ÷ 1,12,500 = **6.00 months**, at the top of the healthy 3–6 month band.\n\n- **A) 0.50 months** - wrong; this divides by **annual** expenses (Rs 13,50,000) instead of monthly.\n- **B) 18.44 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the equity mutual funds as liquid assets.\n- **D) 26.09 months** - wrong; this counts every asset listed, including locked-in PPF, as liquid.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** A household has total assets of Rs 1,62,00,000 and total liabilities of Rs 58,32,000. What is its solvency ratio?\nA) 0.28  B) 0.64  C) 0.36  D) 1.56\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.64**\n\nNet worth = 1,62,00,000 − 58,32,000 = Rs 1,03,68,000. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 1,03,68,000 ÷ 1,62,00,000 = **0.64**. Cross-check: leverage ratio = 58,32,000 ÷ 1,62,00,000 = 0.36, and 0.64 + 0.36 = 1 ✓.\n\n- **A) 0.28** - wrong; this is the difference between the two ratios, not either of them.\n- **C) 0.36** - wrong; that is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency.\n- **D) 1.56** - wrong; this inverts the ratio by dividing total assets by net worth.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which statement about a household contingency fund is correct?\nA) It should be sized at 3–6 months of gross income  B) It should be invested in equity mutual funds so it beats inflation  C) It becomes unnecessary once adequate health insurance is in place  D) It should be sized at 3–6 months of expenses and held in cash-like assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) It should be sized at 3–6 months of expenses and held in cash-like assets**\n\nThe fund exists to meet obligations during a job loss or emergency, so it is sized against **expenses** — what must actually be paid — and parked where **immediate availability and certainty of value** are assured: savings account, sweep-in FD or liquid/overnight funds. Return is secondary.\n\n- **A) It should be sized at 3–6 months of gross income** - wrong; income is not the benchmark. A high earner with modest expenses would over-provision, and the reverse case would under-provision.\n- **B) It should be invested in equity mutual funds so it beats inflation** - wrong; the moment the fund is needed (recession, job loss) is precisely when equity is likely to be down.\n- **C) It becomes unnecessary once adequate health insurance is in place** - wrong; insurance and the emergency fund are complements. Insurance does not cover job loss, deductibles or cash-flow gaps.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** A Mumbai couple's monthly budget balances every month yet collapses in March and October each year. Which category of outflow has most likely been omitted?\nA) Fixed committed outflows  B) Variable discretionary outflows  C) Periodic lumpy outflows  D) Savings and investment outflows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Periodic lumpy outflows**\n\nAnnual insurance premiums, school admission fees, festival spending, vacations and vehicle servicing are **periodic** — predictable but infrequent. They are the most commonly forgotten category, and their absence is exactly what makes an otherwise balanced budget fail in specific months. The fix is to divide each annual item by 12 and set that amount aside monthly.\n\n- **A) Fixed committed outflows** - wrong; EMIs, rent and school fees recur every month and are the hardest items to overlook.\n- **B) Variable discretionary outflows** - wrong; these are often underestimated but they occur every month, so they would not cause a failure concentrated in two specific months.\n- **D) Savings and investment outflows** - wrong; omitting SIPs would make the budget look *better*, not create a shortfall.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** The Deshmukhs take a home loan of Rs 62,00,000 at 8.75% p.a. for 22 years. What is the EMI?\nA) Rs 52,993  B) Rs 23,485  C) Rs 45,208  D) Rs 58,142\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 52,993**\n\nr = 0.0875 ÷ 12 = 0.00729167; n = 22 × 12 = 264; (1.00729167)²⁶⁴ = 6.83303.\nEMI = 62,00,000 × (0.00729167 × 6.83303) ÷ (6.83303 − 1) = 62,00,000 × 0.0498242 ÷ 5.83303 = **Rs 52,993**.\n\n- **B) Rs 23,485** - wrong; this is principal ÷ number of months (62,00,000 ÷ 264), which charges no interest at all.\n- **C) Rs 45,208** - wrong; this is the **first month's interest** (62,00,000 × 0.00729167), the classic near-miss distractor.\n- **D) Rs 58,142** - wrong; this overstates the EMI and does not follow from the formula.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** On the same Rs 62,00,000 loan at 8.75% for 22 years with an EMI of Rs 52,993, how much of the **first** EMI reduces the principal?\nA) Rs 23,485  B) Rs 45,208  C) Rs 52,993  D) Rs 7,785\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 7,785**\n\nInterest for month 1 = outstanding × monthly rate = 62,00,000 × 0.00729167 = Rs 45,208. Principal repaid = EMI − interest = 52,993 − 45,208 = **Rs 7,785**. Only about 15% of the first instalment touches the debt, which is why early prepayment saves so much interest.\n\n- **A) Rs 23,485** - wrong; this is principal ÷ 264, an even principal instalment that an amortising loan does not follow.\n- **B) Rs 45,208** - wrong; that is the **interest** portion, not the principal portion.\n- **C) Rs 52,993** - wrong; that is the whole EMI, most of which is interest in month 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Farida Contractor takes a car loan of Rs 14,00,000 at 9.6% p.a. for 6 years, with an EMI of Rs 25,655. What is the total interest paid over the full tenure?\nA) Rs 3,36,000  B) Rs 4,47,160  C) Rs 8,06,400  D) Rs 18,47,160\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,47,160**\n\nTotal repaid = EMI × n = 25,655 × 72 = Rs 18,47,160. Total interest = 18,47,160 − 14,00,000 = **Rs 4,47,160**.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,36,000** - wrong; this assumes a 4% flat charge and has no basis in the schedule.\n- **C) Rs 8,06,400** - wrong; this applies the rate to the **full** principal for all six years (14,00,000 × 9.6% × 6), ignoring that the balance amortises down every month.\n- **D) Rs 18,47,160** - wrong; this is the **total repaid**, which includes the principal. The question asks for interest only.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Devendra Nayak pledges his family gold with a bank to raise a loan, and the bank keeps the gold in its vault. What is the correct legal description of this security?\nA) Pledge  B) Mortgage  C) Hypothecation  D) Assignment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Pledge**\n\nA **pledge** is security over **movable** property where **possession passes to the lender**. A gold loan is the standard Indian example.\n\n- **B) Mortgage** - wrong; a mortgage is security over **immovable** property, such as the home securing a home loan.\n- **C) Hypothecation** - wrong; hypothecation is security over movable property where **possession stays with the borrower** — a car loan is the standard example.\n- **D) Assignment** - wrong; assignment transfers rights under a contract, as in a loan against an insurance policy.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A borrower carries a Rs 40,000 credit card balance at 42% p.a., a Rs 3,20,000 personal loan at 15% p.a. and a Rs 9,00,000 vehicle loan at 10% p.a. Which repayment strategy minimises the **total interest** paid?\nA) Snowball — repay the smallest balance first  B) Blizzard — clear two small balances, then attack by rate  C) Avalanche — repay the highest interest rate first  D) Pro-rata — pay extra on all three in proportion to their balances\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Avalanche — repay the highest interest rate first**\n\nThe **avalanche** method directs every spare rupee at the costliest debt, here the 42% card, while paying minimums elsewhere. It is **mathematically optimal** and always minimises total interest.\n\n- **A) Snowball — repay the smallest balance first** - wrong; the snowball is psychologically motivating but costs more in total interest because the rate is ignored.\n- **B) Blizzard — clear two small balances, then attack by rate** - wrong; the blizzard is a compromise between motivation and mathematics, so it costs more than a pure avalanche.\n- **D) Pro-rata — pay extra on all three in proportion to their balances** - wrong; spreading the surplus leaves the highest-rate balance outstanding longer, raising total interest.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following intermediaries is regulated by the **RBI** rather than by SEBI?\nA) Portfolio managers  B) Account Aggregators  C) Alternative Investment Funds  D) Credit rating agencies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Account Aggregators**\n\nAccount Aggregators are registered as **NBFC-AA** with the **RBI**. With the customer's explicit consent they retrieve and share financial data across institutions, and they are \"data blind\" — they cannot read or store the data.\n\n- **A) Portfolio managers** - wrong; regulated by SEBI under the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020.\n- **C) Alternative Investment Funds** - wrong; regulated by SEBI under the SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012.\n- **D) Credit rating agencies** - wrong; credit rating agencies are SEBI-regulated. It is **credit information companies** such as CIBIL and Experian that sit with the RBI.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which market infrastructure institution acts as the **central counterparty**, guaranteeing that a trade settles even if one member defaults?\nA) The stock exchange  B) The depository  C) The depository participant  D) The clearing corporation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) The clearing corporation**\n\nThe **clearing corporation** interposes itself between buyer and seller as the **central counterparty (CCP)**, manages margins and operates the settlement guarantee fund, so no participant bears the credit risk of the other side.\n\n- **A) The stock exchange** - wrong; the exchange provides the platform and **matches** trades. It does not guarantee settlement.\n- **B) The depository** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL hold securities in electronic form and do not guarantee trades.\n- **C) The depository participant** - wrong; a DP is the depository's agent and the investor's access point for a demat account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which statement about payment banks in India is correct?\nA) They may lend only to priority sectors, up to Rs 2 lakh per borrower  B) They may accept unlimited deposits but cannot issue debit cards  C) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend  D) They are regulated by SEBI as securities market intermediaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend**\n\nPayment banks accept deposits capped at **Rs 2 lakh** per customer and provide payments and remittance services. They **cannot lend** and cannot issue credit cards.\n\n- **A) They may lend only to priority sectors, up to Rs 2 lakh per borrower** - wrong; that describes the orientation of **small finance banks**, which can lend. Payment banks cannot lend at all.\n- **B) They may accept unlimited deposits but cannot issue debit cards** - wrong; the deposit cap does exist and debit cards are permitted; it is credit cards that are not.\n- **D) They are regulated by SEBI as securities market intermediaries** - wrong; all banks, including payment banks, are regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** A treasury manager wants to park surplus cash for 60 days in an instrument belonging to a market segment regulated by the RBI. Which description fits?\nA) The money market, for instruments maturing up to one year  B) The capital market, for instruments maturing beyond one year  C) The primary market, where securities are issued for the first time  D) The secondary market, where investors trade among themselves\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) The money market, for instruments maturing up to one year**\n\nThe **money market** covers short-term debt of **up to one year** — T-bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit, call money and repos — and is regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n- **B) The capital market, for instruments maturing beyond one year** - wrong; the maturity does not fit a 60-day placement, and the capital market is SEBI's domain.\n- **C) The primary market, where securities are issued for the first time** - wrong; primary versus secondary is a different axis of classification and says nothing about maturity or regulator.\n- **D) The secondary market, where investors trade among themselves** - wrong; for the same reason — this describes where trading happens, not the maturity segment.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which three entities are classified as Market Infrastructure Institutions?\nA) Stock brokers, custodians and registrars  B) SEBI, the RBI and IRDAI  C) Mutual funds, portfolio managers and AIFs  D) Stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Stock exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories**\n\nThese three are systemically important to the functioning of the securities market and are therefore the most heavily regulated participants — they provide the trading platform, guarantee and settle trades, and hold securities in demat form.\n\n- **A) Stock brokers, custodians and registrars** - wrong; these are intermediaries, not infrastructure institutions.\n- **B) SEBI, the RBI and IRDAI** - wrong; these are **regulators**, not market infrastructure.\n- **C) Mutual funds, portfolio managers and AIFs** - wrong; these are managed-investment vehicles, not infrastructure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Purnima Iyer holds 750 shares of a company quoted at Rs 1,120. The company announces a bonus issue in the ratio 3:5. What is her holding and the theoretical ex-bonus price?\nA) 1,050 shares at Rs 800  B) 1,200 shares at Rs 700  C) 1,200 shares at Rs 1,120  D) 2,250 shares at Rs 373\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1,200 shares at Rs 700**\n\nA 3:5 bonus gives three new shares for every five held: 750 × 3/5 = 450 new shares, so the holding becomes **1,200 shares**. Total value is unchanged at 750 × 1,120 = Rs 8,40,000, so the price adjusts to 8,40,000 ÷ 1,200 = **Rs 700**.\n\n- **A) 1,050 shares at Rs 800** - wrong; this applies a 2:5 ratio, giving both the wrong share count and the wrong price.\n- **C) 1,200 shares at Rs 1,120** - wrong; the share count is right but the price cannot stay unchanged, since a bonus raises no money and creates no value.\n- **D) 2,250 shares at Rs 373** - wrong; this treats the ratio as three new shares for every one held.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** What is the key difference between a rights issue and a bonus issue?\nA) A rights issue raises fresh capital; a bonus issue does not  B) A bonus issue raises fresh capital; a rights issue does not  C) Both raise capital, but only the bonus issue is renounceable  D) Neither raises capital; both merely reclassify reserves\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A rights issue raises fresh capital; a bonus issue does not**\n\nA **rights issue** offers existing shareholders the chance to **buy** more shares pro rata at a discount, so money flows into the company, and the entitlement is **renounceable**. A **bonus issue** distributes free shares by **capitalising reserves** — no money is raised, and the shareholder's total value and percentage holding are unchanged.\n\n- **B) A bonus issue raises fresh capital; a rights issue does not** - wrong; this reverses the two.\n- **C) Both raise capital, but only the bonus issue is renounceable** - wrong on both counts: a bonus raises nothing, and it is the **rights** entitlement that is renounceable.\n- **D) Neither raises capital; both merely reclassify reserves** - wrong; a rights issue brings genuine new subscription money into the company.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** In a book-built public issue, an applicant bidding for Rs 3,20,000 worth of shares falls into which category, and what is the minimum allocation to that category?\nA) Retail Individual Investor; at least 35%  B) Qualified Institutional Buyer; up to 50%  C) Non-Institutional Investor; at least 15%  D) Anchor investor; at least 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Non-Institutional Investor; at least 15%**\n\nThe **Rs 2 lakh** line separates the categories: applications up to Rs 2 lakh are **Retail Individual Investors**, and above Rs 2 lakh are **Non-Institutional Investors**. At Rs 3,20,000 the applicant is an NII, and NIIs receive **at least 15%** of a book-built issue.\n\n- **A) Retail Individual Investor; at least 35%** - wrong; retail is capped at Rs 2 lakh per application. The 35% figure belongs to retail, but the category does not fit.\n- **B) Qualified Institutional Buyer; up to 50%** - wrong; QIBs are institutions — mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs — not individual applicants.\n- **D) Anchor investor; at least 15%** - wrong; anchor investors are QIBs allotted a day before the issue opens, carved out of the QIB portion.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Which statement about ASBA in Indian public issues is correct?\nA) The application money is debited immediately and refunded if no allotment is made  B) The money is blocked in the investor's own bank account and debited only to the extent of allotment  C) It is optional and available only to institutional applicants  D) The blocked amount stops earning interest until allotment is finalised\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The money is blocked in the investor's own bank account and debited only to the extent of allotment**\n\nUnder **Applications Supported by Blocked Amount**, funds stay in the applicant's account under a lien. Only the allotted portion is debited and the rest is simply unblocked, and the applicant **continues to earn interest** on the blocked amount throughout.\n\n- **A) The application money is debited immediately and refunded if no allotment is made** - wrong; that is the pre-ASBA process that ASBA replaced.\n- **C) It is optional and available only to institutional applicants** - wrong; ASBA is **mandatory** for public issues and available to all applicants.\n- **D) The blocked amount stops earning interest until allotment is finalised** - wrong; interest continues because the money never leaves the account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** What is the difference between a circuit breaker and a price band in the Indian secondary market?\nA) A circuit breaker is stock-specific; a price band is market-wide  B) Both are market-wide, but a circuit breaker applies only to derivatives  C) Both are stock-specific, but a price band applies only to newly listed stocks  D) A circuit breaker halts the whole market on index moves; a price band caps one scrip's daily movement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) A circuit breaker halts the whole market on index moves; a price band caps one scrip's daily movement**\n\n**Circuit breakers** are **market-wide** halts triggered by index moves of ±10%, ±15% or ±20%, with halt durations depending on the level and the time of day. **Price bands** are **stock-specific** daily limits (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%) beyond which an individual scrip cannot move.\n\n- **A) A circuit breaker is stock-specific; a price band is market-wide** - wrong; this reverses the two.\n- **B) Both are market-wide, but a circuit breaker applies only to derivatives** - wrong; price bands are stock-specific, and circuit breakers are not derivative-only.\n- **C) Both are stock-specific, but a price band applies only to newly listed stocks** - wrong; circuit breakers are market-wide, and price bands apply broadly, not only to new listings.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** A client with Rs 65 lakh to invest wants securities held in his **own** demat account rather than units of a pooled vehicle. Which route fits, and what is its regulatory minimum?\nA) Mutual fund; minimum Rs 500  B) Alternative Investment Fund; minimum Rs 1 crore  C) Portfolio Management Service; minimum Rs 50 lakh  D) Alternative Investment Fund; minimum Rs 25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Portfolio Management Service; minimum Rs 50 lakh**\n\nA **PMS** is not pooled — securities are held in the **client's own name and demat account** — and the SEBI minimum investment is **Rs 50 lakh**, which Rs 65 lakh clears.\n\n- **A) Mutual fund; minimum Rs 500** - wrong; the minimum is right but a mutual fund gives **units of a pooled scheme**, not securities in the client's own name.\n- **B) Alternative Investment Fund; minimum Rs 1 crore** - wrong; the minimum is correct for an AIF, but Rs 65 lakh falls short and an AIF also issues pooled units.\n- **D) Alternative Investment Fund; minimum Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh applies only to **employees or directors of the AIF or its manager**, not to outside investors.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** An issuer markets a \"100% capital protected\" structured product linked to the Nifty. What is the most important caveat an adviser must explain?\nA) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and normally applies only at maturity  B) The protection is guaranteed by SEBI up to Rs 5 lakh  C) The protection applies at every point during the tenure, including on early exit  D) The product carries no market risk because it holds only government securities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and normally applies only at maturity**\n\nA structured product is a **bond or deposit plus a derivative**. The \"capital protection\" comes from the bond leg, so it is only as good as the **issuer's credit**. It also applies at maturity — the product is typically **not** principal-protected on an early exit, and secondary market liquidity is poor.\n\n- **B) The protection is guaranteed by SEBI up to Rs 5 lakh** - wrong; SEBI guarantees nothing. The Rs 5 lakh figure is DICGC **bank deposit** insurance, an unrelated scheme.\n- **C) The protection applies at every point during the tenure, including on early exit** - wrong; this is exactly the misconception the adviser must correct.\n- **D) The product carries no market risk because it holds only government securities** - wrong; the derivative leg supplies the equity-linked upside and carries market risk, and the bond leg need not be sovereign.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A corporate bond is downgraded from BBB− to BB+. What is the significance of this particular step?\nA) It moves from AAA to AA, a routine one-notch change with no consequence  B) It crosses from high yield into investment grade, widening the buyer base  C) It has no rating significance because both notches are within investment grade  D) It crosses from investment grade into high yield, potentially forcing mandate-driven selling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) It crosses from investment grade into high yield, potentially forcing mandate-driven selling**\n\n**BBB− (Baa3) is the dividing line.** A downgrade below it makes the bond sub-investment grade, or \"high yield\". Many institutional mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings, so the downgrade can trigger forced selling and a sharp price fall — the \"fallen angel\" effect.\n\n- **A) It moves from AAA to AA, a routine one-notch change with no consequence** - wrong; the ratings named are BBB− and BB+, nowhere near the AAA band.\n- **B) It crosses from high yield into investment grade, widening the buyer base** - wrong; this reverses the direction of the downgrade.\n- **C) It has no rating significance because both notches are within investment grade** - wrong; BB+ is **below** investment grade.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A company reports a net profit of Rs 810 crore on 18 crore shares outstanding, and its share trades at Rs 1,845. What is its P/E ratio?\nA) 2.44  B) 41.00  C) 24.40  D) 45.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 41.00**\n\nEPS = 810 crore ÷ 18 crore shares = **Rs 45**. P/E = Price ÷ EPS = 1,845 ÷ 45 = **41.00**.\n\n- **A) 2.44** - wrong; that is the **earnings yield** (45 ÷ 1,845 = 2.44%), the inverse of the P/E expressed as a percentage.\n- **C) 24.40** - wrong; this is the earnings yield mis-scaled by a factor of ten.\n- **D) 45.00** - wrong; that is the **EPS in rupees**, not a ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A listed company has a market capitalisation of Rs 24,600 crore, total debt of Rs 5,400 crore and cash of Rs 2,150 crore. What is its enterprise value?\nA) Rs 27,850 crore  B) Rs 21,350 crore  C) Rs 24,600 crore  D) Rs 32,150 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 27,850 crore**\n\nEV = Market capitalisation + Debt − Cash = 24,600 + 5,400 − 2,150 = **Rs 27,850 crore**. It represents what an acquirer effectively pays for the whole business, taking on its debts and receiving its cash.\n\n- **B) Rs 21,350 crore** - wrong; this subtracts the debt and adds the cash, reversing both signs.\n- **C) Rs 24,600 crore** - wrong; that is market capitalisation alone, which ignores the capital structure entirely.\n- **D) Rs 32,150 crore** - wrong; this **adds** cash instead of subtracting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A stock trades at Rs 960 with an EPS of Rs 32, and analysts forecast earnings growth of 24% a year. What is its PEG ratio?\nA) 0.75  B) 0.80  C) 1.25  D) 7.20\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 1.25**\n\nP/E = 960 ÷ 32 = **30**. PEG = P/E ÷ growth rate (%) = 30 ÷ 24 = **1.25**. A PEG above 1 suggests the price is not fully justified by the forecast growth; below 1 is often read as attractive.\n\n- **A) 0.75** - wrong; this uses an incorrect P/E of 18.\n- **B) 0.80** - wrong; this inverts the ratio (24 ÷ 30), which is growth per unit of P/E, not PEG.\n- **D) 7.20** - wrong; this mixes rupee and percentage terms and does not follow the formula.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Which statement correctly captures the relationship between fundamental and technical analysis?\nA) Technical analysis estimates intrinsic value; fundamental analysis studies price and volume  B) Fundamental analysis tells you what to buy; technical analysis tells you when  C) Both rely on discounted cash flow models, differing only in the discount rate  D) Technical analysis is valid only for bonds and fundamental analysis only for equity\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Fundamental analysis tells you what to buy; technical analysis tells you when**\n\nFundamental analysis estimates **intrinsic value** from earnings, assets, industry and management, answering **what** to own. Technical analysis studies **price and volume** history and market psychology, answering **when** to enter or exit. Many practitioners use both together.\n\n- **A) Technical analysis estimates intrinsic value; fundamental analysis studies price and volume** - wrong; this reverses the two definitions.\n- **C) Both rely on discounted cash flow models, differing only in the discount rate** - wrong; technical analysis ignores cash flows and intrinsic value entirely.\n- **D) Technical analysis is valid only for bonds and fundamental analysis only for equity** - wrong; technical analysis can be applied to any traded instrument, including bond yields and interest rate futures.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 pays a 9% annual coupon and matures in 4 years. If the required yield is 7%, what is its price?\nA) Rs 933.68  B) Rs 1,000.00  C) Rs 1,360.00  D) Rs 1,067.74\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 1,067.74**\n\nCoupon = Rs 90 a year. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.07)⁻⁴] ÷ 0.07 = (1 − 0.762895) ÷ 0.07 = 3.38721.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.38721 = Rs 304.85. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.07)⁴ = Rs 762.90.\nPrice = 304.85 + 762.90 = **Rs 1,067.74** — a premium, because the 9% coupon exceeds the 7% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 933.68** - wrong; a discount price arises only when the coupon is **below** the required yield, which is the reverse of this case.\n- **B) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond trades at par only when the coupon equals the required yield.\n- **C) Rs 1,360.00** - wrong; this adds the undiscounted coupons (4 × 90) to face value, discounting nothing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 carries a coupon of 7.4% and trades in the market at Rs 928. What is its current yield?\nA) 6.87%  B) 7.40%  C) 7.97%  D) 8.51%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 7.97%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ Market price = 74 ÷ 928 = **7.97%**. The coupon is always computed on **face value** (7.4% × 1,000 = Rs 74), never on the market price.\n\n- **A) 6.87%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by a price above face value instead of the Rs 928 given.\n- **B) 7.40%** - wrong; that is the **coupon rate**, fixed on face value, which ignores the price actually paid.\n- **D) 8.51%** - wrong; this overstates the yield and does not follow from dividing 74 by 928.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** A bond portfolio of Rs 26,00,000 has a modified duration of 5.8. If yields fall by 40 basis points, what is the approximate change in value?\nA) A gain of about Rs 60,320  B) A gain of about Rs 10,400  C) A loss of about Rs 60,320  D) A gain of about Rs 1,50,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A gain of about Rs 60,320**\n\n%ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −5.8 × (−0.0040) = **+2.32%**. On Rs 26,00,000 that is 26,00,000 × 0.0232 = **Rs 60,320 gain**. Prices move **inversely** to yields, so a fall in yields produces a gain.\n\n- **B) A gain of about Rs 10,400** - wrong; this applies the 0.40% yield change directly to the portfolio and omits the duration multiplier.\n- **C) A loss of about Rs 60,320** - wrong; the magnitude is right but the sign is not — yields **fell**, so the portfolio gains.\n- **D) A gain of about Rs 1,50,800** - wrong; this applies 5.8% to the portfolio, treating duration as if it were the percentage price change itself.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Which statement about the Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme is correct?\nA) It is open from age 55 with no upper deposit limit and pays interest at maturity  B) It is open from age 60, capped at Rs 1.5 lakh a year, and is EEE  C) It has a 15-year tenor with interest compounded but paid only at maturity  D) It is open from age 60, capped at Rs 30 lakh, and pays interest quarterly\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) It is open from age 60, capped at Rs 30 lakh, and pays interest quarterly**\n\nSCSS has a **5-year** tenor extendable by 3 years, is open to those aged **60 and above** (or 55+ on superannuation), is capped at **Rs 30 lakh**, and pays interest **quarterly**. The deposit qualifies under section 80C but the interest is **taxable**.\n\n- **A) It is open from age 55 with no upper deposit limit and pays interest at maturity** - wrong; age 55 applies only on superannuation, the Rs 30 lakh cap exists, and interest is paid quarterly.\n- **B) It is open from age 60, capped at Rs 1.5 lakh a year, and is EEE** - wrong; the Rs 1.5 lakh annual cap and EEE status belong to **PPF** and the Sukanya Samriddhi Account. SCSS interest is taxable.\n- **C) It has a 15-year tenor with interest compounded but paid only at maturity** - wrong; 15 years is PPF's tenor, and interest compounded but paid at maturity describes the **NSC**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** A trader buys one index call option with a strike of 24,500 at a premium of Rs 310. The lot size is 75. At expiry the index closes at 25,060. What is the net profit or loss on the position?\nA) A profit of Rs 42,000  B) A profit of Rs 18,750  C) A profit of Rs 23,250  D) A loss of Rs 23,250\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A profit of Rs 18,750**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(Spot − Strike, 0) = 25,060 − 24,500 = **Rs 560** per unit. Net gain per unit = 560 − 310 premium = **Rs 250**. Total = 250 × 75 = **Rs 18,750 profit**. Break-even was 24,500 + 310 = 24,810, and the index closed above it.\n\n- **A) A profit of Rs 42,000** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value (560 × 75) and forgets to deduct the premium paid.\n- **C) A profit of Rs 23,250** - wrong; this is the premium outlay (310 × 75), which is a cost, not a profit.\n- **D) A loss of Rs 23,250** - wrong; the full premium would be lost only if the index expired at or below the 24,500 strike.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** A put option on a stock has a strike of Rs 1,480 and is bought for a premium of Rs 46. What is the break-even spot price at expiry for the buyer?\nA) Rs 1,434  B) Rs 1,388  C) Rs 1,480  D) Rs 1,526\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 1,434**\n\nFor a long put, break-even = **Strike − Premium** = 1,480 − 46 = **Rs 1,434**. Below this level the buyer profits; between Rs 1,434 and Rs 1,480 the option is in the money but still loss-making, because the premium has not yet been recovered.\n\n- **B) Rs 1,388** - wrong; this deducts the premium twice (1,480 − 92).\n- **C) Rs 1,480** - wrong; that is the strike. At the strike the option has zero intrinsic value and the buyer loses the entire premium.\n- **D) Rs 1,526** - wrong; **adding** the premium to the strike is the break-even rule for a **call**, not a put.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A stock trades at a spot price of Rs 2,840. The risk-free rate is 7.5% p.a. and the stock's dividend yield is 1.5% p.a. What is the fair value of a one-year futures contract?\nA) Rs 2,798.40  B) Rs 2,882.60  C) Rs 3,010.40  D) Rs 3,053.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 3,010.40**\n\nF = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 2,840 × (1 + 0.075 − 0.015)¹ = 2,840 × 1.06 = **Rs 3,010.40**. The futures price sits above spot (contango) because the financing cost of carrying the position exceeds the dividend income forgone.\n\n- **A) Rs 2,798.40** - wrong; this puts futures below spot, implying backwardation, which does not follow when r exceeds q.\n- **B) Rs 2,882.60** - wrong; this applies only the 1.5% dividend yield and ignores the financing cost.\n- **D) Rs 3,053.00** - wrong; this applies the full 7.5% risk-free rate without deducting the 1.5% dividend yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which statement correctly contrasts a forward contract with an exchange-traded futures contract?\nA) Forwards are standardised and margined daily; futures are customised and settled at maturity  B) Forwards are customised OTC contracts carrying counterparty risk; futures are standardised, CCP-guaranteed and marked to market daily  C) Both are guaranteed by the clearing corporation, but only forwards require margin  D) Both are exchange traded, but forwards can be used only for hedging\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Forwards are customised OTC contracts carrying counterparty risk; futures are standardised, CCP-guaranteed and marked to market daily**\n\nThe defining difference is that **futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily through mark-to-market**, while a **forward** is a bilateral OTC agreement — customisable in size and date, illiquid, settled only at maturity, and exposed to the risk that the counterparty fails to perform.\n\n- **A) Forwards are standardised and margined daily; futures are customised and settled at maturity** - wrong; this reverses every attribute.\n- **C) Both are guaranteed by the clearing corporation, but only forwards require margin** - wrong; forwards have no clearing corporation and usually no margin, while futures require both initial and mark-to-market margin.\n- **D) Both are exchange traded, but forwards can be used only for hedging** - wrong; forwards are OTC, and any derivative can be used for hedging, speculation or arbitrage.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** Devika holds 5,000 units of an equity scheme under the IDCW-reinvestment option. The scheme declares an IDCW of Rs 3.60 per unit when the cum-IDCW NAV is Rs 45.60. How many units will she hold after the reinvestment?\nA) 5,000.00  B) 5,365.85  C) 5,394.74  D) 5,428.57\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) 5,428.57**\n\nDistribution received = 5,000 x 3.60 = **Rs 18,000**. An IDCW payout reduces the NAV by exactly the amount distributed, so the ex-IDCW NAV = 45.60 - 3.60 = **Rs 42.00**. Reinvestment buys fresh units at the ex-NAV: 18,000 / 42.00 = **428.57 units**. Total holding = 5,000 + 428.57 = **5,428.57 units**.\n\n- **A) 5,000.00** - wrong; this is the IDCW *payout* option, where the cash leaves the folio and no fresh units are bought.\n- **B) 5,365.85** - wrong; this adds the IDCW to the NAV (18,000 / 49.20) instead of subtracting it.\n- **C) 5,394.74** - wrong; this uses the cum-IDCW NAV of Rs 45.60 (18,000 / 45.60), but units are always allotted at the ex-NAV.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Aditya invests Rs 8,00,000 for one year in a scheme whose portfolio earns a gross return of 12.60%. The regular plan has a TER of 1.95% and the direct plan a TER of 0.75%. By how much will the direct plan holding exceed the regular plan holding after one year?\nA) Rs 6,000  B) Rs 9,600  C) Rs 15,600  D) Rs 21,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 9,600**\n\nThe portfolio is identical; only the cost differs. Net return in the regular plan = 12.60 - 1.95 = **10.65%**, giving 8,00,000 x 1.1065 = **Rs 8,85,200**. Net return in the direct plan = 12.60 - 0.75 = **11.85%**, giving 8,00,000 x 1.1185 = **Rs 8,94,800**. Difference = **Rs 9,600**, which is simply the TER gap of 1.20% applied to Rs 8,00,000.\n\n- **A) Rs 6,000** - wrong; this is 0.75% of the investment, the direct plan's own TER rather than the difference between the two.\n- **C) Rs 15,600** - wrong; this is 1.95% of the investment, the regular plan's full TER rather than the gap.\n- **D) Rs 21,600** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios (2.70%) instead of subtracting one from the other.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Farhan submits an application to purchase units of a **liquid fund** at 12:40 p.m. on Thursday, and the money is credited to the scheme's bank account at 1:10 p.m. the same day. Assuming a normal business week, which NAV will he receive?\nA) Wednesday's NAV  B) Thursday's NAV  C) Friday's NAV  D) The NAV of the day the units are physically allotted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Wednesday's NAV**\n\nFor purchases in **liquid and overnight funds** the cut-off is **1:30 p.m.**, and where the application is submitted and the funds are realised before that cut-off, the investor gets the **previous business day's NAV**. Both events happened before 1:30 p.m. on Thursday, so Wednesday's NAV applies.\n\n- **B) Thursday's NAV** - wrong; same-day NAV is the rule for non-liquid schemes with a 3:00 p.m. cut-off, not for liquid funds.\n- **C) Friday's NAV** - wrong; a next-day NAV would arise only if the funds were realised after the cut-off.\n- **D) The NAV of the day the units are physically allotted** - wrong; the applicable NAV is fixed by the cut-off and realisation rules, not by the allotment processing date.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** A debt scheme is mandated to invest at least 65% of its assets in instruments rated **below AA+**. Under SEBI's scheme categorisation this is a:\nA) Corporate bond fund  B) Credit risk fund  C) Banking and PSU fund  D) Gilt fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Credit risk fund**\n\nA **credit risk fund** must invest **at least 65% in instruments rated below AA+**. It accepts higher credit risk in exchange for a higher yield, and is the debt category most exposed to default and downgrade.\n\n- **A) Corporate bond fund** - wrong; that category invests at least 80% in the **highest-rated** paper, AA+ and above.\n- **C) Banking and PSU fund** - wrong; that category invests at least 80% in the debt of banks, PSUs and public financial institutions, defined by issuer type rather than by rating.\n- **D) Gilt fund** - wrong; that category invests at least 80% in government securities, which carry no credit risk at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Which statement about the structure of a mutual fund in India is correct?\nA) The scheme's assets are owned by the AMC and are available to the AMC's creditors if it fails  B) The trustees hold the scheme's assets in trust for the unit-holders, and at least two-thirds of the trustees must be independent  C) The sponsor must contribute at least 40% of the corpus of every scheme launched  D) The custodian appoints the AMC and supervises its investment decisions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The trustees hold the scheme's assets in trust for the unit-holders, and at least two-thirds of the trustees must be independent**\n\nThe three-tier structure runs **Sponsor to Trust/Trustees to AMC**. The scheme's assets belong to the **unit-holders through the trust**, and SEBI requires **at least two-thirds of the trustees to be independent** so that the oversight of the AMC is genuine.\n\n- **A) The scheme's assets are owned by the AMC and are available to the AMC's creditors if it fails** - wrong; this is precisely what the trust structure prevents. Scheme assets are ring-fenced from the AMC.\n- **C) The sponsor must contribute at least 40% of the corpus of every scheme launched** - wrong; the 40% requirement relates to the **net worth of the AMC**, not to any scheme's corpus.\n- **D) The custodian appoints the AMC and supervises its investment decisions** - wrong; the custodian only holds the securities. Oversight of the AMC belongs to the trustees.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Rohan and Meera Deshmukh, a dual-income Mumbai couple with two school-age children, receive a bonus of Rs 24,00,000 and are unwilling to commit it to equity in a single stroke. Which facility lets them park the money in a liquid fund and move a fixed sum into an equity fund of the same fund house every month?\nA) Systematic Investment Plan (SIP)  B) Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP)  C) Systematic Transfer Plan (STP)  D) Dividend Transfer Plan (DTP)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Systematic Transfer Plan (STP)**\n\nAn **STP** transfers a fixed amount at fixed intervals **from one scheme to another within the same fund house**, typically from debt to equity. It is the standard technique for deploying a lump sum gradually while the uninvested portion continues to earn a return.\n\n- **A) Systematic Investment Plan (SIP)** - wrong; an SIP invests fresh money from the bank account periodically. It is not designed to stage an existing lump sum already held in a scheme.\n- **B) Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP)** - wrong; an SWP takes money out of a scheme at fixed intervals, usually to create retirement income.\n- **D) Dividend Transfer Plan (DTP)** - wrong; a DTP moves only the **distributions** of one scheme into another, not the capital itself.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A discretionary PMS portfolio starts at Rs 3.00 crore. It ends Year 1 at Rs 3.75 crore, Year 2 at Rs 3.20 crore and Year 3 at Rs 4.05 crore. The performance fee is 15% of gains, charged on a high watermark basis. What performance fee is payable for Year 3?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 4,50,000  C) Rs 12,75,000  D) Rs 15,75,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,50,000**\n\nA performance fee was charged in Year 1, setting the **high watermark at Rs 3.75 crore**. Year 2 ended below that peak, so no fee arose and the watermark stayed at Rs 3.75 crore. In Year 3 the fee applies only to the gain **above the watermark**: 4.05 - 3.75 = **Rs 30 lakh**. Fee = 15% x 30,00,000 = **Rs 4,50,000**.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio finished Year 3 above the previous peak, so a fee is due on the excess.\n- **C) Rs 12,75,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 85 lakh rise from the Year 2 value, making the client pay again for recovering ground already lost.\n- **D) Rs 15,75,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 1.05 crore gain over the original Rs 3.00 crore, ignoring that a fee was already taken in Year 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Pranali's PMS portfolio of Rs 2,40,00,000 returns 16% gross for the year. The hurdle rate is 9% and the performance fee is 20% with no catch-up. Ignoring fixed fees and taxes, what is the portfolio's value after the performance fee?\nA) Rs 2,70,72,000  B) Rs 2,74,08,000  C) Rs 2,75,04,000  D) Rs 2,78,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,75,04,000**\n\nGross gain = 16% x 2,40,00,000 = **Rs 38,40,000**, taking the portfolio to Rs 2,78,40,000. With **no catch-up**, the fee applies only to the return **above the hurdle**: 16% - 9% = 7%, i.e. 7% x 2,40,00,000 = **Rs 16,80,000**. Fee = 20% x 16,80,000 = **Rs 3,36,000**. Value after the fee = 2,78,40,000 - 3,36,000 = **Rs 2,75,04,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 2,70,72,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the whole Rs 38,40,000 gain, ignoring the hurdle entirely.\n- **B) Rs 2,74,08,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the hurdle amount itself rather than on the excess above it.\n- **D) Rs 2,78,40,000** - wrong; this is the pre-fee value, and a fee is payable because the return exceeded the hurdle.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Under which type of portfolio management service does the portfolio manager **only recommend**, while the client both takes the decision and executes the transaction?\nA) Discretionary  B) Non-discretionary  C) Advisory  D) Custodial\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Advisory**\n\nThe clean test is *who pulls the trigger*. Under an **advisory** service the manager advises, and the **client decides and executes**. The manager never transacts, which is why a custodian is not mandatory for this service.\n\n- **A) Discretionary** - wrong; here the manager both decides and executes, with full authority delegated to him.\n- **B) Non-discretionary** - wrong; here the client decides but the **manager executes** each approved transaction.\n- **D) Custodial** - wrong; this is not a type of portfolio management service. A custodian merely holds securities.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** SEBI requires a portfolio manager to disclose its performance on which basis?\nA) Gross of fees, using the money-weighted rate of return  B) Net of all fees and expenses, using the time-weighted rate of return  C) Net of fees, using the XIRR of each individual client's own cash flows  D) On the best-performing investment approach, gross of fees\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Net of all fees and expenses, using the time-weighted rate of return**\n\nPerformance must be disclosed **net of all fees and expenses**, computed on a **time-weighted rate of return (TWRR)** basis, and shown for the firm's aggregate rather than for selected accounts. TWRR neutralises the effect of client-driven cash flows and therefore measures the **manager's skill**.\n\n- **A) Gross of fees, using the money-weighted rate of return** - wrong on both counts; gross figures overstate what the client actually earns, and MWRR reflects the client's cash-flow timing, not the manager's skill.\n- **C) Net of fees, using the XIRR of each individual client's own cash flows** - wrong; that is a money-weighted measure of one client's experience, not the standard for comparable disclosure.\n- **D) On the best-performing investment approach, gross of fees** - wrong; this is precisely the cherry-picking the disclosure rules exist to prevent.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A **Category II** AIF has a corpus of Rs 160 crore. What continuing interest must its sponsor or manager maintain in the fund?\nA) Rs 4 crore  B) Rs 5 crore  C) Rs 8 crore  D) Rs 10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 4 crore**\n\nFor **Category I and Category II** AIFs the continuing interest is the **lower of 2.5% of the corpus or Rs 5 crore**. Here 2.5% x 160 crore = **Rs 4 crore**, which is lower than Rs 5 crore, so the sponsor commits **Rs 4 crore**. This money must be real capital at risk and may not be met by waiving management fees.\n\n- **B) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the ceiling, and the rule takes the **lower** of the two figures.\n- **C) Rs 8 crore** - wrong; this applies the 5% rate, which belongs to Category III.\n- **D) Rs 10 crore** - wrong; Rs 10 crore is the Category III ceiling, not applicable to a Category II fund.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A SEBI-registered AIF invests exclusively in road, port and power-transmission project SPVs, uses no leverage and is close-ended with a seven-year tenure. Which category does it fall in?\nA) Category I  B) Category II  C) Category III  D) It is not an AIF at all\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Category I**\n\nAn **Infrastructure Fund** is a named sub-category of **Category I**, the bucket for funds investing in sectors the government or regulators consider socially or economically desirable. Category I funds are the only ones on which incentives or concessions may be granted.\n\n- **B) Category II** - wrong; Category II is the residual bucket for funds that are neither Category I nor Category III, such as private equity, debt and real estate funds.\n- **C) Category III** - wrong; Category III is defined by **complex or leveraged trading strategies**, which this fund does not employ.\n- **D) It is not an AIF at all** - wrong; a privately pooled vehicle established in India with a defined investment policy is squarely an AIF.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** An employee of the **Manager** of an AIF wishes to invest in one of its schemes. What is the minimum amount she must commit?\nA) Rs 10 lakh  B) Rs 25 lakh  C) Rs 50 lakh  D) Rs 1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 25 lakh**\n\nThe general minimum investment in an AIF is **Rs 1 crore**, but **employees and directors of the AIF or of its Manager** may invest a minimum of **Rs 25 lakh**. The Rs 1 crore filter exists to keep uninformed investors out, and insiders who already know the strategy and its risks are not uninformed.\n\n- **A) Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; Rs 10 crore is the minimum **corpus** of an angel fund scheme, and no Rs 10 lakh threshold exists here.\n- **C) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the minimum client investment in a **portfolio management service**, not in an AIF.\n- **D) Rs 1 crore** - wrong; that is the minimum for an ordinary investor, from which employees and directors are expressly relieved.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A Rs 30,00,000 portfolio holds Rs 15,00,000 in equity funds expected to return 14%, Rs 9,00,000 in debt funds expected to return 7.5% and Rs 6,00,000 in a gold ETF expected to return 9%. What is the portfolio's expected return?\nA) 9.55%  B) 10.17%  C) 11.05%  D) 30.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 11.05%**\n\nPortfolio expected return is a straight **weighted average**: weights are 15/30 = 0.50, 9/30 = 0.30 and 6/30 = 0.20.\nE(Rp) = 0.50(14) + 0.30(7.5) + 0.20(9) = 7.00 + 2.25 + 1.80 = **11.05%**. Correlation affects portfolio **risk only** and has no bearing on this figure.\n\n- **A) 9.55%** - wrong; this reverses the weights, applying 0.20 to equity and 0.50 to gold.\n- **B) 10.17%** - wrong; this is the simple average of the three returns (30.5 / 3), which ignores the very different amounts invested.\n- **D) 30.50%** - wrong; this is the sum of the three returns, not a weighted average.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which statement about the Markowitz efficient frontier is correct?\nA) It identifies a single optimal portfolio that every rational investor should hold  B) Every portfolio on it is efficient, and which one an investor should hold depends on his risk tolerance  C) Portfolios lying below and to the right of it are unattainable  D) The global minimum variance portfolio is the best choice for every investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Every portfolio on it is efficient, and which one an investor should hold depends on his risk tolerance**\n\nThe frontier is the **set** of portfolios offering the highest return for each level of risk. It is built from the assets' returns, variances and covariances alone, without knowing anything about the investor. The investor's own risk tolerance then selects the point, at the tangency with his highest attainable indifference curve.\n\n- **A) It identifies a single optimal portfolio that every rational investor should hold** - wrong; the frontier is a menu, not a single dish. Two investors can rationally pick different points on the same frontier.\n- **C) Portfolios lying below and to the right of it are unattainable** - wrong; those portfolios are attainable but **inefficient** (dominated). It is the region **above and to the left** that is unattainable.\n- **D) The global minimum variance portfolio is the best choice for every investor** - wrong; the GMVP suits only an investor whose tolerance places him at the extreme left of the frontier.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Rohan and Meera's Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic allocation of 65% equity and 35% debt, with a permitted band of plus or minus 10 percentage points. Their adviser reduces equity to 57% because he believes large-cap valuations are stretched. This action is best described as:\nA) A revision of the strategic asset allocation  B) Tactical asset allocation  C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement  D) Immunisation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Tactical asset allocation**\n\nThe clean test is *what triggered the change*. Nothing in the couple's goals, horizon or circumstances has altered; the trigger is a **market view**. The new weight of 57% also stays inside the permitted 55%-75% band, so this is a **short-term deviation within the IPS** - the definition of tactical asset allocation.\n\n- **A) A revision of the strategic asset allocation** - wrong; a strategic revision follows a change in the **client's life** - a new goal, a changed horizon, an altered risk capacity.\n- **C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement** - wrong; a breach arises only when the weight moves **outside** the agreed band, which 57% does not.\n- **D) Immunisation** - wrong; immunisation is a bond technique that matches portfolio duration to the investment horizon.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Brinson, Hood and Beebower (1986) found that asset allocation policy explained **93.6%** of what?\nA) The total return earned by the pension plans studied  B) The variation in the quarterly returns of the plans over time  C) The difference in returns between one plan and another  D) The proportion of assets held in equities\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The variation in the quarterly returns of the plans over time**\n\nThe study examined 91 large US pension plans over 1974-1983 and found that the allocation policy explained **93.6% of the variation (variance) in quarterly returns over time**. Ibbotson and Kaplan (2000) later separated the ideas: roughly **90%** of variability over time, about **40%** of the variation across funds, and close to **100%** of the level of return.\n\n- **A) The total return earned by the pension plans studied** - wrong; this is the classic misreading. The result is about the **variability** of returns, not the amount of money earned.\n- **C) The difference in returns between one plan and another** - wrong; cross-sectional differences are explained to a far smaller degree, around 40% in the Ibbotson and Kaplan refinement.\n- **D) The proportion of assets held in equities** - wrong; equity weight is an input to the allocation policy, not the quantity being explained.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A diversified equity fund returned 16.80% over the year with a standard deviation of 18.00% and a beta of 1.25. The risk-free rate was 6.25%. What is the fund's Sharpe ratio?\nA) 0.59  B) 0.93  C) 1.71  D) 8.44\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 0.59**\n\nSharpe ratio = (Rp - Rf) / standard deviation. Excess return = 16.80 - 6.25 = **10.55%**. Sharpe = 10.55 / 18.00 = **0.59** (rounded to two decimals). It measures excess return per unit of **total** risk.\n\n- **B) 0.93** - wrong; this divides the raw return by the standard deviation without deducting the risk-free rate.\n- **C) 1.71** - wrong; this inverts the ratio, dividing risk by excess return.\n- **D) 8.44** - wrong; this is the **Treynor** ratio, 10.55 / 1.25, which uses beta and therefore measures systematic risk only.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Sunita, a resident individual, wishes to start a monthly SIP of Rs 3,000 in a single mutual fund but does not hold a PAN. Which statement is correct?\nA) PAN is mandatory for every mutual fund investment without exception  B) She may invest up to Rs 50,000 per financial year, aggregated across the schemes of that one mutual fund, against a PEKRN  C) She may invest up to Rs 50,000 per financial year in each separate scheme of that mutual fund  D) The PAN exemption is available only to residents of Sikkim\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) She may invest up to Rs 50,000 per financial year, aggregated across the schemes of that one mutual fund, against a PEKRN**\n\nThe micro-SIP relaxation exempts investments of up to **Rs 50,000 per investor per financial year, aggregated across the schemes of one mutual fund**, from the PAN requirement. The investor is issued a **PEKRN** (PAN Exempt KYC Reference Number). Her annual commitment of Rs 36,000 fits within the limit. The facility is open to individuals, NRIs, minors and sole proprietors, but not to HUFs or companies.\n\n- **A) PAN is mandatory for every mutual fund investment without exception** - wrong; the micro-SIP relaxation and the Sikkim exemption both exist.\n- **C) She may invest up to Rs 50,000 per financial year in each separate scheme of that mutual fund** - wrong; the limit is **aggregated across schemes** of the same fund, not applied scheme by scheme.\n- **D) The PAN exemption is available only to residents of Sikkim** - wrong; Sikkim residence is a separate exemption that exists alongside the micro-SIP route.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Which body issues a **14-digit KIN** and holds KYC records usable across banking, securities, insurance and pensions alike?\nA) A KYC Registration Agency under the SEBI (KRA) Regulations, 2011  B) The Central KYC Records Registry operated by CERSAI  C) CDSL Ventures Limited (CVL)  D) FIU-IND\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The Central KYC Records Registry operated by CERSAI**\n\nThe **CKYCR**, set up under **Rule 9(1A) of the PML Rules** and operated by **CERSAI**, covers the **whole financial system** and issues a **14-digit KYC Identification Number (KIN)**. Reporting entities must upload the record within 10 days of starting the relationship.\n\n- **A) A KYC Registration Agency under the SEBI (KRA) Regulations, 2011** - wrong; a KRA's scope is the **securities market only**, and it maintains the record against PAN rather than issuing a KIN.\n- **C) CDSL Ventures Limited (CVL)** - wrong; CVL is an example of a KRA, so the same limitation applies.\n- **D) FIU-IND** - wrong; FIU-IND receives CTRs and STRs under the PMLA. It is not a KYC registry.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Meera's father held a mutual fund folio in his sole name with Meera registered as the sole nominee. He died leaving a will bequeathing his estate equally to Meera and her brother. Which statement is correct?\nA) Nomination overrides the will, so Meera becomes the absolute owner of the units  B) The units are transmitted to Meera, but she holds them on behalf of the legal heirs under the will  C) The AMC must ignore the nomination and transmit the units directly to both children in equal shares  D) A succession certificate is required despite the registered nomination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The units are transmitted to Meera, but she holds them on behalf of the legal heirs under the will**\n\n**A nominee is a trustee, not an owner.** Nomination lets the AMC obtain a valid discharge by paying the registered nominee, but it **does not override succession law**. Meera receives the units and holds them for those entitled under the will. The one exception in Indian law is insurance, where a beneficial nominee under Section 39 of the Insurance Act, 1938 is beneficially entitled.\n\n- **A) Nomination overrides the will, so Meera becomes the absolute owner of the units** - wrong; this is the single most common retail misconception about nomination.\n- **C) The AMC must ignore the nomination and transmit the units directly to both children in equal shares** - wrong; the AMC acts on the registered nomination, which is exactly what makes transmission workable.\n- **D) A succession certificate is required despite the registered nomination** - wrong; succession documents are needed where the sole holder dies with **no** nomination registered.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which statement about **rematerialisation** is correct?\nA) The investor submits a DRF and the demat account is credited  B) The investor submits an RRF, the demat account is debited, and the certificates issued carry new distinctive numbers  C) The investor's original share certificates are returned unchanged by the RTA  D) It is completed on the same day the request is lodged with the depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The investor submits an RRF, the demat account is debited, and the certificates issued carry new distinctive numbers**\n\nRematerialisation converts electronic holdings back into physical certificates. The investor files a **Rematerialisation Request Form (RRF)**, the DP blocks the holding and generates an **RRN**, the issuer/RTA prints and despatches fresh certificates, and the demat account is **debited**. The certificates bear **new distinctive numbers**.\n\n- **A) The investor submits a DRF and the demat account is credited** - wrong; that describes **dematerialisation**, the opposite process.\n- **C) The investor's original share certificates are returned unchanged by the RTA** - wrong; the originals were cancelled at the time of demat and cannot come back.\n- **D) It is completed on the same day the request is lodged with the depository** - wrong; the process normally takes about **30 days**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Jyoti wants to remit Rs 1,60,000 to a mutual fund immediately. Which statement is correct?\nA) The remittance can go through RTGS because RTGS has no minimum value  B) RTGS is unavailable because it has a minimum of Rs 2,00,000, so NEFT or IMPS may be used  C) NEFT cannot be used because NEFT has a minimum of Rs 2,00,000  D) Neither NEFT nor RTGS is available, since both carry a minimum of Rs 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) RTGS is unavailable because it has a minimum of Rs 2,00,000, so NEFT or IMPS may be used**\n\n**RTGS**, operated by the RBI, settles transaction by transaction in real time and carries a **minimum of Rs 2,00,000** with no upper limit. **NEFT**, also run by the RBI, settles in batches and has **no minimum or maximum**. **IMPS**, run by NPCI, is instant with a per-transaction ceiling of the order of Rs 5 lakh. A Rs 1,60,000 transfer therefore goes by NEFT or IMPS.\n\n- **A) The remittance can go through RTGS because RTGS has no minimum value** - wrong; it is the **maximum** that is unlimited in RTGS, not the minimum.\n- **C) NEFT cannot be used because NEFT has a minimum of Rs 2,00,000** - wrong; NEFT has no minimum at all. The Rs 2 lakh floor belongs to RTGS.\n- **D) Neither NEFT nor RTGS is available, since both carry a minimum of Rs 5,00,000** - wrong; no such floor exists for either system.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** A **DDPI** (Demat Debit and Pledge Instruction) executed in favour of a stock broker permits the broker to do only which two things?\nA) Transfer securities for the client's settlement obligations, and pledge or re-pledge them for margin  B) Operate the client's bank account, and transfer securities off-market to any account  C) Open and close accounts in the client's name, and take a loan against the client's securities  D) Merge the client's balances with those of other clients, and change the client's nomination\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Transfer securities for the client's settlement obligations, and pledge or re-pledge them for margin**\n\nThe DDPI was introduced to replace the broadly drawn broker Power of Attorney. It authorises **exactly two** actions: **transfer of securities to meet the client's own settlement obligations**, and **pledge or re-pledge for margin purposes**. Anything wider is outside its scope.\n\n- **B) Operate the client's bank account, and transfer securities off-market to any account** - wrong; arbitrary off-market transfers are precisely the abuse the DDPI was designed to stop.\n- **C) Open and close accounts in the client's name, and take a loan against the client's securities** - wrong; SEBI expressly bars a broker authorisation from permitting either.\n- **D) Merge the client's balances with those of other clients, and change the client's nomination** - wrong; merging client balances is prohibited, and nomination is a personal act that no authorised representative may perform.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Rohan and Meera open a mutual fund folio in their joint names but leave the \"mode of holding\" box on the application form blank. What is the consequence?\nA) The folio defaults to \"Anyone or Survivor\" and either of them may sign  B) The folio defaults to \"Joint\", so every future instruction requires both signatures  C) The application is rejected outright by the RTA  D) The first holder alone may sign all future instructions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The folio defaults to \"Joint\", so every future instruction requires both signatures**\n\nWhere there is more than one holder and the mode of holding is left blank, the folio is treated as **\"Joint\"**, meaning **all holders must sign every transaction**. This causes considerable practical inconvenience later, which is why an adviser should always ensure the box is completed deliberately.\n\n- **A) The folio defaults to \"Anyone or Survivor\" and either of them may sign** - wrong; that mode has to be **chosen** and is never the default.\n- **C) The application is rejected outright by the RTA** - wrong; the form is processed, with the default mode applied.\n- **D) The first holder alone may sign all future instructions** - wrong; the first holder is the tax owner and receives the payouts, but a joint folio still needs every signature.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Nachiket, a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser, agrees a fixed fee of Rs 96,000 per annum with a client family. What is the maximum advance fee he may collect?\nA) Rs 24,000  B) Rs 48,000  C) Rs 96,000  D) Rs 1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 48,000**\n\nAdvance fees may **not exceed the fees for two quarters**. One quarter's fee = 96,000 / 4 = **Rs 24,000**, so two quarters = **Rs 48,000**. Separately, on premature termination the adviser may retain a breakage fee of not more than **one quarter's** fee.\n\n- **A) Rs 24,000** - wrong; one quarter's fee is the **breakage** ceiling, not the advance ceiling.\n- **C) Rs 96,000** - wrong; collecting the whole year in advance is four quarters, double the permitted limit.\n- **D) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the regulatory ceiling on the annual fixed fee itself, and it exceeds even the fee actually agreed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** Devika's family has assets under advice of Rs 36,00,000 with a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser. Comparing the two permitted fee modes, which statement is correct?\nA) The AUA mode permits more, by Rs 35,000  B) The fixed-fee mode permits more, by Rs 35,000  C) The fixed-fee mode permits more, by Rs 90,000  D) Both modes permit exactly Rs 1,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The fixed-fee mode permits more, by Rs 35,000**\n\nUnder the **AUA mode** the ceiling is 2.5% of assets under advice per family: 2.5% x 36,00,000 = **Rs 90,000**. Under the **fixed-fee mode** the ceiling is **Rs 1,25,000** per family. The fixed-fee mode therefore allows **Rs 35,000 more**. The two modes coincide at an AUA of exactly Rs 50 lakh; below that, fixed fee permits more.\n\n- **A) The AUA mode permits more, by Rs 35,000** - wrong; the direction is reversed. At Rs 36 lakh of AUA the percentage ceiling is the tighter of the two.\n- **C) The fixed-fee mode permits more, by Rs 90,000** - wrong; Rs 90,000 is the AUA-mode ceiling itself, not the difference between the modes.\n- **D) Both modes permit exactly Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; the AUA ceiling varies with the size of the portfolio and equals Rs 1,25,000 only at an AUA of Rs 50 lakh.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** On 4 August a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser advises his clients to buy a particular stock. On 9 August, with no change in his assessment, he sells that stock from his own account. Which statement is correct?\nA) This is permitted, as an adviser is free to manage his own portfolio as he wishes  B) This breaches Regulation 15(5), which bars an own-account transaction contrary to the advice given for 15 days, and requires any genuinely revised assessment to be given to the client at least 24 hours in advance  C) This breaches Regulation 15(5), which imposes a 30-day bar with no exceptions  D) This is permitted provided he discloses the trade to the client within seven days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) This breaches Regulation 15(5), which bars an own-account transaction contrary to the advice given for 15 days, and requires any genuinely revised assessment to be given to the client at least 24 hours in advance**\n\nRegulation 15(5) of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 prohibits an adviser from transacting on his own account **contrary to the advice given, for 15 days** from the date of that advice. If the situation genuinely changes within that window, he may transact only **after giving the revised assessment to the client at least 24 hours in advance**. Selling on day five with no revised view is a clear breach.\n\n- **A) This is permitted, as an adviser is free to manage his own portfolio as he wishes** - wrong; the fiduciary duty under Regulation 15(1) makes an adviser's own book subject to explicit restrictions.\n- **C) This breaches Regulation 15(5), which imposes a 30-day bar with no exceptions** - wrong; the period is **15 days**, and there is an exception for a genuinely revised assessment communicated in advance.\n- **D) This is permitted provided he discloses the trade to the client within seven days** - wrong; disclosure after the event does not cure the breach. Any revised view must come **before** the trade.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** An individual SEBI-registered Investment Adviser finds that his client count has crossed **150**. What must he do?\nA) Nothing, provided his assets under advice stay below Rs 50 crore  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual Investment Adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted  C) Appoint a compliance officer and continue as an individual adviser  D) Surrender his registration and re-apply after one year\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Apply for registration as a non-individual Investment Adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted**\n\nThe corporatisation threshold under the IA Regulations is **150 clients**. Once an individual adviser exceeds it he must apply for registration as a **non-individual** adviser and must **not onboard fresh clients** as an individual until the new registration is in place.\n\n- **A) Nothing, provided his assets under advice stay below Rs 50 crore** - wrong; the trigger is the **number of clients**, not the value advised upon.\n- **C) Appoint a compliance officer and continue as an individual adviser** - wrong; appointing a compliance officer is an obligation that follows the change of status. It does not substitute for it.\n- **D) Surrender his registration and re-apply after one year** - wrong; no surrender or waiting period is involved.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Which statement about the appointment of a compliance officer under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 is correct?\nA) Every registered Investment Adviser must appoint one  B) Only a non-individual Investment Adviser must appoint one  C) Only an adviser with assets under advice above Rs 100 crore must appoint one  D) The compliance officer must be a SEBI officer nominated to the firm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Only a non-individual Investment Adviser must appoint one**\n\nRegulation 20 requires a **non-individual** Investment Adviser to appoint a compliance officer responsible for monitoring compliance with the Act, the regulations and SEBI's directions. An **individual** adviser is not required to appoint one, because he personally discharges the compliance function.\n\n- **A) Every registered Investment Adviser must appoint one** - wrong; this ignores the individual/non-individual distinction that the regulation turns on.\n- **C) Only an adviser with assets under advice above Rs 100 crore must appoint one** - wrong; the obligation depends on the **form of the adviser**, not on the size of the book.\n- **D) The compliance officer must be a SEBI officer nominated to the firm** - wrong; the compliance officer is an employee or officer of the adviser, appointed by the adviser itself.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** A designated person of a listed company sells shares worth **Rs 14,00,000** in a single transaction. What continual disclosure is required under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015?\nA) No disclosure, as the value is below the Rs 25 lakh threshold  B) Disclosure to the company within 2 trading days, and by the company to the stock exchange within 2 trading days of receipt  C) Disclosure directly to SEBI within 7 days of the transaction  D) Disclosure to the company within 30 days of the end of the quarter\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Disclosure to the company within 2 trading days, and by the company to the stock exchange within 2 trading days of receipt**\n\nContinual disclosure is triggered where the **traded value exceeds Rs 10 lakh**, either in one transaction or as a series over a calendar quarter. The person discloses to the **company within 2 trading days**, and the company passes it to the **stock exchange within 2 trading days** of receipt.\n\n- **A) No disclosure, as the value is below the Rs 25 lakh threshold** - wrong; the threshold is **Rs 10 lakh**, which Rs 14 lakh clearly exceeds.\n- **C) Disclosure directly to SEBI within 7 days of the transaction** - wrong; the 7-day period relates to **initial** disclosure of holdings on appointment or on becoming a promoter, and the route runs through the company.\n- **D) Disclosure to the company within 30 days of the end of the quarter** - wrong; the deadline is **2 trading days**, not a quarterly filing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Money derived from a crime is moved through three shell companies in two jurisdictions, with securities repeatedly bought and sold along the way. Which stage of money laundering does this describe?\nA) Placement  B) Layering  C) Integration  D) Structuring the beneficial ownership\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Layering**\n\n**Layering** is the second stage, in which a **series of conversions and movements** is used to distance the funds from their criminal source and break the audit trail. Wiring money through shell entities and churning securities is the textbook illustration.\n\n- **A) Placement** - wrong; placement is the **first** stage, where cash is physically introduced into the financial system. It is the stage the CTR and the ban on cash and third-party payments attack.\n- **C) Integration** - wrong; integration is the **final** stage, where the funds re-enter the legitimate economy as apparently clean wealth, for example by buying property.\n- **D) Structuring the beneficial ownership** - wrong; this is not one of the three recognised stages under the PMLA framework.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which of the following is **NOT** a \"security\" as defined in Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956?\nA) A government security  B) A unit issued under a mutual fund scheme  C) An exchange-traded derivative contract  D) A unit-linked insurance policy issued by an insurer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) A unit-linked insurance policy issued by an insurer**\n\nSection 2(h) expressly **excludes a ULIP** or any similar instrument that combines life cover with investment and is issued by an insurer. The exclusion was inserted following the SEBI-IRDA dispute over ULIPs in 2010, and it places such products under IRDAI rather than SEBI.\n\n- **A) A government security** - wrong as the answer; G-Secs are expressly listed as securities in Section 2(h).\n- **B) A unit issued under a mutual fund scheme** - wrong as the answer; mutual fund units are expressly included.\n- **C) An exchange-traded derivative contract** - wrong as the answer; derivatives are expressly included, and Section 18A makes an exchange-traded, clearing-house-settled derivative a valid contract.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Sameer is 58, retires in two years and is profiled as conservative. His adviser recommends a product with a ten-year lock-in that pays the adviser the highest commission on his shelf. This conduct is best labelled:\nA) Churning  B) Mis-selling  C) Front-running  D) Insider trading\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Mis-selling**\n\n**Mis-selling** is recommending a product that does **not suit** the client's needs, risk profile or horizon, typically because it pays the adviser more. A ten-year lock-in for a conservative client with a two-year horizon is right for the seller, not the buyer. A single transaction is enough to constitute mis-selling.\n\n- **A) Churning** - wrong; churning is **excessive trading or switching** to generate repeated commission. It is about frequency, not about one unsuitable recommendation.\n- **C) Front-running** - wrong; front-running means dealing **ahead of a client's known impending order** to profit from the price move that order will cause.\n- **D) Insider trading** - wrong; insider trading involves trading on **unpublished price-sensitive information about a company**, which is absent here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** A scheme's NAV rose from Rs 12 to Rs 33 over 8 years and the brochure headlines a \"175% return\". What is the honest annualised figure the adviser should quote?\nA) 13.48%  B) 21.88%  C) 175.00%  D) 275.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 13.48%**\n\nThe correct measure over a multi-year period is the **CAGR**: (33 / 12)^(1/8) - 1 = 2.75^0.125 - 1 = **13.48% per annum**. Quoting the cumulative 175% without stating the period is the commonest form of return misrepresentation in Indian sales material.\n\n- **B) 21.88%** - wrong; this divides the cumulative 175% by 8 years, which ignores compounding entirely.\n- **C) 175.00%** - wrong; this is the cumulative gain over eight years, not an annual rate.\n- **D) 275.00%** - wrong; this states the value multiple of 2.75 times as if it were a percentage return.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Under the SEBI Code of Conduct for Investment Advisers, what is the correct order of preference for handling a conflict of interest?\nA) Disclose first; avoid only if the client objects  B) Avoid the conflict where possible; where it cannot be avoided, manage it and disclose it fully  C) Manage the conflict internally and disclose it only if SEBI asks  D) Accept the conflict provided the fee charged is fair and reasonable\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Avoid the conflict where possible; where it cannot be avoided, manage it and disclose it fully**\n\nThe Code requires an adviser to **avoid** conflicts as far as possible and, where they are unavoidable, to ensure clients are fairly treated and to **disclose** the conflict. Regulation 15(1) reinforces this by requiring disclosure of all conflicts **as and when they arise**. The hierarchy is **avoid, then manage, then disclose**.\n\n- **A) Disclose first; avoid only if the client objects** - wrong; this inverts the hierarchy and puts the burden of protection on the client rather than the fiduciary.\n- **C) Manage the conflict internally and disclose it only if SEBI asks** - wrong; disclosure is owed to the **client**, not merely to the regulator on demand.\n- **D) Accept the conflict provided the fee charged is fair and reasonable** - wrong; fair charging is a separate obligation under the Code and does not license an avoidable conflict.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** Which of the following is best described as a genuine **ethical dilemma** rather than a simple temptation?\nA) An adviser deciding whether to accept an undisclosed commission from a product manufacturer  B) An adviser deciding whether to trade ahead of a client's large order  C) An adviser weighing a client's insistence on a portfolio far riskier than his profile against the duty to protect him  D) An adviser deciding whether to back-date a risk profile that was never completed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) An adviser weighing a client's insistence on a portfolio far riskier than his profile against the duty to protect him**\n\nA dilemma exists where **two legitimate duties conflict** - here, respect for **client autonomy** against the **duty of care**. Neither option is obviously wrong, which is exactly what makes it a dilemma requiring a structured resolution: explain the risk in rupee terms, offer a compromise, and if the client persists, record both the advice given and his written instruction contrary to it.\n\n- **A) An adviser deciding whether to accept an undisclosed commission from a product manufacturer** - wrong; this is a straightforward breach of Regulation 15(2) and merely a temptation.\n- **B) An adviser deciding whether to trade ahead of a client's large order** - wrong; front-running is prohibited outright under the PFUTP Regulations. There is no competing legitimate duty.\n- **D) An adviser deciding whether to back-date a risk profile that was never completed** - wrong; falsifying a record is plain misconduct, not a balance of duties.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** In the United States, which document must a registered investment adviser deliver to clients, disclosing its services, fees, conflicts of interest and disciplinary history?\nA) Form ADV, particularly the Part 2 brochure  B) The Statement of Advice  C) The Financial Services Guide  D) The Key Information Memorandum\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Form ADV, particularly the Part 2 brochure**\n\nUnder the **Investment Advisers Act of 1940**, administered by the **SEC**, an adviser's disclosure document is **Form ADV**, whose plain-English **Part 2 brochure** is given to clients and covers services, fees, conflicts and disciplinary history. The US fiduciary standard was confirmed in **SEC v. Capital Gains Research Bureau (1963)**.\n\n- **B) The Statement of Advice** - wrong; the SoA is an **Australian** document under the FOFA framework, setting out the advice and its basis.\n- **C) The Financial Services Guide** - wrong; the FSG is also **Australian**, given to the client at the start of the relationship.\n- **D) The Key Information Memorandum** - wrong; the KIM is the abridged **Indian mutual fund** document accompanying an application form.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A policyholder's claim of Rs 6,80,000 was repudiated. He made a written representation to the insurer and received a reply he finds unsatisfactory, and he now approaches the Insurance Ombudsman. Which statement is correct?\nA) The complaint is not maintainable, since claims above Rs 5 lakh must go to a consumer commission  B) The complaint is maintainable, as the value limit is Rs 30 lakh; any award will bind the insurer but not the complainant  C) The complaint is maintainable, and the resulting award will bind both parties equally  D) He must first lodge the complaint on SEBI's SCORES portal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The complaint is maintainable, as the value limit is Rs 30 lakh; any award will bind the insurer but not the complainant**\n\nThe Insurance Ombudsman may consider claims **not exceeding Rs 30 lakh**, provided a written representation was first made to the insurer and it was rejected, answered unsatisfactorily, or left unanswered for a month. The award is **binding on the insurer only** - the complainant remains free to approach a consumer commission or civil court, which is what makes the route a free, low-risk first attempt.\n\n- **A) The complaint is not maintainable, since claims above Rs 5 lakh must go to a consumer commission** - wrong; no such Rs 5 lakh ceiling exists. Rs 5 lakh is the level up to which consumer filing is free of fee.\n- **C) The complaint is maintainable, and the resulting award will bind both parties equally** - wrong; binding the complainant too would deter cautious policyholders from ever filing.\n- **D) He must first lodge the complaint on SEBI's SCORES portal** - wrong; SCORES is for the securities market. The insurance portal is **Bima Bharosa**, run by IRDAI.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** As revised by the Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction) Rules, 2021, before which commission must a consumer complaint be filed where the consideration paid was **Rs 78,00,000**?\nA) The District Commission  B) The State Commission  C) The National Commission  D) The Securities Appellate Tribunal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The State Commission**\n\nUnder the limits currently in force, the **District Commission** takes matters up to **Rs 50 lakh**, the **State Commission** those **above Rs 50 lakh and up to Rs 2 crore**, and the **National Commission** those above Rs 2 crore. Rs 78 lakh therefore falls to the State Commission. Note that jurisdiction is fixed by the **value of the consideration paid**, not by the compensation demanded.\n\n- **A) The District Commission** - wrong; Rs 78 lakh exceeds its Rs 50 lakh ceiling under the 2021 Rules.\n- **C) The National Commission** - wrong; that tier begins above Rs 2 crore.\n- **D) The Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against orders of SEBI, PFRDA and IRDAI. It is not a consumer forum.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** An investor lodged a complaint on SCORES and the entity filed its Action Taken Report on the nineteenth day. She is dissatisfied with the reply. What is her next step?\nA) Appeal to SAT within 45 days  B) Seek a first review by the Designated Body within 15 days of the ATR  C) Seek a review directly by SEBI within 21 days of the ATR  D) File a civil suit, as the SCORES process is exhausted\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Seek a first review by the Designated Body within 15 days of the ATR**\n\nThe SCORES 2.0 clock runs **21 - 15 - 21 - 15 - 21**: the entity has **21 days** to file its ATR, the investor has **15 days** to seek a **first review by the Designated Body** (such as a stock exchange, depository, AMFI or BASL), which has **21 days**; only after that may a **second review by SEBI** be sought within a further 15 days.\n\n- **A) Appeal to SAT within 45 days** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against a **regulator's order**, not investor complaints against an intermediary.\n- **C) Seek a review directly by SEBI within 21 days of the ATR** - wrong; SEBI conducts the **second** review, after the Designated Body has completed the first.\n- **D) File a civil suit, as the SCORES process is exhausted** - wrong; the review stages remain, and a disputed money claim would in any case go to the SMART ODR portal.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Which combination correctly states the grievance obligations of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser?\nA) Registration on SCORES and enrolment on the SMART ODR portal, display of the Investor Charter and escalation matrix, and complaints data updated on the website monthly  B) Registration on SCORES only, with complaints data reported to SEBI once every three years  C) Enrolment on the SMART ODR portal only, since SCORES covers listed companies alone  D) No portal registration is required if the adviser has fewer than 150 clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Registration on SCORES and enrolment on the SMART ODR portal, display of the Investor Charter and escalation matrix, and complaints data updated on the website monthly**\n\nRegulation 21 requires prompt, time-bound grievance redressal, and the supporting framework requires every registered adviser to be on **SCORES** and enrolled on the **SMART ODR portal**, to display SEBI's **Investor Charter for Investment Advisers** and an escalation matrix, and to publish **complaints data updated monthly** on its website. Grievance records are kept for five years, or until final disposal where a matter is in dispute.\n\n- **B) Registration on SCORES only, with complaints data reported to SEBI once every three years** - wrong on both counts; ODR enrolment is mandatory and complaints data is a monthly website disclosure.\n- **C) Enrolment on the SMART ODR portal only, since SCORES covers listed companies alone** - wrong; SCORES covers complaints against **SEBI-registered intermediaries** as well as listed companies.\n- **D) No portal registration is required if the adviser has fewer than 150 clients** - wrong; the 150-client figure is the corporatisation threshold and has nothing to do with grievance obligations.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** An NPS subscriber raised a grievance against her Point of Presence through CGMS. Thirty days have passed with no resolution. To whom should she escalate next?\nA) Directly to the PFRDA Ombudsman  B) To the National Pension System Trust  C) To the Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) To the Insurance Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) To the National Pension System Trust**\n\nThe pension ladder runs: the **intermediary through CGMS (30 days)**, then the **NPS Trust**, then the **PFRDA Ombudsman** (appeal within 45 days), then **PFRDA** (within 30 days), then **SAT**, and finally the Supreme Court on a question of law within 60 days. The NPS Trust is the immediate next rung.\n\n- **A) Directly to the PFRDA Ombudsman** - wrong; the Ombudsman sits above the NPS Trust, and skipping a rung is the classic error in grievance questions.\n- **C) To the Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against **PFRDA's order**, at the very top of the ladder.\n- **D) To the Insurance Ombudsman** - wrong; that forum deals with insurance policy complaints and has no jurisdiction over NPS.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Caselets (6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 1: The Deshmukh household budget, Powai\n\nKunal Deshmukh (39) is a supply-chain manager in Powai, Mumbai, earning Rs 2,10,000 a month. His wife Shreya (36) is a hospital administrator earning Rs 1,15,000 a month. They have two school-age children, Aarav (11) and Ira (8). Total household expenses run to Rs 2,47,000 a month, and that figure already includes EMIs of Rs 91,000 on a home loan and a car loan. They hold Rs 5,40,000 in a savings account and Rs 3,15,000 in a liquid fund; apart from these, all their other holdings are in equity mutual funds, EPF, gold and their flat. Their total assets are valued at Rs 1,96,00,000 and their outstanding loans total Rs 71,00,000. Their adviser wants them to hold an emergency fund equal to six months of household expenses.\n\n**Q91.** What is the Deshmukhs' monthly savings ratio?\nA) 24.00%  B) 31.58%  C) 37.14%  D) 76.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 24.00%**\n\nGross monthly income = 2,10,000 + 1,15,000 = Rs 3,25,000. Savings (surplus) = 3,25,000 − 2,47,000 = Rs 78,000. Savings ratio = Savings / Gross income = 78,000 / 3,25,000 = **24.00%**. (Self-check: the expenses ratio is 2,47,000 / 3,25,000 = 76.00%, and 24 + 76 = 100.)\n\n- **B) 31.58%** - wrong; this is 78,000 / 2,47,000, i.e. savings divided by expenses instead of by income.\n- **C) 37.14%** - wrong; this divides the household surplus by Kunal's income alone (78,000 / 2,10,000).\n- **D) 76.00%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** What is their liquidity ratio?\nA) 2.19 months  B) 3.46 months  C) 5.48 months  D) 6.00 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 3.46 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings account 5,40,000 + liquid fund 3,15,000 = Rs 8,55,000. Equity funds, EPF, gold and the flat are not liquid assets. Liquidity ratio = Liquid assets / Monthly expenses = 8,55,000 / 2,47,000 = **3.46 months** — inside the 3–6 month band, but at the thin end.\n\n- **A) 2.19 months** - wrong; this counts only the savings account (5,40,000 / 2,47,000) and ignores the liquid fund.\n- **C) 5.48 months** - wrong; this divides by expenses net of EMIs (8,55,000 / 1,56,000), but EMIs are exactly the outflow an emergency fund must keep paying.\n- **D) 6.00 months** - wrong; this is the adviser's target, not the computed ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** What is their solvency ratio?\nA) 0.36  B) 0.57  C) 0.64  D) 1.76\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 0.64**\n\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 1,96,00,000 − 71,00,000 = Rs 1,25,00,000. Solvency ratio = Net worth / Total assets = 1,25,00,000 / 1,96,00,000 = 0.6378 = **0.64**. (Self-check: the leverage ratio is 71,00,000 / 1,96,00,000 = 0.36, and 0.64 + 0.36 = 1.)\n\n- **A) 0.36** - wrong; this is the leverage ratio (liabilities / assets), the complement of solvency.\n- **B) 0.57** - wrong; this is net worth divided by assets plus liabilities, a meaningless base.\n- **D) 1.76** - wrong; this is net worth divided by liabilities (1,25,00,000 / 71,00,000), not by assets.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** What is their debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 24.00%  B) 28.00%  C) 36.84%  D) 43.33%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 28.00%**\n\nDebt-to-income = Total EMIs / Gross income = 91,000 / 3,25,000 = **28.00%**, comfortably below the 35–40% ceiling lenders and planners use.\n\n- **A) 24.00%** - wrong; this is the savings ratio, not the debt-servicing ratio.\n- **C) 36.84%** - wrong; this divides EMIs by total expenses (91,000 / 2,47,000) instead of by income.\n- **D) 43.33%** - wrong; this divides EMIs by Kunal's income alone (91,000 / 2,10,000).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** How much more must the Deshmukhs set aside in liquid assets to reach the adviser's six-month emergency fund target?\nA) Rs 6,27,000  B) Rs 10,95,000  C) Rs 14,82,000  D) Rs 19,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 6,27,000**\n\nThe emergency fund is sized against expenses, not income. Target = 6 × 2,47,000 = Rs 14,82,000. They already hold liquid assets of 5,40,000 + 3,15,000 = Rs 8,55,000. Shortfall = 14,82,000 − 8,55,000 = **Rs 6,27,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 10,95,000** - wrong; this sizes the fund against six months of income (6 × 3,25,000 = 19,50,000) and then deducts the liquid assets.\n- **C) Rs 14,82,000** - wrong; this is the full target, ignoring the liquid assets already held.\n- **D) Rs 19,50,000** - wrong; this is six months of income, which is not the basis for a contingency fund.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 2: Nikhil Rao's equity and bond holdings\n\nNikhil Rao, Shreya's brother, holds 1,200 shares of Sundaram Auto Components, currently quoted at Rs 640. The company reported EPS of Rs 32, a book value of Rs 256 per share and a dividend of Rs 9.60 per share; analysts expect earnings to grow 16% a year. Nikhil also holds a corporate bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 carrying a 9% annual coupon, with exactly 4 years left to maturity, currently quoted at Rs 1,062. Worried about a correction before his own bonus is paid, he buys one lot of a Nifty put option struck at 23,800 for a premium of Rs 210 per unit; the Nifty lot size is 25 and the index spot is 24,000.\n\n**Q96.** What is the price-to-earnings ratio of Sundaram Auto Components?\nA) 2.50  B) 5.00  C) 20.00  D) 66.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 20.00**\n\nP/E = Market price / EPS = 640 / 32 = **20.00 times**.\n\n- **A) 2.50** - wrong; this is the price-to-book ratio (640 / 256).\n- **B) 5.00** - wrong; this is the earnings yield in per cent (32 / 640), the reciprocal of the P/E.\n- **D) 66.67** - wrong; this is price divided by dividend per share (640 / 9.60).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** What is the dividend yield on the share?\nA) 1.50%  B) 3.75%  C) 5.00%  D) 30.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.50%**\n\nDividend yield = DPS / Market price = 9.60 / 640 = **1.50%**.\n\n- **B) 3.75%** - wrong; this divides the dividend by book value per share (9.60 / 256).\n- **C) 5.00%** - wrong; this is the earnings yield (32 / 640).\n- **D) 30.00%** - wrong; this is the dividend payout ratio (9.60 / 32), a share of profit, not a yield on price.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** What is the current yield on Nikhil's corporate bond?\nA) 7.23%  B) 8.47%  C) 8.73%  D) 9.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 8.47%**\n\nAnnual coupon = 9% × 1,000 = Rs 90. Current yield = Coupon / Market price = 90 / 1,062 = 0.084746 = **8.47%**.\n\n- **A) 7.23%** - wrong; this is the approximate yield to maturity, which also counts the capital loss to redemption.\n- **C) 8.73%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by the average of face and price (90 / 1,031), which is part of the YTM formula, not the current yield.\n- **D) 9.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate on face value, which applies only if the bond trades at par.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Using the approximation formula, what is the yield to maturity on the bond?\nA) 7.23%  B) 8.47%  C) 9.00%  D) 10.23%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 7.23%**\n\nApproximate YTM = [C + (F − P)/n] / [(F + P)/2] = [90 + (1,000 − 1,062)/4] / [(1,000 + 1,062)/2] = [90 − 15.50] / 1,031 = 74.50 / 1,031 = 0.072260 = **7.23%**. The ordering confirms it: this is a premium bond, so coupon 9.00% > current yield 8.47% > YTM 7.23%.\n\n- **B) 8.47%** - wrong; this is the current yield, which ignores the Rs 62 capital loss at redemption.\n- **C) 9.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate.\n- **D) 10.23%** - wrong; this adds the premium instead of subtracting it — [90 + 15.50] / 1,031 — turning a capital loss into a gain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** At what index level does Nikhil's long put position break even at expiry?\nA) 23,590  B) 23,800  C) 24,010  D) 24,360\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 23,590**\n\nFor a long put, break-even = Strike − Premium = 23,800 − 210 = **23,590**. Below that level the intrinsic value exceeds the premium paid and the position is in profit; his maximum loss is the premium of 210 × 25 = Rs 5,250.\n\n- **B) 23,800** - wrong; this is the strike price, the level at which the option merely starts to have intrinsic value.\n- **C) 24,010** - wrong; this adds the premium to the strike, which is the break-even for a long call, not a long put.\n- **D) 24,360** - wrong; this adds the premium to the spot level of 24,000, which has no meaning for the payoff.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 3: Shreya Deshmukh and the Meridian Flexi Cap Fund\n\nShreya invests in the Meridian Flexi Cap Fund. On the valuation date the scheme's total assets are Rs 4,724 crore, its total liabilities are Rs 44 crore, and 90 crore units are outstanding. The regular plan carries a total expense ratio of 1.85% a year and the direct plan 0.95% a year; the exit load is 1% of the redemption value if units are redeemed within 365 days. Shreya invests a lump sum of Rs 6,00,000 in the direct plan at that day's NAV and redeems the entire holding exactly 8 months later, when the NAV is Rs 57.20. Separately, for her sister's account she runs a SIP of Rs 25,000 a month for four months, and the purchase NAVs turn out to be Rs 52.00, Rs 50.00, Rs 40.00 and Rs 62.50.\n\n**Q101.** What is the NAV per unit of the Meridian Flexi Cap Fund on the valuation date?\nA) Rs 46.80  B) Rs 52.00  C) Rs 52.49  D) Rs 52.98\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 52.00**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) / Units outstanding = (4,724 − 44) crore / 90 crore = 4,680 / 90 = **Rs 52.00**.\n\n- **A) Rs 46.80** - wrong; this divides net assets by 100 crore units rather than the 90 crore actually outstanding.\n- **C) Rs 52.49** - wrong; this uses gross assets of Rs 4,724 crore without deducting liabilities.\n- **D) Rs 52.98** - wrong; this adds the liabilities to the assets instead of subtracting them.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q102.** How many units are allotted for Shreya's Rs 6,00,000 lump-sum purchase?\nA) 10,489.51  B) 11,423.08  C) 11,538.46  D) 12,000.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 11,538.46**\n\nUnits = Amount invested / NAV = 6,00,000 / 52.00 = **11,538.46 units**. There is no entry load in India, so the whole amount is invested.\n\n- **A) 10,489.51** - wrong; this uses the redemption NAV of Rs 57.20 instead of the purchase NAV.\n- **B) 11,423.08** - wrong; this deducts 1% as an entry load before allotment, but entry loads are prohibited and the 1% here is an exit load.\n- **D) 12,000.00** - wrong; this uses an NAV of Rs 50, which is not the computed NAV.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q103.** What amount does Shreya actually receive on redemption after 8 months?\nA) Rs 6,53,400  B) Rs 6,59,400  C) Rs 6,60,000  D) Rs 6,66,600\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 6,53,400**\n\nRedemption value = 11,538.46 × 57.20 = Rs 6,60,000. She has held for 8 months, i.e. within 365 days, so the 1% exit load applies to the redemption value: 1% × 6,60,000 = Rs 6,600. Net proceeds = 6,60,000 − 6,600 = **Rs 6,53,400**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6,59,400** - wrong; this charges the 1% load on the gain of Rs 60,000 instead of on the redemption value.\n- **C) Rs 6,60,000** - wrong; this ignores the exit load, which applies because the holding period is under 365 days.\n- **D) Rs 6,66,600** - wrong; this adds the load to the redemption value instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q104.** What is the average cost per unit of the four SIP instalments?\nA) Rs 48.00  B) Rs 49.86  C) Rs 51.13  D) Rs 52.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 49.86**\n\nUnits bought: 25,000/52.00 = 480.7692; 25,000/50.00 = 500.0000; 25,000/40.00 = 625.0000; 25,000/62.50 = 400.0000. Total units = 2,005.7692 for a total investment of Rs 1,00,000. Average cost = 1,00,000 / 2,005.7692 = **Rs 49.86** — below the simple average NAV of Rs 51.13, which is exactly the rupee-cost-averaging effect.\n\n- **A) Rs 48.00** - wrong; this is not produced by any of the data.\n- **C) Rs 51.13** - wrong; this is the simple arithmetic average of the four NAVs (204.50 / 4), which ignores that more units were bought at the low NAV.\n- **D) Rs 52.00** - wrong; this is only the first instalment's NAV.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q105.** On Shreya's Rs 6,00,000 holding, how much does the direct plan save her in one year compared with the regular plan?\nA) Rs 5,400  B) Rs 5,700  C) Rs 11,100  D) Rs 16,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5,400**\n\nTER difference = 1.85% − 0.95% = 0.90% a year. On Rs 6,00,000 that is 0.90% × 6,00,000 = **Rs 5,400 a year**. The portfolio is identical in both plans; only the embedded distribution commission differs, which is why an Investment Adviser paid a fee by the client recommends direct plans.\n\n- **B) Rs 5,700** - wrong; this is 0.95% of the amount, i.e. the direct plan's own cost, not the saving.\n- **C) Rs 11,100** - wrong; this is 1.85% of the amount, i.e. the regular plan's total cost.\n- **D) Rs 16,800** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios together instead of taking the difference.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 4: Devansh Iyer, PMS and AIF\n\nDevansh Iyer, a colleague of Kunal's, has an investible surplus of Rs 3.5 crore. He places Rs 1.20 crore with a SEBI-registered discretionary portfolio manager. The fee terms are a fixed fee of 1.5% of assets a year plus a performance fee of 20% of returns above a hurdle rate of 9% a year, with no catch-up and with a high watermark. In Year 1 the portfolio grows to Rs 1.44 crore before the performance fee; in Year 2 it falls to Rs 1.32 crore; in Year 3 it rises to Rs 1.56 crore. Separately, he is evaluating a Category III AIF that runs a leveraged long-short strategy and has a corpus of Rs 240 crore. Devansh is in the 30% income-tax bracket.\n\n**Q106.** What are the minimum investment amounts SEBI prescribes for the discretionary PMS and for the Category III AIF respectively?\nA) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020 the minimum client investment in a PMS is **Rs 50 lakh**. Under the SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012 the minimum investment per investor in an AIF is **Rs 1 crore**, in every category. The three minimums to keep straight are mutual fund Rs 100–5,000, PMS Rs 50 lakh, AIF Rs 1 crore.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the relaxed minimum for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager, and for angel investors.\n- **C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; the PMS figure is right but the AIF minimum for an ordinary investor is Rs 1 crore, not Rs 25 lakh.\n- **D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the AIF minimum and Rs 5 crore is the portfolio manager's own net-worth requirement, not a client ticket size.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q107.** What performance fee does the portfolio manager earn in Year 1?\nA) Rs 1,80,000  B) Rs 2,64,000  C) Rs 4,80,000  D) Rs 6,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,64,000**\n\nGain in Year 1 = 1,44,00,000 − 1,20,00,000 = Rs 24,00,000, i.e. 20%. The hurdle absorbs the first 9% of 1,20,00,000 = Rs 10,80,000, and there is no catch-up, so the fee applies only to the excess of 24,00,000 − 10,80,000 = Rs 13,20,000. Performance fee = 20% × 13,20,000 = **Rs 2,64,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,80,000** - wrong; this is the fixed fee of 1.5% on Rs 1.20 crore, a separate charge.\n- **C) Rs 4,80,000** - wrong; this is 20% of the whole Rs 24 lakh gain, ignoring the hurdle rate entirely.\n- **D) Rs 6,60,000** - wrong; this is half the excess return, not 20% of it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q108.** What performance fee may the manager charge in Year 3, applying the high watermark?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 2,40,000  C) Rs 4,80,000  D) Rs 6,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,40,000**\n\nThe high watermark is the highest value the portfolio has previously reached, i.e. Rs 1.44 crore at the end of Year 1. No fee was payable in Year 2 because Rs 1.32 crore is below that peak. In Year 3 the fee applies only to the gain above the watermark: 1,56,00,000 − 1,44,00,000 = Rs 12,00,000, so the fee is 20% × 12,00,000 = **Rs 2,40,000**.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has now exceeded its previous peak, so a fee is due on the excess.\n- **C) Rs 4,80,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the Rs 24 lakh rise from the Year 2 low of Rs 1.32 crore, making the client pay twice for ground merely recovered — precisely what the high watermark prevents.\n- **D) Rs 6,00,000** - wrong; this is 20% of the Rs 30 lakh rise measured from the original Rs 1.20 crore.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q109.** What is the minimum continuing interest the sponsor or manager of that Category III AIF must maintain?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 12 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 10 crore**\n\nFor a Category III AIF the sponsor or manager must maintain a continuing interest of the **lower of 5% of the corpus or Rs 10 crore**. Here 5% × 240 crore = Rs 12 crore, and the lower of Rs 12 crore and Rs 10 crore is **Rs 10 crore**. It must be real money and cannot be met by waiving management fees.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the cap for Categories I and II, not Category III.\n- **B) Rs 6 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category I/II percentage of 2.5% to the corpus.\n- **D) Rs 12 crore** - wrong; this takes 5% of the corpus but ignores the words \"lower of\", which cap the requirement at Rs 10 crore.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q110.** How will the income Devansh earns from the Category III AIF be taxed?\nA) It is taxed at the fund level before distribution, so his own 30% slab is irrelevant to the fund's tax  B) It is fully exempt in his hands because the AIF has already paid securities transaction tax  C) It is passed through and taxed in his hands at his own applicable rate, retaining its character  D) It is taxed at a flat 10% under a concession granted to all registered AIFs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) It is taxed at the fund level before distribution, so his own 30% slab is irrelevant to the fund's tax**\n\nPass-through status under the Income Tax Act is granted to **Category I and Category II AIFs only**. A **Category III AIF is taxed at the fund level**, generally at the maximum marginal rate, and the investor receives income that has already been taxed. Memory hook: \"One and Two pass it through; Three pays for you.\"\n\n- **B) It is fully exempt in his hands because the AIF has already paid securities transaction tax** - wrong; STT is unrelated to the fund's income-tax position, and the receipt is not exempt on that basis.\n- **C) It is passed through and taxed in his hands at his own applicable rate, retaining its character** - wrong; that is the Category I and II treatment, which Category III does not enjoy.\n- **D) It is taxed at a flat 10% under a concession granted to all registered AIFs** - wrong; no such flat concessional rate exists, and only Category I funds may receive government or SEBI concessions at all.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 5: Anagha Kulkarni, individual investment adviser\n\nAnagha Kulkarni is a SEBI-registered **individual** investment adviser practising in Dadar, Mumbai. She currently serves 138 individual clients and is onboarding steadily. Her husband holds an AMFI registration number and runs a mutual fund distribution business from the same premises. One family she advises has assets under advice of Rs 82,00,000 and is billed in the AUA mode; a second client has agreed a fixed fee of Rs 1,20,000 a year. A third client, Mr Fernandes, is 58, retires in two years, has no pension and no other assets, and this portfolio is his entire retirement corpus — yet in her questionnaire he ticks every box describing himself as an aggressive investor who is comfortable with large swings in value.\n\n**Q111.** What is the minimum net worth Anagha must maintain at all times as an individual investment adviser?\nA) Net tangible assets of Rs 1 lakh  B) Net tangible assets of Rs 5 lakh  C) Net worth of Rs 25 lakh  D) Net worth of Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Net tangible assets of Rs 5 lakh**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 as amended in 2020, an individual investment adviser or a partnership firm must maintain **net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh** at all times. A non-individual adviser must maintain a net worth of Rs 50 lakh.\n\n- **A) Net tangible assets of Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the pre-2020 figure for individuals and is the current figure for an individual research analyst.\n- **C) Net worth of Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh was the pre-2020 requirement for non-individual advisers.\n- **D) Net worth of Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; that is the requirement for a non-individual adviser, not an individual.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q112.** Anagha's client count is about to cross 150. What does the regulation require her to do?\nA) Nothing; the client count has no regulatory consequence for an individual adviser  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted  C) Appoint a compliance officer but continue to operate as an individual adviser  D) Surrender her certificate and re-apply after a cooling-off period of six months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted**\n\nAn individual investment adviser whose number of clients **exceeds 150** must apply for registration as a **non-individual** investment adviser, and must not onboard fresh clients as an individual until that registration is obtained. This is the corporatisation threshold.\n\n- **A) Nothing; the client count has no regulatory consequence for an individual adviser** - wrong; the 150-client threshold is an express trigger in the regulations.\n- **C) Appoint a compliance officer but continue to operate as an individual adviser** - wrong; a compliance officer is required of a non-individual adviser, but appointing one does not substitute for changing the registration category.\n- **D) Surrender her certificate and re-apply after a cooling-off period of six months** - wrong; registration is permanent and no cooling-off period is prescribed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q113.** What is the maximum advisory fee Anagha may charge the family with assets under advice of Rs 82,00,000 in a year under the AUA mode?\nA) Rs 1,25,000  B) Rs 2,05,000  C) Rs 2,50,000  D) Rs 4,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,05,000**\n\nIn the AUA mode the ceiling is **2.5% of assets under advice per annum per family of client**, across all services. 2.5% × 82,00,000 = **Rs 2,05,000**. Because the AUA is above the Rs 50 lakh break-even, the AUA mode permits more than the fixed-fee mode.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; Rs 1,25,000 a year is the ceiling in the fixed-fee mode, and only one mode may apply to a client at a time.\n- **C) Rs 2,50,000** - wrong; this applies 2.5% to Rs 1 crore rather than to the actual AUA.\n- **D) Rs 4,10,000** - wrong; this applies 5%, double the permitted cap.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q114.** How must Anagha treat Mr Fernandes's self-declared aggressive risk attitude?\nA) Advise an aggressive equity allocation, since the risk profiling questionnaire is the client's own declaration and binds the adviser  B) Ignore the questionnaire entirely and use only her own judgement, without recording anything  C) Interpret the responses appropriately and let his low capacity to absorb loss govern the advice, communicating the assessed risk profile back to him  D) Obtain a signed waiver of suitability from him and then advise whatever he asks for\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Interpret the responses appropriately and let his low capacity to absorb loss govern the advice, communicating the assessed risk profile back to him**\n\nRegulation 16 requires the adviser to assess the risk the client is **both willing and able** to take, to assess the capacity for absorbing loss, to interpret the responses appropriately without attributing inappropriate weight to particular answers, and to communicate the risk profile to the client. Where willingness and ability diverge, the **lower of the two governs**. Regulation 17 then requires the recommendation to be appropriate to that profile.\n\n- **A) Advise an aggressive equity allocation, since the risk profiling questionnaire is the client's own declaration and binds the adviser** - wrong; the questionnaire is an input to be interpreted, not an instruction that overrides risk capacity.\n- **B) Ignore the questionnaire entirely and use only her own judgement, without recording anything** - wrong; the information must be collected, assessed, communicated and recorded under Regulations 16 and 19.\n- **D) Obtain a signed waiver of suitability from him and then advise whatever he asks for** - wrong; the suitability obligation under Regulation 17 is a fiduciary duty and cannot be waived by the client.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q115.** Anagha's husband proposes to distribute regular-plan mutual funds to her advisory clients, keeping the commission in his own ARN. Is this permissible?\nA) Yes, because the commission is earned by a different person under a different registration  B) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed to each client in writing every quarter  C) No; the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to that adviser's clients  D) No, unless the advisory business is placed in a separately identifiable department or division\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No; the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to that adviser's clients**\n\nRegulation 22 provides that an individual investment adviser **shall not provide distribution services at all**, and that the **family of the individual adviser shall not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**. Spouse, children and parents are within the family of an individual adviser, so the arrangement is prohibited outright.\n\n- **A) Yes, because the commission is earned by a different person under a different registration** - wrong; the rule is drafted precisely to catch this routing through a family member.\n- **B) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed to each client in writing every quarter** - wrong; disclosure does not cure a prohibition, and Regulation 15(2) bars consideration from anyone other than the client.\n- **D) No, unless the advisory business is placed in a separately identifiable department or division** - wrong; the SIDD route with client-level segregation at group level is available only to a **non-individual** adviser.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 6: Rebalancing the Deshmukhs' portfolio after a life change\n\nThe Deshmukhs' Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic asset allocation of 65% equity, 30% debt and 5% gold, with a tolerance band of plus or minus 5 percentage points around each class, reviewed annually. At the review date the portfolio is worth Rs 96,00,000, made up of equity funds Rs 68,64,000, debt funds Rs 22,56,000 and gold Rs 4,80,000. Since the last review two things have changed in their lives: Kunal has moved to a commission-linked role in which roughly a third of his pay is now variable, and Shreya has begun a two-year career break to care for her mother. Their adviser therefore proposes cutting the long-term equity target to 50%, raising debt to 45% and leaving gold at 5%. Equity and debt markets have themselves not changed direction.\n\n**Q116.** What is the current weight of equity in the portfolio?\nA) 65.00%  B) 68.64%  C) 71.50%  D) 76.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 71.50%**\n\nEquity weight = 68,64,000 / 96,00,000 = 0.7150 = **71.50%**. That is 6.5 percentage points above the 65% target, so the class has drifted outside the plus-or-minus-5-point band and rebalancing is triggered. (Debt is 22,56,000 / 96,00,000 = 23.50% and gold is 5.00%.)\n\n- **A) 65.00%** - wrong; that is the strategic target, not the actual drifted weight.\n- **B) 68.64%** - wrong; this is the equity value in lakhs read as a percentage.\n- **D) 76.50%** - wrong; this adds the gold holding to equity, but gold is a separate asset class with its own 5% target.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q117.** Ignoring the proposed change of target for a moment, how much equity must be sold to restore the existing 65:30:5 allocation?\nA) Rs 2,88,000  B) Rs 6,24,000  C) Rs 9,12,000  D) Rs 20,64,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 6,24,000**\n\nTarget equity = 65% × 96,00,000 = Rs 62,40,000. Current equity = Rs 68,64,000. Sale required = 68,64,000 − 62,40,000 = **Rs 6,24,000**. That same amount moves into debt, whose target of 30% × 96,00,000 = Rs 28,80,000 exceeds its current Rs 22,56,000 by exactly Rs 6,24,000. Gold is already on target at Rs 4,80,000.\n\n- **A) Rs 2,88,000** - wrong; this is not produced by the target arithmetic.\n- **C) Rs 9,12,000** - wrong; this overstates the drift by using a 6.5-point gap on a wrong base.\n- **D) Rs 20,64,000** - wrong; that is the sale required to reach the proposed **new** 50% target, not the existing 65% one.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q118.** If the new 50:45:5 allocation is adopted at the same portfolio value, how much equity must be sold in total?\nA) Rs 6,24,000  B) Rs 14,40,000  C) Rs 20,64,000  D) Rs 48,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 20,64,000**\n\nNew target equity = 50% × 96,00,000 = Rs 48,00,000. Current equity = Rs 68,64,000. Sale required = 68,64,000 − 48,00,000 = **Rs 20,64,000**, all of which is redeployed into debt, taking debt from Rs 22,56,000 to its new 45% target of Rs 43,20,000.\n\n- **A) Rs 6,24,000** - wrong; that only restores the old 65% target and stops short of the new one.\n- **B) Rs 14,40,000** - wrong; this is the further sale needed after already rebalancing to 65% (62,40,000 − 48,00,000), not the total from the current position.\n- **D) Rs 48,00,000** - wrong; that is the new target equity holding, not the amount to be sold.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q119.** How should the reduction of the equity target from 65% to 50% be classified?\nA) Tactical asset allocation, because it is a temporary deviation to be reversed later  B) Strategic asset allocation, because it is driven by a change in the clients' circumstances and risk capacity  C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement, because the new weight lies outside the stated band  D) Constant-mix rebalancing, because the target weights remain fixed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Strategic asset allocation, because it is driven by a change in the clients' circumstances and risk capacity**\n\nStrategic asset allocation is about the **client**; tactical asset allocation is about the **market**. Variable income and the loss of a second salary reduce the household's ability to bear risk, so the long-term policy mix itself is revised and the IPS is rewritten. Nothing here rests on a view about market prices.\n\n- **A) Tactical asset allocation, because it is a temporary deviation to be reversed later** - wrong; TAA is a temporary, market-driven deviation that lives inside the existing bands and is reversed. This change is permanent and client-driven.\n- **C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement, because the new weight lies outside the stated band** - wrong; the IPS is being formally amended, which is exactly what a life event is supposed to trigger. A breach is an unauthorised move outside the band.\n- **D) Constant-mix rebalancing, because the target weights remain fixed** - wrong; the target weights are precisely what is being changed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q120.** To implement the shift, the adviser suggests directing the next several months of fresh SIP instalments entirely into the debt fund rather than selling more equity units. What is the principal advantage of doing so?\nA) It guarantees a higher return than selling equity, because debt markets are less volatile  B) It rebalances the portfolio without triggering capital gains tax, exit loads or transaction costs  C) It removes the need to review the Investment Policy Statement again  D) It converts the portfolio from a constant-mix strategy into a buy-and-hold strategy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It rebalances the portfolio without triggering capital gains tax, exit loads or transaction costs**\n\nRebalancing with **new money** — directing fresh SIP instalments and lump sums into the underweight class — moves the weights towards target with **zero selling, and therefore zero capital gains tax, no exit load and no impact cost**. It is the cheapest rebalancing tool an adviser has, though on its own it works only gradually.\n\n- **A) It guarantees a higher return than selling equity, because debt markets are less volatile** - wrong; rebalancing is a risk-control tool, and no return is guaranteed by the choice of implementation route.\n- **C) It removes the need to review the Investment Policy Statement again** - wrong; periodic review remains a standing obligation regardless of how rebalancing is executed.\n- **D) It converts the portfolio from a constant-mix strategy into a buy-and-hold strategy** - wrong; directing new money to the underweight class is a way of maintaining target weights, which is constant-mix behaviour, not buy-and-hold.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section C — Caselets (3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 7: Aarav's education and the Deshmukhs' home loan\n\nKunal (39) and Shreya (36) Deshmukh of Powai, Mumbai, have a combined gross income of Rs 3,25,000 a month and existing EMIs of Rs 91,000 a month. Their elder child Aarav will start an undergraduate course in exactly 7 years. The course costs Rs 28,00,000 at today's prices and education costs are inflating at 9% a year. They have already earmarked Rs 9,00,000 in an equity fund for this goal, and expect it to compound at 11% a year. Any fresh monthly SIP for this goal will be invested in a portfolio expected to return 12% a year, compounded monthly, with instalments at the end of each month. Separately, they are negotiating a home loan of Rs 85,00,000 at 8.75% a year for 22 years, on which the lender will not permit total EMIs to exceed 45% of gross monthly income.\n\n**Q121.** What is the shortfall the Deshmukhs must fund through fresh investing for Aarav's education?\nA) Rs 19,00,000  B) Rs 32,49,966  C) Rs 42,18,510  D) Rs 51,18,510\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 32,49,966**\n\nStep 1 — inflate the goal: 28,00,000 × (1.09)^7 = 28,00,000 × 1.828039 = Rs 51,18,510.\nStep 2 — grow what is already earmarked: 9,00,000 × (1.11)^7 = 9,00,000 × 2.076160 = Rs 18,68,544.\nStep 3 — the gap: 51,18,510 − 18,68,544 = **Rs 32,49,966**.\n\n- **A) Rs 19,00,000** - wrong; this subtracts today's earmarked Rs 9,00,000 from today's cost of Rs 28,00,000, inflating neither figure.\n- **C) Rs 42,18,510** - wrong; this inflates the goal but leaves the earmarked corpus at its present value of Rs 9,00,000 instead of growing it for 7 years.\n- **D) Rs 51,18,510** - wrong; this is the future cost of the goal before giving credit for the existing corpus.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q122.** What monthly SIP, starting at the end of this month, will close that gap in 7 years?\nA) Rs 24,871  B) Rs 25,856  C) Rs 38,690  D) Rs 39,171\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 24,871**\n\nConvert the period first: r = 12% / 12 = 0.01 a month, n = 7 × 12 = 84 months. (1.01)^84 = 2.306723.\nPMT = Gap × r / [(1+r)^n − 1] = 32,49,966 × 0.01 / (2.306723 − 1) = 32,499.66 / 1.306723 = **Rs 24,871 a month**.\n\n- **B) Rs 25,856** - wrong; this uses 11% a year (the rate on the existing corpus) instead of the 12% expected on the fresh SIP.\n- **C) Rs 38,690** - wrong; this simply divides the gap by 84 months, ignoring compounding altogether.\n- **D) Rs 39,171** - wrong; this annuitises the full future cost of Rs 51,18,510 without deducting the grown value of the existing corpus.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q123.** Of the Rs 32,49,966 gap, how much is supplied by investment returns rather than by the Deshmukhs' own contributions?\nA) Rs 11,60,802  B) Rs 18,68,544  C) Rs 20,89,164  D) Rs 32,49,966\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 11,60,802**\n\nTotal contributed through the SIP = 24,871 × 84 = Rs 20,89,164. The SIP is designed to reach Rs 32,49,966. Returns therefore supply 32,49,966 − 20,89,164 = **Rs 11,60,802**, about 36% of the gap.\n\n- **B) Rs 18,68,544** - wrong; that is the future value of the separate Rs 9,00,000 already earmarked, which sits outside the gap being funded.\n- **C) Rs 20,89,164** - wrong; that is the total contributed, not the return element.\n- **D) Rs 32,49,966** - wrong; that is the whole gap, contributions included.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q124.** What monthly EMI would the proposed Rs 85,00,000 home loan carry?\nA) Rs 32,197  B) Rs 61,979  C) Rs 72,652  D) Rs 75,115\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 72,652**\n\nr = 8.75% / 12 = 0.00729167 a month; n = 22 × 12 = 264 months; (1.00729167)^264 = 6.807437.\nEMI = P × r(1+r)^n / [(1+r)^n − 1] = 85,00,000 × (0.00729167 × 6.807437) / (6.807437 − 1) = 85,00,000 × 0.049638 / 5.807437 = **Rs 72,652**.\n\n- **A) Rs 32,197** - wrong; this is principal divided by 264, i.e. the loan with no interest at all.\n- **B) Rs 61,979** - wrong; this is the first month's interest (85,00,000 × 0.00729167), which is only part of the first EMI.\n- **D) Rs 75,115** - wrong; this uses a 20-year tenure (240 months) rather than the 22 years quoted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q125.** Given the lender's 45% cap on total EMIs, what is the largest home loan the Deshmukhs can be sanctioned on the same terms?\nA) Rs 55,25,000  B) Rs 64,64,074  C) Rs 85,00,000  D) Rs 1,45,86,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 64,64,074**\n\nPermitted total EMIs = 45% × 3,25,000 = Rs 1,46,250. Existing EMIs of Rs 91,000 must be deducted, leaving capacity of Rs 55,250 a month — less than the Rs 72,652 the Rs 85 lakh loan would demand, so that loan fails the test. The maximum loan is the present value of an EMI of Rs 55,250 for 264 months at 0.00729167 a month:\nP = 55,250 × [1 − (1.00729167)^−264] / 0.00729167 = 55,250 × 116.9968 = **Rs 64,64,074**, roughly Rs 64.6 lakh.\n\n- **A) Rs 55,25,000** - wrong; this treats the monthly EMI capacity of Rs 55,250 as a loan amount by moving the decimal point.\n- **C) Rs 85,00,000** - wrong; the loan they asked for needs an EMI of Rs 72,652, well above the Rs 55,250 permitted.\n- **D) Rs 1,45,86,000** - wrong; this multiplies the EMI capacity by 264 months without discounting, so it counts all the future interest as principal.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 8: Ranking three fund managers\n\nKunal's adviser reviews three equity funds over the same five-year period. The risk-free rate throughout was 6.5% a year and the market index returned 13.0% a year with a standard deviation of 15.0%.\n\n| Fund | Return | Standard deviation | Beta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Kaveri Equity Fund | 17.4% | 18.0% | 1.15 |\n| Nilgiri Growth Fund | 14.2% | 11.0% | 0.82 |\n| Sahyadri Opportunities Fund | 20.1% | 26.0% | 1.40 |\n\nKunal is also considering a two-fund portfolio: 60% in Kaveri Equity Fund and 40% in a short-duration debt fund that returned 7.8% a year with a standard deviation of 6.0%. The correlation between the two funds is 0.25.\n\n**Q126.** Which fund ranks highest on the Sharpe ratio, and what is that ratio?\nA) Kaveri, 0.61  B) Nilgiri, 0.70  C) Sahyadri, 0.52  D) Sahyadri, 0.97\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Nilgiri, 0.70**\n\nSharpe = (Rp − Rf) / standard deviation.\nKaveri = (17.4 − 6.5) / 18.0 = 10.9 / 18.0 = 0.61.\nNilgiri = (14.2 − 6.5) / 11.0 = 7.7 / 11.0 = **0.70**.\nSahyadri = (20.1 − 6.5) / 26.0 = 13.6 / 26.0 = 0.52.\nNilgiri wins on excess return per unit of **total** risk despite having the lowest raw return.\n\n- **A) Kaveri, 0.61** - wrong; 0.61 is Kaveri's Sharpe ratio correctly computed, but it is not the highest of the three.\n- **C) Sahyadri, 0.52** - wrong; 0.52 is Sahyadri's Sharpe ratio, and it is the lowest, not the highest — its 20.1% return came with 26% volatility.\n- **D) Sahyadri, 0.97** - wrong; this figure comes from dividing the excess return by the beta of 1.40 in a way that matches neither ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q127.** Which fund ranks highest on the Treynor ratio, and what is that ratio?\nA) Kaveri, 9.48  B) Nilgiri, 9.39  C) Sahyadri, 9.71  D) Nilgiri, 0.70\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Sahyadri, 9.71**\n\nTreynor = (Rp − Rf) / beta.\nKaveri = 10.9 / 1.15 = 9.48.\nNilgiri = 7.7 / 0.82 = 9.39.\nSahyadri = 13.6 / 1.40 = **9.71**.\nThe ranking reverses relative to Sharpe: Sahyadri is best on systematic risk and worst on total risk, which is the classic signature of a **poorly diversified** portfolio carrying a lot of unsystematic risk.\n\n- **A) Kaveri, 9.48** - wrong; that is Kaveri's Treynor ratio, correctly computed but second.\n- **B) Nilgiri, 9.39** - wrong; that is Nilgiri's Treynor ratio, the lowest of the three, even though Nilgiri leads on Sharpe.\n- **D) Nilgiri, 0.70** - wrong; 0.70 is Nilgiri's Sharpe ratio, not a Treynor ratio; Treynor divides by beta and is read in percentage points.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q128.** By how much does Kaveri's Jensen's alpha exceed Nilgiri's?\nA) 1.06 percentage points  B) 2.37 percentage points  C) 3.20 percentage points  D) 3.43 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.06 percentage points**\n\nMarket risk premium = 13.0 − 6.5 = 6.5%.\nKaveri: required return = 6.5 + 1.15 × 6.5 = 13.975%; alpha = 17.4 − 13.975 = 3.425%.\nNilgiri: required return = 6.5 + 0.82 × 6.5 = 11.83%; alpha = 14.2 − 11.83 = 2.37%.\nDifference = 3.425 − 2.37 = **1.055, i.e. about 1.06 percentage points**.\n\n- **B) 2.37 percentage points** - wrong; that is Nilgiri's own alpha, not the difference.\n- **C) 3.20 percentage points** - wrong; that is simply Kaveri's return minus Nilgiri's return (17.4 − 14.2), which ignores the very different betas.\n- **D) 3.43 percentage points** - wrong; that is Kaveri's own alpha, not the excess over Nilgiri's.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q129.** What is the standard deviation of the 60:40 portfolio of Kaveri and the debt fund?\nA) 11.06%  B) 11.63%  C) 12.24%  D) 13.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 11.63%**\n\nVariance = w1²σ1² + w2²σ2² + 2·w1·w2·σ1·σ2·ρ\n= (0.6² × 18²) + (0.4² × 6²) + (2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 18 × 6 × 0.25)\n= 116.64 + 5.76 + 12.96 = 135.36.\nStandard deviation = √135.36 = **11.63%**.\n\n- **A) 11.06%** - wrong; this is √122.40, obtained by dropping the covariance term, i.e. assuming a correlation of zero.\n- **C) 12.24%** - wrong; this is not produced by the formula with the stated inputs.\n- **D) 13.20%** - wrong; this is the weighted average of the two standard deviations (0.6 × 18 + 0.4 × 6), which is what the portfolio's risk would be only if the correlation were exactly 1. The 1.57-point gap is the diversification benefit.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q130.** What is the Sharpe ratio of that 60:40 portfolio?\nA) 0.53  B) 0.61  C) 0.70  D) 1.17\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.61**\n\nPortfolio return = 0.6 × 17.4 + 0.4 × 7.8 = 10.44 + 3.12 = 13.56%.\nSharpe = (13.56 − 6.5) / 11.63 = 7.06 / 11.63 = **0.61**. Adding a low-correlation debt sleeve to Kaveri lifts the Sharpe ratio only marginally above Kaveri's own 0.61 here, because the debt fund's excess return over the risk-free rate is itself small.\n\n- **A) 0.53** - wrong; this divides the excess return by the weighted-average standard deviation of 13.20% instead of the true portfolio standard deviation of 11.63%.\n- **C) 0.70** - wrong; that is Nilgiri's stand-alone Sharpe ratio.\n- **D) 1.17** - wrong; this divides the excess return by the debt fund's standard deviation of 6.0% alone.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 9: Nikhil Rao's bond and index positions\n\nNikhil Rao is reviewing a corporate bond and two index positions. The bond has a face value of Rs 1,000, pays an 8.4% annual coupon, has exactly 6 years to maturity, and the market's required yield on comparable paper is 9.6% a year. Its Macaulay duration is 4.85 years. Nikhil holds a portfolio of similar bonds with a total market value of Rs 40,00,000, and the market is pricing in a further 60 basis point rise in yields. Separately, the Nifty spot is 24,000, the three-month risk-free rate is 7% a year, the index dividend yield is 1.2% a year, and the three-month Nifty future is quoted at 24,395. Nikhil also buys **two lots** of a Nifty call option struck at 24,200 at a premium of Rs 285 per unit; the lot size is 25 and the index settles at 24,860 on expiry.\n\n**Q131.** What is the fair price of the corporate bond?\nA) Rs 921.97  B) Rs 947.12  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,054.81\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 947.12**\n\nA bond is an annuity of coupons plus a lump sum at redemption.\nAnnuity factor = [1 − (1.096)^−6] / 0.096 = 4.406792.\nPV of coupons = 84 × 4.406792 = Rs 370.17.\nPV of redemption = 1,000 / (1.096)^6 = 1,000 / 1.729778 = Rs 576.95.\nPrice = 370.17 + 576.95 = **Rs 947.12**, a discount, as it must be when the 8.4% coupon is below the 9.6% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 921.97** - wrong; that is the estimated price *after* the further 60 bp rise in yields, not the price today.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would trade at par only if the required yield equalled the 8.4% coupon.\n- **D) Rs 1,054.81** - wrong; this swaps the coupon and the yield, discounting a 9.6% coupon at 8.4%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q132.** What is the bond's modified duration, and what rupee loss does the 60 basis point rise imply on Nikhil's Rs 40,00,000 holding?\nA) 4.43 and a loss of Rs 1,06,204  B) 4.43 and a loss of Rs 17,701  C) 4.85 and a loss of Rs 1,16,400  D) 5.32 and a loss of Rs 1,27,574\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 4.43 and a loss of Rs 1,06,204**\n\nModified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y) = 4.85 / 1.096 = **4.4252, i.e. about 4.43**.\nPercentage price change ≈ −MD × Δy = −4.4252 × 0.0060 = −2.6551%.\nRupee loss = 2.6551% × 40,00,000 = **Rs 1,06,204**.\n\n- **B) 4.43 and a loss of Rs 17,701** - wrong; the modified duration is right but the yield change has been taken as 10 basis points instead of 60.\n- **C) 4.85 and a loss of Rs 1,16,400** - wrong; this uses the Macaulay duration directly without dividing by (1 + y), overstating the sensitivity.\n- **D) 5.32 and a loss of Rs 1,27,574** - wrong; this multiplies the Macaulay duration by (1 + y) instead of dividing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q133.** Using that duration estimate, what is the bond's approximate price after the 60 basis point rise?\nA) Rs 905.21  B) Rs 921.97  C) Rs 941.44  D) Rs 972.27\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 921.97**\n\nNew price ≈ Current price × (1 + percentage change) = 947.12 × (1 − 0.026551) = 947.12 − 25.15 = **Rs 921.97**. Because the price–yield relationship is convex, the true price will be slightly above this straight-line estimate — duration alone always overstates the fall when yields rise.\n\n- **A) Rs 905.21** - wrong; this applies the modified duration to a 100 basis point move rather than 60 basis points.\n- **C) Rs 941.44** - wrong; this deducts a flat 0.60% from the price instead of multiplying the yield change by the modified duration.\n- **D) Rs 972.27** - wrong; this adds 2.6551% to the price, reversing the sign — prices fall when yields rise.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q134.** What is the fair value of the three-month Nifty future, and how does the quoted price of 24,395 compare?\nA) 24,348; overpriced by about 47 points  B) 24,341; overpriced by about 54 points  C) 24,409; underpriced by about 14 points  D) 25,392; underpriced by about 997 points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 24,341; overpriced by about 54 points**\n\nFair value = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 24,000 × (1 + 0.07 − 0.012)^0.25 = 24,000 × (1.058)^0.25 = 24,000 × 1.014195 = **24,340.68**, i.e. about 24,341. The quoted future at 24,395 is therefore 24,395 − 24,340.68 = **about 54 points above fair value**, an arbitrage opportunity to sell the future and buy the basket, before costs.\n\n- **A) 24,348; overpriced by about 47 points** - wrong; this prorates the net cost of carry simply (24,000 × [1 + 0.058 × 0.25]) instead of compounding it over the quarter.\n- **C) 24,409; underpriced by about 14 points** - wrong; this ignores the 1.2% dividend yield, which reduces the cost of carry.\n- **D) 25,392; underpriced by about 997 points** - wrong; this applies the full annual net carry of 5.8% to a three-month contract, forgetting the exponent T = 0.25.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q135.** What is Nikhil's net profit on the two lots of the Nifty call at expiry?\nA) Rs 9,375  B) Rs 14,250  C) Rs 18,750  D) Rs 33,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 18,750**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(Spot − Strike, 0) = max(24,860 − 24,200, 0) = Rs 660 per unit.\nNet payoff per unit = 660 − 285 premium = Rs 375.\nTotal units = 2 lots × 25 = 50.\nNet profit = 375 × 50 = **Rs 18,750**. (The break-even was 24,200 + 285 = 24,485, comfortably below the settlement level.)\n\n- **A) Rs 9,375** - wrong; this computes the profit on a single lot of 25 units, but he bought two lots.\n- **B) Rs 14,250** - wrong; this is the total premium paid (285 × 50), which is his maximum loss, not his profit.\n- **D) Rs 33,000** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value (660 × 50) with the premium never deducted.\n\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "03", "md": "# Mock Paper 3 — NISM Series X-A (Investment Adviser Level 1)\n\n**Total: 150 marks · 135 questions · Time: 3 hours · Pass: 90/150 (60%) · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n## Section A — Independent MCQs (90 questions × 1 mark = 90 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Jignesh Bhatt, a 52-year-old textile-machinery business owner in Ahmedabad, has engaged an Investment Adviser. Which sequence correctly describes the six-step financial planning process?\nA) Data gathering → Client-planner relationship → Goals → Risk profiling → Portfolio construction → Review  B) Client-planner relationship → Data gathering → Goals → Risk profiling → Portfolio construction → Review  C) Client-planner relationship → Goals → Data gathering → Portfolio construction → Risk profiling → Review  D) Goals → Client-planner relationship → Risk profiling → Data gathering → Review → Portfolio construction\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Client-planner relationship → Data gathering → Goals → Risk profiling → Portfolio construction → Review**\n\nThe six steps in order are: (1) establish the **client–planner relationship** (scope, services, compensation, in writing), (2) **gather data** on the client's financial situation, (3) define **needs and financial goals**, (4) **risk profiling** and asset allocation, (5) **portfolio construction**, (6) **reviewing and rebalancing**. The engagement terms must be settled before any data is collected.\n\n- **A) Data gathering → Client-planner relationship → ...** - wrong; data cannot be collected before the engagement and its scope are agreed.\n- **C) Client-planner relationship → Goals → Data gathering → ...** - wrong; goals are defined after the data is in hand, and risk profiling precedes construction rather than following it.\n- **D) Goals → Client-planner relationship → ...** - wrong on both the starting point and the placement of review before construction.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Jignesh has a net worth of Rs 10.8 crore, stable business income and a 13-year horizon to his target retirement date, but he tells his adviser that a 15% fall in his portfolio would make him \"sell everything the same week\". How should the adviser set the allocation?\nA) To his high risk capacity, since capacity is objective and measurable  B) To his low risk tolerance, since the plan must be built to the lower of capacity and tolerance  C) To the simple average of capacity and tolerance  D) To the risk requirement of the goal, ignoring both capacity and tolerance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) To his low risk tolerance, since the plan must be built to the lower of capacity and tolerance**\n\n**Risk capacity** is the objective *ability* to bear risk (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants). **Risk tolerance** is the subjective *willingness*. Where they conflict, the plan is built to the **lower** of the two — a client with high capacity but low tolerance will panic-sell at the bottom, converting a temporary paper loss into a permanent one.\n\n- **A) To his high risk capacity** - wrong; objectivity does not override the client's demonstrated behaviour under stress.\n- **C) To the simple average** - wrong; there is no averaging rule, and the average still exceeds what he can psychologically hold.\n- **D) To the risk requirement of the goal** - wrong; the risk requirement tells you what return the goal *needs*, but it can never override the binding constraint.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Jignesh's assets are: savings account Rs 3,20,000; liquid fund Rs 2,40,000; equity mutual funds Rs 26,00,000; PPF Rs 19,00,000; residential house Rs 92,00,000; car Rs 7,00,000. His liabilities are a home loan of Rs 38,00,000 outstanding and a business overdraft of Rs 3,50,000. What is his net worth?\nA) Rs 1,49,60,000  B) Rs 1,11,60,000  C) Rs 1,08,10,000  D) Rs 41,50,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 1,08,10,000**\n\nTotal assets = 3,20,000 + 2,40,000 + 26,00,000 + 19,00,000 + 92,00,000 + 7,00,000 = **Rs 1,49,60,000**. Total liabilities = 38,00,000 + 3,50,000 = **Rs 41,50,000**. Net worth = Assets − Liabilities = 1,49,60,000 − 41,50,000 = **Rs 1,08,10,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,49,60,000** - wrong; this is total assets, before deducting any liabilities.\n- **B) Rs 1,11,60,000** - wrong; this deducts only the home loan and ignores the business overdraft.\n- **D) Rs 41,50,000** - wrong; this is total liabilities, not net worth.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Jignesh wants to ensure that his factory shareholding and his Ahmedabad property pass to his two children without dispute after his lifetime. Which component of the scope of financial planning does this fall under?\nA) Personal financial analysis  B) Retirement planning  C) Estate planning  D) Debt management and counselling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Estate planning**\n\n**Estate planning** ensures assets pass to the intended beneficiaries smoothly, using wills, nominations, trusts and joint holdings. Succession of a business shareholding and property is squarely an estate planning issue.\n\n- **A) Personal financial analysis** - wrong; that is the diagnosis stage covering the balance sheet, cash flow, ratios and goal setting.\n- **B) Retirement planning** - wrong; that estimates and builds the corpus needed to fund his own post-retirement expenses during his lifetime.\n- **D) Debt management and counselling** - wrong; that concerns the purpose, cost, maturity and serviceability of borrowing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which statement best describes the defining obligation of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser compared with a product distributor?\nA) The adviser must guarantee that recommended products beat their benchmark  B) The adviser is a fiduciary and must place the client's interest ahead of their own  C) The adviser may recommend only products on which no commission is payable to anyone in the market  D) The adviser must earn income exclusively from the execution of transactions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The adviser is a fiduciary and must place the client's interest ahead of their own**\n\nA **fiduciary** duty means the client's interest comes *ahead of* the adviser's own. A distributor may operate under a lower suitability standard and is typically paid by the manufacturer. SEBI reinforces the distinction through the **segregation of advice and distribution** — an individual Investment Adviser cannot do both.\n\n- **A) Must guarantee outperformance** - wrong; no adviser can guarantee returns, and planning success is measured by whether the goal was met, not by beating a benchmark.\n- **C) Only products paying no commission to anyone** - wrong; the requirement is segregation and disclosure of conflicts, not the removal of commission-bearing products from the market.\n- **D) Income exclusively from execution** - wrong; that describes distribution, the very activity that must be kept separate from advice.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Jignesh invests a business bonus of Rs 18,50,000 in a scheme expected to compound at 9.5% per annum. What will it grow to in 8 years?\nA) Rs 32,56,000  B) Rs 34,91,970  C) Rs 38,23,708  D) Rs 41,86,960\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 38,23,708**\n\nFV = PV × (1+r)^n = 18,50,000 × (1.095)^8. (1.095)^8 = 2.066869. FV = 18,50,000 × 2.066869 = **Rs 38,23,708**.\n\n- **A) Rs 32,56,000** - wrong; this uses simple interest, 18,50,000 × (1 + 0.095 × 8) = 18,50,000 × 1.76.\n- **B) Rs 34,91,970** - wrong; this compounds for only 7 years.\n- **D) Rs 41,86,960** - wrong; this compounds for 9 years.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** An investment made by Jignesh's wife Rupal grew from Rs 6,40,000 to Rs 21,60,000 over 11 years. What is the CAGR?\nA) 9.85%  B) 11.69%  C) 13.42%  D) 21.59%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 11.69%**\n\nCAGR = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (21,60,000 ÷ 6,40,000)^(1/11) − 1 = (3.375)^0.090909 − 1 = 1.11693 − 1 = **11.69% p.a.**\n\n- **A) 9.85%** - wrong; this understates the rate, as would result from spreading the same growth over 13 years.\n- **C) 13.42%** - wrong; this overstates the rate, as would result from using 9 years.\n- **D) 21.59%** - wrong; this is simple-interest thinking — a total gain of 237.5% divided by 11 years, which ignores compounding entirely.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Jignesh starts a monthly SIP of Rs 22,000 for 13 years, expecting 11.5% per annum compounded monthly, with each instalment paid at the end of the month. What corpus does he accumulate?\nA) Rs 34,32,000  B) Rs 44,36,824  C) Rs 78,68,824  D) Rs 79,44,233\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 78,68,824**\n\nr = 0.115 ÷ 12 = 0.00958333; n = 13 × 12 = 156. (1.00958333)^156 = 4.42771. FV = 22,000 × [(4.42771 − 1) ÷ 0.00958333] = 22,000 × 357.674 = **Rs 78,68,824**.\n\n- **A) Rs 34,32,000** - wrong; this is only the total contributed (22,000 × 156), ignoring all returns.\n- **B) Rs 44,36,824** - wrong; this is the return component alone, without adding back the contributions.\n- **D) Rs 79,44,233** - wrong; this treats the SIP as an annuity due (× 1.00958333), but the instalments are paid at the end of each month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Jignesh needs a retirement corpus of Rs 1,75,00,000 in 8 years and expects 10.8% per annum compounded monthly. What monthly investment is required?\nA) Rs 1,15,513  B) Rs 1,82,292  C) Rs 2,73,013  D) Rs 96,340\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 1,15,513**\n\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1+r)^n − 1]. r = 0.108 ÷ 12 = 0.009; n = 96. (1.009)^96 = 2.36348. PMT = (1,75,00,000 × 0.009) ÷ (2.36348 − 1) = 1,57,500 ÷ 1.36348 = **Rs 1,15,513 a month**.\n\n- **B) Rs 1,82,292** - wrong; this is simply 1,75,00,000 ÷ 96, assuming the contributions earn nothing.\n- **C) Rs 2,73,013** - wrong; this solves from the PV side (the EMI formula), treating the corpus as a loan to be amortised.\n- **D) Rs 96,340** - wrong; this understates the payment, as would follow from an incorrect rate conversion.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A debt fund in Jignesh's portfolio is expected to deliver a nominal return of 10.4% while inflation runs at 5.8%. What is the real return?\nA) 3.92%  B) 4.35%  C) 4.60%  D) 5.02%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 4.35%**\n\nReal return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.104 ÷ 1.058) − 1 = 1.043478 − 1 = **4.35%**.\n\n- **A) 3.92%** - wrong; this understates the result, as would happen if the ratio were inverted and adjusted.\n- **C) 4.60%** - wrong; this is the crude subtraction 10.4 − 5.8, which always **overstates** the real return.\n- **D) 5.02%** - wrong; this multiplies by the inflation factor instead of dividing by it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Jignesh compares two deposits. Deposit X is quoted at a nominal 8.6% per annum compounded quarterly. What is its effective annual rate?\nA) 8.60%  B) 8.88%  C) 8.95%  D) 9.15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 8.88%**\n\nEAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1 = (1 + 0.086/4)^4 − 1 = (1.0215)^4 − 1 = 1.088813 − 1 = **8.88%**.\n\n- **A) 8.60%** - wrong; this is the nominal (stated) rate, which never changes with compounding frequency — only the effective rate does.\n- **C) 8.95%** - wrong; this is the EAR under **monthly** compounding, not quarterly.\n- **D) 9.15%** - wrong; this overstates the effect — even daily compounding would not reach this level at a nominal 8.6%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** Jignesh invests Rs 3,00,000 at the **beginning** of each year for 12 years, earning 9% per annum. What is the corpus at the end of year 12?\nA) Rs 36,00,000  B) Rs 60,42,216  C) Rs 65,86,015  D) Rs 71,80,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 65,86,015**\n\nThis is an **annuity due**. FV(ordinary) = 3,00,000 × [((1.09)^12 − 1) ÷ 0.09] = 3,00,000 × 20.14072 = Rs 60,42,216. FV(due) = 60,42,216 × 1.09 = **Rs 65,86,015**.\n\n- **A) Rs 36,00,000** - wrong; this is only the total contributed (3,00,000 × 12).\n- **B) Rs 60,42,216** - wrong; this is the ordinary-annuity value, which assumes payment at the **end** of each year.\n- **D) Rs 71,80,400** - wrong; this applies the (1 + r) uplift twice.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Kaushik Shah has a savings account balance of Rs 2,10,000, a liquid fund of Rs 3,90,000 and equity mutual funds of Rs 4,00,000. His monthly household expenses are Rs 1,45,000. What is his liquidity ratio?\nA) 0.34 months  B) 4.14 months  C) 6.90 months  D) 2.69 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 4.14 months**\n\nLiquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses. Equity mutual funds are **not** liquid assets, because their value is volatile. Liquid assets = 2,10,000 + 3,90,000 = Rs 6,00,000. Ratio = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,45,000 = **4.14 months** — inside the healthy 3–6 month band, though not generous.\n\n- **A) 0.34 months** - wrong; this divides by *annual* expenses (1,45,000 × 12) instead of monthly expenses.\n- **C) 6.90 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the Rs 4,00,000 of equity funds as liquid.\n- **D) 2.69 months** - wrong; this counts only the liquid fund and omits the savings balance.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Nilima Desai has total assets of Rs 1,86,00,000 and total liabilities of Rs 62,00,000. What are her solvency ratio and leverage ratio respectively?\nA) 0.33 and 0.67  B) 0.67 and 0.33  C) 0.50 and 0.50  D) 1.50 and 0.33\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.67 and 0.33**\n\nNet worth = 1,86,00,000 − 62,00,000 = Rs 1,24,00,000. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 1,24,00,000 ÷ 1,86,00,000 = **0.67**. Leverage ratio = Total liabilities ÷ Total assets = 62,00,000 ÷ 1,86,00,000 = **0.33**. Check: 0.67 + 0.33 = 1.\n\n- **A) 0.33 and 0.67** - wrong; the two ratios are transposed.\n- **C) 0.50 and 0.50** - wrong; that would require liabilities to equal exactly half of total assets.\n- **D) 1.50 and 0.33** - wrong; 1.50 is net worth divided by liabilities, and no solvency ratio can exceed 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Hardik Trivedi has a gross monthly income of Rs 2,40,000, total EMIs of Rs 96,000 and other household expenses of Rs 78,000. What is his debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 27.50%  B) 32.50%  C) 40.00%  D) 72.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 40.00%**\n\nDebt-to-income ratio = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 96,000 ÷ 2,40,000 = **40.00%**. This sits at the very top of the healthy 35–40% band, so further borrowing should be resisted.\n\n- **A) 27.50%** - wrong; this is his savings ratio, (2,40,000 − 96,000 − 78,000) ÷ 2,40,000.\n- **B) 32.50%** - wrong; this uses only the non-EMI expenses, 78,000 ÷ 2,40,000.\n- **D) 72.50%** - wrong; this is the total expenses ratio, (96,000 + 78,000) ÷ 2,40,000.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Which statement about a contingency (emergency) fund is correct?\nA) It should be sized at 3-6 months of gross income and held largely in equity funds  B) It should be sized at 3-6 months of expenses and held in savings, sweep-in FDs or liquid funds  C) It should be sized at 12 months of income and held in PPF for the tax benefit  D) It becomes unnecessary once the client holds adequate health insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It should be sized at 3-6 months of expenses and held in savings, sweep-in FDs or liquid funds**\n\nThe fund is sized against **expenses**, because expenses are what must continue to be paid. It requires **immediate availability plus certainty of value**; return is secondary.\n\n- **A) 3-6 months of gross income, held in equity** - wrong on both counts: expenses not income, and equity is likely to be down exactly when the fund is needed.\n- **C) 12 months of income in PPF** - wrong; PPF is locked in and is not a liquid asset, so it cannot serve as an emergency reserve.\n- **D) Unnecessary with health insurance** - wrong; insurance handles medical costs but not job loss, a business downturn or urgent repairs.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Jignesh's household budget balances comfortably in most months but collapses every March, when the annual motor and health insurance premiums and a wedding-season outflow all fall due. What is the correct budgeting remedy?\nA) Increase the credit card limit so the March shortfall can be revolved  B) Reclassify the March items as discretionary and postpone them  C) Divide each such annual expense by 12 and set that amount aside every month  D) Suspend all savings from the budget until the March obligations are cleared\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Divide each such annual expense by 12 and set that amount aside every month**\n\nThese are **periodic (lumpy) outflows** — predictable but infrequent, and the most commonly ignored budget category. A budget that balances monthly but ignores them will always break in the month they fall due. The fix is to provide for them monthly.\n\n- **A) Increase the credit card limit** - wrong; revolving a card balance is the most expensive borrowing available (36–48% annualised) and does not fix the structural gap.\n- **B) Reclassify as discretionary and postpone** - wrong; insurance premiums are contractual fixed outflows and letting cover lapse destroys the protection layer of the plan.\n- **D) Suspend all savings** - wrong; this inverts \"pay yourself first\" and makes savings the residual, which is the classic failure mode.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Jignesh takes a business-premises loan of Rs 28,00,000 at 10.25% per annum for 12 years. What is the EMI?\nA) Rs 19,444  B) Rs 23,917  C) Rs 33,868  D) Rs 43,361\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 33,868**\n\nr = 0.1025 ÷ 12 = 0.00854167; n = 144. (1.00854167)^144 = 3.40341. EMI = 28,00,000 × [0.00854167 × 3.40341] ÷ [3.40341 − 1] = 28,00,000 × 0.0290708 ÷ 2.40341 = **Rs 33,868**.\n\n- **A) Rs 19,444** - wrong; this is principal ÷ number of months (28,00,000 ÷ 144), ignoring interest altogether.\n- **B) Rs 23,917** - wrong; this is only the **first month's interest**, 28,00,000 × 0.00854167.\n- **D) Rs 43,361** - wrong; this adds the first month's interest to principal ÷ n, double-counting the interest burden.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Falguni Mehta takes a Rs 15,00,000 loan at 11% per annum for 7 years, with an EMI of Rs 25,684. What is the total interest she pays over the tenure?\nA) Rs 6,57,456  B) Rs 11,55,000  C) Rs 21,57,456  D) Rs 5,49,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 6,57,456**\n\nTotal interest = (EMI × n) − P = (25,684 × 84) − 15,00,000 = 21,57,456 − 15,00,000 = **Rs 6,57,456**.\n\n- **B) Rs 11,55,000** - wrong; this is simple interest, 15,00,000 × 11% × 7, which ignores the fact that principal is being repaid throughout.\n- **C) Rs 21,57,456** - wrong; this is the **total repaid**, which includes the principal.\n- **D) Rs 5,49,000** - wrong; this understates the interest, as would result from applying the rate to an assumed average balance over too few years.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** Jignesh's home loan has an outstanding balance of Rs 34,50,000 at 8.75% per annum and an EMI of Rs 31,240. In the coming month, how much of the EMI reduces the principal?\nA) Rs 25,156  B) Rs 6,084  C) Rs 31,240  D) Rs 12,167\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 6,084**\n\nMonthly interest = Outstanding × monthly rate = 34,50,000 × (0.0875 ÷ 12) = 34,50,000 × 0.00729167 = Rs 25,156. Principal repaid = EMI − interest = 31,240 − 25,156 = **Rs 6,084**. Only about 19% of the EMI is actually reducing the debt.\n\n- **A) Rs 25,156** - wrong; this is the **interest** component, not the principal.\n- **C) Rs 31,240** - wrong; this is the whole EMI, which covers both interest and principal.\n- **D) Rs 12,167** - wrong; this splits the EMI arbitrarily instead of deriving the interest from the outstanding balance.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Jignesh has three borrowings: a gold loan, a car loan and a home loan. Which description of the security created is correct?\nA) Gold loan is a hypothecation, car loan is a pledge, home loan is a mortgage  B) Gold loan is a pledge, car loan is a hypothecation, home loan is a mortgage  C) Gold loan is a mortgage, car loan is a pledge, home loan is a hypothecation  D) All three are hypothecations because the borrower continues to use the assets\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Gold loan is a pledge, car loan is a hypothecation, home loan is a mortgage**\n\n**Mortgage** = security over **immovable** property. **Pledge** = security over **movable** property where **possession passes to the lender** (the bank holds the gold). **Hypothecation** = security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** (he keeps driving the car).\n\n- **A) Gold hypothecation, car pledge** - wrong; the two are transposed, since the lender physically holds the gold but not the car.\n- **C) Gold mortgage, home hypothecation** - wrong; a mortgage can only be created over immovable property.\n- **D) All three hypothecations** - wrong; the borrower does not retain possession of pledged gold, and a house is immovable.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Jignesh owes Rs 1,80,000 on a credit card at 42% annualised, Rs 4,50,000 on a personal loan at 15% and Rs 9,00,000 on a car loan at 10%. He wants to minimise the total interest he pays. Which repayment strategy should he follow?\nA) Snowball - clear the Rs 1,80,000 card first because it is the smallest balance  B) Avalanche - attack the 42% credit card first because it carries the highest rate  C) Blizzard - clear the car loan first because it is the largest exposure  D) Repay all three in equal proportions each month\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Avalanche - attack the 42% credit card first because it carries the highest rate**\n\nThe **avalanche** method targets the **highest interest rate first** while paying minimums on the rest. It is mathematically optimal — it minimises total interest paid.\n\n- **A) Snowball** - wrong as stated; snowball targets the smallest *balance* regardless of rate and costs more interest overall, even though it happens to pick the same loan here for the wrong reason.\n- **C) Blizzard - clear the car loan first** - wrong; blizzard is a hybrid that clears small balances for momentum and then switches to the highest rate — it never begins with the largest, cheapest loan.\n- **D) Equal proportions** - wrong; spreading repayment evenly leaves the 42% balance outstanding longest relative to its cost.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following entities is regulated by the RBI rather than by SEBI?\nA) Portfolio managers  B) Credit Information Companies  C) Alternative Investment Funds  D) Depositories\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Credit Information Companies**\n\n**Credit Information Companies** (CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark) are regulated by the **RBI** under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, 2005. The RBI also covers banking, monetary policy, currency, the money market, foreign exchange, payment systems and NBFCs.\n\n- **A) Portfolio managers** - wrong; regulated by SEBI under the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020.\n- **C) Alternative Investment Funds** - wrong; regulated by SEBI under the SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012.\n- **D) Depositories** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL are SEBI-regulated market infrastructure institutions.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which statement about payment banks in India is correct?\nA) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend  B) They may accept deposits up to Rs 5 lakh per customer and may lend to priority sectors  C) They may lend freely but cannot be part of the payment and settlement system  D) They are regulated by SEBI because they issue units to their customers\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend**\n\nPayment banks accept deposits **up to Rs 2 lakh** per customer and provide payments and remittance services, but they **cannot lend** or issue credit cards. They are regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n- **B) Rs 5 lakh and priority-sector lending** - wrong; Rs 5 lakh is the DICGC deposit insurance cover, and priority-sector focus describes **small finance banks**.\n- **C) Lend freely but outside the payment system** - wrong; this inverts the model — payment banks sit inside the payment system and are barred from lending.\n- **D) Regulated by SEBI** - wrong; all banks are regulated by the RBI, and payment banks issue deposits, not units.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Jignesh's broker executes a trade for him on the NSE. Which institution guarantees that the trade will settle even if the counterparty defaults?\nA) The stock exchange, which matched the order  B) The depository, which holds the securities in demat form  C) The clearing corporation, acting as central counterparty  D) The depository participant through whom the demat account is held\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) The clearing corporation, acting as central counterparty**\n\nThe **clearing corporation** clears and settles trades and acts as the **central counterparty (CCP)**, guaranteeing settlement so that neither buyer nor seller faces counterparty default. It manages margins and the settlement guarantee fund.\n\n- **A) The stock exchange** - wrong; the exchange **matches** trades and provides price discovery, but does not guarantee settlement.\n- **B) The depository** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL hold securities in electronic form and play no settlement-guarantee role.\n- **D) The depository participant** - wrong; the DP is the agent of the depository and the investor's access point for the demat account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which statement about an Account Aggregator (NBFC-AA) in India is correct?\nA) It lends on its own book against the customer's aggregated financial data  B) It shares the customer's financial data only with explicit consent and is data blind  C) It is registered with SEBI as a market infrastructure institution  D) It guarantees a minimum return to lenders who rely on its data\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It shares the customer's financial data only with explicit consent and is data blind**\n\nAn **NBFC-AA** is RBI-regulated. With the customer's **explicit consent** it retrieves and shares financial data across institutions, and it is **data blind** — it can neither read nor store the data it moves.\n\n- **A) Lends on its own book** - wrong; an AA does not lend at all, and the same prohibition on own-book lending applies to NBFC-P2P platforms.\n- **C) Registered with SEBI as an MII** - wrong; the three MIIs are exchanges, clearing corporations and depositories, while AAs are RBI-registered NBFCs.\n- **D) Guarantees a minimum return** - wrong; guaranteeing returns is expressly prohibited for these platforms.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which combination correctly describes the money market?\nA) Instruments of up to 1 year maturity, regulated by the RBI  B) Instruments of up to 1 year maturity, regulated by SEBI  C) Instruments of over 1 year maturity, regulated by the RBI  D) Instruments of over 1 year maturity, regulated by SEBI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Instruments of up to 1 year maturity, regulated by the RBI**\n\nThe **money market** deals in short-term instruments of **up to one year** — T-bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit, call money and repos — and is regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n- **B) Up to 1 year, SEBI** - wrong regulator; SEBI regulates the securities and capital markets.\n- **C) Over 1 year, RBI** - wrong maturity; anything beyond one year belongs to the capital market.\n- **D) Over 1 year, SEBI** - wrong; this correctly describes the **capital market**, not the money market.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which statement correctly contrasts a rights issue with a bonus issue?\nA) Both raise fresh capital for the company  B) A rights issue raises money for the company, a bonus issue does not  C) A bonus issue raises money for the company, a rights issue does not  D) Neither raises money, since both are made only to existing shareholders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A rights issue raises money for the company, a bonus issue does not**\n\nA **rights issue** offers existing shareholders the chance to **buy** more shares pro rata at a discount, so cash flows into the company; the entitlement is renounceable. A **bonus issue** is a **capitalisation of reserves** that gives free shares — **no money is raised** and the shareholder's total value is unchanged.\n\n- **A) Both raise capital** - wrong; a bonus issue merely converts reserves into share capital within the balance sheet.\n- **C) Bonus raises money, rights does not** - wrong; the two are transposed.\n- **D) Neither raises money** - wrong; being offered to existing shareholders does not prevent a rights issue from raising cash.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Jignesh holds 600 shares of a company trading at Rs 850. The company declares a 3:2 bonus issue. Ignoring market movement, what will be his holding and the theoretical ex-bonus price?\nA) 900 shares at Rs 566.67  B) 1,500 shares at Rs 340.00  C) 1,500 shares at Rs 850.00  D) 1,200 shares at Rs 425.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1,500 shares at Rs 340.00**\n\nA **3:2 bonus** gives 3 free shares for every 2 held: 600 ÷ 2 × 3 = 900 bonus shares, so the holding becomes 600 + 900 = **1,500 shares**. Total value is unchanged at 600 × 850 = Rs 5,10,000, so the price adjusts to 5,10,000 ÷ 1,500 = **Rs 340.00**.\n\n- **A) 900 shares at Rs 566.67** - wrong; 900 is the number of *bonus* shares alone, not the resulting holding.\n- **C) 1,500 shares at Rs 850.00** - wrong; the share count and price must move in offsetting directions, otherwise value would be created out of nothing.\n- **D) 1,200 shares at Rs 425.00** - wrong; 1,200 shares would result from a 1:1 bonus, not 3:2.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** In a book-built IPO, which allocation and threshold combination is correct?\nA) QIBs up to 50%, NIIs at least 15%, retail at least 35%, with the retail limit at applications up to Rs 2 lakh  B) QIBs up to 35%, NIIs at least 15%, retail at least 50%, with the retail limit at applications up to Rs 5 lakh  C) QIBs up to 50%, NIIs at least 35%, retail at least 15%, with the retail limit at applications up to Rs 2 lakh  D) QIBs up to 15%, NIIs at least 35%, retail at least 50%, with the retail limit at applications up to Rs 10 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) QIBs up to 50%, NIIs at least 15%, retail at least 35%, with the retail limit at applications up to Rs 2 lakh**\n\nIn a book-built issue: **QIBs up to 50%**, **Non-Institutional Investors at least 15%**, **Retail Individual Investors at least 35%**. The **Rs 2 lakh** application size separates retail from non-institutional applicants. (Rs 5 lakh is the separate cap for applying through the **UPI** mechanism.)\n\n- **B) 35/15/50 with Rs 5 lakh** - wrong on both the QIB/retail split and the retail definition.\n- **C) NIIs 35%, retail 15%** - wrong; the NII and retail minimums are transposed.\n- **D) QIBs 15%, retail 50%, Rs 10 lakh** - wrong on every element.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A company sets a floor price of Rs 148 for its book-built issue. Under SEBI's price band rule, what is the highest permissible cap price?\nA) Rs 162.80  B) Rs 177.60  C) Rs 185.00  D) Rs 296.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 177.60**\n\nThe cap of the price band must be **within 120% of the floor price**. Maximum cap = 148 × 1.20 = **Rs 177.60**.\n\n- **A) Rs 162.80** - wrong; this applies a 110% limit.\n- **C) Rs 185.00** - wrong; this applies a 125% limit.\n- **D) Rs 296.00** - wrong; this doubles the floor price, treating the rule as 200%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a circuit breaker from a price band?\nA) A circuit breaker is stock-specific while a price band halts the whole market  B) A circuit breaker halts the whole market at index moves of 10%, 15% or 20%, while a price band caps one scrip's daily movement  C) Both are stock-specific, differing only in the percentage applied  D) A circuit breaker is imposed by the clearing corporation and a price band by the depository\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A circuit breaker halts the whole market at index moves of 10%, 15% or 20%, while a price band caps one scrip's daily movement**\n\nA **circuit breaker is market-wide**, triggering trading halts at index movements of ±10%, ±15% and ±20%, with halt durations depending on the level and the time of day. A **price band is stock-specific** (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%), limiting how far one scrip can move in a day.\n\n- **A) Circuit breaker stock-specific** - wrong; the two descriptions are transposed.\n- **C) Both stock-specific** - wrong; a circuit breaker applies to the entire market.\n- **D) Imposed by clearing corporation and depository** - wrong; both are exchange-level risk management mechanisms under SEBI's framework.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Jignesh is comparing the routes to professionally managed money. Which combination of minimum investment amounts is correct?\nA) Mutual fund Rs 500, PMS Rs 25 lakh, AIF Rs 50 lakh  B) Mutual fund Rs 500, PMS Rs 50 lakh, AIF Rs 1 crore  C) Mutual fund Rs 5,000, PMS Rs 1 crore, AIF Rs 5 crore  D) Mutual fund Rs 500, PMS Rs 1 crore, AIF Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Mutual fund Rs 500, PMS Rs 50 lakh, AIF Rs 1 crore**\n\nMutual funds start as low as **Rs 100–500** through a SIP, **PMS requires Rs 50 lakh** and an **AIF requires Rs 1 crore** (Rs 25 lakh for employees and directors of the AIF). As the minimum rises, regulation and disclosure fall and strategy freedom rises.\n\n- **A) PMS Rs 25 lakh, AIF Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the concessional AIF minimum for the fund's own employees and directors.\n- **C) PMS Rs 1 crore, AIF Rs 5 crore** - wrong; both thresholds are overstated.\n- **D) PMS Rs 1 crore, AIF Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; the PMS and AIF thresholds are transposed and inflated.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A distributor tells Jignesh that a \"100% capital protected\" structured product carries no risk to his principal. What is the correct assessment?\nA) The claim is accurate because SEBI mandates a capital guarantee on such products  B) The claim is accurate because the embedded derivative fully offsets any loss  C) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and generally applies only at maturity  D) The claim is accurate only if the product is held in a demat account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) The protection depends entirely on the issuer's creditworthiness and generally applies only at maturity**\n\nA structured product bundles a **bond (or deposit) with a derivative**. The \"protection\" comes from the bond leg growing back to par, so it is only as good as the **issuer's credit**. Such products are also typically **not principal protected before maturity**, are illiquid and carry opaque costs.\n\n- **A) SEBI mandates a guarantee** - wrong; no such guarantee is mandated, and \"capital protection\" is not the same thing as a guarantee.\n- **B) The derivative offsets any loss** - wrong; the derivative leg supplies the upside, not the protection — the bond leg does.\n- **D) Only if held in demat** - wrong; the mode of holding has no bearing on issuer credit risk.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A bond in Jignesh's portfolio is downgraded from BBB− to BB+. What is the significance of this particular boundary?\nA) It is the boundary between secured and unsecured debt  B) It is the boundary between investment grade and high yield  C) It is the boundary between government and corporate debt  D) It is the boundary between short-term and long-term debt\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It is the boundary between investment grade and high yield**\n\n**BBB− / Baa3 is the lowest investment grade rating.** A downgrade below it makes the bond \"high yield\" or \"junk\". Because many institutional mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings, such a downgrade (a \"falling angel\") can force widespread selling.\n\n- **A) Secured vs unsecured** - wrong; security is a feature of the instrument's structure, not of its rating.\n- **C) Government vs corporate** - wrong; ratings assess credit quality, and domestic sovereign debt is treated as effectively risk-free regardless of grade bands.\n- **D) Short vs long term** - wrong; rating grades say nothing about maturity.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A share is quoted at Rs 1,284 and the company reported earnings per share of Rs 42.80. What is its P/E ratio?\nA) 3.33  B) 27.50  C) 30.00  D) 33.33\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 30.00**\n\nP/E = Price ÷ EPS = 1,284 ÷ 42.80 = **30.00**. The investor is paying Rs 30 for every Rs 1 of current earnings.\n\n- **A) 3.33** - wrong; this is the **earnings yield** as a percentage (EPS ÷ Price = 3.33%), which is the inverse of the P/E.\n- **B) 27.50** - wrong; this does not follow from the figures given.\n- **D) 33.33** - wrong; this would require an EPS of about Rs 38.52.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A company has a market capitalisation of Rs 4,200 crore, total debt of Rs 860 crore and cash and equivalents of Rs 315 crore. What is its enterprise value?\nA) Rs 3,655 crore  B) Rs 4,745 crore  C) Rs 5,060 crore  D) Rs 5,375 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,745 crore**\n\nEV = Market cap + Debt − Cash = 4,200 + 860 − 315 = **Rs 4,745 crore**. It represents what an acquirer would effectively pay for the whole business, since the target's cash offsets part of the purchase price.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,655 crore** - wrong; this subtracts the debt and adds the cash, reversing both signs.\n- **C) Rs 5,060 crore** - wrong; this adds the debt but forgets to deduct the cash.\n- **D) Rs 5,375 crore** - wrong; this adds cash instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** A stock trades at a P/E of 30 and analysts forecast earnings growth of 22% per annum. What is its PEG ratio, and what does it suggest?\nA) 0.73, suggesting the stock is attractively priced for its growth  B) 1.36, suggesting the stock is not cheap relative to its growth  C) 6.60, suggesting extreme overvaluation  D) 0.30, suggesting deep value\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1.36, suggesting the stock is not cheap relative to its growth**\n\nPEG = P/E ÷ growth rate (%) = 30 ÷ 22 = **1.36**. A PEG **below 1** is conventionally taken as attractive; at 1.36 the price is running ahead of the forecast growth.\n\n- **A) 0.73** - wrong; this is growth ÷ P/E, the inverse of the PEG formula.\n- **C) 6.60** - wrong; this multiplies rather than divides (30 × 0.22).\n- **D) 0.30** - wrong; this divides the P/E by 100, misreading the growth input.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Jignesh holds 24 stocks, of which 19 are Indian private-sector banks and NBFCs. His adviser says the portfolio is poorly diversified. Which reasoning is correct?\nA) He needs at least 50 stocks before diversification takes effect  B) Diversification requires low correlation rather than merely a high count, so his concentrated sector exposure remains  C) Diversification removes systematic risk, which he has not yet achieved  D) With 24 stocks he has removed every risk except transactional risk\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Diversification requires low correlation rather than merely a high count, so his concentrated sector exposure remains**\n\nDiversification works only when holdings have **low correlation**. Nineteen financial-sector names move together on the same interest-rate, credit and regulatory news, so **sector-specific risk** has not been diversified away. Beyond roughly 15–25 genuinely low-correlation stocks, additional names add little.\n\n- **A) At least 50 stocks** - wrong; there is no such threshold, and 50 correlated stocks would be no better.\n- **C) Diversification removes systematic risk** - wrong; diversification removes **unsystematic** risk only, and systematic (market) risk cannot be diversified away at all.\n- **D) All risk except transactional risk** - wrong; both market risk and his concentrated sector risk remain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond has a face value of Rs 1,000, pays a 9% annual coupon and has 6 years to maturity. If the required yield is 11%, what is its price?\nA) Rs 818.18  B) Rs 915.39  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,095.20\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 915.39**\n\nCoupon = Rs 90 a year. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.11)^−6] ÷ 0.11 = (1 − 0.53464) ÷ 0.11 = 4.23054. PV of coupons = 90 × 4.23054 = Rs 380.75. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.11)^6 = 1,000 ÷ 1.87041 = Rs 534.64. Price = 380.75 + 534.64 = **Rs 915.39** — a discount, because the 9% coupon is below the 11% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 818.18** - wrong; this is 90 ÷ 0.11, the perpetuity formula, which ignores the redemption of principal in year 6.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would trade at par only if the coupon equalled the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,095.20** - wrong; a premium price would require the coupon to exceed the required yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 and a coupon rate of 7.65% is trading at Rs 918 in the market. What is its current yield?\nA) 7.65%  B) 8.00%  C) 8.33%  D) 10.11%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 8.33%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ **Market price**. Coupon = 7.65% × 1,000 = Rs 76.50. Current yield = 76.50 ÷ 918 = **8.33%**. Note the ordering for a discount bond: coupon (7.65%) < current yield (8.33%) < YTM.\n\n- **A) 7.65%** - wrong; this is the **coupon rate**, always computed on face value, which ignores the price actually paid.\n- **B) 8.00%** - wrong; this does not follow from the given coupon and price.\n- **D) 10.11%** - wrong; this is the approximate YTM assuming 4 years to maturity, which additionally captures the capital gain on redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Jignesh holds Rs 24,00,000 of a bond fund whose portfolio has a modified duration of 5.8. Yields rise by 65 basis points. What is the approximate change in the value of his holding?\nA) A loss of about Rs 15,600  B) A loss of about Rs 90,480  C) A loss of about Rs 1,39,200  D) A gain of about Rs 90,480\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A loss of about Rs 90,480**\n\n% change in price ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −5.8 × 0.0065 = **−3.77%**. On Rs 24,00,000 that is 24,00,000 × 0.0377 = **a loss of Rs 90,480**.\n\n- **A) Rs 15,600** - wrong; this applies the 0.65% yield change directly to the corpus, omitting the duration multiplier.\n- **C) Rs 1,39,200** - wrong; this applies 5.8% to the corpus, treating duration itself as a percentage.\n- **D) A gain of Rs 90,480** - wrong direction; bond prices move **inversely** to yields, so a rise in yields produces a loss.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** Jignesh is 52 and wants to move part of his corpus into a scheme paying quarterly interest with an upper deposit limit of Rs 30 lakh. His adviser says he is not yet eligible. Which scheme is being discussed, and why is he ineligible?\nA) PPF, because the annual contribution cap is Rs 1.5 lakh  B) Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme, because the minimum age is 60 (or 55 on superannuation)  C) Kisan Vikas Patra, because it has a maximum investment limit  D) National Savings Certificate, because it requires the investor to be over 60\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme, because the minimum age is 60 (or 55 on superannuation)**\n\n**SCSS** has a 5-year tenor (extendable by 3 years), pays **quarterly** interest and is capped at **Rs 30 lakh**. Eligibility begins at **age 60**, or 55 for those retiring on superannuation or under a VRS. At 52 and still running his business, Jignesh does not qualify.\n\n- **A) PPF** - wrong; PPF runs 15 years with a Rs 1.5 lakh annual cap, has no age bar and pays no quarterly interest.\n- **C) Kisan Vikas Patra** - wrong; KVP has **no maximum** limit, pays nothing until maturity and carries no age condition.\n- **D) National Savings Certificate** - wrong; NSC is a 5-year instrument whose interest is compounded annually but paid at maturity, and it has no age restriction.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Jignesh buys one index call option with a strike of 24,500 at a premium of 210. The lot size is 50. At expiry the index closes at 24,880. What is his net profit or loss?\nA) Profit of Rs 8,500  B) Profit of Rs 19,000  C) Loss of Rs 10,500  D) Profit of Rs 12,350\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Profit of Rs 8,500**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(24,880 − 24,500, 0) = 380. Net per unit = 380 − 210 premium = 170. Profit = 170 × 50 = **Rs 8,500**. The break-even was 24,500 + 210 = 24,710, and the index closed above it.\n\n- **B) Profit of Rs 19,000** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value (380 × 50) without deducting the premium paid.\n- **C) Loss of Rs 10,500** - wrong; this is the full premium outlay (210 × 50), which would be the loss only if the option expired at or below the strike.\n- **D) Profit of Rs 12,350** - wrong; this does not follow from the strike, premium and settlement level given.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Jignesh buys a put option on a stock with a strike price of Rs 1,150 at a premium of Rs 38. What is his break-even price at expiry?\nA) Rs 1,074  B) Rs 1,112  C) Rs 1,150  D) Rs 1,188\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,112**\n\n**Put break-even = Strike − Premium** = 1,150 − 38 = **Rs 1,112**. The stock must close below this level at expiry before the buyer recovers the premium and starts to profit.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,074** - wrong; this deducts the premium twice.\n- **C) Rs 1,150** - wrong; this is the strike price, at which the option has zero intrinsic value and the buyer loses the whole premium.\n- **D) Rs 1,188** - wrong; adding the premium gives the break-even for a **call**, not a put.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A stock index is at 3,600. The risk-free rate is 7% per annum and the dividend yield on the index is 1.5% per annum. Using the cost-of-carry relationship, what is the fair value of the one-year futures contract?\nA) 3,600  B) 3,744  C) 3,798  D) 3,852\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 3,798**\n\nF = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 3,600 × (1 + 0.07 − 0.015)^1 = 3,600 × 1.055 = **3,798**. The futures price sits above spot (contango) because holding the index costs interest, partly offset by the dividends received.\n\n- **A) 3,600** - wrong; this is the spot price, which the futures price equals only **at expiry**, when the two converge.\n- **B) 3,744** - wrong; this applies the dividend yield in the wrong direction and omits part of the financing cost.\n- **D) 3,852** - wrong; this applies the 7% financing cost without deducting the 1.5% dividend yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which statement correctly distinguishes an exchange-traded future from an OTC forward?\nA) The forward is standardised and margined, while the future is customised and unmargined  B) The future is standardised, guaranteed by the clearing corporation and marked to market daily, while the forward is customised, carries counterparty risk and settles only at maturity  C) Both carry counterparty risk, but only the forward is marked to market daily  D) The forward has higher liquidity because its terms can be tailored to each buyer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The future is standardised, guaranteed by the clearing corporation and marked to market daily, while the forward is customised, carries counterparty risk and settles only at maturity**\n\nFutures are **exchange-traded and standardised** in lot size and expiry, carry **mandatory margins** and **daily mark to market**, and the clearing corporation acts as central counterparty, so counterparty risk is eliminated. Forwards are **bilateral OTC** contracts — customisable, illiquid, unmargined and settled only at maturity.\n\n- **A) Forward standardised and margined** - wrong; the two descriptions are transposed.\n- **C) Both carry counterparty risk** - wrong; the CCP eliminates counterparty risk in exchange-traded futures, and it is futures rather than forwards that are marked to market daily.\n- **D) Forward has higher liquidity** - wrong; customisation is precisely what makes a forward hard to exit before maturity.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A mutual fund scheme holds securities valued at Rs 846 crore and cash of Rs 27 crore, and owes Rs 13 crore in accrued expenses. It has 32 crore units outstanding. What is the NAV per unit?\nA) Rs 26.03  B) Rs 26.88  C) Rs 27.28  D) Rs 27.69\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 26.88**\n\nNAV = (Total assets - Total liabilities) / Units outstanding. Total assets = 846 + 27 = **Rs 873 crore**. Net assets = 873 - 13 = **Rs 860 crore**. NAV = 860 / 32 = **Rs 26.875**, i.e. **Rs 26.88**.\n\n- **A) Rs 26.03** - wrong; this drops the cash balance from total assets: (846 - 13) / 32.\n- **C) Rs 27.28** - wrong; this ignores the Rs 13 crore of liabilities: 873 / 32.\n- **D) Rs 27.69** - wrong; this **adds** the liabilities instead of subtracting them: (873 + 13) / 32.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Bhavesh Patel, a 52-year-old business owner in Ahmedabad, redeems 4,000 units of an equity scheme after holding them for 7 months. The NAV on the redemption date is Rs 58.75 and the scheme charges an exit load of 1% for redemptions within 12 months. What amount will he receive?\nA) Rs 2,32,650  B) Rs 2,33,825  C) Rs 2,35,000  D) Rs 2,37,350\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,32,650**\n\nGross redemption value = 4,000 x Rs 58.75 = **Rs 2,35,000**. Exit load = 1% x 2,35,000 = **Rs 2,350**. Net proceeds = 2,35,000 - 2,350 = **Rs 2,32,650**. Equivalently, the redemption price is 58.75 x 0.99 = Rs 58.1625 per unit.\n\n- **B) Rs 2,33,825** - wrong; this applies a 0.5% load instead of the stated 1%.\n- **C) Rs 2,35,000** - wrong; this ignores the exit load altogether, which applies because the holding period is under 12 months.\n- **D) Rs 2,37,350** - wrong; this **adds** the load to the proceeds instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Bhavesh submits a purchase application in a **liquid fund** at 11:50 a.m. on Wednesday and the money is credited to the scheme's bank account at 12:40 p.m. the same Wednesday. Which day's NAV will be applied?\nA) Tuesday's NAV  B) Wednesday's NAV  C) Thursday's NAV  D) Friday's NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Tuesday's NAV**\n\nFor **purchases in liquid and overnight funds** the cut-off is **1:30 p.m.**, and where the application is received and the **funds are realised in the scheme's account before that cut-off**, the applicable NAV is the **previous business day's** NAV. Both conditions are satisfied on Wednesday, so Tuesday's NAV applies.\n\n- **B) Wednesday's NAV** - wrong; same-day NAV is the rule for **non-liquid** schemes with a 3:00 p.m. cut-off, not for liquid funds.\n- **C) Thursday's NAV** - wrong; the next day's NAV would apply only if the funds had **not** been realised before the cut-off.\n- **D) Friday's NAV** - wrong; no rule pushes the allotment two business days forward.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Which SEBI debt scheme category is defined by a requirement to invest **at least 65% of assets in instruments rated below AA+**?\nA) Corporate bond fund  B) Banking and PSU fund  C) Credit risk fund  D) Dynamic bond fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Credit risk fund**\n\nUnder SEBI's 2017 categorisation, a **credit risk fund** must hold a **minimum of 65% in instruments rated below AA+** — it deliberately takes credit risk in exchange for higher yield, and is therefore unsuitable for a client whose debt allocation exists to reduce risk.\n\n- **A) Corporate bond fund** - wrong; this is the mirror image — minimum **80% in AA+ and above**.\n- **B) Banking and PSU fund** - wrong; minimum **80% in the debt of banks, PSUs and public financial institutions**, defined by issuer type rather than by rating.\n- **D) Dynamic bond fund** - wrong; defined by **actively managed duration**, with no credit-quality floor.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Bhavesh is about to invest Rs 18,00,000 in a flexi-cap scheme. The regular plan has a TER of 1.85% and the direct plan a TER of 0.95%. What is the difference in cost between the two plans in the first year?\nA) Rs 9,500  B) Rs 16,200  C) Rs 17,100  D) Rs 33,300\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 16,200**\n\nThe TER difference is 1.85% - 0.95% = **0.90%**. On Rs 18,00,000 that is 0.90% x 18,00,000 = **Rs 16,200** in the first year alone, and it compounds heavily over a long holding period. The portfolio is identical in both plans; only the embedded distribution commission differs.\n\n- **A) Rs 9,500** - wrong; this figure has no basis in the numbers given.\n- **C) Rs 17,100** - wrong; this is the **direct plan's own** annual cost (0.95% of 18,00,000), not the difference.\n- **D) Rs 33,300** - wrong; this is the **regular plan's** annual cost (1.85% of 18,00,000).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** After retiring, Bhavesh will need a predictable monthly cash flow from his mutual fund corpus, and his adviser wants an arrangement that is more tax-efficient than the IDCW option. Which systematic transaction fits?\nA) Systematic Investment Plan (SIP)  B) Systematic Transfer Plan (STP)  C) Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP)  D) Value Averaging Investment Plan (VIP)\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP)**\n\nAn **SWP** redeems a fixed amount at fixed intervals, giving a predictable income stream. Only the **capital-gain component** of each withdrawal is taxed, whereas an **IDCW distribution is fully taxable at the investor's slab rate** — which is why the SWP is the standard retirement-income tool.\n\n- **A) SIP** - wrong; a SIP **invests** a fixed amount periodically and is an accumulation tool.\n- **B) STP** - wrong; an STP moves money **between schemes**, typically to deploy a lump sum gradually; it produces no income for the client.\n- **D) VIP** - wrong; value averaging is a more aggressive **accumulation** method that invests more when markets fall.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Under the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020, what are the minimum net worth of a portfolio manager and the minimum investment by a client?\nA) Rs 2 crore net worth; Rs 25 lakh minimum investment  B) Rs 5 crore net worth; Rs 50 lakh minimum investment  C) Rs 5 crore net worth; Rs 25 lakh minimum investment  D) Rs 50 crore net worth; Rs 1 crore minimum investment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 crore net worth; Rs 50 lakh minimum investment**\n\nA portfolio manager must maintain a **minimum net worth of Rs 5 crore**, and the **minimum investment per client is Rs 50 lakh**. Securities are held in the **client's own name and demat account**, not pooled.\n\n- **A) Rs 2 crore / Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; neither figure appears in the 2020 Regulations for a portfolio manager.\n- **C) Rs 5 crore / Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the AIF minimum for **employees and directors** of the AIF or its manager, not the PMS client minimum.\n- **D) Rs 50 crore / Rs 1 crore** - wrong; Rs 50 crore is an **AMC's** minimum net worth and Rs 1 crore is the **AIF** investor minimum.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** Kirit Shah's discretionary PMS portfolio was worth Rs 1.20 crore at inception. It rose to Rs 1.56 crore in Year 1 (on which a performance fee was charged), fell to Rs 1.32 crore in Year 2, and rose to Rs 1.74 crore in Year 3. The performance fee is 15% on a high watermark basis with no hurdle rate. What performance fee is payable for Year 3?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 2,70,000  C) Rs 6,30,000  D) Rs 8,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,70,000**\n\nThe **high watermark is Rs 1.56 crore**, the highest value previously achieved. The fee applies only to the gain **above that peak**: 1.74 - 1.56 = **Rs 18,00,000**. Fee = 15% x 18,00,000 = **Rs 2,70,000**. Kirit does not pay twice for recovering ground already lost in Year 2.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has risen **above** the previous peak, so a fee is due.\n- **C) Rs 6,30,000** - wrong; this charges the whole rise from the Year 2 trough of Rs 1.32 crore (Rs 42,00,000), which is exactly what the high watermark prevents.\n- **D) Rs 8,10,000** - wrong; this charges the entire gain since inception (Rs 54,00,000), part of which has already borne a fee once.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A Rs 2,00,00,000 PMS portfolio returns 16% for the year. The agreement provides a hurdle rate of 9% and a performance fee of 20% with **no catch-up**. What is the performance fee?\nA) Rs 1,40,000  B) Rs 2,80,000  C) Rs 3,60,000  D) Rs 6,40,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,80,000**\n\nTotal return = 16% x 2,00,00,000 = **Rs 32,00,000**. The hurdle absorbs the first 9% = **Rs 18,00,000**. Excess over the hurdle = 32,00,000 - 18,00,000 = **Rs 14,00,000**. With no catch-up the fee applies **only to that excess**: 20% x 14,00,000 = **Rs 2,80,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,40,000** - wrong; this applies 10% to the excess instead of the stated 20%.\n- **C) Rs 3,60,000** - wrong; this is 20% of the **hurdle amount**, which is precisely the part on which no fee may be charged.\n- **D) Rs 6,40,000** - wrong; this is 20% of the **entire** return, ignoring the hurdle altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Kirit signs a portfolio management agreement under which the manager researches securities and recommends trades, but Kirit places every order himself through his own broker. Which service is this?\nA) Discretionary portfolio management  B) Non-discretionary portfolio management  C) Advisory portfolio management  D) Execution-only distribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Advisory portfolio management**\n\nThe clean test is **who pulls the trigger**. In **advisory** PMS the manager only **recommends**; the client both **decides and executes**. No power of attorney over the client's demat account is needed, and a custodian is not mandatory.\n\n- **A) Discretionary** - wrong; there the manager **decides and executes** without referring back to the client.\n- **B) Non-discretionary** - wrong; there the client decides but the **manager executes** the trades.\n- **D) Execution-only distribution** - wrong; that involves no research or recommendation, which is the entire substance of what the manager is doing here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of Rs 160 crore. What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager maintain?\nA) Rs 4 crore  B) Rs 5 crore  C) Rs 8 crore  D) Rs 10 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 8 crore**\n\nFor a **Category III** AIF the continuing interest is the **lower of 5% of the corpus or Rs 10 crore**. Here 5% x 160 = **Rs 8 crore**, which is lower than Rs 10 crore, so the commitment is **Rs 8 crore**. It must be real money at risk and cannot be met by waiving management fees.\n\n- **A) Rs 4 crore** - wrong; 2.5% of the corpus is the **Category I and II** rate, not Category III.\n- **B) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the **Category I and II** monetary ceiling.\n- **D) Rs 10 crore** - wrong; the rule is the **lower** of the two figures, and 5% of Rs 160 crore falls below Rs 10 crore.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Falguni Amin invests Rs 1.5 crore in a hedge fund registered as a Category III AIF. How is the fund's income taxed?\nA) As a pass-through, taxed in Falguni's hands at her own applicable rate  B) At the fund level, before distribution to investors  C) Exempt at the fund level and exempt in the investor's hands  D) As a pass-through for capital gains only, with all other income taxed at the fund level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) At the fund level, before distribution to investors**\n\n**Pass-through status under the Income Tax Act is granted only to Category I and Category II AIFs.** A **Category III** AIF is **taxed at the fund level**, generally at the maximum marginal rate, so every investor effectively bears the same rate whatever their own slab. That makes Category III relatively less tax-efficient for a low-slab investor — an important suitability point.\n\n- **A) Pass-through** - wrong; that is the treatment for Categories I and II only.\n- **C) Exempt at both levels** - wrong; no AIF category enjoys a blanket exemption.\n- **D) Pass-through for capital gains only** - wrong; the split treatment that does exist runs the other way — for Categories I and II, **business income** is taxed at the fund level while other income passes through.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Which statement about **angel funds** is correct?\nA) They are a sub-category of Category II AIFs  B) A scheme may have a maximum of 49 investors  C) The minimum investment per angel investor is Rs 1 crore  D) Their units may be listed on a stock exchange after the final close\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A scheme may have a maximum of 49 investors**\n\nAn angel fund scheme is capped at **49 investors**, against 1,000 for other AIF schemes. Its other distinctive figures are a **minimum corpus of Rs 10 crore** and a **minimum investment of Rs 25 lakh per angel investor**, drawable down over a maximum of five years.\n\n- **A) Category II** - wrong; angel funds are a sub-category of **venture capital funds** and are therefore **Category I**.\n- **C) Rs 1 crore** - wrong; the Rs 1 crore general AIF minimum **does not apply** to an angel investor, who commits a minimum of **Rs 25 lakh**.\n- **D) May be listed** - wrong; **angel funds shall not be listed**, unlike other close-ended AIFs.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Bhavesh's Rs 80,00,000 portfolio holds Rs 44,00,000 in an equity fund with a standard deviation of 22% and Rs 36,00,000 in a debt fund with a standard deviation of 6%. The correlation between them is +0.20. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?\nA) 12.40%  B) 12.91%  C) 14.80%  D) 16.68%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 12.91%**\n\nWeights: 44/80 = **0.55** and 36/80 = **0.45**.\nVariance = (0.55^2 x 22^2) + (0.45^2 x 6^2) + (2 x 0.55 x 0.45 x 22 x 6 x 0.20)\n= (0.3025 x 484) + (0.2025 x 36) + (0.495 x 132 x 0.20)\n= 146.41 + 7.29 + 13.07 = **166.77**\nStandard deviation = square root of 166.77 = **12.91%**.\n\n- **A) 12.40%** - wrong; this is the answer if the correlation were taken as **zero**, dropping the interaction term entirely.\n- **C) 14.80%** - wrong; this is the **weighted average** of the two standard deviations (0.55 x 22 + 0.45 x 6), which portfolio risk equals only when the correlation is +1.\n- **D) 16.68%** - wrong; this arises from mishandling the variance terms, and the final answer in any case requires the square root.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Bhavesh's adviser revises the estimated correlation between his equity holding and his gold ETF from +0.30 to -0.10, leaving the weights and the two expected returns unchanged. What is the effect on the portfolio?\nA) Both the expected return and the standard deviation fall  B) The expected return is unchanged and the standard deviation falls  C) The expected return falls and the standard deviation is unchanged  D) Both are unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The expected return is unchanged and the standard deviation falls**\n\n**Portfolio return is always the plain weighted average of the component returns — correlation has no effect on it whatsoever.** Correlation enters only the **interaction term** of the risk formula, 2 x w1 x w2 x sigma1 x sigma2 x rho. Lowering rho shrinks that term, so the portfolio's standard deviation falls.\n\n- **A) Both fall** - wrong; adjusting the expected return for correlation is the classic error that loses this mark.\n- **C) Return falls, risk unchanged** - wrong; this reverses the relationship entirely.\n- **D) Both unchanged** - wrong; risk is genuinely lower, and that reduction is the whole mechanism of diversification.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Bhavesh sells his engineering unit and retires two years earlier than planned. His adviser permanently reduces the equity target from 65% to 45%. This action is best described as:\nA) Tactical asset allocation within the existing IPS bands  B) A change in the strategic asset allocation, requiring the IPS to be rewritten  C) Routine rebalancing back to the target weights  D) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A change in the strategic asset allocation, requiring the IPS to be rewritten**\n\nThe clean test is: **if the reason is something that happened in the client's life, it is strategic; if it is something that happened in the market, it is tactical.** Early retirement shortens the horizon and removes his human capital, so the long-term policy mix itself must change — and the IPS is revised to record the new target and bands.\n\n- **A) Tactical** - wrong; TAA is a **temporary, market-driven** deviation that stays inside the bands and is later reversed. This change is permanent and client-driven.\n- **C) Rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing **restores** existing target weights; here the targets themselves are being changed.\n- **D) Breach of the IPS** - wrong; the IPS is being properly amended for a life event, which is exactly what its review procedure contemplates.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Bhavesh's IPS specifies 65% equity / 35% debt on a corpus of Rs 60,00,000, reviewed under a **calendar (annual) rebalancing** rule. Over the year equity returned +26% and debt +6%. How much equity must be sold at the review to restore the target weights?\nA) Rs 2,73,000  B) Rs 3,57,000  C) Rs 10,14,000  D) Nil - no trade is required\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,73,000**\n\nOpening: equity 65% x 60,00,000 = Rs 39,00,000; debt Rs 21,00,000.\nClosing: equity 39,00,000 x 1.26 = **Rs 49,14,000**; debt 21,00,000 x 1.06 = **Rs 22,26,000**; total = **Rs 71,40,000**.\nTarget equity = 65% x 71,40,000 = **Rs 46,41,000**.\nEquity to sell = 49,14,000 - 46,41,000 = **Rs 2,73,000**, which moves into debt to make Rs 24,99,000, exactly 35%.\n\n- **B) Rs 3,57,000** - wrong; this is 5% of the closing corpus, confusing a tolerance band with the trade actually required.\n- **C) Rs 10,14,000** - wrong; this restores the **original rupee amount** of Rs 39,00,000 instead of 65% of the **new** corpus.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; the drift is 68.82% - 65% = 3.82 percentage points, which would avoid action under a **threshold** rule, but this IPS uses a **calendar** rule that trades at the review date regardless.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A fund returned 17.4% over the year. The risk-free rate was 6.8%, its benchmark returned 13.5%, and the fund's beta was 1.15. What is its Jensen's alpha?\nA) 1.88%  B) 2.90%  C) 3.90%  D) 10.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 2.90%**\n\nJensen's alpha = Rp - [Rf + beta x (Rm - Rf)].\nMarket risk premium = 13.5 - 6.8 = **6.7%**. Required return = 6.8 + (1.15 x 6.7) = 6.8 + 7.705 = **14.51%**.\nAlpha = 17.4 - 14.51 = **+2.90%**. The fund beat the index by 3.9 points, but 1 point of that was simply bought by running a beta of 1.15 in a rising market.\n\n- **A) 1.88%** - wrong; this subtracts beta x Rm (1.15 x 13.5) without adding back the risk-free component.\n- **C) 3.90%** - wrong; this is the raw excess over the benchmark (17.4 - 13.5). Alpha is **never** simply Rp - Rm unless beta is exactly 1.\n- **D) 10.60%** - wrong; this is the excess over the risk-free rate (17.4 - 6.8), the **numerator of the Sharpe ratio**, not alpha.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Bhavesh wishes to pay Rs 5,00,000 from his own current account for a mutual fund purchase in a folio where his wife Hetal is the sole holder. Under the PMLA third-party payment rules, is this permitted?\nA) Yes - a spouse is an accepted exception to the third-party rule  B) Yes, provided both sign a third-party payment declaration  C) Yes, but only up to Rs 50,000 per transaction  D) No - a spouse is not among the recognised exceptions, so the payment must come from Hetal's own account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) No - a spouse is not among the recognised exceptions, so the payment must come from Hetal's own account**\n\nPayment must come from the **first or sole holder's own bank account**. The only recognised exceptions are (1) a parent, grandparent or related person paying **for a minor**, capped at **Rs 50,000** per transaction, (2) an **employer paying for an employee** by payroll deduction, (3) a **custodian paying for an FPI or client**, and (4) an AMC or corporate paying **commission in units** to an empanelled distributor. A spouse is not on that list.\n\n- **A) Spouse is an exception** - wrong; there is no family exception other than the minor case.\n- **B) A declaration cures it** - wrong; the declaration is required **within** the four exceptions; it cannot create a new one.\n- **C) Up to Rs 50,000** - wrong; the Rs 50,000 cap belongs to the **minor** exception only, and Hetal is not a minor.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A mutual fund folio is held jointly by Bhavesh and Hetal with the mode of holding recorded as **\"Anyone or Survivor\"**. They now wish to register a nomination. Whose signature is required?\nA) Bhavesh alone, being the first holder  B) Either holder, consistent with the mode of holding  C) Both holders  D) Both holders and the nominee\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Both holders**\n\n**Nomination always requires the signatures of ALL joint holders, even where the mode of holding is \"Anyone or Survivor\".** Ordinary transactions may be signed by any one holder, but nomination is a personal act of every unit holder and is the standing exception to the mode-of-holding rule.\n\n- **A) First holder alone** - wrong; the first holder's PAN governs taxation and payouts, but not the authority to nominate.\n- **B) Either holder** - wrong; this is precisely the assumption the rule defeats.\n- **D) Both holders and the nominee** - wrong; the **nominee's signature is not required** on the nomination form.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Bhavesh's son Dhruv turns 18 on 14 August. A monthly SIP registered in the minor folio is due for debit on 20 August. What happens?\nA) The SIP continues uninterrupted until the change-of-status formalities are completed  B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all standing instructions, including the SIP, cease from the date of majority  C) The guardian may continue to operate the folio for a further six months  D) The SIP continues but redemptions alone are blocked\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all standing instructions, including the SIP, cease from the date of majority**\n\nOn the date a minor attains 18, the folio is **frozen for financial transactions** and every standing instruction — **SIP, SWP and STP** — ceases. The now-major must submit the **Minor Attaining Majority form** with a **banker-attested signature**, PAN and completed KYC, a **bank account in his own name** and a **fresh nomination** before the folio is reactivated.\n\n- **A) Continues uninterrupted** - wrong; the freeze is automatic on the birthday, and the AMC is required to send advance notice beforehand.\n- **C) Guardian continues for six months** - wrong; the **guardian's authority ends completely** on the date of majority.\n- **D) Only redemptions blocked** - wrong; the freeze covers purchases, redemptions and switches alike.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Bhavesh's brother-in-law Nirav is an NRI working in Dubai and owns a commercial shop in Ahmedabad. Into which account must the rental income be credited?\nA) NRE account  B) NRO account  C) FCNR(B) account  D) RFC account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) NRO account**\n\nAn **NRO account holds income earned in India** — rent, dividends, pension, interest and sale proceeds. Its interest is **taxable with TDS deducted at source**, and repatriation of the principal is capped at **USD 1 million per financial year**, net of taxes.\n\n- **A) NRE** - wrong; an NRE account may be credited only with **foreign earnings remitted from abroad**; Indian rent cannot be credited to it.\n- **C) FCNR(B)** - wrong; this is a **foreign-currency term deposit** of 1 to 5 years funded by foreign remittances, not a rupee operating account.\n- **D) RFC** - wrong; a Resident Foreign Currency account is for a **returning Indian** to park foreign-currency balances after becoming resident again.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which of the following is **NOT** an Officially Valid Document (OVD) under the PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005?\nA) Passport  B) Voter's Identity Card issued by the Election Commission  C) PAN card  D) NREGA job card duly signed by a State Government officer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) PAN card**\n\nThe six OVDs are the **passport, driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar, Voter's Identity Card, NREGA job card** and the **NPR letter**. **PAN is not an OVD** — it is a **separate and additional** mandatory requirement under Section 139A of the Income Tax Act. A ration card, bank passbook or utility bill is likewise not an OVD in its own right.\n\n- **A) Passport** - wrong as the answer; it is the first item on the OVD list.\n- **B) Voter's Identity Card** - wrong as the answer; it is expressly an OVD.\n- **D) NREGA job card** - wrong as the answer; it is an OVD when duly signed by a State Government officer.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** Bhavesh registers a new bank account on his folio on 3 September and submits a redemption request on 8 September. Where will the redemption proceeds be credited?\nA) The newly registered bank account  B) The old, already registered bank account  C) Split equally between the two accounts  D) Nowhere - the redemption request is rejected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The old, already registered bank account**\n\nWhere a redemption request is received **within the cooling-off window after a change of bank mandate (commonly 10 calendar days)**, the proceeds are credited to the **old, already registered** account. This is a deliberate anti-fraud control that defeats the classic scam of changing the bank details and immediately redeeming.\n\n- **A) New account** - wrong; the new mandate is not used for payouts until the cooling-off period has passed.\n- **C) Split equally** - wrong; no such splitting rule exists.\n- **D) Rejected** - wrong; the redemption is processed normally — only the destination account is controlled.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Physical share certificates stand in the names \"Bhavesh Patel and Hetal Patel\", while the demat account is opened as \"Hetal Patel and Bhavesh Patel\". What must be done before the certificates can be dematerialised?\nA) Transmission  B) Transposition  C) Transfer  D) Rematerialisation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Transposition**\n\nThe **names and their ORDER** on the certificate must match the demat account exactly. **Transposition changes the ORDER of the SAME names**; it is permitted, is **not treated as a transfer**, and can be filed together with the demat request.\n\n- **A) Transmission** - wrong; transmission passes securities to survivors, nominees or heirs **on death**.\n- **C) Transfer** - wrong; a transfer changes the **names** because of a sale or gift, and physical transfer of listed securities has been barred since 1 April 2019.\n- **D) Rematerialisation** - wrong; that converts electronic holdings **back into certificates**, the opposite of what is wanted here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser advises Bhavesh's family, whose combined Assets under Advice are Rs 1,60,00,000. What is the maximum fee the adviser may charge the family in a year?\nA) Rs 1,25,000  B) Rs 2,50,000  C) Rs 4,00,000  D) Rs 5,25,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 4,00,000**\n\nAn IA may charge in **only one of two modes at a time**: up to **2.5% of AUA per annum per family**, or up to **Rs 1,25,000 per annum per family** under the fixed-fee mode. Here 2.5% x 1,60,00,000 = **Rs 4,00,000**, the higher of the two ceilings, so the AUA mode permits the larger fee. The break-even AUA between the two modes is exactly Rs 50 lakh.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; this is the fixed-fee cap, which the AUA mode exceeds at this level of assets.\n- **B) Rs 2,50,000** - wrong; this figure corresponds to neither cap.\n- **D) Rs 5,25,000** - wrong; this adds the two caps together, but **only one mode may be used per client at a time**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** An RIA advises Bhavesh to invest in a particular mutual fund scheme and separately receives trail commission from the AMC on that investment. Which provision is most directly breached?\nA) Regulation 15(1) - acting in a fiduciary capacity  B) Regulation 15(2) - no consideration from any person other than the client  C) Regulation 15(5) - the 15-day own-account restriction  D) Regulation 16 - risk profiling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Regulation 15(2) - no consideration from any person other than the client**\n\n**Regulation 15(2)** provides that an IA shall not receive any consideration — by way of remuneration, compensation or in any other form — **from any person other than the client being advised** in respect of the underlying products advised upon. Taking trail commission from the AMC breaches it no matter how good the advice was.\n\n- **A) Reg 15(1)** - wrong as the most direct answer; the fiduciary and conflict-disclosure duty is certainly engaged, but 15(2) is the specific prohibition on the payment itself.\n- **C) Reg 15(5)** - wrong; that restricts the adviser from **transacting on its own account contrary to advice given** for 15 days.\n- **D) Reg 16** - wrong; that governs **risk profiling**, a separate obligation altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Jignesh Desai runs his SEBI-registered advisory practice as an individual. What is his minimum net worth requirement?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 5 lakh in net tangible assets  C) Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 lakh in net tangible assets**\n\n**Individuals and partnership firms** registered as investment advisers must hold **net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh**; **non-individuals** (body corporates and LLPs) need a **net worth of Rs 50 lakh**. The 2020 amendment raised these from Rs 1 lakh and Rs 25 lakh respectively.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; that was the **pre-2020** figure for an individual IA.\n- **C) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; that was the pre-2020 figure for a **non-individual** IA.\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; that is the **current non-individual** requirement, not the individual one.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** Jignesh is an individual RIA. His wife holds an AMFI ARN and proposes to distribute mutual funds to his advisory clients, earning commission. Is this permitted under Regulation 22?\nA) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed to the clients  B) Yes, because she is separately registered with AMFI  C) No - an individual IA shall not provide distribution services, and the family of an individual IA shall not provide them to the IA's clients  D) Yes, if the advisory and distribution activities are kept in separate divisions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No - an individual IA shall not provide distribution services, and the family of an individual IA shall not provide them to the IA's clients**\n\nRegulation 22 is absolute for an individual adviser: he may **never** distribute, and his **family** — spouse, children and parents — may not provide distribution services to his advisory clients. The rule closes the obvious loophole of routing the commission through a relative.\n\n- **A) Disclosure cures it** - wrong; this is a **prohibition**, not a disclosure obligation.\n- **B) Separate AMFI registration** - wrong; an ARN does not displace the segregation rule.\n- **D) Separate divisions** - wrong; the separately identifiable department route is available only to a **non-individual** IA, and even then a client and the client's family may take either advisory **or** distribution from the group, never both.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Rajnikant Vora, a caterer with no relationship whatsoever with a listed company, overhears its CFO discussing an unannounced merger and buys the shares the same afternoon. Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, he is:\nA) Not an insider, since he is not a connected person  B) An insider, because he is in possession of unpublished price-sensitive information  C) Guilty of front-running rather than insider trading  D) Exempt, because the information was overheard accidentally\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) An insider, because he is in possession of unpublished price-sensitive information**\n\nRegulation 2(1)(g) defines an insider as a person who is **a connected person OR is in possession of, or has access to, UPSI**. The two limbs are joined by **\"or\"** — a person with **no connection at all** becomes an insider the moment he possesses UPSI, and a merger falls squarely within the ordinary categories of UPSI.\n\n- **A) Not an insider** - wrong; this reads the definition as though both limbs had to be satisfied together.\n- **C) Front-running** - wrong; front-running uses knowledge of an **impending order**, whereas this is information about the **company itself**.\n- **D) Accidental acquisition** - wrong; how the information was acquired is irrelevant, and where an insider in possession of UPSI trades, the trade is **presumed** to have been motivated by it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An IA notices a client transaction of Rs 40,000 supported by an explanation the adviser considers wholly implausible. What is the correct action?\nA) No report is required, as the amount is far below the Rs 10 lakh threshold  B) File a Cash Transaction Report with FIU-IND by the 15th of the following month  C) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days, without informing the client  D) File a Suspicious Transaction Report and simultaneously inform the client so that he may explain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days, without informing the client**\n\nAn **STR has no monetary threshold** and applies whether the transaction was **attempted or executed**. It must be filed with **FIU-IND within 7 working days** of concluding that the transaction is suspicious. Telling the client is **tipping off**, which is itself an offence under the PMLA.\n\n- **A) No report required** - wrong; the Rs 10 lakh threshold belongs to the **CTR**, not the STR.\n- **B) CTR** - wrong; a CTR covers **cash** transactions above Rs 10 lakh and is filed by the 15th of the succeeding month.\n- **D) Inform the client** - wrong; that is precisely the prohibited tipping off.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Under Section 15EB of the SEBI Act, 1992, an investment adviser who fails to comply with SEBI's regulations or directions is liable to a penalty of:\nA) Not less than Rs 1 lakh, extending to Rs 1 lakh for each day of continuing failure, subject to a maximum of Rs 1 crore  B) Not less than Rs 5 lakh, extending to Rs 25 crore or three times the profits made, whichever is higher  C) Not less than Rs 10 lakh, extending to Rs 25 crore or three times the profits made, whichever is higher  D) A flat penalty of Rs 1 crore in every case\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Not less than Rs 1 lakh, extending to Rs 1 lakh for each day of continuing failure, subject to a maximum of Rs 1 crore**\n\nSection **15EB** applies to an **investment adviser or research analyst**. At Rs 1 lakh a day the statutory ceiling of Rs 1 crore is reached in **100 days**; beyond that the monetary penalty stops rising, but the registration consequences — suspension or cancellation — continue to grow.\n\n- **B) Minimum Rs 5 lakh** - wrong; that is **Section 15HA**, for fraudulent and unfair trade practices.\n- **C) Minimum Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; that is **Section 15G**, for insider trading.\n- **D) Flat Rs 1 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the **maximum**, and Section 15J requires the adjudicating officer to weigh the gain made, the loss caused and the repetitive nature of the default.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Mitesh Panchal is an AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor in Ahmedabad. He begins charging clients a **separate advisory fee** for asset allocation advice spanning equities, bonds and mutual funds. Does he need registration as an Investment Adviser?\nA) No - his AMFI registration already covers advice on mutual funds  B) No, provided he continues to receive commission only on mutual funds  C) Yes - the distributor exemption covers only advice that is incidental to mutual fund distribution and carries no separate fee  D) Yes, but only once his client count exceeds 150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Yes - the distributor exemption covers only advice that is incidental to mutual fund distribution and carries no separate fee**\n\nRegulation 4 exempts an AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor only where the advice is **incidental to the distribution of mutual funds**. The exemption **evaporates** the moment he charges a **separate advisory fee** or advises across asset classes — at that point he is providing investment advice **for consideration** and must register.\n\n- **A) AMFI registration suffices** - wrong; an ARN authorises distribution, not fee-based advisory.\n- **B) Commission only on mutual funds** - wrong; the trigger is the separate fee and the widened scope of the advice, not the source of the commission.\n- **D) Only above 150 clients** - wrong; the **150-client threshold** is when an **individual** RIA must convert to a **non-individual** registration; it has nothing to do with whether registration is needed at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** Over one year, Bhavesh's broker-adviser moved his Rs 40,00,000 equity holding across nine different large-cap schemes, each time earning fresh commission, with no change in the underlying strategy or in Bhavesh's risk profile. This conduct is best labelled:\nA) Mis-selling  B) Churning  C) Front-running  D) Unauthorised transactions\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Churning**\n\n**Churning is excessive trading, switching or turnover in a client's portfolio to generate brokerage or fresh commission.** The give-away sign is **a great deal of activity with no change in the underlying strategy**. Each individual scheme may be perfectly sound; it is the **frequency** that is the offence — and every switch is also a taxable event for the client.\n\n- **A) Mis-selling** - wrong; mis-selling is about recommending the **wrong product** for the client's needs, and a single transaction can amount to it.\n- **C) Front-running** - wrong; that means dealing **ahead of a client's known impending order**.\n- **D) Unauthorised transactions** - wrong; that requires trades the client never approved, which is not what the facts describe.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Which provision establishes the fiduciary duty of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser?\nA) Regulation 3 of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013  B) Regulation 15(1) of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013  C) Section 12A of the SEBI Act, 1992  D) Regulation 4 of the PFUTP Regulations, 2003\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Regulation 15(1) of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013**\n\nRegulation 15(1) states that an investment adviser **\"shall act in a fiduciary capacity towards its clients and shall disclose all conflicts of interest as and when they arise\"**. Its two limbs are the **duty of loyalty** (whose side are you on?) and the **duty of care** (did you do the work?).\n\n- **A) Regulation 3** - wrong; that is the **registration** requirement — no person may act as, or hold himself out as, an investment adviser without a SEBI certificate.\n- **C) Section 12A** - wrong; that prohibits manipulative and deceptive devices, insider trading and fraudulent takeovers.\n- **D) PFUTP Regulation 4** - wrong; that lists the deemed **fraudulent and unfair trade practices**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** An adviser learns that a large client will place a substantial buy order in a mid-cap stock the next morning, and buys the stock for his own account that afternoon. This is:\nA) Insider trading under the PIT Regulations, 2015  B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003  C) Permitted, because no unpublished price-sensitive information about the company was used  D) Churning of the client's account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003**\n\n**Front-running is dealing ahead of a client's known impending order** to profit from the price move that order will cause. It is a deemed fraudulent and unfair trade practice under **Regulation 4 of the PFUTP Regulations, 2003**, attracting a penalty under Section 15HA. The memory hook is: **front-run an ORDER, insider-trade on NEWS.**\n\n- **A) Insider trading** - wrong; PIT applies to **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company itself**, not to knowledge of an order book.\n- **C) Permitted** - wrong; the absence of UPSI takes it out of PIT but leaves it squarely inside PFUTP, and it also breaches the adviser's fiduciary duty.\n- **D) Churning** - wrong; churning is excessive trading **in the client's own account**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A SEBI-registered IA's financial year ends on 31 March 2026. Its annual compliance audit report, containing adverse observations, is dated 12 August 2026. By when must the adverse findings and the action taken on them be reported?\nA) 12 September 2026  B) 30 September 2026  C) 31 October 2026  D) 31 March 2027\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 12 September 2026**\n\nThe audit itself must be **completed within six months of the financial year end** (by 30 September 2026). Separately, **adverse findings together with the action taken, duly approved by the management, must be reported within one month of the date of the audit report** — 12 August plus one month gives **12 September 2026**.\n\n- **B) 30 September 2026** - wrong; that is the deadline for **completing the audit**, not for reporting the action taken on its findings.\n- **C) 31 October 2026** - wrong; that is the outer backstop date, but the one-month clock running from the 12 August report expires earlier and governs here.\n- **D) 31 March 2027** - wrong; no reporting obligation runs to the next financial year end.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Under the Code of Conduct for Investment Advisers, what is the correct order of preference for handling a conflict of interest?\nA) Disclose it, then manage it, then avoid it  B) Avoid it, then manage it, and disclose what cannot be avoided  C) Disclose it only; avoidance is not required  D) Obtain the client's written consent, after which no further action is needed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Avoid it, then manage it, and disclose what cannot be avoided**\n\nThe Code requires an adviser to **avoid conflicts as far as possible**, and where a conflict is unavoidable, to **ensure fair treatment of clients and disclose it**. Regulation 15(1) reinforces this by requiring disclosure of all conflicts **as and when they arise**. The hierarchy is **avoid, manage, disclose** — in that order.\n\n- **A) Disclose first** - wrong; this inverts the hierarchy and treats disclosure as a substitute for avoidance.\n- **C) Disclose only** - wrong; disclosure alone is the weakest response and is not what the Code requires.\n- **D) Consent ends the matter** - wrong; informed consent may allow a disclosed conflict to continue, but it does not relieve the adviser of the duty to treat the client fairly and act in the client's best interests.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** A complaint against an Investment Adviser is lodged on SCORES on 5 June. Within how many days must the adviser file its Action Taken Report?\nA) 7 calendar days  B) 15 calendar days  C) 21 calendar days  D) 30 calendar days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 21 calendar days**\n\nUnder **SCORES 2.0** the entity must file its **Action Taken Report within 21 calendar days**. The full rhythm is **21 - 15 - 21 - 15 - 21**: entity 21 days, investor may seek a first review within 15 days, the Designated Body has 21 days, the investor may seek a second review within 15 days, and SEBI has 21 days. Missing the 21-day deadline triggers **auto-escalation** to the Designated Body.\n\n- **A) 7 days** - wrong; 7 working days is the STR deadline under the PMLA, not a SCORES timeline.\n- **B) 15 days** - wrong; 15 days is the window in which the **investor** may seek a review, not the entity's response time.\n- **D) 30 days** - wrong; 30 days is the RBI Ombudsman escalation trigger, not the SCORES ATR clock.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Bhavesh is dissatisfied with a fee charged by his Investment Adviser. What is the correct **first** step?\nA) Lodge a complaint on SCORES  B) Raise the grievance in writing with the adviser through its published grievance mechanism  C) File an appeal before the Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) File a complaint before the District Consumer Commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Raise the grievance in writing with the adviser through its published grievance mechanism**\n\nEvery redress system in Indian finance follows the same ladder: **intermediary first, regulator next, tribunal or adjudicator last.** The firm holds the file, the records and the money, and can correct a genuine error in a day. A complaint that skips this step is **not maintainable** on SCORES, which expressly requires that the investor has already approached the entity.\n\n- **A) SCORES** - wrong; SCORES may be used only after the entity has failed to reply or has replied unsatisfactorily.\n- **C) SAT** - wrong; SAT is an **appellate** forum against a **regulator's order**, not a complaint counter against an intermediary.\n- **D) District Commission** - wrong; a consumer complaint is available in due course, but the required first step is the adviser's own grievance process.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Which statement about the **Insurance Ombudsman** is correct?\nA) It entertains claims up to Rs 20 lakh and its award binds both parties  B) It entertains claims up to Rs 30 lakh and its award binds the insurer but not the complainant  C) It entertains claims up to Rs 30 lakh and its award binds both parties  D) It has no monetary limit and its award is only a non-binding recommendation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It entertains claims up to Rs 30 lakh and its award binds the insurer but not the complainant**\n\nUnder the **Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017** (amended 2021) the value limit is **Rs 30 lakh**, and the award is **binding on the insurer only**. The complainant remains free to go to a consumer commission or a civil court — which is what makes it a free, fast, low-risk route to redress.\n\n- **A) Rs 20 lakh, binds both** - wrong; Rs 20 lakh, plus up to Rs 1 lakh for time and harassment, is the **RBI Ombudsman's** award cap.\n- **C) Binds both** - wrong; making the award bind the complainant too would deter the cautious from ever filing.\n- **D) No limit, recommendation only** - wrong; the Rs 30 lakh limit applies, and where conciliation fails the Ombudsman passes a binding **award**, not a recommendation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Bhavesh's advisory firm wishes to challenge an order passed by a SEBI adjudicating officer. Within what period must the appeal be filed before the Securities Appellate Tribunal?\nA) 30 days  B) 45 days  C) 60 days  D) 90 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 45 days**\n\nAn appeal to **SAT** must be filed within **45 days** of receiving a copy of the order, though SAT may condone delay for sufficient cause. SAT endeavours to dispose of an appeal within **6 months**, and a further appeal lies to the **Supreme Court within 60 days, on a question of law only**. The three numbers are **45 - 6 - 60**.\n\n- **A) 30 days** - wrong; 30 days is the appeal period from the PFRDA Ombudsman to PFRDA, and from a State to the National Consumer Commission.\n- **C) 60 days** - wrong; 60 days is the period for the onward appeal from **SAT to the Supreme Court**.\n- **D) 90 days** - wrong; no stage of this ladder uses a 90-day period.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Bhavesh paid Rs 38,00,000 as consideration for a financial service and alleges deficiency. Under the pecuniary limits **currently in force**, which forum has jurisdiction?\nA) District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  B) State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  C) National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  D) Securities Appellate Tribunal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission**\n\nUnder the **2021 Rules** the limits are: **District up to Rs 50 lakh**, **State above Rs 50 lakh up to Rs 2 crore**, and **National above Rs 2 crore**. Rs 38,00,000 falls within the District Commission's jurisdiction. Note that jurisdiction is fixed by the **value of the consideration paid**, not by the compensation claimed.\n\n- **B) State Commission** - wrong; the State tier begins **above Rs 50 lakh**.\n- **C) National Commission** - wrong; the National tier begins **above Rs 2 crore**.\n- **D) SAT** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against **orders of SEBI, PFRDA and IRDAI**, not consumer complaints against a service provider.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Caselets (6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 1: The Vaghela household in Ahmedabad\n\nJignesh Vaghela (52) runs a ceramic-tile trading firm in Ahmedabad and draws Rs 2,60,000 a month from the business. His wife Nirali (49) earns Rs 70,000 a month from a coaching centre she owns. The household spends Rs 2,05,000 a month, of which Rs 62,000 are EMIs on a shop loan and a car loan. Their liquid holdings are a savings bank balance of Rs 5,60,000 and a liquid fund of Rs 4,40,000; they also hold a gold ETF worth Rs 6,00,000, which they treat as a long-term holding and not as an emergency reserve. Their total assets, including the business premises and the flat they live in, are Rs 3,25,00,000, and their outstanding loans total Rs 78,00,000. Their adviser recommends an emergency fund equal to six months of total household expenses.\n\n**Q91.** What is the household's monthly savings ratio?\nA) 37.88%  B) 48.08%  C) 60.98%  D) 62.12%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 37.88%**\n\nGross monthly income = 2,60,000 + 70,000 = **Rs 3,30,000**. Savings = 3,30,000 − 2,05,000 = **Rs 1,25,000**. Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 1,25,000 ÷ 3,30,000 = **37.88%**.\n\n- **B) 48.08%** - wrong; this divides savings by Jignesh's income alone (1,25,000 ÷ 2,60,000), ignoring Nirali's earnings.\n- **C) 60.98%** - wrong; this divides savings by expenses (1,25,000 ÷ 2,05,000). The denominator of the savings ratio is income.\n- **D) 62.12%** - wrong; this is the *expenses* ratio, 2,05,000 ÷ 3,30,000. Savings ratio and expenses ratio must sum to 100%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** What is the household's solvency ratio?\nA) 0.24  B) 0.32  C) 0.76  D) 1.24\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 0.76**\n\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 3,25,00,000 − 78,00,000 = **Rs 2,47,00,000**. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 2,47,00,000 ÷ 3,25,00,000 = **0.76**. A ratio above 0.5 is healthy, so the household is comfortably solvent.\n\n- **A) 0.24** - wrong; this is the *leverage* ratio, Liabilities ÷ Assets = 78,00,000 ÷ 3,25,00,000. Solvency and leverage must sum to 1.\n- **B) 0.32** - wrong; this is Liabilities ÷ Net worth = 78,00,000 ÷ 2,47,00,000, which is a debt-to-equity measure, not the solvency ratio.\n- **D) 1.24** - wrong; this adds the leverage ratio to 1 instead of subtracting it. A solvency ratio can never exceed 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** What is the household's liquidity ratio?\nA) 2.73 months  B) 4.88 months  C) 6.99 months  D) 7.80 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 4.88 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings bank 5,60,000 + liquid fund 4,40,000 = **Rs 10,00,000**. The gold ETF is excluded because the family has earmarked it as a long-term holding. Liquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses = 10,00,000 ÷ 2,05,000 = **4.88 months**, which sits inside the 3–6 month norm but below the six months the adviser wants.\n\n- **A) 2.73 months** - wrong; this counts only the savings bank balance (5,60,000 ÷ 2,05,000) and drops the liquid fund, which is a genuine emergency asset.\n- **C) 6.99 months** - wrong; this divides by non-EMI expenses of 1,43,000 only. EMIs still have to be paid in an emergency, so total expenses are the correct denominator.\n- **D) 7.80 months** - wrong; this pulls the gold ETF into liquid assets (16,00,000 ÷ 2,05,000), contradicting the family's own treatment of it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** What is the household's debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 18.79%  B) 23.85%  C) 30.24%  D) 62.12%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 18.79%**\n\nDebt-to-income ratio = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 62,000 ÷ 3,30,000 = **18.79%**. Comfortably below the 35–40% prudential ceiling, so there is headroom for further borrowing.\n\n- **B) 23.85%** - wrong; this uses Jignesh's income alone (62,000 ÷ 2,60,000).\n- **C) 30.24%** - wrong; this divides EMIs by expenses (62,000 ÷ 2,05,000). The denominator is income, not expenditure.\n- **D) 62.12%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, not the debt servicing ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** How much more must the household set aside to reach the emergency fund the adviser recommends?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 2,30,000  C) Rs 6,15,000  D) Rs 12,30,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,30,000**\n\nSix months of total household expenses = 6 × 2,05,000 = **Rs 12,30,000**. Liquid assets already held = **Rs 10,00,000**. Shortfall = 12,30,000 − 10,00,000 = **Rs 2,30,000**.\n\n- **A) Nil** - wrong; this assumes a three-month buffer (Rs 6,15,000) is enough. The adviser specified six months.\n- **C) Rs 6,15,000** - wrong; this is three months of expenses, not the shortfall against a six-month target.\n- **D) Rs 12,30,000** - wrong; this is the full six-month requirement and ignores the Rs 10,00,000 the family already holds.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 2: Jignesh's directly held securities\n\nJignesh holds 4,000 shares of Sanand Auto Components Ltd, currently quoted at Rs 1,250 a share. The company reported EPS of Rs 62.50, book value per share of Rs 500 and a dividend per share of Rs 15. He separately holds 1,000 bonds of Rs 1,000 face value each issued by a AAA-rated NBFC, carrying a 9% coupon paid annually, with exactly 4 years left to maturity; the bonds are quoted at Rs 1,062 each. Worried about a pre-election correction, he asks about hedging the equity holding with index futures. The beta of Sanand Auto against the index is 1.20, the index futures are quoted at 24,000 and the lot size is 25.\n\n**Q96.** What is the price-to-earnings ratio of Sanand Auto Components?\nA) 20.00 times  B) 2.50 times  C) 5.00 times  D) 25.00 times\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 20.00 times**\n\nP/E = Market price ÷ EPS = 1,250 ÷ 62.50 = **20.00 times**.\n\n- **B) 2.50 times** - wrong; this is the price-to-book ratio, 1,250 ÷ 500.\n- **C) 5.00 times** - wrong; 5.00 is the *earnings yield* in per cent (62.50 ÷ 1,250), which is the reciprocal of the P/E, not the P/E.\n- **D) 25.00 times** - wrong; no combination of the given figures produces this; it is a rounded-up guess.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** What is the dividend yield on the shares?\nA) 1.20%  B) 3.00%  C) 5.00%  D) 24.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.20%**\n\nDividend yield = DPS ÷ Market price = 15 ÷ 1,250 = **1.20%**.\n\n- **B) 3.00%** - wrong; this divides the dividend by book value per share (15 ÷ 500). Yield is always measured against market price.\n- **C) 5.00%** - wrong; this is the earnings yield, EPS ÷ Price.\n- **D) 24.00%** - wrong; this is the dividend *payout* ratio, DPS ÷ EPS = 15 ÷ 62.50, which measures how much of profit is distributed, not the return to the buyer.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** What is the current yield on the NBFC bonds?\nA) 7.23%  B) 8.47%  C) 8.73%  D) 9.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 8.47%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ Market price = 90 ÷ 1,062 = **8.47%**. The bond trades at a premium, so the current yield sits below the 9% coupon.\n\n- **A) 7.23%** - wrong; this is the approximate YTM, which also captures the capital loss on redemption at par.\n- **C) 8.73%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by the average of face and market price (90 ÷ 1,031). The denominator of current yield is the market price alone.\n- **D) 9.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, which is fixed on face value and does not change with price.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Using the approximation formula, what is the yield to maturity on the bonds?\nA) 7.23%  B) 8.47%  C) 9.00%  D) 10.23%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 7.23%**\n\nApproximate YTM = [C + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2].\nNumerator = 90 + (1,000 − 1,062) ÷ 4 = 90 − 15.50 = **74.50**.\nDenominator = (1,000 + 1,062) ÷ 2 = **1,031**.\nYTM ≈ 74.50 ÷ 1,031 = **7.23%**. Because the bond is at a premium, the ordering Coupon (9%) > Current yield (8.47%) > YTM (7.23%) holds.\n\n- **B) 8.47%** - wrong; this is the current yield, which ignores the Rs 62 capital loss on redemption.\n- **C) 9.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate.\n- **D) 10.23%** - wrong; this flips the sign of the capital adjustment, adding (P − F) ÷ n instead of subtracting it. A premium bond can never yield more than its coupon.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** How many index futures contracts must Jignesh sell to fully hedge the market risk of his equity holding?\nA) 8 contracts  B) 10 contracts  C) 12 contracts  D) 25 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 10 contracts**\n\nValue of the equity holding = 4,000 × 1,250 = **Rs 50,00,000**. Value of one futures contract = 24,000 × 25 = **Rs 6,00,000**.\nNumber of contracts = (Portfolio value × Beta) ÷ Contract value = (50,00,000 × 1.20) ÷ 6,00,000 = 60,00,000 ÷ 6,00,000 = **10 contracts**, sold (short) to offset a fall.\n\n- **A) 8 contracts** - wrong; this is 50,00,000 ÷ 6,00,000 = 8.33 rounded down, ignoring beta. A stock that moves 1.2 times the index needs more than a rupee-for-rupee hedge.\n- **C) 12 contracts** - wrong; this applies beta twice or rounds 8.33 up after an unrelated adjustment.\n- **D) 25 contracts** - wrong; 25 is the lot size, not the number of contracts.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 3: Nirali's mutual fund folio\n\nNirali invests through the Sabarmati Bluechip Fund. On the valuation date the scheme's total assets are Rs 4,820 crore, its liabilities are Rs 20 crore and 12 crore units are outstanding. She holds 5,000 units bought nine months ago at Rs 320 per unit; the scheme levies an exit load of 1% on redemptions within 12 months. She currently holds the regular plan, whose total expense ratio is 1.85% a year, against 0.95% for the direct plan of the same scheme. Separately, she is choosing between a monthly SIP of Rs 25,000 for 5 years and a one-time lump sum of Rs 15,00,000 held for the same 5 years. For both options assume a return of 11% per annum compounded monthly, with SIP instalments made at the end of each month.\n\n**Q101.** What is the NAV per unit of the scheme?\nA) Rs 400.00  B) Rs 401.67  C) Rs 398.33  D) Rs 320.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 400.00**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Liabilities) ÷ Units outstanding = (4,820 − 20) crore ÷ 12 crore units = 4,800 ÷ 12 = **Rs 400.00**.\n\n- **B) Rs 401.67** - wrong; this divides total assets by units without deducting liabilities (4,820 ÷ 12).\n- **C) Rs 398.33** - wrong; this deducts the liabilities twice (4,780 ÷ 12).\n- **D) Rs 320.00** - wrong; this is Nirali's purchase cost, not the current NAV.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q102.** If she redeems all 5,000 units today, what will she receive after the exit load?\nA) Rs 15,84,000  B) Rs 19,80,000  C) Rs 19,60,000  D) Rs 20,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 19,80,000**\n\nGross redemption value = 5,000 × 400 = **Rs 20,00,000**. Holding period is nine months, so the 1% exit load applies: 1% × 20,00,000 = **Rs 20,000**. Net proceeds = 20,00,000 − 20,000 = **Rs 19,80,000**. The load is charged on the redemption value, not on the cost.\n\n- **A) Rs 15,84,000** - wrong; this applies the load to the purchase value (5,000 × 320 × 0.99).\n- **C) Rs 19,60,000** - wrong; this applies a 2% load instead of the stated 1%.\n- **D) Rs 20,00,000** - wrong; this ignores the exit load, which is due because the units are less than 12 months old.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q103.** On a holding of Rs 20,00,000, how much would she save in a year by moving to the direct plan?\nA) Rs 18,000  B) Rs 19,000  C) Rs 37,000  D) Rs 56,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 18,000**\n\nThe TER difference is 1.85% − 0.95% = **0.90%** a year, and it represents the distribution commission embedded in the regular plan. Saving = 0.90% × 20,00,000 = **Rs 18,000** a year. The portfolio is identical in both plans; only the cost, and therefore the NAV, differs.\n\n- **B) Rs 19,000** - wrong; this is 0.95% of the holding, the direct plan's whole expense, not the saving.\n- **C) Rs 37,000** - wrong; this is 1.85% of the holding, the regular plan's whole expense.\n- **D) Rs 56,000** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios together instead of subtracting them.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q104.** What will the SIP of Rs 25,000 a month grow to in 5 years?\nA) Rs 15,00,000  B) Rs 19,87,952  C) Rs 20,06,175  D) Rs 25,93,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 19,87,952**\n\nMonthly rate r = 11% ÷ 12 = 0.0091667; n = 5 × 12 = 60. (1.0091667)^60 = **1.728916**.\nFV = 25,000 × [(1.728916 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667] = 25,000 × 79.5181 = **Rs 19,87,952**.\nTotal contributed is Rs 15,00,000, so returns supplied Rs 4,87,952.\n\n- **A) Rs 15,00,000** - wrong; this is the amount contributed (25,000 × 60), with no growth at all.\n- **C) Rs 20,06,175** - wrong; this treats the SIP as an annuity *due* (×1.0091667). The question states instalments are made at the end of each month.\n- **D) Rs 25,93,374** - wrong; this is the lump-sum alternative, not the SIP.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q105.** What will the lump sum of Rs 15,00,000 grow to over the same 5 years?\nA) Rs 19,87,952  B) Rs 23,25,000  C) Rs 25,27,587  D) Rs 25,93,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) Rs 25,93,374**\n\nFV = 15,00,000 × (1 + 0.11 ÷ 12)^60 = 15,00,000 × 1.728916 = **Rs 25,93,374**. The lump sum beats the SIP by Rs 6,05,422 for the same total outlay, because every rupee is invested from day one rather than drip-fed. The SIP's advantage is not return but the management of timing risk when the money arrives monthly.\n\n- **A) Rs 19,87,952** - wrong; this is the SIP corpus.\n- **B) Rs 23,25,000** - wrong; this applies simple interest (15,00,000 × [1 + 0.11 × 5]).\n- **C) Rs 25,27,587** - wrong; this compounds annually (15,00,000 × 1.11^5) instead of monthly as the question specifies.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 4: Deploying the warehouse sale proceeds\n\nJignesh has sold a warehouse and has Rs 3.5 crore to deploy. He places Rs 1.50 crore with a SEBI-registered discretionary portfolio manager. The mandate carries a fixed fee of 1.5% of assets plus a performance fee of 20% of returns above a hurdle rate of 10% per annum, with no catch-up, charged on a high watermark basis. In year 1 the portfolio rises from Rs 1.50 crore to Rs 1.86 crore; in year 2 it falls to Rs 1.62 crore; in year 3 it rises to Rs 1.98 crore. Assume the high watermark is set at each year-end value before performance fees, and that in years 2 and 3 the fee is tested against the high watermark alone. He also commits Rs 1 crore to a Category II AIF, and his relationship manager is separately pitching a hedge fund that runs a leveraged long-short strategy in listed equities and derivatives.\n\n**Q106.** What are the minimum investment amounts SEBI prescribes for a portfolio management service and for an AIF respectively?\nA) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 1 crore and Rs 50 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020 the minimum client investment in a PMS is **Rs 50 lakh**. Under the SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012 the minimum investment in an AIF is **Rs 1 crore**. The ladder to memorise is mutual fund Rs 100–5,000, PMS Rs 50 lakh, AIF Rs 1 crore.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the reduced minimum for employees and directors of an AIF's manager, not the PMS threshold.\n- **C) Rs 1 crore and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; the two figures are transposed.\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; the PMS figure is right but Rs 25 lakh is the employee/director concession and also the angel investor minimum, not the general AIF minimum.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q107.** What performance fee is payable for year 1?\nA) Rs 3,00,000  B) Rs 4,20,000  C) Rs 7,20,000  D) Rs 2,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,20,000**\n\nGain in year 1 = 1,86,00,000 − 1,50,00,000 = **Rs 36,00,000**. The 10% hurdle absorbs the first 10% × 1,50,00,000 = **Rs 15,00,000**. With no catch-up, the fee applies only to the excess: 20% × (36,00,000 − 15,00,000) = 20% × 21,00,000 = **Rs 4,20,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,00,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the hurdle amount itself rather than of the excess above it.\n- **C) Rs 7,20,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the whole Rs 36,00,000 gain and ignores the hurdle entirely.\n- **D) Rs 2,10,000** - wrong; this applies a 10% fee rate to the excess. The performance fee rate is 20%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q108.** What performance fee is payable for year 2?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 2,40,000  C) Rs 3,00,000  D) Rs 4,20,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Nil**\n\nThe portfolio fell from Rs 1.86 crore to Rs 1.62 crore. There is no gain at all, and the value is below the high watermark of **Rs 1.86 crore**. **No performance fee is payable.** This is exactly what the high watermark protects against — the client must not pay a second time for ground already covered.\n\n- **B) Rs 2,40,000** - wrong; this is the year-3 fee.\n- **C) Rs 3,00,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the hurdle amount in a year in which the portfolio lost value.\n- **D) Rs 4,20,000** - wrong; this simply repeats the year-1 fee, which no fee structure permits in a loss year.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q109.** What performance fee is payable for year 3 on a high watermark basis?\nA) Rs 2,40,000  B) Rs 4,20,000  C) Rs 7,20,000  D) Rs 9,60,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,40,000**\n\nThe high watermark is **Rs 1.86 crore**. The year-3 value is Rs 1.98 crore, so the fee-bearing gain is only the amount above the previous peak: 1,98,00,000 − 1,86,00,000 = **Rs 12,00,000**. Fee = 20% × 12,00,000 = **Rs 2,40,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 4,20,000** - wrong; this is the year-1 fee.\n- **C) Rs 7,20,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the Rs 36,00,000 rise from the year-2 trough of Rs 1.62 crore, which is precisely the double-charging the high watermark prevents.\n- **D) Rs 9,60,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the Rs 48,00,000 rise from the original Rs 1.50 crore, ignoring the fee already taken in year 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q110.** Into which AIF category does the leveraged long-short hedge fund fall, and how is it taxed?\nA) Category I; income is a pass-through taxed in the investor's hands  B) Category II; income is a pass-through taxed in the investor's hands  C) Category III; income is taxed at the fund level  D) Category III; income is a pass-through taxed in the investor's hands\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Category III; income is taxed at the fund level**\n\nA fund employing **diverse or complex trading strategies, including leverage through listed or unlisted derivatives**, is a **Category III** AIF; hedge funds and PIPE funds sit here. Only Category III may use leverage. Pass-through taxation is available to **Categories I and II only**, so a Category III AIF is taxed **at the fund level**.\n\n- **A) Category I** - wrong; Category I covers start-ups, SMEs, social ventures and infrastructure, and may not use leverage.\n- **B) Category II** - wrong; Category II is defined by subtraction — not I, not III, and no leverage other than for day-to-day operations.\n- **D) Category III with pass-through** - wrong; the category is right but the tax treatment is not. Losing the pass-through is the defining tax feature of Category III.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 5: Bhavesh Trivedi, individual investment adviser\n\nBhavesh Trivedi is an individual investment adviser registered with SEBI, practising in Ahmedabad, and he advises the Vaghela family. His net tangible assets are Rs 6,20,000. He currently has 138 individual clients and is onboarding steadily. His wife runs an AMFI-registered mutual fund distribution business from the same office and proposes to distribute regular-plan schemes to Bhavesh's advisory clients, disclosing the commission in writing. Bhavesh charges the Vaghela family a fixed fee of Rs 1,40,000 a year; the family's assets under his advice are Rs 3.2 crore. One of his other clients, a 66-year-old retired schoolteacher, has been risk-profiled as conservative with a three-year horizon and no capacity to bear a loss of capital; Bhavesh has recommended that she place Rs 1 crore in a Category III AIF, and she has signed a letter saying she accepts the risk.\n\n**Q111.** What is the minimum net worth SEBI requires of an individual investment adviser?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 5 lakh  C) Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 lakh**\n\nIndividuals and partnership firms registered as investment advisers must maintain **net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh**, at all times. Bhavesh's Rs 6,20,000 complies.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the pre-2020 individual requirement and is currently the individual Research Analyst figure.\n- **C) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh was the pre-2020 non-individual requirement, raised in 2020.\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the net worth required of a **non-individual** investment adviser.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q112.** Above what number of clients must Bhavesh apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser?\nA) 50  B) 100  C) 150  D) 200\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 150**\n\nAn individual investment adviser whose number of clients **exceeds 150** must apply for registration as a **non-individual** investment adviser, and must **stop onboarding fresh clients** as an individual until that registration is granted. At 138 clients Bhavesh is close to the corporatisation threshold and must plan for it.\n\n- **A) 50** - wrong; there is no 50-client threshold in the IA Regulations.\n- **B) 100** - wrong; 100 is not the trigger. The figure to remember alongside Rs 5 lakh / Rs 50 lakh net worth is 150 clients.\n- **D) 200** - wrong; the threshold is lower than this.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q113.** May Bhavesh's wife distribute regular-plan mutual fund schemes to his advisory clients?\nA) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed to each client in writing  B) Yes, because she is separately registered with AMFI and is a different legal person  C) No, because the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients  D) No, unless each client signs a one-time consent electing to receive both services\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No, because the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**\n\nRegulation 22 is explicit: an **individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services at all**, and the **family of the individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**. The family of an individual IA is the IA, spouse, children and parents. The arrangement is prohibited outright.\n\n- **A) With disclosure** - wrong; SEBI's 2020 answer to this conflict is structural **segregation**, not disclosure. Disclosure does not cure a prohibited arrangement.\n- **B) Separate AMFI registration** - wrong; her own registration is irrelevant. The bar attaches to her relationship with the adviser.\n- **D) One-time consent** - wrong; the one-time choice was a transitional facility for *existing* clients receiving both services, requiring them to elect **one** of the two. It is not a consent mechanism for taking both.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q114.** What is the maximum annual fee Bhavesh may charge the Vaghela family under the assets-under-advice mode?\nA) Rs 1,25,000  B) Rs 1,40,000  C) Rs 8,00,000  D) Rs 16,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 8,00,000**\n\nUnder mode (A), the fee may not exceed **2.5% of assets under advice per annum per family of client**. AUA = Rs 3,20,00,000, so the ceiling is 2.5% × 3,20,00,000 = **Rs 8,00,000**. Under mode (B), the fixed-fee mode, the ceiling is Rs 1,25,000 a year per family — which the Rs 1,40,000 he is charging would breach. The adviser must elect one mode, and here the AUA mode gives far more headroom.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the ceiling under the **fixed fee** mode, not the AUA mode.\n- **B) Rs 1,40,000** - wrong; this is what he currently charges, which is not a regulatory ceiling and in fact breaches the fixed-fee cap.\n- **D) Rs 16,00,000** - wrong; this applies 5% of AUA. The cap is 2.5%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q115.** Which regulation does the recommendation to the retired schoolteacher breach?\nA) None, because she signed a letter accepting the risk  B) Regulation 17, because the advice is not appropriate to the client's assessed risk profile  C) None, because an AIF is a privately placed product outside the scope of investment advice  D) Regulation 16 only, because the risk profile was never communicated to her\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Regulation 17, because the advice is not appropriate to the client's assessed risk profile**\n\nRegulation 16 obliges the adviser to **profile** the client, and that was done — she was assessed as conservative, three-year horizon, no capacity to bear loss of capital. Regulation 17 obliges the adviser to ensure that **all investments advised are appropriate to that risk profile**. A Category III AIF is leveraged, illiquid, locked in for years and taxed at the fund level; recommending it here is a textbook **suitability failure**.\n\n- **A) A signed acceptance** - wrong; a client's consent cannot convert unsuitable advice into suitable advice. The fiduciary duty under Regulation 15(1) is not waivable by a letter.\n- **C) Outside the scope** - wrong; investment advice covers securities and investment products generally, and units of an AIF are squarely within it.\n- **D) Regulation 16 only** - wrong; the profiling was in fact carried out. The failure is at the matching stage, which is Regulation 17. Profiling and suitability are separate, separately tested obligations.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 6: Rebalancing after a change of plan\n\nThe Vaghela family's investment portfolio was built to a strategic asset allocation of 60% equity, 30% debt and 10% gold, with a tolerance band of plus or minus 5 percentage points around each target, reviewed annually. A year ago the portfolio was worth Rs 2,00,00,000. After a strong equity year it now stands at equity Rs 1,45,00,000, debt Rs 62,00,000 and gold Rs 23,00,000. In the meantime Jignesh has signed a term sheet to sell his business in three years and retire at 55 rather than 60, and his adviser proposes a revised strategic allocation of 40% equity, 50% debt and 10% gold.\n\n**Q116.** What is the current weight of equity in the portfolio?\nA) 60.00%  B) 62.50%  C) 63.04%  D) 72.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 63.04%**\n\nCurrent total = 1,45,00,000 + 62,00,000 + 23,00,000 = **Rs 2,30,00,000**. Equity weight = 1,45,00,000 ÷ 2,30,00,000 = **63.04%**.\n\n- **A) 60.00%** - wrong; this is the target weight, not the actual weight after drift.\n- **B) 62.50%** - wrong; no combination of the given figures produces this.\n- **D) 72.50%** - wrong; this divides the current equity value by the *old* total of Rs 2,00,00,000. The denominator must be the current portfolio value.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q117.** Under the family's threshold rebalancing policy, is a rebalance triggered by the equity drift?\nA) Yes, because equity is 3.04 percentage points above target, which breaches the band  B) Yes, because the portfolio value has risen 15%, which breaches the band  C) No, because equity's deviation of 3.04 percentage points is inside the plus or minus 5 percentage point band  D) No, because threshold rebalancing is applied only to the debt allocation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No, because equity's deviation of 3.04 percentage points is inside the plus or minus 5 percentage point band**\n\nEquity is at 63.04% against a 60% target, a deviation of **3.04 percentage points**. The band permits up to 5 percentage points either side, i.e. 55%–65%. Debt is at 62,00,000 ÷ 2,30,00,000 = 26.96%, a 3.04 point deviation, and gold is at exactly 10%. **No band is breached, so a threshold policy would not trade.**\n\n- **A) 3.04 points breaches the band** - wrong; 3.04 is less than 5, so the band holds.\n- **B) The rise in portfolio value** - wrong; a tolerance band is measured on **weights**, not on the change in total value.\n- **D) Debt only** - wrong; bands apply to every asset class in the policy.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q118.** Ignoring the bands, how much equity would have to be sold to restore the original 60:30:10 allocation?\nA) Rs 3,00,000  B) Rs 7,00,000  C) Rs 25,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 7,00,000**\n\nTarget equity = 60% × 2,30,00,000 = **Rs 1,38,00,000**. Current equity = Rs 1,45,00,000. Equity to sell = 1,45,00,000 − 1,38,00,000 = **Rs 7,00,000**, which is bought into debt (target 30% × 2,30,00,000 = Rs 69,00,000 against Rs 62,00,000 held). Gold is already at exactly 10% and needs no trade.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,00,000** - wrong; this misreads the 3.04 percentage point drift as a rupee figure in lakhs.\n- **C) Rs 25,00,000** - wrong; this is 1,45,00,000 less 60% of the *old* Rs 2,00,00,000 total.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; the band question and the restore-to-target question are different. Restoring the target does require a trade even though the band does not compel one.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q119.** Under the revised 40:50:10 allocation, how much equity must be sold?\nA) Rs 7,00,000  B) Rs 53,00,000  C) Rs 65,00,000  D) Rs 92,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 53,00,000**\n\nNew target equity = 40% × 2,30,00,000 = **Rs 92,00,000**. Current equity = Rs 1,45,00,000. Equity to sell = 1,45,00,000 − 92,00,000 = **Rs 53,00,000**, moved into debt, whose target rises to 50% × 2,30,00,000 = Rs 1,15,00,000.\n\n- **A) Rs 7,00,000** - wrong; that is the trade needed under the *old* 60:30:10 policy.\n- **C) Rs 65,00,000** - wrong; this uses the old Rs 2,00,00,000 base (1,45,00,000 − 80,00,000).\n- **D) Rs 92,00,000** - wrong; this is the new **target** equity holding, not the amount to be sold.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q120.** How should the move from 60:30:10 to 40:50:10 be classified?\nA) A tactical asset allocation shift, because it is a temporary deviation to be reversed later  B) A strategic asset allocation change, because it is driven by a change in the client's circumstances  C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement, because it moves outside the tolerance band  D) A routine rebalancing trade, because the total portfolio value is unchanged\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A strategic asset allocation change, because it is driven by a change in the client's circumstances**\n\nThe test is simple: **if the reason for the change is something that happened in the client's life, it is strategic; if it is something that happened in the market, it is tactical.** Jignesh's horizon has shortened from eight years to three and a liquidity event is coming — a life event. The IPS is formally amended and the new weights become the long-term policy mix.\n\n- **A) Tactical** - wrong; tactical allocation is a **temporary** deviation exploiting a perceived market mispricing, funded from the strategic weights and then reversed. Nothing here is temporary.\n- **C) A breach of the IPS** - wrong; a documented, deliberate revision of the IPS is not a breach. A breach is an unauthorised move outside the bands while the IPS still stands.\n- **D) Routine rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing restores the *existing* targets. Here the targets themselves are being changed.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section C — Caselets (3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 7: Jignesh's retirement plan and a loan against property\n\nJignesh Vaghela is 52 and now plans to retire at 60, exactly 8 years from today. In today's money his household expects to spend Rs 1,20,000 a month in retirement; general inflation is assumed at 6% per annum. His adviser sizes the retirement corpus at 25 times the **first year's annual retirement expenditure**. Rs 90,00,000 is already earmarked for retirement and is expected to earn 10% per annum compounded annually until he retires. Any additional saving will be made as a level monthly SIP, at the end of each month, earning 11% per annum compounded monthly for 8 years. Separately, Jignesh is considering a loan against property of Rs 60,00,000 at 9.5% per annum for 10 years, repaid by monthly EMIs. His household's existing EMIs are Rs 62,000 a month against a gross monthly household income of Rs 3,30,000, and the lender will not approve a proposal where total EMIs exceed 40% of gross income.\n\n**Q121.** What will the first year's annual retirement expenditure be, in the rupees of the year he retires?\nA) Rs 14,40,000  B) Rs 21,31,200  C) Rs 22,95,141  D) Rs 24,32,850\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 22,95,141**\n\nStep 1 — inflate the monthly figure: (1.06)^8 = **1.593848**, so the monthly expense at 60 = 1,20,000 × 1.593848 = **Rs 1,91,262**.\nStep 2 — annualise: 1,91,262 × 12 = **Rs 22,95,141**.\n\n- **A) Rs 14,40,000** - wrong; this is today's annual expense (1,20,000 × 12) with no inflation applied at all. Forgetting to inflate the goal is the commonest error in the whole module.\n- **B) Rs 21,31,200** - wrong; this applies simple inflation of 6% × 8 = 48% (1,20,000 × 1.48 × 12) instead of compounding it.\n- **D) Rs 24,32,850** - wrong; this compounds inflation over 9 years instead of 8.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q122.** What is the shortfall between the corpus Jignesh needs at 60 and what the already-earmarked money will have grown to?\nA) Rs 3,80,86,231  B) Rs 1,92,92,299  C) Rs 4,83,78,531  D) Rs 5,73,78,531\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 3,80,86,231**\n\nStep 1 — corpus required = 25 × 22,95,141 = **Rs 5,73,78,531**.\nStep 2 — the earmarked money grows: 90,00,000 × (1.10)^8 = 90,00,000 × 2.143589 = **Rs 1,92,92,299**.\nStep 3 — shortfall = 5,73,78,531 − 1,92,92,299 = **Rs 3,80,86,231**.\n\n- **B) Rs 1,92,92,299** - wrong; this is the grown value of the existing corpus, not the gap.\n- **C) Rs 4,83,78,531** - wrong; this deducts the Rs 90,00,000 at its *present* value without growing it for 8 years.\n- **D) Rs 5,73,78,531** - wrong; this is the full corpus required and ignores the existing earmarked savings altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q123.** What level monthly SIP is needed to fund that shortfall over 8 years?\nA) Rs 2,49,151  B) Rs 2,60,538  C) Rs 3,75,356  D) Rs 3,96,732\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,49,151**\n\nr = 11% ÷ 12 = **0.0091667**; n = 8 × 12 = **96**; (1.0091667)^96 = **2.401254**.\nAnnuity factor = (2.401254 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667 = **152.8641**.\nPMT = 3,80,86,231 ÷ 152.8641 = **Rs 2,49,151** a month. At Rs 3,30,000 of gross income this is not affordable, which is precisely the finding the adviser must put to the client.\n\n- **B) Rs 2,60,538** - wrong; this uses the 10% return applicable to the existing corpus instead of the 11% stated for fresh savings.\n- **C) Rs 3,75,356** - wrong; this annuitises the full Rs 5,73,78,531 corpus and forgets to net off the grown existing savings.\n- **D) Rs 3,96,732** - wrong; this simply divides the shortfall by 96 months, assuming the SIP earns nothing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q124.** What would the EMI be on the Rs 60,00,000 loan against property?\nA) Rs 50,000  B) Rs 74,395  C) Rs 77,639  D) Rs 97,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 77,639**\n\nr = 9.5% ÷ 12 = **0.0079167**; n = 10 × 12 = **120**; (1.0079167)^120 = **2.576247**.\nEMI = 60,00,000 × [0.0079167 × 2.576247] ÷ [2.576247 − 1] = 60,00,000 × 0.0203937 ÷ 1.576247 = **Rs 77,639**.\nTotal repaid = 77,639 × 120 = Rs 93,16,680, of which Rs 33,16,680 is interest.\n\n- **A) Rs 50,000** - wrong; this is principal ÷ number of months, which ignores interest entirely.\n- **B) Rs 74,395** - wrong; this is the EMI computed at 8.5% rather than the stated 9.5%.\n- **D) Rs 97,500** - wrong; this adds the first month's interest (Rs 47,500) to principal ÷ n (Rs 50,000). Interest falls over the tenure, so this overstates the level EMI.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q125.** If Jignesh takes the loan, what will the household's total EMI-to-income ratio be, and will the lender approve it?\nA) 18.79%, approved  B) 23.53%, approved  C) 42.31%, rejected  D) 53.71%, rejected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 42.31%, rejected**\n\nStep 1 — total EMIs = existing 62,000 + new 77,639 = **Rs 1,39,639**.\nStep 2 — ratio = 1,39,639 ÷ 3,30,000 = **42.31%**.\nThat is above the lender's 40% ceiling (which would allow total EMIs of only Rs 1,32,000), so the proposal is **rejected** as structured. Reducing the loan or lengthening the tenure would be the obvious remedies.\n\n- **A) 18.79%, approved** - wrong; this is the existing debt-to-income ratio before the new loan.\n- **B) 23.53%, approved** - wrong; this counts only the new EMI (77,639 ÷ 3,30,000) and ignores the existing EMIs the lender will certainly count.\n- **D) 53.71%, rejected** - wrong; the conclusion is right but the arithmetic is not — this divides total EMIs by Jignesh's Rs 2,60,000 alone rather than by gross household income.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 8: Ranking three portfolio managers\n\nJignesh's family office is evaluating three discretionary managers over an identical five-year period. The risk-free rate is 6.5% per annum, the market returned 13% per annum with a standard deviation of 15% and a beta of 1.00 by definition. The three portfolios recorded:\n\n- **Portfolio Amreli** — return 16.4%, standard deviation 18%, beta 1.15\n- **Portfolio Bhuj** — return 13.8%, standard deviation 11%, beta 0.85\n- **Portfolio Chandkheda** — return 18.2%, standard deviation 24%, beta 1.40\n\nThe family office is also modelling a combined mandate of 65% in Amreli and 35% in Bhuj; the correlation between the two portfolios' returns is 0.35.\n\n**Q126.** By how much does Portfolio Amreli's Sharpe ratio exceed the market's Sharpe ratio?\nA) 0.0333  B) 0.1167  C) 0.4333  D) 0.5500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.1167**\n\nStep 1 — Amreli's Sharpe = (16.4 − 6.5) ÷ 18 = 9.9 ÷ 18 = **0.5500**.\nStep 2 — market Sharpe = (13.0 − 6.5) ÷ 15 = 6.5 ÷ 15 = **0.4333**.\nExcess = 0.5500 − 0.4333 = **0.1167**. Amreli earned more return per unit of total risk than the index did.\n\n- **A) 0.0333** - wrong; this subtracts using a market Sharpe wrongly computed as 6.5 ÷ 12.5 or similar; the market's denominator is its 15% standard deviation.\n- **C) 0.4333** - wrong; this is the market's Sharpe ratio itself.\n- **D) 0.5500** - wrong; this is Amreli's Sharpe ratio, not the excess over the market.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q127.** Which portfolio has the highest Treynor ratio, and what is it?\nA) Amreli, 8.61%  B) Bhuj, 8.59%  C) Chandkheda, 8.36%  D) Chandkheda, 11.70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Amreli, 8.61%**\n\nTreynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ beta.\nAmreli = (16.4 − 6.5) ÷ 1.15 = 9.9 ÷ 1.15 = **8.61%**\nBhuj = (13.8 − 6.5) ÷ 0.85 = 7.3 ÷ 0.85 = **8.59%**\nChandkheda = (18.2 − 6.5) ÷ 1.40 = 11.7 ÷ 1.40 = **8.36%**\n**Amreli wins, narrowly.** Note that it did *not* win on Sharpe — the two measures can disagree because one divides by total risk and the other only by systematic risk.\n\n- **B) Bhuj, 8.59%** - wrong; the value is correct for Bhuj but it is second, not first.\n- **C) Chandkheda, 8.36%** - wrong; the value is correct for Chandkheda but it is the lowest of the three.\n- **D) Chandkheda, 11.70%** - wrong; 11.70 is Chandkheda's excess return in percentage points, before dividing by its beta of 1.40.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q128.** Which portfolio delivered the highest Jensen's alpha, and what is it?\nA) Amreli, 2.43%  B) Bhuj, 1.78%  C) Chandkheda, 2.60%  D) Chandkheda, 5.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Chandkheda, 2.60%**\n\nAlpha = Rp − [Rf + beta × (Rm − Rf)], with the market risk premium = 13.0 − 6.5 = **6.5%**.\nAmreli = 16.4 − [6.5 + 1.15 × 6.5] = 16.4 − 13.975 = **2.43%**\nBhuj = 13.8 − [6.5 + 0.85 × 6.5] = 13.8 − 12.025 = **1.78%**\nChandkheda = 18.2 − [6.5 + 1.40 × 6.5] = 18.2 − 15.60 = **2.60%**\n**Chandkheda has the highest alpha** even though it had the worst Sharpe and the worst Treynor — alpha rewards raw excess return over the CAPM prediction and is not scaled by the risk taken.\n\n- **A) Amreli, 2.43%** - wrong; the value is correct for Amreli but it ranks second.\n- **B) Bhuj, 1.78%** - wrong; the value is correct for Bhuj but it ranks third.\n- **D) Chandkheda, 5.20%** - wrong; this is 18.2 − 13.0, the excess over the market return, which ignores beta and is not Jensen's alpha.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q129.** For the 65:35 combination of Amreli and Bhuj, by how much does the portfolio's standard deviation fall below the weighted average of the two standard deviations?\nA) 2.01 percentage points  B) 4.46 percentage points  C) 13.54 percentage points  D) 15.55 percentage points\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 2.01 percentage points**\n\nStep 1 — portfolio standard deviation:\nσp = √(0.65² × 0.18² + 0.35² × 0.11² + 2 × 0.65 × 0.35 × 0.18 × 0.11 × 0.35)\n = √(0.013689 + 0.001482 + 0.003153) = √0.018324 = **13.54%**\nStep 2 — weighted average of the two standard deviations = 0.65 × 18 + 0.35 × 11 = 11.70 + 3.85 = **15.55%**\nReduction = 15.55 − 13.54 = **2.01 percentage points**, obtained for free because the correlation of 0.35 is below 1.\n\n- **B) 4.46 percentage points** - wrong; this compares the portfolio's 13.54% with Amreli's own 18% standard deviation rather than with the weighted average.\n- **C) 13.54 percentage points** - wrong; this is the portfolio standard deviation itself, not the reduction.\n- **D) 15.55 percentage points** - wrong; this is the weighted average standard deviation, not the reduction.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q130.** Ranked from best to worst on the Sharpe ratio, what is the correct order?\nA) Amreli, Bhuj, Chandkheda  B) Bhuj, Amreli, Chandkheda  C) Chandkheda, Amreli, Bhuj  D) Chandkheda, Bhuj, Amreli\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Bhuj, Amreli, Chandkheda**\n\nSharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp.\nBhuj = (13.8 − 6.5) ÷ 11 = 7.3 ÷ 11 = **0.6636**\nAmreli = (16.4 − 6.5) ÷ 18 = 9.9 ÷ 18 = **0.5500**\nChandkheda = (18.2 − 6.5) ÷ 24 = 11.7 ÷ 24 = **0.4875**\nThe order is **Bhuj, Amreli, Chandkheda**. The highest raw return, Chandkheda's 18.2%, finishes last once its 24% volatility is charged for. Sharpe is the right yardstick here because the family office would hold one manager as a standalone portfolio, so total risk is what the client actually bears.\n\n- **A) Amreli, Bhuj, Chandkheda** - wrong; this is close but reverses the top two. Bhuj's much lower volatility outweighs its lower return.\n- **C) Chandkheda, Amreli, Bhuj** - wrong; this ranks by raw return and ignores risk entirely.\n- **D) Chandkheda, Bhuj, Amreli** - wrong; this also leads with raw return and then misorders the remaining two.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 9: A bond and a hedge on a single stock\n\nJignesh is offered a corporate bond of Rs 1,000 face value carrying a coupon of 7.8% paid annually, with exactly 5 years to maturity, redeemable at par. The market's required yield on comparable paper is 8.6%. The dealer quotes the bond's modified duration as 4.05, and the treasury desk expects yields on this segment to rise by 60 basis points over the next quarter.\n\nJignesh separately holds 400 shares of Mundra Logistics Ltd bought at Rs 1,390 a share; the stock trades at Rs 1,420 today. He is weighing two hedges over the same expiry: **(i)** selling one futures contract of 400 shares at Rs 1,448, or **(ii)** buying one put option contract of 400 shares with a strike of Rs 1,400 at a premium of Rs 38 a share. Assume the stock settles at Rs 1,375 on the expiry date and that in each case the shares themselves are still held.\n\n**Q131.** What is the fair price of the bond at the required yield of 8.6%?\nA) Rs 960.00  B) Rs 968.56  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,032.11\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 968.56**\n\nStep 1 — discount factor: (1.086)^5 = **1.510589**, so 1 ÷ 1.510589 = **0.661989**.\nStep 2 — annuity factor for the coupons = (1 − 0.661989) ÷ 0.086 = 0.338011 ÷ 0.086 = **3.930359**.\nPV of coupons = 78 × 3.930359 = **Rs 306.57**; PV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.661989 = **Rs 661.99**.\nPrice = 306.57 + 661.99 = **Rs 968.56**. Coupon (7.8%) is below the required yield (8.6%), so the bond correctly prices at a **discount**.\n\n- **A) Rs 960.00** - wrong; this approximates the discount as 5 years × the 0.8 percentage point yield gap × Rs 1,000, which ignores discounting altogether.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would price at par only if the coupon equalled the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,032.11** - wrong; this swaps the coupon and the yield (8.6% coupon discounted at 7.8%), which produces a premium instead of a discount.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q132.** What is the current yield on the bond at that price?\nA) 7.56%  B) 7.80%  C) 8.05%  D) 8.60%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 8.05%**\n\nStep 1 — the price from the valuation is **Rs 968.56**.\nStep 2 — current yield = Annual coupon ÷ Market price = 78 ÷ 968.56 = **8.05%**.\nFor a discount bond the ordering Coupon (7.80%) < Current yield (8.05%) < YTM (8.60%) must hold, and it does — a useful check on the price computed in the previous question.\n\n- **A) 7.56%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by Rs 1,032.11, the price produced by swapping coupon and yield.\n- **B) 7.80%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, which is measured on face value. Current yield uses market price.\n- **D) 8.60%** - wrong; this is the required yield, that is the YTM, which also captures the pull to par at redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q133.** If yields rise by 60 basis points as the treasury desk expects, what will the bond be worth?\nA) Rs 921.49  B) Rs 945.02  C) Rs 968.56  D) Rs 992.09\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 945.02**\n\nStep 1 — percentage price change ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −4.05 × 0.0060 = **−2.43%**.\nStep 2 — new price = 968.56 × (1 − 0.0243) = 968.56 − 23.54 = **Rs 945.02**.\n\n- **A) Rs 921.49** - wrong; this uses a yield change of 120 basis points. Sixty basis points is 0.60%, that is 0.0060 in decimal.\n- **C) Rs 968.56** - wrong; this is the price before the yield change.\n- **D) Rs 992.09** - wrong; this adds 2.43% instead of subtracting it. Prices move **inversely** to yields.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q134.** Under hedge (i), what is the combined profit or loss on the shares and the futures position at expiry?\nA) Profit of Rs 18,000  B) Profit of Rs 23,200  C) Profit of Rs 29,200  D) Loss of Rs 6,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Profit of Rs 23,200**\n\nStep 1 — the shares: bought at Rs 1,390, settle at Rs 1,375, so (1,375 − 1,390) × 400 = **−Rs 6,000**.\nStep 2 — the short futures: sold at Rs 1,448, settle at Rs 1,375, so (1,448 − 1,375) × 400 = **+Rs 29,200**.\nCombined = 29,200 − 6,000 = **profit of Rs 23,200**. The futures were sold above spot, so the basis of Rs 28 a share was itself locked in as gain.\n\n- **A) Profit of Rs 18,000** - wrong; this measures the futures gain from today's spot of Rs 1,420 rather than the Rs 1,448 at which the contract was actually sold, and then omits the loss on the shares.\n- **C) Profit of Rs 29,200** - wrong; this is the futures leg alone and ignores the Rs 6,000 loss on the underlying shares.\n- **D) Loss of Rs 6,000** - wrong; this is the unhedged position on the shares alone, which is what the hedge was put on to avoid.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q135.** Under hedge (ii), what is the combined profit or loss on the shares and the put option at expiry?\nA) Loss of Rs 5,200  B) Loss of Rs 6,000  C) Loss of Rs 11,200  D) Profit of Rs 10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Loss of Rs 11,200**\n\nStep 1 — the put: intrinsic value at expiry = max(1,400 − 1,375, 0) = **Rs 25** a share, so gross payoff = 25 × 400 = **Rs 10,000**. Premium paid = 38 × 400 = **Rs 15,200**. Net on the option = 10,000 − 15,200 = **−Rs 5,200**.\nStep 2 — the shares: (1,375 − 1,390) × 400 = **−Rs 6,000**.\nCombined = −5,200 − 6,000 = **loss of Rs 11,200**. The put finished in the money but not profitable — the break-even spot for the long put was 1,400 − 38 = Rs 1,362, and the stock never got there. In-the-money is not the same as profitable.\n\n- **A) Loss of Rs 5,200** - wrong; this is the option leg alone and omits the loss on the shares.\n- **B) Loss of Rs 6,000** - wrong; this is the shares alone and omits the cost of the option.\n- **D) Profit of Rs 10,000** - wrong; this takes the put's gross intrinsic payoff and forgets both the Rs 15,200 premium and the share loss.\n\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "04", "md": "# Mock Paper 4 — NISM Series X-A (Investment Adviser Level 1)\n\n**Total: 150 marks · 135 questions · Time: 3 hours · Pass: 90/150 (60%) · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n## Section A — Independent MCQs (90 questions × 1 mark = 90 marks)\n\n**Q1.** In the six-step financial planning process, which step immediately follows \"defining client needs and financial goals\"?\nA) Establishing the client–planner relationship  B) Gathering data on the client's financial situation  C) Risk profiling and asset allocation  D) Reviewing and rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Risk profiling and asset allocation**\n\nThe order is: (1) establish the client–planner relationship, (2) gather data on the client's financial situation, (3) define client needs and financial goals, (4) **risk profiling and asset allocation**, (5) portfolio construction, (6) reviewing and rebalancing. Step 4 follows step 3.\n\n- **A) Establishing the client–planner relationship** - wrong; that is step 1, the very first step.\n- **B) Gathering data on the client's financial situation** - wrong; that is step 2, which precedes goal definition.\n- **D) Reviewing and rebalancing** - wrong; that is the last step, step 6.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Dr Aravind Rajagopal is a 29-year-old radiologist in Chennai with a stable hospital income, no dependants and a 25-year investment horizon, but he redeemed his entire equity fund holding after a 10% market fall last year. How should his risk profile be described?\nA) High risk capacity and high risk tolerance  B) High risk capacity but low risk tolerance  C) Low risk capacity but high risk tolerance  D) Low risk capacity and low risk tolerance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) High risk capacity but low risk tolerance**\n\n**Risk capacity (ability)** is objective — long horizon, stable income, no dependants and a young age all point to **high** capacity. **Risk tolerance (willingness)** is subjective, and panic-selling after a modest 10% fall reveals **low** tolerance. Where the two conflict, the adviser plans to the **lower** of the two.\n\n- **A) High risk capacity and high risk tolerance** - wrong; the panic sale contradicts high tolerance.\n- **C) Low risk capacity but high risk tolerance** - wrong; it reverses both readings of the evidence.\n- **D) Low risk capacity and low risk tolerance** - wrong; his horizon, income stability and lack of dependants give him high capacity.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Dr Aravind's assets are: savings account Rs 3,15,000; liquid fund Rs 1,85,000; equity mutual funds Rs 7,40,000; PPF Rs 2,60,000; car Rs 6,00,000; gold Rs 4,00,000. His liabilities are an education loan outstanding of Rs 11,20,000 and a credit card balance of Rs 45,000. What is his net worth?\nA) Rs 11,65,000  B) Rs 13,35,000  C) Rs 13,80,000  D) Rs 25,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 13,35,000**\n\nTotal assets = 3,15,000 + 1,85,000 + 7,40,000 + 2,60,000 + 6,00,000 + 4,00,000 = Rs 25,00,000. Total liabilities = 11,20,000 + 45,000 = Rs 11,65,000. Net worth = 25,00,000 − 11,65,000 = **Rs 13,35,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,65,000** - wrong; this is total liabilities, not net worth.\n- **C) Rs 13,80,000** - wrong; this omits the Rs 45,000 credit card balance from liabilities.\n- **D) Rs 25,00,000** - wrong; this is total assets before deducting any liability.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** Deciding what proportion of Dr Aravind's surplus should go into equity, debt and gold given the horizon of each of his goals belongs to which component of the scope of financial planning?\nA) Cash flow management and budgeting  B) Investment planning and asset allocation  C) Tax planning  D) Estate planning\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Investment planning and asset allocation**\n\nChoosing the mix of asset classes to match each goal's horizon and the client's risk profile — and then selecting products within that mix — is precisely **investment planning and asset allocation**.\n\n- **A) Cash flow management and budgeting** - wrong; that is about generating the surplus, not deploying it.\n- **C) Tax planning** - wrong; tax efficiency modifies a choice but does not set the asset mix.\n- **D) Estate planning** - wrong; that concerns transmission of assets to intended beneficiaries.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which statement best describes SEBI's approach to the conflict between advice and distribution?\nA) An individual investment adviser may both advise and distribute provided commissions are disclosed  B) An individual investment adviser must keep advisory activity segregated from distribution activity  C) Distribution is prohibited for all entities registered with SEBI  D) The conflict is resolved entirely by applying a suitability standard\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) An individual investment adviser must keep advisory activity segregated from distribution activity**\n\nSEBI's answer to the commission-driven conflict is **segregation of advice and distribution**. A registered Investment Adviser owes a **fiduciary** duty — the client's interest ahead of the adviser's own — and an individual IA cannot carry on both activities.\n\n- **A) An individual investment adviser may both advise and distribute provided commissions are disclosed** - wrong; disclosure alone does not permit an individual IA to do both.\n- **C) Distribution is prohibited for all entities registered with SEBI** - wrong; distributors are a legitimate, separately regulated category.\n- **D) The conflict is resolved entirely by applying a suitability standard** - wrong; an IA is held to a fiduciary standard, which is higher than suitability.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Dr Aravind invests a lump sum of Rs 3,40,000 in an instrument earning 10.5% p.a. compounded annually. What will it be worth after 9 years?\nA) Rs 6,61,300  B) Rs 7,55,748  C) Rs 8,35,102  D) Rs 9,22,760\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 8,35,102**\n\nFV = PV × (1 + r)^n = 3,40,000 × (1.105)^9. (1.105)^9 = 2.456182. FV = 3,40,000 × 2.456182 = **Rs 8,35,102**.\n\n- **A) Rs 6,61,300** - wrong; this applies **simple** interest: 3,40,000 × (1 + 0.105 × 9).\n- **B) Rs 7,55,748** - wrong; this compounds for only 8 years instead of 9.\n- **D) Rs 9,22,760** - wrong; this over-compounds, using 10 years instead of 9.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** A portfolio Dr Aravind inherited was worth Rs 2,75,000 seven years ago and is worth Rs 6,60,000 today. What is the CAGR?\nA) 13.32%  B) 15.60%  C) 20.00%  D) 140.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 13.32%**\n\nCAGR = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (6,60,000 ÷ 2,75,000)^(1/7) − 1 = (2.4)^(1/7) − 1 = 1.13322 − 1 = **13.32% p.a.**\n\n- **B) 15.60%** - wrong; no valid computation of the compounded rate produces this figure.\n- **C) 20.00%** - wrong; this is simple-interest thinking — the 140% total gain divided by 7 years.\n- **D) 140.00%** - wrong; this is the **total** percentage gain over the whole seven years, not an annual rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** Dr Aravind starts a SIP of Rs 18,000 a month for 12 years in a fund expected to return 11% p.a. compounded monthly, with each instalment paid at the end of the month. What corpus should he expect?\nA) Rs 25,92,000  B) Rs 49,06,048  C) Rs 53,43,013  D) Rs 53,91,990\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 53,43,013**\n\nr = 0.11 ÷ 12 = 0.0091667; n = 12 × 12 = 144; (1.0091667)^144 = 3.720979.\nFV = 18,000 × [(3.720979 − 1) ÷ 0.0091667] = 18,000 × 296.834 = **Rs 53,43,013**.\n\n- **A) Rs 25,92,000** - wrong; this is only the amount contributed (18,000 × 144), ignoring all returns.\n- **B) Rs 49,06,048** - wrong; this treats the SIP as a single annual contribution of Rs 2,16,000 compounded annually.\n- **D) Rs 53,91,990** - wrong; this is the **annuity due** value (× 1.0091667), i.e. payments at the beginning of each month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Dr Aravind wants a corpus of Rs 65,00,000 in 16 years to set up a diagnostic clinic. Assuming 12% p.a. compounded monthly and end-of-month investments, how much must he invest each month?\nA) Rs 11,180  B) Rs 11,292  C) Rs 22,000  D) Rs 33,854\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 11,292**\n\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1 + r)^n − 1]. r = 0.01; n = 192; (1.01)^192 = 6.75622.\nPMT = (65,00,000 × 0.01) ÷ (6.75622 − 1) = 65,000 ÷ 5.75622 = **Rs 11,292 a month**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,180** - wrong; this is the **annuity due** payment (the answer divided by 1.01), i.e. beginning-of-month investing.\n- **C) Rs 22,000** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **D) Rs 33,854** - wrong; this is simply 65,00,000 ÷ 192, ignoring all compounding.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** Dr Aravind's debt fund is expected to return 8.40% p.a. and inflation is running at 5.60%. What is the real rate of return?\nA) 2.65%  B) 2.80%  C) 3.12%  D) 6.53%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 2.65%**\n\nReal return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.084 ÷ 1.056) − 1 = 1.026515 − 1 = **2.65%**.\n\n- **B) 2.80%** - wrong; this is the crude subtraction 8.40 − 5.60, which always overstates the real return.\n- **C) 3.12%** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **D) 6.53%** - wrong; this divides the nominal rate by the inflation rate instead of using the (1 + r) form.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** A bank offers Dr Aravind a deposit at a nominal 9.60% p.a. compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 9.60%  B) 9.83%  C) 9.95%  D) 10.03%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 9.95%**\n\nEAR = (1 + r ÷ m)^m − 1 = (1 + 0.096 ÷ 4)^4 − 1 = (1.024)^4 − 1 = 1.099512 − 1 = **9.95%**.\n\n- **A) 9.60%** - wrong; that is the nominal (stated) rate, which never changes with compounding frequency.\n- **B) 9.83%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **half-yearly** compounding, (1.048)² − 1.\n- **D) 10.03%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **monthly** compounding, (1.008)¹² − 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A charitable trust Dr Aravind supports will receive Rs 96,000 one year from now, and the payment will grow at 3% a year for ever. At a discount rate of 9% p.a., what is the present value of this stream?\nA) Rs 8,00,000  B) Rs 10,66,667  C) Rs 16,00,000  D) Rs 32,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 16,00,000**\n\nGrowing perpetuity: PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) = 96,000 ÷ (0.09 − 0.03) = 96,000 ÷ 0.06 = **Rs 16,00,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 8,00,000** - wrong; this **adds** g to r instead of subtracting it: 96,000 ÷ 0.12.\n- **B) Rs 10,66,667** - wrong; this is a level perpetuity, 96,000 ÷ 0.09, ignoring growth entirely.\n- **D) Rs 32,00,000** - wrong; this divides by the growth rate alone (96,000 ÷ 0.03).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Dr Aravind's gross monthly income is Rs 2,15,000 and his total monthly expenses, including his education loan EMI, are Rs 1,46,200. What is his savings ratio?\nA) 20.00%  B) 32.00%  C) 47.06%  D) 68.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 32.00%**\n\nSavings = 2,15,000 − 1,46,200 = Rs 68,800. Savings ratio = 68,800 ÷ 2,15,000 = **32.00%**. Self-check: savings ratio + expenses ratio = 32% + 68% = 100%. ✓\n\n- **A) 20.00%** - wrong; 20% is the healthy *benchmark* for the ratio, not his computed value.\n- **C) 47.06%** - wrong; this divides savings by **expenses** (68,800 ÷ 1,46,200) instead of by income.\n- **D) 68.00%** - wrong; that is his **expenses** ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Using the asset figures in Q3 and the expense figure in Q13, what is Dr Aravind's liquidity ratio?\nA) 2.33 months  B) 3.42 months  C) 10.26 months  D) 20.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 3.42 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings account 3,15,000 + liquid fund 1,85,000 = Rs 5,00,000 (equity funds and PPF do **not** qualify as liquid). Liquidity ratio = 5,00,000 ÷ 1,46,200 monthly expenses = **3.42 months**, at the thin end of the healthy 3–6 month band.\n\n- **A) 2.33 months** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **C) 10.26 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the equity mutual funds and PPF balance as liquid assets.\n- **D) 20.00%** - wrong; this divides liquid assets by **total assets**; the liquidity ratio is expressed in **months**, not per cent.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Using the balance sheet in Q3, what is Dr Aravind's solvency ratio?\nA) 0.47  B) 0.53  C) 0.87  D) 1.87\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.53**\n\nSolvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 13,35,000 ÷ 25,00,000 = **0.53**. Self-check: solvency + leverage = 0.53 + 0.47 = 1. ✓\n\n- **A) 0.47** - wrong; that is the **leverage** ratio (liabilities ÷ assets = 11,65,000 ÷ 25,00,000).\n- **C) 0.87** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **D) 1.87** - wrong; this inverts the ratio, dividing total assets by net worth.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Dr Aravind has no contingency fund yet. Given monthly expenses of Rs 1,46,200 and monthly income of Rs 2,15,000, which target and location is most appropriate?\nA) Rs 4,30,000 in a diversified equity fund  B) Rs 8,77,200 in a liquid fund  C) Rs 8,77,200 in a mid-cap equity fund  D) Rs 12,90,000 in PPF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 8,77,200 in a liquid fund**\n\nThe contingency fund is sized against **expenses**, not income: six months × Rs 1,46,200 = **Rs 8,77,200**. It must offer immediate availability and certainty of value, so a savings account, sweep-in FD or **liquid fund** is the right home.\n\n- **A) Rs 4,30,000 in a diversified equity fund** - wrong on both counts: it is sized on income (two months of income) and equity may be down exactly when the money is needed.\n- **C) Rs 8,77,200 in a mid-cap equity fund** - wrong; the amount is right but an emergency fund must never sit in equity.\n- **D) Rs 12,90,000 in PPF** - wrong; it is sized on income, and PPF is locked in, so it is not a liquid asset.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Dr Aravind's monthly budget balances comfortably all year, yet every February he has to use his credit card because his annual professional indemnity premium falls due. Which category of outflow has his budget failed to provide for?\nA) Fixed committed outflows  B) Variable discretionary outflows  C) Periodic (lumpy) outflows  D) Savings and investment outflows\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Periodic (lumpy) outflows**\n\nAnnual premiums, festivals, vacations and servicing costs are **periodic outflows** — predictable but infrequent, and the most commonly forgotten item in a household budget. The fix is to divide the annual amount by 12 and set that aside every month.\n\n- **A) Fixed committed outflows** - wrong; these are the monthly contractual items such as EMIs and rent, which his budget already covers.\n- **B) Variable discretionary outflows** - wrong; groceries, fuel and dining recur every month and are controllable.\n- **D) Savings and investment outflows** - wrong; SIPs and recurring deposits are regular monthly commitments, not the cause of the February gap.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Dr Aravind's education loan of Rs 14,00,000 carries interest at 9.5% p.a. with a tenure of 8 years. What is his EMI?\nA) Rs 11,083  B) Rs 14,583  C) Rs 20,875  D) Rs 22,410\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 20,875**\n\nr = 0.095 ÷ 12 = 0.00791667; n = 96; (1.00791667)^96 = 2.131887.\nEMI = 14,00,000 × (0.00791667 × 2.131887) ÷ (2.131887 − 1) = 14,00,000 × 0.01687744 ÷ 1.131887 = **Rs 20,875**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,083** - wrong; this is the **first month's interest** (14,00,000 × 0.00791667), a classic distractor.\n- **B) Rs 14,583** - wrong; this is principal ÷ months (14,00,000 ÷ 96), ignoring interest entirely.\n- **D) Rs 22,410** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** Over the full 8-year tenure of the loan in Q18, how much interest will Dr Aravind pay in total?\nA) Rs 5,32,000  B) Rs 6,04,023  C) Rs 10,64,000  D) Rs 20,04,023\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 6,04,023**\n\nTotal interest = (EMI × n) − P = (20,875 × 96) − 14,00,000 = 20,04,023 − 14,00,000 = **Rs 6,04,023**.\n\n- **A) Rs 5,32,000** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **C) Rs 10,64,000** - wrong; this is **simple** interest at 9.5% for 8 years on the whole principal, ignoring the falling balance.\n- **D) Rs 20,04,023** - wrong; that is the **total amount repaid**, which still includes the Rs 14,00,000 principal.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** In the very first EMI of the loan in Q18, how much goes towards reducing the principal?\nA) Rs 9,792  B) Rs 11,083  C) Rs 14,583  D) Rs 20,875\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 9,792**\n\nInterest for month 1 = outstanding × monthly rate = 14,00,000 × 0.00791667 = Rs 11,083. Principal = EMI − interest = 20,875 − 11,083 = **Rs 9,792**. Just over half of the very first EMI is interest.\n\n- **B) Rs 11,083** - wrong; that is the **interest** component of the first EMI, not the principal component.\n- **C) Rs 14,583** - wrong; this is principal ÷ 96, a straight-line split that amortisation does not use.\n- **D) Rs 20,875** - wrong; that is the whole EMI, which includes the interest.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Dr Aravind takes a car loan under which he keeps possession and use of the vehicle while the lender holds a charge over it. What form of security is this?\nA) Mortgage  B) Pledge  C) Hypothecation  D) Assignment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Hypothecation**\n\n**Hypothecation** is security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** — exactly the arrangement in a car loan.\n\n- **A) Mortgage** - wrong; a mortgage is security created over **immovable** property, as in a home loan.\n- **B) Pledge** - wrong; in a pledge the movable asset's **possession passes to the lender**, as in a gold loan.\n- **D) Assignment** - wrong; assignment transfers rights under a contract, as with a loan against an insurance policy.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** A borrower with four loans decides to clear the smallest outstanding balance first, paying only the minimum on the others. Which repayment strategy is this?\nA) Avalanche  B) Snowball  C) Blizzard  D) Refinancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Snowball**\n\nThe **snowball** method attacks the **smallest balance** first, regardless of interest rate. It is psychologically powerful because of the quick wins, but it costs more in total interest than the avalanche method.\n\n- **A) Avalanche** - wrong; the avalanche targets the **highest interest rate** first and is mathematically optimal.\n- **C) Blizzard** - wrong; the blizzard is a hybrid — clear one or two small balances first, then switch to the highest rate.\n- **D) Refinancing** - wrong; refinancing replaces an existing loan with a cheaper one; it is not a repayment ordering method.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which regulator supervises NBFC-P2P lending platforms and credit information companies in India?\nA) SEBI  B) RBI  C) IRDAI  D) Ministry of Corporate Affairs\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) RBI**\n\nBoth NBFC-P2P platforms and Credit Information Companies (CIBIL, Experian, Equifax, CRIF High Mark) are registered with and supervised by the **RBI**, the latter under the Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, 2005.\n\n- **A) SEBI** - wrong; SEBI regulates the securities markets — exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, PMS, investment advisers, AIFs and depositories.\n- **C) IRDAI** - wrong; IRDAI regulates insurers and insurance intermediaries.\n- **D) Ministry of Corporate Affairs** - wrong; the MCA administers the Companies Act, 2013 and company incorporation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which of the following is a money market instrument?\nA) A 9-month certificate of deposit issued by a bank  B) A 5-year non-convertible debenture  C) A State Development Loan  D) A Sovereign Gold Bond\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A 9-month certificate of deposit issued by a bank**\n\nThe money market covers instruments with an original maturity of **up to one year** and is regulated by the **RBI**. A 9-month CD qualifies.\n\n- **B) A 5-year non-convertible debenture** - wrong; a 5-year tenor places it in the capital market, under SEBI.\n- **C) A State Development Loan** - wrong; SDLs are long-dated state government securities.\n- **D) A Sovereign Gold Bond** - wrong; SGBs are long-tenor gold-linked government securities.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** Which market infrastructure institution acts as the central counterparty and guarantees settlement of trades?\nA) The stock exchange  B) The depository  C) The clearing corporation  D) The depository participant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) The clearing corporation**\n\nThe **clearing corporation** clears and settles trades and acts as the **central counterparty (CCP)**, so that neither buyer nor seller is exposed to counterparty default. It also manages margins and the settlement guarantee fund.\n\n- **A) The stock exchange** - wrong; the exchange **matches** trades and provides price discovery; it does not guarantee settlement.\n- **B) The depository** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL hold securities in electronic (demat) form.\n- **D) The depository participant** - wrong; a DP is the depository's agent and the investor's access point for a demat account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which of the following is a payment bank in India permitted to do?\nA) Accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer  B) Grant personal loans to its depositors  C) Issue credit cards  D) Accept deposits without any per-customer ceiling\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer**\n\nPayment banks may accept deposits subject to a ceiling of **Rs 2 lakh per customer** and provide payments and remittance services. They **cannot lend** and cannot issue credit cards.\n\n- **B) Grant personal loans to its depositors** - wrong; lending is outside a payment bank's licence.\n- **C) Issue credit cards** - wrong; payment banks are expressly barred from issuing credit cards.\n- **D) Accept deposits without any per-customer ceiling** - wrong; the Rs 2 lakh per-customer cap applies.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which statement about an RBI-regulated NBFC-Account Aggregator is correct?\nA) It lends to customers from its own book  B) It retrieves and shares customer financial data only with explicit consent and cannot read or store that data  C) It guarantees a minimum return to customers whose data it aggregates  D) It holds the customer's securities in demat form\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It retrieves and shares customer financial data only with explicit consent and cannot read or store that data**\n\nAn NBFC-AA moves a customer's financial data between institutions **only on the customer's explicit consent**, and is **data blind** — it can neither read nor store the data it transmits.\n\n- **A) It lends to customers from its own book** - wrong; an AA is a data intermediary, not a lender.\n- **C) It guarantees a minimum return to customers whose data it aggregates** - wrong; an AA offers no financial product and therefore no return.\n- **D) It holds the customer's securities in demat form** - wrong; that is the role of a depository and its DP.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a rights issue from a bonus issue?\nA) Both raise fresh capital for the company  B) A rights issue raises capital while a bonus issue does not  C) A bonus issue raises capital while a rights issue does not  D) Neither raises capital for the company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A rights issue raises capital while a bonus issue does not**\n\nA **rights issue** offers existing shareholders new shares pro rata at a discount, and they pay for them — so money flows into the company. A **bonus issue** is merely a **capitalisation of reserves**: free shares, **no money raised**.\n\n- **A) Both raise fresh capital for the company** - wrong; a bonus issue brings in no cash whatsoever.\n- **C) A bonus issue raises capital while a rights issue does not** - wrong; this reverses the two.\n- **D) Neither raises capital for the company** - wrong; a rights issue does raise capital.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** Dr Aravind holds 500 shares of a company quoted at Rs 640. The company announces a 3:5 bonus issue. What is the theoretical share price immediately after the bonus?\nA) Rs 384  B) Rs 400  C) Rs 427  D) Rs 640\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 400**\n\nA 3:5 bonus gives 3 free shares for every 5 held: 500 × 3/5 = 300 new shares, so he now holds 800. His total value is unchanged at 500 × 640 = Rs 3,20,000. Theoretical price = 3,20,000 ÷ 800 = **Rs 400**.\n\n- **A) Rs 384** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **C) Rs 427** - wrong; that is the adjusted price for a **1:2** bonus (640 × 2/3).\n- **D) Rs 640** - wrong; the price must fall proportionately when the share count rises.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** In a book-built IPO, an individual applies for shares worth Rs 2,60,000. In which category will the application be considered?\nA) Retail Individual Investor  B) Non-Institutional Investor  C) Qualified Institutional Buyer  D) Anchor investor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Non-Institutional Investor**\n\nThe dividing line is **Rs 2 lakh**. Applications **up to Rs 2 lakh** are Retail Individual Investors; **above Rs 2 lakh** they fall into the Non-Institutional (HNI) category, which must receive at least 15% of the issue.\n\n- **A) Retail Individual Investor** - wrong; the retail ceiling is Rs 2 lakh and this application exceeds it.\n- **C) Qualified Institutional Buyer** - wrong; QIBs are institutions such as mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs and pension funds.\n- **D) Anchor investor** - wrong; anchor investors are QIBs allotted shares a day before the issue opens.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** Under the ASBA facility, what happens to an IPO applicant's money?\nA) It is debited in full on application and refunded after allotment  B) It is blocked in the applicant's own bank account and debited only to the extent of the allotment  C) It is transferred to the registrar's escrow account on application  D) It is blocked in the merchant banker's account until listing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It is blocked in the applicant's own bank account and debited only to the extent of the allotment**\n\n**ASBA (Applications Supported by Blocked Amount)** is mandatory for public issues. The money stays in the investor's own account, merely **blocked**, and the investor continues to **earn interest** on it. Only the allotted portion is debited; the balance is simply unblocked.\n\n- **A) It is debited in full on application and refunded after allotment** - wrong; that was the pre-ASBA process, which ASBA replaced.\n- **C) It is transferred to the registrar's escrow account on application** - wrong; no transfer out of the investor's account takes place.\n- **D) It is blocked in the merchant banker's account until listing** - wrong; the block is placed on the investor's own bank account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** A trading halt applied to the entire market when the index moves by 10%, 15% or 20% is known as a:\nA) Price band  B) Circuit breaker  C) Margin call  D) Trade-for-trade restriction\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Circuit breaker**\n\nA **circuit breaker** is **market-wide**: it halts trading across the market when the index moves ±10%, ±15% or ±20%, with the halt duration depending on the level breached and the time of day.\n\n- **A) Price band** - wrong; a price band is **stock-specific**, capping one scrip's daily movement (e.g. 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).\n- **C) Margin call** - wrong; that is a demand for additional collateral from a member or client.\n- **D) Trade-for-trade restriction** - wrong; that is a surveillance measure moving a specific stock to compulsory delivery-based settlement.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** What are the minimum investment amounts for a Portfolio Management Service and for an Alternative Investment Fund respectively?\nA) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore**\n\nPMS carries a minimum investment of **Rs 50 lakh**; an AIF carries a minimum of **Rs 1 crore** (relaxed to Rs 25 lakh for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager).\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the concessional AIF limit for AIF employees and directors only.\n- **C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; the AIF minimum is double the PMS minimum.\n- **D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the AIF figure, not the PMS figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** In a \"capital protected\" structured product, the protection ultimately depends on:\nA) A SEBI guarantee of the principal  B) The creditworthiness of the issuer  C) The settlement guarantee fund of the clearing corporation  D) DICGC deposit insurance\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The creditworthiness of the issuer**\n\nA structured product is a bond (or deposit) packaged with a derivative. The \"protection\" is a promise made by the **issuer** — it is only as good as the issuer's credit. Such products are also typically **not** principal-protected before maturity.\n\n- **A) A SEBI guarantee of the principal** - wrong; SEBI regulates disclosure and conduct; it guarantees no product's principal.\n- **C) The settlement guarantee fund of the clearing corporation** - wrong; that guarantees settlement of exchange trades, not an issuer's promise.\n- **D) DICGC deposit insurance** - wrong; DICGC covers bank deposits up to Rs 5 lakh, not structured products.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** The dividing line between investment-grade and high-yield (\"junk\") debt is a credit rating of:\nA) A minus  B) BBB minus  C) BB plus  D) B minus\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) BBB minus**\n\n**BBB− (Baa3)** and above is investment grade; anything below it is high yield. A downgrade from BBB− to BB+ can force institutional selling, because many mandates prohibit sub-investment-grade holdings.\n\n- **A) A minus** - wrong; A− sits comfortably within investment grade but is not the cut-off.\n- **C) BB plus** - wrong; BB+ is the highest **sub**-investment-grade notch, just below the line.\n- **D) B minus** - wrong; B− is deep inside high-yield territory.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** A pharmaceutical company's share trades at Rs 1,485 and its earnings per share is Rs 45. What is its price-to-earnings ratio?\nA) 3.03  B) 27.50  C) 33.00  D) 36.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 33.00**\n\nP/E = Price ÷ EPS = 1,485 ÷ 45 = **33.00**. The investor pays Rs 33 for every Rs 1 of current annual earnings.\n\n- **A) 3.03** - wrong; that is the **earnings yield** in per cent (45 ÷ 1,485), which is the inverse of P/E.\n- **B) 27.50** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **D) 36.00** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** A listed company has 9 crore shares outstanding at Rs 260 each, debt of Rs 640 crore and cash of Rs 190 crore. What is its enterprise value?\nA) Rs 1,890 crore  B) Rs 2,340 crore  C) Rs 2,790 crore  D) Rs 3,170 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,790 crore**\n\nMarket capitalisation = 9 crore × Rs 260 = Rs 2,340 crore.\nEV = Market cap + Debt − Cash = 2,340 + 640 − 190 = **Rs 2,790 crore**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,890 crore** - wrong; this subtracts the debt and adds the cash, reversing both signs.\n- **B) Rs 2,340 crore** - wrong; that is market capitalisation alone, before the debt and cash adjustments.\n- **D) Rs 3,170 crore** - wrong; this **adds** cash instead of subtracting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An analyst notes that a stock trades at a P/E of 28 and forecasts earnings growth of 16% a year. What is its PEG ratio?\nA) 0.57  B) 1.75  C) 4.48  D) 12.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1.75**\n\nPEG = P/E ÷ growth rate (in per cent) = 28 ÷ 16 = **1.75**. A PEG above 1 suggests the price is not obviously cheap relative to the growth on offer.\n\n- **A) 0.57** - wrong; this inverts the ratio (16 ÷ 28).\n- **C) 4.48** - wrong; this multiplies the P/E by the growth rate expressed as a decimal.\n- **D) 12.00** - wrong; this subtracts the growth rate from the P/E.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** Which pairing of analytical approach to its central question is correct?\nA) Fundamental analysis answers when to buy; technical analysis answers what to buy  B) Fundamental analysis answers what to buy; technical analysis answers when to buy  C) Both approaches answer only what to buy  D) Both approaches answer only when to buy\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Fundamental analysis answers what to buy; technical analysis answers when to buy**\n\nFundamental analysis estimates **intrinsic value** from earnings, assets, growth and management, telling you *what* is worth owning. Technical analysis studies **price and volume** to identify entry, exit and stop-loss levels, telling you *when* to act.\n\n- **A) Fundamental analysis answers when to buy; technical analysis answers what to buy** - wrong; this reverses the two.\n- **C) Both approaches answer only what to buy** - wrong; timing is technical analysis's distinctive contribution.\n- **D) Both approaches answer only when to buy** - wrong; fundamental analysis is about value, not timing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 pays a 7% annual coupon and has 4 years to maturity. If the required yield is 9%, what is its fair price?\nA) Rs 708.43  B) Rs 935.21  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,070.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 935.21**\n\nAnnuity factor = [1 − (1.09)⁻⁴] ÷ 0.09 = 3.23972. PV of coupons = 70 × 3.23972 = Rs 226.78. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.09)⁴ = 1,000 ÷ 1.41158 = Rs 708.43. Price = 226.78 + 708.43 = **Rs 935.21** — a discount, because the 7% coupon is below the 9% required yield.\n\n- **A) Rs 708.43** - wrong; this is only the present value of the redemption amount, omitting all four coupons.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would trade at par only if the coupon equalled the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,070.92** - wrong; that is the price at a 5% required yield, where the bond would trade at a premium.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 and a coupon rate of 8.6% has 6 years to maturity and trades at Rs 1,075. What is its current yield?\nA) 7.08%  B) 8.00%  C) 8.60%  D) 9.25%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 8.00%**\n\nCurrent yield = annual coupon ÷ **market price** = 86 ÷ 1,075 = **8.00%**. The coupon is always computed on face value (8.6% × 1,000 = Rs 86), but the current yield divides it by the price actually paid.\n\n- **A) 7.08%** - wrong; that is the approximate **YTM**, [86 + (1,000 − 1,075) ÷ 6] ÷ [(1,000 + 1,075) ÷ 2].\n- **C) 8.60%** - wrong; that is the **coupon rate**, which is measured against face value, not against price.\n- **D) 9.25%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by a price below face value rather than by the actual Rs 1,075.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Dr Aravind holds Rs 8,00,000 of a bond fund whose portfolio has a modified duration of 5.8. If yields fall by 40 basis points, what is the approximate change in the value of his holding?\nA) A gain of Rs 3,200  B) A gain of Rs 18,560  C) A gain of Rs 23,200  D) A gain of Rs 46,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A gain of Rs 18,560**\n\n% ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −5.8 × (−0.0040) = **+2.32%**. On Rs 8,00,000 that is 8,00,000 × 0.0232 = **a gain of Rs 18,560**.\n\n- **A) A gain of Rs 3,200** - wrong; this applies the 0.40% yield change directly to the corpus, ignoring duration altogether.\n- **C) A gain of Rs 23,200** - wrong; this uses a 50 basis point move instead of 40.\n- **D) A gain of Rs 46,400** - wrong; this uses a 100 basis point move instead of 40.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** For a bond trading at a discount to face value, which ordering is correct?\nA) Coupon rate > Current yield > YTM  B) Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM  C) Coupon rate = Current yield = YTM  D) Current yield > Coupon rate > YTM\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM**\n\nA discount bond is bought below face value, so the holder also earns a **capital gain** at redemption. Current yield reflects the low price but not that gain; YTM captures both — so YTM is the highest of the three.\n\n- **A) Coupon rate > Current yield > YTM** - wrong; that is the ordering for a bond trading at a **premium**.\n- **C) Coupon rate = Current yield = YTM** - wrong; all three are equal only when the bond trades at **par**.\n- **D) Current yield > Coupon rate > YTM** - wrong; YTM cannot be the lowest of the three for a discount bond.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Dr Aravind buys one call option on an index with a strike of 2,400 for a premium of Rs 78 per unit, lot size 250. At expiry the index closes at 2,545. What is his net profit?\nA) Rs 16,750  B) Rs 19,500  C) Rs 36,250  D) Rs 55,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 16,750**\n\nBreak-even = strike + premium = 2,400 + 78 = 2,478. Intrinsic value at expiry = max(2,545 − 2,400, 0) = 145. Net profit per unit = 145 − 78 = 67. Total = 67 × 250 = **Rs 16,750**.\n\n- **B) Rs 19,500** - wrong; that is the premium paid (78 × 250), which is his maximum possible loss, not his profit.\n- **C) Rs 36,250** - wrong; that is the gross intrinsic value (145 × 250) before the premium is deducted.\n- **D) Rs 55,750** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Dr Aravind writes (sells) one put option with a strike of 980 for a premium of Rs 26 per unit, lot size 300. At expiry the underlying closes at 915. What is his outcome?\nA) A loss of Rs 11,700  B) A loss of Rs 19,500  C) A profit of Rs 7,800  D) A loss of Rs 27,300\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A loss of Rs 11,700**\n\nShort put payoff = premium − max(strike − spot, 0) = 26 − (980 − 915) = 26 − 65 = −39 per unit. Total = −39 × 300 = **a loss of Rs 11,700**. His break-even was 980 − 26 = 954 and the underlying closed below it.\n\n- **B) A loss of Rs 19,500** - wrong; that is the gross exercise cost (65 × 300) before crediting the premium received.\n- **C) A profit of Rs 7,800** - wrong; that is the premium received (26 × 300), which he keeps in full only if the option expires worthless.\n- **D) A loss of Rs 27,300** - wrong; this adds the premium to the loss instead of offsetting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** An index is trading at 3,200. The risk-free rate is 7.5% p.a. and the index dividend yield is 1.5% p.a. Using F = S × (1 + r − q)^T, what is the fair value of a one-year futures contract?\nA) Rs 3,152  B) Rs 3,248  C) Rs 3,392  D) Rs 3,440\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 3,392**\n\nF = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 3,200 × (1 + 0.075 − 0.015)^1 = 3,200 × 1.06 = **Rs 3,392**. The futures price exceeds spot (contango) because the net cost of carry is positive.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,152** - wrong; this subtracts the dividend yield only and ignores the financing cost entirely.\n- **B) Rs 3,248** - wrong; no valid computation produces this figure.\n- **D) Rs 3,440** - wrong; this applies the 7.5% financing cost but forgets to deduct the 1.5% dividend yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which feature belongs to an exchange-traded futures contract but not to an OTC forward contract?\nA) Contract terms customised to the two parties  B) Daily mark-to-market settlement with the clearing corporation as central counterparty  C) Settlement taking place only at maturity  D) Counterparty risk borne directly by the two parties\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Daily mark-to-market settlement with the clearing corporation as central counterparty**\n\nFutures are **standardised and exchange-traded**, with **mandatory margins**, **daily mark to market**, and a **clearing corporation acting as CCP**, which eliminates counterparty risk. Forwards have none of these features.\n\n- **A) Contract terms customised to the two parties** - wrong; customisation is the defining feature of a **forward**; futures are standardised.\n- **C) Settlement taking place only at maturity** - wrong; that describes a forward. Futures settle daily through mark to market.\n- **D) Counterparty risk borne directly by the two parties** - wrong; that is a forward's key drawback, removed in futures by the CCP.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A mutual fund scheme holds securities valued at Rs 742 crore and cash of Rs 23 crore, and owes Rs 11 crore of accrued expenses. Units outstanding are 37.5 crore. What is the NAV per unit?\nA) Rs 19.49  B) Rs 20.11  C) Rs 20.40  D) Rs 20.69\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 20.11**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ Units outstanding. Total assets = 742 + 23 = Rs 765 crore. Net assets = 765 − 11 = Rs 754 crore. NAV = 754 ÷ 37.5 = **Rs 20.11**.\n\n- **A) Rs 19.49** - wrong; this drops the Rs 23 crore of cash from total assets (731 ÷ 37.5).\n- **C) Rs 20.40** - wrong; this ignores the Rs 11 crore liability altogether (765 ÷ 37.5).\n- **D) Rs 20.69** - wrong; this adds the liability instead of subtracting it (776 ÷ 37.5).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Dr Aravind Rajagopal submits a Rs 3,00,000 lump-sum purchase application for a flexi cap fund at 2:15 p.m. on Tuesday. His bank debits him that evening, but the money is credited to the scheme's account only at 9:40 a.m. on Wednesday. Which day's NAV applies?\nA) Tuesday's NAV, because the application was received before 3 p.m.  B) Wednesday's NAV, because the funds were realised before Wednesday's cut-off  C) Monday's NAV, as for a liquid fund  D) Thursday's NAV, because a full business day must elapse after realisation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Wednesday's NAV, because the funds were realised before Wednesday's cut-off**\n\nFor purchases of **any amount** in any scheme other than liquid and overnight funds, the applicable NAV depends on the **funds being realised (credited to the scheme) before the 3 p.m. cut-off**, not on when the form was submitted. The money reached the scheme at 9:40 a.m. on Wednesday, so Wednesday's NAV applies.\n\n- **A) Tuesday's NAV, because the application was received before 3 p.m.** - wrong; time-stamping the application is not enough. Realisation of funds is the decisive test.\n- **C) Monday's NAV, as for a liquid fund** - wrong; the previous day's NAV rule belongs only to liquid and overnight funds, which have a 1:30 p.m. cut-off.\n- **D) Thursday's NAV, because a full business day must elapse after realisation** - wrong; no such waiting period exists. Units are allotted at the NAV of the day of realisation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Dr Aravind invests Rs 12,000 a month for four months through an SIP. The purchase NAVs are Rs 60, Rs 48, Rs 40 and Rs 50. What is his average cost per unit?\nA) Rs 47.62  B) Rs 48.48  C) Rs 49.50  D) Rs 50.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 48.48**\n\nUnits bought = 12,000/60 + 12,000/48 + 12,000/40 + 12,000/50 = 200 + 250 + 300 + 240 = **990 units**. Total invested = Rs 48,000. Average cost = 48,000 ÷ 990 = **Rs 48.48**. It sits below the simple average NAV of Rs 49.50, and that gap is the benefit of rupee-cost averaging.\n\n- **A) Rs 47.62** - wrong; this follows from an inflated unit count of 1,008, an arithmetic slip in the division.\n- **C) Rs 49.50** - wrong; that is the **simple average of the four NAVs**, (60+48+40+50)/4, not the cost per unit actually paid.\n- **D) Rs 50.00** - wrong; that is merely the midpoint of the highest and lowest NAV, which has no meaning here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Dr Aravind wants a single equity scheme that is required to hold at least 25% each in large cap, mid cap and small cap stocks. Which SEBI category should his adviser recommend?\nA) Flexi cap fund  B) Multi cap fund  C) Large and mid cap fund  D) Focused fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Multi cap fund**\n\nUnder SEBI's scheme categorisation, a **multi cap fund** must invest a **minimum of 25% each in large cap, mid cap and small cap** stocks, guaranteeing a presence across all three segments.\n\n- **A) Flexi cap fund** - wrong; a flexi cap fund needs only 65% in equity and has **no market-cap restriction**, so the manager may hold nothing at all in small caps.\n- **C) Large and mid cap fund** - wrong; that requires at least 35% each in large and mid cap, with no small cap minimum.\n- **D) Focused fund** - wrong; a focused fund is defined by a **maximum of 30 stocks**, not by any market-cap split.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Dr Aravind redeems 3,200 units of an equity fund at an NAV of Rs 46.75 per unit, within the one-year exit load period. The exit load is 1%. What amount will he receive?\nA) Rs 1,48,000  B) Rs 1,48,104  C) Rs 1,49,600  D) Rs 1,51,096\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,48,104**\n\nGross redemption value = 3,200 × 46.75 = **Rs 1,49,600**. Exit load = 1% × 1,49,600 = **Rs 1,496**. Net proceeds = 1,49,600 − 1,496 = **Rs 1,48,104**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,48,000** - wrong; a rounded guess that does not follow from the arithmetic.\n- **C) Rs 1,49,600** - wrong; that is the **gross** value before the exit load is deducted.\n- **D) Rs 1,51,096** - wrong; this **adds** the load to the gross value instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Dr Aravind switches Rs 6,00,000 from an aggressive hybrid scheme to a large cap scheme of the same fund house. How is this treated?\nA) As an internal transfer with no tax consequence  B) As a redemption plus a fresh purchase, and therefore a taxable event  C) As an IDCW reinvestment taxable at slab rates  D) As a transmission, which does not attract capital gains tax\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) As a redemption plus a fresh purchase, and therefore a taxable event**\n\nA **switch** — even within the same AMC — is executed as a **redemption from the source scheme and a purchase in the target scheme**. Capital gains arise on the redemption leg, and any applicable exit load is charged.\n\n- **A) As an internal transfer with no tax consequence** - wrong; staying inside one fund house gives no tax relief. The source units are genuinely redeemed.\n- **C) As an IDCW reinvestment taxable at slab rates** - wrong; IDCW reinvestment is a distribution used to buy fresh units in the **same** scheme, a different transaction entirely.\n- **D) As a transmission, which does not attract capital gains tax** - wrong; transmission is the passing of units on the **death** of a holder.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** Dr Aravind's colleague Dr Meenakshi Sundaram has Rs 38 lakh she wishes to place with a SEBI-registered portfolio manager under a discretionary mandate. What should her adviser tell her?\nA) She may invest, as there is no regulatory minimum for PMS  B) She may invest, as the minimum is Rs 25 lakh  C) She cannot invest, as the minimum client investment is Rs 50 lakh  D) She cannot invest, as the minimum client investment is Rs 1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) She cannot invest, as the minimum client investment is Rs 50 lakh**\n\nUnder the **SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020**, the **minimum investment per client is Rs 50 lakh**. With Rs 38 lakh she falls short and must use mutual funds until the corpus grows.\n\n- **A) She may invest, as there is no regulatory minimum for PMS** - wrong; the minimum is expressly prescribed and operates as a hard eligibility filter.\n- **B) She may invest, as the minimum is Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the AIF minimum for **employees and directors of the AIF or its manager**, and for **angel investors**, not a PMS threshold.\n- **D) She cannot invest, as the minimum client investment is Rs 1 crore** - wrong; Rs 1 crore is the **AIF** minimum. The three minimums run Rs 100–5,000 (mutual fund), Rs 50 lakh (PMS), Rs 1 crore (AIF).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A discretionary PMS portfolio starts at Rs 80 lakh. It rises to Rs 96 lakh in year 1 (performance fee duly charged), falls to Rs 84 lakh in year 2, and rises to Rs 1.05 crore in year 3. The performance fee is 20% on a high watermark basis with no hurdle. What performance fee is payable for year 3?\nA) Rs 1,80,000  B) Rs 3,20,000  C) Rs 4,20,000  D) Rs 5,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 1,80,000**\n\nThe **high watermark** after year 1 is **Rs 96 lakh**. No fee arose in year 2 because Rs 84 lakh is below that peak. In year 3 the fee applies only to the gain **above Rs 96 lakh**: 105 − 96 = **Rs 9 lakh**. Fee = 20% × 9,00,000 = **Rs 1,80,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 3,20,000** - wrong; that is 20% of the year-1 gain of Rs 16 lakh, on which a fee was already charged.\n- **C) Rs 4,20,000** - wrong; that charges 20% on the full Rs 21 lakh rise from the year-2 trough of Rs 84 lakh, which is precisely what the high watermark exists to prevent.\n- **D) Rs 5,00,000** - wrong; that charges 20% on the Rs 25 lakh rise from the original Rs 80 lakh, ignoring the fee already taken in year 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** A PMS client's Rs 1.20 crore portfolio returns 16% in a year. The agreement provides a hurdle rate of 9%, a performance fee of 15%, and no catch-up. What is the performance fee?\nA) Rs 84,000  B) Rs 1,26,000  C) Rs 1,62,000  D) Rs 2,88,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,26,000**\n\nTotal return = 16% × 1,20,00,000 = **Rs 19,20,000**. The hurdle absorbs the first 9% = **Rs 10,80,000**. With **no catch-up**, the fee applies only to the excess: 19,20,000 − 10,80,000 = **Rs 8,40,000**. Fee = 15% × 8,40,000 = **Rs 1,26,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 84,000** - wrong; this applies a 10% rate to the excess instead of the agreed 15%.\n- **C) Rs 1,62,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the hurdle amount of Rs 10.80 lakh, which is exactly the portion the client keeps in full.\n- **D) Rs 2,88,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the entire Rs 19.20 lakh return, ignoring the hurdle altogether.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Under a portfolio management arrangement, the client reviews and approves each proposed trade, after which the manager places the order and settles it. Which service type is this?\nA) Discretionary portfolio management  B) Non-discretionary portfolio management  C) Advisory portfolio management  D) Execution-only broking\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Non-discretionary portfolio management**\n\nApply the \"who pulls the trigger\" test: the **client decides** and the **manager executes**, which is **non-discretionary** PMS.\n\n- **A) Discretionary portfolio management** - wrong; there the manager both decides and executes, with no client approval per trade.\n- **C) Advisory portfolio management** - wrong; there the manager only recommends and the **client** places and settles the trade himself.\n- **D) Execution-only broking** - wrong; a broker gives no portfolio advice and is not a portfolio manager at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A Category III AIF is launched with a corpus of Rs 260 crore. What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager maintain?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6.5 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 13 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 10 crore**\n\nFor a **Category III** AIF the continuing interest is the **lower of 5% of corpus or Rs 10 crore**. Here 5% × 260 = Rs 13 crore, which exceeds Rs 10 crore, so the **lower** figure of **Rs 10 crore** applies.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the cap for **Category I and II** funds. Category III doubles the requirement because it may use leverage.\n- **B) Rs 6.5 crore** - wrong; that applies the Category I/II rate of 2.5% to the corpus instead of the Category III rate of 5%.\n- **D) Rs 13 crore** - wrong; that is 5% of the corpus, but the rule takes the **lower** of that percentage and Rs 10 crore.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** Dr Aravind is in the lowest tax slab and is comparing a Category II AIF with a Category III AIF. On taxation, what should his adviser tell him?\nA) Both are pass-through vehicles, so his own slab rate applies in each case  B) The Category II fund is a pass-through, while the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level  C) The Category III fund is a pass-through, while the Category II fund is taxed at the fund level  D) Both are taxed at the fund level, so his slab rate is irrelevant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The Category II fund is a pass-through, while the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level**\n\n**Pass-through status is granted to Categories I and II only.** Their income is deemed to arise directly to the investor and is taxed at **his own** rate, with the character of the income preserved. **Category III is taxed at the fund level**, generally at the maximum marginal rate, which makes it relatively less tax-efficient for a low-slab investor like Dr Aravind.\n\n- **A) Both are pass-through vehicles, so his own slab rate applies in each case** - wrong; Category III has never enjoyed pass-through status.\n- **C) The Category III fund is a pass-through, while the Category II fund is taxed at the fund level** - wrong; this reverses the rule exactly.\n- **D) Both are taxed at the fund level, so his slab rate is irrelevant** - wrong; Category II income is taxed in the investor's own hands at his own rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** What is the minimum corpus prescribed for a scheme of an angel fund?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 10 crore  C) Rs 20 crore  D) Rs 25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 10 crore**\n\nAn **angel fund** is a sub-category of venture capital fund (Category I) and enjoys a relaxed minimum corpus of **Rs 10 crore**, against Rs 20 crore for other AIF schemes.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the Category I/II sponsor continuing-interest cap and the Category I registration fee, not a corpus requirement.\n- **C) Rs 20 crore** - wrong; that is the minimum corpus for **other** AIF schemes, from which the angel fund is expressly relaxed.\n- **D) Rs 25 crore** - wrong; Rs 25 crore is the turnover ceiling for an angel fund's **investee company**, not a corpus figure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** A two-asset portfolio holds 60% in an equity fund with a standard deviation of 22% and 40% in a gilt fund with a standard deviation of 12%. The correlation between them is 0.30. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?\nA) 14.05%  B) 15.34%  C) 18.00%  D) 19.65%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 15.34%**\n\nσ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ = (0.36 × 484) + (0.16 × 144) + (2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 22 × 12 × 0.30) = 174.24 + 23.04 + 38.02 = **235.30**. Taking the square root, σ_p = **15.34%**.\n\n- **A) 14.05%** - wrong; this is the result if the correlation is treated as zero, dropping the covariance term entirely.\n- **C) 18.00%** - wrong; that is the **weighted average** of the two standard deviations (0.6 × 22 + 0.4 × 12). Portfolio risk equals the weighted average only when ρ = +1.\n- **D) 19.65%** - wrong; this uses the weights rather than the **squared** weights in the first two terms.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** Which of the following is NOT an assumption of Modern Portfolio Theory?\nA) Investors are risk-averse  B) All investors have the same degree of risk aversion  C) Decisions rest solely on expected return and risk over a single holding period  D) Risk is measured by the variance or standard deviation of returns\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) All investors have the same degree of risk aversion**\n\nMPT assumes every investor is **risk-averse**, but expressly allows the **degree** of aversion to differ — which is exactly why different investors select **different points on the same efficient frontier**.\n\n- **A) Investors are risk-averse** - wrong choice; this is a core assumption. Without it the frontier would collapse to a single point.\n- **C) Decisions rest solely on expected return and risk over a single holding period** - wrong choice; this is an assumption. Utility is a function of E(R) and σ only, for one period.\n- **D) Risk is measured by the variance or standard deviation of returns** - wrong choice; this is an assumption, and the one that later attracted criticism for treating upside and downside volatility alike.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Dr Aravind has a 25-year horizon, a secure hospital income and no dependants, so his ability to take risk is high. His risk-profiling questionnaire, however, shows a low willingness, and he redeemed in panic during the last correction. How should his adviser construct the portfolio?\nA) To the higher of the two, because his objective capacity dominates  B) To the lower of the two, while continuing to educate him about long-horizon equity risk  C) To the simple average of the two scores  D) Entirely to his stated willingness, with no further discussion\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) To the lower of the two, while continuing to educate him about long-horizon equity risk**\n\nWhere **ability and willingness conflict, the lower of the two governs**. The adviser may **educate but not override** the client. Building to his ability alone would very likely trigger another panic redemption, converting a paper loss into a permanent one.\n\n- **A) To the higher of the two, because his objective capacity dominates** - wrong; ability never overrides willingness. The capacity to absorb a loss is not the temperament to hold through it.\n- **C) To the simple average of the two scores** - wrong; risk profiling is not an averaging exercise. The binding constraint is the lower measure.\n- **D) Entirely to his stated willingness, with no further discussion** - wrong; the level is right but the conduct is not. Education is part of the adviser's duty of care.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** Dr Aravind's Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic equity allocation of 65% with a permitted band of 55% to 75%. Believing valuations are stretched, his adviser reduces equity to 58%. This is best described as:\nA) Strategic asset allocation  B) Tactical asset allocation  C) Rebalancing to target weights  D) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Tactical asset allocation**\n\nA **short-term, market-view-driven deviation from the policy mix that stays inside the IPS bands** is **tactical asset allocation**. The clean test: a change triggered by a **market** event is tactical, while a change triggered by a **life** event is strategic.\n\n- **A) Strategic asset allocation** - wrong; the 65% policy mix is itself the strategic allocation, and it is unchanged. Only the current position moved.\n- **C) Rebalancing to target weights** - wrong; rebalancing moves the portfolio **back to** 65%, not deliberately away from it.\n- **D) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement** - wrong; 58% sits inside the 55–75% band. It would be a breach only outside the band.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A diversified equity fund returned 15.2% over the year. The risk-free rate was 6.5%, the benchmark returned 12.4% and the fund's beta was 1.10. What is the fund's Jensen's alpha?\nA) −4.94%  B) 2.21%  C) 2.80%  D) 8.70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 2.21%**\n\nJensen's alpha = Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)]. The CAPM required return = 6.5 + 1.10 × (12.4 − 6.5) = 6.5 + 6.49 = **12.99%**. Alpha = 15.2 − 12.99 = **2.21%**.\n\n- **A) −4.94%** - wrong; this applies beta to the whole market return (6.5 + 1.10 × 12.4) instead of to the market **risk premium**.\n- **C) 2.80%** - wrong; that is simply Rp − Rm, which ignores the beta actually taken. Alpha is never the raw gap over the benchmark.\n- **D) 8.70%** - wrong; that is Rp − Rf, the excess return, not the value added over the CAPM requirement.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Dr Aravind, aged 29, asks his father to pay Rs 2,00,000 from the father's own bank account for a mutual fund purchase in Dr Aravind's sole folio. What must the adviser tell him?\nA) It is permitted, since a parent is a close relative  B) It is permitted up to Rs 50,000 with a third-party declaration  C) It is not permitted; the subscription must come from Dr Aravind's own bank account  D) It is permitted provided the father's KYC is completed\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) It is not permitted; the subscription must come from Dr Aravind's own bank account**\n\nUnder the PMLA framework a subscription must come from the **first or sole holder's own bank account**. The only family exception is a **parent, grandparent or related person paying for a MINOR**, capped at **Rs 50,000 per transaction**. Dr Aravind is an adult, so no exception applies.\n\n- **A) It is permitted, since a parent is a close relative** - wrong; there is no general \"close relative\" exception. A spouse paying for a spouse is equally barred.\n- **B) It is permitted up to Rs 50,000 with a third-party declaration** - wrong; the Rs 50,000 ceiling attaches only to payments made for a **minor** investor.\n- **D) It is permitted provided the father's KYC is completed** - wrong; the payer's KYC does not cure a third-party payment where no exception exists.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** A mutual fund folio is held jointly by three holders with the mode of holding \"Anyone or Survivor\". Who must sign a request to register a nomination?\nA) Any one of the three holders  B) The first holder only  C) All three holders  D) Any two of the three holders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) All three holders**\n\nNomination is a personal act of every unit holder. **ALL joint holders must sign the nomination**, even where the mode of holding is \"Anyone or Survivor\" and ordinary transactions need only one signature.\n\n- **A) Any one of the three holders** - wrong; that is the rule for **transactions** such as purchase, redemption or switch, not for nomination.\n- **B) The first holder only** - wrong; the first holder is the tax owner and the payout recipient, but cannot nominate on the others' behalf.\n- **D) Any two of the three holders** - wrong; there is no partial-majority rule. Nomination requires every holder's signature.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** Which of the following is NOT an Officially Valid Document (OVD) under the PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules?\nA) Passport  B) Voter's Identity Card issued by the Election Commission  C) PAN card  D) NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) PAN card**\n\nThe six OVDs are the **passport, driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar, Voter's Identity Card, NREGA job card and the National Population Register letter**. **PAN is not an OVD** — it is a separate and additional mandatory requirement under Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\n- **A) Passport** - wrong choice; the passport is the first-listed OVD.\n- **B) Voter's Identity Card issued by the Election Commission** - wrong choice; it is one of the six OVDs.\n- **D) NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer** - wrong choice; it is an OVD, included specifically to reach investors who hold no other document.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Ms Kavipriya's folio was opened when she was a minor, with her mother as guardian. She turned 18 last week. What happens on that date?\nA) Nothing changes until the guardian files a form; the SIP continues  B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all standing instructions cease  C) The guardian continues to operate the folio until she is 21  D) The folio is automatically closed and the proceeds are paid to the guardian\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all standing instructions cease**\n\nOn the date a minor attains majority the folio is **frozen for financial transactions** and **all SIPs, SWPs and STPs cease**. The now-major must file the \"Minor Attaining Majority\" form with a banker-attested signature, PAN, completed KYC, a bank account in her own name and a fresh nomination. The guardian's authority ends completely.\n\n- **A) Nothing changes until the guardian files a form; the SIP continues** - wrong; the freeze is automatic on the birthday, which is why the AMC must send advance notice beforehand.\n- **C) The guardian continues to operate the folio until she is 21** - wrong; age 21 applies only where a **court** appointed the guardian, and even then the guardian's power over the folio ends on majority.\n- **D) The folio is automatically closed and the proceeds are paid to the guardian** - wrong; the units remain, and no payout may be made to the erstwhile guardian.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** Which non-resident bank account is maintained in foreign currency, may be opened only as a term deposit of one to five years, and carries no exchange-rate risk for the depositor?\nA) NRE account  B) NRO account  C) FCNR(B) account  D) RFC account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) FCNR(B) account**\n\nAn **FCNR(B)** deposit is held in a freely convertible **foreign currency**, may be opened only as a **term deposit of one to five years**, is **fully repatriable**, and its interest is exempt from Indian tax. Because the money stays in foreign currency, the depositor bears **no rupee exchange risk**.\n\n- **A) NRE account** - wrong; NRE is **rupee-denominated**, so the depositor does carry exchange-rate risk, and it may be a savings, current, recurring or fixed deposit account.\n- **B) NRO account** - wrong; NRO is rupee-denominated, holds **Indian income**, is **taxable**, and repatriation is capped at USD 1 million per financial year.\n- **D) RFC account** - wrong; an RFC account is for a **returning Indian who has become resident again**, not for a non-resident.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** Dr Aravind wants to send Rs 1,60,000 to his broker immediately in a single transaction settled in real time on a gross basis. Which rail can he NOT use for this amount?\nA) NEFT  B) RTGS  C) IMPS  D) UPI\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) RTGS**\n\n**RTGS carries a minimum of Rs 2,00,000** per transaction, with no upper limit. At Rs 1,60,000 he falls below the floor and must use another rail.\n\n- **A) NEFT** - wrong choice; NEFT has **no minimum and no maximum** and runs 24×7, though it settles in deferred net batches rather than in real time.\n- **C) IMPS** - wrong choice; IMPS is instant, available 24×7, with a per-transaction ceiling of the order of Rs 5 lakh, comfortably above Rs 1,60,000.\n- **D) UPI** - wrong choice; the standard UPI limit is around Rs 1 lakh but is raised to Rs 5 lakh for capital-market and other specified categories, so the amount can be sent.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** An investor registers a change of bank mandate on a Monday and lodges a redemption request four days later. Where will the redemption proceeds be credited?\nA) To the newly registered bank account, as it is now the default  B) To the old, already registered bank account, under the cooling-off rule  C) Half to each account, pending verification  D) The redemption request will be rejected outright\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) To the old, already registered bank account, under the cooling-off rule**\n\nWhere a redemption is received **within the cooling-off window — commonly 10 calendar days — of a change-of-bank-mandate request**, the proceeds are credited to the **old, already registered** account. It is a deliberate anti-fraud control that defeats the classic scam of changing the bank details and immediately redeeming.\n\n- **A) To the newly registered bank account, as it is now the default** - wrong; the new account becomes the payout account only once the cooling-off period has run.\n- **C) Half to each account, pending verification** - wrong; no such split mechanism exists.\n- **D) The redemption request will be rejected outright** - wrong; the redemption is processed normally. Only the destination account is controlled.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** A SEBI-registered individual Investment Adviser advises a family with Assets Under Advice of Rs 62,00,000 and has opted for the AUA-based fee mode. What is the maximum annual fee chargeable to that family?\nA) Rs 62,000  B) Rs 1,25,000  C) Rs 1,55,000  D) Rs 2,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 1,55,000**\n\nUnder the AUA mode the cap is **2.5% of AUA per annum per family**: 2.5% × 62,00,000 = **Rs 1,55,000**. Above an AUA of about Rs 50 lakh the AUA mode permits more than the fixed-fee mode, which is where the two modes break even.\n\n- **A) Rs 62,000** - wrong; that is 1% of AUA, not the prescribed 2.5% ceiling.\n- **B) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the cap under the **fixed-fee** mode, and only one mode may be used at a time.\n- **D) Rs 2,80,000** - wrong; this adds the two caps together. An adviser may charge under one mode only, and may switch modes only after 12 months.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** What is the minimum net worth requirement for a non-individual Investment Adviser under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013?\nA) Rs 5 lakh of net tangible assets  B) Rs 25 lakh  C) Rs 50 lakh  D) Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 50 lakh**\n\nA **non-individual** Investment Adviser must maintain a net worth of **Rs 50 lakh**.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 lakh of net tangible assets** - wrong; that is the requirement for an **individual** adviser or a partnership firm.\n- **B) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; no such net worth threshold applies to investment advisers.\n- **D) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the minimum net worth of a **portfolio manager** under the 2020 Regulations.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** Dr Aravind's adviser is registered as an individual Investment Adviser and her client count has just crossed 165. What does SEBI require her to do?\nA) Nothing; the number of clients is not regulated  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual Investment Adviser  C) Appoint a compliance officer but remain an individual adviser  D) Surrender her registration and re-register as a distributor\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Apply for registration as a non-individual Investment Adviser**\n\nAn individual adviser whose client base exceeds **150 clients** must corporatise — that is, apply for registration as a **non-individual** Investment Adviser, which brings with it the Rs 50 lakh net worth requirement and a mandatory compliance officer.\n\n- **A) Nothing; the number of clients is not regulated** - wrong; the 150-client threshold is an express trigger in the Regulations.\n- **C) Appoint a compliance officer but remain an individual adviser** - wrong; a compliance officer is mandatory only for **non-individual** advisers, and appointing one does not satisfy the corporatisation requirement.\n- **D) Surrender her registration and re-register as a distributor** - wrong; distribution is a different activity that would abandon the advisory relationship and the fiduciary standard entirely.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser charges his client an advisory fee and also quietly receives trail commission from an AMC on the schemes he recommends. Which obligation does this breach most directly?\nA) Regulation 15(1) - acting in a fiduciary capacity  B) Regulation 15(2) - receiving no consideration from anyone other than the client  C) Regulation 15(12) - not managing client funds or securities  D) Regulation 19 - maintenance of records\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Regulation 15(2) - receiving no consideration from anyone other than the client**\n\n**Regulation 15(2)** provides that an Investment Adviser shall receive **no consideration from any person other than the client** in respect of the advice given. Taking a fee from the client and a commission from the manufacturer is the classic \"double dipping\" violation.\n\n- **A) Regulation 15(1) - acting in a fiduciary capacity** - wrong; the conduct certainly offends the fiduciary standard, but the **specific** prohibition on outside consideration is Regulation 15(2).\n- **C) Regulation 15(12) - not managing client funds or securities** - wrong; that bars an adviser from taking custody or managing assets, which is not what happened here.\n- **D) Regulation 19 - maintenance of records** - wrong; that concerns record keeping, not remuneration.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, a designated person whose trades in the company's securities exceed a threshold value in a calendar quarter must disclose them to the company. What is the threshold and the time limit?\nA) Above Rs 5 lakh, within 2 trading days  B) Above Rs 10 lakh, within 2 trading days  C) Above Rs 10 lakh, within 7 trading days  D) Above Rs 25 lakh, within 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Above Rs 10 lakh, within 2 trading days**\n\nThe continual disclosure obligation is triggered where the traded value **exceeds Rs 10 lakh in a calendar quarter**, and the disclosure must reach the company **within 2 trading days**.\n\n- **A) Above Rs 5 lakh, within 2 trading days** - wrong; Rs 5 lakh is the PMLA threshold for reporting **cross-border wire transfers**, not the PIT disclosure trigger.\n- **C) Above Rs 10 lakh, within 7 trading days** - wrong; 7 trading days is the window within which a **pre-cleared** trade must be executed, not the disclosure period.\n- **D) Above Rs 25 lakh, within 30 days** - wrong on both counts; no such threshold or period exists under these Regulations.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** An adviser notices a client structuring a series of transactions in a manner that strongly suggests money laundering. What is the correct action under the PMLA?\nA) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days and say nothing to the client  B) File a report only if the aggregate exceeds Rs 10 lakh  C) Inform the client first so he may explain, then report within 15 days  D) Report the matter to SEBI within one month and freeze the client's folio\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days and say nothing to the client**\n\nAn **STR has no monetary threshold** and must be filed with **FIU-IND within 7 working days** of forming the suspicion. Telling the client is **\"tipping off\"**, which is itself an offence under the PMLA.\n\n- **B) File a report only if the aggregate exceeds Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; Rs 10 lakh is the **Cash Transaction Report** threshold, reportable by the 15th of the following month. An STR has no threshold at all.\n- **C) Inform the client first so he may explain, then report within 15 days** - wrong; informing the client is tipping off, and the timeline is wrong as well.\n- **D) Report the matter to SEBI within one month and freeze the client's folio** - wrong; the report goes to **FIU-IND**, not SEBI, and an adviser has no power to freeze a folio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** Under the Liberalised Remittance Scheme, what is the maximum a resident individual may remit abroad in a financial year?\nA) USD 1,00,000  B) USD 2,00,000  C) USD 2,50,000  D) USD 10,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) USD 2,50,000**\n\nThe **LRS limit is USD 2,50,000 per financial year per resident individual**, covering permissible current and capital account transactions. Remittances above a prescribed annual threshold attract TCS, at concessional rates for education and medical purposes.\n\n- **A) USD 1,00,000** - wrong; an earlier limit, long since revised upward.\n- **B) USD 2,00,000** - wrong; this has never been the LRS ceiling.\n- **D) USD 10,00,000** - wrong; USD 1 million per financial year is the **NRO repatriation** ceiling, a different rule entirely.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** An Investment Adviser has recommended that clients buy a particular stock. Within what period is she barred from dealing in that stock on her own account contrary to that advice?\nA) 24 hours  B) 7 days  C) 15 days  D) 6 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 15 days**\n\nUnder **Regulation 15(5)**, an adviser must not deal on her own account **contrary to the advice given, for 15 days** from the date of that advice. If her view genuinely changes within that period, she must communicate the revised view to clients **24 hours in advance** of acting on it.\n\n- **A) 24 hours** - wrong; 24 hours is the **advance notice** period for a revised view, not the length of the bar itself.\n- **B) 7 days** - wrong; 7 trading days is the window for executing a **pre-cleared** trade under the PIT Regulations.\n- **D) 6 months** - wrong; 6 months is the **contra-trade** bar for designated persons under the PIT Regulations, and the connected-person lookback period.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** A dealer learns that a large institutional client will place a substantial buy order in a mid cap stock at the opening bell, and buys the stock for his own account minutes beforehand. This conduct is best labelled:\nA) Insider trading, under the PIT Regulations, 2015  B) Front-running, under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003  C) Churning  D) Rebating\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Front-running, under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003**\n\n**Front-running** is dealing **ahead of a known impending order** to profit from the price move that order will cause, and it is dealt with under the **SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003**. Memory hook: front-run an **order**, insider-trade on **news**.\n\n- **A) Insider trading, under the PIT Regulations, 2015** - wrong; insider trading uses **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company itself**, not knowledge of an order book.\n- **C) Churning** - wrong; churning is **excessive trading in the client's own account** to generate brokerage or fresh commission.\n- **D) Rebating** - wrong; rebating is passing back commission or offering gifts and inducements to win business.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** Over one year an adviser switched a client's Rs 40 lakh portfolio between eleven different equity schemes, each individually sound, with no change in the stated strategy, earning fresh commission each time. This is best described as:\nA) Mis-selling  B) Churning  C) Tactical asset allocation  D) Rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Churning**\n\n**Churning is excessive trading, switching or turnover to generate brokerage or fresh commission.** The give-away is exactly what is described here: a great deal of activity and **no change in the underlying strategy**. Mis-selling is about the **wrong product**; churning is about **too many transactions**.\n\n- **A) Mis-selling** - wrong; each scheme was individually suitable. The offence lies in the frequency, not the product choice.\n- **C) Tactical asset allocation** - wrong; TAA is a deliberate, documented deviation **between asset classes** based on a market view, not serial switching within the same class.\n- **D) Rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing restores the portfolio to its **strategic target weights** and is inherently contrarian and infrequent.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** Regulation 15(1) of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 requires an adviser to act in a fiduciary capacity. The fiduciary duty is conventionally split into which two limbs?\nA) Disclosure and documentation  B) Loyalty and care  C) Suitability and diligence  D) Honesty and capability\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Loyalty and care**\n\nThe fiduciary standard has two limbs: the **duty of loyalty** (put the client first, avoid or fully disclose conflicts, take no undisclosed benefit) and the **duty of care** (know the client, risk-profile him, research the product, recommend what fits, and monitor it). Loyalty answers \"whose side are you on?\"; care answers \"did you do the work?\"\n\n- **A) Disclosure and documentation** - wrong; both are important obligations, but they are the tools by which the two duties are discharged, not the duties themselves.\n- **C) Suitability and diligence** - wrong; these sit inside the duty of **care**, and \"suitability\" is in fact the **lower** standard applicable to a distributor.\n- **D) Honesty and capability** - wrong; these are two of the nine heads of the Code of Conduct, not the limbs of fiduciary duty.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** An Investment Adviser's annual compliance audit for the year ended 31 March 2026 produces a report dated 18 September 2026 containing adverse observations. What must the adviser do, and by when?\nA) Nothing; only the auditor reports to SEBI  B) Report the adverse findings alone, by 31 March 2027  C) Report the adverse findings together with the action taken, approved by the management, by 18 October 2026  D) Report the adverse findings within three months of the report date\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Report the adverse findings together with the action taken, approved by the management, by 18 October 2026**\n\nThe audit must be completed within **six months of the financial year end**, and any **adverse findings must be reported together with the action taken on them, duly approved by the management or board, within one month of the date of the audit report**. A report dated 18 September 2026 therefore requires reporting by **18 October 2026**.\n\n- **A) Nothing; only the auditor reports to SEBI** - wrong; the reporting obligation rests on the **adviser**, through BASL.\n- **B) Report the adverse findings alone, by 31 March 2027** - wrong on both counts; the action taken is the operative half of the report, and the deadline is one month from the report date.\n- **D) Report the adverse findings within three months of the report date** - wrong; the period is **one month**, not three.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** Which feature most sharply distinguishes Australia's FOFA regime from the Indian and US approaches to conflicts of interest in financial advice?\nA) It requires advisers to be licensed  B) It bans conflicted remuneration outright, rather than relying on disclosure  C) It imposes a fiduciary duty on advisers  D) It requires records to be kept for five years\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It bans conflicted remuneration outright, rather than relying on disclosure**\n\nUnder the **Future of Financial Advice (FOFA)** reforms, **commissions and volume-based benefits are banned** for personal advice on relevant products. Australia **removes** the conflict rather than merely disclosing it, which is what makes its regime the strictest of the three on remuneration.\n\n- **A) It requires advisers to be licensed** - wrong; all three jurisdictions require registration or licensing, so this distinguishes nothing.\n- **C) It imposes a fiduciary duty on advisers** - wrong; India (Regulation 15(1)) and the US (Advisers Act 1940) both impose a fiduciary standard. Australia frames its obligation as a statutory **best interests duty** with a safe harbour.\n- **D) It requires records to be kept for five years** - wrong; record keeping is common to all three and is not the distinguishing feature.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Dr Aravind lodged a complaint against his broker on SCORES after the firm ignored his written request. Within how many days must the entity file its Action Taken Report?\nA) 7 calendar days  B) 15 calendar days  C) 21 calendar days  D) 30 calendar days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 21 calendar days**\n\nUnder SCORES 2.0 the entity must file its **Action Taken Report within 21 calendar days**. The full rhythm is **21 – 15 – 21 – 15 – 21**: entity 21 days, investor may seek a first review within 15, the Designated Body takes 21, a second review request within 15, and SEBI 21. If the entity misses its 21 days, the complaint **auto-escalates** to the Designated Body.\n\n- **A) 7 calendar days** - wrong; 7 working days is the PMLA deadline for filing a **Suspicious Transaction Report**.\n- **B) 15 calendar days** - wrong; 15 days is the window for the **investor** to seek a review, not for the entity to respond.\n- **D) 30 calendar days** - wrong; 30 days is the RBI Ombudsman escalation trigger and the pension-intermediary resolution period.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Dr Aravind's mother wishes to complain to the Insurance Ombudsman about a repudiated health claim of Rs 6,80,000. What is the maximum claim value the Insurance Ombudsman may entertain?\nA) Rs 20 lakh  B) Rs 21 lakh  C) Rs 30 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 30 lakh**\n\nUnder the **Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017** (amended 2021), the Ombudsman may entertain claims, including expenses claimed, **not exceeding Rs 30 lakh**. Her Rs 6.80 lakh claim is comfortably within the limit, and the process is free of cost.\n\n- **A) Rs 20 lakh** - wrong; Rs 20 lakh is the **RBI Ombudsman's** cap for actual loss, a banking figure.\n- **B) Rs 21 lakh** - wrong; Rs 21 lakh is the theoretical maximum under RB-IOS 2021 (Rs 20 lakh for actual loss plus Rs 1 lakh for time and harassment).\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the current **District Consumer Commission** pecuniary limit.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Under the RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, 2021, what compensation may the Ombudsman award?\nA) Up to Rs 10 lakh for actual loss only  B) Up to Rs 20 lakh for actual loss, plus up to Rs 1 lakh for loss of time, expenses and mental anguish  C) Up to Rs 30 lakh in total  D) Unlimited, since it is a statutory adjudication\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Up to Rs 20 lakh for actual loss, plus up to Rs 1 lakh for loss of time, expenses and mental anguish**\n\nRB-IOS 2021 permits an award of up to **Rs 20 lakh for the actual loss suffered**, and **separately up to Rs 1 lakh** for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish. The two caps are additive, giving a theoretical maximum of Rs 21 lakh.\n\n- **A) Up to Rs 10 lakh for actual loss only** - wrong; the actual-loss cap is Rs 20 lakh, and the harassment component exists in addition to it.\n- **C) Up to Rs 30 lakh in total** - wrong; Rs 30 lakh is the **Insurance** Ombudsman's value limit, a very common swap in the exam.\n- **D) Unlimited, since it is a statutory adjudication** - wrong; the scheme prescribes express monetary ceilings.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Dr Aravind is dissatisfied with his stock broker's handling of an unauthorised trade. He has already complained to the firm and on SCORES without resolution, and now wants a binding decision on the money. Where should he go?\nA) The Securities Appellate Tribunal  B) The SMART ODR portal, for online conciliation and then arbitration  C) Directly to the Supreme Court  D) Back to SEBI, which will adjudicate the claim and order compensation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The SMART ODR portal, for online conciliation and then arbitration**\n\nHaving exhausted the firm and SCORES, a **disputed money claim** goes to the **SMART ODR portal**, where non-binding online conciliation is followed, if necessary, by **binding online arbitration**, enforceable under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996. In one line: **SCORES chases; ODR decides.**\n\n- **A) The Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT is an **appellate** body hearing appeals against a **regulator's order**. It is not a first-instance complaint counter against a broker.\n- **C) Directly to the Supreme Court** - wrong; the Supreme Court hears appeals from SAT on a question of law, not original investor claims.\n- **D) Back to SEBI, which will adjudicate the claim and order compensation** - wrong; SEBI **routes, monitors and enforces timelines**. It does not adjudicate individual money claims or award damages.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Under the pecuniary limits currently in force, a consumer complaint where the consideration paid was Rs 38,00,000 must be filed before which forum?\nA) The District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  B) The State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  C) The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  D) The Securities Appellate Tribunal\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) The District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission**\n\nUnder the **2021 Rules** now in force, the **District Commission hears matters where the consideration paid is up to Rs 50 lakh**, the State Commission takes Rs 50 lakh to Rs 2 crore, and the National Commission anything above Rs 2 crore. Rs 38 lakh therefore goes to the **District Commission**. Note that jurisdiction turns on the **consideration paid**, not on the compensation demanded.\n\n- **B) The State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission** - wrong; the State Commission's floor is above Rs 50 lakh under the current limits.\n- **C) The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission** - wrong; the National Commission hears matters above Rs 2 crore, plus appeals from the State Commission.\n- **D) The Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT hears appeals against orders of SEBI, PFRDA and IRDAI, not consumer complaints.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Caselets (6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 1: Dr Anitha Ramanathan's household budget\n\nDr Anitha Ramanathan is a 29-year-old radiologist working at a private hospital in Chennai. Her gross monthly income is Rs 2,15,000. Her household spends Rs 1,48,000 a month, of which Rs 38,700 is the EMI on her education loan and Rs 14,300 is the EMI on her car loan.\n\nHer assets are: a savings bank balance of Rs 2,80,000; a liquid fund holding of Rs 1,45,000; equity mutual funds of Rs 6,50,000; a PPF balance of Rs 3,20,000; and an apartment, car and gold together valued at Rs 48,05,000 — total assets of Rs 62,00,000. Her liabilities are an education loan of Rs 18,00,000 and a car loan of Rs 6,80,000, a total of Rs 24,80,000.\n\n**Q91.** What is Dr Anitha's monthly savings ratio?\nA) 24.65%  B) 31.16%  C) 45.27%  D) 68.84%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 31.16%**\n\nSavings (surplus) = 2,15,000 − 1,48,000 = **Rs 67,000**. Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 67,000 ÷ 2,15,000 = **31.16%**. Check: savings ratio + expenses ratio = 31.16% + 68.84% = 100% ✓\n\n- **A) 24.65%** - wrong; this is the debt-to-income ratio (53,000 ÷ 2,15,000).\n- **C) 45.27%** - wrong; this divides the surplus by expenses (67,000 ÷ 1,48,000) instead of by income.\n- **D) 68.84%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** What is her liquidity ratio?\nA) 1.98 months  B) 2.87 months  C) 5.03 months  D) 7.26 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 2.87 months**\n\nLiquid assets = savings balance 2,80,000 + liquid fund 1,45,000 = **Rs 4,25,000**. Equity mutual funds and PPF are **not** liquid assets. Liquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly expenses** = 4,25,000 ÷ 1,48,000 = **2.87 months** — below the healthy 3–6 month range.\n\n- **A) 1.98 months** - wrong; this divides liquid assets by monthly *income* rather than monthly expenses.\n- **C) 5.03 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the PPF balance (4,25,000 + 3,20,000 = 7,45,000) as liquid. PPF is locked in.\n- **D) 7.26 months** - wrong; this wrongly counts the equity mutual funds (4,25,000 + 6,50,000 = 10,75,000) as liquid. Equity is volatile and does not qualify.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** What is her solvency ratio?\nA) 0.40  B) 0.60  C) 0.67  D) 1.67\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.60**\n\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 62,00,000 − 24,80,000 = **Rs 37,20,000**. Solvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 37,20,000 ÷ 62,00,000 = **0.60**. Check: solvency + leverage = 0.60 + 0.40 = 1 ✓\n\n- **A) 0.40** - wrong; this is the leverage ratio (liabilities ÷ assets), the complement of solvency.\n- **C) 0.67** - wrong; this divides liabilities by net worth (24,80,000 ÷ 37,20,000), which is a debt-to-equity figure, not the solvency ratio.\n- **D) 1.67** - wrong; this divides total assets by net worth, inverting the ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** How much more must she set aside to build an emergency fund equal to six months of expenses?\nA) Rs 4,44,000  B) Rs 4,63,000  C) Rs 8,65,000  D) Rs 8,88,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,63,000**\n\nThe contingency fund is sized against **expenses**, not income. Target = 6 × 1,48,000 = **Rs 8,88,000**. She already holds liquid assets of Rs 4,25,000. Additional requirement = 8,88,000 − 4,25,000 = **Rs 4,63,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 4,44,000** - wrong; this is a three-month target (3 × 1,48,000) before deducting existing liquid assets.\n- **C) Rs 8,65,000** - wrong; this sizes the fund against six months of *income* (6 × 2,15,000 = 12,90,000) less liquid assets.\n- **D) Rs 8,88,000** - wrong; this is the full six-month target, not the additional amount still needed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** What is her debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 24.65%  B) 31.16%  C) 35.81%  D) 68.84%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 24.65%**\n\nTotal EMIs = 38,700 + 14,300 = **Rs 53,000**. Debt-to-income ratio = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 53,000 ÷ 2,15,000 = **24.65%**, comfortably below the 35–40% ceiling lenders apply.\n\n- **B) 31.16%** - wrong; this is her savings ratio.\n- **C) 35.81%** - wrong; this divides EMIs by expenses (53,000 ÷ 1,48,000) rather than by gross income.\n- **D) 68.84%** - wrong; this is her expenses ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 2: Dr Anitha's direct securities portfolio\n\nDr Anitha holds 800 shares of Kaveri Diagnostics Ltd. The share trades at Rs 640. The company reported earnings per share of Rs 32, a book value per share of Rs 256 and a dividend per share of Rs 9.60.\n\nShe also holds a corporate bond with a **face value of Rs 1,000** carrying a **7% annual coupon**. It has **4 years** remaining to maturity and currently trades at **Rs 940**.\n\nTo protect her equity exposure over the next month, she buys **one Nifty put option** with a strike of **24,500** at a premium of **Rs 210**. The Nifty lot size is **25** and the index currently trades at **24,500**.\n\n**Q96.** What is the P/E ratio of Kaveri Diagnostics Ltd?\nA) 2.50  B) 5.00  C) 20.00  D) 25.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 20.00**\n\nP/E = Price ÷ EPS = 640 ÷ 32 = **20.00**.\n\n- **A) 2.50** - wrong; this is the price-to-book ratio (640 ÷ 256).\n- **B) 5.00** - wrong; this is the earnings yield in per cent (32 ÷ 640), the reciprocal of the P/E.\n- **D) 25.00** - wrong; no combination of the given figures produces 25; it is a rounded-up guess.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** What is the dividend yield on the share?\nA) 1.50%  B) 3.75%  C) 5.00%  D) 30.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.50%**\n\nDividend yield = DPS ÷ Market price = 9.60 ÷ 640 = **1.50%**.\n\n- **B) 3.75%** - wrong; this divides the dividend by book value per share (9.60 ÷ 256) rather than by market price.\n- **C) 5.00%** - wrong; this is the earnings yield (32 ÷ 640).\n- **D) 30.00%** - wrong; this is the dividend payout ratio (9.60 ÷ 32), not a yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** What is the current yield on the bond?\nA) 7.00%  B) 7.22%  C) 7.45%  D) 8.76%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 7.45%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ **Market price** = 70 ÷ 940 = **7.45%**.\n\n- **A) 7.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, which is calculated on face value, not on the price paid.\n- **B) 7.22%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by the average of face and price (70 ÷ 970), which is the denominator used in the approximate YTM formula, not in current yield.\n- **D) 8.76%** - wrong; this is the approximate yield to maturity, which also counts the capital gain at redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** Using the approximation formula, what is the bond's yield to maturity?\nA) 7.45%  B) 8.50%  C) 8.76%  D) 9.04%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 8.76%**\n\nApproximate YTM = [C + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2] = [70 + (1,000 − 940) ÷ 4] ÷ [(1,000 + 940) ÷ 2] = (70 + 15) ÷ 970 = 85 ÷ 970 = **8.76%**. The bond trades at a discount, so the ordering coupon (7.00%) < current yield (7.45%) < YTM (8.76%) holds.\n\n- **A) 7.45%** - wrong; this is the current yield, which ignores the Rs 60 capital gain at redemption.\n- **B) 8.50%** - wrong; this divides the annualised return of Rs 85 by the face value of Rs 1,000 instead of by the average of face and price.\n- **D) 9.04%** - wrong; this divides Rs 85 by the market price of Rs 940 instead of by the average investment.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** At what Nifty level at expiry does her long put break even?\nA) 24,290  B) 24,500  C) 24,710  D) 24,080\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 24,290**\n\nBreak-even for a **long put** = Strike − Premium = 24,500 − 210 = **24,290**. Below that level the put's intrinsic value exceeds the premium paid and the position is in profit.\n\n- **B) 24,500** - wrong; this is the strike, at which the put has zero intrinsic value and the full premium is lost.\n- **C) 24,710** - wrong; strike + premium is the break-even for a long **call**, not a put.\n- **D) 24,080** - wrong; this deducts twice the premium (24,500 − 420), which has no basis in the payoff.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 3: Dr Anitha and the Marina Flexi Cap Fund\n\nThe Marina Flexi Cap Fund reports total assets of **Rs 745 crore**, total liabilities of **Rs 9 crore** and **18.40 crore** units outstanding on the valuation date.\n\nOn that date Dr Anitha invests a lump sum of **Rs 2,40,000** in the **direct plan** of the scheme. The scheme charges an **exit load of 1% of the redemption value** if units are redeemed within 12 months. **Eight months later** she redeems **2,000 units** when the NAV is **Rs 46.50**.\n\nShe also runs a monthly SIP of **Rs 15,000** in the same scheme. Over the first four instalments the NAVs were **Rs 40.00, Rs 50.00, Rs 37.50 and Rs 48.00**.\n\nThe scheme's total expense ratio is **1.85% for the regular plan** and **0.95% for the direct plan**. The two plans hold an identical portfolio.\n\n**Q101.** What is the NAV per unit of the scheme on the valuation date?\nA) Rs 39.51  B) Rs 40.00  C) Rs 40.49  D) Rs 40.98\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 40.00**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ Units outstanding = (745 − 9) ÷ 18.40 = 736 ÷ 18.40 = **Rs 40.00**.\n\n- **A) Rs 39.51** - wrong; this subtracts the Rs 9 crore of liabilities twice (727 ÷ 18.40).\n- **C) Rs 40.49** - wrong; this ignores liabilities altogether (745 ÷ 18.40).\n- **D) Rs 40.98** - wrong; this *adds* liabilities instead of subtracting them (754 ÷ 18.40).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q102.** What are the net proceeds of her redemption of 2,000 units?\nA) Rs 80,000  B) Rs 92,070  C) Rs 92,200  D) Rs 93,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 92,070**\n\nGross redemption value = 2,000 × 46.50 = **Rs 93,000**. The redemption falls within 12 months, so the 1% exit load applies on the redemption value: 1% × 93,000 = **Rs 930**. Net proceeds = 93,000 − 930 = **Rs 92,070**.\n\n- **A) Rs 80,000** - wrong; this is the original cost of the 2,000 units (2,000 × 40), not the redemption proceeds.\n- **C) Rs 92,200** - wrong; this applies the 1% load to the amount originally invested (Rs 800) rather than to the redemption value.\n- **D) Rs 93,000** - wrong; this is the gross value before the exit load is deducted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q103.** What is her average cost per unit across the four SIP instalments?\nA) Rs 43.24  B) Rs 43.75  C) Rs 43.88  D) Rs 44.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 43.24**\n\nUnits bought = 15,000÷40.00 = 375; 15,000÷50.00 = 300; 15,000÷37.50 = 400; 15,000÷48.00 = 312.5. Total = **1,387.5 units** for **Rs 60,000**. Average cost = 60,000 ÷ 1,387.5 = **Rs 43.24**, below the simple average NAV of Rs 43.88 — that gap is rupee-cost averaging at work.\n\n- **B) Rs 43.75** - wrong; it matches no computation on these figures.\n- **C) Rs 43.88** - wrong; this is the simple average of the four NAVs (175.50 ÷ 4), not the cost-weighted average.\n- **D) Rs 44.00** - wrong; a rounded guess that ignores the extra units bought at the low NAV of Rs 37.50.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q104.** On her Rs 2,40,000 lump sum, roughly how much does she save in one year by holding the direct plan rather than the regular plan?\nA) Rs 1,080  B) Rs 2,160  C) Rs 2,280  D) Rs 4,440\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,160**\n\nTER difference = 1.85% − 0.95% = **0.90% a year**. On Rs 2,40,000 that is 0.90% × 2,40,000 = **Rs 2,160** a year, which compounds into a very large sum over a long holding period.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,080** - wrong; this halves the correct TER differential for no reason.\n- **C) Rs 2,280** - wrong; this is the direct plan's own annual expense (0.95% × 2,40,000), not the saving.\n- **D) Rs 4,440** - wrong; this is the regular plan's total annual expense (1.85% × 2,40,000), not the differential.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q105.** Which statement about the direct and regular plans of this scheme is correct?\nA) The direct plan holds a more concentrated portfolio, which is why its TER is lower  B) The two plans hold an identical portfolio; the direct plan carries no distribution commission, so its TER is lower and its NAV rises higher over time  C) The regular plan's higher NAV shows that a distributor adds measurable value to returns  D) An investment adviser must recommend the regular plan so that the distributor can service the client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The two plans hold an identical portfolio; the direct plan carries no distribution commission, so its TER is lower and its NAV rises higher over time**\n\nThe portfolio, fund manager and strategy are the same. The only difference is that the regular plan's TER embeds distribution commission. A lower TER means less is deducted from net assets each day, so the direct plan's NAV compounds ahead of the regular plan's.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; portfolio construction is identical in both plans. The TER gap comes entirely from commission, not from holdings.\n- **C)** - wrong; the regular plan's NAV is **lower**, not higher, and NAV differences between plans reflect cost, not skill.\n- **D)** - wrong; a SEBI-registered investment adviser is paid by the client and must recommend **direct** plans; receiving commission would breach Regulation 15(2).\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 4: Dr Venkataraman's managed-portfolio decisions\n\nDr Venkataraman, 58, is Dr Anitha's senior consultant at the same Chennai hospital. He places **Rs 80,00,000** in a discretionary PMS. The fee terms are a fixed fee of 1.5% of assets a year plus a **performance fee of 20% of returns above a 9% hurdle rate, with no catch-up, charged on a high watermark basis**.\n\nBefore fees, his portfolio is valued at **Rs 96,00,000** at the end of Year 1, **Rs 86,00,000** at the end of Year 2 and **Rs 1,04,00,000** at the end of Year 3.\n\nHe is separately offered units in a **Category II AIF** whose scheme corpus is **Rs 260 crore**. The AIF is structured as a close-ended trust.\n\n**Q106.** What is the minimum amount SEBI requires a single investor to commit to the Category II AIF?\nA) Rs 25 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh  C) Rs 1 crore  D) Rs 20 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 1 crore**\n\nThe minimum investment per investor in an AIF is **Rs 1 crore**. The three minimums to keep straight are: mutual fund Rs 100–5,000, **PMS Rs 50 lakh**, **AIF Rs 1 crore**.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the reduced minimum for **employees and directors** of the AIF or its manager, and for an **angel investor** in an angel fund.\n- **B) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; that is the minimum client investment in a **PMS**, not an AIF.\n- **D) Rs 20 crore** - wrong; Rs 20 crore is the minimum **corpus of the scheme**, not the minimum ticket per investor.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q107.** What performance fee is payable for Year 1?\nA) Rs 1,44,000  B) Rs 1,76,000  C) Rs 3,20,000  D) Rs 4,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1,76,000**\n\nGain in Year 1 = 96,00,000 − 80,00,000 = **Rs 16,00,000** (a 20% return). The hurdle absorbs the first 9%: 9% × 80,00,000 = **Rs 7,20,000**. With no catch-up, the fee applies only to the excess: 16,00,000 − 7,20,000 = Rs 8,80,000. Performance fee = 20% × 8,80,000 = **Rs 1,76,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,44,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the hurdle amount itself (20% × 7,20,000) instead of 20% of the excess above it.\n- **C) Rs 3,20,000** - wrong; this charges 20% of the whole gain, ignoring the hurdle rate entirely.\n- **D) Rs 4,00,000** - wrong; this is 5% of the opening portfolio and corresponds to no term in the fee schedule.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q108.** What performance fee is payable for Year 2?\nA) Nil  B) Rs 1,20,000  C) Rs 1,76,000  D) Rs 2,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Nil**\n\nThe high watermark set in Year 1 is **Rs 96,00,000**. The Year 2 value of Rs 86,00,000 is **below** that peak, so no gain above the watermark has been produced and **no performance fee** is payable. The fixed fee is still charged; only the performance fee is suspended.\n\n- **B) Rs 1,20,000** - wrong; this is the 1.5% fixed fee on Rs 80,00,000, which is a different fee component, not a performance fee.\n- **C) Rs 1,76,000** - wrong; this repeats Year 1's fee, which the high watermark exists precisely to prevent.\n- **D) Rs 2,00,000** - wrong; no gain above the watermark arose, so nothing can be charged.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q109.** In Year 3, on how much gain is the performance fee charged?\nA) Rs 8,00,000  B) Rs 18,00,000  C) Rs 24,00,000  D) Rs 1,04,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 8,00,000**\n\nUnder the high watermark principle, the fee applies only to the gain **above the previous peak of Rs 96,00,000**: 1,04,00,000 − 96,00,000 = **Rs 8,00,000**. The client does not pay twice for recovering ground already lost.\n\n- **B) Rs 18,00,000** - wrong; this measures the rise from the Year 2 trough of Rs 86,00,000, which would charge the manager's fee on the recovery.\n- **C) Rs 24,00,000** - wrong; this measures the rise from the original Rs 80,00,000, ignoring the fee already paid in Year 1.\n- **D) Rs 1,04,00,000** - wrong; this is the portfolio value, not a gain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q110.** What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager of the Rs 260 crore Category II AIF maintain?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6.5 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 13 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5 crore**\n\nFor Categories I and II the continuing interest is the **lower of 2.5% of corpus or Rs 5 crore**. Here 2.5% × 260 crore = Rs 6.5 crore, which exceeds Rs 5 crore, so the requirement is the **lower** figure of **Rs 5 crore**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6.5 crore** - wrong; this takes 2.5% of corpus without applying the \"lower of\" cap.\n- **C) Rs 10 crore** - wrong; Rs 10 crore is the cap for a **Category III** fund, where the requirement is doubled to 5% or Rs 10 crore.\n- **D) Rs 13 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category III percentage (5% × 260 crore) and also ignores the cap.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 5: Sundar Iyer's advisory practice\n\nSundar Iyer is an **individual** SEBI-registered investment adviser in Chennai and a BASL member. He advises Dr Anitha. His current position is as follows.\n\n- His net tangible assets are **Rs 6,80,000**.\n- He has **138 individual clients** and expects to cross **150** during the next quarter.\n- Dr Anitha's family assets under advice total **Rs 34,00,000**, and he wishes to charge the maximum fee permitted under the **assets-under-advice mode**.\n- His **spouse** holds an ARN and runs a mutual fund distribution business. She has offered to execute Dr Anitha's mutual fund purchases and collect the trail commission.\n- Dr Anitha's risk-profiling questionnaire scores her **willingness** to take risk as \"aggressive\", but her **ability** is limited: an outstanding education loan of Rs 18,00,000 and liquid assets covering under three months of expenses.\n\n**Q111.** What is the minimum net tangible asset requirement Sundar Iyer must satisfy at all times?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 5 lakh  C) Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 lakh**\n\nAn **individual** investment adviser (and a partnership firm) must hold **net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh**. His Rs 6,80,000 satisfies this.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the pre-2020 figure for an individual IA and is the current requirement for an individual research analyst.\n- **C) Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh was the pre-2020 requirement for a non-individual IA.\n- **D) Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the **net worth** requirement for a **non-individual** investment adviser.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q112.** What must he do when his client count crosses 150?\nA) Nothing; the 150-client limit applies only to non-individual advisers  B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted  C) Appoint a compliance officer and continue onboarding clients as an individual  D) Transfer the excess clients to another registered adviser within 30 days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser and stop onboarding fresh clients as an individual until that registration is granted**\n\nThe corporatisation threshold under the IA Regulations is **150 clients**. An individual IA who exceeds it must apply for registration as a **non-individual** IA and must **stop onboarding fresh clients** as an individual in the meantime.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the threshold applies specifically to **individual** advisers; non-individuals face no such cap.\n- **C)** - wrong; appointing a compliance officer is an obligation of a **non-individual** IA and does not cure the breach of the 150-client threshold.\n- **D)** - wrong; the regulations require corporatisation, not the transfer or shedding of existing clients.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q113.** What is the maximum annual fee he may charge Dr Anitha's family under the assets-under-advice mode?\nA) Rs 68,000  B) Rs 85,000  C) Rs 1,25,000  D) Rs 1,70,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 85,000**\n\nThe AUA-mode cap is **2.5% of assets under advice per annum, per family of client**. 2.5% × 34,00,000 = **Rs 85,000**. Note that the caps are ceilings, and the Code of Conduct separately requires fees to be fair and reasonable.\n\n- **A) Rs 68,000** - wrong; this applies 2% rather than the prescribed 2.5% cap.\n- **C) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; Rs 1,25,000 is the cap under the **fixed fee** mode. Only one mode may apply to a client at a time, and this question asks for the AUA-mode maximum.\n- **D) Rs 1,70,000** - wrong; this applies 5% of AUA, double the permitted cap.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q114.** How should he handle the gap between Dr Anitha's willingness and her ability to take risk?\nA) Follow her willingness, since a signed questionnaire records her informed consent  B) Average the two scores and advise a moderate allocation  C) Advise within her ability to take risk, since the lower of willingness and ability governs, and record the reasoning  D) Decline to advise her until the education loan is fully repaid\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Advise within her ability to take risk, since the lower of willingness and ability governs, and record the reasoning**\n\nRegulation 16 requires assessment of the risk the client is **willing and able** to take, including capacity to absorb loss. Regulation 17 then requires the advice to be appropriate to that profile. Where the two diverge, the **lower** — here her limited ability, given the loan and thin liquidity — governs.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; consent does not override the suitability obligation, and Regulation 16 expressly forbids attributing inappropriate weight to particular questionnaire answers.\n- **B)** - wrong; averaging is not a recognised method and would still expose her to risk beyond her capacity to absorb loss.\n- **D)** - wrong; nothing prevents advising a client with a loan; the adviser simply calibrates the recommendation to her capacity.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q115.** May his spouse distribute mutual funds to Dr Anitha and earn commission?\nA) Yes, provided the commission is disclosed in the client agreement  B) Yes, provided the spouse's business is a separately identifiable department  C) Yes, provided Dr Anitha gives written consent  D) No; the family of an individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: D) No; the family of an individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**\n\nRegulation 22 is absolute for an individual IA: he shall not provide distribution services at all, and **his family shall not provide distribution services to his advisory clients**. The one-line rule is that an individual adviser may never distribute, while a corporate adviser may distribute but never to its own advisory clients.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; disclosure does not cure the prohibition. Regulation 15(2) also bars any consideration from a person other than the client.\n- **B)** - wrong; the separately identifiable department route is available only to **non-individual** advisers, not to an individual adviser's family.\n- **C)** - wrong; client consent cannot waive a regulatory prohibition.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 6: Dr Anitha's portfolio after a change in circumstances\n\nDr Anitha's Investment Policy Statement sets a strategic asset allocation of **70% equity, 25% debt and 5% gold**, with a **tolerance band of ±5 percentage points** around each target. At the start of the review year her portfolio was worth **Rs 12,00,000**, invested exactly at target: equity Rs 8,40,000, debt Rs 3,00,000 and gold Rs 60,000.\n\nOver the year **equity returned +26%, debt +7% and gold +14%**.\n\nAt the review meeting she tells her adviser that she has accepted a **two-year super-specialty fellowship** starting next month, during which her income will fall by more than half. Her adviser therefore rewrites the IPS to a permanent allocation of **50% equity, 40% debt and 10% gold**.\n\n**Q116.** What was the portfolio's return for the year?\nA) 15.67%  B) 20.65%  C) 23.15%  D) 26.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 20.65%**\n\nPortfolio return is the weighted average of component returns: (0.70 × 26%) + (0.25 × 7%) + (0.05 × 14%) = 18.20% + 1.75% + 0.70% = **20.65%**. Cross-check in rupees: the portfolio ends at Rs 14,47,800, a gain of Rs 2,47,800 on Rs 12,00,000 = 20.65% ✓\n\n- **A) 15.67%** - wrong; this is the simple average of the three asset returns (26 + 7 + 14) ÷ 3, ignoring the weights.\n- **C) 23.15%** - wrong; it matches no weighting of the given returns.\n- **D) 26.00%** - wrong; this is the equity return alone, not the portfolio return.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q117.** What is the equity weight at the end of the year, before any rebalancing?\nA) 70.00%  B) 71.15%  C) 73.10%  D) 88.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 73.10%**\n\nEnd values: equity 8,40,000 × 1.26 = **Rs 10,58,400**; debt 3,00,000 × 1.07 = **Rs 3,21,000**; gold 60,000 × 1.14 = **Rs 68,400**. Total = **Rs 14,47,800**. Equity weight = 10,58,400 ÷ 14,47,800 = **73.10%**.\n\n- **A) 70.00%** - wrong; that is the target weight, which drift has already moved away from.\n- **B) 71.15%** - wrong; it matches no computation on these figures.\n- **D) 88.20%** - wrong; this divides the year-end equity value by the *opening* corpus of Rs 12,00,000 instead of by the year-end total.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q118.** Under a pure ±5 percentage point threshold rule, and before the fellowship is taken into account, what action is required on the equity holding?\nA) None; the drift of 3.10 percentage points is inside the band  B) Sell Rs 2,20,800 of equity, because a threshold rule is checked annually  C) Sell equity down to 65%, the lower edge of the band  D) Buy equity, because the weight has fallen below target\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) None; the drift of 3.10 percentage points is inside the band**\n\nDrift = 73.10% − 70.00% = **3.10 percentage points**, inside the ±5 point band, so a pure threshold rule takes no action. A calendar rule, by contrast, would trade on the review date regardless of the size of the drift — read the question carefully to see which rule applies.\n\n- **B)** - wrong; this is the trade a **calendar** rule would require. A threshold rule asks whether the band has been breached, not whether it is the review date.\n- **C)** - wrong; threshold rebalancing restores the **target** weight, not the edge of the band, and in any case no breach has occurred.\n- **D)** - wrong; the equity weight has risen above target, not fallen below it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q119.** How much equity must be sold to move the portfolio to the new 50:40:10 allocation?\nA) Rs 2,58,120  B) Rs 3,34,500  C) Rs 3,58,400  D) Rs 4,58,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 3,34,500**\n\nNew equity target = 50% × 14,47,800 = **Rs 7,23,900**. Equity to sell = 10,58,400 − 7,23,900 = **Rs 3,34,500**. The proceeds fund debt (5,79,120 − 3,21,000 = Rs 2,58,120) and gold (1,44,780 − 68,400 = Rs 76,380), which sum back to Rs 3,34,500 ✓\n\n- **A) Rs 2,58,120** - wrong; this is the amount that must be **added to debt**, only part of the equity sale.\n- **C) Rs 3,58,400** - wrong; it matches no step in the calculation.\n- **D) Rs 4,58,400** - wrong; this applies the 50% target to the **opening** corpus (10,58,400 − 6,00,000) rather than to the year-end total.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q120.** How should the move from 70:25:5 to 50:40:10 be classified?\nA) Tactical asset allocation, because it is triggered by strong equity returns  B) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement, because it moves equity outside the ±5 point band  C) A change in strategic asset allocation, because the client's circumstances have changed, requiring the IPS to be rewritten  D) Constant proportion portfolio insurance, because equity is reduced as risk rises\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) A change in strategic asset allocation, because the client's circumstances have changed, requiring the IPS to be rewritten**\n\nStrategic asset allocation is about the **client**; tactical asset allocation is about the **market**. A permanent fall in income and a two-year period of reduced earning capacity are life events, so the policy portfolio itself must change and the IPS must be rewritten.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; TAA is temporary, market-driven and must stay **inside** the IPS bands. This change is permanent and driven by the client's life.\n- **B)** - wrong; a formal amendment of the IPS is not a breach of it. A breach is a deviation outside the bands **without** rewriting the policy.\n- **D)** - wrong; CPPI reduces equity as assets approach a floor using the formula equity = m × (assets − floor). Nothing here is driven by a cushion calculation.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section C — Caselets (3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 7: Dr Anitha's clinic goal and her education loan\n\nDr Anitha, now 29, wants to set up her own diagnostic clinic in **8 years**. The equipment and fit-out would cost **Rs 45,00,000 today**, and costs of this kind are inflating at **8% a year**.\n\nShe has already earmarked **Rs 6,00,000** in a balanced fund that is expected to return **10% a year**. Any fresh monthly investment will go into a portfolio expected to return **12% a year, compounded monthly**, with contributions made at the **end** of each month.\n\nHer **education loan** has an outstanding balance of **Rs 18,00,000** at **9.5% a year** with **84 months** remaining.\n\nHer gross monthly income is **Rs 2,15,000**. Her lender will sanction a further equipment loan only if her **total EMIs stay within 45% of gross monthly income**. Any new equipment loan would be at **11% a year for 60 months**.\n\n**Q121.** What shortfall must she fund from fresh savings for the clinic goal?\nA) Rs 39,00,000  B) Rs 70,43,033  C) Rs 77,29,186  D) Rs 83,29,186\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 70,43,033**\n\nStep 1 — inflate the goal: 45,00,000 × (1.08)^8 = 45,00,000 × 1.85093 = **Rs 83,29,186**.\nStep 2 — grow what is already earmarked: 6,00,000 × (1.10)^8 = 6,00,000 × 2.14359 = **Rs 12,86,153**.\nStep 3 — the gap: 83,29,186 − 12,86,153 = **Rs 70,43,033**.\n\n- **A) Rs 39,00,000** - wrong; this subtracts today's earmarked Rs 6,00,000 from today's cost of Rs 45,00,000, inflating neither figure.\n- **C) Rs 77,29,186** - wrong; this deducts the earmarked Rs 6,00,000 at its **present** value from the inflated goal, forgetting that it grows at 10% for 8 years.\n- **D) Rs 83,29,186** - wrong; this is the inflated cost of the goal before crediting the existing earmarked fund.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q122.** What monthly SIP must she start today to fund that shortfall?\nA) Rs 44,039  B) Rs 47,718  C) Rs 52,081  D) Rs 73,365\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 44,039**\n\nConvert the period first: r = 12% ÷ 12 = **0.01**, n = 8 × 12 = **96**. (1.01)^96 = **2.59927**.\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1+r)^n − 1] = 70,43,033 × 0.01 ÷ (2.59927 − 1) = 70,430.33 ÷ 1.59927 = **Rs 44,039 a month**.\n\n- **B) Rs 47,718** - wrong; this solves an **annual** annuity for the gap at 12% and then divides by 12, which mismatches the compounding period.\n- **C) Rs 52,081** - wrong; this annuitises the full inflated goal of Rs 83,29,186 instead of the gap, ignoring the earmarked fund altogether.\n- **D) Rs 73,365** - wrong; this simply divides the gap by 96 months, assuming the investment earns nothing.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q123.** Of the Rs 70,43,033 shortfall, how much will be supplied by investment returns rather than by her own contributions?\nA) Rs 12,86,153  B) Rs 28,15,289  C) Rs 42,27,744  D) Rs 70,43,033\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 28,15,289**\n\nTotal contributed over the 8 years = 44,039 × 96 = **Rs 42,27,744**. The corpus reaches Rs 70,43,033, so returns supply 70,43,033 − 42,27,744 = **Rs 28,15,289** — about 40% of the shortfall.\n\n- **A) Rs 12,86,153** - wrong; this is the grown value of the separately earmarked balanced fund, which is outside the SIP entirely.\n- **C) Rs 42,27,744** - wrong; this is the total she contributes, which is the complement of what was asked.\n- **D) Rs 70,43,033** - wrong; this is the whole shortfall, of which contributions are the larger part.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q124.** How much interest will she pay over the remaining 84 months of the education loan?\nA) Rs 29,419  B) Rs 6,71,196  C) Rs 11,97,000  D) Rs 24,71,196\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 6,71,196**\n\nStep 1 — the EMI: r = 9.5% ÷ 12 = 0.00791667, n = 84, (1.00791667)^84 = 1.93941.\nEMI = 18,00,000 × (0.00791667 × 1.93941) ÷ (1.93941 − 1) = **Rs 29,419**.\nStep 2 — total interest = (EMI × n) − P = (29,419 × 84) − 18,00,000 = 24,71,196 − 18,00,000 = **Rs 6,71,196**.\n\n- **A) Rs 29,419** - wrong; this is the monthly EMI, not the interest over the remaining term.\n- **C) Rs 11,97,000** - wrong; this applies simple interest (18,00,000 × 9.5% × 7 years), ignoring that the principal amortises down each month.\n- **D) Rs 24,71,196** - wrong; this is the **total repaid**, which includes the Rs 18,00,000 of principal.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q125.** Given the lender's 45% ceiling, what is the largest additional equipment loan she can service?\nA) Rs 30,96,757  B) Rs 40,39,860  C) Rs 44,49,826  D) Rs 67,331\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 30,96,757**\n\nStep 1 — total EMI capacity = 45% × 2,15,000 = **Rs 96,750**.\nStep 2 — headroom after the existing EMI = 96,750 − 29,419 = **Rs 67,331 a month**.\nStep 3 — capitalise that at 11% for 60 months: r = 0.00916667, (1.00916667)^60 = 1.72892, annuity factor = [1 − (1.72892)^−1] ÷ 0.00916667 = **45.9930**.\nMaximum loan = 67,331 × 45.9930 = **Rs 30,96,757**.\n\n- **B) Rs 40,39,860** - wrong; this is 67,331 × 60, treating the loan as interest-free.\n- **C) Rs 44,49,826** - wrong; this capitalises the **full** Rs 96,750 capacity, forgetting that Rs 29,419 of it is already committed to the education loan.\n- **D) Rs 67,331** - wrong; this is the monthly EMI headroom, not the loan principal it supports.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 8: Ranking three funds for Dr Anitha\n\nDr Anitha's adviser compares three funds over the same three-year period. The **risk-free rate is 6.5%**, and the benchmark, the Nifty 500 TRI, returned **12.0%** with a standard deviation of **15.0%**.\n\n| Fund | Annualised return | Standard deviation | Beta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Adyar Growth Fund | 16.4% | 22.0% | 1.30 |\n| Besant Balanced Fund | 11.8% | 12.0% | 0.70 |\n| Cooum Value Fund | 14.0% | 17.5% | 1.05 |\n\nThe adviser also models a two-fund portfolio holding **60% in Adyar Growth Fund and 40% in Besant Balanced Fund**. The **correlation between the two funds' returns is 0.45**.\n\n**Q126.** Ranked by Sharpe ratio, best first, what is the correct order?\nA) Adyar, Besant, Cooum  B) Adyar, Cooum, Besant  C) Cooum, Adyar, Besant  D) Besant, Cooum, Adyar\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Adyar, Besant, Cooum**\n\nSharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp.\nAdyar: (16.4 − 6.5) ÷ 22.0 = 9.9 ÷ 22.0 = **0.4500**\nBesant: (11.8 − 6.5) ÷ 12.0 = 5.3 ÷ 12.0 = **0.4417**\nCooum: (14.0 − 6.5) ÷ 17.5 = 7.5 ÷ 17.5 = **0.4286**\nOrder: **Adyar, Besant, Cooum**.\n\n- **B) Adyar, Cooum, Besant** - wrong; Cooum's 0.4286 is below Besant's 0.4417, so the last two are reversed. This ordering follows raw return, not risk-adjusted return.\n- **C) Cooum, Adyar, Besant** - wrong; Cooum has the **lowest** Sharpe of the three, not the highest.\n- **D) Besant, Cooum, Adyar** - wrong; this is close to reverse order and would follow from dividing by return rather than by standard deviation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q127.** What is the difference between the highest and the lowest Treynor ratio among the three funds?\nA) 0.02  B) 0.05  C) 0.47  D) 4.24\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 0.47**\n\nTreynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp.\nAdyar: 9.9 ÷ 1.30 = **7.6154** (highest)\nBesant: 5.3 ÷ 0.70 = **7.5714**\nCooum: 7.5 ÷ 1.05 = **7.1429** (lowest)\nDifference = 7.6154 − 7.1429 = **0.47**.\n\n- **A) 0.02** - wrong; 0.0214 is the spread of the **Sharpe** ratios (0.4500 − 0.4286), not the Treynor ratios.\n- **B) 0.05** - wrong; this is the gap between the top two funds only (7.6154 − 7.5714), not between the highest and the lowest.\n- **D) 4.24** - wrong; this omits the risk-free rate from the numerator (16.857 − 12.615), which is not the Treynor ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q128.** What is Cooum Value Fund's Jensen's alpha?\nA) −5.10%  B) +1.73%  C) +2.00%  D) +2.75%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) +1.73%**\n\nStep 1 — CAPM required return = Rf + β(Rm − Rf) = 6.5 + 1.05 × (12.0 − 6.5) = 6.5 + 5.775 = **12.275%**.\nStep 2 — alpha = Rp − required return = 14.0 − 12.275 = **+1.725%, i.e. +1.73%**.\n\n- **A) −5.10%** - wrong; this uses the market **return** rather than the market **risk premium** in the CAPM equation (6.5 + 1.05 × 12.0 = 19.1).\n- **C) +2.00%** - wrong; this is simple excess return over the benchmark (14.0 − 12.0), which ignores the beta of 1.05. Alpha equals raw excess return only when beta is exactly 1.\n- **D) +2.75%** - wrong; this is Adyar Growth Fund's alpha (16.4 − 13.65), not Cooum's.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q129.** What is the standard deviation of the 60:40 Adyar–Besant portfolio?\nA) 14.04%  B) 15.95%  C) 16.53%  D) 18.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 15.95%**\n\nσp = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ)\n= √(0.36 × 484 + 0.16 × 144 + 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 22 × 12 × 0.45)\n= √(174.24 + 23.04 + 57.024) = √254.304 = **15.95%**.\nThe weighted average of the two standard deviations is 18.00%, so diversification saves **2.05 percentage points** of risk.\n\n- **A) 14.04%** - wrong; this drops the covariance term entirely, which is only valid when the correlation is zero.\n- **C) 16.53%** - wrong; this uses a correlation of 0.60 instead of the stated 0.45.\n- **D) 18.00%** - wrong; this is the weighted average of the two standard deviations (0.6 × 22 + 0.4 × 12). Return is a weighted average; risk is not.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q130.** What is the Sharpe ratio of that 60:40 portfolio?\nA) 0.45  B) 0.51  C) 0.57  D) 0.91\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 0.51**\n\nStep 1 — portfolio return = (0.60 × 16.4) + (0.40 × 11.8) = 9.84 + 4.72 = **14.56%**.\nStep 2 — Sharpe = (14.56 − 6.5) ÷ 15.95 = 8.06 ÷ 15.9469 = **0.51**. Blending the two funds beats either one alone (0.4500 and 0.4417) because the correlation of 0.45 removes risk without removing return.\n\n- **A) 0.45** - wrong; this uses the weighted-average standard deviation of 18.00% in the denominator (8.06 ÷ 18.00), ignoring the diversification benefit.\n- **C) 0.57** - wrong; this uses the zero-correlation standard deviation of 14.04% (8.06 ÷ 14.045).\n- **D) 0.91** - wrong; this omits the risk-free rate from the numerator (14.56 ÷ 15.95).\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 9: Dr Anitha's fixed income and derivatives sleeve\n\nDr Anitha holds **500 bonds** of Chennai Metro Infra Ltd. Each has a **face value of Rs 1,000**, pays a **9% annual coupon** and has **5 years** to maturity. The market's required yield on the bond is **11%**. Its **modified duration is 4.10**.\n\nHer adviser expects yields to rise by **60 basis points** over the next quarter. To offset that, she **sells one Nifty futures contract at 24,950** (lot size **25**) when the index spot is **24,800**.\n\nSeparately, on the same lot size of 25, she builds a collar on the index: she **buys one Nifty 24,800 put at a premium of Rs 265** and **sells one Nifty 25,200 call at a premium of Rs 118**. At expiry the Nifty settles at **24,320**.\n\n**Q131.** What is the fair price of one Chennai Metro Infra bond?\nA) Rs 926.08  B) Rs 962.09  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,077.79\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 926.08**\n\nA bond is an annuity of coupons plus a lump sum at redemption.\n(1.11)^5 = **1.685058**, so the discount factor for year 5 is 1 ÷ 1.685058 = **0.593451**.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 0.593451) ÷ 0.11 = **3.69590**.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.69590 = **Rs 332.63**. PV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.593451 = **Rs 593.45**.\nPrice = 332.63 + 593.45 = **Rs 926.08** — a discount, because the 9% coupon is below the 11% required yield.\n\n- **B) Rs 962.09** - wrong; this discounts the same cash flows at 10% instead of the stated 11%.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; the bond would price at par only if the coupon equalled the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,077.79** - wrong; this swaps coupon and yield, pricing an 11% coupon at a 9% yield, which produces a premium instead of a discount.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q132.** What is the current yield on the bond at that price?\nA) 8.35%  B) 9.00%  C) 9.72%  D) 11.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 9.72%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ Market price = 90 ÷ 926.08 = **9.72%**. For a discount bond the ordering must be coupon (9.00%) < current yield (9.72%) < YTM (11.00%), which this satisfies.\n\n- **A) 8.35%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by the premium price of Rs 1,077.79 obtained by swapping coupon and yield.\n- **B) 9.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, computed on face value rather than on the price actually paid.\n- **D) 11.00%** - wrong; this is the yield to maturity, which additionally counts the Rs 73.92 capital gain at redemption.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q133.** If yields rise by 60 basis points, what is the approximate value of her 500-bond holding?\nA) Rs 3,49,132  B) Rs 4,51,649  C) Rs 4,63,040  D) Rs 4,74,431\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 4,51,649**\n\nStep 1 — market value today = 500 × 926.08 = **Rs 4,63,040**.\nStep 2 — %ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −4.10 × 0.0060 = **−2.46%**, a loss of 4,63,040 × 0.0246 = **Rs 11,391**.\nStep 3 — new value = 4,63,040 − 11,391 = **Rs 4,51,649**.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,49,132** - wrong; this treats the 60 basis points as 6%, giving a 24.6% fall.\n- **C) Rs 4,63,040** - wrong; this is the value before the yield rise.\n- **D) Rs 4,74,431** - wrong; this adds 2.46% instead of subtracting it. Price and yield move in opposite directions.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q134.** Taking the bond loss and the futures position together, what is the net result on the sleeve?\nA) Rs 4,359 net gain  B) Rs 11,391 net loss  C) Rs 15,750 net gain  D) Rs 27,141 net gain\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 4,359 net gain**\n\nStep 1 — the short futures position gains when the index falls: (24,950 − 24,320) × 25 = 630 × 25 = **Rs 15,750**.\nStep 2 — net the bond loss against it: 15,750 − 11,391 = **Rs 4,359 net gain**. The hedge more than offset the interest-rate loss on this occasion, which is basis risk working in her favour rather than a design feature.\n\n- **B) Rs 11,391 net loss** - wrong; this counts only the bond loss and ignores the futures gain entirely.\n- **C) Rs 15,750 net gain** - wrong; this counts only the futures gain and ignores the bond loss.\n- **D) Rs 27,141 net gain** - wrong; this **adds** the two figures instead of netting the loss against the gain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q135.** What is her net profit on the collar at expiry?\nA) Rs 3,675  B) Rs 8,325  C) Rs 12,000  D) Rs 14,950\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 8,325**\n\nStep 1 — net premium paid = (265 − 118) × 25 = 147 × 25 = **Rs 3,675 outflow**.\nStep 2 — at a settlement of 24,320 the put's intrinsic value is 24,800 − 24,320 = 480, worth 480 × 25 = **Rs 12,000**; the 25,200 call expires worthless, so the short call keeps its premium, already counted in step 1.\nNet profit = 12,000 − 3,675 = **Rs 8,325**.\n\n- **A) Rs 3,675** - wrong; this is the net premium outlay, not the profit.\n- **C) Rs 12,000** - wrong; this is the put's gross payoff before recovering the net premium paid.\n- **D) Rs 14,950** - wrong; this adds the call premium received to the put payoff while forgetting the Rs 6,625 put premium paid.\n\n</details>\n"}, {"num": "05", "md": "# Mock Paper 5 — NISM Series X-A (Investment Adviser Level 1)\n\n**Total: 150 marks · 135 questions · Time: 3 hours · Pass: 90/150 (60%) · Negative marking: 25% of the marks for a wrong answer.**\n\n## Section A — Independent MCQs (90 questions × 1 mark = 90 marks)\n\n**Q1.** Meenakshi Deshpande, a 45-year-old schoolteacher in Pune who was recently widowed, lists her position as: savings account Rs 3,20,000; liquid fund Rs 2,10,000; mutual funds Rs 18,50,000; EPF and gratuity received Rs 9,60,000; her flat in Kothrud Rs 62,00,000; gold jewellery Rs 6,40,000. Her liabilities are a home loan outstanding of Rs 11,40,000 and a car loan outstanding of Rs 2,60,000. What is her net worth?\nA) Rs 19,40,000  B) Rs 33,40,000  C) Rs 87,80,000  D) Rs 1,01,80,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 87,80,000**\n\nTotal assets = 3,20,000 + 2,10,000 + 18,50,000 + 9,60,000 + 62,00,000 + 6,40,000 = **Rs 1,01,80,000**. Total liabilities = 11,40,000 + 2,60,000 = **Rs 14,00,000**. Net worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 1,01,80,000 − 14,00,000 = **Rs 87,80,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 19,40,000** - wrong; this deducts the liabilities from only the financial assets (Rs 33,40,000), ignoring the flat and the gold.\n- **B) Rs 33,40,000** - wrong; this is the total of her financial assets alone, with no liabilities deducted.\n- **D) Rs 1,01,80,000** - wrong; this is total assets, not net worth. Net worth requires the liabilities to be subtracted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q2.** Meenakshi has a 20-year horizon, no dependants other than a self-supporting son, and a stable salary — giving her a high risk capacity. However, she becomes acutely anxious when her portfolio value falls and says she could not tolerate a 20% drawdown. How should her adviser set the risk level of the plan?\nA) Plan to her risk capacity, since it is objective and measurable  B) Plan to the lower of capacity and tolerance  C) Plan to the average of capacity and tolerance  D) Plan to her risk requirement alone, since the goal return is what matters\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Plan to the lower of capacity and tolerance**\n\nRisk **capacity** is the objective *ability* to bear risk (horizon, income stability, net worth, dependants); risk **tolerance** is the subjective *willingness*. Where the two conflict, the plan must be built to the **lower** of the two. A client with high capacity but low tolerance will panic-sell in a drawdown, converting a temporary paper loss into a permanent one.\n\n- **A) Plan to her risk capacity** - wrong; being objectively able to bear a loss does not mean the client will hold through it. Ignoring tolerance invites panic-selling.\n- **C) Plan to the average** - wrong; averaging is not the prescribed rule and still leaves the portfolio above the client's psychological limit.\n- **D) Plan to her risk requirement alone** - wrong; the return the goal needs is a third input, but it can never override the constraint set by capacity and tolerance.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is the correct sequence of the six steps of the financial planning process?\nA) Data gathering → Relationship → Goals → Risk profiling → Review → Portfolio construction  B) Relationship → Data gathering → Goals → Risk profiling → Portfolio construction → Review  C) Goals → Relationship → Risk profiling → Data gathering → Portfolio construction → Review  D) Relationship → Goals → Data gathering → Portfolio construction → Risk profiling → Review\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Relationship → Data gathering → Goals → Risk profiling → Portfolio construction → Review**\n\nThe prescribed order is: (1) establish the client–planner relationship, (2) gather data on the client's financial situation, (3) define needs and financial goals, (4) risk profiling and asset allocation, (5) portfolio construction, (6) reviewing and rebalancing.\n\n- **A) Data gathering first** - wrong; data cannot be gathered before the engagement, scope and compensation are agreed in writing, and review always comes last.\n- **C) Goals first** - wrong; goals cannot be defined before the relationship is established or before data is collected.\n- **D) Goals before data gathering** - wrong; goals are defined *after* data gathering, and risk profiling must precede portfolio construction, not follow it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q4.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser is described as owing a *fiduciary* duty to clients. What does this mean in practice?\nA) The adviser must guarantee that recommended products will not lose money  B) The adviser must place the client's interest ahead of the adviser's own  C) The adviser must only recommend products approved by the manufacturer  D) The adviser must ensure recommendations are merely suitable, without regard to conflicts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The adviser must place the client's interest ahead of the adviser's own**\n\nA fiduciary standard requires the adviser to act in the client's best interest, disclose conflicts, and put the client first. It is the defining obligation of a registered IA and underpins SEBI's requirement that advice be segregated from distribution.\n\n- **A) Guarantee against loss** - wrong; no adviser can guarantee investment outcomes, and promising assured returns is itself prohibited.\n- **C) Only manufacturer-approved products** - wrong; that describes a distribution arrangement, which is the very conflict the fiduciary standard is designed to neutralise.\n- **D) Merely suitable** - wrong; suitability is the *lower* standard applied to some distribution models. Fiduciary duty is stricter than suitability.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q5.** Which of the following statements best describes a well-framed financial goal?\nA) \"Build a large corpus for my son's post-graduate education\"  B) \"Invest Rs 25,000 a month in an equity fund with a good track record\"  C) \"Accumulate Rs 46 lakh by June 2034 for a two-year post-graduate course\"  D) \"Earn at least 14% a year on my investments for the next ten years\"\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) \"Accumulate Rs 46 lakh by June 2034 for a two-year post-graduate course\"**\n\nA good goal is **specific, quantified in future (inflation-adjusted) rupees, and dated**, so it can be prioritised and funded. Option C states the purpose, the future cost and the date.\n\n- **A) \"A large corpus\"** - wrong; it names a purpose but has no amount and no date, so nothing can be computed from it.\n- **B) \"Invest Rs 25,000 a month...\"** - wrong; this is a *means* (a product decision), not a goal. It does not say what is being funded or when.\n- **D) \"Earn at least 14% a year\"** - wrong; a return target is not a life goal, and it is not within the investor's control.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q6.** Meenakshi invests a lump sum of Rs 18,00,000 from her late husband's provident fund settlement in an instrument compounding at 9.5% per annum. What will it be worth after 7 years?\nA) Rs 29,97,000  B) Rs 31,02,825  C) Rs 33,97,593  D) Rs 37,20,364\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 33,97,593**\n\nFV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ = 18,00,000 × (1.095)⁷. (1.095)⁷ = 1.887552, so FV = 18,00,000 × 1.887552 = **Rs 33,97,593**.\n\n- **A) Rs 29,97,000** - wrong; this is simple interest, 18,00,000 × (1 + 0.095 × 7). It ignores compounding.\n- **B) Rs 31,02,825** - wrong; this compounds for only 6 years, one period short.\n- **D) Rs 37,20,364** - wrong; this compounds for 8 years, one period too many.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q7.** Meenakshi wants Rs 32,00,000 available in 12 years to buy a smaller retirement flat. If she can earn 8.5% per annum compounded annually, what single amount must she set aside today?\nA) Rs 12,02,245  B) Rs 13,04,436  C) Rs 15,84,158  D) Rs 18,66,667\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 12,02,245**\n\nPV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ = 32,00,000 ÷ (1.085)¹². (1.085)¹² = 2.661901, so PV = 32,00,000 ÷ 2.661901 = **Rs 12,02,245**.\n\n- **B) Rs 13,04,436** - wrong; this discounts for only 11 years.\n- **C) Rs 15,84,158** - wrong; this uses simple interest in the denominator, 32,00,000 ÷ (1 + 0.085 × 12).\n- **D) Rs 18,66,667** - wrong; this is an arbitrary proportional reduction with no basis in the discounting formula.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q8.** A holding of Meenakshi's grew from Rs 6,50,000 to Rs 15,60,000 over exactly 9 years. What is the compound annual growth rate?\nA) 10.22%  B) 12.45%  C) 15.56%  D) 24.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 10.22%**\n\nCAGR = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 = (15,60,000 ÷ 6,50,000)^(1/9) − 1 = (2.4)^0.1111 − 1 = 1.10216 − 1 = **10.22% p.a.**\n\n- **B) 12.45%** - wrong; it does not follow from any correct rearrangement of FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ at these inputs.\n- **C) 15.56%** - wrong; this is simple-interest thinking — the total gain of 140% divided by 9 years.\n- **D) 24.00%** - wrong; this grossly overstates the compounded rate and ignores that returns compound on a rising base.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q9.** Meenakshi starts a monthly SIP of Rs 14,500 at the end of every month for 13 years, expecting 10.5% per annum compounded monthly. What corpus will she accumulate?\nA) Rs 22,62,000  B) Rs 36,75,994  C) Rs 47,93,357  D) Rs 48,35,299\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 47,93,357**\n\nr = 0.105 ÷ 12 = 0.00875; n = 13 × 12 = 156; (1.00875)¹⁵⁶ = 3.892543.\nFV = 14,500 × [(3.892543 − 1) ÷ 0.00875] = 14,500 × 330.5763 = **Rs 47,93,357**.\n\n- **A) Rs 22,62,000** - wrong; this is merely the total contributed (14,500 × 156), ignoring all returns.\n- **B) Rs 36,75,994** - wrong; this arises from a period mismatch between the rate and the number of periods.\n- **D) Rs 48,35,299** - wrong; this is the annuity **due** value (× 1.00875). The question specifies payment at the *end* of each month.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q10.** A bank quotes a nominal rate of 8.4% per annum on a deposit, compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?\nA) 8.40%  B) 8.58%  C) 8.67%  D) 8.73%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 8.67%**\n\nEAR = (1 + r/m)^m − 1 = (1 + 0.084/4)⁴ − 1 = (1.021)⁴ − 1 = 1.086683 − 1 = **8.67%**.\n\n- **A) 8.40%** - wrong; this is the nominal (quoted) rate. The nominal rate never changes with frequency; only the effective rate does.\n- **B) 8.58%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **half-yearly** compounding, (1.042)² − 1.\n- **D) 8.73%** - wrong; this is the EAR for **monthly** compounding, (1.007)¹² − 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q11.** Meenakshi's debt portfolio is expected to return 11.25% per annum while inflation runs at 5.75%. What is her expected real return?\nA) 5.00%  B) 5.20%  C) 5.50%  D) 6.05%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 5.20%**\n\nReal return = [(1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation)] − 1 = (1.1125 ÷ 1.0575) − 1 = 1.052009 − 1 = **5.20%**.\n\n- **A) 5.00%** - wrong; a rounded guess that does not follow from the formula.\n- **C) 5.50%** - wrong; this is the crude subtraction 11.25 − 5.75, which always **overstates** the real return.\n- **D) 6.05%** - wrong; this multiplies rather than divides, which is the route from real to nominal, not the reverse.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q12.** A charitable trust that Meenakshi's late husband endowed is expected to pay Rs 3,60,000 at the end of next year, growing at 4% a year in perpetuity. At a discount rate of 9%, what is the present value of the stream?\nA) Rs 27,69,231  B) Rs 40,00,000  C) Rs 72,00,000  D) Rs 90,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 72,00,000**\n\nGrowing perpetuity: PV = PMT₁ ÷ (r − g) = 3,60,000 ÷ (0.09 − 0.04) = 3,60,000 ÷ 0.05 = **Rs 72,00,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 27,69,231** - wrong; this **adds** g to r (3,60,000 ÷ 0.13) instead of subtracting it.\n- **B) Rs 40,00,000** - wrong; this is a level perpetuity, 3,60,000 ÷ 0.09, ignoring the 4% growth.\n- **D) Rs 90,00,000** - wrong; it does not follow from the growing perpetuity formula at these inputs.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q13.** Meenakshi's monthly household inflow is Rs 1,24,000 and her total monthly outflow is Rs 86,180. What is her savings ratio?\nA) 22.85%  B) 30.50%  C) 43.89%  D) 69.50%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 30.50%**\n\nSavings = 1,24,000 − 86,180 = Rs 37,820. Savings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 37,820 ÷ 1,24,000 = **30.50%**. Self-check: savings ratio + expenses ratio = 30.50 + 69.50 = 100 ✓\n\n- **A) 22.85%** - wrong; not derivable from these figures by any correct route.\n- **C) 43.89%** - wrong; this divides savings by **expenses** (37,820 ÷ 86,180) rather than by income.\n- **D) 69.50%** - wrong; this is the **expenses** ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q14.** Meenakshi holds Rs 5,30,000 across her savings account and a liquid fund. Her household expenses total Rs 10,34,400 a year. What is her liquidity ratio?\nA) 0.51  B) 1.95 months  C) 6.15 months  D) 12.30 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 6.15 months**\n\nLiquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses. Monthly expenses = 10,34,400 ÷ 12 = Rs 86,200. Ratio = 5,30,000 ÷ 86,200 = **6.15 months**, just at the top of the healthy 3–6 month band.\n\n- **A) 0.51** - wrong; this divides by **annual** expenses. The liquidity ratio must always read in months.\n- **B) 1.95 months** - wrong; not derivable from these figures.\n- **D) 12.30 months** - wrong; this is double the correct figure and would require liquid assets of Rs 10,60,000.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q15.** Ashwini Kulkarni's personal balance sheet shows total assets of Rs 76,50,000 and total liabilities of Rs 22,95,000. What is her leverage ratio?\nA) 0.30  B) 0.43  C) 0.70  D) 3.33\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 0.30**\n\nLeverage ratio = Total liabilities ÷ Total assets = 22,95,000 ÷ 76,50,000 = **0.30**. Self-check: solvency ratio (0.70) + leverage ratio (0.30) = 1 ✓\n\n- **B) 0.43** - wrong; this divides liabilities by **net worth** (22,95,000 ÷ 53,55,000), which is not how leverage is defined here.\n- **C) 0.70** - wrong; this is the **solvency** ratio, net worth ÷ total assets.\n- **D) 3.33** - wrong; this inverts the ratio, dividing assets by liabilities.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q16.** Meenakshi's adviser recommends she build a contingency fund. Which statement about the contingency fund is correct?\nA) It should be sized at 3–6 months of gross income  B) It should be sized at 3–6 months of expenses and held in liquid, capital-stable instruments  C) It should be invested in equity mutual funds so that it grows faster than inflation  D) It is unnecessary once adequate health and life insurance are in place\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It should be sized at 3–6 months of expenses and held in liquid, capital-stable instruments**\n\nThe fund is sized against **expenses** — what must actually be paid — and demands **immediate availability plus certainty of value**: savings balance, sweep-in FD or liquid/overnight funds. Return is secondary.\n\n- **A) 3–6 months of gross income** - wrong; income is not the benchmark. A high earner with modest expenses would end up holding far more idle cash than needed.\n- **C) Invested in equity** - wrong; equity is exactly the wrong home. Job loss and recessions tend to coincide with market falls, so the money would be needed precisely when it is down.\n- **D) Unnecessary with insurance** - wrong; insurance pays only for specified insured events and only after a claim process. The fund covers income gaps and unplanned costs immediately.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q17.** Meenakshi's monthly budget balances neatly in most months, yet she runs a deficit every March and every October. The most likely cause is that her budget has failed to provide for:\nA) Fixed outflows such as EMIs and rent  B) Variable outflows such as groceries and fuel  C) Periodic outflows such as annual insurance premiums and festival spending  D) Savings outflows such as SIPs and recurring deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Periodic outflows such as annual insurance premiums and festival spending**\n\n**Periodic (lumpy) outflows** are the most commonly forgotten category — predictable but infrequent, so they never appear in a typical month and then break the budget when they fall due. The fix is to divide each annual item by 12 and set that amount aside monthly.\n\n- **A) Fixed outflows** - wrong; EMIs and rent recur every month and are the easiest items to capture in a monthly budget.\n- **B) Variable outflows** - wrong; these recur monthly too. They can cause general overspending but not a deficit confined to two specific months.\n- **D) Savings outflows** - wrong; omitting SIPs from the budget would show up as an apparent *surplus*, not a recurring deficit.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q18.** Meenakshi refinances the outstanding home loan on her flat — Rs 22,00,000 at 8.75% per annum for 12 years. What is the EMI?\nA) Rs 15,278  B) Rs 16,042  C) Rs 24,728  D) Rs 27,315\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 24,728**\n\nr = 0.0875 ÷ 12 = 0.00729167; n = 12 × 12 = 144; (1.00729167)¹⁴⁴ = 2.846785.\nEMI = P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] = 22,00,000 × (0.00729167 × 2.846785) ÷ 1.846785 = **Rs 24,728**.\n\n- **A) Rs 15,278** - wrong; this is simply principal ÷ number of months (22,00,000 ÷ 144), which ignores interest entirely.\n- **B) Rs 16,042** - wrong; this is the **first month's interest** (22,00,000 × 0.00729167), a standard distractor.\n- **D) Rs 27,315** - wrong; it overstates the EMI and does not follow from the formula at these inputs.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q19.** For the loan in the previous question — Rs 22,00,000 at 8.75% for 12 years, EMI Rs 24,728 — how much of the **first** EMI goes towards repaying principal?\nA) Rs 8,686  B) Rs 15,278  C) Rs 16,042  D) Rs 24,728\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 8,686**\n\nInterest for month 1 = Outstanding × monthly rate = 22,00,000 × 0.00729167 = Rs 16,042. Principal for month 1 = EMI − Interest = 24,728 − 16,042 = **Rs 8,686**. Only about 35% of the first instalment actually reduces the debt — which is why early prepayment saves so much.\n\n- **B) Rs 15,278** - wrong; this is principal ÷ 144, a straight-line split that an amortising loan never follows.\n- **C) Rs 16,042** - wrong; this is the **interest** component of the first EMI, not the principal component.\n- **D) Rs 24,728** - wrong; this is the whole EMI, which still contains the interest portion.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q20.** For the same loan — Rs 22,00,000 at 8.75% for 12 years, EMI Rs 24,728 — what is the total interest paid over the full tenure?\nA) Rs 11,55,000  B) Rs 13,60,832  C) Rs 22,00,000  D) Rs 35,60,832\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 13,60,832**\n\nTotal interest = (EMI × n) − P = (24,728 × 144) − 22,00,000 = 35,60,832 − 22,00,000 = **Rs 13,60,832**.\n\n- **A) Rs 11,55,000** - wrong; this is simple interest on the full principal and understates the actual outgo.\n- **C) Rs 22,00,000** - wrong; this is the principal itself, not the interest.\n- **D) Rs 35,60,832** - wrong; this is the **total amount repaid**, which includes the principal. The question asks only for interest.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q21.** Meenakshi has three loans: a home loan on her Kothrud flat, a car loan, and a gold loan taken against her jewellery. Which describes the security arrangements correctly?\nA) Home loan — pledge; car loan — mortgage; gold loan — hypothecation  B) Home loan — mortgage; car loan — hypothecation; gold loan — pledge  C) Home loan — hypothecation; car loan — pledge; gold loan — mortgage  D) Home loan — mortgage; car loan — pledge; gold loan — hypothecation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Home loan — mortgage; car loan — hypothecation; gold loan — pledge**\n\n**Mortgage** = security over **immovable** property (the flat). **Hypothecation** = security over **movable** property where **possession stays with the borrower** (she drives the car). **Pledge** = security over **movable** property where **possession passes to the lender** (the bank holds the gold).\n\n- **A) Pledge / mortgage / hypothecation** - wrong; it reverses all three. Immovable property can never be pledged or hypothecated.\n- **C) Hypothecation / pledge / mortgage** - wrong; a flat cannot be hypothecated, and gold held by the lender is a pledge, not a mortgage.\n- **D) Mortgage / pledge / hypothecation** - wrong; the home loan is right but the car and the gold are swapped. Possession is the deciding test.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q22.** Meenakshi owes Rs 1,80,000 on a credit card at 42% annualised, Rs 4,50,000 on a personal loan at 15%, and Rs 9,00,000 on a car loan at 10%. Her adviser recommends the **avalanche** method. Which debt does she attack first with her surplus?\nA) The car loan, because it is the largest balance  B) The credit card, because it carries the highest interest rate  C) The credit card, because it has the smallest balance  D) The personal loan, because it sits between the other two on both rate and size\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The credit card, because it carries the highest interest rate**\n\nThe **avalanche** method targets the **highest interest rate** first while paying minimums on the rest. It is mathematically optimal — it minimises total interest paid. Memory hook: **Ava**lanche → **A** for **A**PR.\n\n- **A) The car loan, largest balance** - wrong; no named strategy selects on the largest balance, and doing so leaves the costliest debt compounding at 42%.\n- **C) The credit card, smallest balance** - wrong; the card happens to be the right target, but the **reason** stated describes the **snowball** method, which selects on balance size, not rate.\n- **D) The personal loan** - wrong; at 15% it is not the costliest debt, so avalanche would not select it first.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q23.** Which of the following is **not** within SEBI's regulatory remit?\nA) Portfolio managers and Alternative Investment Funds  B) The foreign exchange market  C) Depositories and clearing corporations  D) Credit rating agencies\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The foreign exchange market**\n\nThe **foreign exchange market is regulated by the RBI**, which administers FEMA. SEBI's remit is the securities markets — exchanges, brokers, mutual funds, portfolio managers, investment advisers, AIFs, credit rating agencies and depositories.\n\n- **A) Portfolio managers and AIFs** - wrong as an answer; both are SEBI-regulated, under the Portfolio Managers Regulations, 2020 and the AIF Regulations, 2012.\n- **C) Depositories and clearing corporations** - wrong as an answer; these are Market Infrastructure Institutions regulated by SEBI.\n- **D) Credit rating agencies** - wrong as an answer; CRAs such as CRISIL and ICRA are registered with and regulated by SEBI.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q24.** Which statement about payment banks in India is correct?\nA) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend  B) They may accept deposits up to Rs 5 lakh per customer and may issue credit cards  C) They may lend, but only to priority sector borrowers  D) They are regulated by SEBI as securities market intermediaries\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) They may accept deposits up to Rs 2 lakh per customer but cannot lend**\n\nPayment banks accept deposits **up to Rs 2 lakh per customer** and provide payments and remittance services, but they **cannot lend** and cannot issue credit cards.\n\n- **B) Rs 5 lakh and credit cards** - wrong; Rs 5 lakh is the **DICGC deposit insurance** cover, an unrelated figure, and payment banks are barred from issuing credit cards.\n- **C) Priority sector lending only** - wrong; that describes the focus of **small finance banks**, which are full-service lenders.\n- **D) Regulated by SEBI** - wrong; all banks, payment banks included, are licensed and regulated by the **RBI**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q25.** In the Indian securities market, which institution acts as the central counterparty and guarantees settlement of trades?\nA) The stock exchange  B) The depository  C) The clearing corporation  D) The depository participant\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) The clearing corporation**\n\nThe clearing corporation clears and settles trades and interposes itself as the **central counterparty (CCP)**, so no buyer or seller is exposed to the other's default. It manages margins and the settlement guarantee fund.\n\n- **A) The stock exchange** - wrong; the exchange provides the trading platform and **matches** trades. It does not guarantee settlement.\n- **B) The depository** - wrong; NSDL and CDSL hold securities in **electronic (demat)** form. Custody is not a settlement guarantee.\n- **D) The depository participant** - wrong; a DP is the **agent of the depository** and the investor's access point for a demat account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q26.** Which statement correctly distinguishes the money market from the capital market?\nA) Money market instruments mature within one year and fall under the RBI; capital market instruments have longer maturities and fall under SEBI  B) Money market instruments mature within three years and fall under SEBI  C) The money market is where shares are issued; the capital market is where they are traded  D) Both markets are regulated by SEBI, with the RBI overseeing only bank deposits\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Money market instruments mature within one year and fall under the RBI; capital market instruments have longer maturities and fall under SEBI**\n\nThe dividing line is a **one-year maturity**. Money market instruments — T-bills, commercial paper, certificates of deposit, call money, repo — sit with the **RBI**. Longer-dated securities such as shares, bonds and debentures sit with **SEBI**.\n\n- **B) Three years and SEBI** - wrong on both counts: the boundary is one year and the regulator is the RBI.\n- **C) Issuance versus trading** - wrong; that is the **primary versus secondary** market distinction, not money versus capital market.\n- **D) Both under SEBI** - wrong; the RBI's remit extends far beyond bank deposits, covering the whole money market and the foreign exchange market.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q27.** Which statement about an NBFC-Account Aggregator is correct?\nA) It lends to customers on the strength of the financial data it collects  B) It shares a customer's financial data only with the customer's explicit consent, and is \"data blind\"  C) It is registered with and regulated by SEBI  D) It guarantees a minimum return on the data-linked products it recommends\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It shares a customer's financial data only with the customer's explicit consent, and is \"data blind\"**\n\nAn **NBFC-AA** is RBI-regulated. It retrieves and shares a customer's financial data across institutions strictly on **explicit consent**, and it is **data blind** — it cannot read or store the data passing through it.\n\n- **A) It lends** - wrong; an AA performs no lending or investment function whatsoever. Its role is purely consent-based data flow.\n- **C) Regulated by SEBI** - wrong; Account Aggregators are NBFCs registered with and regulated by the **RBI**.\n- **D) Guarantees a return** - wrong; an AA neither recommends nor distributes products, let alone guarantees returns.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q28.** Sameer Joglekar holds 480 shares of a company quoted at Rs 1,250 just before the record date for a **2:3 bonus issue** (two free shares for every three held). Ignoring market movement, what is the theoretical price per share after the bonus?\nA) Rs 500  B) Rs 625  C) Rs 750  D) Rs 833\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 750**\n\nValue before = 480 × 1,250 = Rs 6,00,000. Bonus shares = 480 × (2 ÷ 3) = 320, so the holding becomes 800 shares. A bonus raises **no money**, so total value is unchanged: price = 6,00,000 ÷ 800 = **Rs 750**.\n\n- **A) Rs 500** - wrong; this assumes the share count trebles to 1,440, which would be a 2:1 bonus, not 2:3.\n- **B) Rs 625** - wrong; this is the price after a **1:1** bonus, where the count doubles to 960.\n- **D) Rs 833** - wrong; this applies the ratio the wrong way round, treating the holding as rising to only 720 shares.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q29.** In a book-built public issue, an individual applying for shares worth Rs 3,20,000 falls into which category, and what is the minimum allocation prescribed for that category?\nA) Retail Individual Investor; at least 35%  B) Non-Institutional Investor; at least 15%  C) Qualified Institutional Buyer; up to 50%  D) Anchor investor; at least 15%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Non-Institutional Investor; at least 15%**\n\nThe **Rs 2 lakh** line separates retail from non-institutional applicants. An application of Rs 3,20,000 exceeds it, so the investor is an **NII/HNI** — a category entitled to **at least 15%** of a book-built issue.\n\n- **A) Retail; 35%** - wrong; retail applications are capped at **Rs 2 lakh**, and this one is above the threshold.\n- **C) QIB; 50%** - wrong; QIBs are institutions — mutual funds, banks, insurers, FPIs, pension funds — never individuals.\n- **D) Anchor investor; 15%** - wrong; anchor investors are QIBs allotted a day before the issue opens, carved out of the QIB portion.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q30.** Which statement about ASBA in a public issue is correct?\nA) The application money is debited immediately and refunded if shares are not allotted  B) The application money is blocked in the investor's own bank account and debited only to the extent of the allotment  C) ASBA is optional and available only to institutional investors  D) Interest on the blocked amount accrues to the issuer until allotment\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The application money is blocked in the investor's own bank account and debited only to the extent of the allotment**\n\nUnder **Applications Supported by Blocked Amount**, funds are **blocked, not debited**. Only the allotted portion is debited; the balance is simply unblocked, so no refund cycle is needed.\n\n- **A) Debited and refunded** - wrong; that describes the pre-ASBA regime, which ASBA replaced precisely to remove refund delays.\n- **C) Optional and institutional only** - wrong; ASBA is **mandatory** for public issues and is used by every investor category.\n- **D) Interest accrues to the issuer** - wrong; the money never leaves the investor's account, so the **investor continues to earn interest** on it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q31.** A single stock is not permitted to move more than 10% in either direction in a trading day, while the entire market halts if the index moves 15%. These two mechanisms are respectively:\nA) A circuit breaker and a price band  B) A price band and a circuit breaker  C) A margin rule and a settlement guarantee  D) A surveillance measure and a capital adequacy norm\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A price band and a circuit breaker**\n\nA **price band is stock-specific**, capping one scrip's daily movement (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%). A **circuit breaker is market-wide**, halting trading when the index moves ±10%, ±15% or ±20%.\n\n- **A) Circuit breaker and price band** - wrong; the two mechanisms are correctly named but assigned the wrong way round.\n- **C) Margin rule and settlement guarantee** - wrong; margins are collateral against potential loss and the settlement guarantee fund covers member default. Neither limits price movement.\n- **D) Surveillance and capital adequacy** - wrong; surveillance monitors for manipulation and capital adequacy sets broker net-worth norms.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q32.** In a pure **Offer for Sale (OFS)** component of an IPO, who receives the money paid by the incoming investors?\nA) The company, which uses it for the objects stated in the prospectus  B) The selling shareholders, with the company receiving nothing  C) The company and the selling shareholders in the ratio of their holdings  D) The merchant banker, who releases it to the company after listing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The selling shareholders, with the company receiving nothing**\n\nIn an **OFS existing shares change hands**. The proceeds go to the **selling shareholders**, usually promoters or early investors. No new shares are created and the company's capital is unchanged, so the company receives nothing.\n\n- **A) The company** - wrong; that is a **fresh issue**, where new shares are created and the money reaches the company. Many IPOs combine both components.\n- **C) Split between them** - wrong; a split arises only when the issue explicitly contains both a fresh issue and an OFS. In a pure OFS there is no fresh component.\n- **D) The merchant banker** - wrong; the merchant banker manages the issue but is not a beneficiary of the proceeds.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q33.** Meenakshi has Rs 62,00,000 to deploy and wants the securities held **in her own name** rather than as units of a pooled vehicle. Which route fits, and what is its minimum investment?\nA) Mutual fund; Rs 500  B) Portfolio Management Service; Rs 50 lakh  C) Alternative Investment Fund; Rs 1 crore  D) Portfolio Management Service; Rs 25 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Portfolio Management Service; Rs 50 lakh**\n\nA **PMS is not pooled** — securities are held in the client's own name and demat account, and the portfolio can be customised. The regulatory minimum is **Rs 50 lakh**, which her Rs 62,00,000 comfortably satisfies.\n\n- **A) Mutual fund; Rs 500** - wrong on the key requirement; an MF investor holds **units** of a pooled scheme, not the underlying securities.\n- **C) AIF; Rs 1 crore** - wrong; an AIF is also pooled and issues units, and Rs 62,00,000 is in any case below the Rs 1 crore minimum.\n- **D) PMS; Rs 25 lakh** - wrong minimum; Rs 25 lakh is the reduced **AIF** threshold for employees and directors of the AIF, not the PMS minimum.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q34.** A distributor offers Meenakshi a five-year \"capital protected\" structured product linked to the Nifty. What is the single most important caveat she should understand?\nA) The capital protection is guaranteed by SEBI  B) The capital protection depends entirely on the creditworthiness of the issuer  C) The product can be redeemed at its protected value on any business day  D) The product carries no market risk of any kind\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The capital protection depends entirely on the creditworthiness of the issuer**\n\nA structured product is a **bond (or deposit) plus a derivative**. The \"protection\" comes from the bond leg maturing back to par — so it is only as good as the **issuer's credit**. If the issuer defaults, the protection fails.\n\n- **A) Guaranteed by SEBI** - wrong; no regulator guarantees the payoff on any market-linked product.\n- **C) Redeemable at protected value any business day** - wrong; these products are typically **illiquid** with no meaningful secondary market, and are **not principal-protected before maturity**.\n- **D) No market risk** - wrong; the derivative leg is fully exposed to the underlying index, and the value before maturity can fall well below par.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q35.** A corporate bond is downgraded from BBB− to BB+. What is the significance of this particular step?\nA) It is a routine move within the investment grade band with no structural consequence  B) The bond crosses from investment grade into high yield, and many institutional mandates prohibit holding it  C) The bond becomes a government-guaranteed instrument  D) The bond's coupon automatically increases to compensate holders\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The bond crosses from investment grade into high yield, and many institutional mandates prohibit holding it**\n\n**BBB− (Baa3) is the dividing line.** A downgrade below it makes the bond sub-investment grade — \"high yield\" or \"junk\". Because many institutional mandates bar sub-investment-grade holdings, such a \"falling angel\" can trigger forced selling and a sharp price fall.\n\n- **A) Routine within investment grade** - wrong; BB+ is **below** the investment grade cut-off, so the move is precisely not routine.\n- **C) Government-guaranteed** - wrong; a rating change alters nothing about who stands behind the bond.\n- **D) Coupon increases automatically** - wrong; the coupon is fixed by the terms of issue, and no rating-linked step-up is implied here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q36.** Sahyadri Auto Components reported a net profit of Rs 84 crore for the year and has 3.5 crore shares outstanding. The share trades at Rs 456. What is its P/E ratio?\nA) 5.26  B) 19.00  C) 24.00  D) 38.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 19.00**\n\nEPS = Net profit ÷ Shares = 84 crore ÷ 3.5 crore = **Rs 24**. P/E = Price ÷ EPS = 456 ÷ 24 = **19.00**.\n\n- **A) 5.26** - wrong; this is the **earnings yield** as a number (24 ÷ 456 = 5.26%), the inverse of the P/E.\n- **C) 24.00** - wrong; this is the **EPS in rupees**, not the multiple.\n- **D) 38.00** - wrong; it doubles the correct multiple and does not follow from the figures given.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q37.** Godavari Cements has a market capitalisation of Rs 2,340 crore, total debt of Rs 610 crore and cash of Rs 275 crore. What is its enterprise value?\nA) Rs 1,455 crore  B) Rs 2,005 crore  C) Rs 2,675 crore  D) Rs 3,225 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,675 crore**\n\nEV = Market capitalisation + Debt − Cash = 2,340 + 610 − 275 = **Rs 2,675 crore**. EV is what it would cost to buy the whole business including its debts, net of the cash that comes with it.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,455 crore** - wrong; this subtracts both debt and cash from market cap.\n- **B) Rs 2,005 crore** - wrong; this reverses both signs, deducting debt and adding cash.\n- **D) Rs 3,225 crore** - wrong; this **adds** cash instead of deducting it. Cash reduces the effective purchase cost.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q38.** An analyst covering a consumer company records a P/E of 27.6 and forecasts earnings growth of 23% a year. What is the PEG ratio, and what does it suggest?\nA) 0.83; attractive relative to growth  B) 1.20; not cheap relative to growth  C) 4.60; extremely expensive  D) 6.35; extremely expensive\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 1.20; not cheap relative to growth**\n\nPEG = P/E ÷ growth rate (%) = 27.6 ÷ 23 = **1.20**. A PEG **below 1** is conventionally read as attractive, so 1.20 indicates the stock is not cheap once growth is taken into account.\n\n- **A) 0.83; attractive** - wrong; this inverts the ratio (23 ÷ 27.6) and reaches the opposite conclusion.\n- **C) 4.60** - wrong; not derivable from these inputs by any correct route.\n- **D) 6.35** - wrong; this divides the P/E by a growth figure expressed in the wrong units and grossly overstates the ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q39.** An analyst studies a stock's 50-day and 200-day moving averages, its RSI, and its support and resistance levels before deciding when to buy. This describes:\nA) Fundamental analysis, which answers *what* to buy  B) Technical analysis, which answers *when* to buy  C) Bottom-up fundamental analysis of the company  D) A discounted cash flow valuation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Technical analysis, which answers *when* to buy**\n\nTechnical analysis studies **price and volume history** — moving averages, RSI, support and resistance — to time entries and exits, ignoring intrinsic value. The clean split: fundamental analysis tells you **what** to buy; technical analysis tells you **when**.\n\n- **A) Fundamental analysis** - wrong; fundamentals mean financial statements, the economy, the industry and management quality, none of which is being examined here.\n- **C) Bottom-up fundamental analysis** - wrong; bottom-up still starts from the company's business merits, not from its chart.\n- **D) DCF valuation** - wrong; a DCF discounts projected cash flows to an intrinsic value and uses no price history at all.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q40.** A corporate bond has a face value of Rs 1,000, pays a 7% annual coupon, and has exactly 4 years to maturity. If the market's required yield is 9%, what is its price?\nA) Rs 902.35  B) Rs 935.21  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,070.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 935.21**\n\nCoupon = Rs 70 a year. Annuity factor = [1 − (1.09)⁻⁴] ÷ 0.09 = 3.23972, so PV of coupons = 70 × 3.23972 = Rs 226.78. PV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ (1.09)⁴ = 1,000 ÷ 1.41158 = Rs 708.43. Price = 226.78 + 708.43 = **Rs 935.21** — a **discount**, because the coupon (7%) is below the required yield (9%).\n\n- **A) Rs 902.35** - wrong; it over-discounts and does not follow from the stated cash flows.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; a bond prices at par only when the coupon equals the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,070.92** - wrong; this is the price at a required yield of 5%, i.e. a premium — the opposite of the situation described.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q41.** A bond with a face value of Rs 1,000 carries an 8.6% coupon and trades in the market at Rs 1,062. What is its current yield?\nA) 7.50%  B) 8.10%  C) 8.60%  D) 9.13%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 8.10%**\n\nCurrent yield = Annual coupon ÷ **Market price** = 86 ÷ 1,062 = 0.080979 = **8.10%**.\n\n- **A) 7.50%** - wrong; not derivable from the coupon and price given.\n- **C) 8.60%** - wrong; this is the **coupon rate**, which is always computed on face value and ignores the price actually paid.\n- **D) 9.13%** - wrong; this divides the coupon by Rs 942, treating the bond as trading at a discount when it is at a premium.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q42.** Meenakshi holds Rs 8,00,000 of a bond fund whose portfolio has a modified duration of 5.4. Market yields fall by 40 basis points. What is the approximate change in the value of her holding?\nA) A gain of about Rs 17,280  B) A loss of about Rs 17,280  C) A gain of about Rs 43,200  D) A gain of about Rs 1,728\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A gain of about Rs 17,280**\n\n% ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −5.4 × (−0.0040) = **+2.16%**. On Rs 8,00,000 that is 8,00,000 × 0.0216 = **a gain of about Rs 17,280**. Prices move inversely to yields, so falling yields produce a gain.\n\n- **B) A loss of about Rs 17,280** - wrong sign; a loss of this size would follow a 40 bp **rise** in yields.\n- **C) A gain of about Rs 43,200** - wrong; this applies a 1% yield move instead of 0.40%.\n- **D) A gain of about Rs 1,728** - wrong; it is out by a factor of ten, treating 40 basis points as 0.04%.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q43.** A bond is trading at a discount to its face value. Which ordering of its yield measures is correct?\nA) Coupon rate > Current yield > YTM  B) Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM  C) Coupon rate = Current yield = YTM  D) Current yield > Coupon rate > YTM\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Coupon rate < Current yield < YTM**\n\nFor a **discount** bond the price is below face value, so dividing the coupon by that lower price gives a current yield above the coupon rate. YTM is higher still because it also counts the **capital gain** realised at redemption, which the current yield ignores.\n\n- **A) Coupon > Current yield > YTM** - wrong; that is the ordering for a **premium** bond, where a capital loss at maturity drags the YTM down.\n- **C) All three equal** - wrong; the three coincide only when the bond trades exactly at **par**.\n- **D) Current yield > Coupon > YTM** - wrong; it correctly places the current yield above the coupon but wrongly puts YTM at the bottom, ignoring the redemption gain.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q44.** Rohit Bhagwat buys one call option on Bharat Chemicals with a strike of Rs 2,400 at a premium of Rs 62. The lot size is 250 shares. At expiry the stock closes at Rs 2,505. What is his net profit or loss?\nA) A profit of Rs 10,750  B) A profit of Rs 26,250  C) A loss of Rs 15,500  D) A profit of Rs 41,750\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A profit of Rs 10,750**\n\nIntrinsic value at expiry = max(2,505 − 2,400, 0) = Rs 105. Net payoff per share = 105 − 62 = Rs 43. Total = 43 × 250 = **a profit of Rs 10,750**. Break-even was Strike + Premium = 2,400 + 62 = Rs 2,462.\n\n- **B) A profit of Rs 26,250** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value (105 × 250) with no deduction for the premium paid.\n- **C) A loss of Rs 15,500** - wrong; this is the full premium outgo (62 × 250), which he would lose only if the option expired worthless below Rs 2,400.\n- **D) A profit of Rs 41,750** - wrong; this adds the premium to the intrinsic value instead of subtracting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q45.** Meenakshi's adviser illustrates a protective put using a put option struck at Rs 1,150 bought for a premium of Rs 30, with a lot size of 400. At expiry the underlying closes at Rs 1,082. What is the net profit or loss on the option alone?\nA) A profit of Rs 12,000  B) A profit of Rs 15,200  C) A profit of Rs 27,200  D) A loss of Rs 12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A profit of Rs 15,200**\n\nIntrinsic value = max(1,150 − 1,082, 0) = Rs 68. Net payoff per share = 68 − 30 = Rs 38. Total = 38 × 400 = **a profit of Rs 15,200**. Break-even for a long put = Strike − Premium = 1,150 − 30 = Rs 1,120, and the close of Rs 1,082 is below it.\n\n- **A) A profit of Rs 12,000** - wrong; this is the premium amount (30 × 400) misread as the gain.\n- **C) A profit of Rs 27,200** - wrong; this is the gross intrinsic value (68 × 400) before deducting the premium paid.\n- **D) A loss of Rs 12,000** - wrong; the full premium is lost only if the underlying closes **at or above** the Rs 1,150 strike.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q46.** A stock trades at a spot price of Rs 2,600. The risk-free rate is 6.5% per annum and the expected dividend yield on the stock is 1.5% per annum. Using F = S × (1 + r − q)^T, what is the fair value of a one-year futures contract?\nA) Rs 2,470  B) Rs 2,730  C) Rs 2,769  D) Rs 2,808\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,730**\n\nF = S × (1 + r − q)^T = 2,600 × (1 + 0.065 − 0.015)¹ = 2,600 × 1.05 = **Rs 2,730**. The futures price exceeds spot (contango) because the financing cost of carrying the position exceeds the dividend income forgone.\n\n- **A) Rs 2,470** - wrong; this subtracts the net carry instead of adding it, which would imply backwardation.\n- **C) Rs 2,769** - wrong; this uses the risk-free rate alone (2,600 × 1.065), forgetting to net off the dividend yield.\n- **D) Rs 2,808** - wrong; this **adds** the dividend yield to the risk-free rate. Dividends received reduce the cost of carry, so the yield must be deducted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q47.** Which of the following is the single most important difference between an exchange-traded futures contract and an OTC forward contract?\nA) Forwards can be customised, while futures cannot be customised in any respect at all  B) Futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily through mark to market, while forwards carry counterparty risk and settle only at maturity  C) Only forwards can be used for hedging; futures are purely speculative instruments  D) Futures require no margin, because the exchange absorbs all losses\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Futures are guaranteed by the clearing corporation and settled daily through mark to market, while forwards carry counterparty risk and settle only at maturity**\n\nThe clearing corporation stands as central counterparty to every futures trade, eliminating counterparty risk, and gains and losses are settled **daily** into margin accounts. A forward is a bilateral promise settled only at maturity, so each side bears the other's default risk throughout its life.\n\n- **A) Customisation** - wrong as *the* key difference; customisation is a genuine distinction, but the statement overstates it and misses the counterparty-risk point that actually defines the two instruments.\n- **C) Only forwards hedge** - wrong; futures are used extensively for hedging, and forwards can equally be used to speculate.\n- **D) No margin on futures** - wrong; the opposite is true — futures require **mandatory** initial and mark-to-market margin, which is exactly how the settlement guarantee is funded.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q48.** A mutual fund scheme holds securities valued at Rs 846 crore and cash of Rs 22 crore, and owes Rs 13 crore towards accrued expenses. It has 37 crore units outstanding. What is the NAV per unit?\nA) Rs 22.51  B) Rs 23.11  C) Rs 23.46  D) Rs 23.81\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 23.11**\n\nNAV = (Total assets - Total liabilities) / Units outstanding. Total assets = 846 + 22 = **Rs 868 crore**. Net assets = 868 - 13 = **Rs 855 crore**. NAV = 855 / 37 = **Rs 23.11**.\n\n- **A) Rs 22.51** - wrong; obtained by deducting the liabilities but forgetting to add the cash: 833 / 37.\n- **C) Rs 23.46** - wrong; obtained by ignoring the Rs 13 crore of liabilities: 868 / 37.\n- **D) Rs 23.81** - wrong; obtained by *adding* the liabilities instead of subtracting them: 881 / 37.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q49.** Sunita Deshpande, a 45-year-old teacher in Pune, submits an application at 2:30 p.m. on Monday to invest Rs 6,00,000 in an equity-oriented scheme. The money is credited to the scheme's bank account at 11:00 a.m. on Tuesday. Which NAV will she receive?\nA) Monday's closing NAV  B) Tuesday's closing NAV  C) The previous Friday's closing NAV  D) Wednesday's closing NAV\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Tuesday's closing NAV**\n\nFor purchases of **any amount**, the applicable NAV depends on the **funds being realised (credited to the scheme) before the cut-off**, not merely on when the application was time-stamped. The money reached the scheme at 11:00 a.m. on Tuesday, which is before the 3:00 p.m. cut-off for non-liquid schemes, so **Tuesday's NAV** applies.\n\n- **A) Monday's closing NAV** - wrong; this would be the answer only if the funds had also been realised before 3:00 p.m. on Monday.\n- **C) The previous Friday's closing NAV** - wrong; the previous day's NAV rule applies only to liquid and overnight fund purchases, and even then only when funds are realised before the 1:30 p.m. cut-off.\n- **D) Wednesday's closing NAV** - wrong; there is no one-day lag once funds are realised before the cut-off.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q50.** Sunita wants a diversified equity scheme that is *required* to hold a minimum of 25% each in large cap, mid cap and small cap stocks. Which SEBI category does this describe?\nA) Flexi cap fund  B) Multi cap fund  C) Large & mid cap fund  D) Focused fund\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Multi cap fund**\n\nUnder SEBI's 2017 categorisation a **multi cap fund must hold at least 25% each in large cap, mid cap and small cap** stocks, giving it a mandatory presence across all three segments.\n\n- **A) Flexi cap fund** - wrong; a flexi cap fund needs a minimum of 65% in equity but has **no market-cap-wise restriction** at all.\n- **C) Large & mid cap fund** - wrong; this category requires a minimum of 35% each in large cap and mid cap, with no small cap floor.\n- **D) Focused fund** - wrong; a focused fund is defined by a **maximum of 30 stocks**, not by any cap-wise allocation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q51.** Sunita redeems 4,200 units at an applicable NAV of Rs 68.40. The scheme charges an exit load of 1% for redemptions within 12 months, and her units are 7 months old. What amount will she receive?\nA) Rs 2,81,534  B) Rs 2,84,407  C) Rs 2,87,280  D) Rs 2,90,153\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,84,407**\n\nGross value = 4,200 x 68.40 = **Rs 2,87,280**. Exit load = 1% x 2,87,280 = **Rs 2,872.80**. Net proceeds = 2,87,280 - 2,872.80 = **Rs 2,84,407** (rounded to the nearest rupee).\n\n- **A) Rs 2,81,534** - wrong; this applies a 2% load instead of the stated 1%.\n- **C) Rs 2,87,280** - wrong; this is the gross redemption value before the exit load is deducted.\n- **D) Rs 2,90,153** - wrong; this *adds* the load to the gross value instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q52.** Sunita invests Rs 15,000 a month through an SIP for four months, at NAVs of Rs 60, Rs 50, Rs 30 and Rs 40. What is her average cost per unit?\nA) Rs 35.00  B) Rs 42.11  C) Rs 45.00  D) Rs 50.00\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 42.11**\n\nUnits bought = 15,000/60 + 15,000/50 + 15,000/30 + 15,000/40 = 250 + 300 + 500 + 375 = **1,425 units**. Total invested = 4 x 15,000 = **Rs 60,000**. Average cost = 60,000 / 1,425 = **Rs 42.11**. Note that this is **below** the simple average NAV of Rs 45.00 - that gap is the benefit of rupee-cost averaging.\n\n- **A) Rs 35.00** - wrong; this is the average of only the two lowest NAVs (30 and 40).\n- **C) Rs 45.00** - wrong; this is the simple arithmetic average of the four NAVs, which ignores that a fixed rupee amount buys more units when the NAV is low.\n- **D) Rs 50.00** - wrong; this is the average of the three highest NAVs (60, 50 and 40).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q53.** Sunita invests a lump sum of Rs 30,00,000. The regular plan of the scheme has a TER of 1.55% and the direct plan of the same scheme has a TER of 0.62%. How much extra cost would she bear in the first year by choosing the regular plan?\nA) Rs 18,600  B) Rs 27,900  C) Rs 46,500  D) Rs 65,100\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 27,900**\n\nThe two plans hold an **identical portfolio**; only the embedded distribution commission differs. TER difference = 1.55% - 0.62% = **0.93%**. Extra cost = 0.93% x 30,00,000 = **Rs 27,900** in the first year, and it compounds every year thereafter.\n\n- **A) Rs 18,600** - wrong; this is the direct plan's own TER of 0.62% applied to the corpus, not the difference.\n- **C) Rs 46,500** - wrong; this is the regular plan's full TER of 1.55%, not the incremental cost.\n- **D) Rs 65,100** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios (2.17%) instead of taking the difference.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q54.** A discretionary PMS portfolio starts at Rs 2.40 crore. In Year 1 it rises to Rs 3.00 crore and a performance fee is charged. In Year 2 it falls to Rs 2.65 crore. In Year 3 it rises to Rs 3.30 crore. The performance fee is 15% on a high watermark basis with no hurdle. What performance fee is payable for Year 3?\nA) Rs 4,50,000  B) Rs 9,75,000  C) Rs 13,50,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 4,50,000**\n\nThe **high watermark after Year 1 is Rs 3.00 crore** - the highest value on which a fee has already been charged. The Year 3 fee applies only to the gain **above that peak**: 3.30 - 3.00 = **Rs 30 lakh**. Fee = 15% x 30,00,000 = **Rs 4,50,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 9,75,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 65 lakh rise from the Year 2 trough of Rs 2.65 crore, which would make the client pay twice for recovering lost ground.\n- **C) Rs 13,50,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the Rs 90 lakh rise from the original Rs 2.40 crore, ignoring the fee already paid in Year 1.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has crossed its previous peak, so a fee is due on the excess.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q55.** A portfolio manager runs a Rs 1.60 crore portfolio that returns 16% for the year. The fee terms are a 9% hurdle rate and a 20% performance fee, with **no catch-up**. What performance fee is payable?\nA) Rs 1,12,000  B) Rs 2,24,000  C) Rs 2,88,000  D) Rs 5,12,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,24,000**\n\nTotal return = 16% x 1,60,00,000 = **Rs 25,60,000**. The hurdle absorbs the first 9% = 9% x 1,60,00,000 = **Rs 14,40,000**. Return above the hurdle = 25,60,000 - 14,40,000 = **Rs 11,20,000**. With no catch-up the fee applies only to that excess: 20% x 11,20,000 = **Rs 2,24,000**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1,12,000** - wrong; this applies a 10% rate to the excess rather than the stated 20%.\n- **C) Rs 2,88,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the hurdle amount itself, which is exactly the portion the hurdle protects.\n- **D) Rs 5,12,000** - wrong; this charges 20% on the whole return and ignores the hurdle entirely - which would be the answer only if there were no hurdle.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q56.** Rajendra Bhosale signs a PMS agreement under which the portfolio manager researches securities and sends him written recommendations, but Rajendra places every order himself through his own broker. Which type of portfolio management service is this?\nA) Discretionary  B) Non-discretionary  C) Advisory  D) Execution-only distribution\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Advisory**\n\nApply the clean test - **who pulls the trigger?** Here the manager only **recommends**; the client both **decides and executes**. That is the **advisory** service under the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020.\n\n- **A) Discretionary** - wrong; in a discretionary service the manager both decides **and** executes, with full authority delegated to him.\n- **B) Non-discretionary** - wrong; there the **client decides** but the **manager executes** each approved transaction.\n- **D) Execution-only distribution** - wrong; this is not a category of portfolio management service at all, and no advice would be given in it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q57.** Under the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020, what is the minimum net worth of a portfolio manager and the minimum investment required from a client?\nA) Rs 5 crore and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 50 crore and Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 2 crore and Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 5 crore and Rs 1 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5 crore and Rs 50 lakh**\n\nA portfolio manager must maintain a **minimum net worth of Rs 5 crore**, and the **minimum investment per client is Rs 50 lakh**. Remember the ladder of minimums: mutual fund Rs 100-5,000, **PMS Rs 50 lakh**, AIF Rs 1 crore.\n\n- **B) Rs 50 crore and Rs 1 crore** - wrong; Rs 50 crore is an **AMC's** minimum net worth and Rs 1 crore is the **AIF** investor minimum.\n- **C) Rs 2 crore and Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the minimum for employees/directors of an AIF or its manager, and for an angel investor.\n- **D) Rs 5 crore and Rs 1 crore** - wrong; the net worth figure is right but Rs 1 crore is the AIF minimum, not the PMS minimum.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q58.** A fund registered in India invests in later-stage unlisted companies through growth capital and buyouts. It does not use leverage other than for day-to-day operational needs. Under the SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012, which category does it fall in?\nA) Category I  B) Category II  C) Category III  D) It cannot be registered as an AIF\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Category II**\n\n**Category II is the residual bucket** - not Category I, not Category III, and no leverage beyond day-to-day operational needs. **Private equity funds**, debt funds, real estate funds, distressed-asset funds and fund-of-funds sit here.\n\n- **A) Category I** - wrong; Category I is reserved for socially or economically desirable sectors - venture capital (including angel), SME, social venture and infrastructure funds.\n- **C) Category III** - wrong; Category III is for **complex or diverse trading strategies employing leverage**, such as hedge funds and PIPE funds.\n- **D) It cannot be registered as an AIF** - wrong; a privately pooled PE fund established in India is squarely within the AIF definition.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q59.** A Category III AIF has a corpus of Rs 260 crore. What is the sponsor/manager's minimum continuing interest?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6.50 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 13 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 10 crore**\n\nFor **Category III** the continuing interest is the **lower of 5% of the corpus or Rs 10 crore**. Here 5% x 260 = **Rs 13 crore**, which is higher than Rs 10 crore, so the **lower** figure of **Rs 10 crore** applies.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the Category I and II ceiling, not Category III's.\n- **B) Rs 6.50 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category I/II rate of 2.5% (2.5% x 260) instead of Category III's 5%.\n- **D) Rs 13 crore** - wrong; this is 5% of the corpus, but the rule is the **lower of** that and Rs 10 crore.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q60.** Sunita's cousin, whose taxable income falls in the 5% slab, invests Rs 1 crore in a Category III AIF. Which statement about the taxation of her income from the fund is correct?\nA) It is taxed in her hands at her own slab rate because Category III enjoys pass-through status  B) It is taxed at the fund level, so her low personal slab rate gives her no benefit  C) It is exempt at the fund level and taxed only when she redeems  D) Pass-through applies to capital gains but not to interest\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) It is taxed at the fund level, so her low personal slab rate gives her no benefit**\n\n**Pass-through status is granted only to Category I and Category II AIFs.** A **Category III** AIF is **taxed at the fund level**, generally at the maximum marginal rate, before distribution. Every investor therefore effectively bears the same rate whatever their own slab - which makes Category III relatively **less tax-efficient for a low-slab investor**, an important suitability point.\n\n- **A) It is taxed in her hands at her own slab rate because Category III enjoys pass-through status** - wrong; Category III is precisely the category that does **not** have pass-through status.\n- **C) It is exempt at the fund level and taxed only when she redeems** - wrong; the tax is paid by the fund on the income as it arises, not deferred to redemption.\n- **D) Pass-through applies to capital gains but not to interest** - wrong; where pass-through does apply (Categories I and II) the **character of every stream is preserved**; the only carve-out is the fund's **business income**.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q61.** Sunita's Rs 18,00,000 corpus is placed Rs 10,80,000 in an equity fund (standard deviation 22%) and Rs 7,20,000 in a short-duration debt fund (standard deviation 6%). The correlation between them is 0.25. What is the portfolio's standard deviation?\nA) 13.99%  B) 14.55%  C) 15.60%  D) 195.84%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 13.99%**\n\nWeights: w1 = 10,80,000/18,00,000 = **0.60**; w2 = **0.40**.\nsigma_p squared = (0.60^2 x 22^2) + (0.40^2 x 6^2) + (2 x 0.60 x 0.40 x 22 x 6 x 0.25)\n= 174.24 + 5.76 + 15.84 = **195.84**.\nsigma_p = square root of 195.84 = **13.99%**, comfortably below the 15.60% weighted average of the two risks.\n\n- **B) 14.55%** - wrong; this is the answer if the correlation is taken as 0.50 rather than 0.25.\n- **C) 15.60%** - wrong; this is the simple weighted average of the standard deviations (0.6 x 22 + 0.4 x 6), which would be the portfolio risk only if the correlation were exactly +1.\n- **D) 195.84%** - wrong; this is the **variance**, not the standard deviation - the square root step has been forgotten.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q62.** From the same set of assets, Portfolio P offers an expected return of 12.5% with a standard deviation of 15%, and Portfolio Q offers an expected return of 12.5% with a standard deviation of 18%. Which statement is correct?\nA) Q is inefficient because P offers the same expected return at lower risk  B) Q is efficient because it offers a higher risk premium  C) Both are efficient because their expected returns are equal  D) P is unattainable because it lies above the efficient frontier\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Q is inefficient because P offers the same expected return at lower risk**\n\nUnder the **dominance rule**, P dominates Q: **same return with less risk**. Every dominated portfolio is discarded, so Q lies **below and to the right** of the efficient frontier and is **inefficient**. P survives the test and may lie on the frontier.\n\n- **B) Q is efficient because it offers a higher risk premium** - wrong; Q offers the **same** expected return, so its extra 3 percentage points of risk earn nothing at all.\n- **C) Both are efficient because their expected returns are equal** - wrong; efficiency is judged on **return and risk together**, never on return alone.\n- **D) P is unattainable because it lies above the efficient frontier** - wrong; P is stated to be constructible from the same assets, so it is attainable by definition.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q63.** Sunita's IPS sets a strategic allocation of 55% equity and 45% debt on a corpus of Rs 60,00,000 (Rs 33,00,000 equity, Rs 27,00,000 debt). Over the year equity returns 28% and debt returns 6%. Under an **annual calendar rebalancing** rule, how much equity must be sold?\nA) Rs 1,63,350  B) Rs 3,26,700  C) Rs 9,24,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 3,26,700**\n\nYear-end equity = 33,00,000 x 1.28 = **Rs 42,24,000**. Year-end debt = 27,00,000 x 1.06 = **Rs 28,62,000**. Total corpus = **Rs 70,86,000**.\nTarget equity = 55% x 70,86,000 = **Rs 38,97,300**.\nEquity to sell = 42,24,000 - 38,97,300 = **Rs 3,26,700**. (Debt then becomes 28,62,000 + 3,26,700 = Rs 31,88,700, exactly 45%.)\n\n- **A) Rs 1,63,350** - wrong; this is only half the required trade and leaves the portfolio still overweight equity.\n- **C) Rs 9,24,000** - wrong; this is the whole equity gain, which would restore the **starting rupee amount** of Rs 33,00,000 rather than the 55% **weight**.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; \"nil\" is the answer under a pure **threshold** rule, because the drift to 59.61% is 4.61 percentage points and inside a plus/minus 5 point band. The question specifies a **calendar** rule, under which the review date itself triggers the trade.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q64.** After her husband's death Sunita's household income has halved and she now supports two school-going children alone. She asks her adviser to reduce equity permanently from 60% to 40% of her portfolio. This decision is best described as:\nA) Tactical asset allocation  B) A change in the strategic asset allocation  C) Routine rebalancing  D) A breach of the IPS\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A change in the strategic asset allocation**\n\nApply the clean test: **a life event means strategic; a market event means tactical.** A permanent fall in income and the loss of a second earner reduce her **ability to take risk**, so the long-term policy mix itself - and the IPS that records it - must be rewritten.\n\n- **A) Tactical asset allocation** - wrong; tactical shifts are **short-term deviations within the IPS bands** driven by a **market view**, and they are meant to be reversed.\n- **C) Routine rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing restores the **existing** target weights after drift; here the target itself is being changed.\n- **D) A breach of the IPS** - wrong; a properly documented, client-driven revision of the policy mix is exactly how an IPS is meant to be updated, not a breach of it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q65.** A fund returned 15.4% over the year. Its beta is 0.85, the risk-free rate is 6.2% and the market returned 13.0%. What is Jensen's alpha?\nA) 2.40%  B) 3.42%  C) 5.78%  D) 9.20%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 3.42%**\n\nCAPM required return = Rf + beta x (Rm - Rf) = 6.2 + 0.85 x (13.0 - 6.2) = 6.2 + 0.85 x 6.8 = 6.2 + 5.78 = **11.98%**.\nJensen's alpha = 15.4 - 11.98 = **+3.42%**.\n\n- **A) 2.40%** - wrong; this is simply the fund's return minus the market return, which ignores the beta of 0.85 and therefore over-charges the manager for market exposure he did not take.\n- **C) 5.78%** - wrong; this is only the risk-premium component, beta x (Rm - Rf), not the alpha.\n- **D) 9.20%** - wrong; this is the excess return over the risk-free rate, before deducting the return required for the systematic risk taken.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q66.** Sunita's brother offers to transfer Rs 2,00,000 from his own savings account to buy mutual fund units in Sunita's sole-holder folio. What is the correct position?\nA) Permitted, because a sibling is a close relative  B) Permitted up to Rs 50,000 with a declaration from both  C) Not permitted  D) Permitted if the brother is added as a second holder\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Not permitted**\n\nUnder PMLA the subscription must come from the **investor's own bank account** - for a folio, that of the **first/sole holder**. Any other source is a **third-party payment**. The only recognised exceptions are payment by a parent/grandparent/related person **for a MINOR** (capped at Rs 50,000 per transaction), an **employer for an employee** through payroll deduction, a **custodian for an FPI or client**, and an **AMC/corporate paying commission in units**. A sibling paying for an adult investor fits none of them.\n\n- **A) Permitted, because a sibling is a close relative** - wrong; there is no general \"close relative\" exception. Even a spouse paying for a spouse is a prohibited third-party payment.\n- **B) Permitted up to Rs 50,000 with a declaration from both** - wrong; the Rs 50,000 ceiling belongs to the **minor** exception only, and Sunita is an adult.\n- **D) Permitted if the brother is added as a second holder** - wrong; holders cannot simply be added to an existing folio, and in any case payouts and payments are anchored to the **first** holder's account.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q67.** Sunita's son, for whom she is the guardian on a minor's folio, turns 18 on 14 September. What happens to the folio on that date?\nA) The folio continues unchanged until the AMC is informed  B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all SIP, SWP and STP instructions cease  C) Only fresh purchases stop; redemptions continue on the guardian's signature  D) The units are automatically transferred to a new folio in his own name\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The folio is frozen for financial transactions and all SIP, SWP and STP instructions cease**\n\nOn the date the minor attains majority the folio is **frozen for financial transactions** and every **standing instruction (SIP, SWP, STP) stops**. To unfreeze it, the now-major must submit the \"Minor Attaining Majority\" form with a **banker-attested signature**, his own **PAN and KYC**, a **bank account in his own name** and a **fresh nomination**. The guardian's authority ends completely on that date.\n\n- **A) The folio continues unchanged until the AMC is informed** - wrong; the freeze operates automatically on the birthday, and the AMC is required to send advance notice before it.\n- **C) Only fresh purchases stop; redemptions continue on the guardian's signature** - wrong; a redemption signed by the erstwhile guardian after that date is **invalid** - the account has changed owner in law.\n- **D) The units are automatically transferred to a new folio in his own name** - wrong; no new folio is created; the same folio is regularised once the documents are submitted.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q68.** A mutual fund folio is held jointly by Sunita and her sister with the mode of holding \"Anyone or Survivor\". Sunita now wishes to change the nomination. Whose signature is required?\nA) Any one holder  B) The first holder only  C) All the holders  D) The nominee's and the first holder's\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) All the holders**\n\nNomination is a **personal act of every unit holder**. **All joint holders must sign a nomination or a change of nomination, even where the mode of holding is \"Anyone or Survivor\"** - the single-signature convenience applies only to ordinary transactions, never to nomination.\n\n- **A) Any one holder** - wrong; this is the rule for redemptions, switches and other transactions in an \"Anyone or Survivor\" folio, not for nomination.\n- **B) The first holder only** - wrong; the first holder's primacy governs tax status and payouts, not the nomination.\n- **D) The nominee's and the first holder's** - wrong; the **nominee never signs** the nomination form - the nominee is a recipient, not a party to the instruction.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q69.** Which of the following is **NOT** an Officially Valid Document (OVD) under the PML (Maintenance of Records) Rules, 2005?\nA) Passport  B) NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer  C) PAN card  D) Voter's Identity Card issued by the Election Commission\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) PAN card**\n\nThe six OVDs are the **passport, driving licence, proof of possession of Aadhaar, Voter's Identity Card, NREGA job card** and the **NPR letter**. **PAN is not an OVD** - it is a **separate and additional** mandatory requirement under Section 139A of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\n- **A) Passport** - wrong choice for \"not an OVD\"; the passport is the first item on the OVD list.\n- **B) NREGA job card signed by a State Government officer** - wrong choice; it is a listed OVD when duly signed.\n- **D) Voter's Identity Card issued by the Election Commission** - wrong choice; it is a listed OVD.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q70.** An NRI wants a rupee-denominated bank account into which he will remit his overseas earnings, where both principal and interest are fully repatriable and the interest is exempt from Indian income tax. Which account should he open?\nA) NRO account  B) NRE account  C) FCNR(B) account  D) RFC account\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) NRE account**\n\nThe **NRE (Non-Resident External)** account is held in **Indian rupees**, is credited with **foreign earnings remitted from abroad**, is **fully repatriable** for both principal and interest, and its **interest is exempt** from Indian income tax.\n\n- **A) NRO account** - wrong; the NRO account holds **Indian-source income**, its interest is **taxable** with TDS, and repatriation of principal is capped at **USD 1 million per financial year**.\n- **C) FCNR(B) account** - wrong; it meets the repatriability and tax-exemption tests but is held in **foreign currency** as a **term deposit only** (1 to 5 years), not in rupees.\n- **D) RFC account** - wrong; a Resident Foreign Currency account is for a **returning Indian** who has become resident again, not for a non-resident.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q71.** Sunita submits a change-of-bank-mandate request on 4 August and lodges a redemption request on 9 August. Into which account will the redemption proceeds be credited?\nA) The new bank account  B) The old, already registered bank account  C) Split equally between the two accounts  D) Neither - the redemption is rejected\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The old, already registered bank account**\n\nWhere a redemption is received **within the cooling-off window (commonly 10 calendar days) of a change-of-bank-mandate request**, the proceeds are credited to the **old, already registered account**. This is a deliberate **anti-fraud control** - it defeats the classic scam of changing the bank details and immediately redeeming.\n\n- **A) The new bank account** - wrong; the new mandate is not used for payouts until the cooling-off period has run.\n- **C) Split equally between the two accounts** - wrong; redemption proceeds are never split across bank accounts.\n- **D) Neither - the redemption is rejected** - wrong; the redemption is processed normally; only the destination account is controlled.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q72.** Which statement correctly distinguishes a KRA from the Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR)?\nA) The KRA issues a 14-digit KIN and covers all financial sectors  B) The CKYCR issues a 14-digit KIN and covers all financial sectors, while a KRA covers only the securities market  C) The CKYCR covers only the securities market, while a KRA covers all financial sectors  D) Both are operated by SEBI under the KYC Registration Agency Regulations, 2011\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The CKYCR issues a 14-digit KIN and covers all financial sectors, while a KRA covers only the securities market**\n\nA **KRA** is set up under the **SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011** and serves the **securities market only** (CVL, NDML, CAMS, KFin, DotEx). The **CKYCR**, operated by **CERSAI** under **Rule 9(1A)** of the PML Rules, spans **banking, securities, insurance and pensions** and issues a **14-digit KIN**, with upload required **within 10 days** of starting the relationship.\n\n- **A) The KRA issues a 14-digit KIN and covers all financial sectors** - wrong; the KIN belongs to CKYCR, and a KRA's scope is limited to the securities market.\n- **C) The CKYCR covers only the securities market, while a KRA covers all financial sectors** - wrong; this reverses the two scopes.\n- **D) Both are operated by SEBI under the KYC Registration Agency Regulations, 2011** - wrong; CKYCR is operated by **CERSAI** under the **PML Rules**, not by SEBI.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q73.** Sunita's Assets Under Advice with her SEBI-registered Investment Adviser are Rs 82,00,000. Under the **AUA mode**, what is the maximum advisory fee the IA may charge her family per annum across all services?\nA) Rs 1,25,000  B) Rs 2,05,000  C) Rs 2,50,000  D) Rs 4,10,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 2,05,000**\n\nUnder the AUA mode the cap is **2.5% of AUA per annum per family of client**. 2.5% x 82,00,000 = **Rs 2,05,000**. (Note this exceeds the fixed-fee cap because her AUA is above the Rs 50 lakh break-even point.)\n\n- **A) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the ceiling under the **fixed fee mode**, the alternative the adviser did not choose.\n- **C) Rs 2,50,000** - wrong; this treats the cap as a flat rupee figure rather than 2.5% of the actual AUA.\n- **D) Rs 4,10,000** - wrong; this applies 5% of AUA, double the permitted rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q74.** A SEBI-registered Investment Adviser charges his client an advisory fee and, on the same schemes he recommended, also receives trail commission from the AMC. Which provision of the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013 does this most directly breach?\nA) Regulation 15(1)  B) Regulation 15(2)  C) Regulation 15(5)  D) Regulation 19\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Regulation 15(2)**\n\n**Regulation 15(2)** states that an investment adviser **shall not receive any consideration - by way of remuneration, compensation or in any other form - from any person other than the client being advised** in respect of the underlying products or securities advised upon. Taking a trail commission from the AMC is exactly that, however good the advice was.\n\n- **A) Regulation 15(1)** - wrong; 15(1) is the general fiduciary duty and conflict-disclosure obligation. It is offended too, but the specific and direct prohibition on third-party payment is 15(2).\n- **C) Regulation 15(5)** - wrong; 15(5) is the 15-day bar on the adviser transacting on his own account **contrary to the advice** given to clients.\n- **D) Regulation 19** - wrong; Regulation 19 deals with the **maintenance of records** and the annual compliance audit.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q75.** What is the net worth requirement for an **individual** SEBI-registered Investment Adviser?\nA) Net tangible assets of Rs 1 lakh  B) Net tangible assets of Rs 5 lakh  C) Net worth of Rs 25 lakh  D) Net worth of Rs 50 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Net tangible assets of Rs 5 lakh**\n\nSince the 2020 amendment, **individuals and partnership firms must hold net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh**, and **non-individuals a net worth of not less than Rs 50 lakh**.\n\n- **A) Net tangible assets of Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the **pre-2020** figure for an individual IA and is now the individual **Research Analyst** requirement.\n- **C) Net worth of Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh was the **pre-2020** requirement for a non-individual IA.\n- **D) Net worth of Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; that is the current requirement for a **non-individual** IA, not an individual.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q76.** The spouse of an individual SEBI-registered Investment Adviser is an AMFI-registered mutual fund distributor and wishes to earn commission by distributing schemes to the adviser's own advisory clients. Is this permitted?\nA) Permitted, because the spouse is separately registered with AMFI  B) Permitted if the commission is disclosed to the clients  C) Not permitted  D) Permitted only in respect of direct plans\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Not permitted**\n\nUnder **Regulation 22**, an **individual investment adviser shall not provide distribution services at all**, and **the family of the individual IA shall not provide distribution services to the IA's clients**. The rule closes precisely this loophole.\n\n- **A) Permitted, because the spouse is separately registered with AMFI** - wrong; separate registration does not cure the conflict; the prohibition is framed at the **family** level.\n- **B) Permitted if the commission is disclosed to the clients** - wrong; here the law **bans** the arrangement rather than merely requiring disclosure. Disclosure is the fallback only where a conflict cannot be avoided.\n- **D) Permitted only in respect of direct plans** - wrong; direct plans pay no commission at all, so there would be nothing to distribute for a fee.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q77.** An investment adviser's failure to comply with SEBI's directions continues for 140 days. What is the maximum **monetary** penalty that may be imposed under Section 15EB of the SEBI Act, 1992?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 1.40 crore  D) Rs 25 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 1 crore**\n\nSection 15EB prescribes a penalty of **not less than Rs 1 lakh**, extending to **Rs 1 lakh for each day** the failure continues, **subject to a maximum of Rs 1 crore**. The ceiling is reached after **100 days** (Rs 1,00,00,000 / Rs 1,00,000), so a 140-day default still caps at **Rs 1 crore** - though the registration consequences (suspension, cancellation) continue to grow.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; that is the **minimum** penalty, and also the daily rate, not the maximum.\n- **C) Rs 1.40 crore** - wrong; this multiplies 140 days by Rs 1 lakh and ignores the statutory ceiling.\n- **D) Rs 25 crore** - wrong; Rs 25 crore is the ceiling for **insider trading (15G)** and **fraudulent and unfair trade practices (15HA)**, not for a Section 15EB default.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q78.** While waiting in a lift, a person with no business or personal connection to a listed company overhears two of its directors discussing an unannounced acquisition, and buys the company's shares the same afternoon. Under the SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations, 2015, his position is that:\nA) He is not an insider because he has no connection with the company  B) He is an insider because he is in possession of UPSI  C) He is an insider only if he traded within six months of overhearing  D) He is a connected person by virtue of the lift conversation\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) He is an insider because he is in possession of UPSI**\n\nRegulation 2(1)(g) defines an insider as any person who is **(i) a connected person OR (ii) in possession of, or having access to, UPSI**. The two limbs are joined by **\"or\"**, so possession of UPSI alone makes him an insider, and trading on it breaches Regulation 4.\n\n- **A) He is not an insider because he has no connection with the company** - wrong; this reads the definition as requiring a connection, which it does not.\n- **C) He is an insider only if he traded within six months of overhearing** - wrong; the **six-month** period is the look-back for identifying a **connected person**, not a time limit on the possession limb.\n- **D) He is a connected person by virtue of the lift conversation** - wrong; a connected person needs an association with the company in some capacity giving access to UPSI - a chance overhearing is not such an association. He is caught by the second limb, not the first.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q79.** An investment adviser notices a client routing a Rs 45,000 transaction whose stated source of funds is wholly implausible and inconsistent with the client's declared profile. What is the correct action?\nA) Report it in the monthly Cash Transaction Report, as the amount is below Rs 10 lakh  B) Take no action, as reporting applies only to transactions above Rs 10 lakh  C) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days and not inform the client  D) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with SEBI within 15 days and warn the client\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with FIU-IND within 7 working days and not inform the client**\n\nA **Suspicious Transaction Report has NO monetary threshold** and applies whether the transaction is **attempted or executed**. It must be filed with **FIU-IND within 7 working days** of concluding that the transaction is suspicious. **Telling the client is \"tipping off\" and is itself an offence** under PMLA.\n\n- **A) Report it in the monthly Cash Transaction Report, as the amount is below Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; a CTR covers **cash transactions above Rs 10 lakh** and is filed by the 15th of the following month; it is the wrong report for this fact pattern.\n- **B) Take no action, as reporting applies only to transactions above Rs 10 lakh** - wrong; the Rs 10 lakh threshold belongs to the CTR, never to an STR.\n- **D) File a Suspicious Transaction Report with SEBI within 15 days and warn the client** - wrong on all three counts: STRs go to **FIU-IND**, within **7 working days**, and the client must **never** be warned.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q80.** Which of the following is **NOT** a \"security\" as defined in Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956?\nA) A Government security  B) A unit of a mutual fund scheme  C) A unit-linked insurance policy  D) A debenture of an incorporated company\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) A unit-linked insurance policy**\n\nSection 2(h) expressly **excludes a unit-linked insurance policy (ULIP)** or any similar instrument that combines life risk cover with investment and is issued by an insurer - the exclusion inserted after the 2010 SEBI-IRDA dispute over ULIPs. Fixed deposits, bank accounts, gold, real estate and insurance policies are likewise not securities.\n\n- **A) A Government security** - wrong choice; G-Secs are expressly included in the definition.\n- **B) A unit of a mutual fund scheme** - wrong choice; units issued to investors under a mutual fund scheme are expressly included.\n- **D) A debenture of an incorporated company** - wrong choice; shares, scrips, stocks, bonds and debentures are the core of the definition.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q81.** In the eleven months before her husband's death, Sunita's previous adviser switched her equity holdings between schemes eleven times. Her stated investment strategy and risk profile did not change once during the period, and each switch generated fresh commission. This conduct is best labelled:\nA) Mis-selling  B) Churning  C) Front-running  D) Rebalancing\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Churning**\n\n**Churning is excessive trading, switching or turnover in a client's portfolio to generate brokerage or fresh commission.** The give-away sign is exactly what is described: **a lot of activity with no change in the underlying strategy**.\n\n- **A) Mis-selling** - wrong; mis-selling is about the **wrong product** for the client's needs, and a single transaction can be mis-selling. Here the issue is the **frequency**, not necessarily the schemes.\n- **C) Front-running** - wrong; front-running means dealing **ahead of a client's known impending order** to profit from the price move it will cause.\n- **D) Rebalancing** - wrong; rebalancing restores agreed target weights on a documented calendar or threshold rule; it is neither frequent nor commission-driven.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q82.** An adviser learns that a large client is about to place a substantial buy order in a mid-cap stock, and buys the stock for his own account fifteen minutes earlier. This is:\nA) Insider trading, dealt with under the PIT Regulations, 2015  B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003  C) Churning, dealt with under the IA Code of Conduct  D) Mis-selling, dealt with under Regulation 17\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Front-running, dealt with under the PFUTP Regulations, 2003**\n\n**Front-running** is dealing in a security **ahead of a substantial impending client order** of which one has unpublished knowledge. It is a deemed manipulative and unfair practice under **Regulation 4(2) of the SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003**. Memory hook: **front-run an ORDER, insider-trade on NEWS.**\n\n- **A) Insider trading, dealt with under the PIT Regulations, 2015** - wrong; insider trading uses **unpublished price-sensitive information about the company itself**, not knowledge of an order book.\n- **C) Churning, dealt with under the IA Code of Conduct** - wrong; churning is excessive turnover in the **client's** portfolio, not a trade in the adviser's own account.\n- **D) Mis-selling, dealt with under Regulation 17** - wrong; nothing unsuitable has been recommended to the client here.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q83.** An adviser recommends a complex market-linked debenture to a client without having researched the product or considered available alternatives. Which limb of his fiduciary duty under Regulation 15(1) has he primarily breached?\nA) The duty of loyalty  B) The duty of care  C) The duty of confidentiality  D) The duty of disclosure\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The duty of care**\n\nThe **duty of care** requires **competent, diligent and suitable** advice - know the client, research the product, consider alternatives, recommend what fits, and monitor it afterwards. Recommending something he has not researched is a failure of care. (Loyalty answers \"whose side are you on?\"; care answers \"did you do the work?\")\n\n- **A) The duty of loyalty** - wrong; loyalty concerns putting the client first and not profiting at his expense - undisclosed commission, front-running, misuse of client information. No such self-interest is alleged here.\n- **C) The duty of confidentiality** - wrong; no client information has been revealed or misused.\n- **D) The duty of disclosure** - wrong; disclosure is a component of loyalty and the Code of Conduct, but the defect described is the **absence of analysis**, not the absence of a disclosure.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q84.** A distributor's brochure claims \"225% return!\" for a scheme whose NAV rose from Rs 12 to Rs 39 over 8 years. What is the honest annualised (CAGR) figure?\nA) 15.87%  B) 12.50%  C) 28.13%  D) 225.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 15.87%**\n\nCAGR = (39 / 12) raised to the power (1/8), minus 1 = (3.25)^0.125 - 1 = 1.1587 - 1 = **15.87% per annum**. The \"225%\" headline is the arithmetically true **absolute** return over the whole 8 years ((39 - 12)/12), quoted without its period - the commonest form of return misrepresentation in India.\n\n- **B) 12.50%** - wrong; this is 100 divided by 8, an arbitrary figure with no basis in the data.\n- **C) 28.13%** - wrong; this simply divides the 225% absolute return by 8 years, which ignores compounding altogether.\n- **D) 225.00%** - wrong; this is the absolute return over 8 years, not an annual rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q85.** The annual compliance audit of a SEBI-registered Investment Adviser throws up adverse observations. Within what time must those observations, together with the action taken on them, be reported?\nA) Within 7 days of the audit report  B) Within one month of the date of the audit report  C) Within six months of the end of the financial year  D) Within one year of the audit report\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Within one month of the date of the audit report**\n\nThe audit must be conducted by a member of **ICAI or ICSI**. **Adverse findings, together with the action taken on them and duly approved by the management/board/proprietor, must be reported within one month of the date of the audit report.** Note that the reportable item is the finding **plus** the action taken - reporting the observation alone is incomplete.\n\n- **A) Within 7 days of the audit report** - wrong; no seven-day rule applies to this reporting.\n- **C) Within six months of the end of the financial year** - wrong; six months (i.e. by 30 September) is the deadline for **completing the audit**, not for reporting the action taken on adverse findings.\n- **D) Within one year of the audit report** - wrong; this is far outside the prescribed window and would defeat the purpose of contemporaneous remediation.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q86.** Under SCORES 2.0, within how many days must the entity complained against file its Action Taken Report?\nA) 7 calendar days  B) 15 calendar days  C) 21 calendar days  D) 30 calendar days\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 21 calendar days**\n\nThe SCORES 2.0 rhythm is **21 - 15 - 21 - 15 - 21**: the **entity has 21 calendar days** to file its Action Taken Report; the investor may seek a first review within 15 days; the **Designated Body** has 21 days; a second review may be sought within 15 days; and **SEBI** has 21 days. If the entity misses its 21 days, the complaint **auto-escalates** without the investor doing anything.\n\n- **A) 7 calendar days** - wrong; seven **working** days is the STR deadline under PMLA, not the SCORES clock.\n- **B) 15 calendar days** - wrong; 15 days is the window in which the **investor** may seek a review, not the entity's response time.\n- **D) 30 calendar days** - wrong; 30 days is the RBI Ombudsman escalation trigger and the IA's own grievance-redressal condition of registration, not the SCORES ATR deadline.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q87.** Dissatisfied with her adviser's fee billing, Sunita lodges a complaint on SCORES without ever having raised the matter with the adviser. What is the likely outcome?\nA) SEBI will investigate the billing and order compensation  B) The complaint is not maintainable until she has first taken it up with the adviser  C) SEBI will forward it directly to the Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) SEBI will refer it to the consumer commission on her behalf\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The complaint is not maintainable until she has first taken it up with the adviser**\n\nEvery Indian redress system follows the same ladder: **intermediary first, regulator next, tribunal or adjudicator last.** SCORES expressly requires that the investor has **first approached the entity** and received no reply, an unsatisfactory reply, or no action. A complaint that skips the firm is bounced back.\n\n- **A) SEBI will investigate the billing and order compensation** - wrong; SEBI **routes, tracks and escalates** complaints and enforces timelines. It does **not adjudicate individual money claims** - that is for an ombudsman, an ODR arbitrator, a consumer commission or a civil court.\n- **C) SEBI will forward it directly to the Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT is an **appellate** body against regulators' orders, not a complaint counter.\n- **D) SEBI will refer it to the consumer commission on her behalf** - wrong; SCORES does not transfer complaints to consumer fora; the investor chooses that route herself.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q88.** Sunita has a disputed money claim of Rs 3,20,000 against a stock broker. She has already complained to the broker and escalated through SCORES without a satisfactory outcome. What is the appropriate next step?\nA) File an appeal before the Securities Appellate Tribunal within 45 days  B) Initiate proceedings on the SMART ODR portal  C) File directly before the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission  D) Approach the RBI Integrated Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Initiate proceedings on the SMART ODR portal**\n\n**SCORES chases; ODR decides.** Having exhausted the market participant and SCORES, a **disputed money claim** goes to the **SMART ODR portal** (smartodr.in), introduced by SEBI's circular of 31 July 2023 - **online conciliation** first (non-binding, target about 21 days) and then **online arbitration**, producing a binding award enforceable under the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996.\n\n- **A) File an appeal before the Securities Appellate Tribunal within 45 days** - wrong; SAT hears appeals by a person **aggrieved by an ORDER of a regulator**. It is not a first-instance forum for an investor's claim against a broker.\n- **C) File directly before the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission** - wrong; even if the consumer route were used, a Rs 3.20 lakh claim would go to the **District Commission** (up to Rs 50 lakh under the 2021 Rules), not the National Commission.\n- **D) Approach the RBI Integrated Ombudsman** - wrong; RB-IOS 2021 covers banks, NBFCs, payment system participants and credit information companies, not stock brokers.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q89.** Under the RBI Integrated Ombudsman Scheme, 2021, what is the maximum total compensation the Ombudsman may award?\nA) Rs 1 lakh  B) Rs 20 lakh  C) Rs 21 lakh  D) Rs 30 lakh\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 21 lakh**\n\nThe Ombudsman may award up to **Rs 20 lakh for the actual loss suffered** **plus** a separate amount of up to **Rs 1 lakh** for loss of time, expenses, harassment and mental anguish. The two caps are **separate and additive**, giving a theoretical maximum of **Rs 21 lakh**.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh** - wrong; that is only the mental-agony and time component.\n- **B) Rs 20 lakh** - wrong; that is only the actual-loss component and ignores the additional Rs 1 lakh head.\n- **D) Rs 30 lakh** - wrong; Rs 30 lakh is the value limit of the **Insurance Ombudsman**, a different scheme.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q90.** Sunita's late husband's life insurer has partly repudiated a claim of Rs 26 lakh. She made a written representation to the insurer and received a reply she finds unsatisfactory. Which forum should she approach next?\nA) SCORES  B) The Insurance Ombudsman  C) The Securities Appellate Tribunal  D) The RBI Integrated Ombudsman\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The Insurance Ombudsman**\n\nShe has satisfied the precondition - a **written representation to the insurer** followed by an unsatisfactory reply. The Insurance Ombudsman (Insurance Ombudsman Rules, 2017, as amended in 2021; 17 offices) hears **partial or total repudiation of a claim** from an individual policyholder, is **free**, requires no lawyer, and covers claims **not exceeding Rs 30 lakh** - so her Rs 26 lakh claim is within the limit. The award binds the **insurer only**, leaving her courts open.\n\n- **A) SCORES** - wrong; SCORES is SEBI's portal for the **securities market**; matters within another regulator's purview are expressly excluded.\n- **C) The Securities Appellate Tribunal** - wrong; SAT does hear appeals against **IRDAI orders**, but it is not a first-instance forum for a policyholder's claim against an insurer.\n- **D) The RBI Integrated Ombudsman** - wrong; RB-IOS 2021 covers banks, NBFCs, payment system participants and credit information companies, not insurers.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section B — Caselets (6 caselets × 5 questions × 1 mark = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 1: Kavita Deshpande's household after a bereavement\n\nKavita Deshpande is 45 and teaches physics at a school in Kothrud, Pune. Her husband Anirudh died eight months ago. She now draws a salary of Rs 78,000 a month and a family pension of Rs 22,000 a month. Household expenses for her and her 13-year-old daughter Ira come to Rs 64,000 a month, of which Rs 23,680 is the EMI on the home loan on their flat. She holds Rs 1,45,000 in a savings account and Rs 2,75,000 in a liquid fund. Her total assets — the flat, the insurance and settlement proceeds she received, her gold and her financial investments — come to Rs 1,32,00,000, and the outstanding home loan is Rs 21,12,000. Because the household now runs on a single earner with a dependent child, her adviser wants the contingency fund built up to nine months of expenses.\n\n**Q91.** What is Kavita's monthly savings ratio?\nA) 36.00%  B) 46.15%  C) 56.25%  D) 64.00%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 36.00%**\n\nGross monthly income = 78,000 + 22,000 = Rs 1,00,000. Savings = 1,00,000 − 64,000 = Rs 36,000.\nSavings ratio = Savings ÷ Gross income = 36,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = **36.00%**.\nCheck: expenses ratio = 64,000 ÷ 1,00,000 = 64.00%, and 36.00 + 64.00 = 100 ✓\n\n- **B) 46.15%** - wrong; this uses only her salary (36,000 ÷ 78,000) and ignores the family pension, which is part of gross income.\n- **C) 56.25%** - wrong; this divides savings by expenses (36,000 ÷ 64,000) instead of by income.\n- **D) 64.00%** - wrong; this is the expenses ratio, the complement of the savings ratio.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q92.** What is her liquidity ratio?\nA) 2.27 months  B) 4.20 months  C) 6.56 months  D) 10.42 months\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 6.56 months**\n\nLiquid assets = 1,45,000 (savings account) + 2,75,000 (liquid fund) = Rs 4,20,000.\nLiquidity ratio = Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly expenses** = 4,20,000 ÷ 64,000 = **6.5625 = 6.56 months** — comfortably inside the healthy 3–6 month band, and just above it.\n\n- **A) 2.27 months** - wrong; this counts only the savings account (1,45,000 ÷ 64,000). A liquid fund redeems in one business day and is a liquid asset.\n- **B) 4.20 months** - wrong; this divides by gross income (4,20,000 ÷ 1,00,000). The liquidity ratio is measured against expenses, because expenses are what must be paid.\n- **D) 10.42 months** - wrong; this strips the EMI out of expenses (4,20,000 ÷ 40,320). The EMI still has to be paid during an emergency, so it stays in.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q93.** What is her solvency ratio?\nA) 0.16  B) 0.19  C) 0.84  D) 1.19\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 0.84**\n\nNet worth = Total assets − Total liabilities = 1,32,00,000 − 21,12,000 = Rs 1,10,88,000.\nSolvency ratio = Net worth ÷ Total assets = 1,10,88,000 ÷ 1,32,00,000 = **0.84**.\nCheck: leverage ratio = 21,12,000 ÷ 1,32,00,000 = 0.16, and solvency + leverage = 1 ✓\n\n- **A) 0.16** - wrong; this is the leverage (debt-to-assets) ratio, not solvency.\n- **B) 0.19** - wrong; this divides liabilities by net worth (21,12,000 ÷ 1,10,88,000), which is a debt-to-equity figure.\n- **D) 1.19** - wrong; this inverts the ratio (assets ÷ net worth). Solvency can never exceed 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q94.** What is her debt-to-income ratio?\nA) 23.68%  B) 30.36%  C) 37.00%  D) 40.32%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 23.68%**\n\nDebt-to-income ratio = Total EMIs ÷ Gross income = 23,680 ÷ 1,00,000 = **23.68%**, well inside the 35–40% comfort ceiling.\n\n- **B) 30.36%** - wrong; this uses only her salary as the denominator (23,680 ÷ 78,000).\n- **C) 37.00%** - wrong; this divides the EMI by expenses (23,680 ÷ 64,000) rather than by income.\n- **D) 40.32%** - wrong; this is the non-EMI expense burden (40,320 ÷ 1,00,000), not the debt burden.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q95.** How much more must Kavita set aside to reach the contingency fund her adviser recommends?\nA) Rs 1,56,000  B) Rs 3,84,000  C) Rs 4,80,000  D) Rs 5,76,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 1,56,000**\n\nTarget = 9 months × monthly **expenses** = 9 × 64,000 = Rs 5,76,000.\nExisting liquid assets = 1,45,000 + 2,75,000 = Rs 4,20,000.\nShortfall = 5,76,000 − 4,20,000 = **Rs 1,56,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 3,84,000** - wrong; this is a six-month target (6 × 64,000), not the nine months the adviser recommends, and it ignores what she already holds.\n- **C) Rs 4,80,000** - wrong; this sizes the fund against income (9 × 1,00,000 = 9,00,000, less 4,20,000). The contingency fund is sized against expenses.\n- **D) Rs 5,76,000** - wrong; this is the full nine-month target and ignores the Rs 4,20,000 already in place.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 2: The portfolio Kavita inherited\n\nAmong Anirudh's assets Kavita has inherited a securities portfolio. It contains 1,600 shares of Sahyadri Cements Ltd, currently quoted at Rs 845. The company reported an EPS of Rs 42.25, has a book value of Rs 325 per share and paid a dividend of Rs 12 per share. It also contains 40 bonds of Godavari Power Ltd — face value Rs 1,000 each, an annual coupon of 8%, exactly 4 years to maturity, a market price of Rs 940 and a modified duration of 3.35. Kavita's equity holdings across all shares in the portfolio are worth Rs 19,60,000 in total and carry a portfolio beta of 1.25. Her adviser is considering a short index-futures hedge; the Nifty futures contract is quoted at 24,500 with a lot size of 25.\n\n**Q96.** What is the price-to-earnings ratio of Sahyadri Cements?\nA) 2.60  B) 7.69  C) 20.00  D) 70.42\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 20.00**\n\nP/E = Market price ÷ EPS = 845 ÷ 42.25 = **20.00**.\n\n- **A) 2.60** - wrong; this is the price-to-book ratio (845 ÷ 325).\n- **B) 7.69** - wrong; this divides book value by EPS (325 ÷ 42.25), which is not a valuation ratio at all.\n- **D) 70.42** - wrong; this divides price by dividend per share (845 ÷ 12), the inverse of the dividend yield.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q97.** What is the dividend yield on the Sahyadri Cements shares?\nA) 1.42%  B) 3.69%  C) 5.00%  D) 28.40%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) 1.42%**\n\nDividend yield = DPS ÷ Market price = 12 ÷ 845 = 0.014201 = **1.42%**.\n\n- **B) 3.69%** - wrong; this divides the dividend by book value (12 ÷ 325), not by market price.\n- **C) 5.00%** - wrong; this is the earnings yield (42.25 ÷ 845 = 1 ÷ P/E), not the dividend yield.\n- **D) 28.40%** - wrong; this is the dividend payout ratio (12 ÷ 42.25), the share of earnings distributed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q98.** Using the approximate formula, what is the yield to maturity on the Godavari Power bonds?\nA) 8.00%  B) 8.51%  C) 9.79%  D) 10.11%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 9.79%**\n\nApproximate YTM = [C + (F − P) ÷ n] ÷ [(F + P) ÷ 2]\nCoupon C = 8% × 1,000 = Rs 80. Capital pull-to-par = (1,000 − 940) ÷ 4 = Rs 15 a year.\nAverage investment = (1,000 + 940) ÷ 2 = Rs 970.\nYTM ≈ (80 + 15) ÷ 970 = 95 ÷ 970 = 0.097938 = **9.79%**.\nSanity check: the bond trades at a discount, so coupon (8%) < current yield (8.51%) < YTM (9.79%) ✓\n\n- **A) 8.00%** - wrong; this is the coupon rate, which equals YTM only when the bond trades at par.\n- **B) 8.51%** - wrong; this is the current yield (80 ÷ 940), which ignores the Rs 60 capital gain to redemption.\n- **D) 10.11%** - wrong; this divides the annual return of Rs 95 by the market price (940) instead of by the average of price and face value.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q99.** If required yields rise by 60 basis points, what is the approximate percentage change in the price of the Godavari Power bond?\nA) −0.20%  B) −2.01%  C) −3.35%  D) +2.01%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) −2.01%**\n\n%ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −3.35 × 0.0060 = −0.0201 = **−2.01%**.\nIn rupees that is 940 × 0.0201 = Rs 18.89 a bond, taking the price to about Rs 921.11.\n\n- **A) −0.20%** - wrong; this uses 6 basis points (0.0006) instead of 60 basis points (0.0060).\n- **C) −3.35%** - wrong; this is the price change for a 100 basis point move, not a 60 basis point move.\n- **D) +2.01%** - wrong; the magnitude is right but the sign is not. Prices move **inversely** to yields, so a yield rise produces a price fall.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q100.** What index-futures position should the adviser take to fully hedge Kavita's equity holdings?\nA) Sell 3 contracts  B) Sell 4 contracts  C) Buy 4 contracts  D) Sell 5 contracts\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Sell 4 contracts**\n\nValue of one futures contract = 24,500 × 25 = Rs 6,12,500.\nBeta-adjusted exposure to be hedged = 19,60,000 × 1.25 = Rs 24,50,000.\nNumber of contracts = 24,50,000 ÷ 6,12,500 = **4**. A long equity portfolio is hedged by **selling** (going short) futures, so that a market fall produces a futures gain offsetting the portfolio loss.\n\n- **A) Sell 3 contracts** - wrong; this ignores the beta (19,60,000 ÷ 6,12,500 = 3.2). A portfolio with a beta of 1.25 moves more than the index and needs a proportionately larger hedge.\n- **C) Buy 4 contracts** - wrong; buying futures doubles the long exposure instead of hedging it.\n- **D) Sell 5 contracts** - wrong; this over-hedges and turns the position into a net short bet on the market.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 3: Kavita's first mutual fund investments\n\nKavita decides to place part of her lump sum in the Pratipada Flexi Cap Fund. The **direct plan** of the scheme holds total assets of Rs 4,286 crore, has liabilities of Rs 26 crore and 84 crore units outstanding. The direct plan's total expense ratio is 0.95% a year; the regular plan of the same scheme charges 1.85%. The scheme levies an exit load of 1% on redemptions made within 12 months of allotment. Kavita invests a lump sum of Rs 6,00,000 in the direct plan at today's NAV. Nine months later she redeems 4,000 units when the NAV is Rs 58.40. Separately, she runs a Rs 25,000 monthly SIP in another scheme, whose first four instalments were allotted at NAVs of Rs 50.00, Rs 62.50, Rs 40.00 and Rs 50.00.\n\n**Q101.** What is the NAV per unit of the direct plan?\nA) Rs 50.40  B) Rs 50.71  C) Rs 51.02  D) Rs 51.33\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50.71**\n\nNAV = (Total assets − Total liabilities) ÷ Units outstanding\n= (4,286 − 26) crore ÷ 84 crore = 4,260 ÷ 84 = **Rs 50.71** (50.7143).\n\n- **A) Rs 50.40** - wrong; this subtracts the Rs 26 crore of liabilities twice (4,234 ÷ 84).\n- **C) Rs 51.02** - wrong; this ignores liabilities altogether (4,286 ÷ 84). Net assets, not gross assets, drive NAV.\n- **D) Rs 51.33** - wrong; this adds the liabilities instead of subtracting them (4,312 ÷ 84).\n\n</details>\n\n**Q102.** How many units will Kavita's Rs 6,00,000 lump sum buy?\nA) 11,688.31  B) 11,759.24  C) 11,831.46  D) 11,903.63\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 11,831.46**\n\nUnits = Amount invested ÷ NAV = 6,00,000 ÷ 50.7143 = **11,831.46 units**.\n\n- **A) 11,688.31** - wrong; this uses Rs 51.33, the NAV obtained by adding liabilities instead of subtracting them.\n- **B) 11,759.24** - wrong; this uses Rs 51.02, the NAV obtained by ignoring liabilities.\n- **D) 11,903.63** - wrong; this uses Rs 50.40, the NAV obtained by double-counting liabilities.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q103.** What redemption proceeds will she receive on the 4,000 units sold in month nine?\nA) Rs 2,31,264  B) Rs 2,33,292  C) Rs 2,33,600  D) Rs 2,35,936\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,31,264**\n\nGross redemption value = 4,000 × 58.40 = Rs 2,33,600.\nThe redemption falls within 12 months, so the 1% exit load applies to the **redemption value**: 1% × 2,33,600 = Rs 2,336.\nNet proceeds = 2,33,600 − 2,336 = **Rs 2,31,264**.\n\n- **B) Rs 2,33,292** - wrong; this charges the 1% load on the gain only (4,000 × 7.69 = 30,760). Exit load is levied on the redemption value, not on the profit.\n- **C) Rs 2,33,600** - wrong; this is the gross value before the exit load is deducted.\n- **D) Rs 2,35,936** - wrong; this adds the load instead of deducting it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q104.** On an average holding of Rs 6,00,000, how much does Kavita save in a year by using the direct plan rather than the regular plan?\nA) Rs 5,400  B) Rs 5,700  C) Rs 11,100  D) Rs 16,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 5,400**\n\nTER difference = 1.85% − 0.95% = 0.90%.\nAnnual saving = 0.90% × 6,00,000 = **Rs 5,400**. The portfolio is identical in both plans; only the distribution commission embedded in the regular plan's TER differs, and it is that saving which compounds into the direct plan's higher NAV over time.\n\n- **B) Rs 5,700** - wrong; this is the direct plan's own annual cost (0.95% × 6,00,000), not the saving.\n- **C) Rs 11,100** - wrong; this is the regular plan's total annual cost (1.85% × 6,00,000), not the difference.\n- **D) Rs 16,800** - wrong; this adds the two expense ratios (2.80%) instead of taking the difference.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q105.** What is the average cost per unit of her first four SIP instalments?\nA) Rs 49.38  B) Rs 50.00  C) Rs 50.63  D) Rs 51.25\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 49.38**\n\nUnits bought: 25,000 ÷ 50.00 = 500; 25,000 ÷ 62.50 = 400; 25,000 ÷ 40.00 = 625; 25,000 ÷ 50.00 = 500. Total = **2,025 units**.\nTotal invested = 4 × 25,000 = Rs 1,00,000.\nAverage cost = 1,00,000 ÷ 2,025 = **Rs 49.38**.\nThe simple average of the four NAVs is (50 + 62.50 + 40 + 50) ÷ 4 = Rs 50.63, so the average cost is Rs 1.25 lower — that gap is rupee-cost averaging at work.\n\n- **B) Rs 50.00** - wrong; this is the NAV of the first and fourth instalments, not the average cost of all four.\n- **C) Rs 50.63** - wrong; this is the simple arithmetic average of the four NAVs. A fixed rupee SIP buys more units at low NAVs, so the average **cost** is always below the average **NAV**.\n- **D) Rs 51.25** - wrong; this weights the NAVs by their own level rather than by units bought, which reverses the averaging effect.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 4: Kavita considers a PMS and an AIF\n\nAfter keeping her contingency fund and her goal money aside, Kavita has an investible surplus of Rs 1,15,00,000. Her adviser puts three managed options in front of her. The first is a **discretionary PMS** charging a fixed fee of 1.5% a year plus a performance fee of 15% of returns above a hurdle rate of 9%, with no catch-up and charged on a high watermark basis. The second is a long-short **Category III AIF** with a corpus of Rs 260 crore. The third is a private equity **Category II AIF** with a corpus of Rs 140 crore. Kavita places Rs 80,00,000 in the PMS. In year 1 the portfolio grows to Rs 94,40,000 before fees, so the high watermark is set at Rs 94,40,000. In year 2 it falls to Rs 88,00,000, and in year 3 it recovers to Rs 97,00,000.\n\n**Q106.** What are the minimum investment amounts SEBI prescribes for a PMS and for an AIF respectively?\nA) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh  B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore  C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh  D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 1 crore**\n\nUnder the SEBI (Portfolio Managers) Regulations, 2020 the minimum client investment in a PMS is **Rs 50 lakh**. Under the SEBI (Alternative Investment Funds) Regulations, 2012 the minimum investment per investor in an AIF is **Rs 1 crore**. The ladder to remember is: mutual fund Rs 100–5,000 → PMS Rs 50 lakh → AIF Rs 1 crore.\n\n- **A) Rs 25 lakh and Rs 50 lakh** - wrong; Rs 25 lakh is the reduced AIF minimum for employees and directors of the AIF or its manager, and for angel investors.\n- **C) Rs 50 lakh and Rs 25 lakh** - wrong; the PMS figure is right but the AIF general minimum is Rs 1 crore, not Rs 25 lakh.\n- **D) Rs 1 crore and Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the portfolio manager's own minimum **net worth**, not a client ticket size.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q107.** What performance fee may the portfolio manager charge for year 1?\nA) Rs 1,08,000  B) Rs 1,20,000  C) Rs 2,16,000  D) Rs 14,16,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 1,08,000**\n\nGross gain = 94,40,000 − 80,00,000 = Rs 14,40,000, i.e. an 18% return.\nThe 9% hurdle absorbs the first 9% × 80,00,000 = Rs 7,20,000.\nReturn above the hurdle = 14,40,000 − 7,20,000 = Rs 7,20,000.\nWith **no catch-up**, the fee applies only to that excess: 15% × 7,20,000 = **Rs 1,08,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 1,20,000** - wrong; this is the fixed fee (1.5% × 80,00,000), a separate charge, not the performance fee.\n- **C) Rs 2,16,000** - wrong; this charges 15% on the entire gain of Rs 14,40,000 and ignores the hurdle.\n- **D) Rs 14,16,000** - wrong; this applies 15% to the closing portfolio value rather than to the excess return.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q108.** Ignoring the hurdle rate, on what amount of gain may a performance fee be charged in year 3 under the high watermark principle?\nA) Rs 2,60,000  B) Rs 9,00,000  C) Rs 17,00,000  D) Nil\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 2,60,000**\n\nThe high watermark is the highest value the portfolio has previously reached — **Rs 94,40,000** at the end of year 1. Year 2's fall to Rs 88,00,000 attracts no performance fee. In year 3 the fee applies only to the gain **above the watermark**: 97,00,000 − 94,40,000 = **Rs 2,60,000**.\n\n- **B) Rs 9,00,000** - wrong; this is the year-3 rise from Rs 88,00,000. Charging on it would make the client pay a second time for recovering ground already lost — exactly what the high watermark prevents.\n- **C) Rs 17,00,000** - wrong; this is the gain over the original Rs 80,00,000 investment, on part of which a fee has already been charged in year 1.\n- **D) Nil** - wrong; the portfolio has closed above the watermark, so a fee is payable on the excess.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q109.** What continuing interest must the sponsor or manager of the Category III AIF maintain in that fund?\nA) Rs 5 crore  B) Rs 6.5 crore  C) Rs 10 crore  D) Rs 13 crore\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 10 crore**\n\nFor a **Category III** AIF the sponsor or manager must maintain a continuing interest of the **lower of 5% of the corpus or Rs 10 crore**.\n5% × Rs 260 crore = Rs 13 crore; the lower of Rs 13 crore and Rs 10 crore is **Rs 10 crore**.\n\n- **A) Rs 5 crore** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the cap for Category I and Category II funds, which are not permitted to use leverage.\n- **B) Rs 6.5 crore** - wrong; this applies the Category I/II rate of 2.5% to the corpus. Category III doubles the rate to 5%.\n- **D) Rs 13 crore** - wrong; this is 5% of the corpus but ignores the word \"lower of\" — the Rs 10 crore ceiling caps it.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q110.** Which statement about the taxation of the two AIFs is correct?\nA) Both funds enjoy pass-through status, so Kavita is taxed on her share of income at her own rate  B) The Category II fund enjoys pass-through status while the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level  C) The Category III fund enjoys pass-through status while the Category II fund is taxed at the fund level  D) Neither fund enjoys pass-through status; both are taxed at the fund level\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) The Category II fund enjoys pass-through status while the Category III fund is taxed at the fund level**\n\nPass-through status under the Income Tax Act is granted to **Category I and Category II** AIFs only. Their income is deemed to arise directly to the investor, keeping its character (capital gains stay capital gains) and taxed at the investor's own rate. **Category III has no pass-through** — the fund is taxed before distributing, generally at the maximum marginal rate.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; Category III is expressly outside the pass-through regime.\n- **C)** - wrong; this reverses the rule. The memory hook is \"One and Two pass it through; Three pays for you.\"\n- **D)** - wrong; the Category II fund does enjoy pass-through status.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 5: Kavita's adviser and his compliance obligations\n\nKavita is advised by Nachiket Raibagi, an **individual** investment adviser registered with SEBI and a member of BASL, practising from Aundh in Pune. He currently has 138 clients and holds net tangible assets of Rs 6,40,000. He charges Kavita under the **assets under advice (AUA) mode**. Kavita's total AUA with him is Rs 1,15,00,000, of which Rs 15,00,000 sits in regular-plan schemes bought some years ago under a pre-existing distribution arrangement with Nachiket's wife, who holds an AMFI registration number. His wife has now suggested that she sell Kavita a regular-plan balanced advantage fund. Kavita is a first-time investor; the risk-profiling questionnaire she filled in scored her \"aggressive\" largely because she ticked that she wants to \"grow the money fast\".\n\n**Q111.** What is the maximum advisory fee Nachiket may charge Kavita in a year under the AUA mode?\nA) Rs 37,500  B) Rs 1,25,000  C) Rs 2,50,000  D) Rs 2,87,500\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Rs 2,50,000**\n\nAny portion of the assets held under a **pre-existing distribution arrangement** with the adviser or any entity in his group or family must be **deducted** from the AUA before the fee is computed.\nEligible AUA = 1,15,00,000 − 15,00,000 = Rs 1,00,00,000.\nMaximum fee = 2.5% × 1,00,00,000 = **Rs 2,50,000 a year, per family of client**.\n\n- **A) Rs 37,500** - wrong; this applies 2.5% only to the Rs 15,00,000 that must be excluded.\n- **B) Rs 1,25,000** - wrong; that is the cap under the **fixed fee** mode. Only one mode may apply to a client at a time, and here the AUA mode is in force.\n- **D) Rs 2,87,500** - wrong; this applies 2.5% to the whole Rs 1,15,00,000 without deducting the assets under the distribution arrangement.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q112.** What minimum net worth must Nachiket maintain, and does he comply?\nA) Rs 1 lakh; he complies  B) Rs 5 lakh; he complies  C) Rs 50 lakh; he does not comply  D) Rs 5 crore; he does not comply\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 5 lakh; he complies**\n\nAn **individual** investment adviser (or a partnership firm) must maintain **net tangible assets of not less than Rs 5 lakh** at all times. Nachiket holds Rs 6,40,000, so he complies with a cushion of Rs 1,40,000.\n\n- **A) Rs 1 lakh; he complies** - wrong; Rs 1 lakh was the pre-2020 requirement, and is also the individual research analyst's figure.\n- **C) Rs 50 lakh; he does not comply** - wrong; Rs 50 lakh is the net worth required of a **non-individual** investment adviser.\n- **D) Rs 5 crore; he does not comply** - wrong; Rs 5 crore is the minimum net worth of a **portfolio manager**, who handles client money rather than merely advising.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q113.** Nachiket expects to add about 20 clients over the next year. What does the IA Regulations require of him?\nA) He may continue indefinitely as an individual adviser with no further action  B) He must apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser once his client count exceeds 150, and stop onboarding fresh clients until that registration is granted  C) He must apply for registration as a portfolio manager once his client count exceeds 150  D) He must permanently cap his client base at 150\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) He must apply for registration as a non-individual investment adviser once his client count exceeds 150, and stop onboarding fresh clients until that registration is granted**\n\nThe corporatisation threshold in the IA Regulations requires an individual adviser whose number of clients **exceeds 150** to apply for registration as a **non-individual** investment adviser, and to stop taking on fresh clients in his individual capacity until that registration is obtained.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the 150-client threshold is a hard regulatory trigger, not a guideline.\n- **C)** - wrong; portfolio management is a different activity — managing client money, not advising on it. Growth in client numbers does not turn an adviser into a portfolio manager.\n- **D)** - wrong; the rule does not cap the practice; it requires a change in the form of registration.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q114.** May Nachiket's wife sell Kavita the regular-plan balanced advantage fund?\nA) Yes, provided the commission earned is disclosed to Kavita in writing  B) Yes, provided Kavita signs a consent letter acknowledging the conflict  C) No — the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's advisory clients  D) Yes, provided the distribution is routed through a separately identifiable department or division\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) No — the family of an individual investment adviser may not provide distribution services to the adviser's advisory clients**\n\nUnder the segregation rule, an **individual** investment adviser shall not provide distribution services at all, and the **family of the individual adviser shall not provide distribution services to the adviser's clients**. The client's family is likewise treated as one unit, so the arrangement cannot be rerouted through Kavita's daughter either.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; disclosure does not cure a prohibited arrangement. Regulation 15(2) also bars any consideration from a person other than the client being advised.\n- **B)** - wrong; client consent cannot override a statutory prohibition.\n- **D)** - wrong; the separately identifiable department route is available only to **non-individual** advisers, and even then never to the same client who receives advice.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q115.** How should Nachiket treat the \"aggressive\" score the questionnaire produced for Kavita?\nA) Act on it and advise a 90% equity allocation, since the tool has classified her  B) Assess both her willingness and her ability to bear risk, and advise within the lower of the two  C) Have her sign a waiver accepting higher risk and then proceed with the aggressive allocation  D) Decline to advise her until she has at least three years of investing experience\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Assess both her willingness and her ability to bear risk, and advise within the lower of the two**\n\nRegulation 16 requires the adviser to assess the risk the client is **willing and able** to take, to assess her **capacity to absorb loss**, to interpret responses appropriately without attributing inappropriate weight to any one answer, and to ensure the questionnaire is not misleading or leading. Regulation 17 then requires the advice to be **suitable** — matched to that profile. A recently widowed sole earner with a dependent child and no investing experience has limited **ability** to bear risk whatever her stated willingness, and the lower of the two governs.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; the tool's output is an input to the adviser's judgement, not a substitute for it. The regulations expressly require the adviser to check that the tool is fit for purpose.\n- **C)** - wrong; a waiver does not discharge the fiduciary duty or the suitability obligation.\n- **D)** - wrong; there is no experience requirement for a client. Lack of experience is a factor in choosing suitable products, not a bar to advice.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 6: Rebalancing after a change in circumstances\n\nWhile Anirudh was alive the couple's Investment Policy Statement set a strategic asset allocation of 65% equity, 30% debt and 5% gold, with tolerance bands of plus or minus 5 percentage points around each target. After his death Kavita and her adviser rewrote the IPS: the new strategic allocation is 45% equity, 45% debt and 10% gold, with the same plus or minus 5 percentage point bands. Her combined portfolio is currently worth Rs 1,20,00,000, made up of Rs 66,00,000 in equity funds, Rs 42,00,000 in debt funds and Rs 12,00,000 in gold. A bank fixed deposit of Rs 9,00,000 matures in three months, and she will begin a Rs 25,000 monthly SIP from next month.\n\n**Q116.** What is the current weight of equity in the portfolio, and is it inside the IPS band?\nA) 55.00%, inside the band  B) 55.00%, outside the band  C) 50.00%, at the upper edge of the band  D) 45.00%, inside the band\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) 55.00%, outside the band**\n\nEquity weight = 66,00,000 ÷ 1,20,00,000 = **55.00%**.\nThe new target is 45% with a band of 40% to 50%. At 55% the equity sleeve is **10 percentage points above target and 5 percentage points outside the upper band**, which triggers a rebalance.\n\n- **A) 55.00%, inside the band** - wrong; the weight is right but 55% lies beyond the 50% upper limit.\n- **C) 50.00%, at the upper edge of the band** - wrong; 50% is the upper band limit, not the actual weight.\n- **D) 45.00%, inside the band** - wrong; 45% is the target, not the current weight.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q117.** How much equity must be sold to bring the portfolio back to its strategic target weight?\nA) Rs 6,00,000  B) Rs 12,00,000  C) Rs 18,00,000  D) Rs 24,00,000\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 12,00,000**\n\nTarget equity value = 45% × 1,20,00,000 = Rs 54,00,000.\nCurrent equity value = Rs 66,00,000.\nAmount to sell = 66,00,000 − 54,00,000 = **Rs 12,00,000**. Rebalancing restores the **target**, not merely the band edge.\n\n- **A) Rs 6,00,000** - wrong; this trims equity only to the 50% band edge (Rs 60,00,000), leaving the portfolio riskier than the IPS specifies.\n- **C) Rs 18,00,000** - wrong; this takes equity down to Rs 48,00,000, which is 40% — the **lower** band edge, an over-correction.\n- **D) Rs 24,00,000** - wrong; this reduces equity to Rs 42,00,000 (35%), matching the current debt sleeve rather than the strategic target.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q118.** Once that equity is sold, how should the proceeds be deployed across debt and gold?\nA) Rs 12,00,000 to debt and nil to gold  B) Rs 6,00,000 to debt and Rs 6,00,000 to gold  C) Rs 12,00,000 to gold and nil to debt  D) Rs 8,00,000 to debt and Rs 4,00,000 to gold\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 12,00,000 to debt and nil to gold**\n\nTarget debt = 45% × 1,20,00,000 = Rs 54,00,000 against a current Rs 42,00,000 — a shortfall of **Rs 12,00,000**, and at 35% the debt sleeve is outside its 40–50% band.\nTarget gold = 10% × 1,20,00,000 = Rs 12,00,000, which is exactly what she already holds. Gold needs **nothing**.\n\n- **B) Rs 6,00,000 to debt and Rs 6,00,000 to gold** - wrong; this would push gold to Rs 18,00,000, or 15%, five percentage points outside its band.\n- **C) Rs 12,00,000 to gold and nil to debt** - wrong; this leaves debt far below target and takes gold to 20%.\n- **D) Rs 8,00,000 to debt and Rs 4,00,000 to gold** - wrong; debt would still fall short of target and gold would overshoot.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q119.** How is the move from a 65:30:5 allocation to a 45:45:10 allocation best described?\nA) A tactical asset allocation shift  B) A strategic asset allocation change  C) A breach of the Investment Policy Statement  D) A routine rebalancing trade\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) A strategic asset allocation change**\n\nThe clean test is: if the reason for the change is something that happened in the client's **life**, it is strategic; if it is something that happened in the **market**, it is tactical. The loss of a second income and the shift to a single earner with a dependent child has permanently lowered Kavita's risk capacity, so the long-term policy mix itself is rewritten.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; tactical asset allocation is a deliberate, **temporary** deviation from the strategic weights to exploit a perceived short-term mispricing, and it lives inside the IPS bands.\n- **C)** - wrong; the IPS was formally revised with the client. A breach is a move outside the bands **without** revising the IPS.\n- **D)** - wrong; rebalancing restores existing target weights. Here the targets themselves have changed.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q120.** Which course of action would restore the target weights at the lowest tax and transaction cost?\nA) Redeem the entire equity portfolio and rebuild it in the new proportions  B) Direct the maturing Rs 9,00,000 deposit and the new SIP instalments into the underweight debt sleeve, selling equity only for the remaining gap  C) Borrow against the equity holdings to buy debt funds and avoid selling anything  D) Take no action and wait for equity markets to fall until the weights correct themselves\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Direct the maturing Rs 9,00,000 deposit and the new SIP instalments into the underweight debt sleeve, selling equity only for the remaining gap**\n\nRebalancing with **new money** — fresh lump sums, maturing deposits and redirected SIP instalments — moves the weights with **no selling, no capital gains tax and no exit load**. It is the cheapest rebalancing tool an adviser has, and here it covers Rs 9,00,000 of the Rs 12,00,000 gap immediately.\n\n- **A)** - wrong; a full redemption crystallises the entire unrealised gain and incurs the maximum transaction cost for a change that needs only a Rs 12,00,000 shift.\n- **C)** - wrong; leverage raises portfolio risk at exactly the moment the client's risk capacity has fallen, and the interest cost is a certain drag against an uncertain return.\n- **D)** - wrong; rebalancing is a **risk-control** discipline. Waiting leaves her carrying 55% equity — a risk level she and her adviser have already agreed is too high.\n\n</details>\n\n## Section C — Caselets (3 caselets × 5 questions × 2 marks = 30 marks)\n\n### Case 7: Kavita's goal-based plan\n\nKavita's daughter Ira is 13. Her postgraduate education is expected to begin in exactly 6 years and would cost Rs 18,00,000 at today's prices; education inflation is assumed at 8% a year. Kavita has already earmarked Rs 6,00,000 for this goal in a balanced fund expected to earn 9% a year. Any shortfall will be met by a monthly SIP earning 11% a year compounded monthly, running for the full 6 years. Separately, her home loan has an outstanding balance of Rs 21,12,000 at 8.7% a year with exactly 144 monthly instalments remaining. Her bank has offered her a top-up loan of Rs 8,00,000 at 9.5% a year for 7 years to renovate the flat. Her gross monthly income is Rs 1,00,000, and her adviser applies a ceiling of 40% of gross monthly income on total EMIs.\n\n**Q121.** What will Ira's postgraduate education cost on the day it is needed?\nA) Rs 26,64,000  B) Rs 28,56,374  C) Rs 30,18,780  D) Rs 30,84,884\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 28,56,374**\n\nFuture cost = Today's cost × (1 + inflation)^n = 18,00,000 × (1.08)^6.\n(1.08)^6 = 1.5868743.\n18,00,000 × 1.5868743 = **Rs 28,56,374**.\n\n- **A) Rs 26,64,000** - wrong; this applies simple interest, 18,00,000 × (1 + 0.08 × 6). Inflation compounds.\n- **C) Rs 30,18,780** - wrong; this inflates at 9%, the expected **return** on the earmarked fund, instead of at the 8% education inflation rate.\n- **D) Rs 30,84,884** - wrong; this compounds for 7 years instead of 6.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q122.** After allowing for the money already earmarked, what is the shortfall Kavita must fund?\nA) Rs 10,06,260  B) Rs 18,50,114  C) Rs 22,56,374  D) Rs 28,56,374\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: B) Rs 18,50,114**\n\nStep 1 — the earmarked corpus grows: 6,00,000 × (1.09)^6 = 6,00,000 × 1.6771001 = Rs 10,06,260.\nStep 2 — the gap: 28,56,374 − 10,06,260 = **Rs 18,50,114**.\n\n- **A) Rs 10,06,260** - wrong; this is the grown value of the earmarked money, not the shortfall.\n- **C) Rs 22,56,374** - wrong; this subtracts the Rs 6,00,000 at its **present** value and ignores six years of growth on it.\n- **D) Rs 28,56,374** - wrong; this is the full future cost and takes no credit at all for the money already set aside.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q123.** What level monthly SIP, earning 11% a year compounded monthly for 6 years, will close that shortfall?\nA) Rs 18,256  B) Rs 19,484  C) Rs 25,696  D) Rs 28,186\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 18,256**\n\nConvert the period first: r = 11% ÷ 12 = 0.0091667 a month, n = 6 × 12 = 72 months.\n(1.0091667)^72 = 1.928949.\nPMT = FV × r ÷ [(1+r)^n − 1] = 18,50,114 × 0.0091667 ÷ 0.928949\n= 16,959 ÷ 0.928949 = **Rs 18,256 a month** (spreadsheet: `=ABS(PMT(11%/12, 72, 0, 1850114))`).\n\n- **B) Rs 19,484** - wrong; this uses **annual** compounding, dividing the annual instalment of Rs 2,33,809 by 12. The rate and the number of periods must describe the same period.\n- **C) Rs 25,696** - wrong; this simply divides the shortfall by 72 months and gives the investment no return at all.\n- **D) Rs 28,186** - wrong; this funds the **entire** future cost of Rs 28,56,374 and ignores the Rs 6,00,000 already earmarked.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q124.** How much interest will Kavita pay over the remaining 144 instalments of the home loan?\nA) Rs 12,97,920  B) Rs 22,04,928  C) Rs 34,09,920  D) Rs 55,21,920\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 12,97,920**\n\nStep 1 — the EMI. r = 8.7% ÷ 12 = 0.00725, n = 144, (1.00725)^144 = 2.829895.\nEMI = 21,12,000 × [0.00725 × 2.829895] ÷ [2.829895 − 1] = 43,331 ÷ 1.829895 = **Rs 23,680** a month.\nStep 2 — the interest. Total repaid = 23,680 × 144 = Rs 34,09,920.\nInterest = 34,09,920 − 21,12,000 = **Rs 12,97,920**.\n\n- **B) Rs 22,04,928** - wrong; this applies simple interest, 21,12,000 × 8.7% × 12 years, ignoring the fact that the principal is being repaid month by month.\n- **C) Rs 34,09,920** - wrong; this is the **total repaid**, principal included — the classic \"wrong quantity\" answer.\n- **D) Rs 55,21,920** - wrong; this adds the outstanding principal to the total repayment, counting the principal twice.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q125.** If Kavita takes the Rs 8,00,000 top-up loan, how will her total EMI burden stand against her adviser's 40% ceiling?\nA) It would fall short of the ceiling by about Rs 3,245  B) It would fall short of the ceiling by about Rs 16,320  C) It would exceed the ceiling by about Rs 3,245  D) It would exceed the ceiling by about Rs 6,755\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) It would fall short of the ceiling by about Rs 3,245**\n\nStep 1 — the top-up EMI. r = 9.5% ÷ 12 = 0.0079167, n = 7 × 12 = 84, (1.0079167)^84 = 1.93944.\nEMI = 8,00,000 × [0.0079167 × 1.93944] ÷ [1.93944 − 1] = 12,283 ÷ 0.93944 = **Rs 13,075** a month.\nStep 2 — the test. Total EMIs = 23,680 + 13,075 = Rs 36,755.\nCeiling = 40% × 1,00,000 = Rs 40,000.\nHeadroom remaining = 40,000 − 36,755 = **about Rs 3,245**, so the top-up just fits — though with very little margin for a rate rise.\n\n- **B) It would fall short of the ceiling by about Rs 16,320** - wrong; Rs 16,320 is the headroom **before** the top-up (40,000 − 23,680) and takes no account of the new EMI.\n- **C) It would exceed the ceiling by about Rs 3,245** - wrong; the magnitude is right but the direction is reversed. Rs 36,755 is below Rs 40,000, not above it.\n- **D) It would exceed the ceiling by about Rs 6,755** - wrong; this applies a 30% ceiling (Rs 30,000) rather than the 40% the adviser uses.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 8: Choosing a manager for the equity sleeve\n\nKavita's adviser has put three funds through a five-year risk-and-return screen. Over the period the risk-free rate averaged 6.5% a year and the benchmark, the Nifty 500 TRI, returned 12.0% a year. Warkari Bluechip Fund returned 15.4% a year with a standard deviation of 14.0% and a beta of 0.95. Deccan Opportunities Fund returned 17.2% a year with a standard deviation of 20.5% and a beta of 1.40. Godavari Balanced Fund returned 11.8% a year with a standard deviation of 8.0% and a beta of 0.62. Separately, the adviser is modelling a two-asset portfolio for the rest of her money: 65% in an equity fund with a standard deviation of 18% and 35% in a debt fund with a standard deviation of 6%, the correlation between them being 0.25.\n\n**Q126.** Which fund has the highest Sharpe ratio, and what is it?\nA) Warkari Bluechip, 0.64  B) Deccan Opportunities, 0.52  C) Godavari Balanced, 0.66  D) Godavari Balanced, 1.48\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) Godavari Balanced, 0.66**\n\nSharpe = (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp.\nWarkari: (15.4 − 6.5) ÷ 14.0 = 8.9 ÷ 14.0 = **0.64**\nDeccan: (17.2 − 6.5) ÷ 20.5 = 10.7 ÷ 20.5 = **0.52**\nGodavari: (11.8 − 6.5) ÷ 8.0 = 5.3 ÷ 8.0 = **0.66** — the highest.\nThe lowest-returning fund wins on Sharpe because its total risk is barely a third of Deccan's.\n\n- **A) Warkari Bluechip, 0.64** - wrong; the ratio is correctly computed but it is second, not first.\n- **B) Deccan Opportunities, 0.52** - wrong; the highest raw return carries the lowest Sharpe ratio here, which is exactly the point of a risk-adjusted measure.\n- **D) Godavari Balanced, 1.48** - wrong; this is 11.8 ÷ 8.0, computed without first subtracting the risk-free rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q127.** Which fund has the highest Treynor ratio, and what is it?\nA) Warkari Bluechip, 9.37%  B) Deccan Opportunities, 7.64%  C) Godavari Balanced, 8.55%  D) Warkari Bluechip, 16.21%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Warkari Bluechip, 9.37%**\n\nTreynor = (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp.\nWarkari: 8.9 ÷ 0.95 = **9.37%** — the highest\nDeccan: 10.7 ÷ 1.40 = **7.64%**\nGodavari: 5.3 ÷ 0.62 = **8.55%**\nNote that Sharpe and Treynor **disagree**: Godavari wins on total risk, Warkari on systematic risk. That happens when a fund carries a lot of diversifiable risk relative to its beta, and it is the classic reason for choosing the measure that matches the use — Sharpe for a standalone portfolio, Treynor for one sleeve of an already-diversified one.\n\n- **B) Deccan Opportunities, 7.64%** - wrong; the ratio is correct but it is the lowest of the three.\n- **C) Godavari Balanced, 8.55%** - wrong; the ratio is correct but Godavari wins on Sharpe, not on Treynor.\n- **D) Warkari Bluechip, 16.21%** - wrong; this is 15.4 ÷ 0.95, computed without subtracting the risk-free rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q128.** What is Jensen's alpha for Deccan Opportunities Fund?\nA) +3.00%  B) +5.20%  C) +7.70%  D) +10.70%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) +3.00%**\n\nStep 1 — the CAPM required return: Rf + β(Rm − Rf) = 6.5 + 1.40 × (12.0 − 6.5) = 6.5 + 1.40 × 5.5 = 6.5 + 7.7 = **14.2%**.\nStep 2 — the alpha: 17.2 − 14.2 = **+3.00%**.\n\n- **B) +5.20%** - wrong; this is the excess over the benchmark (17.2 − 12.0) and makes no adjustment for the fund's beta of 1.40.\n- **C) +7.70%** - wrong; this is the beta-adjusted market risk premium (1.40 × 5.5), a component of the calculation rather than the answer.\n- **D) +10.70%** - wrong; this is the raw excess return over the risk-free rate (17.2 − 6.5), which is the numerator of Sharpe and Treynor, not alpha.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q129.** What is the standard deviation of the 65:35 two-asset portfolio the adviser is modelling?\nA) 1.54%  B) 11.89%  C) 12.39%  D) 13.80%\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: C) 12.39%**\n\nσp = √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ)\n= √(0.65² × 0.18² + 0.35² × 0.06² + 2 × 0.65 × 0.35 × 0.18 × 0.06 × 0.25)\n= √(0.0136890 + 0.0004410 + 0.0012285)\n= √0.0153585 = **12.39%**.\nThe weighted average of the two standard deviations is 0.65 × 18 + 0.35 × 6 = 13.80%, so 1.41 percentage points of risk have disappeared purely because the correlation is below 1.\n\n- **A) 1.54%** - wrong; this is the portfolio **variance** (0.0153585), not the standard deviation. The square root has been omitted.\n- **B) 11.89%** - wrong; this assumes zero correlation and so drops the covariance term entirely.\n- **D) 13.80%** - wrong; this is the weighted average of the two standard deviations. Portfolio return is a weighted average; portfolio **risk** is not, whenever correlation is below 1.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q130.** Ranked by Jensen's alpha from highest to lowest, what is the correct order of the three funds?\nA) Warkari Bluechip, Deccan Opportunities, Godavari Balanced  B) Deccan Opportunities, Warkari Bluechip, Godavari Balanced  C) Godavari Balanced, Warkari Bluechip, Deccan Opportunities  D) Deccan Opportunities, Godavari Balanced, Warkari Bluechip\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Warkari Bluechip, Deccan Opportunities, Godavari Balanced**\n\nCAPM required return = 6.5 + β × 5.5 in each case:\nWarkari: 6.5 + 0.95 × 5.5 = 11.73%; alpha = 15.4 − 11.73 = **+3.68%**\nDeccan: 6.5 + 1.40 × 5.5 = 14.20%; alpha = 17.2 − 14.20 = **+3.00%**\nGodavari: 6.5 + 0.62 × 5.5 = 9.91%; alpha = 11.8 − 9.91 = **+1.89%**\nOrder: **Warkari (+3.68%) → Deccan (+3.00%) → Godavari (+1.89%)**. All three added value over CAPM, but the highest raw return did not produce the highest alpha because it was bought with the most beta.\n\n- **B)** - wrong; this ranks on raw return, putting Deccan first. Its 1.40 beta means CAPM already expected 14.2% from it.\n- **C)** - wrong; this is close to the Sharpe ranking, not the alpha ranking. Sharpe divides by total risk; alpha compares against a beta-adjusted CAPM benchmark.\n- **D)** - wrong; Godavari has the **lowest** alpha of the three, not the second highest.\n\n</details>\n\n### Case 9: The debt sleeve and a derivative overlay\n\nKavita's adviser is working through the fixed income and derivative parts of her portfolio. Bond X has a face value of Rs 1,000, pays a 9% annual coupon, has exactly 5 years to maturity and carries a required yield of 11%. The adviser also models what happens a year from now, when 4 years remain and the required yield has fallen to 9.5%. Bond Y is a separate holding worth Rs 24,00,000 at present with a modified duration of 6.8; the adviser stresses it for a 75 basis point rise in yields. On the derivatives side the Nifty spot is 24,800 and the lot size is 25. Kavita has bought one Nifty 24,900 call at a premium of Rs 185. She also holds an index-tracking equity portfolio worth Rs 24,60,000 with a beta of 1.0, protected by four Nifty 24,600 put contracts bought at a premium of Rs 142 each. Two expiry scenarios are examined: **Scenario 1**, the Nifty at 25,340, and **Scenario 2**, the Nifty at 23,700.\n\n**Q131.** What is the fair price of Bond X today?\nA) Rs 926.08  B) Rs 962.09  C) Rs 1,000.00  D) Rs 1,077.79\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 926.08**\n\nA bond is an annuity of coupons plus a lump sum at redemption.\n(1.11)^5 = 1.6850582, so the discount factor is 1 ÷ 1.6850582 = 0.5934513.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 0.5934513) ÷ 0.11 = 3.6959.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.6959 = Rs 332.63\nPV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.5934513 = Rs 593.45\nPrice = **Rs 926.08**. Coupon (9%) is below the required yield (11%), so the bond must trade at a **discount** ✓\n\n- **B) Rs 962.09** - wrong; this discounts at 10%, the midpoint of the coupon and the yield, rather than at the required yield of 11%.\n- **C) Rs 1,000.00** - wrong; a bond prices at par only when the coupon equals the required yield.\n- **D) Rs 1,077.79** - wrong; this swaps the coupon and the yield, pricing an 11% coupon at a 9% yield, and so produces a premium instead of a discount.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q132.** A year from now, with 4 years to maturity and the required yield down to 9.5%, what will Bond X be worth and what capital gain will that represent per bond?\nA) Rs 983.97, a gain of Rs 57.89  B) Rs 983.97, a loss of Rs 57.89  C) Rs 962.09, a gain of Rs 36.01  D) Rs 1,000.00, a gain of Rs 73.92\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 983.97, a gain of Rs 57.89**\n\nStep 1 — reprice at the new yield and the shorter maturity. (1.095)^4 = 1.4376610, so the discount factor is 0.6955730.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 0.6955730) ÷ 0.095 = 3.2044947.\nPV of coupons = 90 × 3.2044947 = Rs 288.40; PV of redemption = 1,000 × 0.6955730 = Rs 695.57.\nNew price = **Rs 983.97**.\nStep 2 — the gain: 983.97 − 926.08 = **Rs 57.89 a bond**. Falling yields lift bond prices, and the bond is still at a small discount because 9% coupon < 9.5% yield ✓\n\n- **B) Rs 983.97, a loss of Rs 57.89** - wrong; the price is right but the direction is reversed. A fall in yields produces a capital **gain**.\n- **C) Rs 962.09, a gain of Rs 36.01** - wrong; Rs 962.09 comes from discounting five years of cash flows at 10%, not four years at 9.5%.\n- **D) Rs 1,000.00, a gain of Rs 73.92** - wrong; the bond would price at par only if the required yield fell all the way to the 9% coupon rate.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q133.** If yields rise by 75 basis points, what happens to the Rs 24,00,000 holding of Bond Y?\nA) A loss of about Rs 1,22,400, leaving about Rs 22,77,600  B) A loss of about Rs 12,240, leaving about Rs 23,87,760  C) A loss of about Rs 1,63,200, leaving about Rs 22,36,800  D) A gain of about Rs 1,22,400, taking it to about Rs 25,22,400\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A loss of about Rs 1,22,400, leaving about Rs 22,77,600**\n\nStep 1 — the percentage impact: %ΔP ≈ −Modified duration × Δy = −6.8 × 0.0075 = **−5.10%**.\nStep 2 — the rupee impact: 24,00,000 × 5.10% = **Rs 1,22,400**, leaving 24,00,000 − 1,22,400 = **Rs 22,77,600**.\n\n- **B) A loss of about Rs 12,240** - wrong; this uses 7.5 basis points instead of 75 basis points.\n- **C) A loss of about Rs 1,63,200** - wrong; this applies a full 100 basis point move (−6.8%) rather than 75 basis points.\n- **D) A gain of about Rs 1,22,400** - wrong; the magnitude is right but the sign is not. Rising yields cut bond prices.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q134.** Under Scenario 1, what is Kavita's net profit on the 24,900 call, and where was its break-even?\nA) Rs 6,375, break-even 25,085  B) Rs 11,000, break-even 24,900  C) Rs 6,375, break-even 24,715  D) Rs 13,500, break-even 25,085\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) Rs 6,375, break-even 25,085**\n\nStep 1 — intrinsic value at expiry = max(Spot − Strike, 0) = 25,340 − 24,900 = 440 points.\nNet gain per unit = 440 − 185 (premium) = 255 points; × lot size 25 = **Rs 6,375**.\nStep 2 — break-even for a long call = Strike + Premium = 24,900 + 185 = **25,085**. The Nifty closed 255 points above it, which is exactly the 255-point profit per unit ✓\n\n- **B) Rs 11,000, break-even 24,900** - wrong; Rs 11,000 is the gross payoff (440 × 25) with the premium never recovered, and the strike is not the break-even.\n- **C) Rs 6,375, break-even 24,715** - wrong; the profit is right but the premium has been **subtracted** from the strike. That is the break-even formula for a long **put**.\n- **D) Rs 13,500, break-even 25,085** - wrong; Rs 13,500 measures the move from the spot of 24,800 rather than from the 24,900 strike, and ignores the premium.\n\n</details>\n\n**Q135.** Under Scenario 2, what is the net outcome on Kavita's Rs 24,60,000 index-tracking portfolio together with its four protective puts?\nA) A net loss of about Rs 14,200  B) A net loss of about Rs 90,000  C) A net loss of about Rs 1,04,200  D) A net gain of about Rs 75,800\n<details><summary>Answer & Explanation</summary>\n\n**Correct: A) A net loss of about Rs 14,200**\n\nStep 1 — the portfolio. The index falls from 24,600 (the strike, and the level at which the portfolio was worth Rs 24,60,000) to 23,700, a fall of 900 points or 3.66%. With a beta of 1.0 the portfolio loses 24,60,000 × 900 ÷ 24,600 = **Rs 90,000**.\nStep 2 — the puts. Payoff = max(Strike − Spot, 0) = 24,600 − 23,700 = 900 points, × 25 per lot × 4 lots = **Rs 90,000 gain**.\nPremium paid = 142 × 25 × 4 = **Rs 14,200**.\nNet = −90,000 + 90,000 − 14,200 = **a loss of Rs 14,200**, the cost of the insurance. The portfolio ends at about Rs 24,45,800 instead of Rs 23,70,000.\n\n- **B) A net loss of about Rs 90,000** - wrong; this is the unhedged portfolio loss and gives no credit for the put payoff.\n- **C) A net loss of about Rs 1,04,200** - wrong; this adds the premium to the portfolio loss but forgets that the puts finished deep in the money and paid out Rs 90,000.\n- **D) A net gain of about Rs 75,800** - wrong; this counts the put payoff but omits the offsetting fall in the portfolio it was bought to protect.\n\n</details>\n"}], "primer": "# 🔢 Financial Maths Primer — every formula in the NISM X-A syllabus\n\n> **One page to rule the numerical half of the exam.**\n> Module 1 alone is **37 marks** and is almost entirely arithmetic. Modules 3 and 5 add bond pricing, option payoffs and risk-adjusted returns. Everything numerical in the paper is on this page.\n\n**How to use it:** don't read this once. Work each example with a spreadsheet open until you can reproduce it without looking. Then use the Quick Reference at the end as your revision sheet.\n\n---\n\n## 0. The two habits that prevent most lost marks\n\n**Habit 1 — convert the period first.** Before touching a formula, ask: *is this annual or monthly?*\n\n| If the question says | Then r = | And n = |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12% a year, 5 years, **annual** | 0.12 | 5 |\n| 12% a year, 5 years, **monthly** | 0.12 ÷ 12 = **0.01** | 5 × 12 = **60** |\n| 12% a year, 6 years, **half-yearly** | 0.12 ÷ 2 = **0.06** | 6 × 2 = **12** |\n| 10% a year, 8 years, **quarterly** | 0.10 ÷ 4 = **0.025** | 8 × 4 = **32** |\n\n> ⚠️ Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods is the single most common error in the whole paper. **r and n must always describe the same period.**\n\n**Habit 2 — read what is actually being asked.** Distractors are built from the *near misses*:\n\n| Asked for | Common distractor offered |\n|---|---|\n| The EMI | The **first month's interest**, or principal ÷ n |\n| The corpus | The **total contributed** (ignoring returns) |\n| The interest paid | The **total repaid** (including principal) |\n| The gap/shortfall | The **grown corpus** itself |\n| The real return | Nominal **minus** inflation (crude subtraction) |\n| CAGR | Total gain ÷ years (simple-interest thinking) |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Time Value of Money — the engine (Chapter 2)\n\nEverything below is one equation rearranged five ways.\n\n$$FV = PV \\times (1+r)^n$$\n\n| Solve for | Formula | Excel |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Future value** | FV = PV × (1+r)ⁿ | `=FV(rate,nper,pmt,pv,type)` |\n| **Present value** | PV = FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ | `=PV(rate,nper,pmt,fv,type)` |\n| **Rate (CAGR)** | r = (FV ÷ PV)^(1/n) − 1 | `=RATE(nper,pmt,pv,fv)` |\n| **Periods** | n = ln(FV ÷ PV) ÷ ln(1+r) | `=NPER(rate,pmt,pv,fv)` |\n\n**Worked:** ₹4,00,000 grows to ₹10,00,000 in 8 years.\nr = (10,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000)^(1/8) − 1 = 2.5^0.125 − 1 = 1.1214 − 1 = **12.14% p.a.**\n\n### The Rule of 72\n\n$$\\text{Years to double} \\approx \\frac{72}{\\text{rate }\\%}$$\n\n| Rate | Doubles in | Rate | Doubles in |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 6% | 12 years | 9% | 8 years |\n| 7.2% | 10 years | 12% | 6 years |\n| 8% | 9 years | 18% | 4 years |\n\nUse it **in reverse** as a sanity check: \"money quadrupled in 12 years\" = two doublings = 6 years each ≈ 12% p.a.\n\n### Simple vs compound\n\n| | Formula | ₹1,00,000 at 10% for 20 years |\n|---|---|---|\n| Simple | FV = PV(1 + r×n) | **₹3,00,000** |\n| Compound | FV = PV(1+r)ⁿ | **₹6,72,750** |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Compounding frequency and effective rates\n\n$$FV = PV \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m \\times n} \\qquad EAR = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1$$\n\n**A nominal 12%, one year, ₹1,00,000:**\n\n| Frequency | m | EAR | Value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Annual | 1 | 12.000% | ₹1,12,000 |\n| Half-yearly | 2 | 12.360% | ₹1,12,360 |\n| Quarterly | 4 | 12.551% | ₹1,12,551 |\n| Monthly | 12 | 12.683% | ₹1,12,683 |\n| Daily | 365 | 12.747% | ₹1,12,747 |\n| Continuous | ∞ | 12.750% (e^r − 1) | ₹1,12,750 |\n\n> 🧠 The **nominal rate never changes** — only the effective rate rises, and by shrinking amounts. **Always compare deposits on EAR.** Excel: `=EFFECT(nominal, periods)` and `=NOMINAL(effective, periods)`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Annuities — regular payments (Chapter 2)\n\nAn **annuity** = equal cash flows at equal intervals (a SIP, an EMI, a pension).\n\n$$FV_{ord} = PMT \\times \\frac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r} \\qquad PV_{ord} = PMT \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Annuity DUE} = \\text{Annuity ORDINARY} \\times (1+r)}$$\n\n**Ordinary** = paid at the **end** of each period (the default). **Due** = paid at the **beginning**, so every payment earns one extra period → always worth more.\n\n**Worked (FV):** ₹10,000 a month, 15 years, 12% p.a. monthly.\nr = 0.01, n = 180, (1.01)¹⁸⁰ = **5.9958**\nFV = 10,000 × [(5.9958 − 1) ÷ 0.01] = 10,000 × **499.58** = **₹49,95,800**\n*(Contributed ₹18,00,000 — so returns supplied ₹31,95,800, nearly two-thirds.)*\n\n### Solving for the payment\n\n| You know | Formula | Excel |\n|---|---|---|\n| The **target corpus** (FV) | PMT = FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] | `=PMT(rate,nper,0,fv)` |\n| The **loan amount** (PV) — the EMI | PMT = PV × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] | `=PMT(rate,nper,-pv)` |\n\n### Perpetuity\n\n$$PV = \\frac{PMT}{r} \\qquad\\text{growing: } PV = \\frac{PMT_1}{r-g}\\ \\ (\\text{needs } g<r)$$\n\n₹60,000 a year forever at 8% → 60,000 ÷ 0.08 = **₹7,50,000**.\nGrowing at 4% → 60,000 ÷ (0.08 − 0.04) = **₹15,00,000**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Inflation and real returns\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Real return} = \\frac{1 + \\text{nominal}}{1 + \\text{inflation}} - 1} \\qquad \\text{Future cost} = \\text{Today's cost} \\times (1+i)^n$$\n\n**Nominal 9%, inflation 6%:** (1.09 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **2.83%** — *not* 3%.\n\n**The post-tax reality check.** An FD at 7%, inflation 6%, tax 30%:\n- Post-tax nominal = 7% × 0.70 = **4.9%**\n- Real = (1.049 ÷ 1.06) − 1 = **−1.04%**\n\nThe \"safe\" investment **loses purchasing power**. Always compute in this order: **tax first, then deflate.**\n\nReversed, to find the nominal return a goal needs:\n$$\\text{Nominal} = (1+\\text{real})(1+\\text{inflation}) - 1$$\n\n---\n\n## 5. ⭐ The four-step goal calculation\n\nEvery goal caselet in the exam is this, and only this:\n\n| Step | Do | Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | Inflate the goal to its future cost | Cost × (1 + i)ⁿ |\n| **2** | Grow what is already earmarked | Existing × (1 + r)ⁿ |\n| **3** | Find the gap | Step 1 − Step 2 |\n| **4** | Annuitise the gap | PMT = Gap × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n\n**Worked.** Education costing ₹20,00,000 today, needed in 10 years, inflation 7%. Already saved ₹5,00,000. Expected return 11% p.a. (monthly).\n\n1. Future cost = 20,00,000 × (1.07)¹⁰ = 20,00,000 × 1.9672 = **₹39,34,400**\n2. Existing grows = 5,00,000 × (1.11)¹⁰ = 5,00,000 × 2.8394 = **₹14,19,700**\n3. Gap = **₹25,14,700**\n4. r = 0.0091667, n = 120, (1.0091667)¹²⁰ = 2.9853\n   PMT = (25,14,700 × 0.0091667) ÷ 1.9853 = **₹11,611 a month**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Loans and amortisation (Chapter 4)\n\n$$EMI = P \\times \\frac{r(1+r)^n}{(1+r)^n - 1} \\qquad \\text{Total interest} = (EMI \\times n) - P$$\n\n**Worked:** ₹40,00,000 at 9% for 20 years. r = 0.0075, n = 240, (1.0075)²⁴⁰ = **6.00915**\nEMI = 40,00,000 × (0.0075 × 6.00915) ÷ 5.00915 = **₹35,989**\nTotal interest = (35,989 × 240) − 40,00,000 = **₹46,37,360** — more than the loan itself.\n\n### The amortisation split each month\n\n| Component | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Interest | Outstanding balance × monthly rate |\n| Principal | EMI − interest |\n| New balance | Old balance − principal repaid |\n\n**Month 1 of the loan above:** interest = 40,00,000 × 0.0075 = **₹30,000**; principal = 35,989 − 30,000 = **₹5,989**.\n**83% of the first EMI is interest** — which is why early prepayment saves so much, and why a borrower halfway through a 20-year loan has repaid only about a third of the principal.\n\n### Outstanding balance at any point\n$$\\text{Outstanding} = EMI \\times \\frac{1 - (1+r)^{-m}}{r} \\quad (m = \\text{months remaining})$$\n\n### Prepay or invest?\nCompare the **loan rate** with the **post-tax expected return**. Repaying is a **risk-free, tax-free return equal to the interest rate**. A certain 11% beats an expected 10%.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Financial position ratios (Chapter 3)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Healthy | Unit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Savings | Savings ÷ Gross income | ≥ 20% | % |\n| Expenses | Expenses ÷ Gross income | ≤ 80% | % |\n| Leverage | Total liabilities ÷ Total assets | < 0.5 | decimal |\n| Solvency | Net worth ÷ Total assets | > 0.5 | decimal |\n| Liquidity | Liquid assets ÷ **Monthly** expenses | 3–6 | **months** |\n| Financial assets | Financial assets ÷ Total assets | higher | % |\n| Debt-to-income | Total EMIs ÷ Gross income | < 35–40% | % |\n\n**Three self-checks that catch most errors:**\n1. Savings % + Expenses % = **100**\n2. Solvency + Leverage = **1**\n3. Liquidity is in **months** — a percentage-looking answer means you divided by the wrong thing\n\n> ⚠️ If given **annual** expenses, divide by 12 before computing liquidity. And **equity and PPF are not liquid assets.**\n\n---\n\n## 8. Bond maths (Chapter 9)\n\n$$P = C \\times \\frac{1-(1+y)^{-n}}{y} + \\frac{F}{(1+y)^n}$$\n\nA bond is just an **annuity (the coupons) plus a lump sum (the redemption)**.\n\n| Situation | Adjustment |\n|---|---|\n| Semi-annual | **Halve** C and y, **double** n |\n| Zero-coupon | P = F ÷ (1+y)ⁿ |\n| Perpetual | P = C ÷ y |\n| Between coupons | Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest |\n\n**Worked:** ₹1,000 face, 8% annual coupon, 5 years, required yield 10%.\nAnnuity factor = (1 − 1.10⁻⁵) ÷ 0.10 = **3.7908**; PV of coupons = 80 × 3.7908 = ₹303.26\nPV of redemption = 1,000 ÷ 1.61051 = ₹620.92 → **Price ₹924.18** (a discount, because 8% < 10%).\n\n### Price, coupon and yield\n\n| Coupon vs required yield | Price | Ordering |\n|---|---|---|\n| Coupon **>** yield | **Premium** | Coupon > Current yield > YTM |\n| Coupon **=** yield | **Par** | All three equal |\n| Coupon **<** yield | **Discount** | Coupon < Current yield < **YTM** |\n\n**Current yield** = annual coupon ÷ **market price**. **YTM** = the rate equating PV of all cash flows to price (assumes coupons reinvested at the YTM and held to maturity).\n\n**Approximate YTM** (handy when no calculator function is allowed):\n$$YTM \\approx \\frac{C + (F-P)/n}{(F+P)/2}$$\n\n### Duration\n$$\\text{Modified duration} = \\frac{\\text{Macaulay duration}}{1+y} \\qquad \\boxed{\\%\\Delta P \\approx -\\text{MD} \\times \\Delta y}$$\n\n- **A zero-coupon bond's Macaulay duration = its maturity.** (Most-tested duration fact.)\n- Longer maturity → **higher** duration. Higher coupon → **lower** duration.\n- Expect rates to **fall** → go **long** duration.\n\n**Worked:** MD 6.2, yields rise 0.75% → −6.2 × 0.0075 = **−4.65%**. On ₹10,00,000 that is a **₹46,500** loss.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Equity valuation (Chapter 8)\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Breaks when |\n|---|---|---|\n| P/E | Price ÷ EPS | Earnings are **negative** |\n| Earnings yield | EPS ÷ Price (= 1 ÷ P/E) | — |\n| P/B | Price ÷ Book value per share | Assets are intangible |\n| P/S | Price ÷ Sales per share | Ignores profitability entirely |\n| PEG | P/E ÷ growth % | Growth forecast is unreliable (**<1** = attractive) |\n| EV | Market cap + Debt − Cash | — |\n| EV/EBITDA | EV ÷ EBITDA | — (capital-structure neutral) |\n| Dividend yield | DPS ÷ Price | A **falling price** inflates it |\n| EVA | NOPAT − (Capital × Cost of capital) | — (positive = real value created) |\n\n**DCF:** Value = Σ CFₜ ÷ (1+r)ᵗ + TV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ, where **TV = CF ÷ (r − g)** and **g must be < r**.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Option payoffs (Chapter 10)\n\n| Position | Max gain | Max loss | Break-even |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Long call** | Unlimited | Premium | **Strike + Premium** |\n| **Long put** | Large (capped) | Premium | **Strike − Premium** |\n| **Short call** | Premium | **UNLIMITED** ⚠️ | Strike + Premium |\n| **Short put** | Premium | Large (capped) | Strike − Premium |\n\n**Intrinsic value:** Call = max(Spot − Strike, 0) · Put = max(Strike − Spot, 0). **Time value** = Premium − Intrinsic, and it decays to **zero** at expiry.\n\n**Futures fair value:** F = S × (1 + r − q)^T. Normally **F > S** (contango), and **F converges to S at expiry**.\n\n> ⚠️ **ITM ≠ profitable.** A call struck at ₹1,000 bought for ₹40 with spot at ₹1,020 has ₹20 of intrinsic value but **loses ₹20** — the premium must be recovered first.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Portfolio risk and return (Chapters 14 & 16)\n\n### Expected return and risk\n\n$$E(R_p) = \\sum w_i R_i \\qquad \\sigma_p = \\sqrt{w_1^2\\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1w_2\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\rho_{12}}$$\n\nPortfolio **return** is a simple weighted average. Portfolio **risk is not** — it is lower than the weighted average whenever **ρ < 1**. That gap is the entire benefit of diversification.\n\n### Return measures\n\n| Measure | Formula | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Holding period return | (End − Start + Income) ÷ Start | Single period |\n| CAGR / geometric mean | (End ÷ Start)^(1/n) − 1 | **Compounded** growth |\n| Arithmetic mean | Simple average of returns | **Always ≥ GMR**; gap widens with volatility |\n| Annualising | (1 + HPR)^(1/years) − 1 | Converting a part-year return |\n| TWRR | Chain-links sub-period returns | Measures the **manager** (removes client cash flows) |\n| MWRR | The IRR of all cash flows | Measures the **investor's** actual experience |\n\n### Risk-adjusted measures\n\n| Measure | Formula | Divides by |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sharpe** | (Rp − Rf) ÷ σp | **Total** risk |\n| **Treynor** | (Rp − Rf) ÷ βp | **Systematic** risk |\n| **Sortino** | (Rp − Rf) ÷ downside deviation | **Downside** risk only |\n| **Information ratio** | Alpha ÷ Tracking error | Active risk |\n| **Alpha (Jensen)** | Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)] | — |\n\n**CAPM:** Required return = Rf + β × (Rm − Rf).\n\n> 🧠 **Sharpe vs Treynor:** use **Sharpe** for a whole portfolio (total risk matters), **Treynor** for one sleeve of an already-diversified portfolio (only its systematic contribution matters).\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Quick Reference — everything on one screen\n\n| # | Need | Formula |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Future value | PV × (1+r)ⁿ |\n| 2 | Present value | FV ÷ (1+r)ⁿ |\n| 3 | CAGR / rate | (FV÷PV)^(1/n) − 1 |\n| 4 | Periods | ln(FV÷PV) ÷ ln(1+r) |\n| 5 | Rule of 72 | Years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate% |\n| 6 | Non-annual compounding | PV × (1 + r/m)^(mn) |\n| 7 | Effective annual rate | (1 + r/m)^m − 1 |\n| 8 | FV of annuity | PMT × [((1+r)ⁿ − 1) ÷ r] |\n| 9 | PV of annuity | PMT × [(1 − (1+r)⁻ⁿ) ÷ r] |\n| 10 | Annuity due | Ordinary × (1+r) |\n| 11 | PMT from target | FV × r ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| 12 | EMI | P × r(1+r)ⁿ ÷ [(1+r)ⁿ − 1] |\n| 13 | Total interest | (EMI × n) − P |\n| 14 | Monthly interest | Outstanding × r |\n| 15 | Outstanding balance | PV of remaining EMIs |\n| 16 | Perpetuity | PMT ÷ r |\n| 17 | Growing perpetuity | PMT₁ ÷ (r − g), g < r |\n| 18 | Real return | [(1+nom) ÷ (1+inf)] − 1 |\n| 19 | Future goal cost | Cost × (1 + i)ⁿ |\n| 20 | Nominal from real | (1+real)(1+inf) − 1 |\n| 21 | Savings ratio | Savings ÷ Income |\n| 22 | Solvency ratio | Net worth ÷ Assets |\n| 23 | Leverage ratio | Liabilities ÷ Assets |\n| 24 | Liquidity ratio | Liquid ÷ **Monthly** expenses |\n| 25 | Debt-to-income | EMIs ÷ Income |\n| 26 | Bond price | C×[(1−(1+y)⁻ⁿ)÷y] + F÷(1+y)ⁿ |\n| 27 | Current yield | Coupon ÷ Market price |\n| 28 | Approx YTM | [C + (F−P)/n] ÷ [(F+P)/2] |\n| 29 | Modified duration | Macaulay ÷ (1+y) |\n| 30 | Bond price change | ≈ −MD × Δy |\n| 31 | Enterprise value | Mkt cap + Debt − Cash |\n| 32 | Earnings yield | EPS ÷ Price |\n| 33 | PEG | P/E ÷ growth% |\n| 34 | EVA | NOPAT − (Capital × WACC) |\n| 35 | DCF terminal value | CF ÷ (r − g) |\n| 36 | Call break-even | Strike + Premium |\n| 37 | Put break-even | Strike − Premium |\n| 38 | Call intrinsic | max(Spot − Strike, 0) |\n| 39 | Futures fair value | S × (1 + r − q)^T |\n| 40 | Portfolio return | Σ wᵢRᵢ |\n| 41 | Two-asset risk | √(w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ) |\n| 42 | CAPM | Rf + β(Rm − Rf) |\n| 43 | Sharpe | (Rp − Rf) ÷ σ |\n| 44 | Treynor | (Rp − Rf) ÷ β |\n| 45 | Alpha | Rp − [Rf + β(Rm − Rf)] |\n| 46 | Information ratio | Alpha ÷ Tracking error |\n| 47 | NAV | (Assets − Liabilities) ÷ Units |\n\n---\n\n## The five traps that cost the most marks\n\n1. **Period mismatch** — annual rate with monthly periods. Convert *both*.\n2. **Forgetting to inflate the goal** — today's cost is never the target.\n3. **Real return by subtraction** — use the division formula; and **tax before deflating**.\n4. **Ordinary vs due** — \"beginning of the period\" means × (1+r).\n5. **Answering the wrong quantity** — total repaid instead of interest, contributions instead of corpus, the grown corpus instead of the gap.\n\n> **In the exam:** the test machine has a spreadsheet. Before you start, type `=PMT(`, `=FV(`, `=PV(`, `=RATE(` once each to confirm they work. Then convert every rate to its per-period value **before** you touch a formula.\n", "excel": "# 🧮 The Spreadsheet Guide — Every Calculation the X-A Exam Can Throw At You\n\n> **Why this file exists.** The NISM test centre gives you a spreadsheet, not a financial calculator. Candidates lose marks not because they don't know the formula but because they fumble the *signs*, the *rate units*, or the *annuity-due switch* under time pressure. This guide makes those three things automatic.\n>\n> Everything here works identically in **Microsoft Excel**, **LibreOffice Calc**, **Google Sheets** and **WPS Spreadsheets**. The NISM test machine typically ships with LibreOffice Calc.\n\n---\n\n## Part 0 — The 90-second setup you do at the start of the exam\n\nBefore you attempt a single numerical question, build this in the spreadsheet. It takes a minute and it pays for itself on question three.\n\n| Cell | Put this in | Why |\n|------|-------------|-----|\n| A1 | `rate` | label |\n| B1 | *(leave blank)* | you'll type the periodic rate here |\n| A2 | `nper` | label |\n| B2 | *(blank)* | number of periods |\n| A3 | `pmt` | label |\n| B3 | *(blank)* | payment per period |\n| A4 | `pv` | label |\n| B4 | *(blank)* | present value |\n| A5 | `fv` | label |\n| B5 | *(blank)* | future value |\n| A6 | `type` | label |\n| B6 | `0` | 0 = end of period (ordinary), 1 = beginning (due) |\n\nThen in **B8** put:\n\n```\n=FV(B1,B2,B3,B4,B6)\n```\n\nand in **B9**:\n\n```\n=PV(B1,B2,B3,B5,B6)\n```\n\nand in **B10**:\n\n```\n=PMT(B1,B2,B4,B5,B6)\n```\n\nand in **B11**:\n\n```\n=RATE(B2,B3,B4,B5,B6)\n```\n\nand in **B12**:\n\n```\n=NPER(B1,B3,B4,B5,B6)\n```\n\nNow every TVM question is: *fill in the four you know, read the one you don't.* You never retype a formula again.\n\n> ⚠️ **The one trap in this setup:** a blank cell is read as `0`. If a question has no PMT, leaving B3 blank is correct. But if a question has no PV and you leave B4 from the *previous* question sitting there, your answer will be wrong and look plausible. **Clear B1:B6 between questions.** Select B1:B6 → `Delete`. Two keystrokes. Do it every single time.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — The sign convention (this is where most marks are lost)\n\nSpreadsheets treat money as a **cash flow**, not a quantity. So:\n\n- Money **leaving your pocket** → **negative**\n- Money **coming into your pocket** → **positive**\n\nThat is the *entire* rule. Everything else follows.\n\n### What that means in practice\n\n| Situation | PV | PMT | FV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| You invest ₹1,00,000 today and it grows | `-100000` (you paid it out) | 0 | *result comes out positive* |\n| You save ₹5,000 a month towards a goal | 0 | `-5000` (you pay it out) | *result positive* |\n| You take a ₹30,00,000 home loan | `30000000` (bank gave it to you) | *result negative* — you pay the EMI | 0 |\n| You want ₹50,00,000 in 15 years, how much monthly? | 0 | *result negative* | `50000000` |\n\n### The lazy shortcut that always works\n\nIf you don't want to think about signs at all, **wrap the formula in `ABS()`** and reason about the direction yourself:\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(8%/12, 240, 3000000))\n```\n\nThis returns the EMI as a clean positive number. The exam options are always positive rupee amounts, so `ABS()` costs you nothing and saves you from a minus-sign disaster.\n\n> **But** — and this matters — `ABS()` only saves you when there is **one** outflow and **one** inflow. The moment a question has *both* a lump sum **and** a stream of payments (e.g. \"you have ₹5 lakh today and will add ₹10,000 a month\"), the signs of PV and PMT must **agree with each other** (both negative, if both are money you're putting in). Get that wrong and `ABS()` will happily return a confidently wrong number.\n\n**The rule to memorise: PV and PMT flowing the same direction take the same sign.**\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — The rate and period units (the second-biggest mark-loser)\n\n**`rate` and `nper` must be in the same unit.** Always. No exceptions.\n\n| The question says | rate | nper |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12% p.a., 5 years, **annual** compounding | `12%` | `5` |\n| 12% p.a., 5 years, **monthly** compounding/payments | `12%/12` | `5*12` |\n| 12% p.a., 5 years, **quarterly** | `12%/4` | `5*4` |\n| 12% p.a., 5 years, **half-yearly** | `12%/2` | `5*2` |\n| 9% p.a. on a 20-year home loan (EMI is monthly) | `9%/12` | `20*12` |\n\nWrite the arithmetic **into the formula** — `12%/12`, not `0.01`. It documents itself, and if you misread the tenure you fix one number instead of recomputing a decimal.\n\n> 🎯 **Exam tip:** whenever a question mentions **EMI**, **SIP**, or **monthly**, your rate is divided by 12 and your nper is multiplied by 12. Make that reflexive.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 — The core five functions\n\n### `FV` — what will it grow to?\n\n```\n=FV(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])\n```\n\n**Q: ₹2,00,000 invested for 8 years at 11% p.a. What is the maturity value?**\n\n```\n=FV(11%, 8, 0, -200000)          →  ₹4,60,910\n```\n\n**Q: ₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% p.a. What is the corpus?**\n\n```\n=FV(12%/12, 15*12, -10000, 0)    →  ₹49,95,800\n```\n\n**Q: Both — ₹5,00,000 today *plus* ₹10,000 a month, 15 years, 12%.**\n\n```\n=FV(12%/12, 15*12, -10000, -500000)   →  ₹79,93,700\n```\n\n*(Note both are negative — both are money you put in.)*\n\n---\n\n### `PV` — what is it worth today?\n\n```\n=PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])\n```\n\n**Q: You need ₹40,00,000 in 12 years. At 9%, what lump sum today gets you there?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PV(9%, 12, 0, 4000000))     →  ₹14,22,140\n```\n\n**Q: A pension pays ₹30,000 a month for 20 years. At 8%, what is it worth today?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PV(8%/12, 20*12, 30000))    →  ₹35,86,630\n```\n\n---\n\n### `PMT` — how much per period?\n\n```\n=PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])\n```\n\nThis is the **EMI function** and the **SIP function**. Same formula, different story.\n\n**Q (EMI): ₹35,00,000 home loan, 9% p.a., 20 years. EMI?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(9%/12, 20*12, 3500000))     →  ₹31,490\n```\n\n**Q (SIP): You need ₹1,00,00,000 in 18 years at 12%. Monthly SIP?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(12%/12, 18*12, 0, 10000000)) →  ₹13,195\n```\n\n> Note where the target goes: for a **loan** the amount is `pv` (you have the money now, you owe it). For a **goal** the amount is `fv` (you don't have it, you want it later). Putting the goal in the wrong slot is the single most common SIP-question error.\n\n---\n\n### `RATE` — what return does this imply?\n\n```\n=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])\n```\n\n**Q: ₹5,00,000 grows to ₹9,00,000 in 7 years. What is the CAGR?**\n\n```\n=RATE(7, 0, -500000, 900000)     →  8.76%\n```\n\n**Q: You pay ₹25,000 a month for 5 years and receive ₹20,00,000. What return is that?**\n\n```\n=RATE(5*12, -25000, 0, 2000000)  →  0.9356% per month\n```\n\n⚠️ **`RATE` returns the rate *per period*.** That answer is monthly. To annualise it the exam way:\n\n```\n=RATE(5*12, -25000, 0, 2000000)*12        →  11.23% nominal annual\n=(1+RATE(5*12,-25000,0,2000000))^12-1     →  11.82% effective annual\n```\n\nRead the question: \"annual rate\" usually means the **nominal** (×12) figure; \"effective annual return\" means the **compounded** one.\n\n> 💡 `RATE` is solved iteratively and occasionally returns `#NUM!` if it can't converge. Add a guess as the 6th argument: `=RATE(nper, pmt, pv, fv, type, 0.1)`.\n\n---\n\n### `NPER` — how long will it take?\n\n```\n=NPER(rate, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])\n```\n\n**Q: ₹3,00,000 at 10% p.a. — how long to double?**\n\n```\n=NPER(10%, 0, -300000, 600000)   →  7.27 years\n```\n\n*(Sanity check with the Rule of 72: 72 ÷ 10 = 7.2. Close. Good.)*\n\n**Q: ₹8,00,000 credit-card debt at 18% p.a., paying ₹20,000 a month. How many months?**\n\n```\n=NPER(18%/12, -20000, 800000)    →  61.5 months  (≈ 5 years 2 months)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 — Annuity due (the `type` argument)\n\nThe 5th argument of `FV`/`PV` and the 5th of `PMT` is `type`:\n\n- `0` or omitted → **ordinary annuity** — payment at the **end** of each period\n- `1` → **annuity due** — payment at the **beginning** of each period\n\n**When is it due?** Rent, insurance premiums, lease payments, and *most SIPs* are paid at the **start** of the period. Loan EMIs are paid at the **end**.\n\n**Q: ₹10,000 a month for 10 years at 12%, paid at the beginning of each month.**\n\n```\n=FV(12%/12, 10*12, -10000, 0, 1)    →  ₹23,23,391\n=FV(12%/12, 10*12, -10000, 0, 0)    →  ₹23,00,387\n```\n\nThe difference — **₹23,004** — is exactly **one period's interest** on the whole corpus: `23,00,387 × 1% = 23,004`. That is not a coincidence, it is the identity below.\n\n> ✅ **The identity worth memorising:** `Annuity due = Ordinary annuity × (1 + r)`. If you forget the `type` argument entirely, you can still fix an ordinary-annuity answer by multiplying by `(1+r)`. This appears in the exam as a standalone conceptual question, so know it as a formula *and* as a spreadsheet switch.\n\n---\n\n## Part 5 — Rates: nominal, effective, and real\n\n### Effective annual rate from a nominal rate\n\n```\n=EFFECT(nominal_rate, npery)\n```\n\n**Q: 12% p.a. compounded monthly — what is the effective rate?**\n\n```\n=EFFECT(12%, 12)            →  12.6825%\n=(1+12%/12)^12-1            →  12.6825%   (same thing, done manually)\n```\n\n### Nominal from effective (the reverse)\n\n```\n=NOMINAL(effective_rate, npery)\n```\n\n**Q: You need an effective 15%. What nominal rate compounded quarterly delivers it?**\n\n```\n=NOMINAL(15%, 4)            →  14.2232%\n```\n\n### Real (inflation-adjusted) return — **the formula the exam actually wants**\n\n```\n=(1+nominal)/(1+inflation)-1\n```\n\n**Q: Investment returns 11%, inflation is 6%. Real return?**\n\n```\n=(1+11%)/(1+6%)-1           →  4.717%\n```\n\n⚠️ **Not 5%.** The exam deliberately offers 5% as a distractor for candidates who subtract. Subtracting is an approximation that is only acceptable when both numbers are small; NISM tests the exact formula.\n\n### The goal calculation — inflate first, then discount\n\nThis is the classic 5-mark caselet, and it is always the same two steps.\n\n**Q: A child's education costs ₹15,00,000 today. It is 12 years away, education inflation is 9%, and you can earn 12%. What monthly SIP is needed?**\n\n**Step 1 — inflate the goal to what it will actually cost on the day:**\n```\nB1: =FV(9%, 12, 0, -1500000)            →  ₹42,18,997\n```\n\n**Step 2 — solve for the SIP that reaches *that* number at your nominal return:**\n```\nB2: =ABS(PMT(12%/12, 12*12, 0, B1))     →  ₹13,223\n```\n\n**Answer: about ₹13,220 a month.** Two cells, no algebra.\n\n> ⚠️ **A warning about the \"just use the real rate\" shortcut.** You will see people compute `real = (1.12/1.09)−1 = 2.752%` and run `PMT` on today's ₹15,00,000. That gives about **₹8,890** — and it is *not* an alternative route to the same answer. It answers a **different question**: it is the *first* instalment of a SIP that you **escalate by 9% every year** (rising to roughly ₹22,940 by the final year). Both plans reach the goal; they are different products.\n>\n> **NISM asks for the level SIP.** Unless a question explicitly says the investment is \"stepped up\" or \"increased annually with income\", inflate the goal and use the **nominal** rate — Step 1 then Step 2, every time.\n\n---\n\n## Part 6 — Loans and amortisation\n\n### The EMI\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(annual_rate/12, years*12, loan_amount))\n```\n\n### How much of *this month's* EMI is interest vs principal?\n\n```\n=IPMT(rate, per, nper, pv)      ← interest portion of payment number `per`\n=PPMT(rate, per, nper, pv)      ← principal portion of payment number `per`\n```\n\n**Q: ₹30,00,000 at 8.5% for 20 years. Split the 1st and the 120th EMI.**\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT (8.5%/12,      20*12, 3000000))   →  ₹26,035   the EMI itself\n=ABS(IPMT(8.5%/12, 1,   20*12, 3000000))   →  ₹21,250   interest\n=ABS(PPMT(8.5%/12, 1,   20*12, 3000000))   →  ₹4,785    principal\n=ABS(IPMT(8.5%/12, 120, 20*12, 3000000))   →  ₹14,952   interest\n=ABS(PPMT(8.5%/12, 120, 20*12, 3000000))   →  ₹11,083   principal\n```\n\n**Read that.** In month 1, **82%** of the EMI is interest. By month 120 it's still **57%**. That shifting mix is the whole point of an amortisation schedule and it's a standard conceptual question: *\"In the early years of a home loan, the EMI consists mainly of ____.\"* Answer: **interest**.\n\n`IPMT + PPMT = PMT` always. Use that as your check.\n\n### Total interest paid over the life of a loan\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(rate,nper,pv))*nper - pv\n```\n\n**Q: ₹30,00,000 at 8.5% for 20 years — total interest?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PMT(8.5%/12,240,3000000))*240 - 3000000   →  ₹32,48,000\n```\n\nYou pay **more in interest than you borrowed**. This is the number that makes prepayment questions make sense.\n\n### Outstanding balance after *k* payments\n\n```\n=ABS(FV(rate, k, PMT(rate,nper,pv), -pv))\n```\n\nOr, more readably, the PV of the remaining payments:\n\n```\n=ABS(PV(rate, nper-k, PMT(rate,nper,pv)))\n```\n\n**Q: ₹30,00,000 at 8.5% / 20 years. Outstanding after 5 years?**\n\n```\n=ABS(PV(8.5%/12, 240-60, ABS(PMT(8.5%/12,240,3000000))))   →  ₹26,43,800\n```\n\nAfter paying for a quarter of the tenure you've retired barely **12%** of the principal. That's the amortisation curve, and it's why prepaying **early** saves so much more than prepaying late.\n\n### Cumulative interest / principal over a range\n\n```\n=CUMIPMT(rate, nper, pv, start_period, end_period, type)\n=CUMPRINC(rate, nper, pv, start_period, end_period, type)\n```\n\n**Q: Interest paid in years 1–5 of that loan?**\n\n```\n=ABS(CUMIPMT(8.5%/12, 240, 3000000, 1, 60, 0))   →  ₹12,05,900\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 7 — Uneven cash flows: `NPV`, `IRR` and `XIRR`\n\nEvery function so far assumes **equal** payments. Real portfolios don't work that way.\n\n### `NPV` — present value of an uneven stream\n\n```\n=NPV(rate, value1, value2, ...)\n```\n\n⚠️ **The single most misunderstood function in the whole exam.** Excel's `NPV` discounts the **first** value by one period — it assumes cash flows start at the **end of period 1**. So if you have an initial investment at **time 0**, it must sit *outside* the function:\n\n```\n=C0 + NPV(rate, C1:Cn)\n```\n\nwhere `C0` is negative (your outlay).\n\n**Q: Invest ₹10,00,000 now; receive ₹3,00,000 / ₹4,00,000 / ₹5,00,000 over three years. At 10%, is it worth doing?**\n\n```\nA1: -1000000\nA2:  300000\nA3:  400000\nA4:  500000\n\n=A1 + NPV(10%, A2:A4)       →  ₹−21,037  → reject, NPV is negative\n```\n\nIf you had written `=NPV(10%, A1:A4)` you'd get **−₹19,124** — wrong, because it discounts the time-0 outlay by a year it shouldn't.\n\n### `IRR` — the rate that makes NPV zero\n\n```\n=IRR(values, [guess])\n```\n\nValues **must** include the time-0 outflow, and they **must** be evenly spaced.\n\n```\n=IRR(A1:A4)                 →  8.90%\n```\n\nSince IRR (8.90%) < required return (10%), reject — consistent with the negative NPV. **NPV and IRR always agree on accept/reject for a simple project.** That consistency is itself an exam question.\n\n### `XIRR` — the one you'll actually use for a client portfolio\n\n```\n=XIRR(values, dates, [guess])\n```\n\nReal investments happen on **arbitrary dates**. `XIRR` handles that and returns an **annualised effective** rate.\n\n```\n        A                B\n1   01-04-2021      -100000\n2   15-09-2021       -50000\n3   20-01-2023       -75000\n4   31-03-2025       280000     ← final value, positive\n\n=XIRR(B1:B4, A1:A4)         →  6.81% p.a.\n```\n\n> 🎯 **This is the function to know for the portfolio-evaluation chapters.** When a client asks \"what return did I actually get on my SIP?\", `XIRR` is the answer — it is the money-weighted return. Contrast it with **TWRR**, which strips out the timing of cash flows to measure the *manager's* skill. NISM asks you to distinguish the two: **XIRR/MWRR judges the investor's outcome; TWRR judges the manager.**\n\n### `MIRR` — when reinvestment rate ≠ IRR\n\n```\n=MIRR(values, finance_rate, reinvest_rate)\n```\n\nKnow that it exists and *why*: plain IRR implicitly assumes interim cash flows are reinvested at the IRR itself, which is often unrealistic. MIRR lets you set a realistic reinvestment rate. That \"unrealistic reinvestment assumption\" is IRR's standard textbook criticism.\n\n---\n\n## Part 8 — Returns: CAGR, absolute, annualised\n\n| Measure | Formula | When to use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Absolute return** | `=(End-Begin)/Begin` | Holding period ≤ 1 year, or when the question says \"total return\" |\n| **CAGR** | `=(End/Begin)^(1/years)-1` | Holding period > 1 year, single in / single out |\n| **Annualised (short period)** | `=(1+absolute)^(365/days)-1` | Converting a 4-month return to annual |\n| **XIRR** | `=XIRR(...)` | Multiple, irregular cash flows |\n\n**Q: ₹1,00,000 becomes ₹1,75,000 in 6 years. CAGR?**\n\n```\n=(175000/100000)^(1/6)-1    →  9.78%\n```\n\n**Q: 8% return earned over 5 months. Annualised?**\n\n```\n=(1+8%)^(12/5)-1            →  20.3%\n```\n\n### Holding-period return with income\n\n```\n=(End - Begin + Income)/Begin\n```\n\nA stock bought at ₹450, sold at ₹520, paying ₹12 dividend:\n```\n=(520-450+12)/450           →  18.22%\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 9 — Bonds\n\n| Want | Function |\n|---|---|\n| Price from yield | `=PRICE(settlement, maturity, coupon, yld, redemption, frequency, [basis])` |\n| Yield from price | `=YIELD(settlement, maturity, coupon, pr, redemption, frequency, [basis])` |\n| Macaulay duration | `=DURATION(settlement, maturity, coupon, yld, frequency, [basis])` |\n| Modified duration | `=MDURATION(settlement, maturity, coupon, yld, frequency, [basis])` |\n| Accrued interest | `=ACCRINT(issue, first_interest, settlement, rate, par, frequency, [basis])` |\n\n**Q: A bond maturing 31-Mar-2032, 7.5% coupon paid half-yearly, yielding 8%, settling 01-Apr-2025. Price per ₹100 face?**\n\n```\n=PRICE(DATE(2025,4,1), DATE(2032,3,31), 7.5%, 8%, 100, 2)   →  ₹97.35  (approx)\n```\n\nYield (8%) > coupon (7.5%) → the bond trades at a **discount** (below ₹100). Always sanity-check that direction:\n\n| Relationship | Bond trades at |\n|---|---|\n| Coupon > Yield | **Premium** (above par) |\n| Coupon = Yield | **Par** |\n| Coupon < Yield | **Discount** (below par) |\n\n### The duration question, done without dates\n\nMost exam bond questions give you duration directly and ask for the price change:\n\n```\n% price change ≈ −Modified duration × Δyield (in decimal)\n```\n\n**Q: Modified duration 6.2, yields rise 50 bps. Price impact?**\n\n```\n=-6.2 * 0.005               →  −3.1%\n```\n\nAlso know:\n```\nModified duration = Macaulay duration / (1 + y/n)\n```\n```\n=DURATION(...)/(1+yld/frequency)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 10 — Portfolio risk and return\n\n### Expected return of a portfolio\n\n```\n=SUMPRODUCT(weights_range, returns_range)\n```\n\nWeights in `A1:A4`, expected returns in `B1:B4`:\n```\n=SUMPRODUCT(A1:A4, B1:B4)\n```\n\n### Standard deviation of a return series\n\n```\n=STDEV.S(range)      ← sample (use this for historical returns)\n=STDEV.P(range)      ← population (use only if told it's the full population)\n```\n\n> In LibreOffice Calc these are `STDEV()` and `STDEVP()`. Both accept the Excel names too. **Use the sample version for historical return data** — that's the convention NISM follows.\n\n### Covariance, correlation, beta\n\n```\n=COVARIANCE.S(range1, range2)\n=CORREL(range1, range2)\n=SLOPE(asset_returns, market_returns)      ← this IS beta\n```\n\n`SLOPE` is the fastest way to get beta from raw data. Equivalently:\n\n```\nBeta = Covariance(asset, market) / Variance(market)\n     = Correlation × (σ_asset / σ_market)\n```\n```\n=CORREL(A1:A60,B1:B60)*STDEV.S(A1:A60)/STDEV.S(B1:B60)\n```\n\n### Two-asset portfolio standard deviation\n\n```\nσp = √( w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2·w₁·w₂·σ₁·σ₂·ρ )\n```\n```\n=SQRT(w1^2*s1^2 + w2^2*s2^2 + 2*w1*w2*s1*s2*rho)\n```\n\n**Q: 60% in A (σ 18%), 40% in B (σ 12%), correlation 0.30.**\n\n```\n=SQRT(0.6^2*0.18^2 + 0.4^2*0.12^2 + 2*0.6*0.4*0.18*0.12*0.3)   →  13.07%\n```\n\n> 🎯 **The diversification point, in one number.** The weighted-average σ is `0.6×18 + 0.4×12 = 15.6%`. The actual portfolio σ is **13.07%**. That **2.53 percentage points of risk vanished for free** — purely because ρ < 1. This is the single most important idea in Modern Portfolio Theory, and the exam tests it as *\"portfolio risk is less than the weighted average of individual risks whenever correlation is less than 1.\"* Only at **ρ = +1** does the gain disappear entirely.\n\n### The performance ratios\n\n| Ratio | Formula | Risk measure used | Answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Sharpe** | `=(Rp-Rf)/σp` | Total risk (σ) | Return per unit of **total** risk |\n| **Treynor** | `=(Rp-Rf)/βp` | Market risk (β) | Return per unit of **market** risk |\n| **Sortino** | `=(Rp-Rf)/downside_σ` | Downside deviation | Return per unit of **bad** volatility |\n| **Jensen's Alpha** | `=Rp-(Rf+β*(Rm-Rf))` | — | Excess over what CAPM predicted |\n| **Information Ratio** | `=(Rp-Rb)/tracking_error` | Tracking error | Skill vs the **benchmark** |\n\n**Worked: Rp = 14%, Rf = 6%, σ = 11%, β = 1.2, Rm = 12%.**\n\n```\nSharpe   =(14%-6%)/11%                    →  0.727\nTreynor  =(14%-6%)/1.2                    →  0.0667  (i.e. 6.67%)\nAlpha    =14%-(6%+1.2*(12%-6%))           →  0.8%\n```\n\n> **When to use which:** Sharpe for a **standalone** portfolio (total risk is what the client bears). Treynor for **one sleeve of an already-diversified** portfolio (only market risk is left). Alpha for **did the manager beat CAPM**. Information ratio for **did the manager beat the benchmark, consistently**.\n\n---\n\n## Part 11 — Goal Seek: solving backwards without algebra\n\nSometimes a question can't be inverted with a function. Goal Seek brute-forces it.\n\n**Excel:** `Data → What-If Analysis → Goal Seek`\n**LibreOffice Calc:** `Tools → Goal Seek`\n\nThree boxes:\n- **Set cell** — the formula cell whose result you want to fix\n- **To value** — the number you want it to equal\n- **By changing cell** — the input it's allowed to move\n\n**Q: You can save ₹18,000 a month for 14 years. What return do you need to reach ₹75,00,000?**\n\n```\nB1: 10%                    (a guess — the rate)\nB2: =FV(B1/12, 14*12, -18000, 0)\n```\nGoal Seek → Set cell `B2`, To value `7500000`, By changing cell `B1`. It returns roughly **11.6%**.\n\n*(`RATE` would also solve this one. Goal Seek earns its keep on multi-step models — inflation-adjusted goals, blended portfolios, retirement corpus draw-downs — where no single function inverts.)*\n\n---\n\n## Part 12 — Formatting so you don't misread your own answer\n\nUnder time pressure, `1234567.891` gets misread as ₹12 lakh instead of ₹12.3 lakh. Two habits fix this:\n\n**Round for display, never mid-calculation:**\n```\n=ROUND(ABS(PMT(9%/12,240,3500000)), 0)\n```\nRound **once, at the end**. Rounding an intermediate rate to 2 decimals and feeding it forward is a classic source of \"my answer is close to option B but not exact.\"\n\n**Convert to lakh / crore explicitly:**\n```\n=B2/100000    &\" lakh\"\n=B2/10000000  &\" crore\"\n```\n\n> ⚠️ If your answer is *close to* an option but not exact, the cause is almost always (a) an intermediate rounding, (b) ordinary-vs-due, or (c) nominal-vs-effective rate. Check those three before assuming the option list is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## Part 13 — Function reference card\n\n*(Print this. Everything above, compressed.)*\n\n| Function | Signature | Returns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `FV` | `(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])` | Future value |\n| `PV` | `(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])` | Present value |\n| `PMT` | `(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])` | Payment per period (EMI / SIP) |\n| `RATE` | `(nper, pmt, pv, [fv], [type], [guess])` | Rate **per period** |\n| `NPER` | `(rate, pmt, pv, [fv], [type])` | Number of periods |\n| `IPMT` | `(rate, per, nper, pv)` | Interest part of payment `per` |\n| `PPMT` | `(rate, per, nper, pv)` | Principal part of payment `per` |\n| `CUMIPMT` | `(rate, nper, pv, start, end, type)` | Cumulative interest |\n| `CUMPRINC` | `(rate, nper, pv, start, end, type)` | Cumulative principal |\n| `NPV` | `(rate, values...)` | PV of flows **starting at period 1** |\n| `IRR` | `(values, [guess])` | IRR, evenly spaced flows |\n| `XIRR` | `(values, dates, [guess])` | Annualised IRR, irregular dates |\n| `MIRR` | `(values, finance_rate, reinvest_rate)` | IRR with an explicit reinvestment rate |\n| `EFFECT` | `(nominal_rate, npery)` | Effective annual rate |\n| `NOMINAL` | `(effective_rate, npery)` | Nominal annual rate |\n| `PRICE` | `(settle, mat, coupon, yld, redemption, freq)` | Bond price per ₹100 |\n| `YIELD` | `(settle, mat, coupon, pr, redemption, freq)` | Bond YTM |\n| `DURATION` | `(settle, mat, coupon, yld, freq)` | Macaulay duration |\n| `MDURATION` | `(settle, mat, coupon, yld, freq)` | Modified duration |\n| `STDEV.S` | `(range)` | Sample standard deviation |\n| `CORREL` | `(r1, r2)` | Correlation coefficient |\n| `SLOPE` | `(y_range, x_range)` | Beta |\n| `SUMPRODUCT` | `(r1, r2)` | Weighted average (portfolio return) |\n\n---\n\n## Part 14 — The ten errors that actually cost marks\n\nRanked by how often they bite.\n\n1. **Rate not converted to the period.** `9%` where `9%/12` was needed. Every EMI and SIP question.\n2. **`nper` not converted.** `20` where `20*12` was needed. Same questions, same failure.\n3. **PV and PMT signs disagreeing** when both are contributions. `ABS()` hides this instead of fixing it.\n4. **Goal amount in `pv` instead of `fv`.** A loan's amount is PV; a target corpus is FV.\n5. **Forgetting `type=1`** on annuity-due questions. Answer is off by exactly a factor of `(1+r)`.\n6. **Subtracting inflation** instead of `(1+n)/(1+i)-1`. The subtraction answer is always offered as a distractor.\n7. **`RATE` read as annual** when it's per period. Multiply by 12, or compound it — read which one the question wants.\n8. **Time-0 flow inside `NPV`.** Must be `=C0 + NPV(rate, C1:Cn)`.\n9. **Stale cells.** Yesterday's `pv` still sitting in B4. Clear B1:B6 between every question.\n10. **Rounding mid-calculation.** Round once, at the end, for display only.\n\n---\n\n## Part 15 — Drill set (do these until they take 30 seconds each)\n\nCover the answers.\n\n| # | Question | Formula | Answer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ₹5,00,000 at 9% for 10 years, annual compounding | `=FV(9%,10,0,-500000)` | ₹11,83,682 |\n| 2 | ₹5,00,000 at 9% for 10 years, monthly compounding | `=FV(9%/12,120,0,-500000)` | ₹12,25,680 |\n| 3 | Monthly SIP for ₹50,00,000 in 12 years at 12% | `=ABS(PMT(12%/12,144,0,5000000))` | ₹15,671 |\n| 4 | EMI on ₹45,00,000, 8.75%, 25 years | `=ABS(PMT(8.75%/12,300,4500000))` | ₹36,996 |\n| 5 | Total interest on Q4 | `=36996*300-4500000` | ₹65,98,800 |\n| 6 | ₹2,00,000 → ₹5,00,000 in 9 years. CAGR? | `=(500000/200000)^(1/9)-1` | 10.72% |\n| 7 | Effective rate of 10% compounded quarterly | `=EFFECT(10%,4)` | 10.381% |\n| 8 | Real return: 13% nominal, 7% inflation | `=(1+13%)/(1+7%)-1` | 5.607% |\n| 9 | Years to triple ₹1,00,000 at 11% | `=NPER(11%,0,-100000,300000)` | 10.53 |\n| 10 | ₹8,000/month, 20 years, 11%, paid at start | `=FV(11%/12,240,-8000,0,1)` | ₹69,88,600 |\n| 11 | Sharpe: Rp 15%, Rf 6.5%, σ 13% | `=(15%-6.5%)/13%` | 0.654 |\n| 12 | Alpha: Rp 15%, Rf 6.5%, β 1.15, Rm 12% | `=15%-(6.5%+1.15*(12%-6.5%))` | 2.175% |\n| 13 | Price impact: MD 7.4, yields fall 40 bps | `=-7.4*-0.004` | +2.96% |\n| 14 | 2-asset σ: 70/30, σ 20%/10%, ρ 0.2 | `=SQRT(.7^2*.2^2+.3^2*.1^2+2*.7*.3*.2*.1*.2)` | 14.89% |\n| 15 | Outstanding on Q4's loan after 10 years | `=ABS(PV(8.75%/12,180,36996))` | ₹37,01,700 |\n\n*(Answers are rounded; the exam's options will be far enough apart that rounding never changes which one you pick.)*\n\n---\n\n## One last thing\n\nThe spreadsheet is not the hard part — the hard part is **reading the question correctly**. Before you type anything, answer these three out loud:\n\n1. **What am I solving for?** → picks the function\n2. **What's the period?** → sets `rate` and `nper`\n3. **Which way is the money moving?** → sets the signs\n\nDo that and the spreadsheet is a typing exercise. Skip it and you'll get a confident, well-formatted, wrong answer.\n\n**Good luck. 🎯**\n"};
